12685.text:
The point is at 51°S, 9°W, in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies southwest of Bouvet Island and well west of the Greenwich meridian. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge runs near 0–5°W at this latitude, where the ridge is offset by the Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone near 50°S. At 9°W the seafloor is probably on the western flank of the ridge, or in the basin close to it.

The ridge crest in this area is about 2,500–3,000 m deep, and the flanks deepen to about 3,500–4,000 m. I estimate about -3,400 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

12050.text:
The point is at 43°S, 161°E, in the Tasman Sea southwest of Tasmania and west of New Zealand's South Island. It is south of the Lord Howe Rise and near the Macquarie Ridge region, east of Tasmania. Tasmania sits at about 147°E, and the New Zealand Fiordland coast at about 167°E, so 161°E is roughly midway between them.

The Tasman Basin abyssal plain lies at about 4000-4800 m depth. At 43°S, 161°E the area is probably part of the Tasman Basin, around 4,500 m deep. The Challenger Plateau is further north, at about 40°S, 168°E. The South Tasman Rise is at 47°S, 147°E. The Macquarie Ridge begins southwest of Fiordland, near 166°E. So 161°E is west of the ridge, on the abyssal plain, at roughly 4,600 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

12218.text:
Latitude -45, longitude 137 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. The Great Australian Bight is farther north, and the Southern Ocean south of Australia at 45°S is generally abyssal. The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins around 50°S, with depths of about 4000-5000 m.

At 45°S, 137°E the region lies south of the continental shelf, which ends at about 36-38°S. The Southeast Indian Ridge passes through about 47°S at 140°E, and the Australian-Antarctic Discordance sits near 48-49°S, 126°E. At 137°E, the ridge axis is around 49-50°S, so 45°S is on the northern flank. Seafloor there would be roughly 3500-4000 m deep, so I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4347.text:
The point at 41.0°N, 125.0°W is in the Pacific Ocean, offshore of northern California. Cape Mendocino is at about 40.44°N, 124.4°W, and Trinidad Head is at about 41.05°N, 124.15°W. The coast near 41°N sits at about 124.1°W, so the point is roughly 70 km offshore.

The continental shelf there extends about 30 to 40 km out. Beyond that the slope descends toward the Cascadia basin, with the Mendocino Ridge and Gorda plain to the south and west. At 125°W the Cascadia subduction margin has a slope reaching about 2000 m depth. The accretionary wedge there probably puts the seafloor at around -1500 to -2500 m. I estimate the depth at about -1900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

9187.text:
Location: -13, -165 is in the South Pacific, west of the Cook Islands (Penrhyn is at -9, -158) and east of Samoa (-14, -172). Tokelau is at -9, -171. So -13, -165 lies in open ocean in the Samoan Basin / central Pacific basin region. Depth is likely around 5000 m or more; the area between Samoa and Cook Islands has depths of roughly 5000-5500 m. The Penrhyn Basin lies to the east, with depths around 5200-5600 m.

I estimate -5200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

13574.text:
Location: -61.0, -31.0 is in the Southern Ocean/Scotia Sea region, east of the South Orkney Islands (which sit near -60.7, -45) and west of the South Sandwich Islands (which are near -57 to -59, -26). At -61, -31 the point lies in the Weddell Sea/Scotia Sea basin, north of the South Scotia Ridge... The South Scotia Ridge runs from the South Orkneys eastward to around -30 longitude at roughly -60 to -61. The Scotia Sea's eastern part has depths of ~3000-4000 m. The area near -61, -31 is probably on the northern edge of the Weddell basin near the ridge's eastern end; depth is probably around 3000-4000 m.

I'll estimate -3500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

12676.text:
Location: 51°S, 27°W is in the South Atlantic, west of the South Sandwich Islands and northeast of South Georgia. South Georgia lies around 54°S, 36°W, so this point is east of the Falkland Plateau and north of the Scotia Sea, in the Argentine Basin's eastern side or near the Georgia Basin.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is near 14°W at this latitude. At 27°W, 51°S the point lies in the Georgia Basin / Argentine Abyssal plain area, with depths of about 4000–5000 m. The North Scotia Ridge runs along about 53–54°S, so 51°S is north of it. The Georgia Basin has depths of around 3500–4000 m, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge lies farther east near 50°S, 15°W. The area at 27°W is probably around 3000–4000 m deep. I'll estimate -3600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

11502.text:
Latitude -37.0, longitude 145.0 is in Victoria, Australia, about 100 km north of Melbourne. It is near the Great Dividing Range, close to Yea or Seymour. Seymour is at about -37.03, 145.13, with an elevation of roughly 140 m. Yea is at -37.2, 145.43. The point at 145.0 sits west of Seymour, near Pyalong/Tooborac, around -37.0, 144.9 to 145.0. Tooborac is at roughly 270 m. Heathcote is at -36.9, 144.7 at about 200 m. Pyalong is at -37.13, 144.85 at about 300 m. Around 145.0, -37.0 is the Mt Piper / Mangalore area, near Tallarook Ranges, so the terrain is hilly, roughly 250 m.

I'll estimate 260 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":260}

12730.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 81 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Heard Island (Heard is at about -53.1, 73.5). The point is near the Kerguelen Plateau, whose northern part lies around 50°S to 46°S at 68-78°E. Longitude 81 is east of the plateau's main body. The Kerguelen Plateau's southern part spans about 55°S, 75-80°E. At -51, 81°E, the point is probably on the eastern flank of the plateau, or in the basin to the east.

The Princess Elizabeth Trough lies further south. The seabed at -51, 81 could be around 3,000 to 4,000 m deep. The southern Kerguelen Plateau is shallower, roughly 1,000 to 2,000 m. The Raggatt Basin is around 57°S. The Labuan Basin is east of the plateau near 50°S, 80°E, with depths around 3,500 m. I'll estimate -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

10274.text:
The point (-25, -151) is in the South Pacific, southeast of Tahiti and near the Austral Islands (Tubuai is at about -23.4, -149.5; Rapa is at about -27.6, -144.3). There is no island at this location, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is part of the Pacific plate, with depths around 4,000 to 4,500 m. The area lies on the flank of the French Polynesia swell, where depths are typically about 4,300 m. I estimate 4,400 m of depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

9714.text:
Latitude -17.0, longitude 169.0 is in Vanuatu. Epi island lies near -16.7, 168.2, and Efate near -17.7, 168.4. Tongoa and Emae sit around -16.9, 168.5. Shepherd Islands are around -16.9, 168.6. Longitude 169.0 is east of these islands, in the sea between Vanuatu and Fiji, at the eastern edge of the New Hebrides arc. East of the arc lies the North Fiji Basin, whose depths run about 2000-3000 m. The New Hebrides trench is on the west side, so the east side is the back-arc. Anatom island is at -20.2, 169.8, and Aneityum is further south. Erromango is at -18.8, 169.0. Tanna is at -19.5, 169.3. So at -17, 169 there is only ocean.

East of Epi at 169.0, the seafloor is probably about 1500-2500 m deep. I estimate about -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

7992.text:
The point at latitude 1.0, longitude -35.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, off the northeast coast of Brazil. The Brazilian coast near the equator is around -50 to -44 longitude, so -35 is well offshore.

This location is on the western side of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge region, roughly in the Ceará Rise or the equatorial Atlantic basin. The Ceará Rise lies near 3°N, -43°, so -35 is east of it. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is near -30 at the equator. At -35, the seabed is likely abyssal plain or the ridge flank, with a depth of around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Ceará Abyssal Plain sits around 4,500 m, and the Romanche fracture zone region is near -18 to -25, so this point is farther west. I estimate about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

11734.text:
The point (-41, -111) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. It lies roughly 2,000 km west of the Chilean coast, and this region is on the Nazca/Antarctic plate, east of the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise sits near 115°W at 41°S, which is the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The ridge there is around 113-115°W at about 40°S, so -111 would be just east of the axis, on the ridge flank. Ridge crest depth there is about 2,300-2,800 m, and the flank at 2-4° east of the axis would be a bit deeper, around 3,000 m.

This is an estimate from memory of the ridge geometry, not a looked-up value, so I'll go with about -3,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

10592.text:
Latitude -27, longitude 125 falls in Western Australia, in the Gibson Desert/Great Victoria Desert area. The terrain there is inland and probably around 450 m.

The point is east of Wiluna and near the Gibson Desert's southern edge. Elevations in this region run roughly 400-500 m, so I estimate about 460 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":460}

11849.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 119 lies in the Southern Ocean south of Western Australia. The Australian coast at this longitude is near -34, so the point is about 7° (roughly 780 km) offshore. It is south of the continental shelf, in the area around the Diamantina Zone and the Naturaliste Plateau, which is further west near 115°E and -34°S.

The Southern Ocean floor south of Australia at -41° is typically about 4000-5000 m deep. The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins further south, at about -45° to -50°. At -41, 119, the seafloor is probably on the abyssal plain or the lower continental rise, around 4,500 m deep. I estimate -4,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

3462.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude -95.0 is in northwestern Ontario, near Lake of the Woods, which sits at about 49°N. At 51°N, -95°W the location is north of Kenora (49.77°N, -94.49°W) and near Red Lake. It is probably about 50 km west of Dryden, in the Canadian Shield, in the Ontario–Manitoba border area. The Manitoba border is at -95.15°, so the point is just inside Ontario, near the English River and Lac Seul. Lac Seul spans roughly 50.2–50.5°N and -92 to -94°W, so it does not cover this point. Land here would be around 300–350 m, and the nearby Whitemouth and Winnipeg River areas are at about 300 m.

I'll estimate Solid at about 330 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":330}

534.text:
This point (85°N, 169°E) lies in the central Arctic Ocean. It sits near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from the New Siberian Islands toward Ellesmere Island and Greenland. At 85°N the ridge axis is at roughly 140–150°E on the Siberian side and about 130°W on the other, and it crosses the pole between them. So 169°E is on the Amerasian side of the ridge toward the Makarov Basin, not on the ridge itself.

The Makarov Basin lies between the Lomonosov and Alpha-Mendeleev ridges. Its floor is around 4000 m deep. The Lomonosov Ridge crest is at about 1000–1500 m. At 85°N, 169°E the point is likely in the Makarov Basin, close to its Lomonosov side. Lomonosov Ridge longitudes near 85°N are about 150–175°E, with the ridge running from 81°N, 140°E to 90°N and then to the Greenland side. Because of that, this point could be on the ridge flank, and I estimate the depth at about 2000 m. Depths along the ridge are variable, so I'll choose a middle value of about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

8979.text:
Location: -9.0, 139.0 is in the Arafura Sea/Torres Strait region south of New Guinea. New Guinea's southern coast near 139°E is around -8.1 (Indonesian Papua, Asmat/Mappi region). At -9.0, 139.0, this lies in the Arafura Sea, a short distance off the coast. The Arafura shelf there has depths of about 30-50 m. Frederik Hendrik Island (Yos Sudarso) lies about -7.5 to -8.5 and 138-139°E. At -9.0 it's the shelf, so water.

Depth is likely around 40 m, although the shelf near the Aru Islands deepens slightly to the west. At 139°E, -9, the depth is about 35-50 m, so I'll say -40.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

16151.text:
The point is at 89°S, 83°E, on the East Antarctic plateau, about 100 km from the South Pole along the 83°E meridian. It is grounded ice, so the surface type is Solid.

The South Pole ice surface is about 2835 m. Moving toward 83°E, the surface rises gradually. Dome A (80.4°S, 77°E) is about 4090 m, and Vostok (78.5°S, 106°E) is about 3488 m. At 89°S, 83°E the surface is probably around 2900 m. The plateau near 88–89°S on the side toward Dome A rises slowly, roughly 2.5 m per km, which would give about 3000 m at 100 km. I settle on about 2950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

3767.text:
The point 49.0°N, 155.0°E lies in the Kuril Islands region. Matua island is at about 48.08°N, 153.2°E, and Paramushir is at about 50.3°N, 155.7°E. Onekotan is at about 49.45°N, 154.7°E, and Kharimkotan is at about 49.1°N, 154.5°E. The point at 155.0°E is therefore east of Kharimkotan, on the Pacific side. Kharimkotan spans roughly 154.4–154.6°E, so 155.0°E is about 0.5° farther east, or roughly 36 km.

The Kuril Trench lies further east, at about 156–157°E at this latitude. The area between the island chain and the trench is the Pacific slope, with depths of about 2000–5000 m. Onekotan's eastern coast is near 154.8°E, so the point is about 15 km offshore of that. At 49°N, 155.0°E the depth is probably around 2000–3000 m, since the trench axis is about 150 km further east and reaches about 8000–9000 m. I estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15202.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -15 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, on the Filchner Ice Shelf. The Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf front lies around 78°S, and the Filchner ice shelf extends from about -30° to -40° longitude. At -15°, the area is the Coats Land / Dronning Maud Land coast. The coast near -15° lies at about 71-72°S, so -79 would be inland on the grounded ice sheet, in the Theiskeil/Shackleton Range area. The Shackleton Range sits near -25° longitude and 80°S. At -15°, -79°, the position is east of the Filchner ice shelf, on the interior ice sheet of Coats Land, where the surface is roughly 2000 m or more.

The ice surface is probably around 1500-2500 m. Weighing that, I'll estimate about 2000 m, giving Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

1776.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 133.0°E is in northern Siberia, in the Lena Delta region. The Lena Delta spans roughly 72-73.5°N and 123-130°E, so this point lies southeast of it. Near 71°N, 133°E the area is around the Yana-Lena lowlands, close to the Buor-Khaya Gulf, which is at about 71.5°N, 129°E. The Laptev Sea coast near Tiksi sits at about 71.6°N, 128.8°E. The Yana Bay coast is further east, near 71°N, 135°E, and the Yana delta is near 71.5°N, 136°E. At 133°E, 71°N, the point is probably on the Primorskaya lowland or the Kharaulakh area, which is land at low elevation.

Land is more likely than water here. The Yana River delta begins around 71°N, 136°E, so 133°E is west of it, on the Yana-Lena peninsula-like area. The Bykovsky Peninsula is at 71.7°N, 129°E, and the Kharaulakh ridge is near 71°N, 127°E. The Chondon/Yana bay coast lies near 71.5°N, 133°E, though I'm unsure of its exact position. I'll guess land at roughly 20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":20}

5001.text:
Latitude 35.0°N, longitude 103.0°E falls in Gansu province, China, near Linxia and Lanzhou. Lanzhou is at about 36.06°N, 103.8°E. Linxia is at about 35.6°N, 103.2°E. The point at 35.0°N, 103.0°E lies south of Linxia, around Hezuo and Xiahe in Gannan. Hezuo is at 34.98°N, 102.9°E with an elevation of about 2900 m. Terrain at 103.0°E is probably about 3000 m.

The point is just east of Hezuo, so I estimate about 2950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2980}

7083.text:
Latitude 11°N, longitude -53° lies in the western tropical Atlantic, roughly 500 km northeast of the mouth of the Amazon and offshore of French Guiana/Suriname. The location is on the Demerara Abyssal Plain, or possibly the lower part of the continental rise. The Demerara Rise sits around 9°N, 54°W, with depths of about 1000-3000 m on the plateau. At 11°N, 53°W the seafloor is probably on the Demerara Abyssal Plain, at roughly 4500-4800 m depth.

The Demerara Rise's northern edge is near 10-11°N, and the abyssal plain to the north lies at about 4800 m. I'll estimate -4750 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4750}

11806.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 33 lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa. It is south of the Natal Valley and Mozambique Basin, near the Mozambique Ridge's southern end and the Agulhas Plateau region.

The Mozambique Basin lies around 30-35°E and 30-37°S. At 41°S, 33°E we are probably south of the Mozambique Ridge, near the Africa-Antarctic Ridge region. The Agulhas Plateau is at about 26°E, 40°S, so 33°E is east of it. The Southwest Indian Ridge is around 40-45°S at 33°E, with the Del Caño Rise near 45°S, 45°E, and the Prince Edward fracture zone near 35°E. At 33°E the ridge axis is probably around 50°S, so 41°S is likely the Enderby/Agulhas basin, with depths of about 4500-5000 m. I estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

8834.text:
The point is at 9°S, 151°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of the Line Islands and northwest of the Society Islands, and the Marquesas are to the northeast near 9°S, 140°W. The Tuamotus lie further southeast, so this point is in open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is Pacific abyssal plain, usually about 4,500–5,000 m deep. The Penrhyn basin is around 5,000 m deep and lies near 9°S, 158°W. At 151°W the floor is probably a little shallower, so I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

4474.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude 129.0 is in North Korea, in the Hamgyong region. It lies inland, west of the east coast near Kimchaek (40.67N, 129.2E). Chongjin is at 41.8N, so this is not near it.

The point is probably in Kimchaek's hinterland, or in Orang/Hwadae county. Hwadae is around 41.3N, 129.5E. The area around 41.0N, 129.0E is mountainous, in Kilju or Myongchon county, where elevations typically run 500-1000 m. Myongchon is at about 41.08N, 129.43E. Going west from there toward Kwanmo Peak (Kwanmobong, 2,540 m, at about 40.9N, 129.2E), the terrain rises sharply. Because this point sits near the Kwanmo highlands, I estimate about 1,300 m, with some uncertainty.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

14270.text:
Location: -69, -79 is in the Bellingshausen Sea, west of the Antarctic Peninsula, near the southern Alexander Island coast. Alexander Island extends from about -67.5 to -72 latitude and is centered around longitude -70. At -79 longitude and -69 latitude, we are west of Alexander Island, in the Bellingshausen Sea, in open ocean or near the continental shelf edge.

The Bellingshausen Sea continental shelf there is roughly 500 m deep, and farther out the shelf break gives deeper water. At -79 longitude, about 600 km west of the peninsula, Alexander Island's west coast is around -73 to -75 longitude at this latitude. So the point is roughly 300 km offshore. It could be the continental rise, with depths of about 3000 m. The Bellingshausen abyssal plain lies further north, around -64. At -69 latitude and -79 longitude, the seabed is probably about 3500 m deep, based on a rough recollection of the area.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

13567.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -45 lies in the Scotia Sea, north of the South Orkney Islands, which sit at about -60.7 latitude and -45 longitude. Coronation Island spans roughly -60.6 to -60.75 latitude and -45.0 to -46.0 longitude, so the point is close to the islands. Laurie Island lies further east, at about -45.6 longitude and -60.7 latitude.

At -61.0, the point is about 15 to 20 km south of Coronation Island's southern coast. The South Orkney plateau is shallow, with depths of a few hundred meters. South of the islands, the Powell Basin lies toward the south and west. Powell Island is at about -60.7 latitude and -45.0 longitude, so -61.0 is south of it. Depths there are probably around 500 to 1000 m. I'll go with -800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

12490.text:
The point is at 49°S, 39°W, in the South Atlantic. It lies southwest of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Falklands/Malvinas region, close to the North Scotia Ridge and the Georgia Basin. South Georgia is at about 54°S, 36°W, so this point is well north of it. The area is probably the Argentine Basin's eastern edge or the Georgia Basin, where depths are around 5000 m.

At 49°S, 39°W, I expect the seafloor to be abyssal plain, roughly 5000 m deep. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far to the east, around 15°W at this latitude, and the Falkland Plateau lies farther west at about 50°W, so neither is a factor here. The point is probably in the Georgia Basin or the Argentine Basin, at about -5000 m. I'll use -4800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7411.text:
The point 7°N, 117°W lies in the eastern Pacific, roughly 1,500 km southwest of Mexico. It is north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone, which sits near 10°N, and is not far from the East Pacific Rise, which is near 105°W at this latitude. The seafloor here is abyssal plain on crust of moderate age, around 20–30 Myr, and typical depths in this area are about 3,800–4,200 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a lookup of the exact point.

The seafloor at 7°N, 117°W is about 4,000 m deep, so I predict an elevation of about -3,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

13529.text:
The point at 61°S, 121°W is in the Southern Ocean, in the southeast Pacific. It lies south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern portion, in the Amundsen Sea basin region. The East Pacific Rise/Pacific-Antarctic Ridge near 61°S runs around 150°W to 120°W, curving toward the southeast. At 61°S the ridge is near about 150°W, so 121°W is likely on the eastern flank, where seafloor depths are about 3500–4000 m.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge reaches roughly 65°S, 175°W, then 56°S, 143°W, then 60°S, 150°W. After that it goes through the Udintsev fracture zone near 55°S, 145°W, and continues northeast as the East Pacific Rise toward 50°S, 114°W. So 61°S, 121°W is south of that, on the Antarctic plate, in the Bellingshausen/Amundsen abyssal plain at about 4000–4900 m depth. I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

9786.text:
The coordinates -19.0, -47.0 fall in Minas Gerais, Brazil, near the Triângulo Mineiro region. The area is close to Araxá, Uberaba and Sacramento, with the Rio Grande to the south. Plateau elevations there are around 900-1000 m.

At 19°S, 47°W the location lies a little north of Araxá, which is at about 19.6°S, 46.9°W, and southeast of Uberaba, at 19.75°S, 47.9°W. The terrain is the Alto Paranaíba plateau at roughly 950 m. Near Santa Juliana and Perdizes (19.0°S, 47.4°W) elevations are about 900-1000 m, so I estimate 960 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":960}

14637.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -65 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, at the base of the Antarctic Peninsula. That area lies on the Larsen ice shelf region or the Ronne Ice Shelf's western edge. The Ronne-Filchner ice shelf extends west to about 75°S at -65°. At -73°, -65°, the location is probably the Orville Coast or Lassiter Coast area. The Lassiter Coast runs around 74°S, 62-65°W. The Orville Coast is around 75.5°S, 63°W. At 73°S, 65°W, I think this is the Black Coast / Nantucket Inlet area, where the Larsen C ice shelf ends near 69°S and the Larsen D/Smith Inlet sits near 74°S. At 73°S there could be the Gardner Inlet region, but the Antarctic Peninsula's east side at 73°S is likely grounded ice of the plateau (Palmer Land, around 72-73°S, 65°W). Palmer Land's interior plateau there is roughly 1500-2000 m high.

Estimated at 73°S, 65°W: Palmer Land interior, the ice surface is about 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

528.text:
Latitude 85°N, longitude 157°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands region across the pole toward Ellesmere and Greenland. At 85°N the ridge sits at roughly 140°E to 150°E, with its crest about 1000 to 1500 m deep, and the Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side. At 157°E and 85°N, the position is probably on the Amundsen Basin side or on the Lomonosov Ridge itself.

Near 88°N the ridge is at about 90°E to 140°E. At 85°N the ridge crosses roughly 150°E, and the Makarov Basin lies on the other side, toward the East Siberian side, at about 4000 m depth. The Lomonosov Ridge at 85°N is near 155°E, with depths around 1000 to 1500 m. I will estimate a depth of about 1800 m, which allows for the point being on a ridge flank.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

10585.text:
Latitude -27, longitude 111 is in the Indian Ocean, west of Western Australia (Shark Bay is near 113E, -25). This point is about 300 km offshore of the coast at roughly 114E.

The area lies off the Western Australian margin. Just west of the Carnarvon/Gascoyne shelf are the Wallaby Plateau and Zenith Plateau, around -22 to -30 lat and 100-112 lon. The Wallaby Plateau is at roughly 24-26S, 107-112E, with depths of about 2000-3000 m. At 27S 111E the point is probably on the southern edge of the Wallaby Plateau, or on the slope toward the Perth Abyssal Plain. I estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

1742.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude 65.0 (east) places this in the Kara Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya (which lies around 55-60°E at this latitude). Novaya Zemlya's southern island sits at about 71°N, and its east coast near 70-71°N is at roughly 55-58°E. Because 65°E is well east of that, the point is in the Kara Sea, off the Yamal Peninsula's northwest. The Yamal Peninsula is at about 70-71°N, with its west coast near 66-67°E and Cape Kharasavey at 71.2°N, 66.8°E. At 65°E and 71°N, the point is just west of Kharasavey, so it is in the sea.

The southwestern Kara Sea is shallow, with depths around 50-100 m. Near the Yamal coast, 65°E and 71°N is about 100 km from the coast. The depth there is probably about 50 m, and I'll estimate -45 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

3765.text:
Latitude 49.0°N, longitude 151.0°E lies in the Pacific Ocean, east of the Kuril Islands. The central Kurils are near 47-48°N at about 151-153°E: Simushir sits near 47.0°N, 152.0°E, and Chirpoi and Brat Chirpoyev are near 46.5°N. Matua is at 48.08°N, 153.2°E, Rasshua at 47.75°N, 153.0°E, and Ushishir at 47.5°N, 152.8°E. Shiashkotan is at 48.8°N, 154.1°E, Ekarma at 48.96°N, 153.9°E, and Kharimkotan at 49.1°N, 154.5°E. Paramushir starts further north, around 50°N, 155°E.

So 49°N, 151°E is west of the island arc, in the Sea of Okhotsk. The Kuril Basin lies there, with depths of 3000 m or more. The basin's deepest part is about 3300 m, near 47°N, 149°E. At 49°N, 151°E, which is close to the arc's western side, the depth is probably around 2000-3000 m. I'll estimate about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

14164.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 69 is in East Antarctica, near Mac. Robertson Land, close to the Prydz Bay and Amery region. The Mawson Station is at 62.87E, and Prydz Bay's coast lies around 68-69S at 69E. At -67 latitude and 69E, that is inland of the coast, probably on the ice sheet.

The coast near 69E is around -68.5 to -69.5 (the Amery Ice Shelf front is at about -68.5, 71-75E). Mac. Robertson Land coast near 69E is at roughly -67.6 (Mawson coast at -67.6, 62.9E). Then at 69E the coast is near -69 or so, at the Prydz Bay edge. So -67 is inland of the coast, on grounded ice, with an elevation of perhaps 1500 m. The Mawson station is at 62.9E with the plateau rising to 2000 m about 100 km inland. At 69E, -67, that is about 200 km from the coast, so I estimate roughly 2000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

8568.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 37.0 is in north-central Tanzania. It lies south of Kilimanjaro and east of the Masai Steppe, near Same and Mkomazi, in the area between Dodoma and Korogwe. The Pare and Usambara mountains are further north and east. At -5, 37 the terrain is probably Maasai steppe country at roughly 500 m.

Several nearby places help narrow this. Kibaya is around -5.3, 36.7 at about 1200 m. Kondoa is at -4.9, 35.8 at about 1400 m. Korogwe is at -5.15, 38.45 at about 250 m. Handeni is at -5.4, 38.0 at about 600 m. At 37.0 the longitude is just west of the Mkomazi/Same area, so the terrain is probably the Maasai steppe, around 800 m. Mkomazi is at about 500 m, and Same town at -4.07, 37.7 is about 900 m. The point is near the Gonja and Pare southern foothills region, so I estimate 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

5767.text:
The point 25.0°N, 165.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. It is west of French Frigate Shoals (23.7°N, 166.2°W) and east of Pearl and Hermes (27.9°N, 175.8°W). It is also north of the Hawaiian chain and sits on the Pacific abyssal plain, near the northeast of the Hawaiian Ridge.

The Hawaiian Ridge runs from the southeast to the northwest. At 25°N, 165°W, the nearby ridge axis is around 23.5°N, 166°W near French Frigate Shoals. So the point is about 150 km north-northeast of that axis. Seafloor there should be about 4500–5000 m deep, and the moat and flexural arch near the ridge would make it about 4800 m. I'll estimate -4800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

14265.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -89 lies in the Bellingshausen Sea region, west of the Antarctic Peninsula. The Peninsula's west coast sits near -67 to -68 latitude at about -67 longitude. At -89 longitude and -69 latitude, this is open Bellingshausen Sea, north of Thurston Island (about -72, -98) and west of Alexander Island (-71, -70). The area is probably deep continental shelf or slope, and I'm not sure whether sea ice or an ice shelf covers it.

Bathymetry in the Bellingshausen Sea at -69, -89 is likely about 3000 m deep, since the continental rise is there and the shelf edge is near -71. I'll estimate around -3200 m, with a type of Water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

9327.text:
Location: -13.0, 115.0 is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Sumba/Sumbawa and south of Bali/Lombok. This is the Argo Abyssal Plain area, off northwest Australia. The Java Trench lies to the north, around -11 S at 115 E, depths up to ~6000 m, but the abyssal plain south of it is around 5,000-5,700 m deep.

At -13 S, 115 E, the Argo Abyssal Plain depth is roughly 5,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

1885.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -9.0 is in the Norwegian Sea/Greenland Sea, near Jan Mayen, which sits at about 71°N, 8.5°W. At 69°N, 9°W we are southwest of Jan Mayen, in the Norwegian Sea, close to the Jan Mayen Fracture Zone or the Iceland Plateau. The Jan Mayen Ridge lies further southwest, around 69-70°N, 7-8°W. The Jan Mayen Basin and Ridge span roughly 68-71°N, and the ridge crest is about 1000 m deep or less. The Greenland Basin floor is about 3000 m deep.

The Norway Basin is east of the Jan Mayen Ridge at around 3000-3300 m. At 69°N, 9°W, the point is likely on the Jan Mayen Plateau/Ridge, with a depth of about 1500 m. The Jan Mayen Ridge runs from about 70.5°N, 8°W southwest to 67.5°N, 11°W, so 69°N would sit near 9.5°W along the ridge axis. That puts the point on the ridge, where the depth is roughly 1000-1500 m. I'll estimate -1400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1400}

13731.text:
Latitude -63.0, longitude -77.0 is in the Bransfield Strait / Drake Passage region west of the Antarctic Peninsula. The South Shetland Islands lie around 62-63°S and 58-63°W, so -77 longitude is well west of them. That puts the point in open ocean in the Bellingshausen Sea / Drake Passage area, southwest of the South Shetlands.

The Drake Passage near 63°S, 77°W is north of the continental shelf edge. The Hero Fracture Zone and the Shackleton Fracture Zone are further east. Here the depth is likely about 3500-4000 m, and the area is probably close to the Pacific margin of the Antarctic Peninsula's trench, the South Shetland Trench, which lies at about 62-63°S and 60-70°W. West of the trench the seafloor is Phoenix plate abyssal floor at roughly 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

36.text:
This point is at 89°N, 107°W, which is in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole. It sits on the Lomonosov Ridge side of the Amerasian Basin; the ridge runs from Ellesmere Island/Greenland across the pole toward Siberia. Longitude 107°W at 89°N is on the Canadian/Alaskan side of the ridge, so it should lie in the Amerasian Basin (Makarov Basin side). Makarov Basin depths are about 4000 m. The Lomonosov Ridge crest lies roughly along 140°E to 40°W, so the ridge is on the other side of the pole from 107°W. The ridge at the pole is near 90°N, 50-60°W-ish, and at the 89°N latitude it lies near 140°E and 40°W. The point at 107°W lies on the Makarov Basin side, where the floor is about 4000 m deep.

The IBCAO values near the pole are around -4200 m in the Amundsen Basin and about -4000 m in the Makarov Basin. I'll estimate -3950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

5253.text:
Latitude 31.0, longitude -113.0 is in Sonora, Mexico. The Gulf of California coast near Puerto Peñasco sits at about 31.3N, -113.5, so this point is inland from the coast, in the Pinacate region or the desert to its west. The coast near El Golfo de Santa Clara is at about 31.7N, -114.5, and the Sonoran coast near Puerto Lobos is at about 30.3N, -112.9.

At 31.0N, -113.0, the point is probably in the Sonoyta/Pinacate area. The Pinacate lava field spans roughly 31.7N to 32.0N, so this point is south of it. Fixing the coast near Desemboque (30.6N, -112.4) and Puerto Peñasco (31.3N, -113.55), the coast between runs northwest-southeast. At longitude -113.0, the coast is at about 31.0N, which makes this point close to the coast, near Bahía Adair or Estero Morua. The elevation there is likely low, about 20 m, and the surface is solid land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":25}

10078.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude 177.0 lies in the South Pacific, south of Fiji's Lau and Viti Levu groups. Fiji's southernmost islands, such as Kadavu at about -19, 178, are well north of this point. The Lau Ridge and Lau Basin lie to the east, and the Fiji Plateau to the north. Near -21, 177 the seafloor is likely the southern Lau Basin or the South Fiji Basin, with depths around 2000–3000 m.

The South Fiji Basin is about 3000 m deep or more, and the Lau Basin is around 2000 m. At 177°E the point is west of the Lau Ridge (about 178.5–180°E) and east of the Kadavu/Viti Levu region. The Hunter Fracture Zone lies near -22. The area is probably South Fiji Basin, roughly 2500–3000 m deep. I'll estimate -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

14331.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 43 is in East Antarctica, in Enderby Land / Queen Maud region. Enderby Land coast sits around -67 at 45°E. At -69, 43°E, we are about 200 km inland of the coast, so it is likely grounded ice sheet. The ice surface elevation there is probably around 2000 m.

Looking at it more carefully: the coast near 43°E is around -66.5 to -67 (Cape Borley at 52°E, -66; Shirase Coast at 38°E). At -69 the ice is on the slope up to the plateau, roughly 1500–2500 m. I estimate about 2000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

3007.text:
The point 57°N, 75°E lies in the West Siberian Plain, in Omsk Oblast near the border with Novosibirsk Oblast. This is the Baraba lowland, which is flat and sits at roughly 100-120 m elevation. There are many lakes in the area, but this exact point is most likely land.

I estimate the surface is Solid at about 110 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

8301.text:
The point (-3.0, -137.0) is in the central Pacific Ocean, south of the Clipperton Fracture Zone and east of the Line Islands, roughly between the Marquesas and the Galápagos. That region is abyssal plain, with typical depths of about 4,200 to 4,500 m.

Seafloor age in this area is around 40 to 50 million years, which fits a depth near 4,300 m. The equatorial Pacific is also a sediment-rich region. I did not look up a specific bathymetry value, so I am estimating about -4,350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4350}

4693.text:
The point (37°N, 153°W) is in the North Pacific, roughly midway between Hawaii and California, northeast of the Hawaiian Ridge. The seafloor there is abyssal plain, typically 5,000 to 5,500 m deep.

The Murray Fracture Zone runs near 30–35°N, and the Pioneer Fracture Zone lies near 38°N. Seafloor age in this region is about 80 million years, which fits a depth of around 5,200 m. I estimate about -5,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

6875.text:
The point 13°N, 109°W is in the eastern Pacific, west of Mexico and southwest of Clipperton Island (which sits near 10.3°N, 109.2°W). It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor here is part of the East Pacific Rise flank region. The East Pacific Rise axis is near 105°W at this latitude, so 109°W lies to the west of it, on seafloor roughly 5–10 million years old. Typical depths for that age are about 3,500–3,800 m, though the Clipperton fracture zone area to the south is somewhat different. I estimate a depth of about 3,600 m, which gives an elevation of about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

8735.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude 11.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean off Angola, about 200 km west of the coast. The Angolan coast near Ambriz and Soyo lies around 13°E at -7°S, and the coastline near -7° is at roughly 12.8°E. That puts 11.0°E about 200 km offshore, on the continental slope or the abyssal plain.

The Angola margin is fairly wide here. The shelf is narrow, about 30 to 50 km, and the slope then descends to roughly 3000 m. The Congo fan lies at -6°S, and the Congo canyon runs out to about 5°E. At 11°E, 200 km from the coast, the depth is probably around 2500 to 3000 m, so I estimate -2900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

6420.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 61.0°E is in the Arabian Sea, east of Oman and southwest of Pakistan. The Oman coast at that latitude sits near 57.8°E, so this point is well offshore.

The location is probably on the Arabian Basin abyssal plain, possibly near the Murray Ridge. The Murray Ridge runs from about 23.5°N, 61.5°E southwestward to about 22°N, 60°E, so 19°N is south of it. Water depth in this part of the Arabian Sea is typically about 3,300 to 3,500 m. The Gulf of Oman abyssal plain is around 3,000 m, and the Arabian Basin around 19°N, 61°E is roughly 3,400 m. I could not pin down a more precise value, so I'm using an estimate of -3400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

10194.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 49 is in the Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar. Madagascar's east coast at latitude -23 lies near longitude 47.0 (Farafangana is at about -22.8, 47.8). So 49.0E is roughly 130 km offshore.

The shelf there is narrow, and the Madagascar Basin is deep. At about 130 km offshore the depth is likely around 3000-4000 m. The slope drops quickly, and the abyssal plain at 50E is about 4500 to 5000 m deep. At 49E I would estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

6873.text:
Location 13°N, 113°W is in the eastern Pacific, southwest of Mexico, west of the East Pacific Rise (which is near 105-110°W at this latitude). At 13°N the rise crest is around 104°W, so 113°W is on the Pacific plate flank. The seafloor age there is about 10-15 Myr, with depth around 3,000-3,500 m near the rise but deeper further west. The Clipperton fracture zone is near 10°N. At 13°N, 113°W the depth is probably about 3,700 m.

The region near 13°N, 113°W sits on abyssal hills at roughly 3,600-3,900 m depth. I'll estimate -3,750 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3750}

1211.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 83.0°E lies in the Kara Sea, north of the Taymyr Peninsula. The Taymyr coast is at about 76°N, and Cape Chelyuskin is at about 104°E. At 83°E, the northern Taymyr coast is around 75.5°N, for example near the Minin skerries at 77°N, 83°E?

The Kara Sea there is open shelf. Near 77°N, 83°E the seabed is probably about 50–100 m deep. Islands nearby include the Arctic Institute Islands at about 75.5°N, 82°E, and Vize Island at 79.5°N, 77°E. At 77°N, 83°E the point is north of the Arctic Institute Islands and northeast of the Voronin Trough, which lies further east. The shelf there is likely 50–100 m deep, so I estimate about -80 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-80}

7915.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 171.0°E lies in the central Pacific. The Marshall Islands are to the north (Majuro is at about 7°N, 171°E), and Kiribati's Gilbert Islands lie to the southeast (Tarawa is at about 1.4°N, 173°E). Nothing on land is at 3°N, 171°E, so this should be open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is the deep Pacific basin, probably 5000 to 5500 m deep. I'm estimating from the general bathymetry of the region, not from a specific depth reading, so I'll go with about -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

5106.text:
The point 33°N, 47°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of Bermuda. Bermuda sits near 64.8°W, so this point is well away from it. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 33°N lies at roughly 40–42°W, which puts 47°W on the western flank. The Azores plateau is farther north-east. The region between the ridge and Bermuda is abyssal plain at depths of about 5000 m, and the flank near 47°W is probably a bit shallower, around 4500–5000 m. There may also be seamounts nearby, such as the Corner Rise seamounts, but those are near 35°N and 50°W.

I estimate a seabed depth of about 4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

10733.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 47 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, east of South Africa and southwest of Madagascar. It lies south of Madagascar's southern tip (about -25.6) and east of the African coast (about 32E), so it is open ocean.

The area is probably in the Madagascar Basin or the Mozambique Basin. The Madagascar Basin lies east of the Madagascar Ridge, which sits around 45-47E at these latitudes. The Madagascar Ridge near -29 and 47E has depths of about 2000-3000 m. The ridge runs from about 26S to 35S near 44-48E, with a typical depth of about 2500 m, and the Mozambique Basin to the west is about 5000 m deep. At 47E the point is probably on the eastern flank or crest of the ridge. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

1409.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 119.0 E lies in the Laptev Sea region of Siberia. The Taymyr Peninsula spans roughly 100-115E, and the Lena Delta is around 126E at 73N. The Khatanga Gulf is near 74N, 110E.

At 119E, 75N, the point is in the Laptev Sea, since the coast near 119E is around 73N (the Buor-Khaya Gulf is at 71N, 130E). The Laptev Sea shelf is shallow, and at 75N it is probably 40-50 m deep. The Anabar Bay coast is near 73.5N, 113E, and the Olenyok Bay coast is near 73N, 120E. The Laptev Sea shelf is about 50 m deep at 75N, and the shelf break is near 78N. I'll estimate -45 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

9877.text:
Latitude -19, longitude 135 falls in the Northern Territory, Australia, in the Barkly Tableland area south of Newcastle Waters. The Barkly region lies roughly between -18 and -20 latitude, and 135E is east of Tennant Creek (134.2E), near the Barkly Homestead area (135.8E, -19.7). Elevation there is about 220 m.

The point is probably around 230 m, so I'll answer 225 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":225}

11352.text:
The point (-37, -155) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of Tahiti and well north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. It is in open ocean on the Pacific plate, in the region of the southern Pacific basin between the Austral and Pitcairn island chains.

The seafloor age there is likely about 70-80 Ma, so the depth should be around 4,500-5,000 m. I did not check this against bathymetry data. The Mendaña fracture zone and the Austral seamounts lie farther north, and at 37°S the depth is probably about 4,400 m, so I'll use roughly -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4350}

12366.text:
Latitude -47, longitude 73 lies in the southern Indian Ocean. Kerguelen Islands are at about -49.3 lat, 69.5 lon. The Kerguelen Plateau extends from about 46°S to 64°S, and the northern part of the plateau around 46-47°S, 73-75°E is shallow, though not as shallow as the islands. Heard Island is at -53, 73.5, so 47°S is well north of it.

The northern Kerguelen Plateau at 47°S, 73°E probably has a depth of about 2000-3000 m. The plateau's northern part, near 46°S, 75°E, is around 2000 m deep. Toward the west, at 73°E, the depth is probably about 3000 m. I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

4304.text:
Latitude 43.0°N, longitude 149.0°E lies in the Pacific Ocean, southeast of Iturup (Etorofu) island in the Kuril Islands. Iturup spans roughly 44.5°N to 45.5°N, and Kunashir lies near 43.8°N, 145.8°E. Shikotan is near 43.8°N, 146.7°E. At 43°N, 149°E the point is south of the Kuril chain, on the Pacific side. The Kuril Basin is to the north (Okhotsk Sea side). The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs southeast of the islands, so at 43°N, 149°E we are likely on the slope toward the trench. The trench axis is near 44°N, 150°E at about 8000 m deep. At 43°N, 149°E, which is about 150 km from the islands, the depth is likely around 5000 m.

The trench axis near 43°N lies around 150.5°E. The point at 149°E would be on the landward slope, perhaps 4500 to 5500 m deep. I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

12228.text:
Location: -45, 157 is in the Southern Ocean southwest of New Zealand and southeast of Tasmania, east of the Macquarie Ridge. Macquarie Island sits at about -54.6, 158.9, so this point is well north of it. The Tasman Sea and the Emerald Basin are around here, and the area south of Tasmania is about 4000-4500 m deep.

The Macquarie Ridge runs from about 160E at -50 down toward Puysegur at 166E, -47. At -45, 157E, the point lies on the abyssal plain west of the ridge, in the Tasman Basin, where depths are about 4500 m. The Tasman abyssal plain near -45, 157 is about 4600 m deep, and I'm estimating about -4650 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4650}

14290.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -39 lies in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, off the Antarctic coast. Queen Maud Land's coast is at about -70 latitude near 0°. At -39°, the coast is farther south: Princess Martha Coast at around -15° to -20°, and Coats Land at about -30° to -35°. The Weddell Sea's eastern coast near -39° would be at roughly -77° (the Filchner Ice Shelf is at -40° to -30° and -78°). So -69°, -39° is open Weddell Sea, which is water.

Depth there: the Weddell Sea abyssal plain is about 4000-5000 m deep. At -69 and -39 we are in the central Weddell Sea, probably about 4,500 m deep. The Maud Rise is at -65, 3 E, so it doesn't apply. Weddell Sea depth around -69, -39 is likely about -4800 m. I'll go with -4700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

5558.text:
The point 29°N, 137°E lies in the Philippine Sea, east of the Ryukyu/Nansei islands and south of Japan. It sits roughly between the Amami Plateau and the Izu-Ogasawara (Bonin) arc, in the western part of the Philippine Sea Plate, which includes the Shikoku Basin, the Kyushu-Palau Ridge, and the West Mariana Ridge.

The Kyushu-Palau Ridge runs near 136°E at these latitudes, so 137°E could be just east of it, in the Shikoku Basin or the Parece Vela Basin. Shikoku Basin depths are around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Kyushu-Palau Ridge crest is shallower, around 2,000 to 3,000 m. At 29°N I would expect the ridge near 136.5°E, so 137°E is likely on its eastern flank or in the basin, at roughly 4,500 m depth. I'm estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

5797.text:
The point 25.0°N, 105.0°W is in northern Mexico, near the Durango–Zacatecas–Chihuahua area. Durango state spans roughly 22.3–26.8°N, and 25°N, 105°W falls in the northeastern part of Durango, in the Bolsón de Mapimí region, near Mapimí and Ceballos.

Ceballos sits at about 26.5°N, 104.1°W, at roughly 1,200 m. Mapimí is at 25.8°N, 103.85°W, at about 1,100 m. At 25.0°N, 105.0°W the terrain is closer to the Sierra Madre foothills, around 1,500 m. Torreón (25.5°N, 103.4°W) is at about 1,120 m, and Santiago Papasquiaro (25.04°N, 105.42°W) is at about 1,740 m. Lying between these, the point is probably in the Sierra and Llanos area around 1,700 m, so I estimate about 1,650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1650}

15056.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 53 is in East Antarctica, in Queen Maud Land / Enderby Land. Longitude 53°E at 77°S is inland, roughly in the area south of the Prince Charles Mountains, toward Dome A. The Lambert Glacier is around 70°E, and the Prince Charles Mountains are near 66-70°E at 72-74°S. At 53°E, 77°S the surface is on the plateau, probably 2500-3000 m. Dome Fuji is at 77°S, 39°E with a surface elevation of 3800 m. Dome A is at 80°S, 77°E with 4000 m. So 53°E, 77°S probably sits around 3200 m.

The Recovery Glacier is at about 30°W, so it is not relevant. The terrain is grounded ice, so the type is Solid. I'll estimate 3200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3200}

9719.text:
The point is at -17.0, 179.0 E, which is in Fiji, east of Vanua Levu and Taveuni. Taveuni sits near -16.85, 179.97 (about 180E), so 179.0E lies well west of it. Vanua Levu spans roughly 178.6 to 179.9E at latitudes of about -16.3 to -16.8. Viti Levu lies at about -17.8 and 178.0E. So -17.0, 179.0 falls in the Koro Sea / Lomaiviti area, north of Ovalau (-17.7, 178.8) and south of Vanua Levu's southern coast (about -16.7 at 179.0E).

This is open water between Vanua Levu and the Lomaiviti group. Water depths in the area are around 1000 to 2000 m. I'm estimating from the general geography rather than a specific bathymetry value. The Lomaiviti basin is fairly deep, so I'll estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

2028.text:
Latitude 67.0, longitude -83.0 falls in northern Canada, on Baffin Island's western side or the Melville Peninsula region. Melville Peninsula spans roughly 66-70°N and 81-85°W. At 67°N, 83°W, this is near Hall Beach, which sits at 68.8°N, 81.2°W. Foxe Basin lies to the east. The southern Melville Peninsula near 67°N, 83°W is probably land near Repulse Bay, which is at 66.5°N, 86.2°W. Committee Bay is at about 68°N, 86°W.

At 67°N, 83°W, the point is likely on the Melville Peninsula's interior, a low-lying area. The elevation is probably around 100 m. The Gulf of Boothia lies west of the peninsula, and Foxe Basin lies east at about 81°W. At 67°N the peninsula runs from about 82°W to 86°W, so 83°W is likely on land, perhaps near the eastern coast. I estimate about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

724.text:
The point 81°N, 171°W lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Chukchi Borderland, in the Amerasian Basin. The Chukchi Plateau sits around 77-80°N near 170-180°W, and the Mendeleev Ridge is further west. At 81°N, 171°W the point is probably just north of the Chukchi Plateau, on its slope toward the Canada Basin, or on the Chukchi Abyssal Plain.

From IBCAO, the Chukchi Cap reaches about 80°N and then drops steeply north. The Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. At 81°N, 171°W the point is probably on the northern slope or on the Northwind Ridge region. The Northwind Ridge lies near 74-77°N at 155-160°W, so it is not relevant here. The Chukchi Plateau extends north to about 80°N, with the Arctic Mendeleev Ridge continuing to the northwest. At 81°N, 171°W, depths are probably around 2000-3000 m, so I'll estimate about 2300 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

3650.text:
The point 49.0°N, 79.0°W is in northern Ontario/Quebec, in the Abitibi region near the Ontario–Quebec border. It sits in the Clay Belt, which is flat, and the surface there is roughly 250-300 m above sea level.

Lake Abitibi lies at about 48.65°N, 79.6°W, so it is not at this point. Land around Cochrane, Ontario (49.07°N, 81°W) is about 250 m. Amos, Quebec (48.57°N, 78.1°W) is about 300 m. Taking the area near 49°N, 79°W as lowland forest and wetland, I estimate about 275 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":275}

605.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -49.0° is in north Greenland, north of Humboldt Glacier, which is farther south at about 79-80°N. At 83°N, 49°W the coast of Greenland (Nares Strait and Lincoln Sea) is at roughly 82-83°N between about 30°W and 60°W. Greenland's northern tip, Kaffeklubben Island, is at 83.67°N, 30°W. Cape Morris Jesup is at 83.6°N, 33°W. At 49°W, the coast is around 82.0-82.5°N. Offshore from Nyeboe Glacier (about 81.8°N, 55°W), the coast runs northeast, so by 49°W the coast is near 82.5°N.

That puts 83.0°N, 49°W slightly north of the coast, in the Lincoln Sea. The Lincoln Sea shelf there is probably 200-400 m deep, so I'll estimate a seabed elevation of about -350 m. I'm not certain how far the shelf extends, but the point likely sits near the shelf edge or on the shelf.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

6306.text:
The point 19.0°N, 167.0°W is in the central North Pacific, southwest of Hawaii and northeast of Johnston Atoll. Johnston Atoll is at about 16.7°N, 169.5°W, so this point is roughly 300 km away from it and lies in open ocean.

The surrounding seafloor is Pacific abyssal plain, with typical depths around 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Hawaiian-Emperor chain and the Line Islands seamounts are nearby, but I am not aware of a specific seamount at this location. I estimate the depth at about 5,300 m, so the elevation is about -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

4449.text:
Latitude 41.0°N, longitude 79.0°E lies in the Tian Shan, near the China–Kyrgyzstan border. Aksu is at roughly 80.3°E, 41.1°N, and Kyrgyzstan's Inylchek region is at about 79–80°E, 42°N. At 41°N, 79°E, the point is in the southern Tian Shan foothills of Xinjiang, northwest of Aksu, in the Keping area. The Keping Tagh ranges and Uchturpan are around 79.5°E, 41.2°N, and Uchturpan sits at about 1,400 m. Atush (Artux) is at 76°E, so it is not nearby.

Moving west along 41°N, the Kokshaal range lies further north and the Tarim basin edge is further south, at about 40°N. At 79°E, the ground at 41°N is probably in the Keping mountains, with elevations of about 1,500–2,500 m. The Aksu River's Tarim headwaters flow through lower ground near 80°E. I estimate about 1,800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

13505.text:
The point is at 61°S, 169°W, in the South Pacific sector of the Southern Ocean. It lies south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region, and the ridge crest near 61°S is around 170°W to 150°W. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about 65°S 175°W up to about 56°S 145°W, so at 61°S the axis is near 170–160°W. That puts the point close to the ridge, where depths are roughly 2500–3500 m.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge there is a fast-spreading ridge with a crest depth of about 2500–3000 m. The Udintsev Fracture Zone lies near 57°S 145°W. At 169°W and 61°S the point is likely on the ridge flank at roughly 3000 m depth. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

892.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 165.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands (which are around 75°N, 140-150°E). That puts it on the East Siberian/Arctic shelf edge or in the Amerasian Basin side. Near 81°N, 165°E, the point is north of the shelf break, which is around 77-78°N there. It sits near the Mendeleev Ridge region, whose crest runs from about 80°N, 180°W toward Wrangel. The Mendeleev Ridge is roughly at 175°E-180° and 80-83°N, and 165°E is at its western flank, toward the Podvodnikov Basin and Lomonosov Ridge. The Podvodnikov Basin is about 2000-2800 m deep. The Lomonosov Ridge is at around 140-150°E at 81°N. At 165°E, 81°N, the point probably lies in the Podvodnikov Basin at about 2000 m depth, or on the Mendeleev Ridge slope.

I estimate a seabed elevation of about -1900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

8164.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude -51.0 is in northern Brazil, near the mouth of the Amazon. Marajó Island spans roughly 0.5°N to 1.5°S and 48°W to 51°W. At -51.0 longitude and -1.0 latitude, the point is likely in the Amazon River channel, west of Marajó, on the Breves/Furo channels side. Breves is at -1.68, -50.48, so this point is northwest of Breves. That area is the Anapu/Amazon channel region: the Estreito de Breves, with the Amazon's northern channel running further north.

The point at -1.0, -51.0 is likely land, since it sits in the Marajó western portion or the Portel region, which is swampy forest. The Amazon's main channel runs near 0°, and the Jarí's mouth is at -0.5, -51.9. Marajó's western part has many rivers, but the point is probably floodplain land at a few meters elevation. I'll answer Solid at about 5 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":5}

13019.text:
Latitude -55, longitude -61 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Tierra del Fuego and east of Cape Horn. Cape Horn is at about -55.98, -67.27, and Isla de los Estados is at about -54.8, -64.5. At -61 longitude the position is east of Isla de los Estados, near the Burdwood Bank, which lies around -54.5, between -66 and -56.

The Burdwood Bank is a submarine plateau with depths of about 50-200 m. At -55, -61 it is probably on the southern edge of the bank, where the slope drops toward the Burdwood/Namuncurá bank edge. The bank's southern slope falls steeply to over 3000 m, toward the Scotia Sea and the North Scotia Ridge trench. The bank spans roughly -54 to -54.9 latitude, so -55.0 is probably at or just beyond its southern edge, on the slope. I estimate the seabed there at about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

3726.text:
Latitude 49.0, longitude 73.0 is in central Kazakhstan, near Karaganda (49.8N, 73.1E). At 49.0N it is south of Karaganda, in the Kazakh Uplands (Saryarka), where elevations are typically 500-600 m. Lake Balkhash is much farther south, and the Samarkand reservoir is near 49.9N, so this point should be land.

Near 49.0N, 73.0E the terrain is steppe at roughly 550 m. I'll estimate 560 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":560}

11736.text:
The point is at 41°S, 107°W, in the southeast Pacific, west of Chile. It lies on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise, which sits near 115°W at this latitude. The Chile Rise runs from the triple junction near 46°S, 76°W toward the southwest. The East Pacific Rise at 41°S is around 112–115°W, so 107°W is east of the crest on the Nazca plate flank, where the seafloor is fairly young and shallow.

Seafloor age here is roughly 10–15 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3000 + 350·sqrt(age). For 12 Myr that is about 3000 + 1200 = 4200 m. The area is also near the Chile Rise and its fracture zones, so I estimate about 3700 m depth, which fits a ridge flank in the Nazca plate area. The Nazca-Pacific ridge crest near 41°S is at about 111°W with a depth of about 2700 m. At 107°W, about 4° east (roughly 340 km), at a spreading half-rate of about 70 mm/yr that corresponds to about 5 Myr. The depth is then about 2900 + 350·2.2 = 3700 m, which agrees with my estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

13424.text:
The point is at 59°S, 29°E, in the Southern Ocean. It lies south of the Southwest Indian Ridge and southeast of the Weddell Sea region. It is well north of Antarctica, which sits near 70°S at that longitude, so the surface is water.

The seabed here is the Enderby Basin or the Weddell-Enderby Basin margin. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from about 40°S, 30°E down to the Bouvet triple junction at 55°S, 0°E. At 29°E the ridge is therefore around 50°S or north of it. At 59°S, 29°E the seabed is probably abyssal plain at roughly 4000–5000 m depth, though the Conrad Rise at about 53°S, 40–50°E is further east. I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

14770.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -159 lies in the Ross Sea sector of Antarctica, in the eastern Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°E to 150°W, with its front near 78°S at those longitudes. Since -75 is north of the shelf front, I would expect open water here, with the point lying in the Ross Sea.

Marie Byrd Land's coast sits near 77°S at 159°W, which is the ice shelf edge. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to around -78 at its front, and the coast of Marie Byrd Land borders it at about -77 to -78 for longitudes 150–160°W. So -75, -159 is in the open Ross Sea, to the north of the shelf front. Its depth is perhaps 500–600 m on the continental shelf. The eastern Ross Sea shelf is about 500 m deep, and at 75°S, 159°W it could be around -550 m. I'll go with that estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

10821.text:
The point (-31, -137) lies in the South Pacific, roughly southeast of Pitcairn and Easter Island and west of the East Pacific Rise. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor there is part of the Southeast Pacific basin, with typical abyssal depths of about 3,500 to 4,000 m. The location is far from the ridge crest, which is near 115°W at this latitude. Even so, the area near 137°W, 31°S is still fairly shallow. Seafloor age there is about 20-30 Myr, with a depth of about 4,000 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the East Pacific Rise are farther east, and the Austral and Gambier area lies to the west. I'll estimate about -3,900 m. This is an estimate from regional depth patterns, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

1902.text:
The coordinates 69.0°N, 25.0°E fall in Finnmark, Norway, near Karasjok and Alta. Alta is at about 69.97°N, 23.27°E, and Karasjok at about 69.47°N, 25.5°E. So 69.0°N, 25.0°E is on the Finnmarksvidda plateau, south of Karasjok, near the Finnish border. The border runs around 25.5°E at that latitude, so the point is probably in Norway, near Suolovuopmi (Soulovuobmi) or the Cuovddatmohkki area. Plateau elevations there are about 300-400 m.

Lakes are present on the plateau but the point is most likely on land. I estimate 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

10814.text:
The point (-31, -151) is in the South Pacific, southeast of Tahiti and the Australs, so it is open ocean. Seafloor depth in this region is typically around 4,000 to 4,500 m on the Pacific plate. The crust there is roughly 50 to 70 million years old, which fits a depth near 4,300 to 4,500 m.

I'm estimating this from regional knowledge rather than a specific lookup, so I'm using a typical depth of about 4,400 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4400}

9563.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -133 is in the South Pacific, in the western Tuamotu region. The Tuamotus span roughly -135 to -149 longitude, so -133 lies east of them. Nearby are the Gambier Islands (-23, -135) and Pitcairn (-25, -130), both far to the south. At -17, -133 there is open ocean on the Pacific plate, where seafloor depths are typically around 3800-4200 m. This area lies east of the East Pacific Rise's flank at an age of about 40-60 Ma, giving depths near 4000 m. The Tuamotu plateau area is shallower, but -133 is beyond it.

I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

4428.text:
Latitude 41.0°N, longitude 37.0°E is in northern Turkey. The Black Sea coast near Ünye/Fatsa sits at roughly 41.1°N, 37.3°E, and the coast around 37.0°E lies near 41.0–41.2°N. Samsun is at 36.3°E, and Ordu at 37.9°E. Fatsa is at 37.5°E, 41.03°N, and Ünye is at 37.28°E, 41.13°N. The coast near 37.0°E is therefore at about 41.15°N (Cape Yason, near Persembe, is at 37.7°E, 41.1°N).

At 41.0°N, 37.0°E the point is inland, south of the coast by roughly 15–20 km, in hilly terrain. That is south of Ünye, in the mountains. Elevation is perhaps 800–1000 m, so I'll estimate about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

704.text:
Latitude 83.0N, longitude 149.0E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands and the Laptev Sea shelf. The location is beyond the shelf break, near the Lomonosov Ridge region. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands toward Greenland, roughly along 140-150E at 83N. The ridge crest there is around 1000-1500 m deep, while the Amundsen Basin lies to the west of it at about 4200 m and the Makarov Basin to the east at about 4000 m.

At 83N, the ridge sits around 140E, so 149E could be on the Makarov side of the ridge, where the water gets deeper. The shelf break of the Laptev Sea is near 81N at 140E. By 83N, 149E, it is probably the ridge flank or the Makarov Basin edge. I estimate about 2000 m of water depth, so the seabed is around -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

2628.text:
Latitude 61.0, longitude 37.0 falls in northwestern Russia, in the Vologda Oblast area near Lake Onega. Lake Onega spans roughly 60.8–62.9°N and 34.5–36.5°E. At 37.0°E and 61.0°N the point is likely just east of Onega's southeastern shore, which is around 36.5°E near Vytegra (61.0°N, 36.45°E). So this point is on land.

Vytegra is at about 61.0°N, 36.45°E. At 37.0°E the terrain is roughly 100–130 m, on the Vepsian Upland, where elevations reach 150–200 m. A reasonable estimate is about 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

6475.text:
The point 19.0°N, 171.0°E lies in the central Pacific, northwest of the Marshall Islands. That area holds many guyots and seamounts, and the seafloor there is typically 5000-5800 m deep.

Wake Island sits at about 19.3°N, 166.6°E, which is well to the west. The Marshall Islands' northern atolls, such as Bikar and Taongi, lie near 12-14°N, 170°E. So 19°N, 171°E is in open ocean on the Pacific plate. Nearby seamounts include the Marcus-Wake seamounts to the west and the Magellan seamounts, but this point is likely abyssal plain or a seamount flank. I estimate a depth of about -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

8086.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 153.0°E lies in the western Pacific, north-northeast of New Ireland (Papua New Guinea). New Ireland's northern tip is near 2.5°S, 150.8°E, and the Feni and Tabar islands are around 3-4°S, 153°E. The point is about 4° north of those, in open ocean.

This area is the Ontong Java Plateau's northwestern margin or the Caroline Basin. The Ontong Java Plateau is centered around 2°S, 157°E, so 1°N, 153°E is probably on the western side of it, where the seafloor is about 3000-4000 m deep. The Caroline Basin to the northwest is deeper, around 4000 m. West of the plateau, the New Ireland Basin lies at roughly 2500-3500 m. I'll estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

10245.text:
Latitude -23.0, longitude 151.0 is in Queensland, Australia. Gladstone is at about -23.85, 151.25, and Rockhampton at about -23.38, 150.5. A point at -23.0, 151.0 lies inland from the coast, in the area south of the Capricorn coast near Mount Larcom and Raglan, which is roughly 25 km from the coast.

Curtis Island and Port Curtis sit around 151.2 to 151.3 longitude, and the coast near Keppel Bay is at about -23.4. At -23.0, 151.0 the location is about 40 km northwest of Gladstone, which puts it near Marmor or the Bajool area. That area is mostly land, with the Calliope range nearby. I'd estimate the elevation at about 80 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

13000.text:
Latitude -55, longitude -99 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and south of the Chile Rise region. This is open ocean far from land, so the surface type is Water.

For the seabed depth, I did not look up a bathymetry source, so this is an estimate. The Chile Rise and the East Pacific Rise lie farther west and north, and the Chile Trench is at about 74°W. Around 99°W and 55°S the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or part of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge sits near 115°W at 55°S, so 99°W is some distance east of it. Seafloor of moderate age there should lie at roughly 3500–4000 m depth. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2938.text:
Latitude 57.0, Longitude -63.0 is in northern Quebec/Labrador. Labrador's coast sits around -62 to -61 at 57N, and Ungava Bay is further north. At 57N, -63W, the location is inland in the Labrador Peninsula, north of Schefferville (54.8N, -66.8W) and west of the Torngat/Labrador coast. The Labrador coast near Hebron is at about 58.2N, -62.6W, and near Nain at 56.5N, -61.7W. At 57N, the coast is around -61.9W, so -63W is about 60 km inland, in the Torngat/Kiglapait mountains area. That area is mountainous, with elevations around 500-1000 m. Near the Kiglapait Mountains and Kaumajet, the terrain is rugged. I'd guess about 600 m.

There are also large lakes in the area, such as Lac de la Hutte Sauvage (Mistinibi) at 56.2N, -63.7W, but that is south of this point. So the point is likely land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":550}

4252.text:
Latitude 43.0, longitude 45.0 falls in the North Caucasus, in the Chechnya/Ingushetia/North Ossetia area, close to the Georgian border. The Greater Caucasus runs along roughly 42.5–43°N here. Around 45°E, 43°N the terrain is in the mountains of Chechnya, near Itum-Kale and Shatili (Shatili is at about 42.65°N, 45.17°E).

Argun river valleys at this latitude sit at about 1000–1500 m, and the surrounding ridges reach 2000–3000 m. The Chechen-Georgian border at 45°E lies at roughly 42.8–43.0°N. Itum-Kale is at 42.78°N, 45.55°E with an elevation of about 900 m. Point 43.0°N, 45.0°E is north of Shatili, in Ingushetia/Chechnya near the Assa river and Galanchozh, in the mountains at about 1200 m. The Ingushetia mountain area near Dzheirakh and Targim (42.8°N, 44.8°E) is also in this range. I'll estimate about 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

2799.text:
The point 59.0°N, 19.0°E lies in the Baltic Sea, east of Stockholm's archipelago and west of Gotland, in the Baltic proper. Gotland sits at about 18.5°E, 57–58°N, so it is well south of this point. Stockholm's outer archipelago ends near 19.0°E at about 59.5°N, so 59.0°N is open sea.

The Baltic proper in this area is deep, roughly 100–200 m. The Norrtälje–Åland Sea region at 59°N, 19°E is around 100 m deep. The Western Gotland Basin reaches about 250 m at 58°N, 19°E. I estimate the seabed here at about −150 m, though that is a judgment from regional depths rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

10488.text:
Latitude -27, longitude -83 is in the southeast Pacific, west of Chile and east of Easter Island (about -109.4 longitude). Salas y Gómez is near -105.5. The point lies over the Nazca plate, west of the Peru-Chile Trench, which is near -71 at this latitude.

The Nazca Ridge runs near -20 to -15 latitude, so it is not relevant here. At -27, -83, the seafloor is roughly 3800-4200 m deep, on the flank of the East Pacific Rise's eastern side. The Easter Seamount chain and the Nazca-Sala y Gómez ridge begin farther west, at about -103. The seafloor at -83 is about 30-40 Myr old and lies about 1000 km from the trench, so the depth is around 4000 m. I will estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11231.text:
The point (-35, -37) is in the South Atlantic, roughly east of Uruguay and southwest of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The Rio Grande Rise lies near 30°S, 35°W, and the Argentine Basin sits to the west. At 35°S, 37°W the water is about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The Brazil Basin is farther north, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is near 14°W, which is too far east to matter. Between the Rio Grande Rise and the Argentine Basin, the depth in this area is probably around 4,800 m.

This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

567.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 125°W lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Ellesmere Island and Axel Heiberg sit near 80-83°N, but at 125°W the point is well to the west, north of Borden and Meighen islands. The Canadian Arctic islands at 125°W reach only about 77-78°N (Prince Patrick Island is near 76°N, 120°W), so 83°N is open ocean.

That area is the Canada Basin margin or the Alpha Ridge region. Alpha Ridge is roughly 85°N, 110°W to 150°W. At 83°N, 125°W the seafloor is probably on the abyssal plain or the lower slope of the Canada Basin. The depth in the Canada Basin is about 3800 m, and the Alpha Ridge is about 1500-2500 m. Near 83°N, 125°W the bathymetry is probably the Canada Abyssal Plain edge, with the Sever Spur or the Alpha Ridge flank nearby. I estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

11953.text:
The point (-43, -33) is in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Argentine Basin. The ridge axis near 43°S sits at roughly 15–18°W, so this location is well to the west of it. The seafloor there is probably the western flank of the ridge, or the edge of the Argentine Basin, at around 4500–5000 m depth.

The Rio Grande Rise is farther north, near 30°S, and the Islas Orcadas Rise is farther south, so neither affects this point. Near 43°S, 33°W the depth is typically about 4800 m. I'll estimate -4850 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4850}

13908.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -83 lies in the Bellingshausen Sea, west of the Antarctic Peninsula. Adelaide Island is near -67.5, -68.5, and Alexander Island is near -72, -70. Longitude -83 at latitude -65 is well offshore, past the continental shelf edge. The shelf edge in this area is around -75 to -80 longitude at 68S, so at 65S and -83 the point is probably on the continental rise or abyssal plain, with a depth of roughly 3500 to 4000 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -3800 m. This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2384.text:
The point is 63.0°N, 91.0°W. That is in Nunavut, west of Hudson Bay. Chesterfield Inlet sits at about 63.3°N, 90.7°W, and Baker Lake is at about 64.3°N, 96°W. The coast near Chesterfield Inlet is at about 63.35°N, 90.7°W, so 63.0°N, 91.0°W is just inland, south-west of the inlet town, and Hudson Bay itself lies further east. The Chesterfield Inlet channel runs northwest toward Baker Lake, so the point could fall on land or on water.

Rankin Inlet is at 62.8°N, 92.1°W. The coast runs from Rankin Inlet northeast to Chesterfield Inlet, at roughly 63.3°N, 90.7°W. At 91°W the coast is probably around 62.9–63.0°N, so the point is likely right on the coast. I'll treat it as land, with low elevation of about 40 m, since terrain near the coast there is roughly 30–60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":40}

15190.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -39 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, in the area of the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Filchner Ice Shelf and Ronne Ice Shelf span roughly -30 to -80 longitude. At -79, -39 the location is probably the Filchner Ice Shelf's western part or the Berkner Island area. Berkner Island sits around -46 to -49 longitude, and the Filchner trough lies near -35 to -40 longitude at -78 to -79 latitude. So this point is likely floating ice shelf over the Filchner Trough, where the seabed is deep, roughly -500 to -1000 m. Near the Filchner ice front, the trough bed is about -600 m, and further south it reaches about -1000 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

5268.text:
Latitude 31.0, longitude -83.0 falls in south-central Georgia, near the Ben Hill / Irwin / Coffee county area. Ocilla is at about 31.6, -83.25, and Fitzgerald is at about 31.7, -83.25. Tifton is at 31.45, -83.5. Lat 31.0, lon -83.0 is probably near Lakeland or Statenville, in Echols or Lanier County. Valdosta is at 30.83, -83.28, and Homerville is at 31.04, -82.79. The point is near the Alapaha River, and Lakeland is at 31.04, -83.08, so it is close to Lakeland.

The elevation there is about 60 m. The land is flat and sandy, and Lakeland sits at roughly 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

8847.text:
This location, 9°S and 125°W, is in the central-eastern Pacific Ocean, southeast of the Marquesas Islands. It sits on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor age there is roughly 40-50 Ma, and typical depths in that region are around 4,000 to 4,300 m.

The Marquesas fracture zone lies in the area, and the seafloor is about 4,200 m deep. I'm estimating from typical regional depths, not from a lookup of this exact point, so I'll use -4150 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4150}

13593.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 7 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Antarctic Circumpolar Current region. It lies near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Bouvet Triple Junction area, with Bouvet Island at 54.4°S, 3.4°E. At 61°S, 7°E the area is close to the Mid-Atlantic Ridge/Southwest Indian Ridge and the American-Antarctic Ridge. The Southwest Indian Ridge begins at the Bouvet triple junction near 55°S, 1°W, and the ridge runs northeast. The Atlantic-Indian Basin is south of it at roughly 4000-5000 m depth.

At 61°S, 7°E, the seafloor is likely in the Atlantic-Indian Basin at around -4500 m, possibly with some influence from the Atlantic-Indian Ridge to the west. I'll estimate -4400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

10565.text:
Latitude -27, longitude 71 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar. It lies near the Central Indian Basin's western side, east of the Mascarene Plateau and the Rodrigues Ridge area, and near the Southwest Indian Ridge/Central Indian Ridge junction.

The Central Indian Ridge runs roughly from 25°S, 70°E southward toward the Rodrigues Triple Junction at about 25.5°S, 70°E. The point at -27, 71 is just southeast of the triple junction. Seafloor there is young, on the flank of the Central Indian Ridge, so depth should be roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m. The Southeast Indian Ridge branch heads southeast from the triple junction, and at -27, 71 we are probably near the axis or flank of that branch. Ridge depths there are about 3,000 to 3,500 m, so I estimate around -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

4228.text:
Latitude 43.0, longitude -3.0 is in northern Spain, inland from the Bay of Biscay coast, which sits near 43.4 N at this longitude. The point is in the province of Burgos or Cantabria, near the Burgos–Vizcaya–Cantabria border, in the Valles Pasiegos / Las Merindades area.

Longitude -3.0 at 43.0 N is close to Espinosa de los Monteros, which is around -3.55, 43.08. It is also near Villasuso de Mena (-3.2, 43.1) and the Mena valley. The Ordunte mountains lie at about -3.2, 43.1. Orduña is at -3.0, 43.0, and the Orduña Pass is nearby. The Orduña valley floor is around 280 m, and the town sits at about 290 m. Just east of -3.0 is the Álava enclave of Aguayo/Orduña, and to the west is the Burgos Mena valley, at about 250 m. The point may fall in the Orduña valley or on the Sierra Salvada escarpment to its south. Sierra Salvada's crest runs along 42.95 N, and the area at 43.0 N is the northern slope that drops down to Orduña. I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

1851.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -77.0 is in Nunavut, on Baffin Island's western side, or near the Gulf of Boothia region. Baffin Island's west coast sits around -80 to -90 longitude at 69N. Foxe Basin lies to the east of Melville Peninsula (about -84 to -80), and the Melville Peninsula spans 68-69N. At 69N, -77 is probably in the Gulf of Boothia or the Foxe Basin area near Igloolik (69.4N, -81.8). Going east from there, Baffin Island's Brodeur Peninsula is at about 72N. The northwest Baffin coast, around Admiralty Inlet, is at 73N. Baffin Island's interior at 69N, -77 lies in the Baffin lowland near Lake Amadjuak? No, that's at 65N. Foxe Basin's eastern shore at 69N is around -75 to -77 (Steensby Inlet area, 69.5N, -77). Steensby Inlet is at roughly 70N, -78.

The area is probably the Baffin Island interior, near Barnes Ice Cap's northern region or Prince Charles Island? Mary River mine is at 71.3N, -79. Point 69N, -77 is southwest of that, likely land on the Baffin plateau at a few hundred meters. Foxe Basin's coastline is at about 69N, -78 to -79 (Hall Beach is 68.8N, -81.2), so the coast at this latitude is near -79 to -80. So -77 is inland. Elevation is perhaps 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

9970.text:
The point is at 21°S, 39°W, which lies in the Atlantic Ocean off the coast of Espírito Santo, Brazil. The coast there is near 40.3°W, so the point is about 150 km offshore. That area is the Abrolhos/Espírito Santo continental shelf and slope region. The Vitória-Trindade seamount chain runs along about 20.5°S. The Besnard Bank and Davis Bank sit around 38°W to 35°W, and Vitória-Trindade chain seamounts begin near 39°W, so this point could fall on or near a seamount.

The shelf edge in this area is around 39.5°W to 38.5°W, and the slope descends beyond it. At 21°S, 39°W the depth is probably around 1000 to 2000 m on the slope, since the Abrolhos plateau is to the north and the Espírito Santo basin to the south. I'll estimate -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

6890.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude -79.0 is in the Caribbean Sea, southwest of Jamaica and east of Nicaragua. It lies in the Colombian Basin, off the Nicaraguan Rise to the northwest. San Andrés island is at about 12.5°N, 81.7°W, which is well to the west of this point.

At 79°W the Colombian Basin is deep, roughly 3,500–4,000 m. The Nicaraguan Rise sits north of about 14°N, and the abyssal plain of the Colombian Basin lies south of that. From memory, the depth near 13°N, 79°W is about 3,900 m, though this is an estimate and not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4296.text:
Latitude 43.0°N, longitude 133.0°E is in Primorsky Krai, Russia. The coast near Nakhodka sits at about 132.9°E, 42.8°N, and Partizansk is near 133.1°E, 43.1°N. That puts the point inland in the Partizansky district, in the Sikhote-Alin southern mountains.

The Partizansk river valley is around 43.1°N, 133.0°E. The surrounding hills reach roughly 300-700 m, and the Partizanskaya river valley floor is at about 50-100 m. The point is probably in the hills, so I estimate about 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

1873.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude -33.0 W lies in central-east Greenland, on the ice sheet. The Greenland ice sheet there is high: the summit is around 72.5 N, -38 W at 3200 m. At 69 N, -33 W, the ice surface is roughly 2700 m. The east side slopes down toward the coast near Kangerlussuaq (68 N, -32 W), but that is about 100+ km east, so the coast is further east at about -30 to -25 W. At -33 W, 69 N, the ice surface is probably about 2500 m.

The surface elevation near the ice-sheet divide is about 3000 m at -37 W. Going east, at -33 W it would be around 2600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2600}

10289.text:
The point (-25, -121) lies in the South Pacific, east of Easter Island (about -109.4 longitude) and west of the Chile coast. It is also southeast of the Pitcairn/Henderson area (around -128 longitude). It sits on the East Pacific Rise's eastern flank, near the Easter Microplate region. The East Pacific Rise axis is around -113 at this latitude, so -121 is on the Pacific plate flank, about 8° west of the ridge, roughly 800 km.

Crust age there is perhaps 10-15 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3000 + 350*sqrt(age). With sqrt(12) ≈ 3.5, that is about 3000 + 1200 = 4200 m. The nearby seafloor is around 3600-4000 m, so I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

167.text:
Latitude 89°N, longitude 155°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, about 100 km from the North Pole. This is near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs close to the pole. The ridge is at 88-90°N and its longitude positions are roughly 140°E on the Siberian side and 50°W on the Greenland side. At 89°N, 155°E the point is on the Siberian side of the ridge, likely in the Amundsen Basin or on the ridge flank.

The Amundsen Basin is about 4200 m deep. The Lomonosov Ridge crest is about 1000-1500 m deep. Near the pole, the ridge is about 1000-1700 m deep, and the Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side. Longitude 155°E at 89°N is offset from the pole by 1° of latitude, which is 111 km. The direction from the pole along the 155°E meridian points toward the Siberian side (New Siberian Islands at 140-150°E), which is the same side as the Lomonosov Ridge's Siberian flank, and the Amundsen Basin is on the 0-180° side. The ridge crosses the pole roughly along the 140°E to 40°W line. Since 155°E is about 15° away from that line, the distance from the ridge axis is 111 km × sin(15°) ≈ 29 km. That puts the point on the ridge or its flank, on the Makarov Basin side, since 155°E lies on the Amerasian side of the ridge line. The depth is probably about 1500-2500 m, so I'll estimate 1900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

14199.text:
The point is at -67.0, 139.0, in East Antarctica, in Adélie Land. Dumont d'Urville station sits at about -66.66, 140.0, and the coast near 139°E lies around -66.7 to -66.8. At -67.0 we are therefore roughly 25-30 km inland from the coast, on the ice sheet slope.

Ice surface elevation there is probably around 500-700 m. The Adélie coast ice rises steeply, reaching about 1000 m by roughly 60-80 km inland. At 30 km inland I estimate about 500 m, and I take the surface to be grounded ice (Solid).

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":550}

11111.text:
The point (-33, 83) is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and west of Australia. It lies near the Ninetyeast Ridge, which sits at about 90°E, so 83°E is west of the ridge. The area near 33°S, 83°E is in the Southern Indian Ocean basin, probably the Perth Basin or the Central Indian Basin. Typical abyssal depth there is about 4000 to 5000 m.

The Southeast Indian Ridge is further south, near 40°S or more. The seafloor at this latitude, south of the Amsterdam–St Paul plateau (38°S, 78°E), is likely about 4,500 m deep. I estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

12245.text:
Latitude -47, longitude -169 is in the South Pacific, southwest of New Zealand's Campbell Plateau and east of it. It lies in the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from around (-55, -140) to (-62, -150) and then toward the southwest, so it is far from this point. At -47, -169 the seafloor is abyssal plain at roughly 4,500-5,000 m depth. It is south of the Chatham Rise and east of the Campbell Plateau, which sits near 170E. The Southeast Pacific basin there is typically about 4,800 m deep.

I'll estimate -4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

13399.text:
Latitude -59, longitude -21 lies in the Scotia Sea, northeast of the South Orkney Islands (which sit near -60.5, -45) and west of the South Sandwich Islands (about -57, -27). It is in the Weddell Sea/Scotia Sea area, and the South Sandwich arc lies at roughly -27 to -26 longitude, so -21 is east of the arc.

East of the South Sandwich Islands is the South Sandwich Trench at about -30 to -27 longitude, with depths up to about 8000 m. At -21 the point is on the South American–Antarctic plate (the Atlantic side), where the seafloor is abyssal, around 3000–4000 m deep. The seafloor at -59, -21 is likely about -3500 m, with the Weddell Sea basin to the south. The ridge to the east is the American–Antarctic Ridge at around -2 to 0 longitude, so the point is in between. I estimate about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

698.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 137°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands (about 75°N), in the Eurasian Basin or near the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near 140°E at 85°N and runs toward the Laptev Sea side. At 83°N, 137°E the point is probably on the Laptev Sea continental slope or in the Amundsen Basin edge. The Laptev shelf edge is around 78-79°N, so at 83°N the seabed is deep.

The Amundsen Basin has depths of about 4000 m. The Lomonosov Ridge is at about 140°E near 87°N. At 83°N, 137°E the point is likely in the Nansen Basin, whose floor lies around 3500-4000 m deep. The Gakkel Ridge is at about 85°N, 100°E, and by 137°E it is near 80-81°N, so 83°N is on the Amundsen Basin side. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

10451.text:
The point is at 27°S, 157°W, in the South Pacific. The Austral Islands lie to the west of it (Rapa is near 27.6°S, 144.3°W, and Raivavae near 23.9°S, 147.7°W), and the Cook Islands are farther north. No island sits at this position, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor in this part of the South Pacific is usually 3,500 to 4,500 m deep. The area is east of the Austral seamount chain, probably on abyssal plain or low rises on the Pacific plate. The crust there is roughly 70 to 100 Myr old, which suggests a depth of about 4,300 to 4,500 m. I'll estimate -4400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

15155.text:
Location: -79.0, -109.0 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector/Marie Byrd Land, inland of the Getz/Dotson area. At 79°S, 109°W, this is likely the interior ice sheet of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, near the Pine Island/Thwaites upstream region or the Hudson Mountains. Pine Island's catchment lies around 76°S, 100°W. Thwaites is around 77°S, 107°W. At 79°S, 109°W it is grounded ice on the ice divide between Thwaites and the Ross side, with a surface elevation of roughly 1200 m.

The ice surface near 79°S, 109°W is probably about 1300 m. The bed there is around 0 to -500 m, but since grounded ice is classed as Solid, the surface elevation is what I report.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

2413.text:
Latitude 63.0 N, longitude -33.0 W is in the Irminger Sea / Denmark Strait area, southeast of Greenland. Greenland's southeast coast near Kangerlussuaq is around 68N, 32W, and at 63N the coast lies near 43W. So 33W is well offshore, about 500 km east of the coast. The location is on the Reykjanes Ridge's western flank, or in the Irminger Basin. The Reykjanes Ridge axis at 63N is around 27W, and the basin floor west of it is about 2,000-2,500 m deep. At 33W the depth is probably about 2,000 m, since the ridge flank slopes down from about 1,000 m at the axis to around 2,500 m.

At 63N the ridge crest is near 25-26W with depth about 700-1000 m. The flank at 33W is about 7 degrees west, roughly 350 km away. Depth there is probably about 2,000-2,500 m. I'll answer -2000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

5110.text:
The point 33.0°N, 39.0°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 33°N lies at about 40–42°W, so this point is slightly west of the axis, on the western flank. The Azores are farther northeast, and the Kane Fracture Zone is farther south at about 24°N, so neither is relevant here.

Ridge crest depths in this area are around 2,000–3,000 m, and the flank at about 1–3° off-axis would be roughly 3,000–3,500 m deep. Bathymetry near 33°N, 39°W is probably around 3,400 m, so I estimate about -3,300 m. This is an estimate from regional ridge geometry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

4073.text:
Location 45.0N, 47.0E is in the Caspian Depression in southwestern Russia, in Astrakhan Oblast or Kalmykia, near the Kazakhstan border. The Caspian Sea's northern shore lies at about 46.5N at this longitude, so 45N is inland. Kalmykia's Black Lands (Chernye Zemli) sit at roughly -20 to -25 m, and the area near 45N, 47E is around -20 m.

Further east at 47E there is the Kuma-Manych area, but the Caspian shoreline at 45N is around 48-49E. The point is therefore on land, in the Kalmykia/Astrakhan border region, with an elevation of about -22 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":-22}

13475.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 131 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and north of Antarctica. It sits near the Southeast Indian Ridge, which here is the Australian-Antarctic spreading ridge. The ridge crosses around 50°S at 131°E, and the Australian-Antarctic Basin lies to the south. The ridge near 130°E is at about 51°S, so -59 is in the basin, with depths of roughly 4000-4500 m. The Antarctic margin begins around -64.

I'll estimate the seabed at about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

5262.text:
The coordinates 31.0, -95.0 fall in East Texas, near Trinity County and Houston County, around the Crockett and Groveton area. Groveton is at about 31.05, -95.12, and Lufkin is at 31.34, -94.73. The Trinity River lies to the west at about -95.0 to -95.1. Lake Livingston is at about 30.7, -95.0, so this point is north of it and probably not in water.

Terrain in this area is roughly 70 to 100 m. I estimate about 80 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

9760.text:
The point -19, -99 is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 2,000 km west of Chile and south-southwest of the East Pacific Rise crest. The rise sits near 113°W at this latitude, so -99° is to its east, on the Nazca plate flank. The Chile Rise and Easter Island are farther away, with Easter Island near 109°W, 27°S.

Seafloor age here is about 20–30 Myr, which suggests a depth near 3,800–4,000 m. Moving east from the East Pacific Rise at 19°S, the depth is about 3,000 m at 110°W and about 3,700 m at 100°W. Farther toward the Peru Basin at 90°W it is about 4,200 m. I estimate about -3,900 m at 99°W. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a lookup of the exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

3612.text:
The point 49°N, 155°W lies in the North Pacific, roughly midway between the Aleutians and Hawaii, northeast of the Hawaiian-Emperor chain. It sits in the open ocean over abyssal plain. The seafloor here is probably about 5,000 to 5,500 m deep, so I estimate a depth of roughly 5,300 m.

This is a depth estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5300}

1812.text:
The point 69.0°N, 155.0°W is on the North Slope of Alaska, south of Teshekpuk Lake and north of the Brooks Range. Teshekpuk Lake sits near 70.5°N, 153.5°W, so this point is well south of it. I'd place it near the Ikpikpuk and Colville drainages, probably in the foothills or the southern edge of the coastal plain. Elevation in that area is around 300 m.

Checking the geography further: the Brooks Range foothills at 69°N, 155°W are roughly 400 m. The Umiat area (69.4°N, 152.1°W) is about 80 m. The Ikpikpuk River headwaters near 69°N, 155°W are at roughly 150–250 m. The point could also fall in the Colville region or the Utukok uplands. Given these ranges, I estimate about 230 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":230}

1389.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 79.0 E lies in the Kara Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya and west of the Taymyr Peninsula. Siberian coastline near the Taymyr is at about 75N, 100E, and the Yamal and Gydan peninsulas reach roughly 73N. Open Kara Sea at 75N, 79E is therefore water.

Kara Sea depths in the central part are typically 50 to 100 m. Near 75N, 79E the depth is probably around 40 to 60 m, so I estimate a seabed elevation of about -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

12270.text:
The point (-47, -119) is in the South Pacific, far from any land, so the surface type is Water. It lies southeast of New Zealand and west of Chile, on or near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank. The ridge crest is at roughly -55 to -65 latitude near longitude -120, and at -47 the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or rise, around 3500-4000 m deep.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge reaches about -50 latitude near -115 longitude, with depths of 2500-3000 m on the crest. At -47, -119 the seafloor is possibly part of the Easter microplate region's southern extension or the Udintsev fracture zone area. Seafloor age is probably 20-30 Myr, giving a depth near 3600 m. I estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

13018.text:
Latitude -55, longitude -63 is in the Drake Passage region, south of Tierra del Fuego (Isla de los Estados is near -54.8, -64.5). At -63 longitude and -55 latitude, that is open ocean, south of the Burdwood Bank (which sits around -54.5, -60 to -65). Burdwood Bank is at roughly -54 to -54.8 latitude with depths of 50-200 m. The point at -55.0 is near the southern edge of the bank, where the slope descends into the Yaghan Basin, so depth could be roughly 1000 m or more.

The Burdwood Bank's southern edge is steep, at about -54.9 to -55.0. At -55, -63 I would guess a depth around -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

7183.text:
The point 11°N, 147°E lies in the western Pacific, northeast of Papua New Guinea and south of the Marianas. It is near the Caroline Islands region, in the Caroline Basin or Philippine Sea area. The Caroline Basin has depths of about 4000 m, and the Pacific plate to the east is deeper at 4500–5000 m.

At 147°E, 11°N, the area is west of Chuuk (151°E, 7°N) and south of Guam (144.8°E, 13.4°N). This is probably in the Caroline Basin or near the Caroline Ridge, with depths of around 4500 m. The Mariana Trench axis is near 142–147°E only at about 11°N, where Challenger Deep is at 142.2°E, 11.4°N, so 147°E is well east of the trench. Pacific abyssal plain depth there is around 4500–5000 m, though the area is also close to the Caroline Islands seamounts. I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

15091.text:
The point (-77, 123) is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land, near Vostok Subglacial Highlands/Dome C region. Dome C is at about -75.1, 123.35, with an elevation of 3233 m. At -77, 123 the ice surface is probably about 3300 m. Vostok Station is at -78.46, 106.8, with an ice surface of 3488 m. The area south of Dome C toward -77 is roughly 3100-3300 m, so I'll estimate about 3250 m.

This is grounded ice, so the surface type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3280}

1825.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude -129.0 W is in the Northwest Territories, Canada. It lies west of Great Bear Lake and east of the Mackenzie River delta. Franklin Bay is farther north, near 69.5 N, -126 W, and Paulatuk is at 69.35 N, -124.07 W. The Anderson River is at about -128 W. Norman Wells is at 65.3 N, so this point is well north of it. Inuvik is at 68.36 N, -133.7 W. Tuktoyaktuk is at 69.4 N, -133 W.

At 69 N, -129 W the terrain is tundra on the Anderson Plain, with elevation around 200 m. The Horton River is near -126 W. The Smoke Mountains and Parry Peninsula are in the area, and the Eskimo Lakes are near -131 W. The land here is probably rolling hills at about 150 m, though lakes are possible. I'll answer Solid at 180 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":180}

10057.text:
The point at latitude -21.0, longitude 135.0 is in the Northern Territory of Australia, in the Tanami/Davenport region southeast of Tennant Creek. It is inland, so the surface is solid.

Elevation in this area is probably around 350 m. Nearby reference points are Barrow Creek at about 400 m (latitude -21.5, longitude 133.9) and Tennant Creek at about 376 m. The Plenty Highway region around longitude 135, latitude -21 sits on the Sandover and Bonney Downs plains, roughly 300 to 350 m. I'll use 320 m, which is an estimate from those neighbors rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":320}

11871.text:
Location: -41.0, 163.0 is in the Tasman Sea, west of New Zealand's South Island and southeast of Tasmania. It lies roughly 500 km west of the South Island, in the Tasman Sea basin near the Lord Howe Rise's southern extension and the Challenger Plateau region. The Tasman Abyssal Plain is at around 4,500-5,000 m depth. The Challenger Plateau is at 168-172°E, so 163°E is west of it. At 41°S, 163°E, I think the depth is around 4,500 m, on the abyssal plain edge.

The Lord Howe Rise runs through 160°E at around 30°S and fades southward. At 41°S, the Tasman basin floor is around -4,400 m. The Westland Basin lies further east. I'll go with -4,500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

14679.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 19 is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The coast there is near 70°S at this longitude, so -73 is inland, in the Wohlthat Mountains / Orvin Mountains region. The Wohlthat Mountains lie around 71.5°S, 13°E. The Orvinfjella and Mühlig-Hofmann Mountains are near 72°S, 5°E, and the Sverdrupfjella is near 72°S, 0°E. At 19°E, 73°S, the ice sheet is on the plateau behind the mountains, probably around 3000 m elevation.

The Wohlthat range extends to about 15°E, and the ice surface just south of it is roughly 2500–3000 m. I'll estimate 3000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

11731.text:
Latitude -41, longitude -117 lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. It is far from the coast, which is at about -73 longitude. The East Pacific Rise is near -112 at latitude -40 or so, and its crest sits at roughly 2,800-3,000 m depth. At -117 the point is west of the ridge crest, on the Pacific plate flank, so the seafloor should be a little deeper, about 3,000-3,500 m.

Around latitude -41 the East Pacific Rise is intermediate-to-slow spreading, and its crest is closer to -111. The point is about 6° of longitude west of the crest, or roughly 500 km. With a half-spreading rate of about 40 mm/yr, that is about 12 Myr of age, which gives a depth of about 2,600 + 350·√12 ≈ 3,800 m. I'll estimate about -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

5948.text:
The point 23.0°N, 163.0°W lies in the Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. It is near French Frigate Shoals (23.75°N, 166.2°W) and Gardner Pinnacles (25.0°N, 168°W), but about 300 km east of the shoals, so it is open ocean.

That puts it south of the Hawaiian Ridge and north of Kauai (22°N, 159.5°W). Kauai is about 3.5° of longitude east, roughly 360 km. Seafloor in this area is typically 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The Hawaiian moat and arch are present, and the seafloor south of the ridge near Necker is about 4,700 m. I estimate about -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

2018.text:
Latitude 67.0, longitude -103.0 is in Nunavut, Canada, near the Kitikmeot region. It lies south of Queen Maud Gulf and west of the Boothia Peninsula, on the mainland. Rae Strait is farther east, near -95. Queen Maud Gulf spans roughly -102 to -95 at about 68 N. The point at 67 N, -103 is inland, southwest of Queen Maud Gulf. Nearby are Bathurst Inlet (around -108, 67.5 N) and Contwoyto Lake (-110). Lakes in this area include Tahiryuaq (Takijuq) Lake, near -113, and Napaktulik Lake. I believe the elevation is about 200 m, so I'll treat it as land.

The terrain there is tundra with many small lakes, and the elevation is perhaps 150–250 m. I'm choosing 190 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":190}

11805.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 31 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa and south of the Natal Valley. It lies near the Indian-Antarctic Ridge region, but the more relevant features are the Agulhas Plateau (around 26°E, -40°S) and the Mozambique Basin. At 31°E, -41°S, the seafloor is probably on the Southwest Indian Ridge's northern flank or in the Agulhas Basin. The Southwest Indian Ridge near 31°E lies at about -45° to -50°S. The Del Caño Rise and the Conrad Rise are further east, around 40-50°E. So this point is probably in the Agulhas Basin or Transkei Basin, at a depth of roughly 4500 to 5000 m.

I'll estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

4166.text:
The point 43.0°N, 127.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 500 km west of the Oregon coast (Coos Bay is near 124.3°W). That puts it west of the continental slope, on the abyssal plain or the Juan de Fuca plate.

The Gorda and Juan de Fuca ridges lie farther west. The Juan de Fuca Ridge is near 130°W at 45–48°N, and the Blanco Fracture Zone is around 43–44°N, 127–130°W. At 127°W the Cascadia Basin floor is about 2800–3000 m deep. The Blanco Transform sits near 43.5°N at 128–129°W, and the Gorda Ridge reaches about 42–43°N near 126.7–127.5°W. The Gorda Ridge's southern end is near 40.5°N, 127.5°W, and its northern end near 43°N, 127.5°W joins the Blanco. The point is therefore near the northern Gorda Ridge, where depths run about 3000–3300 m. I'll estimate -3100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

11204.text:
The point (-35, -91) lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and south of the Galápagos. It sits roughly 1,500 km west of the Chilean coast at 35°S, and the Chile Rise and East Pacific Rise are farther west and south.

The Chile Trench is near 74°W, so -91° is well to its west. This area is on the Nazca Plate, which is fairly young, with seafloor depths around 3,500–4,000 m. The Juan Fernández ridge is near 33°S, 80°W, so it is not close. The Nazca plate seafloor at 91°W and 35°S is about 3,000 m of age-related depth. The Chile Rise spreading center runs near 35°S at about 95°W to 100°W, so this point is slightly east of the ridge. Ridge crest depth is about 2,500–3,000 m, which suggests roughly 3,300 m at this location.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

12506.text:
The point is at 49°S, 7°W, in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies southwest of Bouvet Island (54.4°S, 3.4°E) and roughly south of Tristan da Cunha, in the area of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge near 49°S is at about 10°W to 12°W longitude, and the ridge axis crosses the Bouvet fracture zone region. Near 49°S the ridge axis is at around 8°W to 10°W, with crest depths of about 2500 to 3500 m.

The point at 7°W is therefore near the eastern flank of the ridge. The Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone is farther north, at about 50°S, 10°E, which is not relevant here. I estimate a depth of about 3000 m on the ridge flank.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

1556.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude 53.0 (east) places the point in the Barents Sea, near the northern coast of Novaya Zemlya's southern island. Novaya Zemlya spans roughly 51–69°E, with its southwestern coast at about 52–55°E for latitudes 71–73°N. Matochkin Strait sits near 73.3°N, 55°E. At 73°N the west coast of the southern island is around 54°E, so 53°E is probably just offshore in the Barents Sea. Water depth there is about 100–200 m, but the coastal shelf to the west is shallow, so I'll estimate roughly 150 m deep.

Answer is Water, with a seabed elevation of about -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

5571.text:
Latitude 29.0°N, longitude 163.0°E lies in the northwest Pacific, east of Japan and south of the Emperor Seamounts region. That area is near the Marcus-Wake seamounts and the Mid-Pacific Mountains is further east, so the seabed there could be abyssal plain at around -5500 m or a seamount.

Nearby, the Japanese Seamount Province sits around 30°N, 150-160°E. The Magellan seamounts are at about 20°N, 155°E. At 29°N, 163°E I expect the Pacific abyssal plain at roughly -5800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5700}

13541.text:
The point at 61°S, 97°W lies in the southeast Pacific, southwest of Drake Passage. It is west of the Antarctic Peninsula and south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs near 60°S at about 150°W and bends toward the Chile Rise near 125°W, 55°S. The Chile Rise and Eltanin fracture zones are around 90°W. The Bellingshausen Sea basin is further south.

At 61°S, 97°W the seafloor is probably abyssal plain at roughly 4000 to 4500 m depth. I'll estimate -4300 m. This is an estimate from regional geography, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

10404.text:
Latitude -25, longitude 109 lies in the southeastern Pacific, west of Easter Island (Easter Island is at about -27.1, -109.4). Longitude 109.0 is positive, so it is East, not West. That puts the point in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia at roughly 25°S, 109°E.

Western Australia's coast is at about 114°E, so the point is about 500 km offshore. It is likely on the Wallaby Plateau or in the Cuvier Abyssal Plain area. The Wallaby Plateau is around 22°S, 112–10°E. The Cuvier Abyssal Plain lies near 23–26°S, 105–110°E with depths of about 5000–5500 m. I'll estimate about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

8902.text:
The point (-9.0, -15.0) lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa. It is near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits around 13°W at this latitude, and Ascension Island is at 7.9°S, 14.4°W, so the point is about 1 degree south of Ascension's latitude and roughly 0.6° west of its longitude.

The ridge axis near 9°S is at about 13.5°W. At 15°W the point is on the western flank of the ridge, where depths are typically around 3000 to 3500 m. The area near Ascension has a shallower ridge crest, but the flank at 15°W is probably around 3000 m deep. I estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

12314.text:
The point is at 47°S, 31°W in the South Atlantic. It lies southwest of the South Sandwich Islands and east of South Georgia, which is around 36°W. It sits near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs roughly through 15–20°W at this latitude, and the point is west of the ridge. The area is probably the western flank of the ridge, or the Georgia Basin to the west. Around 47°S, the ridge axis is near 11–16°W. The point at 31°W is therefore on the western flank, with depth around 3500–4000 m.

The North Scotia Ridge is further south, at about 53°S, so it is not a factor here. Depths here are likely about 3,500–4,500 m. I estimate 4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

12961.text:
This location, 55°S and 177°W, is in the South Pacific Ocean south of New Zealand and east of the Antipodes and Campbell Plateau. It sits on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern flank, roughly southeast of the Macquarie Ridge. Bathymetry there is likely abyssal, around 4,000-5,000 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crest sits further south and east, near 55°S at about 140°W, so this point is probably on the deep basin floor.

South of the Chatham Rise, near 177°W and 55°S, the Southwest Pacific Basin has depths of around 4,500-5,000 m, so I estimate about -4,900 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1421.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 143.0 E lies in the Laptev Sea / East Siberian Sea region. The New Siberian Islands are at about 75N, 140E (Kotelny Island at 75N, 140E, Bunge Land to the south). Anjou Islands: Kotelny spans about 137.5-146E and 75-76.1N. Longitude 143 on Kotelny's eastern side at 75N: the island's southern coast is around 74.9-75.1N, and Bunge Land is to the south. The point is therefore probably near the southern coast of Kotelny, possibly on land.

Kotelny's southern coast near 143E is roughly 75.2N, with the Bunge Land lowlands extending south of that. A point at 75.0N, 143E could be on Bunge Land, a flat sandy plain only a few meters above sea level, or it could be in the Sannikov Strait or the shallow water to the east. Bunge Land lies between about 74.5-75.0N and 142-146E, so the point is probably on it. I'll go with Solid at about 5 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":5}

12189.text:
The point is at 45°S, 79°E, in the southern Indian Ocean. It lies southeast of Amsterdam Island (about 37.8°S, 77.5°E) and well north of the Kerguelen Plateau, which sits near 50°S and 70°E. The area is crossed by the Southeast Indian Ridge and the Amsterdam–St Paul plateau lies further north, so the seabed here should be a mid-ocean ridge flank at roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m depth.

The Southeast Indian Ridge axis near 78°E runs at about 40°S to 45°S, with the ridge crest at about 2,500 to 3,000 m depth. Around 45°S, 79°E the seabed is probably deeper than that, near 3,500 m, on or near the ridge flank. I'm estimating from this regional picture rather than from a specific depth value, so I'll take about -3,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

12162.text:
The point at 45°S, 25°E is in the Southern Ocean, south of South Africa. It lies southeast of the Agulhas Plateau and the Agulhas Ridge region, and the Southwest Indian Ridge is farther south.

The Agulhas Plateau sits around 40°S, 26°E, with depths of about 2500 m. At 45°S, 25°E the seafloor is probably the Agulhas Basin or the Southwest Indian Ridge's northern flank. The Prince Edward Islands are at 46.9°S, 37.7°E, which is well to the east. The Du Toit fracture zone and the Andrew Bain fracture zone are around 30-35°E at 50°S. The Southwest Indian Ridge near 25°E lies around 52-53°S, so 45°S is likely in the Agulhas Basin or Agulhas Passage, where depths are about 4000-5000 m. I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

6094.text:
Latitude 23.0°N, longitude 129.0°E lies in the Philippine Sea, east of the Ryukyu Islands (Okinawa is near 26.5°N, 128°E) and southeast of the Ryukyu arc. The Ryukyu Trench is to the east of the arc at around 128–130°E at these latitudes. The Amami Plateau and Daito Ridge are further north, near 25–28°N. At 23°N, 129°E, the area is the Philippine Sea Plate west part, near the Gagua Ridge (about 123°E) and the Ryukyu Trench (about 127–128°E at 23–24°N). The Ryukyu Trench near 23.5°N, 126–127°E reaches about 6,000 m depth. At 129°E, we are east of the trench, so on the Philippine Sea basin (the Okinawa-Amami basin), which is typically 5,000 to 5,500 m deep.

I estimate a depth of about -5,300 m, which is an estimate rather than a retrieved value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

4238.text:
Latitude 43.0, longitude 17.0 falls in the Adriatic region of Croatia. The Pelješac peninsula lies at about 42.9N, 17.0E, and Korčula is near 42.95N, 17.0E. At 43.0N the point is just north of Pelješac, in the Neretva channel and Pelješac area. The Pelješac peninsula runs from about 17.2E at its base to 17.4E at its tip near Ston, so 17.0E is at the western end, near Orebić at 17.18E. To the west is Korčula, which spans about 16.7E to 17.2E at roughly 42.95N. At 43.0N, 17.0E, Korčula's north coast is at about 42.96N. The Pelješac channel lies between 42.95N and 43.0N, near Orebić at 42.98N, 17.18E, and 17.0E lies to the west of that.

North of Korčula is open sea toward Hvar, whose south coast is at about 43.1N. At 43.0N, 17.0E this is probably water, in the channel between Hvar and Korčula, with a depth of roughly 100 m. The Korčula channel is deeper in places, but the Neretva channel and the passage south of Hvar are around 100 m deep, so I estimate about -100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

15624.text:
The point is at 83°S, 109°E, which is in East Antarctica, on the plateau between Vostok (78.5°S, 106.8°E) and Dome A. Vostok station sits at about 3488 m, and the ice surface at 83°S, 109°E should be somewhat higher, roughly 3,200 to 3,500 m. Surface elevation increases toward Dome A at 80.4°S, 77°E, but this point is farther from the dome than Vostok is.

Thinking about the ice-surface slope: Vostok is at 3488 m, and the surface descends toward the Wilkes Land coast. The South Pole is at 2835 m. At 83°S, 109°E, the point is poleward of Vostok on the side toward the pole and the Transantarctic ridge. I estimate the elevation there at about 3,200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3250}

3567.text:
Latitude 51.0°N, longitude 115.0°W (Alberta, Canada) is in the Canadian Rockies foothills, west of Calgary near Kananaskis. Longitude 115.0 is ambiguous as stated, but with a positive 115.0 it would be in Mongolia/China (115°E, 51°N is in Russia/Mongolia border, Zabaykalsky Krai, near the Chita region). I take it literally as 115°E.

At 51°N, 115°E, the area is in eastern Zabaykalsky Krai near Baley and Gazimursky, which is steppe and low mountains at roughly 700 m. The Ingoda and Onon rivers lie near 51°N, 115°E, with the Onon at about 650 m. The area near Aginskoye is around 51°N, 114.5°E, at about 700 m. I estimate 750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":750}

9684.text:
Latitude -17, longitude 109 lies in the Indian Ocean, west of Australia and south of Java. It is far from land, so the surface type is Water.

The point is in the eastern Indian Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of the Australian coast (Dampier is around 117E). That area is mostly the Wharton Basin and Perth Abyssal Plain, which have depths of about 5,000 to 5,500 m. Around 17S, 109E the seafloor is probably about 5,000 m deep, so I estimate -5,000 m. This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

4025.text:
The point at 45°N, 49°W is in the North Atlantic, southeast of Newfoundland. The Newfoundland Basin lies east of the Grand Banks, and the Newfoundland Ridge (Fogo Seamounts) sits further south. At 45°N, 49°W the seafloor is likely abyssal at about 4,000–5,000 m depth. The Grand Banks edge is near 48–50°W at 45°N, with the continental slope reaching about 3,000–4,000 m. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far east at about 30°W.

This is from memory, so the estimate is approximate. I believe the point lies in the Newfoundland Basin at roughly 4,800 m depth, though the continental rise there could make it somewhat shallower. I'll go with -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

1514.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -31.0 is in East Greenland, inland from the coast. Scoresby Sund lies near 70.5°N, 22°W, and Kong Oscar Fjord is near 72.5°N, 24°W. At 31°W the point is well inland, on the Greenland ice sheet.

Ice-sheet surface elevation in this region is roughly 2,500 to 2,900 m. The EGRIP camp is at 75.6°N, 36°W with a surface elevation of about 2,700 m. The summit is at 72.6°N, 38.5°W and is about 3,200 m. At 73°N, 31°W the surface is probably around 2,400 to 2,600 m, which I estimate as 2,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

190.text:
The point is at 87°N, 159°W, in the Arctic Ocean on the Amerasian side of the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs roughly along 140°E to 40°W through the pole, so 159°W at 87°N is on the Amerasian side. That side has the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge nearer to 80°N and the Makarov Basin closer to the pole.

The Makarov Basin lies between the Lomonosov Ridge and the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge, at depths of about 4000 m. At 87°N, 159°W, the point is probably in the Makarov Basin or on the flank of the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge at 87°N is near 140°E–50°W, with the crest around 1000–1500 m depth. Longitude 159°W at 87°N is about 3 degrees of latitude from the pole, roughly 330 km away. The ridge on the Amerasian side is at about 150°W–170°W near 88°N, though I'm unsure of this. The Makarov Basin floor is around 4000 m deep, but the ridge flank would be shallower, so I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14431.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -117 is in the Amundsen Sea sector of West Antarctica, off the Pine Island/Thwaites area. Thwaites is around 75°S, 106°W, and Pine Island Bay lies near 74.5°S, 102°W. At -71, -117 we are north of the Getz Ice Shelf region (Getz is roughly 74°S, 125°W). The continental shelf edge in the Amundsen Sea is near 71 to 72°S at this longitude, so the point is probably open ocean over the outer shelf or the slope. Sea ice and ocean cover most of it.

Bathymetry here is probably around 500 to 3000 m deep. At 117°W the shelf break is near 72 to 73°S, so at 71°S the point is likely on the continental slope or rise, perhaps around -3000 m. I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

2340.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude -179.0 is in the Bering Sea, south of St. Lawrence Island (which sits near 63.4N, -170) and south of the Gulf of Anadyr. Land at this longitude is far to the west, since the Chukotka coast near Cape Navarin is around 62.3N, 179E. At -179 (181E), the point is east of Cape Navarin, so it lies over the Bering Sea shelf edge or slope.

The Navarin shelf edge runs northwest to southeast through about 62N, 179W. At 63N, 179W the point is likely on the outer shelf or just inside it, at a depth of about 100 m. The Gulf of Anadyr is to the northwest, and the Bering shelf is broad here, with depths of 80 to 120 m. I'll estimate about -110 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

11518.text:
The point -37.0, 177.0 lies in the Bay of Plenty, New Zealand. The coast near Whakatane is at about 177.0°E, 37.95°S, so this point is roughly 100 km north of the coast, near the continental shelf edge. Whakaari (White Island) is at 177.18°E, 37.52°S, and the point is about 55 km north of that. The Bay of Plenty shelf is about 100–200 m deep out to roughly 37.0°S. Beyond the shelf, the Havre Trough lies to the northeast, and the Colville Ridge is near 179°E.

Around 37°S, 177°E the seabed is probably the upper slope, at about 1000 m depth. The shelf edge in the Bay of Plenty is around 37.5°S, so at 37.0°S the seabed is on the slope toward the Havre Trough, which is about 2000 m deep. I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

15002.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -55 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, at the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly 60°W to 75°W at lower latitudes, and the Filchner part spans about 30–50°W. At -55°, -77° is probably in the Filchner-Ronne area near the Berkner Island boundary or the Foundation Ice Stream region. Berkner Island sits at about 78.5°S, 46°W, so at -77, -55 we are west of Berkner Island and likely on the Ronne Ice Shelf front, which is floating ice shelf. The seabed there is probably several hundred meters deep, perhaps about -500 m. Bedmap suggests the Ronne shelf's sea floor near the front is around -400 to -600 m.

I'll go with Water, at about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-520}

6726.text:
The point (15°N, 47°W) lies in the open Atlantic Ocean, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 15°N is around 45°W, so this point is about 2° west of the axis, on the western flank. The seafloor there is probably 3,000–4,500 m deep. Near 15°N the ridge axis sits at roughly 3,000 m depth and the flanks deepen, so at 47°W the bottom is probably around 4,000 m. The Fifteen-Twenty fracture zone (about 15°20'N) is close by and could make the local depth greater.

I estimate the depth at about 4,500 m, which is a guess rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

12224.text:
Latitude -45, longitude 149 is in the Southern Ocean, southeast of Tasmania, which lies at about -43 latitude and 147 longitude. The point is south of Tasmania and east of the South Tasman Rise. The South Tasman Rise sits around -47 latitude and 147 longitude, with depths of about 1000-3000 m.

At 149E and -45, the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain of the Tasman Basin's southern portion, at roughly 4000-4500 m deep. The continental slope of Tasmania runs along about 147-148E at -44. The area east of the rise should be around 4000 m, and I estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

6336.text:
Latitude 19.0 N, longitude -107.0 W lies in the Pacific Ocean, southwest of the southern tip of Baja California and west of Mexico's mainland coast. The Revillagigedo Islands are further west, near -111. Socorro Island is at about 18.8 N, -111. Cabo Corrientes is at about 20.4 N, -105.7. The Tres Marías are at 21.5 N, -106.5.

The point is therefore in open ocean, south of the Tres Marías and west of the Banderas/Colima coast. The East Pacific Rise's northern part and the Rivera Plate lie here. Depth near 19 N, -107 should be about 3000 m or more. The Middle America Trench is near -105 at 18 N. West of the trench the Rivera plate seafloor is about 3000 m deep. The Tres Marías Escarpment and the Tres Marías basin are at around 2500–3000 m. I estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

9773.text:
The point (-19.0, -73.0) is in the Pacific Ocean off northern Chile. Iquique is at about -20.2, -70.1, and the coastline near -19 sits at roughly -70.2. So the point is about 2.8° west of the coast, or around 300 km offshore.

The Peru-Chile Trench lies about 100-150 km off the coast at this latitude, with depths of around 7,000-8,000 m. The deepest part near 20°S is about 7,500 m or more. At 73°W, which is roughly 250 km offshore, the point is probably west of the trench axis, on the outer trench wall or the Nazca Plate. The trench axis near 19°S is around 71.5°W, so 73°W would be on the oceanic plate side, where the seafloor is about 4,500-5,000 m deep. The outer slope descends toward the trench, so I estimate about -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

648.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude 37.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Svalbard and Franz Josef Land. Svalbard's Nordaustlandet reaches about 80.5°N and Franz Josef Land's northernmost islands (Rudolf Island) reach about 81.8°N, so 83°N is open ocean. The point is north of the Eurasian shelf, probably near the continental slope or the Nansen Basin edge.

The shelf break north of Svalbard sits near 81–82°N at about 18–30°E. At 37°E, the shelf edge north of Franz Josef Land is near 82°N. At 83°N the seabed is likely on the slope or in the abyssal plain of the Nansen Basin, with depths of about 3000 m or more. IBCAO shows depths of roughly 3000–3800 m in the Nansen Basin at 83°N, 37°E. I estimate about −3400 m, since the slope is probably already descending there.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

9089.text:
Latitude -11, longitude -1 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, in the Gulf of Guinea region far to the southwest of Africa. It lies roughly 1,000 km west of the Angolan coast, which sits near 13°E at this latitude. It is also east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near 13°W at this latitude, so the point is on the eastern flank, in the Angola Basin.

The Angola Basin floor is about 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. The Guinea Rise and Walvis Ridge are not near this point. At 11°S, 1°W, the depth is probably about 5,000 m, so I estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

8241.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 103.0 is in central Sumatra, in Jambi province, which is inland. The location sits south of the equator, near the border of Jambi and West Sumatra. Kerinci is at about -1.7, 101.3, and Muara Bungo is at about -1.5, 102.1. At 103.0 E, -1.0 S, the area is likely lowland near Batang Hari, east of Muara Tebo, around -1.2, 102.4. It is probably peat and swamp lowland, with elevation around 30 m.

Sungai Penuh and Kerinci are farther west, so this point is in lowland between Jambi city (-1.6, 103.6) and the hills to the west. Nearby lowland elevations are roughly 20-50 m. I'll estimate 35 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":35}

2977.text:
Latitude 57.0, longitude 15.0 is in southern Sweden, in Småland. It lies east of Halmstad and west of Västervik, near Eksjö/Vetlanda. Eksjö is at about 57.67°N, so this point is further south, around Värnamo (57.18°N, 14.04°E) to Växjö (56.88°N, 14.8°E). The point is just north-east of Växjö, near Lake Åsnen or the Uppvidinge area. Terrain there is about 150-200 m.

Lake Salen and Lake Rumskulla are possible nearby lakes, but I can't place the point in one, so I treat it as land. I estimate the elevation at about 170 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

11482.text:
Latitude -37, longitude 105 lies in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia and east of Amsterdam Island. It is south of the Christmas Island region and west of Australia's Naturaliste Plateau, which sits near 112-115E and 34-35S.

At 105E, 37S the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain or the flank of the Broken Ridge/Southeast Indian Ridge system. Broken Ridge is at about 30S, 95-100E, and the Southeast Indian Ridge is near 38S, 78E (Amsterdam) and further southeast at about 45S, 100E. The Diamantina zone is near 35S, 105E, so the depth there is probably about 4000-5000 m. I'll estimate -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

5006.text:
Latitude 35.0, longitude 113.0 falls in Henan/Shanxi province, China. The point is near Jiyuan and Qinyang, close to the Taihang and Wangwu mountains. Jiyuan is at about 35.07°N, 112.6°E, and Qinyang at 35.09°N, 112.95°E. At 113.0°E, 35.0°N the point is just south of Qinyang, between Qinyang and the Yellow River near Mengzhou (34.9°N, 112.8°E). The Yellow River lies around 34.9°N here, so this point is likely on the North China Plain's edge, in the alluvial plain north of the river.

Qinyang sits at roughly 100 m elevation. South of it, toward Wuzhi and Mengzhou, the plain is around 100 m, and the river bed there is about 100 m. I estimate 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

10598.text:
Latitude -27, longitude 137 is in northern South Australia, near the Lake Eyre region, in the Simpson Desert/Stuart Creek area. Lake Eyre sits at about -28.4, 137.3, so this point is about 160 km north of it. It lies near Lake Eyre North's northern edge or the Andamooka area. Lake Eyre North spans roughly -28.0 to -29.0, so -27.0 is north of it, in the Warriner Creek, Peake and Denison area, and the Oodnadatta track runs near -27.5. The elevation there is about 50–100 m. Around -27, 137 is the Kati Thanda–Lake Eyre National Park's northern Simpson Desert margin, where the terrain is sand dunes at roughly 80 m.

I'll estimate 70 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":70}

8897.text:
The point (-9.0, -25.0) lies in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 9°S is at about 13-14°W, so -25° is about 11° of longitude to the west, on the western flank. Ocean crust age there is roughly 50-60 Myr, which gives a depth of about 4,500-5,000 m. The Brazil Basin lies to the west, with depths around 5,000 m. The Ceará Rise is farther north, near 3°N, 43°W, so it is not relevant here.

Near 9°S, 25°W the Atlantic is likely about 4,900 m deep. Fracture zones such as the Ascension, near 8°S, could cause local variation. I estimate -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

11946.text:
The point (-43, -47) is in the South Atlantic, southwest of the mid-Atlantic ridge and east of Argentina. It lies roughly off the Argentine Basin, east of the Rio de la Plata, so the surface type is Water.

The Argentine Basin has abyssal depths of about 5000 m. At 43°S, 47°W the location is east of the continental slope, which sits near 53°W, and in the basin floor. The Zapiola Rise is further southeast at around 45°S, 40°W, so this point is probably on the basin plain at about -5200 m. I estimate the seabed at about -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

12251.text:
Latitude -47, longitude -157 is in the South Pacific, southwest of the Cook Islands and south of French Polynesia, and far from any land. It lies on the Pacific plate, in the region between the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Austral and Cook island chains.

The seafloor here is abyssal plain, with typical depths of around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The crust at this position is probably about 60 to 80 million years old, which would fit a depth near 4,900 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so this is an estimate from typical depths for that crustal age.

