15358.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -63 lies in the southern Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, within the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf covers roughly 75-83°S, 30-80°W, so this point sits on the shelf, which counts as Water. The value to report is the seabed elevation beneath the shelf.

At 81°S, 63°W the point is on the western part of the Ronne Ice Shelf, south of Berkner Island and east of the Ellsworth Mountains and the Orville Coast. Bedmap2 puts the seabed under much of the Ronne shelf at roughly -400 to -900 m. Near the Antarctic Peninsula side the cavity is deeper, but at 63°W and 81°S I estimate the seabed at about -600 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

6680.text:
The point (15°N, 139°W) lies in the central Pacific Ocean, northeast of Hawaii and southwest of Baja California. This is open deep ocean, so the surface type is Water.

For the seabed elevation I did not look up a bathymetry dataset. I estimated from the typical depth of abyssal plains in this part of the Pacific, which is about 4,800 to 5,200 m, with seafloor age and sediment cover making the lower part of that range slightly more likely. The area lies north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone and is fairly deep. I settled on about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

11912.text:
The point (-43, -115) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of Easter Island and west of the Chile Rise. It is on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise, which is near 113°W at around 43°S. Along this part of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge/East Pacific Rise, the axis runs near -115° at about -43°. Axis depths there are about 2500-3000 m. The ridge at those latitudes is a fast-spreading ridge whose crest sits near 2300-2800 m depth. The point could be on or near the ridge crest, so I estimate a depth of roughly 2800 m, or slightly more if it is off-axis.

I could not pin the ridge position precisely, so this is an estimate: about -2900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

12264.text:
The point at -47, -131 lies in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and west of Chile, on the Pacific plate. It is north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which runs roughly along 55-65°S at this longitude, and well away from the East Pacific Rise crest. The seafloor here is probably about 40 to 50 Myr old and around 3,500 to 4,000 m deep.

Seafloor depth in this area is typically around 4,000 m, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at about -57°S, -140°W has a depth near 2,700 m. At -47, -131 the crust is perhaps 30 to 40 Myr old, which gives a depth of roughly 3,700 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

3454.text:
The coordinates 51.0°N, 111.0°W fall in southern Alberta, near the Alberta–Saskatchewan border region. More precisely, 111°W at 51°N is close to Bassano and Brooks. Bassano is at about 112.5°W, and Brooks is at about 111.9°W, so 111.0°W lies east of Brooks. That puts the point near Tilley, which is about 50.45°N, 111.5°W, or in the Suffield area. The area is prairie, and elevation there is roughly 750 m.

Nearby reference points: Medicine Hat is at 110.7°W, 50.04°N, with an elevation of about 715 m. Oyen is at 110.5°W, 51.4°N, about 700 m. The Red Deer River passes near 111°W at about 51.2°N, around Jenner, where the elevation is about 600 m. At 51.0°N the point is likely just south of the river valley, on prairie at about 720 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":720}

5195.text:
Latitude 33.0 N, longitude 131.0 E is in Kyushu, Japan, in Oita Prefecture near Kokonoe and Kuju, northwest of Aso. Aso caldera is around 32.88 N, 131.1 E. The point at 33.0, 131.0 is near Kusu or Kokonoe. Hita is at 33.32 N, 130.94 E, and Yufuin at 33.27 N, 131.37 E. Kuju mountains are at about 33.08 N, 131.24 E. Kokonoe town is at about 33.2 N, 131.2 E.

Just north of Aso, Oguni at about 33.08 N, 131.05 E lies at roughly 400 m. Kurokawa is at about 33.06 N, 131.1 E, around 700 m. At 33.0, 131.0 the area is probably in the hills south of Oguni, near Minamioguni or Kikuchi's upper reaches. Elevation there is likely around 600 m, so I estimate 650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":650}

2535.text:
Latitude 61.0, longitude -149.0 is in Alaska, on the north side of Prince William Sound near the Chugach Mountains. It lies east of Anchorage (about 61.2, -149.9) and south of the Matanuska area. Turnagain Arm is at about 60.95 to 61.0 N and runs from about -149.9 to -149.0 at its eastern end near Portage (60.85, -148.9). So the point is probably in the mountains north of Portage, near Portage Glacier or Placer River. Portage Glacier is at about 60.78, -148.85.

At 61.0, -149.0, that puts it in the Chugach Mountains around Placer Creek or Twentymile River. The Twentymile River valley sits near 60.95, -148.9, and the mountains north of it reach about 1500 m. Bartlett Glacier and Spencer Glacier are nearby but farther south. The location is mountainous, with an elevation of about 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1250}

6908.text:
The point 13°N, 43°W lies in the central Atlantic Ocean, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Lesser Antilles. The ridge axis at that latitude is near 45°W, so the point is close to the ridge, on its western flank.

At 13°N the ridge axis sits around 44.5°W, with a rift valley depth of about 4000 m. Roughly 1.5° off-axis (about 160 km) to the west, the seafloor is probably 3500–4500 m deep. The Fifteen-Twenty fracture zone is near 15°N, so this point is south of it. Seafloor ages out to this distance are about 8 Myr, which corresponds to a depth of about 3500–4000 m. I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

14041.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -177 is in the Southern Ocean, north of the Ross Sea, off the Pacific-Antarctic margin. The Antarctic coast near the Ross Sea is at about -78 to -70 at this longitude, and Cape Adare lies near 170E. At -177 longitude, the Ross Sea continental shelf ends around -73 to -74. At -67 it is deep ocean, in the region of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Balleny Islands to the west, on the abyssal plain or continental rise. Depth is probably about -3000 to -4000 m.

The Southeast Indian/Pacific-Antarctic basin there: the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further north, around -60 to -65 at -175, and the Adare Basin is to the west. At -67, -177 the depth is likely about 3400 m. I estimate -3400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

12453.text:
The point -49°, -113° lies in the South Pacific, west of Chile and southeast of the East Pacific Rise's southern portion, so it is open ocean.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from 55°S, 140°W up toward 40°S, 110°W. At 49°S, the ridge crest is near 115°W, so this point may sit close to the ridge, with depths around 2,500 to 3,000 m near the crest. The Chile Rise and the East Pacific Rise south of the Easter microplate meet the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge near 35-40°S. Around 49°S the ridge axis is at about 114-115°W, which puts -113° very close to the axis. Depths there are about 2,300 to 2,800 m. I'll estimate -2,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

14778.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -143 lies in the Ross Sea sector of Antarctica, near the eastern edge of the Ross Ice Shelf. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 150°W at the Marie Byrd Land coast, and the Edward VII Peninsula lies near 155°W, 77°S. At 75°S, 143°W the ice shelf front is around 78°S, so this point is probably in the Marie Byrd Land region, which is grounded ice or an ice shelf (Sulzberger Ice Shelf is at about 77°S, 150°W).

The coast of Marie Byrd Land near 75°S is around 75–76°S at 140°W (the Ruppert Coast and Hobbs Coast lie further east, at 75°S, 135°W). At 143°W, 75°S the ground is likely inland, perhaps over the Ford Ranges. Ice there is likely grounded with a surface elevation near 600–1000 m. The Ross Ice Shelf boundary near 143°W is probably around 77–78°S, and the Sulzberger Bay shoreline is near 77°S. So 75°S is inland, about 200 km from the coast, and the ice surface would be around 1000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

4471.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude 123.0 is in Liaoning province, China. It lies near Liaoyang and Anshan, roughly 41.0N 123.0E, and sits on the Liao River plain. Liaoyang is at 41.27N, 123.17E, and Anshan at 41.1N, 122.99E. Anshan is at the edge of the plain, with hills to its southeast. At 123.0E the point falls essentially on Anshan city, where the elevation is about 30 m. Haicheng lies to the southwest, and Anshan's urban area is at about 20-40 m.

I estimate 25 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":25}

11578.text:
Latitude -39.0, longitude -63.0 is in Argentina, in southern Buenos Aires Province. Locating it more precisely: Bahía Blanca sits near -38.7, -62.3, so the point is west-southwest of the city, which puts it inland near the Río Colorado/Buenos Aires border area. The Colorado River is near -39.0 at about -64 to -63. The point is probably in the Pampas, around Médanos or Algarrobo, at an elevation of about 60 m.

Looking at neighboring towns: Algarrobo is at about -38.9, -63.4, with an elevation of roughly 90 m. Pedro Luro is at about -39.5, -62.7, with an elevation of about 40 m. Between these, the point is likely near 70 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":70}

1947.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 115.0°E is in northern Siberia, in the Anabar Plateau / Olenyok area. The Anabar river lies around 113-115°E, and the Anabar Plateau reaches about 900 m at 70°N, 113°E. At 69°N, 115°E the point is near the Olenyok basin, southeast of the Anabar Shield. The Popigai crater is at 71.6°N, 111°E, so it is not close.

The Anabar river at 69°N runs at about 113.5°E, in the lowlands at roughly 100-200 m. The region around 115°E and 69°N is the Central Siberian Plateau's northern edge, probably about 200-300 m. I'll estimate 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

7136.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 53.0°E is in the Gulf of Aden/Indian Ocean region, east of Somalia's Horn of Africa. Cape Guardafui (Cape Gardafui) lies at about 11.8°N, 51.3°E. Ras Hafun is at about 10.4°N, 51.4°E. So 53°E is about 150 km east of Ras Hafun, which puts it in open ocean in the Somali Basin, near the Guardafui Channel's eastern side. Socotra lies at 12.5°N, 54°E, so this point is south-southwest of Socotra.

The depth here is probably about 4,500 to 5,000 m. The area between Socotra and the Somali coast has the Socotra-Hafun saddle, but at 11°N, 53°E the seabed is likely abyssal at roughly -4,800 m. East of the Guardafui Channel, the Somali Basin reaches about 5,000 m. The channel itself is about 1,000 to 2,000 m deep, and there is a steep drop east of Ras Hafun. I estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

619.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -21.0° is north of Greenland. Peary Land's northern coast, near Kaffeklubben Island, is at about 83.6°N, -30°. Lat 83.0, lon -21 lies in northeast Greenland, near Nioghalvfjerdsfjorden (79°N), well south of it. Station Nord is at 81.6°N, -16.6°. At 83°N, -21° the coast of Greenland lies at about 82.0–83.0°N, since the northeast coast around -20° is near 82.5°N (Lamberts Land? Actually, the coast near Cape Bridgman is at 83.0°N, -24°). Cape Bridgman sits near 83.0°N, -24.5°, so at -21° and 83.0° we're likely just offshore in the Lincoln Sea/Arctic Ocean, north of the coast at roughly 82.2°N, -21°.

The shelf there is probably 200-400 m deep, since the Greenland shelf north of Peary Land is narrow. I'll estimate -350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

12773.text:
The point at 51°S, 167°E lies southwest of New Zealand's South Island, south of Fiordland. It is near the Campbell Plateau's western edge, west of Campbell Island, which sits at about 52.5°S, 169°E. The Campbell Plateau is mostly 500-1000 m deep. The Macquarie Ridge runs from Fiordland down toward Macquarie Island at 54.5°S, 159°E, so at 167°E, 51°S the point may be near the Macquarie Ridge or the Solander trough area.

The Campbell Plateau's western edge is around 166°E at this latitude. To the west is the Emerald Basin, which is deeper than 3000 m. I'm not certain where exactly 167°E falls. The Macquarie Ridge lies at roughly 160-166°E, with the Puysegur trench at about 166°E, 48-50°S. At 51°S the ridge is near 165.5°E, so 167°E is probably on the plateau's western margin, a slope or shelf with a depth of around 700 m. I'll estimate -650 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-650}

11416.text:
The point (-37, -27) lies in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 37°S sits near 13°W, so this location is on the South American plate side, in the western Atlantic basin. The Rio Grande Rise is farther west, at roughly 30°W to 35°W and 30°S. At 27°W and 37°S the seafloor is probably abyssal, around 4500 to 5000 m deep.

The ridge flank at 37°S, 27°W is roughly 14° from the axis. At a spreading half-rate of about 1.7 cm/yr, that is around 1500 km, or about 85 million years old, which gives a depth of about 5000 m. The Rio Grande Rise region is shallower, but this point is south of it. I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

8624.text:
Latitude -5.0, Longitude 149.0 lies in the Bismarck Sea, north of New Britain. New Britain's north coast near Willaumez Peninsula is around 5.5°S, 150°E. At 149°E, the north coast of New Britain is around 5.8°S, so the point is offshore at about 5.0°S.

The area between New Britain and the Admiralty/Manus islands is the Bismarck Sea basin, which is deep, around 1500-2500 m. Near the New Britain coast the shelf is narrow, and 80 km north of the coast the depth is probably around 1000-2000 m. The Bismarck Sea's floor around 4°S, 148-149°E is about 2000 m deep. The Vitiaz Strait is to the southwest, and Umboi and Sakar lie near 5.5°S, 148°E. At 5°S, 149°E I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

4537.text:
The point 39.0, -105.0 is in Colorado, near the Rampart Range and Pike National Forest. It lies west of Castle Rock and Sedalia and east of Deckers. Longitude -105.0 at latitude 39.0 is about 20 km south of Sedalia, with the Rampart Range to the west near -105.1.

Terrain in this area is probably around 2,400 to 2,700 m. Perry Park, near Larkspur, sits at about 39.2 N, -105.0 and is roughly 2,000 m. At 39.0 N, -105.0, we're in the foothills southwest of Larkspur, near Dakan, Jarre Canyon and the Rampart Range front. Elevations there are about 2,400 to 2,500 m, so I estimate 2,450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2450}

6172.text:
Latitude 21.0, longitude -75.0 is in eastern Cuba. Las Tunas province lies around -76.9 and Holguín around -76.3, so -75.0 is further east, in Guantánamo province or just offshore. The northern coast of Cuba at -75.0 is near Baracoa, which is at about 20.35, -74.5. Cuba's northern coast around longitude -75.0 sits at about 20.7 to 20.8, near Cayo Moa and Banes at 20.96, -75.7. Near Nipe Bay the coast is about 20.8, -75.7. Farther east, at -75.0, the coast is about 20.65, near Sagua de Tánamo and Moa (20.65, -74.9).

So 21.0, -75.0 is about 35 km north of the coast, in the Atlantic. Depth there is probably on the Bahamas side, in the Old Bahama Channel approach or the northern slope. The Cuban shelf is narrow in this area and drops off quickly, so the depth is likely 1000 to 2000 m. I'll guess about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

14837.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -25 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica. This is the Filchner Ice Shelf region, whose western edge sits around 40-50°W, and the Brunt Ice Shelf near 25°W lies at about 75.5°S. Brunt Ice Shelf is at roughly 75.5°S, 26°W, and Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf is at about 72°S, 17°W. So -75, -25 is likely on or near the Brunt Ice Shelf or the grounded ice at Stancomb-Wills and Halley. Halley station is at 75.58°S, 26.2°W. At 75.0°S, 25°W, this is probably just south of the Dronning Maud Land coast, inland of the Brunt ice front, on the grounded ice of the Caird Coast. The Caird Coast runs about 75-77°S at 20-30°W, with the ice front near 75.5°S, so 75.0°S is probably in the Weddell Sea, north of the Brunt ice front. The Brunt extends north to about 75.2°S at 25°W, so this point is likely at the ice front or on the shelf. Water under the shelf or the ocean there would have a seabed depth of roughly -500 m, or deeper on the continental shelf there, perhaps around -600 m.

I'll answer Water with a seabed elevation of about -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

1884.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude -11.0 W lies in the Greenland Sea / Norwegian Sea region, northeast of Iceland and west of Jan Mayen. Jan Mayen sits at about 71 N, 8.5 W. Greenland's coast is at about -22 W or further west at this latitude, so -11 W is open ocean.

The point is on the Mohns Ridge / Iceland Plateau side. The Greenland Basin lies north of the Jan Mayen Fracture Zone and has depths around 3000 m. Between Iceland and Jan Mayen, the Jan Mayen Ridge and the Iceland Plateau are shallower, at about 1000–2000 m. At 69 N, 11 W, the Iceland Plateau lies south of Jan Mayen at about 70.5 N, so this point is probably at the Iceland Plateau's northern edge, near the Kolbeinsey Ridge's northern end. Kolbeinsey Ridge runs from about 67 N, -18 W to Jan Mayen. At 69 N it sits near -16 W. At -11 W we are east of the ridge, in the Iceland Plateau and the Jan Mayen Basin, with depths of about 1800 m. I'll guess -1900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

7150.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude 81.0 is in the Bay of Bengal, east of Sri Lanka's northern tip. Sri Lanka's Jaffna peninsula lies near 9.7N, 80.0E, and the east coast at 81.0E runs from about 7N to 8.5N, so 11N, 81E is well offshore in the sea.

The Bay of Bengal off the Tamil Nadu coast at that point is probably about 100 km offshore. The coast near Point Calimere is at about 10.3N, 79.85E, and the shelf there is narrow, so the depth at 81E is probably around 2,000 to 3,000 m. This is an estimate from geography, not a lookup. I'll take roughly 2,800 m of depth.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -2800}

5650.text:
The point 27°N, 39°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis. The ridge axis near 27°N is at about 45°W, so this is east of the axis, not west of it. Correcting that, the axis near 26°N sits at roughly 44–45°W, and 39°W is about 5–6° east of it.

At a spreading rate of about 25 mm/yr per side, 5° of longitude is about 500 km, which corresponds to roughly 20 Myr crust. Basement depth for crust of that age is about 3,500 + 350·√20 ≈ 5,000 m, but the flanks of the ridge are rougher and shallower. Typical depths here are about 4,800–5,000 m. Without a specific bathymetry lookup, I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

3402.text:
Latitude 53.0 N, longitude 145.0 E lies in the Sea of Okhotsk. The Russian coast near Ayan/Okhotsk is at about 56 N, and Sakhalin's northern tip is at 54 N, 143 E. The Shantar Islands are around 55 N, 137 E. So 53 N, 145 E is open sea east of Sakhalin and southeast of the Okhotsk coast.

This point is in the central-northern Sea of Okhotsk. The Deryugin Basin is at about 52 N, 144-145 E, with depths around 1,000 m or more. The Deryugin Basin lies east of Sakhalin at about 52-54 N and 144-146 E, with depths of roughly 800-1,700 m. I estimate the depth here at about 900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

13146.text:
The point (-57, -167) is in the South Pacific Ocean, southwest of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from (-65, -170) up to (-55, -140) or so. At -57 latitude and -167 longitude, the area is likely abyssal plain or the flank of the ridge, and I estimate the seabed at around -3500 to -4000 m.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge near 57S is at about 150W to 140W, and near 62S it is at about 160W. At 167W and 57S we are west of the ridge, on the flank of the Pacific plate. The Udintsev Fracture Zone lies near 56S, 145W to 150W. The Pitman Fracture Zone is near 62S, 170W. The area around 57S, 167W is probably a depth of about 4000 m, so I'll go with -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4413.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude 7.0 (east) is in northern Portugal, in the Douro region near Vila Real and Sabrosa, close to the Douro river valley. The Douro near Pinhão is at about 41.19 N, -7.54 W, so the longitude may be meant as 7.0 E, which would put the point in the Mediterranean.

Taken literally, 7.0 E at latitude 41.0 is in the Tyrrhenian Sea, west of Italy and east of Sardinia (Sardinia sits at about 8.5 to 9.5 E). The point is therefore in the open western Mediterranean, between the Balearic Sea and Sardinia, north of Algeria. That basin floor is the Algero-Provençal basin, with depths of roughly 2,700 to 2,900 m at 41 N, 7 E. The Sardinia margin lies further east, so I estimate about -2,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

14300.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -19 is in the Weddell Sea sector of East Antarctica, off Queen Maud Land. The Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf spans roughly 16°W to 34°W, with its front near -72 to -73 at these longitudes. At -69, -19 we are probably inland of the ice shelf front, in the Dronning Maud Land coastal region.

The Brunt Ice Shelf is near -75.5, -25. The Fimbul Ice Shelf is around 0 to -3°E. At 19°W, the coast is around -71 to -72 (Princess Martha Coast). So -69, -19 is in the open Weddell Sea, which is water. The bathymetry there is likely Weddell Sea abyssal plain, around -4000 m. The Maud Rise is at 3°E, -65, so not relevant. At -69, -19 the depth is probably about -4000 m, though it may be shallower, as the continental slope is farther south. I'll estimate -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

226.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -87° lies in the Arctic Ocean, close to the North Pole. It is on the Lomonosov Ridge side, or near the Amerasian Basin. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from Greenland/Ellesmere across near 88-90°N, at about 140°E to 40°W longitude. At 87°N, the ridge crosses around 50-60°W and 140°E. Longitude -87° is on the Amerasian side of the ridge, in the Canada Basin/Alpha Ridge region. The Alpha Ridge extends to roughly 85-87°N at longitudes of 110-150°W. At -87° longitude, the point is likely in the Amundsen Basin or just near the Lomonosov Ridge flank. The Amundsen Basin is about 4200 m deep. The Lomonosov Ridge crest is around -1000 to -1500 m.

The Lomonosov Ridge near 87°N lies around 140°E and about 50°W... Longitude -87° is far from -50°, about 37° of longitude. At 87°N, 37° of longitude spans about 0.6*37... roughly 130 km ×... a degree of longitude is 5.8 km, so 37° is about 215 km. That would put it on the Greenland side of the ridge, in the Amundsen Basin side (the Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side, though). The ridge crosses the pole from Greenland to Siberia, and the Makarov Basin lies on the Siberian side of the pole. At -87° longitude, the point is on the Canadian side, in the Makarov Basin, which is about 4000 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

10854.text:
Latitude -31.0, longitude -71.0 is in Chile, near the coast. The coast at 31°S sits around 71.6°W (Los Vilos is at 32°S, 71.5°W; Limarí/Tongoy is at 30.25°S, 71.5°W). So -71.0 is inland, roughly 50 km from the coast. The area is in the Coastal Range, in the Choapa/Limarí region, near Canela or Illapel (Illapel is at 31.63°S, 71.17°W). The terrain there is hilly, with elevations around 500-800 m.

At 31.0°S, 71.0°W, I'd estimate about 700 m. The area lies between Combarbalá (31.18°S, 71.0°W, elevation about 900 m) and Canela Baja. Combarbalá's town elevation is about 850 m, so I'll say 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

13886.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -127 lies in the Southern Ocean (Amundsen-Bellingshausen sector), well off the Antarctic coast, which is at about -73 to -74 at this longitude. The point is north of the continental shelf, in the deep ocean. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies further north-west, around -65 and -135 to -140. At -127 the seafloor there is likely about 3000-4000 m deep.

Ridge crest near -65 is at about longitude -175 to -150, so -127 is probably the abyssal plain or a ridge flank. Depth is estimated at about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15714.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -71 is in West Antarctica/Antarctic Peninsula's southern base area, near the Thiel Mountains and Ellsworth Mountains. The Ellsworth Mountains are around 78-79°S, 85°W. The Thiel Mountains are at 85°S, 91°W. At 85°S, 71°W, we are in the Foundation Ice Stream / Support Force Glacier area, bordering the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf, which is at about 82°S. So 85°S, 71°W is grounded ice on the Antarctic ice sheet. The Support Force Glacier is at about 83°S, 55°W. At 85°S, 71°W the ice surface elevation is probably about 1000 m, maybe 1100 m.

The Foundation Ice Stream is at about 84°S, 65°W, with surface elevation around 500-1000 m. Moving toward 85°S, 71°W, it is near the Pensacola-Pole transect (which goes from 84°S, 50°W toward the pole). Elevations along this transect rise from about 1000 m to 2000 m or more near 85°S. Near the Thiel Mountains the surface is about 2000 m. At 85°S, 71°W I'd estimate about 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

1099.text:
Latitude 77.0 N, longitude 141.0 W lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Alaska and Yukon, in the Beaufort Sea / Canada Basin. The nearest land is Prince Patrick Island, which is at about 76.5 N, 119 W, and Borden Island, at about 78.5 N, 111 W. At 141 W the Canadian Arctic Archipelago is far to the east, so this point is open ocean.

The Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. Near 77 N, 141 W the seabed is probably on the abyssal plain or the lower continental rise. The Beaufort continental shelf edge is near 72 N at this longitude, and the Canada Basin floor around 77 N is roughly 3700 to 3850 m deep. I estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

11006.text:
The point (-33, -127) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of Easter Island and west of Chile. This area is on the Pacific plate, east of the East Pacific Rise, which is near -110 longitude at this latitude. Seafloor depth there is typically 3500-4000 m, and the ocean floor at -127 is around 20 Myr old, so a depth of about 3800 m is plausible.

