At 51°N, 111°E the location is in Zabaykalsky Krai, Russia, near the Mongolian border. It is roughly west of Chita, in the Khilok/Onon area. Elevations there are about 800-1000 m. The Ingoda and Onon basins are around 600-700 m, and the area near 51°N, 111°E is probably in the Khilok valley or the hills near Petrovsk-Zabaykalsky (51.27°N, 108.8°E), which is farther west. At 111°E, 51°N, the Yablonovy range is to the north and the Kyra and Ononsky districts lie to the south-east. I estimate about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

2053.text:
The point 67.0°N, 33.0°W lies off East Greenland, in the Denmark Strait / Irminger Sea region. The Greenland coast near Kangerlussuaq is at about 68°N, 32°W. At 67°N the coast is around 34-35°W, so 33°W is just offshore. The continental shelf there is broad, with depths of 200-500 m. Near Kangerlussuaq the shelf edge is around 33°W at 67°N, and the shelf there is cut by a trough, the Kangerlussuaq Trough. The shelf edge probably lies near 35°W at 66°N. The Blosseville Coast runs about 68-70°N at 22-30°W, which is further north and east. Greenland's coast at 67°N is at about 33-34°W (Kangerlussuaq fjord mouth at 68.5°N, 32°W). So 67°N, 33°W could be right at the coast or on land, and the coast might lie further east.

Checking this: the Watkins Range sits near 68°N, 30°W. At 67°N the coast, near Cape Dalton and Cape Brewster, lies roughly at 66-67°N, 24-32°W. Cape Brewster is at 70°N, so that is not useful. Kangerlussuaq is at 68.2°N, 32°W. South of it the coast trends southwest, reaching 66°N at about 35°W, so at 67°N the coast is around 33.5°W. At 33°W the point is therefore probably just offshore, or on the mountainous coast. I'll go with water, at a depth of about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

2435.text:
The point 63.0°N, 11.0°E is in central Norway, in the Trøndelag region. It lies south of Trondheim, near Røros and Oppdal. Røros is at about 62.57°N, 11.38°E. Oppdal is at about 62.6°N, 9.69°E. So 63.0°N, 11.0°E is near Holtålen/Selbu, around Gauldalen, with Selbusjøen to the northwest.

Holtålen is at about 62.83°N, 11.1°E, with elevations of around 600 m. Moving north toward 63.0°N, the area around Gauldal and Haltdalen (Haltdalen at 62.93°N, 11.0°E, roughly 500 m) is similar. At 63.0°N, 11.0°E, the Selbu mountains lie to the north, so I estimate about 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

10908.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 37 is in the Indian Ocean, off the coast of South Africa/Mozambique. The South African coast near East London sits at about 27.9°E, so 37°E is roughly 900 km east of the coast. That puts it in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Durban, in the Natal Basin or near the Mozambique Basin's southern edge.

The Natal Valley lies at roughly 31–35°E. The Mozambique Ridge is at about 33–35°E, running from 27°S to 35°S. At 37°E, 31°S the point is east of the Mozambique Ridge, in the Mozambique Basin, where depths are about 5000 m. I'll estimate -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

8656.text:
The point at latitude -7.0, longitude -147.0 is in the central Pacific, south of the equator and northwest of the Marquesas Islands (around 9°S, 140°W). It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there lies on the Pacific plate in the Marquesas Fracture Zone region. Typical depths in this area are about 4,000–5,000 m. I could not recall a measured depth for this exact spot, so I estimate roughly -4,400 m from regional depths.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4400}

6686.text:
The point is at 15°N, 127°W in the northeast Pacific, far from any land, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain, typically about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. This area lies west of the East Pacific Rise and south-west of the Clipperton fracture zone, on crust roughly 30 to 40 million years old. Crust of that age usually sits at a depth of about 4,600 to 4,200 m, so I estimate about 4,700 m depth. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4650}

4520.text:
The point 39°N, 139°W lies in the northeast Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of northern California. That region is abyssal plain, lying west of the Mendocino Fracture Zone and east of the Hawaiian-Emperor area. The Pacific plate seafloor here is about 70 million years old or older, and the typical depth is around 4,500 to 5,000 m.

Near 39°N, 139°W I think the depth is about 4,800 m, though I am estimating from typical values rather than a specific chart. The Murray Fracture Zone lies further south, at about 32–35°N, so this point is likely north of it, on abyssal hills at roughly 4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4750}

15016.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -27 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, at the eastern side of the Weddell Sea. Longitude -27 at lat -77 lies near the Filchner Ice Shelf front, which is around 40°W to 30°W, so this is probably the Filchner-Ronne area. The Filchner Ice Shelf lies roughly between 30°W and 45°W at about 78°S. At 27°W and 77°S we are east of that, around the Brunt Ice Shelf and Stancomb-Wills area (Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf is near 20°W, 72°S). Coats Land has the Theron Mountains at about 26°W, 79°S. The Shackleton Range is at 25°W, 80°S. At 77°S, 27°W the surface is likely the grounded ice sheet of Coats Land, or possibly the edge of the Filchner Ice Shelf. The ice shelf edge near 77°S is around 35°W to 40°W, and the coast near 27°W is at about 77.5°S (Dawson-Lambton Glacier, near 26°W, 77.8°S). So 77°S is probably in the Weddell Sea, either an ice shelf or sea ice.

I'll guess Water, with a seabed depth of about -500 m. The Weddell Sea shelf there is fairly deep, probably 400 to 600 m, but it could be a floating shelf. I'll go with -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

1852.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -75.0 is on Baffin Island, in the Foxe Basin/Prince Regent area. Baffin Island's northern part spans roughly 70°N, -75°W, near the Barnes Ice Cap and Eglinton Fiord region, but that is not quite right either. The Barnes Ice Cap sits near 70.0°N, -73.5°W. At 69.0°N, -75.0°W, the point is southwest of the ice cap, on the Baffin interior plateau. This is the area near Nettilling Lake? No, Nettilling Lake is at 66.5°N. At 69°N, -75°W, the location is near the Foxe Basin's north end, west of Clyde River. The Baffin Island coast lies near Admiralty Inlet at -85°W, so this point is inland, in the Borden/Mary River region. Mary River is at 71.3°N, -79°W, so it is not there.

Probably the point is on the plateau, with an elevation of about 400 m, and I'll assume it is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

15181.text:
Location: -79, -57 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, within the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly longitudes -30 to -80, and at 79°S, 57°W it is on the Ronne Ice Shelf, west of Berkner Island (which lies near 45-50°W). So the surface is floating ice shelf, which counts as Water, and I need the seabed elevation beneath it.

Bathymetry under the Ronne Ice Shelf in this area is typically a few hundred meters below sea level. In Bedmap2, the central Ronne region has a seabed around -400 to -700 m. Near 79°S, 57°W the ice shelf is roughly 300-500 m thick, and the water column is probably 400-600 m deep. I'll estimate -520 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -520}

10992.text:
The point is at 33°S, 155°W, in the South Pacific. That puts it east of the Tuamotus and Austral Islands and southwest of Easter Island, in open ocean on the Pacific plate. The seafloor age there is roughly 40–60 Ma, which suggests a typical abyssal depth of about 4,000–4,500 m. The region lies in the southern Pacific basin and is probably not far from the Austral seamount chain, but I don't expect a seamount at this exact location.

I'll estimate the seabed at about -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12721.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 63 is in the southern Indian Ocean. The Kerguelen Islands lie at about -49.3 latitude and 69.5 longitude, and the Kerguelen Plateau extends south and west of them. At 63°E, 51°S the point is on the western flank of the northern Kerguelen Plateau, west of the islands. Heard Island is at 73.5°E, so it is not nearby.

The plateau's western edge is around 64-66°E at this latitude. The area near 63°E is probably the deep basin to the west, the Crozet Basin, or the slope down to it. Depths there would be roughly 3000 to 4000 m. The region between Crozet and Kerguelen has depths around 3500 m, and the plateau's western slope is steep. I am estimating about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

9009.text:
The point is at 11°S, 161°W, in the South Pacific near the northern Cook Islands and Tokelau. Nearby features: Manihiki Atoll is at about 10.4°S, 161.0°W, and Rakahanga is at about 10.0°S, 161.1°W. Manihiki sits on a seamount that rises from the Manihiki Plateau, where depths are typically 2,000–4,000 m.

At 11°S, the point is about 0.6° (roughly 67 km) south of Manihiki. That puts it on the flank of the plateau or in the surrounding basin. The Manihiki Plateau spans roughly 10–12°S and 160–167°W, with depths around 2,500–3,500 m. The western edge near 161°W is probably around 3,000–4,000 m deep. I'll estimate -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

6795.text:
Latitude 15.0, longitude 91.0 is in the Pacific Ocean? No. Longitude 91°E and latitude 15°N place it in the Bay of Bengal/Andaman Sea region. Longitude 91°E lies west of the Andaman Islands (which sit around 92-94°E), so this point is in the open Bay of Bengal. The Ninetyeast Ridge is at about 90°E, so 91°E is just east of it. Water depth there is around 3000 to 3500 m. The Bay of Bengal floor at 15°N, 91°E is probably about -3000 m, and the Nicobar Fan sediments are around 2500 to 3000 m deep at that spot.

I estimate about -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

13219.text:
Latitude -57, longitude -21 is in the South Atlantic, south of the Southern Ocean's northern boundary and west of the South Sandwich Islands (which lie around -26 to -28 longitude). Longitude -21 at lat -57 is in the Georgia Basin / the Scotia Sea's eastern part, near the North Scotia Ridge's eastern end. South Georgia is at about -36 longitude, and the South Sandwich Islands are at -26, with the trench to their east, so this point is west of the arc, in the East Scotia Sea basin or the abyssal plain.

The East Scotia Sea floor is typically around 3000-4000 m deep. At -57, -21 the seafloor is probably about -3200 m, and I am estimating that rather than recalling a precise value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

1126.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude -87.0° (87°W) falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Devon Island spans roughly 74.5–76.6°N and 80–92°W, so 77°N is just north of it, in Jones Sound or Norwegian Bay. Jones Sound lies at about 76°N, and Norwegian Bay at about 77.5°N and 90°W. Ellesmere Island's southern coast is around 76.5–77°N at 85–90°W (Hell Gate and Cardigan Strait, near Bjorne Peninsula at 77.2°N, 85°W).

At 87°W and 77°N, this is probably Baumann Fiord or Norwegian Bay. Graham Island lies to the northwest. Bjorne Peninsula sits at 77.3°N, 84.5°W, and Grise Fiord is at 76.4°N, 82.9°W. The south coast of Ellesmere near 87°W runs along Hendriksen Strait and Norwegian Bay at about 77°N. Norwegian Bay is west of Table Island, and Cape Sparbo is not relevant. Based on that, I place the point in the water of Norwegian Bay's eastern part, at roughly 100 to 300 m depth. I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

7238.text:
The point 9.0°N, 103.0°W lies in the eastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 900 km southwest of Mexico. It is east of the East Pacific Rise, which sits near 104-105°W at this latitude. The Clipperton Fracture Zone is near 10°N, and the Rivera and Cocos plates are nearby.

The East Pacific Rise crest at 9°N is at about 104.3°W, with a depth of about 2500 m. At 103°W the crust is roughly 1.3° east of the crest, about 140 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 55 mm/yr, that crust is about 2.5 My old. The depth is then about 2600 + 350*sqrt(2.5), which is roughly 3000 m near the ridge flank. Bathymetry at 9°N, 103°W is probably about -3000 m, so I'll estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

5608.text:
Location 27°N, 123°W is in the northeastern Pacific, west of Baja California, roughly 1,500 km offshore. The seafloor there is abyssal plain at about 4,000 m depth, and the northeast Pacific basin around this area is typically 3,800 to 4,300 m deep.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup of the actual value.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4000}

3493.text:
The point 51.0°N, 33.0°W is in the North Atlantic, just west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 51°N is at about 29-30°W, close to the Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone, which lies near 52°N. At 33°W the seafloor is on the western flank of the ridge, probably at 3000-3500 m depth. The Maxwell fracture zone region is also nearby, and the western flank here is typically around 3200-3600 m.

I estimate a depth of about 3400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

8068.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 117.0°E is in East Kalimantan, Borneo, Indonesia. It lies inland, west of the Mahakam River's lower reaches and north of the Kutai lakes area, so I'm treating it as land.

Kutai Barat is around 0°N, 115.5°E, and the Kutai Kartanegara coast is near 117°E at about 0°N. At 1°N, 117°E, the location is in the Kutai Timur/Bulungan region. The Kelai and Telen rivers are around there, and the Mahakam is farther south. The coast is at about 117.5°E at 1°N. The terrain is probably lowland hills, and I estimate an elevation of roughly 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

14800.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -99 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. Pine Island Bay sits near -74.5, -102, and the Thwaites area near -75, -106. At -99 longitude and -75 latitude, the location is probably west of Cosgrove and Abbot ice shelf areas. Abbot Ice Shelf spans roughly -98 to -92 longitude at about -72 to -73, so -75 is further south, which suggests grounded ice at the edge of the Ellsworth/Bakutis coast region. That would be the Thurston Island and Eights coast area, where the ice sheet inland of the Abbot coast lies at around -75, -99. Ice there is likely grounded with a surface elevation of around 1000 m.

I'm not certain, though, because there may be an ocean embayment. The Pine Island Glacier basin lies around -75.5, -100 and has a surface elevation of about 500–1000 m. The Pine Island ice shelf's grounding line is near -75.1, -100. The point at -99 is therefore likely grounded ice, roughly 800 m in elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

7471.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 3.0°E is in southwestern Nigeria, in the area of Ogun/Oyo state. It is about 7°N, 3°E, close to Abeokuta (7.15°N, 3.35°E) and Ibadan (7.4°N, 3.9°E). Ijebu-Ode is near 6.8°N, 3.9°E. This point is west of Abeokuta, around Ilaro/Ifo, and it is land.

Elevation there is probably around 80 m. Ilaro is about 70 m, and the area north of it is roughly 60–100 m. I'll use 75.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":75}

5473.text:
Location: 29°N, 33°W is in the North Atlantic, just east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 29°N sits around 42-43°W. Azores are at 38°N, so this point is well south of them. At 33°W, the crust is about 10-15 Myr old on the eastern flank, and depths there are roughly 3500-4000 m. The area south of the Azores plateau around 29°N, 33°W is likely abyssal hills, with depths of about 3800 m.

Looking at the ridge more carefully: at 30°N the axis is near 42°W, so 33°W is about 9° east, roughly 800 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 1.2 cm/yr this gives about 70 Myr. That gives depth of 2500 + 350*sqrt(70) ≈ 5400 m, but the flank is often shallower, and the region has the Atlantis-Great Meteor seamounts further east. The nearby area also has the Azores-Biscay Rise, so I'll estimate about 4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

4098.text:
The point 45.0°N, 97.0°E is in western Mongolia. It lies in Govi-Altai or Bayankhongor province, near the Gobi Altai. Bayankhongor's Bayanlig and Galuut area is around 99°E, while the Gobi-Altai capital Altai city is at 46.4°N, 96.3°E. So the point is somewhat south-southeast of Altai city, around the Gobi Altai range's southern foothills, with elevation likely about 2000 m.

Nearby, Biger lies at about 97.0°E, 46.2°N, with an elevation of about 1100 m. Tsogt is at 49.4°N, 96.5°E. Khalyun Gol is farther south of the point. The Gobi Altai ranges (Ikh Bogd at 44.9°N, 100°E) are to the east. At 45°N, 97°E there is a basin region, perhaps the Dzungarian Gobi area, so I estimate about 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

5244.text:
The point 31°N, 131°W is in the northeast Pacific, roughly 900 km west of Baja California and northern Mexico. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The region lies west of the Guadalupe Island area and sits on abyssal plain at depths around 4,000 to 4,300 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so the depth is an estimate from my general knowledge of this part of the Pacific. Seafloor age here is moderate, roughly 30 to 40 Myr, which fits a depth near 4,000 m. I settled on about -4,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4100}

12765.text:
The point (-51.0, 151.0) is in the Southern Ocean, southeast of Tasmania and south of Australia. It lies east of the South Tasman Rise and west of the Macquarie Ridge, in the Tasman Sea/Southwest Pacific basin region. The Macquarie Island ridge sits near 159E, so 151E is well to its west.

Seafloor depth in this area is probably around 4,000 to 4,500 m. The South Tasman Sea basin at 51S, 151E is probably abyssal, so I estimate about -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

2585.text:
Latitude 61.0, longitude -49.0 is off the southwest coast of Greenland. Southern Greenland near 61°N reaches only to about -48 to -42 longitude at its southern tip (Cape Farewell is at 59.8N, -43.9). At 61N the west coast lies around -48.0 to -48.5 (Julianehåb/Qaqortoq area is at 60.7N, -46.0; the coast near 61N is around -48.0 at Paamiut, which is at 62N, -49.7). At 61N, -49.0 the point is probably in the sea, west of the coast near Frederikshåb Isblink (~62N). The coast at 61N sits around -48 to -47, so -49 is about 50 km offshore.

The shelf off southwest Greenland is wide, with depths of about 100-500 m. The Julianehåb shelf at that distance might be around 200-1000 m. The continental slope near 61N is about -50 to -51 longitude, so at -49 the depth would be about 300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

13226.text:
The point at 57°S, 7°W is in the South Atlantic, southwest of Bouvet Island (54.4°S, 3.4°E) and north of the Weddell Sea. It lies south of the Atlantic-Indian Ridge. The North Scotia Ridge is far to the west, around 40°W, and the South Sandwich Islands are at about 27°W.

The nearby ridge is the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs around 10–15°W at 55°S. At 57°S the ridge sits near 0 to −5°W, close to the American-Antarctic Ridge junction at roughly 55°S, 0°E. The point at 7°W could therefore be on the western flank of the ridge or in the basin. Ridge crest depths there are around 2500–3000 m, and the flanks run about 3500–4000 m. The Weddell Sea basin to the south is about 4500 m deep. I'll estimate about −3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

8291.text:
The point (-3.0, -157.0) lies in the central Pacific, south of the equator and southeast of Kiritimati (Christmas Island, at about 1.9°N, 157.4°W). It is also well south of Fanning and Washington Islands, and the nearest land is probably Malden Island at about 4.0°S, 154.9°W. That is about 2° away, so this point is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is part of the Line Islands area, roughly 4,500 to 5,500 m deep. The Line Islands ridge lies further east, around 158°W to 150°W, so the depth here is probably about 5,000 m. I could not pin it down more precisely, so I am estimating -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

2726.text:
The point 59.0°N, 127.0°W lies in northern British Columbia, near the Yukon and Northwest Territories border. The Liard River region is close by. The Liard River at Fort Liard is at about 60°N, 123.5°W, so this point is west of there, in the Liard Plateau or the Kechika and Rocky Mountains area.

Fort Halkett is near 58.6°N, 125.8°W at about 400 m. The Toad River area is around 58.85°N, 125.2°W. The Kechika River runs near 59.0°N, 127.5°W, with valley elevations around 700 m. Surrounding ridges run to roughly 1500 m. The point is probably in the Liard Plateau or Kechika Valley, where I estimate about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

10450.text:
The point is at -27, -159, in the South Pacific. That puts it southeast of the Cook Islands and Austral Islands, near Rapa (about -27.6, -144.3) and well west of it. It is also well east of Rarotonga (-21.2, -159.8), so no island is nearby.

The seafloor here is part of the Pacific plate, with typical abyssal depths of roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m. The region around 27°S, 159°W is probably about 4,800 m deep, though there may be some seamounts in the Austral–Cook chain. I'll go with about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

15890.text:
The point is at 87°S, 79°W, in West Antarctica near the Ellsworth Mountains and the Thiel Mountains region. The South Pole is at 90°S, and longitude 79°W lies along the direction toward the Weddell Sea sector and the Ronne Ice Shelf.

At 87°S, 79°W the position is roughly 300 km from the pole, on the side toward the Thiel Mountains (about 85°S, 90°W) and the Pensacola Mountains (about 84°S, 55°W). This area is the interior of the ice sheet, so the surface is grounded ice. The ice surface elevation there is probably about 2,000 to 2,500 m. The Foundation Ice Stream and Academy Glacier are near 84°S, 70°W, with surface elevations of about 1,000 m. At 87°S, 79°W the surface is probably higher, near 2,200 m. The Thiel Mountains region at 85°S has surface elevations of about 2,000 m. Toward the pole the surface rises to 2,800 m. At 87°S, 79°W I estimate about 2,300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

10662.text:
The point (-29, -95) lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and south of Easter Island (about -27, -109). It sits on the Nazca plate, east of the East Pacific Rise and west of the Chile Trench, which is near -74. The Nazca Ridge and the Sala y Gomez ridge run in other areas. Seafloor depth in this part of the Pacific is typically about 3500–4000 m.

The seafloor age here is around 20–30 Ma. The Chile Rise and the Easter microplate region are farther west. At 95°W, 29°S, the depth is probably near 3800 m, so I estimate -3800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

15006.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -47 falls in the southern Weddell Sea, in the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf region. The Filchner Ice Shelf spans roughly -30 to -45 W, and the Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly -45 to -80 W. At -77 S, -47 W we are likely on the Ronne Ice Shelf's eastern part, near Berkner Island. Berkner Island sits around -79 S, -45 W, so at -77 S, -47 W it is probably west of the island's northern tip, which is about -78 S. That puts the point on the Ronne Ice Shelf, which is floating.

The seabed under the Ronne Ice Shelf in this area is probably several hundred meters deep, around -500 m. Bedmap data suggest the Ronne Depression is about -600 to -1000 m deeper toward the south, and near Berkner Island the seabed is around -400 to -800 m. I'll estimate -600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

4071.text:
The point 45.0°N, 43.0°E lies in southern Russia, in the Stavropol Krai area. Stavropol itself is at about 45.04°N, 41.97°E, so this point is roughly 80 km east of it. That puts it near the Kalaus river region or the Levokumka area, close to the border with Kalmykia.

The Stavropol upland drops toward the east. Elevations near 43°E at 45°N are probably around 100-150 m. Neftekumsk is at about 44.75°N, 44.98°E, with an elevation of about 80 m. Blagodarny is at 45.1°N, 43.4°E, with an elevation of about 150 m. Based on those nearby places, I estimate about 140 m for this point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":140}

9838.text:
Latitude -19, longitude 57 is in the Indian Ocean, east of Mauritius (which sits near -20.2, 57.5) and a little northwest of it. The point is north of Mauritius, close to the Mascarene Plateau region. The Mascarene Plateau runs north from Mauritius and Réunion toward Saya de Malha, but it lies mostly east of 59°E. At 57°E and 19°S, the seafloor is probably the abyssal Mascarene Basin, with a depth of about 4,000 to 4,500 m. Mauritius is at 57.5°E, and the Mascarene Basin lies to its west with depths around 4,500 m. North of Mauritius at 57°E, depths are likely near 4,000 m.

I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

9272.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 5 E is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Angola, which is at about 12-13°E on the coast. The point is roughly 800 km offshore. It is east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge (about 13°W at that latitude) and lies in the Angola Basin, where depth is typically about 5000 m. Near 5°E, 13°S the Angola Basin abyssal plain is about 5200 m deep. The Walvis Ridge is further south, at about 20°S or below, so it does not affect this point.

I estimate a seabed elevation of about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

13381.text:
Latitude -59, longitude -57 is in the Drake Passage, north of the South Shetland Islands (which lie at about -62). It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The Drake Passage is deep. Near the Shackleton Fracture Zone, around 57°W and 59°S, the seabed is probably about 3,000 to 3,500 m down. The Shackleton Fracture Zone runs from Cape Horn toward the Antarctic Peninsula, roughly along 58–60°W at this latitude. I'm not sure whether this point sits on the ridge or beside it. The ridge crest there is about 1,500–2,500 m deep, and the basins on either side are about 3,500–4,000 m deep. At 59°S, 57°W I would guess a depth of about 3,400 m, which gives an elevation of -3,400 m. This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

726.text:
Latitude 81°N, longitude 167°W is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Chukchi Borderland and the Alaskan/Siberian shelf. The Chukchi Plateau sits around 75-80°N at 170-160°W. The Chukchi Borderland extends to roughly 80°N. At 81°N, 167°W the position is probably near the northern edge of the Chukchi Plateau or the Mendeleev Ridge slope, toward the Canada Basin.

The Canada Basin floor is around 3800 m deep. The Mendeleev Ridge lies at about 178°W to 180° and 80-83°N, with depths of roughly 1500-2500 m. At 167°W and 81°N we are probably on the slope between the Chukchi Borderland and the Canada Basin or the Mendeleev Plain. In IBCAO, the Northwind Ridge runs near 154-160°W, and the Chukchi Plateau is at 165-170°W up to about 79°N. North of that, the terrain drops to the Canada Basin at about 2000-3000 m. I'll estimate -2300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

2741.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -97.0 is in northern Manitoba, west of Hudson Bay. Churchill is at about 58.77 N, 94.17 W, so this point is roughly 150 km inland to the west-southwest. That area lies near Nueltin Lake and the Seal River region, but Nueltin sits farther west at about 60 N, 100 W. At 59 N, 97 W the terrain is likely Precambrian shield with many small lakes, at about 250 m elevation.

I'm treating it as land. A mid-range guess for this area is about 240 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":240}

11029.text:
The point (-33.0, -81.0) is in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 600 km west of Valparaíso, Chile. This lies west of the Chile Trench, on the Nazca Plate. The Juan Fernández Ridge is further north at around 33.6°S and 78-80°W, so the point is close to that ridge area. The Juan Fernández Islands are at about 33.6°S, 78.8°W, and the ridge extends west. At 81°W there may be seamounts, but the typical seafloor depth here is about 3500-4000 m.

The Juan Fernández chain extends west-southwest from the islands toward the Alejandro Selkirk area and beyond, so at 33°S, 81°W there could be a seamount, but I can't confirm one. I'll estimate the depth at about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2044.text:
The point 67.0°N, 51.0°W is in West Greenland, near Kangerlussuaq (about 67.0°N, 50.7°W). Kangerlussuaq airport sits at roughly 50 m elevation, and the fjord head is at about 51.1°W. The Ice Sheet margin lies near 50.0°W, east of this point.

The fjord runs from about 50.7°W westward along 67.0°N. At 51.0°W the point is probably on land just west of the fjord head, close to the Sandflugtdalen and Qinnguata Kuussua area. The airport is at 67.01°N, 50.71°W. At 51.0°W the point is about 11 km west of the airport. Kangerlussuaq fjord is a narrow river valley there, with a braided river flowing along about 67.0°N. The river and the lake Ferguson are along that valley, and the terrain nearby is hills of 100–300 m. I'll estimate the surface as Solid at about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

1319.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -61.0 is in northern Baffin Bay, off northwest Greenland. Melville Bay lies around 75–76°N, 60–65°W, and the Greenland coast near Upernavik is at about 72.8°N, 56°W. At 75°N the Greenland coast sits near 58–60°W, around Cape York at 76°N, 66.5°W. Further west, Baffin Island's coast is at about 70°N, so nothing solid is nearby. The point is therefore in open Baffin Bay.

The water depth in northern Baffin Bay at this location is roughly 600–2000 m. The central basin reaches about 2300 m near 73–74°N, 65–68°W. At 75°N, 61°W, I estimate about 1000 m. The Melville Bay shelf is closer to Greenland at about 60°W and 75.5°N, so this point may be on the slope. I'll guess -800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

8765.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude 71.0 is in the central Indian Ocean, near the Chagos Archipelago. The Chagos Archipelago spans roughly 5-7.5°S, 71-72.5°E. Peros Banhos is at about 5.3°S, 71.8°E. Salomon Islands is at about 5.3°S, 72.2°E. Egmont Islands is at about 6.6°S, 71.3°E. Great Chagos Bank is at about 6-7.5°S, 71-73°E. At -7.0, 71.0, the point is at the western edge of the Great Chagos Bank. The bank's western rim has depths of a few tens of meters, with a steep drop-off to the west. The point may be just off the bank, on the slope, where the depth is perhaps 500-1000 m.

The Great Chagos Bank extends from about 71.2°E to 73.0°E. At 71.0°E, 7°S, it is probably the slope west of the bank, since the Egmont Islands at 71.3°E sit on the western edge of the bank. So the point is about 30 km west of the edge, and the depth is probably around 1500 m. The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge has deep surroundings of around 3000-4000 m, with steep flanks, so I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

8616.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 133.0 lies in the Banda Sea / Arafura region, south of the Bird's Head of Papua. Land near there is at about -3.5 S, 133 E (Kaimana area is around -3.6, 133.7). At -5.0 S, 133 E the position is in the sea, south of the Papua coast and west of the Aru Islands (about 134 E, -6). The Aru Islands are at 133.5–134.5 E, so 133.0 E is west of them. That puts the point in the Arafura Sea shelf or the Banda Sea margin.

The Kai Islands are at about 132.7 E, -5.5 S. The point at -5.0, 133.0 is north of the Kai Islands and likely in deeper water of the Seram Trough's southeastern extension. The Aru Basin and Kai area have depths of roughly 500–1500 m. The shelf near Kai is shallow, but north of it the water deepens toward the Seram Trough, so I estimate about -1000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1000}

16053.text:
Latitude -89, longitude -113 is near the South Pole, about 111 km from it, on the West Antarctic side. The South Pole itself sits at about 2835 m on the ice surface. Longitude -113 lies along the direction toward the Amundsen Sea sector, and the ice surface elevation there at 89°S is roughly 2700-2800 m. The surface slopes down toward West Antarctica, but at 1° from the pole it is still around 2750 m.

This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 2750}

5940.text:
The point is at 23.0°N, 179.0°W, in the North Pacific northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. The nearest named features are Kure Atoll (28.4°N, 178.3°W) and Pearl and Hermes. Both are well to the north, so this point is open ocean south of the Hawaiian-Emperor chain.

The seafloor in this region is abyssal plain, typically around 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. Because the point lies south of the Northwestern Hawaiian Ridge, I expect it to sit on Pacific plate abyssal seafloor at about 5,300 m depth. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

2707.text:
The point 59.0°N, 165.0°W lies in the Bering Sea. Bristol Bay and the Alaska Peninsula are to the east. The Yukon-Kuskokwim delta sits near 61-62°N, 165°W, and Nunivak Island is at about 60°N, 166°W. St. Matthew Island is at about 60.4°N, 172°W, so it is not nearby. At 59°N, 165°W we are south of Nunivak, in the open shelf of the eastern Bering Sea off the north side of Bristol Bay. Shelf depths there are roughly 50-60 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the shelf, not a looked-up value.

I estimate the seabed at about -55 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-55}

5937.text:
The point is at 25°N, 175°E, in the North Pacific northwest of Midway. Midway is at about 28°N, 177°W (183°E), so this point is well west of it. It lies in the area of the Northwestern Pacific basin near the Emperor Seamounts and Hawaiian chain bend, and the Emperor chain runs near 170-173°E at 30°N or so. At 25°N, 175°E, I think this is deep ocean floor, roughly 5000-5500 m deep. Marcus-Wake seamounts sit near 20-25°N, 153-170°E, which is further west. Nearby is the Pacific plate abyssal region with depths around 5500 m.

I estimate a depth of about 5400 m, so the elevation is -5400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

9907.text:
The coordinates -21.0, -165.0 fall in the South Pacific, south of the Cook Islands. Rarotonga is near -21.2, -159.8, and Niue is near -19, -170, so this point is roughly 5 degrees west of Rarotonga. That puts it in open ocean in the Cook Islands/Niue region.

The seafloor in this area is likely abyssal plain at about 5000 to 5500 m depth. The Tonga Trench lies farther west, so the point is not in the trench. Regional seafloor depths are around 5000 m, and I'll estimate -5200 m. This is a regional estimate, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

3579.text:
Latitude 51.0°N, longitude 139.0°E is in the Russian Far East, in Khabarovsk Krai. It is near the Sikhote-Alin mountains, west of the Tatar Strait. The coast near Sovetskaya Gavan is around 49°N, 140.3°E, and De-Kastri is at about 51.5°N, 140.8°E. At 139°E and 51°N the point is inland, in the Sikhote-Alin range, probably the Bureya/Amgun area.

Lake Kizi is at about 51.1°N, 140.5°E, so it is not at this point. The Amgun River is at roughly 50°N, 136°E to 52.5°N, 139.5°E, and the Amur river is farther north-east. The point is probably in hilly terrain, perhaps near the Gorin river, with elevation around 500 m. This is an estimate, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

3676.text:
The point 49°N, 27°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis. The ridge axis at 49°N lies near 28-29°W, so this point is just east of the ridge axis or on its flank. The Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone is near 52°N, so 49°N is south of it. The ridge crest at that latitude is around 28°W with depths of about 2500-3000 m. At 27°W, which is a little east of the axis, the depth is probably about 3000 m.

I'm estimating about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

7220.text:
The point is at 9°N, 139°W, in the central Pacific Ocean. That is northeast of Hawaii and south of the Clarion-Clipperton zone, in the northeast Pacific basin. The seafloor there is abyssal plain at roughly 4,800–5,200 m depth.

The Clipperton Fracture Zone lies around 10°N, with depths near 4,900 m. Seafloor age is about 50–60 Myr in this area, which is consistent with a depth around 5,000 m. I'll estimate -4,950 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4950}

13982.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 65 E is off the Antarctic coast near Prydz Bay and Mac. Robertson Land. The coast there is around 67-68°S at 65°E, since Mawson Station sits at 67.6°S, 62.9°E. At 65°S the point is therefore in the Southern Ocean, north of the coast, and the seabed depth is the quantity needed.

This area lies near the Kerguelen Plateau's southern part and the Princess Elizabeth Trough, whose depths are about 3000-4000 m. At 65°S, 65°E the seabed is probably the abyssal plain or continental rise, at roughly -3500 m. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9634.text:
Latitude -17, longitude 9 E is in the South Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Angola/Namibia. The Angolan coast near 17°S is at about 12°E, so 9°E is roughly 300 km offshore. This is on the Angola Basin continental rise or abyssal plain edge. The Walvis Ridge lies further south, around 20°S at 9°E, so at 17°S the seafloor is in the Angola Basin at roughly 4500-5000 m depth.

The shelf at 17°S is narrow and the slope descends to about 4000 m by 10°E. At 9°E, I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

869.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 119.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea / north of the Taymyr Peninsula. Severnaya Zemlya is at roughly 79-81°N, 95-107°E, so 119°E is east of it. At 81°N the point is in the northern Laptev Sea, near the shelf edge, which lies at about 78-79°N in this region. The continental slope north of the Laptev shelf drops steeply, so at 81°N, 119°E the seabed is probably around 2000-3000 m deep.

Nansen Basin / Gakkel Ridge region: the Gakkel Ridge runs near 120°E at about 82-85°N, and the Laptev slope lies near 80-81°N. At 81°N, 119°E the depth is likely about 2500 m. I'll estimate -2700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

13921.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -57 is on the northeastern Antarctic Peninsula, near the Trinity Peninsula and Larsen area. The Antarctic Peninsula at 65°S runs roughly from 64°W to 60°W on the west side, and the east coast near Larsen B/C is around 60-62°W. At 57°W, 65°S, the point is east of the peninsula, in the Weddell Sea, which is the former Larsen A/B region offshore. The Larsen B ice shelf extended to about 60°W, so 57°W is probably open water in the Weddell Sea. Seabed depth there is likely around 400-500 m, since the continental shelf is there, and it could be deeper further east.

I estimate that the point is water, with a seabed depth of about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

9399.text:
The point (-15, -101) is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Peru and Chile, on the eastern flank of the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise crest lies near 113°W at 15°S, so 101°W is about 12° east of it. Seafloor in this area is roughly 3,000-4,000 m deep. The Nazca plate seafloor there is around 20-30 million years old, which fits a depth of about 3,800 m. I'm estimating from this regional picture, not from a lookup of the exact cell.

I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

10454.text:
The point is at 27°S, 151°W, in the South Pacific. That puts it south of Tahiti and the Society Islands and near the Austral Islands: Rurutu is at about 22.5°S, 151.3°W and Tubuai at about 23.4°S, 149.5°W, both well to the north. Raivavae is at 23.9°S, 147.7°W, also not close. At 27°S, 151°W there is probably no island, only open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is Pacific abyssal plain with some seamounts. The Austral seamount chain runs through the region, but I can't place a specific feature at this point. A typical depth for the area is about 4,000 to 4,500 m, so I'll estimate roughly -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

15514.text:
Location: 83°S, 111°W is in West Antarctica, inland of the Amundsen Sea sector, near the Thwaites/Pine Island divide area and toward the Ellsworth Land interior. The Ross Ice Shelf is at roughly 180° to 160°W, so this point is not on it. At 111°W and 83°S, the ice sheet is grounded with a surface elevation of around 1,500–2,000 m.

The West Antarctic Ice Sheet divide lies near 79.5°S, 112°W with a surface elevation of about 1,800 m. Moving south to 83°S, the surface rises toward the Ellsworth Mountains and the Pole region, but along 111°W it is likely about 1,200–1,500 m. I estimate 1,300 m, a rough guess.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

1453.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -153.0 is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Alaska's North Slope (Barrow is at about 71.3N, 156.7W). That puts the point in the Beaufort Sea, roughly 200 km or more offshore.

The continental shelf edge there is near 72N, and the Chukchi Borderland and Canada Basin lie to the north. At 73N, 153W the seafloor is probably on the continental slope. Near 73N, 150W I recall depths of around 3000 m, with the Beaufort slope descending steeply from about 71.5N. At 153W the shelf break is near 71.5N, so 73N is about 170 km north of it. The slope there would be at roughly 2000–3500 m depth. I'll estimate about -3000 m, though the nearby Northwind Ridge could make it shallower, so this is uncertain.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

13585.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -9 is in the Southern Ocean, in the Atlantic sector. It lies north of the Weddell Sea and southwest of Bouvet Island, close to the Southwest Indian Ridge and the American-Antarctic Ridge. The American-Antarctic Ridge runs from the South Sandwich Trench area eastward toward Bouvet, and the Weddell basin lies to the south.

At 61°S, 9°W the seafloor is probably abyssal basin or ridge flank. The Bouvet triple junction sits near 54°S, 2°E, and the Weddell Sea abyssal plain is around 5000 m deep. The Atlantic-Indian Basin, around 60°S and 0°E, is about 4500 to 5000 m deep. Along the American-Antarctic Ridge, depths are around 3000 to 4000 m. At this point, which is west of 0° and north of the Weddell basin, I estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1322.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -55.0 is in northwest Greenland, in Baffin Bay west of Upernavik, which sits near 72.8N, 56W. At 75N, 55W the coast of Greenland lies around 58-60W, so the point is probably over the ice sheet's western margin or just offshore. Melville Bay's coast is at roughly 75.5N, 59W, and the ice sheet margin there reaches about 60W at this latitude. At 55W, 75N, the point is therefore inland on the ice sheet.

Ice surface elevation there is probably around 1500 m. Moving inland from the Melville Bay margin, the ice rises to about 1000 m at 50 km, and the point is roughly 100 km from the margin, since 4 degrees of longitude at 75N is about 115 km. That gives an elevation of around 1400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1450}

9747.text:
The point (-19, -125) lies in the South Pacific, west of the East Pacific Rise and east of the Tuamotus. The East Pacific Rise crest is near 113°W at this latitude, so 125°W is about 12° west of it, on the Pacific plate flank. Seafloor age there is roughly 15-20 Myr. Depth would be about 3500 + 350·sqrt(age), which gives about 4500 m at 17 Myr. The Pacific South flank is shallower than that, near 4000 m. Along the rise flank at 19°S, 125°W the depth is about 3900-4200 m. The Easter Island and Pitcairn region has depths of about 3500-4000 m at 120-125°W, and the area around 125°W, 19°S is likely about 4000 m.

I estimate a seabed depth of about 4050 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4050}

10493.text:
The point -27, -73 lies in the southeast Pacific, off the coast of Chile near Atacama (Caldera is at about -27.07, -70.8). At longitude -73 it is about 200 km offshore. Easter Island is at -109, so it is not nearby. Salas y Gómez is also much farther west.

The Peru-Chile Trench at this latitude lies roughly 100-150 km offshore, with depths of about 5000-6000 m. The trench axis near 27°S is at roughly -71.5 to -72, so -73 would be just west of it, on the Nazca Plate's outer trench slope. Depths there are about 4500-5000 m. The Copiapó area has trench depths of about 5000 m, and the seabed seaward of the trench is about 4000-4500 m deep. I estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

12321.text:
The point (-47, -17) is in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and southwest of Bouvet. The ridge axis near 47°S lies at roughly 14°W, and the sea floor on the western flank is typically 3000-4000 m deep. At 17°W, about 3 degrees west of the axis, the depth should be around 3500 m. Close to 47°S there is the Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone to the south, near 49°S, and the Meteor/Islas Orcadas area is farther west, so neither affects this point directly.

I estimate the sea floor at about -3600 m. This is a regional estimate from ridge-flank depths, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

15712.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -75 is in West Antarctica, south of the Ellsworth Mountains and the Thiel Mountains area. The Thiel Mountains lie near -85.5, -91, and the Ellsworth Mountains near -78.5, -85. At -85, -75 the point is on the interior ice sheet near the Foundation Ice Stream and Institute Ice Stream headwaters. Surface elevation there is probably about 1000-1500 m. The Support Force Glacier is near -83, -65, and the Foundation Ice Stream runs from about -84 to -83 at roughly -60. The Pensacola-Pole transect passes near -85, -60 with surface elevation around 1500 m. Moving to -75 longitude it is probably similar, perhaps 1200 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1250}

14886.text:
Location: -75, 73E in East Antarctica. This is near the Amery Ice Shelf region (Amery is about 70-71°E, extending south to roughly -73°S). At -75°S, 73°E we are inland on the grounded ice sheet, south of the Lambert Glacier area. The Lambert Glacier basin lies around 70-75°E, with the ice surface at -75 around 1000-2000 m. The grounded ice surface elevation near the upper Lambert/Mellor glacier is probably about 1500 m. Further south at 80°S the plateau is about 2500 m. At -75 and 73E, the surface is likely around 1000 m.

I'll estimate about 1100 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 1100}

8325.text:
The point (-3.0, -89.0) is in the Pacific Ocean, about 3°S and 89°W. That is roughly 400 km southwest of the Galápagos, which lie around 0 to -1.5°S and -89 to -91°W. The Galápagos platform is at about -0.5°S, so this point is south of it.

The area lies on the southern flank of the Galápagos platform, toward the Galápagos Rise and the Carnegie Ridge region. The Carnegie Ridge sits at about 1°S, 85°W and runs east. Seafloor depth to the south of the Galápagos is around 2500 to 3000 m. At 3°S, 89°W, the Galápagos Spreading Center is further north, at about 2°N. The seafloor here is therefore probably young Nazca plate crust at about 2800 m depth, which is my estimate rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

3289.text:
Latitude 53.0, longitude -81.0 is in James Bay, Canada. James Bay spans roughly 51–55.5°N and 78.5–82.5°W. Its southern end is around 51.3°N, and the western shore is at about -82.3° near 52–53°N (the Ontario coast near Attawapiskat, at about 52.9°N, -82.4°). At -81.0°, 53.0°N is therefore in open water, about 1.4° of longitude (roughly 65 km) east of the Ontario coast. James Bay is very shallow, with typical depths of 10–50 m. At this point the water is probably about 20–30 m deep.

I'm estimating the seabed at about -25 m from the typical depth, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-25}

6609.text:
The coordinates 17.0°N, 79.0°E fall in Telangana, India, near Nalgonda district. That is close to the Musi River area and Nagarjuna Sagar, which lies at roughly 16.57°N, 79.31°E. Nearby towns include Miryalaguda at about 16.87°N, 79.57°E and Suryapet at 17.14°N, 79.62°E. Nalgonda town is at about 17.05°N, 79.27°E, and Hyderabad is at 17.4°N, 78.5°E.

The Deccan plateau in this region sits at roughly 300 to 400 m. Since 79.0°E is west of Nalgonda, I estimate about 400 m for the elevation. The point is on land, so the surface type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":390}

8192.text:
The point (-1.0, 5.0) is in the Gulf of Guinea, in the Atlantic Ocean. It lies south of Nigeria and Ghana and west of Gabon, so it is open ocean.

The seabed depth there is roughly 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Gulf of Guinea abyssal plain at about 1°S, 5°E is near the mid-ocean region, and I estimate about -4,400 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the area, not a looked-up value.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4400}

2913.text:
The point 57.0°N, 113.0°W is in northern Alberta, northeast of Peace River and west of Fort McMurray. It lies near the Athabasca River region and the Wabasca lowlands, where the terrain is boreal plain at roughly 500-600 m.

Fort McMurray (56.7°N, 111.4°W) sits at about 250-300 m. Wabasca (55.9°N, 113.8°W) is at about 540 m. The Loon River area near 57°N, 115°W is around 600 m. Moving from there toward 113°W, the land is near the Marten Hills and Trout Mountain area, which is higher. I estimate the elevation at about 600 m. The Athabasca River is further east, so this spot is probably in the uplands between Red Earth Creek and the Athabasca River, at roughly 550 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":570}

12460.text:
The point (-49.0, -99.0) lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, well west of southern Chile. It is south of the Chile Rise and the Chile Triple Junction, which is near 46°S, 75°W. The location is about 1,800 km west of South America, on the Nazca/Antarctic plate near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the East Pacific Rise.

The East Pacific Rise at 49°S is near 113°W, so this point is east of the ridge crest. The seafloor age there is about 15–20 Myr, and ocean depth in this region is typically 3,500–4,000 m. Further east, the Chile Rise and its fracture zones lie closer to 90°W at about 45°S. Taking a depth of roughly 3,700 m for this location is a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

13322.text:
The point is at 59°S, 175°W, in the Southern Ocean south of New Zealand. It lies southeast of the Macquarie Ridge and Campbell Plateau, and north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is near 65°S at 175°W, and the Balleny region is farther west, so this point is probably on the abyssal plain or the flanks of the ridge. Depths there are about 3000-4000 m.

Near 60°S, 175°W the seafloor is roughly 3500 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from around 62°S, 160°W toward 65°S, 175°E. At 59°S, 175°W the point lies north of the ridge, with a depth of about -3600 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the area, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

8904.text:
Latitude -9, longitude -11 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and northwest of Ascension Island (which lies at about -7.9, -14.4). Ascension is at -14.4 longitude, so this point is 3.4° east of the island.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis near 8-9°S sits around 13-14°W. Ascension is on the ridge flank near the axis, so -11 longitude lies on the eastern flank. Ridge flank depths there are around 3000-4000 m. The Ascension fracture zone is near -8°S. At -9, -11, the depth is probably about 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

15028.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -3 is in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The Ekström Ice Shelf is at about 70.5°S, so -77 is far inland, near the Heimefrontfjella and Kirwanveggen area at roughly -75 to -74°S. At -77, -3 the location is on the polar plateau, in the interior south of the Kirwanveggen and Heimefrontfjella ranges, and ice surface elevation there is probably about 2500-3000 m.

The Recovery Glacier region lies near -81, -30. At -77, -3, the ice sheet surface is probably around 2,600 m, so I'll estimate 2,700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2700}

743.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -133.0° lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Prince Patrick Island is at about 76°N and Meighen Island at about 80°N, 99°W, so this point is well to the northwest of them. Ellef Ringnes is at about 78°N, 102°W. At 81°N, 133°W the point is in the Arctic Ocean off the Canada Basin margin, north of the Beaufort Sea.

The Canadian continental margin lies north of Prince Patrick Island, with the shelf edge around 76-77°N at this longitude. The Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. At 81°N, 133°W the point is probably near the Canada Basin edge, since the Alpha Ridge is further north, around 85°N. The Sverdrup Basin margin and the Arctic Ocean continental slope would put the depth at roughly 3500-3800 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

5072.text:
Location 33.0N, -115.0W is in southeastern California, Imperial County, east of the Salton Sea. It lies near the Chocolate Mountains / Imperial Valley area, roughly 33.0N 115.0W, north of Glamis and near Niland to the west. The Salton Sea is at about -115.8 to -115.5 longitude, so -115.0 is well east of it.

This area is around the Chocolate Mountains Aerial Gunnery Range, east of Imperial Valley, with elevations of roughly 200-400 m. Just east of Glamis (-114.95, 33.03) the elevation is about 100 m. At -115.0, 33.0 it is just west of Glamis, with dunes at about 90 m. The Imperial Sand Dunes run from about -115.0 to -114.7 at latitude 32.9 to 33.0. Elevation is roughly 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

15129.text:
Location: -79.0 latitude, -161.0 longitude (that is 161°W). This is in the Ross Ice Shelf, Antarctica. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 155°W to 160°E and extends north to about -78 at the front. At -79, -161 it is probably within the ice shelf, near the eastern part. Ross Ice Shelf front is around -77.5 to -78 at 160°W. So this is floating ice shelf, and the answer is Water with seabed elevation.

Seabed depth under the eastern Ross Ice Shelf at 79°S, 161°W: Bathymetry of the Ross Sea there is about 500-600 m deep. Ross Ice Shelf cavity depths from the Bedmap2/RTOPO: near Roosevelt Island (~79.5S, 162W) the seabed is shallow, about -300 m. At 161°W, 79°S, that is just northwest of Roosevelt Island, so roughly -400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

11600.text:
The point is at 39°S, 19°W, in the South Atlantic. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and south of the Rio Grande Rise, and it is close to the Tristan da Cunha region, which is at about 37°S, 12°W.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 39°S sits at roughly 13°W to 15°W. At 19°W, the seafloor is about 5 degrees west of the ridge axis, on the western flank. Depths there are typically 3500–4500 m, and the Tristan hotspot track (the Rio Grande Rise to Walvis Ridge) does not reach this longitude at this latitude. I estimate a depth of about 4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

2159.text:
Latitude 67.0, longitude 179.0 E is in Chukotka, Russia. Chukotka's coast on the Arctic side is near 69-70°N, and the Gulf of Anadyr lies to the south at about 64-65°N. At 67°N, 179°E, the point is inland, in the Chukotka highlands, probably near the upper Amguema or Vaamchyn area.

The Amguema River basin lies around 67-68°N, 178°E. Terrain near 179°E, 67°N is probably hilly, roughly 300-500 m. The Chaun lowlands are farther west. The Anyuy range and the Chukotka mountains in this part have elevations of about 500 m. This is an estimate rather than a lookup, so I'll use 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

8409.text:
Latitude -3.0, Longitude 79.0 is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Sri Lanka and the Maldives, roughly 3°S of the equator. The area is open ocean near the Central Indian Basin, where depths are typically about 4,500 to 5,000 m.

Longitude 79°E at 3°S is west of the Ninetyeast Ridge (which sits near 90°E) and east of the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge (about 73°E). That places it in the Central Indian Basin, whose floor is around 4,500 to 5,200 m deep. I estimate about -4,950 m, which is an estimate from general bathymetry rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

8824.text:
The coordinates -9.0, -171.0 are in the Pacific near Tokelau. Fakaofo atoll sits at about -9.38, -171.22, Nukunonu at about -9.17, -171.85, and Atafu at about -8.55, -172.5. Since -9.0, -171.0 is not on any of these, it is open ocean.

The seabed in this region of the central Pacific, north of Samoa, is typically around 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. Tokelau sits on a seamount chain, but the surrounding abyssal plain is about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. I'll estimate -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

2446.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude 33.0 is in Russia's Republic of Karelia, east of Finland. It lies roughly southeast of Kostomuksha and near Segezha or Medvezhyegorsk. Lake Onega is at about 61.5N, 35.5E, so this point is not in the lake. Lake Segozero is at about 63.3N, 33.7E, and Vygozero is at about 63.5N, 34.7E.

At 63.0N, 33.0E, the point is probably west of Segozero, in the area of the Karelian uplands. Elevations there are about 150 m, and Segozero's surface is about 118 m. I'll treat the point as land at about 140 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":140}

3505.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude -9.0 lies south of Ireland, off the coast of County Cork. The Irish coast at this latitude is around -9.5 to -9.7 near Mizen Head (51.45N, -9.8). At 51.0N, the coast is near Baltimore (51.48N, -9.37), so 51.0N is about 50 km south of the coast, in the Celtic Sea. The water depth there is about 100-110 m.

The continental shelf edge is around 49.5N, -10. At 51N, -9, depth is approximately 105 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-105}

654.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude 49.0°E lies north of Franz Josef Land and Svalbard's Nordaustlandet, in the Arctic Ocean. Franz Josef Land's northernmost point, Rudolf Island, is at about 81.8°N, so 83°N is roughly 140 km farther north. That puts the point in the deep water of the Nansen Basin margin or on the continental slope.

The continental slope north of Franz Josef Land descends from the shelf break near 82°N. At 83°N, 49°E the seabed is probably around 3000 m deep. The IBCAO map shows depths of about 3000–3500 m in the Nansen Basin at 83°N, 50°E. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

12556.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 93 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies near the Southeast Indian Ridge, which runs roughly through (-49, 70) to (-50, 100), and the Kerguelen Plateau is further west near 70-80E. At 93E, 49S the seafloor is probably the flank of the ridge or the Australian-Antarctic Basin edge, so the depth is likely around 3500-4000 m.

Ridge-axis depths are about 2500-3000 m. The ridge near 93E is at roughly 48-49S, and the Amsterdam-St Paul plateau is further north. The Southeast Indian Ridge at 88-100E sits at about 47-50S, so this point could be close to the axis, giving a depth of about 3200 m. I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

13938.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -23 is in the Weddell Sea, off the Antarctic coast of Dronning Maud Land and the Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf area. The Antarctic coast near 0° is at about -70 to -71 latitude, and at -23° longitude the coast (Caird Coast) is around -74 to -77. So -65, -23 is open ocean in the Weddell Sea, east of the Antarctic Peninsula.

The Weddell Sea basin there is deep, around 4000-5000 m. At 65°S, 23°W it lies in the Weddell Abyssal Plain, with a depth of roughly 4800 m. I estimate -4700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

1397.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 95.0 E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Russia. Cape Chelyuskin is at about 104 E, 77.7 N. The Taymyr Peninsula's northern coast near 75 N lies around 95 E, close to the Khatanga Gulf and Taymyr Bay, which sits near 75 N, 100 E. Further west, at 95 E and 75 N, is the Taymyr Peninsula's north-central area, near Lake Taymyr (about 74.5 N, 101 E) and the Byrranga Mountains (about 75 N). The Byrranga Mountains run through 74-76 N, and at 95 E the terrain should be fairly low hills.

The coast of the Kara Sea near Cape Sterlegov is at 75.4 N, 88.5 E. At 95 E, the Taymyr coast near Vilkitsky Island is about 76 N, so 75 N is inland, probably in the southern foothills of the Byrranga Mountains. A plausible land elevation is about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

14567.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 155E is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land / Victoria Land. Longitude 155E at 71S lies inland of the Oates Coast, near the Pennell Coast (about 70.5S at 160E). The coast near 155E is around 69-70S, so 71S is inland on the ice sheet, perhaps 20-30 km south of the coast at an elevation of roughly 1500-2000 m.

That is near the area between the Rennick Glacier and the Mertz Glacier region. The Oates Land coast runs about 69S at 155E, and further inland the ice sheet reaches around 2000 m at 71S. I'll estimate 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

16148.text:
This point is at 89°S, 77°E, which is on the East Antarctic Plateau, about 100 km from the South Pole along the 77°E meridian. That puts it on grounded ice.

The ice surface near the South Pole is about 2835 m. Along 77°E the surface rises gradually toward Dome A, which is near 80°E, 81°S. At 89°S, the surface is probably around 2900 m. The geoid (EGM2008) height there is about -20 to -30 m, but the elevations are mostly given relative to the geoid, so I'll use roughly 2900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

15569.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -1 is in Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land near the Theron Mountains and Shackleton Range, south of the Brunt Ice Shelf and Slessor Glacier. The Shackleton Range lies at roughly 80°S, 25°W, so -1° longitude is farther east. At 83°S, 1°W, I expect the point to be on the inland ice sheet of the East Antarctic plateau, where ice-surface elevations are around 2000 m or more.

The Recovery Glacier catchment lies near 81°S, 20°W. Around 83°S, 0°E, the surface is probably about 2000-2500 m. I'll estimate 2300 m, treating the point as grounded ice and therefore Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

12115.text:
The point -45.0, -69.0 is in Argentina, in Chubut province, east of the Andes. It lies near Río Mayo to the northwest, around -45.7, -70.3, and Sarmiento to the northeast, around -45.6, -69.07. Lake Musters and Lake Colhué Huapi sit near -45.4, -69.0 and -45.5, -68.8, so the point is slightly south of them, in Patagonian steppe.

Sarmiento is at about 270 m. South of it the terrain rises toward the plateau, and I estimate about 500 m at the point. This is an estimate from recalled landmarks, not a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":520}

8098.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 177.0°E lies in the central Pacific, about 1° north of the equator. It is west of the Gilbert Islands: Tarawa is at about 1.4°N, 173°E, and Butaritari at about 3°N, 172.8°E. Banaba is at 0.9°S, 169.5°E. Nothing is known to lie at 177°E, so this is open ocean.

The seabed there is likely part of the Pacific basin near the Gilbert Ridge, with depths of about 4,500 to 5,500 m. The Gilbert Islands chain runs northwest to southeast, and the area east of Tarawa toward 177°E is probably abyssal plain at around 5,000 m. I'll estimate -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

7386.text:
The point 7°N, 167°W lies in the central Pacific, south of Hawaii and east of the Marshall Islands. It is north of the equator and well away from Kiribati's Line Islands to the east. Johnston Atoll is at 16.7°N, 169.5°W, so it is far from this point. The nearest land is likely Kingman Reef and Palmyra, at about 6°N, 162°W. This point is therefore in open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is Pacific abyssal plain, with depths around 5000 to 5500 m. This area is south of the Line Islands ridge and the Musicians seamounts. I'll estimate about -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

13395.text:
The point -59, -29 lies in the Scotia Sea, east of the South Sandwich Islands' western side? The South Sandwich Islands sit around 56-59°S and 26-28°W. At -59.0, -29.0, this is just west of the arc, near Southern Thule/Bellingshausen (about -59.4, -27.0). The South Sandwich arc is at longitude about -27 to -26, so -29 is west of it, in the back-arc East Scotia Sea. Depths there range around 3000-4000 m, and the East Scotia Ridge is at about -30 longitude with depths near 2500-3000 m. The point is probably between the ridge and the arc, at roughly 3000 m depth.

I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

12149.text:
The point (-45, -1) is in the South Atlantic, southwest of the Southwest Indian Ridge and Mid-Atlantic Ridge junction area. The Bouvet triple junction is at about 54.5°S, 1°W, so this point is well north of it. At 45°S, 1°W the seafloor lies in the southern South Atlantic, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis, which is near 15°W at this latitude. The Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone and the Islas Orcadas area lie further to the west and south.

The ridge axis near 45°S sits around 15°W, so 1°W is roughly 14° east of it, about 1000 km. The crust there is about 30 million years old, so the depth should be around 4000 to 4500 m. Based on this reasoning I estimate about -4300 m. The seafloor here may be somewhat shallower, but I'll keep the estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

11888.text:
The point is at 43°S, 163°W, in the South Pacific Ocean, southeast of New Zealand and well away from any land. The surface type is therefore Water.

The seabed there lies on the Pacific abyssal plain, roughly 100 km or more from the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge (which lies farther south and east). Abyssal depths in this region are about 4,500–5,500 m. I'm estimating from the regional setting, not a lookup, so I'll use about -5,000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5000}

14462.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -55 lies in the Weddell Sea, off the east coast of the Antarctic Peninsula. The Larsen ice shelves sit further west. At -71 lat the Antarctic Peninsula is around lon -65 to -60 at its eastern coast, so -55 is about 400 km out. This area is the western Weddell Sea, near the Ronne Ice Shelf's eastern edge. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans from about -85 to -58 longitude at latitudes -75 to -78, and its front lies around -74 to -75. At -71, -55 is open sea or the Filchner trough area. The Larsen C ice shelf extends to about -60 at -68 to -69. So at -71, -55 it is likely open water or perennial sea ice, with a continental shelf depth of about 400-600 m.

I judge it to be Water, with a seabed elevation of about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

12421.text:
The point is at 49°S, 177°W, in the South Pacific southeast of New Zealand. The Antipodes Islands lie at about 49.7°S, 178.8°E, which is far from this longitude, and the Bounty Islands at about 47.7°S, 179°E are also well to the west. So the point is in open ocean.

The seabed here is probably on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern flank or the Campbell Plateau's eastern edge. The Campbell Plateau extends to roughly 170°W at its eastern margin, but 177°W is east of the Chatham Rise and the plateau, in deeper water. From memory, depths in this area are around 4000-5000 m, so I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

6304.text:
Latitude 19.0, longitude -171.0 is in the central Pacific, northwest of Samoa and southeast of the Hawaiian chain. Johnston Atoll is at 16.7N, 169.5W, and Palmyra is at 5.9N, 162W, so neither is nearby. The point sits in open ocean south-southeast of the Northwestern Hawaiian Islands (Midway is at 28N, 177W).

The seafloor here is probably abyssal plain at about 5,000 to 5,500 m depth. I recall the Pacific plate around 19N, 171W being roughly 5,500 m deep, so I'll use -5400 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

12267.text:
This point (47°S, 125°W) lies in the South Pacific, southwest of South America. It is north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and east of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor there is probably abyssal plain or hills at roughly 3,500–4,000 m depth.

At 47°S the East Pacific Rise is near 115°W, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about 55°S, 140°W up to about 40°S, 110°W. At 47°S the ridge crest is therefore near 113–115°W. At 125°W the seafloor is about 10° of longitude west of the crest. At a half-spreading rate of about 40 mm/yr, that is roughly 800 km, or about 20 Myr in age. Depth for 20 Myr crust is about 2600 + 350·√20 ≈ 4,100 m, but the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is shallow and fast-spreading, so I estimate about 3,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2515.text:
The point 63.0°N, 171.0°E lies in far northeastern Russia. Anadyr Gulf is at about 64°N, 177°E, and the Koryak coast along the Bering Sea sits around 60°N, 165°E. The Kamchatka isthmus is near 62°N, 164°E.

At 171°E, 63°N the location is inland, in the Chukotka/Koryak highlands. Olyutorsky and Koryak areas lie to the east, and the Penzhina Bay head is at about 62.5°N, 165°E. The Anadyr Lowland is farther north, near 64–65°N, 170–178°E. So 63°N, 171°E is probably in the Koryak Highlands or the Anadyr basin's southern margin. This area is mountainous, with elevations of about 500–1000 m, so I'll estimate roughly 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

14753.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 167 is in northern Victoria Land, Antarctica, near the Ross Sea coast. Terra Nova Bay is around -75, 164, and Cape Adare is around -71.3, 170. At -73, 167 the Pennell Coast is to the north of Terra Nova Bay, with the coast near longitude 169-170 at that latitude, so the point is likely inland on the continent.

The Victoria Land coast near -73 lies at roughly 169.5E (Pennell Coast, around Cape Hallett at -72.3, 170.2). At 167E the point is probably in the Admiralty Mountains or the plateau, inland by about 100 km. That puts the surface at a high elevation, perhaps 2000 m. Around Rennick Glacier (-70.5, 160) the ice sits at about 1000-2000 m. I estimate about 2200 m, grounded ice.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

15319.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -141 lies in the Ross Ice Shelf area, in the eastern part near Marie Byrd Land coast. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°E to 150°W, and at -141 longitude we are east of that. The shelf's eastern edge is around Edward VII Peninsula (~-155°) and the Sulzberger Bay region, so the area at -141 is probably the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part, near the Marie Byrd Land coast at about -78 to -80 latitude. At -81 and -141 this is probably the Ross Ice Shelf, near Hobbs Coast / Shirase Coast. Ice streams (Kamb, Bindschadler) flow into the shelf around -150 to -140, around -82. At -141, -81 it is likely the Ross Ice Shelf, which counts as Water with seabed around -600 m.

Seabed depth in the eastern Ross Embayment is about -500 to -700 m. I'll go with -590.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-590}

10009.text:
Latitude -21, longitude 39 lies in the Mozambique Channel, east of the Mozambique coast. The coast near Inhambane and Vilankulo is at about 35°E, and at 21°S the coast is around 35.3°E. So 39°E is roughly 400 km offshore, in the Mozambique Basin or the Channel.

The Mozambique Channel at 21°S has a deep basin of about 2500–3000 m. Farther east, near 39–40°E at 21°S, the Mozambique Basin is deeper still, around 3000 m, but a bit north of there the Davie Ridge runs near 41°E. At 21°S, 39°E I estimate the depth at about -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

14570.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 161 is in East Antarctica, in northern Victoria Land. Cape Adare is at 170E and Mertz is at 145E, so 161E at -71 sits inland of the Pennell Coast, around the Rennick Glacier or Wilson Hills area. The Rennick Glacier lies near 160-161E, and the coast is at about -70.5 near 161E, so this point is probably on the continent.

The Lillie Glacier is at about 163E and the Rennick Glacier at about 160.5E, and the point is right on or near the Rennick. Ice surface elevation there is about 1000-1500 m. Between the glaciers the Wilson Hills terrain is perhaps 1500 m. I'll estimate 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

8718.text:
The point (-7.0, -23.0) lies in the central Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and south of the equator. It is on the western flank of the ridge, near the Ceará Rise region at roughly 4°S, 35°W, though that rise is farther west.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis at 7°S is near 13°W, so 23°W is about 10° west of the axis. The crust there is about 20 Myr old, which suggests a depth of about 3500–4000 m. The Romanche and Chain fracture zones are near 0°, so they are not a factor. Between 5°S and 10°S the Atlantic basin is around 4000–5000 m deep at 23°W. I estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

3227.text:
Latitude 55.0°N, longitude 155.0°E lies in the Sea of Okhotsk, east of Sakhalin and south of the Kamchatka coast. Kamchatka's west coast is near 156°E at 55°N, so 155°E is probably inland on the peninsula. The west coast of Kamchatka near 55°N is at about 155.6°E (around Ust-Bolsheretsk, at 52.8°N, 156.3°E). At 55°N the west coast sits near 155.7°E, so 155°E is in the Sea of Okhotsk, roughly 50 km offshore. The shelf there is shallow, perhaps 50–100 m deep.

The shelf off western Kamchatka slopes gently. At 55°N, 155°E I estimate a depth of about 100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

15562.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -15 lies in Antarctica, in the Filchner-Ronne region. The Filchner Ice Shelf spans roughly 30–45°W, and the Ronne Ice Shelf lies to the west of that, from about 45°W to 80°W. At 15°W and 83°S, the point is east of the Filchner Ice Shelf, inland in Dronning Maud Land. This is near the Theron Mountains and Shackleton Range, which lie around 26°W and 80–81°S. At 83°S, 15°W, the surface is likely the grounded ice sheet at about 2000 m or more. The Recovery Glacier region lies near 81°S, 28°W, and the Pensacola Mountains are at 84°S, 60°W.

Between 80°S and 85°S at 15°W, the ice surface is probably a plateau at roughly 2000–2500 m. I'll estimate 2200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

9975.text:
The point -21, -29 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, east of Brazil and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It is near the Vitória-Trindade chain, but that chain sits at about -20.5 latitude and between -29 and -40 longitude. Trindade is at -20.5, -29.3, so this point is close to Trindade Island, which is at about 20.5°S, 29.3°W. The point at 21.0°S, 29.0°W is about 55 km south-southeast of the island.

The Trindade and Martin Vaz area sits on a seamount chain, with Martin Vaz at about 20.5°S, 28.85°W. The surrounding seafloor is roughly 3000–5000 m deep, and the chain base is around 4000 m. At 55 km from the island, the point could be on a flank, perhaps at about -3500 m. The Vitória-Trindade ridge extends east from Trindade, with seamounts further east, but this point is south of that line. I estimate the depth at about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

8670.text:
The point (-7.0, -119.0) is in the open South Pacific, southwest of the Galápagos and east of the Marquesas. It lies on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise, where the seafloor is typically about 3,500–4,000 m deep.

At 119°W and 7°S the crust is roughly 15–25 million years old, which fits a depth near 4,000 m. The region just west of the East Pacific Rise at 100–110°W is about 3,000–3,500 m deep, and the Clipperton–Galápagos area farther out is around 4,000 m. I'll estimate about -3,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

9911.text:
The coordinates -21.0, -157.0 fall in the South Pacific, south of the Cook Islands. Rarotonga is at about -21.23, -159.78, and Aitutaki is at -18.85, -159.78. Since 157°W is about 270 km east of Rarotonga, there is no island at this point. Mangaia is at -21.93, -157.93, which is about 1° away, so it doesn't cover this point either.

The surrounding seafloor is roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The area is in the Austral/Cook region, where the seafloor is around 4,000 to 5,000 m. I'll estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

8581.text:
The point (-5.0, 63.0) is in the western Indian Ocean, southeast of the Seychelles and northeast of the Mascarene Plateau. It lies near the Carlsberg Ridge region and the Mascarene Basin.

The Mascarene Basin lies around 60-65°E, 10-20°S, so this point is not in it. At 5°S, 63°E the area is more likely the Central Indian Basin's western edge or the Carlsberg Ridge flank. The Carlsberg Ridge runs northwest-southeast from about 10°N, 57°E to 0°, 67°E, so at 5°S the Central Indian Ridge is further south. The point therefore sits in the basin between the Mascarene Plateau and the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge, at a depth of about 4000 to 4500 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14135.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 11 E lies off Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The Antarctic coast near 11°E is around 70°S, at the Fimbul Ice Shelf, which is around 70.5°S. At -67 the point is about 350 km north of the coast, so it is in the Southern Ocean, not on land or an ice shelf.

The seafloor there is on the abyssal plain, or close to the Maud Rise region. Maud Rise is centered near 65°S, 3°E, and its top is about 1,500 m deep. At 67°S, 11°E the seafloor is probably on the continental rise or abyssal plain at about 4,000 to 5,000 m deep. The Lazarev Sea has depths of about 4,500 m in this area, so I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

8455.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 171.0 lies in the Pacific Ocean, in the Kiribati region. The Gilbert Islands run from about 3°N to 3°S around 172–173°E. Nearby atolls are Arorae (2.6°S, 176.8°E), Tamana (2.5°S, 175.97°E), Onotoa (1.9°S, 175.5°E) and Nikunau (1.3°S, 176.4°E). At 171°E, 3°S, no island is nearby, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor there is part of the western Pacific basin, around the Ellice Basin and the Gilbert Ridge region. The depth is probably about 4,500 m. The area west of the Gilbert Islands, near Nauru (0.5°S, 166.9°E) and Banaba, is abyssal plain, typically 4,000–5,000 m deep. I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

15497.text:
Location: 83°S, 145°W is in West Antarctica, on the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 78°S at its front and reaches ~85°S at its southern edge in places. At 145°W, the grounding line lies near Marie Byrd Land/Edward VII Peninsula... Longitude 145°W at 83°S is within the Ross Ice Shelf (near the Shirase/Siple Coast region, close to the Reedy Glacier at ~140°W, 85°S). The shelf there is floating, so the answer should be the seabed depth.

The seabed under the Ross Ice Shelf is about 500-800 m below sea level in the eastern part. Bedmap2 shows the seabed near 83°S, 145°W at roughly -600 m. I'll go with -600.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -600}

8109.text:
The point is at latitude -1.0, longitude -161.0, in the central equatorial Pacific. The nearest land is far away: the Line Islands lie to the east and Phoenix Islands to the west, so this is open ocean.

The seafloor here is roughly 5,000 to 5,300 m deep. The area lies south of the Line Islands ridge and north of the Manihiki Plateau, in the Penrhyn or Central Pacific basin, where depths are around 5,000 to 5,500 m. I'll estimate -5,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

3184.text:
Latitude 55.0°N, longitude 69.0°E lies in northern Kazakhstan, near the Russian border. That puts it in the West Siberian Plain, around Petropavlovsk (54.87°N, 69.15°E). The elevation there is about 130 m.

The point is just west of Petropavlovsk, on the Ishim steppe, where the ground is around 130–140 m. I'll estimate 135 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":135}

13918.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -63 is on the Antarctic Peninsula's west side, near the Graham Coast and Lallemand Fjord area. The Antarctic Peninsula at 65°S spans about 62-64°W. Anvers Island lies at 64.6°S, 63.5°W, and the Graham Coast is at about 65°S, 64°W. The point at -63 longitude is farther east, so it could be on the plateau side, or in Lallemand Fjord (about 67.5°S) or the Gerlache Strait area.

Places near 65°S, 63°W: the Danco Coast is further north. At 65°S, the west coast is near 64°W (Cape Errera, Darbel Islands at 65.4°S 64.2°W). Moving east to 63°W, we are inland on the Detroit Plateau side, where the plateau reaches about 1500-2000 m. The Bruce Plateau is at 66°S and the Forbidden Plateau is around 65°S 63°W. The Graham Coast at 65°S has the Lamplugh Island and Beascochea Bay at 65.2°S 64°W. The mountain-top elevation at 63°W is likely about 1800 m, so I'd estimate a plateau elevation of 1700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1700}

10386.text:
Latitude -25, longitude 73 is in the southern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and southeast of the Mascarene Plateau. It lies near the Central Indian Ridge region, roughly south of the Rodrigues area, but the Central Indian Ridge is around 66-70°E at this latitude. At 73°E, 25°S the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain in the Mascarene Basin or Central Indian Basin, typically 4000-5000 m deep.

The Southwest Indian Ridge triple junction is near 25°S, 70°E. East of the ridge, the seafloor is about 4000-4500 m deep, so I estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

5109.text:
The point (33°N, 41°W) is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 33°N sits near 40°W, so this location may lie on the western flank of the ridge, or close to the axis.

The Azores–Gibraltar region is further east. At 33°N the ridge crest is around 38–40°W, so 41°W is on the western flank. Depths there are probably 3000–4000 m. The ridge flanks near 33°N, 41°W are about 3500 m deep, and the Atlantis fracture zone is near 30°N. I'll estimate about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

4978.text:
Latitude 35.0°N, longitude 57.0°E is in northeastern Iran, in Razavi Khorasan province. It lies southwest of Mashhad, near Kashmar and Bardaskan, and probably in the desert area near the edge of the Dasht-e Kavir. Kashmar sits at about 35.24°N, 58.47°E at roughly 1,050 m. Bardaskan is at about 35.26°N, 57.97°E at roughly 1,000 m. Sabzevar is at 36.2°N, 57.7°E at about 970 m.

The point at 57.0°E is west of Bardaskan. Its latitude of 35.0°N is south of the Sabzevar plain, toward the Kavir edge, probably near Kavir-e Namak or the Ahmadabad area. Elevations there are likely around 800–900 m. I'll estimate about 850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":850}

8152.text:
The point -1.0, -75.0 lies in the Amazon basin of eastern Peru, near the Ecuador border. It is probably close to the Napo or Tigre River region, in lowland rainforest at roughly 150–200 m elevation.

The Napo River near Pantoja sits around -0.95, -75.18, and Rio Curaray is near -2. So -1.0, -75.0 is just east of Pantoja, in Peru's Loreto region. Ground elevation there is probably about 180 m. The Napo at Mazán is around 100 m, and the Napo near Pantoja is about 170 m. I'll estimate 170 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":175}

12576.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 133 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. The area lies on the Australian-Antarctic Basin, south of the Southeast Indian Ridge, which sits at roughly -50 to -48° latitude near 133°E. The ridge there is at about -4000 m depth. Around 49°S, 133°E the seafloor is probably 3500-4500 m deep.

The Southeast Indian Ridge axis near 130°E is at about 50°S, and the Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies around 120-128°E. At 133°E, 49°S the seafloor is near the ridge flank, with depths of about 3800 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

1557.text:
Latitude 73.0 N, longitude 55.0 E falls in the Barents Sea / Kara Sea region. Novaya Zemlya's southern island spans roughly 70.5–76 N, with its west coast near 52–55 E at these latitudes. At 73 N the island lies roughly between 54 E (west coast, Russkaya Gavan area) and 58 E (east coast). So 55 E at 73 N is probably on land, near the west coast.

Novaya Zemlya's west coast at 73 N: Cape Sakhanikha and Mashigin Bay lie near 72.5 N, 54 E. The coast around 73 N is near 54.5 E. So 55 E is just inland, in mountainous terrain at an elevation of perhaps 200–400 m. It is also possible to be a coastal fjord. I'll say Solid, 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

5582.text:
The point 27.0°N, 175.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. Midway Atoll is at about 28.2°N, 177.4°W, and Kure Atoll is at 28.4°N, 178.3°W, so this point is east of Midway and about 200 km from it. The Hawaiian Ridge runs along the northwest chain, and this location is probably just off the ridge to the northeast, on the abyssal plain or the flank of the seamounts.

Lisianski is at 26.07°N, 173.97°W, and Pearl and Hermes is at 27.9°N, 175.8°W. That puts the point between them, roughly 100 km from Pearl and Hermes and 100 km from Lisianski, so it is probably on the ridge's flank or near it. Ocean depth along the ridge between the islands is around 1000–3000 m, with surrounding abyssal depths of about 4500–5500 m. Since it lies between the atolls, it could be on the ridge itself, but the ridge is narrow. I estimate a depth of about 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

13407.text:
Location: 59°S, 5°W is in the South Atlantic sector of the Southern Ocean, between the South Sandwich Islands and Bouvet Island. It lies south of the Atlantic-Indian Ridge and north of the Weddell Sea basin. Bouvet is at 54.4°S, 3.4°E. The South Sandwich Islands are around 27°W, so this point is well west of Bouvet and east of the Weddell basin.

The point likely sits near the southwestern flank of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge / American-Antarctic Ridge. The American-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly along 55-60°S, from Bouvet triple junction westward toward the South Sandwich Trench. Around 59°S, 5°W, the seafloor is probably the Weddell Sea basin's northern edge or the Enderby Basin side, at about 3000-4000 m depth. I estimate about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9660.text:
Latitude -17, longitude 61 lies in the southwestern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and northeast of Réunion and Mauritius. It is near the Mascarene Plateau region, but that plateau runs north from Mauritius at about 60°E, from Saya de Malha (about 10°S) down to Nazareth Bank (about -14.5°S, 60.5°E) and Cargados Carajos (about -16.5°S, 59.6°E).

At -17, 61 the point is just east of Cargados Carajos, which is at 59.6°E, so it is likely off the plateau on its eastern flank. The Mascarene basin to the east is about 4500-5000 m deep. The plateau edge is steep, and at 61°E, about 1.4° east of the shoals, the depth is probably around 4000 m. The Mascarene Basin floor near 62°E is roughly 4800 m deep. I estimate a depth of about 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

5560.text:
The point 29.0°N, 141.0°E lies in the western Pacific, south of Japan and east of the Izu-Bonin (Ogasawara) arc. The Ogasawara Islands sit around 27°N, 142°E, and Torishima is near 30.5°N, 140.3°E. The Izu-Ogasawara arc runs roughly along 140–142°E, so 29°N, 141°E is near the arc, but I don't know of an island there. Sofu Gan (Lot's Wife) is at 29.8°N, 140.3°E, which is not at this point.

The Bonin Trench is east of the arc at about 143°E. The arc itself and the back-arc basin (Shikoku Basin) to the west are deeper than the arc ridge. At 141°E, 29°N the point is probably on the western side of the arc, on the Shikoku Basin or the Ogasawara Plateau margin. I estimate the depth at about 1,500 m, since the arc ridge there is relatively shallow, with a few seamounts.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

4015.text:
The coordinates 45.0°N, 69.0°W fall in central Maine. That is near Dover-Foxcroft and Guilford, in Piscataquis County. The Piscataquis River runs close to Guilford at about 45.17°N, 69.38°W. Sebec Lake sits near 45.3°N, 69.1°W, which is to the north of this point, so the point is probably not on water.

Terrain in this area is roughly 100–150 m. Milo is at 45.25°N, 68.99°W with an elevation of about 90 m. Moving south toward 45.0°N, the point lies near Brownville Junction or Atkinson, or possibly Corinna. Corinna is at 44.92°N, 69.1°W. Land around 45.0°N, 69.0°W is therefore probably 100–120 m, and I'll estimate 110 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

3185.text:
The coordinates 55.0°N, 71.0°E fall in the West Siberian Plain, in Omsk Oblast, Russia. Omsk city sits at about 54.99°N, 73.37°E, so this point is roughly 150 km west of Omsk. That area is flat steppe and forest-steppe, with elevations of about 100 m. This is an estimate from memory of the region, not a looked-up value.

The area near 55°N, 71°E, around Isilkul (about 54.9°N, 71.3°E), is Omsk Oblast with elevations of about 110 m. Lakes are possible there, but I am treating this point as land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":115}

2002.text:
The point 67.0°N, 135.0°W lies in the Northwest Territories, Canada. It is west of the Mackenzie River and east of the Richardson Mountains, near the Peel River and Arctic Red River area. The Peel Plateau and Peel River are around 67.0°N, 135°W, and the Peel River itself is near 67.2°N, 134.9°W. Land elevation in that area is typically 200–500 m.

The Dempster Highway crosses the Peel River at Fort McPherson (67.43°N, 134.88°W), where the elevation is about 30 m. At 67.0°N the terrain is the Peel Plateau, with elevations around 300 m. That puts the point on land, and I estimate about 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

8137.text:
The point (-1°, -105°) lies in the eastern equatorial Pacific, roughly 1,000 km west of the Galápagos. The Galápagos are at about -91° longitude, so this is well to the west. The location sits on the East Pacific Rise flank or in the Galápagos region's western basin, probably between the Galápagos spreading center and the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise crest at the equator is near -102°, so -105° is on its western flank, where depths are about 3,000 m.

At the equator the rise crest is deeper, around 2,900 m. At 3° west of the crest, about 330 km, the seafloor age is roughly 5 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3,000 m. Since -105° is west of the crest, I'd estimate roughly 3,100 m. The Pacific there is around 3,000–3,300 m, with Cocos-Nazca spreading center influence further north. I'll answer -3100.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

6078.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude 97.0 falls in northern Myanmar, in Kachin State near the China border. It lies east of Myitkyina and around the Irrawaddy basin's eastern edge, in hilly terrain, probably near Bhamo or Mansi. Bhamo is at about 24.27°N, 97.23°E, so this point is farther south, near Namhkam or Mongmit, around Shan State's northern edge. The Shweli River valley is near 23.9°N, 97.9°E.

At 23.0°N, 97.0°E the terrain is probably in the hills west of the Shweli, around Mogok and Momeik. Mogok is at 22.9°N, 96.5°E, so this point is east of it. The Irrawaddy is near 96.2°E at that latitude, so the area is probably hilly, roughly 1000 m. I'll estimate 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

7912.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 165.0°E lies in the western Pacific, north of Nauru (0.5°S, 166.9°E) and south-southwest of Kosrae (5.3°N, 163°E). It is within the Nauru Basin or on its margin, and I did not find a specific island or atoll at this point.

Nauru Basin depths are about 4,500–5,000 m. The Ontong Java Plateau is further south, and the Caroline Islands are to the west. I recall the Nauru Basin as roughly 4,500 m deep, so I estimate -4,700 m. This is from memory rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

14698.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 57 is in East Antarctica, in the Prince Charles Mountains / Lambert Glacier region. The Lambert Glacier runs roughly along 70°E and 70-73°S. The Amery Ice Shelf sits near 70°E, 70-72°S. At 57°E, 73°S, we are west of the Lambert Glacier, near the Prince Charles Mountains (Mount Menzies is at about 73.2°S, 61°E). The Mawson Escarpment is around 62-67°E, so 57°E, 73°S is probably inland on the ice sheet, with a surface elevation of around 2000 m.

The Fisher Glacier and Mount Stinear are near 68°E and 73°S. At 57°E, 73°S, the area lies west of the Prince Charles Mountains in the ice plateau of Princess Elizabeth Land and Enderby Land. The surface there is perhaps about 2200 m, so I'll estimate 2200.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

12135.text:
Location: -45, -29 is in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge near 45°S lies around 15°W, so -29 is on the western flank, in the Argentine Basin's eastern edge. The South Sandwich Islands are far to the southeast, and the Georgia Basin lies to the west of the ridge.

Seafloor depths near 45°S, 29°W are probably around 4000-4500 m. The ridge crest at 45°S is near 17°W, and 12° of longitude is roughly 900 km. Crustal age there is about 40-50 Myr, which gives a depth of roughly 4,000 m. The Falkland Plateau and the Maurice Ewing Bank are further west, near 50°S, 45°W. So I estimate about -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

11988.text:
The point at 43°S, 37°E lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, south of South Africa and southwest of the Prince Edward Islands, which are near 46.9°S, 37.7°E. That puts it north of the Prince Edward Islands, in the area of the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Del Caño Rise.

The Del Caño Rise is around 45°S, 44°E. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs through roughly 40–42°S at 37°E, with depths of about 3000–4000 m. The Africana fracture zone and the Andrew Bain fracture zone are nearby. At 43°S, 37°E the point is probably just south of the ridge crest, in the Crozet basin or on the ridge flank, so I estimate a depth of around -3500 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

11382.text:
Latitude -37, longitude -95 lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and roughly 2,500 km offshore. This is on the Chile Rise/East Pacific region. The East Pacific Rise lies near -110 at this latitude, and the Chile Rise is farther south, near -45. At -95 and -37, the seafloor is the Nazca plate, about 20–30 Myr old, with depths around 3,800–4,000 m.

I estimate a depth of about -3,900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3900}

8760.text:
The point (-7.0, 61.0) is in the central Indian Ocean, east of the Chagos region and south of the equator. The Chagos Archipelago lies near 72°E, so 61°E is well to its west. That area is close to the Carlsberg Ridge and Mascarene Basin, but 7°S, 61°E sits roughly in the Mascarene Basin region west of the Central Indian Ridge. The Central Indian Ridge runs near 66-68°E at this latitude, so 61°E is west of it.

The Mascarene Plateau (Saya de Malha) lies near 60°E but at 10-12°S. At 7°S, 61°E the seabed is probably the northern Mascarene Basin or the edge of the plateau at around 4000-5000 m depth. I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

8038.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 57.0°E is in the western Indian Ocean, east of Somalia and roughly 1,500 km from the African coast. It lies in the Somali Basin / Arabian Basin region, near the Carlsberg Ridge's southwestern side, west of the Chagos-Laccadive area. Depths in the Somali Basin are about 4,500–5,000 m.

The Somali Basin abyssal plain sits near 4,800 m, while at 57°E, 1°N the seafloor may be a little shallower. I'll estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

8220.text:
Latitude -1, longitude 61 is in the western Indian Ocean, east of Africa and about 1° south of the equator. It lies in the Somali Basin / Arabian Basin area, southwest of the Carlsberg Ridge. The Carlsberg Ridge runs northwest to southeast from about 57°E, 10°N down to the Central Indian Ridge near 66°E, 0°N, so the point may be near the Mascarene Basin's northern edge.

The Somali Basin sits at roughly 50-55°E, and the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge is near 73°E. At 61°E, 1°S we are probably in the Central Indian Basin's western part or the Carlsberg Ridge flank, where depths are about 4000-4500 m. The Carlsberg Ridge at the equator is near 62-63°E, so the point is close to the ridge axis, west of it. The ridge flank there is probably around 3500-4000 m deep. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7862.text:
The point (3.0°N, 65.0°E) is in the Arabian Sea / northern Indian Ocean, west of the Chagos-Laccadive region and near the Carlsberg Ridge. The Carlsberg Ridge runs northwest-southeast, passing roughly 3°N near 65°E. The ridge axis near 3°N sits around 64.5°E to 65°E, so the point could be on the ridge flank or in the rift valley. Ridge crest depths are about 3000-4000 m, with the rift valley deeper, around 3500-4000 m.

I estimate a depth of about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

14081.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -97 lies in the Bellingshausen Sea, west of the Antarctic Peninsula. Alexander Island is near -70 and -70 longitude, so this point is well offshore in open ocean.

The Bellingshausen Sea continental shelf and slope are in this area. At -67 latitude and -97 longitude, the seabed is probably the abyssal plain or continental rise, at a depth of around 4000-4500 m. The Antarctic coast near -100 longitude lies at about -73, so the point is roughly 6 degrees of latitude from the coast, which is about 650 km. That places it beyond the shelf, on the rise or abyssal plain. I estimate a depth of about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

16040.text:
Latitude -89, longitude -139 is near the South Pole, on the West Antarctic side. The South Pole itself sits at about 2835 m ice surface elevation. At 89°S along longitude -139 (about 41°W of the 180° meridian... roughly toward the Ross Ice Shelf side), the ice sheet surface is around 2,700 m.

The Ross Ice Shelf is at about 80–85°S, so 89°S is on grounded ice, near the Transantarctic Mountains and the polar plateau edge. Longitude -139 (139°W) at 89°S lies in the direction of Marie Byrd Land/Ross Ice Shelf, and the Pole region there is roughly 2,500–2,800 m. Surface elevation at 89°S is about 2,800 m near longitudes 0 to 90°E, and decreases toward the Ross side. I estimate 2,650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2650}

10161.text:
Latitude -23, longitude -17 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Namibia and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 23°S sits at about 13°W, so -17 is on its western flank. Depths there are around 3500-4000 m. The Rio Grande Rise is further west, near 30°W, and Walvis Ridge is further south-east, near 20°S at 5°E, so neither is close.

Ridge crest depths are about 2500 m and the flanks deepen with age. At 4° west of the ridge axis the age is roughly 10 Myr at a 2 cm/yr half-rate, which gives a depth of about 3500 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3900}

13418.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 17 lies in the South Atlantic sector of the Southern Ocean, south of the Agulhas Basin and north of the Weddell-Enderby Basin. It is near the American-Antarctic Ridge and the Southwest Indian Ridge, but those are farther west and north. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (about 55°S, 1°W) to the northeast, so at 17°E it sits around 53°S. The point at 59°S is therefore south of the ridge, in the Enderby Basin or Weddell Basin area, where depths are roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

I'll take about -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

10284.text:
The point -25, -131 is in the South Pacific, east of the Gambier Islands and Pitcairn (Pitcairn is at about -25.07, -130.1). Longitude -131 is roughly 80 km west of Pitcairn, and Henderson and Ducie lie east of it. This puts the point on the flank of the Pitcairn seamount chain.

Pitcairn sits on a seafloor about 3000 to 3500 m deep. The Pitcairn hotspot volcano, Adams Seamount, lies southeast of the island. To the west the seafloor is likely about 3500 to 3800 m deep, and the East Pacific Rise flank in this area is around 3000 to 3500 m. I estimate about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

11514.text:
Latitude -37, longitude 169 is in the Pacific Ocean, northeast of New Zealand's North Island. It lies east of the Northland coast and southwest of the Kermadec region. The location is near the Colville/Kermadec ridge area, about 600 km east of East Cape, which is at about 178E, so 169E is actually west of East Cape.

New Zealand's North Island spans roughly 172–178E. At 169E and 37S the point is in the Tasman Sea, about 300 km west of Cape Reinga (172.7E). Water depth there is roughly 4,000 m on the abyssal plain. The Norfolk Ridge lies further northwest, and the South Fiji Basin is to the north. The Tasman Basin floor is around 4,500 m deep near 37S, 169E, though the West Norfolk Ridge is near 168E at 34S. Further south, the Lord Howe Rise sits at about 162E. So 169E should be in the deep Tasman Basin, or on the eastern side near the Challenger Plateau and the New Zealand margin. I estimate about -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

828.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 37.0°E lies in the Arctic, in the northern Barents Sea, east of Nordaustlandet in Svalbard (which reaches about 80.5°N at 20–27°E). Kvitøya sits at about 80.1°N, 32.5°E, and Franz Josef Land begins at about 80–81°N, 45–65°E. At 81°N, 37°E the point is open sea north of Kvitøya, which is on the slope toward the Arctic Ocean basin.

Bathymetry there is likely about 300–500 m deep. The Barents Sea shelf north of Kvitøya drops off in the St. Anna Trough area, which lies around 80–82°N, 60°E. At 81°N, 37°E, which is north of Nordaustlandet and east of Kvitøya, the shelf edge is around 82°N, so the depth is probably about 250 m. I'll estimate -280 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-280}

14087.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -85 is in the Bellingshausen Sea, off Antarctica. The Antarctic Peninsula base is near -67.5 lat at about -68 lon, and Alexander Island lies around -72 lon. At -85 lon, the coast is much farther south, since the Eights Coast is near -75. So this point is open ocean over the continental rise or abyssal plain of the Bellingshausen Sea, probably at a depth of about 3500-4500 m.

The area around -67, -85 is likely abyssal plain, at roughly -4000 m. I'll estimate -4200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

8580.text:
The point (-5.0, 61.0) is in the western Indian Ocean, roughly southeast of the Seychelles and east of the Mascarene Plateau. It lies near the Carlsberg Ridge region, or more precisely the Central Indian Basin to the west of the ridge. The Mascarene Plateau runs along about 60°E from 5°S to 20°S, but its northern part, Saya de Malha, is near 10°S. At 5°S, 61°E the seafloor is the Mascarene Basin or the area just north of the plateau, with depths around 4,000-5,000 m.

I could not pin the depth down more precisely, so I estimate about -4,400 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4400}

10705.text:
Latitude -29, longitude -9 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of southern Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 29°S lies around 13°W to 14°W, so -9 is on the eastern flank. Depths there are around 4000 to 4500 m. The Walvis Ridge is further north, at about 20°S to 30°S near the African coast, but it lies east of this point. The Rio Grande Rise is in the west, so it is not relevant here.

The region around 29°S, 9°W is part of the Angola/Cape Basin flank. I estimate the seabed at about 4300 m depth, so the elevation is roughly -4300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1899.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude 19.0 E is in northern Norway, in Troms. Tromsø sits at about 69.65 N, 18.96 E, and Senja is around 69.3 N, 17.5 E. At 69.0 N, 19.0 E the location is inland, south of Balsfjord and near Målselv, in the Dividalen/Rostadalen area. Mountains there reach 1000-1500 m. Balsfjord lies at about 69.2 N, 19.2 E, and Målselv at about 69.0 N, 19.2 E.

Målselv valley floor is around 100 m. Rostadalen is at about 68.95 N, 19.7 E. At 19.0 E, 69.0 N the point is likely in hills west of Målselv, between Malangen and Målselv, with an elevation of roughly 500 m. I'll guess 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

11628.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 37 lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa and south of Madagascar. The Madagascar Basin and Mozambique Basin are to the north, and the Southwest Indian Ridge lies further south, at about 45-50°S near 37°E, where the Del Cano Rise sits to the east. At -39 and 37, the seafloor is probably the Agulhas Basin or Mozambique Basin, with a typical depth around 4500-5000 m.

The Mozambique Basin's southern part is about 5000 m deep. The Agulhas Plateau is at about 26°E, 40°S, so it is not close. The Africana Rise and the Southwest Indian Ridge are further south. I recall that the Southwest Indian Ridge near 37°E sits at about 50°S, with the Prince Edward Islands at 38°E, 46.5°S. At -39 the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain at about 4800 m, so I estimate -4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

12499.text:
The point is at 49°S, 21°W, in the South Atlantic. It lies southwest of Bouvet Island, which is near 54.4°S, 3.4°E, and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge near 49°S sits at about 12–15°W, so this point is on the western flank, in the Argentine Basin's eastern edge or near the Georgia Basin.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis at 49°S is around 16°W, since the ridge is near the Bouvet triple junction and the North Scotia ridge area is further south. The point is about 5° west of the axis, so the depth is probably around 3,500–4,000 m. The Argentine Basin floor is deeper at about 5,000 m, but at 21°W the crust is only about 10 Myr old, so I'd estimate about 3,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

3107.text:
Latitude 55.0, longitude -85.0 falls in James Bay or in Hudson Bay's southern portion. James Bay spans roughly 51-55.5°N and 78-82.5°W. At 85°W, 55°N, the point is west of James Bay, which puts it on the Ontario coast near the Hudson Bay Lowlands. The coast near Cape Henrietta Maria is at about 55.1°N, 82.3°W. The Ontario coast at 55°N lies near 82-83°W, so 85°W is offshore in Hudson Bay.

Hudson Bay depths in this area are shallow, around 50-100 m. Near 55°N, 85°W the water is probably about 60 m deep, so the seabed is at roughly -60 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

1762.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 105.0°E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Siberia, Russia. The Khatanga Gulf lies around 73–74°N, 105–110°E, so 71°N is well south of it. The Taymyr lowlands near 71°N, 105°E are drained by the Khatanga and Popigay rivers, and the Popigay is farther east at about 72°N, 110°E. The Anabar plateau is further east still. Near 105°E, 71°N the terrain is the North Siberian Lowland, probably at about 50–100 m. Lake Taymyr is at about 74°N, so it is not a factor here.

The area is probably north of the Byrranga foothills and south of the Khatanga Gulf. I'm not certain of the exact value, so I'll estimate the elevation at about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

12953.text:
Latitude -53, longitude 167 is in the Southern Ocean southwest of New Zealand, south of the Macquarie Ridge region. Campbell Island sits at about -52.5, 169.1, and Macquarie Island at -54.6, 158.9. The point at 167E, 53S lies west of Campbell Plateau, near the Macquarie Ridge and Emerald Basin. The Macquarie Ridge runs along roughly 160-161E at 54S and reaches about 166E near 50S, so the point is probably on the western flank of the Campbell Plateau or in the trough between the ridge and the plateau.

The Campbell Plateau is about 500-1000 m deep. The Emerald Basin to the south and southwest is about 4000+ m deep. The Macquarie Ridge near 167E, 53S is likely a few hundred meters up from the surrounding basin floor. The point is probably on the ridge's eastern slope, so I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

4544.text:
The coordinates 39.0, -91.0 fall in east-central Missouri, near the Missouri River and Mississippi River region. They are west of the Mississippi River (about -90.2 at that latitude) and north of the Missouri River (which runs near 38.7 around -91). This puts the point in Montgomery or Warren County, around Montgomery City or Wellsville. Land there is rolling, typically 200-250 m. The Missouri River floodplain sits near 150 m, and the uplands nearby are around 220-250 m.

I estimate 230 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the area, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":230}

14792.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -115 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. Pine Island Bay is near 74.5°S, 102°W, and the Pine Island Glacier is around 75°S, 100°W. Thwaites is around 75.5°S, 106°W. At -115 longitude we are west of Thwaites, in the Amundsen Sea Embayment toward the Getz area. Getz Ice Shelf lies around 74°S, 125°W. The Dotson and Crosson ice shelves sit near 75°S, 112°W to 110°W, so -115 may be near Crosson or Dotson, which are floating ice shelves. Dotson is near 74.5°S, 112°W, and Crosson is near 75.2°S, 110°W. At -115 and -75 it could be the Kohler Range or the grounded ice between Land and Getz. The Kohler Range is around 75.5°S, 110°W. Between Dotson and Getz there is the Siple Coast edge, so this could be grounded ice near the Mount Murphy area (75.3°S, 110.7°W).

I'll guess it is grounded ice with an elevation of about 500 m. Alternatively, it could be a floating ice shelf with a seabed around -600 m. Without a firm indication, I pick Solid, since the inland ice slopes up from the coast, which sits at about 74.5°S there.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

7964.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude -91.0° lies in the Pacific Ocean, north of the Galápagos. The Galápagos Islands span roughly 1.7°N to 1.4°S, and Darwin and Wolf islands are at about 1.68°N, -92°. Since 1.0°N, -91.0° is about 100 km southeast of Darwin, it is open water.

The seafloor there is on the Galápagos platform's northern flank, between the Galápagos Spreading Center (around 2°N) and the islands. Depths in this area are roughly 2000-3000 m. The northern part of the platform near Darwin and Wolf is fairly deep, with the surrounding seafloor around 2000 m. At 1°N, -91°, the depth is probably about 2500 m, so I'll estimate -2400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

11110.text:
The point at -33, 81 is in the southern Indian Ocean, roughly southeast of Amsterdam Island (which sits near 37.8S, 77.5E). It lies near the Southeast Indian Ridge, or just west of the Ninetyeast Ridge. The Ninetyeast Ridge is at about 88-90E, so 81E is not on it.

The area between Amsterdam Island and the Broken Plateau is mostly abyssal plain or ridge flank. The Southeast Indian Ridge crosses near 38S, 78E, with depths around 2500-3500 m. At 33S, 81E, the seafloor is probably the Perth/Central Indian Basin flank at about 4000-5000 m. I estimate around 4300 m depth.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

12629.text:
The point at 51°S, 121°W is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. It lies on or near the East Pacific Rise's southern extension (the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further south, around 55-65°S at this longitude). The Chile Rise is further east. At 51°S, 121°W the seafloor is probably abyssal, with a depth around 3,500-4,000 m. The crust there is roughly 20-30 Myr old, which suggests a depth of about 3,800 m.

I estimate a depth of -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

15823.text:
The point is at 85°S, 147°E, in East Antarctica. It lies on the polar plateau, in the Transantarctic Mountains region near the Ross Ice Shelf side. At 85°S, 147°E the area is likely the east Antarctic ice sheet inland of the Shackleton/Beardmore area. The Beardmore Glacier is near 83°S, 170°E, and the South Pole is at 90°S. Longitude 147°E at 85°S is on the plateau, which is on the other side of the pole from the Ross Ice Shelf front, toward Wilkes Land. The Queen Alexandra Range is at about 85°S, 166°E. The point at 147°E is further toward the interior, near the head of the Lennox-King or Nimrod glaciers, and the surface elevation there is probably around 2800-3000 m.

The ice-surface elevation near 85°S, 147°E is roughly 2900 m, so I'll answer with that.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

11140.text:
This location (-33.0, 141.0) is in far western New South Wales, Australia, west of the Darling River and near Menindee or Wilcannia. The area is semi-arid plains, and the elevation there is roughly 60-100 m.

The Menindee Lakes lie around 142.3°E, so they are not at this point. Ivanhoe is at 144.3°E and Broken Hill at 141.47°E, -31.95°. At 141.0°E, -33.0°, the terrain is east of the South Australian border and southwest of Broken Hill, in the Mallee/Barrier Range plains near Lake Frome Creek. The elevation is probably about 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

12830.text:
Latitude -53, longitude -79 is in the Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. The Chilean coast at that latitude lies around -74 to -75 longitude, so -79 is roughly 300 km offshore. The Chile Trench is near -75 to -76 at this latitude, so -79 is probably west of the trench, on the Nazca/Antarctic plate. Here the seafloor is around 3500-4000 m deep. At 53°S the Chile Rise triple junction is at about 46°S, so the plate here is Antarctic, with a seafloor age of roughly 20-30 Myr, giving a depth of about 3800 m.

The trench axis near 53°S is shallow, around 2500-3000 m, because of sediment fill. The area at -79 is probably an abyssal plain at about 3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

12534.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 49 is in the southern Indian Ocean. The Crozet Islands lie around 46°S, 51-52°E, so this point is southwest of them, and the Del Cano Rise is near 45°S, 45°E. The Crozet Plateau extends toward about 49°S, though its southern edge is uncertain. The Southwest Indian Ridge lies further west, with the Indomed and Du Toit fracture zones near 46-50°S, 40-50°E.

At 49°S, 49°E, the point sits south of the Del Cano Rise and the Crozet Plateau, in the Crozet Basin or the Enderby Basin region. Depths there are probably around 3000-4000 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge axis is near 50°S at about 40°E and runs northeast to about 38°S at 78°E, so at 49°E the ridge axis would be around 47-48°S. That puts the point close to the ridge, where depths of about 3000 m are plausible. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

2685.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 151.0 E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Magadan/Yakutia region. It lies in the Okhotsk-Kolyma uplands, near the Chersky range's southern part and the Aldan–Magadan area, not far from the Kolyma headwaters.

The point is far inland, since the Okhotsk coast is at about 59.5 N. It is probably mountainous at roughly 800 m. The Ulakhan-Chistay and Suntar-Khayata ranges lie further east, near 142 E. At 151 E and 61 N, the Kolyma upper reaches (Kulu river) are at about 62 N, 148 E. The area is likely the Omsukchan or Yano-Oymyakon highlands, and on a plateau the elevation is perhaps 900 m. I'll go with 850.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":850}

2280.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude 61.0°E is in Russia, in the Yamalo-Nenets / Khanty-Mansi area on the eastern flank of the Northern Urals. Longitude 61°E at 65°N is near the Polar/Subpolar Urals. The Urals' main ridge there lies around 60–61°E, so this point is probably in the western Siberian foothills or the mountains themselves.

The Sob River sits near 66.9°N, 65.8°E. Near 65°N the Urals run at about 60°E (Narodnaya is at 65.04°N, 60.17°E, 1895 m). A point 0.8° further east, about 22 km at this latitude, would be on the eastern slope. The distance is 0.83° × 111 km × cos65° ≈ 39 km east of Narodnaya, which puts it in the foothills at roughly 200–300 m. The Lyapin River region at 61°E has elevations of about 150–250 m. I'll estimate 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

15937.text:
This point is at 87°S, 15°E, in East Antarctica on the polar plateau, about 3 degrees of latitude from the South Pole. The South Pole ice surface is at roughly 2835 m. Along the 0–30°E meridian away from the pole, the plateau rises toward Dome A and Dome Fuji, though at 87°S the change is modest. At 87°S, 15°E the surface is probably around 2900–3000 m. I estimate about 2950 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid and the elevation is the ice-surface height.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

