12102.text:
The point (-45, -95) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. It lies around 2,000 km west of the Chilean coast. The Chile Rise (the spreading ridge) is at about -38 to -45 latitude, roughly -92 to -80 longitude. The East Pacific Rise's southern branch, the Chile Rise, trends from the Chile Triple Junction (46°S, 75°W) to the west-northwest, reaching about 36°S, 98°W. At -45, the ridge is only near -80 to -85 longitude, so -95 is likely on the Nazca/Antarctic plate flank. The Chile Rise segments near 45°S are offset by the Chile Transform fault (Valdivia fracture zone near 40°S). The Antarctic plate lies to the south and the Nazca plate to the north, so the point could be near the Chile Rise axis, with depths around 3,000–3,500 m. The Chile Rise at 45°S runs approximately east-west along the Darwin fracture zone, from about 45°S, 80°W to 47°S, 90°W. At -95 the seafloor is probably on older crust of about 10-15 Myr, with a depth near 3,700 m.

I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

715.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 171°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands and Laptev/East Siberian Sea region. At this position it is probably near the Lomonosov Ridge or the Amerasian Basin edge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands shelf toward Greenland and crosses roughly 88°N at about 140°E. At 83°N, 171°E, we are likely on the Mendeleev Ridge side or in the Podvodnikov Basin. The Mendeleev Ridge is around 80 to 83°N, 170 to 180°E (it runs roughly along 175°E), with depths of about 1000 to 2500 m. The Podvodnikov Basin lies between the Lomonosov Ridge and the Mendeleev Ridge at about 2000 to 2700 m depth.

At 171°E and 83°N, this is likely the Mendeleev Ridge's western flank or the Podvodnikov Basin. I estimate a depth of about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

690.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 121°E is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands and the Laptev Sea. The Laptev shelf extends to about 80°N. At 83°N, 121°E the seafloor is probably the continental slope or the Nansen Basin edge, near the Gakkel Ridge region. The Gakkel Ridge runs roughly from 85°N 60°E toward the Laptev Sea at about 80°N 120°E. At 83°N 121°E the position is likely in the Amundsen Basin or Nansen Basin, where depths are around 3,500–4,000 m. Slope depth at 83°N is probably about 3,000 m, so I estimate roughly -3,300 m.

This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3300}

14122.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -15 is in the Weddell Sea / Dronning Maud Land offshore region. The Antarctic coast near 15°W is around 71-72°S, where the Riiser-Larsen and Brunt ice shelves sit. At -67, -15 we are well offshore in open ocean, over the continental slope or abyssal plain of the Weddell Sea.

The coast at 15°W is near 71.5°S, about 4.5° of latitude (500 km) to the south. The shelf break there is around 72-73°S under the Riiser-Larsen ice shelf, so at 67°S we are in deep ocean. The Maud Rise lies at about 65°S, 3°E, which is far from this point. Depths in the eastern Weddell Sea basin at 67°S, 15°W are probably around 4500-5000 m. I'll estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

612.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -35.0° is north of Greenland. Peary Land reaches about 82.9°N at its northern coast near Kaffeklubben Island (83.67°N, around -29.8°). At -35° longitude, the northern coast of Greenland sits around 83.5°N (near Cape Morris Jesup at about -33.4°, 83.63°N). So 83.0°N, -35.0° is probably on land in northern Greenland, or on the ice cap edge, in the area of the Roosevelt Range and the Wulff Land coast.

The coast west of Cape Morris Jesup runs southwest, reaching about 83.4°N at -36°. That puts 83.0°N, -35° about 40 km inland. Terrain there is mountainous, with elevations of roughly 500-1000 m. It is probably Solid, and I'll estimate about 700 m.

