This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid. I estimate the ice surface at about 2760 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2760}

842.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 65.0°E lies in Franz Josef Land, whose islands sit roughly between 79.8–81.9°N and 44–66°E. The easternmost islands include Ostrov Vil'cheka (Wilczek Land) at about 80.7°N, 60°E, and Ostrov Ziegler and Ostrov Greem-Bell around 81°N, 62–64°E. Alexandra Land lies near 80.8°N, 47°E, and Rudolf Island is at 81.8°N, 58°E. Ostrov Kuna, Ostrov Gukera, Ostrov Luigi and Ostrov Rainer are also in the group. The easternmost part of the archipelago is Cape Kohlsaat on Ostrov Bruce, with Ostrov Grili and Ostrov Ziegler near 64–65°E. Ostrov Ziegler is at 80.5°N, 57°E, so that does not place anything at this point.

Near 81°N, 65°E I do not recall any island, so it is probably open water in the northeast part of the archipelago. The nearby Barents/Kara shelf is about 100–300 m deep, and I will guess the seabed here is about 200 m below sea level. This is a guess rather than a recalled value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-200}

13583.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -13 is in the Southern Ocean, in the Atlantic sector. It lies north of the Weddell Sea and south of the South Atlantic basin, near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge junction (Bouvet triple junction is around 55S, 1W). The South American-Antarctic Ridge lies near 60S, 15W to 10W, so the point could sit on or near the ridge axis.

The ridge's eastern end is near the Bouvet triple junction at 55S, 0-2W. The ridge runs from there southwest toward the South Sandwich area, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge continues southward. At 61S, 13W the point may lie in the Weddell-Enderby basin, or near the ridge flank. The Southwest Indian Ridge starts at Bouvet and runs east-northeast. The American-Antarctic Ridge runs from Bouvet (54S, 1W) west-southwest to about 60S, 25W. That makes 61S, 13W somewhat south of the ridge, so the seafloor is probably deep, around 3500 m on the southern flank. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

12247.text:
The point at -47, -165 is in the South Pacific, southwest of New Zealand's Campbell Plateau and well east of it. The longitude of -165 is about 195°E, which is east of New Zealand (the Chatham Rise sits near 176°W to 180°). It lies in the open South Pacific Basin, south of the Chatham Rise, on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank region. Depths there are around 4,500–5,000 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies further south at about 55–60°S near this longitude, so the seafloor here is likely abyssal plain at roughly 4,800 m below sea level.

I am estimating the depth from general bathymetry knowledge rather than a lookup, so the figure is approximate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

883.text:
The point is at 81°N, 147°E, which lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands. Those islands reach about 76-77°N, so 81°N is well beyond them.

This area sits on the northern edge of the East Siberian/Laptev shelf, near the shelf break. The Laptev Sea shelf break is around 78-80°N near 125-140°E. At 147°E and 81°N the seabed likely drops into the Amundsen Basin or onto the Lomonosov Ridge slope. The Lomonosov Ridge is at roughly 140°E near 87°N, so it is not close. Near 81°N, 147°E the continental slope descends toward the Eurasian Basin (Nansen Basin), probably at about 2000-3000 m depth. I'll estimate around -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

4373.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude -73.0 falls in Long Island Sound, just south of Connecticut. Bridgeport sits near 41.18, -73.19, and Long Island's north shore is near 40.95 at -73.0. At 41.0 N, the point is about 5 km off the Long Island shore (Port Jefferson is at 40.95, -73.08). The Sound is roughly 30 km wide here, from Connecticut at about 41.17 to Long Island at about 40.96 in the north-south direction, so 41.0 is close to the Long Island side.

Port Jefferson harbor is at 40.95 N, -73.08. At -73.0 the Long Island shore is around 40.96 N (Mount Sinai and Wading River area), so 41.0 is about 4 km north of the shore, in water. Depth there is probably about 20 to 30 m. The central Sound near this longitude is about 30 to 40 m deep, so I'll estimate about -25 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-25}

7169.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude 119.0 lies in the South China Sea / Sulu Sea region. The Palawan island chain runs from about 8.5N, 117.3E up to 11.4N, 119.5E. At 11N, 119E, the point sits just northwest of northern Palawan. The northern tip of Palawan is around 11.4N, 119.5E, and the Calamian Islands (Busuanga) are at about 12N, 120E. Palawan's coast near 11N is around 119.3E (Roxas is at 10.3N, 119.3E; El Nido at 11.2N, 119.4E). So 119.0E at 11N is west of El Nido by about 40 km, which is in the open sea on the west side of Palawan, on the continental shelf margin.

The shelf off northwest Palawan (Palawan shelf) has depths of roughly 100–200 m. Further west is the steep slope into the Palawan Trough. At 40 km west of El Nido, depth is probably about 100–200 m, so I'll estimate -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

6207.text:
Latitude 21.0, longitude -5.0 falls in Mauritania, in the Adrar/Tagant region near the Mali border, within the Sahara. This area is the Mauritanian Adrar plateau or the El Djouf sand sea. Elevations there are roughly 300-450 m. Southeast of Atar (20.5N, -13), the terrain near -5 longitude is the Taoudenni basin edge, which sits at about 250-300 m. At 21N, -5E the area lies in the El Djouf, with elevations around 330 m.

I'll estimate 320 m.

