{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

1511.text:
The point 73.0°N, 37.0°W lies in central-east Greenland, inland of the Scoresby Sund region. Scoresby Sund is at about 70°N, 22°W, and the coast at 73°N is near 22°W, so 37°W is well inland on the ice sheet.

The ice sheet surface there is high. At 73°N, 37°W it is near the summit region, close to the GRIP site at 72.6°N, 37.6°W, where the elevation is 3230 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3220}

6781.text:
Latitude 15.0°N, longitude 63.0°E lies in the Arabian Sea, east of Socotra and southeast of the Oman coast. It is far from land, in the open sea between the Arabian Basin and the Carlsberg Ridge region.

The Arabian Sea basin floor here is typically about 3,500 to 4,000 m deep. The Owen Fracture Zone and the Carlsberg Ridge lie farther southwest. The Murray Ridge is to the north, near 23°N. At 15°N, 63°E the position is in the Arabian Basin, which is a deep abyssal plain, roughly 3,000 to 4,000 m deep. Its western part is shallower toward the Owen Basin, and the Arabian Basin sits at about 3,500 m. I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7801.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude -57.0° is in the interior of northern Guyana/Suriname, near the border. Suriname's southwestern region (Sipaliwini) lies around 3°N, 57°W, and Guyana's Upper Takutu–Upper Essequibo region is to the west.

The location sits in the Guiana Shield, covered by forested hills. The Kutari/Kuruni region is roughly there, and the Essequibo headwaters are near 2°N, 56°W. Elevation is likely about 300 m. The Eilerts de Haan mountains in Suriname lie near 3.5°N, 56.5°W, with hills of 300–700 m. The Kayser Mountains sit around 3.1°N, 56.5°W. At 57°W, near the Kutari River, the Wassari Mountains are further west. I estimate about 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

14473.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -33 is in the Weddell Sea, off the Antarctic coast. The Filchner Ice Shelf and Filchner Trough lie further south and west, and Coats Land is to the east. At -33 longitude, the coast is around -77 latitude, near the Filchner-Ronne ice shelf front. So -71 latitude, -33 longitude is open Weddell Sea.

The seabed there is likely the continental slope or deep basin. The Weddell Sea abyssal plain is at about 4000-5000 m depth, and the continental shelf edge near Dronning Maud Land and Coats Land is around -70 to -74 at longitudes -20 to -30. At -33, -71 the water is probably deep, roughly 4,000 m. The Explora Escarpment and Weddell Abyssal Plain sit around -65 to -70, so I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

15238.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 57 is in East Antarctica, in the interior near the Lambert Glacier / Prince Charles Mountains region. The Prince Charles Mountains sit around 70-73°E and 69-74°S, so this point is well south and west of them. The area is near the Recovery Glacier / Dome Argus approach, probably at about 2000-3000 m ice surface elevation. Dome Argus is at about -80.4, 77.4 and reaches roughly 4000 m. At -79, 57, which is between Support Force Glacier and Recovery, the surface is probably around 2500 m.

Pole of Inaccessibility is at -85.8, 65. The Shackleton Range is at about -80.5, -25. At 57°E and -79°, the ice surface is plausibly about 2900 m, so I'll go with 2800 m. The point is grounded ice, so it is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

8442.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 145.0 lies in the Bismarck Sea, north of the Papua New Guinea mainland (Sepik coast at about -3.6). The Admiralty Islands are at about 147°E, so they are not nearby. Manam Island is at about -4.1, 145.0, and the coast near Wewak is at about -3.55, 143.6. At 145.0E the north coast near Bogia is at about -4.2.

So -3.0 is about 120 km north of the coast, in the Bismarck Sea basin. The Bismarck Sea depths there are about 2000 to 2500 m. The Manus Basin is further east, at about 3.0S, 147E, and is deeper. The area near 3S, 145E is likely the North Bismarck basin, with depths around 2000 m or more. I'll estimate -2200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2200}

13115.text:
The point is at 55°S, 131°E, in the Southern Ocean south of Australia. It lies over the Southeast Indian Ridge region, and the Australian-Antarctic Basin is to the south.

The Southeast Indian Ridge near 131°E sits at about 50-51°S. At 55°S the seafloor is on the southern flank of the ridge, probably around 3500-4000 m deep. The Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies near 120-128°E, where depths are greater than normal. At 131°E and 55°S I estimate roughly -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

5973.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude -113.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, west of Baja California Sur. Cabo San Lazaro is near 24.8N, 112.3W, and the Baja coast at 23N is around -110 to -109.5W. At -113 the point is roughly 350 km offshore.

The seabed there is abyssal plain or the lower continental rise, at roughly 3500 m depth. The East Pacific Rise and Gulf of California mouth are farther east, and the Pacific floor off southern Baja is about 3400–3700 m deep. Guadalupe Island is much farther north, and the Revillagigedo Islands are to the south and east. I estimate about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

8660.text:
The point (-7, -139) is in the central Pacific, south of the equator and northwest of the Marquesas Islands, which lie around 9-10°S, 139-140°W. At 7°S the point is about 250 km north of Nuku Hiva, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor here is part of the Pacific basin, with depths of roughly 4000-4500 m near the Marquesas Fracture Zone region. The Marquesas plateau is shallower, but 7°S lies north of it. I estimate a seabed depth of about 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

13002.text:
The point (-55, -95) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile, so the surface is Water and I need a seabed depth.

It sits south of the Chile Rise and west of the Chile Trench, on the Antarctic plate or near the Chile Ridge. The Chile Rise runs from about 46°S, 75°W, southwest toward the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge near 55-60°S, 115°W. The Chile Ridge segments reach roughly 52°S at 92°W (the Chile Triple Junction is at 46°S). The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further southwest, around 56°S, 140°W, so -55, -95 is on the Antarctic plate seafloor, likely in the Southeast Pacific Basin. Typical depths there are about 3500-4000 m, and the Chile Rise at 55°S is at about 2500-3500 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

7402.text:
The point (7°N, 135°W) is in the open Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water. It lies north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone, in the abyssal plain region.

I did not look up a bathymetry value. I estimated the seabed depth from typical depths in this part of the Pacific, where seafloor age is moderate and depths run roughly 4,300–5,000 m. For this location I chose about -4,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

10031.text:
Latitude -21, longitude 83 is in the southern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and southeast of the Mascarene Plateau. It sits near the Central Indian Basin's western side, south of the Central Indian Ridge region.

Rodrigues is at about 63.4°E, so 83°E is well east of it. The Central Indian Ridge runs near 66-70°E at this latitude. East of the ridge lies the Central Indian Basin, with depths of about 4500-5500 m. At 21°S and 83°E there are also the Indian Ocean's abyssal plains, around 4000-5000 m. Around 83°E, 21°S, the Southeast Indian Ridge isn't relevant. I'd estimate roughly 5000 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

12532.text:
Location: 49°S, 45°E is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southwest of Marion/Prince Edward Islands (which lie near 46.9°S, 37.7°E). The Crozet Islands are near 46°S, 51°E. At 49°S, 45°E, the area is between the Crozet Plateau and the Southwest Indian Ridge. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs near 50-53°S in this longitude range, and the Del Cano Rise lies around 45°E, 46°S. The Crozet Basin lies to the east and the Enderby Basin to the south.

At 49°S, 45°E, the seafloor is probably on the southern flank of the Del Cano Rise, with depths around 3000 m. The Del Cano Rise has depths of about 2000-3000 m, and the basin to the south reaches about 4000 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

885.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 151.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands (which sit at about 75°N). It is north of the Laptev Sea shelf edge, in the Eurasian Basin or near the Lomonosov Ridge.

The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near 140-150°E at 81-87°N. At 81°N the shelf break is roughly at 78-79°N, so 81°N is on the continental slope or in the Amundsen Basin edge. The Laptev Sea slope descends to the Nansen Basin, whose floor is about 3500-4000 m deep. At 151°E and 81°N, I estimate the seabed depth at about 2800 m. This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

10058.text:
Latitude -21, longitude 137 is in the Northern Territory/Queensland border region of Australia, in the Barkly Tableland area south-east of Tennant Creek. It is inland, so the surface is Solid. Elevation there is likely around 200 m.

More precisely, 137°E and 21°S lies near the Northern Territory–Queensland border (138°E) to the east and Tennant Creek (134°E, 19.6°S) to the northwest. That puts it in the Georgina Basin, near the Toko Range and Marqua area. Elevations in this area are typically 200–300 m, so I estimate about 230 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":230}

12304.text:
The point is at 47°S, 51°W, in the South Atlantic east of Patagonia. That puts it in the open ocean, southeast of the Argentine coast and roughly north of the Falkland Islands (which sit near 51-52°S, 58-60°W).

The Argentine Basin lies at about 5000 m depth, but the continental slope and the Argentine continental rise are closer to shore. At 47°S the shelf edge is near 60°W, so 51°W is well offshore. This location is likely on the abyssal plain or the Zapiola area, where depths are around 5000 m. The North Scotia Ridge and the Falkland Plateau lie further south, at 50-52°S, so they do not apply here. At 47°S, 51°W the depth is probably around 5000 m, possibly a little less. I estimate -5050 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5050}

7902.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 145.0°E is in the western Pacific, north of Papua New Guinea and east of the Caroline Islands region. It lies near the Caroline Basin, northwest of the Manus area, and the Caroline Ridge is further north. The Eauripik Rise sits around 2°N, 143°E, and the Ayu Trough is near 3°N, 133°E, so those are not at this point. Manus is at about 2°S, 147°E, and the Mussau/New Hanover islands are south of the equator.

The Caroline Basin floor is about 4000 m deep, and the Eauripik Rise is shallower at roughly 2000 to 3000 m. At 145°E, 3°N the point is probably in the basin between the Eauripik Rise and the Caroline Islands, with depth around 4000 m. I estimate about -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

4146.text:
The point (43°N, 167°W) is in the North Pacific, south of the Aleutian Islands and well east of the Emperor Seamounts. The surrounding seafloor is abyssal plain. Depth in this region is roughly 5,400 to 5,600 m.

Since the point is open ocean, the surface type is Water, and I estimate the seabed at about -5,500 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5500}

13105.text:
Latitude -55, longitude 111 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and southwest of the Australian-Antarctic Ridge region. The Southeast Indian Ridge runs roughly through (-50, 110) to (-55, 135)... At 111°E the ridge axis is near 49-50°S, so -55 lies south of it on the Australian-Antarctic Basin's northern flank, with typical depth around 3500-4000 m. The Southeast Indian Ridge is shallower (~2500 m) near 50°S. Moving south toward 55°S, the seafloor deepens to about 3800 m.

This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

5269.text:
Latitude 31.0, longitude -81.0 is off the Georgia coast. Georgia's coast sits near -81.4 at about 31.0 (Sapelo Island is around 31.4, -81.25). Wassaw Sound lies near 31.9, and St. Andrew Sound is near 30.97, -81.4. At 31.0, the coastline is near -81.4, with Cumberland Island at about -81.45. So -81.0 is roughly 40 km offshore on the continental shelf. Water depth there is about 15-20 m.

The 20 m isobath is around -80.9 at this latitude, so I estimate a depth of about 17 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-17}

1185.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 31.0°E lies in the Barents Sea, east of Svalbard. Nordaustlandet reaches only about 27°E, and Kvitøya is at about 80°N, 32.5°E, so this point is open sea.

The Barents Sea in this area, between Svalbard and Franz Josef Land at 77°N and 31°E, is roughly 250 to 350 m deep. The Olga Basin lies near 76–77°N and 30–35°E. Its depth is about 200–300 m, and the Olga Basin trough deepens toward the northeast. I estimate about -280 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-280}

1008.text:
Latitude 79.0°N, longitude 37.0°E lies in the Arctic, in the Franz Josef Land / Svalbard region. Franz Josef Land spans about 80–81.8°N and 44–65°E, and Svalbard's Nordaustlandet lies at about 79–80.5°N and 18–28°E. So 79°N, 37°E is east of Svalbard and south of Franz Josef Land, in the northern Barents Sea. The nearest land is Kvitøya, at about 80.1°N and 32.5°E, and Victoria Island is at about 80.2°N and 36°E. Neither is at 79°N, so this point should be open sea.

Seabed depth in that part of the Barents Sea is typically about 200–300 m. The Kvitøya trough lies to the north of the point, and the area around 79°N, 37°E is probably around 250 m deep. I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

12433.text:
The point -49, -153 is in the South Pacific Ocean, southeast of French Polynesia and well away from any land. It sits on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or near it. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from about -55,-140 up toward -40,-110, so -153 is west of the ridge axis, on the older Pacific plate. Seafloor depth there is likely about 4,000 to 4,500 m. Nearby features include the Udintsev Fracture Zone to the south, but the area around -49, -153 is probably abyssal plain or hills at roughly 4,300 m depth.

I'm estimating from the regional setting rather than a known value, so I'll use about -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

13538.text:
The point at -61.0, -103.0 is in the Southern Ocean, in the Pacific sector near the southern Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Amundsen Sea. It lies west of the Drake Passage and north of the Bellingshausen Sea.

The seabed there is likely abyssal plain or the Bellingshausen basin, at a depth of about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Antarctic continental shelf is farther south, at about -70°. At 61°S and 103°W the seafloor is probably about 4,600 m deep, since the Bellingshausen Plain sits around 4,500 to 5,000 m. I'll estimate -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

1579.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 99.0°E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of northern Siberia. The Khatanga Gulf lies around 74-75°N, 105-112°E. The Taymyr coast on the Laptev Sea sits near 76°N, and the Taymyr Peninsula's northern edge is at 77°N. At 73°N, 99°E the point is inland, south of Taymyr Lake (about 74.5°N, 101°E), in the Byrranga foothills or the lowlands of the North Siberian Lowland. The terrain there is likely low, roughly 50-100 m.

The Taymyr lowland near Khatanga (72°N, 102°E) is about 30-50 m. At 73°N, 99°E, I estimate around 60 m, on land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

9591.text:
The point (-17, -77) lies in the Pacific Ocean, west of southern Peru. The Peru-Chile Trench runs roughly 150-250 km offshore here. The coast near Arequipa/Camaná sits at about -16.6 lat, -72.7 lon, so the point is about 400 km west of the coast. The trench axis at 17°S is around 77-78°W (Nazca Ridge subducts near 15°S), so the point is probably close to the trench axis or on its landward slope.

At 17°S the trench is about 6000 m deep, and at 15°S it is shallower because of the ridge. The trench axis at 17°S lies near 74-75°W, so I need to check that position. The Peru trench at 12°S is at about 77.5°W, and it trends southeast, reaching about 73.5°W at 18°S. So at 17°S the axis is near 74.5°W, and 77°W is west of the trench, on the Nazca plate's oceanic floor, which is about 4500-5000 m deep. Near 15-16°S the Nazca Ridge reaches about 3000-4000 m, but at 17°S, 77°W the ridge's southern end is around 2000 m depth shoaling... The Nazca Ridge trends northeast from about 26°S, 83°W to 15°S, 75°W. At 17°S it would be near 77-78°W, so the point may sit on or near the ridge. The ridge is about 200 km wide with depths of 2000-3500 m. I'll estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

10142.text:
Latitude -23, longitude -55 falls in Paraguay, in the Amambay/Canindeyú region near the Brazilian border. That area is part of the Paraná plateau, with elevations of roughly 300-500 m.

More precisely, -23.0 latitude is close to Pedro Juan Caballero, which sits at about -22.55 latitude, -55.73 longitude, at roughly 650 m. At -55.0 longitude, the point is east of the Amambay range, in Canindeyú, near Curuguaty and Salto del Guairá. Salto del Guairá is at -24.05, -54.3. The Paraná river lies at about -54.6 at that latitude. So -55.0, -23.0 is in the Canindeyú/Amambay area, where elevations are around 300-400 m. I'll estimate 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

7526.text:
The point 7.0°N, 113.0°E lies in the South China Sea, east of Borneo's northwest coast and in the Spratly region. It is north of Borneo (Sarawak is about 4°N), and the Spratly Islands are around 8–11°N, 111–116°E. At 7°N, 113°E the point is probably in the Dangerous Grounds area, but it is not on a known reef, so it is probably deep water.

The Dangerous Grounds have a deep trough on the Borneo side, with depths of about 1,500–2,500 m near 7°N, 113°E. Reefs such as Swallow Reef (Layang-Layang) are at 7.37°N, 113.85°E. Louisa Reef is at 6.3°N, 112.3°E. Near 7°N, 113°E the seabed is likely on the slope of the Dangerous Grounds, with depths of about 1,800 m. I'll estimate -1,900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

10368.text:
Latitude -25, longitude 37 lies in the Mozambique Channel, east of Mozambique's coast. The coast near Inhambane is at about 35°E, and Maputo is at 32.6°E. At -25° the coastline is around 33.5°E, so 37°E is roughly 350 km offshore. This is in the Mozambique Basin, where depths are around 3000 m. The Mozambique Channel at -25° is open ocean, and the basin floor is about 3000 to 3500 m deep. Further east, the Mozambique Ridge sits around 33–35°E at latitudes -27 to -30, so it is not relevant here.

Depth at -25, 37 is probably about 3000 m. I'll estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

8934.text:
Latitude -9.0, Longitude 49.0 is in the western Indian Ocean, east of Tanzania and northeast of Madagascar. The area lies in the Somali Basin or Mascarene region, southwest of the Seychelles, well away from land. Seychelles Bank is around 55°E and 4°S. At 9°S, 49°E the position is in open ocean, near the northern end of the Madagascar Basin, close to the Amirante area, and abyssal depths there are around 4500-5000 m.

The Comoros lie at 12°S, 44°E, and Madagascar's northern tip is at 12°S, 49°E. At 9°S, 49°E we are about 330 km north of Madagascar's tip, in the Somali Basin southern part, with depths around 4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

11919.text:
The point is at 43°S, 101°W in the southeast Pacific Ocean. It lies west of the Chile Rise and Chile Trench, on the Nazca/Antarctic plate region. The East Pacific Rise is at about 110°W near 43°S, which is the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region further south. At 101°W and 43°S the seafloor is likely the flank of the Chile Rise or the Nazca plate, with depths around 3500-4000 m.

The Chile Rise runs from the Chile Triple Junction (46°S, 76°W) southwest-to-northwest toward the East Pacific Rise at about 38°S, 110°W. The Chile Rise's Darwin and Challenger fracture zones lie near this area. At 43°S, 101°W the point is probably close to the rise axis, which is around 4000 m deep. I estimate about -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

14466.text:
Location: -71.0 latitude, -47.0 longitude is in the Weddell Sea region of Antarctica. Longitude -47 at latitude -71 is near the Filchner Ice Shelf front area. The Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly -30 to -80 longitude, with the Filchner part around -40 to -30 and the Ronne at -60 to -80. At -47 and -71, this is likely in the Weddell Sea, north of the Ronne Ice Shelf front (which is around 75°S at -50). So -71 is open ocean in the Weddell Sea, which is often covered by sea ice. The seabed there is probably the continental slope or deep basin, roughly -3000 m or so.

The Weddell Sea shelf edge at about -47 is around 74°S; at 71°S, -47 the water depth is about 4000 m, I'd estimate. The Weddell Sea abyssal plain is around 4000-5000 m deep near 65°S, but at 71°S, -47 it's probably about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

7502.text:
Location: 7°N, 65°E is in the Arabian Sea / northern Indian Ocean, southwest of India and east of Socotra. It lies near the Carlsberg Ridge region and the Arabian Basin. The Carlsberg Ridge runs northwest-southeast, from about 10°N 57°E to 2°N 67°E. At 7°N the ridge axis is near 60°E, so 65°E is east of the axis, probably on the ridge flank or in the Arabian Basin, at a depth of around 3500-4500 m.

The ridge axis at 3°N is about 64°E, and at 5°N about 62°E. At 7°N it is about 60°E. So 65°E is about 5° east of the axis, on the flank, and the depth should be about 4000 m. The Arabian Basin there is about 4200 m deep. I'll guess -4100.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4100}

7510.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 81.0°E is in Sri Lanka's central highlands. Sri Lanka spans roughly 79.7–81.9°E and 5.9–9.8°N. At 7.0°N, 81.0°E, the point sits east of Nuwara Eliya (80.77°E) and near Badulla (81.05°E, 6.99°N). Badulla is at about 670 m, and the surrounding terrain is hilly, so a coordinate just west of it would likely be somewhat higher. At exactly 81.0°E, 7.0°N, I'd estimate about 800 m, though I'm unsure of the exact local terrain.

Since Badulla sits near 81.05°E at roughly 680 m, and the Uva basin slopes down eastward, I'll go with 750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":750}

14891.text:
Location: -75, 83E in East Antarctica. This is inland of the Amery/Davis Sea coast. At 83E, 75S is roughly in the interior near Vostok Subglacial Highlands... Vostok is at 106E, 78S. At 75S 83E, the ice sheet surface is about 2,500-3,000 m. Dome A is at 80E, 80S (4,000 m). Princess Elizabeth Land coast is around 69S at 80E. So 75S is about 600 km inland, with an elevation around 2,800 m.

Ice-surface elevation near 75S 83E is probably about 2,900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

2829.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 79.0°E lies in western Siberia, in the Tomsk Oblast region (Vasyugan swamps area). This is flat, swampy lowland. The elevation there is roughly 80-100 m. The Ob River near Kolpashevo (58.3°N, 82.9°E) sits around 60 m. At 79°E, 59°N, close to the Tomsk–Tyumen border, the ground is likely 80-90 m.

I'm estimating about 85 m and treating it as land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":85}

6174.text:
Latitude 21.0, longitude -71.0 is in the Turks and Caicos region. Grand Turk sits at about 21.45N, -71.14W. The Caicos Bank lies to the west and southwest, around 21.5N, -72.0W. At 21.0N, -71.0W the point is south of Grand Turk and Salt Cay (about 21.33N, -71.2W). South of those islands, the Turks Island Passage runs between the Turks Bank and Mouchoir Bank. Mouchoir Bank is at about 21.0 to 20.7N, -70.5 to -71.0W, and its western edge lies near -70.9W.

The passage is very deep, over 2000 m. At 21.0N, -71.0W the point is likely on the western edge of Mouchoir Bank or in the passage. Mouchoir Bank spans about 20.6–21.1N and -70.7 to -70.4W, so -71.0 would be west of the bank, in the deep passage. The Turks Island Passage depth is about 1500 to 2000 m near 21N, so I'll estimate about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

14760.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -179 is in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°W to 160°E at latitudes from about -78 to -83, and its front sits near -78. At -75, longitude -179 is therefore north of the ice shelf, in the open Ross Sea. That is probably near the Pennell Bank / continental shelf area. The Ross Sea shelf is typically 500 m deep, and the bank areas are shallower at about 300-500 m. Around 75°S, 179°W the depth is about 500 m. That is a rough estimate from memory of the shelf, not a looked-up value.

The surface type is therefore Water, with a seabed elevation of about -520 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-520}

11254.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 9 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Cape Town (which is at about 18.4°E, 34°S). That puts the point roughly 800 km west of the South African coast.

The seabed there is likely the Cape Basin, at about 4,500 to 5,000 m depth. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is much farther west, near 14°W. Walvis Ridge is farther north, near 30°S. At 35°S, 9°E the Cape Basin floor is probably around -4,900 m. I'll estimate -4,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

12548.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 77 lies in the southern Indian Ocean. The Kerguelen Islands are centered around 49.35°S, 69.5°E, so 77°E is well east of them. The Kerguelen Plateau extends from about 50°S to the southeast, and its northern part sits around 46-49°S at 70-80°E.

Heard Island is at 53°S, 73.5°E. At 49°S, 77°E the position is on the eastern side of the northern Kerguelen Plateau, near the edge of the Kerguelen-Heard plateau. Depths there are roughly 1000-2500 m. The Kerguelen Plateau's main body at 49°S spans about 64-78°E. Just east of the plateau lies the Southeast Indian Ridge and the Australian-Antarctic Basin. The plateau's eastern flank near 77°E could be around 2000 m deep. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

14529.text:
