964.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -51.0 is in Greenland's interior ice sheet, in northwest Greenland. Melville Bay lies near 75–76°N and Kane Basin near 79°N, 70°W. At 51°W the coast is far to the east, with Humboldt Glacier at about 79.8°N, 67°W. The northwest Greenland coast runs from about 77°N, 70°W up to Washington Land at about 80°N, 65°W. At 51°W and 79°N the point is well inland, roughly 600 km from the west coast.

The Greenland ice sheet there is roughly 2000 m high. Near 79°N, 51°W the surface is around 1900 m. The NEGIS (Northeast Greenland Ice Stream) starts around 77°N, 35°W, so it is not nearby. Using the ice-sheet saddle at roughly 79°N, 50°W, I estimate the elevation at about 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1850}

11383.text:
Location: -37, -93 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile, around 2,000+ km from the coast. This is on the Nazca Plate near the Chile Rise region. The Chile Rise is at about 36°S, 92°W, so there's a ridge-crest influence. The East Pacific Rise's southern end meets the Chile Rise near 38°S, 93°W. Depth in the region is around 3000-3500 m near the ridge, though the Juan Fernandez area lies further north. Ridge axis depths are about 2800-3000 m at the southern EPR near 36°S, 110°W; near the Chile Rise at 92°W, depths are maybe 3500 m.

I estimate -3400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

4626.text:
Latitude 39.0, longitude 73.0 (assuming east) is in Central Asia, in the Pamir region of Tajikistan/Kyrgyzstan. The Alay valley is near 39.5N 72-73E, and the Trans-Alay range lies to the south at about 39.2N, with Peak Lenin at 39.34N, 72.88E.

At 39.0N 73.0E, the point lies south of the Trans-Alay range, in the northern Pamir near the Kyzylsu and Muksu valleys and Karakul lake (39.0N, 73.5E). Karakul sits at about 3900 m. The point is about 50 km west of the lake, in the Pamir with high terrain around 4500 m. The Markansu/Kyzylsu area is about 4000-4500 m, so I'll estimate 4400.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":4400}

10981.text:
The point (-33.0, -177.0) is in the South Pacific, northeast of New Zealand. It lies near the Kermadec Ridge region, west of the Kermadec Trench. The Kermadec Islands are at about 29-31°S, 178°W, so this point is south and east of them. The trench axis near 33°S is around 177-178°W. The trench is at roughly 176.5°W at 33°S, so the point could be close to the trench axis or on its eastern side (the Pacific plate). The Pacific plate seafloor there is about 5,000-6,000 m deep, while the trench reaches 8,000-10,000 m at 32-35°S.

The trench at 33°S is probably around 177.5°W, with depths near 8,000 m. At -177.0, the point is east of the Kermadec Ridge and trench, so it would be on the Pacific plate. The trench at that latitude is at about -176.8 and the Kermadec Ridge is west of it at about -179, so the point is likely at the trench or on its slope. I'll estimate -6,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-6000}

2752.text:
The point 59.0°N, 75.0°W lies in northern Quebec (Nunavik), near the Hudson Bay coast. Hudson Bay's eastern shore sits around 77-78°W at this latitude. Around 59°N, the coast near Inukjuak is at 58.45°N, 78.1°W, and Kuujjuarapik is at 55°N. Ungava Bay is farther east, at about 68°W. So 75°W at 59°N is inland on the Ungava Peninsula, west of Kuujjuaq and near Lac Klotz, though possibly not exactly at it. Lac Klotz is at about 59.4°N, 73.5°W. The point is likely land, with elevation around 300 m.

The Nunavik plateau near 59°N, 75°W is rolling terrain at roughly 250-400 m. There are many lakes, but the most likely answer is land at about 330 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":330}

7871.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 83.0°E lies in the Indian Ocean, southeast of Sri Lanka and south of India. Sri Lanka sits at about 80-82°E, so this point is well east of it, in open ocean. Because it is open ocean, the surface type is Water.

The point is in the central Indian Ocean basin, in the Ceylon Plain / Central Indian Basin. Depths there are typically about 4,200 to 4,500 m. The Ninety East Ridge is near 90°E, which is farther east, so it does not affect this point. I did not look up bathymetry data, so the depth is an estimate from regional knowledge, and I'll use about -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

7156.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude 93.0 is in the Andaman Sea, east of the Andaman Islands. The Andaman Islands lie at roughly 92.5 to 93.0°E. At 11°N, the Middle Andaman and South Andaman are around 12.5°N and 11.7°N, and Port Blair sits at 11.6°N, 92.7°E. Little Andaman is at about 10.7°N, 92.5°E. So 11.0°N, 93.0°E lies east of South Andaman, in the Andaman Sea.

The sea floor there is probably around 600 to 1000 m deep. The Andaman Sea's shelf east of the islands is shallow in places, and the basin deepens toward the east. At 93°E, 11°N, I estimate about -900 m, based on the depth near Ritchie's Archipelago, which has a shelf to the east. I'm not certain of that value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

3320.text:
The point 53.0°N, 19.0°W lies in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and west of Ireland. It is east of the Reykjanes Ridge, which sits near 30°W at 53°N (the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is around 30-35°W there). So the point is in the West European Basin or the Iceland Basin area.

Looking at the bathymetry, at 53°N the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis is near 35°W. At 19°W, the seafloor is the abyssal plain of the Iceland Basin or Charlie-Gibbs region, with depths of about 3500-4000 m. The Rockall Plateau lies at about 15-20°W, but at 57-60°N. At 53°N, 19°W the seafloor is the northern West European Basin, around 4000 m deep. The Hatton-Rockall area is further north. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

3411.text:
Location: 53.0°N, 163.0°E. This is in the Pacific Ocean off the southeast coast of Kamchatka. Avacha Bay lies at about 53.0°N, 158.6°E, so 163°E is roughly 300 km east of the Kamchatka coast. The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs parallel to the coast, with its axis at about 160-161°E at this latitude. The trench is deepest at around 9,000-10,000 m near 44-46°N, and at 53°N it reaches about 7,000-8,000 m. East of the trench the seafloor is Pacific abyssal plain at about 5,000-5,500 m depth.

At 53°N the trench axis is about 160.5°E, so 163°E is about 150 km east of it. That puts the point on the outer trench slope and rise, where the depth is about 5,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

5312.text:
Latitude 31.0°N, longitude 5.0°E places this point in northern Algeria's Sahara, near the Grand Erg Occidental / Oued Mya region. It is south of the Atlas and north of Hassi Messaoud (which is at 31.7°N, 6.1°E).

Hassi Messaoud sits at about 140 m. Near 5°E, 31°N the terrain is part of the Grand Erg Oriental's western edge, or the Hassi R'Mel/Ghardaïa area to the west. Ghardaïa (32.5°N, 3.7°E) is at about 500 m. Ouargla (31.9°N, 5.3°E) is at about 150 m. The point at 31.0°N, 5.0°E is south of Ouargla, in the sand sea or the Mya depression, where elevations are roughly 150 m. I estimate about 160 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":160}

262.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -15° is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Greenland. At 87°N, 15°W, the point lies near the Lomonosov Ridge region. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole from Greenland/Ellesmere toward the New Siberian Islands. It sits roughly along 140°E to 60°W on the Greenland side. At 87°N it's near about 50°W to 30°W, so 15°W is probably on the Eurasian side of the ridge, in the Amundsen Basin. The Amundsen Basin depth is about 4200 m.

The ridge near the pole sits at roughly 1000-1500 m depth. On the Greenland side at 87°N, the ridge is around 40-60°W, so 15°W is likely in the Amundsen Basin, a bit toward the Gakkel Ridge side. The abyssal plain depth there is about -4200 m. I estimate -4150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4150}

15272.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 125 is in East Antarctica, in the interior of Wilkes Land. It lies roughly between Dome C (-75, 123) and Vostok (-78.5, 106.8). Dome C is at about 3233 m. Moving south to -79 at longitude 125, the ice surface should be around 3000 m, which fits the region south of Dome C toward the Aurora basin. The Aurora Subglacial Basin lies near -75 and 118 to 120, so this point is probably on grounded ice at around 2900 m.

That figure is an estimate from nearby known surface elevations, not a lookup of this exact point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

740.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -139.0°W lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Canada's Arctic Archipelago. Ellef Ringnes Island is around 78.5°N, 101°W and Meighen Island is at about 80°N, 99°W, so neither is near. Axel Heiberg and Ellesmere are at longitudes -90 to -60, also far away. At -139°, 81°N is in the Canada Basin region, north of the Beaufort Sea and west of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago's Prince Patrick Island (about 77°N, -118°). The point is therefore in open ocean.

The Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. At 81°N, -139°, this is probably near the Canada Basin's northern part, toward the Alpha Ridge and Mendeleev Ridge, which lie further north at about 85°N. The Chukchi Borderland is at about 75-80°N, -160°. The point at 81°N, -139° is likely on the abyssal plain, with depth around 3700 m.


{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3750}

14189.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 119 is in East Antarctica, near the Wilkes Land coast. The Shackleton Ice Shelf and Denman Glacier lie farther east, around 100°E. At 119°E the coast is near Dalton Iceberg Tongue and the Sandford Ice Tongue region, roughly 66.5°S, which is close to Law Dome (112°E) and Vanderford Glacier (109°E). The coast at 119°E is around 66.3°S, so -67 is about 70 km inland.

Ice surface there would be around 1000-1500 m. In that area, the Totten Glacier lies near 116°E, and the coast near 119°E is roughly at 66.5°S. At 67°S the ice would be grounded, with a surface elevation of roughly 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

10441.text:
Latitude -27, longitude -177 lies in the South Pacific, northeast of New Zealand. The Kermadec Islands are near -29.2, -178; Raoul Island is at about -29.25, -177.9. The point at -27, -177 is northeast of the Kermadecs, in the Pacific basin east of the Kermadec Trench, and probably over the Kermadec Ridge or the abyssal plain.

The trench axis near latitude -27 sits at about -175.5 longitude, with depths of about 8,000 m or more. The Kermadec Trench is at roughly -176.5 at -30 and about -175 at -25. The Colville and Kermadec ridges lie west of the trench. East of the trench is the Pacific plate, with the Louisville seamount chain further south and west. At -177, the point is probably west of the trench axis, on the Kermadec Ridge's eastern flank, or in the trench itself. The trench axis around -27 is near -175.3, so -177 is about 1.7° west (~170 km), which puts it on the arc and forearc slope. The forearc slope depth there would be roughly 3,000–5,000 m. Without a better constraint I'll estimate about -3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

5586.text:
The point 27.0°N, 167.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. Nearby features: Kure Atoll is at 28.4°N, 178.3°W, Midway at 28.2°N, 177.4°W, and French Frigate Shoals at 23.7°N, 166.2°W. Gardner Pinnacles is at 25.0°N, 168.0°W and Maro Reef at 25.4°N, 170.6°W. Laysan is at 25.8°N, 171.7°W, and Lisianski at 26.0°N, 173.9°W.

So 27°N, 167°W is about 330 km north of French Frigate Shoals. It is probably on the abyssal plain north of the Hawaiian Ridge, where depths are about 5000 m. The ridge's moat and arch could make it slightly shallower or deeper. Seafloor north of the Hawaiian chain at this longitude is typically around 4800 to 5200 m. I estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

