The point is at 13°S, 161°W, in the South Pacific. That puts it west of the Cook Islands and east of Samoa, near Tokelau to the north and the northern Cook Islands (Manihiki, Rakahanga) at about 10°S, 161°W. At 13°S there is no island, so the surface is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is the Penrhyn Basin / Manihiki Plateau area. The Manihiki Plateau spans roughly 8–13°S and 160–172°W, with depths of about 2,500–3,000 m on the plateau. The southern edge is near 13°S. At 161°W, the plateau's southeast corner could be around 3,000–4,000 m deep, while the Penrhyn Basin is about 5,000 m deep. I'll estimate roughly -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

