The point is -51.0, -37.0, in the South Atlantic. It lies northeast of South Georgia (which is around -54.5, -36.5) and southeast of the Falkland Islands. It is likely on the North Scotia Ridge or the nearby basin.

The North Scotia Ridge runs from the Falklands/Burdwood Bank eastward toward South Georgia, at about -54 latitude and to the west of -40 longitude. At -51 and -37, the point is north of the ridge, in the Georgia Basin, where depths are around 3000-4000 m. The Georgia Basin is centered around -50, -40 (roughly 3500-4000 m deep), so I estimate about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

14424.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -131 lies in the Amundsen Sea sector of West Antarctica, off the Marie Byrd Land coast. The Getz Ice Shelf extends to about 75°W–135°W, with its front near -74 to -75 lat. At -71 lat, the ocean is likely open Amundsen Sea or continental shelf. The Marie Byrd Land coast is around -74 to -75 at longitude -131, so -71 is about 400 km offshore, on the outer shelf or continental slope. Shelf edge in that area is near -73, so at -71 the water is probably deep, around -3000 m. In the Amundsen Sea, the continental slope runs near 72°S at 130°W, so -71 is likely abyssal plain or lower slope, maybe -3500 m.

Checking the coastline: at 130°W the Getz Ice Shelf front is around 74°S. The shelf break is around 72.5°S. So -71 is beyond the shelf break and into the slope/rise, with depth about -3000 m.

