12992.text:
The point at 55°S, 115°W is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, and the area is open water. It lies on or near the flank of the Pacific-Antarctic Rise, which is at roughly 56°S, 140°W, and further east at 115°W the rise is around 57–60°S. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about 65°S, 175°W to 55°S, 130°W and then to the Chile Rise near 45°S, 80°W, so at 115°W it sits near 52–55°S. That puts this point close to the ridge crest, where depths are about 2500–3000 m, though the exact position relative to the crest is uncertain.

The East Pacific Rise's southern extension curves toward the southeast, and near 55°S, 115°W the depth is probably about 3000 m. I'll estimate -3000 m, which fits either the crest or the nearby flank.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

3283.text:
Location: 53.0N, 93.0W is in northern Ontario, in the Hudson Bay Lowlands, roughly 150 km south-southwest of the Hudson Bay coast near the Manitoba border. The Severn River region lies near 55N, so this point is within the Hudson Bay Lowlands in the Attawapiskat/Winisk area. It is probably wetland or muskeg with an elevation of around 150 m.

Looking at it more closely, the Ontario-Manitoba border is at 95W. At 53N, 93W, this is close to Sandy Lake (53.0N, 93.3W) and the Severn headwaters. Sandy Lake sits at about 53.0N, 93.0W, with a surface elevation of about 280 m. The point could fall on the lake, but I'm not sure. Sandy Lake's elevation is about 277 m, and its east end sits near 92.9W. Its depth is shallow, around 10 m or less. I'll treat the point as land at about 280 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":280}

12612.text:
The point (-51, -155) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and southwest of Chile, far from any land. It is on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern flank or in the abyssal plain.

The East Pacific Rise at 51°S lies around longitude -115 to -120. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about (-55, -140) to (-65, -170), so at -155 it lies farther south. At -51, -155, the seafloor is probably abyssal at about 4000-4500 m depth. The Udintsev fracture zone lies around 56°S, so this point is north of it. The seafloor age is moderate, and the depth is likely around -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4100}

1427.text:
Latitude 75°N, longitude 155°E is in the East Siberian Sea / Laptev Sea boundary region, north of the New Siberian Islands. The New Siberian Islands lie around 75°N, 140-150°E, with Kotelny at about 76°N, 138-142°E and Bennett Island at 76.5°N, 149°E. So 155°E at 75°N is open sea east of the islands, on the East Siberian shelf. The shelf there is shallow, around 40-60 m deep, and depths increase northward toward the shelf break at about 77-78°N.

At 75°N, 155°E I estimate about -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

10176.text:
Latitude -23.0, longitude 13.0 is in Namibia, inland of Walvis Bay, in the Namib-Naukluft area. The coast at that latitude lies near 14.5°E, so 13.0°E is over the ocean, about 150 km offshore. The coastline near Walvis Bay (-23°) is at about 14.5°E, and Lüderitz is at 15.1°E.

The continental shelf off Namibia is fairly wide. At 13°E, 23°S, the shelf break is at about 200 m depth, roughly 100-150 km offshore. Walvis Bay is at 14.5°E, so 13°E is 1.5° ≈ 153 km offshore. The shelf there is about 100 km wide, and the shelf edge sits around 13.4°E. At 13°E the seabed is probably on the upper slope, around 700 m depth. Further out the slope reaches about 3000 m at 11°E. Estimated depth is about -800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

4069.text:
Latitude 45.0 N, longitude 39.0 E is in Krasnodar Krai, Russia. Krasnodar city sits at about 45.04 N, 38.98 E, so this point is essentially at Krasnodar. The city's elevation is roughly 30 m. The Kuban River runs nearby, but the point itself is most likely on land, so I classify it as Solid.

I estimate the bare-ground elevation at about 28 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":28}

430.text:
Latitude 85°N, longitude 39°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Greenland. Peary Land, at roughly 83.6°N, is the northernmost land, so 85°N is about 150 km beyond the coast. That puts the point over the Lincoln Sea / Arctic Ocean margin, close to the Morris Jesup Rise and the Greenland continental shelf edge.

The Morris Jesup Rise is centered near 85°N, 10°W to 0°. At 39°W, the point is probably in the deep area north of the Greenland slope, with depths around 3000 to 4000 m. In the IBCAO bathymetry, the region north of Greenland near 85N, 40W is probably around 3,000 m deep. The shelf north of Greenland extends to about 83.5 to 84N, and then the slope descends into the Amundsen Basin. The Lomonosov Ridge sits at around 140°W to 50°E, passing near the pole. At 85N, 39W the point is in the Amundsen Basin, close to the Greenland side, where depths are about 4000 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

13550.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -79 is in the Southern Ocean, in the Drake Passage region west of the Antarctic Peninsula and south of Cape Horn. Cape Horn sits near -56°S, -67°W, and the South Shetland Islands are around -62°S, -58°W, so this point is well to the west of both and in open ocean.

The point lies near the Shackleton Fracture Zone region, in the southeast Pacific. The Shackleton Fracture Zone runs from about -58°S, -70°W toward the Antarctic Peninsula. At -79°W the seafloor is likely abyssal, around 4000 m deep. The Pacific side west of the Drake Passage there is probably about -4000 m, and the Antarctic–Phoenix ridge and the Hero Fracture Zone are also nearby. I'll estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

4803.text:
Latitude 37.0, longitude 67.0 falls in northern Afghanistan, in Balkh province near Mazar-i-Sharif (36.7N, 67.1E) and Balkh city (36.75N, 66.9E). The point at 37.0N is north of Mazar, in the plains toward the Amu Darya, which lies at about 37.1N near Hairatan. The terrain there is flat desert plain, roughly 300 m above sea level.

Hairatan sits at about 300 m, and Mazar-i-Sharif at about 380 m. Elevation falls from Mazar northward, so 37.0N should be about 330 m. The point is not water.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":330}

1344.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -11.0 is in the Greenland Sea, off northeast Greenland. The coast there (Kronprins Christian Land / Wollaston Forland) lies around -18 to -20° longitude at 75°N. Clavering Island is at about -18°, and the shelf extends east to roughly -10°. At -11° the point is on the outer shelf or the slope toward the Greenland Sea, and the Greenland Basin lies further east.

The Northeast Greenland shelf is about 300 m deep, with the Norske Trough and the shelf break near -8° to -10° at 75°N. So at -11° the point is probably near the shelf edge, and I estimate the seabed depth at about -400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

2525.text:
The point is at 61.0°N, 169.0°W, which is in the Bering Sea. St. Lawrence Island sits near 63°N, 170°W, and the Yukon delta is near 62°N, 165°W, so this point is south-southwest of St. Lawrence Island in open water. The Bering Sea shelf in that area is shallow, and I estimate the seabed at about 50 to 60 m below sea level. This is an estimate from the geography, not a looked-up value.

The shelf break runs roughly from the Pribilofs (57°N, 170°W) northwest toward Cape Navarin. At 61°N, 169°W the point is well inside the shelf, which fits a depth of about 50 m. The Anadyr Strait region is about 50 m deep, and the shelf south of St. Lawrence Island is about 60 to 70 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-55}

8435.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 131.0 is in eastern Indonesia, near Misool in the Raja Ampat region. Misool lies at roughly -1.9 to -2.2 latitude and 130-130.5 longitude. Seram is at about -3.0 and 129-130.5, and its eastern tip is near 130.8. The Bomberai Peninsula in West Papua begins around 132.5. So at -3.0, 131.0 the point probably falls in the sea, in the Seram Sea or Ceram Sea area east of Seram. Nearby places include Kaimana (133.7, -3.6), the Gorong islands (131.4, -3.9), and Kofiau and Misool in the north.

The point is south of Misool and east of Seram's tip, so it is likely in the Ceram Sea or the Berau Gulf area. Water depth there is uncertain. The Seram Trough is deep, over 3000 m, but it lies to the south and west. Around 131E, -3S the sea could be about 1000 m deep. I'll guess -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

11610.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 1°E is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge near 40°S sits around 10-15°W, with Tristan da Cunha at 37°S, 12°W. At 1°E the seafloor is likely the Atlantic basin floor of about 4500-5000 m depth, though the Agulhas Ridge and Meteor Rise lie further east and south. The Walvis Ridge is farther north, near 30-35°S. The Cape Basin at about 39°S, 1°E is roughly 5000 m deep.

I estimate about -4900 m from this reasoning rather than from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1309.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -81.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Devon Island's northern coast is around 76.5°N and Somerset Island spans roughly 72–74.3°N at -93 to -95°W. Lancaster Sound sits at about 74°N, between Devon Island and Baffin Island's north coast at around 73.7°N, and runs from about -90° to -80°.

At 75°N, -81°W, this is probably the southern part of Devon Island, whose south coast is near 74.5°N at -81°W. Devon's southern coast runs along Lancaster Sound at about 74.6°N at -85° and about 74.5°N at -80°. So 75.0°N is on Devon Island, inland about 50 km. The Devon Ice Cap lies further east, at roughly -82°W and 75.3°N, with its top near 1800 m. At -81°W, 75.0°N, this is near the ice cap's southwestern margin. The Devon Ice Cap spans about 74.3–75.8°N and -80 to -86°W, so the point is probably on the ice cap's southern part, at an elevation of roughly 1000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

1205.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 71.0°E is in the Kara Sea region. Taymyr Peninsula lies around 100°E, and the Severnaya Zemlya islands are at about 95-105°E. Novaya Zemlya's northern tip is near 76.9°N, 68°E, with its northern end around 77°N, 69°E. Longitude 71°E at 77°N is therefore just east of Novaya Zemlya's northern tip (Cape Zhelaniya at 76.95°N, 68.55°E), in the Kara Sea.

The Kara Sea there is fairly shallow, around 100-300 m. East of Zhelaniya, the St. Anna Trough lies further west at about 78°N, 70°E, and its depth reaches over 400 m. At 77°N, 71°E, the seabed is probably around 200 m deep. I'll go with -250.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

14394.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 169 lies in the Southern Ocean off Cape Adare / northern Victoria Land. Cape Adare is at about -71.3, 170.2, so -69 is well north of the coast, around 250 km north of the coast (the coast near Oates Land is about -70.5 at 166°E). At 169°E the Antarctic coast is around -70.5 to -71, so -69 is open ocean over the continental slope or rise.

The continental shelf edge is near -70 in this area. The Balleny Islands are around -67, 164. At -69, 169 the water is likely deep, on the continental rise at about -3000 m. The slope in the Ross Sea region, near Cape Adare, extends to roughly -69 at 170°E, with depths of 2000–3000 m. I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

10273.text:
The point is at -25, -153 in the South Pacific, east of the Austral Islands and southeast of Tahiti. It lies in open ocean on the Pacific plate, in the region of the Austral/Gambier seamount chains. Mururoa is at about -21.8, -138.9 and Pitcairn at about -25.07, -130.1, so this point is well west of both.

The seafloor in this area is typically about 4,000 to 4,500 m deep. I'm estimating from that regional depth rather than from a specific chart value, and I'll take about -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

6720.text:
The point 15.0°N, 59.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, east of the Lesser Antilles. Barbados is at about 13.2°N, 59.5°W, so this point is roughly 200 km north of Barbados and east of Martinique and St. Lucia, in open Atlantic.

The seafloor there is on the abyssal plain east of the Barbados accretionary prism. Depths around 15°N, 59°W are roughly 5000 m. The Tiburon Rise and the Barbados Ridge lie to the west, and the abyssal plain near 15°N, 58°W is about 4900–5000 m deep. I estimate about -4950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

15174.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -71 is in Antarctica, near the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf region. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly longitudes -30 to -80 and latitudes -75 to -83, so this point is probably on the Ronne Ice Shelf, which is floating.

Checking the geography: the Ronne Ice Shelf's western part lies near Berkner Island (-45) and the Orville Coast (-63). At -79, -71 we are west of the Orville Coast, near the Ellsworth Mountains and Fletcher Promontory (-78, -84). The Ronne ice shelf is bounded by the Antarctic Peninsula at about -65 to -70 longitude at latitude -75 to -77, and the Gardner Inlet is near -75. At -79, -71 it is likely still the ice shelf, or possibly the grounded ice of the Rutford/Institute ice streams. The Institute Ice Stream is at about -81, -75, and the Rutford at -78, -83. Moving down to -79, -71, this is probably the Ronne Ice Shelf front area south of Henry Ice Rise (-77, -60), and the seabed there is deep, perhaps -500 to -800 m, so I'll answer Water with a seabed elevation of about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

1918.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 57.0°E lies in northern Russia, in the Nenets Autonomous Okrug. It is near the Barents Sea coast, close to the Pechora Sea and Kolguev Island. Kolguev is at about 69.2°N, 49°E, so it is further west. Longitude 57°E at 69°N is on the mainland tundra, east of the Pechora Sea and southwest of the Yugorsky Peninsula. The Pechora Sea coast near Varandey is at about 68.8°N, 58°E, so 69°N, 57°E is probably in the water just offshore, northwest of Varandey. The coast there runs roughly from 68.3°N, 55°E to Varandey at 58°E. At 57°E the coast is probably around 68.6°N, so 69.0°N falls in the sea. The Pechora Sea is shallow, around 10–20 m deep near the coast.

I estimate it is water with a seabed about 15 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-15}

10191.text:
Latitude -23.0, longitude 43.0 is in southwestern Madagascar, near Toliara. Toliara sits at about 43.68°E, 23.35°S, so 43.0°E is about 70 km west of the city, in the Mozambique Channel, off the coast.

The Madagascar coast at latitude -23 lies near 43.6°E. At 43.0°E we are about 70 km offshore. The shelf off Toliara is fairly wide, and the Great Reef (Grand Récif) is near the coast. The shelf edge lies roughly 40–50 km offshore, so at 70 km the seabed is on the upper slope at about 500–1000 m depth. I'll estimate -800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

11994.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 49 lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and southwest of the Crozet Islands. It is near the Southwest Indian Ridge, but the ridge is farther south, at roughly -50 to -45 at this longitude. The Del Cano Rise sits around 45°S, 45-50°E, with depths of about 2000-3000 m. The Crozet Plateau is further east, near 51°E, 46°S.

At 43°S, 49°E the bathymetry is probably the northern flank of the Del Cano Rise or the Madagascar Basin edge. The Southwest Indian Ridge crosses about 40°S at 50°E, because the Indian Ocean triple junction is near 25°S, 70°E and the ridge runs southwest toward the Prince Edward Fracture Zone, around 35°S at 40°E. The Atlantis II Fracture Zone is at about 32°S, 57°E. So the ridge at 49°E lies at roughly 38-40°S, and the Del Cano Rise is south of that at 44-46°S. That puts this point in the ridge-flank or rise region, where depths are probably about 3000-4000 m. I'll estimate -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

6351.text:
Latitude 19.0, longitude -77.0 is in the Caribbean Sea, south of Cuba and northwest of Jamaica. Jamaica spans roughly 76.2 to 78.4 W and 17.7 to 18.5 N, so 19.0 N is north of the island. Cuba's south coast lies near 19.9 N at this longitude, which leaves the point in open water.

The point is in the Cayman Trough (Bartlett Trough), which lies between Cuba and Jamaica. The trough's depth here is about 4000 to 5000 m. The Cayman Trough floor runs from about -4000 to -6000 m, with the deepest part to the west near 81 W. At 77 W, between Jamaica and Cuba, the depth is roughly 3000 to 4000 m, because the Windward Passage region is shallower. I estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9720.text:
The point is at 19°S, 179°W, which lies in the South Pacific east of Fiji. Fiji's Lau Islands are nearby: Vanua Balavu sits near 17.2°S, 179°W, and Lakeba near 18.2°S, 178.8°W. Fulaga is at about 19.1°S, 178.6°W, and Ogea Levu is at about 19.1°S, 178.45°W. The point at 179°W is therefore slightly west of Fulaga, in open water between the Lau Group and Tonga.

This area lies in the Lau Basin, to the west of the Lau Ridge. The Lau Ridge runs along roughly 178.5°W near this latitude. Near 179°W the seabed is probably the Lau Basin floor or the slope of the ridge. Basin depths there are typically around 2000 to 3000 m. Because the point is only about 0.4° from Fulaga, I would expect it to be on the ridge flank at about 1500 to 2500 m depth. I'll estimate -2300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

2877.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 175.0°E is in the western Bering Sea, east of the Kamchatka/Koryak coast. The Koryak coast near Cape Olyutorsky is at about 60°N, 170°E, and the Gulf of Anadyr is farther north. Kamchatka's east coast at 59°N lies near 163°E, so 175°E is about 12° of longitude east, roughly 680 km offshore.

This area is the Commander Basin and Bering Sea basin region. The Shirshov Ridge runs from about 58°N, 170°E northward. The Aleutian Basin, in the deep Bering Sea, is around 3800 m deep. At 59°N, 175°E, the point is in the Aleutian Basin west part, with a depth of about 3800 m. The Shirshov Ridge sits near 170-172°E at 58-60°N, so 175°E is east of the ridge, in the basin. The Bering Sea continental slope lies to the northeast, around 60°N, 178°E. So the point is probably abyssal, at about -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3750}

3808.text:
The point 47.0, -123.0 lies in western Washington. It is roughly in the Olympic Peninsula's southern area, near Shelton in Mason County, or slightly south of it. Shelton is at about 47.21, -123.10, so 47.0 N is about 24 km south of Shelton. That puts the point in the Black Hills or the Capitol State Forest area, near Mud Bay and Olympia. Olympia is at 47.04, -122.9, so the point is west of Olympia by about 8 km. The Black Hills lie around -123.1, and the Capitol Forest area is near 47.0, -123.1.

The point is probably in hilly terrain, with elevation around 100-200 m. Near Littlerock, at 46.93, -123.0, the elevation is about 50 m. The Black Hills ridge at 47.0, -123.0 could be about 100 m. I'll estimate 110 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

10096.text:
The point (-23, -147) lies in the South Pacific, east of Tahiti and the Tuamotus and southeast of the Society Islands. The Tuamotu archipelago sits at roughly 14-23°S and 135-149°W, so the southern end of the chain is near this point. Its southeastern atolls, such as Marutea Sud, Morane and Maria, are farther east, and Tematangi is near 21.7°S, 140.7°W. Around 23°S, 147°W the nearest island is probably Tubuai or Rapa, but both are farther south. The Tuamotu plateau area is deep, and this location is likely open ocean.

Seafloor depth in this area is probably about 4000 m. The region lies west of the East Pacific Rise on seafloor of moderate age, and the Austral-Tuamotu area is probably around 3500-4500 m deep. I will estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

879.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 139.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands, which sit at about 75°N. It is in the Laptev Sea, near the shelf break. The shelf edge in this area is around 80°N at 139°E, and the continental slope runs down to the Nansen Basin. The Laptev shelf is wide, and the shelf break there lies at roughly 78–79°N, so 81°N is on the continental slope. I'd estimate the depth at about 2,000 m, but the figure is uncertain.

Checking against known bathymetry: the Laptev Sea slope at 130°E drops from 100 m at about 78°N to 3,000 m at about 81°N. The Gakkel Ridge axis lies near 81–82°N at 120–130°E. At 139°E and 81°N, the depth is probably 2,500–3,000 m. I'll go with -2700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

8962.text:
The point (-9.0, 105.0) lies in the Indian Ocean, south of Java and southwest of Christmas Island (which is at about -10.5, 105.7). It is also south of the Sunda Trench axis, which runs roughly along 10.5°S at 105°E, so I need to place it relative to the trench.

South of Java at 105°E, the trench is deep, around 6,000-7,000 m, and its axis sits near -10.5° latitude. The trench is shallower to the east and deeper near Christmas Island. At -9.0 the point is on the inner trench slope or the forearc basin, with depths of roughly 4,500-5,500 m. Java's coast at 105°E is about -6.8, and the shelf drops off to the forearc at about -8. I estimate the depth at -9.0 to be about 5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

12456.text:
Location: -49, -107 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile, south of the Easter Island region. It lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region? The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is at about -55 to -60 at that longitude, and the East Pacific Rise runs around -50 at about -115. At -49, -107 the seafloor is probably on the flank of the ridge, so the depth is roughly 2500-3500 m.

The Chile Rise runs from about -46, -76 southwest toward the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge junction near -55, -125, so the ridge is at about -52, -115. At -49, -107, we are about 600 km east of the ridge, and seafloor age of about 10 Myr gives a depth of about 3200 m. I'll estimate -3100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

6142.text:
The point at 21°N, 135°W is in the open North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and west of Baja California. It is far from any land, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain at roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m depth. The nearby seafloor is part of the Pacific plate at about 60-70 million years of age, which fits a depth near 4,800 m. I estimate -4,800 m from that general knowledge rather than from a specific lookup.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4800}

6627.text:
The point 17°N, 115°E lies in the South China Sea, east of the Paracel Islands (about 16.5°N, 112°E) and west-southwest of Macclesfield Bank (about 16°N, 114°E). The central basin lies farther east, and the abyssal plain is around 4000 m deep near 15°N, 116°E.

At 17°N, 115°E, the location is on the northern continental slope, southeast of Hainan and south of Pratas. The slope descends from the Xisha trough toward the basin. Macclesfield Bank spans roughly 15.5–16.5°N and 113.5–114.5°E. Since 115°E lies east of it, the depth there is probably about 3,000–4,000 m. The Xisha Trough runs roughly east-west at 16–17°N, 111–115°E, with depths near 2,000–3,500 m, so I estimate about -3,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

14233.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -153 is in the Ross Sea sector/Marie Byrd Land coast area. Marie Byrd Land coast lies near -75 to -77 at this longitude, so -69 at -153 is in the Amundsen Sea/Southern Ocean, well off the coast and north of the continental shelf. The Marie Byrd Land coast at 153°W is around 77°S, so 69°S is about 900 km offshore. This is deep ocean, likely abyssal plain or continental rise, with depth of roughly 4500 m.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further north, near 60°S at 150°W. At 69°S the seafloor is probably about 4,000-4,500 m deep, so I'll estimate -4300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1137.text:
Latitude 77.0 N, longitude -65.0 W is in northwest Greenland / Baffin Bay area. Thule (Pituffik) is at 76.5N, 68.7W. At 77N, 65W, that is inland, east of Thule on the Greenland ice sheet margin. Humboldt Glacier is at 79N. Around 77N, 65W the ice sheet edge sits near 68-69W, so 65W is on the ice sheet, roughly 30 km inland from the margin. Elevation is perhaps 800-1000 m.

Checking the coast: Wolstenholme Fjord and Inglefield Bredning lie around 77.5N, 66-70W. Inglefield Bredning is at 77.3N, 66W, so the point at 77.0N, 65W may be just south of it. The coast of Inglefield Bredning's southern shore is around 77.1N, and Melville Bay begins further south. The ice cap there (Hayes Peninsula, Steensby Land) is a plausible location. I'll go with Solid, about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

7918.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 177.0°E is in the central Pacific, near the Marshall Islands and Gilbert Islands (Kiribati). The Gilberts lie around 173°E, and the Marshalls are further north and west, so this point is open ocean. It sits south-east of the Marshalls, in the area between the Marshalls and the Phoenix Islands, and I take the seafloor there to be deep, around 5,000 m.

The nearby Nauru Basin and Ellice Basin are mostly 4,500–5,500 m deep. I'm estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup, so I'll choose -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

1738.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 57.0°E is in the Barents Sea region, near Novaya Zemlya. Novaya Zemlya's southern island spans roughly 70.5–76°N, with its western coast near 52–55°E at these latitudes. Kara Strait lies around 70°N, 58°E. At 71°N the island's southern part runs from about 52°E to 58°E. Matochkin Shar is at about 73.3°N, so 71°N is on the southern island, roughly Belushya Guba (71.5°N, 52.3°E). The island's east coast at 71°N is around 57°E (Kara Sea side), and the southern tip near Kara Strait is at about 70.5°N, 58°E. So 57°E, 71°N is probably near the east coast and could be either land or water.

The east coast near 71°N: Cape Mauritz? Kara Sea coast at about 56–57°E near 71°N, and the coast trends northeast. Around 71°N the east coast is near 57.5°E, I think. So this point is probably land, in the lowland near the coast, with an elevation of about 50 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

13976.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 53 E is off the coast of East Antarctica, in the Southern Ocean near Enderby Land / Kemp Land. The coastline there lies around 67° S to 67.5° S (Mawson Coast near 62° E, Kemp Land near 55° E at about 66.5° S). So -65 S, 53 E is open ocean on the continental shelf break or slope.

Bathymetry here: the shelf edge is about 66° S, and the continental rise lies north of it. At 65° S, 53° E, the seabed is probably on the continental slope or rise, at roughly -2000 to -3500 m. Off Enderby Land, the slope is steep and the shelf is narrow. The Kerguelen Plateau lies further north, and the Princess Elizabeth Trough sits around 62-64° S, 80° E, which is farther east. At 53° E, 65° S is probably around -3000 m, so I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

1850.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -79.0 is in Nunavut, Canada, on the Melville Peninsula and Foxe Basin side. The Gulf of Boothia lies near -85 to -88 longitude at around 70 N, and Committee Bay is near 68 N, -87 W. At -79 W, 69 N, Foxe Basin is to the east of the Melville Peninsula, which spans roughly -82 to -85 W. The Foxe Basin coast near Igloolik (69.4 N, -81.8 W) is nearby, so -79 W falls in the Foxe Basin waters east of Igloolik.

Foxe Basin is shallow, with typical depths of about 30–100 m. Northwest Foxe Basin near 69 N, -79 W is probably Water, with a seabed around -50 m. Baffin Island's western coast lies near -78 to -79 W around 68.5 N, so there is some chance this is land. The Baffin coast at Koukdjuak is at about 66 N, -73 W, and Steensby Inlet is at about 70 N, -78 W. The Baffin west coast near 69 N therefore sits around -80 to -78 W, which makes the point ambiguous. Foxe Basin north of 68 N has the Rowley and Koch islands in the east, and the Baffin Island coast along Foxe Basin runs from Hall Beach (68.8 N, -81.2 W) toward Steensby Inlet. Hall Beach is on the Melville Peninsula at -81.2 W, so -79 W is only about 2 degrees east, roughly 85 km, across Foxe Basin. The Baffin shore there is around -77 W, so the point is most likely in the water. I'll go with a depth of about -60 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

8119.text:
The point (-1.0, -141.0) is in the central equatorial Pacific, east of the Marquesas Islands and southwest of the Galápagos. The surface type is Water.

Seafloor depth in this region is typically about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The area lies west of the East Pacific Rise on crust roughly 30 to 40 million years old, which suggests a depth near 4,300 m. I'm estimating from regional knowledge rather than a specific lookup, so I'll use about -4,350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4350}

9688.text:
Latitude -17, longitude 117 is off the northwest coast of Australia. The Western Australian coast near Broome is at about 122°E, and the Pilbara coast near Port Hedland is at about 118.6°E, 20.3°S. So the point at 17°S, 117°E lies in the Indian Ocean on the continental shelf, northwest of the Kimberley/Pilbara coast.

The Rowley Shoals are at about 17.3°S, 119°E, so they lie to the east of this point. The point is near the shelf edge, in the Rowley Shelf and Exmouth Plateau region. At 117°E, 17°S, the bathymetry is probably the slope toward the Argo Abyssal Plain, or the outer shelf. The shelf edge in this area is near 118°E, so 117°E is probably on the slope at roughly 500 to 1000 m depth. The Rowley Shoals' seaward side drops quickly to over 1000 m, and the Argo Abyssal Plain is at about 5000 m. The Scott Plateau lies further north, near 14°S. I estimate the depth at about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

10002.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude 25.0 falls in northern Botswana, in the Kalahari. It lies south of the Makgadikgadi pans, which sit around -20.5 to -21 latitude and 25.5 longitude. The Makgadikgadi pans are at about 900 m, and the Kalahari near this point is about 950 to 1000 m. Lake Ngami is at -20.5, 22.9. The Boteti River runs near 24.5 east. At longitude 25.0, latitude -21.0, the area is probably sandy Kalahari at around 960 m.

I estimate about 950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":950}

970.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -39.0 is in northeast Greenland, on the Greenland ice sheet. Northeast Greenland's ice stream, Nioghalvfjerdsfjorden, sits near -20 longitude, and the Zachariae Isstrøm is near -21. At -39 longitude and 79 N the point is well inland, west of the coast, which is around -18 to -20 at that latitude. The EGRIP camp is at 75.6 N, -36 W with a surface elevation of about 2700 m. Summit at 72.6 N, -38.5 W is 3216 m. NGRIP at 75.1 N, -42.3 W is 2917 m. At 79 N, -39 W the surface is probably about 2400 m. The ice divide runs near -38 at this latitude, with elevation of about 2500 m.

I'll estimate 2450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2450}

1662.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -95.0 is in the Canadian Arctic. Boothia Peninsula spans roughly 70-72N and 94-96W. The northern part of Boothia is around 72N, and the Gulf of Boothia lies to the east at about 90W. At 71N, 95W, Boothia Peninsula's west coast borders Rae Strait and James Ross Strait near 96W, 69.5N. King William Island is at 69N, 98W.

So 71N, 95W is probably on the Boothia Peninsula, which is land. Its width at 71N runs from about 96.5W (the Franklin Strait coast) to 92W (the Gulf of Boothia). The west coast near 71N is at about 97W, at Cape Alexander and Bellot Strait (72N, 94.5W). So 95W at 71N is likely inland, with elevations of about 150 m. Inland Boothia has hills of 200-300 m, so I'll estimate 180 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":180}

440.text:
Location: 85°N, 19°W is in the Arctic Ocean north of Greenland. Greenland's northern coast (Peary Land) is at about 83.6°N, so 85°N is roughly 150 km north of the coast. This area lies near the Lomonosov Ridge/Morris Jesup Rise region. The Morris Jesup Rise is at about 85°N, 0-15°W, and it is a plateau with depths around 1000-1500 m. At 19°W and 85°N the position is between the Greenland continental shelf and the rise. The Greenland shelf north of Peary Land is narrow, and the slope drops to the Amundsen Basin at about 4000 m. The Morris Jesup Rise's western side is at about 20°W, with the Amundsen Basin further north and east. At 85°N, 19°W the depth is probably around 2500 m, on the slope between the rise and the Greenland margin.

I estimate the seabed at about -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

11838.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 97 lies in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It is east of the Ninetyeast Ridge (about 90°E), and the Wharton Basin lies to the north. At -41°, 97°E the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain south of the Broken Ridge area, with typical depths around 4,000 to 5,000 m.

Broken Ridge sits near 31°S, and the Southeast Indian Ridge near 41°S lies further east, around 80°E at 40°S (Amsterdam Island) and about 100°E at 47°S. At 97°E the ridge axis is around 45°S. The seafloor at 41°S is therefore young crust north of the ridge, with a depth of roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m. I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9520.text:
Latitude -15.0, longitude 141.0 lies in northern Queensland, Australia, on the Gulf of Carpentaria coastal plain. The Gulf coast near the Norman River and Karumba is around -17.5, 140.8. At -15, 141 the point is near the Mitchell River delta region, south of Aurukun and Kowanyama. Kowanyama is at about -15.5, 141.75. The coast at longitude 141 lies near -14.9 to -15.0, around the Staaten and Mitchell River mouths. The Mitchell delta enters at approximately -15.2, 141.6, so the coast at 141.0 is probably farther west, near -14.8 at the Archer Bay area.

Archer Bay is at about -13.4, 141.6. The coast runs from Aurukun (-13.35, 141.7) south to the Mitchell delta (-15.2, 141.6), and then southwest to Karumba (-17.5, 140.8). At -15, 141.0 the point is therefore offshore in the Gulf, in shallow water about 10 m deep. Water is the likelier type.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-12}

250.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -39° is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Greenland. Greenland's northern coast sits at about 83.6°N, so 87°N is about 380 km from land. The area lies near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from the New Siberian Islands to Ellesmere/Greenland and crosses near 87-88°N at longitudes of roughly 50°W to 140°E.

The Lomonosov Ridge is on the Greenland side near 80-90°W, but at the pole its position is about 140°E to 50°W. At 87°N, 39°W, this is likely on or near the ridge or the Amundsen Basin edge. Amundsen Basin depths are about 4200 m, and the ridge crest is around 1000-1500 m deep. The ridge on the Greenland side tends to sit at about 60°W at 85°N and shifts toward the pole, so at 87°N it should be near 50-60°W. Longitude 39°W would then be just east of the ridge, on the Amundsen Basin flank, so I estimate a depth of about 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

7131.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 43.0°E lies in Djibouti or near it. Djibouti's land spans roughly 41.8–43.4°E and 10.9–12.7°N. The Gulf of Tadjoura is at about 11.8°N, 43°E, so 11.0°N, 43.0°E is south of the gulf. This is near Lake Abbe? No, Lake Abbe is at 11.15°N, 41.8°E. The point is in the southern Djibouti region, close to the Ethiopian and Somaliland border. Somaliland's border with Djibouti runs about 11.0°N near 43.0°E, so the point is likely in the Danakil/Afar-to-Somaliland highlands. Elevation there is probably around 700 m.

Looking at the terrain more closely, the Gulf of Zeila coast is at about 11.3°N, 43.2°E. Inland at 11.0°N, 43.0°E the terrain is the Gabiley/Borama lowlands, roughly 600–900 m. Going back to the Djibouti region, Dikhil is at 11.1°N, 42.4°E at about 400 m, and the Goda mountains are further north. Moving east toward 43°E and southward from the coast, the elevation is probably around 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

9589.text:
The point at latitude -17.0, longitude -81.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, about 400 km west of the southern coast of Peru. That places it near the Peru-Chile Trench region, but still on the Nazca Plate, west of the trench axis.

The trench lies about 100-150 km off the coast in this area, so the point is well seaward of it. The abyssal plain there is typically 4,000-5,000 m deep. Near 17°S, 81°W the depth is probably around 4,500 m, so I estimate the seabed at about -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

13990.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 81 lies in East Antarctica, offshore of the Sabrina/Davis Sea region. Davis Station sits at 68.6S, 78E, and the Vestfold Hills are near 68.5S. At 65S, 81E we are about 400 km north of the coast, which puts the point in the Southern Ocean off the Princess Elizabeth Land coast.

The Kerguelen Plateau lies near 60S, 80E at roughly 50-80E, but the plateau's southern part (the Princess Elizabeth Trough) is around 64-65S. Depths in the Princess Elizabeth Trough are about 3000-4000 m, and I estimate about -3500 m at this point. The continental slope there is also near 65-66S, with the rise at 4000 m. I'll go with -3600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9113.text:
Latitude -11, longitude 47 is in the western Indian Ocean. Comoros lies at about -11.7, 43.3, and northern Madagascar's tip is at about -12, 49.3. Longitude 47 at latitude -11 is therefore in the Mozambique Channel's eastern side, between Comoros and Madagascar, north of Madagascar's coast near Mahajanga (-15.7, 46.3). The coast of Madagascar there is at about -14 to -15, so this point is open ocean.

The Comoros basin floor lies at roughly -3500 m. The area east of the Comoros toward Madagascar's northwest is about 3000 to 3500 m deep. I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

12832.text:
Latitude -53, longitude -75 lies in the Pacific Ocean off the coast of southern Chile. The Chilean coast near Wellington Island sits around -49 to -50 latitude at about -75.5 longitude, and the Taitao Peninsula is near -46.5. At -53 latitude the islands (Madre de Dios, Hanover and others) are around -74 to -75 longitude, so -75 could be slightly offshore or on the outer islands. Examples: Desolación Island is near -53.0, -74.1. The outer coast near -53, -74.8 includes small islands such as Vidal Gormaz and the Furias. So -75.0 is probably just offshore in the Pacific.

The Chile Trench sits further west, around -77 to -76 at this latitude, so the continental shelf edge should be near -75.5. The point is likely on the shelf or upper slope, with a depth of roughly -200 m to -1000 m. I estimate about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

1989.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude -161.0 W is in northwest Alaska. It lies in the Kobuk-Selawik lowlands, north of Kotzebue Sound and south of the Baird Mountains. The Kobuk River is around 67.0 N near Kiana and Ambler, with Kiana at about 66.97 N, 160.4 W. So this point is slightly west of Kiana, in the Kobuk valley near Squirrel River, and the terrain there is low hills at roughly 50-100 m.

The Squirrel River joins the Kobuk near 66.9 N, 160.4 W. At -161, 67.0 N, the point is north of the Kobuk, in the Waring Mountains area or the Selawik Hills, at perhaps 150 m. I'll estimate 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

12371.text:
Latitude -47, longitude 83 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Kerguelen (which is near 69E, -49) and southwest of Amsterdam Island (77E, -38). The location is on the Southeast Indian Ridge region or near the Indian-Antarctic basin. The Southeast Indian Ridge at longitude 83 is around -42 to -45 latitude, and the Kerguelen Plateau's eastern flank sits near 80E. Further south, around -47 and 83E, the seafloor is probably the Australian-Antarctic basin's western part, with depths of roughly 3500-4000 m. This is an estimate rather than a lookup.

The ridge crest is at about -3000 m, and the area south of the ridge is probably around -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5196.text:
Latitude 33.0, longitude 133.0 is just off the southern coast of Shikoku, Japan, near Cape Ashizuri. Cape Ashizuri sits at about 32.72°N, 133.02°E, so 33.0°N is about 31 km north of the cape. That puts the point inland in Tosa-Shimizu or Sukumo territory, or possibly near Tosa Bay.

Checking the nearby coast: Tosa-Shimizu is at about 32.78°N, 133.0°E. The coast near Ashizuri runs from Susaki (33.39°N, 133.28°E) down to Tosa-Shimizu. At 133.0°E the coast is around 32.78°N, so 33.0°N is inland, about 25 km north of the coast. This area is the Shimanto River region around Mihara and Kuroson, with Nakamura at 32.99°N, 132.93°E. The point at 133.0°E is just east of Nakamura, so it is land. Terrain there is hilly, roughly 200–400 m. I estimate about 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

13841.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 143 is in the Southern Ocean, north of Antarctica (the coast is at about -66.5 at that longitude). The area lies south of Australia, between the Antarctic margin and the Southeast Indian Ridge, which is at about -50 to -52 near 143E.

The Antarctic continental rise and abyssal plain at -63 should have depths of about 4000 m. The Australian-Antarctic Basin there is roughly 4000-4500 m deep. The continental slope sits at about -65 to -66, so -63 is probably on the rise or abyssal plain at around 4200 m depth. I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

10735.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 51 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, east of South Africa and southwest of Madagascar. It lies roughly in the Mozambique Basin or the Natal Valley area, so I need the seabed depth there.

Durban sits at about 31°E, so 51°E is about 20° further east. The Madagascar Basin lies east of the Madagascar Ridge, and the Madagascar Ridge runs along roughly 45–48°E. At -29° and 51°E the position is east of the ridge, in the Madagascar Basin, where depths are around 5000 m. This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup.


{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

1175.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 11.0°E lies west of Svalbard, in the Fram Strait. Prins Karls Forland is at about 78.5°N, 11°E, and Bellsund is near 77.5°N, 14°E. Spitsbergen's west coast at 77°N is around 14°E, so 11°E is open ocean, roughly 100 km offshore. This is the continental shelf break or slope area west of Spitsbergen. The shelf edge there is around 13°E, at depths of 200-300 m. At 11°E and 77°N, the seabed probably lies on the upper continental slope, about 1000-1500 m deep.

The Knipovich Ridge is near 77°N, 8°E, with depths of about 2500 m. The slope between the shelf and the ridge is steep. The shelf break near Bellsund is about 12°E at 77°N, so 11°E should be on the slope at roughly 1000 m depth. I'll estimate -1300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

6320.text:
The point 19°N, 139°W lies in the open North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and far from any land, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain or abyssal hill terrain on the Pacific plate. Crust of this age (roughly 60–80 Myr) at that location usually sits at about 4,500 to 5,000 m depth. Hawaii is at about 155°W, so this point is well east of the Hawaiian swell. The Clarion fracture zone is near 18–20°N at longitudes of about 120–130°W, and there are some features at 139°W, but I have no specific bathymetry for them. I'll estimate about -4,800 m, which is an estimate from typical depths rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

1966.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 153.0°E lies in northeastern Siberia (Sakha Republic). It is in the Yana-Indigirka lowland, near the Kolyma lowland to the east. The Indigirka river is at about 148°E and the Kolyma at about 161°E, so 153°E is between them, in the Alazeya plateau region. The Alazeya River flows around 152-154°E at about 69°N. The terrain there is lowland tundra, with elevations of about 50-100 m.

I'll estimate 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

6799.text:
The point 15.0°N, 99.0°E lies in western Thailand, in Kanchanaburi/Tak province near the Myanmar border. It is probably in the Thung Yai / Huai Kha Khaeng area or just south of it. Bhumibol Dam is at about 17.2°N, 99.0°E, so it is not close. The Khwae Noi river and Srinagarind reservoir are near 14.4°N, 99.1°E, which is also some distance away.

At 15.0°N, 99.0°E the location is about 60 km northwest of Srinagarind, in the mountains of Umphang/Thung Yai Naresuan or Mae Wong. Elevation there is likely 400-700 m. I'll estimate 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":500}

2402.text:
The point 63.0°N, 55.0°W lies in the Davis Strait / Labrador Sea region, west of Greenland. Greenland's southwestern coast near Paamiut (62°N, 49.7°W) is well to the east, and Baffin Island is far to the northwest. At 55°W and 63°N the point is open ocean in the Davis Strait south of the Davis Strait sill, which is around 65-66°N.

Depths there: the Greenland shelf extends out about 100-200 km from the coast at this latitude. Fylla Bank sits near 63.5°N, 52.5°W. At 55°W the point is probably beyond the shelf edge, on the continental slope, with a depth of about 1500-2500 m. The Labrador Sea basin lies further south, and the central Davis Strait at 63°N, 55°W is roughly 2000 m deep. I'll estimate -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

14120.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -19 is in the Weddell Sea, off Antarctica's Queen Maud Land coast. The Antarctic coast near 0° to -20° is around 70-71°S, so -67 at -19° is open ocean, northeast of the Riiser-Larsen Sea region and within the Weddell Sea basin.

Depths there are typically about 4000-5000 m. The Weddell Abyssal Plain lies at roughly 4800 m, and at 67°S, 19°W the seabed is probably near 4500 m. I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

6567.text:
Latitude 17.0, longitude -5.0 is in Mali, in the southern Sahel/Sahara transition. It lies near the Mauritania border, west of Timbuktu (which is at about 16.8N, -3.0), and north of the Niger inland delta region. The terrain there is probably sandy plain or dune fields, with an elevation of roughly 280 m.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup of the actual elevation data.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":280}

7898.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 137.0°E lies in the western Pacific Ocean, north of New Guinea (Papua) and southwest of Palau. The nearest land is the Papua coast near 1°S, 137°E, and this point is about 3° (roughly 330 km) north of that coast, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor there is probably the Caroline Basin or the area near the Eauriparik Rise (Caroline Ridge). The Caroline Basin is about 4000 m deep, and the Caroline Ridge near 3°N, 137°E is likely shallower. I don't have a precise bathymetry value for this exact point, so I am estimating about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

12677.text:
The point at 51°S, 25°W is in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Falkland Plateau. It is roughly north of South Georgia, which sits near 54°S, 36°W. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 51°S lies at about 14°W, so this point is on the western flank, in the Argentine Basin or the eastern part of the Georgia Basin. The seafloor there is probably 3,500 to 4,500 m deep.

The North Scotia Ridge is further south, and the Rio Grande Rise is much further north. The South American plate crust at 25°W is roughly 40 to 50 Myr old, which gives a depth of about 4,000 m. I'll take -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

13725.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -89 lies in the Bellingshausen Sea / Drake Passage region, west of the Antarctic Peninsula. The Peninsula tip is at about -63 latitude and -57 longitude, so -89 is far to the west, in open ocean.

The seafloor there is probably the deep abyssal plain or the continental rise. South of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Shackleton Fracture Zone, the area near -63, -89 is likely around 4000 m deep. The Bellingshausen abyssal plain is about 4000-4500 m deep, so I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4200}

9177.text:
Latitude -11, longitude 175 is in the South Pacific, east of the Solomon Islands and Santa Cruz Islands and west of Tuvalu/Tokelau. Tuvalu lies at about 176-179E and 6-10S, so Nikumaroro is not nearby. Nearest land would be Duff or Reef Islands near 166E, and Rotuma farther east, so this point is open ocean.

The area is the Ellice Basin, where depths are around 4,500 to 5,500 m. A typical value is around -5,000 m. I'll estimate -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

5993.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude -73.0 is in the Atlantic, east of the Bahamas. Mayaguana lies near 22.4N, 73.0W, and Acklins and Crooked Island are near 22.5N, 74W. At 23.0N, 73.0W the point sits just north of Mayaguana, probably on the Bahamas platform edge or in deeper water to the east.

Mayaguana's northern coast is around 22.45N. Further north there is deep water, since the Bahama Banks edge runs around there. The Caicos Platform is to the southeast. The area between Mayaguana and Rum Cay/San Salvador (24N, 74.5W) is a deep basin, with depths of around 4000 m or more, and the Atlantic's abyssal plain north of Hispaniola reaches about 5000 m. At 23N, 73W, I estimate a depth of roughly 3000–4500 m. I'll say about -4000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

9170.text:
Latitude -11.0, longitude 161.0 is in the Solomon Islands region. Guadalcanal lies around 160°E, 9.5°S, and Rennell Island is near 160.2°E, 11.6°S. Point (-11, 161) is therefore southeast of Guadalcanal and west of Santa Cruz, in the Coral Sea/Solomon Sea area, and appears to be open ocean. Nearby seafloor includes the Indispensable Strait and the area south of San Cristobal (Makira, at 162°E, 10.5°S). Makira's western tip is near 161.5°E, 10.4°S, so this point lies southwest of Makira in open water.

The seafloor there is probably the deep basin between Rennell and Makira. The South Solomon Trench lies north of Rennell, running roughly through 10.5°S at 161°E, with depths of 4000-5000 m. Depth at this point is plausibly about 3500-4500 m. I'll estimate -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2385.text:
Location: 63.0N, 89.0W is in Nunavut, near Hudson Bay's northwest. Chesterfield Inlet is at about 63.3N, 90.7W. Rankin Inlet is at 62.8N, 92.1W. At 89W, 63N, that is near the Wager Bay/Roes Welcome Sound area? Wager Bay is at about 65.5N, 88-90W. Repulse Bay is at 66.5N. Coast of Hudson Bay near 63N lies around 90W (Chesterfield Inlet's mouth at 63.3N, 90.7W). At 89W, 63N, that would be just east of the inlet mouth, on the coast or in the water. The coastline runs northeast from Chesterfield Inlet toward Whale Cove (62.2N, 92.6W)... actually the coast goes from Rankin Inlet northeast to Chesterfield, then northeast to Baker Lake? No. Past Chesterfield Inlet, the coast continues east to Cape Fullerton (63.9N, 88.8W), so the coast at 89W is at about 63.9N, and 63.0N, 89W would be in the water of Hudson Bay, with a depth of about 50 m near the coast. Southeast of Chesterfield, the water is shallow, around 30-60 m.

I'd say Water, about -45 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

5667.text:
The point (27.0°N, 5.0°W) is in southwestern Algeria, near the Morocco border, in the Tindouf basin / Hamada du Draa region. It is close to the Algerian-Mauritanian-Moroccan border area, north of Tindouf's latitude of about 27.67°N and at a longitude of -8.1°. At -5.0°, the point lies east of Tindouf, in the Erg Chegaga/Hamada region, probably in the Hamada du Draa or Erg Iguidi area. Elevation there is likely around 400 m.

Around 27°N, 5°W the area is the Hamada of Guir/Draa toward the Saoura. Elevation estimates are roughly 450 m. I'll go with 440.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":440}

8815.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude 171.0 is in the central Pacific. It lies south of Tarawa (1°N, 173°E) and near the Tokelau/Gilbert region. The nearest islands are Nikunau and Onotoa (about -1.8°, 175.5°E), Tamana, and Arorae (-2.6°, 176.8°E). The Tokelau atolls sit near -9°, -171.8° (that is, 188°E), so they are far away. Longitude 171°E at latitude -7° is west of Nukulaelae and Funafuti (-8.5°, 179°E), which puts it in open ocean between the Marshall Islands and the Ellice Islands. Nothing is nearby, so it is water.

Depth in this area is probably about 5000 m. The seafloor there is Pacific abyssal plain, with the Ellice Basin to the south at roughly 5500 m. At -7°, 171°E the depth is likely around 5000 to 5500 m. I'll estimate -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

12904.text:
Latitude -53, longitude 69 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, near the Kerguelen Plateau. Heard Island is at about -53.1, 73.5, and Kerguelen Island spans roughly -48.5 to -50 latitude and 68.5 to 70.5 longitude. So -53, 69 is south of Kerguelen Island, on the southern Kerguelen Plateau or its western flank.

The northern Kerguelen Plateau is about 500-1500 m deep. The southern part, near -53 and 69-75, is typically 1000-3000 m deep. At 69°E, the point sits west of the Heard Island plateau, and the Kerguelen-Heard area is generally 1000-2000 m deep. The plateau's western edge falls off into the Enderby basin or the Crozet basin. The region at -53, 69 is probably around 2000 m deep, perhaps slightly shallower, so I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

8480.text:
Latitude -5, longitude -139 is in the central Pacific Ocean, southeast of Hawaii and north of the Marquesas Islands. The Marquesas lie around 9-10°S, 139-140°W, so this point is about 5° north of them, in open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is typically abyssal plain at about 4,000-4,500 m depth. The Marquesas Fracture Zone region sits at around 4,000 m. I'll estimate about -4,300 m, which is a rough regional estimate rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

6033.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude 7.0 falls in southern Algeria, in the central Sahara. The Ahaggar (Hoggar) mountains lie around 23°N, 5.5°E, and the Tassili/Tefedest area is nearby. At 7°E, 23°N the terrain is probably the eastern edge of the Ahaggar massif or the plateau east of it, toward the Amadror basin.

The Amadror basin is around 24.5°N, 6.5°E at roughly 400-500 m. Further south at 23°N, 7°E I would expect the terrain to sit at about 700-900 m. The Ahaggar is high at 22-23°N, 5-6°E, with elevations of 1,500-2,000 m. East of it, near 7°E, the ground falls toward the Tanezrouft and Tin Seririne areas, around 800 m. I'll estimate 850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":850}

6409.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 39.0°E is in the Red Sea, off the Sudan coast. The Sudan coast near Sinkat/Tokar lies at about 37.5°E at this latitude, and Port Sudan (19.6°N) is at 37.2°E. So 39.0°E is well offshore, past the coast.

The Red Sea is roughly 200-300 km wide at 19°N, and the coast near 19°N is at about 37.3°E. The Saudi coast at 19°N is around 41°E (near Qunfudhah, at 19.1°N, 41.1°E). The axial trough runs through the middle, at about 38-39°E at this latitude. The main trough depth there is around 1000-2000 m. At 19°N the axial trough is deep, about 2000 m, and 39°E is probably near it. I'll estimate -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

1172.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 5.0°E lies in the Greenland Sea / Fram Strait region, southwest of Svalbard (Bear Island is at about 74.4°N, 19°E, so it is not nearby). The location is west of Spitsbergen, whose west coast is around 12–14°E.

At 77°N, 5°E, the seafloor is probably close to the Knipovich Ridge / Molloy area. The Knipovich Ridge runs from about 74°N, 8°E up to about 78°N, 8°E. At 77°N, 5°E the water is likely about 2,500 m deep, so I'll estimate the seabed at around -2,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2600}

3145.text:
The point 55.0°N, 9.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, northwest of Ireland. Donegal's coast is at about 8.3°W, and Malin Head is at 7.4°W, 55.4°N. At 9°W, 55°N, this is roughly 50 km west of the Donegal coast, off the northwest shelf.

The seabed there is probably around 100 m deep. The shelf west of Donegal is about 100-150 m deep at this longitude, and the shelf edge is farther west near 11-12°W. Depths in the area are about 80-120 m, so I'll estimate -110 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

6654.text:
Latitude 17.0°N, longitude 169.0°E is in the central Pacific. It lies west of the Marshall Islands and south of Wake Island. Wake is at 19.3°N, 166.6°E, and the Marshall Islands start around 170°E. The nearest atoll, Bikar, is at about 12°N, 170°E. A point at 17°N, 169°E is therefore open ocean in the Pacific plate region.

The seafloor in this area is Cretaceous crust, with depths of roughly 5000 to 5800 m in the basin. The Marshall Islands seamounts are nearby, but I have no specific seamount at this spot. I estimate the seabed at about -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

8860.text:
The point (-9.0, -99.0) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Peru and roughly 2,500 km offshore. It lies just west of the East Pacific Rise's northern portion, so the seafloor depth there is probably around 3,700-4,000 m.

The East Pacific Rise crest sits near 110°W at 9°S, and 99°W is about 11° east of it. At a half-spreading rate of about 70 km/Myr, that is about 1,200 km, so the crust is roughly 17 Myr old. The Nazca plate seafloor of that age lies at about 3,500 m depth. The Nazca plate sits at about 3,900 m in this region, so I'll estimate -3,850 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3850}

4813.text:
Latitude 37.0°N, longitude 87.0°E lies in the Qaidam/Altyn-Tagh region of China, in Qinghai or Xinjiang. The Altyn-Tagh range runs around 38-39°N at this longitude, so 37°N 87°E is probably in the Kunlun Mountains north of the Tibetan Plateau, near the Qaidam basin's western edge.

The Qaidam basin spans roughly 90-98°E, so 87°E is west of it. The area is likely the Kumkol basin or the Arkatag range in the Kunlun, with elevations of about 4000-5000 m. The Kumkol basin is around 36.5°N, 88°E at about 4600 m. At 37°N 87°E, the terrain is probably the northern Kunlun slopes, at about 4000 m. I'll estimate 4100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":4100}

9157.text:
Latitude -11.0, longitude 135.0 is in the Arafura Sea, north of Arnhem Land, Australia. Arnhem Land's northern coast is near -11.9 at Cape Wessel (136.8E) and Elcho Island lies at -12.0, 135.8E. The Wessel Islands extend to about -11.0 at 136.5E. At 135E, the coast sits near -12.0 (Cape Hotham area ~ -12.0, 131E; the Goulburn Islands at 133.4E). Point -11, 135 is therefore offshore, in the Arafura Sea, where depths are about 50 m.

The Arafura shelf is typically 50-60 m deep, and near the Wessel Islands it is somewhat shallower. I estimate -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

2696.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 173.0 E is in the western Bering Sea, east of Kamchatka and southeast of the Koryak coast. Kamchatka's east coast lies near 163 E at this latitude, and the Koryak coast near 61 N is around 165-170 E, so 173 E is offshore. The Gulf of Anadyr and Olyutorsky Bay sit at about 60 N, 170 E. The Navarin shelf is northeast of there, around 61 N, 177 E.

At 173 E, 61 N the position is probably still on the shelf edge or the upper slope. The Shirshov Ridge lies further south, at about 58 N, 170 E. The Bering Sea shelf break in this region runs from about 60 N, 175 E toward the northwest, so 61 N, 173 E may be on the shelf near the Navarin Canyon. Depths there are probably around 100-200 m, but I am not sure. The Koryak coast runs from Olyutorsky Bay at about 60 N, 170 E northward to Cape Navarin at 62.3 N, 179 E. Cape Navarin is at about 62.3 N, 179 E, so the coast trends northeast from Olyutorsky and 61 N, 173 E is probably in the Gulf of Anadyr/Koryak coast area. The coast at 173 E is probably near 61.5 N, so the point may be close to shore. I'll take a depth of about 100 m on the shelf.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

8966.text:
Latitude -9.0, longitude 113.0 is in the Indian Ocean, south of Java (Java's south coast is around -8.3 at this longitude). The Java Trench lies roughly 200-250 km south of the coast, with depths of 6000-7000 m near 10-11°S. At -9.0 we are about 80-100 km offshore, which should be on the lower slope or the forearc basin.

South of eastern Java, the trench axis is at about -10.5 S at 113 E, with depth near 6500 m. The shelf is narrow. At -9.0 the seabed is probably around 3000 m deep, possibly 2500 to 3500 m. I'll estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

1178.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 17.0°E lies in the Svalbard region. Spitsbergen's west coast is at about 14°E at 77°N, and Hornsund is at about 15.5°E, 77°N. At 17°E, 77°N, the point is on the east side of southern Spitsbergen, near Sørkapp Land and the area north of it. Hornsund fjord spans roughly 15–16.5°E at 77.0°N. Storfjorden lies east of Spitsbergen at about 19–20°E. Land at 17°E, 77°N is likely inland glaciated terrain, e.g. the area between Hornsund and Storfjorden, such as Torell Land, which has peaks and ice caps. The elevation is probably around 400 m.

Torell Land is at about 77.3°N, 16°E. At 77.0°N, 17°E the point is probably east of Hornsund's head (Brepollen is at about 15.0°E, 77.0°N). The area is ice-covered, likely at about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

7555.text:
Latitude 7.0 N, longitude 171.0 E is in the Marshall Islands. Ujae atoll sits near 8.9N 165.7E, Kwajalein near 9.2N 167.4E, Lae near 8.9N 166.3E, Wotho near 10N 166E, and Namu near 8.1N 168E. Farther east, Jabwot/Ailinglaplap is at about 7.3N 168.7E, Jaluit at 5.9N 169.6E, Namorik at 5.6N 168.1E, and Kili at 5.6N 169.1E. Mili is at 6.1N 171.9E and Knox (Narikrik) at 5.9N 171.1E. Arno is at 7.1N 171.6E, and Majuro at 7.1N 171.4E. Aur is at 8.3N 171.1E.

The point at 7.0N 171.0E lies about 50 km west of Majuro, so it is likely open ocean. The seafloor there is deep, roughly 4,800 to 5,000 m. The area between Ailinglaplap and Majuro is a deep basin of about 5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

8690.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude -79.0 is off the coast of northern Peru. Trujillo sits near -8.1, -79.0, and the coast at -7.0 lies around -79.4 (Chicama is at about -7.8, -79.4). At latitude -7, the coast is roughly at longitude -79.5 (Pacific side near Pacasmayo, -7.4, -79.57). So -79.0 at latitude -7.0 is inland, since the coast there is near -79.6 or further west.

The area sits between the coast and the Andes foothills, in the Chicama/Jequetepeque valleys. Cascas is at about -7.48, -78.8 and has an elevation of about 1,300 m. At -7.0, -79.0 the terrain is probably in the western Andes foothills, near Contumazá (-7.37, -78.8, about 2,400 m). The point is north of Contumazá, toward the Jequetepeque valley, where the elevation is perhaps 600–1,000 m. I'll estimate 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

2120.text:
The point 67.0°N, 101.0°E is in central Siberia, in the Putorana Plateau region or just south of it. Putorana lies around 68-69°N, 93-98°E. At 101°E and 67°N, this is the Tunguska/Kotuy area, near the northern edge of the Central Siberian Plateau. The Kotuy River basin lies at roughly 70-71°N, so this point is south of it. It is likely on the plateau at around 500 m, though it could be a river valley, since the Vilyui/Kochechum drainage is in this area.

The Kochechum River runs near 67°N, 100-101°E. The Tembenchi River is around 66°N, 99°E. The terrain here is plateau, with elevations of roughly 400-600 m. I'll estimate 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

3546.text:
The point 51.0°N, 73.0°E is in central Kazakhstan, in the Karaganda region, roughly 100 km north-northeast of Karaganda city and near the Nura River area. Nearby points are Temirtau at 50.05°N, 72.96°E and Astana at 51.1°N, 71.4°E. This part of the Kazakh Uplands is steppe at roughly 400–500 m elevation.

The Nura River flows northwest in this area, and Lake Korgalzhyn lies further west, so the point is probably not on a lake. Terrain near 51°N, 73°E, north of the Samarkand reservoir, is about 450 m. I'll answer Solid at about 440 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":440}

10780.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 141 is in far northeastern South Australia, right on the border with Queensland and New South Wales (141°E is the SA/NSW border). It lies in the Strzelecki Desert and Cooper Creek region, near Cameron Corner (-28.998, 141.0), where the elevation is roughly 100 m.

Cameron Corner's elevation is about 120 m, so I'll estimate 110 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 110}

12058.text:
The point is -43.0, 177.0, which lies east of New Zealand's South Island. It is south-east of Hawke's Bay and well east of Banks Peninsula (172.6E). The Chatham Rise sits around 44S, running east from New Zealand, and its crest is at about 400-500 m depth. At 43S, 177E the seafloor is on the northern flank of the Chatham Rise.

The Hikurangi Trough lies to the north-west, near 42S, 177E. Its depth is around 3000 m near 41S, 178E. The Chatham Rise crest is around 44S at depths of about 300-500 m, and the northern flank descends to about 3000 m toward the Hikurangi Plateau at 42S. At 43S the depth is probably around 1000 m, so I estimate -900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

7572.text:
The point 5°N, 155°W lies in the central Pacific Ocean, south of Hawaii and north of the equator, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain at roughly 4,800–5,300 m depth. The Line Islands ridge is farther west, near 160°W, so it is not a factor here. This region is part of the Pacific plate's abyssal area, where depths are typically around 5,000 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so this is an estimate from regional knowledge.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

15904.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -51 is in the interior of Antarctica, about 3 degrees from the South Pole. Longitude -51 (51°W) points toward the Weddell Sea sector, so this is on the East Antarctic plateau side near the Pensacola Mountains and Foundation Ice Stream. The Pole is at about 2835 m. Moving toward 51°W at 87°S, the surface lies in the Dome A–Pole region heading toward the Filchner area, with an elevation around 2700 m.

The Foundation Ice Stream is at about 83-85°S, 60°W. At 87°S, 51°W the ice surface should be about 2500-2700 m, so I estimate 2650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2650}

15687.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -125 is in West Antarctica, on the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge or the grounded ice just beyond it. The Ross Ice Shelf's edge near longitude -150 to -160 runs at about -78 latitude, and the Siple Coast area lies near -83 to -85 at longitudes -130 to -150. At -85, -125 I would expect to be south of the Ross Ice Shelf, in the Marie Byrd Land interior or the Transantarctic and Horlick area. That area is grounded ice at roughly 1500-2500 m elevation.

The ice shelf's southern grounding line near the Reedy Glacier is at about -85 and -130 to -140, where the ice is grounded. At -125 longitude and -85 latitude, the position is on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet near the Ross Embayment's edge, with an ice surface of about 1200-1800 m. I'll estimate 1700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1700}

7013.text:
Latitude 13.0°N, longitude 167.0°E is in the western Pacific, near the Marshall Islands. Kwajalein is at about 8.7°N, 167.7°E, and Wake Island is at about 19.3°N, 166.6°E. Bikar and Taongi are around 12°N, 170°E. Ujelang is at about 9.8°N, 160.9°E. Nothing I know of lies at 13°N, 167°E, so it is open ocean.

Seabed depth in that area is about 5,000 to 5,500 m, since the region northwest of the Marshall Islands lies on the Pacific plate's abyssal plain. The Marshall Islands seamounts sit on seafloor around 5,000 to 5,700 m deep. I'll estimate -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

3035.text:
Latitude 57.0°N, longitude 131.0°E lies in Russia's Khabarovsk Krai, north of the Sea of Okhotsk coast, in the Dzhugdzhur/Ket-Kap area. The Sea of Okhotsk coast near Ayan is at about 56.4°N, 138°E, so this point is well inland. It is probably in the Dzhugdzhur range's western flank or the Uda River basin, with elevation around 900 m.

The Maya River basin is to the west, and the Dzhugdzhur ridge runs near 57°N, 138°E. At 131°E, 57°N, the area is the upper Selemdzha/Tugur region, in the Stanovoy highlands' eastern part, probably in the Tokinsky Stanovik range or upper Zeya. Elevation there is roughly 1000 m, so I'll estimate 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

6485.text:
The point is at 17°N, 169°W, in the central North Pacific. It lies southwest of the Hawaiian chain, roughly 600 km southwest of Johnston Atoll (16.7°N, 169.5°W). Johnston Atoll is at about 169.5°W, so the point is about 50 km east of it, and Johnston sits on a seamount.

At 0.5° of longitude, the distance is about 53 km. The Johnston platform is a few tens of km across, so the point is probably off the platform on its flank or on the surrounding seafloor. Depth around Johnston is roughly 4,500–5,000 m, and the flank 50 km out would be about -4,800 m. I'll estimate -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1151.text:
Latitude 77.0, longitude -37.0 is in northeast Greenland. The Greenland Ice Sheet extends to about -20° to -22° longitude at this latitude, and the coast sits near -18° to -20°. So -37° is well inland, on the ice sheet.

The northeast Greenland ice stream begins near 77°N, 37°W. Ice surface elevation there is around 2,300 m. The summit of the ice sheet is near 72.5°N, 38°W at 3,200 m. The NEGIS onset is at about 75.6°N, 35.9°W, with surface elevation near 2,700 m. The NGRIP site at 75.1°N, 42.3°W is at 2,917 m. At 77°N, 37°W, the surface is probably about 2,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

1506.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -47.0 is on the Greenland ice sheet, in the interior of northwest Greenland. It lies east of Upernavik and inland. The ice sheet surface there is probably around 2,500 m.

For comparison, NEEM is at 77.45N, 51W with a surface elevation of about 2,450 m. Summit is at 72.6N, 38.5W with a surface elevation of 3,216 m. Moving west from Summit to 47W at 73N, the surface drops to roughly 2,500–2,700 m. I'll estimate 2,600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2600}

8089.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 159.0°E is in the western equatorial Pacific, north of the Solomon Islands and northeast of Papua New Guinea. The nearest land is Pohnpei, which is farther north and east, and Nukumanu and Takuu are to the south. I believe this point is open ocean in the Ontong Java Plateau region, which spans roughly 0°–5°S and 155°–170°E, so it is probably just north of the plateau.

The ocean floor north of the plateau, around the equator at 159°E, is probably about 4000 to 4500 m deep. The Caroline Basin lies to the northwest at about 4000 m. Going by that, I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

12285.text:
The point (-47, -89) lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. It is far from the coast, about 1,800 km west of Chile's coast at 47°S, which is near the Chile Rise/Triple Junction at 46.5°S, 75°W.

At 89°W the seafloor is on the Nazca/Antarctic plate. The Chile Trench is at about 75°W, and the Chile Rise runs west-southwest from the triple junction. At 47°S, 89°W the ridge segments are around 36°S to 46°S, with the Chile Rise at around 46°S, 80°W and then trending to the Pacific-Antarctic ridge near 55°S, 115°W. Depths near the rise axis are about 3000-3500 m, and in the area of 89°W, 47°S the seafloor is probably on the Antarctic plate, with age of about 15-20 Myr and a depth of around 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

3354.text:
Latitude 53.0, longitude 49.0 is in Russia's Samara Oblast, near Syzran and the Volga region. The Samara Reservoir (Saratov Reservoir) lies farther west, near 49.5°E to 48°E at about 53°N. The Volga near Syzran is around 48.5°E, 53.15°N, so 49.0°E is a bit east of the river.

That puts the point in the Zhiguli hills area or the Volga uplands. East of Syzran, around Chapayevsk (49.7°E, 52.98°N), the terrain is lowland on the Volga's left bank, with elevations of about 50-100 m. The Syzran–Samara railway runs through there. I estimate roughly 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

1675.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -69.0 is in northwest Greenland, near Melville Bay / Baffin Bay. At 71°N, 69°W, the Greenland coast is at roughly 76°N around Thule, so this point is not there. The west coast of Greenland at 71°N is around 52-55°W, so 69°W lies well to the west, in Baffin Bay.

Baffin Island's northern coast near Pond Inlet is at about 72.7°N, 77°W, and Clyde River is at 70.5°N, 68.5°W. So the point is near Clyde River on the east coast of Baffin Island. Clyde Inlet and Patricia Bay are around there. Clyde River sits on the coast at 70.47°N, 68.59°W, so 71.0°N, 69.0°W is about 55 km north-northwest of it, inland on the Baffin Island plateau or in the Barnes Ice Cap/Penny Highlands region. The coast runs northwest, with Scott Inlet around 71.0°N, 71°W. At 69°W the coast is probably near 70.6°N, so this point is likely land. The terrain there is the rugged Baffin Mountains, with elevations of about 500-1000 m, so I estimate 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

15328.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -123 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector near the Ross Ice Shelf edge. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°E to 155°W, with its eastern edge near Marie Byrd Land and the Edward VII Peninsula at about -155°W. At -123°W and -81° latitude, this is inland, in the Marie Byrd Land interior near the Thwaites/Pine Island divide, so likely grounded ice.

The West Antarctic Ice Sheet surface elevation around there is about 1000–1500 m. Because Marie Byrd Land's -81°, -123° is toward the Hudson Mountains and Pine Island's upper catchment, the elevation is probably around 1200 m, so I'll estimate 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

5719.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude 99.0 falls in northwest Yunnan, China, near the Jinsha (Yangtze) River area. The Lijiang and Jianchuan region lies around 26.5°N, 99.9°E, and Lanping is at about 26.45°N, 99.4°E. Weixi is at 27.2°N, 99.3°E. The Lancang (Mekong) River runs near 99.0–99.3°E at this latitude, and the Jinsha is further east near 99.7°E.

At 99.0°E, 27.0°N, the point is probably in the Nu-Lancang divide, the Biluo Snow Mountain or Yunling range. The Salween (Nu) River sits near 98.9°E at 27°N, with valley floor elevation around 1500 m. The Biluo Snow Mountain lies between the Nu and Mekong at about 99.0°E. The Mekong at 27°N is near 99.1°E, with valley elevation around 1800 m. This point is likely on the mountain ridge between them, so I estimate about 3500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3500}

13953.text:
Location: 65°S, 7°E is in the Southern Ocean, off Antarctica's Princess Astrid Coast / Maud Rise region. Maud Rise is near 65°S, 3°E, with a depth of about 2000 m at its top. At 7°E, 65°S, the seafloor is probably on the flank of Maud Rise or in the Weddell basin, where depths are about 3000-4000 m. The Antarctic coast at this longitude is around 70°S, so this point is open ocean.

Maud Rise is centered near 65°S, 3°E, and its summit is at about 2000 m. At 7°E the point sits on the eastern flank, so depth is roughly 3000 m. I estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

14789.text:
Location: -75°, -121° is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. Pine Island Bay lies near -74.5, -102, and Getz Ice Shelf is around -74.5, -125. At longitude -121 and latitude -75, this is probably the Getz Ice Shelf region, near its eastern part. The Getz Ice Shelf spans roughly -135 to -115 longitude, with its front at about -74 to -75 latitude, so -75 is likely within the ice shelf or just inland at the grounding line.

The ice shelf is floating, so the answer would be Water, with seabed depth below, probably around -600 m. Bathymetry under Getz is typically 500-1000 m deep, so I'll estimate -650.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -650}

14362.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 105E is in East Antarctica, inland of the Budd Coast/Wilkes Land coast. Vostok Station is at 78.5S, 106.8E. The coast near 105E is around 66.3S (Shackleton Ice Shelf is at ~96E, Denman Glacier ~99E, Vanderford ~109E). At 69S, 105E we are roughly 300 km inland, where the ice surface is probably about 2000-2500 m.

Along the Law Dome / Casey traverse, the elevation at 69S is approximately 2000 m. The Vanderford Glacier region is at 109E. At 105E, 69S, the surface is probably ~2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

6226.text:
The point 21.0°N, 33.0°E is in southeastern Egypt/northern Sudan, in the Eastern Desert near the Red Sea Hills. The Red Sea coast at that latitude is around 37°E, so this point is well inland and on land.

The Egyptian-Sudanese border at 22°N lies to the north. At 21°N, 33°E we are in Sudan, near the Nile and Lake Nasser's southern end. The Nile at that latitude is around 31°E, so the point is east of the river in the Nubian Desert. Elevation there is roughly 400 m, based on my recollection of the area rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

11579.text:
Latitude -39, longitude -61 is in Argentina, Buenos Aires province, near the coast. The coast near Bahía Blanca lies around -38.7, -62.3. At -61.0 the coast is near Monte Hermoso (-38.98, -61.3) and Pehuen-Co (-39.0, -61.55). Longitude -61.0 at lat -39 is therefore slightly inland, near Coronel Dorrego, Argentina. Monte Hermoso is at -61.3, and the coast runs northeast from there. At -61.0 the coast is around -38.85 (Balneario Sauce Grande is at -38.95, -61.15). So -39.0 is about 10 km inland, on land.

Elevation is probably about 50 m. Coronel Dorrego (-38.72, -61.28) sits at about 70 m. Inland dunes near the coast reach roughly 20-30 m, so I'll say 30.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":30}

5718.text:
Latitude 27.0°N, longitude 97.0°E lies in northern Myanmar (Kachin State), near the border with Arunachal Pradesh, India and Yunnan, China. The area is mountainous, close to the Patkai range and the Hkakabo Razi region, so it is solid ground.

Northern Kachin at 97°E is rugged, with valleys around 500–1500 m and ridges above 2000 m. The Mali Hka and N'Mai Hka rivers join farther west, at about 97.5°E and 25.7°N. At 27°N, 97°E the terrain is probably the Dulong/Irrawaddy headwaters region, with elevations around 1500–2500 m. I'll estimate 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

4893.text:
The point 35.0°N, 113.0°W is in northwestern Arizona. It lies near Seligman and Williams, a bit west of Seligman and south of Peach Springs. Seligman is at about 35.33°N, 112.88°W, at roughly 1,610 m. Peach Springs is at 35.53°N, 113.42°W, at about 1,400 m.

At 35.0°N, 113.0°W the terrain is the Aubrey Valley / Black Mountains area, south of Peach Springs. Aubrey Valley floor is about 1,300 m, and the Aquarius Mountains are to the south. The point is roughly 35 km southwest of Seligman, so it is probably in the southern Aubrey Valley or near Picacho Butte. Valley elevations there are around 1,300–1,400 m, so I estimate about 1,380 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1380}

8250.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 121.0 is in Sulawesi, Indonesia. Central Sulawesi lies near the Gulf of Tomini to the north and Poso to the northwest. At 1°S, 121°E the location is south of the Tomini Bay coast, which is at about 0.5°S. This is the eastern arm region near Ampana/Lake Poso area. Lake Poso is at about 1.9°S, 120.6°E, so it is not a match.

At 1°S the Tomini coast near Ampana is at about 0.9°S, 121.6°E. At 121.0°E the coast is around 0.75°S, which puts our point inland in the mountains, south of the coast. Elevation there is likely high, about 1000–2000 m. The area between Poso and Ampana, for example near Bancea or Tojo, is mountainous. I'll estimate 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

13705.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -129 is in the Southern Ocean (Pacific sector), northwest of the Antarctic Peninsula and well away from land. It lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or just south of it. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from 65°S, 175°W up toward 55°S, 140°W, so at -63, -129 we are probably east of the ridge on the abyssal plain, with a depth of around 3500-4000 m. The Antarctic Peninsula and the Amundsen Sea are far from this point, so the area is deep ocean.

I estimate the seabed at about -3900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3900}

12580.text:
The point -49.0, 141.0 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and southwest of Tasmania. It is south of the South Tasman Rise and about 700 km from the Tasmanian coast. The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins further south, and the Southeast Indian Ridge sits around -50 to -51 latitude near 141°E. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so the depth is an estimate from this regional setting.

At -49, 141 the seafloor is probably abyssal, around 3,500 to 4,500 m deep. The Southeast Indian Ridge axis near 140°E is at about -50 to -51, and the area north of it, around -49, is probably the flank of the ridge at roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m. The George V Fracture Zone and the Tasman Fracture Zone are also nearby. I will estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5942.text:
The point 23°N, 175°W lies in the North Pacific, north of the Hawaiian Islands. Kure Atoll is at about 28.4°N, 178.3°W and Midway at 28.2°N, 177.4°W, so this point is well south and east of both. It is in open ocean, in the region of the Northwest Hawaiian Ridge and the abyssal plain south of the Emperor/Hawaiian chain.

The seafloor there is probably about 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. The Hawaiian chain near 25°N, 170°W has seamounts, but this location is south of the ridge. The abyssal plain in this region is typically around 5,300 m, and I am estimating from that rather than from a specific bathymetry value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

6106.text:
Latitude 23°N, longitude 153°E is in the western North Pacific, east of the Bonin Islands (Ogasawara, about 27°N, 142°E) and north of the Marianas. That area is in the Philippine Sea/Pacific plate region, in the West Mariana Basin or Pacific abyssal plain.

At 153°E, 23°N, the Pacific plate has old seafloor at roughly 5500-6000 m depth. The Mariana Trench region is at 142-147°E, so this point lies east of the trench. There are seamounts in the area (the Magellan seamounts are near 150-155°E, 12-20°N, and the Wake seamounts are around 19°N, 166°E). At 23°N, 153°E the seafloor is probably abyssal plain at about -5800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

9904.text:
Latitude -21, longitude -171 is in the South Pacific, south of Niue (about -19, -169.9) and east of Tonga. Niue is at 19°S, 170°W, so this point lies roughly 120 km southwest of Niue, in open ocean.

The seafloor there is part of the Pacific basin near the Tonga Trench's eastern side. The Tonga Trench lies at about -173 to -175 longitude, so -171 is east of it. Depths of around 4,500 to 5,000 m are likely, though the Niue platform rises nearby. At -21, -171 I estimate about 4,800 m deep, possibly with a seamount.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

11828.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 77 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of the Amsterdam and St. Paul islands (which sit near 77.5°E, 38°S). Because this point is about 3° south of them, it is probably on the southern flank of the plateau or on the Amsterdam–St Paul plateau's edge.

The Southeast Indian Ridge crosses this region. The ridge passes near 78°E at about 38°S, and the St. Paul and Amsterdam plateau sits around 38°S. At 41°S, 77°E the seafloor is likely ridge flank, with depths around 3000 to 4000 m. The ridge axis there is shallow, around 2500 to 3000 m, and this point is likely near the ridge, so I estimate about -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

10077.text:
The point is at 21°S, 175°E. That is in the South Pacific, southeast of Fiji and southwest of Tonga, in the area of the Lau Basin and the Tonga Trench region.

Tonga Trench lies at about 175°W, which is 185°E, so 175°E is well to the west of it. Longitude 175°E sits between Fiji (178°E) and New Caledonia (165°E). The seafloor there is in the South Fiji Basin or the Lau Basin margin. The Lau Basin is at about 177–178°W and 18–22°S, and the South Fiji Basin is at about 175–178°E, 25°S. At 21°S, 175°E the depth is probably about 2500–3000 m, near the Three Kings Ridge or the Lau-Colville ridge. The Colville Ridge is at about 179°E, and the Three Kings Ridge is at about 173°E and 31°S. Around 21°S, 175°E there is the Fiji plateau edge or the southern Lau/North Fiji Basin. I estimate a depth of about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

10390.text:
Latitude -25, longitude 81 lies in the southeastern Indian Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of Australia and south of the equator. It is east of Madagascar and the Mascarene Plateau and south of the Central Indian Basin. The Ninety East Ridge is near 90°E, so 81°E is west of it. The Central Indian Basin has depths of about 5,000 to 5,500 m, and at 25°S, 81°E the seafloor is likely about 5,000 m deep.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup of bathymetry data.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

12979.text:
Location: -55, -141 is in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and west of Chile, on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge area? The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is near -55 at about longitude -140 (the ridge runs from about -65,-170 up to -55,-140, and then to -40,-110). So the ridge crest is near here at depth of about 2,500-3,000 m. Elevation is roughly -2800 m.

Looking further, at 55°S the ridge axis is around 140°W; the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crest there sits at about 2,300-2,700 m depth. I'll estimate -2700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

15471.text:
Location: -81.0 latitude, 163.0 E longitude is in Antarctica, in the Ross Sea sector. The Ross Ice Shelf front is around -77.5, and the Transantarctic Mountains lie near 160-165E at roughly -80 to -85. At -81, 163E, this is probably the Darwin/Hatherton glacier region or the Mulock Glacier area. The Mulock Glacier is near -79.5, 161E, and the Skelton Glacier is near -78. The Darwin Glacier is at about -79.9, 159E, and the Hatherton at -79.9, 157E. At -81, 163E, the Byrd Glacier is near -80.3, 160E, so this point is just east of the Byrd Glacier mouth. It would be along the Ross Ice Shelf edge, where the shelf meets the mountains. The Ross Ice Shelf edge at 163E is probably around -80.5 to -81. Possibly this is on the floating shelf, with the seabed at roughly -700 m. Alternatively it could be the mountain area of the Churchill Mountains, but those lie further west.

I'll guess floating ice shelf with a seabed around -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

15316.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -147 lies in West Antarctica, in the Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°W to 160°E and extends to about 78-85°S. At longitude -147 (147°W), latitude -81 is within the eastern part of the Ross Ice Shelf, near the Marie Byrd Land coast. The shelf edge on the eastern side is near Edward VII Peninsula (~155°W), and the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern boundary is around Siple Coast (~150°W-140°W at 81-82°S). At 147°W and 81°S the area is likely the floating ice shelf, so the surface type is Water and I need the seabed depth.

Bedmap data for the Ross Ice Shelf: seafloor depths of about -500 to -600 m in the eastern part. Near the Siple Coast, the sea floor is around -600 m. At 81°S, 147°W, this is in the Ross Embayment's eastern region, near Roosevelt Island (about 79.5°S, 162°W). Bathymetry there is roughly -400 to -700 m. I'll estimate -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

2627.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 35.0 E is in Russia, in the Republic of Karelia near Lake Onega. Lake Onega spans roughly 60.9–62.9 N and 34.5–36.5 E. Its southern part is around 61.0–61.5 N, with the southeastern shore near 35.5 E, and Svir River mouth at about 60.5 N, 33.5 E. The lake's southern end, Vonga bay, sits around 61.0 N, 35.5 E.

The western shore of the south lake runs near 34.5–35.0 E at 61.0 N. The Svir river leaves from Svirskaya Bay at about 60.9 N, 34.5 E. Therefore 61.0 N, 35.0 E could be water in the southern part of the lake, in the Bolshoye Onego area. The Andoma area is at 61.6 N, 36.4 E, and Vytegra is at 61.0 N, 36.45 E. The southern shore between Vytegra and the Svir mouth runs at about 61.0 N from 36.4 E to the southwest. At 35.0 E the southern shore is probably around 61.2 N, so 61.0 N would be land, not water. Pondering: Kizhi is at 62.07 N, 35.2 E. The lake's southern shore near 35 E is around 61.3 N (Lake Onega's south-west Lizhma area, around 61.5 N, 35.5 E?). The Svir bay's southern end at 60.9 N, 34.6 E suggests the lake extends down to 61 N at 35 E in the west, though the shore east of the bay is at about 61.2 N. Overall it is uncertain, and the point is near the southwestern shore, possibly in Svirskaya Bay's northern part. I'd lean toward land, with an elevation of about 90 m (the lake level is 33 m).

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

13588.text:
Location: 61°S, 3°W is in the Southern Ocean, in the Weddell Sea region northeast of the South Sandwich area, and west of Bouvet Island (54°S, 3°E). It lies south of the South Atlantic and near the American-Antarctic Ridge and the Southwest Indian Ridge junction, though the exact region is the Weddell Basin/Enderby area.

The Weddell Abyssal Plain lies further south and west. At 61°S, 3°W the seafloor is likely about 3,500 to 4,500 m deep. The Bouvet Triple Junction is at 54°S, 2°E, and the Southwest Indian Ridge extends northeast of there. The American-Antarctic Ridge runs westward from the junction at about 55-60°S. At 61°S, 3°W we are probably south of the ridge in the Weddell basin, with depths around -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7209.text:
The point 9°N, 161°W lies in the central Pacific, south of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands. It is open ocean, and I'm estimating the seabed depth from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup.

The seafloor in this area is Cretaceous-age crust, typically around 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. Nearby, the Line Islands ridge lies further west, while the Clipperton fracture zone area to the east sits at about 5,000 m or deeper. I'll estimate about -5,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

2008.text:
The point 67.0°N, 123.0°W is in the Northwest Territories, Canada. It lies west of Great Bear Lake and east of the Mackenzie River, near the Anderson River and Colville Hills area. Fort Good Hope sits at about 66.26°N, 128.6°W, so this point is well east of it. Norman Wells is at 65.28°N, 126.8°W. The point is closer to the Hornaday and Horton River region, so it is probably in the plains of the Great Bear Plain with elevations of roughly 300 to 450 m.

Great Bear Lake's western edge is around 121°W at 66°N, with McVicar Arm reaching about 66.0°N, 123°W. At 67°N, 123°W the point is probably land, in the hills between the Great Bear River and the Anderson River. Elevation is about 350 m. Lakes such as Tunago and Sloan are possible nearby, but land is the more likely surface.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":340}

13597.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 15 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Africa-Antarctica region. It lies near the Southwest Indian Ridge, or just south of it, in the Atlantic-Indian basin. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (about 54°S, 1°E) northeast to the Indian Ocean, so at 61°S it would be south of the ridge. The area around 60°S, 15°E is likely the Enderby Basin or the Weddell-Enderby Basin margin, with the Maud Rise further south at about 65°S, 3°E. The Africa-Antarctic Ridge is at around 55-60°S near 0-20°E, and the American-Antarctic Ridge lies to the west.

Depths there are probably around 3000-4500 m. I'll guess -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

3680.text:
The point 49°N, 19°W is in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and west of Ireland. It lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, whose axis is near 30-35°W at this latitude, so the point is in the western part of the West European Basin or the Irish/Iceland basin area. Seafloor depth there is likely about 3,500-4,000 m.

Nearby features support this. The Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone is near 52°N, 30°W. At 49°N, 19°W the seafloor is probably abyssal plain, and it could be slightly shallower on the flank of the Azores-Biscay Rise, which sits around 45°N, 20°W. I'll estimate about -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

2036.text:
Location: 67°N, 67°W is in Baffin Island, Nunavut, Canada. Cumberland Peninsula is around 66-67°N, 63-68°W. Cumberland Sound is at about 65°N, 66°W. At 67°N, 67°W, this is north of Cumberland Sound, near Penny Ice Cap, which sits around 67°N, 66°W. The Penny Ice Cap is at elevations up to ~1900 m. Around 67.0, -67.0 is the western edge of the Penny Ice Cap or the area near Pangnirtung Pass (66.1°N, 65°W) - so it is further north. Land on the Cumberland Peninsula at this location probably has an elevation of roughly 800-1000 m. The west side of the peninsula has Nettilling Fiord and the Foxe Basin lowlands to the west, but the Penny Ice Cap's western margin is near 67°N, 67°W, where the elevation is around 1000 m.

I'll say Solid, 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

3479.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude -61.0 lies in the Gulf of St. Lawrence, on the Quebec Lower North Shore. The coast there sits near 50.2°N at -61°, with Natashquan at about 50.2°N, -61.8°. Moving north from the Gulf, land begins at roughly 50.2°N, so 51.0°N is about 90 km inland. That area is the Canadian Shield interior in Quebec, with lakes and an elevation of roughly 300-400 m.

Checking the coast further: Harrington Harbour is at 50.5°N, -59.5°. Near -61°, the coast is around 50.25°N. So 51.0°N is inland, in the Petit Mécatina river region, with elevation around 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":380}

10469.text:
The point (-27, -121) is in the South Pacific, east of Easter Island (which sits near -109.4) and west of the Chile coast. It lies on the southeastern Pacific basin, near the East Pacific Rise flank and the Easter Microplate region. The East Pacific Rise crest is around -112 at this latitude, so -121 is on the Pacific plate flank, roughly 600-900 km west of the crest.

Seafloor age there is about 10-15 Myr. Depth is roughly 2500 + 350*sqrt(age), which gives about 3600-3900 m. But the area around Pitcairn and the Easter hotspot chain is shallower. The region near -27, -121 is on the Pacific plate, close to the Ahu and Orongo seamounts, but the depth is likely around 3700 m. I'll estimate -3700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

6904.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude -51.0 is in the western tropical Atlantic, roughly 700 km or more off the coast of French Guiana and northern Brazil. The Demerara Abyssal Plain lies in this region, with typical depths of about 4,800 to 5,000 m. The Ceará Rise sits further southeast, and the Demerara Plateau is closer to the coast near 7°N, 54°W, so neither should affect this point.

The surface type is Water. I estimated the seabed depth from typical Demerara Abyssal Plain values rather than a lookup, so the figure is approximate. At this point I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

4059.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude 19.0 falls in the Srem region of Serbia/Croatia. The Sava river is near 44.95°N at about 19.0°E, and the Fruška Gora mountain is further north at roughly 45.15°N. Sremska Mitrovica sits at about 44.97°N, 19.61°E. At 19.0°E the Sava flows near Bosut/Jamena, and the Croatia–Serbia border lies around 19.0°E, 45.0°N. This is flat floodplain, probably near Morović or Bosut forest, with an elevation of about 80 m.

The Sava at this longitude is around 44.9°N (near Šabac, 19.7°E, 44.75°N; Brčko, 18.8°E, 44.87°N). At 45.0°N, 19.0°E the point is north of the Sava, in the Spačva/Bosut lowlands. Ground elevation there is roughly 80 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":82}

8700.text:
The point (-7.0, -59.0) is in the Brazilian Amazon, in southern Amazonas state near the Pará border. It is probably in the Madeira–Tapajós interfluve, on the Transamazon Highway (BR-230) side. Elevation there is likely around 100 m.

Nearby rivers: the Madeira lies at about -59.5 to -60 near Nova Olinda do Norte. At -7, -59 the point is in the Aripuanã/Juruena basin to the south, and the Tapajós is further east at about -55. So the point is on land, with terrain around 90-120 m. I'll estimate 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

8994.text:
The point is at 9°S, 169°E, in the southwest Pacific. The Santa Cruz Islands (Solomon Islands, Temotu) lie around 10.7°S, 166°E, and Vanuatu starts further south near 13°S, so there is no land near this location. It sits east of the Solomon Islands and north of the Santa Cruz group, in the Solomon Sea / Coral Sea region, probably over open ocean on the Pacific plate.

The North Fiji Basin is further southeast. The seafloor here is probably around 4000 to 4500 m deep. The area lies west of the Vanuatu trench, which begins near 13°S, so the depth is likely ordinary basin floor. I estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14101.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -57 is on the northeastern Antarctic Peninsula's east side, in the Weddell Sea region. The Antarctic Peninsula at 67°S spans roughly 62-70°W on the west side. At 57°W, 67°S, this is east of the peninsula, near the Larsen C ice shelf area. Larsen C extends from about 66° to 69°S, with its eastern edge near 60°W or so. At 57°W, it is probably open Weddell Sea, or possibly the former Larsen B/C. The seabed there is likely a few hundred meters deep. The Weddell Sea continental shelf off Larsen C is about 400-600 m deep.

I'll classify it as water with a seabed elevation of about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

6786.text:
The point 15.0°N, 73.0°E lies in the Arabian Sea, west of India's Goa and Karnataka coast. The coast at this latitude is near 74.1°E, so the point is roughly 120 km offshore, on the continental shelf or slope. The shelf edge off Goa is at about 200 m depth, and the shelf is about 100 km wide here, so the point is near the shelf edge. Farther west the seabed drops toward 2000 m or more, and the Laccadive-Chagos ridge region lies to the south.

At 15°N the shelf break is around 72.5°E, so at 73.0°E the depth is probably about 100 m. This is an estimate from the shelf geometry, not a lookup of the actual bathymetry. The shelf off Goa is roughly 80 to 100 km wide, and the coast at 15°N is near 73.8°E. The point is therefore about 80 km offshore, which puts it on the outer shelf at a depth of about 80 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-85}

13314.text:
The point at 57°S, 169°E lies in the Southern Ocean, south of New Zealand. Campbell Island is at about 52.5°S, 169°E, and the Macquarie Ridge runs to the southwest near 160°E, so this point is well east of the ridge. The Campbell Plateau extends south to roughly 55°S. South of that, the seafloor drops toward the Southeast Indian/Pacific-Antarctic Ridge basin.

At 57°S, 169°E the seafloor is probably on the southern slope of the plateau or in the deeper basin. The Emerald Basin is at about 59°S, 160°E, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies near 170°E at about 62°S. Depths near 57°S, 169°E are likely around 3,500 to 4,500 m. I'll estimate about -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

12651.text:
Latitude -51, longitude -77 is in the Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. The Chilean coast at this latitude is around -75 longitude (Wellington Island, about -75.5), so -77 lies about 100 km offshore.

The Chile Trench lies further north, and near the Chile Triple Junction (~46.5°S) it is shallower. At 51°S the trench is filled with sediment and the Nazca/Antarctic plate subducts, so the depth is probably around 4000 m. The trench axis at 51°S is probably near -76 to -77 longitude. Depths there are likely 3500–4500 m, and I'll estimate about -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

6450.text:
Latitude 19.0 N, longitude 121.0 E lies in the Luzon Strait, just north of Luzon in the Babuyan Islands area. Calayan Island is near 19.3 N, 121.5 E, and Camiguin Island is near 18.9 N, 121.9 E. At 121.0 E the point is west of these islands, in the Babuyan Channel or the Luzon Strait.

Northwestern Luzon's coast at Cape Bojeador is near 18.5 N, 120.6 E. Longitude 121.0 at 19.0 N is therefore about 50 km offshore, north of Luzon's northern coast near Pasuquin and Claveria (18.6 N, 121.0 E). The sea there is part of the Balintang and Babuyan channels, with depths of roughly 500 to 1000 m. The Bashi Channel to the north is about 2000 m deep. Between Luzon and the Babuyan Islands the Babuyan Channel is deep, around 1000 m or more. West of Calayan the seabed is probably near 800 m deep. I'll estimate -900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

1001.text:
Latitude 79.0N, longitude 23.0E is in Svalbard. Nordaustlandet lies roughly between 79-80.5N and 17-27E, and its southern coast is near 79.5N at longitude 23E. Hinlopen Strait lies at about 79.5N, 19E. Lomfjordhalvøya and Wahlenbergfjorden are around 79.3N, 20E. The eastern part of Nordaustlandet near 23E has Palanderbukta at 79.5N, 22E. Storfjorden and Barents Sea lie south of 78.5N.

At 79.0N, 23E, the point is south of Nordaustlandet's southern coast. Kvitøya is further east. The area between Nordaustlandet and Edgeøya (78N) is open water in Freemansundet/Storfjorden. The southern coast of Nordaustlandet at 23E is about 79.6N (Duvefjorden is at 80N, 22E). Therefore 79.0N is in the sea, the northern Barents Sea, with depth around 200 m. East of Spitsbergen at 79N, 23E, the depth is perhaps around 200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-200}

10380.text:
The point is at 25°S, 61°E in the Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and south of the Mascarene Islands. This area lies near the Mascarene Basin and the Southwest Indian Ridge region. The Central Indian Ridge sits near 65–70°E at this latitude, so 61°E is west of the ridge. The Mascarene Plateau runs north of about 20°S, so this point is south of it. The surrounding seafloor is likely abyssal plain at roughly 4,500–5,000 m depth. I'm estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry, not from a data lookup.

The Madagascar Basin has depths of about 5,000 m near 25°S, 55°E. Moving east toward the Central Indian Ridge, the depth shoals to about 4,000 m. At 61°E I estimate about 4,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

6995.text:
Latitude 13.0°N, longitude 131.0°E lies in the western Pacific Ocean, east of the Philippines. It is north of Palau and Halmahera and west of the Mariana region. This area is within the Philippine Sea, near the Philippine Sea Plate's West Philippine Basin, where depths are typically around 5000 to 6000 m.

The West Philippine Basin sits at about 5500 m depth. At 13°N, 131°E, the point is near the Palau-Kyushu Ridge, which runs roughly along 134°E, so it lies west of the ridge in the basin. The Central Basin Fault and Central Basin Spreading Ridge run through the basin. Typical depth there is about 5000 to 5800 m, so I estimate about -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

7750.text:
The point is at 3°N, 159°W, in the central Pacific south of Hawaii. It lies north of Teraina (Washington Island) at about 4.7°N, 160.4°W and Kiritimati at 1.9°N, 157.4°W, and it is not on either island. It is open ocean.

The seafloor here is in the Line Islands region, which is generally 4,500–5,500 m deep. Just north of the equator the seafloor is about 5,000 m deep, so I estimate about -5100 m. This is an estimate from regional depths, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5150}

2596.text:
The point 61°N, 27°W is in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland and southwest of Iceland. It lies just west of the Reykjanes Ridge, whose crest runs near 30°W at this latitude, so it sits in the Irminger Basin.

Depths in the Irminger Basin are typically 2500–3000 m. The Reykjanes Ridge axis is near 1000–1500 m deep at about 60°N, 28–30°W. At 27°W the point is on the ridge's eastern flank, where the seafloor is probably about 2000 m deep. The ridge axis at 61°N is near 28.5°W, so 27°W is about 1.5° east of it, roughly 80 km. Ridge flank depths at that distance are about 1800–2000 m. I'll estimate 1900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

13006.text:
The point (-55, -87) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile and roughly 1,500 km from the coast. It lies south of the Chile Rise and the Chile Triple Junction, which is near 46°S, so it sits on the Antarctic plate or the Nazca plate boundary area. The Chile Rise runs along roughly 55°S, 80–90°W, where the Chile Rise spreading segments meet the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, and the Hero and Chile fracture zones are nearby.

At 55°S, 87°W, the seafloor is probably about 3,500–4,000 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's eastern end reaches the Chile Rise near 60°S, 70°W, and the Chile Rise segments sit near 55–56°S, 90°W. The seafloor near the ridge could be around 3,000 to 3,500 m deep, so I estimate -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

4340.text:
The point 41.0°N, 139.0°W lies in the northeast Pacific, roughly 700 km or more west of Cape Mendocino (about 124.4°W). That places it over the abyssal plain, west of the Gorda Ridge and the Mendocino Fracture Zone region. The Mendocino Fracture Zone runs along about 40.4°N, so this point is just north of it.

Seafloor depth in this area is typically about 4,500 to 5,000 m. The crust is roughly 30 million years old or more, and the depth there is around 4,800 m. I'll estimate -4,750 m from that general knowledge, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4750}

15301.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -177 lies in the Ross Ice Shelf, which floats, so the surface type is Water. The elevation to report is the seabed beneath the ice shelf.

The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 78°S to 85°S, and at 81°S, 177°W it is near the central-eastern part of the shelf. Seabed depths under the Ross Ice Shelf are typically 400–800 m below sea level. Bedmap2 puts the bed at about -600 m in the central shelf, and around 180°W the Ross Sea floor is somewhat deeper. I estimate about -580 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-580}

12633.text:
Latitude -51, longitude -113 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. The location is far from land, south of the East Pacific Rise and in the southeast Pacific basin.

The East Pacific Rise at 51°S lies around 115°W, so this point is near the ridge crest. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge sits near 55°S, 140°W, and further north the East Pacific Rise at 40°S is around 110°W. At 51°S the rise is probably near 114°W, so the depth would be about 2500-3000 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge trends northeast from the Pacific-Antarctic junction near 62°S, 150°W, reaching about 51°S, 114°W. That puts this location on or near the ridge axis, where depths are around 2300-2800 m. I'll estimate -2600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2650}

12239.text:
The point is at 45°S, 179°E, which is in the Pacific Ocean east of New Zealand's South Island. It lies on the Chatham Rise region, which sits around 44°S. Chatham Islands are at about 44°S, 176.5°W. At 179°E, 45°S the Chatham Rise is shallow, with depths of roughly 400–1000 m. The rise's crest sits near 42-44°S. At 45°S it is on the southern flank, so I estimate about -800 m.

The Bounty Trough lies south of the rise at around 45-46°S, with depths of 4000+ m to the west and about 3000 m near 180°. At 179°E, 45°S, I'd guess the point is on the southern flank of the rise, roughly -1000 m, but it could be deeper. I'll go with -900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

2960.text:
The point 57°N, 19°W is in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and west of Rockall. It lies near the Iceland Basin, west of the Hatton-Rockall area and east of the Reykjanes Ridge. The Reykjanes Ridge at 57°N sits around 30-33°W, so this point is well to the east of it.

The Iceland Basin floor in this area is about 2800-3000 m deep. Rockall Plateau is at roughly 13-15°W, and Hatton Bank is around 18-20°W at 57-58°N. Hatton Bank is centered near 58-59°N, 19-20°W, with depths of 1000-1500 m on its top. At 57°N, 19°W the point is probably on the southern flank of Hatton Bank or in the basin between Hatton and Edoras Bank. Depth there is likely about 1500-2500 m. I'll estimate 1900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

3469.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude -81.0 is at the southern end of James Bay, Ontario. Akimiski Island sits around 53N, 81W, so 51N is south of it. The coast of James Bay near Moosonee lies at about 51.3N, 80.6W, and Charlton Island is at about 52N, 79.5W. The point at 51.0N, 81.0W is west of Moosonee, so it is probably in the bay, just offshore from the Ontario coast near the Albany River mouth, which is at about 52.2N. The coast near Ekwan Point runs from about 53N down to Moosonee at 51.3N, then turns east along the south shore of James Bay at about 51.3N. At longitude -81, the coast is therefore around 51.3N to 51.5N, which puts 51.0N inland on the Hudson Bay Lowlands.

Elevation there is low, roughly 10 to 30 m, and probably near 20 m. The point is about 50 km inland of the coast, and the lowlands rise about 0.5 m/km, which gives roughly 20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":20}

9614.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -31 is in the South Atlantic, east of Brazil (Brazil's coast is near -39). It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is around -13 at this latitude. The location is therefore on the western flank of the ridge, in the Brazil Basin or the Brazil Basin's northern part.

Around 17°S, the ridge axis is near 13°W. At 31°W, the crust is about 18° of longitude away, or about 2000 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 35 mm/yr, that is roughly 57 Myr old. The depth would be about 2500 + 350·√57 ≈ 5100 m. The Rio Grande Rise is farther south, near 30°S, so it does not affect this point. Depths in that area are roughly 4500–5000 m, so I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

14642.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -55 is in the Weddell Sea, in the area of the Ronne-Filchner Ice Shelf. The Filchner Ice Shelf front is around -77 to -78 and the Ronne Ice Shelf covers roughly -75 to -83 at longitudes -60 to -75. Near -73, -55 the point is probably open Weddell Sea, off the Antarctic Peninsula's eastern side. The Larsen C ice shelf is at about -67 to -69 and longitude -62, so it is not relevant here.

The area at -73, -55 is probably in the southern Weddell Sea continental shelf, east of Berkner Island (which sits at about -79). The shelf there is perhaps 500 m deep or more. The Ronne Ice Shelf front runs from about -75 at -60 toward Berkner Island at -48, so -73 is north of the front and is open water or sea ice. The seabed depth in the central Weddell Sea at -73, -55 is likely around 500-600 m on the shelf, or it could be deeper. The Weddell Sea shelf there is perhaps 400-500 m, and the continental slope is further north. I'll guess -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

14397.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 175E is in the Ross Sea region, off the Antarctic coast near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge side. Northern Victoria Land coast lies around 70-71S at 166-170E, and Cape Adare is at 71.3S, 170E. At 69S, 175E the point is in the open ocean north of the Ross Sea continental shelf, beyond the shelf break (~70S at this longitude). Here the continental slope and the Southern Ocean abyssal plain are in play. Near 69S 175E the depth is probably about 2500-3000 m, since the Pacific-Antarctic basin lies at 3000+ m and the Adare Basin is around 2500-3000 m. The Balleny Islands are at 66.5S, 163E, so they are not close.

I estimate the seabed at about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

2689.text:
Latitude 61.0°N, longitude 159.0°E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Kolyma Highlands/Magadan region. The area is near the upper Kolyma basin, where the terrain is mountainous or hilly, so it is almost certainly land.

More specifically, it lies north of the Okhotsk coast and east of the Kolyma River. The Kolyma Highlands cover roughly 60-64°N and 150-160°E. The Omolon massif and the Kolyma lowland lie further north, and the Korkodon River valley is around 62°N, 154°E. At 159°E, 61°N there is likely a mix of the Penzhina/Kolyma highlands and the Sredinny range area. I'd estimate an elevation of about 500 m. This is a rough estimate from general geography rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":520}

8470.text:
The point (-5.0, -159.0) is in the central Pacific, south of the equator and east of the Line Islands. Palmyra is at 5.9°N, 162°W and Kingman Reef is at 6.4°N, so neither is nearby. Christmas Island (Kiritimati) is at 2°N, 157°W, and Jarvis Island is at 0.37°S, 160°W. At 5°S, 159°W there is no land, so the surface is water.

The seafloor in this area is the Central Pacific basin, with depths around 5000 to 5500 m. The Line Islands ridge runs through about 158 to 160°W, but at 5°S the area is between Penrhyn and Christmas. The Manihiki Plateau lies further south, at 10°S. I estimate a depth of about 5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

14733.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 127 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. It lies inland of the coast, which sits near -66.5 at this longitude. Dome C is at about -75, 123, with an elevation of 3233 m. Vostok is at -78.5, 106.8, at about 3488 m. At -73, 127 the ice surface is probably around 2900-3000 m. It is on the Aurora/Vincennes subglacial basin side, and I estimate about 2800 m.

Because the point is grounded ice, the surface type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2850}

12893.text:
Latitude -53, longitude 47 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, south of the Prince Edward Islands, which sit at about 46.9°S, 37.7°E. The point is east of the Conrad Rise region, near the Southwest Indian Ridge. The Crozet Plateau is around 46°S, 51°E, which is north of this point. The Southwest Indian Ridge near 53°S, 47°E has depths of about 3000–4000 m, and the Del Cano Rise lies near 45°S, 45°E. South of the ridge, the Enderby Basin is deeper than 4000 m. The ridge axis around 47°E lies near 51–52°S, because the Indomed and Du Toit fracture zones are at about 46–48°E, so the ridge is offset there. At 53°S, 47°E the seafloor is probably on the ridge flank or in the Africa–Antarctica basin at about 3500 m.

I'll estimate -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

3053.text:
The point 57.0°N, 167.0°E is in the northwest Pacific. It lies south of the Commander Islands (about 55°N, 166°E) and east of Kamchatka, in the western Bering Sea / North Pacific region. The Commander Islands sit near 55°N, 165-166°E, so 57°N, 167°E is northeast of them, in the Aleutian Basin's western part.

The Aleutian Basin depth is around 3800 m, but the Komandorsky Basin lies west of the Shirshov Ridge. The Shirshov Ridge runs north from about 56°N, 170°E, so 167°E is west of it, in the Komandorsky Basin. That basin is roughly 3500–3900 m deep, so I estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

10124.text:
Latitude -23, longitude -91 is in the Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and roughly 1,500 km southwest of the Galápagos. It lies near the East Pacific Rise flank, though the rise crest is further east at around -113 longitude at this latitude. The Nazca plate seafloor here is about 20-30 million years old, so depth is probably around 3,500-4,000 m.

Near 23°S, 91°W, the seafloor sits west of the Chile Rise and Easter Island, which is at 109°W. East of Easter Island the depth is typically around 3,800-4,000 m. The Nazca Ridge is at about 20°S, 80°W, and further east of that the seafloor deepens toward the Peru-Chile trench. At 91°W the depth is likely about 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8257.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 135.0 lies in western New Guinea (Papua Barat), south of Cenderawasih Bay. The Bird's Head sits north of -1, and the neck of the peninsula near Wondama runs around lat -2 to -3. At -1.0, 135.0, the point is probably in the sea at the northern part of the Bird's Head, in the Pacific north of Manokwari (which is at 134.08E, -0.86S). Yapen is at 136E, -1.8S. Cenderawasih Bay's west coast is the Wandammen Peninsula at about 134.5E, -2.5S.

The coast near 135E is therefore uncertain. The Bird's Head's northeast coast runs from Manokwari (134.08E, -0.86S) southeast toward the Wandammen neck at about 134.0E, -2.5S. This means 135E at -1S is east of the coast, in open Cenderawasih Bay, whose northern edge is at about -0.8S. The bay is deep, with the area around 135E, -1S probably at 1000 to 2000 m depth. I'll estimate -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

9853.text:
Latitude -19, longitude 87 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, east of the Central Indian Ridge and west of Ninetyeast Ridge. The Ninetyeast Ridge is near 88-90°E at this latitude, so 87°E is probably just west of it, on the Indian Ocean abyssal plain.

Seafloor depths in this area are typically 3500-5000 m. The Central Indian Ridge sits near 66-70°E, so 87°E is far from it. The basin west of the Ninetyeast Ridge at 19°S is the Central Indian Basin, with depths around 4000-5000 m. I estimate about -4900 m for 87°E, though the ridge flank might be shallower. The Ninetyeast Ridge crest at 19°S is around 88.5-89°E at about 2000-3000 m depth, so 87°E is likely on the western flank at roughly 4,500 m. I'll go with -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7527.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 115.0°E falls in the South China Sea, in the Spratly Islands area. That region lies east of Vietnam and northwest of Borneo. Its Palawan trough and Dangerous Grounds have mixed depths. Swallow Reef is near 7.4°N, 113.8°E, and Louisa Reef is near 6.3°N, 113.3°E. Point 7.0°N, 115.0°E is northwest of Brunei and Sabah, with the Borneo coast near 6°N, 116°E. This point lies in the deeper trough area north of the Borneo-Palawan trough, so I'd estimate the seabed at about -1800 m.

The trough runs from the Sabah northwest margin up to Palawan, and depths there reach 2000–3000 m. At 7°N, 115°E the Dangerous Grounds continental slope drops toward the Northwest Borneo trough, so around 1900 m is plausible.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

5970.text:
The point 23°N, 119°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, west of Baja California and Guadalupe Island (which is at about 29°N, 118.3°W). That puts it in the open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

For depth, this area is on the Pacific plate, south-west of the Baja borderland. Abyssal plain depths near there are around 3,500–4,000 m. The Revillagigedo Islands are further east at about 19°N, 111°W, so they do not affect this point. Seafloor age to the west of the former Guadalupe spreading ridge is about 20–30 Myr, which suggests a depth near 3,800 m. I'm estimating from that rather than from a bathymetry lookup, so I settled on -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

856.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 93.0°E is in the Kara Sea region north of the Taymyr Peninsula. Severnaya Zemlya spans roughly 78-81°N and 90-106°E. Its northern tip, Komsomolets Island, reaches about 81°N at roughly 95°E. The point at 81.0, 93.0 lies just near the northwestern edge of Komsomolets Island. Cape Arctic (Mys Arkticheskiy) is at about 81.2°N, 94°E, and the western coast of Komsomolets is around 95°E at that latitude. The point at 93°E is therefore likely in the sea, west of the island, in the Kara Sea or the Arctic Ocean shelf area.

Bathymetry west of Severnaya Zemlya is around 100-300 m deep, and the shelf edge is just north. At 81°N the depth is probably about 150 m, though the Voronin Trough lies nearer to 95°E and 80°N, so I'll guess -200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-200}

3665.text:
The point 49°N, 49°W lies in the North Atlantic, east of Newfoundland. It is off the Grand Banks, which extend to about 48°W at 45°N. At 49°N, 49°W the point is northeast of Newfoundland, roughly 400 km east of the coast at 53°W. That places it beyond the Grand Banks shelf edge, on the continental slope or rise toward the Newfoundland Basin.

The Flemish Cap sits at about 47°N, 45°W. The Newfoundland Basin lies between the Grand Banks and Flemish Cap, with the Flemish Pass at roughly 47–48°N, 47°W and depths of about 1,200 m. At 49°N, 49°W the point is north of the Grand Banks, and Orphan Basin lies around 49–50°N, 47–50°W with depths of about 2,000–3,000 m. The northern Grand Banks edge near 49°N is around 51–52°W, so 49°W is within Orphan Basin. I estimate a depth of about 2,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

6135.text:
The point at 21°N, 149°W is in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and roughly 1,000 km from the islands. The seafloor there lies on the Pacific plate abyssal plain, where depths are typically around 4,800–5,500 m. This region is likely part of the abyssal hills, and the seafloor depth is about 5,000 m. I did not look this up; the figure is an estimate from typical depths for this area.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -50 00}

