{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5000}

1690.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -39.0 is in East Greenland. Scoresby Sund is at about 70.5N, -22W, so this point is far inland, well west of the coast, which puts it on the Greenland ice sheet. The coast near 71N is around -22 to -25W, so -39W is about 300 km inland.

The ice sheet surface there should be about 2,500 to 2,700 m. The summit is at 72.6N, -38.5W with a surface of 3,216 m. Going about 1.6° south, the surface drops to roughly 3,000 m. At 71N, -39W, I estimate about 2,950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

14350.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 81 is in East Antarctica, in Prydz Bay / Princess Elizabeth Land. Prydz Bay spans roughly 70-78°E, and the Amery Ice Shelf is near 70°E at -70 to -72. The Vestfold Hills (Davis Station) are at 78°E, -68.5. At 81°E, -69, we are east of Davis, around the coast of Princess Elizabeth Land. The coast there lies near -68.5 to -69 at 81-82°E, with the Sørsdal Glacier at about 79°E, -68.7. Near 81°E the coastline is around -67.5 to -68, so -69 is probably inland on the ice sheet, perhaps 50-100 km from the coast. The ice surface there is likely about 1500-2000 m, though the Sørsdal region is lower, so I'll estimate about 1100 m.

The ice surface near the coast at -68.5 is a few hundred meters. Moving inland to -69, about 50-100 km, the surface is probably 1000-1500 m. I'll go with 1300.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

2576.text:
Latitude 61.0, longitude -67.0 falls in the Ungava Bay / Hudson Strait region. Ungava Bay spans roughly 58-61°N and 60-71°W. Its southern shore is around 58.5°N at 68°W. The Hudson Strait's southern coast, on the Ungava Peninsula, sits at about 62°N near 70-72°W. Baffin Island's southern coast, with Resolution Island and the Frobisher Bay mouth, is at about 61-63°N, 64-68°W.

At 61°N, 67°W we are therefore in the eastern Hudson Strait or the mouth of Ungava Bay. Baffin Island's Meta Incognita Peninsula lies at roughly 62-63°N, and its southern tip at Cape Hope Advance is near 61.1°N, 69.6°W. Cape Hopes Advance sits on the Quebec side at 61.05°N, 69.6°W. So 67°W at 61°N is in the water of Hudson Strait, about 2° east of the cape, and the Strait is deep there, around 300-400 m near the mouth of Ungava Bay. I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

10207.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 75 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and southeast of the Mascarene Plateau. It is south of the Central Indian Basin and near the Southwest Indian Ridge/Central Indian Ridge region.

Rodrigues is at about 63.4°E, so 75°E is well east of it. The Central Indian Ridge runs around 66-70°E at this latitude. At 75°E, 23°S, the seafloor is probably in the Mascarene or Central Indian Basin at roughly 4500 m depth. Bathymetry there is around -4400 to -5000 m, so I estimate -4600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

1401.text:
Latitude 75.0°N, longitude 103.0°E is on the Taymyr Peninsula in Russia. Taymyr's northern coast runs around 76–77°N near Cape Chelyuskin, and Khatanga Bay is near 74°N. The Taymyr Peninsula at 75°N, 103°E lies near the Taymyr River and Lake Taymyr, which is at about 74.5°N and 101°E. At 103°E, 75°N, that is near the Byrranga Mountains' eastern part and the Taymyr River area, so it is land.

Elevation there is perhaps 100 m. The Byrranga Mountains are typically 200–500 m. Further east, at 103°E and 75°N, the terrain is the Byrranga foothills, so I estimate about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

14699.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 59 is in East Antarctica, in the Prince Charles Mountains and Lambert Glacier region. The Lambert Glacier runs near 70°E and the Amery Ice Shelf sits near 70-75°E at about -69°S. Mac. Robertson Land lies at roughly 60-70°E, and the coast is near -67.5°S. At -73°S, 59°E we are about 600 km inland on the plateau.

The surface there is probably around 2000-2500 m. Mount Menzies is near 73.5°S, 61.5°E, and the surrounding ice is about 2000 m high. Dome Argus is far to the south, so this point is on the slope toward the Lambert Glacier. I'll estimate an ice surface of about 2200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

13440.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 61 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Indian Ocean sector. It lies north of Antarctica and southwest of Kerguelen, which sits at about -49, 69. The area is likely near the Indian-Antarctic Ridge or the abyssal plain south of the Southeast Indian Ridge.

The Southeast Indian Ridge is further north, near -50 to -40 at longitude 61. The Kerguelen Plateau extends south to about -60, but at longitudes 70-90. At 61°E, -59°, the region is probably the Australian-Antarctic basin's western end, or the Enderby Basin, at a depth of about 4000-5000 m. I'll estimate -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

13197.text:
Latitude -57, longitude -65 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Tierra del Fuego and southeast of Cape Horn, which is at about -55.98 latitude and -67.27 longitude. The point is in the Drake Passage, north of the Shackleton Fracture Zone region, and close to the Burdwood Bank, which sits near -54.5 latitude and -60 to -65 longitude.

At -57 latitude, the seafloor south of the Burdwood Bank is the northern part of the Drake Passage. Depths there are typically around 3000 to 4000 m. The North Scotia Ridge and Burdwood Bank are further north, near -54.5. Between them lies the Yaghan Basin, with depths of about 3000 to 4000 m. I'll estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

1413.text:
The point 75.0°N, 127.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea region, near the Lena Delta and Olenek Bay. The Lena Delta spans roughly 72.5–73.9°N, and the Taymyr coast sits at about 74–75°N between 105 and 115°E. At 127°E, the coast near the Lena Delta's western edge is around 73.5°N, and the delta's eastern part reaches about 129°E. The New Siberian Islands begin near 75°N at 138°E, so this point is open sea in the Laptev Sea, which is shallow at this latitude.

Water depths at 75°N, 127°E are probably about 20–40 m. The Laptev shelf deepens northward, with the 50 m isobath near 76°N, so I estimate roughly 35 m depth here.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-35}

2962.text:
The point 57°N, 15°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of Scotland. It is southwest of Rockall, which sits at about 57.6°N, 13.7°W. It is on the Rockall Plateau or in the Rockall Trough. The Rockall Trough's western margin runs close to 15°W at this latitude. The Rockall Bank spans roughly 13–15.5°W at 57°N, and the Hatton Bank lies further northwest.

The Rockall Plateau is about 1000–1500 m deep. At 57°N the western edge of Rockall Bank is around 15°W, and the Rockall Bank's slopes drop to about 1000–2000 m there. East of the bank, the trough reaches about 2000–2900 m. West of the bank, the Hatton-Rockall Basin is about 1200–1500 m deep. Since 57°N, 15°W is near the western edge of the bank, I estimate a depth of about 1000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1100}

1234.text:
The point is at 77°N, 129°E, which is in the Laptev Sea region. The Lena Delta lies near 73°N, 126°E, and the Laptev Sea coast near 129°E is at about 73°N. The Taymyr Peninsula is farther west. The New Siberian Islands are farther east, at about 140°E and above 75°N. So 77°N, 129°E is open Laptev Sea, north of the coast.

The Laptev shelf is shallow in the south and deepens northward. At 77°N, the depth is probably around 40–50 m, though the shelf break is near 78–79°N. Depths at 77°N, 129°E are likely about 50 m, so I'll use -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

14491.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 3 E is in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The Jutulsessen and Gjelsvikfjella area lies around 71-72°S, 1-5°E, and the coast is near 70°S at this longitude (Fimbul Ice Shelf is at 0-3°W, 70.5°S). At 3°E the coast is around 70.3°S, near Maudheim and the Jelbart Ice Shelf edge.

Fimbul Ice Shelf extends from about 3°W to 3°E. At 71°S, 3°E the point is likely on grounded ice. The Jutulstraumen glacier is near 0° longitude, and east of it lies the Gjelsvikfjella and Mühlig-Hofmannfjella mountains at about 72°S, 3-5°E. At 71°S, 3°E the ice is probably grounded at about 1000 m elevation. The Jelbart ice shelf runs to about 71°S at 0°E to 3°W, so 3°E at 71°S is likely the ice sheet slope behind the coast, at roughly 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

1430.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 161.0 E lies in the East Siberian Sea region, near the Laptev Sea / New Siberian Islands area. The New Siberian Islands span roughly 73-76 N and 137-150 E, so 161 E is east of them. The coast of mainland Siberia at 161 E is around 70.5 N, which puts this point about 450 km offshore in the East Siberian Sea. Water depth there is roughly 50 m, since the shelf is shallow. Wrangel Island is at 71 N and 180 E, so it is not nearby.

Depths in the northern East Siberian Sea at 75 N, 161 E are likely near the shelf edge, perhaps 60-100 m, while the shelf break lies around 76-77 N. I estimate about -80 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-80}

15110.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 161 is in the Transantarctic Mountains region of Victoria Land, near the Ferrar/Taylor Valley area. The Dry Valleys are around -77.5, 163. At -77, 161 it is west of the Dry Valleys, near the Mackay Glacier / Wright Valley head, and probably in the Convoy Range or Cook Mountains region. Polar plateau ice there is roughly 2000 m, and the mountains are around 1500-2500 m.

Wright Valley is at about -77.5, 162. Longitude 161 at -77 is near the Describe/Willett Range, and the Mackay Glacier is at -76.9, 160.5. The Mackay Glacier's upper reaches there are at about 1000-1500 m. I'll estimate 1700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1700}

10129.text:
The point at 23°S, 81°W lies in the southeast Pacific, roughly 1,500 km west of the Chilean coast. It is east of the Easter Island region (which is near 109°W) and sits on the Nazca Plate. Nearby features are the Nazca Ridge (to the north) and the Sala y Gómez Ridge (to the west), and the area is likely abyssal plain or lower ridge flank.

The Peru-Chile Trench lies near 71°W, so this point is well west of it. Depths in the Chile Basin at this latitude are around 4,000 m. At 81°W the seafloor is probably 3,900–4,100 m deep. The Nazca Ridge's southern end is near 26°S, 85°W, and the Nazca Ridge elevated area has depths of about 3,000 m, so this location may be near its flank. I estimate a depth of about 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

3408.text:
Latitude 53.0 N, longitude 157.0 E is on the Kamchatka Peninsula. Petropavlovsk-Kamchatsky sits at about 53.0 N, 158.65 E, so 157.0 E is roughly 100 km west of it. The Avacha Gulf coast and the Pacific are to the east, while the Sea of Okhotsk coast lies at about 156 E at this latitude.

The west coast of Kamchatka at 53 N is near 156.2 E, so 157.0 E should be inland, in the western lowlands or the Sredinny Range foothills. The Sredinny Range lies farther east, around 158 E at 54 N. At 53 N, 157 E, the terrain is probably the lowland east of the west coast, with elevation of perhaps 100–300 m. The Sredinny Range here has volcanoes such as Khangar, at about 54.8 N. Near 53 N, 157 E lies the Bolshaya River area, and I would estimate about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

9792.text:
Latitude -19, longitude -35 is in the Atlantic Ocean, east of Brazil (Bahia/Espírito Santo coast is around -39 to -40 longitude). At -35 longitude, this lies off the Brazilian margin, past the continental shelf, near the Vitória-Trindade seamount chain's eastern side. The Vitória-Trindade chain runs along about -20.5 latitude from -39 to -29. At -19 and -35, the area is likely the abyssal plain of the Brazil Basin, with depth around 4,500–5,000 m. There is also a Rio Grande Rise further south, and at -19 the seafloor lies around -4,500 m.

I estimate about -4,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

12871.text:
The point is at 53°S, 3°E, in the South Atlantic near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Bouvet Island area. Bouvet Island sits at about 54.4°S, 3.4°E, so this point is roughly 1.4° north of it, which is open ocean.

The seafloor here is the Bouvet Triple Junction region, where the American-Antarctic Ridge meets the Southwest Indian Ridge. That ridge region is typically 2,500–3,500 m deep. At 53°S, 3°E the point is likely on the ridge flank or the Bouvet plateau, so I estimate a depth of about 3,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

13143.text:
The point is at 57°S, 173°W, in the South Pacific Ocean southeast of New Zealand. It lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or nearby abyssal plain. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs around 56-65°S between 150°W and 170°W, and near 173°W it is roughly at 62°S, so this point is probably north of the ridge.

The Udintsev fracture zone is near 57°S, 145°W, which is far from this point. At 57°S, 173°W, the Pitman fracture zone and the Antarctic plate boundary are close. The Macquarie Ridge is at 160°E, so it is not relevant. The seafloor here is likely about 4,500–5,000 m deep. Near 57°S, 173°W the region lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank, with a typical depth of roughly 4,000 m. I'll go with about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

14858.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 17 E is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica, inland of the coast. The coast is at about -70 at this longitude. At -75 we are in the Mühlig-Hofmann or Wohlthat mountains region, roughly 500 km inland. The Wohlthat Mountains lie near 12 E, -72, and the Orvin mountains near 5 E, -72. At -75, 17 E, the ice sheet surface is probably about 3000 m. The Antarctic plateau near the Recovery/Jutulstraumen area sits at around 2500-3000 m, and at -75 the elevation is likely about 3000 m.

I estimate 3000 m.

