8491.text:
The point is at 5°S, 117°W, in the open eastern Pacific, southwest of the Galápagos and about 3,000 km west of South America. It lies on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise, which sits near 110°W at this latitude.

The seafloor here is roughly 10–20 million years old. Seafloor of that age usually lies at about 3,800–4,200 m depth. The Clipperton fracture zone is further north, so I'll take a typical depth of about 4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

5198.text:
Latitude 33.0°N, longitude 137.0°E lies in the Pacific Ocean south of Honshu's Kii Peninsula. It is roughly 150 km south of Cape Shionomisaki, over the Nankai Trough region. The Kii Peninsula's southern tip is at about 33.4°N, 135.8°E, so this point is to the southeast, in open water.

The Nankai Trough axis lies near 32°N at 137°E, and the trough floor is about 4,500 m deep. At 33°N the point sits on the Nankai accretionary prism or the slope just north of the trough axis, where depths run from about 2,000 to 4,000 m. The Shikoku Basin lies south of the trough. Near 137°E the trough is around 33°N–32.5°N, so 33.0°N is probably on the lower slope at roughly 3,000–3,500 m depth. I'll estimate -3,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

3799.text:
The point (47°N, 141°W) is in the northeast Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of the Oregon/Washington coast. It lies in the abyssal plain region west of the Gorda/Juan de Fuca ridges and the Cascadia basin, and south of the Gulf of Alaska seamounts.

The Surveyor Fan and the Surveyor Channel lie farther north. At 47°N, 141°W the seafloor is probably abyssal plain at around 3,000 to 4,000 m depth. The Juan de Fuca Ridge is at about 130°W, and the crust there is young and shallow, about 2,200 m. Moving west, the crust ages and deepens. At 141°W, about 11° of longitude (~830 km) west of the ridge, the crust is roughly 15–20 Myr old, which gives a depth of about 3,000 + 350·√t ≈ 2,500 + 350·√17 ≈ 3,900 m. Sediment would also lower the bottom depth relative to basement. The Pacific plate there is roughly 30 Myr old, so a depth around 4,000 to 4,500 m is plausible. I estimate about 4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

811.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 3.0°E lies north of Svalbard, in the Arctic Ocean near the Yermak Plateau and Lena Trough area. Svalbard's northern edge (Nordaustlandet) is at about 80.5°N, but at 3°E we are well west of it. Svalbard's main island, Spitsbergen, spans roughly 10–25°E, so this point is in the ocean.

The Yermak Plateau is centered around 81°N, 5–10°E, with depths of about 600–1000 m. The Molloy Deep and Lena Trough are further west. At 3°E and 81°N, the point sits on the western flank of the Yermak Plateau, or in the Lena Trough, where depths are around 3000 m. The Lena Trough lies at approximately 81°N, 0–3°E, with depths of about 3000–4000 m. The plateau's western slope drops steeply to the trough. Taking a mid-slope depth gives roughly -1800 m. I'm uncertain, but I'll estimate -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

1711.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 3.0°E is in the Norwegian Sea, northwest of Norway's northern coast. The North Cape is at about 71.17°N, 25.8°E, so this point is far west of it. Jan Mayen is at 71°N, 8.5°W, which is also far away. The point therefore lies in open ocean.

The Norwegian Sea basin there is deep. At 71°N, 3°E, the Lofoten Basin lies to the north and east of the Vøring Plateau. Its depths are about 3000 m, and the Mohn Ridge lies further north. Near 71°N, 3°E the depth is probably around 3200 m. The Lofoten Basin floor is around 3250 m, so I estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

8997.text:
Location: -9.0, 175.0 is in the western Pacific, east of the Solomon Islands and near Tuvalu. Nui island in Tuvalu is at about -7.2, 177.15, and Nanumea at -5.7, 176.1. Niutao is at -6.1, 177.3. Santa Cruz Islands lie near -10.7, 166. Point -9, 175 is in open ocean between the Gilbert Islands (Kiribati) and Tuvalu, west of Nukufetau (-8.0, 178.5), so it is water.

The depth there is probably around 3000 to 4500 m. The Ellice Basin has depths of about 5000 m or more, and this area sits at the western edge of the Ellice Basin, so I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

8178.text:
The point (-1, -23) is in the equatorial Atlantic Ocean, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis crosses the equator near -13 to -15 longitude. The St. Paul fracture zone area is near -29 longitude at 0.9 N, and the Romanche fracture zone lies near 0 N between -18 and -13.

At -23 longitude the crust is about 8–10 Myr old, and the seafloor there is probably around 4000 m deep. The Romanche and Chain fracture zones sit near 0 to -1.5 latitude, with troughs reaching 5000 to 7000 m. The Chain fracture zone lies slightly south of the equator, near -1.2 S at -20 to -14 longitude, and I think it extends further west as a trough. The point could fall in or near that trough, but I can't confirm it. I'll estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

11990.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 41 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa and southwest of the Crozet Islands. It lies near the Southwest Indian Ridge, but the ridge there is mostly at about 50°E and further east. The Del Cano Rise is near 45°S, 45-50°E. At 43°S, 41°E the area is probably abyssal plain or basin on the flank of the Africa-Antarctic Ridge, with a depth around -4500 m.

The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (about 55°S, 0°E) northeast to the Indian Ocean triple junction at about 25°S, 70°E. At 41°E it sits near 40°S, since the ridge crosses the Prince Edward fracture zone near 35°E at about 45°S. The ridge axis around 41°E should therefore be near 41-42°S, which puts this point near the ridge, probably on its flank at roughly 3000-4000 m depth. I'll estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5219.text:
The point 33.0°N, 179.0°E is in the North Pacific, east of Japan and well north of the Hawaiian-Emperor chain. The region is the northwest Pacific abyssal plain, southeast of the Shatsky Rise and near the Emperor seamounts to the east. I'm estimating depth from memory of regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup.

The Shatsky Rise sits at about 32–35°N, 157–161°E, so it is well to the west. The Emperor Seamounts run from about 30°N, 171°E northward to the bend near 170°E, so 179°E is east of them. The seafloor there is Pacific abyssal plain at roughly 5500–6000 m depth. I'll take 5600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

7794.text:
Latitude 3.0, Longitude -71.0 falls in eastern Colombia, in the Llanos region near the Vichada/Meta border. It is south of the Meta River, which lies near 4.5-6°N. Near 3°N, 71°W the terrain is Llanos savanna at roughly 150 m elevation. The Orinoco is at about 70.0°W near 4°N, and the Vichada River is at about 4.5°N, so this point is probably in the Vichada plains, where elevation is about 100-200 m. Rivers like the Tomo and Guaviare lie nearby, but the point is most likely on land.

I estimate about 140 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":140}

9374.text:
The coordinates -15.0, -151.0 fall in French Polynesia. Tahiti sits near -17.6, -149.6, and Tetiaroa is near -17.0, -149.5. The Tuamotu atolls lie around -15, -148 (Rangiroa is at -15.1, -147.7). Mataiva is at -14.9, -148.7, and Tikehau is at -15.0, -148.2. At longitude -151 the closest feature is Manuae/Scilly (-16.5, -154.7), and Bellingshausen is farther west, so this point is likely open ocean.

The seafloor between the Society Islands and the Tuamotus here is about 3,500-4,500 m deep. The area north of Tahiti and west of the Tuamotus is roughly 3,800 m deep. I estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

10966.text:
Latitude -31.0, longitude 153.0 is in the Tasman Sea off the coast of New South Wales. The coast near Port Macquarie (-31.43, 152.9) and Coffs Harbour (-30.3, 153.1) is at about 153.0 E at this latitude. At -31.0 the coastline is near 153.0 E, around Scotts Head and Nambucca Heads, where the coast sits at about 153.0 E. Nambucca Heads is at -30.64, 152.99, and Scotts Head is at -30.75, 153.0. At -31.0 the coast is around 152.85 E (Crescent Head is at -31.18, 152.98; South West Rocks is at -30.88, 153.03). So the coast is near 153.0 E at -31.0, and the point is likely just offshore. Crescent Head at -31.18 is at 152.98, and the coast runs close to 153.0 around there. At -31.0, the coast near Hat Head is at about 153.07 E, so the point is probably on land at Hat Head National Park, or just offshore.

Hat Head is at -31.07, 153.07. The coast at -31.0 is around 153.04 E (Smoky Cape is at -30.92, 153.09). So 153.0 is likely land, in Hat Head National Park, with a low elevation of about 10 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":10}

80.text:
Latitude 89°N is in the Arctic Ocean, close to the North Pole. The Lomonosov Ridge runs near this area, roughly from Greenland toward the Siberian shelf. Near the pole, the ridge crosses at about 90°N, 140°E and 80°N, 140°W or so. At longitude -19 and 89°N, the point sits on the Greenland side of the pole. The Lomonosov Ridge on the Greenland side is at about 88-89°N near 50°W to 0°. Depths over the ridge crest are around 1000-1500 m, while the Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side, at about 4200 m deep.

The ridge's position matters here. Near the pole the ridge is at about 140°E to 40°W, so the line from the pole toward 40°W is the ridge axis. Longitude -19 is on the Amundsen Basin side, which lies toward 0 to 20°E. At 89°N the point is about 111 km from the pole. The ridge axis at 40°W is about 21° away in longitude, which at that radius is roughly 40 km. So the point is probably on the ridge flank, with a depth of about 2500 m. The Amundsen Basin floor is around 4200 m, and the ridge crest is around 1000 m. I'll estimate a flank depth of about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

15759.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 19 lies in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land, on the plateau south of the Shackleton Range and Recovery Glacier region. The Shackleton Range is near 80-81°S, 25°W, so it is not close. At 85°S, 19°E the ice surface is on the high plateau, probably around 2,500-3,000 m.

The South Pole is at 2,835 m. Along the 0-30°E meridians at 85°S, the surface is probably around 3,000 m or a bit higher, since the Pole-Dome A direction rises. The Pole sits about 2,835 m, and 90°E at 85°S is around 3,300 m. At 19°E, 85°S I estimate roughly 2,900 m, and I'll answer 2,950.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

241.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -57° is in the central Arctic Ocean. It lies near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from Greenland/Ellesmere across the pole toward Siberia. At 90°N the ridge is roughly 1000-1500 m deep. The ridge axis around 87°N is near 60°W–140°E, and the Lomonosov Ridge sits on the Greenland side at about 60°W at lat 87, so this point could be on or near the ridge. The Amundsen Basin is on the Eurasian side, at about 4000 m depth. The Makarov Basin is on the Canadian side, at about 4000 m depth.

The ridge near 87°N is at roughly 1000-1200 m depth, but the crest is narrow, about 50-100 km wide. Longitude -57 at 87°N is about 3° from the pole, around 156 km away. The ridge at 87°N lies about 50°W–60°W, according to the IBCAO bathymetry, where the ridge crosses 88°N at about 60°W. So the point is likely on or near the ridge, and I'll estimate a depth of about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

4018.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude -63.0 lies in Nova Scotia. The Minas Basin is around 45.3N, -64. The Nova Scotia Atlantic coast near 45.0N is at about -62 to -63 (Sheet Harbour is at 44.9N, -62.5). At -63.0, 45.0N, this is inland in northern Nova Scotia, near the Guysborough/Pictou/Antigonish area. Guysborough County is at about -61.5. The Musquodoboit area is at 44.9N, -63.0, so 45.0N, -63.0 is inland in the Halifax/Guysborough highlands, with elevation around 100 m.

The Liscomb Game Sanctuary is near 45.0N, -62.3. West of that, around -63.0, is the Halifax County interior, with elevations of about 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

14732.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 125 lies in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. The location is roughly inland of the Sabrina Coast and near the Aurora Subglacial Basin region. The coast at this longitude is around -66 to -67, so -73 is about 700 km inland.

At about 125E and 73S the ice surface is roughly 2500 m on the plateau slope. Dome C is at 123E, 75S with a surface of about 3200 m. At 73S the surface is probably about 2900 m, so I estimate 2850 m. The point is on grounded ice, so it is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2850}

5092.text:
The point 33.0°N, 75.0°W lies in the western North Atlantic, roughly 400 km southeast of Cape Hatteras. Cape Hatteras is at about 35.2°N, 75.5°W, and the continental shelf break there is near 36°N. At 33°N the shelf break is farther west, around 78°W. The point is therefore over the Blake Plateau's eastern edge or the Blake Escarpment, or on the continental rise.

The Blake Plateau at 31–32°N, 77–78°W is about 800 m deep. Its edge, the Blake Escarpment, sits near 76–77°W at 30–32°N and drops to about 4,500 m. At 33°N, 75°W the point is probably east of the escarpment on the Blake Basin or abyssal plain, with depths around 4,800 m. I recall the Hatteras Abyssal Plain at roughly 5,000 m near 32°N, 73°W, so a depth near 4,900 m is a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

3939.text:
Latitude 47.0 N, longitude 139.0 E lies in the Sea of Japan, off the Russian coast (Primorsky/Khabarovsk). The Russian coast near Sovetskaya Gavan is at about 140.3 E, and Sakhalin's west coast is at about 142 E. At 139 E and 47 N we are therefore west of Sakhalin and east of the Sikhote-Alin coast, in the northern Sea of Japan, in the Tatar Strait region to the south.

The Japan Basin is deeper to the south. At 47 N, 139 E the depth is probably about 2,500–3,000 m, because the Tatar Strait shallows only north of about 48–49 N. The Japan Basin's northern part is roughly 3,000 m deep. The continental slope off Primorye is fairly steep, and at 47 N the coast is at about 138.3 E, so 139 E is about 60 km offshore. That puts the point on the slope, perhaps at about 1,500–2,000 m depth. I'll estimate -2,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

2001.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude -137.0 W lies in northern Yukon, Canada, near the Arctic Circle. It is close to the Dempster Highway, in the Eagle Plain / Peel Plateau area. The Dempster crosses the Arctic Circle at about 66.56 N, 136.3 W. At 67.0 N, 137 W the terrain is near the Eagle Plain basin edge, near the Bonnet Plume or Richardson Mountains foothills.

Elevations in Eagle Plain are about 400-900 m. Near 67 N, 137 W, the Eagle River and Eagle Plain area is roughly 500-600 m. I'll estimate 550 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 550}

7817.text:
The point (3°N, 25°W) lies in the central Atlantic Ocean, north of the equator and west of Africa. It is near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which at this latitude sits around 30–32°W, so the point is east of the ridge crest on the African side. The St. Peter and St. Paul Rocks are at about 0.9°N, 29.3°W, which is well away from this point.

Seafloor depth east of the ridge at this latitude is typically about 4000 m, and the Sierra Leone Basin is deeper at roughly 4500–5000 m. The point is only about 5° from the ridge crest. Crust of that age would normally lie at about 3500–4000 m depth, but fracture zones such as the St. Paul fracture zone, around 1°N, and the Romanche fracture zone, near the equator, add variation. At 3°N, 25°W the depth is probably around 4300 m, so I estimate -4300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14981.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -97 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. Pine Island Bay lies near -74.5, -102, and the Thwaites area near -75.5, -106. At -77, -97, we are further inland, near the Ellsworth Land coast. The Abbot Ice Shelf is around -73, -95, and the Thurston Island area around -72, -98. At -77, -97 we are probably on the grounded ice of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, in the Bentley Subglacial Trench's western flank or the Ellsworth/Hudson Mountains area. The Pine Island glacier basin's upper catchment sits around -77, -95, with an ice surface elevation of roughly 1200 m.

The Bentley Subglacial Trench is closer to -80, -110, so this point should be a grounded ice divide area. I'd estimate the ice surface at about 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

2426.text:
The point 63.0°N, 7.0°W lies in the Faroe Islands region. The Faroes span roughly 61.4–62.4°N, so 63.0°N is north of the islands, in the Faroe-Shetland channel/Faroe Bank region north of the Faroes. Because the islands end near 62.4°N, this point is in open ocean.

The seafloor north of the Faroes at 63°N, 7°W is on the Faroe plateau/shelf, with depths around 200-1000 m. The Faroe shelf extends roughly 50-100 km beyond the islands. At 63.0°N, about 70 km north of the northern tip near 62.4°N, depths on the north slope are roughly 500-1000 m. The Faroe-Iceland ridge lies to the northwest, and the Norwegian Sea basin to the northeast. At 7°W, 63°N, the depth is probably around 800 m, so I'll estimate -800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

8111.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude -157.0 is in the central Pacific, south of the equator and southwest of Kiritimati (Christmas Island, about 1.9°N, 157.4°W). The nearest land is Malden Island at about 4°S, 155°W, and Jarvis Island at 0.4°S, 160°W, so this point is open ocean.

Seafloor depth in this part of the Central Pacific Basin, near the Line Islands region, is typically about 5000 to 5500 m. I estimate about -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

6770.text:
Latitude 15.0°N, longitude 41.0°E. This is in the Red Sea region. The Eritrean coast lies around 39-40°E at this latitude. At 15°N the Eritrean coast is near 40.0°E (Massawa is at 15.6°N, 39.5°E), and the Dahlak Archipelago lies around 40°E. At 41°E, 15°N, the point is in the Red Sea's eastern side, since Yemen's coast near Hodeidah is at about 42.9°E, 14.8°N. The Red Sea at 15°N spans from roughly 40.2°E to 42.6°E, so 41°E is in the water.

The Red Sea main trough there is deep, but near 41°E it is probably the shelf edge or slope. The axial trough at 15°N lies near 41.5°E with depths of about 1000 m. The Eritrean shelf with the Dahlak islands extends to about 40.5°E, so at 41°E the depth is perhaps 600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

664.text:
Latitude 83.0N, longitude 69.0E is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Severnaya Zemlya (which lies around 79-81N, 95-105E) and north of Franz Josef Land (80-81.8N, 45-65E). At 83N, 69E the point is north of Franz Josef Land, in the deep Arctic Ocean basin or on the slope.

The Franz Josef Land northernmost point is Rudolf Island at 81.8N, about 58E. At 83N, 69E, the point is about 1.2 degrees north of that, roughly 130 km. The shelf edge north of Franz Josef Land is around 82N, and the continental slope descends to the Nansen Basin at about 3000 m or deeper. At 83N the depth is probably about 2000-3500 m. In IBCAO, the Nansen Basin at 83N, 69E is around 3000 m deep, and the slope extends to about 82.5N. I'll estimate -3100.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

2910.text:
The point 57.0°N, 119.0°W is in northwestern Alberta, near the Peace River country. It falls roughly between Grande Prairie and High Level, probably near the Peace River lowlands north of the Peace River.

The Peace River at Fort Vermilion is near 58.4°N, 116°W. At 57°N, 119°W, the area is about 100 km northeast of Grande Prairie (55.17°N, 118.8°W) and near the Smoky/Peace River region. This is plausibly the Clear Hills or Buffalo Head area, or the Notikewin area. Terrain there is roughly 500-700 m, and I'll estimate 650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":650}

9151.text:
Latitude -11.0, longitude 123.0 lies in the Timor Sea / Indian Ocean, southwest of Timor island and northwest of Australia. Rote island is at about -10.7, 123.1, so this point is just south of Rote, in the Timor Sea north of the Timor Trough. The Timor Trough runs south of Timor at roughly -10 to -11 latitude at longitude 123, with depths around 3000 m. The trough axis is about 3000 m deep near 122-124°E, and the point is just south of Rote, which puts it on the inner slope of the trough.

The Sahul shelf lies to the south, so the trough's deepest part is around -11°. The Timor Trough reaches about 3300 m near 123°E, so I estimate the seabed at about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

12520.text:
Location: 49°S, 21°E is in the southern Indian Ocean/South Atlantic sector, southwest of Prince Edward Islands (which are at ~46.9°S, 37.7°E), so it is open ocean. It lies south of Africa, near the Southwest Indian Ridge. The ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (~55°S, 1°E) northeast to the Indian Ocean triple junction. At 21°E the ridge is around 52-53°S, so 49°S is north of the ridge. The Agulhas Plateau is at about 40°S, 26°E, and the Agulhas Basin is south of it. The depth there is approximately 4500-5000 m. The Islas Orcadas fracture zone lies nearby, but the Agulhas Basin is about 4800 m deep.

I estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

3466.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude -87.0 is in northern Ontario, in the Hudson Bay Lowlands. It lies west of James Bay, near the Attawapiskat River region, and the coast is at about 52.9°N, -82°W. At -87 longitude, 51°N is inland, near the Ontario/Albany River area south of Fort Albany and probably around Ogoki and Marten Falls. The Albany River is at about 51.5°N, -86°W. Lake Nipigon is at 49.8°N, -88.5°W, so this point is north of it. Terrain here is about 200 m above sea level, since the Lowlands slope from roughly 150 m inland to 0 m at the coast. This is a rough estimate from memory and I'm not certain of it.

I'd guess the point is on land, at roughly 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

2168.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -163.0 is in western Alaska. Norton Sound lies at about 64°N, -161 to -162°, and the Seward Peninsula is north of 65°. The Yukon-Kuskokwim delta is to the south. At 65.0N, -163.0 the point is in Norton Sound's northern part, or on land.

The Norton Sound coast runs from Nome (64.5N, -165.4) east to Golovin (64.55N, -163.0) and Elim (64.6N, -162.26). Golovin sits at about 64.55N, -163.03, so 65.0N at -163 is about 50 km north of Golovin, inland on the southern Seward Peninsula. The terrain there is hilly, with elevations of about 200 m. Candidates in the area are the Kwiniuk and Niukluk river valleys and the Darby Mountains, near 64.9N, -162. At -163, 65.0N it is probably near the Fish River or Kachauik area, with hills at around 300 m. I'll estimate 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

13248.text:
Latitude -57, longitude 37 is in the Southern Ocean, southeast of Africa and southwest of the Indian Ocean sector, near the Enderby Basin and Atlantic-Indian Basin. The nearby features are the Southwest Indian Ridge to the north and the Conrad Rise to the east at about 50°S, 40–50°E. At 57°S, 37°E the seafloor is probably the Atlantic-Indian Basin or the southern flank of the Antarctic-Weddell region, at roughly 4,000–5,000 m depth.

The Islas Orcadas Rise and Maud Rise lie further south and west, and Maud Rise is near 65°S, 3°E, so it is not relevant here. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (54°S, 1°E) northeast toward 50°S, 30°E, and it is north of this point. At 57°S, 37°E the point is south of the ridge in the Enderby Basin's western part, so I estimate a depth of about -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

3646.text:
Latitude 49.0, longitude -87.0 falls in northern Ontario, north of Lake Superior. Lake Superior's north shore near Nipigon is at about 48.9°N, -88.3°W. Longitude -87.0 at latitude 49.0 is inland, northeast of Lake Nipigon and west of Longlac, which is near 49.8°N, -86.5°W. Marathon sits at 48.7°N, -86.4°W, and the Lake Superior shore near Pic River is around -86.3°W. At -87.0 the shore near Terrace Bay is at 48.8°N, -87.1°W. So 49.0°N, -87.0°W is about 20 km north of Terrace Bay, which puts it on land. Terrain there is roughly 300-400 m.

Lake Superior's surface is at 183 m, and the land inland of Terrace Bay rises to about 300 m. I'll estimate 330 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":330}

7349.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 119.0°E lies in the Sulu Sea, southeast of Palawan, which is at about 9°N, 118°E. At 119°E the point is east of Palawan's coast. Palawan's northern part is around 118.7°E at 9°N, and the east coast near Taytay or Roxas sits at about 119.5°E for 10.5°N. At 9°N, Palawan spans roughly 117.5–118.2°E (Brooke's Point is at 8.77°N, 117.85°E). So 119°E is in the Sulu Sea, east of Palawan.

The Sulu Sea basin there is deep, around 4000 m, but the area east of Palawan at 119°E has a slope, with the Palawan shelf edge close to the coast. The Sulu Sea basin depth is about 4500 m in the central part near 8.5°N, 120°E. At 119°E, 9°N, which is about 100 km off the coast, I estimate roughly -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

7243.text:
The point (9.0°N, 93.0°W) lies in the Pacific Ocean, about 500 km southwest of the Gulf of Tehuantepec and south of Mexico/Guatemala. It is offshore of the Middle America Trench, on the Cocos Plate.

The trench axis is about 150–200 km off the coast, roughly at 13–14°N at this longitude. At 9°N, 93°W, the seafloor is on the Cocos Plate, south of the Tehuantepec Ridge. The Tehuantepec Ridge runs from about 14°N, 96°W southwest toward 10°N, 100°W, so this point is southeast of it. The Cocos Plate seafloor there is roughly 3500–4000 m deep, and I'm estimating about 3600 m since the seafloor is a bit shallower toward Cocos Ridge (which lies farther southeast).

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9423.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -53 is in Brazil, in the state of Goiás/Mato Grosso border region. It lies on the central Brazilian plateau, near Barra do Garças (-15.89, -52.26) and Nova Xavantina. At -15.0, -53.0, this is around Poxoréu or Primavera do Leste (-15.56, -54.3). Elevation there is roughly 500-700 m. Poxoréu is at about 450 m. The area near -15, -53 is probably the Serra Azul region or the plateau near General Carneiro, at roughly 600 m.

I estimate 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

15141.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -137 is in West Antarctica, in the Ross Sea sector. The Ross Ice Shelf extends roughly from 160°W eastward to the Marie Byrd Land coast. At 137°W, 79°S, I believe this is the eastern part of the Ross Ice Shelf, near the Edward VII Peninsula / Sulzberger Bay region. Sulzberger Bay is around 150°W and 77°S. The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge is near Marie Byrd Land at about 135-150°W with the coast at about 78-79°S, so this point is probably on the ice shelf, possibly near the grounding zone of the Hal Flood Range. Ice streams from the Executive Committee Range feed in here, but I think it is the shelf.

The seabed beneath the eastern Ross Ice Shelf is around -600 to -800 m (Bedmap2 shows roughly -700 m). I'll go with -700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

13978.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 57 is off the coast of East Antarctica, in the Southern Ocean north of Enderby Land and Mac. Robertson Land. The coast near longitude 57E is around -67.5 (Mawson station is at 62.9E, -67.6). So at -65 we are about 2.5 degrees north of the coast, in the ocean over the continental shelf break or slope.

The shelf off Enderby Land is narrow. Farther west, around 50E, the Antarctic coast is near -66.5. At -65, 57E, the depth is probably about 2000 to 4000 m. The Kerguelen Plateau lies far to the east, so it does not apply here. The continental slope there probably begins at about -66, and the abyssal plain at -64 is about 4000 m deep. I estimate -3200 m at -65.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

12836.text:
Latitude -53.0, longitude -67.0 is in southern Patagonia near Tierra del Fuego. Río Grande is at about -53.8, -67.7. At -53.0, -67.0, the point lies in northern Tierra del Fuego, just south of the Strait of Magellan. The strait's eastern entrance is near -52.4, -68.4 (Primera Angostura), and Bahía Inútil is around -53.5, -69.5. The Atlantic coast is near -52.9, -68.4 (Cabo Espíritu Santo at -52.6, -68.6).

At longitude -67.0 and latitude -53.0, the point is inland in Argentine Tierra del Fuego, which is flat pampa. Elevation is roughly 50-100 m. The coast near San Sebastián Bay lies at about -53.2, -68.2, so -67.0 is farther east. The Atlantic coast at this latitude runs near -68.0 to -67.5, and east of that is the Atlantic Ocean. Cabo Español is at about -52.9, -68.4, with the coast trending southeast to Río Grande at about -67.7, -53.8. At -53.0 the coast is at about -68.3, so -67.0 is in the sea, a shallow shelf of roughly 50 m depth. That is plausible, since the shelf there is shallow, so I'd estimate about -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

8554.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 9.0 lies in the Atlantic Ocean, west of Gabon and the Congo coast. The Gabon coast near Cape Lopez is at about 0.6°S, 8.7°E. At 5°S the African coast is at roughly 12°E, so 9°E is about 300 km offshore, in the Angola Basin.

Seabed depth there is probably about 4,500 to 5,000 m. The continental shelf is narrow, and the abyssal plain of the Angola Basin sits at about 5,000 m depth. At 5°S, 9°E I estimate roughly 4,900 m. The Congo submarine canyon and fan lie near 6°S, 11°E, with the fan extending west. At 9°E the depth is probably about 4,000 to 5,000 m, so I'll go with about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

122.text:
Latitude 89°N is in the Arctic Ocean, close to the North Pole. The Lomonosov Ridge runs near the pole, from about 88°N, 140°E toward Greenland. Longitude 65°E at 89°N is probably on the Amerasian side or the Eurasian side of the ridge. At the pole itself the depth is about 4261 m, and the ridge crest is around 1000–1500 m deep. The ridge axis runs from Greenland (about 60°W) across the pole to the Siberian shelf (about 140°E). So 65°E at 89°N is not on the crest, and it lies about 1° from the pole, roughly 111 km away.

The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole along a line near 90°W–90°E, which is only approximate. The ridge near the pole is roughly aligned with the 140°E–50°W meridians. At 65°E we are about 75° away from the ridge's 140°E direction, so the point is in the Amundsen Basin side, which is about 4200 m deep. The Amundsen Basin is at 4000–4400 m near the pole, and the pole itself is in the Amundsen Basin at about -4261 m. I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

15737.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -25 is in Antarctica, in the area south of the Weddell Sea near the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf and the Foundation Ice Stream / Support Force Glacier region. At 85°S, 25°W, this is in the Foundation Ice Stream / Pensacola Mountains region (which sits around 83-85°S, 50-60°W). Longitude -25 at 85°S is east of the Pensacola Mountains, toward the Recovery Glacier and Shackleton Range area (Shackleton Range is about 80.5°S, 25°W). At 85°S, 25°W, this should be inland on the Antarctic ice sheet, probably the upper Recovery Glacier catchment, with an ice-surface elevation around 1500-2500 m. I'd estimate about 2000 m.

The ice there is grounded, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

14210.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 161 lies off the coast of East Antarctica in the Southern Ocean, in the area north of Oates Land and Wilkes Land. The Antarctic coast near 161°E is at about -69.5° to -70°, so -67 is roughly 300 km offshore. That puts it on the continental slope or rise, probably around 2500-3000 m deep.

The Sabrina Coast is at 120°E and the Oates Land coast is near 160°E at about -69.5°. At -67 and 161E, the water is probably deep, perhaps 3000 m or more. The continental shelf break there is around -66.5 to -67 at some longitudes. The Southern Ocean abyssal plain reaches -4000 m at about -64, so I estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

3675.text:
The point 49.0°N, 29.0°W lies in the North Atlantic, just west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 49°N is near 28-29°W, close to the Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone, which is at about 52°N. At 49°N the ridge crest sits near 29.5°W, with depths of roughly 2500-3500 m on the flanks and in the rift valley.

The point is therefore on or near the ridge crest, and I estimate the seabed at about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

3154.text:
Latitude 55.0, longitude 9.0 is in southwestern Denmark, in southern Jutland. Tønder is at about 54.93 N, 8.87 E, and Ribe is at 55.33 N, 8.76 E. The Danish Wadden Sea coast lies near 9.0 E at around 55.0 N, so the point could fall on land or on tidal flats.

The coast near Højer is at about 8.7 E. At 9.0 E and 55.0 N we are inland, near Løgumkloster (54.94 N, 8.95 E) and Bredebro, which are marshland and geest. Løgumkloster sits at about 10 m elevation, and the area to the east around 9.0 E is likely 10 to 20 m. I'll estimate 12 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":12}

580.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -99.0° is in the Canadian Arctic. Ellesmere Island's northern coast is around 82-83°N at about -62 to -90°. Longitude -99 at 83°N is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Axel Heiberg Island (which reaches about 81°N) and Meighen Island (80°N). Land at this longitude stops near 81.5°N, so 83°N is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Sverdrup Islands, in the Canada Basin margin.

The seafloor there is probably the continental slope or the Arctic Ocean abyssal plain. North of Ellef Ringnes (about 79 to 78.5°N), the shelf edge lies at roughly 80-81°N, so 83°N is beyond it. The depth there is possibly around 3500 m, since the Canada Basin is about 3800 m deep. At 83°N, -99°, the area is near the Canada Basin's edge, and the Alpha Ridge lies further north and west. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

8356.text:
The point (-3.0, -27.0) lies in the equatorial Atlantic, east of Fernando de Noronha, which is near -3.85, -32.4. It is west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which crosses the equator near -15 to -20 longitude, and the St. Peter and St. Paul rocks sit at about -29.3. The point is therefore in the western equatorial Atlantic basin, in the Ceará Abyssal Plain / Fernando de Noronha ridge area.

The Ceará Rise lies further north, around 4°N, -43. The Fernando de Noronha chain extends east-west along about -3.8. At -27 longitude and 3°S, the seafloor is probably a flank of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge or an abyssal basin. The ridge axis near the equator is at about -14 to -17, with fracture zones such as the St. Paul fracture zone at 0-1°N. Seafloor 10° west of the axis would be about 1,000 km from it, with an age of about 25 Myr, giving a depth of around 4,000 to 4,500 m. I'll estimate -4300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

9295.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 51 is in the western Indian Ocean, east of northern Madagascar (Cape Amber is at about 49.3°E, -11.95°S) and northeast of Madagascar's coast. Madagascar's east coast near -13 lies at around 50°E, so 51°E is roughly 100 km offshore. This is the Madagascar Basin, or the slope east of Madagascar. The seafloor there is probably about 3000-4000 m deep. The Mascarene basin is around 4500 m deep, and the continental slope off northeast Madagascar is steep, so at 100 km offshore the depth is probably around 3500 m.

I estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

5491.text:
Latitude 29.0°N, longitude 3.0°E is in central-northern Algeria, in the Sahara. It sits in the Grand Erg Occidental region, west of Timimoun and north of Adrar, and the Grand Erg Occidental dunes lie roughly at 30°N, 0–2°E. At 29°N, 3°E the terrain is likely the area near the edge of the erg, or the Tademaït plateau to the southeast. The plateau is at about 28.5°N, 2.5°E, with elevations around 400–800 m.

El Golea is at 30.6°N, 2.9°E at about 400 m. The Tademaït plateau lies at 28.5–29.5°N, so 29°N, 3°E could be on the northern plateau edge, where elevation is about 450 m. The surface is solid land. I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

4103.text:
The point 45.0°N, 107.0°E lies in Mongolia, in the Gobi region. It is in Ömnögovi or Dundgovi province, near Mandalgovi, which sits at about 45.76°N, 106.27°E at roughly 1,400 m. Dalanzadgad is at 43.5°N, so it is further south. At 45°N, 107°E the terrain is probably steppe at about 1,300 m.

I could not recall the exact elevation for this spot, so I am estimating about 1,250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1250}

4908.text:
The coordinates 35.0°N, 83.0°W fall in western North Carolina, near the Georgia border. This area is around Macon County and Franklin, NC, in the Nantahala region of the Blue Ridge mountains.

Franklin sits near 35.18°N, 83.38°W at about 640 m. Moving east to 83.0°W at 35.0°N puts the point in the Nantahala National Forest, near the Jackson/Transylvania county line, not far from Highlands and Cashiers. Lake Glenville is farther north, so this point is probably not in a lake. The Whitewater River and Horsepasture River area is around 35.0°N, 83.0°W, and Lake Jocassee is at about 82.95°W, 34.97°N, which is close. The point may lie just north of Lake Jocassee, in the Toxaway area, where the terrain is steep. Lake Toxaway is at 35.1°N, 82.95°W. Nearby Whitewater Falls is at 35.03°N, 83.0°W, so the point is close to Whitewater Falls on the NC/SC border. The upper falls are at about 1100 m, and the gorge drops steeply. A plausible elevation is around 900 m, and the surface type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

13377.text:
Latitude -59, longitude -65 is in the Drake Passage, south of Cape Horn (which sits at about -56°S, -67°W). The point lies north of the South Shetland Islands, which start near -61°S. The seafloor in this area is the Drake Passage basin, with depths of roughly 3000-4000 m. The Shackleton Fracture Zone is further east, around -57°S, -58°W, and the area near -59, -65 is deep ocean at roughly 3500 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

2796.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude 13.0 is in Sweden, in Värmland near Lake Vänern. Vänern's northern shore lies around 59.0-59.3°N. At 13.0°E, the lake's northern part includes Kil and Karlstad (13.5°E), and the Kil area at 59.5°N. Around 59.0°N, 13.0°E, the point is near the Åmål/Säffle area. Säffle is at 59.13°N, 12.92°E, on the lake's northwestern shore. Just south of Säffle lies the Vänern bay area at 59.0°N, 13.0°E; this is probably water. Vänern's western part, Dalbosjön, has Åmål at 59.05°N, 12.7°E. The bay east of Säffle, around 59.0°N, 13.0°E, is likely the lake's northern Värmlandssjön and Dalbosjön area. Lake Vänern's surface is at 44 m. At 13.0°E, the lake spans around 58.9–59.2°N in the Värmlandssjön region, as the lake's northern shore near Kristinehamn is at 59.3°N, 14.1°E. So the point is plausibly water, with depths of about 10-20 m near Säffle, giving a bed elevation of about 44 - 15 = 29 m.

The lake's mean depth is 27 m, but it is shallower in the northwest. I'll estimate a bed elevation of 25 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":25}

4419.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude 19.0 is in Albania. The Adriatic coast near Durrës lies at about 41.3N, 19.45E, and Lushnjë is at about 40.94N, 19.7E. At 19.0E, 41.0N, the point is west of the coast, so it falls in the Adriatic Sea.

The coast near Fier and Divjakë sits at around 19.4-19.5E at 41.0N. The Karavasta lagoon is at about 19.45E. So 19.0E is roughly 40 km offshore. The Adriatic shelf there is shallow, with depths of roughly 50-100 m, and the 200 m isobath lies further out toward the southern Adriatic basin. At 41N, 19E the depth is probably around 100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-95}

2404.text:
Location: 63.0N, 51.0W is off southwest Greenland, in the Labrador Sea/Davis Strait region. Nuuk is at 64.2N, 51.7W, so 63N, 51W lies about 130 km south-southeast of Nuuk. The coast near 63N is around 50.5W to 51W; Paamiut is at 62.0N, 49.7W. At 63N the coast is at roughly 50.5W, with fjords and islands. Frederikshåb Isblink lies near 62.5N, 49.5W. The longitude 51W at 63N is probably just offshore, on the continental shelf, with a depth of maybe 100-200 m.

The coast near 63N: Qeqertarsuatsiaat (Fiskenæsset) is at 63.1N, 50.7W. So 51W is slightly west of it, and the area has many islands and shelf banks. Fylla Bank is at about 63.5N, 52.5W, with depths of roughly 50-100 m. At 51W the point is likely shallow water, so I estimate about -100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

9181.text:
Location: -13.0, -177.0 is in the South Pacific, northeast of Samoa and Tokelau (Samoa is at about -14, -172; Tokelau at -9, -172). Longitude -177 lies west of the Cook Islands and east of Wallis and Futuna (-176.2, -13.3), so it is close to Wallis. Wallis and Futuna sit near longitude -176.2 and -178, and Futuna is at about -14.3, -178.1. Uvea (Wallis) is at -13.3, -176.2. The point -13.0, -177.0 is about 90 km west of Wallis, so it is open ocean.

Seafloor depth there is probably between 4000 and 5000 m, in the Samoan basin and the Tonga region to the south. Between Wallis and Samoa the depth is around 4000-5000 m, so I estimate -4800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7181.text:
Location 11°N, 143°E is in the western Pacific, near the Mariana Trench's southern end. The Challenger Deep is at 11.37°N, 142.59°E, at roughly -10,900 m. At 11.0°N, 143.0°E, we are about 55 km southeast of Challenger Deep. The trench axis runs WSW-ENE there. The trench south slope on the incoming Pacific plate side, to the southeast, would be around -8,000 to -9,000 m.

Challenger Deep's three basins lie along the trench axis around 142.2-142.6°E, 11.3-11.4°N. At 143.0°E the trench axis is probably near 11.6°N or so, because the trench curves northeastward. So 11.0°N, 143.0°E is about 70 km south of the axis, on the outer slope or the Pacific plate, where depths are about 5,000-6,000 m. The outer trench wall descends from ~5,500 m to ~8,000 m within roughly 40 km of the axis. Estimating about 70 km from the axis gives roughly -6,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-6000}

9225.text:
The point (-13, -89) lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Peru, about 1,500 km offshore. It is on the Nazca Plate, west of the Peru-Chile Trench and east of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor there is roughly 4,000 m deep.

The Nazca Ridge is farther east, around 15°S 80°W. At 89°W the seafloor age is about 30 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3,800–4,000 m. I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3900}

8630.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 161.0 lies in the western Pacific, northeast of the Solomon Islands. Bougainville is at about 155°E and Choiseul at about 157°E, so 161°E is east of the main Solomon Islands chain. Ontong Java Atoll sits at about 5°S, 159°E. The Ontong Java Plateau covers roughly 0–10°S and 150–170°E, and its seabed is typically 1,700–2,500 m deep.

At 5°S, 161°E, the point is on the plateau's eastern part, away from the atoll, so I estimate the seabed at about -2,000 m. Most of the plateau is at 1,800–2,500 m depth, so I chose -2,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2100}

7414.text:
This location (7°N, 111°W) is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, southwest of Mexico, and the seabed there is likely abyssal plain. That region lies near the East Pacific Rise's western flank, roughly 1,500 km west of Central America. The Rise itself is near 105°W at this latitude, so 111°W sits on its western flank, where the crust is about 5-10 Myr old. Depths there are around 3,500 to 4,000 m. The Clipperton Fracture Zone lies near 10°N, and the area south of it is about 3,800 m deep.

I estimate a depth of about 3,800 m, so the elevation is about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5824.text:
The point (25°N, 51°W) is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and northeast of the Bahamas. This area is part of the Atlantic abyssal plain, with typical depths around 5,000 to 5,500 m. I am estimating from general knowledge of the region rather than looking up a bathymetry dataset.

The ridge axis near 25°N is at about 45°W, so 51°W lies on the western flank. Depths there are roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m, and I will use 5,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

7536.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 133.0°E lies in the western Pacific near Palau, which is around 7.5°N, 134.5°E. The point is about 1.5° west of Palau, in the Philippine Sea, close to the Palau Trench region west of Palau.

The Palau Islands sit on the Palau-Kyushu Ridge's southern end, and the Palau Trench lies to the east of the ridge near 134-135°E. At 133°E the point is west of the islands, in the West Philippine Basin or on the slope leading into it. Depths in that area are around 4000 to 5000 m, and the trench to the west of Palau runs to about 8000 m. I can't pin down the exact depth, so I'll estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

3737.text:
Latitude 49.0, longitude 95.0 lies in western Mongolia, in the Altai region. It sits near the Uvs Nuur basin, which is around 50°N, 92.8°E, so this point is east of that basin.

The area is in Zavkhan or Khovsgol province. Zavkhan's Tes river region is near 49°N, 95°E, and Khyargas Nuur is at 49.2°N, 93.4°E, so the point is east of that lake. Otgon Tenger is at 47.6°N, 97.5°E. The terrain near 49°N, 95°E is probably steppe at the foot of the mountains, roughly 1800 to 2200 m. The Tes river flows through around 49.6°N, 95°E at about 1500 m. I'll estimate 2000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

9203.text:
The point at 13°S, 133°W lies in the South Pacific, west of the Marquesas and Tuamotu region and east of the Society Islands. The Tuamotus span roughly 135–149°W, so this point is west of them and in open ocean. The seafloor there is part of the Pacific plate, likely abyssal plain or hills at around 4000 m depth. The region is east of the Tuamotus and south of the Marquesas, and it sits on the East Pacific Rise flank, where the seafloor is about 4200 m deep, with older crust farther west.

I estimate a depth of about 4300 m from regional knowledge, not from a lookup of this exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1448.text:
The point 73.0°N, 163.0°W lies in the Chukchi Sea, north of Alaska and Siberia. Wrangel Island is near 71°N, 179°W, and Point Barrow is near 71.3°N, 156.5°W. At 73°N, 163°W the location is north of the Alaskan coast (about 71°N), so it is open sea.

The Chukchi shelf there is roughly 40-50 m deep. The Chukchi Borderland and Northwind Ridge start further north and west, near 74-75°N and 160°W. The shelf break near 72-73°N at 163°W is probably around 100-200 m deep, and from memory of the bathymetry the depth here is roughly 100 m. Hanna Shoal is near 72°N, 160°W and is shallow at about 30 m. The shelf break north of Barrow lies near 72°N, and at 73°N the seabed slopes down toward the Canada Basin. I estimate a depth of about 300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

1256.text:
Latitude 77.0 and longitude 173.0 (east) falls in the Ross Sea, near Ross Island and McMurdo Sound at about 166E, 77.8S. But the latitude is given as +77.0, which is Northern Hemisphere, and longitude 173E.

At 77N, 173E, this is in the East Siberian Sea or Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands. The New Siberian Islands lie at about 75N, 140-150E. Wrangel Island is at 71N, 179E. At 77N, 173E the point is north of the East Siberian shelf, near the shelf break. The shelf extends to roughly 77-78N in this sector, so the depth there is probably 100-500 m. Near 77N, 173E the De Long Islands lie at 76.5N, 149E, which is far to the west. The shelf edge in this area is around 77-78N, so I estimate a depth of about 300 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

7385.text:
The point at 7°N, 169°W is in the central Pacific, south of the Hawaiian chain and well east of the Marshall Islands. The nearest land is Johnston Atoll at about 16.7°N, 169.5°W, roughly 10° away, so this is open ocean.

The seafloor here lies in the Central Pacific Basin, east of the Marshall Islands. I don't know the exact depth at this spot, so I'm estimating from the typical depth of this region, which is about 5000 to 5500 m. I'll use about -5300 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5300}

1310.text:
The point 75.0N, 79.0W lies in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Lancaster Sound is at about 74.1N, and Prince Regent Inlet is near 90W. Devon Island's southern coast is around 74.5N, 80-90W, and Bylot Island sits at about 73N, 78W.

Candidates at 75N, 79W: Devon Island's eastern end, near Dundas Harbour at 74.5N, 82W, is further west. Devon Island's east coast runs near 80W at 75N. The Baffin Bay coast of Devon Island near Cape Sherard is at about 74.6N, 80.4W, and the coast near 75N, 79W is probably just offshore. Devon's east coast is around 78-80W at 75.5N. The Philpots Peninsula and Cape Sherard lie at about 74.7N, 80W. So 75N, 79W is probably in Baffin Bay near the coast, in water, with a depth of a few hundred meters. The shelf there is perhaps 300-500 m deep. I'll estimate -450.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

3590.text:
Latitude 51.0 N, longitude 161.0 E lies in the western Pacific, south-east of Kamchatka. The Kamchatka peninsula's southern tip (Cape Lopatka) is at about 50.9 N, 156.7 E, and the Kuril Islands run south-west from there. At 161 E, 51 N the point is east of Kamchatka and the Kurils, in the open Pacific. Most of this area is the northwest Pacific basin, where the Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs along roughly 160 E at this latitude. The trench lies east of Kamchatka, where depths reach 8-9 km at around 45 N, 153 E. Further north-east, at 51 N, the trench axis is near 159-160 E. That puts 161 E just east of the trench axis, on the outer trench wall or in the abyssal plain, at about 5,000-5,500 m depth. The Meiji seamount chain is much farther north, so it is not a factor here.

I'll estimate about -5,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

10020.text:
The point -21.0, 61.0 is in the Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and southeast of Mauritius (Mauritius is near 57.5E, 20.2S, and Réunion near 55.5E, 21S). At 61E it lies east of the Mascarene Plateau (Cargados Carajos Shoals and Saya de Malha lie further north) and near Rodrigues at 63.4E, 19.7S. Rodrigues sits on a ridge that runs east from Mauritius.

Around 61E, 21S the seafloor is in the Mascarene Basin, which is about 4,500–5,000 m deep. The Mascarene Basin lies between Madagascar and the Mascarene Plateau, and the ridge from Mauritius to Rodrigues is a separate feature. At 61E, 21S the point is south of the plateau and west of Rodrigues. The depth there is probably about 4,800 m, so I'll estimate -4,800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

9468.text:
Latitude -15.0, longitude 37.0 falls in Mozambique. The Zambezia province coast sits near -17 to -18 latitude at around 37 to 38 longitude. At -15 latitude, the coast of Mozambique is around 40.5 E (Nampula province), so 37 E is inland.

Inland at -15, 37 is in the Niassa/Zambezia/Nampula area. Terrain here is the Alto Molócuè / Gurué region, with elevation around 500-600 m. Gurué is at -15.47, 36.98, with elevation about 700 m, and the Namuli mountains are nearby. At -15.0, 37.0 it is slightly north of Gurué, in the Ile/Alto Molocue area, with elevation about 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":560}

1193.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 47.0°E lies in the Barents Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya's northern island. Novaya Zemlya's northern tip is near 76.9°N, 68°E, so 47°E is well to the west of it. Franz Josef Land is at roughly 80–81°N, 45–60°E, which is to the north. Kvitøya is near 80°N, 32°E. At 77°N, 47°E the point is open sea in the northeastern Barents Sea.

Depths there are probably about 200–300 m. The Barents Sea near the Great Bank (Central Bank) is shallower, with depths around 100–200 m, and that bank sits near 74–75°N, 40–45°E. The area north of it, around 77°N 47°E, is probably around 200 m deep. The basin between Franz Josef Land and Novaya Zemlya (St. Anna Trough) lies further east at about 78–80°N, 65°E. I'll estimate -230 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-230}

9917.text:
Location: -21.0, -145.0 is in the South Pacific, east of the Tuamotus (which lie around 14-23°S, 135-149°W). The Tuamotu atolls near -21, -145 include Hao (-18.3, -141), Tematangi (-21.7, -140.7), and Anuanu... At longitude -145 and latitude -21, the area is south of the main Tuamotu chain's western end. Nearby atolls are Marutea Sud (-21.5, -135.5) and Maria. There are no known islands at exactly -145, -21, so it is likely open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is Pacific abyssal plain, roughly 3800-4300 m deep. The Tuamotu plateau lies north, and the Austral seamounts lie southwest. At -21, -145 the depth is probably about 4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

10759.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 99 is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, west of Australia, roughly 1,500 km off the coast. The seafloor there lies near the Wharton Basin and Broken Ridge region. Broken Ridge is around 30°S, 95-100°E, but its crest is at about 1,000-2,000 m depth. The Wharton Basin abyssal plain is about 5,000-5,500 m deep. Ninetyeast Ridge runs along 90°E, so 99°E is east of it.

Broken Ridge spans roughly 29-33°S and 93-100°E, so the point could be near its northern edge. The northern escarpment of Broken Ridge drops steeply to the Wharton Basin. At 29°S, 99°E, the point is probably on the northern flank or in the basin. The Diamantina zone lies further south. I estimate a depth of about 4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

4994.text:
Latitude 35.0°N, longitude 89.0°E falls in northern Tibet (Qiangtang / Hoh Xil area) in western China. It lies on the Changtang plateau, roughly north of the Tanggula range. In this region the plateau sits at about 5000 m.

The point (35N, 89E) is in the Qinghai-Tibet plateau. Nearby, Lake Ulan Ul is around 34.8N, 90.4E, and Hoh Xil lies at about 35.5N, 92E. At 89E, 35N, the terrain is roughly 5000-5200 m, and I'm not aware of a lake at this exact spot. I'll estimate 5100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":5100}

4581.text:
