This location is in the Azores–Biscay Rise area, or the Iberian abyssal plain's western side. Abyssal depths there are about 4,500–5,000 m. The Azores Plateau extends east to about 24°W. The Atlantic floor at 17°W, 39°N is probably around 4,800 m deep, so I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

3955.text:
Latitude 47.0 N, longitude 171.0 E is in the northwest Pacific Ocean, east of Kamchatka and the Kuril Islands and south of the Emperor Seamounts. The point lies on the Pacific plate abyssal plain at roughly 5500 to 5600 m depth. The Emperor Seamount chain begins farther north near 50 N, 165 E and the Hawaiian chain is farther south, so I assume no seamount sits at this point.

This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a lookup of the actual bathymetry.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

6855.text:
The point 13°N, 149°W lies in the central Pacific, north of Tahiti and southeast of Hawaii. The nearest land is the Tuamotus, roughly 15°S, so this is open ocean.

The seafloor depth in this area is typically about 4,500–5,200 m. The location is on the Pacific plate, with seafloor of Cretaceous age, so I expect a depth near 5,200 m. The Clipperton–Clarion fracture zone sits further north, and this point is south of the Line Islands ridge region. I settled on about -5,150 m as a best estimate from regional depth patterns, not from a lookup of this exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5150}

13848.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 157 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and east of the Kerguelen region. It is far from any land, since the Antarctic coast is at roughly -66 to -67 at this longitude. The area is south of the Southeast Indian Ridge, in the Australian-Antarctic Basin or on its northern margin near the Tasmanian-Antarctic abyssal plain.

The Southeast Indian Ridge sits near -50 to -55 at 140-150E. At 157E, -63, the Balleny fracture zone region is further east, around 163E and -66. The seafloor here is probably abyssal plain at about 4000 m depth, though the Australian-Antarctic Basin there is typically 4000-4500 m deep. The Antarctic continental margin begins near -65 to -66, so -63 is on the abyssal plain at around -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

15332.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -115 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. It lies inland of the Amundsen Sea coast, near the Pine Island and Thwaites glacier catchments. Thwaites is around -75 latitude and -107 longitude, so this point is farther south and west, around the Ellsworth Land / Marie Byrd Land interior of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet. Ice-sheet surface elevation here is likely about 1000-1500 m, and the Hollick-Kenyon Plateau region is at roughly -80, -105.

The Getz and Sulzberger ice shelves are farther north and west, and the Ross Ice Shelf edge is near -80 and -150. At -81, -115 the ice is probably grounded. Nearby features include the Executive Committee Range at about -76, -126, and the Mount Siple area. The Pine Island headwaters at about -79, -100 are at roughly 1500 m. The Kohler Range is around -77, -110. At -81, -115 the surface is probably around 1200 m. Bed topography there may be below sea level, but the question asks for the ice surface elevation, so I'll estimate 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

10119.text:
The point (-23, -101) is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and north of Easter Island (which lies near -27, -109). It sits on or near the East Pacific Rise flank. The East Pacific Rise crest is near -110 longitude at this latitude, roughly -23, -114. At -101, the seafloor is on the Nazca plate, with depths around 3000-3500 m. The Sala y Gómez ridge is further south, near -26 to -27.

The seafloor age at 13° of longitude east of the ridge crest is about 15-20 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3200 m. Near -101, -23, the region is also close to the Nazca Ridge, though that is farther east. I estimate a depth of about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

1143.text:
Latitude 77.0, longitude -53.0 is in northwest Greenland. Melville Bay lies around 75-76°N, 60-62°W, and the Greenland coast near 77°N is at roughly 70°W (Thule is at 76.5°N, 68.7°W). So -53° at 77°N is well inland on the ice sheet, east of the coast.

The ice sheet surface there should be about 2000 m. For reference, Camp Century is at 77.2°N, 61.1°W and sits at about 1880 m. Moving east to 53°W, the surface rises toward the northern ice divide, so roughly 2200 m is plausible.

