Depth there is probably about 4000 m. The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge runs near 73°E, and east of it the Indian Ocean basin is roughly 4000-4500 m deep at 75°E, 5°N. This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a lookup, so I'll use -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

10502.text:
Latitude -27, longitude -55 is in Misiones province, Argentina, near the Paraguay border. The Paraná River runs along the northern/western edge of Misiones near Posadas (-27.37, -55.9). The Uruguay River lies further east at about -54 to -53.8.

At -55.0, -27.0 the point is in the interior of Misiones, near Oberá (-27.49, -55.12) and Leandro N. Alem (-27.6, -55.3). Just to the north lies San Ignacio and Montecarlo, and a point at about -27.0, -55.0 sits near Eldorado/Puerto Rico. Puerto Rico, Misiones is at about -26.8, -55.0, and the Paraná is at about -55.0 to -54.6 near there, which is east of Puerto Rico? Actually the Paraná at Puerto Rico is at about longitude -55.02 and latitude -26.8. At latitude -27.0 the Paraná runs near -55.5 to -55.3 (Instituto/Santa Ana is at -27.37, -55.58). The river flows from the northeast to the southwest, so at -27.0 it is near -55.3 or so. The point at -55.0 is therefore likely inland, in Misiones near Montecarlo (-26.57, -54.75) or Caraguatay. The elevation there is about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":260}

8402.text:
The point is at 3°S, 65°E, in the western Indian Ocean. That places it east of the Carlsberg Ridge and north of the Central Indian Ridge. The Carlsberg Ridge runs northwest-southeast from about 60°E at 5°N to about 70°E at 0°. Near 65°E the ridge axis is around 3°N, so 3°S is south of it.

South of the Carlsberg Ridge, the region between the Mascarene Basin and the Central Indian Basin is mostly abyssal. The Central Indian Basin lies at about 4000-5000 m depth. At 3°S, 65°E, the seafloor is probably on the flank of the ridge or in the basin, around 4000 m deep. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

