Latitude 75°N, longitude 15°E lies in the Barents Sea/Norwegian-Greenland Sea region, south of Svalbard. Bear Island is at about 74.4°N, 19°E, and Spitsbergen's southern tip is at about 76.5°N, 16°E, so 75°N, 15°E is open water.

The area west of Bear Island is the Bear Island Trough mouth fan region, where the continental shelf edge lies around 15°E. Bathymetry near 75°N, 15°E should be on the continental slope, with depth around 1500–2000 m. The shelf break is near 74°N, 15°E at roughly 500 m depth. The Knipovich Ridge lies further west, around 8°E at 76°N. At 75°N, 15°E the seabed is probably the upper-to-mid slope, so I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

3924.text:
The point 47.0°N, 109.0°E lies in central Mongolia, in the Töv/Khentii/Sükhbaatar region. Its position relative to Ulaanbaatar (47.9°N, 106.9°E) places it to the east-southeast, probably in Khentii or Töv province near Bayankhongor. Terrain there is typically steppe at 1,000–1,400 m.

More precisely, Choibalsan is at 48.07°N, 114.5°E, and Öndörkhaan is at 47.3°N, 110.65°E with an elevation of about 1,000 m. Moving west from Öndörkhaan toward 109°E, the point should be in Khentii province near Norovlin or Bayan-Adraga. Elevations there are about 1,000–1,100 m, so I estimate 1,050 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1050}

