Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -117°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Ellef Ringnes Island (which reaches about 78.5°N) and north of Axel Heiberg Island (about 81°N). Ellesmere Island's northern coast is around 83°N, but at -117° we are far west of it. That puts the point in the Arctic Ocean beyond the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, near the Amundsen Basin/Canada Basin margin.

The continental shelf off the Sverdrup Islands is narrow. At 83°N, -117°, the water is probably on the slope or in the deep Canada Basin, where depths run from about 2000 to 3800 m. The Alpha–Mendeleev Ridge is further west, near -150° to 180°. The Canada Basin's southern edge is near 80°N at -120°, and by 83°N the seafloor is probably abyssal plain at roughly 3400 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

1210.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 81.0°E lies in the Kara Sea, north of the Taymyr Peninsula. Taymyr's northern coast is at about 77°N near Cape Chelyuskin (104°E), but at 81°E the coast is at roughly 73-75°N. Severnaya Zemlya lies at 95-105°E. Around 81°E, the nearby islands are the Arctic Institute Islands and Izvestiy TSIK Islands (around 75-77°N, 80-83°E). The Izvestiy TSIK Islands are at about 75.9°N, 82.8°E, and the Arctic Institute Islands at about 75.5°N, 82°E. At 77°N, 81°E the point is probably open sea.

The depth of the northern Kara Sea there is likely about 200 m. I'll go with -250 m.

