Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 25.0°E is in northern Norway, in Finnmark. The Nordkinn peninsula lies near 71.0N, 27.7E, and the North Cape (Magerøya) is near 71.17N, 25.8E. Hammerfest is at 70.66N, 23.7E, and Sørøya spans roughly 70.6–70.8N, 22–23.5E. So 71.0N, 25.0E should be open sea between Sørøya and Magerøya, in the Barents Sea or Norwegian Sea.

The seabed there is likely around 200–300 m deep. The shelf north of Finnmark is typically 200–350 m deep, so I estimate about -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

11524.text:
The point (-39.0, -171.0) lies in the South Pacific Ocean, southeast of New Zealand and south of Tonga/Samoa. It is far from any land, so the surface is Water.

The seabed depth is an estimate. The area sits north of the Chatham Rise and east of the Kermadec Trench, in the deep Pacific basin. Typical depths there are about 4,500 to 5,500 m. The Louisville Seamount Chain lies farther west, around 170°W to 180°W at latitudes of 26°S to 50°S. The chain crosses roughly 40°S near 167°W, so the point at 171°W is probably just east of it, on abyssal plain. I estimate a depth of about 5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

