The seafloor depth here is probably about 4,500 to 5,000 m, in the Central Pacific basin east of the Gilbert Islands. I'll go with about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

278.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 17°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs from the Siberian shelf past the pole toward Greenland. At 90°N its longitude is undefined, so the ridge's position at 87°N has to be estimated.

Near 87°N the ridge crest is at roughly 140°E and 60°W, which puts it on the 140°E–40°W side. At 17°E and 87°N the point is on the Eurasian side of the pole, so it would be in the Amundsen Basin, which is about 4200 m deep. The Lomonosov Ridge crest sits at about 88°N at 60°W, with Amundsen Basin depths of about 4000–4400 m to the Nansen side. Longitude 17°E is toward the Gakkel Ridge side, where the Amundsen Basin is around 4300 m deep. I'll go with about -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

5486.text:
The point 29.0°N, 7.0°W is in southwestern Algeria / southeastern Morocco, near the Algeria–Morocco border. That area lies in the Saharan region around the Guir/Saoura valleys, near Béchar, which is at about 31.6°N, 2.2°W. At 29°N, 7°W, the location is probably in the Draa/Hamada region, close to the Erg Chegaga/Foum Zguid area. It is probably west of Tindouf-Béchar, in the Hamada du Draa, at roughly 400 m elevation.

