{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11299.text:
The point at 35°S, 99°E lies in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It is roughly 1,000 km west of Cape Leeuwin, which is at about 115°E. The area is close to the Wallaby Plateau and Naturaliste Plateau, though those sit near 112°E, so this point is west of them.

Near 99°E and 35°S, the seafloor is likely the abyssal plain of the Perth Abyssal Plain or the Wharton Basin's southern part. The Perth Basin abyssal plain is around 5,000 m deep. The Broken Ridge is at about 30°S, 100°E, and the Diamantina Zone is further south. At 35°S, 99°E the seafloor is probably about 4,000 to 5,000 m deep. The Broken Ridge spans roughly 31°S to 33°S, so 35°S would be south of it, in the deeper basin to the south (the Diamantina fracture zone region), where depths are about 4,500 m. I'll estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

3386.text:
The coordinates 53.0°N, 113.0°W put this point in central Alberta, Canada, roughly 100 km south-southwest of Edmonton. It lies near Wetaskiwin and Pigeon Lake, possibly a bit south, and I'm estimating from memory of the area rather than looking anything up.

Wetaskiwin sits at about 53.0°N, 113.4°W, at roughly 780 m elevation. Pigeon Lake is at about 53.0°N, 114.0°W. At 113.0°W the point is east of Wetaskiwin, near Ponoka County and Camrose County, around Hay Lakes and Millet. Millet is at about 53.1°N, 113.5°W. Terrain there is about 750 m, falling toward the east, so I estimate 740 m. The point is probably land and not a lake.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":745}

8413.text:
The point (-3.0, 87.0) lies in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Sumatra and east of the Chagos region. It sits near the Ninety East Ridge, which runs along roughly 88–90°E, so the point is slightly west of the ridge crest.

The Ninety East Ridge crest is typically 1,500–3,000 m deep. West of it, the Central Indian Basin floor is about 4,500–5,000 m deep. At 87°E and 3°S the seafloor is probably on the western flank of the ridge or in the basin, at around 4,500 m. I could not pin this down more precisely, so I estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

15636.text:
Location: -83.0 latitude, 133.0 longitude is in East Antarctica, in the interior of the continent. Longitude 133°E at 83°S lies in Wilkes Land, inland from the coast and roughly 700 km from the South Pole, near the Australian sector. The ice sheet surface there is likely about 2,500–3,000 m. For comparison, Dome C (75°S, 123°E) sits at about 3,200 m, and the South Pole is at 2,835 m. At 83°S, 133°E the surface is probably about 2,900 m, since the ice slopes up toward Dome A and Dome C, with the Transantarctic Mountains lying further along toward 150°E.

The surface there is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

7357.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 135.0°E lies in the western Pacific, east of the Philippines and south of Guam. It is near Palau, which sits at about 7.5°N, 134.5°E, so the point is roughly 150 km north of Palau. Palau's northern end is Kayangel at about 8.1°N, so 9°N is open ocean. The area has the Palau Trench to the east and the Palau-Kyushu Ridge to the north. The Palau Islands sit on the Palau-Kyushu Ridge, which runs north along about 135°E, so at 9°N, 135°E the point probably lies on or near that ridge.

The ridge crest depth is probably around 3000-4000 m, though the ridge is not well constrained here. The basin to the west is about 5000-6000 m deep. The Palau Trench is around 8°N, 135-136°E and is about 8000 m deep. The trench axis runs from about 6°N to 9°N, west of the Palau Ridge. Since I can't pin down exactly where the point falls, I'll estimate a depth of about 4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

2451.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude 43.0 (east) is in northwestern Russia, in the Arkhangelsk region. Its latitude is about that of the Pinega/Mezen area, and the Mezen river basin lies near 44–45°E. At 63°N, 43°E the point is probably in the upper Vaga/Ustyansky area, or just north of Kotlas, which is at 61.2°N, 46.7°E. That area is a plateau-like lowland, with elevation around 100–150 m.

I estimate the elevation at about 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

7387.text:
The point 7°N, 165°W is in the central Pacific, south of Hawaii and roughly northwest of Palmyra Atoll (5.9°N, 162°W) and Kingman Reef. It sits in open ocean, and the seafloor there is deep abyssal plain at roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m depth.

The area lies between the Line Islands and the Central Pacific basin. Typical depths in this region are about 5,300 m, so I estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

15310.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -159 is in the Ross Ice Shelf region (Ross Sea sector). The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 78°S to 85°S, between about 160°E and 150°W. At -159 longitude and -81 latitude, this is in the interior of the ice shelf, a floating ice shelf, so it counts as Water. The reported value should be the seabed elevation beneath it.

The seabed under the Ross Ice Shelf is typically around -500 to -600 m. Bedmap2 puts the seabed near 81°S, 159°W at roughly -550 m. Some areas of the central Ross embayment are deeper, near -600 m. I'll go with -560 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -560}

11720.text:
This location (41°S, 139°W) is in the South Pacific Ocean, well away from any land. It lies in the southeast Pacific basin, east of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and south of the Austral and Pitcairn island region. Seafloor depths here are typically about 3,800 to 4,200 m.

The seafloor age is moderate. The East Pacific Rise lies far to the east of this longitude at that latitude, roughly 115°W at 41°S, so the crust here is about 30 to 40 million years old. That age fits a depth of roughly 4,000 m. I estimate −4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

7649.text:
Latitude 5.0°N, longitude -1.0° (that is, 1°W) lies in the Gulf of Guinea, off the coast of Ghana. The Ghana coast near Cape Three Points is at about 4.75°N, 2.1°W. At longitude 1°W the coast sits near 5.0°N, close to Cape Coast and Takoradi (about 5.1°N, 1.25°W), and Elmina is at about 5.08°N, 1.35°W. Moving east, Cape Coast is at 1.25°W, Saltpond at about 5.2°N, 1.06°W, and Accra at 0.2°W with a coast near 5.5°N. So at 1.0°W the coast is at roughly 5.22°N, which puts 5.0°N, 1.0°W about 25 km offshore.

The continental shelf there is narrow, about 30 km wide. At 25 km offshore the depth is probably around 60 to 80 m, so I estimate a seabed elevation of about -70 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-70}

11018.text:
Location: -33, -103 is in the southeastern Pacific, west of Chile (Chile coast at ~-71). It is far from land, on the Nazca Plate, east of the East Pacific Rise (~-113 at that latitude). Seafloor age is about 15-20 Myr, so the depth is roughly 3500-3800 m. The Chile Rise region is further south. At 33°S, 103°W, the depth is around 3700 m. Nearby features include the Juan Fernández and Easter Island chains, but those are not at this spot.

I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

4577.text:
The point is at 39.0°N, 25.0°W, in the North Atlantic. The Azores are at roughly 37–39.7°N and 25–31°W. Flores and Corvo lie near 31°W, and Santa Maria is near 25.1°W at 36.95°N. São Miguel spans about 25.8°W to 25.1°W at 37.8°N. So 39°N, 25°W is north of São Miguel and northeast of Terceira (27.2°W, 38.7°N), which puts it in open ocean.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge runs west of this point, at about 29–30°W near 39°N. The Azores Plateau extends east of the ridge, so the seafloor here is probably around 2,500–3,000 m deep. The area north of São Miguel and east of Terceira is fairly deep, with depths of roughly 2,000–3,000 m. I estimate about -2,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

10930.text:
Location: -31, 81E is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia and east of Amsterdam Island. It lies near the Southeast Indian Ridge? The ridge is around 78E at 38S (Amsterdam/St Paul), and farther south it runs toward 50S, 90E. At 31S, 81E, this is probably in the Indian Ocean basin, northeast of the Amsterdam plateau. The Ninetyeast Ridge is at 90E, so 81E lies west of it, in the Central Indian Basin or the Perth Basin region. Depths there are around 4000-5000 m. The Southeast Indian Ridge at 31S would be well to the southwest, so this is the abyssal plain, around -5000 m.

Central Indian Basin depths near 30S, 80E are roughly 5000 m. I'll go with -5000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

15940.text:
Latitude -87, longitude 21 is on the East Antarctic plateau, about 3 degrees (roughly 330 km) from the South Pole. Longitude 21°E at 87°S lies toward the Dronning Maud Land and Recovery Glacier side of the plateau, along the direction of the Pole–Dome A line. The Pole sits at about 2835 m and the ice surface rises toward Dome A. At 87°S, 21°E the ice surface is likely near 2900 m, perhaps a bit higher. This is a recollection of the general terrain, not a lookup of the exact cell.

For comparison, the Thiel Mountains are at about 85°S, 80°W, which is on the opposite side. The Pensacola Mountains are near 84°S, 55°W, also opposite. At 21°E the ice flows toward the Recovery and Support Force glaciers, with surface elevations around 2700–3000 m. I'll estimate 2800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

7161.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude 103.0 is in the Gulf of Thailand, south of the Cambodian coast. Koh Kong and Sihanoukville lie further east at around 103.5E and 10.6N, and Koh Tang is at about 10.2N, 103.1E. At 11.0N, 103.0E the point lies west of the Cambodian coast near Koh Kong, which is at about 11.6N, 103.0E. That puts it in the sea, southwest of the coast.

The Gulf of Thailand is shallow, and the depth here is probably about 40 to 50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

4558.text:
The point at 39°N, 63°W lies in the open North Atlantic, south of Georges Bank and Nova Scotia. It is east of Cape Hatteras and well offshore, in the region beyond the continental slope. The Atlantic continental shelf edge sits near 40°N, 67–70°W, and the New England Seamounts chain begins near 39–40°N, 67°W and runs eastward, with Bear Seamount at about 39.9°N, 67.4°W. Kelvin Seamount is around 38.8°N, 63.9°W, and Rehoboth around 38°N, 60°W. So 39°N, 63°W is close to the seamount chain, though not clearly on a peak.

The surrounding abyssal plain (Sohm Abyssal Plain) is about 4,800–5,000 m deep. The Gulf Stream region here is at roughly 4,700 m depth, and the seamounts rise from it. I estimate about -4,750 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4750}

10204.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 69 is in the southern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and south of the Mascarene area. The Mascarene Plateau lies near 60°E, so this point is farther east. It is probably near the Central Indian Basin or the Southwest Indian Ridge region.

The Central Indian Ridge runs from about 70°E at 25°S, close to this location. The Rodrigues Triple Junction is at about 70°E, 25.5°S. So 69°E, 23°S is near the ridge axis or its flank, where depths are roughly 3000-4000 m. West of the ridge the Mascarene Basin is about 4500 m deep, and the ridge crest is about 3000 m deep. The point is within about 1° of the axis, so I estimate around -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

7889.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 119.0°E is in northeastern Borneo (East Kalimantan, North Kalimantan, Indonesia). The Sulawesi Sea coast lies near 118° E at this latitude, and Tarakan is at about 117.6° E. Land extends east to about 118.2° E at 3°N, near Tanjung Selor (117.4° E) and Sebatik. The coast around 3°N is roughly 117.8° E, so 119.0° E is in the Celebes Sea, offshore from Tarakan. Water depth here is likely on the continental shelf, which extends out to about 118.5° E, and then the slope drops toward the Celebes Sea basin. At 119° E, 3°N the depth is probably around 1000–2000 m. The Celebes Sea basin is about 4000-5000 m deep to the east, near 120° E, 4° N. The Makassar Strait's northern end is near 2°N, 118° E, so a depth of about 1500 m is plausible.

I'll estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

10200.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 61 lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and south of the Mascarene Islands (Réunion is at 55.5°E, 21°S; Mauritius at 57.5°E). The point is east of Rodrigues (63.4°E, 19.7°S) and south of the Mascarene Plateau, so it is open ocean in the Mascarene Basin.

The Mascarene Basin has depths of about 4,500–5,000 m. At 23°S, 61°E, the point is probably in the basin's eastern part, near the Southwest Indian Ridge's northern extension, so I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

765.text:
Latitude 81.0, Longitude -89.0 is in the Canadian Arctic. Northwestern Ellesmere Island lies roughly between 76-83N and 60-95W. At 81N, -89W, this is near the north coast of Ellesmere Island, west of Cape Columbia (which is at about 83N, -70W). Around 81N, -89W is Greely Fiord and Eureka Sound, with Axel Heiberg Island to the west.

Greely Fiord is at about 80.7N, -85 to -90W. Axel Heiberg's north end is around 81.4N, -92W. So 81N, -89W is likely in Greely Fiord, or on the land south of it. Greely Fiord runs from roughly 80.5N, -78W west to about 81N, -90W. At 81N, -89W it is probably still the fjord, near its mouth with the Arctic Ocean-side channels. Nansen Sound is at about 81N, -91W, running north-south between Axel Heiberg and Ellesmere. So this point is at the junction of Nansen Sound and Greely Fiord, which is water. The depth there is probably several hundred meters; Nansen Sound has depths of around 300-500 m. I'll estimate -300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

10300.text:
The point at 25°S, 99°W is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and east of Easter Island (which sits near 109°W). It lies on the flank of the East Pacific Rise region, roughly 2,000 km east of the rise crest, which is near 113°W at this latitude. It is also west of the Chile Rise and the Nazca plate interior.

The Nazca plate seafloor here is about 20-30 Myr old. The Easter Island region has depths of around 3,000-3,500 m near the rise. At 99°W, the seafloor is probably about 3,600-3,800 m deep, and the Nazca Ridge (Sala y Gómez) trends further east. I'll estimate -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

3576.text:
Latitude 51.0°N, longitude 133.0°E lies in Khabarovsk Krai, Russia, in the Amur region. It is northwest of Komsomolsk-on-Amur and near the Amur River's middle reach. Komsomolsk is at about 50.55°N, 137°E, so this point is well west of it. The area is probably in the Bureya/Badzhal region or the Amur lowlands.

The Amur near Khabarovsk is at 48.5°N, 135°E. At 51°N, 133°E, the Amgun and Gorin rivers drain the area near the Badzhal range, so the terrain is likely lowland or hills. Lake Chlya sits at about 50.5°N, 136.5°E, and the Selemdzha is further west. I'd estimate the elevation at about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

4180.text:
The point 43.0°N, 99.0°W is in south-central South Dakota, near the Nebraska border. It lies in Gregory County or Tripp County, close to the Missouri River. Gregory County is at roughly 99.0°W, and Fort Randall Dam sits near 98.55°W, so the point is west of the reservoir. Lake Francis Case runs along about 98.7–99.0°W near 43.0°N, but the lake's main body lies further east around Platte and Chamberlain. At 43.0°N, the Missouri River near Pickstown is at about 98.5°W, so the point is likely land.

Land in that area is typically around 580 m. Gregory County uplands are about 600 m, and the point lies near the Tripp/Gregory line, which is rolling prairie. I'll estimate 590 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":590}

4662.text:
The point 39.0°N, 145.0°E lies in the northwest Pacific, east of Honshu, Japan, and east of the Japan Trench axis. The trench axis at about 39°N is near 143.8°E, with depths of roughly 7,500 m. At 145°E the seafloor is on the outer trench slope and the abyssal plain, which should be about 5,500 m deep.

Reference depths: at 39°N the Japan Trench is at about 143.5–144°E, and the Pacific plate seafloor to the east is around 5,300–5,600 m deep. The outer rise sits near 145°E at roughly 5,400 m. I'll estimate -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

4201.text:
The point 43.0°N, 57.0°W lies in the northwest Atlantic, south of Newfoundland's Burin Peninsula and southeast of Nova Scotia. Sable Island is near 43.9°N, 60°W, so this point is well east of it. The Scotian Shelf edge lies roughly along 44°N at 57°W, and the Laurentian Channel mouth is near 47°N, 58°W. At 43°N, 57°W the point is likely on the continental slope, since the shelf there reaches about 44.5°N toward the Banquereau and Misaine banks.

The shelf edge near 57°W sits at roughly 44.3°N. About 1.3° of latitude further south (about 140 km) the slope should reach 3000 to 4000 m depth. The slope is fairly gradual there, so I estimate about 3700 m deep, though this is an estimate rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

1694.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -31.0 is in East Greenland, near Scoresby Sund. Scoresby Sund lies around 70.5N, -22 to -26. At -31 longitude and 71N, that is inland, on the Greenland ice sheet's eastern flank, west of Liverpool Land and Scoresby Sund. The ice sheet there is probably about 2000 m high.

Checking the surroundings: the east coast at 71N runs at about -22 (Jameson Land). Stauning Alps are at 72N, -24. By -31, we are about 300 km inland. The ice sheet surface near 71N, -31 is about 2500 m. The summit at 72.5N, -38 is 3200 m. At -31 and 71N, I estimate about 2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

2506.text:
Latitude 63.0 N, longitude 153.0 E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Sakha (Yakutia) / Magadan border region. It lies in the Kolyma/Indigirka upland, near the Chersky Range's southern part and the Moma-Selennyakh depression area. Longitude 153 E at latitude 63 N is probably near the upper Nera or Indigirka headwaters, or the Kolyma Upland and Ulakhan-Chistay range. I'd estimate the terrain at about 900 m, since the area is mountainous.

The Suntar-Khayata range is at about 62.5 N, 141 E, which is further west. At 153 E, 63 N we are around the upper Kolyma, near the Yasachnaya or Korkodon area. The Korkodon basin is at roughly 63 N, 154 E, with elevations of about 300-600 m. The Kolyma upland is at roughly 500-1000 m. I'll go with 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

12339.text:
Location: 47°S, 19°E is in the South Atlantic/Southern Ocean, south of South Africa (Cape Agulhas is at about 34.8°S, 20°E). It lies on the southwest Indian Ridge region. The Agulhas Plateau sits at about 40°S, 26°E, so this point is farther southwest. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (about 55°S, 1°E) northeast to the Indian Ocean, passing near 47°S around 25-30°E. At 19°E the ridge is around 52°S. The Africa-Antarctic Ridge's northern part lies near 45°S, 20°E?

Between the Agulhas Ridge (about 45°S, 10°E) and the Southwest Indian Ridge, the area at 47°S, 19°E is probably the Agulhas Basin or Atlantic-Indian Basin, with depths around 4500 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge has an offset near the Du Toit and Andrew Bain fracture zones (about 47°S, 30-35°E), and Prince Edward Fracture Zone near 35°E. At 19°E the ridge is around 51-52°S, so 47°S is on the flank, with depth around 3500-4500 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

8546.text:
Latitude -5, longitude -7 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa and south of the equator. It is near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs at roughly 13°W at this latitude, so -7 is east of the ridge. Ascension Island sits at 7.9°S, 14.4°W, which is farther west and south, so it is not a factor.

This point is in the eastern part of the Guinea/Angola basin side, in the equatorial Atlantic. Depths in the Sierra Leone/Guinea basins are around 4500 to 5000 m. East of the ridge at 5°S and 7°W, the seafloor is probably 4000 to 5000 m deep, so I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

13558.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -63 lies in the Drake Passage, north of the South Shetland Islands. The islands span about -61.0 to -63.5 latitude and -53 to -63 longitude. Livingston Island is around -62.6, -60.4, and Smith Island is around -62.97, -62.5. At -61.0, -63.0 we are well north of Smith Island, in the Drake Passage near the southern edge of the Antarctic Polar Front region.

The Shackleton Fracture Zone lies further west, at about -60 to -61 latitude and -57 to -60 longitude. At -61, -63 the bathymetry is probably around 3000 to 4000 m deep. The Bransfield Basin sits southeast of here. Drake Passage depths are about 3500 to 4000 m, and the area north of the South Shetland Trench is about 3000 to 4000 m. The trench lies along the northwest side of the islands, at roughly -62.5 and -62 longitude, with depths of about 5000 m. At -61, -63 we are about 150 km from the trench, on the abyssal plain or the Phoenix plate, so I estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

1197.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 55.0°E is in the Barents Sea region. Novaya Zemlya's northern island spans roughly 73-77°N, with its northern tip near 76.9°N, 68°E, so 55°E is well to the west. Franz Josef Land lies at about 80-81°N, 45-65°E, which is also well to the north. The point is therefore open sea in the northeastern Barents Sea, east of Svalbard and south of Franz Josef Land.

Depths in this area are roughly 200-300 m. The Central Bank is shallower, but it sits further west near 75°N, 40°E. The area north of Novaya Zemlya near 77°N, 55°E is probably around 200 m deep, though I am not certain.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-220}

12473.text:
The point is -49.0, -73.0, in southern Chile/Argentina at roughly 49°S, 73°W. This is the Southern Patagonian Ice Field region. The ice field spans about 48.3°S to 51.5°S, at roughly 73-74°W. At 49°S, 73°W, that is near the eastern part of the northern ice field, close to Lago Viedma (which lies around 72.5°W, 49.5°S). Near 49°S, 73°W, the area is Southern Patagonian Icefield, likely close to Chico Glacier or the Upsala region. Upsala Glacier is at about 49.9°S, 73.3°W. At 49.0°S, 73.0°W the point is probably on the icefield's northern part, near Cerro Gorra Blanca or the Viedma Glacier's accumulation area. The Viedma Glacier's upper reaches are at about 49.0°S, 73.3°W. So the point is likely on grounded ice at around 1,500 m elevation.

Estimating the ice-surface elevation there at about 1,400 m, I'll go with Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1400}

5184.text:
Latitude 33.0°N, longitude 109.0°E falls in southern Shaanxi province, China, in the Qinling–Daba mountain region. It is near Ankang and Ningshan, and just north of the Han River valley. The Han River at Ankang is at about 109.0°E, 32.7°N, with an elevation of about 250 m. At 33.0°N the point is probably in the hills north of Ankang, around Ningshan or Hanyin, with a typical elevation of about 800–1000 m.

I can't pin down the exact terrain, so I'll estimate about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

5285.text:
The point is at 31°N, 49°W, which is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of Bermuda (64.8°W). It sits on the Sohm Abyssal Plain or near the southern edge of the Newfoundland Basin, and just northeast of the Bermuda Rise. The Bermuda Rise is around 30°N, 60°W, so this location is farther east.

Typical depths in this region are about 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Sohm Abyssal Plain is roughly 5,000 to 5,400 m deep, and the western flank of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 49°W would be somewhat shallower. The ridge axis is around 42°W at this latitude, so 49°W is about 7° west of it, on ridge-flank crust roughly 60 million years old. That age implies a depth of about 5,000 m, so I'll estimate -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

7664.text:
Latitude 5.0°N, longitude 29.0°E lies in South Sudan, in the western part of the country near the Central African Republic and DRC borders (Western Equatoria, near Yambio/Tambura). That area is Solid land at roughly 650 m elevation.

Yambio is at about 4.57°N, 28.4°E and sits at around 650 m. Tambura is at about 5.6°N, 27.5°E. The point at 5.0°N, 29.0°E is east of Yambio, toward Rafili and Mundri. Elevations there are about 650-700 m, so I'll estimate 680 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":680}

6423.text:
The point 19.0°N, 67.0°E lies in the Arabian Sea, well offshore of Pakistan and India. It is southwest of the Indus Fan and east of the Owen Fracture Zone. Water depth in this area is roughly 3000 to 3500 m.

The Indus Fan is deep here. The Carlsberg Ridge is far to the southwest. At 19°N, 67°E the seabed is on the abyssal plain of the Indus Fan, probably around 3,200 m deep, so I estimate -3200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

4467.text:
The coordinates 41.0°N, 115.0°E fall in Hebei Province, China, near Zhangjiakou. More precisely, the point is around Shangyi or Guyuan County, on the Bashang plateau at the northern edge of Hebei, near the Inner Mongolia border. Typical elevation there is about 1,400 to 1,500 m.

This is an estimate from my general knowledge of the region, not a lookup of terrain data. Guyuan County sits at about 41.67°N, 115.7°E, and Shangyi at about 41.0°N, 113.9°E. At 41.0°N, 115.0°E the point is near Chongli or Zhangbei, around Chicheng. Zhangbei is at 41.15°N, 114.7°E with an elevation of about 1,400 m. Just south, the terrain drops toward the Zhangjiakou basin. This location is probably in the Chongli/Zhangbei area at roughly 1,400 m, so I'll use 1,450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1450}

5839.text:
Latitude 25°N, longitude 21°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of Africa (the Western Sahara coast is near 17°W), so this is open ocean.

The point is roughly 500 km west of Cape Blanc and north of the Cape Verde region. The seafloor in this area is mostly abyssal plain at about 4,500–5,000 m depth. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is much farther west, near 40°W. The Canary Islands are at 28°N, 16°W, so they are not close by. The seafloor near 25°N, 21°W is the Canary Basin or Seine Abyssal Plain edge, and I estimate about -4,600 m from memory rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

10157.text:
The point (-23, -25) lies in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 23°S sits at roughly 13°W, so 25°W is about 12° west of it. That puts the seafloor at an age of around 30-40 Myr, which suggests a depth of about 4000-4500 m.

The Rio Grande Rise is farther southwest, at about 30°S and 35°W, so it does not apply here. The Brazil Basin lies to the west, with depths of 4500-5000 m. At 25°W, 23°S the seafloor is on the eastern flank of the Brazil Basin, and the depth is probably around 4300 m. I'll go with -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

8033.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 47.0°E is in Somalia, inland from the coast. The Somali coast near 1°N runs from about Kismayo (0.4°S, 42.5°E) northeast toward Mogadishu (2°N, 45.3°E), and at 47°E the coast lies near 3–4°N. So 1°N, 47°E is well inland, in the Galguduud/Middle Shabelle region, or possibly just in the Indian Ocean. The coast is at about 3.5°N at 47°E, since Hobyo is near 5.35°N, 48.5°E and Adale is near 2.75°N, 46.2°E. At 47°E the coast is around 3.3°N, so 1°N is inland.

The terrain here is flat, semi-arid plain at roughly 100–150 m elevation. I'd estimate about 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

11844.text:
The point is at 41°S, 109°E, in the Southern Ocean south of Western Australia. Australia's southwest corner is near 115°E, 35°S, so this is well offshore. It lies south of the Great Australian Bight, west of Tasmania, over the abyssal plain or the southern margin of the Diamantina/Naturaliste region. I estimate the seabed there at about 4,500 m depth.

The Naturaliste Plateau is at roughly 33°S to 36°S and 112°E to 117°E, so it is well north of this point. The continental slope south of Australia at 109°E sits near 35°S to 36°S. At 41°S the water is deep, around 4,500 to 5,000 m, so I chose -4600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

718.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 177°E lies in the Arctic Ocean. It is north of the New Siberian Islands and Wrangel Island, near the Lomonosov Ridge/Alpha-Mendeleev area. The Lomonosov Ridge is at roughly 140-170°E at 87°N, but near 83°N, 177°E the point is on the Siberian side of the Amerasian Basin, and probably on the Mendeleev Ridge.

The Mendeleev Ridge runs from around 80°N, 180° toward 83°N, 170°E, with depths of about 1000-2000 m. The East Siberian shelf edge is at about 77-78°N, and the Mendeleev Plain lies north of that. At 83°N, 177°E I estimate the depth is about 1800 m on the Mendeleev Ridge. The ridge crest is about 1000-1500 m deep, and the Podvodnikov Basin (about 2500-2700 m) lies to the west, between the Lomonosov and Mendeleev ridges. I'll guess about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

3460.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude -99.0 is in Manitoba, Canada. It lies near Lake Manitoba and Lake Winnipegosis, but the area is mostly in the interlake and Westlake region. Lake Manitoba spans about 50.0–51.9°N and about -98.8 to -98.0°W, with its western shore near -98.8. Lake Winnipegosis lies at about -99.9 to -99.2°W. At -99.0, 51.0°N, the point is likely land between the two lakes, near Alonsa and Ste. Rose du Lac. Lake Manitoba's surface is at 247 m, and the land around there is about 250–270 m. Near Alonsa, the elevation is about 260 m.

I estimate the point is solid ground at about 265 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":265}

6597.text:
Latitude 17.0°N, longitude 55.0°E is in southern Oman, the Dhofar region. Salalah sits at about 17.0°N, 54.1°E, so 55.0°E is roughly 90 km east of Salalah. The coast at that longitude is around 16.8°N, near Hasik at 17.4°N, 55.3°E and Sharbithat at 17.9°N, 56°E, so the coast runs northeast. Shuwaymiyah is near 17.8°N, 55.9°E.

At 55.0°E the coast is at about 17.2°N. Mirbat is at 17.0°N, 54.7°E, and Sadah is at 17.05°N, 55.07°E. So 17.0°N, 55.0°E is very close to the coast near Sadah, and the point is probably on land just inland. The mountains there are about 500 m high, with the escarpment rising behind the coast. East of Mirbat the coastal plain is narrow, and Sadah lies on the coast with mountains behind it. Since the point is right at the coast, it could be on the plain or in the hills. I'll estimate the elevation at about 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

6538.text:
The point 17.0°N, 63.0°W lies in the Caribbean/Atlantic region east of the Anguilla Bank and north of Saba and St. Eustatius. Anguilla and St. Martin are at about 18°N, 63°W, and Saba is at 17.6°N, 63.2°W. Sint Eustatius is at 17.5°N, 63.0°W, so 17.0°N is south of those islands. Nearby, Redonda is at 16.9°N, 62.35°W, and the Saba Bank is at about 17.5°N, 63.5°W.

At 17.0°N, 63.0°W, the point is in open water west of Nevis and Montserrat. The seafloor there is the Aves Ridge/Caribbean side, with depths of around 1000–1500 m. East of Saba Bank, the Caribbean slope descends, and I estimate about -1300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

13073.text:
The point (-55, 47) is in the southwest Indian Ocean, south of the Prince Edward Islands (which sit near -46.9, 37.7). It lies southeast of the Atlantic-Indian Ridge and southwest of the Crozet Plateau, roughly in the Crozet Basin or the Southwest Indian Ridge region.

The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from about (-54, 0) through (-50, 30) and (-47, 50) toward the Indian triple junction. At longitude 47 the ridge is near -48 to -50 latitude. The Conrad Rise sits near -53, 40-50 and has a depth of about 2000-3000 m, with the Del Cano Rise near -45, 45. So (-55, 47) is probably on the southern flank of the Conrad Rise, where depths are about 3000 m. The Conrad Rise has peaks near -53, 41 that reach about 1500 m. South of it, the Enderby Basin is about 4000 m deep. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

10148.text:
The point -23.0, -43.0 lies in the Atlantic Ocean off Rio de Janeiro state, Brazil. Rio is at about -22.9, -43.2, but the coast near Ilha Grande and Cabo Frio sits further east and west. Cabo Frio is at about -23.0, -42.0, and the coast near Rio at -23.0 runs roughly from -43.2 (Barra da Tijuca) to -43.5 (Sepetiba). So -43.0 at latitude -23.0 is south of Niterói and Maricá. Maricá's coast is at about -22.95, -42.9, so the coastline at longitude -43.0 is near latitude -22.93. That puts -23.0 about 7 km offshore, in the ocean.

Water depth there is shallow, about 20 to 30 m, since the shelf is wide and the 50 m isobath lies roughly 20 to 30 km offshore. I estimate a seabed depth of about -30 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-30}

1797.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 175.0°E lies in the East Siberian/Chukchi Sea region. Wrangel Island is at about 71.2°N, 179.5°W, so 175°E is west of it. The point is in the Chukchi Sea, west of Wrangel Island, where the shelf is shallow, about 40-50 m deep. Between the New Siberian Islands and Wrangel Island, the East Siberian Sea at 175°E is around 50 m deep.

I estimate about -48 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-48}

3195.text:
Latitude 55.0°N, longitude 91.0°E is in southern Siberia, in Krasnoyarsk Krai near the border with Khakassia. It sits north of the Sayan Mountains, close to the Abakan–Divnogorsk area. The Krasnoyarsk Reservoir lies at about 91.5°E, 53–55°N, so the point is near but probably not in it.

The Kuznetsk Alatau is further west, around 88°E. At 91°E, 55°N the terrain is more likely the Chulym-Yenisei basin or the Kuznetsk foothills, a hilly forest-steppe area at roughly 300–500 m. The Yenisei itself at Divnogorsk is near 92°E, 55.9°N. The Krasnoyarsk Reservoir extends south along the Yenisei, with the Syda and Tuba arms near 91.5°E, 54.5°N. At 55.0°N the reservoir lies around 91.4°E, so 91.0°E is probably just west of it, on land. This is the Bolshoi Ulug-Oy/Kyzyl area, which is hilly at about 400 m, so I'll estimate 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

2727.text:
The point 59.0°N, 125.0°W lies in the Northwest Territories/British Columbia border region. The 60°N border is just north of it, so the point is in northern British Columbia, near the Liard River and Fort Nelson Lowland, east of the Liard Plateau and Kechika area.

Around 59°N, 125°W, the Liard River flows near Fort Liard (60.2°N, 123.5°W), where the elevation is about 200 m. The point is further southwest, in the Liard Plateau/Kotaneelee area. I estimate the terrain there at roughly 600 m, in the foothills of the Liard Range. Nearby, the Liard River at Lower Post (59.9°N, 128.5°W) is about 600 m. The Petitot River headwaters are around 59°N, 123°W at roughly 500 m. The Fort Nelson area (58.8°N, 122.7°W) is about 380 m. The Muskwa-Kechika lowlands near 125°W are about 700 m, so I estimate 750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":750}

4704.text:
The point (37.0°N, 131.0°W) is in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 900 km west of the California coast. That puts it in the abyssal plain region west of the Pacific-Farallon spreading ridge remnants, near the Murray Fracture Zone. Seafloor depths at this location are typically around 4000 to 4500 m. The Murray Fracture Zone lies around 33–35°N, and the area at 37°N, 131°W is probably abyssal hills at about 4200 m.

This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a lookup of the exact cell.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4250}

9168.text:
Latitude -11, longitude 157 is in the Solomon Sea / Coral Sea region, east of Papua New Guinea. The Solomon Islands lie around 160°E at -9 to -10°, with Rennell Island at about 160.2°E, -11.6°. Longitude 157°E at -11° is west of the Solomon Islands, in the Solomon Sea / Coral Sea area, between the Louisiade Archipelago (about 152°E) and the Solomon Islands. It is probably in the Coral Sea Basin or the Pocklington/Woodlark Basin region. The depth there is probably around 3000 to 4500 m.

The Solomon Sea's deep basin is around 150–154°E. At 157°E, -11°, the Coral Sea's northeastern part includes the Louisiade Trough and the Indispensable Reefs near 159°E, -12.5°. The seafloor is likely about 3500 m deep, so I'll estimate -3800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

579.text:
Latitude 83.0, Longitude -101.0 is in the Canadian Arctic. Ellesmere Island's northern coast is around 83°N at about -70 to -80°W, while Axel Heiberg Island reaches about 81°N, and Meighen Island is at 80°N, -100°W. At 83°N, -101°W the position is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. The nearest land is Ellef Ringnes at 78.5°N, so this point is about 4.5° of latitude (roughly 500 km) from land.

This area is north of the Sverdrup Basin margin, which puts it past the continental shelf. The shelf break is near 81 to 82°N there, and the Canada Basin and Alpha Ridge lie further north. At 83°N, -101°W the depth is probably the continental slope or the abyssal plain edge, likely around 2000 to 3500 m. I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

12545.text:
Latitude -49.0, longitude 71.0 is in the southern Indian Ocean, on the Kerguelen Islands. Grande Terre spans roughly 48.4°S to 49.7°S and 68.7°E to 70.6°E, so 71.0°E lies just east of the main island. The east coast of Kerguelen is around 70.5°E at 49°S. The Courbet Peninsula sits near 49.2°S and 70.2°E, and the eastern extreme is about 70.6°E. So the point is likely just offshore.

The seabed east of Kerguelen is on the Kerguelen Plateau, where the shelf is shallow, around 100-500 m deep. Offshore to the east at 0.4° (about 30 km), the depth is probably about 200-400 m. I'll estimate -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

496.text:
The point is at 85°N, 93°E, in the Arctic Ocean north of the Taymyr/Severnaya Zemlya region. It lies beyond the Laptev Sea shelf, in the Eurasian Basin near the Gakkel Ridge.

The Gakkel Ridge runs roughly from 85°N, 0°E toward the Laptev Sea, passing about 85–86°N near 90°E. Basin depths there are about 4000 m, and the ridge axis is about 3500–5000 m deep. Around 85°N, 93°E the seabed is likely in the Amundsen Basin or on the ridge flank, at roughly -4000 m. IBCAO values in the Amundsen Basin are about -4200 m. Near the Gakkel Ridge, the rift valley depth is about -4000 to -5000 m. I'll estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

214.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -111° lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs from the Ellesmere/Greenland side across the pole toward the Siberian shelf. Near 87°N it sits roughly along 140°E to 50°W, meaning that on the Canadian side of the pole (111°W) we are probably on the Amerasian side of the ridge, in the Amundsen Basin or the Makarov Basin.

The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near 87-88°N at about 60°W to 140°E, with the Amundsen Basin on the Eurasian side. At longitude 111°W and 87°N, the point is on the opposite side of the pole from 69°E. The ridge axis near the pole runs roughly along the 140°E–40°W line. A longitude of 111°W is 71° from 40°W, so the point is on the Makarov Basin side (the Amerasian side). The Makarov Basin depth is about 4000 m, though the ridge flank may be shallower, so I estimate around -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7275.text:
The point 9°N, 29°W is in the tropical North Atlantic, west of the Cape Verde islands and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 9°N sits at about 40°W, so this location is on the eastern flank, in the open ocean.

Seafloor age here is roughly 50 to 60 million years, which would normally put the depth near 4,500 to 5,000 m. The area is also close to the Sierra Leone Rise, which lies around 5°N to 7°N and 20°W to 25°W, and the Cape Verde Rise sits further north. The seafloor near 9°N, 29°W is probably around 4,800 m deep, so I estimate -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

13702.text:
Location: 63°S, 135°W is in the South Pacific Ocean, well north of Antarctica's coast (which lies at about 74°S there). It lies in the southeastern Pacific, near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from 65°S 170°W to about 55°S 140°W, so at 63°S 135°W we are east of the ridge, on the flank. The seafloor here is probably around 3,500-4,000 m deep.

The Antarctic-Pacific ridge crest near 135°W is around 57-58°S, so at 63°S we would be on the ridge's eastern flank, in young-to-moderate age crust. The depth there is probably about 3,800 m, though it could be a bit more. I'll go with -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15896.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -67 is on the Antarctic plateau, in the Pensacola Mountains / Foundation Ice Stream region, roughly 3 degrees from the pole. The pole is at about 2835 m. At 87°S along 67°W, which lies toward the Weddell Sea side, the ice is probably grounded at roughly 2000 to 2500 m.

The Foundation Ice Stream and the Support Force Glacier are near 83°S, 60°W, so 87°S is further inland. The Thiel Mountains sit near 85°S, 90°W. At 87°S, 67°W, the ice-surface elevation is probably about 2400 m, since the surface rises toward the pole and the Recovery Glacier catchment lies nearby. I estimate roughly 2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

5023.text:
Latitude 35.0, longitude 147.0 is in New South Wales, Australia, roughly 35°S if the latitude is south. But the latitude is given as positive, so it is 35°N, 147°E, which is in the western Pacific Ocean. That point lies east of Japan, south of Honshu's east coast, around the Shatsky Rise or the Izu-Bonin area.

Japan sits at about 140°E. At 147°E and 35°N the water is deep, in the northwest Pacific basin. The Japan Trench is at about 143–144°E at 35°N. East of the trench the seafloor is the abyssal plain at about 5500–6000 m. Further east at 147°E the depth is around 5800 m. The Shatsky Rise starts at about 158°E, so it is not a factor here. The Mariana Trench region is further south. I'll use -5900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5900}

10903.text:
The point is at latitude -31.0, longitude 27.0, which falls in South Africa's Eastern Cape. It lies near Molteno, Sterkstroom and Queenstown. Queenstown is at about -31.9, 26.9, and Sterkstroom is at about -31.55, 26.55. The point is therefore north of Queenstown, around Dordrecht or Indwe. Dordrecht is at about -31.37, 27.05 and sits at roughly 1450 m. Moving further north toward Lady Grey (-30.7, 27.2), the terrain rises toward the Drakensberg foothills.

At -31.0, 27.0 the point is near the Stormberg area, west of Barkly East (-30.97, 27.58) and southwest of Rhodes. Elevations there are around 1500–1800 m, so I estimate about 1600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1600}

2160.text:
Latitude 65.0 N, longitude -179.0 (179 W) lies in the Bering Sea region near Chukotka. The Gulf of Anadyr is centered around 64N, 177-179W. Chukotka's coast near Cape Navarin is at about 62N, 179E. The Gulf of Anadyr's northern coast is around 64.5N, 177E to 178W, and the Chukotka peninsula's south coast near Providenya is at 64.4N, 173W.

At 65N, 179W, the point is on land in Chukotka, north of the Gulf of Anadyr. Chukotka's land at 65N spans from about 170E to 170W. The coast at Anadyr is 64.7N, 177.5E, and the Gulf of Anadyr's northern shore runs eastward to Cape Chukotsky at 64.2N, 173W. At 179W, the coast runs around 64.5N. So 65N is inland, about 50 km from the coast, in the Chukotka highlands near the Anadyr lowlands, with elevation of roughly 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

14641.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -57 is in the southern Weddell Sea region, near the Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly 60-83°W and 75-83°S. At -73 lat, -57 lon, this is east of the Antarctic Peninsula's Orville Coast/Lassiter Coast area. The Antarctic Peninsula at 73°S is around 60-65°W, so -57 lon is offshore, to the east of the coast. The Ronne ice shelf front is near 75°S at about 60°W, so 73°S, 57°W is open Weddell Sea, which is mostly water. The seabed there is the continental shelf, perhaps 400-500 m deep, so I estimate about -500 m.

Larsen C Ice Shelf extends to about 69°S, so it is not relevant here. The coast near 73°S is around 61°W (Lassiter Coast), so 57°W is about 4° of longitude east, roughly 120 km offshore. The Weddell shelf there is deep, around 500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

6212.text:
Latitude 21.0°N, longitude 5.0°E is in southern Algeria, in the Sahara near the Tamanrasset region, west of the Hoggar massif and in the Tanezrouft/Adrar des Ifoghas area. That puts it on land, so the surface type is Solid.

For elevation, I'm relying on recalled regional terrain rather than a lookup. The Hoggar highlands are around 5–7°E and 22–23°N, while 21°N, 5°E sits on the plateau at the southwestern margin of the Hoggar, near the Mali border region. The Mali border is near 4.2°E at that latitude. Elevations there are about 400–500 m. The Tin Zaouatene area, around 20°N, 2.9°E, is roughly 500 m. I'll estimate 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

15201.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -17 is in Antarctica, in the Weddell Sea sector. This is near the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf's eastern part, around the Filchner Ice Shelf, or possibly Berkner Island. Berkner Island spans roughly 78-79.5°S and 40-50°W, so -17 longitude (17°W) is well to the east of it. The Filchner Trough lies near 30-40°W. At 17°W, 79°S, this is likely the Brunt Ice Shelf / Stancomb-Wills area (which sits near 75°S, 25°W), so 79°S at 17°W lies inland on the Coats Land / Dronning Maud Land area. Slessor Glacier is near 79°S, 28°W. At 17°W, 79°S this is likely grounded ice sheet, perhaps with the Shackleton Range further east (80°S, 25°W). So it is grounded ice at an elevation of about 1500 m.

Ice surface elevation inland of the Recovery Glacier region is around 1000-1500 m. I'll guess 1300.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

5900.text:
The coordinates 25.0°N, 101.0°E fall in Yunnan, China, near Chuxiong and Lufeng, west of Kunming. The plateau there sits at roughly 1,800 to 2,000 m. Chuxiong city (25.03°N, 101.55°E) is at about 1,800 m. At 101.0°E, the terrain is near Nanhua or Zhongshan, which is mountainous, so I estimate about 2,000 m.

This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up elevation value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

13240.text:
Location: 57°S, 21°E is in the Southern Ocean, south of Africa. It lies southwest of the Indian Ocean sector, near the Atlantic-Indian Ridge and the Enderby Basin. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (54°S, 1°E) northeast toward 50°S, 30°E. The Conrad Rise is at about 53°S, 40°E. At 57°S, 21°E we are south of the ridge, in the Atlantic-Indian Basin, where depths are about 4000 to 5000 m. The Agulhas Ridge/Fracture Zone lies at about 40-45°S, so it is not relevant here. The Atlantic-Indian Basin is around 57°S, 20°E, and depths there are about 4500 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge at 21°E sits at about 52-53°S, so 57°S is to the south, on the Antarctic plate side of the ridge, where depth is about 4000 m.

I estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12014.text:
Location: -43, 89E is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. This area lies near the Southeast Indian Ridge or the Kerguelen Plateau's eastern side. The Southeast Indian Ridge crosses about 40-50°S at longitude 89°E, roughly at 43°S. Ocean Amsterdam Island is at 77.5°E, 38°S. At 89°E, the ridge axis is near about -42 to -43°S, with depths of ~3000-3500 m on the axis, and the flanks around 3000-4000 m.

The Ninetyeast Ridge is at 90°E but only extends to about 31°S. So at 43°S, 89°E we're on the SEIR with a depth of about -3300 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3300}

6204.text:
Latitude 21.0°N, longitude -11.0° places this point in Mauritania, in the Sahara region. It lies north of Atar (20.5°N, -13.0°) and east of it, in the Adrar region near the Richat structure area (21.1°N, -11.4°). The Richat structure is at about 21.12°N, -11.40°, so this point is about 40 km east of it.

Elevation in that area is roughly 400 m. The Richat structure itself sits at about 400-485 m, and the surrounding plateau of the Adrar is around 400-450 m. I estimate 420 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 420}

9086.text:
Latitude -11, longitude -7 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, northwest of Saint Helena (about -15.9, -5.7) and southeast of Ascension Island (about -7.9, -14.4). The point lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs near 14°W, in the Angola Basin or on the flank of the ridge.

Ocean depth there is probably about 4,000 to 5,000 m. The ridge crest at that latitude sits near 13–14°W at roughly -3,000 m. At 7°W, which is 7° east of the ridge and about 600 km away, the crust is roughly 15 million years old. Depth for that age is about 2,500 + 350·√15 ≈ 3,850 m. The area is also near the Bode Verde fracture zone and the St. Helena seamount chain, so I estimate about -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

15166.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -87 is in West Antarctica, in the interior near the Ellsworth Mountains and the Thomas Hills. The Ellsworth Mountains (Vinson) lie around 78.5°S, 85.6°W, so this point is slightly west of Vinson at 79°S, 87°W. That area is the Ronne-Filchner side, where the Institute Ice Stream flows. The Minnesota Glacier and the Nimitz Glacier are nearby, and the ice surface there is about 1000-1500 m.

Looking at the geography more carefully, the Sentinel Range runs from about 77.5°S to 79°S, between 85° and 87°W. The southern part of the Sentinel Range, around 78.5-79°S, is mostly the Craddock Massif area. Longitude -87 is west of the range, on the Rutford Ice Stream side or the Fletcher Promontory. Rutford Ice Stream is around 79°S, 82°W. The point at 87°W is therefore likely on the ice plateau west of the Sentinels, with a surface elevation of roughly 1000 m. It is grounded ice, so Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

955.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -69.0 is in northwest Greenland/Ellesmere region. Longitude -69 at 79N: Smith Sound/Kane Basin lies around 79N, -70 to -65. Kane Basin spans roughly 79-80N, between Ellesmere Island (west, about -75 to -70) and Greenland (east, about -67 to -60). At 79N, the Greenland coast is near -72 to -70? Actually, Etah is at 78.3N, -72.6W. Humboldt Glacier is at 79.9N, -67W. Kane Basin at 79N spans from Ellesmere's coast at about -76 (Cape Sabine at 78.7N, -74.5) to Greenland at about -70 (Cape Alexander 78.5N, -73). The Greenland coast at 79N is near Inglefield Land, and the ice sheet margin there. Inglefield Land runs from 78.5N to 79.5N, with the coast at Kane Basin near -70 to -66. At 79N, -69, this is likely Inglefield Land, on land or ice cap. Inglefield Land interior has the Greenland ice sheet edge; at -69, 79N, probably land or small ice caps at around 500 m elevation.

Coast of Inglefield Land: Cape Agassiz 79.7N, -66... Kane Basin's Greenland coast near 79.0N is at about -70.5 (Dallas Bugt, Hall Basin?). Dallas Bugt is around 79.2N, -69? Actually Dallas Bugt is the bay near Humboldt Glacier... Hmm, Kane Basin coast near Greenland: Cape Frazer ~79.0N, -66? I'll guess solid, ~600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

3943.text:
