{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

3480.text:
Latitude 51.0 N, longitude -59.0 W lies in the open Atlantic, well east of Newfoundland's southwest corner and north of Nova Scotia. Sable Island is at about 43.9 N, 60 W, and the Gulf of St. Lawrence lies at roughly 47-50 N, 60-65 W, so neither is relevant. Newfoundland spans about 53-59 W at 47-51.6 N, with the Northern Peninsula reaching about 51.6 N at 56 W. At 59 W and 51 N, the point is therefore west of Newfoundland, in the Gulf of St. Lawrence.

Anticosti Island is at about 49.5 N, 63 W. The Quebec Lower North Shore (Blanc-Sablon at 51.4 N, 57 W, and Natashquan at 50.2 N, 61.8 W) is the nearest coast. At longitude -59, the coast near Harrington Harbour (50.5 N, 59.5 W) and Tabatière (50.8 N, 58.9 W) is close. Tabatière at 50.83 N, 58.97 W is on the coast, so 51.0 N, 59.0 W is just north of it, likely on land, since the coast there trends east-northeast. Mutton Bay is at 50.8 N, 59.0 W, and Gros Mécatina is nearby. Moving north from the coast at 59 W, the land is low-lying Canadian Shield terrain with elevation around 100 m. The coast at that longitude is around 50.8 N, so 51.0 N is about 20 km inland, with elevation perhaps 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

3130.text:
The point 55.0°N, 39.0°W lies in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland and west of the Reykjanes Ridge. It sits in the Irminger Basin, where the seabed is typically around 3000 m deep.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 55°N is at about 30°W, so 39°W is well west of it. The Irminger Basin floor at this longitude is roughly 3000 to 3200 m deep. I estimate a depth of about 3000 m, which gives an elevation of about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15499.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -141 is in West Antarctica, in the Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°W to 160°E, out to about -150° near Marie Byrd Land. At -141° and -83°, this is near the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge, close to the Shirase and Siple Coast area, but possibly on the Edward VII Peninsula side. The grounded ice of Marie Byrd Land is east of about -150° at -78°. At -83° the shelf's eastern boundary extends further south, towards the Transantarctic Mountains side near the Reedy Glacier, which sits at about -133° to -140° longitude (Reedy Glacier is near 85°S, 135°W). The Ross Ice Shelf at 83°S could extend to roughly 150°W, and east of that would be the Marie Byrd Land ice sheet, such as the Whillans/Kamb ice streams, around 83°S 140°W.

Kamb Ice Stream is around 82.5°S, 135°W, grounded. At -83, -141 it is probably grounded ice near the shelf edge, with an ice surface elevation of about 100 to 300 m. The Ross Ice Shelf grounding line near 83°S is around 150°W to 155°W, so 141°W is likely grounded ice, perhaps on the Siple Coast ridge. I'll estimate 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

12365.text:
The point is at 47°S, 71°E, in the southern Indian Ocean. The Kerguelen Islands lie at roughly 48.5–50°S, 68.5–70.5°E, so this point is north of them. The Kerguelen Plateau covers about 46–64°S, with the northern part near 46–49°S, 60–80°E.

Plateau depths there are typically 1000–2000 m. The northern Kerguelen Plateau near 47°S, 71°E probably sits at about 1000–1500 m depth. The Kerguelen main island's northern coast is near 48.7°S and 69°E, and the shelf extends northeast. Heard Island is at 53°S, so it is not relevant. At 47°S, 71°E, I think the area is a plateau about 1,500 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

7989.text:
Latitude 1.0, longitude -41.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, off the northern coast of Brazil. The Brazilian coast near the Amazon mouth lies around -50 longitude at 0 latitude, and the coast near Fortaleza (-3.7, -38.5) is farther south. At 1°N, -41°, the point is well offshore, roughly 400 km from the coast.

This area is the Ceará Rise's western margin or the Brazilian equatorial margin abyssal plain. The Ceará Rise is near 3°N, -43°, with depths of about 3000-4000 m. The Ceará abyssal plain lies at about 4000-4500 m. At 1°N, -41°, the depth is probably around 4000 m. I'll estimate -4200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

10584.text:
Latitude -27.0, longitude 109.0 is in the southeastern Pacific, roughly 300 km east of Rapa Nui (Easter Island, at about -27.1, -109.4). The longitude is given as +109.0, which is east longitude, so the point is in the Indian/Southern Ocean region south of Australia's west coast, not near Easter Island.

East 109°, 27°S lies in the Indian Ocean, west of Australia's Shark Bay area (Australia's coast is at about 113.5°E). The point is about 500 km offshore, near the Wallaby-Zenith Plateau region. The Wallaby Plateau sits near 25°S, 112°E, and the Perth Basin and Cuvier Abyssal Plain lie to the southwest. At 27°S, 109°E the seafloor is probably the Cuvier Abyssal Plain, with depths of around 5000 m. This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry, not a known value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

11022.text:
The point (-33, -95) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and far from the coast. It lies on the Nazca Plate, east of the East Pacific Rise (which sits near -110 to -115 at this latitude). The seafloor age here is roughly 20-30 Myr, and the depth is probably about 3500-4000 m.

The Chile Rise and the Juan Fernández area lie farther east and north, around -33 and -80. At -95 longitude the seafloor is likely about 3800 m deep, so I estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

9654.text:
Latitude -17.0, longitude 49.0 is in the Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar. Madagascar's east coast near -17 lies at roughly longitude 49.4 (Toamasina is at -18.15, 49.4; Mananara at -16.2, 49.8; Antongil Bay is near -15.5, 49.8). At latitude -17, the coast is roughly 49.2 to 49.3 (near Fenoarivo Atsinanana, -17.38, 49.42; Soanierana Ivongo, -16.9, 49.58). So the coast at -17 is about 49.5, and longitude 49.0 lies inland on land.

Inland at 49.0, -17.0 is east-central Madagascar, near the eastern escarpment and the Analanjirofo/Alaotra-Mangoro region. Lake Alaotra is at -17.5, 48.5. Terrain here is hilly forest at around 500-800 m. East of Lake Alaotra, near Didy and Moramanga to the south, the elevation is roughly 800 m. At 49.0 and -17.0 the elevation is probably around 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

14221.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -177 lies in the Ross Sea region, east of Cape Adare. Cape Adare is at about 170°E, so -177 (183°E) is farther east, off the Pennell Coast and Ross Sea continental slope. The Antarctic coast at 170°E is around -71.5. At 177°W the coast is the Ross Sea ice shelf edge near -78, so -69 is open ocean north of the continental shelf break.

The shelf break there is around -70 to -72, so -69 is deeper, probably on the continental slope or rise. The Pacific-Antarctic ridge is further north. The Ross Sea western area has the Iselin Bank at about -71 to -73 and 176°W to 180. At -69, -177 the depth is probably about 3000 to 3500 m, so I'll estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

13374.text:
Latitude -59, longitude -71 is in the Drake Passage, south of Cape Horn (about -56°S) and north of the South Shetland Islands (about -62°S). The location is open ocean.

Bathymetry here is the Drake Passage floor. Near 59°S, 71°W the depth is probably about 3,500–4,000 m, in the Yaghan Basin or near the Hero Fracture Zone. The Hero fracture zone and the Shackleton Fracture Zone lie further east. At 71°W the seabed is likely around 3,700 m deep, so I'll estimate -3700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

8236.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 93.0 is in the Indian Ocean, west of Sumatra and south of the equator. The Mentawai Islands lie around 99-100°E, so 93°E is well to the west of them, in open ocean. The Sunda trench is at roughly 95-100°E at this latitude, so 93°E is on the Indian Ocean abyssal plain or the outer rise, probably near the Wharton Basin. The Ninety East Ridge is at 90°E, so this point is just east of it, with depths of about 5000 m, probably around 4900-5200 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

15097.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 135 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. Dome C is at 75°S, 123°E and Vostok at 78.5°S, 106.8°E. At 135°E and 77°S the point is inland from the Adélie/George V coast, which sits near 66-67°S, so it lies about 1100 km inland. That area is the Aurora/Wilkes subglacial region, where the ice surface is around 2500-3000 m.

Dome C is about 3200 m, and the surface descends toward 135°E. At 77°S, 135°E the ice surface should be roughly 2900 m, and I am treating it as grounded ice, so it is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

13703.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -133 is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, in the Pacific-Antarctic region. It lies north of the Antarctic Peninsula and west of Drake Passage, well away from any land. The location is near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which runs roughly through -65 latitude at -135 longitude, so the seafloor may be shallower than the typical abyssal depth.

The ridge crest in this area is at about 2,500 to 3,000 m depth, and the flanks are deeper. At -63, -133 the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge probably lies further south, near -65 or -66 at -135. The Udintsev fracture zone is near -57, -145. So this point is probably on the ridge's northern flank at a depth of about 3,500 to 4,000 m. I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

5919.text:
The point 25.0°N, 139.0°E lies in the western Pacific, in the Philippine Sea. It is south of the Izu-Bonin (Ogasawara) islands and north of the Volcano Islands (Iwo Jima is at 24.78°N, 141.3°E), which puts it about 2.3° of longitude west of Iwo Jima. That area is the Shikoku Basin / Kyushu-Palau Ridge region. The Kyushu-Palau Ridge runs roughly along 136°E, so 139°E is east of it, in the western part of the Parece Vela/Shikoku Basin area, where depths are typically 4,500–5,500 m. The Izu-Ogasawara trench is farther east, at about 142-143°E.

The Shikoku Basin floor at 25°N is roughly 4,800 m deep. Some of the basin between the ridge and the Bonin arc is shallower, around 4,500 m, and the West Mariana Ridge lies near 141°E. I'll estimate about -4,700 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4700}

13328.text:
The point is at 59°S, 163°W, in the South Pacific Ocean. That is southeast of New Zealand and north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which lies near 65°S at this longitude. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about 63°S, 170°W toward 57°S, 140°W, so at 163°W the ridge may sit near 62°S. That puts this point north of the ridge crest, on the flank.

Ridge crest depths are about 2500-3000 m. Flank depths at this distance are probably about 3500-4000 m. The point may also be near the Udintsev Fracture Zone, which is at about 57°S, 145°W, so that is not close enough to matter. I estimate a seabed depth of about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14004.text:
Location: -65, 109E is in East Antarctica, offshore of Wilkes Land. Coast near 109E is around -66.5 (Law Dome is at 112E, -66.7; Vanderford Glacier at 109E, -66.7). At -65, this is ocean, north of the coast. The continental shelf edge is near -65.5 to -66 here, and the continental slope lies further north. At -65, 109E, the depth is probably about 2000-2500 m. Bathymetry: around Wilkes Land the shelf break is ~ -66 at 110E, with the slope descending to ~ -3000 m by -64. So at -65, about -2500 m.

Sea ice is temporary, so the answer is Water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

812.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 5.0°E lies north of Svalbard, in the Arctic Ocean. Svalbard's northernmost islands (Nordaustlandet) are at about 80°N and 20°E+, and the Yermak Plateau lies around 80-81°N, 5-10°E. Since this point is at 5°E, it is on or near the western Yermak Plateau or the slope toward the Molloy and Lena troughs.

The Yermak Plateau's crest is at about 80.5°N, 8°E, with depths of roughly 500-800 m. At 81°N, 5°E, the depth is probably around 1000-1500 m. The plateau's western flank descends to the Fram Strait, where depths exceed 2000 m. The Lena Trough is at about 81°N, 0-3°E, with depths of about 3000 m. At 5°E the point sits on the slope between the two, so I estimate a seabed depth of about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

10801.text:
The point (-31, -177) lies in the South Pacific, northeast of New Zealand. It is near the Kermadec Ridge and Trench region, but the Kermadec Islands are at about 29°S, 178°W, so this point is east of them. The Kermadec Trench lies around 178-179°W at that latitude, so -177 is east of the trench, on the Pacific plate.

Seafloor there is typically about 5,000 m deep, since the abyssal plain east of the trench sits near 5,000-5,500 m. The Louisville seamount chain crosses around 30°S, 180° to 175°W, so a seamount is possible, but I have no specific one at this point. I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

7208.text:
The coordinates 9°N, 163°W are in the central Pacific, south of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands (Palmyra is at about 5.9°N, 162.1°W). This is open ocean in the Pacific basin, so the surface is water.

Seabed depth here is typically 5000 to 5500 m. The area lies north of the equatorial region and southwest of the Line Islands ridge, on Cretaceous seafloor. Depths of around 5200 m are common in this part of the basin, so I estimate -5200 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

3047.text:
The point is 57.0°N, 155.0°E. That is in the Sea of Okhotsk, off the west coast of Kamchatka. The Kamchatka west coast lies near 156°E at 57°N, so 155°E would be roughly 60–80 km offshore, which is on the shelf.

The west Kamchatka shelf is shallow, around 100 m deep near the coast. The coast at 57°N is near 156.0°E, and the shelf extends far to the west. At 155°E the depth is likely about 100 m, so I estimate -100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

2368.text:
The point 63.0°N, 123.0°W is in the Northwest Territories, Canada. It lies west of Great Bear Lake and south of Norman Wells, near the Mackenzie River. The Mackenzie River at Wrigley (63.2°N, 123.4°W) is at about 150 m elevation. The Mackenzie Mountains are further west, and the Franklin Mountains are east of the river.

At 123.0°W, 63.0°N, the point is east of Wrigley, probably in the Mackenzie lowlands or foothills of the Franklin Mountains. Wrigley sits at about 150 m, and the nearby hills reach roughly 300–600 m. The Camsell River and Root River are in the area. I estimate the elevation at about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

7441.text:
Latitude 7.0, longitude -57.0 falls in Guyana, near the Suriname border. Guyana's coast is at roughly 6.8°N at this longitude, near the Corentyne River, which forms the border at about -57.2 to -57.1. Suriname's coast near Nickerie is about 5.95°N, so at 7.0°N, -57.0 we are offshore, in the Atlantic. The Guyana coast near Black Bush Polder and Skeldon is at about 6.0°N, and the Berbice coast near New Amsterdam is at 6.25°N, -57.5. Moving west along the coast toward the Essequibo mouth at 7.0°N, -58.4, the coast reaches about 6.8°N at -58.0 (Georgetown is at 6.8°N, -58.15). So at -57.0 the coast sits near 6.3°N, and 7.0°N is about 70 km offshore.

The continental shelf there is shallow, and the depth at 70 km offshore is about 20 to 30 m. This is water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-28}

11668.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 117 is in the Southern Ocean south of Western Australia. The coast near Albany is at about -35, so this point is roughly 400 km offshore.

The Naturaliste Plateau lies to the southwest, around 34-35°S and 112-116°E. The continental margin south of Western Australia drops steeply, and by 39°S the seabed is probably abyssal, around 4000-5000 m deep. The Diamantina Zone and the Southern Ocean abyssal plain are further south, at about 45°S and beyond. At 39°S, 117°E the depth is probably about 4,500 m, though it could be less on the lower slope. I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

7542.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 145.0°E lies in the western Pacific, south of Guam and north of Chuuk (Truk) at about 7.4°N, 151.8°E. The Caroline Islands near this longitude include Namonuito atoll at about 8.7°N, 150°E, and the Mortlock Islands around 5.5°N, 153°E. The point is west of these, in open ocean.

The nearest land is probably Satawal or Lamotrek, at about 7.3°N, 146.9°E, with Woleai at 143.9°E. So 145°E is between Woleai and Lamotrek, and the surrounding seafloor is likely deep ocean basin. The Caroline Ridge lies further west, around 138 to 140°E. The area near 145°E, 7°N is likely the Caroline Basin or the Eauripik Rise region. The Eauripik Rise is at about 2 to 6°N, 142°E, and the Mariana Trench's southern end is at about 11°N, 142°E. West Caroline Basin depths are about 4000 m. At 7°N, 145°E, I estimate roughly -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

2953.text:
The point 57°N, 33°W lies in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and east of southern Greenland. It sits near the Reykjanes Ridge's southern extension, west of the ridge axis, in the Irminger Sea or Iceland Basin area.

The Reykjanes Ridge axis at 57°N is around 33–34°W, roughly. At 57°N the ridge crest runs about 31°W, with depths near 1500–2000 m on the crest. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 52°N is at about 30°W, and at 60°N it is at about 28°W. So at 57°N the axis is around 31°W. At 33°W the point is about 2° west of the axis, on the western flank. Flank depths there are about 2000–2500 m, so I estimate roughly -2300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

8247.text:
Latitude -1.0, Longitude 115.0 is in Borneo (Kalimantan), Indonesia. It lies in East Kalimantan, west of the Makassar Strait, roughly in the Barito basin area near the South Kalimantan/East Kalimantan border. The coast at this latitude is around 116.5°E, so 115.0°E is inland. The area is probably hilly lowland, with an elevation of about 50-100 m.

Latitude -1 is near Muara Teweh (-0.95, 114.9), which sits on the Barito River at roughly 30-50 m elevation. The point is close to the Barito, and the surrounding terrain is low hills of about 50 m, so I'll estimate 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

7617.text:
Latitude 5.0 N, longitude -65.0 W is in southern Venezuela, in Bolívar state. It lies near the Gran Sabana and the Guiana Shield, south of the Orinoco and east of the Caura River, so the surface is solid.

I'm estimating the elevation from memory of the region. The Gran Sabana plateau is at roughly 1000 m, but 5 N, 65 W is probably west of that, in the Caura or Paragua basin. The Paragua River sits near 5.5 N, 63 W, and the Caura headwaters, around the Jaua-Sarisariñama area, are near 4.8 N, 64.8 W. The Sarisariñama tepui is at about 4.5 N, 64.2 W. Around 5 N, 65 W the terrain is likely forested lowland hills at about 300 m, so I'll estimate 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

12298.text:
Latitude -47, longitude -63 lies in the South Atlantic, off Patagonia. Argentina's coast near Peninsula Valdés is around 42.5°S, 64°W. At 47°S the coast is near Puerto Deseado (65.9°W), and the coast at 63°W is well offshore, about 200 km east of the coast. That puts the point on the Patagonian shelf, where depth is about 100 m at 66°W and increases eastward. The shelf edge lies near 55°W at this latitude, and the Malvinas/Falklands Islands are at 51–61°W but at 51–52°S, so they are not relevant here.

The shelf depth at 47°S, 63°W is probably around 100–110 m. That is the estimate I'll report.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-105}

8545.text:
The point (-5.0, -9.0) is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa. It lies south of the equator and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs near 13°W at this latitude, so it sits in the eastern part of the basin.

Ascension Island is at 7.9°S, 14.4°W, and the Guinea Basin lies further northeast. Near 5°S, 9°W the seafloor is on the eastern flank of the ridge, within the Sierra Leone Basin or Guinea Basin. Depths there are about 4000 to 5000 m, and I estimate around -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

10374.text:
Latitude -25, longitude 49 is in the Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar's southern tip. Madagascar's southeast coast near Tolagnaro (Fort Dauphin) is at about -25.0, 47.0, so longitude 49 is about 2 degrees east of the coast, roughly 200 km offshore.

The Madagascar Basin lies there, with depths of about 4500-5000 m. The shelf is narrow on the east side, and the Mascarene Basin is further east. At 49E, 25S the depth is probably around 4800 m, so I estimate -4800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

1572.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 85.0°E lies in the Kara Sea, just off the Taymyr Peninsula's western coast. The Taymyr coast near Dikson is at about 73.5°N, 80.5°E. At 85°E, the Taymyr coast sits around 74-75°N, near Cape Sterlegov (75.4°N, 88.9°E) and the Minin skerries. The Kara Sea south of 75°N at 85°E is open sea, so this is water.

For depth, the Kara Sea shelf in this area is typically 30-50 m deep. The Gulf of Yenisei is farther west and south, and near 73°N, 85°E the depth is probably about 40 m. I'll estimate -40.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

3430.text:
The point 51.0°N, 159.0°W lies in the North Pacific, south of the Alaska Peninsula and the Aleutian Islands. The Aleutian Trench runs roughly along 51-52°N near 175°W, curving northeast to about 55°N at 160°W. At 159°W the trench axis is near 54°N, which is south of the Alaska Peninsula.

At 51°N, 159°W we are well south of the trench, on the abyssal plain of the Pacific plate. Depths there are about 5000 m. The trench is around 54°N and the outer rise lies south of it, so 51°N is about 3 degrees (about 330 km) away. The seafloor there is probably around 5000 to 5300 m deep. The Patton-Murray seamounts are near 54°N, 150°W, which is not close. I'll estimate -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

5939.text:
Latitude 25.0°N, longitude 179.0°E is in the northwest Pacific, north of the Northwest Hawaiian Islands. Midway is at 28°N, 177°W, and Kure at 28.4°N, 178.3°W, so this point is well to the west and south of them. It lies near the Hawaiian-Emperor chain's western side, probably in the abyssal plain or on a seamount flank.

The seafloor there is roughly 5,500 m deep. The region west of the Hawaiian ridge and near 25°N 179°E has a few seamounts, but the abyssal depth is around 5,500–5,800 m. I'll estimate -5,600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

7766.text:
This location (3°N, 127°W) is in the eastern equatorial Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water. The question is the seabed depth.

I'm estimating from general knowledge of the region, not from a lookup. The seafloor there lies west of the East Pacific Rise on crust roughly 20-30 million years old. Depths in that area are typically about 4000-4300 m. The area north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone, which sits near 10°N, is usually about 4300-4500 m deep. Around 3°N, 127°W the seafloor is likely about 4200 m deep, so I'll use -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

7930.text:
The point is at 1°N, 159°W, in the central Pacific. It lies north of the equator and south of Kiritimati (Christmas Island, at 1.87°N, 157.4°W) and well south of Palmyra (5.9°N, 162°W). Washington Island (Teraina) is at 4.7°N, 160.4°W, and Fanning (Tabuaeran) is at 3.9°N, 159.4°W. Nothing is near 1°N, 159°W, so it is open ocean.

The seabed depth there is roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m. This area is in the Central Pacific Basin, east of the Line Islands ridge region, and I recall depths of about 5,000 m or a little more. I'll estimate -5200 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5200}

12233.text:
Latitude -45.0, longitude 167.0 lies southwest of New Zealand's South Island. Fiordland's coast is around 166.5-167°E at -45°, so the point is near Doubtful Sound and Dusky Sound. Doubtful Sound is at about -45.3, 167.0. Fiordland's coast at -45.0 sits near 167.1-167.3°E, with Lake Te Anau further east at 167.8. Around -45.0, 167.0 are the Alpha and Charles Sound areas and the Hall Arm region. Bligh Sound is at -44.78, 167.5 and Caswell Sound at -44.95, 167.1. Nancy Sound is at -45.1, 167.0 and Charles Sound at -45.05, 167.1. The coast near -45.0 is probably at 166.9-167.0, so this point may be right at the coastline. Offshore the shelf drops quickly, and the Tasman Sea depth at 167.0 would be around -300 m or more. The land inland is mountainous, so the point could be either.

I'll go with Water at a moderate depth, since the coast near Charles Sound lies a bit east of 167.0.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

265.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -9° lies in the central Arctic Ocean. It is north of Greenland, on the Lomonosov Ridge side. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands to Ellesmere Island/Greenland, passing close to the pole. Near 87°N, the ridge is at roughly 90°E to 140°W on the pole side. On the Greenland side, it crosses near 85°N, 60°W and the pole at about 0°E–60°E.

At longitude -9° and 87°N, the point is probably in the Amundsen Basin, near the Greenland side of the ridge. The Amundsen Basin depth is about 4200 m, and the Lomonosov Ridge there is at about 1000–1500 m. The ridge crosses the pole roughly along the 140°W to 40°E meridian line. So at 9°W, close to the pole, the point is on the Amundsen Basin side, close to the ridge. The ridge near the pole runs toward Greenland at about 80°W–60°W at 84°N. At the pole, the ridge is at about 140°E to 40°W, so 9°W at 87°N is possibly the Amundsen Basin flank. I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4150}

5117.text:
The point (33.0°N, 25.0°W) is in the North Atlantic, roughly 500 km north-northeast of the Azores. The Azores sit at about 38°N, 28°W, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis runs near 30°W at this latitude. At 25°W the seafloor is east of the ridge crest on the flank, so it should be deeper than the ridge itself.

The Azores Plateau extends south to about 36°N. At 33°N, 25°W the seafloor is likely abyssal hills or ridge flank at around 3,500–4,000 m depth. The Atlantis fracture zone lies near 30°N, and the Oceanographer fracture zone near 35°N. Ridge flank depths at about 5 Myr crustal age are around 3,500 m, and I estimate the crust at 25°W is about 10 Myr old. That gives roughly 3,800 m, so I'll go with about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9287.text:
Latitude -13.0, Longitude 35.0 falls in northern Mozambique and Malawi. Lake Malawi (Nyasa) spans roughly 34.4–35.3°E and 9.5–14.5°S. At -13.0 latitude, the lake's eastern shore in Mozambique is near 34.85°E. At latitude 13°S, the lake's width runs from about 34.4°E to 34.85°E, so 35.0°E is likely just east of the shore, on land.

The eastern shore near Metangula (12.7°S, 34.8°E) is at about 34.8°E. At 13°S the shore is around 34.85°E. So 35.0°E is about 15 km inland, in the Niassa highlands, with elevation of roughly 600–900 m. The lake surface is about 474 m, and the land rises steeply from it. I estimate about 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

1483.text:
Latitude 73.0, Longitude -93.0 falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Bathurst Island spans roughly 75-76.5°N, so it is too far north. Prince of Wales Island spans about 71.5-73.9°N and 98-101°W, which is west of this point. Somerset Island sits at roughly 72-74°N and 90-95°W, so the point is on Somerset Island. Its southern part lies near 72-73°N, and Prince Regent Inlet runs along its east side near -90°W.

Somerset Island at 93°W, 73°N is probably interior. The island's western coast is around 95°W and the Peel Sound is at about 97°W. The island's northwest portion is mostly plateau terrain. The interior elevation is likely about 200 m, so I'll answer Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":210}

16047.text:
Latitude -89, longitude -125 is close to the South Pole, on the West Antarctic side of the pole. Longitude -125 lies in the direction of Marie Byrd Land, and the Ross Ice Shelf is at roughly 180 to -150. Near -89 latitude the points sit on the polar plateau at about 2800 m, and the plateau is high there. At the pole the ice surface is about 2835 m. Along the -125 meridian, going away from the pole toward Marie Byrd Land, the elevation drops slowly, so at 1 degree away (111 km) it would be around 2700 m.

The area is probably grounded ice at the head of the Ross Ice Shelf glaciers. Longitude -125 is far from the Transantarctic Mountains, which sit around 180 to -170 and beyond. Moving along the meridian 0 to -180, the West Antarctic Ice Sheet lies on the -90 to -180 side, and its surface at 89°S is about 2700 m. I'll estimate 2650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2650}

5135.text:
Latitude 33.0, longitude 11.0 is in southern Tunisia, near the Gulf of Gabès region. Gabès is at about 33.88 N, 10.1 E, and Medenine at about 33.35 N, 10.5 E. So 33.0 N, 11.0 E lies inland, near Dahar or Ksar Ghilane, west of Tataouine (32.93 N, 10.45 E). Tataouine is at roughly 250 m.

Moving east from there, the Dahar plateau gives way to the Jeffara plain toward the coast. Ben Gardane is at 33.14 N, 11.22 E and is about 20 m. At 11.0 E and 33.0 N I would expect the area south of Medenine, around Beni Khedache, on the Jeffara plain or the foot of the Dahar. I estimate about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

7644.text:
The point is at 5.0°N, 11.0°W. That puts it in the Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Liberia. The Liberian coast near 5°N runs at about 8°W, so 11°W is roughly 330 km offshore.

The continental shelf off Liberia is narrow, and the water there is deep. Further out, the area lies near the Sierra Leone Rise, which sits around 5°N and 20°W, so it is not at this point. Between the coast and the rise the seabed in this region is probably abyssal plain at roughly 4,000 to 4,500 m depth. Recalling the sea floor off Liberia, the Liberian basin near 5°N, 11°W is probably about 4,500 m deep, and it may be closer to 4,000 m. I'll go with -4,300 m as an estimate.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

8458.text:
The point (-3.0, 177.0) lies in the central Pacific, south of the equator and just west of the Gilbert Islands (Kiribati). Abemama is near 0.4°N, 173.8°E, and Tamana is near 2.5°S, 175.97°E. Arorae is near 2.6°S, 176.8°E, which is very close to this point, though 177.0 E at -3.0 is about 40 km south-southeast of Arorae's position, so the point is open ocean.

The seabed around the southern Gilberts is deep, roughly 4,000 to 5,000 m. Arorae is a steep-sided atoll rising from about 4,500 m depth. At 40 km away the depth is probably around 4,500 m. I am estimating this from regional knowledge, not from a bathymetry lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

8828.text:
The point at 9°S, 163°W lies in the central Pacific, near the Tokelau Islands. Atafu is at about 8.55°S, 172.5°W, and Nassau and Pukapuka are around 165.8°W, so 163°W is east of those, in open ocean. The Manihiki Plateau lies around 10°S, 161°W and has depths of about 2,000 to 3,000 m. The Manihiki atoll is at 10.4°S, 161°W. At 9°S, 163°W the point is probably on the western or northwestern edge of the plateau. The plateau is roughly 10–11°S, 163–173°W in extent, and depths there are about 3,000–5,000 m. North of it the basin floor is about 5,000 m deep.

I estimate the seabed at about -4,800 m, with some uncertainty about whether this is plateau or basin.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

11856.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 133 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Great Australian Bight and well off the coast of South Australia. The coast near Ceduna is at about -32, so this point is roughly 1000 km south of the coast.

The area south of Australia at 41°S lies beyond the continental shelf and slope, on the abyssal plain. The Australian-Antarctic Basin is deep there, and the southern margin of Australia's slope reaches roughly -4000 m near 36°S. At 41°S, 133°E the depth is probably around 4500 m. I'm estimating this from general knowledge of the bathymetry rather than a lookup, so I'll use -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

15018.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -23 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, at the Filchner-Ronne area. Longitude -23 is east of the Filchner Ice Shelf, which spans roughly -30 to -40. Around -23, -77 lies the Brunt/Stancomb-Wills region? Brunt Ice Shelf is near -25 to -20 at about -75.5. Further south at -77, -23 is likely the Slessor Glacier region or the grounded ice of Coats Land / Dronning Maud Land margin. Slessor Glacier is around -30 to -26 longitude, latitude -79 to -78. At -77, -23 it is likely grounded ice in Coats Land, with an ice surface elevation of perhaps 1000 m.

The Theron Mountains sit around -79, -27. At -77, -23 the ice is probably inland of the Caird Coast, which lies near -74 to -77 at longitudes -20 to -30. The ice surface there is probably around 1000 to 1200 m, so I'll estimate 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

7467.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude -5.0° is in central Côte d'Ivoire, near Bouaké (about 7.69°N, -5.03°). The point lies slightly south of Bouaké, close to the Lac de Kossou area, which sits around 7.0°N, -5.5°. Kossou dam is at about 7.0°N, -5.47°, so the lake's eastern reach may be near -5.3. At -5.0 the point is probably land, on the plateau at about 200 m.

I estimate the elevation at about 190 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":190}

13441.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 63 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Kerguelen Plateau and southeast of Crozet. Kerguelen is at about 49°S, 69°E. At 59°S, 63°E the seabed is in the Australian-Antarctic Basin or on its edge, and the area just south of the plateau is deep. The southwest Indian Ridge lies further north, near 50°S at that longitude. The Princess Elizabeth Trough sits around 64°S, 80°E, so it is not relevant here.

I estimate the depth at roughly 3500 to 4500 m and will go with -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14599.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -141 is in the Ross Sea sector of Antarctica, near the Ford Ranges and Marie Byrd Land coast. The Sulzberger Ice Shelf sits around -77 latitude and -150 longitude, so -73 is farther north. At longitude -141 the Marie Byrd Land coast lies near -77, which puts -73 in the Amundsen Sea or the open Ross Sea, probably open ocean.

The coast near Marie Byrd Land at -145 is around -77 (the Sulzberger Bay area), and the Executive Committee Range sits near -76.8 and -126. At -141 and -73 there is no land, since the continental shelf edge is around -74 to -76. That makes this point continental slope or shelf. West of 135°W the Ross Sea's eastern edge near Edward VII Peninsula is at -77 and -155. The open ocean here is probably deep, and the continental shelf edge there is near -76 to -74, so the seabed is probably around -2500 m. I'll guess the slope at about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

1454.text:
Latitude 73.0, Longitude -151.0 is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Alaska's North Slope (Barrow is at about 71.3N, -156.8W). At 73N the point lies in the Beaufort Sea, north of the coast and beyond the continental shelf edge, which sits at roughly 71N near 151W. The Canada Basin begins around there, and the slope runs from about 71.5N to 73N.

Near 150W the shelf break is at about 71.3N. By 73N the depth would be around 3000 m or more. The Chukchi Borderland lies further west, at about 75N and 160W. At 73N and 151W the bathymetry is the Canada Basin slope, probably around 3,500 m deep. Barrow Canyon is at about 72N and 156W. IBCAO shows the 3,000 m contour near 72.5N at 150W, and the abyssal plain at about 3,800 m, so 73N would be around 3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

1833.text:
The point 69.0°N, 113.0°W is in Nunavut/Northwest Territories, near Coronation Gulf and Kugluktuk (67.8N, 115.1W). At 69N, 113W, it sits on Victoria Island's southern part or in Dease Strait. Victoria Island's southern coast near Cambridge Bay is at 69.1N, 105W. The southwest of Victoria Island at 113W reaches about 70N at the western end of Wollaston Peninsula (Wollaston Peninsula spans about 112-118W, 69-70N). Coronation Gulf lies at about 68N, and Wollaston Peninsula's southern coast is around 68.5N. So 69N, 113W is likely on land on Victoria Island, with elevation around 100 m.

Ground there is fairly low, about 50-150 m, so I estimate 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

15536.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -67 is in Antarctica, in the Filchner-Ronne region. At 83°S and 67°W, this is near the Foundation Ice Stream / Support Force Glacier area, or the Ronne Ice Shelf's southern margin. The Ronne Ice Shelf extends to about 83°S at longitudes of roughly 60-80°W. The Pensacola Mountains lie around 84°S, 55°W, and the Foundation Ice Stream is near 83°S, 59°W. The Rutford Ice Stream is at about 79°S, 82°W. The Institute Ice Stream is at about 82°S, 75°W. The Bungenstock Ice Rise lies around 82°S, 77°W.

At 83°S, 67°W, the area is probably the southern Ronne Ice Shelf or the grounding zone near the Foundation Ice Stream and the Academy Glacier. The Ronne ice shelf grounding line in the south is around 83.5°S near 65°W, so this point may be on the shelf, which would make it Water. The seabed there is perhaps around -500 m. Alternatively it could be grounded ice at an elevation of about 100 m. I'll go with Water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

4766.text:
Latitude 37.0, longitude -7.0 falls in southwestern Spain/Portugal. The Algarve coast sits near 37.0°N, and the Guadiana River mouth is near -7.4°. At -7.0° longitude, the coast is roughly 37.17°N (near Tavira/Cabanas, at -7.6 to -7.9). Near Huelva, the coast is at about 37.2°N, with Isla Cristina at -7.3°. So 37.0, -7.0 is south of the coast, in the Gulf of Cádiz.

The coast near -7.0 (Mazagón/Huelva area) is at about 37.13°N, so the point is about 15 km offshore. The shelf there is gentle, with depth around 30 to 40 m at 15 km offshore, so I estimate about -35 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-35}

10636.text:
The point is at 29°S, 147°W, in the South Pacific. That puts it east of the Austral Islands and south of the Society Islands, in open ocean in the southeast Pacific basin. The nearest land is Rapa Iti at about 27.6°S, 144.3°W, which is a few hundred kilometers away, so this is not land.

I'm estimating the depth from memory of regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup. Seafloor in this part of the Pacific is typically 4000 to 4500 m deep, and the area is on the Pacific plate with seafloor roughly 70 Myr old. That age fits a depth of about 4300 to 4400 m, so I'll use about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

10632.text:
The point is at -29.0, -155.0, in the South Pacific southeast of Tahiti and the Austral Islands, so it is open ocean and the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is deep ocean floor on the Pacific plate, with typical depths of about 4000–5000 m in this region. The nearby Austral seamounts and the French Polynesia region sit on seafloor of roughly 4300–4500 m depth. I could not pin down a more exact value, so I'm using an estimate of -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1428.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 157.0 E lies in the Laptev Sea region, north of the Siberian coast. The Lena delta is at about 73 N, 126 E, and the New Siberian Islands span roughly 73-76 N, 138-150 E. Kotelny Island is near 75 N, 140 E, and Bunge Land is near 74.5 N, 142 E. So 157 E is east of the New Siberian Islands, in the East Siberian Sea or the Laptev Sea boundary region. The coast near 157 E is at about 71 N, so 75 N is open sea well north of land.

The shelf there is fairly shallow. Near 75 N, 157 E, the shelf edge is at about 77-78 N, and depths there are likely around 100-200 m. I estimate the seabed at roughly -100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

10464.text:
The point is at -27.0, -131.0 in the South Pacific. That is east of Easter Island (-27.1, -109.3) and west of Pitcairn (-25.07, -130.1), so it sits near the Pitcairn–Gambier area. Pitcairn is at -25.07, which is about 2 degrees of latitude north, so the point is not on the island.

The seafloor in this part of the Pacific is the East Pacific Rise flank region, with typical depths of 3000–4000 m. The Pitcairn hotspot seamounts lie to the southeast, near -25.4, -129.3. Around -27, -131 the crust is fairly young, and the depth is probably about 3500–4000 m. I'll estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2389.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude -81.0 is in northern Hudson Bay or near Southampton Island and Foxe Basin. Southampton Island spans roughly 63.5–66N and 80–87W. At 63N, 81W we are just south of Southampton Island, in northwestern Hudson Bay, between Coats Island (62.8N, 82.5W) and the Southampton Island south coast. Coats Island lies at 62.5–63.5N, 81.5–83W, so 81W is just east of its eastern tip. That places the point probably in water.

Hudson Bay depths there are about 100 m or less. Between Coats Island and Mansel Island the water is likely 100–150 m deep, so I estimate -110 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

6120.text:
Location: 21°N, 179°W, in the North Pacific, north of the Hawaiian Islands chain's northwestern end. Kure Atoll is at 28.4°N, 178.3°W, and Midway is at 28.2°N, 177.4°W. At 21°N, 179°W, this is well southwest of them, in the deep Pacific plain.

The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain runs to the northwest, but 21°N, 179°W is south of it. The area is probably abyssal plain at about 5,500 m depth, possibly 5,300 m. I'll estimate -5400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

11557.text:
The point is at 39°S, 105°W, in the southeast Pacific Ocean. It lies west of Chile, roughly 3,000 km offshore, and just east of the East Pacific Rise. The rise crest is near 113°W at this latitude, so the point sits on its eastern flank. The seafloor there is probably about 3,500 to 3,800 m deep.

Checking against the nearby geography: the Chile Rise lies farther south, near 38°S and 92°W. At 105°W the crust is about 10 to 15 Myr old, which fits a depth of about 3,400 m. I'll estimate -3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

14349.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 79 lies in East Antarctica, near Prydz Bay. Prydz Bay is roughly 70-78°E at about -68 to -69.5°, and the Amery Ice Shelf sits further south at about -70 to -73°. The Ingrid Christensen Coast is near 76-80°E, with the Vestfold Hills at 78°E and -68.5°. Davis Station is at 77.97°E, -68.58°. So at 79°E, -69.0 we are slightly east of Davis and about 50 km south of the coast, which is around -68.6 to -68.7 at that longitude. This puts the point on the ice sheet, likely grounded ice on the edge of the plateau.

The ice surface there is probably around 1000-1500 m. Ice rises quickly inland: at -69 near 79°E, which is about 40 km inland, the surface is probably about 1000 m. The Sørsdal Glacier is at about 78.5°E and -68.7°, flowing into the sea, and it is floating at its tongue. The point at 79°E, -69.0 might be near the glacier, but I will go with grounded ice at about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

15154.text:
The point is at 79°S, 111°W, in West Antarctica. That places it in the Marie Byrd Land interior, on the ice sheet toward the Amundsen Sea sector and the Pine Island/Thwaites drainage basins. Pine Island's catchment lies at roughly 75°S, 100°W, and the Thwaites catchment extends to about 78°S, 110°W.

At 79°S, 111°W the ice surface is likely about 1,500 m or a bit higher. The ice there sits on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet divide region, where surface elevations are around 1,200 to 1,800 m. This is a rough estimate from memory of the region rather than a lookup. The point is grounded ice, so the type is Solid and the elevation is the ice surface.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1350}

783.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -53.0° (West) falls in northwest Greenland, in the Nares Strait region. Kane Basin lies near 80°N, 70°W, and Washington Land is around 80°N, 65°W. Humboldt Glacier is near 80.5°N, 67°W. At 53°W and 81°N, this is in northern Greenland, east of Nares Strait. Hall Land sits at about 81.5°N, 60°W, and Nyeboe Land at 81.7°N, 50°W. J.P. Koch Fjord is near 81°N, 50°W. At 53°W and 81°N, the point is likely on the Greenland ice sheet margin or in the ice-free land of Warming Land and Nyeboe Land. The ice cap there is probably grounded ice with an elevation around 800 m.

I'll estimate Solid at roughly 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

4614.text:
Latitude 39.0, longitude 49.0 falls in the Caspian Sea region. Azerbaijan's coast is near 49°E at about 40°N, and Iran's Caspian coast is at about 37°N, so the point at 39°N, 49°E lies in the western part of the South Caspian basin. The shoreline near Lankaran (about 38.75°N, 48.85°E) is close by, so the point could be near the coast or slightly offshore. Lankaran is at about 48.85°E, and the coast there runs roughly north-south, with the coast near 39°N at about 49.0°E. Because Lankaran is at 38.75°N, 48.85°E, and the coast heads northeast toward Alat (about 39.0°N, 49.4°E), the point at 39.0°N, 49.0°E is likely inland, on the Mughan/Salyan plain. That area is below sea level near the Kura mouth.

The Caspian surface is about -28 m. The coast near 49.0°E at 39.0°N: the Kura river mouth is at about 39.4°N, 49.3°E, and the coast at 39.0°N is around 49.3°E. Therefore the point is probably land on the Mughan plain at roughly -20 m elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":-20}

5523.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude 67.0 falls in Balochistan, Pakistan. It is near Surab and Kalat, to the south of Quetta. Surab sits at about 28.5°N, 66.3°E, so this point lies a little east of there and a little north of Khuzdar. Khuzdar is at 27.8°N, 66.6°E. The area is mountainous, with the Central Brahui range, and the terrain around Mastung and Kalat is high.

Kalat is at 29.0°N, 66.6°E and has an elevation of about 2000 m. Longitude 67.0 is further east. That puts the point near the Mula river area or the Kirthar/Pab range, likely between Kalat and Khuzdar, at roughly 1700 m. I'll estimate 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

11732.text:
Location: -41, -115 is in the South Pacific, east of the East Pacific Rise, west of Chile. The East Pacific Rise crest sits near 112-115°W at about 40°S... The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies further south, around 55-65°S at 115°W. At 41°S, 115°W, the seafloor is on the Nazca/Pacific plate area, with depths around 3000-3800 m. The EPR at 40°S is near 110°W, with a crest depth of about 2800 m. At 115°W the seafloor is about 3,500 m deep, maybe 3,300.

I'll answer -3400.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3400}

13434.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 49 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of the Indian Ocean basin, roughly north of the Enderby Basin and west of the Kerguelen Plateau. Antarctica's coast is at about -67 to -68 at this longitude, so the point is open ocean.

The Enderby Basin and Crozet Basin depths are typically 4000-5000 m. Around -59, 49 the area is likely the southern part of the Indian-Antarctic Ridge region or the Enderby abyssal plain. The Southwest Indian Ridge sits at about -50 to -53 near 49E, and the Princess Elizabeth Trough lies further to the east. The southern flank at -59 should be abyssal plain at about 4,500 m, so I estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

8995.text:
Latitude -9.0, longitude 171.0 lies in the Pacific Ocean. The nearest land is Nukunonu, Fakaofo and Atafu in Tokelau, which sit near 172°W (longitude -172), so they are on the other side of the dateline. East longitude 171° is near the Marshall Islands and Kiribati in the north and Tuvalu to the south-west. Tuvalu's Nanumea is at about 176°E and -5.7°, so it is not close either.

The point is therefore open ocean, roughly between Tuvalu and Tokelau, to the east of the Ellice Islands. The Samoa Basin and the Ellice Basin are both nearby, and seafloor depths in this area are around 5000 to 5500 m. I'll take about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

13736.text:
Latitude -63.0, longitude -67.0 is in the Antarctic Peninsula region. The South Shetland Islands lie at about -62 to -63 latitude and -58 to -63 longitude, and Anvers Island is at about -64.5, -63.5. At -67 longitude and -63 latitude, the point is west of the Palmer Archipelago and north of Brabant Island, in the Gerlache Strait / Bransfield Strait western entrance, so it is probably open ocean.

Brabant Island spans -64.0 to -64.5 and Anvers Island starts around -64.3. The point at -63.0 is therefore well north of both, in the Drake Passage near the western end of the Bransfield Strait. The South Shetlands' Smith Island sits at -62.97, -62.5, which is east of the point. The point is therefore ocean, with a depth of perhaps 1000 m on the shelf edge. The shelf northwest of the Peninsula is about 500 m deep, and the shelf break occurs further out. At -63, -67 the seafloor is probably on the shelf, around -500 m. Bathymetry there is rough, with the Bransfield basin to the east. I'll estimate -900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

556.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -147.0° lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago and Alaska, in the Canada Basin / Alpha Ridge region. Alpha Ridge is around 85°N, 150°W. At 83°N, 147°W, the seafloor is probably on the southern flank of Alpha Ridge or in the Canada Basin abyssal plain. Depths in the Canada Basin are about 3800 m. Toward the Alpha Ridge the seafloor shallows to about 1500-2500 m.

In IBCAO, the area near 83N, 147W is probably around 2000 m deep. The Chukchi Borderland is at about 75-80N, 160-170W, so it is not close. Mendeleev Ridge is on the other side. Canada Basin's northern edge near 83N, 140W is roughly 3500 m deep, and the Alpha Ridge begins near 84N. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

5394.text:
The point 31°N, 169°E lies in the northwest Pacific, east of the Mariana/Izu region and south of Japan. It is in open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The nearby seafloor is probably the Pacific plate abyssal plain, with some seamounts. Southeast of Japan, the area near 31°N, 169°E is roughly the western Pacific basin, east of the Shatsky Rise and Marcus-Wake seamounts. Typical depths there are about 5,500–6,000 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge rather than a lookup, so I'll use about -5,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

4426.text:
The point 41.0°N, 33.0°E is in northern Turkey, in the Kastamonu/Karabük area. The Black Sea coast is at roughly 41.9°N near İnebolu (33.7°E), so this point is inland, about 100 km from the coast. Kastamonu city is at 41.38°N, 33.78°E. Karabük is at 41.2°N, 32.6°E. Ilgaz is at 40.9°N, 33.6°E. So the point lies between Karabük and Çankırı, near Eskipazar or Araç; Araç is at 41.24°N, 33.33°E.

The terrain there is mountainous, with elevations of roughly 1000 to 1400 m. Around Çerkeş (40.8°N, 32.9°E) the elevation is about 1100 m, and the 41.0°N, 33.0°E point is probably in the hills near Kurşunlu or Korgun at about 1200 m. I'll estimate 1150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1150}

5885.text:
Latitude 25.0°N, longitude 71.0°E lies in the Thar Desert, in Rajasthan, India, near the border with Pakistan. Jaisalmer is at about 26.9°N, 70.9°E, and Barmer is at about 25.75°N, 71.4°E. A point at 25.0°N, 71.0°E is south of Barmer, near the Sindh border, in the area around Gadra Road/Chohtan. Elevation there is about 50 m.

Parts of the Rann of Kutch are further south and west, near 24°N, 70°E, so this point is on land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":45}

8025.text:
Latitude 1.0 N, longitude 31.0 E lies in western Uganda, near the Semliki/Lake Albert region. Lake Albert spans about 1.2–2.0 N and 30.4–31.2 E. At 1.0 N the area is south of the lake, around Kibaale and Hoima district, with Lake Albert's southern end near 1.2 N at about 30.9 E.

The terrain here is plateau at roughly 1000–1200 m. The Albertine rift escarpment sits to the west, near 30.7 E at 1.0 N, so 31.0 E is likely on the plateau. Hoima is at 1.43 N, 31.35 E at about 1100 m. The lake's surface is 619 m. Since 31.0 E at 1.0 N is southeast of the lake's southern tip, the elevation is probably around 1050 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1050}

1130.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude -79.0°W lies in the Canadian Arctic. Devon Island's southern coast is near 74.5°N, and its northern coast is near 76.5-77°N. Jones Sound lies at roughly 76°N, 80°W, and Ellesmere Island's Colin Archer Peninsula is near 76.5°N, 80°W. Ellesmere's southern coast at about 76.4°N lies between Jones Sound and Hell Gate. At 77°N, 79°W, the point is in Ellesmere Island's interior, east of Baumann Fiord, around Sverdrup Pass or the Prince of Wales Icefield. The Prince of Wales Icefield is near 78.5°N, 80°W. Ellesmere's southern part at 77°N, 79°W is near Makinson Inlet (77.2°N, 78.5°W) and Vendom Fiord (77.0°N, 79°W?). Makinson Inlet is at about 77.2°N, 78°W. Vendom Fiord is at about 77.3°N, 83°W. So this point is probably near Makinson Inlet, which could be fjord water or the surrounding land.

Makinson Inlet runs roughly north-south around 77.0-77.3°N and 78-79°W, so the point could be in the fjord, with a depth of perhaps -200 m. Alternatively it could be on the nearby land, with an elevation of roughly 500-700 m. I think the fjord is more likely, so I'll go with water, though I'm uncertain.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

13781.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 23 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Africa. It is well north of the Antarctic coast, which is at about -70 at this longitude. The area is south of the Southwest Indian Ridge, in the Enderby Basin or near the Del Cano Rise, and the Conrad Rise is further east at about 40-50E. At 63S, 23E the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or the flank of the Atlantic-Indian Basin, at roughly -4000 to -5000 m.

The Southwest Indian Ridge sits near 53S at 23E, and the Antarctic margin lies further south. Between them is the Atlantic-Indian Basin at about 4500 m depth. At 63S, 23E the seafloor is probably on the Weddell Sea side of the Bouvet-to-Enderby region, with depths around 4800 m. I'll estimate -4800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

5795.text:
Latitude 25.0, longitude -109.0 is in the Gulf of California / Pacific region. Sinaloa's coast near Topolobampo sits around 25.6N, 109.05W, so 25.0N, 109.0W is south of that, near the Sinaloa coast. The coast around Navolato and Altata is at about 24.6N, 107.9W. Near 109.0W, the coast runs from Topolobampo (25.6N, 109.05W) toward Isla San Ignacio (25.4N, 108.9W) and Lechuguilla. At 25.0N, 109.0W the point is likely in the Gulf, roughly 40 km southwest of the coast, since the coast at that latitude is near 108.4W.

The Gulf is shallow here, with the shelf running out to about 108.9W. The coastline near Agiabampo is at 26.3N. South of Topolobampo, the coast trends southeast: Playa Las Glorias is at 25.3N, 108.5W, and Altata at 24.6N, 107.9W. So at 25.0N the coast is near 108.2W. The point at 109.0W is about 80 km offshore, where the depth is perhaps 60 to 100 m. The gulf deepens toward the southwest, and Pacific-side Baja is at about 110W. I'll estimate -100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

1714.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 9.0°E lies in the Norwegian Sea, northwest of Norway's coast (the North Cape is at about 25.8°E) and southeast of Jan Mayen (8°W, 71°N). Bear Island is at 19°E, 74.4°N, so it is not nearby.

The coast of Norway at 71°N is around 25°E (Nordkinn, near 28°E), and the continental shelf edge off northern Norway lies at about 15°E at 71°N. Near 9°E, 71°N, we are west of the shelf edge, on the continental slope or in the Lofoten Basin. The Lofoten Basin is about 3,200 m deep, centered near 70°N, 3°E. The Vøring Plateau lies near 67°N. At 71°N, 9°E, the slope towards the Lofoten Basin is probably around 2,000–2,500 m deep. The Bear Island Trough fan is farther east. The shelf break north of Lofoten sits near 14°E at 69°N, and at 71°N it is near 17°E. So 9°E is well off the shelf, on the slope or basin edge, and I estimate about -2,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

4309.text:
Latitude 43.0, longitude 159.0 is in the northwest Pacific, southeast of Kamchatka and east of the Kuril Islands. The Kuril Islands run from about 43.5N 146E to 50N 156E, so 159E at 43N is well east of them. That puts the point in or near the Kuril-Kamchatka Trench, whose axis lies roughly at 44N 150E to 50N 160E. At 43N the trench axis is near 147-148E, so 159E is on the abyssal plain east of the trench, with a depth of about 5,500 m. The Detroit Seamount chain is farther north, around 51N, so it is not a factor. Abyssal depths here are around 5,300-5,600 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -5,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

6286.text:
Latitude 21°N, longitude 153°E lies in the western Pacific, east of the Mariana Islands and northeast of the Caroline region. It sits in the Pacific Plate's Pigafetta Basin or nearby seamount areas.

Depths in this area are typically 5,500-6,000 m. The Pigafetta Basin is around 6,000 m deep. Near 21°N, 153°E there are the Magellan Seamounts, which lie roughly 150-160°E and 12-20°N, so this point is near their northern edge. Abyssal plain depth is probably around -5,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

7571.text:
The point 5°N, 157°W lies in the central Pacific, south of Hawaii and near Kiritimati (Christmas Island, about 1.9°N, 157.4°W) and Palmyra (5.9°N, 162°W). Kiritimati is too far south to matter, and Palmyra is too far west. So this point is open ocean.

The nearby seafloor is part of the Line Islands region, with a typical depth of about 5000 m. The area south of Hawaii and north of the equator near 157°W is roughly 4900–5400 m deep, so I estimate about -5100 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5100}

10732.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 45 is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Madagascar and east of South Africa. Madagascar's southern tip is near 25.5°S, 45°E, so 29°S is roughly 400 km south of it. That area lies in the Madagascar Basin, or near the southern edge of the Madagascar Plateau and the Mozambique Basin, so the seabed is deep.

The Mozambique Basin reaches 4,500–5,000 m deep around 30°S, 40°E. At 45°E, 29°S, the Madagascar Ridge lies further east, at about 47–48°E, with depths of 2,000–3,000 m. The location 45°E is west of the ridge, probably in the Mozambique Basin's eastern part or on the basin–ridge slope. I estimate a depth of about 4,800 m, perhaps slightly shallower.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

10341.text:
Latitude -25, longitude -17 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 25°S sits near 13°W, so this point is west of the axis, on the western flank. The Rio Grande Rise is farther west, near 30–35°W, so it is not a factor here.

Seafloor depth on the ridge flank at that distance from the axis is roughly 3500–4000 m. Seafloor age is about 5–10 Myr, which fits a depth near 3800 m. I will estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

6921.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude -17.0 lies in Senegal, west of Kaolack and Fatick. The Atlantic coast at this latitude is near -16.8 to -17.0: Dakar is at 14.7 N, and the Saloum delta is around 13.7 N, -16.7. At 13.0 N the coast sits near -16.75 (Gambia's coast at Banjul is 13.45 N, -16.6), so -17.0 is about 25 km offshore.

Offshore here the continental shelf is fairly wide. The 50 m depth contour is perhaps 20 to 30 km out, and the shelf edge near 100 to 200 m is roughly 50 km out. At 25 km offshore the depth should be about 30 to 40 m, so I estimate -35 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-35}

12457.text:
Latitude -49, longitude -105 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile, so the surface type is Water and the answer is the seabed depth.

This point lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region, but that ridge is further south and west. At 49°S the East Pacific Rise is around 115°W, so 105°W is east of the rise. The Chile Rise is at about 35°S–45°S near 80°W–95°W, with its triple junction at roughly 46°S, 76°W. At 49°S, 105°W the crust is Antarctic plate seafloor, perhaps 10–20 Myr old, so a depth of about 3,500–4,000 m is expected. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at 49°S sits near 114°W, so 105°W is about 9° of longitude east, roughly 650 km. At a spreading rate of about 70 mm/yr per side, that gives about 9 Myr of crust and a depth of about 3,000 m on the ridge flank. I'll use roughly -3,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

13678.text:
The point is at 61°S, 177°E, in the Southern Ocean south of New Zealand. It lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Macquarie Ridge region, but more likely on the Southeast Indian/Pacific-Antarctic ridge system. The Balleny Islands are around 66°S, 163°E, so they are not close.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from 62°S, 160°W up to about 65°S, 175°E and then toward the Balleny area. At 61°S, 177°E the seafloor is probably part of the Pacific-Antarctic Basin or the Emerald Basin, with depths around 3000-4000 m. The Macquarie Ridge is further west, near 160°E. Between 170°E and 180°E at 60°S there is the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern extension, with depths around 2500-3000 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

1973.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude 167.0 E is in northeastern Siberia, Chukotka, near the Anyuy or Chaun area. The East Siberian Sea coast sits around 69.5-70 N at 167 E (Chaunskaya Bay is near 69.5 N, 168-170 E). At 69 N, 167 E we are probably inland, in the lowlands south of Chaun Bay. The Chaun lowland is flat tundra, and the elevation there is probably 50-150 m.

Chaunskaya Bay's southern shore is around 68.8 N at 170 E. At 167 E the coast is near 69.7 N. So 69.0 N is inland, within the lowlands of the Chaun River basin and the Pegtymel/Alarmaut uplands, where elevations are about 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

15708.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -83 is in West Antarctica, south of the Ellsworth Mountains and Thiel Mountains area. The Ellsworth Mountains sit near 78-79°S, 85°W. At 85°S, 83°W, the location is near the Institute Ice Stream and Foundation Ice Stream headwaters, inland of the Ronne Ice Shelf. The grounded ice surface there is around 1000-1500 m. The Thiel Mountains lie near 85°S, 91°W, with surface elevation about 2000 m. At 83°W, I'd guess about 1100 m.

The Support Force Glacier and Foundation Ice Stream are around 83°S, 60°W. The Institute Ice Stream is near 82°S, 75°W. At 85°S, 83°W, the surface is probably around 1200 m; the interior of West Antarctica near the Pole is 2000+ m, and the region south of the Ellsworth Mountains sits around 1500 m. I'll say 1400.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1400}

4331.text:
The point 41.0°N, 157.0°W lies in the North Pacific, roughly 1,000 km north-northeast of Hawaii. It is in open ocean on the abyssal plain, northeast of the Hawaiian Ridge and south of the Aleutian region.

The seafloor in this area is typically around 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. I did not look up a bathymetry value for this exact spot, so I am estimating about -5,350 m from that typical range.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5350}

3634.text:
The point 49.0°N, 111.0°W is on the Canada–US border near Coutts, Alberta, in the area of Milk River Ridge, close to the Sweetgrass Hills to the south. Elevation there is roughly 1,100 m. Sweetgrass/Coutts sits at about 1,080 m, and the Milk River Ridge area is a little higher. At longitude -111.0, the point is about 20 km west of Coutts (at -111.96?). Coutts is actually at about -111.96, so -111.0 is 1° east of it, near Wildhorse, Alberta, in the Milk River area. Wildhorse is at -110.5 and about 1,000 m. At -111.0 the elevation is probably around 900 m, with the Milk River nearby.

I estimate about 950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":950}

912.text:
The point 79°N, 155°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Alaska and the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. It sits in the Canada Basin, which has abyssal depths of about 3800 m. The Beaufort Sea continental slope lies near 72-73°N, so at 79°N the seabed is on the abyssal plain.

Banks Island is at about 125°W, and the Alaskan coast is at 71°N. The Chukchi Borderland lies near 75-80°N and 160-170°W, with the Northwind Ridge at about 74-77°N and 155-160°W. At 79°N and 155°W, the point is north of the Northwind Ridge and within the Canada Basin, whose depth there is about 3600-3800 m. The Canada Basin floor is roughly 3800 m at 78°N and 150°W, so I estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

7033.text:
This location (11°N, 153°W) is in the central Pacific, southeast of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor there is abyssal plain at roughly 5,000 m depth. The region lies north of the Clipperton-Clarion area, in the central Pacific basin. The crust is about 80 Myr old, which would typically put the depth near 5,200 m. I estimate about -5,200 m, from the general depth of this basin rather than a specific chart lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

4776.text:
The point 37.0°N, 13.0°E lies in the Mediterranean Sea, in the Strait of Sicily. Sicily's southwestern coast is around 37.5°N at 13°E, and the coast near Sciacca is at about 37.5°N, 13.08°E. Since 37.0°N is about 55 km south of that, the point is open sea.

The Sicilian shelf is relatively shallow here. The Adventure Bank lies at roughly 37.3°N, 12.7°E, and depths there are about 100 m or less. At 37.0°N, 13.0°E I think the seabed is still on the shelf but sloping toward the Malta Graben, which is further southeast. I estimate a depth of about 150 m, but this is a guess, since the point could sit on the slope instead.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

11470.text:
The point (-37, 81) is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Amsterdam Island (which sits near -37.8, 77.5) and east of the Central Indian Ridge. It lies in the region of the Southeast Indian Ridge and the Amsterdam–St Paul plateau, but at 81°E it is east of the plateau. The nearby Southeast Indian Ridge axis runs about -38 to -40 at 78–80°E, so the point is likely on the ridge flank, with depths around 3500–4500 m.

I'm estimating about -3800 m from this regional context rather than from a specific bathymetry lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2140.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude 141.0 E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Sakha Republic. That area lies near the Yana-Indigirka lowland and the Chersky Range foothills, close to the Indigirka basin. At 141 E and 67 N the terrain is probably in the Chersky Range or its western foothills, roughly 500-1000 m. The Momo-Selennyakh depression is around 142-143 E, 67 N. The Elgin plateau and the Ulakhan-Chistay range sit near 141 E, 67 N, with the Polousny range to the north.

The Yana river headwaters lie near 67 N, 135 E, and the Indigirka at 141 E is around 68 N. The Moma river is at about 66 N, 143 E. Ulakhan-Chistay is around 141 E, 67-68 N, with heights of 1000-2000 m, but the lower land between ranges is probably nearer 600 m. I'll estimate 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

11738.text:
Location: 41°S, 103°W is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile (Chile coast is around 73°W). It lies on the Chile Rise/East Pacific Rise flank region. The East Pacific Rise at that latitude is around 110°W at 41°S, with the Chile Rise further southeast. At 103°W, 41°S, the seafloor is likely about 3,000-3,500 m deep, on the eastern flank of the East Pacific Rise. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies further south; at 41°S the rise is less pronounced, with depths around 3,500 m.

I estimate about -3,400 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3400}

10260.text:
Latitude -25, longitude -179 is in the South Pacific, just south of Fiji and Tonga and north of New Zealand. It lies near the Kermadec Ridge region, but the ridge sits around -175 to -178 longitude. Minerva Reefs are at about -23.6, -178.9, so this point is about 1.4° south of them. The area is on the Lau-Colville ridge or the basin west of the Kermadec Trench, where depths are likely 2000–4000 m.

The Colville Ridge lies near -179 to -178 at latitude -25 and has depths of about 1000–2000 m. The Lau Basin sits west of it, and the South Fiji Basin is further west and south, around 3000 m deep. At -25, -179 the point is probably on or near the Colville Ridge, so I estimate a depth of about -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

10029.text:
Location: -21°S, 79°E is in the southern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and southeast of the Mascarene region. It lies in the Central Indian Basin or its western margin, and the Indian Ocean abyssal plains there are around 4,500–5,500 m deep.

Around 21°S, 79°E, the Southwest Indian Ridge is far to the southwest and the Central Indian Ridge is at about 65–70°E, so this is east of the ridge. The Ninety East Ridge is at about 90°E. That puts the point in the Central Indian Basin, whose depth is about 5,000–5,500 m. I'll estimate -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

253.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -33° lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Greenland. Greenland's north coast is at about 83°N, so 87°N is well into the Arctic Basin. Near 87°N, 33°W, the area is close to the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from the New Siberian Islands to Ellesmere/Greenland and passes near the pole around 140°E to 40°W. At the pole the Lomonosov Ridge is roughly 1000-1500 m deep. On the Greenland side of the pole, the Amundsen Basin lies to the east of the ridge and the Makarov Basin to the west.

The longitude of -33° is on the Greenland/Eurasian side of the pole, so it is probably in the Amundsen Basin, which is about 4000 m deep. The ridge crosses the pole roughly along the 140°E to 40°W line, so -33° is about 7° from the ridge axis at 87°N, which is only a few kilometers away. The ridge is near -40° on the Greenland side, so this point may sit on or near the ridge flank, and the depth could be about 1500-2500 m. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

9454.text:
The point -15.0, 9.0 lies in the South Atlantic, off the coast of Angola. Angola's coast at 15°S is near 12.2°E, so 9°E is roughly 340 km offshore. Because the continental shelf is narrow there, the point is probably over the continental slope or rise, or on the abyssal plain edge of the Angola Basin.

The Angola Basin floor is about 5000 m deep. The Walvis Ridge sits further south, around 20°S, so it is not a factor here. At 15°S and 9°E, I estimate the depth at about 4,500 to 5,000 m, which I'll round to 4,800 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7090.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude -39.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, but that does not match Brazil's coast, so I checked the geography. Brazil's easternmost point is near -35°W, and at 11°S the coast sits near -37°W. Latitude 11.0 could be north, though. At 11°N, -39°W is in the central equatorial Atlantic, well offshore from Africa and Brazil.

The sign convention gives 11°N, so this is open Atlantic. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near -41°W at that latitude, and the Fracture Zone region is around 10-11°N. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge at 11°N is at about -43°W, so -39°W is east of the ridge on the African side. There the seafloor is about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. I'll estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

11285.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 71 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and southwest of Amsterdam Island (about -37.8, 77.5). It is near the Southwest Indian Ridge and Central Indian Ridge region, though not exactly on either. The Southwest Indian Ridge is further southwest, at around -40, 62. The Central Indian Ridge runs from about -25, 70 to the triple junction at -25.5, 70, then the Southeast Indian Ridge heads southeast toward Amsterdam Island.

At -35, 71 the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or flank terrain, with depths of about 3500-4500 m. The Southeast Indian Ridge passes near -38, 78, so 71E is west of it. The Mid-Indian Basin lies to the north and the Crozet Basin to the west. The area is likely a ridge flank or a plateau area such as the one around -35, 75, so I estimate roughly 3800 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

1284.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -131.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Banks Island spans roughly 71.5–74.5°N and 115–125°W, and Prince Patrick Island lies near 76–77°N, 117–124°W. Melville Island is at about 75°N but between 105 and 119°W. So -131 lies west of Banks Island, in the Beaufort Sea / M'Clure Strait mouth area.

Banks Island's northwest tip, Cape Kellett and Cape Prince Alfred, is around 74.4°N, 124.5°W. At 75°N, 131°W, the point is north-northwest of that, in the Arctic Ocean beyond the continental shelf edge of the Beaufort Sea. The shelf there is narrow, and the shelf break lies roughly at 73–74°N near 125–130°W. Near 75°N, 131°W the water is probably on the upper continental slope, with depth around 500–1500 m. The mouth of M'Clure Strait is a trough with depths of a few hundred meters, but at -131° the point is west of it, on the slope toward the Canada Basin. I estimate a depth of about 1000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1000}

2768.text:
The point 59°N, 43°W is in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland's southern tip (Cape Farewell is at about 60°N, 44°W). At 59°N, 43°W the point lies south-southeast of Cape Farewell, in the Irminger Sea or Labrador Sea boundary region. The southern Greenland shelf extends only a short distance from the cape, so this point is probably off the shelf.

The seafloor here is likely the Eirik Ridge or the slope of the Greenland continental margin, at roughly 2500–3000 m depth. The Eirik Drift lies southwest of Greenland at about 57°N, 47°W, and its depth is around 3000 m. At 59°N, 43°W the depth is probably about 2800 m, so I'll estimate −2900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

1237.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 135.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea, north of the Lena Delta and the New Siberian Islands region. The Lena Delta is at about 73°N, 126°E, and the Laptev Sea shelf extends north to about 78°N. At 77°N, 135°E, the point is on the outer shelf, where depths are probably around 50 to 100 m. The shelf break lies near 78-79°N in this area.

The Laptev shelf is wide, and the 100 m isobath sits around 77-78°N near 130-140°E. The Severnaya Zemlya islands are further west, near 100°E, and the New Siberian Islands are at about 75°N, 140-150°E, so neither affects this point. At 135°E the shelf edge is near 78°N. Depth at 77°N is probably about 60 m, so I will estimate -60 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

11268.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 37 lies in the Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa. Mozambique's coast sits near 35°E at around -20°, and South Africa's east coast is near 31°E at -30°. At -35, 37E the point is well off the coast, southeast of Durban. The Natal Valley and Mozambique Basin lie in this region, and the Mozambique Ridge is around 33-35°E, -30 to -34°. At 37°E, -35° the point is probably on the southern Mozambique Basin or the edge of the Mozambique Ridge, and the depth is likely around 5000 m. The southern Mozambique Basin is roughly 5000 m deep, and the Mozambique Ridge's southern end reaches about -33° to -26°S with depths of 2000-3000 m. East of the ridge, near 37°E, -35°, the basin floor is around 5000 m.

I estimate -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

3243.text:
Latitude 53.0, longitude -173.0 is in the Bering Sea / North Pacific, south of the Aleutian Islands. Attu Island is near 173°E, which is on the other side of the antimeridian, so it is not nearby. At 173°W the Aleutian Islands lie around 52°N. The Islands of Four Mountains are near 170°W, and Atka is near 174°W, 52.2°N. Amukta Pass is near 172°W. Atka Island at 174°W, 52°N is about 1° of latitude south of this point.

The Aleutian chain at 173°W runs roughly through 52.3°N, in the gap between Atka and Amlia (Amlia is at 173.5°W, 52.1°N). The point at 53.0°N is about 0.7° north of that, or roughly 75 km, which places it on the Bering Sea side. The northern shelf of the Aleutian arc there drops off quickly. The Bering Sea basin is about 3800 m deep, and the slope north of Amlia reaches about 2000 m depth by 53°N. I'll estimate about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

13034.text:
The point -55, -31 lies in the South Atlantic, east of the South Sandwich Islands' west side and southeast of South Georgia. South Georgia is around -54.3, -36.5. The South Sandwich Islands sit at about -56 to -59 and -27 to -26. The point is therefore in open ocean, in the area of the Northeast Georgia Rise or the South Sandwich plate's western basin.

The western part of the Georgia Basin / Scotia Sea region lies near -31 longitude, and the depth there is roughly 3000 to 4000 m. Between South Georgia and the South Sandwich arc is the east Scotia Sea, with depths around 3000 to 4000 m. At -55, -31 the seafloor is probably around 3,500 m deep. I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

6960.text:
Latitude 13.0°N, longitude 61.0°W is in the Atlantic Ocean, east of the Lesser Antilles. Barbados is at about 13.1°N, 59.6°W, and St. Vincent is at about 61.2°W, 13.25°N. So the point lies about 0.2° east of the St. Vincent longitude and slightly south, which puts it in the water just west of St. Vincent, in the Caribbean Sea.

St. Vincent spans roughly 13.13–13.38°N and its southern tip is near 13.13°N, 61.2°W. The point at 13.0°N, 61.0°W is therefore about 15 km south-southeast of that tip. This is on the Atlantic side, in the Grenadines area. The Grenadines bank extends south from St. Vincent toward Bequia and Mustique, but those lie west of 61.0°W: Mustique is at 61.18°W and Canouan at 61.33°W. The point at 61.0°W is east of the islands, in open Atlantic water. The seabed there is the Atlantic slope, with a depth of about 1,000–2,000 m. I estimate 1,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

4590.text:
Latitude 39.0, longitude 1.0 E lies in the western Mediterranean, between mainland Spain (Valencia coast is at about 0°W) and Ibiza (about 1.4°E, 38.9°N). Ibiza's western coast is at about 1.2°E, so 1.0°E at 39.0°N is just west of Ibiza, in the sea.

The area between the Valencia coast and Ibiza is the Ibiza Channel, with depths of around 700-800 m in its deepest part. The Ibiza Channel runs between Denia/Cabo de la Nao (38.7°N, 0.2°E) and Ibiza/Formentera. At 39.0°N, 1.0°E, the position is northwest of Ibiza, about 25 km from its coast. The Valencia Trough lies to the north, and the sill in the Ibiza Channel is about 800 m deep. Depth here is likely around 700-800 m, so I estimate -750.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-750}

13356.text:
The point is at 59°S, 107°W, in the southeast Pacific Ocean. That area lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank, west of the Chile Rise and south of the Eltanin fracture zone region. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge sits near 65°S at 120°W and runs north toward about 56°S at 145°W, so this point is east of the ridge. Seafloor there is typically around 3,500–4,000 m deep.

The Eltanin and Udintsev fracture zones are near 57–60°S at 120–140°W. At 107°W the seafloor is probably abyssal, at roughly 4,000 m. I estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

3880.text:
The coordinates 47.0°N, 21.0°E fall in western Romania, in the Crișana region of the Pannonian Plain, near Oradea, which lies at about 47.05°N, 21.9°E. At 21.0°E the point is west of Oradea, close to the Hungarian border. The border near Oradea is around 21.5°E, so this point is in Hungary, near Berettyóújfalu (47.22°N, 21.54°E) or Püspökladány (47.32°N, 21.12°E). The location is probably around Sárrétudvari or Biharnagybajom, in the Hortobágy and Sárrét area. Terrain there is about 85–90 m.

I estimate the elevation from the plain's typical height rather than a lookup, so it is approximate.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":88}

7129.text:
The coordinates 11.0°N, 39.0°E fall in the Ethiopian Highlands, in the Amhara region. That places the point near Lake Tana's southeast, roughly in North Shewa or South Wollo. Lake Tana is at about 12°N, 37.3°E, so this point is not on it.

The area near 11°N, 39°E is around Dessie to Debre Sina. Dessie lies at 11.13°N, 39.63°E at about 2,500 m. Debre Birhan is at 9.68°N. Points near 11.0°N, 39.0°E are west of the escarpment, near Mehal Meda (10.3°N, 39.7°E) and Debre Tabor (11.85°N, 38.0°E). The area around Kombolcha and Kemise is lower, and the Beshilo River canyon lies near 11°N, 39.2°E. I estimate the elevation at around 2,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

1872.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -35.0 is in Greenland, on the east side. East Greenland's coast near Kangerlussuaq lies at about 68.5N, -32W. At -35 longitude and 69N, the point is inland, on the ice sheet, roughly 100 km from the coast. That puts it in the ice sheet's eastern slope, where the surface is likely at about 2400 m.

The coast near Kangerlussuaq is around -32 to -33 longitude at 68.5N. The ice sheet margin there is around -33.5, and the ice sheet at -35 and 69N is probably at about 2000 m. Summit is at 72.5N, -38.5, with a surface of 3200 m. Moving south and east, the elevation drops. I'll estimate 2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

15400.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 21 is in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land. It lies south of the Shackleton Range and the Recovery Glacier region (the Shackleton Range is at about 80-81°S, 20-30°W, which is west longitude). Here the longitude is 21°E, so the point is inland of Queen Maud Land, in the interior near the Sør Rondane and Wohlthat mountains and farther south of them.

At 81°S, 21°E the ice surface is probably around 3,000 m. The polar plateau there is high, roughly 2,800-3,400 m. I'll estimate 3,000 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 3000}

14933.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 167 is in Victoria Land, Antarctica, near the Ross Sea coast. At -75 S, 167 E the location is near Terra Nova Bay and Campbell Glacier, about 74.7 S, 164 E. Moving east, the Northern Foothills and the Priestley Glacier area give way to the Victoria Land coast near Cape Adare at 170 E, 71 S. The Ross Sea coast near 75 S lies at about 162-165 E (Terra Nova Bay is at 164 E, 75 S), and Drygalski Ice Tongue is at 166 E, 75.3 S.

So 167 E, 75 S is just east of Terra Nova Bay, in the Ross Sea. It could be sea, the Nansen Ice Sheet, or the Drygalski Ice Tongue area. The coast near 75 S runs around 164-165 E, and Drygalski extends to about 166.5 E at 75.4 S. The point at 167 E, 75 S is probably open water in the Ross Sea (the western Ross Sea), where the seabed lies at roughly 500-900 m depth. Off Terra Nova Bay the seabed is about -600 m, and the shelf may be somewhat deeper, so I'll use -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

15013.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -33 is in the Weddell Sea region of Antarctica. Longitude 33°W at 77°S falls in the Filchner Ice Shelf area, which spans roughly 30–50°W and 78–83°S. At 77°S, 33°W the position is likely near the Filchner Trough or the Brunt Ice Shelf/Riiser-Larsen area. The Brunt Ice Shelf is around 75.5°S, 25°W. Berkner Island lies at about 79°S, 45°W. The Filchner Trough runs along roughly 35°W at 76–77°S, with a seabed depth of around 500–1000 m, and the continental shelf there is deep. The Filchner ice front is near 78°S, so 77°S, 33°W is probably open water over the shelf, in the trough.

I estimate the seabed at about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

13933.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -33 is in the Weddell Sea, east of the Antarctic Peninsula and north of the Filchner area. At this point the sea is open ocean, far from the coast of Antarctica (which lies near -70 to -72 at this longitude).

The Weddell Sea abyssal plain at this location is probably about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The Weddell Abyssal Plain sits around -4,800 m, and the South Sandwich trench region lies farther east. At -65, -33 the depth is probably about -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

11435.text:
The point (-37.0, 11.0) is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of South Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It lies near the Agulhas Ridge/Cape Basin region, which is roughly 1,500 km west of Cape Town.

The Cape Basin there is about 5,000 m deep. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge sits near 14°W to 0°E at this latitude, so 11°E is east of the ridge. The Walvis Ridge is further north at about 30°S. The Agulhas Ridge runs from about 40°S 15°E to 46°S 5°E, so it is farther south than this point. At 37°S 11°E, the seafloor depth is probably around 4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

15192.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -35 is in the southern Weddell Sea, in the Filchner Ice Shelf region. The Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf front lies around 77-78°S at about 35-40°W, so -79 at -35 is probably on the Filchner Ice Shelf, which is floating. The Filchner Trough lies in the east, near 36°W, with a seabed depth of about 600-1000 m at the ice front. Further south, near 79°S, under the ice shelf, the seabed would be perhaps 500-700 m deep.

Bedmap puts the Filchner Ice Shelf at roughly 30-50°W, to the south of 77°S. At 35°W, 79°S the ice is probably still floating, since the grounding line there is further south at about 80.5°S. The seabed is likely about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-650}

15420.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 61 is in East Antarctica, on the interior ice sheet, south of Prydz Bay and the Amery Ice Shelf region. The Amery Ice Shelf's grounding line is near -73. At -81, 61E the position lies in the Princess Elizabeth Land / Dome A direction. Dome A is at about -80.4, 77E at 4093 m. Near 61E, -81, the surface is probably around 3500 m. Gamburtsev Mountains lie around 72-80E, and at 61E, -81 the ice surface is likely about 3300-3600 m.

I estimate 3500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3500}

12442.text:
The point (-49, -135) is in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and west of South America. It lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or nearby abyssal plain. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs around 55-65°S at that longitude, with the East Pacific Rise at roughly 50°S near 114°W. At 135°W and 49°S, the seafloor is probably abyssal plain of moderate age, with a depth of around 4,000 to 4,500 m.

Near -49, -135, the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is at about 56°S, 145°W, so this point is a few hundred kilometers away from the ridge axis. The seafloor there is likely about 3,800 to 4,200 m deep. I estimate -4000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4000}

5057.text:
The point at 33°N, 145°W is in the northeast Pacific, roughly midway between Hawaii and California. It is open ocean over abyssal plain, so the surface type is Water.

Seafloor depth in this region is typically about 4,500 to 5,500 m. The area lies north of the Murray Fracture Zone, on Pacific plate crust roughly 50 to 60 million years old. Crust of that age usually sits at around 5,000 m depth. I am estimating from memory of typical depths for this region, not from a lookup of the exact cell, so I chose about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

14144.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 29 lies off the coast of East Antarctica (Enderby Land / Queen Maud Land area). The Antarctic coast near 29°E is at roughly 69.5°S, so -67 is in the Southern Ocean, offshore. The sea floor there is probably the continental rise or abyssal plain, at roughly 3,500 to 4,500 m depth.

The Indian-sector continental margin there is steep, with the shelf break near 68-69°S. At 67°S, 29°E the depth is likely about -3,800 m, on the abyssal plain just north of the margin. I'll go with -3,900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

562.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 135°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago (Ellef Ringnes and Meighen Islands sit near 80°N, 100°W). At 83°N, 135°W the point is in the Canada Basin, near the Alpha Ridge and the edge of the Canadian polar margin.

The Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. The Alpha Ridge is shallower, around 1500 to 2500 m. Around 83°N, 135°W the point is probably on the Canada Basin side. The Beaufort Sea slope runs from about 72°N to 78°N, and by 83°N the depth is probably about 3600 to 3800 m. The Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge begins around 85°N at 150°W. The IBCAO depth at 83°N, 135°W is about 3800 m. I'll answer -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

10206.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 73 is in the south-central Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and the Mascarene Plateau and south of the Chagos area. It is near the Central Indian Basin and the Central Indian Ridge. The Central Indian Ridge sits around 66-70°E at this latitude, and the Rodrigues Ridge lies near 63°E. At 73°E, 23°S, the seafloor is probably in the Central Indian Basin, or on the flank of the ridge system, at roughly 4000-4500 m depth.

The Mascarene Basin lies west of the Mascarene Plateau, which is on the order of 55-60°E. The Central Indian Ridge is around 69°E at 23°S, so 73°E is east of it. The Southwest Indian Ridge and the Rodrigues triple junction are near 25.5°S, 70°E, so 73°E is about 3° east of the ridge axis. Crust there is young, so depth should be around 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15985.text:
Latitude -87 is about 300 km from the South Pole, on the East Antarctic plateau. Longitude 111°E lies on the side of the pole toward Vostok, which is at 106.8°E and about 78.5°S. At -87, 111°E the ice surface should be around 2,800 to 3,000 m. Ice near the pole is about 2,835 m. The area toward 90°E at -87 is probably near 3,000 m or a bit higher, and the Dome A direction (80°E, -80.4) reaches 4,000 m. Along 111°E, the surface climbs gradually from the pole toward Vostok at 3,488 m, so at -87 it is probably about 3,000 m.

This is an estimate from known landmarks, not a lookup, so I'll go with roughly 2,950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

1882.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude -15.0 W lies in the Greenland Sea / Norwegian Sea, northwest of Jan Mayen (which sits at about 71N, 8.5W). Iceland's northern coast is near 66.5N, and Greenland's coast is at about -22W or farther west. So this point is open ocean.

The bathymetry here is the Greenland Basin / Iceland Plateau area, or possibly the Kolbeinsey Ridge region. The Kolbeinsey Ridge runs north from Iceland at around -18W to -15W, reaching about 70N near Jan Mayen. The Jan Mayen Fracture Zone is around 71N. At 69N, -15W, the area is likely the Iceland Plateau, with depths of about 1500-2000 m. The Iceland Plateau is roughly 1000-1800 m deep, and the Kolbeinsey Ridge axis is about 1000-1500 m deep. I'll estimate about -1700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1700}

15383.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -13 is in Antarctica, in the Weddell Sea sector. It lies near the Filchner Ice Shelf front, which is around 78-79°S at about -40 to -30° longitude. At -81 and -13, the point is inland, on the Coats Land / Dronning Maud Land side, in the Theron Mountains / Shackleton Range area. The Shackleton Range is at about -80.5 latitude and -25 longitude. At -13 longitude and -81 latitude, we are east of that, on the polar plateau, with ice surface elevation around 1500-2500 m.

The Recovery Glacier region is near -81, -28. Further east at -13, the ice sheet sits at roughly 2000 m, perhaps a bit less, on the plateau of Coats Land/Queen Maud Land. The South Pole–Queen Maud Land traverse passes around 80°S, 0°E at about 2000 m. I'll estimate 1800 m, as it is grounded ice.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

5233.text:
The point at 31°N, 153°W lies in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed here is abyssal plain, roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m deep, on Pacific plate crust about 80 million years old. The Murray Fracture Zone lies near 31°N, and I recall seafloor depths in this region of about 5,200 m. I'm estimating from that recollection rather than from a lookup, so I'll use -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

2365.text:
Location: 63.0N, 129.0W is in the Northwest Territories, Canada, in the Mackenzie Mountains area, east of the Yukon border. The Selwyn Mountains and Mackenzie Mountains lie around 62-63N, 128-131W. Norman Wells is at 65.3N, 126.8W. At 63N, 129W we are near the Keele River headwaters and Tsichu River, in the Mackenzie Mountains, with elevations of about 1200-1800 m.

Nearby: Keele Peak is at 63.4N, 130.3W (2,972 m). The Nahanni Range lies to the south. At 129W, 63N, it is probably in mountainous terrain at about 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

5440.text:
The coordinates 29.0°N, 99.0°W fall in south Texas, in the Rio Grande Plain. The nearest towns are Pearsall, to the northeast, and Dilley, to the east. Uvalde is at about 29.2°N, 99.8°W, and Pearsall is at about 28.89°N, 99.1°W. Pearsall's elevation is about 170 m, so this point, which lies slightly north of Pearsall, should be near 200 m.

The Nueces River flows through this area, around Cotulla and Tilden, and further north at roughly 29.1°N, 99.0°W near the Frio River. The Frio flows near Pearsall, so the point may sit close to it. The terrain slopes from about 230 m in the north to about 170 m in the south. I estimate roughly 200 m, which is a judgment from nearby town elevations rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

13738.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -63 lies in the Bransfield Strait / Drake Passage region near the South Shetland Islands. The South Shetlands run from about 62°S to 63.5°S and from 54°W to 63°W. Livingston Island is at about 62.6°S, 60.4°W, and Deception Island at 63°S, 60.6°W. At 63°W, 63°S the point is west of Deception, near the Bransfield Strait's northwest edge, or possibly in the Drake Passage just south of the South Shetlands. The islands' southwest end is around Low Island (63.3°S, 62.2°W) and Smith Island (62.97°S, 62.5°W). So -63, -63 is just west of Smith Island, in open water.

The depth in this area is probably around 500 to 1000 m. The Bransfield Strait is deep in its center, but this point is on the northwest side, where the shelf drops to the Drake Passage. I'll estimate -700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

2958.text:
The point at 57°N, 23°W is in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and west of the Rockall Plateau. It lies in the Iceland Basin, west of the Reykjanes Ridge, whose axis at 57°N is near 33°W. At 23°W the position is east of the ridge, so it falls in the Iceland Basin, where the seabed is typically 2800–3000 m deep.

The Iceland Basin at 57°N, 23°W is about 2900 m deep. Further east, Hatton Bank lies near 19°W and 58°N. The basin floor in this area is likely around 2900 m, so I'll estimate −2900 m. This is a regional estimate rather than a measured value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

13992.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 85 is off the coast of East Antarctica, near Davis Sea / Queen Mary Land. The coast there is around 66°S at 85°E. The Davis Sea lies to the east, and Queen Mary Land's coast is near 66°S, so -65 is likely ocean just north of the coast. Around 85°E the coastline sits at about 66.0-66.5°S, with the Shackleton Ice Shelf further east, around 95-100°E. Near 85°E, the continental shelf lies off the coast, and the Gaussberg area is at 89°E, 66.8°S.

So the point is likely water over the continental shelf edge or slope. At 65°S, 85°E the seabed depth is probably around 2000-3000 m, since the shelf there is narrow and the slope descends quickly. I'll estimate -2500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

12710.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 41 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of the Crozet Islands (about 46°S, 51°E) and well south of the Madagascar Basin. It lies between the Crozet Plateau and the Southwest Indian Ridge, in the Crozet Basin or the Enderby Basin area. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs roughly from 40°E at 50°S, near the Indomed and Du Toit fracture zones, and the Prince Edward Islands are at 38°E, 46.5°S. At 41°E, 51°S the seafloor is probably on the ridge flank, or in the Crozet Basin at about 3,000 to 4,000 m depth.

The ridge near 51°S, 30°E is the Bouvet triple junction region, and the Southwest Indian Ridge then runs northeast toward 35°E, 53°S. Around 41°E the ridge is probably near 50–52°S, so this point could sit on or near the ridge axis. Ridge crest depths there are about 2,500 to 3,000 m, so I'll estimate about -3,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

1360.text:
Location: 75°N, 21°E is in the Barents Sea, south of Svalbard (Bear Island is at 74.4°N, 19°E) and east of Bear Island. Bear Island's coordinates are about 74.35°N, 19.0°E, so 75.0°N, 21.0°E lies about 70 km north-northeast of it. The Bear Island region is a shallow bank, and the area north of it is around 100-200 m deep. Spitsbergenbanken lies to the northwest of this point, and the Hopen Trench lies to the east. At 75°N, 21°E, the depth is probably about 250 m.

The Bear Island Trough runs to the southwest, and the region around 75°N, 20°E is likely about 200 m deep. I estimate the seabed at about -230 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-230}

3768.text:
Latitude 49.0, longitude 157.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, near the central Kuril Islands. Matua and Rasshua are around 48.0–48.1°N, 153.2°E, and Simushir is around 47.0°N, 152°E. Paramushir and Onekotan are near 49.4°N, 154.8°E, so 157°E is east of the island chain. That places it in the Kuril-Kamchatka Trench region or the area just west of it.

The trench axis near 49°N lies at roughly 159–160°E, with depths of about 8000–9000 m. At 157°E, the point is on the Pacific side of the Kuril Islands, where the slope drops from the arc into the trench. Onekotan sits at 154.7°E and the Kuril Basin (Okhotsk side) lies to the west. The trench's western wall runs from about 156°E, where the shelf edge is, to 159°E, where the axis is. So at 157°E the depth is probably around 4500–5500 m. I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

4165.text:
The point 43.0°N, 129.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, about 600 km west of the Oregon coast (the coast is near 124°W). That places it west of the Juan de Fuca Ridge and the Blanco Fracture Zone. The Gorda and Juan de Fuca ridges are at roughly 127–130°W, but their latitudes differ: the Gorda Ridge sits near 42–43°N around 126.5–127.5°W, and the Juan de Fuca Ridge is at 44–48°N around 130°W.

At 129°W, 43°N, the point is on the Juan de Fuca plate or the Pacific plate abyssal plain. Seafloor depth there is probably around 3,000–3,500 m. The Blanco Fracture Zone runs from about 43.5°N, 127.5°W southwest to 44°N, 130°W, so the point is near it but probably south of it. The Gorda plate region around 42°N, 127°W has a seafloor depth of about 3,000 m. Further west, the abyssal plain at 129°W is likely around 3,200 m deep. I'll estimate -3,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

3746.text:
Latitude 49.0 N, longitude 113.0 W, assuming the longitude is west since none is stated. That puts the point on the Alberta–Montana border region, near Waterton and Cardston, just north of the US border at 49°N.

At 113°W the terrain is southern Alberta prairie near the foothills. Cardston sits at about 113.3°W, 49.2°N, at roughly 1,130 m. Further east, Milk River is at 112.1°W and about 1,000 m. At 49.0°N, 113.0°W the point is roughly 20 km east of Waterton Lakes and just north of Montana's Chief Mountain Highway. It is near the Km-Km area south of Spring Coulee, with elevation around 1,250 m. I estimate about 1,230 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1230}

1656.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -107.0 is in the Canadian Arctic. Victoria Island's northern part lies roughly at 70-73°N and 100-118°W. At 71°N, -107° the point is near the east side of Victoria Island, close to Prince Albert Sound or Hadley Bay. Hadley Bay is at about 72.5°N, 108°W. Storkerson Peninsula sits near 72°N, 107°W. The southern limit of Victoria Island is about 68.5-69°N, so 71°N is in the middle of the island's eastern part.

Wait: the east coast of Victoria Island at about 106-107°W near 69-70°N has Wollaston Peninsula at 69-70°N, 108-112°W. Further north, Cape Back and Richard Collinson Inlet are at around 71.5°N, 117°W. The east coast near Cambridge Bay is at 105°W, 69°N. Going north, the east coast reaches about 106°W at 70°N, and Victoria Strait lies to the east. Near 71°N, the coast runs approximately to Cape Colborne, 70.5°N, 106°W? Then the coast turns west into Albert Edward Bay, about 70.5-71°N, 101-103°W (Albert Edward Bay is at 70.5°N, 102°W). The point at -107 and 71 would be on the land of the Storkerson Peninsula/Collinson Peninsula, which is the northeast part of Victoria Island near 70-71°N, 105-108°W. Collinson Peninsula is at around 70.5°N, 106°W, so the point is likely land with an elevation of roughly 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

5817.text:
The point 25°N, 65°W lies in the western Atlantic, southeast of Bermuda (which is at about 32°N, 64.8°W) and north of Puerto Rico. This area is part of the Hatteras/Nares Abyssal Plain region. The Nares Abyssal Plain sits at roughly 23-25°N, 63-65°W, with depths around 5,800 to 5,900 m.

I estimate the seabed at about 5,850 m below sea level.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5850}

14205.text:
Location: -67°S, 151°E is off the coast of East Antarctica (Adélie Land / George V Land). The coast around 67°S at 151°E is open ocean on the continental shelf, since the coast there lies near 66.5-67°S at about 140-150°E. George V Coast stretches from 142°E to 153°E, with the coastline around 66.5-67°S. At 151°E, the coast is near 68°S (Oates Land begins ~155°E). The Mertz Glacier tongue is at ~145°E. At 151°E the coast is around 67.5°S, so 67°S is likely ocean over the continental shelf edge or slope. The shelf there is deep, about 500 m, with the shelf break around 66°S. Depth is probably around -1000 m, though it could be more.

I'll guess -1500 m: at 67°S, 151°E, the continental slope is likely there.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

1440.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude -179.0° (179°W) lies in the Chukchi Sea / East Siberian Sea region north of Chukotka. Wrangel Island sits at about 71°N, 179–180°W, so 73°N is north of it, in the Chukchi Sea shelf area. Depths there are around 40–50 m on the shelf, and the shelf break lies further north at about 76°N. The Chukchi Sea at 73N, 179W is likely about 50 m deep, though the Herald Canyon region is nearby at around 73°N, 175°W with depths of roughly 60 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -55 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-55}

14908.text:
The point -75, 117E is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land, inland of the coast. The coast near 66°S puts this point about 1,000 km inland. Vostok is at 78.5°S, 106.8°E with an ice surface around 3,488 m. At 75°S, 117°E the surface is probably about 2,800–3,000 m. Dome C is at 75.1°S, 123.3°E with a surface of 3,233 m. Moving west to 117°E, the surface is probably slightly lower, around 3,000–3,100 m. I'll estimate 3,050 m.

This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3050}

12444.text:
The point at -49.0, -131.0 is in the South Pacific, southwest of Chile and far from any land. It sits near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region, but that ridge lies further south, at roughly 55-65°S in this longitude range. At 49°S, 131°W the seafloor is likely abyssal plain or lower ridge flank, with typical depths around 4000 m.

The East Pacific Rise runs at about 50°S near 115°W, which is further east. At 131°W the seafloor is older and probably deeper, so I estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

15143.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -133 lies in West Antarctica, in the eastern Ross Sea area. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 150°W at its eastern edge, and Marie Byrd Land lies east of that. At -133 longitude and -79 latitude, the position is probably in Marie Byrd Land, inland of the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge near Sulzberger Bay (about -77 latitude, -150). The Ross Ice Shelf's edge at 79°S sits around 155°W to 160°W, with the Edward VII Peninsula to the north. Closer to -133, the coast of the Ford Ranges or Crary Mountains is not nearby, so this is probably the ice sheet.

The Ross Ice Shelf grounding line runs near the Siple Coast, around 83°S at 150°W. At 79°S, 133°W, the position lies on the Marie Byrd Land interior, though it could be the Ross Ice Shelf's far-eastern part. The shelf's eastern boundary near Hobbs Coast is about 135°W to 140°W at 76-77°S. At 79°S and 133°W, this is likely grounded ice in the Executive Committee Range region or the Hal Flood Range. Ice elevation there is roughly 1000-1500 m. I'll estimate about 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

12240.text:
The point is at 47°S, 179°W, in the South Pacific east of New Zealand. The Chatham Islands sit near 44°S, 176.5°W, and the Antipodes Islands near 49.7°S, 178.8°E, so neither is close enough to be land here. The point lies south of the Chatham Rise, which is around 44°S, and the area is part of the Pacific basin south of the rise, near the Bounty Trough or Bounty Platform. The Bounty Islands are at 47.7°S, 179°E, which is on the opposite side of the antimeridian, so they don't apply.

The Bounty Trough runs eastward along about 46–47°S, with depths of roughly 4000–5000 m near 180°. At 179°W it is probably around 4,500 m deep. Further east, the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is still far away, so I estimate the seabed at about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

9930.text:
The point at -21, -119 lies in the South Pacific, east of the East Pacific Rise and west of Easter Island. The East Pacific Rise crest is near 113-114°W at this latitude, so -119 is on its western flank. Seafloor age there is roughly 5-8 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3,500-4,000 m. The Pacific superswell region has somewhat shallower bathymetry, though, and the area near 20°S, 119°W is likely around 3,800 m deep.

I estimate a depth of about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8520.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude -59.0 is in the Brazilian Amazon, in southern Amazonas state. It lies south of the Amazon River, near the Madeira River and the Maués area. The location is probably land, in a plateau-like region of terra firme.

The Madeira River runs at about -59.5 longitude near -4 to -5 latitude, so the point could be near it, but it is more likely upland. Elevation in that area is roughly 50–100 m. I estimate about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

3133.text:
The point 55°N, 33°W is in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and east of Greenland. It sits just east of the Reykjanes Ridge, in the Irminger Sea or Iceland Basin area. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 55°N lies at about 35°W, so 33°W is on the eastern flank, where depths are around 2,500-3,000 m.

The Reykjanes Ridge axis at 55°N is near 34-35°W, with depths around 1,500-2,000 m. At 33°W, about 1° east of the axis (roughly 60 km), the flank depth is probably around 2,000 m. I'll estimate -2100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2100}

1780.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 141.0°E lies in the East Siberian Sea region, near the Laptev Sea / East Siberian Sea boundary. The New Siberian Islands are around 75°N, and the Siberian coast near the Indigirka delta is at about 71.5°N, 150°E. At 141°E, the coast near the Yana Bay and Buor-Khaya is around 71.5°N, with Dmitry Laptev Strait further east. The Laptev Sea shelf at 71°N, 141°E is east of the Lena delta (which sits at ~126°E), and the Yana mouth is at ~136°E, 71.5°N. The Svyatoy Nos peninsula is at ~140.7°E, 72.9°N. The point at 71°N, 141°E is therefore in the sea north of the coast, east of the Yana Bay. The coast near 141°E is at about 72°N, so 71°N would be inland.

Checking this: the Bolshoy Lyakhovsky Island lies at 73.3°N, 142°E. The mainland coast at 141°E, near the Ust-Yansk / Kondratyevo area, is around 71.5°N to 72°N. The Yana-Indigirka lowland lies at 71°N, 141°E, so this is land and the terrain is flat tundra, with elevation of maybe 20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":25}

8818.text:
The point is at 7°S, 177°E, in the western Pacific. That puts it south of Tuvalu: Nanumea is near 5.7°S, 176.1°E, and Niutao is near 6.1°S, 177.3°E. Nui is near 7.2°S, 177.15°E and Vaitupu is near 7.5°S, 178.7°E, so the point lies in open ocean close to Nui, a few tens of km away.

The seabed in this area is deep, typically 3000–4500 m. The Ellice Basin lies to the south and west, with depths of about 3500–5000 m. Tuvalu's atolls sit on seamounts that rise steeply from roughly 3500–4000 m. The point is about 25 km from Nui, so the slope there could be somewhere around 3000–4000 m deep. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

14779.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -141 is in West Antarctica, in the Ross Sea sector. The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part extends to about 150°W, and Marie Byrd Land lies east of there. At -141° and -75°, the area is in the Ross Ice Shelf, near its eastern edge. The Ross Ice Shelf front is at about -78 latitude, and the shelf spans from about -77 to -85, but in the east, the coast of Marie Byrd Land at 140°W lies around -77. At -75 and -141, that would be Marie Byrd Land's interior or the Sulzberger Ice Shelf region, with Sulzberger Bay near -77 and -148. So -75 at -141 is likely inland, on grounded ice in Marie Byrd Land, with an ice surface perhaps around 1000 m. The Executive Committee Range is at -76.8, -126. Near -75, -141 the ice is likely the Ford Ranges' eastern side; surface elevation is perhaps 600-1000 m.

I estimate a surface of about 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

200.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -139° lies in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole, on the Amerasian side. The Lomonosov Ridge runs roughly from Ellesmere/Greenland across the pole toward the Siberian shelf, at about 140°E to 40°W. At 87°N, the ridge sits near 60°W to 50°W longitude on the Greenland side, and near 140°E on the Siberian side. The point at -139° is on the Canada Basin side, at about 87°N, 139°W. That is toward the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge region, but at 87°N it is closer to the Amerasian Basin edge, adjacent to the Lomonosov Ridge on the Makarov Basin side. The Makarov Basin has depths around 4000 m. Longitude -139° at 87°N is on the Canada/Makarov side, so the depth is probably about 3800 to 4000 m.

I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

10889.text:
The point (-31, -1) is in the South Atlantic Ocean, far from land. It lies roughly northeast of Tristan da Cunha (about -37, -12) and southwest of Africa. It is also east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs at about -14 to -15°W at this latitude, so the point sits on the eastern flank, on the Angola/Cape Basin side.

Seafloor on the ridge flank around 1°W at 31°S is probably about 3500-4500 m deep. The Walvis Ridge is farther north, near 30-35°S at 0-5°E, so it is not directly relevant. The Rio Grande Rise is to the west and also not relevant. At this age of crust, roughly 60-70 Ma at 1°W, depth would be around 4500-5000 m. I'll estimate about -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

11589.text:
Latitude -39, longitude -41 is in the South Atlantic, east of Argentina and Uruguay. It lies far from the coast, around the western edge of the Argentine Basin. The Rio Grande Rise is farther northeast, and the Vema Channel is near -31 lat and -39 lon.

The Brazil Basin and Argentine Basin depths at -39 lat are roughly 5000 to 5500 m. At this longitude the Zapiola Rise is farther southwest, near -44 lat and -45 lon. The Argentine Basin floor is about 5000 m deep, so I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

9893.text:
Latitude -19, longitude 167 is in the Pacific, near Vanuatu and New Caledonia. Vanuatu's southern islands are Aneityum at about -20.2, 169.8, Tanna at -19.5, 169.3, and Erromango at -18.8, 169.1. New Caledonia's Loyalty Islands (Maré) are at about -21.5, 168. Longitude 167 at latitude -19 is west of Erromango by about 2 degrees, which puts it in the basin between New Caledonia and Vanuatu. That area is the New Caledonia Trough or the southern part of the North Loyalty Basin, so the point is probably ocean.

The Loyalty Ridge runs from Maré to the northwest at around 167-168E at -20, and the Loyalty Basin lies to the east of it. Point -19, 167 is north of Île Art (Belep) at -19.7, 163.7. West of the Loyalty Islands, the d'Entrecasteaux ridge sits near -15, 167, and the Loyalty Islands chain ends near Ouvéa at -20.6, 166.5. The point at -19, 167 is just north of Ouvéa and in the Loyalty Basin or on the ridge's northern extension. The depth there is probably about 2000-3000 m, so I'll estimate -2500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

4511.text:
The point at 39°N, 157°W lies in the North Pacific, north of the Hawaiian Islands and northeast of the Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain. The Emperor chain lies near 170°E to 170°W, and the Hawaiian ridge sits at about 20°N, so this location is in open abyssal plain. The Murray Fracture Zone runs near 30°N, and the seafloor here is Cretaceous-age crust about 80-100 Myr old. Typical depths in this area are around 5,500 m. The Mendocino fracture zone is at about 40°N farther east, but at 157°W the depth is probably 5,400-5,600 m.

I estimate a depth of about 5,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

3773.text:
The point 49°N, 167°E is in the North Pacific, east of Kamchatka and south of the Aleutians. It lies in the northwest Pacific basin, east of the Kuril-Kamchatka trench area and west of the Emperor Seamounts. The seafloor there is abyssal plain at roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m depth.

The Emperor Seamount chain runs near 170°E at about 45–50°N, but 167°E is west of it, so the point is on the abyssal plain. I estimate the depth at about -5,400 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5400}

6156.text:
Latitude 21.0, longitude -107.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, southwest of the Mexican coast near Nayarit and Mazatlán. The coast at that latitude is around -105.4 (San Blas is at 21.5, -105.3), so this point is about 150 km offshore.

The Pacific floor near the mouth of the Gulf of California there is deep. The Middle America Trench lies further south, and the area around the Tres Marías islands lies to the east. At -107, 21 N, I estimate the depth at roughly 3000 m. The East Pacific Rise lies further west, near -108 to -109 at 21 N, with the rise crest at around -109, and the Gulf mouth floor is about 2500-3000 m deep. I am estimating rather than looking up a bathymetry value, so I'll use -2900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

7532.text:
Latitude 7.0, longitude 125.0 is in Mindanao, Philippines, near Davao City. Davao City is at about 7.07°N, 125.6°E, so 125.0°E lies west of it, in the Davao del Sur and Cotabato area. That is inland, near Mount Apo, which sits at about 6.99°N, 125.27°E. At 125.0°E the terrain is likely the foothills on the western side of Apo, around Kidapawan or Bansalan, so the surface is Solid.

Longitude 125.0 is about 30 km west of Apo's summit. Bansalan is at about 6.78°N, 125.2°E, and Magsaysay is near 6.75°N, 125.2°E. Digos is at 6.75°N, 125.35°E. Sta. Cruz is at 6.83°N, 125.4°E. The point 7.0°N, 125.0°E is probably near the Davao del Sur and Cotabato border, around Makilala or Matanao. Hmm, Kidapawan is at 7.01°N, 125.09°E with an elevation of about 300 m. A point 10 km west of it would be around 7.0°N, 125.0°E, which is near Magpet or Makilala and probably lower, roughly 200 m. I'll estimate 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

5630.text:
The point 27.0°N, 79.0°W is in the Atlantic Ocean, east of Florida and north of the Bahamas. It lies in the Florida Straits region, east of Palm Beach, roughly 120 km east of the Florida coast (Florida's coast is near 80.0°W). It is just east of the Florida Current at the western edge of the Blake Plateau and near the Blake Escarpment, and north of Grand Bahama (about 26.6°N, 78.5°W).

At 79°W and 27°N, the depth is probably about 800 m. The Florida-Bahamas Straits at 27°N have a Florida Current depth of about 800 m near 79.5°W. Moving east toward 79°W, the Blake Plateau slope and the Blake Escarpment lie further north and east. At 27°N, the Straits channel is the deepest part, with depths of about 800 m. I'll estimate -810.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-810}

13786.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 33 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Southwest Indian Ridge and north of Antarctica. The Antarctic coast near 33°E lies around 66-69°S, so this point is open ocean.

Bathymetry here is probably abyssal plain or the flanks of the Indian-Antarctic basin. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs near 55°S at this longitude. Farther south, around 63°S, 33°E, the depth is roughly 4000-4500 m, so I estimate about -4400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

14935.text:
The point is at 75°S, 171°E, which is in the western Ross Sea, near Terra Nova Bay and Ross Island. Ross Island is near 77.5°S, 167°E. At 75°S, 171°E, the location is off the Victoria Land coast, east of Cape Adare and the Northern Foothills. The coast near Terra Nova Bay (75°S, 164°E) lies about 7° of longitude to the west. At 75°S, 1° of longitude is about 29 km, so 7° is about 200 km. That puts the point in open Ross Sea, north of the Ross Ice Shelf edge (about 78°S). It is on the continental shelf, near the Drygalski Basin and Pennell Bank.

Pennell Bank sits around 73-74°S, 175-180°E, with depths of 300-500 m. Near 75°S, 171°E the seabed is probably about 500-600 m deep, since the Joides Basin and Drygalski trough are deeper, around 800-1000 m. I'll estimate -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

3443.text:
The point 51.0°N, 133.0°W is in the northeast Pacific, west of Vancouver Island and Haida Gwaii. Vancouver Island's west coast is near 128°W, so this point is about 400 km offshore.

The Explorer Ridge and the Juan de Fuca system lie further south, around 49.5°N and 130°W. The Queen Charlotte Fault is near 132°W at 52°N. At 51°N, 133°W the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or the lower flank of the Explorer plate region, at about 2,700-3,200 m depth. Seafloor near 51°N, 130°W is roughly 2,600 m. Moving west to 133°W it deepens a little, and the Pacific plate abyssal depths there are about 3,000 m. Some seamounts exist in the area, such as the Dellwood Knolls near 51°N, 130°W. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15871.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -117 is in West Antarctica, on the inland ice sheet near the Pole. The South Pole itself is at about 2835 m. At 87°S along longitude 117°W (the Marie Byrd Land / Ross side direction), the ice surface is about 2,000–2,500 m.

Longitude -117 at 87°S is roughly 330 km from the Pole. The direction toward 117°W runs toward the Ross Ice Shelf and Marie Byrd Land. The Pole is on the plateau at 2835 m, and the surface descends toward the Ross side. The Thiel Mountains lie near 85°S, 90°W, which is a different direction. In the area near 87°S, 117°W, the ice surface is probably around 2,600 m, with the Pole at 2835 m and the elevation falling gradually. This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2650}

3062.text:
The point 55.0°N, 175.0°W lies in the North Pacific, south of the Aleutian Islands. The Aleutian Trench runs south of the islands, and at this longitude the islands are near 52°N (Attu is at 173°E; Near Islands, Rat Islands, Andreanof Islands at 175°W is around Adak at 176.6°W, 51.9°N). So 55°N is about 3° north of the islands, in the Bering Sea.

The Bering Sea basin (Aleutian Basin) is deep, about 3800 m, in the western part. At 175°W, 55°N, this is the southern Aleutian Basin, just north of the Aleutian Ridge. The Bowers Basin is further west. The depth just north of the arc is about 3500–3800 m. The Bering Sea abyssal plain is about 3800 m, and the slope north of the arc near 53–54°N descends from about 1000 m to 3000 m. At 55°N it is likely around 3,700 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

7746.text:
The point is 3°N, 167°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands (Kingman Reef is at about 6.4°N, 162.4°W; Palmyra at about 5.9°N, 162°W). Nothing I know of is at 3°N, 167°W, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is the Pacific abyssal plain, typically 4,500 to 5,500 m deep. The area lies north of the Tokelau and Phoenix islands and west of the Line Islands ridge. The Line Islands chain runs from about 160°W toward the northwest, so 167°W is west of it, and I estimate the depth there at about 5,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

8471.text:
The point is at 5°S, 157°W, in the central Pacific. Kiritimati (Christmas Island) lies near 1.9°N, 157.4°W, and Malden Island is near 4.0°S, 154.9°W. Starbuck Island is near 5.6°S, 155.9°W. So 5°S, 157°W is about 100 km west of Starbuck and is open ocean.

The seafloor there is in the Line Islands region, with depths of roughly 5000 to 5500 m. I'll estimate about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

9971.text:
Latitude -21, longitude -37 is in the South Atlantic, off the coast of Brazil. The Brazilian coast near Espírito Santo/Bahia sits at about -40 longitude at latitude -21 (Cabo Frio is at -42). At -37 longitude, that is about 300 km east of the coast. The Vitória-Trindade seamount chain lies at about -20.5 latitude, running from the coast at -40 out to Trindade at -29.3. At -37 there are seamounts in the chain, such as Jaseur and Davis, though Davis is at about -34 and Jaseur at about -36.

Jaseur Seamount is near -20.7, -36.0 with a summit depth of about 100 m or less. Our point (-21, -37) is west of that, in the area between seamounts on the Vitória-Trindade ridge. The ridge base is around 3000-4000 m deep, and the region between seamounts is often about 3000 m, so I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

5435.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude -109.0 falls in northern Mexico, in Sonora near the Chihuahua border, east of Sahuaripa and in the Sierra Madre Occidental foothills. The coast is much farther west, so the surface is solid.

Sonora's Sierra Madre foothills there are rugged. The Río Yaqui and Río Moctezuma drainages are lower, around 500 m, while ridges reach about 1500 m. The point sits near the Sonora–Chihuahua border, which runs close to -109.05 at this latitude, so it is probably in the mountains. I estimate about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

1563.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude 67.0 (east) lies in the Kara Sea region at 67°E. Novaya Zemlya spans roughly 51–69°E, with its southern island at about 70–76°N. At 73°N, the island's east coast sits around 55–60°E, and its northern tip near 77°N reaches about 68°E. The Yamal Peninsula is at about 70–73°N and 66–72°E. Yamal's northern tip is near 73°N, 69°E, and Cape Bely on Belyy Island is at about 73°N, 70°E.

At 67°E and 73°N, this is probably the Kara Sea just west of the Yamal's northern coast. Yamal's northwest coast near 70°N runs at about 66.8°E, and by 72°N the coast is near 69°E. So 73°N, 67°E is in the sea, in the open Kara Sea west of Belyy Island, where depths are about 20–50 m. I'll estimate about -30 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-30}

12572.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 125 lies in the Southern Ocean south of Australia, on the Southeast Indian Ridge, which is the Australian-Antarctic spreading ridge. The ridge crest in this area is at roughly 50°S, 125°E, with depths around 2500-3500 m. The Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies near 120-128°E, 48-49°S, where depths are greater, around 4000 m. The ridge axis there sits at about 4000 m, with flanks at 3500-4500 m.

I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15961.text:
Latitude -87, longitude 63 is on the East Antarctic plateau, about 3° from the South Pole. At that longitude the surface is roughly 2,800–3,000 m or higher. Using the known South Pole surface elevation of about 2,835 m, the ice rises toward the Dome A direction, which lies at roughly 80°E, 80°S. At -87 latitude and 63°E, the surface is probably around 2,900–3,000 m.

This is an estimate from those reference points, not a looked-up value. I'll go with 2,950 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 2950}

11217.text:
The point is at latitude -35, longitude -65, which falls in Argentina, near the border of La Pampa, San Luis, and Córdoba provinces. It is most likely in southern San Luis or northern La Pampa.

San Luis province's southern edge is around -35.0 to -36.0 at that longitude. Terrain there is flat plains at roughly 300-400 m. At -65 longitude the elevation is about 350 m, which fits the area near Villa Mercedes (-33.7, -65.5, about 500 m), with the land sloping down to the south and east. La Pampa's northwest at -35, -65 is about 300 m. I estimate 320 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":320}

8440.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 141.0 is on the northern coast of Papua New Guinea, near the Indonesian border. The border lies at 141.0°E, and the coast near Vanimo is at about -2.7°S, 141.3°E. At -3.0 and 141.0 the point should be inland in the Papua (Indonesia) area, south of the coast near Jayapura's western side.

The coast around 141.0°E sits at about -2.6°S. At -3.0 we are about 40 km inland, in the Cyclops Mountains region to the east (140.5°E) and the lowland Tami area. The Van Rees mountains lie further south-west. The point is probably in foothills, with the Bewani Mountains to the west, and the elevation is probably a few hundred meters. I'll guess 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

11280.text:
The point -35, 61 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and southwest of Amsterdam Island (which is at about -37.8, 77.5). It is near the Southwest Indian Ridge and Central Indian Ridge region. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from around (-40, 50) toward the Rodriguez Triple Junction at about (-25.5, 70). At -35, 61 the ridge axis may be close, or the point may sit on the Mid-Indian Basin flank.

The ridge axis near 61°E is probably around -30 to -33 latitude, since the Central Indian Ridge runs from the triple junction at -25.5, 70 to the southwest. Between roughly 50 and 70°E the Southwest Indian Ridge runs from (-38, 47) to (-26, 70), so at 61°E it is about -32. The point at -35 is therefore a few hundred km south of the axis, on the ridge flank, where depths are around 3000-4000 m. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

3076.text:
Location 55N, 147W is in the Gulf of Alaska, south of Kodiak and southeast of the Alaska Peninsula. It lies in the deep Pacific basin, far from the continental slope, which is roughly 56-57N at 147W. The Alaska abyssal plain here is about 3,500-4,500 m deep. The Surveyor Fan and Surveyor Channel run through this region, and the seafloor there is around 3,800 m. Seamounts such as Patton and Murray lie farther east at about 54N, 150W, so they are not at this point.

I estimate a depth of about 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15838.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 177 is in Antarctica, on the Ross Ice Shelf's southern part or the area near the Transantarctic Mountains. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about -85 at longitude 180 (the Ross Ice Shelf's southern edge reaches ~-86 near 170-180E). The Beardmore Glacier mouth is near -83.5, 171E, and the Shackleton Glacier is near -85, 177E (flowing into the Ross Ice Shelf at about -84.5, 177E). The location at -85, 177E is likely near the Shackleton Glacier's lower part or the ice shelf, and the Ross Ice Shelf grounding line is around -85 there.

The Shackleton Glacier is at about 176-178W, which is not 177E. At 177E and -85, the point is likely on the Ross Ice Shelf, since the Transantarctic Mountains near 85S lie around 160-170E, then to the east at 180 the Reedy Glacier is near -85.5, 180... The shelf edge is around -85 to -86 there. I'd guess it's floating ice shelf: Water, with seabed elevation about -600 m (Ross Ice Shelf cavity depth in the south is around -500 to -700 m).

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

293.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 47°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge, which crosses the pole roughly along the 140°E to 40°W meridians. At 47°E the point is slightly off the ridge on the Eurasian side. The Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side of the ridge, and its floor is about 4000 to 4400 m deep. The ridge crest is about 1000 to 1500 m deep.

The ridge axis near 87°N sits around 140°E and -40°W (320°E). A point at 47°E is 93° from 140°E, and at 87°N that is only about 5° of arc at the surface, or roughly 5.2° × 111 km × sin(3°)... more simply, the longitude difference matters little near the pole. The ridge runs through the pole, so 47°E and 227°E (that is, 133°W) are opposite each other. The ridge line at about 140°E and 320°E is a different line from the 47°E–227°E line. Points at 47°E are therefore about 90° of longitude away from the ridge axis. At 87°N, 3° from the pole is about 333 km, so the point lies roughly 330 km from the ridge, which puts it in the Amundsen Basin. Its depth there is about 4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

3414.text:
Latitude 53.0 N, longitude 169.0 E is in the western North Pacific, southeast of Kamchatka and south of the Commander Islands. The Commander Islands lie near 55N, 166E, and the Aleutians begin further east at about 172E (Attu is at 173E). The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench is at roughly 50N, 160E. At 53N, 169E the seafloor is probably abyssal plain, about 5000 to 5500 m deep, though the western edge of the Aleutian Basin and the Bowers Ridge region are further north.

The point is south of the Komandorsky Islands and the Aleutian Ridge, so it falls in the Pacific basin south of the arc. Depths there are around 5000 to 5500 m. I'll estimate -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

15517.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -105 is in West Antarctica, inland of the Amundsen Sea sector, near the Ellsworth Land and Marie Byrd Land region. It lies on the grounded West Antarctic Ice Sheet, away from the Ross Ice Shelf, which begins east of about -150° and south of -78°.

This point is roughly 800 km from the pole. The ice surface there is probably about 1,500 to 2,000 m. The Thwaites and Pine Island catchments top out around 2,000 m or more, and the Ellsworth Land interior near -83, -105 is likely around 1,800 m. I'll estimate 1,900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

4185.text:
