Latitude 67.0, longitude -87.0 is in Nunavut, Canada, on the Melville Peninsula or near Committee Bay. Melville Peninsula spans roughly 66-70°N and 81-87°W. Committee Bay lies at about 68°N, 86°W, and the west coast of the peninsula runs near 87°W at 67°N. Repulse Bay is at 66.5°N, 86.2°W, so 67°N, 87°W is just west of Repulse Bay.

The Rae Isthmus sits near 66.7°N, 86-87°W, between Committee Bay/Simpson Peninsula and Repulse Bay. Simpson Peninsula extends west at about 68°N, and the Gulf of Boothia lies to the west. Ayr Lake is near 66.5°N, 86.5°W, and Hall Beach is at 68.8°N, 81°W. At 67°N, 87°W the point is probably on land near the Rae Isthmus, or on the Gulf of Boothia coast at about 87°W. The Rae Isthmus is low land, around 50 m elevation. I'll go with solid at about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

14441.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -97 is in the Amundsen Sea sector of West Antarctica, west of Thurston Island (which lies around 72°S, 100°W). At -71, -97 the location is likely open ocean or the Abbot Ice Shelf area. The Abbot Ice Shelf spans roughly 70–73°S and 80–100°W, and its western part is near 98°W, so this point is probably on or near it.

The seabed under the Abbot Ice Shelf and the nearby continental shelf is typically 500–1000 m deep. I'll estimate a seabed depth of about -600 m. Because floating ice shelves count as Water, the answer is Water with the seabed elevation.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

2245.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude -9.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, northwest of the Faroe Islands and east of Iceland. Iceland's east coast is near -13.5°W at 65°N, so -9° is about 250 km offshore. This area is in the Norwegian Sea, south of Jan Mayen. It lies on the Iceland-Faroe Ridge's northern flank or the Icelandic plateau, where depths are around 1500-2000 m.

The Iceland-Faroe Ridge sits at about 62-64.5°N, and its depth is about 500 m. At 65°N, -9°W, we are north of the ridge, in the Norwegian Basin's southwestern edge near the Aegir Ridge. The Icelandic Plateau at 66°N, -10°W is roughly 1500 m deep. At 65°N, -9°W the depth is probably about 1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

8688.text:
The coordinates -7.0, -83.0 fall in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 700 km west of Peru's coast, so the surface type is Water.

Off Peru, the Peru-Chile Trench lies near 80-81°W at about -6000 m. At 83°W the seafloor is on the Nazca Plate, west of the trench. Near 7°S, the trench is around 79.5°W. At 83°W the seafloor is probably about 4,000 m deep, which is typical for the abyssal plain there. The Nazca Ridge lies further south, and the Carnegie Ridge is further north near the Galápagos, so neither is likely to affect this point. I estimate -4200 m, a rough estimate based on typical depths for the region rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

12067.text:
The point (-45, -165) is in the South Pacific, southwest of the Chatham Rise and well south of the Cook Islands. It is roughly 1,500 km southeast of New Zealand, in the deep ocean basin of the Southwest Pacific Basin. Depths there are typically about 4,500 to 5,500 m.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies farther south, around 55-65°S, so it does not apply here. The seafloor at this location is probably abyssal plain at about 5,000 m depth. I estimate -5,100 m from the typical regional depth, not from a specific lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

14432.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -115 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. That area lies north of Thwaites Glacier and Pine Island Bay, near the Getz and Dotson ice shelves. Thwaites Glacier is around -75 S and -107 W, Pine Island Bay is near -74.5 S and -102 W, and the Getz Ice Shelf is around -74 S and -125 W. The coast of Marie Byrd Land at longitude -115 sits near -74 S, so -71 S is open ocean on the continental shelf edge or slope. The Amundsen Sea shelf break lies around -72 to -73 S, so -71 S is probably deep water, perhaps 3000 to 4000 m deep. I'm estimating the depth from this geography rather than from a lookup, and I'll take about -3900 m.

Water is the surface type, since this is open ocean.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11625.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 31 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa. It lies south of the Natal Valley and east of the Agulhas Plateau, in the Transkei Basin or on the Southwest Indian Ridge side.

The Transkei Basin is around 31°S, 33°E. At 39°S, 31°E the seabed is probably on the Agulhas-Southwest Indian Ridge region, and the Atlantic-Indian Basin sits at roughly 4,500-5,000 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge near 39°S runs at about 45°E, so it is not at 31°E. The Agulhas Plateau spans about 25°E at 40°S, so this point is east of it. The Mozambique Basin lies further north. The Agulhas Passage is around 4,500 m. I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4850}

5410.text:
The point 29.0°N, 159.0°W is in the North Pacific, north of the Hawaiian Islands. Kure Atoll is at about 28.4°N, 178.3°W and Midway at 28.2°N, 177.4°W, so neither is close. The Hawaiian chain near 159°W lies at about 22°N (Kauai), so this point is about 7° north of it, in open ocean.

Seafloor depth there is roughly 4,500–5,500 m. The area north of Hawaii sits on Cretaceous-age crust at about 4,800–5,500 m. The Hawaiian Arch and moat lie just north of the islands at about 24°N, so 29°N is beyond them on the abyssal plain. I'll estimate about -5,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

6150.text:
The point 21°N, 119°W lies in the open Pacific, west of Mexico and southwest of Baja California. It is roughly 1,000 km west of Baja and north of the Revillagigedo Islands.

The seafloor here is abyssal plain on Pacific plate crust about 30-40 Ma old. Depths in this region are typically 3,800-4,300 m. The area is west of the East Pacific Rise and north of the Clarion Fracture Zone, which is at about 18°N. Depths near 21°N, 119°W are likely around 4,000 m, so I estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

15551.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -37 is in Antarctica, in the Weddell Sea sector. Here the Filchner Ice Shelf lies roughly between 75-80°S, and the Foundation Ice Stream and Support Force Glacier are near 83°S, 50°W. The Pensacola Mountains are around 84°S, 60°W. At 37°W and 83°S, the position is in the Recovery Glacier or Slessor Glacier region. Slessor Glacier is at about 80°S, 30°W. Recovery Glacier is around 81-82°S, 28-35°W. Further south at 83°S, 37°W is likely grounded ice on the plateau, the Dronning Maud Land / Coats Land interior, with an ice surface elevation of roughly 1000 m.

The Recovery ice stream's upper catchment extends toward 85°S. The Bailey Ice Stream is around 80°S, 28°W. At 83°S, 37°W, the ice surface is probably about 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

4159.text:
