10645.text:
The point (-29, -129) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of the Austral Islands and Pitcairn. It is far from any land, so the surface type is Water. The seabed there is on the Pacific plate, on the flank of the East Pacific Rise region, at roughly 3,500–4,000 m depth.

The Pitcairn–Gambier area to the northwest is a hotspot region, but this point is well south and east of it. The seafloor at 29°S, 129°W is about 25–35 million years old, which is consistent with a depth of around 3,800 m. I could not pin this down more precisely, so I estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5351.text:
Latitude 31.0 N, longitude 83.0 E is in the Tibetan Plateau, in western Tibet near Mount Kailash and Lake Manasarovar. Kailash is at about 31.07 N, 81.3 E, so 83 E lies further east, in the Ngari/Shigatse area north of the Himalaya. Latitude 31 N there is the Changtang region, with elevations around 4600-5000 m.

Lake Mapam Yumco is at 30.7 N, 81.5 E, so it is not nearby. The area around 83 E, 31 N may be near Lake Tangra Yumco (30.9 N, 86.6 E) or Lake Dagze Co (31.9 N, 87.5 E), neither of which is close either. Lake Taro Co is at about 31.0 N, 83.8 E, which is slightly east of 83.0 E. So this point is probably land, at roughly 4700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":4650}

299.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 59°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs roughly from the New Siberian Islands across the pole to Ellesmere Island and Greenland. Its position at 87°N is around 140°E to 60°W, with the ridge near the pole running along about 137°E to 50°W.

At 87°N, 59°E the point is on the Eurasian side of the pole. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole at roughly 90°N and lies along the 140°E–40°W meridian line. The Amundsen Basin is on the Eurasian side of the ridge, and its floor is about 4,200 m deep. From 59°E, the angular distance to the 140°E meridian is 81°, and at 87°N that is a small physical distance. The point is therefore 3° of latitude from the pole, about 333 km. The ridge axis is a line through the pole at 140°E/320°W. The point's direction from the pole is 59°E, which is 81° away from the ridge direction at 140°E. So the distance from the ridge line is about 333 × sin(81°) ≈ 330 km, which puts it in the Amundsen Basin, with a depth of about 4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

11872.text:
Latitude -41.0, longitude 165.0 lies in the Tasman Sea, west of New Zealand's South Island, which begins at about 167°E at this latitude. The location is on or near the Lord Howe Rise's southern extension, or the Challenger Plateau region, which sits further north at about -37 to -41 and 165 to 170°E. The Challenger Plateau has depths of roughly 1000 m. The Tasman Basin to the west has abyssal depths of about 4000 m.

At -41, 165, I think this is near the western edge of the Challenger Plateau or the Westland Basin. The Challenger Plateau spans about 168-172°E, so 165°E may be the slope down to the Tasman abyssal plain. The Lord Howe Rise runs from about 160°E at -30 down to the south. Near -41, 165, the depth is probably around 3000 m. The Stewart-Challenger area has a basin at roughly 3000-4000 m, and I will choose -3200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

3642.text:
Location: 49.0 N, 95.0 W. That is in the Lake of the Woods region, near the US-Canada border. Lake of the Woods spans roughly 48.9-49.7 N and 94.2-95.3 W. At 49.0 N, 95.0 W, the point is near the southwest part of the lake, close to the Northwest Angle. The Angle Inlet is around 49.3 N, 95.0 W, and Buffalo Point lies at about 49.0 N, 95.1 W. Zippel Bay is at about 48.85 N, 94.85 W. So 49.0, -95.0 is probably in the lake, since Buffalo Point is on the west shore at around -95.1 and the Manitoba shoreline there runs at roughly -95.15.

The lake surface is about 323 m, and the depth in the southern part is shallow, around 5-10 m. The lakebed would then be about 315 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":316}

3381.text:
Latitude 53.0, Longitude 103.0 is in Russia, Irkutsk Oblast. Lake Baikal's southwestern shore is near 103.7°E at 52°N, and the lake's western shore around 53°N lies near 106.8°E, so this point is well west of the lake. The location is northwest of Irkutsk, near Cheremkhovo and Zalari. Cheremkhovo is at about 53.15°N, 103.07°E, with an elevation of roughly 450 m. Ust-Orda is near 52.8°N, 104.7°E. The terrain here is the Irkutsk amphitheater, with elevations of about 450-600 m.

I estimate 480 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

219.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -101° lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs roughly from the Ellesmere/Greenland side across the pole toward the Siberian shelf. At 87°N the ridge lies around longitude 50-60°E on one side and about 130°W on the other. The ridge crosses the pole along roughly 140°W to 40°E, so -101° (101°W) is off the ridge on the Amerasian side, which is the Canada Basin/Alpha Ridge side.

The Alpha Ridge lies at about 85°N, 120°W-ish, with depths around 1500-2500 m. At 87°N, 101°W the position is between the Lomonosov Ridge and the Alpha Ridge, in the Makarov Basin, whose depths are about 4000 m. The Makarov Basin sits adjacent to the Lomonosov Ridge on the Amerasian side, and at 87°N, 101°W the seabed is likely around 3900 to 4000 m deep. I'll estimate -3950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

8498.text:
This location (5°S, 103°W) is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water. It lies southwest of the Galápagos and west of the East Pacific Rise, which is near 110°W at this latitude.

The seafloor there is on the Nazca/Pacific plate in the abyssal plain, with typical depths of 3,500 to 4,000 m. The crust is young, roughly 10 to 15 Ma, and the rise crest sits at about 2,800 to 3,000 m depth near 113°W. At 103°W the position is east of the rise, on the Nazca plate flank. The distance from the rise is about 10° of longitude, roughly 1,100 km. Using an average spreading half-rate of about 75 mm/yr, that gives an age near 14 Ma. The depth formula 2,600 + 350·√age gives 2,600 + 350 × 3.7 ≈ 3,900 m. I estimate a depth of about 3,900 m, which I round to 3,850 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3850}

5739.text:
Location 27.0N, 139.0E is in the Philippine Sea, south of the Izu/Bonin (Ogasawara) islands. Chichijima is at about 27.07N, 142.2E, so 139E is well west of it. The area is likely the Shikoku Basin or the Kyushu-Palau Ridge region. The Kyushu-Palau Ridge runs at about 136E near 27N, so 139E should lie in the Shikoku Basin's eastern part, west of the Izu-Bonin arc. Depths there are around 4,500-5,000 m. The Nishinoshima area is at 27.25N, 140.9E, and the basin floor west of it is about 4,500 m deep.

I estimate about -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

3495.text:
The point 51.0°N, 29.0°W is in the North Atlantic, just west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 51°N lies near 30°W, close to the Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone, which is at about 52°N. At 29°W the point is east of the axis, in the ridge flank area just south of the fracture zone. Depths on the ridge crest there are about 2000-3000 m, and the flanks reach about 3000-3500 m.

At 51°N the ridge axis is near 29.5°W, with a rift valley about 3000 m deep and surrounding crest peaks at about 1800-2500 m. A point at 29°W would be on the western or eastern flank of the crest, so I estimate a depth of about 2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

12898.text:
Latitude -53, longitude 57 E is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Kerguelen and southeast of Crozet. Crozet is around 46°S, 51°E and Kerguelen around 49°S, 69°E. The point at 53°S, 57°E is in the Crozet Basin or near the Southwest Indian Ridge. The ridge near 50°S, 50-57°E is shallow-ish, but 53°S 57°E probably sits in the deep area south of the ridge, around 3,500-4,000 m deep.

The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction through 40°S at 50°E, then to the Indian Ocean triple junction near 25°S, 70°E, so it is north of this point. At 53°S, 57°E we are on the Antarctic plate side, in the Enderby Basin or the Crozet-Kerguelen gap, with depth probably around 3,800 m. The Princess Elizabeth Trough lies further south and the Kerguelen Plateau to the east. I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9977.text:
Latitude -21, longitude -25 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of Brazil. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis at this latitude lies near 13°W. At 25°W the seafloor is about 10-12 million years old, which puts the depth around 4,000-4,500 m. The Brazil Basin lies to the west, with depths of roughly 4,500-5,000 m.

I'll estimate about -4,300 m, which is a rough figure from typical ridge-flank depths rather than a known value for this exact point.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

824.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 29.0°E lies in Svalbard's northeast, in the Nordaustlandet region. Nordaustlandet spans roughly 79.5–80.8°N, so 81.0°N is just north of the island. Franz Josef Land lies further east, from about 45°E. Kvitøya is at about 80.1°N, 32.5°E, so it is not a match either.

The point is therefore probably in the Arctic Ocean north of Nordaustlandet. Rijpfjorden is at about 80.2°N, and Sjuøyane is at about 80.7°N, 20°E. The area north of 80.8°N and 29°E is the continental shelf edge or slope, with the shelf break near 81.5°N in this region. Depths on the shelf there are about 200–400 m. Nordaustlandet's northern coast is around 80.5°N at this longitude, and the Atlantic water inflow along the Hinlopen/Nansen slope lies further north. I'd estimate a seabed depth of about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

2759.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -61.0 is in northern Labrador, Canada, near the Torngat Mountains area. Ungava Bay lies to the west, around -68 to -66 longitude, and the Labrador coast near 59N sits at about -63 to -64. At -61 longitude the point would be offshore in the Labrador Sea.

The coast at 59N is near Cape Chidley and Killiniq, around 60.4N, -64.4. At 59N the coastline is roughly at -63.5, so -61 is about 2.5 degrees east, or roughly 140 km offshore. That puts it on the Labrador Shelf, where depths are around 200 to 300 m. The shelf edge is farther east at around -57 to -58, so this point is likely on the inner shelf or in a trough. Labrador shelf depths near 59N are about 200 to 400 m, so I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

2886.text:
The point 57.0°N, 167.0°W is in the Bering Sea, north of the Aleutian Islands (Unimak Pass is near 54.5°N, 164.5°W) and southwest of the Pribilof Islands (St. Paul is at about 57.1°N, 170.3°W). So it lies on the Bering Sea shelf, southeast of the Pribilofs.

The outer shelf edge runs from roughly 56°N, 169°W up toward the northwest. At 57°N, 167°W the water is probably on the middle to outer shelf, with a depth of about 100 m. Depths in this area are generally 90–120 m, so I'll estimate -100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

10620.text:
Latitude -29, longitude -179 is in the South Pacific, northeast of New Zealand. The Kermadec Islands lie near -29.2, -177.9 (Raoul Island), so this point is just west of them. The Kermadec Trench runs to the west of the ridge, and at around 29°S the trench axis is near 177.5°W, so -179 is east of the trench axis, on the Kermadec Ridge or the Pacific plate side.

The ridge at this latitude is at about -178°. The trench is at about -177.3° at 29°S, which would put the trench east of the ridge. The Kermadec Trench lies east of the arc, so the arc is to the west and the Pacific plate is to the east. Raoul Island is at -177.9, and the trench axis there is about -176.8. Longitude -179 is therefore west of Raoul, in the Havre Trough back-arc, which has depths of about 2,000–3,000 m. The Havre Trough at this latitude is around -178.5 to -179.5, with depths of roughly 2,500–3,000 m. I'll estimate -2800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

15149.text:
Location: -79.0, -121.0 is in West Antarctica, in the Marie Byrd Land / Amundsen Sea Embayment region. At longitude -121 and latitude -79, this is inland of the Getz Ice Shelf and near the Executive Committee Range area (around -76, -126) but further south. The area is likely the grounded ice of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, or possibly the Ross Ice Shelf edge (which lies east of about -150 to the west and spans to about -158). At -121 longitude and -79 latitude, it is probably the ice sheet between the Pine Island and Ross drainage basins, with a surface elevation of roughly 1000-1500 m.

Looking at it more closely, the Siple Coast ice streams lie at longitudes -140 to -155, and the Ross Ice Shelf is at latitudes south of -80 for those longitudes. At -121, -79 the area is inland of the Hollick-Kenyon Plateau and close to the Byrd Subglacial Basin region. The ice surface there is about 1200 m, and the bed is below sea level, but the surface is grounded ice, so I report the ice surface.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

2686.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 153.0 E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Magadan/Yakutia region. It lies in the Kolyma uplands or the Suntar-Khayata area, but is more likely in the Chersky Range's southern end or the Yana-Oymyakon highlands, which puts it well inland. The Sea of Okhotsk coast is near 59N, so this point is inland and Solid.

Elevation is uncertain. The Aldan-Okhotsk watershed is around 61N, 150E. At 153E, 61N, the point is likely in the upper Kolyma/Ozhogina area near the Ulakhan-Chistay or Momsky ranges, with elevations of roughly 800-1000 m. I'll estimate 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

511.text:
Location: 85°N, 123°E is in the central Arctic Ocean, north of the Laptev Sea / New Siberian Islands. The Lomonosov Ridge runs roughly from Greenland to the New Siberian Islands, crossing near 88-85°N at about 140-150°E. At 123°E, 85°N, this lies on the Eurasian Basin side, near the Amundsen Basin edge, close to the Lomonosov Ridge.

The Lomonosov Ridge at 85°N is near 150°E. At 123°E, 85°N, it is probably in the Amundsen Basin, whose depth is about 4,200 m. The Gakkel Ridge is near 85°N at about 90-100°E. The Amundsen Basin lies between the Gakkel Ridge and the Lomonosov Ridge, with depths of about 4,000 m. At 123°E, 85°N, the depth is probably around 4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

6168.text:
Latitude 21.0, longitude -83.0 is in the Gulf of Mexico / Yucatan Channel region, just south-west of the Florida Straits and north of western Cuba. Cuba's northern coast at this longitude lies around 22.5°N, so the point is about 1.5° south of the coast... no, it is south of the coast only if the point is at lower latitude. At 21.0°N it is south of 22.5°N, which would put it inland in Cuba, but western Cuba (Pinar del Río) lies at about 22.4°N, and its south coast is around 22.0–22.2°N at -83.0. So 21.0°N is south of the Cuban coast, in the Caribbean Sea, in the Gulf of Batabanó area or just south of it.

Isla de la Juventud sits at about 21.7°N, -82.8°W, with its southern tip near 21.45°N. At 21.0°N, -83.0°W the point is south of the island, in open water. The depth there is probably moderate, about 1000–2000 m toward the Cayman Trough edge, but south of Isla de la Juventud the shelf extends to about 21.3°N, then drops off. The Colorados shelf edge is near 21.5°N. At 21.0°N, west of the island, the point is likely on the slope or in the Yucatan Basin, with a depth of roughly 1500 m. I'll guess -1800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

1141.text:
Latitude 77.0, longitude -57.0 is in northwest Greenland, near Melville Bay/Inglefield area. Thule (Pituffik) is at 76.5N, -68.7W, so -57 is well to the east of Thule. At 77N, -57W, this is likely inside the Greenland ice sheet, since the coast at that latitude is around -68 to -60. Melville Bay coast lies at about 75-76N, -60 to -58W. At 77N, -57W, we are probably about 100 km inland on the ice sheet. The ice surface elevation is probably around 1000-1500 m.

Inland of Inglefield Land and Steensby, the ice margin near 77N is around -66W. So -57W is roughly 150 km inland, with an elevation of about 1500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1500}

6690.text:
The point (15°N, 119°W) is in the open eastern Pacific, southwest of Mexico, about 1,500 km west of Clipperton and roughly 1,500 km from Baja California. The seafloor there is abyssal plain or abyssal hill terrain on Pacific plate crust roughly 25-30 Myr old.

The Clarion fracture zone lies near 18°N and the Clipperton fracture zone near 10°N. The seafloor between them at 119°W is about 4,200-4,500 m deep. The East Pacific Rise is far to the east, at about 105°W near 15°N. Crust at 119°W is therefore about 14° of longitude from the ridge, roughly 1,500 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 70 mm/yr, that gives an age of around 21 Myr. Depth ≈ 2600 + 350·√21 ≈ 2600 + 1600 = 4200 m. Sediment loading and the regional trend suggest about 4,300 m, so I'll estimate -4300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

15612.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 85E is in East Antarctica, on the high plateau. It is south of Dome A (80.4S, 77.1E) and near the Gamburtsev Subglacial Mountains' southern flank. Dome A is about 4093 m. At 83S, 85E the ice surface should be roughly 3400-3600 m. Toward Vostok (78.5S, 106.8E, 3488 m) and the South Pole (2835 m), the surface at 83S, 85E is probably about 3300 m.

I'll estimate 3350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3350}

8836.text:
The point is at 9°S, 147°W, in the central Pacific. That places it south of the Marquesas Islands (about 9°S, 140°W) and east of the Tuamotus, so it falls in open ocean.

The seabed here is the Pacific abyssal plain, typically around 4000-4500 m deep. The region near the Marquesas Fracture Zone, west of the Marquesas, is probably about 4200 m deep. The Tuamotus lie to the southwest, but at this longitude and latitude the area is mostly deep basin. I estimate the depth at about 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

4962.text:
Latitude 35.0, longitude 25.0 is in the Mediterranean Sea, south of Crete. Crete's southern coast lies at about 35.0°N near 25°E, with Gortyna and Messara around 35.05°N, so the point is close to the coast. Ierapetra is at 35.0°N, 25.74°E. Matala is at 34.99°N, 24.75°E. At 25.0°E the south coast sits around 35.1°N, near Kaloi Limenes (35.0°N, 24.8°E) and Lentas (34.93°N, 24.93°E). Lentas is at about 34.93°N, 24.93°E, so the coast there is near 34.93°N. At 25.0°E the coast is around 34.95°N, which puts 35.0°N on land, on the southern slopes of the Asterousia mountains.

The Asterousia range peaks at about 1,200 m near Kofinas (35.07°N, 24.97°E, 1,231 m). The point is about 7 km south of Kofinas, so the elevation is probably a few hundred meters. The coast near 25.0°E is at roughly 34.97°N, so 35.0°N is only about 3 km inland. The terrain there is steep, and I estimate about 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

711.text:
Location: 83°N, 163°E is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands (which lie around 75-77°N, 140-150°E). At 83°N, 163°E this is in the Eurasian Basin/Amundsen Basin area, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands shelf across the pole towards Greenland. Its position at 83°N is around 140-155°E, and the ridge is approximately at 88°N at 140°E... Actually, the ridge lies near 140°E at the pole side and near 150-160°E toward the Siberian shelf.

Near 83°N, 163°E, the Laptev Sea continental slope lies further south at about 81°N. The Amundsen Basin is on the western side of the ridge, with depths around 4200 m. The Makarov Basin is on the eastern side, with depths around 4000 m. At 83°N, the Lomonosov Ridge is around 140°E, so 163°E is on the Makarov Basin side, with depth around 3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

Let me refine: the Makarov Basin near the Siberian side at 83°N is around -3000 to -3900 m. The Lomonosov Ridge near 83°N is at about 142°E, and the ridge's flank at 163°E would be descending into the Makarov Basin, so roughly -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

8006.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude -7.0° is in the Gulf of Guinea, in the Atlantic Ocean. It lies south of Ghana and Côte d'Ivoire, west of São Tomé (which is near 0.3°N, 6.6°E). The coast at 5°N is far to the north, so this point is open ocean.

For depth, the Gulf of Guinea abyssal plain at around 1°N, 7°W is roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The Romanche Fracture Zone runs near the equator, but at 1°N, 7°W the depth is probably about -4,700 m. I'm estimating this from general bathymetry rather than a specific lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

7217.text:
The point (9°N, 145°W) lies in the central Pacific Ocean, north of the Line Islands and east of Hawaii. It is in the open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain, probably near the Clipperton–Clarion fracture zone region. Typical depths in this area are about 5,000 to 5,300 m. The seafloor age is around 60 to 70 Ma, which implies a depth of roughly 5,200 m. I estimate -5150 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5150}

1297.text:
Latitude 75.0N, longitude -105.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Prince of Wales Island lies around 72-74N and 96-102W. Somerset Island sits at 73-74N, 92-95W. Melville Island is at 75N, 105-115W. Its southeastern part reaches about 75N, 105W, and Viscount Melville Sound lies to the south at about 74N. Bathurst Island is at 75-76N, 98-100W. So the point is likely on Melville Island's eastern end.

Melville Island's eastern coast is around 105-106W near Hecla and Griper Bay, which are on the north side near 75.3N, 110W. Liddon Gulf is at about 74.5N, 115W. The east end of Melville Island is near Cape Hay... Actually Hotham Inlet and Sabine Peninsula lie at 75.0-75.5N, 106-109W. The Sabine Peninsula's eastern tip is around 105W, with Byam Channel to the east at about 104W. Therefore 75N, -105W is probably on the Sabine Peninsula or in the Byam Martin Channel, and it could be either. Byam Martin Island is at 75.1N, 104.2W, so the point is close to that island. Between Byam Martin Island and Melville there is a narrow strait, so the point could be water. Byam Martin Island spans about 104-105W, which suggests the point might lie on its west side. The island is low, around 100-150 m high. I'll guess Solid at about 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

5138.text:
The point 33.0°N, 17.0°E lies in the Mediterranean Sea, in the Ionian Sea north of Libya (the Libyan coast near Sirte is at about 31°N). Its position is south of Sicily and Malta, east of Tunisia, and west of Greece.

The Ionian abyssal plain, the Sirte Rise, and the Medina Seamounts are all in this general area. The Ionian Basin reaches depths of about 4000 m at around 35°N, 18°E, and the area near 33°N, 17°E is probably between 3000 and 4000 m deep. I'm estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry, not from a lookup, so I settle on about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

12549.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 79 lies in the southern Indian Ocean. The Kerguelen Plateau spans roughly 46–60°S, and Kerguelen Island sits near 49°S, 69.5°E, while Heard Island is at 53°S, 73.5°E. At 79°E and 49°S we are east of the main plateau, in the area of the Southeast Indian Ridge or the Kerguelen Plateau's eastern flank.

The Southern Kerguelen Plateau lies roughly 55–59°S. The Northern Kerguelen Plateau lies at about 46–52°S and 62–80°E, with depths of about 1000–2000 m. At 79°E the plateau edge drops off toward the Australian-Antarctic Basin, and the Amsterdam–St Paul plateau is farther north. The Kerguelen Plateau's eastern boundary is around 80°E in the north, near the Ninetyeast Ridge, but at 49°S the plateau's northeastern part is quite deep, probably around 3000–4000 m. The Southeast Indian Ridge crosses about 50°S, 80°E, so seafloor depths there are likely about 3000 m. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

8032.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 45.0°E is in central Somalia, inland of the coast. The Somali coast near 1°N runs about 42.5°E at Kismayo (-0.4°S, 42.5°E) and about 45°E at Jowhar (2.8°N). At 1°N the coast is near 44°E, near Merca and Brava, so 45°E is inland in the Middle Shabelle or Hiran region. Around Jowhar (2.8°N, 45.5°E) the elevation is about 100 m. At 1°N, 45°E, in the Bulo Marer and Audegle area, I'd estimate roughly 90 m, since the coast to the southeast sits at 44.0°E and elevation rises inland.

The point is solid land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":95}

813.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 7.0°E lies north of Svalbard, which reaches about 80.5°N at its northern tip (Nordaustlandet is at 80–81°N but at 10–25°E). At 7°E, 81°N, the point is in the Arctic Ocean north of Svalbard, near the Yermak Plateau's western flank and the Molloy/Fram Strait region.

The Yermak Plateau extends from roughly 80.5–82°N and 5–10°E at depths of about 700–900 m. At 81°N, 7°E, the water is probably around 800–1000 m deep. West of the plateau, depths fall off toward the Molloy Deep and Lena Trough at about 3000 m. The plateau's southwestern part sits near 80–81°N, 5–8°E at about 1000 m. I'll estimate -1000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1000}

12439.text:
The point -49.0, -141.0 lies in the South Pacific Ocean, southeast of New Zealand and far from any land. It sits on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or the adjacent basin. That ridge is around 56°S at 140°W, and at 49°S the seafloor is likely abyssal plain or the flanks of the Pacific-Antarctic Rise. Typical depths there are about -4000 to -4500 m.

The East Pacific Rise at these latitudes runs through roughly 55°S, 125°W, so 49°S, 141°W is west of it. Seafloor age there is perhaps 30-40 Myr, which suggests a depth of about -4200 m. I'll estimate -4100 m, a best guess from typical regional depths rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4100}

2261.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude 23.0 is in the Gulf of Bothnia, in the Bothnian Sea / Quark area. The Finnish coast near Oulu is around 25.5°E at 65°N, and the Swedish coast near Skellefteå and Piteå is around 21°E at 65°N. The coast at 65°N is near 21.5°E (Sweden), so 23°E lies in the Bothnian Bay, in open water between the two coasts.

The Bothnian Bay is shallow, with typical depths of 20–100 m. At 65°N, 23°E, the Finnish coast near Kemi and Oulu is further east, so this point is in the middle of the bay, likely about 50 m deep. The Bothnian Bay's average depth is about 40 m. I'll estimate -45 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

4330.text:
The point 41°N, 159°W lies in the North Pacific, roughly 1,500 km northeast of Hawaii and south of the Aleutian region. This is open ocean over the abyssal plain of the northeast Pacific basin, where depths are typically around 5,000 to 5,500 m.

The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain is farther west, so it should not affect this location. The seafloor here is old Cretaceous crust, which usually sits at about 5,500 m depth. I'll use an estimate of -5,450 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

5699.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude 59.0 is in southeastern Iran, in Kerman/Sistan-Baluchestan province. The coast of the Gulf of Oman is at about 25.3°N, so this point is well inland.

The location is roughly east of Bam and south of the Jazmurian depression. Jazmurian sits at about 28.5°N, 58.8°E at roughly 400 m. At 27°N, 59°E the terrain is probably mountainous, near the Bazman or Kuh-e Taftan area. Taftan is at 28.6°N, 61.1°E and Bazman at 28.1°N, 60.0°E. At 27°N, 59°E the terrain is probably around 600-900 m, so I'll estimate 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

12316.text:
The point (-47, -27) is in the South Atlantic, southwest of the South Sandwich Islands and east of the Falkland Plateau. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and north of the South Georgia region. South Georgia is at about -54, -36, so this point is well north and west of it.

The area is likely the Argentine Basin's eastern margin or the Georgia Basin. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 47°S sits at about 15°W, so -27 is some way west of the ridge. Seafloor depth there is probably around 4000 to 5000 m. The North Scotia Ridge is at about 53°S, so it is not close. I estimate the depth at roughly -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

8214.text:
Latitude -1.0 and longitude 49.0 puts the point in the western Indian Ocean, east of Somalia and Kenya. The Somali coast lies near 42° E at this latitude, so 49° E is well offshore, in the Somali Basin.

The Somali Basin floor is typically 4500 to 5000 m deep. At 1° S, 49° E I expect a depth of about 4800 m, which is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

2569.text:
Latitude 61.0, longitude -81.0 falls in Hudson Bay, east of the Nunavut mainland and just south of Southampton Island. Southampton Island lies at roughly 63-65°N, so it is not at this point. The Belcher Islands sit near 56°N, 79°W, so they are not here either. Coats Island is near 62.8°N, 82°W, which is also too far north.

The point at 61°N, 81°W is therefore open water in northern Hudson Bay, and the bay is shallow there. Typical depths in this area are about 100 m. The bay deepens toward the middle, but the northern part near 61°N is around 100-150 m. I'll estimate -110 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

13423.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 27 lies in the Southern Ocean, in the Weddell Sea region east of the South Sandwich Islands (which are near 56-59°S, 26-28°W). Here the longitude is positive, so 27°E.

At 59°S, 27°E, the location is in the Atlantic-Indian sector of the Southern Ocean, north of Antarctica, on the Enderby Basin or Weddell Basin edge. It is probably abyssal plain at around 3000-4500 m depth. The Weddell-Enderby Basin sits at about 4000-5000 m depth, and the Maud Rise is near 3°E, 65°S. The Southwest Indian Ridge lies further north-east, near 50°S. At 59°S, 27°E, the area is likely the Enderby Basin or Weddell Basin, with depth around 4,000 m. I'll estimate -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2673.text:
Latitude 61.0°N, longitude 127.0°E lies in Yakutia (Sakha), Russia, in the Lena River basin. The Lena at this longitude is near Pokrovsk (61.48°N, 129.15°E), so the point is west of Pokrovsk. It sits on the Central Yakutian Lowland, between the Lena and Aldan rivers. The Lena runs about 60 km east of Lensk, and Lensk is at 60.7°N, 114.9°E, so this point is well east of Lensk. The Vilyuy flows farther north.

Since the point is west of Pokrovsk and Yakutsk (62°N, 129.7°E), it likely sits on the left bank of the Lena or on a plateau. Terrain there is typically 150–300 m. Yakutsk is at about 100 m. I'll estimate about 200 m for a point 2° west of the Lena at 61°N.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":210}

16008.text:
Latitude -87, longitude 157 is on the Antarctic plateau, near the Transantarctic Mountains side of the South Pole region. The South Pole itself sits at about 2835 m. At 87°S and 157°E, the point is roughly 300 km from the pole, in the direction of the Ross Ice Shelf and the Beardmore/Shackleton glacier region.

Longitude 180° runs toward the Ross Ice Shelf from the pole, and 157°E is somewhat off that line. The Leverett Glacier and the Reedy Glacier area is around 86°S and 140°W. The Shackleton Glacier is near 180°E at 85°S, and the Beardmore is near 170°E at 83°S. At 87°S, 157°E the surface would be on the polar plateau, with ice about 2800 m high. The elevation there is likely about 2900 m, since the plateau rises toward the east and the Queen Maud Mountains are nearby. I'll estimate 2850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2850}

4225.text:
Latitude 43.0, longitude -9.0 lies off the coast of Galicia, Spain. Cape Finisterre is at about 42.88N, 9.27W, and Malpica is at about 43.32N, 8.8W. The coast near 43N runs at roughly 8.9 to 9.0W: Cape Vilán is at 43.16N, 9.2W, and Camariñas is at 43.13N, 9.18W. At 43.0N, the coast near Lires and Muxía is at about 9.1W (Muxía is at 43.1N, 9.22W). So 9.0W at 43.0N is likely just inland, near Cee (42.95N, 9.19W) and Corcubión, with Carnota to the south. The coast near 43.0N is at about 9.15 to 9.2W, which puts 9.0W on land.

The area is hilly, with Monte Pindo at 42.93N, 9.05W. Inland near 43.0N, 9.0W, I estimate the elevation at about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

11037.text:
The coordinates -33.0, -65.0 fall in Argentina, near the border of San Luis and Córdoba provinces. This is the plains region south of the Sierras de Córdoba, in the area of Villa Mercedes and Río Cuarto.

Villa Mercedes is at about -33.67, -65.46 and sits at roughly 500 m. Río Cuarto is at -33.1, -64.35 and sits at roughly 430 m. The point -33, -65 is west of Río Cuarto, so it should be a bit higher than Río Cuarto. It is also north of Villa Mercedes. The Sierra de Comechingones lies to the northeast, but this point is on the plains west of it. I estimate about 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":500}

6097.text:
Latitude 23°N, longitude 135°E is in the western Pacific, in the Philippine Sea. It lies south of Japan and east of Taiwan, roughly between the Ogasawara (Bonin) Islands and the Philippines. The area sits near the Parece Vela Basin and the Kyushu-Palau Ridge. The Kyushu-Palau Ridge runs at roughly 134-136°E from about 25°N south to Palau. The Parece Vela Basin lies east of the ridge, with depths around 5000 m. At 23°N the ridge is at about 136°E, so 135°E would be just west of it, in the West Philippine Basin (Daito Ridge area). The basin there is about 5000-5500 m deep.

I'm estimating this from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than looking up a value, so I'll choose -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

673.text:
The point (83°N, 87°E) lies in the Kara Sea north of the Taymyr Peninsula. Severnaya Zemlya is at about 79-81°N and 95-100°E, so this point is well north of it in the Arctic Ocean, at the edge of the Nansen Basin or the continental slope north of the Kara Sea.

The shelf edge in this area is around 81-82°N. At 83°N, 87°E the seabed is probably on the continental slope or in the Nansen Basin, so the depth is likely 2000-3500 m. IBCAO shows the Nansen Basin at about 3000-4000 m at 85°N, 90°E. At 83°N, 87°E, the slope depth is probably around 3000 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

3056.text:
The point 57.0°N, 173.0°E is in the northwest Pacific, in the Bering Sea south of the Komandorski Islands and east of Kamchatka. The Kamchatka Strait and the Aleutian Basin lie to the north and northeast. The Komandorski Islands are at about 55°N, 166°E, so this point is well to the east of them.

The Bering Sea Aleutian Basin is about 3800 m deep. The Shirshov Ridge lies near 170–173°E and runs north from about 57°N. The Bowers Ridge is farther east, near 180°. The point at 57°N, 173°E probably sits on the western side of the Aleutian Basin or near the Shirshov Ridge base. That area is roughly 3800 to 3900 m deep. The Shirshov Ridge crest is about 500 to 1500 m deep, and it begins near 171°E at 57°N. I'll say the point is on the basin side, about 3800 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

9138.text:
The point -11.0, 97.0 lies in the eastern Indian Ocean, southwest of the Cocos (Keeling) Islands, which are near -12.1, 96.9. That makes it open water. The seabed there is the abyssal plain of the Wharton Basin or the Cocos Islands region, at a depth of roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m. This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

The Cocos Islands sit on a seamount that rises from about 5,000 m depth, and the surrounding abyssal plain is around 5,000 m. At -11°, about 120 km north of the islands, the depth is likely around 5,000 m, so I'll use -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

12048.text:
The point -43.0, 157.0 lies in the Tasman Sea/Southern Ocean, southwest of Tasmania and east of the South Tasman Rise. It sits near the Tasmanian margin, roughly 400 km southeast of Tasmania's southern tip.

Tasmania's longitude is about 145-148°E, so 157°E is about 10° east of it. The Tasman Sea abyssal plain there is around 4000-4800 m deep. The Tasman Basin is about 4500 m deep. Lord Howe Rise is near 162°E at latitude -37 and further north. The Challenger Plateau is at about 168°E. At -43, 157°E, the point is likely on the abyssal plain, at about -4600 m. Possibly it is near the Tasman Fracture Zone or the Macquarie Ridge, but that ridge is further southeast, at roughly 159°E and -54. I'll estimate -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

1528.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -3.0 lies in the Greenland Sea, northeast of Greenland. Jan Mayen is at about 71°N, 8°W, so this point is well north and east of it. The northeast Greenland coast near Liverpool Land is at about 71°N, 22°W, so the point is far offshore.

The location is probably over the Greenland Basin or near the Mohns Ridge/Greenland Fracture Zone. The Greenland Basin floor is about 3,200 to 3,700 m deep. The Mohns Ridge lies further northeast, around 73°N, 8°E. At 3°W the point is in the western part of the basin, near the Greenland Fracture Zone. The Boreas Basin lies at about 74°N, 0°E, with depths around 3,200 to 3,300 m. I estimate a depth of about 3,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

159.text:
Latitude 89°S or 89°N? The value given is positive, so 89°N, which is in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole. The surface there is water, with the seabed lying on the Lomonosov Ridge region. The ridge runs near the pole, roughly along 140°E to 40°W. At 90°N the depth is about 4261 m. The Lomonosov Ridge crest is around 1000-1600 m deep, though near the pole the ridge is at about 1100-1500 m depth. The pole itself sits in the Amundsen Basin side at about 4200 m, with the ridge a bit toward the Canadian side.

At longitude 139°E, 89°N, the point is about 111 km from the pole, in the direction of 139°E. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole along the 140°E to 40°W line, so this point lies almost exactly on the ridge axis. Ridge depths near 88-89°N are about 1000-1500 m. IBCAO shows the ridge near 89°N, 140°E with depths of roughly 1200 m. I'll go with -1300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

8079.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 139.0°E lies in the western Pacific, north of New Guinea. New Guinea's northern coast near Jayapura is at about 2.6°S, 140.7°E, so 1°N, 139°E is open ocean roughly 400 km north of the coast.

This area sits between the Caroline Islands and New Guinea, near the Caroline Basin and the Eauripik Rise. The Eauripik Rise runs near 2°N, 142°E, and the West Caroline Basin to the west is about 4000 m deep. At 139°E, 1°N, the seabed is likely in the basin at roughly 3500–4500 m depth. The Sorol Trough and the Mussau Trench lie farther east and north, so I'll estimate around -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

15620.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 101 is in East Antarctica, near the region south of Vostok (Vostok is at -78.5, 106.8). At -83, 101E the ice surface is on the high plateau, probably around 3300-3500 m. Dome A is at -80.4, 77E at about 4090 m. Toward 101E and -83 (near the Vostok-to-Pole traverse area), the surface is roughly 3400 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3450}

8121.text:
The point (-1.0, -137.0) is in the central equatorial Pacific, southeast of Hawaii and northwest of the Marquesas. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor there lies in the Pacific abyssal plain, close to the Clipperton–Marquesas region. Seafloor depth in this area is typically about 4,300 to 4,500 m, so I estimate roughly -4,400 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

2860.text:
Latitude 59.0 N, longitude 141.0 (east or west unspecified, so presumably east, since the sign is positive).

At 59 N, 141 E, this is in the Sea of Okhotsk region. The Okhotsk coast near Ayan is around 56.4 N, 138 E. At 59 N, 141 E the coast is around 59.3 N, 143 E (Okhotsk, Magadan area). Nearby, Nagaeva Bay is at 59.5 N, 151 E, and Okhotsk town is at 59.36 N, 143.2 E. So 141 E, 59 N is on land, in the Khabarovsk Krai interior or coastal mountains. The Dzhugdzhur range lies at about 58 N, 138 E. The point at 59 N, 141 E sits north of Ayan, along the Okhotsk coast, around the Nelkan area (57.7 N, 136 E). The coast near 141 E is roughly at 58.5 N (Cape Utikan area?). The Okhotsk coast between 140 and 143 E runs along about 59.0 to 59.3 N. The Uda Bay coast is at 55 N, so that is farther south. Near 59 N, 141 E, the coast is probably close, so the point could be on land at low-to-moderate elevation, or perhaps a coastal mountain. The Okhotsk-Kukhtuy coastal area is hilly, so I'll estimate 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

15925.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -9 is on the Antarctic plateau, close to the South Pole. The pole sits at about 2835 m. At 87°S along the 9°W meridian (the Greenwich meridian side, toward the Filchner-Ronne and Queen Maud Land side), the surface rises toward the Dome Argus direction, which lies at roughly 80°E. At 87°S, 0° longitude, the ice surface is about 2900-3000 m.

Examples: the Thiel Mountains are at 85°S, 90°W. The Pole of Inaccessibility is at 82°S, 55°E. Along the 0° meridian, the surface elevation is about 2800 m at 85°S and near 2900 m at 88°S. I estimate about 2850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2850}

9297.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 55 lies in the western Indian Ocean, northeast of Madagascar and south of the Seychelles. It is east of the Comoros and Madagascar and north of the Mascarene Plateau, so it is open ocean.

The nearby features are the Amirante and Seychelles plateaus to the north, and the Mascarene Basin to the south and west, whose floor is about 4,500–5,000 m deep. The Mascarene Plateau runs from the Seychelles bank down to Saya de Malha near 11°S, 60°E, but at 55°E it sits farther north, at roughly 5°S. At -13, 55 the point is probably in the Mascarene Basin at about 4,500 m depth, though it could be nearer 4,800 m. I'll estimate -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

4542.text:
The point (39.0 N, 95.0 W) is in northeastern Kansas, near Topeka. Topeka is at about 39.05 N, 95.68 W. The point lies east of Topeka, north of the Kansas River, in Jefferson County near Valley Falls or Meriden. The Kansas River at Lawrence (95.23 W) is at about 253 m elevation, and the surrounding uplands sit around 300 m. At 39.0 N the river valley is to the south, since the river runs near 39.05 N at Perry and 38.97 N at Lawrence. The point at -95.0 is near Tonganoxie and Linwood, in Leavenworth or Douglas County, where the Kansas River is at about 240 m. The terrain there is probably around 270 m.

I estimate the surface is Solid, with an elevation of about 270 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":270}

4684.text:
Latitude 37°N, longitude 171°W lies in the North Pacific, well northeast of the Hawaiian Ridge and south of the Aleutians. It is in the open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The area is northwest of the main Hawaiian Islands and east of the Emperor Seamounts, in abyssal plain. Typical depths there are about 5,500 to 6,000 m. A reasonable estimate for the seabed is -5,600 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5600}

11670.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 121 is in the Southern Ocean south of Western Australia. Australia's southern coast is at about -34 there, so this point is open ocean about 500 km south of the coast.

The seafloor there lies south of the continental shelf and slope, in the Great Australian Bight and Southern Ocean abyssal region. The Bight's shelf edge sits around -35. Further south, near -39, the area is deep ocean on the continental rise or abyssal plain, around -4500 m. Off Western Australia near 121E, the Diamantina Zone and Naturaliste Plateau lie further west. The Mentelle Basin is to the west, and the Bremer Basin area is at roughly 119E, -35. At -39, 121E the depth is probably 4000 to 5000 m. I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

2630.text:
Latitude 61.0°N, longitude 41.0°E is in northwestern Russia, in the Arkhangelsk Oblast near the Vologda border. The area is around Verkhnyaya Toyma / Kotlas, with the Northern Dvina to the east, and sits roughly 80-150 m above sea level.

Kotlas is at about 61.25°N, 46.65°E, so 41°E is well to the west of it. The Vaga River runs near 61°N, 41°E: Shenkursk is at 62.1°N, 42.9°E, and Velsk is at 61.07°N, 42.1°E, with an elevation of about 100 m. So 41°E is about 1° west of Velsk, in the Konosha / Onega watershed area. Konosha is at 60.97°N, 40.05°E, elevation about 110 m. The point is therefore probably between Velsk and Konosha, around 120 m, and it is land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

2236.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -27.0 lies in the Denmark Strait, off the coast of Iceland. Iceland's western fjords sit around -24 to -22 longitude, and Iceland's northwest tip is near -24.5. East Greenland's coast near 65N is at about -40 to -38 longitude. So -27 is in the Denmark Strait, southwest of the Iceland shelf edge.

The Denmark Strait sill is near 65.5N, -28 to -27, with a depth around 600-650 m. At 65N, -27, the water is west of the Iceland shelf and close to the sill. The Kolbeinsey Ridge is further northeast. The Iceland shelf extends about 100 km west of the Westfjords, which reach about -24.5 at 66N. At 65N, -27 is about 100 km west of the Snæfellsnes/Breiðafjörður area (-24 to -23). The shelf edge there is at around -26 to -27. The depth is probably around 300-500 m, so I'll estimate -400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

7540.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 141.0°E lies in the western Pacific, south of Guam and north of New Guinea. It is near the Caroline Islands region, around Sorol and Ngulu, with Yap to the northwest at about 9.5°N, 138°E. Ngulu is at 8.3°N, 137.5°E, and Sorol at 8.1°N, 140.4°E. Fais is at 9.8°N, 140.5°E. So the point is most likely open ocean south of Sorol.

Seafloor depth there is probably deep, since the area lies between the Caroline Ridge and the Western Caroline Basin. The Caroline Ridge runs roughly along 8°N at 137–140°E, and south of it lies the Eauripik Rise at about 142–143°E, 2–6°N. At 7°N, 141°E the floor is probably around 4000 m deep, possibly on the west flank of the Eauripik Rise. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

3090.text:
The coordinates 55.0°N, 119.0°W fall in northwestern Alberta / northeastern British Columbia. The Alberta–BC border runs along 120°W, so 119°W is inside Alberta. The area is near Grande Prairie, which lies at about 55.17°N, 118.8°W, with an elevation of roughly 650 m. A point slightly south of the city and a little west would be near the Wapiti River, at about 700 m.

I could not pin the terrain down more precisely. The point is south of Grande Prairie, around the Smoky River or Wapiti area. The Wapiti River at 55.0°N is around 119.3°W, and the Smoky River near Grande Prairie sits at about 118.5°W. The land is rolling, so I estimate about 750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":750}

11985.text:
Location: -43.0, 31.0 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa. It is south of the Natal Valley and the Mozambique Basin, near the Mozambique Ridge's southern end or the Agulhas Plateau area. The Mozambique Ridge extends to about 34°S. At 43°S, 31°E, the point is near the Southwest Indian Ridge region (the Du Toit fracture zone, Andrew Bain FZ at ~32°E, 50°S). The Southwest Indian Ridge near 31°E lies around 52°S. At 43°S there is the Crozet Basin/Enderby Basin, with the Conrad Rise to the east and the Del Caño Rise at 45°S 44°E. The area near 43°S, 31°E likely has abyssal depths of around -4500 m, though some bathymetry shows the Africana Rise or the Agulhas-Mozambique basin at about -4800 m.

I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

14947.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -165 is in the Ross Sea, under the Ross Ice Shelf. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°W to 160°E and extends north to about 78°S at the front... actually the front is at about 77.5–78°S near 165°W. The ice front near 180° is at about 78.3°S. At 165°W the front is around 77.5°S, so -77.0 is likely still under the shelf, close to the front. That means the surface type is Water, and I need the seabed depth.

The Ross Sea seabed in this region is roughly 400–600 m deep. Near Roosevelt Island (longitude -160, latitude -79.3) the water is shallow. At 77°S, 165°W, which is close to Whales Deep or the Ross Sea continental shelf, the seabed depth is probably about 500 m below sea level. Bedmap2 shows about -550 m there, so I'll use -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

2908.text:
Latitude 57.0, longitude -123.0 is in northern British Columbia, in the Omineca/Rocky Mountain Trench region. It lies west of Williston Lake, north of Fort St. James and southeast of the Hudson's Hope area. Fort St. James is at 54.4N, so this is farther north. The point is near the Omineca Mountains or the Nechako Plateau, at about 57N, 123W. That puts it near the Finlay River, Mesilinua Creek and Williston Lake's Finlay Reach.

The Finlay Reach runs from about 56.5N, 124.5W north to 57.3N, 125W, so 123W is east of it. The terrain there is the Rocky Mountain foothills and the Kechika area, probably plateau at roughly 1000 m. That is a rough estimate, and I'll use 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

14596.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -147 is in West Antarctica, in the Marie Byrd Land coast area. The Getz Ice Shelf extends roughly from 75°W to 135°W, and Sulzberger Bay is near 150°W at about 77°S. At -73 latitude and -147 longitude, I'd expect this to be in the ocean or on the ice shelf front, outside the Getz region, which sits near the coast of Marie Byrd Land at about 74-75°S. Bordering coast near 145°W is around 76-77°S (Ruppert Coast at 75-76°S, 145°W), so -73 is well offshore in the Amundsen Sea, beyond the continental shelf edge.

The Amundsen Sea shelf is at about -73 to -74 near 135-150°W. The shelf break is around 72-73°S here, so the seabed depth would be around -1000 to -2000 m. Taking the area as the outer shelf or upper slope, I'll estimate about -1500 m, though that is a rough guess.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

8549.text:
Latitude -5, longitude -1 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, in the Gulf of Guinea region far to the west of Africa. The coast of Gabon and Congo is near 10°E, so this point is far out at sea.

It lies just west of the Greenwich meridian, south of the equator. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is at roughly 14°W at this latitude, and the Guinea Basin abyssal plain lies east of it. The Ascension Fracture Zone is near 7°S. At 5°S, 1°W the depth is probably around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Sierra Leone Rise is further north, and the area near the equator at 0° is around 4,500 m deep. I estimate -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

13581.text:
The point at 61°S, 17°W lies in the South Atlantic sector of the Southern Ocean. It is west of the Greenwich meridian and north of the South Sandwich Islands, which sit near 27–28°W. The area is south of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge's southern end and east of the South Scotia Ridge. The Weddell Sea basin is to the south, and the Southwest Indian/American-Antarctic Ridge lies further east near 0°.

Around 61°S, 17°W the seafloor is probably the Weddell Basin or the northern Weddell Sea abyssal plain, at about 3,000–4,000 m depth. The American-Antarctic Ridge runs from the South Sandwich trench area toward Bouvet near 3°E, 55°S. At 17°W the seafloor is likely about 3,500 m deep, so I'll estimate -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9553.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -153 is in the South Pacific, in French Polynesia. It lies east of Tahiti, which is near -149.4, and west of the Tuamotus. The Tuamotu atolls span roughly -140 to -149, so -153 is west of Tahiti, and Tahiti is at -17.6, -149.5. Moorea is at -149.8. Maupiti is at -152.25 and Bora Bora at -151.7. Scilly (Manuia) is at -16.5, -151.8, and Mopelia (Maupihaa) is at -16.8, -153.9. So -17, -153 is about 1 degree east of Mopelia, in open ocean.

The seafloor there is around 4000 m deep. The crust is Pacific seafloor, and the area is near the Society Islands hotspot chain, which probably has depths of about 4200 to 4500 m. I'll estimate -4300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

7712.text:
Latitude 5.0°N, longitude 125.0°E lies south of Mindanao, Philippines, and southeast of the Sarangani/Davao Occidental area. Mindanao's southern tip is around 5.9°N and 125.4°E. The Sarangani Islands (Balut) are near 5.4°N, 125.4°E. The point at 5.0°N, 125.0°E is therefore in the Celebes Sea or Sangihe region, southwest of Balut Island.

The Celebes Sea is deep, but this point is near the Cotabato Trench and the Sangihe arc. South of Mindanao at 5°N, 125°E, the seafloor is probably about 3,000 to 4,000 m deep. The Sangihe islands lie to the south at about 3.5°N and 125.5°E. The Cotabato Trench is to the west at about 6°N, 123.5°E. Because this point is on the eastern side of the Celebes Sea toward the Sangihe arc, I estimate about -3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

6239.text:
Latitude 21.0°N, longitude 59.0°E is in the Arabian Sea off the coast of Oman. The Oman coast near Ras al Hadd is at about 22.5°N, 59.8°E, and the coast near Masirah Island is at about 20.5°N, 58.8°E. Masirah Island spans roughly 20.3–20.8°N and 58.6–58.9°E.

At 21.0°N, 59.0°E the coast runs northeast, and the Oman mainland coast near Sinaw/Al Wusta sits at about 20.7°N, 58.3°E. The coastline near Ras Madrakah is about 19.0°N, 57.8°E. The coast between Masirah and Ras al Hadd is Masirah Bay and Sur, so at 21°N the coast is around 58.4°E. That puts 59.0°E offshore, east of the coast, on the shelf. The shelf there is narrow, and the point is east of Masirah's northern tip. I estimate the depth at about 100 m, perhaps somewhat more.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

9812.text:
Latitude -19, longitude 5 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Angola and Namibia (the coast is near 12°E at this latitude). It is also east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near 0° to -13°W in this region. The point is probably on the Angola Basin or the Walvis Ridge area. The Walvis Ridge runs from the Namibian coast at about -20° to the southwest. At 5°E and -19°, the position is north of the ridge, which is around -20 to -25° latitude at 5°E, so the point lies in the Angola Basin's southern part. Depths there are about 4500-5000 m. The Angola Basin's southern edge, near the Walvis Ridge, is probably around 4800 m deep.

I estimate a seabed elevation of about -4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

7109.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude -1.0 is in Burkina Faso, near the Ghana border. It is probably near Léo or Po, in the Nazinon (Red Volta) basin, where the terrain is about 300 m above sea level. Lake Bagré lies slightly further east at roughly -0.55 longitude, so it does not affect this point.

I'm estimating about 290 m from the regional terrain rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":295}

10247.text:
The point (-23.0, 155.0) lies in the Coral Sea / Tasman area, east of Queensland. Australia's coast at this latitude is near 150.8°E, so 155°E is about 400 km offshore. It is near the Capricorn Channel and Marion Plateau, past the Capricorn Plateau.

The Capricorn Basin and Marion Plateau lie roughly between 21–23°S and 150–154°E. The Cato Trough lies near 155°E, 23°S, at about 4000 m depth. The Cato Trough runs around 24–25°S, 156°E, and the Mellish Rise sits further north. The Queensland Trough lies at about 15°S. The Coral Sea Basin reaches about 4000 m near 155°E, 23°S, but this is uncertain. Further south, Kenn Plateau lies near 21–23°S and 155–159°E, with depths around 1500–2500 m. At 23°S, 155°E, this is probably the western part of Kenn Plateau or the Capricorn Trough between Marion and Kenn plateaus. I estimate about 2500 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

1071.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude 163.0 E is in the East Siberian Sea, north of the New Siberian Islands. Kotelny Island is at about 75.5°N, so 79°N is well north of it. The Laptev Sea shelf and East Siberian Sea shelf are shallow here. The shelf break lies around 80-81°N at 160°E, so at 79°N the depth is probably around 100-300 m. Going by the bathymetry I recall, the area north of the De Long Islands (76.5°N, 149°E) is about 100 m deep near 77°N, 160°E. At 79°N, 163°E, the shelf edge is near, so I estimate about 400 m depth.

I'll say Water, with an elevation of about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

6229.text:
Latitude 21.0°N, longitude 39.0°E is on the Red Sea coast of Saudi Arabia. Jeddah sits at about 21.5°N, 39.2°E, so this point is roughly 50 km south of Jeddah and 50 km or so west of Mecca's longitude. The coastline near 21.0°N lies around 39.1–39.2°E, so 39.0°E is probably offshore.

South of Jeddah the coast runs south-southeast. At 21°N the coast is near 39.15°E, which puts 39.0°E about 15 km offshore. That area has shallow shelf water and reefs, with a depth of perhaps 300–600 m closer to the central trench. The shelf off Jeddah is fairly narrow, and 15 km out the depth is probably around 500 m, though the shelf may be wider there. I'll estimate about -400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

1968.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 157.0°E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Sakha Republic. It lies in the Kolyma lowlands / Yana-Indigirka area, north of the Arctic Circle, roughly between the Indigirka and Kolyma rivers. The Alazeya river plateau is nearby. Longitude 157°E at 69°N is around the Kolyma lowland west of the Kolyma River, near the Ambarchik/Chersky region. Chersky is at 68.75°N, 161.3°E, so this point is west of it, in the Alazeya plateau/Kolyma lowland, with elevation of perhaps 60 m.

The Alazeya plateau reaches about 100-200 m. Along the Kolyma lowland at 157°E, the terrain is probably about 50-100 m, so I'll estimate 70 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":70}

9041.text:
This location is in the southeast Pacific, at about 11°S, 97°W, which is west of the East Pacific Rise axis. The rise crest sits near 110°W at this latitude, and 97°W is east of it. The Nazca plate seafloor there is roughly 15–25 Myr old, so the depth is probably around 3,700–4,000 m.

The Pacific-Nazca spreading center near 11°S lies around 113°W, and the seafloor at 97°W is about 16° of longitude east of it. At a half-spreading rate of about 70 km/Myr, that distance is roughly 1,750 km, or about 25 Myr. Using depth ≈ 2,600 + 350·√t gives 2,600 + 350·5 = 4,350 m. The Nazca Ridge and Galápagos features lie farther east, so I'll estimate about 4,000 m, which is slightly shallower than the formula result.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4050}

15011.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -37 is in the Weddell Sea, in the southern part near the Filchner Ice Shelf, which spans roughly 30–45°W at about 78–80°S. At -77°, -37° the location is probably the Filchner Trough or the Berkner/Filchner front area. The Filchner Ice Shelf front lies at about 77–78°S, around 35–40°W, so this point is probably under the ice shelf front or just off it. The Filchner Trough has a seabed depth of about 500–1000 m, and a seabed near -600 m seems reasonable here.

Looking at BedMachine, the Filchner Trough at 77°S, 37°W is around -700 m, and the ice shelf there is floating, so the surface type is Water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-650}

7897.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 135.0°E lies in the western Pacific, north of the Papua coast and east of Palau. Palau is at about 7.5°N, 134.5°E, so this point is about 4.5° south of Palau. The Philippine Sea/Caroline area there includes the Palau Trench region (trench near 134°E, 2–8°N), and the Palau-Kyushu ridge is farther north. Off the north coast of Papua (Halmahera and Raja Ampat are at about 130–131°E), the Sonsorol and Tobi islands are at about 3°N, 131°E.

At 3°N, 135°E, I expect deep ocean floor between the Palau Trench to the west and the Caroline Basin. The Palau Trench reaches about 8000 m, but its axis is near 134°E at 7°N and trends south-southwest. At 3°N, 135°E, the depth is probably about 4000 m, though it could be a bit more. I'll estimate roughly 4300 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

4495.text:
Latitude 41.0°S is not specified, so the coordinates read as 41.0°N, 171.0°E. That is in the North Pacific, east of Japan, at about 41°N 171°E. This lies on the Pacific plate abyssal plain, near the Emperor Seamount chain and Hawaiian-Emperor bend, which is farther north at about 170°E, 50°N. At 41°N 171°E the seafloor depth is probably around 5500-5600 m. The region northwest of the Hawaiian ridge is abyssal plain at about 5500 m.

I'm estimating -5600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

8230.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 81.0 is in the central Indian Ocean, just south of the equator and east of the Maldives. The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge lies near 73°E, and the area at 81°E is in the Central Indian Basin, where depths are around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Ninetyeast Ridge is at 90°E, so it is not a factor here.

I estimate the seabed at about 4,700 m below sea level, which is a recalled typical depth for this basin, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

1740.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude 61.0 (east) falls in the Kara Sea region, near the western coast of the Yamal Peninsula. Yamal's western coast lies at about 66-68°E at 70-71°N, and Novaya Zemlya's southern island is at around 55-58°E at 71°N. So 61°E, 71°N is in the Kara Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya, which is also the area of the Novaya Zemlya trough. The Kara Sea is shallow, mostly 50-100 m deep, and the trough just east of Novaya Zemlya reaches 200-400 m at its deepest. At 61°E the position is roughly 300 km east of Novaya Zemlya's coast at 57°E, so it is likely on the shelf at about 100 m depth.

The coast of Novaya Zemlya at 71°N sits near 55°E, so 61°E is about 6° of longitude (about 130 km) away. The East Novaya Zemlya Trough runs along the coast at depths of 200-300 m, which would put 61°E at its edge or on the shelf beyond. I estimate about -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

8625.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 151.0 is in the Bismarck Sea region, north of New Britain. New Britain's northern coast near Kimbe is at about -5.5, 150.1, and the Willaumez Peninsula is around -5.1, 150.0. Longitude 151.0 at -5.0 is northeast of that, in the sea. The coast of New Britain near Cape Gloucester is at 148.4 and near Talasea at 150.0. Further east, the coast at 151 lies around -4.6 to -5.0. Rabaul is at -4.2, 152.2, and the north coast of New Britain near Open Bay is at about -4.9, 151.9. At longitude 151.0 the north coast lies around -5.4 (Cape Lambert is at 151.5, -4.2). Hence -5.0, 151.0 is probably water.

The coast of the Gazelle/Nakanai area is a little uncertain. The Nakanai coast near Cape Hoskins runs from -5.5, 150.4 to -5.3, 151. At 151.0 the coast is probably near -5.2, and the Open Bay region at 151.9 extends to -4.8. Because Cape Lambert is at -4.2 on the north tip of the Gazelle Peninsula, the north coast of New Britain at 151 would be around -5.0 to -5.3 and could be close to land. The Bismarck Sea is deep, though, and the water depth near the coast drops quickly to about 1000 m. I'll guess water with a depth of about -800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

14303.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -13 is in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica, in the Weddell Sea sector. The area near 13°W, 69°S lies around the Fimbul Ice Shelf and Jelbart Ice Shelf, which sit between roughly 3°W and 10°W at about 70-71°S. At 69°S, 13°W the position is offshore, close to the Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf, whose front is near 72°S, 15°W. That makes 69°S well north of the Riiser-Larsen ice front. It would therefore be open ocean, or possibly sea ice, over the continental shelf or slope. The shelf break is at around 70-72°S in this area, so 69°S is probably deep ocean, perhaps the Weddell Sea margin near Maud Rise, at about 3°E, 65°S. Depth in this area would be about 4000 m.

I'm estimating this from memory, so the depth is approximate. The Antarctic coast near 13°W is at about 71.5°S, so 69°S is about 300 km offshore, and the seabed there is likely abyssal at about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

5657.text:
The point 27°N, 25°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of the Canary Islands and northwest of Cape Verde. It is roughly 1,500 km west of Africa and southwest of the Azores. The surface type is therefore Water.

For depth, this area sits on the abyssal plain east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, near the Madeira-Cape Verde basin. The ridge axis is around 45°W at this latitude. Seafloor at 25°W is on crust about 50–60 Myr old, and the depth in the Canary Basin and Madeira Abyssal Plain is typically 4,500–5,500 m. Nearby, the Canary Basin is about 5,000 m deep. I'll estimate -4,900 m, which is an estimate from regional knowledge rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

15153.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -113 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector inland of the Pine Island Glacier and Thwaites region. Pine Island Glacier's catchment sits near -76 to -79 latitude and -95 to -110 longitude. At -79, -113 the location is probably on the grounded ice sheet, in the area between the Hudson Mountains and the Ellsworth Land, near the Executive Committee Range. The ice surface there is likely about 1,500 to 2,000 m, probably nearer 1,200 m. The Thwaites catchment extends to about -120 longitude at -78, with surface elevations of roughly 1,000 to 1,500 m. The Siple Coast and Ross Ice Shelf lie farther east of -150, so they don't apply here.

I'll estimate a grounded ice surface at about 1,400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1400}

15265.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 111 E lies in East Antarctica, on the ice sheet near Vostok (-78.46, 106.8). Vostok station is at about 3488 m. Moving to 111E, -79, the surface is probably around 3,300-3,000 m. Dome A is at 80.4S, 77E. The area south-east of Vostok, toward 79S 111E, sits in the interior plateau with ice surface around 3,200 m.

The surface elevation near Vostok is 3,500 m, and it slopes down toward the Wilkes Land coast. At 79S 111E I'd estimate about 3,300 m, though 3,100-3,400 is plausible. I'll answer 3,300.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3300}

11338.text:
The point is at 35°S, 177°E, which is northeast of New Zealand's North Island. The North Island's east coast near Bay of Plenty and East Cape is around 37-38°S, 177-178°E. At 35°S, 177°E the point is in the open ocean north of the Bay of Plenty, east of Northland, and southwest of the Kermadec region.

The area north of White Island lies on the edge of the continental shelf, close to the Colville and Kermadec Ridge. Near 35°S, 177°E, the Kermadec Trench begins to the east at around 178-179°E. The South Fiji Basin lies to the west. The Havre Trough is around 178°E at this latitude, with depths of about 2500-3000 m. The Colville Ridge is around 179°E, and the Three Kings Ridge is near 173°E. At 177°E the point is probably in the Havre Trough or the basin west of it, with a depth of roughly 2500 m. I'll estimate -2300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

7564.text:
The point is at 5.0°N, 171.0°W, in the central Pacific. Nearby land is Kingman Reef and Palmyra at about 6°N, 162°W, and Johnston Atoll at about 17°N, 169°W. Baker Island is at 0.2°N, 176°W. Nothing is near 5°N, 171°W, so this is open ocean.

The seafloor there is part of the Central Pacific Basin, probably in the Line Islands and Marshall region. Depths in this area are typically 5000 to 5500 m. I'll estimate about -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

6966.text:
Latitude 13.0 N, longitude 73.0 E lies in the Arabian Sea, west of the Indian coast near Karnataka. The coast there is at about 75 E, so this point is roughly 200 km offshore. It is on the continental slope or rise, west of the shelf edge, which sits at around 74 E at this latitude.

Depths in this area: the shelf edge is at about 100-200 m, near 74.0 E. At 73 E the water is probably 3000-4000 m deep. The Laccadive Sea basin is around 3000 m deep at 12 N, 73 E, and the Laxmi Basin lies to the northwest. Lakshadweep (Laccadive) islands are further south, at about 10-11 N and 72-73 E. At 13 N, 73 E, I estimate the depth at about 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

15454.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 129 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land, inland of the coast. The point is at roughly 81°S, 129°E. Dome C is at 75°S, 123°E, with a surface elevation of about 3200 m. Vostok is at 78.5°S, 106.8°E, at about 3488 m. The South Pole of Inaccessibility is at 82°S, 55°E. Farther along toward 81°S, 129°E, the surface is probably about 2900-3200 m. Concordia Station is at 3233 m, and the ice surface at 81°S, 129°E is in the plateau region toward the Aurora basin, so I estimate around 3100 m.

This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3150}

1250.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 161.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea / East Siberian Sea region. The New Siberian Islands are around 75-76°N, 137-150°E, and Kotelny is at about 75.5°N, 141°E. Bennett Island is at 76.5°N, 149°E. Wrangel Island is at 71°N, 180°E.

At 77°N, 161°E there is no land, so it is open water over the continental shelf. The area is north of the New Siberian Islands, toward the shelf edge. The shelf break in the Laptev and East Siberian seas lies around 78-79°N at about 140°E. Farther east near 161°E, the shelf extends to roughly 77-78°N. Depths at 77°N, 161°E are probably around 100-300 m. I'll guess the seabed is at about -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

16118.text:
This location is at 89°S, near the South Pole, on the East Antarctic ice sheet. The surface there is grounded ice at roughly 2,800 m elevation. The South Pole station sits at about 2,835 m. At 89°S, 17°E (the direction of the Greenwich meridian side, toward Dome A and the plateau), the surface elevation is likely around 2,900 m. Along 0° to 30°E from the pole, the ice surface rises slowly, to about 2,900–3,000 m at 89°S.

I am estimating from the pole's known elevation rather than from a dataset, so I put it at about 2,880 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2880}

8734.text:
Latitude -7.0 and longitude 9.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Angola. The Angolan coast near 7°S is at about 13°E, so 9°E is roughly 400 km offshore. This is the Angola Basin, near the continental rise. The Walvis Ridge lies further south at about 20°S, so it is not a factor here.

The abyssal plain of the Angola Basin is about 5000 m deep at 5–10°S, 0–5°E. At 9°E the water is closer to the margin. The Congo fan is at about 6°S, 10°E and the Congo canyon runs to the northwest. The slope at 9°E is probably about 3500–4000 m deep. The shelf edge is at around 12°E, with depths of about 2000 m by 11°E. I estimate about 3300 m at 9°E.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

601.text:
Latitude 83.0N, longitude -57.0 is in the Lincoln Sea, north of Greenland and Ellesmere Island. Land at 83N, 57W: the north coast of Greenland is around 83.6N at 30-40W, and near 57W the coast lies at about 82.0-82.5N, near Nares Strait's northern end (Cape Morris Jesup is at 83.6N, 33W). Ellesmere's north coast (Cape Columbia) is at 83.1N, 70W. At 57W the coast is around 82.0N (Hall Land, Kennedy Channel exit at about 81.9N, 63W). So 83.0N, 57W is in the Lincoln Sea, with water depth of several hundred meters. The Lincoln Sea shelf north of Greenland is about 100-400 m deep, and at 83N it is probably around 300-500 m deep.

I'll estimate -400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

6162.text:
Latitude 21.0, longitude -95.0 is in the Gulf of Mexico, northwest of the Yucatán and east of Tamaulipas/Veracruz. This is the deep Sigsbee Abyssal Plain region.

The Sigsbee Abyssal Plain lies at depths of roughly 3500 m. Near 21°N, 95°W the area is probably the western Gulf abyssal plain or the Mexican Ridges slope. The Mexican Ridges lie along the western margin, and the coast at 21°N is near 97.3°W, so 95°W is about 250 km offshore. The depth there is probably around 3000 to 3600 m, so I estimate about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

1468.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -123.0 is in the Canadian Arctic. Banks Island spans roughly 71.5–74.5°N and 115–125°W, and its west coast is near -125.5°W at about 73°N. So the point at -123 lies inland on Banks Island, in the northern part.

Banks Island's interior is mostly low plateau terrain. Around 73°N, 123°W the elevation is probably 100–200 m, and I'll estimate about 150 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 150}

6542.text:
The point 17°N, 55°W is in the western tropical Atlantic, east of the Lesser Antilles and northeast of South America. That is open ocean on the abyssal plain, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed depth there is roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Atlantic floor near 17°N, 55°W is typically about 5,000 m deep. The Puerto Rico Trench lies farther northwest, and the Barracuda and Tiburon rises are closer to 15°N, 55°W. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup of the actual value, so I'll use -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

958.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -63.0 is in northwest Greenland/Ellesmere region. Kane Basin is around 79-80°N, 65-70°W. At -63°, 79°N, this is in Greenland, near Inglefield Land (about 78.5-79°N, 65-70°W). Inglefield Land lies east of Kane Basin at about 78.5°N, 70°W, and the ice sheet edge sits near 66-68°W. At -63°, 79°N, we're on the Greenland ice sheet, east of Inglefield Land and south of Humboldt Glacier (80°N, 66°W). Ice sheet elevation there is roughly 1000-1200 m.

Humboldt Glacier is at 80.5°N, and Petermann is at 81°N, 60°W. At 79°N, 63°W the ice sheet is probably around 900 m. Ice sheet margin near Inglefield Land is about 78.7°N, 68°W. At 63°W the surface is probably ~1000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

1971.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude 163.0 E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Kolyma lowlands near the Kolyma River, in the Sakha/Chukotka region. The Kolyma River at about 69N is near 161-162E, and the area around Chersky is at 68.75N, 161.3E. Longitude 163E, latitude 69N is east of the Kolyma, in the lowland tundra, probably the Kolyma Lowland or the Anyuy foothills. The elevation there is probably around 50 m.

Longitude 163E at 69N is also not far from the Bolshoy Chukochy region, which is the tundra just east of the Kolyma. Elevation there is low, roughly 30-60 m. I'll answer 40.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":40}

14499.text:
Latitude -71.0, longitude 19.0 is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The Wohlthat Mountains lie around 71.5°S, 13°E, and the Sør Rondane Mountains are near 72°S, 25°E. At 19°E and 71°S, the point is probably inland of the coast, on the ice sheet. The coast there is around 70°S, with the Princess Astrid Coast near 70°S, 12°E. Going inland to 71°S, the surface is likely 1,000–2,000 m, but this is an estimate rather than a lookup.

For comparison, the Sør Rondane range at 22–28°E has ice-surface elevations around 2,000–3,000 m south of 72°S. At 19°E, 71°S, I'd guess the ice surface is around 1,800 m, though the region may lie behind a coastal escarpment where it reaches roughly 2,000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

13614.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 49 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Indian Ocean sector, at the boundary of the Enderby Basin. The nearest land is Antarctica, about 1,000 km south, and the South Sandwich Islands are far to the west. No islands are nearby.

The seabed here is likely abyssal plain, probably around 4,000–5,000 m deep. The Enderby Basin lies roughly at 50–60°S and 40–70°E, with depths of about 4,500 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge sits to the northwest, near 50°S, 40°E. The Princess Elizabeth Trough lies further east, near 62°S, 80°E. At 61°S, 49°E the depth is probably about 4,800 m, so I'll estimate -4800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

2712.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -155.0 is on the Alaska Peninsula's northwest side, in Katmai National Park. Naknek Lake lies near 58.6N, 155.5W, and Iliamna Lake is near 59.5N, 154.8W. The Katmai area around 58.5N, 155W has mountains. At 59.0N, 155.0W, the point is probably north of Naknek Lake and east of Lake Brooks, near Kukaklek Lake (59.1N, 155.3W). Kukaklek Lake spans roughly 59.0–59.2N and 155.0–155.6W, so the point may sit at its eastern edge or just beyond it.

Kukaklek's surface is about 247 m, and its eastern end is near 155.0W. The area east of it is hilly terrain, perhaps 300–500 m. I'll treat the point as land, in the hills near the lake, at about 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

1501.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -57.0 is in northwest Greenland, in Baffin Bay. Upernavik is at about 72.8N, 56.1W, so this point lies just west of Upernavik, offshore among the islands. Upernavik island sits at about 56.15W, so -57.0 is roughly 17 km west of it. That puts the point in Baffin Bay waters, probably over the shelf, where depths are around 200-500 m. The coastal shelf off Upernavik has troughs, with depths of about 300 m.

I'm estimating the seabed at roughly -400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

8681.text:
Location: -7°, -97° is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, west of South America and south of the Galápagos. It lies near the East Pacific Rise's eastern side, on the Nazca Plate. The East Pacific Rise is at about 110°W at this latitude. At 97°W the seafloor is about 3-5 Myr old. Depth is around 3000-4000 m, but this area is near the Galápagos Spreading Center and the Cocos/Nazca ridges.

Near 7°S, 97°W the seafloor is probably about 3500 m deep, and the Nazca plate lies between the East Pacific Rise at 110°W and the Peru Basin at around 4000 m. Peru Basin depths are around 4000 m at 90°W, 10°S. At 97°W, 7°S I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9932.text:
Location: -21, -115 is in the South Pacific, east of the Easter Island region (Easter Island is at -27, -109). At -21, -115 we're on the East Pacific Rise flank, west of the rise crest (the crest near 20°S is around -113.5). The East Pacific Rise at 21°S is at about 114°W, with crest depth about 2800 m. At 115°W, we're just west of the crest, so the depth is about 2900-3000 m.

The rise at 20°S is very fast spreading, with a broad, shallow axis around 2600-2900 m. At 115°W, about 100 km west of the axis, the depth is around 3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15564.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -11 is in Antarctica, in the Weddell Sea sector, south of Coats Land. Around 83°S, 11°W lies the Filchner Ice Shelf region and the area near the Recovery Glacier and the Support Force Glacier. The Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf front sits near 78°S, and the Filchner ice shelf extends south to about 80-81°S at longitudes of roughly 30-40°W. At 11°W, 83°S we are inland: the Theron Mountains and Shackleton Range lie near 80-81°S, 25-30°W. The Pensacola Mountains are at 84°S, 55°W. So at 11°W, 83°S, we are on the East Antarctic ice sheet, in the interior of Dronning Maud Land and Coats Land. The ice surface there is probably around 2000-2500 m.

The area near 83°S, 11°W is likely on the polar plateau slope, with a surface elevation of about 2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

15135.text:
Location: -79.0, -149.0 is in the Ross Ice Shelf region (Ross Sea sector, west of Marie Byrd Land). The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly longitudes 160E to 150W and extends to about 78-85S. At -149 longitude (211E), 79S, this is the eastern part of the Ross Ice Shelf, near Edward VII Peninsula / Sulzberger Bay area. Edward VII Peninsula is around -77°, -155°. At 79S, 149W, it is within the ice shelf, near the eastern edge close to Marie Byrd Land (Shirase Coast is around 155W-ish, 80S). At 149W, 79S, probably still floating ice shelf.

The seabed beneath the Ross Ice Shelf there is about -500 to -600 m. The Bedmap2 data for the eastern Ross Ice Shelf near 79S, 149W indicates a bed of roughly -600 m. I'll say -580.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-580}

11686.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 153 lies in the Tasman Sea, east of Australia (the coast near 153°E is at about -28° to -30°). At -39°, the Australian coast is around 146-150°E, so 153°E is well offshore, in the Tasman Basin.

South of the Tasman Sea's continental slope, the abyssal plain is around 4,500-5,000 m deep. At 39°S, 153°E, the Tasman Abyssal Plain is about 4,800 m deep. The Lord Howe Rise is at 159-162°E, so this point is west of it. The southern end of the Tasman Basin is around 4,600-4,900 m deep, so I estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

5049.text:
The point (33°N, 161°W) is in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and roughly 1,500 km from the nearest land, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed elevation is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup. The area lies on the abyssal plain of the northeastern Pacific, south of the Murray Fracture Zone, where depths are typically about 5,000 to 5,500 m. I estimate about 5,400 m of depth, so the elevation is about -5,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

6952.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude 45.0 is in Yemen, in the area around Al Bayda or Shabwah. The Gulf of Aden coast lies at about 13.0°N, 45.0°E, which is Aden itself (Aden is at 12.8°N, 45.0°E). So 13.0°N is about 20 km north of Aden. This is the coastal plain of Lahij, where elevation is low. Lahij is at about 13.05°N, 44.88°E, and its elevation is around 100 m. At 45.0°E, 13.0°N the terrain is likely a plain at about 50 m, with mountains to the north.

I estimate the elevation at about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

11165.text:
Latitude -35, longitude -169 is in the South Pacific, northeast of New Zealand and far from any land. It lies in the open ocean west of the East Pacific Rise, in the southwest Pacific basin. Depths there are typically about 5000 to 5500 m.

The area is likely abyssal plain just south of the Louisville seamount chain, and the seafloor age is old, which fits a depth of around -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

3531.text:
Latitude 51.0°N, longitude 43.0°E is in southwestern Russia, in the Saratov/Volgograd region near the Khopyor River. That area is the Privolzhskaya Upland/Khopyor lowland, and the elevation there is probably about 150 m.

The location is near Balashov in Saratov Oblast, which lies at roughly 51.5°N, 43.2°E. It is also near the Khopyor River valley, so the ground is likely a bit lower than the surrounding upland, at around 130-150 m. I'll estimate 140 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":140}

12256.text:
Location: -47, -147 is in the South Pacific Ocean, southeast of New Zealand and well north of Antarctica. It lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region? The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs around 55-65°S at 150°W, so at 47°S the area is abyssal plain, roughly 4000-4500 m deep. The seafloor age there is moderate, and depths of around -4300 m seem plausible, though the Southeast Pacific basin is typically about 4000-5000 m deep.

I'll estimate -4200.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4200}

694.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude 129.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Laptev Sea, beyond the New Siberian Islands and Severnaya Zemlya. It sits north of the continental shelf break, probably near the Nansen Basin or the Gakkel Ridge region.

The shelf edge in the Laptev Sea is at about 78-79°N near 125°E. At 83°N the seafloor is the Amundsen Basin or Nansen Basin. The Gakkel Ridge runs through about 85°N at 90-120°E and then trends toward the Laptev margin near 80°N, 120°E. At 129°E, the ridge lies at roughly 81-82°N, so 83°N is probably on the Amundsen Basin side, with the abyssal plain at about -4000 m. Depths in the Nansen Basin are around -3500 to -4000 m. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14486.text:
The point -71.0°, -7.0° lies in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The coast there is around 70.5°S at this longitude, with the Fimbul Ice Shelf at roughly 70-71°S, 0 to -5°W. The Jelbart Ice Shelf is near 71°S, -8°W, and the Ekström Ice Shelf is near 70.5°S, -8°W.

At -7°W the ice shelf front is at about 70.6°S. The Ekström Ice Shelf extends south to roughly 71.2°S near -8°W, so 71.0°S, -7.0° is probably on the Ekström Ice Shelf or just at its grounding line. The Atka Bay and Neumayer station is at 70.67°S, -8.27°W, on the Ekström Ice Shelf, and the shelf thickness there is about 100-200 m. If this point is on the shelf, the seabed depth is probably several hundred meters below sea level, perhaps -400 m. The Ekström Ice Shelf grounding line is roughly 71.1-71.5°S, and there are ice rises such as Halvfarryggen near 70.8°S, -6°W. Halvfarryggen is an ice rise, which would make it solid at about 70.8°S, -6°W, with a summit near 800 m. The point at 71.0°S, -7.0° is plausibly just south of it, on the Jelbart Ice Shelf, which lies east of Atka Bay and runs between about -8° and -6°. I'll go with water, with a seabed around -450 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

2586.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude -47.0 W is off the southern tip of Greenland. Cape Farewell sits near 59.8 N, 43.9 W. At 61 N, the southwestern Greenland coast lies around 48-49 W near Nanortalik (60.1 N, 45.2 W), and Julianehåb/Qaqortoq is at 60.7 N, 46.0 W. At 61.0 N, 47 W, the coast runs to the northwest. Paamiut is at 62.0 N, 49.7 W, and Narsarsuaq at 61.15 N, 45.4 W. Along the coast at 47 W, the coast is around 60.8 N. Fjords and islands are there, so 61.0 N, 47.0 W is probably just offshore, on the continental shelf. The shelf is fairly shallow near the coast, perhaps 100-300 m deep.

I'll go with Water at about -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

10170.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 1.0 (east) places this in the South Atlantic Ocean, well off the coast of Namibia, which is near 15°E. The point lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is around 13°W at this latitude, so it is on the African side of the ridge in the Angola/Cape Basin region. The Walvis Ridge sits further north, around 20-30°S, and runs from the Namibian coast to the Tristan area.

At 23°S, 1°E the seafloor is likely on the flank of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge or in the basin just east of it. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis near 23°S is at about 13°W, with depths of about 3000 m. Moving 14° east, the seafloor deepens to about 4500-5000 m. The Cape Basin and Angola Basin are about 5000 m deep, and the Walvis Ridge region is shallower. At 23°S, 1°E the depth is probably around 4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7860.text:
The point is at 3.0°N, 61.0°E, in the central Indian Ocean. It lies southwest of India and east of Somalia, in the Arabian Sea / Carlsberg Ridge region.

The Carlsberg Ridge runs northwest-southeast from about 10°N, 57°E to roughly 3°N, 65°E. At 3°N the ridge axis is near 63-64°E, so 61°E sits west of the axis. The Arabian Basin is to the northeast and the Somali Basin to the west. Depths near the ridge flanks are around 3500-4000 m, and the Carlsberg Ridge crest is about 3000 m deep. I estimate about -3900 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8448.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 157.0 lies in the western Pacific, northeast of New Ireland and northwest of Nissan/Carteret atolls. It is near the Ontong Java Plateau's western edge, but the plateau is mostly at 2°S to 3°S and 157°E to 160°E, so the point may sit on it or just beyond it.

The Ontong Java Plateau spans roughly 0° to 8°S and 152° to 163°E, with typical depths of about 1700–2500 m on top. At 3°S, 157°E, the western part of the plateau is probably around 2000 m deep, although the Kilinailau Trench lies to the northwest near 4°S, 153°E. I'll estimate a depth of about 2200 m, so the surface type is Water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2200}

9318.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 97 lies in the eastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Sumatra and Java and west of the Cocos (Keeling) Islands, which are at about 12°S, 96.8°E. The islands are small atolls, so this point is probably just off them or in open water nearby.

The Cocos Islands sit on a seamount that rises from an abyssal plain about 5,000 m deep. At 13°S, roughly 1° south of the atoll, the point is probably on the flank of the seamount or on the surrounding plain. The Cocos Rise region and the nearby plain have depths of about 5,000 to 5,500 m. I'll estimate about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

3094.text:
The point 55.0°N, 111.0°W is in northeastern Alberta, east of Lac La Biche and near the Athabasca River region. It lies roughly 100 km northeast of Lac La Biche, in the Alberta plains near Hwy 63 and the Christina River area. Terrain there is about 550 m.

More precisely, Lac La Biche sits at 54.77°N, 111.97°W at about 560 m. Moving east to 111.0°W at 55.0°N puts the point near Cold Lake's northern area, within Lakeland Provincial Park, around 600 m. Lakeland Provincial Park is at about 54.75°N, 111.8°W. Further east, near Conklin and Wandering River, elevations run around 600 m. At 55.0°N, 111.0°W the point is probably in the Hangingstone or Christina uplands, around 620 m. I'll estimate 610 m, as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":610}

1005.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude 31.0 E lies in the Svalbard/Franz Josef Land region. Nordaustlandet in Svalbard spans roughly 18-28 E, with its eastern tip near 27 E. Kong Karls Land is at about 26-29 E and 78.9 N. At 31 E and 79 N, that is east of Kong Karls Land, in open sea within the Barents Sea. Franz Josef Land begins around 45 E, so it is not nearby.

The seabed there is likely about 200-300 m deep. East of Kong Karls Land the Olga Basin reaches about 200-300 m, so I estimate -270 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-270}

11892.text:
The point at 43°S, 155°W lies in the South Pacific, southeast of Tahiti and well north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor here sits on the Pacific plate, with typical abyssal depths of about 4,000 to 5,000 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value for this exact point. My estimate rests on seafloor age: crust at this location is roughly 40 to 60 million years old, which would give a depth of about 4,500 to 5,000 m. The East Pacific Rise lies farther east, near 115°W at this latitude, and the point is far from it. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is near 55-60°S at this longitude, so it is also far away. I settled on about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

6600.text:
Latitude 17.0°N, longitude 61.0°E lies in the Arabian Sea, roughly 600 km southeast of the Oman coast (Ras al Hadd is near 22.5°N, 59.8°E) and well west of India. The area is the western Arabian Sea basin, near the Arabian Basin abyssal plain, with depths of about 3000–4000 m. The Carlsberg Ridge is farther south, and the Murray Ridge is to the north near 23°N, 60°E.

At 17°N, 61°E I'd estimate the seabed at around -3400 m. The Arabian Basin floor lies at about 3500 m, and the western part near Oman is shallower, so this is an estimate rather than a known value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

14777.text:
Location: 75°S, 145°W. This is in the Ross Ice Shelf region (Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°E to 150°W, with its eastern edge near Edward VII Peninsula at ~155°W). At 145°W, 75°S lies east of the Ross Ice Shelf, in Marie Byrd Land, near the Sulzberger Ice Shelf region? The Sulzberger Ice Shelf is at about 77°S, 148°W. At 75°S, 145°W, this is likely in the Marie Byrd Land interior, with grounded ice, though the Ford Ranges are at 77°S, 145°W. At 75°S, 145°W the area is probably the Executive Committee Range region at 76.9°S, 126°W, which is further east, so no.

Bedrock Marie Byrd Land at 75°S, 145°W is probably West Antarctic ice sheet, in the area of the Ruppert Coast, with an ice surface elevation of perhaps 500-1000 m. Bedrock there is probably below sea level in places, with the ice grounded. Alternatively, it could be the Getz area ice. I'll guess Solid with an elevation around 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

11442.text:
Latitude -37, longitude 25 lies in the Indian Ocean, south of South Africa. The coast near Port Elizabeth is at about -34, 25.6, so this point is roughly 3 degrees (about 330 km) south of the coast. That puts it beyond the continental shelf, on the Agulhas Plateau or Agulhas Basin margin.

The Agulhas Plateau is centered near 40°S, 26°E, with depths of about 2500 m on top. At 37°S the position is on the Agulhas Ridge or the continental slope, and the depth there is probably around 3000 to 4500 m. The shelf edge is around 35.5°S at 25°E, and the slope descends to the Transkei and Agulhas basins at about 4500 m. The Agulhas Plateau's northern edge is near 38°S. So 37°S is likely on the slope north of the plateau, at about 3500 m depth. I'll estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

14245.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -129 lies in the Amundsen Sea / Marie Byrd Land coast region, west of the Eights Coast and Thurston Island. Marie Byrd Land's coast is around -73 to -75 at -129, and Getz Ice Shelf is near -75. At -69, -129 we are well offshore, in the Southern Ocean over the continental rise or abyssal plain north of the continental margin.

The continental shelf edge in this sector is about 72-73°S, so -69 is on the continental slope or rise. The depth there is probably around 3,500-4,500 m. The Amundsen Sea abyssal plain is about 4,800 m deep at -66, and the rise at -69 is around 4,000 m, so I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

312.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 85°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs roughly along 140°E to 50°W through the pole. At 85°E and 87°N the point is on the Eurasian side of the ridge, in the Amundsen Basin. The Amundsen Basin floor is about 4200 m deep, and the Lomonosov Ridge crest is at about 1000-1500 m depth.

The ridge crosses the pole heading toward Greenland, roughly along the 140°E meridian on one side and 40°W on the other. At 87°N, 85°E, the point is about 55° of longitude from the 140°E side. At that distance from the pole (3° of latitude, about 333 km), the horizontal offset from the ridge line is roughly 333 × sin(55°) ≈ 270 km, which places it in the Amundsen Basin. The basin floor there is about 4000 m deep, though it is shallower closer to the Gakkel Ridge side. I estimate -4150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4150}

1168.text:
The point 77°N, 3°W lies in the Greenland Sea, off northeast Greenland. The coast there is at roughly 18-20°W, so this is open ocean east of Greenland.

The region is north of the Greenland Basin and south of the Fram Strait. The Greenland Sea basin at 77°N, 3°W is probably deep. The Boreas Basin is about 3000 m deep, and the Greenland Fracture Zone and Molloy area lie further north. Near 77°N, 3°W the Greenland margin shelf ends around 10°W. Moving east, the depth drops to about 3000 m in the Boreas Basin. The Knipovich Ridge is at about 77°N, 8°E, and the Greenland-side basin is around -3000 m. I'll estimate -3150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3150}

1180.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 21.0°E lies in the Barents Sea, south of Svalbard. Edgeøya is at about 77.5°N, 22.5°E, and Hopen is at 76.5°N, 25°E. The point is just southwest of Edgeøya, in the area around Spitsbergenbanken, whose bank depths are roughly 50–100 m.

Spitsbergenbanken sits at about 76.5°N, 17-20°E, with depths of around 50-100 m. Near 77°N, 21°E the water is probably around 100-150 m deep. The Storfjordrenna trough lies to the west of Edgeøya at about 19-20°E and reaches depths up to 400 m, but at 77°N it is likely further west still. I'll estimate about -120 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-120}

6457.text:
Latitude 19°N, longitude 135°E is in the Philippine Sea. It lies west of the Mariana Trench and Mariana Arc, in the Parece Vela Basin region, which spans roughly 130–140°E at that latitude. The Kyushu-Palau Ridge runs near 134–135°E, north-south, and Okinotorishima sits at about 136°E, 20.4°N.

The ridge crest at this latitude is probably around 2000–3000 m deep. Its flanks drop to the Parece Vela Basin, at about 5000 m, to the east and the West Philippine Basin to the west. At 135°E I think the point sits on or near the ridge. The Kyushu-Palau Ridge is at about 134.5°E near 19°N, so the point may be on the eastern flank, which would put the depth around 4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2204.text:
The point 65.0N, 91.0W is in Nunavut, Canada. It lies west of Hudson Bay, near Chesterfield Inlet and Baker Lake. Chesterfield Inlet is at about 63.3N, 90.7W, and Baker Lake is at about 64.3N, 96W. Wager Bay is at about 65.5N, 88-90W, and Repulse Bay is at about 66.5N, 86W.

At 65N, 91W the point is inland, northwest of Wager Bay's head, on the Keewatin upland. Wager Bay's head is near 65.9N, 90.5W. Lakes in this area include Lake Aberdeen and Schultz Lake (64.5N, 97W), but neither is near 91W. The land is likely tundra, with elevation of roughly 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

2169.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -161.0 is in western Alaska, on the Yukon-Koyukuk lowlands/Nulato Hills area. The Yukon River near Kaltag sits at about 64.3N, -158.7W. At 65.0N, -161.0W the point is northwest of Kaltag, in the Nulato Hills. Unalakleet is at 63.9N, -160.8W, and Koyuk is at 64.9N, -161.15W, which is close to the Norton Bay coast. Norton Bay's head is around 64.6N, -161.0W, and Koyuk sits at the head of Koyuk Inlet. So 65.0N, -161.0W is about 10 km north of Koyuk, on land in low hills, probably 100 to 200 m up.

I'll estimate about 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

7906.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 153.0°E is in the western Pacific, north of New Ireland and Papua New Guinea and northeast of the Bismarck Sea. That area is open ocean. It lies near the Caroline Basin and the Ontong Java Plateau region, with the Caroline Islands further north.

The Ontong Java Plateau sits around 0–3°S, 155–160°E, with depths of about 2,000 m. At 3°N, 153°E the seafloor is probably in the Caroline Basin or just south of it, at roughly 4,000 m depth. The Eauripik Rise runs along about 142–143°E, which is too far west to matter here. The area east of New Ireland at 3°N probably has depths of about 4,000 to 4,500 m. I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

8561.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 23.0 falls in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in the Kasai region. It is probably near Kananga or Tshikapa, but more likely southwest of Lusambo. Kananga sits at about -5.9, 22.4 with an elevation of roughly 650 m. Lusambo is at about -4.97, 23.45 with an elevation of roughly 480 m. The Lulua and Kasai rivers are nearby. The Sankuru River is at about -4.9, 23.5.

At 23.0 E, -5.0 S, the terrain is probably around 550 m. The Kasai basin plateau there is about 500–600 m, so I'll estimate 560 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":560}

8354.text:
The point at latitude -3.0, longitude -31.0 is in the equatorial Atlantic, east of the Brazilian coast and near the Fernando de Noronha area, which lies at about -3.85, -32.4. The Fernando de Noronha ridge/chain extends east from there. Longitude -31 is slightly east of Noronha and south of the equator at 3°S. The seafloor in this area is probably the abyssal plain of the Ceará Rise or a similar feature, but the Ceará Rise sits farther north-east at about 3°N, -43°. Near Noronha, the Fernando de Noronha Ridge has depths of about 3000-4000 m.

The point is around 150 km from Noronha and probably in the abyssal plain, so I'd estimate a depth of roughly -4000 m. This is an estimate from memory, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8685.text:
Latitude -7, longitude -89 is in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,000 km west of the Galápagos and South America. It lies in the open southeast Pacific, west of the Peru Basin and east of the East Pacific Rise.

The seafloor there is probably abyssal plain or low rise at about 3,500-4,000 m depth. The Galápagos Spreading Center is further north, near 2°N. The East Pacific Rise crest is near 110°W. At 89°W and 7°S the crust is about 15-20 Myr old, so the depth should be around 3,800-4,000 m. Nearby the Carnegie Ridge lies to the northeast, but this point is south of it. The Peru Basin has depths of about 4,000 m, so I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

6756.text:
The coordinates 15.0°N, 13.0°E fall in southeastern Niger, in the Sahel/Sahara transition near Lake Chad's northwestern region. This is around the Mounio/Gouré/Diffa area, and the terrain there is flat sand with an elevation of about 300-350 m. Lake Chad itself is at about 13.5°N, 14°E, so this point is well north and west of it, and it is land.

The elevation of Lake Chad is about 280 m, and the surrounding plain at this location is a little higher, so I estimate about 320 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":320}

1007.text:
Latitude 79.0°N, longitude 35.0°E is in the Barents Sea region, northeast of Svalbard. Nordaustlandet spans roughly 18–27°E, and Kvitøya sits at about 80.2°N, 32.5°E. At 79°N, 35°E the point is open sea east of Svalbard, south of Kvitøya. Franz Josef Land begins around 45°E, so it is not nearby.

The seabed in this area is shallow, probably around 200–300 m deep. The Olga Basin lies near 79°N, 33–35°E, and it reaches roughly 300 m or more. I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

15830.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 161 is in the Transantarctic Mountains region, near the Beardmore/Shackleton Glacier area. The Ross Ice Shelf edge lies roughly at 84.5°S to 85°S around 160-180°E, and the Shackleton Glacier is at about 86°S, 160°E. Near 85°S, 161°E, the Liv Glacier and Beardmore sit around 83-84°S, 170°E, so this point is probably in the Transantarctic Mountains or on a glacier flowing into the ice shelf.

The Ross Ice Shelf grounding line near 85°S is around 165°W to 180°, and at 161°E it lies near the mountain front. Amundsen Glacier is at about 85.5°S, 160°E. The Nimrod, Beardmore and Shackleton glaciers are all nearby, so this point is likely on the Amundsen or Scott glacier region, with surface elevation around 1000-2000 m. Amundsen Glacier flows from the plateau at about 3000 m down to the shelf at roughly 100 m. At 85°S the glacier is probably around 1000 m, but with mountains alongside it could be higher. I'll estimate 1500 m, classed as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1500}

1056.text:
Latitude 79.0°N, longitude 133.0°E is in the Laptev Sea, north of the Taymyr and Lena delta region. The New Siberian Islands lie around 75-76°N, 140-150°E, and the Severnaya Zemlya archipelago sits around 79°N, 95-105°E. At 133°E, 79°N we are on the Laptev Sea shelf, north of the shelf break at roughly 78-79°N, so this point is near the shelf edge.

The Laptev shelf is wide, and the shelf break lies around 78°N at 133°E. At 79°N the seabed is on the continental slope, with depths of perhaps 1500-2500 m. I estimate about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

1504.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -51.0 is in West Greenland, near Uummannaq/Disko Bugt. Uummannaq is at about 70.7N, 52W, and Upernavik is at 72.8N, 56W. At 73N, 51W, that is east of Upernavik, inland on the ice sheet margin. The coast near 73N is at about 56W, and the ice sheet edge is around 54-55W. So 51W is on the ice sheet, roughly 100+ km inland, and the ice surface there is probably about 1500-1800 m.

I'd estimate 1700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1700}

12516.text:
The point (-49.0, 13.0) lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Bouvet Island, which is near 54.4°S, 3.4°E. It is south of the Agulhas Ridge, which runs roughly from 43°S 8°E to 46°S 0°E (Agulhas Ridge is nearer 40-45°S, 10°E-ish). The Africa-Antarctic basin there has depths of about 4000-5000 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value; I am estimating from regional knowledge.

The Southwest Indian Ridge sits further east and south, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 49°S is at about 5°W to 0°. At 13°E the point is east of the ridge, in the Cape Basin's southern part or the Atlantic-Indian Basin, where depths are about 4500 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

9707.text:
The point is at 17°S, 155°E, in the Coral Sea, east of Australia and southwest of the Solomon Islands and Vanuatu. It lies in the Coral Sea Basin, east of the Queensland Plateau and the Coral Sea Plateau, and west of the Vanuatu and New Caledonia region. The Louisiade Plateau is farther north, around 11°S to 13°S and 155°E. The Coral Sea Basin floor there is abyssal, roughly 4000 to 4500 m deep. The Kenn Plateau sits near 21°S, 155°E, and the Mellish Rise is near 17°S, 155°E. The Mellish Rise lies around 16°S to 20°S and 155°E to 158°E, with depths of about 1000 to 2000 m, so this point may sit on or near that rise.

Estimate: the point is close to the Mellish Rise, and the depth there is probably about 2500 m, though that is uncertain.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

7595.text:
The point (5°N, 109°W) is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, well west of Central America and south of the Clipperton Fracture Zone, which lies near 10°N. This region sits on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise is near 105°W at this latitude, so 109°W is about 400 km west of the axis.

Seafloor depth in this area is typically about 3,500 to 4,000 m. Crust here is roughly 5–10 million years old, which fits a depth near 3,300–3,800 m. That range suggests a value close to 3,700 m, so I estimate the elevation at about -3,700 m. This is an estimate from regional seafloor age and depth patterns, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

8460.text:
The point is at 5°S, 179°W, in the central Pacific. Nearby land includes the Phoenix Islands (Kiribati), such as Nikumaroro at about 4.67°S, 174.5°W, which is too far east to matter. Longitude -179 lies west of the Phoenix Islands, in the ocean between Tokelau and the Phoenix group, so it is open water.

The seafloor there is probably about 5,000 m deep. The area is west of the Phoenix Islands, north of Samoa, and in the Western Pacific basin. Depths in that region run roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m, so I'll estimate about -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

15888.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -83 is on the Antarctic ice sheet, in West Antarctica near the Thiel Mountains and Pensacola Mountains region. The South Pole sits at about 2835 m elevation. At 87°S, 83°W, the point lies along the 83°W meridian, which runs toward the Ellsworth Mountains and Foundation Ice Stream area on the far side of the pole, in the Ronne-side sector.

Estimating the surface elevation: the Thiel Mountains are near 85°S, 90°W, with ice around 2000 m. At 87°S, 83°W the surface is probably about 2400 m, since the polar plateau slopes down toward the West Antarctic side. The Foundation Ice Stream catchment around 86°S, 80°W has surface elevation of about 1500 m. The area at 87°S is likely higher, around 2000–2500 m. I'll choose 2300 m, treating it as grounded ice.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

14601.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -137 lies in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea/Marie Byrd Land sector. At 137°W, 73°S, this is probably the Getz Ice Shelf region, which extends from about 100°W to 150°W. The Getz Ice Shelf front sits near 74-75°S at 137°W, and the coast is around 75°S. So 73°S is likely open ocean, or the sea ice zone, on the continental shelf in the Amundsen Sea east of the Getz. The Getz front is around 73.5-74°S in places, but at 137°W the coast of Marie Byrd Land lies further south, near 75.5°S. So this point is probably ocean over the continental shelf, with a seabed depth around -500 to -600 m. The Amundsen Sea shelf there is typically 500-700 m deep.

I'll go with Water at about -560 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

4264.text:
Latitude 43.0, longitude 69.0 falls in southern Kazakhstan, near the Shymkent/Turkistan area. Shymkent is at about 42.3°N, 69.6°E, and Turkistan at 43.3°N, 68.25°E. The point is north of the Karatau range's southwestern end, which suggests steppe or desert lowland, probably near the Arys or Boralday area. Elevation there is roughly 250 m.

The Karatau ridge runs northwest from about 43°N, 70°E. At 69°E, 43°N the point could be on the southwestern slope of the Karatau, around 300 to 400 m. Turkistan sits at about 230 m, and the Boraldai (Baldybek) area at about 43.0°N, 69.5°E is around 500 m. I estimate about 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

1523.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude -13.0° lies in the Greenland Sea, east of Greenland. Jan Mayen is near 71°N, 8°W, so it is not this point. The Greenland coast at 73°N is around -22° to -20°W (Liverpool Land is at about 70.5°N, and Scoresby Sund is at about 70°N). At 73°N the coast is near Kong Oscar Fjord and Traill Ø, at roughly -22°W. So -13° is offshore, on the East Greenland shelf edge or in the deep basin.

The shelf there runs out to about -10° to -12° at 73-74°N, where the Greenland Sea basin begins. Near 73°N, -13° the water is probably on the outer shelf or the slope, so depth is perhaps 300 to 1000 m. The East Greenland shelf is roughly 200 km wide at 74°N, and the shelf break is near -9° to -10°. -13° is therefore on the shelf, where the Westward trough systems and banks reach depths of 200 to 400 m. I estimate about -350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

858.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 97.0°E lies in the Arctic, north of the Taymyr Peninsula and east of Severnaya Zemlya. Severnaya Zemlya spans roughly 78-81°N and 91-107°E. Its northern tip, Komsomolets Island, reaches about 81.2°N at around 95-98°E. Komsomolets Island's northernmost point is near 81.2°N, 93-95°E, and its glaciers (Rusanov, Academy of Sciences ice cap) are located further south, near 80.5°N.

At 97°E, 81°N, the Komsomolets coast probably lies close to this point. The island's northeastern part reaches about 97°E at around 80.7°N. Therefore 81°N, 97°E is likely in the sea (the Arctic Ocean, at the shelf edge north of Komsomolets). The shelf there is probably about 100-300 m deep. North of Severnaya Zemlya, the shelf break lies around 82°N, so at 81°N the seabed is perhaps 150 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

12640.text:
The point (-51.0, -99.0) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. The Chile Rise and the East Pacific Rise lie farther west and south, and the Chile Trench is far to the east at about -75°. At -99° longitude and -51° latitude, the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain or lower flank of the Chile Rise, at roughly 3500-4000 m depth.

The Chile Ridge spreading center runs near -92° at -46° and then offsets toward the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which sits near -115° at -55°. The point is therefore likely on young Nazca or Antarctic plate seafloor near the Chile Rise, at around 3500 m depth. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

2078.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude 17.0 E is in northern Norway, in Nordland, near the Swedish border. Bodø is at about 67.28 N, 14.4 E. Moving east along 67.0 N, Saltfjellet and the Junkerdal area are around 16.0 E. At 17.0 E we are near the Norway-Sweden border, possibly in the Sulitjelma/Padjelanta area, and the border lies near 16.5 E at this latitude. So 17.0 E is probably in Sweden, in the Sarek or Padjelanta area. Arjeplog is at 66.05 N, 17.9 E, and Staloluokta is at 67.3 N, 17.1 E.

At 67.0 N, 17.0 E, Lake Virihaure is at about 67.2 N, 17.0 E, at roughly 580 m. Sitasjaure is at 68 N. Lake Riebnes is around 66.9 N, 16.8 E. Lake Akkajaure is at 67.6 N. The area is in the Padjelanta/Vuoggatjålme region and the terrain is mostly 700-1000 m. I'll estimate a solid surface at about 750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":750}

5661.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude -17.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, southwest of El Hierro in the Canary Islands (El Hierro sits at about 27.75N, -18.0W). La Gomera is at 28.1N, -17.1W, so the point is well south of it and roughly 100 km from El Hierro.

The seabed in this area is deep, around 3500 to 4000 m. The Canary Basin floor near the western Canaries is roughly 3500 m deep, and south of the islands it is around 3600 m. I estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

10155.text:
The point (-23, -29) is in the South Atlantic, east of the Brazilian coast and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The Vitória-Trindade chain lies near -20 latitude but at about -29 to -38 longitude, and Trindade itself is at -20.5, -29.3, so this point is about 2.5° south of Trindade.

The seafloor there is part of the Brazil Basin and its eastern portion, with depths of roughly 4500-5000 m. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near -23 latitude sits at about -14 longitude, so the point is well west of it. The ocean there is about 100 Ma old, which fits a depth of around 4500-5000 m. I don't recall a specific seamount at this location, so I'll estimate about -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

15252.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 85E is in East Antarctica, inland, in the area between Vostok (78.5S, 106.8E) and Dome A (80.4S, 77E). The ice surface there is around 3,000 to 3,500 m. Dome A is about 4,090 m at 80.37S, 77.35E. Vostok is at about 3,488 m. At 79S, 85E, the surface is probably around 3,600 m. The Dome A ridge runs toward Vostok, and the Lake Vostok region is at about 3,500 m. At 85E the surface is probably around 3,700 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so it counts as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3650}

13012.text:
The point -55.0, -75.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. The Chilean coast near 55°S is at roughly -70 to -72 longitude, so -75 is open ocean, west of the Chile Trench.

The Chile Trench lies further north. Near 55°S the trench is less pronounced, and the plate boundary turns toward the Chile Rise triple junction near 46°S. South of there, the Antarctic plate subducts under South America at the trench, which sits close to the continental margin at about -75 to -76 longitude near 55°S. The trench depth there is around 4000-5000 m. At -75 the point may be on the outer slope or trench floor, so I estimate the seabed at about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

6725.text:
The point at 15°N, 49°W lies in the western tropical Atlantic, east of the Lesser Antilles and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. This area is abyssal plain, which is typically about 4,800–5,000 m deep. The Vema Fracture Zone is farther south, near 10°N. The Demerara Abyssal Plain lies to the southwest, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is near 45°W at this latitude. At 49°W the seafloor is probably on the western flank of the ridge, at roughly 5,000 m.

This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a looked-up value. I chose about -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

1259.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 179.0°E lies in the East Siberian Sea / Chukchi Sea region, north of Wrangel Island (which sits at about 71°N, 179°W). At 77°N the position is in the Arctic Ocean north of the East Siberian shelf, near the shelf break. The De Long Islands are around 76°N, 148-153°E, so they are far west and not relevant.

The shelf break north of the New Siberian Islands and Wrangel Island lies near 77-78°N at 180°. The Chukchi Borderland and Arctic Mendeleev Ridge are further north, near 80°N. At 77°N, 179°E the seabed is likely outer shelf or upper slope, with a depth of roughly 100-500 m. IBCAO shows the shelf at about 75-76°N near 180° with depths of about 50-100 m, and the slope descending past 77°N toward the Mendeleev Plain. I'll estimate about -200 m, which is a guess.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-200}

9913.text:
The point is at 21°S, 153°W, in the South Pacific near the Cook Islands. Rarotonga lies at about 21.2°S, 159.8°W and Aitutaki at 18.9°S, 159.8°W. The Society Islands (Tahiti) are around 17.6°S, 149.5°W, and the Austral Islands (Rurutu, Tubuai) are around 22–23°S, 151–150°W. So 153°W is east of the Cook Islands and west of the Australs, which puts the point in open ocean.

The seafloor there is Pacific abyssal plain, with some seamounts and the Austral–Cook volcanic chain nearby. Typical depth in this area is about 4,500–5,000 m. Mauke, Mitiaro and Atiu sit near 158°W, so they are too far west to matter. Because this point is in the ocean, the surface type is Water. I estimate a depth of about 4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

10463.text:
The point -27, -133 lies in the South Pacific, east of the Gambier Islands (about -23.1, -135) and southeast of Pitcairn (-25.07, -130.1). It is open ocean on the Pacific plate, in the region of the southeastern Pacific south of the Tuamotus.

The seafloor depth here is likely about 3,800-4,200 m. Pitcairn sits on the Pitcairn hotspot, and the Easter Island chain lies to the east. At -27, -133 the seafloor is on the East Pacific Rise flank, with an age of about 20-30 Myr and a typical depth around 3,600-4,000 m. The East Pacific Rise axis is near -113, so this point is about 20° of longitude west of it, which gives roughly 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

13707.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -125 is in the South Pacific, well north of Antarctica's coast. It lies in the Southeast Pacific Basin, south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The ridge near 63°S is around 120°W to 150°W. The ridge crest at about -65 to -62°S, 120-125°W is at roughly -2500 to -3000 m depth. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at 125°W sits near 56-58°S, though, and at 63°S the area is the Antarctic plate's Bellingshausen Sea abyssal plain, with depths of about -4000 to -4500 m.

Checking the ridge position: it runs from the Macquarie triple junction (62°S, 160°E) to the Chile Rise around 55°S, 70°W, passing about 65°S at 175°W, 60°S at 150°W, and 56°S at 125°W. So -63°S, -125°W is south of the ridge on the Antarctic plate, with seafloor age perhaps 20 Myr and depth around -3800 m. Nearby are the Udintsev fracture zone and others, so I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

859.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 99.0°E lies in the Arctic, north of the Taymyr Peninsula. It is near Schmidt Island and Ushakov Island in the Severnaya Zemlya archipelago, which spans roughly 79–81.3°N and 90–106°E. October Revolution Island sits at about 79–80.5°N and 95–100°E. Komsomolets Island is at about 80.5–81°N and 94–98°E, and Schmidt Island is at about 81.0°N, 91°E. Ushakov Island is at 80.8°N, 79.5°E, so it is too far west to matter. Bolshevik Island runs to about 78–79°N.

The northern tip of Komsomolets Island is about 81.2°N at 95°E, so at 99°E, 81°N the point is probably in the Arctic Ocean or the Kara Sea, just east of Komsomolets. Likely this is the shelf north of the archipelago, with a depth of perhaps 100–300 m. The Severnaya Zemlya shelf there is fairly shallow, and the shelf break lies near 81–82°N. I estimate a seabed depth of about -200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-200}

1212.text:
Latitude 77°N, longitude 85°E lies in the Kara Sea, to the north of the Taymyr Peninsula. The Taymyr coast sits at about 76°N at this longitude, with Cape Chelyuskin at 104°E. Around 85°E the northern Taymyr coast reaches roughly 75.5°N, near the Gulf of Yenisei and the Nordenskiöld Archipelago, which is at about 76°N, 96°E. The Kara Sea shelf at 77°N, 85°E should therefore be open sea, with depths of about 50 to 100 m.

The Ushakov Island and Vize Island area is at about 80°N, 80°E, so it is farther north and does not affect this point. Bathymetry in the northern Kara Sea near 77°N, 85°E is probably 50 to 100 m deep. I'll estimate -60 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

7176.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 133.0°E lies in the western Pacific Ocean, east of the Philippines and north of Palau. This is the Philippine Sea, in the region near the Palau Trench and the Philippine Basin. The nearby Palau-Kyushu Ridge is at around 134°E, and the area west of it is the Philippine Sea basin, with depths of about 5000 m.

Palau is at 7.5°N, 134.5°E, and the Palau Trench runs along roughly 134°E south of 9°N. At 11°N, 133°E the seafloor is likely in the West Philippine Basin's southern part at around 5000 to 5500 m depth. I'll estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

8971.text:
Latitude -9.0, longitude 123.0 lies in the Savu Sea, south of Flores and Lembata and north of Sumba and Timor. Rote and Savu islands sit further south, around -10.5 to -10.7 latitude. Sumba spans roughly 119-121.8 E, so 123 E is east of Sumba. Timor's northern coast is at about -9.2 near 124 E, and at 123 E Timor's coast is around -10.2. Alor and Pantar are at about 124 E, -8.3. Flores's eastern end, at about 123 E, is around -8.4 to -8.5.

So -9.0, 123.0 is open sea in the Savu Sea, to the south of Flores's east end and north of Timor. Depths in the Savu Sea are around 2000-3000 m, and the Savu Basin reaches about 3500 m. Near 123 E, -9 N, the Flores–Wetar strait area is deeper. Sawu Sea depths near -9, 122.5 are about 3000 m, so I estimate about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

13915.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -69 is on the Antarctic Peninsula's west coast, near the Wilhelmina Bay/Graham Coast area. The Argentine Islands and Pléneau Island sit near -65.2, -64.2, so this point is farther west. Longitude -69 at latitude -65 is further west than Adelaide Island, which lies near -67.7, -68.9 (at latitude -67.7). At -65, -69 we are in the open ocean west of the Biscoe Islands, which lie around -65.4, -65.5. So the point is on the continental shelf in open water.

Shelf depths there are around 400-500 m, though the shelf break is farther out. The Biscoe Islands are at about -65.5 lat, -65.5 lon, so -69 lies about 150 km west of them. Depth is probably about 500 m, so I'll estimate -480.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-480}

105.text:
Latitude 89.0°N lies in the Arctic Ocean, about 111 km from the North Pole. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole region, running roughly from the Siberian shelf toward Greenland/Ellesmere. At 89°N, 31°E, the point is near the ridge, on the Eurasian side of the pole.

Depths along the ridge crest near the pole are about 1000-1700 m. Depths at the pole itself are about 4260 m, in the Amundsen Basin on the Eurasian side. The Lomonosov Ridge near 89°N lies around 140°E to 120°W, on the opposite side of the pole from 31°E. The longitude 31°E direction is toward the Amundsen Basin, whose abyssal plain is about 4200 m deep. The Gakkel Ridge is on the Eurasian side, but it meets the Amundsen Basin at around 85°N, 60-90°E, so it is farther away. At 89°N, 31°E, the depth is therefore roughly 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14683.text:
Location: 73°S, 27°E is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The coast there is around 69-70°S at 27°E, so 73°S is inland, in the mountains and plateau south of the coast. Near 27°E the Sør Rondane mountains lie at about 72°S, 23-28°E. The area 73°S, 27°E is just south of the Sør Rondane range, where the ice sheet surface is probably around 3000 m. The ice from the plateau is dammed by the mountains, so the surface there is high, perhaps 2800-3200 m.

I estimate 3100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3100}

3172.text:
Latitude 55.0 N, longitude 45.0 E lies in European Russia, near the border of Nizhny Novgorod Oblast and Chuvashia/Mordovia. Nizhny Novgorod is at about 44°E, 56.3°N, and the Sura River runs through the area near 45°E. The Volga is at about 46–47°E at this latitude, so 45°E is west of it.

This point falls in southern Nizhny Novgorod Oblast or Mordovia, near Ardatov, on the Volga Upland, where elevations run about 150–200 m. I'll estimate 170 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

4431.text:
The coordinates 41.0°N, 43.0°E fall in northeastern Turkey near the Georgian and Armenian border, in the Kars/Ardahan area. Ardahan is at about 41.1°N, 42.7°E with an elevation of roughly 1800 m. Lake Çıldır is at about 41.05°N, 43.2°E, and its surface is at about 1959 m. At 43.0°E the point is probably just west of the lake, on the plateau, where the elevation is about 2000 m.

Lake Çıldır spans roughly 43.13–43.30°E, so 43.0°E lies west of it. The terrain there is the Ardahan–Çıldır plateau at about 2000 m, so I'll estimate 2050 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2050}

8345.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude -49.0 is in Pará, Brazil. The Tocantins River runs near longitude -49.2 at about -3.8 (Tucuruí is at -3.77, -49.67), so this point is probably in the lowlands east of the Tocantins. It lies near Cametá, which is at about -2.24, -49.5, so the point is south of that and east of the river.

The Tocantins at Baião is at about -2.79, -49.67. At -3.0, -49.0 the point is about 70 km east of the river, in terra firme with elevations of roughly 50-100 m. The Pará region around Mocajuba and Limoeiro do Ajuru sits at about -49.4. Moju is at -1.9, -48.8. Tailândia is at -2.95, -48.95, which is nearly this exact point. The elevation there is about 50 m, so I estimate around 45 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":45}

3297.text:
The point 53.0°N, 65.0°W lies in the interior of Labrador/Quebec, near the Labrador-Quebec border west of Churchill Falls. Churchill Falls is at about 53.5°N, 64.1°W. The Smallwood Reservoir spans roughly 53.5–54.5°N, 63–65°W, so this point could fall near it, but the reservoir sits mostly north of 53.5°N.

At 53.0°N, 65.0°W the terrain is in the Labrador Plateau, with elevations of about 500–600 m. The area is just south of the Smallwood Reservoir, whose surface is at 471 m. The Ashuanipi area lies to the west, at about 52.5°N, 66.5°W. The Michikamau region is at 54°N, 64°W. I judge this point to be land at roughly 550 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":560}

2808.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 37.0°E is in Russia, in the Vologda Oblast. It lies west of the Rybinsk Reservoir, north of Cherepovets, near Lake Beloye and the Kirillov area. Lake Beloye is at about 60.15°N, 37.6°E, so this point is south of it. Cherepovets is at 59.13°N, 37.9°E, so the point is about 60 km west of Cherepovets, near Vashkinsky and Babayevo. Babayevo is at 59.39°N, 35.9°E. The point is probably in the Kirillov or Vozhega area, on terrain around 150 m, but I'm not sure of the exact value.

The Sheksna River and the Rybinsk Reservoir, which has a surface elevation of 113 m, are further east. Terrain at this point is likely about 140 m, so I'll say 145 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":145}

13954.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 9 E is in the Southern Ocean off Antarctica's Queen Maud Land coast. The Antarctic coast there is around 69-70°S, so -65 is open ocean. The area is south of the Southwest Indian Ridge, near the Maud Rise region (about 65°S, 3°E, where the seafloor rises to about -2000 m). At 9°E the depth is probably about -3500 to -4500 m. The Maud Rise's flanks are around -4000 m to -5000 m, so I estimate about -4800 m.

I'm not certain, so I'll settle on -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

4470.text:
The point 41.0°N, 121.0°E lies in Liaoning province, China. Jinzhou is at about 41.1°N, 121.1°E, and the Bohai coast is further south-west. Liaohe delta/Panjin is around 41.1N, 122E. At 121.0E, 41.0N, the location is near Linghai or Jinzhou's northern outskirts, on the Liaohe plain close to the Daling River. Elevation there is probably about 10-20 m.

The coast at Jinzhou Bay is around 40.8N, 121.1E, so 41.0N is about 20 km inland. The terrain is flat and low, so I estimate about 12 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":12}

4535.text:
The coordinates 39.0, -109.0 fall in eastern Utah, near the Colorado border. This is in the Book Cliffs area, east of Green River and northeast of Moab. The Utah–Colorado border lies at -109.05, so the point is just inside Colorado, in Mesa County, north of the Colorado River near the Westwater area.

Southeast of Thompson Springs, the terrain is desert at about 1500 m. Closer to the border, near Cisco, Utah (39.04, -109.23), the elevation is about 1300 m. At -109.0, 39.0, the point is in the Colorado desert area around Agate and Rabbit Valley. Rabbit Valley is at -109.0 longitude, so the elevation there is around 1500 m. The Book Cliffs lie north of 39.1. The land south of the point toward the Colorado River is lower. I estimate about 1560 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1560}

10537.text:
Latitude -27, longitude 15 is in southern Namibia, in the Namib/Tsau Khaeb region, inland from the coast. The coast at this latitude (near Lüderitz at -26.6) lies around 15.1°E, and Lüderitz is at 15.16°E. At -27.0 the coast is around 15.0°E, so the point is likely near the coast. The coastline south of Lüderitz runs roughly north-south at about 15.1°E near -27, so 15.0°E is probably just offshore, though that is uncertain.

Checking against known coordinates: Elizabeth Bay is at about -26.95, 15.1. Hottentot Bay is at about -26.1, 14.9. Lüderitz is at -26.65, 15.15. Near -27, the coast sits around 15.05°E, perhaps a little further east near Bogenfels (-27.4, 15.4). The coast is therefore at about 15.2°E at -27.4 and about 15.1°E at -27.0. That puts 15.0°E offshore by about 10 km, in shallow water with a seabed depth of roughly -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

178.text:
Latitude 89°N, longitude 177°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean near the North Pole, close to the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs through the pole region, with crest depths of about 1000-1500 m on the Amerasian side. At 89°N, 177°E the point is on or near the ridge. IBCAO gives depths near the pole of about 4000 m, with the Lomonosov Ridge crest at roughly 1000-1700 m. The ridge lies roughly along 140°W to 40°E, so it crosses the pole along the 40°E–140°W meridian line. A point at 177°E sits somewhat off that axis, on the Amerasian side. At 89°N, 1° of latitude is 111 km from the pole, and the point lies at 177°E, which is 37° from the 140°E meridian, so the distance from the ridge axis is roughly 60 km. That likely puts it on the ridge flank or the Amundsen Basin side, with depths of perhaps 2000-3000 m.

The Lomonosov Ridge axis near the pole is at about 90°N, 140°E in the direction of the Siberian side and toward Greenland at 40°W, which suggests the line 140°E to 40°W. So 177°E is 37° from the 140°E direction, and at 1° from the pole that gives about 67 km. The ridge is about 50-60 km wide, so the point is on the flank toward the Makarov Basin, which is about 4000 m deep. I'll estimate about 2500 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

3847.text:
The point 47°N, 45°W lies in the North Atlantic, roughly east of Newfoundland and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. This is the Newfoundland Basin, where abyssal depths are about 4,500–5,000 m.

The Newfoundland Ridge and the Newfoundland Seamounts sit further south and east. The J-Anomaly Ridge is near 40°N, 40°W, and the Newfoundland Basin floor at 47°N, 45°W should be around 4,000–4,500 m deep. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge crest at that latitude is near 28–30°W, so 45°W is far to the west of it. The Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone, near 52°N, is also to the north. The sea floor at 45°W is therefore likely abyssal plain, which I estimate at about 4,700 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

5727.text:
The coordinates 27.0°N, 115.0°E fall in Jiangxi province, China. That area is near Fuzhou/Ji'an, in the Gan River basin, east of the Luoxiao mountains. The terrain there is hilly, so I estimate roughly 100 m, with the surface type being land.

More specifically, 27°N 115°E is close to Yihuang/Nanfeng in Fuzhou prefecture. Ji'an is at 27.1°N 115.0°E, with the Gan River at about 50 m elevation. At 115.0°E the point sits just east of the Gan River, in hills around 80–100 m. I'll say 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

2123.text:
The point 67.0°N, 107.0°E is in central Siberia, in the Putorana/Anabar region of Krasnoyarsk Krai, on the Central Siberian Plateau. It lies south of the Anabar Plateau and east of the Norilsk area (about 88°E), and near the Kotuy River basin (Kotuy around 71°N, 103°E).

At 67°N, 107°E, the terrain is likely the northern edge of the Evenkia plateau, at roughly 300–500 m. The Moyero River is near 67.5°N, 103–105°E, and the Kotuy River sits around 70°N, 103°E. The point is probably on the Markha or Vilyuy plateau, so I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

3954.text:
The point 47°N, 169°E lies in the northwest Pacific Ocean, east of Kamchatka and the Kuril Islands and south of the Aleutian–Komandorski area. The Emperor Seamount chain lies farther east, near 170°E at latitudes above 40°N. The Detroit Seamount is at about 51°N, 167°E, so it is not at this point. At 47°N, 169°E the seafloor is the open abyssal plain of the northwest Pacific, with typical depths of about 5,500 m.

Because I am working from memory of regional bathymetry rather than looking up this exact point, the depth is an estimate. I chose roughly -5,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

10162.text:
The point at -23, -15 is in the South Atlantic, west of Namibia and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 23°S sits at roughly 13°W, so -15 lies just west of the axis, on the western flank. Ridge crest depths there are about 2500–3000 m, and the flanks deepen gradually. At 23°S the axis is near -13.5°W, so 1.5° west is about 150 km off-axis. The seafloor there is young, around 5 Myr old, and should be about 3000–3500 m deep. This is an estimate from the ridge geometry, not a looked-up value.

I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

11043.text:
Location: -33, -53 is in the Atlantic Ocean off southern Brazil/Uruguay. The coast of Uruguay/Brazil near Chuy is around -33.7, -53.4, so -33, -53 is offshore, about 60 km southeast of the Brazilian coast near Rio Grande (-32.0, -52.1). The coastline at latitude -33 sits at about -52.7 longitude, so -53 would be inland, which puts it in Brazil/Uruguay territory.

Checking more carefully: the Brazil–Uruguay border near Chuy is at -33.7, -53.45. The coast at -33.0 is around -53.5 (the coast runs northeast, with Taim at -32.6, -52.6). The point at -33, -53 lies near Santa Vitória do Palmar, which is inland, west of the coast. Santa Vitória do Palmar is at -33.5, -53.37. The coast near -33.0 is at about -53.1 to -52.9. So the point is right on the coast or just inland, in the Lagoa Mirim/Mangueira region. The Lagoa Mangueira is at about -33.0 to -33.4, -53.0 to -53.2, so this point could be on Lagoa Mangueira, a long narrow lake near the coast. Either way, it's near sea level, so I'll go with solid at a low elevation, around 5 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":6}

5257.text:
The point is 31.0°N, 105.0°W, which is in West Texas/Chihuahua. The Rio Grande near Fort Hancock is around 31.3°N, 105.85°W, so this point is east of the river. It lies in northern Chihuahua, Mexico, or in Hudspeth County, Texas. The Texas–Mexico border follows the river southeast, passing near Presidio at 29.5°N, 104.4°W. At 105°W the river sits at about 31.0°N, so the point may be very close to the river.

The river runs from Fort Hancock (31.3°N, 105.85°W) toward Ruidosa (30.0°N, 104.7°W). Interpolating, at 105°W it would be near 30.6°N, so 31.0°N is northeast of the river, in Texas. The area is the Hudspeth County desert, near the Quitman Mountains and Sierra Blanca. Sierra Blanca is at 31.17°N, 105.35°W with an elevation of about 1400 m. The terrain at 31.0°N, 105.0°W is probably a basin of about 1200 m, so I estimate 1210 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1210}

14377.text:
Location: -69.0, 135.0 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. The coast near 135°E is around 66.5°S, so 69°S is about 250 km inland. This is near the Dome C–Vostok region, but at 135°E, 69°S the ice surface elevation is probably around 2000 m, rising from the coast. In the area south of Dumont d'Urville (140°E), the ice surface at 69°S is about 2,300 m. At 135°E it should be similar, so I estimate about 2,200 m.

The type is grounded ice, so Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2250}

9807.text:
Latitude -19, longitude -5 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It is southeast of Ascension and northwest of St Helena. St Helena sits at about -15.9, -5.7, so this point is roughly 3 degrees south of it.

The area lies on the Guinea Basin and Angola Basin side, and the St Helena seamount chain runs nearby. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 14°W to 13°W at this latitude is about 9 degrees west of the point. The seafloor between the ridge and the African margin there is typically about 4,000 to 5,000 m deep, and at -5 longitude and -19 latitude I estimate about -4,500 m. Seafloor age is about 60 Myr, which fits roughly 4,500 m depth. The Walvis Ridge is near -20 at longitude 0 to 5, so the point could sit on the flank of the Walvis Ridge region, but it is still more likely a basin depth. I'll go with -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

9276.text:
Latitude -13.0, longitude 13.0 falls in Angola, inland from the coast near Benguela/Lobito. The coast there is at about 13.4°E, so 13°E is slightly inland, roughly 30-50 km from the coast. At -13.0 latitude the coast is near Catumbela and Benguela (-12.5 to -12.6, 13.4°E). At 13.0°E, the point is about 50 km inland, where elevation is probably 300-600 m. The escarpment lies around 13.5-14°E, but I'm unsure of the exact position. Wait: if the coast is at 13.4°E, then 13.0°E is west of it, in the ocean.

Checking the coast: Lobito is at 13.55°E, -12.35°. Benguela is at 13.4°E, -12.58°. Farther south, the coast near Cuio is at about 13.2°E, -13.0°. Near Egito Praia (-13.1), the coast is at about 13.2°E. So 13.0°E is about 20 km offshore, which makes it water. The shelf there is narrow, and the depth at 20 km offshore is probably about 100-200 m. I'll say -150.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

6587.text:
Latitude 17.0°N, longitude 35.0°E is in eastern Sudan, between the Atbara River and the Red Sea hills, near Kassala state. It is roughly 150 km west of the Eritrean border and well inland from the coast, so the surface is solid.

Kassala town (15.45°N, 36.4°E) sits at about 500 m. The Butana plain around 17°N, 35°E is lower, near 400 m. The Atbara River at about 17.7°N, 34°E is around 350 m, and Khartoum is at about 380 m. The area near 17°N, 35°E is part of the Butana plain, at roughly 480 m. I estimate 470 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":470}

7852.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 45.0°E is in central Somalia, in the Galguduud/Middle Shabelle region, inland from the coast. The Indian Ocean coast at that latitude is near 47.5°E, so this point is on land.

Terrain here is flat, semi-arid plain at roughly 150-200 m. Near Adado (6.1°N, 46.6°E) the elevation is about 300 m, while the Shabelle River near Jowhar (2.78°N, 45.5°E) is about 100 m. Moving west from Jowhar to 45.0°E, the elevation should be about 120 m. I'll estimate 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

7691.text:
The point at 5°N, 83°E is in the Indian Ocean, southeast of Sri Lanka and southwest of the Bay of Bengal. Sri Lanka's southern tip sits near 6°N, 80.5°E, so this point is well offshore.

The seabed there is the deep Indian Ocean basin. I don't recall a measured value, so I'm estimating a depth of about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Ninetyeast Ridge is farther east at about 90°E, and the Bay of Bengal fan sediments lie to the north. The Central Indian Basin is generally 4,000 to 5,000 m deep. At 5°N, 83°E, I estimate about -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

13674.text:
The point is at 61°S, 169°E, in the Southern Ocean south of New Zealand. It lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Macquarie Ridge region. The Balleny Islands are around 66.5°S, 163°E, so they are not close. The Southeast Indian Ridge and Pacific-Antarctic Ridge junction is near 62°S, 160°E. At 61°S, 169°E the seafloor is probably the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank or the Emerald Basin, at roughly 3000 to 4000 m depth.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about 62°S, 160°E northeast toward 56°S, 145°W. At 169°E the ridge axis is around 63–64°S, so 61°S is on the flank, probably at about 3000 m depth. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

3589.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude 159.0 is in Kamchatka, Russia. The Kamchatka peninsula at 51°N spans roughly 156°E to 156.8°E on the west coast and extends to about 158°E on the east. The southern tip, Cape Lopatka, is at about 50.9°N, 156.7°E. The east coast near 51°N is at about 156.5°E, and Avacha Bay is at 53°N, 158.6°E.

So 159°E at 51°N is east of the southern Kamchatka coast, in the Pacific Ocean. The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs parallel to the coast roughly 150-200 km offshore. At 159°E, 51°N, the distance from the coast (~156.5°E) is about 2.5° of longitude, or about 175 km. That puts the point around the lower slope or the trench's western side, with depth of about 5000-7000 m. The trench axis near 51°N is at around 159.5-160°E, with depth about 7000+ m. At 159°E I estimate about -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

10840.text:
Latitude -31, longitude -99 is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of Chile's coast (Chile coast is at about -71.5). It lies west of the Chile Trench and east of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor there is on the Nazca Plate, with ages of about 30-40 Myr. Typical depths are around 3,800-4,100 m.

The Juan Fernández Ridge is at about -33 and extends from the islands near -79 toward the east. At -99, the seafloor is probably a normal abyssal plain/hills at about -3,900 m. The East Pacific Rise at -31 is near longitude -112, so -99 is about 13° east of the ridge crest. That is roughly 1,200 km, and at a spreading rate of about 150 mm/yr it corresponds to about 8 Myr. Seafloor depth would then be about 2500 + 350·sqrt(8), which is roughly 3,500 m. Using this estimate, I'd say around -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

8512.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude -75.0 is in northeastern Peru, in the Loreto region. It sits near the Marañón River, west of the Ucayali confluence and southwest of Nauta. The Marañón runs at roughly -4.5, -74.5 around that area, and the point itself is probably lowland Amazon forest.

Terrain there is flat, with elevations of about 120-150 m. The Marañón at Nauta (-4.5, -73.6) is near 100 m. At -5.0, -75.0, the point is likely in the Pacaya-Samiria region or close to it, where the ground is about 125 m. I'll estimate 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

14161.text:
