{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

280.text:
The point (87°N, 21°E) lies in the central Arctic Ocean. It is north of Greenland and Svalbard, close to the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands across the pole toward Greenland, and its position on the 'Eurasian side' of the pole is roughly 140°E to 50°W, so near the pole at 87°N it sits around 140°E and 40°W. At 21°E, 87°N, the point is on the Amundsen Basin side, close to the ridge. The Amundsen Basin is about 4000–4400 m deep, and the Nansen Basin is similar.

The Lomonosov Ridge at 87°N is at about 140°E and about 50°W in the IBCAO model. The ridge crest at longitude 21°E would be within about 3 degrees of the pole, which is only about 330 km away. The Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side, and its depth around 87°N, 20°E is about 4200 m. I estimate a seabed elevation of about -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

9518.text:
Latitude -15.0, longitude 137.0 is in northern Australia. The Gulf of Carpentaria lies around 138-140°E at -15°, and the coast near Borroloola is at about 136.3°E, -16.1°. At -15.0, 137.0 the point is probably in the Gulf near the Limmen Bight coast. The Limmen Bight River mouth is around -15.3, 135.5. The Wellesley Islands lie further east at about 139.5°E. Groote Eylandt is at about -14.0, 136.5°E.

At -15, 137 the point is likely water in the Gulf, south-west of Groote Eylandt and east of the Limmen Bight coast. The coast near Port Roper is at about -14.75, 135.4. The Sir Edward Pellew Group is at about -15.6, 136.8, with Vanderlin Island at about -15.7, 137.0. So -15.0, 137.0 is north of the islands, in the water. The Gulf is shallow there, about 20-30 m deep, so I'll use -25.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-25}

583.text:
Latitude 83.0, longitude -93.0 is in the Arctic, north of Ellesmere Island and Axel Heiberg Island. Ellesmere's northern coast is at about 83.1°N near 70–80°W, and Axel Heiberg's northern tip is around 81°N, so 83°N, 93°W is probably in the Arctic Ocean. Borden Island and Meighen Island are near 78–80°N, so they do not reach this far north. The point is probably open Arctic Ocean beyond the shelf north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago.

The Sverdrup Basin's continental margin lies around 81–82°N in this region. At 83°N, 93°W the ocean is likely deep, with the Canada Basin edge near 3000+ m depth. The shelf break north of Axel Heiberg is around 82°N, and the slope descends toward the Canada Basin, so about 83°N the depth might be 2000 to 3500 m. I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

8840.text:
Latitude -9, longitude -139 is in the South Pacific, near the Marquesas Islands. The Marquesas lie around 8-10°S and 138-140°W. Ua Huka is at about 8.9°S, 139.5°W, and Nuku Hiva at 8.9°S, 140.1°W. Hiva Oa is at 9.75°S, 139°W. So -9.0, -139.0 is just north of Hiva Oa, roughly 0.75° (about 80 km) away, and east of Ua Huka (139.5°W), so it is likely open ocean.

The Marquesas rise from a seafloor about 3000-4000 m deep. The channel between islands is probably around 3000 m deep. Ocean depth near the Marquesas Plateau is typically 3000-4000 m, so I estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

9968.text:
