9180.text:
Latitude -13, longitude -179 is in the South Pacific, near Samoa and Tokelau and northeast of Fiji. Wallis and Futuna sit at about -13.3, -176.2, and Samoa at about -172 to -171. Longitude -179 is west of those, between Fiji (Taveuni at about -179.9) and Wallis. This area is in the Lau Basin or north Fiji basin region, and the seafloor there is likely 2000-5000 m deep.

Rotuma is at -12.5, 177. Longitude -179 is 1° east of the dateline. Taveuni at -16.8, -179.9 is too far south to matter. At -13, -179 the seafloor is probably in the North Fiji Basin or Lau Basin margin, and a typical depth there is about -4500 m. The area between Wallis and Futuna to the east and Fiji to the west is roughly 3000-4500 m deep, so I estimate -4200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

14481.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -17 is in Antarctica, in the Weddell Sea sector near Dronning Maud Land. The Fimbul Ice Shelf lies around 0 to -3° E, and the Jelbart and Atka ice shelves are near -8°. At 17°W, the Brunt Ice Shelf is at about 26°W and Riiser-Larsen at about 16°W, around latitude 72-73°S. So 71°S, 17°W is probably in the Weddell Sea, near the Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf and the coast.

The coast near 17°W lies at about 72°S. Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf spans roughly 16 to 35°W at about 72-73°S, so 71°S is likely open ocean (or sea ice) north of the coast. The seabed there is probably the deep continental slope or abyssal plain, at roughly -3000 m or more. Near 71°S, 17°W the Weddell Sea shelf break is around 72°S, so the seabed here would be on the slope at about -2500 m. I'll go with -2800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

1042.text:
Latitude 79.0°N, longitude 105.0°E lies in the Taymyr region, in the Arctic near the Laptev Sea. Severnaya Zemlya sits at roughly 79°N, 95-105°E. Its islands are Komsomolets, Pioneer, October Revolution and Bolshevik. Bolshevik Island extends to about 78.5°N, and Bolshevik's eastern coast is near 102-104°E.

The Taymyr Peninsula's north coast is around 77-78°N at 104°E, with Cape Chelyuskin at 77.7°N, 104.3°E. So 79°N, 105°E is north of the cape, in the Vilkitsky Strait area or the sea to the north. The Vilkitsky Strait lies at about 78°N, between Bolshevik Island and the mainland. At 79°N, 105°E that leaves open sea east of Severnaya Zemlya, which is the Laptev Sea's northwestern edge. Depths there are probably 100-300 m, since the shelf break lies further north. I'll estimate about -200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-200}

