{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

1446.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 167.0°W is in the Chukchi Sea, north of Wrangel Island (which sits near 71°N, 179°W) and north of Alaska. The location is northwest of Point Barrow (71.4°N, 156.5°W), well offshore. The Chukchi shelf there is roughly 40–50 m deep, but at 73°N the shelf starts to approach the Chukchi Borderland and the shelf break. Around 73°N, 167°W the depth is probably about 100 m, since the Herald Canyon and the shelf edge lie farther north near 74–75°N. The shelf there is about 50 m deep and I estimate it at 60 m, though the northern Chukchi Sea at 73°N could be nearer 150 m.

I'll go with -90 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-90}

5554.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude 129.0 lies in the East China Sea / Ryukyu region, near the Tokara Islands. Amami Oshima is at about 28.3N, 129.5E. The Tokara Islands such as Nakanoshima (29.85N, 129.85E) and Suwanosejima (29.64N, 129.71E) are to the northeast, and Kodakarajima is at about 29.2N, 129.3E. So 29.0N, 129.0E is probably in the sea west of the Tokara chain.

The Tokara Strait area has depths of around 500 to 1000 m. West of the Ryukyu arc is the Okinawa Trough, which is about 1000 m deep at 29N, 128.5E. At 129.0E the point is probably on the arc's western slope or the edge of the trough, so I estimate the seabed at about -700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

927.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude -125.0 W falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Banks Island spans roughly 71-74.5 N and Prince Patrick Island about 76-77 N at around -118 to -124. Borden Island and Brock Island lie near 78.5 N, -111 W, and Meighen Island near 80 N, -99 W. At 79 N, -125 W the area is north of Prince Patrick Island, which is in the Arctic Ocean near the edge of the Beaufort Sea continental margin. Prince Patrick's northwest tip is around 77.0 N, -120 W, so 79 N, -125 W is open ocean.

The nearby continental shelf edge is probably around 77-78 N west of Prince Patrick. At 79 N, -125 W the seabed would be on the slope or in the deep Canada Basin edge, and the Arctic Ocean there is probably 1000-2500 m deep. Shelf break off Prince Patrick Island and Ellef Ringnes lies near 78 N, so at 79 N, -125 W I estimate about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

11751.text:
The point (-41.0, -77.0) is in the Pacific Ocean, about 500 km west of Chile's coast at 41°S (Chiloé is near 74°W). That puts it west of the Chile Trench, on the Nazca plate, roughly 3 degrees of longitude beyond the trench axis.

The trench axis at this latitude lies near 74.5°W, with a depth of about 4,000 m (shallower here because of sediment fill). At 77°W the seafloor is on the outer trench slope and the abyssal plain, at roughly 4,000 m depth. The Chile Rise and the triple junction are farther south, near 46°S. At 41°S, 77°W the seafloor is about 4,000 m deep, and I estimate it at about -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

14515.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 51 is in East Antarctica, in Enderby Land / Queen Maud Land. Along 51°E the coast is at roughly 66-67°S (Enderby Land), so 71°S lies about 400 km inland. That puts it on the ice sheet, probably in the Prince Charles Mountains' western area or the plateau east of Queen Maud Land. The ice surface there is probably around 2500 m. Longitude 51°E at 71°S is near the Fimbul region's eastern side and Shirase glacier area, not the Lambert Glacier, which sits near 70°E.

I estimate the ice-surface elevation at about 2800 m, grounded ice, and I am not certain of the exact value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

11984.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 29 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa and west of the Prince Edward Islands (about 37.7°E, -46.9°S). This area lies near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Del Caño Rise, though the Del Caño Rise is further east, around 45°E.

At 29°E, 43°S, the seafloor is probably the Atlantic-Indian Basin, or the southern flank of the Southwest Indian Ridge. The ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (about 55°S, 1°W) northeast to the Indomed fracture zone region near 38°S, 46°E. At 29°E the ridge axis is near 41-42°S, and the Andrew Bain fracture zone is at about 30°E, 50°S. The Du Toit fracture zone is near 31°E, and the Prince Edward fracture zone is near 35°E. So 43°S, 29°E is likely just off the ridge axis, with depths around 3000-4000 m. Taking a middle estimate of about -3600 m is reasonable.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

15641.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 143 is in Antarctica. Longitude 143E at 83S lies near the Ross Ice Shelf's western edge, close to the Transantarctic Mountains and the Shackleton/Nimrod Glacier area. The Nimrod Glacier is around 160E, 82.5S, and the Beardmore is around 170E. At 143E, 83S, that is deep inside East Antarctica, since the Ross Ice Shelf edge there is at about 156-160E. This region is the polar plateau near the Queen Alexandra Range and Shackleton, so it is probably grounded ice on the plateau.

At 143E, 83S, the ice surface is likely about 2,500 to 3,000 m. Probably around 2,700 m, near the head of the Mullock/Lennox-King glaciers, but those are further east. The area is west of the Transantarctic Mountains, perhaps on the plateau at around 2,800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2750}

12118.text:
The point is at 45°S, 63°W. That is in the Atlantic, off Patagonia, east of Península Valdés. Península Valdés lies near 42.5°S, 64°W. At 45°S the coast sits around 65.5°W (Comodoro Rivadavia is at 67.5°W), so 63°W is roughly 200 km offshore on the Patagonian shelf. Shelf depths there are about 100 m, and the shelf edge lies farther east near 55–58°W.

At 45°S, 63°W the depth is probably about 100 m, perhaps 95 to 110 m. I'll estimate -100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

6502.text:
The point 17°N, 135°W lies in the open North Pacific, roughly northeast of Hawaii and west of Baja California, so the surface type is Water. The remaining question is the seabed depth.

Abyssal plain depths in this region are typically 4,500 to 5,000 m. The seafloor age here is about 50 to 60 million years, which puts the depth near 4,500 to 4,900 m. The nearby Clipperton Fracture Zone lies further south, and the Clarion Fracture Zone is around 18 to 20°N. The sea floor between them is about 4,800 m deep in many places. I could not pin the value down more precisely than that, so I am estimating about 4,750 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4750}

9124.text:
Latitude -11, longitude 69 is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Diego Garcia, which sits at about -7.3, 72.4. The location is near the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge region, but the Chagos Archipelago lies at roughly 70-73E and 5-8S, so this point is south of it. The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge runs north-south around 73E. At 69E, 11S, the point is in the Mascarene Basin or the Central Indian Basin region, with depths of about 4500-5000 m.

The Carlsberg Ridge is further north, and the Central Indian Ridge sits near 66-70E at around 10-20S. Around 11S the Central Indian Ridge is at roughly 66E, so 69E may be on its flank at about 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate about -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

14010.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 121 lies in East Antarctica, off the Sabrina Coast / Wilkes Land coast. The coast near 121°E is at roughly -66.3° (Sabrina Coast, near the Totten Glacier region at 116°E, -67°). So -65° is offshore, over the continental shelf slope or the rise.

The continental shelf edge there is around -65.5°, so at -65 the point is probably on the continental slope, with a seabed depth of roughly 2000-3000 m. The Sabrina Coast shelf is about 500 m deep and the slope falls to about 3000 m by -64°. I estimate -2500 m at -65.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

735.text:
Latitude 81.0, longitude -149.0 is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago and the Beaufort Sea, in the Canada Basin. Ellesmere and Axel Heiberg islands lie near 80°N but at longitudes of -90 to -100, and the Canadian Arctic continental margin lies near 75–77°N at -120 to -150. So this point is well out in the Canada Basin, near the Canada Abyssal Plain, which has depths around 3800 m.

The Alpha Ridge sits at about 85°N and the Chukchi Borderland at about 77°N, 165°W. At 81°N, 149°W the point is in the deep basin, where the depth is roughly 3700–3900 m. I'll estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

12395.text:
The point -47.0, 131.0 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. It is roughly 900 km south of the Great Australian Bight, over the Australian-Antarctic Basin. The Southeast Indian Ridge lies further south at about 50°S near 131°E, and the Australian-Antarctic Discordance is around 120-128°E. At 47°S, 131°E the depth is probably about 4000 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

The Southeast Indian Ridge crest in this region sits near 48-50°S at 130°E, with depths of about 2500-3500 m. At 47°S the seafloor is therefore probably on the northern flank of the ridge, at roughly 3800-4000 m deep. I'll use -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

1135.text:
The point 77.0°N, 69.0°W is in northwest Greenland, near Thule/Pituffik and Wolstenholme Fjord. Pituffik is at about 76.5°N, 68.8°W. Moving north to 77.0°N, the point is near Wolstenholme Ø and Granville Fjord, close to the ice sheet margin. Inglefield Bredning lies further north, near 77.5°N, 66°W.

At 77.0°N, 69.0°W, the position is roughly 55 km north of Pituffik. Wolstenholme Fjord's mouth is around 76.5°N, 69°W, and Granville Fjord is around 76.9°N, 68°W. Land northeast of Pituffik is the Steensby Land/Dundas area. At 69°W, 77°N, the ice cap edge at Thule is around 76.4°N, 68°W, so this point could be on the ice cap or on land near Bylot-type terrain. Baffin Bay's coast near Cape Atholl is at 76.45°N, 69.5°W, and the coast north of that goes up to Cape Parry at 77.1°N, 71°W. So 69°W at 77°N is probably land, on the Wolstenholme–Kap Parry plateau, where elevations are a few hundred meters. I'll estimate about 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

12370.text:
Latitude -47, longitude 81 is in the southern Indian Ocean. It lies southeast of Kerguelen (about 49°S, 69°E) and well east of it, and southwest of Amsterdam Island (38°S, 77°E). The region near 47°S, 81°E sits south of the Southeast Indian Ridge's northern part, in the Indian Ocean basin.

The Southeast Indian Ridge runs from around 38°S, 78°E down to about 50°S, 100°E, so at 47°S, 81°E we are probably southwest of the ridge axis, which sits near 47°S, 88°E. Ridge flanks there are about 3000-4000 m deep. The Kerguelen Plateau's eastern flank is near 80°E at these latitudes. The plateau's northern part extends to about 46°S, and its eastern edge is around 78-80°E. The region near 81°E could be the Kerguelen Plateau's eastern margin or the basin east of it, which is about 3500-4000 m deep. I'll estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5963.text:
This location is in the northeast Pacific, roughly 1,500 km west of Baja California and northeast of Hawaii. It lies on abyssal plain, so it is open ocean and the surface type is Water.

Seafloor depth in this region is typically about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The area around 23°N, 133°W sits on the old Pacific plate, with seafloor age of about 60 to 80 million years, which corresponds to depths of roughly 4,300 to 4,600 m. I take about 4,500 m as the estimate, so the elevation is about -4,500 m. This is a regional estimate from typical depths, not a lookup of the exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4480}

14879.text:
Location: 75°S, 59°E in East Antarctica, inland of Prydz Bay and the Amery Ice Shelf area. The Amery Ice Shelf is at about 70-72°S and 68-71°E, so 75°S, 59°E is well inland, in the Mac. Robertson Land / Princess Elizabeth Land interior. Near 75°S, 59°E lies the Lambert Glacier's western flank, with the Prince Charles Mountains to the north. The ice surface there is probably about 2,500 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so Solid. For ice surface elevation, the plateau near 75°S, 59°E is about 2,400-2,700 m. I'll estimate 2,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

3388.text:
Latitude 53.0, Longitude 117.0 (positive east) places the point in southern Siberia, in the Zabaykalsky/Amur area, close to the Chita-Amur region. At 117°E and 53°N this is near the Shilka and Argun rivers' basin, in the Nerchinsk area, on the Russia–China border. The Argun River is at about 117–120°E, 50–53°N, and Nerchinsk is at 52°N, 116.5°E. So the point is likely in the hills east of Chita, to the north of Nerchinsk, in the Nercha River area.

The terrain there is hilly, with elevations of around 600-800 m. I'll estimate 700.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 700}

15187.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -45 is in the southern Weddell Sea, near the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly 60°W to 75°W, and the Filchner part spans about 30°W to 45°W. At 45°W and 79°S the location is probably on the western side of the Filchner Ice Shelf or just at the edge of Berkner Island, which lies around 45-50°W and 78-79.5°S. Berkner Island is centered near 46°W, 79°S and is grounded ice with a dome about 900 m high. Its extent in longitude runs from about 41°W to 51°W, so this point is on Berkner Island.

Berkner Island's northern dome peaks near 78.5°S, 45°W at roughly 890 m, and the southern dome is near 79.5°S, 46°W at about 700 m. At -79, -45 the ice surface is probably about 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

8145.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude -89.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 300 km southwest of the Galápagos Islands, which lie at about 90-91°W. The point sits south of the Galápagos platform and near the Galápagos Spreading Center, which is around 2°N. The seabed in that area is probably 2,800-3,200 m deep.

The Galápagos Rise region at 1°S, 89°W is on the Nazca plate and likely has depths near 2,900 m, so I'll estimate -2900 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

5412.text:
The point 29°N, 155°W lies in the North Pacific, northeast of the Hawaiian Islands and roughly 600 km north of the Big Island. That puts it in open ocean on the abyssal plain, where depths are typically 4,500–5,500 m.

The Hawaiian Arch (flexural bulge) is northeast of Hawaii, and the seafloor around 29°N, 155°W is probably about 5,000 m deep. I'm estimating from the general bathymetry of the area rather than a specific lookup, and I'll use -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

2533.text:
The coordinates 61.0°N, 153.0°W fall in southwest Alaska, west of Cook Inlet. The area is near the Lake Clark region, in the Chigmit Mountains/Alaska Range southwestern part. Lake Clark sits at about 60.2°N, 154.3°W. At 61.0°N, 153.0°W, the terrain is likely the Neacola Mountains or the upper Chakachatna/Tlikakila area. The Tlikakila River is around 60.6°N, 153.8°W. The Mount Spurr area is at 61.3°N, 152.25°W. The Skwentna River is further east, near 61.9°N, 151.5°W.

At 61.0°N, 153.0°W, this is probably mountainous, possibly with glaciers. The Neacola Mountains lie around 60.8°N, 153.5°W. I'd estimate the elevation at about 1500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1500}

4509.text:
The point 39°N, 161°W is in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and roughly midway between Hawaii and California, so the surface type is Water.

I'm estimating the depth from general knowledge of the region rather than from a lookup. The area is abyssal plain, with typical depths of about 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Mendocino and Murray fracture zones lie further east and north, and the Hawaiian Ridge is far to the southwest. I'm taking a depth of about 5,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

8814.text:
The point (-7.0, 169.0) is in the central Pacific, east of the Solomon Islands and west of the Gilbert Islands (Kiribati). It lies south of the equator at about 7°S, east of Nauru and Tuvalu (Nanumea is around 5.7°S, 176°E). Santa Cruz Islands are around 10°S, 166°E. So this point is open ocean in the Ellice Basin area.

The Ellice Basin has seafloor depths of roughly 4,500 to 5,500 m, and the area near 7°S, 169°E is probably about 5,000 m deep. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

965.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -49.0 lies in northern Greenland. At that longitude the point is on the Greenland ice sheet, inland from the northeast coast. Northeast Greenland near 79°N spans from about -20° at the coast to -60° on the west side. At -49°, the point is in the interior north of the Humboldt Glacier region. The ice sheet there is roughly 2000 m thick, and the surface elevation is likely about 1500 to 2000 m.

The NEGIS (Northeast Greenland Ice Stream) is near 79N at about -25 to -35. At -49, the surface is around 1700 m. I'll estimate 1750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1750}

1376.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 53.0 E falls in the Barents Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya's northern island. Novaya Zemlya's northern island spans roughly 73°–77°N and 52°–69°E. At 75°N its western coast lies around 56–57°E near Matochkin Shar (73.3°N, 56°E), while the northern island's west coast at 75°N is around 58°E. So 53°E at 75°N is in the sea, west of the coast.

The Barents Sea there is fairly shallow, around 200–300 m deep. Closer to Novaya Zemlya, the East Novaya Zemlya trough is on the east side, and the western side near the coast is about 100–200 m deep. At 53°E, 75°N, I estimate about -230 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-230}

3311.text:
The point at 53°N, 37°W lies in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It is in the Irminger Sea or the western Iceland Basin, near the Reykjanes Ridge's southwestern extension. The Reykjanes Ridge axis sits near 52.5°N, 32°W, so this point is west of it.

The ocean floor at 37°W, 53°N is probably the western flank of the ridge, or the Irminger Basin, at roughly 3000 m depth. The Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone is near 52.5°N, between 30°W and 35°W. West of it, at 37°W, the floor is likely the Irminger Basin margin at about 3200 m depth. Since the seafloor is about 3000–3500 m deep here, I estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

7820.text:
The point 3.0°N, 19.0°W lies in the equatorial Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa. It sits near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, whose axis is around 20 to 30°W at this latitude, so the point is on the eastern side of the ridge.

The seafloor there is probably about 4,000 to 5,000 m deep. The St. Paul fracture zone and the Romanche fracture zone lie near the equator, and the Romanche runs from roughly 18°W to 13°W at about 0°. The point at 3°N, 19°W is north of that and sits in the Sierra Leone Basin or on the ridge flank. I estimate a depth of about 4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

3194.text:
Latitude 55.0°N, longitude 89.0°E is in southern Siberia, in Krasnoyarsk Krai near the Khakassia and Kemerovo borders. This is the northern foothills of the Kuznetsk Alatau or the Chulym plateau. The Chulym River region lies at roughly 55°N, 89°E, with elevations around 200-300 m.

Ach­insk is at 56.3°N, 90.5°E at about 200 m. Bogotol is at 56.2°N, 89.5°E at about 250 m. At 55°N, 89°E the terrain is probably in the Kuznetsk Alatau's eastern foothills, or the Chulym-Yenisei basin. Nearby, Nazarovo is at 56°N, 90°E. Sharypovo is at 55.5°N, 89.2°E at about 300 m. Slightly south, the terrain rises toward the hills around Ilyinka, so I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":380}

14775.text:
Location: -75, -149 is in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica, west of Marie Byrd Land. The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part extends to about 150°W near 75°S, and this point sits within the Ross Ice Shelf, which floats. That makes it Water, so the answer is the seabed elevation beneath the shelf.

Bathymetry under the eastern Ross Ice Shelf at 75°S, 149°W: Roughly 500-700 m deep. The Ross Sea continental shelf is deep, about 500-800 m. Near Sulzberger Bay, the coast is around 77°S, and the Ross Ice Shelf front is near 78°S at that longitude. At 75°S, 149°W we are probably in Marie Byrd Land's offshore area. Sulzberger Bay lies at about 76.5°S, 148°W, with the ice shelf there, and the coast lies around 77°S. So 75°S would be open sea or shelf ice. Bathymetry here is approximately -600 m on the outer shelf, and deeper toward the continental slope at around 74°S. I'll go with -650 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-650}

9554.text:
Latitude -17.0, longitude -151.0 is in French Polynesia. Tahiti sits at about 17.65°S, 149.45°W, and Moorea at about 17.5°S, 149.8°W. Tetiaroa is at 17.0°S, 149.55°W, so it is further east. Longitude -151.0 is about 1.5° west of Tetiaroa, which is roughly 160 km. The nearest land to the west would be Tahaa and Raiatea at about 151.4°W, 16.8°S, and Huahine at 151.0°W, 16.7°S.

Huahine spans roughly 16.65 to 16.85°S, so -17.0 is about 15 to 40 km south of it. That puts the point in open ocean between Huahine and Maupiti, on the flank of the Society Islands. Depth there is probably around 2500 to 3500 m, so I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

7235.text:
The point 9°N, 109°W is in the eastern Pacific, west of Central America and about 1,000 km southwest of Mexico's Clipperton region (Clipperton is near 10.3°N, 109.2°W). It is in open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor here is part of the East Pacific Rise flank. The East Pacific Rise crest sits near 104°W at this latitude, so 109°W lies west of it on the Pacific plate. Crust of about 5-8 Myr is typically 3,000-3,500 m deep, so the depth should be around 3,300 m. Clipperton is on a ridge and has some local seamounts, but those are a minor effect. Nearby depths of roughly 3,400-3,700 m seem plausible. I estimate -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

12447.text:
The point is at 49°S, 125°W, in the South Pacific Ocean. It lies southeast of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region and west of the Chile Rise. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from 55°S, 140°W up to about 40°S, 110°W, and at 49°S it sits near 115°W. So 125°W is about 10° west of the ridge axis, on crust roughly 8–10 Myr old.

Seafloor depth on the flanks of the fast-spreading Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is around 3000–3500 m near the axis, deepening to about 4000 m at that age. The ridge crest here is about 2200–2800 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at 49°S is near 113°W, so 125°W is about 12° of longitude away. At 49°S, 1° of longitude is about 73 km, so 12° is about 880 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 5 cm/yr, that is roughly 17 Myr of crust, giving a depth of about 2600 + 350·√17 ≈ 4000 m. I estimate about 3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11687.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 155 is in the Tasman Sea, east of Australia and west of New Zealand. It lies south of Lord Howe Rise, in the Tasman Basin abyssal plain. Depths in this area are about 4,500-4,800 m. The Lord Howe Rise sits near 159-162°E, and the Tasman abyssal plain around 155°E at 39°S is about 4,700 m deep.

The Tasman Basin there is probably around 4,600 m, so I'll answer -4600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

4563.text:
The point (39°N, 53°W) is in the North Atlantic, southeast of Newfoundland. It lies east of the Grand Banks and south of the Flemish Cap. The New England Seamounts run along roughly 38-39°N, but they sit further west at about 60-50°W. The Corner Rise seamounts are near 35°N, 50°W, and the Kelvin seamounts are near 38°N, 55-60°W. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far to the east, around 30°W.

I don't have an exact depth for this spot, so I'm estimating. The abyssal plain in this area is about 5000 m deep, and the Newfoundland Basin sits at 4800-5200 m. I estimate about -4900 m, which fits the Sohm Abyssal Plain and Newfoundland Basin region. The point could also sit on the flanks of the seamounts, which would make it shallower, but I'll go with -4900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

15988.text:
The point is at 87°S, 117°E, in East Antarctica on the polar plateau, roughly 300 km from the South Pole along the 117°E meridian. The pole sits at about 2835 m. Moving toward the 117°E side, which is the direction toward Dome A and Vostok, the ice surface rises. At 87°S on that side, I estimate the surface at roughly 2900 to 3000 m.

I'm working from memory of the ice surface rather than a lookup. The surface at 86°S, 120°E is around 3000 m, so I'll take 2950 m for this point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

1751.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 83.0°E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Russia, to the north of the Yenisei Gulf. The Yenisei Gulf lies at about 72-73°N, 80°E. At 71°N, 83°E the point is on the Gydan/Taymyr lowlands, east of the Yenisei, near the Dudypta or Pyasina area. The location is probably land at a low elevation.

The Yenisei River at 71°N is around 83°E. The estuary runs from about 70°N, 83°E up to the gulf, so the point might be near the river's east bank. Around Dudinka (69.4°N, 86°E), the river flows north-northwest. At 71°N the river is near 83°E, around the Yenisei Gulf's southern part, where it is wide. The gulf's eastern shore is near 83°E at 71-72°N, so the point could be water. I can't determine that precisely, so I'll go with land. The terrain near the Yenisei coast there is low, so I'll estimate about 20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":20}

3824.text:
Latitude 47.0, Longitude -91.0 falls in northwestern Wisconsin, south of Lake Superior. Lake Superior's southern shore near Bayfield/Apostle Islands is at about 46.8-47°N, around -90.8°W. At -91.0 and 47.0 N, the point is out in the lake. The Wisconsin shore at -91.0 sits near 46.6°N (Cornucopia is at 46.86, -91.1), and Bayfield Peninsula's northern tip is around 46.95, -90.8. At 47.0, -91.0, the point is about 15 km north of Cornucopia, so it is in the lake.

Lake depth there: the western arm of Superior near the Apostle Islands is about 50-100 m deep, and deeper to the north. The lake surface is 183 m, so a lakebed depth of ~80 m gives about 100 m elevation. The area north of Cornucopia, roughly 15 km out, is perhaps 100 m deep, giving a lakebed elevation of about 83 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":90}

398.text:
Latitude 85°N, longitude -103° is in the Arctic Ocean. It lies north of Ellef Ringnes Island and the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, and in the Amerasian Basin close to the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge region.

The Lomonosov Ridge runs from Ellesmere Island/Greenland across the pole to Siberia, roughly along 140°E to -60°W. At 85°N the ridge lies around 60°W to 150°E. At -103° and 85°N, the point is on the Amerasian side, west of the ridge. The Canada Basin side of the Alpha Ridge has depths of about 1500-3000 m. The Alpha Ridge is roughly at 85°N, 110°W to 150°W, with crest depths around 1400-2000 m. At -103° and 85°N, the point is probably on the southern flank of the Alpha Ridge or in the Canada Basin margin, where the depth could be about 2000 m. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

14717.text:
Location: -73, 95E in East Antarctica. That is offshore of Wilkes Land, near the Shackleton Ice Shelf region (which sits around 96-101E, 65-66S). At 73S, 95E we are inland on the ice sheet, near Vostok Subglacial Highlands/Gamburtsev toward Dome... Vostok is at 78S, 106E. At 73S, 95E the ice surface is around 2800-3000 m, so the surface type is Solid.

Surface elevation at 73S, 95E: the ice sheet interior of Wilkes Land at that latitude is roughly 2700 m. Dome Charlie is at 75S, 123E, 3300 m. Along 95E, 73S is about 1000 km from the coast, so around 2800 m is a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

10706.text:
Latitude -29, longitude -7 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of southern Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which lies near 14°W at this latitude. The point sits in the Angola/Cape Basin region, or possibly on the Walvis Ridge's southern side. The Rio Grande Rise is farther west, around -30° longitude, so it is not relevant here.

The Cape Basin has depths of about 4500-5000 m. At 29°S, 7°W the seafloor is on the eastern flank of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, roughly 600 km from the axis. The seafloor age there is about 30 Myr, which suggests a depth near 4000 m. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 29°S is at about 13°W, so 7°W is about 6° east, or 600 km. Depth there is probably around 3700-4200 m, so I'll estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

4154.text:
The point 43°N, 151°W lies in the North Pacific, south of the Aleutians and south of Alaska. That area is the abyssal plain of the Pacific basin, roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. The Surveyor Fan and Aleutian Trench are farther north and east, so they do not apply. The point sits near the Emperor Seamounts' eastern side, but those seamounts are farther west, around 170°E to 170°W, and are not close.

I estimate a seabed depth of about 5,300 m. This is an estimate from the general bathymetry of the region, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

9486.text:
Location: -15, 73E is in the central Indian Ocean, southwest of the Chagos Archipelago (which lies around -5 to -8, 72E). At -15, 73E the area is near the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge's southern part, close to the Nazareth Bank/Saya de Malha side? Those are at 60E. At 73E, -15 the seafloor lies in the Central Indian Basin's western edge, east of the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge. The ridge near Chagos is at 72-73E down to about -8, then the Chagos Trough lies around 73-74E, -10 to -15, with depths around 4000 m. Further south at -15 and 73E the seafloor is about 4,000 to 4,500 m deep, with the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge's southern end at around -12.

I'll estimate -4200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

14281.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -57 is in the Weddell Sea sector, on the east side of the Antarctic Peninsula. Here the Larsen ice shelf region lies around -68 to -69 latitude, -62 to -60 longitude. At -57 longitude the point is farther east, in the open Weddell Sea, near the Ronne Ice Shelf's eastern edge or the sea ice zone.

The Ronne Ice Shelf front is at about -75 latitude, and the Larsen C is at about -67 to -69 latitude between -65 and -60 longitude. At -57, -69 we are probably in the Weddell Sea, east of the Larsen C ice shelf's outer edge, which is about -60. The Weddell Sea shelf there is likely 400-600 m deep, probably the continental shelf, so I estimate a seabed of about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

4301.text:
Latitude 43.0°N, longitude 143.0°E is in eastern Hokkaido, Japan. The Tokachi plain lies around 143°E, 42.9°N. Toward the coast, Hiroo is at about 143.3°E, 42.3°N, and Tokachi Harbor is at 143.7°E, 42.7°N. At 43.0°N, 143.0°E the point is inland, near Ikeda and Makubetsu, northeast of Obihiro (143.2°E, 42.9°N). Toward the west, Nakasatsunai and Taiki sit around 143.1°E, 42.5°N.

Obihiro is at about 40-80 m elevation. A point slightly west of Obihiro at 143.0°E, 43.0°N lies in the Tokachi plain's western edge, near Shimizu or Shintoku. Shimizu is at about 142.88°E, 42.99°N with an elevation of roughly 100 m. At 143.0°E, the point is between Shimizu and Obihiro, so I estimate about 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

8160.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude -59.0 lies in the Brazilian Amazon, in the state of Amazonas. It is north of the Amazon River, which at this longitude is near -3.2 latitude. The location is close to the Uatumã River region, near Presidente Figueiredo and Balbina, which is around -1.9 latitude and -59.5 longitude. At -1.0, -59.0 the terrain is probably forested upland on the Guiana Shield margin, roughly 100 m in elevation. The area may fall within the Jatapu River basin, where elevations are about 50-120 m.

I estimate 90 m, based on regional knowledge rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

15635.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 131 is in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. It lies roughly 7 degrees from the pole, in the region toward Wilkes Land and the Dome A/Dome F sector. Longitude 131°E at -83° is probably near the Aurora Subglacial Basin or the area south of Dome C (123°E, -75). The ice surface there is likely about 2800-3200 m, since the plateau at -83° near 131°E is high. I'll estimate roughly 3000 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

15805.text:
This location is at 85°S, 111°E, in East Antarctica on the polar plateau. It lies roughly 650 km from the South Pole, in the direction of Vostok, which sits at 78.5°S, 106.8°E. Ice surface elevation near Vostok is about 3,500 m. Toward the pole the plateau rises, and around 85°S, 111°E it is probably near 3,200 m. Dome A is at 80.4°S, 77°E with an ice surface of about 4,090 m. The area around 85°S, 110°E is on the plateau at roughly 3,000–3,300 m, and I estimate 3,100 m from that. This is an estimate from nearby reference points, not a lookup of the actual value.

The surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3100}

13125.text:
The point (-55, 151) is in the Southern Ocean, southeast of Tasmania and south of the Tasman Sea. It lies roughly along the Macquarie Ridge region, which is to the southeast, near 159E, 55S. At 151E the position is west of the ridge, over the abyssal plain or the South Tasman Rise. The South Tasman Rise sits around 48-50S, 147E, so 55S is south of it. The seafloor there is probably the Australian-Antarctic basin margin at about 4000 to 4500 m depth.

I'm estimating about -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

12900.text:
The point is at 53°S, 61°E, in the southern Indian Ocean. Heard Island is near 53.1°S, 73.5°E, and Kerguelen is at about 49°S, 69°E, so neither is nearby. The point lies southwest of Kerguelen, in the basin between the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Kerguelen Plateau. The Crozet Plateau is farther west, around 46°S, 51°E.

The seafloor here is probably the Crozet Basin or the Australian-Antarctic basin edge. The Southwest Indian Ridge lies around 50°S at 61°E and the Antarctic-Indian ridge is farther south. The Kerguelen Plateau's western flank sits near 65–70°E, so at 61°E, 53°S the depth is probably about 3500 m. I'll estimate -3400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

13700.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -139 lies in the Pacific sector of the Southern Ocean, well north of Antarctica's coast (which is around -75 at this longitude). It is south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which runs roughly through -55 to -65 at longitudes near -140 to -150. At about -63, -139, the seafloor is likely abyssal, around 4000 m deep, though the ridge crest could be shallower, near 2500-3000 m.

The East Pacific Rise and Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lie near (-65, -135) and (-60, -150). The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about (-56, -140) to (-65, -170), so at -139 the ridge is around -55 to -57. At -63 the seafloor is therefore probably on the flank of the Antarctic side, with depth around 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

3934.text:
Latitude 47.0°N, longitude 129.0°E lies in Heilongjiang Province, China. It is near the Sanjiang Plain and the Songhua River region, probably around Yilan or Boli County. Longitude 129°E is roughly east of Harbin, near Qitaihe or Jixi. The terrain there is hilly, but around 47°N and 129°E I'd expect the Wanda Mountains or the edge of the plain, with an elevation of about 300 m.

Jixi is at 45.3°N and 130.9°E. Qitaihe is at 45.8°N and 131°E. Hegang is at 47.3°N and 130.3°E. Tangyuan is at 46.8°N and 129.8°E. Yilan is at 46.3°N and 129.6°E. Point 47°N, 129°E is therefore west of Hegang, near the Xiaoxing'an Ling foothills, in the Tangwang River or Boli area. Elevation there is likely about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

8249.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 119.0 is in Sulawesi, Indonesia. Central-west Sulawesi sits near 119.0E, and the Makassar Strait coast lies around 119.7E at about -1°, near Donggala/Palu at -0.9, 119.85. At 119.0E and -1.0 we are therefore inland, in the mountains of West Sulawesi (Mamuju Utara / Donggala area), or just in the Makassar Strait.

Sulawesi's west coast around -1° is at about 119.4E, with the coast running from Pasangkayu (-1.18, 119.4) northeast to Donggala (-0.67, 119.74). So 119.0E is offshore in the Makassar Strait, about 45 km west of Pasangkayu. The water there is deep: the Makassar Strait near the coast drops quickly to about 1500–2000 m, and the central part at this latitude is around 2000 m deep. I estimate about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

3125.text:
Latitude 55°N, longitude 49°W lies in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland's southern tip and in the Labrador Sea/Irminger Sea region. Greenland's southern tip is at about 60°N, 44°W, so this point is well south of it and in open ocean.

The point is probably on the western flank of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge's basin, in the Labrador Sea basin or its southern edge. Labrador Sea depths are about 3000–3500 m. At 55°N, 49°W the water is likely about 3500 m deep. The Labrador Basin is deepest around 3800 m near 56°N, 51°W, and the point is near the basin axis southeast of that, so I estimate about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

2723.text:
The coordinates 59.0°N, 133.0°W fall in northern British Columbia, near the Yukon border. The location is roughly west of Atlin Lake and south of Teslin. Atlin is at 59.57°N, 133.7°W. Atlin Lake spans about 59.3–60.0°N, around 133.5°W, so this point lies southeast of Atlin Lake. The area is the Atlin plateau and mountains at the southern end of the Teslin plateau, with terrain around 1000–1500 m.

Nearby, the Nakina River and Sloko area lie to the southwest. The Inklin River is around 133.2°W, 59.4°N. The Taku River sits at about 133.5°W, 58.8°N, so the point is east of the Taku and likely in hilly terrain. I estimate about 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

9062.text:
The coordinates -11.0, -55.0 fall in Brazil, in northern Mato Grosso near the Pará border. It is south of the Amazon basin on the Brazilian Shield plateau, around the Teles Pires and Juruena area.

The Teles Pires river lies near longitude -55.7 at latitude -11. Sinop is at -11.86, -55.5, with an elevation of about 370 m. At -11.0, -55.0 the area is near Cláudia and Santa Carmem, and also close to the Rio Ferro and Peixoto de Azevedo region. Terrain there is about 350 m, so I estimate 340 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":340}

2780.text:
The point at 59°N, 19°W is in the North Atlantic, south of Iceland and west of the Rockall Plateau. It sits in the Iceland Basin, near the western side where the Reykjanes Ridge begins. The Reykjanes Ridge axis is around 30°W at 59°N, so 19°W is east of the ridge, in the Iceland Basin. Depths there are typically about 2800 m.

The Iceland Basin at 59°N, 19°W is roughly 2700–2900 m deep, so I estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

2855.text:
The point 59.0°N, 131.0°E lies in the Russian Far East, in Sakha (Yakutia) or the Khabarovsk region. It is inland, northwest of the Sea of Okhotsk, in the Ulakhan-Bom / Dzhugdzhur area. The Aldan River is at about 58–63°N, 125–135°E, so the point is likely in the Aldan highlands. Nearby, the Maya River flows around 58.5°N, 135°E. At 59°N, 131°E the terrain is probably mountainous plateau, with an elevation of around 800 m. The Yudoma-Maya highlands there reach about 1000 m.

I estimate 850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":850}

10737.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 55 lies in the southwestern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and east of South Africa. It is roughly 1,300 km east of Durban, and that area is part of the Madagascar Basin or the southern Mozambique Basin. The seabed there is probably around 5,000 m deep.

This is a rough estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value. The Madagascar Basin lies near 25–35°S and 50–55°E at about 5,000 m depth. The Southwest Indian Ridge is farther south, and the Mozambique Plateau is farther west. I'll go with about -5100 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5100}

2756.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -67.0 falls in northern Quebec/Labrador, near Ungava Bay. Ungava Bay spans roughly 58-61°N and 64-69°W. The bay's southern coast is at about 58.5°N near Kuujjuaq/Tasiujaq, where Tasiujaq sits at 58.7°N, -69.9°W. At -67.0 longitude the western shore of the bay lies near 58.5-59°N, and the mouth of the George River is around 58.5°N, -65.8°W. So 59.0°N, -67.0°W is most likely in the bay, in the shallow southern part, because the coast around -67 is at about 58.3°N.

Water depth there is shallow, perhaps 20-50 m deep, and the bay has large tidal flats. I'll estimate -30 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-30}

3876.text:
The point 47.0°N, 13.0°E lies in Austria, in the Hohe Tauern/Ankogel area near the Salzburg–Carinthia border. Gastein valley is at about 47.1°N, 13.1°E. Bad Hofgastein is at 47.17°N, 13.1°E, and Mallnitz is at 46.98°N, 13.17°E. The point at 47.0°N, 13.0°E should be in the Hafner/Ankogel group's western side, near Murtal/Lungau, or southwest of Gastein. Candidate features are the Hannoverhaus and Radstädter Tauern, but the Mur source is farther east at 13.3°E.

The area west of the Ankogel group around 13.0°E, 47.0°N is near the Tauernmoossee region, the Sonnblick (12.96°E, 47.05°N), and the Rauris valley. Sonnblick is at 3106 m. The point is about 5 km south of Sonnblick, at the head of the Seebachtal/Anlauftal, close to Mallnitz. The Hoher Sonnblick sits at 12.957°E, 47.054°N. South of it at 47.0°N, 13.0°E lies the Goldberg group ridge, with the Kleines Fleißtal and Großes Fleißtal. This is probably high terrain around 2500 m. The Ankogel side of Gastein, with Naßfeld at 1600 m, lies at about 13.0°E, 47.05°N, so the terrain is mixed. I estimate about 2400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

11722.text:
Latitude -41, longitude -135 is in the South Pacific, far from any land. It lies southeast of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern part and east of the East Pacific Rise, roughly west of Chile at about 4,000 km offshore. The seafloor there is abyssal plain, probably 4,000 to 4,500 m deep. The crust is a moderate age, around 30-40 Myr. Depth at that age would be about 4,000 m, plus sediment effects.

At -41 S, -135 W the East Pacific Rise lies near -115 W at that latitude, so this point is about 20° of longitude away (~1,700 km). With a half-spreading rate of about 70 mm/yr, that is about 25 Myr, giving a depth of about 3,500 m by the 2600+365√t rule (about 3,300 m at 25 Myr). Allowing for fracture zones and the rougher terrain, I'll estimate about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11311.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 123 lies in the Southern Ocean off the south coast of Western Australia. The coast near Esperance is at about -33.9, 121.9, and near Cape Arid at about -33.8, 123.2. So -35 is roughly 130 km offshore, which puts it on the continental shelf edge or the upper slope.

The shelf in this region is wide. The shelf break south of Esperance lies around -34.5 to -35, at roughly 200 m depth. Beyond it the slope drops steeply toward the Diamantina or Great Australian Bight plateau regions. At 123°E, -35 is probably on the shelf or just past it, at about 120 m depth on the outer shelf or 1000 m on the slope. The Recherche Archipelago shelf is broad, and the 200 m isobath is near -34.8 at 123°E. I'll estimate about -600 m, allowing for the slope beginning near this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

10642.text:
The point (-29, -135) is in the South Pacific, southeast of Pitcairn and Gambier, west of Easter Island. It lies in open ocean on the Pacific plate, northeast of the Austral/Pitcairn region and on the flank of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor there is probably around 3500-4000 m deep.

East Pacific Rise crest is near 110°W at this latitude, so 135°W is about 25° west of it. At roughly 2.5 cm/yr per side, that is far enough that the crust is about 40-50 Myr old, giving a depth of about 4000 m. Pitcairn-Gambier hotspot swells are nearby, but the seafloor there is usually around 3800-4200 m deep. I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

4696.text:
This location (37°N, 147°W) is in the northeast Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of California. That region is abyssal plain at about 4,500–5,000 m depth. It lies west of the Murray Fracture Zone, which runs near 35°N, and the seafloor there is around 4,900 m deep.

The Pacific plate crust at this location is roughly 50 million years old (Cretaceous to Eocene), which fits a depth of about 4,900 m. I'll estimate -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

4297.text:
Latitude 43.0°N, longitude 135.0°E lies in the Sea of Japan. Land at 43°N reaches only about 133°E near Primorsky Krai, Russia (Nakhodka is at 132.9°E), and the coast near Terney is around 136°E at 45°N. At 43°N, the Russian coast is near 134.0°E (Cape Povorotny is about 133°E, and the coast near Olga Bay is at 43.7°N, 135°E). At 43°N, 135°E the coast is probably about 1° of longitude farther west, around 134°E, so the point is in the sea, roughly 100 km offshore.

The Japan Basin lies to the east of the Primorye shelf. The shelf is narrow there, and depths drop to about 2000–3000 m within about 100 km. At 43°N, 135°E I estimate a depth of about 2500 m, since the Japan Basin floor is about 3500 m deep farther southeast.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2600}

3287.text:
The point 53.0°N, 85.0°W lies in northern Ontario, in the Hudson Bay Lowlands. It is west of James Bay, whose western shore is near 82°W at this latitude. The Attawapiskat River region is around 52.9°N, 82–88°W, so the point falls inland in the lowlands, around the upper Attawapiskat or Ekwan area.

The terrain there is flat and swampy, with elevations of roughly 100–150 m. The Attawapiskat Lake is at about 52.2°N, 87.5°W with a surface elevation near 200 m. At 85°W the ground is probably around 120 m. The Hudson Bay Lowlands slope from about 200 m inland down to sea level at the coast, and at 82°W the coast is at 0 m. Since 85°W is about 3° inland, roughly 200 km, and the slope is about 0.5 m/km, the elevation is near 100 m. I estimate 110 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

5504.text:
The point 29°N, 29°E lies in the Western Desert of Egypt. It is roughly between Bahariya Oasis (28.35°N, 28.87°E) and Fayoum (29.3°N, 30.8°E), and south of the Qattara Depression (about 30°N). The Qattara Depression lies further north and west, near 29.9°N, 27.5°E.

At this location the terrain is a desert plateau. Bahariya sits at about 100 m, and the surrounding plateau is about 200 m. I estimate the elevation at about 190 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":190}

14580.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -179 is in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 78°S, and its northern edge lies near 77-78°S. At 73°S and 179°W the location is in the open Ross Sea, east of Cape Adare and north of the ice shelf front. This area is near the continental shelf, with the Pennell Bank, Ross Sea shelf depths of about 500 m, and the shelf break near 70-71°S.

Longitude -179 at 73°S is in the central-eastern Ross Sea, close to the Glomar Challenger Basin and the Iselin Bank. Iselin Bank lies near 70-72°S at 175-180°W, with depths of about 500 m. At 73°S the seafloor is likely about 500-600 m deep, and I'll go with about -560 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

12748.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 117 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and southwest of Cape Leeuwin. This area is close to the Diamantina Zone and the Southern Ocean abyssal plain, south of the Naturaliste Plateau. The Naturaliste Plateau sits at about 34°S and 112°E, so it is far to the north. At 51°S, 117°E the seafloor is probably on the flank of the Southeast Indian Ridge, or on the Australian-Antarctic Basin north of the ridge. The ridge near 117°E runs at about 49-50°S, with depths of about 3000 m. At 51°S the seafloor would be slightly south of the ridge axis, so I estimate a depth of about -3800 m. The Kerguelen Plateau is farther west, near 80°E, so it does not apply here.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the ridge's position, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2442.text:
Latitude 63.0 N, longitude 25.0 E is in central Finland. It lies near Lake Lappajärvi or Kyyjärvi, around the Central Ostrobothnia and Central Finland border, so I estimate it is land.

Kyyjärvi sits at about 63.04 N, 24.57 E, and Karstula at about 63.88 N, 24.8 E. Further along, Kivijärvi is at about 63.1 N, 25.1 E, and Lake Kivijärvi lies around 63.1 N, 25.05 E. The point is therefore close to Kivijärvi, but I can't confirm whether it falls on the lake or on land. Terrain in this area is around 150 m. I'll go with land at about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

3907.text:
The point 47.0°N, 75.0°E lies in central Kazakhstan. It is south of Karaganda and north of Lake Balkhash, which sits at about 46°N and 74–75°E on its northern shore side. Balkhash's northern shore is roughly 46.5–46.8°N around 75°E, so the point could be close to the lake. The lake's western part is narrow, with the Sary-Esik peninsula near 74.5°E, and the eastern part lies east of 75°E. At 75°E the lake spans roughly 45.6–46.6°N, so 47.0°N is probably just north of the shore, on land.

For elevation, the northern shore area is around 400 m, and the Balkhash level is about 342 m. Terrain just north of the lake is about 400–450 m. I estimate 420 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":420}

10108.text:
The point (-23.0, -123.0) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of Tahiti and west of Easter Island. It is near the East Pacific Rise flank, roughly at 23°S, 123°W. The Easter Island microplate region is further east, around 26°S, 115°W. The East Pacific Rise at 23°S sits near 114°W, so this point is about 9° west of the ridge crest. Seafloor age there is about 15–20 Myr, with depths around 3,800–4,000 m. The region also has Pitcairn–Gambier volcanic chains at about 25°S, 130°W, but this point is not on them.

I estimate the depth at about 3,900 m, so the elevation is about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

12410.text:
The point at -47.0, 161.0 is in the Southern Ocean southwest of New Zealand, about 600 km south of Tasmania's longitude range and west of Campbell Island (which sits near 52.5°S, 169°E). That places it in the Tasman Sea/Southern Ocean region, near the Macquarie Ridge. Macquarie Island lies at about 54.6°S, 158.9°E, which is well to the south, so the point is not on it.

At 47°S, 161°E the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain or the Emerald Basin margin, with depths around 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Macquarie Ridge runs from about 160°E at 54°S up toward the Puysegur Ridge near 166°E at 47°S, so at 161°E and 47°S the point should be west of the ridge. The seafloor there is likely the South Tasman Rise or the basin floor to its west, at roughly 4,000 m depth. I'll estimate -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12356.text:
Latitude -47, longitude 53 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of the Crozet Islands. The Crozet Islands sit at roughly 46.4°S, 51.7°E, so this point is about 1.4° east and 0.6° south of them. The Crozet plateau is shallow, around 2000 to 3000 m deep, and lies mostly west and south of the islands. At 53°E the point may be east of the plateau, where the Southwest Indian Ridge region is about 3000 to 4000 m deep.

The Del Cano Rise lies near 45°S, 45 to 50°E, so it is not at this point. East of Crozet, around 53°E and 47°S, depths are probably about 3000 to 3500 m. I estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

11162.text:
The point at -35, -175 is in the South Pacific, northeast of New Zealand and southwest of Tonga. It lies near the Kermadec Trench region, but the trench is at about -178 to -176 longitude at latitude -35, so this point is east of the trench axis. The Kermadec Ridge and the trench run along roughly 178-179W at that latitude, with the trench to the east of the ridge. At -175 the point is on the Pacific plate, east of the trench axis.

The trench axis near 35S is around 177.5W, and the Pacific plate seafloor to the east is typically about 5000-5500 m deep. The Louisville seamount chain runs through about 35S, 175W. The Louisville Ridge crosses the Kermadec Trench near 26S, so at 35S the chain is further southeast, around 38S 170W. The point is probably abyssal plain or the flank of a seamount, so I estimate about -5300 m.

