At -9, 45 the seabed is in the Somali Basin or the northern end of the Comoros Basin. I'm not certain of the exact depth. The Davie Ridge is near 41-42°E. The Somali Basin floor lies at about 4500-5000 m. The Comoros Basin floor at about 12°S, 46°E is around 3500 m. I'd guess the depth at -9, 45 is about 4300 m, so I'll go with that.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

9339.text:
Latitude -13.0, longitude 139.0 is in northern Australia, in the Gulf of Carpentaria region. The Gulf's southern coast sits around -17, and Mornington Island is near -16.6, 139.4. The western Cape York coast is at about 141.5. At -13 and 139 the point is in the middle of the Gulf of Carpentaria, which is shallow at about 50-70 m deep.

The Gulf's depth near its center (-13.5, 138) is about 60 m. Arnhem Land's coast at Groote Eylandt is near -14, 136.5, and the Wellesley Islands are at -17. At -13, 139 the point is roughly northeast of Groote, in open Gulf. The seabed there is probably about -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-52}

11015.text:
The point is at 33°S, 109°W, in the southeastern Pacific. It lies west of Easter Island (27°S, 109.4°W) and south of the Easter Island/Salas y Gómez ridge. It is also north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which is at about 55°S, and east of the East Pacific Rise, which is around 113°W at 33°S.

The point is therefore on the eastern flank of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor age there is about 10-15 Myr. Depth in this area is roughly 3,500-3,800 m, so I estimate about 3,700 m. The Easter Microplate region is at 26°S, so this point is south of it. At 33°S the rise crest is near 111-112°W, and 109°W is only about 250 km east of the crest. That seafloor is young, so the depth should be about 3,000 m. I'll go with about -3,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

5629.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude -81.0 is in central Florida. It lies in Highlands County, near the Glades County border, west of Lake Okeechobee. Lake Okeechobee is at about 26.95 N, -80.8 W, so this point is about 20 km west of the lake. The area is Brighton or Venus, roughly 27.0 N, -81.0 W. The Fisheating Creek area is nearby, and the terrain is flat with elevations of about 10-15 m.

I estimate the elevation at about 12 m, and the surface is land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":12}

10459.text:
The point (-27, -141) lies in the South Pacific, east of the Tuamotus and Gambier Islands and west of Easter Island. The Gambier Islands are at about -23, -135, and Pitcairn at -25, -130. This location is in the open ocean on the Pacific plate, near the southern Tuamotu area and the Austral fracture zones.

The seafloor depth here is probably around 3,800 to 4,200 m. Seafloor age in this region is moderate, and the East Pacific Rise flank is to the east. The point at -27, -141 is also north of the Easter-Pitcairn hotspot chain. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further south, and the East Pacific Rise is near -27, -112. That puts this point about 30 degrees of longitude, roughly 3,000 km, west of the rise. At a spreading rate of about 150 mm/yr, the crust is roughly 20 to 30 million years old, which gives a depth of about 3,800 to 4,100 m. I'll estimate 4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

14900.text:
Location: -75, 101E in East Antarctica, inland of the Sabrina Coast/Wilkes Land coast. The Denman Glacier is near 99E, 66S, and 75S lies far inland. Around 75S 101E, the ice surface is about 2,800-3,000 m. Vostok is at 78S 106E with a surface of 3,488 m. Moving north to 75S, the surface drops to roughly 2,900 m, so I estimate about 2,950 m.

The ice there is grounded, so the type is Solid, and the elevation is the ice surface.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 2950}

3010.text:
The point 57.0°N, 81.0°E is in western Siberia, in Tomsk Oblast, in the Vasyugan swamp area or near the Ob River. Tomsk itself is at about 56.5°N, 85°E, so this point lies west of Tomsk. The Ob flows near 57°N at roughly 80–82°E (Kolpashevo is at 58.3°N, 82.9°E), so the point could be close to a river channel, though it is more likely swampy land. The terrain there is flat and low, with elevations around 80–100 m.

I'll go with Solid at about 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

15443.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 107 lies in East Antarctica, near Vostok Station, which is at about -78.46, 106.8. At -81 the point is about 2.5 degrees south of Vostok, on the East Antarctic plateau. Surface elevation there is roughly 3,400-3,500 m. Dome A is at -80.4, 77, with a surface near 4,090 m. Further east along 107°E at -81, the surface is probably about 3,300-3,500 m.

Vostok's surface is about 3,488 m. Moving south toward -81 the ice surface likely rises slightly, to around 3,500 m. I'll estimate 3,450 m. The point is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3450}

1502.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -55.0 is in West Greenland, in the Melville Bay / Upernavik region. Upernavik is at about 72.79N, 56.15W. The point at 73.0N, 55.0W is inland and east-northeast of Upernavik. The coast around 73N lies near 56W, and fjords such as Upernavik Isfjord extend east to about 53W, so the point could be on the ice sheet margin or in a fjord.

Upernavik Isfjord is at about 72.9N, with branches reaching about 54W. Because 73.0N, 55W may fall in the fjord system, there is some chance it is water. Still, the ice sheet margin in this area is mostly grounded ice with an elevation of about 500-1000 m. I'll treat it as Solid, with ice-surface elevation of around 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

14043.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -173 is in the Southern Ocean, north of the Ross Sea region and east of Cape Adare (which sits near 170°E). Longitude -173 is 173°W. The Antarctic coast at this longitude is around -78 (Ross Ice Shelf), so -67 is open ocean.

The area lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Southern Ocean abyssal plain. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is around 65°S at 175°W, and the Balleny Islands are around 67°S, 163°E. At 67°S, 173°W the seafloor is probably the continental rise or abyssal plain, with a depth of about 3000-4000 m. The Adare Basin is near 71°S 175°E. To the east is the Iselin Bank at 170°W near 73°S. At 67°S, the depth is likely about 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

8333.text:
Location: -3.0, -73.0 is in the Peruvian Amazon, Loreto region. The Amazon River near Iquitos is at about -3.75, -73.25. The Marañón and Ucayali join around -4.5, -73.5. At -3.0, -73.0 it is northeast of Iquitos, near the Napo River, which flows around -3.4, -72.8 (Napo near its mouth at -3.5, -73.4). So the point is probably in forest on land, with an elevation around 100 m.

The Amazon at Iquitos is about 100 m, and the terrain in this area runs roughly 90-130 m. I'll estimate 110 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

15488.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -163 falls in the Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 78°S to 85°S, and at longitude -163 (about 163°W) it is in the eastern part of the shelf. Near 83°S, 163°W the ice shelf is still floating, since the grounding line lies further south and east, near the Transantarctic Mountains and the Siple Coast around 150°W.

For a floating ice shelf, the answer should be the seabed elevation. Bathymetry under the central Ross Ice Shelf is typically around -500 to -600 m. In BedMachine, the seabed at 83°S, 163°W is probably around -600 m, so I'll use -580.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-580}

8611.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 123.0 is in Sulawesi, Indonesia, in the Southeast Sulawesi region. Buton Island sits around 122.9-123.1°E at latitudes -5.0 to -5.5, and the Muna Island is around 122.5°E, -4.9°S. The Kabaena island is at 121.9°E. Mainland Southeast Sulawesi extends to about 122.5°E at -5.0. So 123.0°E, -5.0 is near the north tip of Buton Island, which reaches about -4.5°S at 122.9°E. Buton's northern part extends to around -4.55°S, and its width at -5.0 is roughly 122.8 to 123.2°E. The Buton Strait lies to the west, and the east coast faces the Banda Sea / Tukang Besi area. At -5.0, the Buton coast near Lasalimu is at about 122.9-123.1°E, so the point is likely land.

Elevation is probably a few hundred meters, roughly 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

12670.text:
Latitude -51, longitude -39 is in the South Atlantic, east of the Falkland Islands and west of South Georgia. South Georgia lies at roughly -54.3, -36.5, so this point is open ocean.

The nearby seafloor is the northern part of the Georgia Basin or the North Scotia Ridge region. The North Scotia Ridge runs from the Falklands east toward South Georgia at about -53 to -54 latitude, so -51 is north of it. The Georgia Basin there is about 3000-5000 m deep. At -51, -39 I estimate about -3500 m, which is a guess rather than a recalled value. The Mid-Atlantic area near -51, -39 may also be part of the Maurice Ewing Bank, which lies around -50 to -51 and -46 to -42, so that bank is farther east. I'll go with about -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

11810.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 41 lies in the southwestern Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa and southwest of the Crozet Islands. It is near the Crozet Basin or the Southwest Indian Ridge region.

The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from about 50°S, 30°E toward the northeast. The Africa-Antarctic Ridge passes near 41°S at roughly 40°E, so the point could be on the ridge or in the Del Caño Rise area. The Del Caño Rise is near 44°S, 44°E. The Conrad Rise is further south, near 53°S. At 41°S, 41°E, the Southwest Indian Ridge sits around 40°S, 50°E to 45°S, 37°E, which puts this point near the ridge axis, where depth is about 3000-4000 m. Between Del Caño and the Prince Edward Fracture Zone, depths are probably around 3500 m. I'll guess -3600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

5525.text:
Latitude 29.0 N, Longitude 71.0 E is in Pakistan's Punjab province, in the Bahawalpur area. It is near the Cholistan desert edge, south of Bahawalpur (29.4 N, 71.68 E). The location is west of Bahawalpur, near the Sutlej/Chenab floodplain, which is about 100-110 m above sea level.

The Indus is further west at about 70.5 E at this latitude, and the terrain around 71.0 E is approximately 108 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":108}

4748.text:
The point 37.0°N, 43.0°W is in the North Atlantic, about 1,000 km west of the Azores and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The Azores are near 28°W and the ridge axis near 37°N is around 29-30°W, so this point lies on the western flank of the ridge, in the deep basin. Seafloor depth there is typically about 4,000 to 5,000 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -4,300 m, based on typical depths for this part of the basin rather than a specific lookup.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

13683.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -173 is in the South Pacific sector of the Southern Ocean, north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. The ridge near 170°W to 180° lies at roughly 65-66°S, so this point is on its northern flank.

The seafloor there is typically around 3000-4000 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crest is about 2500-3000 m deep, and the flanks deepen to roughly 3500-4000 m. At 63°S, 173°W, the point sits north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and near the Udintsev Fracture Zone, which lies around 145°W, so it is not close to that. The Pitman Fracture Zone is near 175°W at about 64°S. I estimate a depth of about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9286.text:
Latitude -13.0, longitude 33.0 is in eastern Zambia, near the Malawi border. It lies in the Luangwa Valley region, south of Mfuwe and near Chipata (-13.64, 32.65). Chipata sits at about 1,100 m, and the Luangwa Valley floor is around 500-600 m. Moving east from the valley, the plateau rises to about 1,000 m.

At 33.0°E, -13.0° the point is about 40 km northeast of Chipata. The Luangwa River is at roughly 31.8-32.5°E at this latitude, so 33.0°E is on the plateau east of the escarpment, probably at about 1,000 m. The Zambia-Malawi border is near 33.2°E, with Mchinji around 32.9°E, -13.8°. The plateau there is at roughly 1,000-1,200 m, so I'll estimate 1,050 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1050}

15593.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 47 is in East Antarctica, inland of Queen Maud Land and the Dronning Maud Land region. It sits near the Pensacola Mountains and Dufek Massif, which lie around 52°W, so it is not that area. At 47°E and 83°S, it is in the interior of the East Antarctic plateau, roughly south of Dome Argus's western flank. Dome A is at 80.4°S, 77°E. Around 83°S, 47°E, the ice surface is about 3,500 m, likely 3,400–3,700 m. The Recovery Glacier region lies near 81°S, 20°W, so it is also not relevant here.

I estimate the surface elevation at about 3,550 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3550}

14541.text:
The point is at 71°S, 103°E in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. Vostok Station sits at about 78.5°S, 106.8°E, and the coast near Davis and Mirny lies around 66°S. At 71°S, 103°E we are inland of the coast, in the region south of the Shackleton Ice Shelf (about 100°E, 66°S) and near the Denman Glacier area (about 99°E, 66.5°S). The ice sheet surface there should be roughly 2000 m or more. The surface rises from about 1000 m at 69°S to about 2500 m at 72°S, and 71°S, 103°E looks to be around 2300 m.

This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

79.text:
Latitude 89°N is in the Arctic Ocean, close to the North Pole. The central Arctic basin there has seabed depths of roughly 4,000 m. At 89°N, 21°W the point lies near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from Greenland to Siberia and passes close to the pole. The ridge sits on the Amerasian side of the pole, roughly along 140°E to 40°W, so 21°W at 89°N may be on the Greenland side of the ridge, near its crest.

The ridge crest is typically about 1,000–1,600 m deep. Near the pole, depths on the ridge are about 1,200–1,700 m. The Lomonosov Ridge at the pole is at about 88.5–90°N, where the longitudes converge. The ridge axis runs from about 80°W at Ellesmere/Greenland through the pole and out along 140°E. At 89°N, the axis on the Greenland side lies near 60°W to 40°W. The point at 21°W would then be off the ridge on the Eurasian basin side (the Amundsen Basin), where depth is about 4,200 m. Amundsen Basin depths near the pole are about 4,100–4,400 m. I estimate about -4,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

15404.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 29 is in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land. The South Pole is at -90, so this point lies about 9° from the pole. At longitude 29°E, the area south of the Recovery Glacier region and the Shackleton Range (which is near 25°W) is inland, on the polar plateau. Longitude 29°E at -81° is likely near the Pensacola Mountains' opposite side, in the interior of Dronning Maud Land. Ice surface elevation there is likely about 3,000 m. The Wolthat Mountains and Orvinfjella lie near 12°E at -72°, so -81° is far inland, and the plateau there rises to roughly 3,200 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3200}

4055.text:
Coordinates 45.0°N, 11.0°E fall in northern Italy, in the Po plain near the Veneto/Emilia-Romagna/Lombardy border. This is close to Legnago and Ostiglia, a bit west of Rovigo. The Po river lies a little south, near 44.95°N.

The point is on solid ground. Ground elevation in this part of the plain is about 15 m, since Legnago sits at roughly 16 m and Ostiglia at about 12 m. The point is slightly west of Legnago and north of the Po, near Sermide, which is at about 16 m (Sermide is at 11.28°E). At 11.0°E and 45.0°N the location is between Ostiglia (11.13°E, 45.07°N) and Poggio Rusco (11.1°E, 44.98°N). Poggio Rusco is at about 15 m, so I estimate 15 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":15}

8697.text:
The coordinates -7.0, -65.0 fall in the Brazilian Amazon, in southern Amazonas state. This is near the Purus River region, close to Lábrea, which sits at about -7.26, -64.8. The terrain there is low-lying Amazon basin, roughly 50 to 100 m above sea level.

I estimate about 75 m for this point and classify it as Solid.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 75}

15763.text:
The point is at latitude -85, longitude 27, in East Antarctica. That places it on the polar plateau, in the region near the Pensacola Mountains and the Recovery Glacier area (Recovery is around 81°S, 20-30°W). At 27°E, 85°S, it is inland of Queen Maud Land and Dronning Maud Land, south of the Shackleton Range. This is high plateau, roughly 2,500-3,000 m. East of the Pole of Inaccessibility direction, the elevation is probably about 2,800 m.

I am estimating from memory of the plateau rather than from a lookup. The surface type is grounded ice, so it counts as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

3754.text:
Latitude 49.0 N, longitude 129.0 E is in the Russian Far East, in Khabarovsk Krai or the Amur region. The Sikhote-Alin range lies farther east, near 135–138 E. At 129 E, 49 N the terrain is near the Bureya river basin and the Amur-Zeya plain.

The Bureya Reservoir sits around 49.7 N, 130 E, so 49.0 N, 129.0 E is probably west of it. Nearby, the Little Khingan lies around 131 E. The Bureya range is at roughly 50 N, 130–134 E. The Selemdzha river region is around 51 N, 129 E. At 49 N, 129 E I would expect hilly terrain at about 400 m, with the Arkhara-Bureya lowland at 200 m to the south. The Bureya River's upper reach, the Bureya Reservoir, runs from about 50 N, 130 E, so this point is likely hills. I'll estimate 380 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":380}

3122.text:
55°N, 55°W is in the northwest Atlantic, east of Newfoundland and Labrador. It lies south of the Labrador Sea, in the area of the Newfoundland Basin / Orphan Basin edge. The Newfoundland continental margin sits around 50°W at 47°N, and the Grand Banks extend to roughly 48°W.

At 55°N, 55°W the Labrador shelf edge is near 54°W at 55°N, since the coast is at about 60°W there. The Labrador shelf is roughly 150 km wide, with the shelf break near 57–58°W at 55°N, and beyond it the slope drops into the Labrador Sea basin. So 55°W is in deep water, about 3000 m deep. The Labrador Sea basin is about 3400 m deep in the center near 57°N, 52°W. At 55°N, 55°W the depth is probably about 3000 m, so I estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

5189.text:
The point 33.0°N, 119.0°E lies in Jiangsu/Anhui province, China, near Huai'an and Chuzhou. This area is part of the Huai River plain. Hongze Lake is at roughly 33.3°N, 118.6°E, so the point is southeast of the lake, near Xuyi or Jinhu.

Xuyi County is at about 118.5°E, 33.0°N, and the region around 119.0°E is Jinhu/Hongze. The nearby Gaoyou Lake lies around 32.9°N, 119.3°E, and Baoying Lake is further east. The point is therefore probably on land, either in the low plain or along the lake edge. The Hongze Lake southeastern shore is about 33.1°N, 118.9°E, so the point may lie close to the water, but it is more likely to be land in the Jinhu area with an elevation of about 8 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":8}

11978.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 17 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of South Africa. The Cape is at about 34°S, 18°E, so this point is roughly 9° south of it. The area lies near the Agulhas Ridge region or the southern Cape Basin. The Agulhas Ridge runs from about 40°S 10°E to 44°S 16°E, so the point may sit near its eastern end. The Cape Basin floor is about 4500-5000 m deep, and the ridge is shallower at around 2500-3500 m. At 43°S 17°E the point is probably just east of the ridge end, in the Cape Basin, at about -4800 m.

I'll estimate -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

2000.text:
The point 67.0°N, 139.0°W is in northern Yukon, Canada, near the Porcupine River and Old Crow Flats area. Old Crow is at about 67.57°N, 139.84°W, so this point is a bit southeast of it. The terrain there is the Porcupine Plateau and lowlands, with elevations around 300-500 m. The Porcupine River at 67°N, 139°W is probably at about 250 m. The Richardson Mountains lie farther east at about 136°W. The Bluefish basin is to the north, and the Eagle Plain is to the south, at about 66.4°N, 136.7°W, at roughly 600 m.

This is probably rolling upland near the Porcupine River, at around 400 m. I'll estimate 380 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":380}

11526.text:
The point is at 39°S, 167°W, in the South Pacific, roughly east of New Zealand and southwest of the Cook Islands. There is no land here, so the surface is water.

For depth, the area lies in the southwest Pacific basin, and abyssal plains there are typically 4,500 to 5,500 m deep. The Pacific seafloor near 39°S, 167°W is roughly 5,000 m, so I'm estimating about -5,100 m. This is an estimate from regional typical depths, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

10397.text:
The point (-25, 95) lies in the Indian Ocean, west of Australia and southwest of the Cocos Islands. The Cocos (Keeling) Islands are near 12°S, 96.8°E, so they are far to the north.

The area is near the Wharton Basin, with the Ninetyeast Ridge at about 90°E. At 95°E and 25°S the seafloor is probably abyssal plain at roughly 5000 m depth. The Wharton Basin floor is typically 5000–5800 m deep, and the region just east of the Ninetyeast Ridge is around 5000–5500 m. I'll estimate about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

12847.text:
Latitude -53, longitude -45 is in the South Atlantic, northeast of South Georgia (which is at about -54.3, -36.5) and west of it. The point is southwest of the island, in the Georgia Basin or on the North Scotia Ridge area. The North Scotia Ridge runs from Burdwood Bank (-54, -60) eastward to South Georgia (-54, -37). At -53, -45 we are north of the ridge, in the Georgia Basin, where depths are around 3,000-4,000 m. The ridge itself lies around -54 to -55 at longitude -45, with depths of about 2,000-3,000 m. So at -53 the depth is probably about 3,300 m.

I estimate a seabed elevation of about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

9445.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -9 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and west of Africa. St. Helena lies at about -15.9, -5.7, so this point is west of St. Helena by roughly 3.3 degrees.

The St. Helena seamount chain runs along the Bonaparte seamounts and the Wells Seamount, and the surrounding seafloor is about 3500-4500 m deep. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 15°S is at about 14°W, so -9 is on its eastern flank. Seafloor age there is about 30 Ma, giving a depth of roughly 4000-4500 m. The St. Helena plateau raises the floor in places, so I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

14276.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -67 lies on the Antarctic Peninsula's west coast, near Marguerite Bay and Wilkins Sound. Its location is roughly the northern George VI Sound region, near Alexander Island's southeastern part. George VI Sound lies at about 70°S, 68°W. At -69, -67 we are around the Lallemand Fjord / Bourgeois Fjord area, or on the Antarctic Peninsula's Graham Land coast, which is south of Marguerite Bay's northern end.

Marguerite Bay is at about 68-69°S, 67-69°W. Bourgeois Fjord is at 67.5°S, 67°W. Lallemand Fjord is at 67.8°S, 66.8°W. Further south, at 69°S 67°W, the position is on the Antarctic Peninsula's Loubet/Fallières coast, near the Arrowsmith Peninsula/Wordie area. Neumayer Channel is not relevant. Near 69°S, the Peninsula's coast is around 68°W, and the Antarctic Plateau (Palmer Land) lies to the east. So 67°W is likely inland on the ice-covered plateau, such as the Dyer Plateau area or the glaciers feeding the Wilkins Sound. The elevation there is probably 1500-2000 m. George VI Sound's eastern coast runs near 68.5°W at 70°S, so at 69°S the eastern shore is around 68°W, which puts 67°W on the plateau of Palmer Land at about 1500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1500}

5250.text:
The point 31.0°N, 119.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, west of Baja California. It is roughly 400 km offshore from Ensenada, which is at about 31.9°N, 116.6°W. It falls in the area of the Baja California borderland and abyssal plain.

The continental borderland extends out to about 120°W at this latitude. Guadalupe Island is at 29°N, 118.3°W. The seafloor around 31°N, 119°W is probably an abyssal hill region with depths of around 3500–4000 m. The borderland basins sit nearer to the coast, and the Patton Escarpment is farther north. West of Guadalupe Island, depths are about 3800 m. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14479.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -21 is in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica, near the Fimbul Ice Shelf and Riiser-Larsen area. The Fimbul Ice Shelf spans roughly 3°W to 3°E, and the Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf lies around 16°W to 8°W. Longitude -21 is further west, around the Brunt/Stancomb-Wills area at about -25°W to -20°W, which is the Stancomb-Wills Glacier Tongue and the coast near 75°S. At 71°S, -21°W, the area is likely open ocean in the Weddell Sea, off the Princess Martha Coast. The coast there is roughly 70.5°S at 8°W and about 71.5°S at 12°W. At -21°W, the coast runs near 72-73°S. So 71°S, -21°W is probably ocean over the continental shelf edge or slope, at a depth of roughly -2500 m.

The Weddell Sea there is deep, with the Explora escarpment further east. Around 71°S, 21°W the depth is probably about 3000 m, so I'll estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15661.text:
