5019.text:
Latitude 35.0, longitude 139.0 is in Japan. Sagami Bay lies around 35.0N, 139.2E, and the Izu Peninsula's eastern coast is near 139.1E at about 35.0N. Atami is at 35.1N, 139.07E. Ito is at 34.97N, 139.1E. Manazuru is at 35.15N, 139.15E.

At 139.0E, 35.0N the point is inland on the Izu Peninsula, west of Ito. Nearby places are Izu-Kogen and Mount Omuro at 139.09E. The Kano River valley lies near Shuzenji (34.97N, 138.93E), and Amagi-san is at 34.85N, 138.95E. Around 35.0N, 139.0E, the area is the Kawazu/Yugashima region. Mount Amagi is further south, and the Izu Peninsula's northern portion near Shuzenji sits at roughly 100-200 m. The point is northeast of Shuzenji, near Mount Daruma (981 m, 34.93N, 138.8E), so it is probably in hilly terrain at about 400 m. I'll estimate 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

2366.text:
The point 63.0°N, 127.0°W lies in the Northwest Territories, Canada. It is near the Mackenzie Mountains foothills, west of Great Bear Lake and south of Norman Wells. The Mackenzie River sits at about 65°N, 126°W near Fort Good Hope, and Wrigley is at about 63.2°N, 123.4°W. So this point is about 110 km west of Wrigley, in the Mackenzie Mountains' eastern front.

Terrain there is probably around 800–1200 m. The Keele River is at about 64.5°N, 125.5°W. The Redstone River is at about 62.3°N, 125°W. At 63°N, 127°W the terrain is mountainous, so I estimate about 1000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

2654.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 89.0 E is in central Siberia, in Krasnoyarsk Krai. It sits in the West Siberian Plain's eastern edge, near the Yenisei lowlands and the Sym/Kas area, within the Turukhansk and Yeniseysk region. The Yenisei River runs at about 86–87°E at this latitude, so 89°E is east of the river, in the area of the Central Siberian Plateau's western edge. Elevations there are probably around 100–200 m.

Surface type is Solid. Elevation is a rough estimate of about 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

8161.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude -57.0 is in Brazil, near the Amazon River. Parintins is at about -2.63, -56.73, and Óbidos is at about -1.9, -55.5. The Amazon at longitude -57 runs around latitude -2.1 near Terra Santa and Juruti at -2.15, -56.1. At -1.0, -57.0 we are north of the river, in the Pará/Amazonas border region near the Nhamundá River, which lies at about -2.2 to -1.5 at longitude -57. The Trombetas is further east. So this point is probably land, in the northern Amazon lowlands.

The elevation there is probably about 50 to 100 m. Nearby terrain north of the Amazon in this area is hilly, in the Guiana shield foothills, with elevations of roughly 50-100 m. I'll say 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

9715.text:
Latitude -17, longitude 171 is in the South Pacific, west of Vanuatu. Vanuatu spans roughly 166.5–170.5E, with Epi at about 168E and Aneityum at about 170E at -20. At -17, 171E is east of Vanuatu, in the North Fiji Basin's eastern edge or the New Hebrides Trench region. The trench lies on the west side of the arc for the subduction under Vanuatu, so this point is east of the arc, around 171E. The Vanuatu arc's eastern side, near Espiritu Santo at 167E, has the back-arc basin, and 171E is about 400 km east of Santo. That area is part of the North Fiji Basin, where the depth is about 3000 m. The Hunter fracture zone lies further south.

I estimate the depth at roughly -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15646.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 153 is in Antarctica, in the Transantarctic Mountains region near the Beardmore/Nimrod/Byrd glacier area. The Ross Ice Shelf edge lies around -83 to -85 at longitudes near 170°E to 180°. At 153°E and -83°, this is on the East Antarctic plateau side, west of the Transantarctic Mountains. The Byrd Glacier is at about 160°E, -80.3°. The Mulock Glacier is around 160°E, -79°. The Beardmore Glacier is at about 170°E, -83.5°. So 153°E at -83° is inland on the plateau, with an ice surface elevation around 2500 m or so.

The area near the Shackleton Glacier is at 155°W, so it's not relevant. The Law Dome is also not relevant. Near the Byrd Glacier head, around -82 and 150°E, the ice elevation is about 2000-2500 m. I'll estimate 2400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

6449.text:
The point 19.0°N, 119.0°E lies in the South China Sea, west of Luzon, so the surface is Water. I estimated the seabed depth from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup.

Luzon's west coast is at about 120.3°E, and the South China Sea basin lies to the west of it. At 119°E, 19°N the seafloor is roughly 4,000 m deep. The Manila Trench runs near 120°E at this latitude. At 19°N the trench is at about 120°E and reaches depths of 4,000–5,000 m. Longitude 119°E is about 1° west of it, on the abyssal plain edge. The basin floor there is about 4,000 m deep, so I estimate -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

10146.text:
The coordinates -23.0, -47.0 fall in São Paulo state, Brazil, near Sorocaba and Salto, about 50 km from Itu. This is inland, so the surface is Solid.

The terrain there is the interior plateau, with elevations of roughly 550-700 m. Sorocaba sits near 23.5°S, 47.45°W at about 550 m. Salto is at 23.2°S, 47.29°W at about 550 m. Indaiatuba, at 23.09°S, 47.2°W, is around 650 m. Point -23.0, -47.0 is near Itupeva, Jundiaí and Cabreúva, where elevations run about 700 m. I'll estimate 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

7394.text:
This location (7°N, 151°W) is in the central Pacific Ocean, south of Hawaii and north of the equator. The nearest islands are the Line Islands, far to the southwest, and none are close, so the surface is open water.

The seafloor in this region is typically abyssal plain at about 4,500–5,500 m depth. The area lies between the Clarion-Clipperton Zone and the Line Islands, where depths are around 5,000 m. I estimate about -5,100 m, though I did not look up a bathymetric value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

4429.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude 39.0 is on the Turkish Black Sea coast, near Trabzon (Trabzon is at about 41.0N, 39.72E). Longitude 39.0 is west of Trabzon, near Tirebolu (about 40.98N, 38.8E) and Görele (41.04N, 39.0E). Görele sits almost exactly at 39.0E, so the point is either on land at the coast or just offshore.

Görele's town center is at about 41.03N, 39.00E, so 41.0N, 39.0E is slightly south of the town center. That puts it on land, in the coastal hills just inland. The terrain rises quickly there, so I estimate about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

5613.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude -113.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, west of Baja California. Baja's Pacific coast at 27°N is around -114.1 (Guerrero Negro is at -114.0, Punta Eugenia at 27.8N, -115). At -113.0 and 27.0N, the location is in the Gulf of California, near Santa Rosalía (27.3N, -112.27). The peninsula there spans from about -114.1 on the Pacific side to -112.5 on the Gulf side. Longitude -113.0 at 27.0N is therefore on the peninsula, in the Vizcaíno Desert / Sierra San Francisco area, so the surface is land.

The Sierra San Francisco is near 27.6N, -112.9. At 27.0N, -113.0 the terrain is around the Vizcaíno plateau, possibly 300-500 m. San Ignacio is at 27.3N, -112.9, at an elevation of about 150 m. South of it, near 27.0N, -113.0, is the Sierra Santa Marta / Volcán Las Tres Vírgenes area (27.47N, -112.59). I'll estimate about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

14841.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -17 lies in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, near the Filchner Ice Shelf front. The Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf front runs roughly from -78 at -40 longitude to about -75 at -30 or so. At -17 longitude the coast is the Brunt Ice Shelf and Riiser-Larsen area, around -72 to -73 latitude, so -75 at -17 would be inland on the Antarctic ice sheet in Coats Land / Dronning Maud Land.

Coats Land at -75, -17 is probably grounded ice at a few hundred meters up to about 1000+ m. The Theron Mountains sit near -79, -27 and the Shackleton Range near -80, -25. At -75, -17 the ice sheet surface is plausibly around 1500 m. The coast at -17 is around -72.5 (Halley at -75.6, -26 is on the Brunt Ice Shelf). Halley at -26 is floating, so at -17 and -75 the position is east of the Brunt shelf, which likely makes it grounded ice. The ice surface there is perhaps 1000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

7017.text:
Location 13°N, 175°E is in the central Pacific, in the Marshall Islands region. The Marshall Islands lie between about 160°E and 173°E, with Ujelang at 161°E and Majuro at 171°E. Mili is about 172°E and Knox Atoll about 172°E. So 175°E lies east of the Marshalls, in open ocean in the Pacific basin.

The seafloor here is roughly 5,000-5,500 m deep. The area just east of the Marshalls is near the Pacific abyssal plain, where depths are about 5,300 m. I'll estimate -5400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

12656.text:
Latitude -51, longitude -67 is off the coast of Patagonia, Argentina. The Argentine coast near Río Gallegos sits at about -51.6, -69.2, and the coast at -51, -67 is well east of that. The Falkland Islands are at about -59 to -57 longitude, so they are not nearby either. That puts the point in the Atlantic, on the Patagonian shelf, roughly 250 km offshore from the coast at about -68.5.

The shelf depth there is about 100 m. The Magellan Strait entrance (Cabo Vírgenes) is at -52.3, -68.4, so this point is northeast of it. Depths in that area are around 80-100 m. I'll estimate -95 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-95}

8931.text:
Latitude -9.0, longitude 43.0 is in the western Indian Ocean, off the coast of East Africa. Tanzania's coast is at about 39°E, so this point is well offshore. It lies east of the Tanzanian coast and near the Comoros, which sit at about 11-12°S and 43-44°E, north of the Comoros basin.

The point is probably in the Somali Basin or the northern Mozambique Channel region. At 9°S, 43°E, the seafloor is likely deep, with the Comoros Basin to the south at roughly 3000-3500 m depth. To the north-west, the Davie Ridge runs near 41-42°E. Seafloor at 43°E, 9°S is probably around 4000 m deep, though I am not certain. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7149.text:
The coordinates 11.0°N, 79.0°E fall in Tamil Nadu, India, near Tiruchirappalli. Tiruchirappalli is at about 10.8°N, 78.7°E, and Ariyalur and Perambalur lie nearby. The point is probably in the Kaveri delta region or just north of it, around Ariyalur or Thanjavur.

Thanjavur is at 10.78°N, 79.14°E with an elevation of about 60 m. Kumbakonam is at 10.96°N, 79.38°E at about 25 m. The point at 11.0°N, 79.0°E is near Jayankondam and Ariyalur, at roughly 11.2°N, 79.07°E. The Kollidam river lies near 11.0°N. Elevation there is about 40 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":40}

5612.text:
The point 27.0°N, 115.0°W is in the Pacific Ocean, west of Baja California. Isla Guadalupe sits at about 29°N, 118.3°W, and Cedros Island at about 28°N, 115.2°W. The coast of Baja at 27°N is near 114.2°W (Guerrero Negro, Vizcaíno Bay). So the point is roughly 100 km offshore, about 0.8° west of the coast, and about 27°N, 115°W is west of Cedros Island.

That area is the continental borderland and the abyssal plain edge. The continental shelf is narrow there, and depths of 3,500 to 4,000 m are typical about 150 km offshore. At 100 to 150 km offshore I'd estimate about 3,800 m. The seabed in this region west of Baja, around 115°W, is about 3,900 m deep, so I'll estimate −3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

1697.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -25.0 is in East Greenland, near Scoresby Sund. Scoresby Sund's mouth is around 70.5N, -22W. Jameson Land lies near 71N, -23 to -24W. At -25 and 71N, this is at the northwest edge of Jameson Land or the Liverpool Land area. Scoresby Sund's fjord system: the Hall Bredning basin is around 70.4N, -26W. Milne Land is at 70.8N, -26 to -28W. At 71.0N, -25.0W, this is probably the Hurry Inlet or Scoresby Sund's northern arm, near Jameson Land's northwestern side.

Hurry Inlet is at about 70.5N, -22.5W. Fønfjord is at 71.4N, -25.5W, and Gåsefjord is at 71.2N, -24.5W. Gåsefjord runs east-west near 71.2N, from -26 to -23. Point 71.0, -25.0 might be land, between Gåsefjord and Hall Bredning, which would be the Jameson Land northern peninsula, with elevation of a few hundred meters. Alternatively, it could be in the fjord area near Rypefjord. I'll guess land at about 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

11842.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 105 is in the southeast Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. The seafloor there is probably abyssal plain or near the flank of the Southeast Indian Ridge.

The ridge is at about -50 latitude near 105°E, so -41 is north of it. The Diamantina Zone and the Naturaliste Plateau lie to the west near 115°E, -35 to -50. At 105°E, -41, the area is likely abyssal plain, with depth around 4500 to 5000 m. The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins south of about -45. At -41 and 105 the depth is probably about -4800 m, so I'll answer -4700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

5528.text:
The coordinates 29.0°N, 77.0°E fall in northern India, near the Haryana/Uttar Pradesh border around the Yamuna River. This is close to Sonipat/Baghpat, and Delhi lies to the south at roughly 28.6°N, 77.2°E. Elevation in this area is about 220 m.

The point is almost certainly land (Solid), and 220 m is my estimate from memory of the regional terrain, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":225}

5999.text:
The point 23°N, 61°W is in the western North Atlantic, east of the Bahamas and northeast of the Caribbean. This area lies on the Hatteras/Nares abyssal plain region, where depths are about 5,500 to 5,800 m. The Nares Abyssal Plain sits south of Bermuda and north of Puerto Rico, at roughly 22–24°N and 60–65°W, with depths near 5,800 m.

I'm estimating about -5,600 m, based on typical depths for this plain rather than a lookup of this exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

3635.text:
The coordinates 49.0°N, 109.0°W fall on the Canada–US border, near the Saskatchewan–Montana line. That puts the point just east of the Cypress Hills area, in the plains south of Consul, Saskatchewan. The Cypress Hills are at about 110°W, so this point is east of them.

The Frenchman River valley is nearby. Val Marie sits at about 107.7°W with an elevation of roughly 790 m. At 109°W the terrain is plains, around 900 m. Climate Canada puts Climax, Saskatchewan (49.2°N, 108.4°W) at about 970 m. Wildhorse, Alberta (49.0°N, 111.5°W) is around 1100 m. Between those, I estimate about 950 m for this point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":950}

14415.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -149 is in the Ross Sea sector of Antarctica, at the eastern edge of the Ross Ice Shelf, north of Marie Byrd Land. The Ross Ice Shelf front is near 78°S, and the Marie Byrd Land coast sits at about 75–77°S in that area. At 71°S, 149°W, we are well offshore in the Amundsen/Ross Sea open ocean, on the continental rise or slope.

The Southern Ocean there is deep. Around 71°S, 149°W the Pacific-Antarctic margin lies north of the continental shelf, which is at about 74°S. The depth is probably about 3,500 to 4,000 m, so I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

1375.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 51.0 E is in the Barents Sea region near Novaya Zemlya. Novaya Zemlya's northern island extends from about 73°N to 77°N, between roughly 55°E and 69°E. At 75°N its western coast lies around 56-57°E, so 51°E is open water west of the island. In the Barents Sea around 75°N 51°E, depths are about 200-300 m. The area is near the eastern Barents Sea shelf, where the bottom is roughly 250 m deep, and the Novaya Zemlya trench lies closer to the island.

I estimate a seabed elevation of about -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

5307.text:
The coordinates 31.0°N, 5.0°W fall in southeastern Morocco, near the Draa-Tafilalet region. That puts the point south of the High Atlas, around Erfoud and Rissani, though Erfoud is closer to 31.43°N, 4.23°W.

At 5°W, 31°N the point lies west of Alnif, in the Jbel Saghro/Ouarzazate area. Alnif is at about 31.1°N, 5.2°W with an elevation of roughly 850 m. Tazzarine is at about 30.77°N, 5.55°W and sits near 800 m. Nekob is at 31.1°N, 5.85°W at about 1000 m. The Draa valley around Agdz is at 30.7°N, 6.45°W. The point should be in the Jbel Ougnat/Saghro foothills, and I estimate about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

13940.text:
The point is at 65°S, 19°W, in the Weddell Sea, off the Antarctic coast of Queen Maud Land. The Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf lies around 16°W to 34°W at roughly 72°S, so 65°S is well north of it and in open ocean.

The Weddell Sea's open-water area near 65°S, 19°W is part of the deep Weddell Basin. Seabed depth there is likely around 4,000 to 5,000 m. The Maud Rise sits near 65°S, 3°E, which is far to the east, so it does not apply here. At 19°W the basin floor is probably about 4,800 m deep, so I'll estimate -4800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

2881.text:
The point is 57.0°N, 177.0°W, in the Bering Sea. It lies northwest of the Aleutian Islands and southwest of the Pribilof Islands. The Bering Sea shelf edge runs through roughly 57°N, 170°W to 60°N, 179°W. At 57°N, 177°W the point is probably off the shelf on the slope, or in the deep basin, since the Aleutian Basin is deep (about 3,500 m). The shelf break near 57°N is around 168–170°W, and farther west the slope falls away toward the basin. The Bowers Ridge is farther south and west, near 54°N, 180°. At 57°N, 177°W the depth is probably around 3,000 m.

This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup. I estimate the seabed at about 3,300 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

14314.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 9°E is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The Princess Astrid Coast sits at about 70°S, and the coastline near 9°E lies near 70.3°S, where the Fimbul Ice Shelf extends from about 3°W to 3°E. At 9°E the ice shelf front is around 70.5°S, so -69 is inland of it.

This puts the point on the grounded ice sheet, probably on the slope up from the coast. The surface elevation there is probably around 1000-1500 m. The Jutulstraumen glacier is near 0°E, and at 9°E and 69°S the ice rises toward the escarpment, so I estimate about 1300 m. The mountains of Sverdrup/Mühlig-Hofmann are near 5-7°E at 71.8°S, and the Orvin Mountains lie further east. The point is about 300 km from the coast, so a surface elevation of about 2000 m seems plausible. Ice sheet profiles in this region reach roughly 2000 m about 200-300 km inland, and I settle on about 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

15007.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -45 is in the southern Weddell Sea, in the area of the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly 60°W to 30°W at about 77–78°S, and the Filchner Ice Shelf lies near 40°W. At -45° longitude and -77° latitude, this is most likely the Filchner-Ronne front area. The Berkner Island ice rise sits near 45°W, but it spans roughly 77.5–79.5°S at 46°W, so -77 is probably just north of its tip. The northern tip of Berkner Island is about 78°S, so this point is likely on the ice shelf or open water north of the island.

The Ronne Ice Shelf front is around 75–76°S at 60°W, and near 45°W the Filchner-Ronne front is about 77.5°S. So -77 is probably within the floating shelf or just at its edge. Either way it is Water, with a seabed depth in the Filchner Trough region. The trough is deep (about 500–1000 m), and the seabed near 77°S, 45°W is probably around -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

12088.text:
The point -45, -123 lies in the South Pacific, southwest of Chile, so it is open ocean.

This is east of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and west of the Chile Rise. The Chile Rise runs from the Chile Triple Junction at about 46.5°S, 75°W toward the southwest. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at 45°S is near 111°W, so -123 is on the Pacific plate, west of the ridge crest. The ridge flanks there are fairly shallow, with depths around 3,500-4,000 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crest sits at about 2,500-3,000 m depth, and the seafloor 12° of longitude away is probably about 3,800 m deep. I estimate a depth of roughly -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8807.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude 155.0 lies in the Solomon Sea region, east of Bougainville (which is near -6.2, 155.4) and southwest of Buka. Bougainville's southern part extends to about -6.9 at longitude 155.5, and its southwest coast is around 155.0 at about -6.6. At -7.0, 155.0 the point is likely ocean, just south of Bougainville's coast, in the Solomon Sea.

The Solomon Sea's depth near the Bougainville southwest coast drops quickly. Shortland Islands are at about -7.0, 155.8, so this point is west of them. The depth there is probably around 1500–2500 m. The New Britain trench is further west, so I'll estimate -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

2235.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -29.0 is in the Denmark Strait, off the east coast of Greenland. The Greenland coast near Kangerlussuaq is at about 68°N, 32°W. At 65°N the east Greenland coast lies near 40°W, and Iceland's west fjords reach about 24°W. So -29°W at 65°N is in the middle of the Denmark Strait, which is water.

The strait's sill is around 65–66°N, 27–30°W, where the depth is roughly 600–700 m. The Denmark Strait sill is about 620 m deep. Here the seabed is probably deeper, since the position is slightly south of the sill and the Kolbeinsey Ridge and the Greenland shelf slope are nearby. I'd estimate about -800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

1804.text:
The point 69.0°N, 171.0°W lies in the Chukchi Sea, north of the Bering Strait and northeast of Wrangel Island. Wrangel Island sits near 71°N, 179°W, and the Chukotka coast near 170°W is at about 66-67°N. At 69°N, 171°W the point is therefore open sea.

Chukchi Sea depths in this area are typically 40-50 m. I'll estimate about -45 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -45}

973.text:
Location 79N, 33W is in northeast Greenland, near the 79°N Glacier (Nioghalvfjerdsfjorden) region. That glacier's floating tongue lies around 79.5N, 20W, so 33W is far inland on the ice sheet, where the surface is roughly 2,000-2,500 m.

The NEGIS ice stream begins near 77.5N, 35W. At 79N, 33W the ice sheet surface elevation is about 2,400 m. Summit elevation at 72N is 3,200 m. At 79N, 33W, I estimate about 2,350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2350}

12564.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 109 is in the Southern Ocean, southwest of Australia and south of the Indian Ocean basin. It lies near the Southeast Indian Ridge, which runs roughly through (-49, 109)? The ridge is at about -48 to -50 latitude near 110°E, and the Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies further east, near 120°E. Depths along the ridge are about 3000-4000 m. The Kerguelen Plateau is farther west, around 70-80°E, so it is not a factor here. South of Australia, the seafloor at -49 and 109 is probably around 3500 m deep.

I estimate the seabed at about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

1577.text:
The point 73°N, 95°E lies in the Taymyr region of northern Russia, on the northern coast near the Khatanga Gulf and Taymyr Bay. The Khatanga Gulf sits around 74°N, 110°E, so 95°E is farther west, in the Taymyr Peninsula area.

The Taymyr Peninsula's northern coast is at roughly 76°N, and the southern Taymyr coast near Khatanga is at about 72°N, 102°E. At 73°N, 95°E, the point is therefore inland on the peninsula, in the Byrranga foothills or lowlands. The North Siberian Lowland there is mostly 50–100 m. Lake Taymyr is at about 74.5°N, 101°E, so it is not at this point. I'll assume land at about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

8667.text:
The point (-7.0, -125.0) is in the open South Pacific, southwest of the Galápagos and east of the Marquesas. It lies in the eastern Pacific abyssal plain, north of the Pacific-Antarctic ridge. The East Pacific Rise is farther west, around 110°W, so this point sits on the plain to its east, on crust roughly 30-40 Myr old.

Seafloor depth there is likely about 4000-4300 m. The area around 5°S, 125°W is a fairly deep basin at about 4,200 m, and I recall that the seafloor near the equator at 125°W is about 4,400 m. I'll estimate -4,150 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4150}

11780.text:
The point is at 41°S, 19°W, in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies southwest of Tristan da Cunha (37°S, 12°W) and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs near 14°W at this latitude. At 19°W it sits west of the ridge on the western flank, in the Argentine Basin's eastern margin. The Rio Grande Rise is farther north-west, so it doesn't apply here.

Seafloor depth on the western flank of the ridge at about 5° from the axis is around 3500–4000 m. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis at 41°S is near 14°W, and 19°W is about 400 km west of it. Seafloor age there is roughly 15 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3500–4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2764.text:
Latitude 59.0 N, longitude -51.0 W is in the Labrador Sea/Davis Strait region, southwest of Greenland. Southern Greenland's tip (Cape Farewell) is at about 59.8 N, 43.9 W, and the west coast at 61 N is near 48-50 W. At 59 N, 51 W we are well offshore, roughly 300 km west of Cape Farewell, so this is the Labrador Sea.

Depth there is probably about 3000 m. The Labrador Sea basin reaches about 3500 m at its center near 57 N, 50 W. At 59 N, 51 W, which is nearer the Greenland shelf edge, the depth is perhaps 3300 m.

