7877.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 95.0°E lies in the Indian Ocean, west of northern Sumatra. Aceh's coast is around 5°N, 95.3°E, and Simeulue island sits near 2.6°N, 96°E. At 95.0°E the point is about 100 km west of Simeulue, which puts it in the forearc region west of the Sunda trench's accretionary prism.

The trench lies at roughly 92-93°E at this latitude. The Sunda trench is about 5000 m deep near 3°N, 92.5°E. Between the trench and Simeulue is the forearc basin and the outer arc ridge, which includes the Banyak and Simeulue islands. Simeulue is on the outer arc high, and west of it lies the Simeulue basin. At 95°E, which is west of Simeulue by about 1°, the seabed is probably on the outer arc slope or in the trench's accretionary wedge, with depths around 1500 m. I am estimating this from the regional geometry rather than from a known bathymetry value, so I'll go with about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

9238.text:
Latitude -13, longitude -63 is in Bolivia/Brazil border region (Rondônia/Beni/Santa Cruz). Longitude -63 at lat -13 is in the Bolivian Beni/Santa Cruz department near the Itenez (Guaporé) River, in lowland Amazon plains. The elevation there is around 150 m.

Guaporé river near -12.5, -64 has an elevation of about 140 m. At -13, -63 the area is Noel Kempff Mercado park's west side, with an elevation of about 170 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

8000.text:
The point is at 1°N, 19°W in the equatorial Atlantic, which is open ocean. It lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near 28-30°W at this latitude, and west of the Guinea basin. The area is near the Romanche and Chain fracture zones, which sit around 0° latitude and 15-25°W. The Chain fracture zone is at about 1°S and the Romanche at about 0°. The ridge-transform intersection is near 18°W, and the Romanche Fracture Zone's northern ridge crest lies about 0.5°N. Depths there are deep, around -4000 m, and the transverse ridge north of the Romanche rises to about -2000 m or shallower.

At 1°N, 19°W, the point is probably north of the Romanche trough on the edge of the Sierra Leone/Ceara region. I estimate a depth of about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

1522.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -15.0 lies in the Greenland Sea, off northeast Greenland. The coast there, near Kong Oscar Fjord and Scoresby Sund to the south, sits around -22 to -25 longitude at 73°N. Traveling east, the coastline at 73°N is near -20 to -22 (Traill Island is at about 72.5N, -23). So -15 is well offshore, on the East Greenland shelf edge or slope.

The shelf there extends roughly to -17 or -18 longitude at this latitude. Jan Mayen is at 71N, -8.5, which is far from this point. The Greenland Basin and the Jan Mayen Fracture Zone lie in this area, with depths of about 2500-3000 m. At -15, 73N, the point is likely on the continental slope or in the basin, with a depth of roughly 2000-3000 m. I'll estimate -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

6655.text:
The point is 17°N, 171°E, in the central Pacific. That places it northwest of the Marshall Islands, roughly 400 km west of Wake Island (19.3°N, 166.6°E) and east of the Marianas. The nearest Marshall atolls are Bikini and Enewetak, near 11°N, 165°E, and Wake is at 19°N, 167°E, so no land is close.

The region lies in the Pacific plate's deep basin, partly among seamounts. The Pigafetta Basin and Central Pacific Basin are typically 5000-6000 m deep, with seamounts rising from them. At 17°N, 171°E the seafloor is probably about 5500 m deep, though I can't confirm this against a bathymetry grid. I'll estimate -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

10960.text:
Latitude -31.0, longitude 141.0 is in far western New South Wales, Australia, near the South Australia border (141°E). It lies northeast of Broken Hill (141.47°E, -31.95°S), in the Barrier Ranges and plains area near Tarrawonga and Yancannia.

Terrain in this area is about 200 m. Further north near Cobham Lake (-31.2, 141.5), elevation is about 200 m, and the plains near Frome Creek and Lake Frome are lower. I'll estimate about 190 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 190}

14610.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -119 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. Pine Island Bay lies near -74.5, -102, and the Getz Ice Shelf is near -74, -125. At -73, -119 we are north of the Martin and Mount Siple area, probably on the continental shelf in the open Amundsen Sea. The coast there sits near 74°S, so -73 is probably ocean, though the Getz Ice Shelf front is around 74°S–75°S. It could be open water over the continental shelf, with a seabed depth of roughly -500 to -600 m.

The shelf there is deep, typically 500–700 m, so I'll estimate -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

10838.text:
The point at -31, -103 is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and roughly 3,000 km from the coast. It lies near the East Pacific Rise's eastern flank, in the Southeast Pacific Basin.

The East Pacific Rise crest is near 110°W at 30°S, so -103 is east of the rise. The crest is at about -2,800 m. Farther east the seafloor ages and deepens. The seafloor near 103°W at 31°S is roughly 15-25 Myr old, which gives a depth of about 3,500-3,900 m. The Easter microplate region is at 26°S, 110°W, which is nearby. The seafloor there is likely about 3,700 m deep, so I estimate -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

3456.text:
The point 51.0°N, 107.0°W is in southern Saskatchewan. It lies roughly 60 km south of Saskatoon, near Lake Diefenbaker. Lake Diefenbaker's main body runs around 51.0°N, from about -107.5° to -106.8°, but this is uncertain. Near the Gardiner Dam, the lake is at about 51.27°N, -106.87°W. The South Saskatchewan River arm runs west-southwest from there, passing about 50.9°N, -107.5°W. At -107.0°, the lake could be near 51.0°N, so the point may be on water.

Lake Diefenbaker's surface is about 556 m. The nearby plains sit at roughly 600 m. Near Elbow, at 51.13°N, -106.58°W, the lake is wide. Going west, the old Qu'Appelle valley and the South Saskatchewan valley are around 51.0°N, -107.0°W. The reservoir there is likely the valley arm, which is narrow. I can't confirm the point falls in the water, so I'll guess land on the valley slope or plateau, with an elevation of about 580 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":575}

6513.text:
The point 17.0°N, 113.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, southwest of Baja California and west of Mexico's mainland. It is roughly 500 km south of the Revillagigedo Islands (Socorro is at about 18.8°N, 111°W), which puts it west of the East Pacific Rise's northern extension and near the Clarión fracture zone area.

The seafloor there is abyssal, roughly 3,500–4,000 m deep. The Rivera plate and East Pacific Rise lie to the east at about 105–110°W, and seafloor at 113°W near 17°N is around 5–8 Myr old, which gives a depth near 3,600 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so this is an estimate from seafloor age and regional setting.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

7522.text:
Latitude 7.0, longitude 105.0 is in the South China Sea / Gulf of Thailand region. The Mekong Delta's southern tip (Ca Mau) sits near 8.6N, 105.0E, so 7.0N is about 180 km south of it, in open sea. The Gulf of Thailand mouth and the Vietnamese shelf are shallow here, with depths of roughly 50 m at this location.

The shelf between the Malay Peninsula and Vietnam is around 50-70 m deep, and I estimate about -55 m at this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-55}

1260.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -179.0 places the point in the Arctic Ocean, in the Chukchi Sea / Chukchi Borderland region. Wrangel Island sits near 71°N, 179°W–180°, so it is well to the south. At 75°N, 179°W, the seafloor is most likely the Chukchi Plateau or Borderland, with depths of roughly 500–2000 m. The Chukchi Plateau lies around 75–80°N and 165–175°W, with depths of about 400–800 m. The Chukchi Abyssal Plain lies at 75°N and 175°E–180°, at about 2000–2500 m depth. The Northwind Ridge extends from about 74°N at 160°W northward.

At 75°N, 179°W the point is west of the Chukchi Borderland and north of the Chukchi Sea shelf, which ends at around 72°N. That puts it on the continental slope, or on the Mendeleev Plain / Chukchi Abyssal Plain edge. I'd estimate a depth of about 1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

15195.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -29 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, at the Filchner-Ronne area. The Filchner Ice Shelf lies roughly between 30°W and 40°W, and the Ronne Ice Shelf is farther west. Near 79°S, 29°W the location is probably east of the Filchner Ice Shelf front, which is around 78°S, 35-40°W. The Slessor Glacier and Recovery Glacier lie east of that, near 80°S, 25-30°W. The Slessor Glacier is at about 79-80°S, 28°W, and it is grounded ice, but its lower part may be floating.

Coats Land and the Theron Mountains are near 80°S, 28°W. The Theron Mountains sit at about 79.5°S, 28°W. So 79°S, 29°W could be on the Slessor Glacier ice stream, whose surface elevation is around 500-800 m. Bedrock beneath Slessor is below sea level, but grounded ice counts as Solid, so I report the ice surface elevation. I estimate about 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

2996.text:
Latitude 57.0°N, longitude 53.0°E falls in Perm Krai, Russia, near the Kama River region. The Kama flows around 56-58°N, 52-56°E. Near 57°N 53°E the area is around Chaikovsky (56.77°N, 54.15°E) and Votkinsk (57.05°N, 53.99°E). At 53.0°E, 57°N the point is roughly in Udmurtia or Perm Krai near Kez or Verkhnekamsk uplands. The Kama reservoir lies to the east, so 53°E is probably not in the water. The terrain is rolling upland with elevations of about 150 m.

The Cheptsa River is nearby, in Udmurtia/Kirov region. Elevation is about 140 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

12134.text:
Latitude -45, longitude -31 is in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and northeast of South Georgia. The ridge axis near 45°S sits around 14–16°W, so this point is on the western flank, in the Argentine Basin's eastern part or the southern edge of the Rio Grande Rise region. Seafloor depths there are probably around 3500–4000 m, and the site is likely in the deep basin west of the ridge.

I'm estimating about -3900 m from general knowledge of the region rather than from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4873.text:
The point (35°N, 153°W) lies in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and west of California. This area is abyssal plain, with typical depths around 5,000 to 5,500 m.

The Murray Fracture Zone runs near 30–35°N in this region. The seafloor age here is roughly 80 million years. Using the age-depth relation, depth is about 2500 + 350·√80 ≈ 5,600 m. Sediment loading and the actual regional bathymetry suggest about 5,300 m, so I'll estimate −5,350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5350}

9275.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 11 is in the Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Angola. Angola's coast near Benguela (-12.6, 13.4) is at about 13.4°E. At 11°E the point is roughly 250 km offshore, so it is water.

The continental shelf off Angola is narrow, about 50 km wide. Beyond it the slope drops to the Angola Basin, where depths are around 4500-5000 m. At 250 km offshore the depth is probably about 3500-4500 m. The Walvis Ridge lies further south, near -18 to -20, so it does not affect this point. I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

10892.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 5 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of South Africa (the coast is near 18°E at this latitude). It is roughly 1,300 km from the coast.

The location is east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and within the Cape Basin or the Cape Rise region. The Cape Basin has depths of about 5000 m, and the Walvis Ridge sits further north at about -20° to -30°. At 31°S, 5°E the seafloor is probably abyssal plain at roughly 4500-5000 m depth. Further west, the ridge at 31°S is around 13°W, so 5°E is on the eastern flank, where depths of about 4800 m are typical. I'll estimate -4850 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4850}

4171.text:
The point 43.0°N, 117.0°W lies in southwestern Idaho or eastern Oregon. The Idaho–Oregon border is at about 117.03°W, so this is just inside Idaho, in Owyhee County. It is north of the Owyhee River, near the Succor Creek area; Jordan Valley, Oregon is at 43.0°N, 117.05°W, with an elevation of about 1330 m.

Just east of the border, the terrain around Triangle and the Owyhee uplands is probably at about 1400 m. Succor Creek is at about 117.0°W, 43.2°N. At 43.0°N, 117.0°W, the point is likely on the plateau near the Idaho side of the Oregon border, at around 1350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1350}

2483.text:
The point is 63°N, 107°E, in central Siberia, Russia. That area lies in the Sakha Republic and Krasnoyarsk Krai region, on the Central Siberian Plateau, probably near the Markha or Vilyuy river basins. The Vilyuy basin is at about 63N, 112E. At 107E, 63N the area is near the Tunguska plateau, just west of the Vilyuy upper reaches, and elevation there is likely around 400-500 m.

I'm estimating about 450 m and treating the surface as land rather than water.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

2158.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude 177.0 E lies in Chukotka, Russia. Wrangel Island is at about 71 N, so it is not relevant here. The Chukchi Sea coast is near 67-68 N at 177 E, and the Gulf of Anadyr is farther south at about 64 N. At 67 N, 177 E, the point is inland in the Chukotka highlands, probably near the Chaun/Amguema region. The Amguema River is at about 67.5 N, 178 E, and the Anyuy mountains lie west of it. The terrain there is mountainous, with elevations of roughly 300-600 m.

I estimate about 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

11603.text:
The point at latitude -39, longitude -13 is in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies west of Tristan da Cunha (about -37.1, -12.3) and south of it, near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs roughly along 13°W at this latitude. Gough Island is at -40.3, -9.9, so this point is not on it.

The ridge crest near -39 is at about 13–14°W, and the Tristan hotspot area has shallower seafloor. The depth there is probably around 2500–3500 m. The Tristan-Gough region around -39 sits on the ridge flank with depths near 3000 m. I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

3398.text:
Latitude 53.0, longitude 137.0 E lies in the Sea of Okhotsk region of Russia. The Khabarovsk Krai coast near Ayan is at about 56°N, 138°E. The Shantar Islands are around 55°N, 137-138°E. At 53°N, 137°E, the coast runs near 54°N, 137°E, so this point is south of the coast and in the sea. The Okhotsk coast near Nikolayevsk-on-Amur sits at about 53.1°N, 140.7°E, and the Tatar Strait lies at 141°E. At 137°E and 53°N we are on the northern edge of the Sea of Okhotsk, west of Sakhalin, where Sakhalin Bay and the Amur estuary are at 54°N, 141°E. The coast of Okhotsk (Udskaya Bay) is around 54.5°N, 135-137°E. So 53°N, 137°E is in the Sea of Okhotsk, with the Sakhalin northern part at 142°E. The mainland coast at 137°E is around 54°N, so the point is about 1° south, in the sea. Depth is probably shallow, about 100 to 200 m. The shelf off Shantar likely sits at about 100 m, but deeper water is to the east. Pick -150.

Wait, the mainland coast: Udskaya Bay is at about 54.3°N, 135.5°E. The coast then trends southeast toward Nikolayevsk at 140.7°E, 53.1°N. At 137°E the coast would be around 54°N. Following that, 53°N, 137°E could be land, since the Okhotsk coast on that stretch is the Tugur peninsula and Tugur Bay (53.8°N, 136.8°E). Tugur Bay is at about 53.8°N, so 53°N, 137°E is south of it, which suggests land in the Badzhal/Selemdzhinsk uplands, perhaps hills at about 500 m. The Amgun River lies near 52.5°N, 137.5°E. The Tugur coast is at about 53.7°N, so this point is inland, with the Ulya and Tugur ridges around 400-800 m. I'll say 600.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

13913.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -73 lies on the Antarctic Peninsula's western side. Adelaide Island is near -67.5, -68.5, and the Biscoe Islands are near -65.4, -65.5. At -65, -73 we are about 400 km west of the Peninsula coast, in the Bellingshausen Sea / Pacific sector beyond the continental shelf edge. Shelf break there is around -70 longitude at that latitude, so the point is probably on the continental slope or rise, with depth around 3000-4000 m.

South of the Peninsula, the Pacific margin has a trench-like feature, but the Antarctic Peninsula margin is near -66 -70. At -65, -73 the depth is likely about -3500 m. The Peninsula's western margin has a slope reaching abyssal depths of about 4000 m, and the trench is shallow and filled with sediment. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

4999.text:
Latitude 35.0°N, longitude 99.0°E lies in Qinghai province, China. It is near the Qinghai Lake–Gonghe Basin area to the north-east, or the Madoi/Golog area to the south-west.

Qinghai Lake is at about 36.9°N, 100.2°E, so this point is not on the lake. At 35.0°N, 99.0°E the location is south of Gonghe, in the Golog/Hainan prefecture area. The Yellow River runs nearby: Xingsuhai is at about 35.3°N, 99.9°E, and Madoi is at about 34.9°N, 98.2°E. The Ngoring and Gyaring lakes are at about 97.5–98.5°E, so this point is east of them. The terrain is high plateau at about 4200–4400 m, so I estimate 4300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":4300}

15317.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -145 is in the Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 78°S to 85°S and from 160°W westward to the Transantarctic Mountains. At 145°W, 81°S the point is in the eastern Ross Ice Shelf, near the Marie Byrd Land coast and Edward VII Peninsula, which is at about 155°W. I take 145°W, 81°S to be still on the ice shelf, since the shelf's eastern edge runs along the Siple Coast and Marie Byrd Land at about 150-140°W, 78-80°S. That puts this point on the floating shelf, so the type is Water and I need the seabed depth.

The seabed under the eastern Ross Ice Shelf is about 500-700 m deep. Bedmap2 gives roughly -600 m for this area, and I settle on about -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

12762.text:
The point is at 51°S, 145°E, in the Southern Ocean southwest of Macquarie Island and south of Tasmania. Tasmania's southern tip is near 43.6°S, so the point is about 7.4° (roughly 800 km) south of it. Macquarie Island is at about 54.6°S, 158.9°E, which is far to the east.

This region lies south of Tasmania on the South Tasman Rise, which extends to about 48°S and has depths of roughly 1000–2000 m. At 51°S the seafloor drops into deeper water, so the depth is probably around 3000–4000 m. East of the point, the Macquarie Ridge sits near 159°E. The Australian-Antarctic Basin lies farther south, at 4000 m or more. For 51°S, 145°E, I estimate about 3500 m depth. This is a judgment from regional bathymetry rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

12971.text:
The point is at 55°S, 157°W, in the South Pacific Ocean, southeast of New Zealand and south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies near 55–65°S at around 150°W, and at 157°W it may sit near 56–60°S. In that area, the seafloor away from the ridge is typically 3,000–4,000 m deep.

The ridge crest in this region is around 2,500–3,000 m deep. At 55°S, 157°W the point is probably on the northern flank. The Udintsev fracture zone is near 55–57°S, 145°W, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge reaches 55°S near 140°W, so 157°W is west of that and the seafloor there is likely 4,000 m or deeper. I estimate roughly -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

13268.text:
Latitude -57, longitude 77 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Kerguelen (about -49, 69) and southwest of Heard Island (-53, 73.5). It is south of the Kerguelen Plateau's main part, in the area around the Princess Elizabeth Trough or the southern Kerguelen Plateau. The southern Kerguelen Plateau extends from about -55 to -64 at longitudes 75-85, with depths of roughly 1000-3000 m. At -57, 77 the plateau is probably around 2000-3000 m deep. Between the plateau and Antarctica, the Princess Elizabeth Trough reaches about 3500-4000 m, but that is further south at about -62.

I'll estimate -2500 m, though I am not certain of the exact depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

1408.text:
Latitude 75.0N, longitude 117.0E is in the Laptev Sea region, north of the Taymyr Peninsula and the Khatanga Gulf. The Laptev Sea shelf is at roughly 74-76N, 110-130E. The Taymyr coast sits near 76N at 105-112E, and the Khatanga Gulf mouth is near 74N, 110E. The Anabar Bay is near 73.5N, 113E, and the Olenyok Bay is near 73N, 120E. The New Siberian Islands lie farther east. The Lena Delta is at about 73N, 125E.

So 75N, 117E is open Laptev Sea shelf, with a depth of about 30-50 m. At 75N the shelf is around 40-50 m deep, and the shelf edge is near 78N. I'll estimate -40 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

13361.text:
The point is at 59°S, 97°W, in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Drake Passage. It lies south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region and north of the Antarctic continental margin.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about 65°S 175°W up to about 55°S 140°W, then toward the Chile Rise near 45°S 80°W. At 97°W and 59°S the seafloor is likely the Bellingshausen Basin or the abyssal plain, with a depth around 4000-4500 m. I estimate this from general bathymetry rather than from a specific lookup. Near 60°S 95°W the depth is about -4,000 m, and the area is probably east of the ridge crest, in the older and deeper seafloor of the southeast Pacific basin. I estimate -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

6589.text:
Latitude 17.0°N, longitude 39.0°E lies in the Red Sea, off the coast of Eritrea. The Eritrean coast near Massawa is at about 15.6°N, 39.5°E. At 17°N the coast sits near 38.3°E, since the shoreline trends northwest. So 39.0°E is offshore, roughly 70 km from the coast.

The Dahlak shelf extends along this stretch, but at 17°N it is narrow. The Red Sea axial trough lies around 38–39°E at this latitude, so depth here is probably several hundred meters to over 1000 m. The main trough at 17°N runs about 2000 m deep, and the point may sit on its eastern side. The Eritrean coast at 17°N is near 38.9°E, though, which could put the point near the coast instead. I think the coast at 17°N is around 38.5°E (Eritrea's northern coast near Ras Kasar is at 18.0°N, 38.6°E), so 39.0°E is offshore, east of the coast. The Dahlak Archipelago is at 15.7°N. Offshore at 17°N, 39°E I estimate a depth of about −600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

14039.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 179 E is in the Southern Ocean, off the Antarctic coast. The Balleny Islands lie around 66.9°S, 163°E, far to the west, and Victoria Land and the Ross Sea are further west still. At 179°E, 65°S the area sits over the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region, north of the Antarctic continental shelf, which is at about 70°S or farther south. The seafloor there is abyssal at roughly 3,000 to 4,000 m depth.

The Southeast Indian Ridge and Pacific-Antarctic Ridge junction is near 62°S, 160°E. At 65°S, 179°E the seafloor is probably the continental rise or abyssal plain south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, so depth is about -3,000 to -3,500 m. I'll estimate -3,100.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

13495.text:
The point is at 59°S, 171°E, in the Southern Ocean south of New Zealand. It lies southeast of the Macquarie Ridge and south of the Campbell Plateau, in the Pacific-Antarctic region.

The Macquarie Island ridge runs near 159°E, so this point is well east of it. The Emerald Basin and the Southeast Indian Ridge are also nearby. At 59°S, 171°E, the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies farther east, near 180°E and 65°S. The Campbell Plateau extends to about 53°S, so this point is south of it, in deep ocean of roughly 4,000–5,000 m. Seafloor to the south, toward the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, is shallower at about 3,000–4,000 m, and the Southeast Indian Ridge also runs southeast of New Zealand. I estimate about 4,800 m depth for this location, though this is an estimate from regional knowledge and not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

3908.text:
Latitude 47.0 N, longitude 77.0 E is in Kazakhstan. Lake Balkhash spans roughly 73.5–79 E at about 46 N, and its northern shore runs around 46.5 N. At 77 E the lake is narrow, in the strait near Uzynaral at about 74.5 E, so the lake there is on the western side. The eastern lake is at 78–79 E. At 77 E and 47 N, the lake's north shore sits around 46.8 N, and the Bertys bay is near 46.6 N and 75 E. I'm not certain of that, but I think the point is near the northern shore of the lake, possibly on land.

Lake Balkhash's western part runs from 73.5 to about 75 E, with the Saryesik Peninsula at about 74.5 E. The eastern part spans about 75 to 79 E, with its north shore at about 46.5 N near 77 E. So 47.0 N is probably on land, north of the lake. The terrain is steppe/desert at roughly 400 m. The lake surface is at about 342 m, so land just north should be about 350–400 m. I estimate 370.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":370}

14325.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 31 is in East Antarctica, in Enderby Land / Queen Maud Land, a bit inland from the coast. The coast near 31°E lies around -69.5 to -70, close to the Lazarev Sea and the Riiser-Larsen area. At 31°E the coast is near 69.7°S, so -69.0 could be just inland, on the ice sheet edge, or at the coast.

The Prince Harald Coast is near 35°E, with the Lützow-Holm Bay area at 39°E. At 31°E and 69°S, the Sør Rondane Mountains are at about 23°E, so that is not it. The ice sheet here probably sits at roughly 1500-2500 m. Coastal ice shelves such as the Riiser-Larsen shelf lie at 16-34°W, so that does not apply. Around 31°E the coast is probably near 69.5°S, with the ice sheet slope steep. At 69.0 that is about 50 km inland, so the ice surface might be near 1500 m. I'll guess 1700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1700}

1906.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 33.0°E lies on the Kola Peninsula in Russia, near the Barents Sea coast. The coast sits around 69.2–69.4°N at this longitude, so the point is probably inland. Specifically, the Murmansk region near Pechenga is at about 69.5°N, 31°E. Kola Bay is at about 33.0°E, running from 69.0°N north to Murmansk at 68.97°N, 33.07°E. Murmansk itself is at 68.97°N, 33.08°E, and Kola Bay's eastern shore is near 33.1°E.

At 69.0°N, 33.0°E the point is just north of Murmansk, on the western side of Kola Bay. The bay is narrow, about 2 km wide, near Murmansk and extends north. At 69.0°N the bay is at about 33.05°E, so 33.0°E may be land on the west shore, with hills about 150 m high. I'll classify it as Solid with an elevation of about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

5734.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude 129.0 lies in the Pacific/East China Sea region near the Ryukyu Islands. Okinawa is at about 26.5°N, 128°E, and Kume Island is at 26.35°N, 126.8°E. Kitadaito is at 25.9°N, 131.3°E. Point 27.0°N, 129.0°E is east of Amami and Okinawa, and just north of Okinawa's northern tip, which is near 26.87°N, 128.25°E. Amami Oshima is at 28.3°N, 129.4°E and Tokunoshima is at 27.8°N, 128.9°E. So 27.0°N, 129.0°E is south of Tokunoshima and southeast of Okinawa, putting it in the Philippine Sea on the eastern side of the Ryukyu arc.

The Ryukyu Trench lies further east, and the area just east of the arc is the forearc slope. The Amami Plateau is northeast of here. The forearc basin and slope at about 129°E, 27°N probably sit at a depth of roughly 2000 m. The East China Sea side, the Okinawa Trough, is west of the arc at around 127–128°E. Because 129°E is east of the arc, the depth is likely 1500–3000 m. I'll estimate -2200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2200}

10066.text:
The point (-21.0, 153.0) lies in the Coral Sea, east of Queensland. It is about 250 km east of Fraser/Capricorn region's latitude, which is around -23, so this is further north. The area east of Mackay, near the Marion Plateau, sits at roughly -21 latitude and 152 to 153 longitude. Further east lies the Coral Sea basin, with the Cato Trough and Mellish Rise to the northeast, and the Capricorn Basin to the south.

The Marion Plateau extends to about 152.5°E at depths of 300 to 1000 m. At 153°E the seafloor is probably on the slope into the Cato Trough or Townsville Trough, at roughly 1500 to 2500 m depth. I'll estimate about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

15959.text:
Latitude -87, longitude 59 is in East Antarctica, near the South Pole, on the polar plateau. The South Pole itself sits at about 2835 m of ice surface elevation. At 87°S, along longitude 59°E, the surface is at roughly 3,000 m or higher. This area lies in the direction of the Dome A / Pole of Inaccessibility side, since 0° to 90°E is the East Antarctic plateau. Moving from the pole at 90°E toward Dome A (80.4°S, 77°E), elevation rises to 4000 m. At 87°S, 59°E the elevation is probably around 3,200 m.

Because the ice is grounded, the surface type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3200}

3034.text:
The coordinates 57.0°N, 129.0°E fall in the Russian Far East, in the Aldan highlands / Dzhugdzhur area near the border of Yakutia and Amur/Khabarovsk. This is likely the southern Aldan Highlands or the Stanovoy range's northern slopes, where I'd expect elevations of roughly 800–1200 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup.

Rivers like the Maya and Uchur flow in the area, and the Dzhugdzhur range lies to the east at about 134°E. At 129°E, 57°N, the terrain is probably the Uchur-Maya plateau, at about 900 m.


{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

11264.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 29 is in the Indian Ocean southeast of South Africa. The coast near East London is at about 27.9E, 33S, so this point is well offshore, roughly 400 km southeast of the coast.

The seabed there is probably the Natal Valley or the Agulhas Plateau/Transkei Basin area. The Transkei Basin lies around 35S, 30-35E, with depths of about 4500-5000 m. At 29E, 35S the point is south of the continental slope. The Agulhas Plateau sits further southwest, at about 26E, 40S. The Agulhas Passage lies near 35S, 26E. The Mozambique Basin is to the east. At 35S, 29E the depth is probably around 4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

3284.text:
The point 53.0N, 91.0W is in northern Ontario, in the Hudson Bay Lowlands. It lies west of the Attawapiskat River headwaters and south of the Severn River, near the Winisk River basin. The region is mostly flat muskeg and peatland.

Elevation in this area is roughly 150-200 m. Lake St. Joseph sits at about 53.0N, 90.7W at about 335 m, which is higher than this point. Further north, Big Trout Lake at 53.8N, 90.0W is about 220 m. At 53N, 91W the ground is probably around 200-230 m, though the Hudson Bay Lowlands start near 52N, 86W, which is farther east. I'll estimate 220 m and classify it as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":225}

1200.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 61.0°E is in the Kara Sea region. Novaya Zemlya spans roughly 70°N to 77°N, with its northern tip (Cape Zhelaniya) near 76.95°N, 68.5°E. At 61°E and 77°N we are west of the northern island, in the Barents Sea/Kara Sea strait region. Novaya Zemlya's northern island at around 77°N spans from about 60°E to 68°E. Its western coast near 76°N is about 62°E, and near 77°N the coast is around 63°E. Hmm, at 77°N the northern island is narrow, with the western coast near Severny Island at about 65°E at 76.5°N.

The Barents Sea side to the west of Novaya Zemlya has depths of about 200 m. Franz Josef Land lies farther north, so 77°N, 61°E is probably open water in the Barents Sea off the west coast of Novaya Zemlya. The Novaya Zemlya trench runs along the coast with depths up to 300+ m. Near 61°E the depth is likely around 200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-210}

13490.text:
The point -59, 161 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Macquarie Island (about -54.5, 159) and north of the Antarctic coast. It sits near the Macquarie Ridge and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The Australian-Antarctic basin and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge are nearby. The Macquarie Ridge extends south-southwest from Macquarie Island to roughly -59, 159. The Balleny Islands are around -66, 163.

Near -59, 161 the seabed probably belongs to the Southeast Indian Ridge / Pacific-Antarctic Ridge transition, or to the Macquarie Ridge's southern end, with a typical depth of about 3000-4000 m. I estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5552.text:
Latitude 29.0°N, longitude 125.0°E lies in the East China Sea, southwest of Kyushu and northeast of Taiwan. It is east of the Chinese coast (about 122°E at this latitude), and the continental shelf edge sits near 127°E at this latitude, where the Okinawa Trough begins.

At 125°E, 29°N the water depth is probably around 100 to 200 m. The shelf break is at about 200 m depth and the Okinawa Trough axis runs roughly through 127°E, 28°N. The 100 m isobath is near 125.5°E at 29°N, and the shelf is about 100 m deep around 125°E. The trough's western slope begins around 126°E. I estimate about -110 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

13089.text:
Location: 55°S, 79°E is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Kerguelen Plateau (which is around 49°S, 69°E, extending to ~60°S near 78-80°E?). The Kerguelen Plateau's southern part extends to about 59°S at around 80°E, with Princess Elizabeth Trough further south. At 55°S, 79°E, we are likely on the southern Kerguelen Plateau, east side, with depths around 2000-3000 m. Southern Kerguelen Plateau's depth is typically 1000-2000 m. The Raggatt Basin is around 55-58°S, 80-82°E, with depths ~2500 m. The Banzare Bank is at 60°S, 77°E. Elan Bank at 59°S, 76°E. At 55°S 79°E, I'd estimate about -2800 m (the eastern flank of the plateau descending to the Australian-Antarctic Basin to the east, or the Raggatt Basin).

I'll say -2300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

934.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude -111.0 W is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Melville Island spans roughly 74.5–76.5 N, so it lies to the south. Prince Patrick Island is around 76.5N, 119W. Borden Island is at about 78.5N, 111W, and Mackenzie King Island at about 77.5N, 111W. Borden Island spans roughly 78.2–78.8N, so 79.0N is just north of it. Brock Island is at about 77.9N, 114.5W. The point is likely in the strait north of Borden Island, in the Arctic Ocean margin. Meighen Island is at 80N, 99W, so not that. Lougheed Island is at 77.4N, 105W. So the point is probably water.

The seabed there is in the shallow channel region between the islands and the Arctic Ocean shelf, with depths of perhaps 200-300 m. The continental shelf edge lies north of Borden Island, around 79-80N. I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

6403.text:
The point 19°N, 27°E lies in the Sahara in northeastern Chad/southwestern Libya, near the Egypt–Libya–Sudan border region. It is in the Libyan Desert, close to the Sudan/Libya border, and sits roughly north of the Ennedi and east of Tibesti. The nearby terrain is a flat sandy plain, probably around 500 m. The Kufra basin lies around 24°N, 23°E at about 400 m. To the south, Uweinat (22°N, 25°E) is much higher. At 19°N, 27°E, the northern Sudan desert plateau is about 550 m.

I'll estimate 560 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":560}

7047.text:
The point (11°N, 125°W) lies in the open Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water. It sits northeast of the Clipperton Fracture Zone and west of the East Pacific Rise, on abyssal plain of roughly 50 Ma crust.

The seabed there is typically about 4,500 to 4,000 m deep. Crust of that age usually sits near 4,500 to 5,000 m depth, and in this region the Pacific abyssal depth is commonly around 4,600 m. I am estimating this from regional knowledge rather than a lookup, so I settled on about -4,650 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4650}

8288.text:
The point is at 3°S, 163°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of the equator, east of the Phoenix Islands and west of the Line Islands. Nearby land is Kiribati's Line Islands, with Caroline Island far to the west at about 10°S, 150°W, so there is no land at this location. It is open ocean.

Seafloor depth in this region is about 5000 to 5500 m. The area sits in the central Pacific basin, north of the Tokelau region. Depths near the equator at 163°W are roughly 5200 m, so I estimate -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

14527.text:
Location: -71, 75E in East Antarctica. This is near the Lambert Glacier / Amery Ice Shelf region. The Amery Ice Shelf front is around 68.5S, 72E, and the shelf extends south to about 72S at 70E. At 75E, 71S is likely on grounded ice, east of the Lambert Glacier, near Mawson Escarpment / Prince Charles Mountains region. The Prince Charles Mountains lie around 70-73S, 62-68E. At 75E, 71S is on the inland ice sheet east of the Amery Ice Shelf, probably near Law Dome? No, that is at 112E. Surface elevation there is probably around 1500-2000 m.

The Amery Ice Shelf's eastern edge is about 74-75E at 69-70S, and grounding lines on the east side are near 71S. Ice flow from the east is relatively low, with elevations around 1000-2000 m. I'll estimate 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

5482.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude -15.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, just east of the Canary Islands. Lanzarote is at about 29.0N, -13.6 and Fuerteventura at about 28.4N, -14.0. Gran Canaria is at 28.0N, -15.6. So the point at -15.0 and 29.0 is northwest of Fuerteventura and southwest of Lanzarote, in open ocean between Gran Canaria and Africa.

The depth between the islands and Africa is about 1000 m near Lanzarote and Fuerteventura. West of Fuerteventura the seabed drops to roughly 2000 to 3000 m. At 29N, -15, which is about 100 km west of Lanzarote, the depth is probably around 2500 m, perhaps a little more. I'll estimate -2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

5540.text:
The coordinates 29.0°N, 101.0°E fall in western Sichuan, near the Ganzi/Yajiang/Jiulong area on the eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau. Jiulong County sits at about 29.0°N, 101.5°E, and Yajiang at 30.0°N, 101.0°E. At 29.0°N, 101.0°E the point is probably in the Muli or Jiulong area, close to the Yalong River, which runs near 101.2°E at this latitude. The terrain is deeply dissected, with valleys around 2500–3000 m and ridges above 4000 m.

I can't pin down the exact local terrain, so I estimate about 3800 m, which is a typical value for the ridges and slopes in this region.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3800}

357.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 175°E lies in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole. This is on the Lomonosov Ridge side, which runs from the Siberian shelf past the pole toward Greenland. The ridge crosses near 87°N at about 140°E–150°E on one side and about 60°W on the other. The longitude 175°E is on the Amerasian side of the pole, over the Makarov Basin, which lies adjacent to the Lomonosov Ridge. The Makarov Basin is about 4000 m deep.

At 87°N the Lomonosov Ridge is near 130–140°E, with crest depths around 1000–1500 m. The Makarov Basin lies at about 170°E–180° at this latitude, with depths of about 3900–4000 m. I estimate roughly -3950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

1538.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 17.0°E lies in the Barents Sea, north of Norway and southwest of Bear Island (Bjørnøya, at about 74.4°N, 19°E). Bjørnøya is therefore about 1.4° north, so this point is in open water.

Bathymetry here is the Bear Island Trough (Bjørnøyrenna) region, which runs east–west at roughly 73°N. West of Bear Island the trough opens toward the shelf edge at about 15°E. The Norwegian continental slope near 73°N sits around 10–13°E, so at 17°E we are on the shelf in the trough, where depths are about 400–450 m. I estimate about -420 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-420}

9712.text:
Latitude -17, longitude 165 lies in the Coral Sea/Vanuatu region. Vanuatu spans roughly 166-170E, so 165E is west of the islands. Espiritu Santo is at about 167E, so this point is in the basin west of Vanuatu, the New Hebrides Basin or the d'Entrecasteaux zone. The Coral Sea basin there is about 3500-4500 m deep. Just west of the New Hebrides arc at 165E, I'd estimate the depth at roughly 3000 m, since the Santo trough sits around 166E.

At 17S the Vanuatu west-side backarc has the North Loyalty Basin at about 165E, with depths near 3500 m. The Loyalty Ridge runs around 167E at 21S, but at 17S it is not clear. I'll go with -3300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

1561.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude 63.0 E is in the Kara Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya. The southern tip of Novaya Zemlya is around 70.5N, 58E, and the northern island reaches about 77N, 68E. At 73N, the island's east coast sits near 55-58E for the southern island, but the northern island begins around 73N, with Matochkin Strait at about 73.3N, 56E. The coast east of there is around 60E at 74N, so 63E at 73N is probably in the Kara Sea.

The Kara Sea there is shallow, typically 50-100 m. Near Novaya Zemlya's east coast there is a trough, the Novaya Zemlya Trough, which is deeper at 200-400 m, though it lies closer to the coast, around 58-60E at 73N. At 63E the depth is probably about 100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

2816.text:
Latitude 59.0 N, longitude 53.0 E is in Perm Krai, Russia, west of the Urals. It lies near the Kama River basin, around Kudymkar or Komi-Permyak territory. Kudymkar is at about 59.0 N, 54.65 E, so this point is roughly 100 km west of it. The area is hilly upland of the Verkhnekamsk Upland, with typical elevations of 150-250 m.

The Kama River near Solikamsk is at 56 E, so it is not at this point. The Kosa and Veslyana rivers are nearby. I estimate the elevation at about 180 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":180}

8203.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 27.0 is in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, in the Maniema/South Kivu area west of Lake Kivu. Lake Kivu lies at about 29°E, so this point is well inland.

The terrain there is forested hills at roughly 900-1100 m. Kalehe and Shabunda are at about 2°S, 27°E, and Walikale is at about -1.4°S, 28°E. Near -1°S, 27°E the point is in the Kivu-Maniema lowlands, probably around 800 m. I'll estimate 850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":850}

6019.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude -21.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, west of the Sahara coast (the Mauritania coast is near -17°). It is about 400 km offshore, in the deep Atlantic.

The location lies south of the Canary Islands and west of Cape Blanc, in the Canary Basin or Cape Verde abyssal plain. Depths in this area are about 4,500 to 5,000 m. At 23°N, 21°W the seafloor is most likely around -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

11421.text:
The point (-37, -17) is in the South Atlantic, west of Tristan da Cunha (about -37.1, -12.3) and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 37°S sits around 14–15°W, so -17 lies on the western flank of the ridge. Seafloor depth there is roughly 3,500–4,000 m, and the Rio Grande Rise is further west, so it does not affect this point.

I estimate a depth of about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

621.text:
Location: 83.0°N, 17.0°W. This is north of Greenland's northeast coast. Peary Land's northern coast is around 83.6°N at about 30°W, and Nord is at 81.6°N, 16.6°W. At 17°W, Greenland's northeast coast (Kronprins Christian Land and Amdrup Land) lies near 81-82°N. So 83°N, 17°W is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Greenland, off the shelf off Lambert Land / Hovgaard Ø region.

The shelf north of Greenland at 83°N, 17°W is probably beyond the shelf edge. The Greenland shelf in the northeast extends to about 81.5-82°N at the Belgica Bank, and the Fram Strait continental slope lies near 82.5°N. At 83°N, 17°W, the depth may be around 1000 to 2000 m. The Lomonosov Ridge is far to the north, and the Greenland margin slope goes down to the Lena Trough and Molloy area. Nearby is the Westwind Plateau or Morris Jesup Rise, which lies at roughly 85°N, 10°W with depths of about 1000 m. At 83°N, 17°W, I estimate a depth of about 400-1000 m, so I'll say -800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

8729.text:
The point (-7.0, -1.0) lies in the South Atlantic, southeast of Ascension Island (about -7.95, -14.4) and well east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It is in the Gulf of Guinea region, west of Africa, in open ocean.

I'm estimating depth from memory of the regional bathymetry, not from a lookup. The Guinea Basin and the Angola Basin here are typically 4,500 to 5,500 m deep. The point at 7°S, 1°W sits south of the Gulf of Guinea, east of the ridge, where the ridge flank is around 3,500 to 4,500 m. The ridge axis near 7°S is at roughly 13°W. At 1°W the seafloor is about 12° east of the axis, which is around 25 Myr old crust or older, so depth is roughly 4,500 m, and I'll estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1915.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude 51.0 (east, positive) lies in northwestern Russia, in the Barents Sea region. The Kanin Peninsula sits near 68° N, 45° E, and the Kola Peninsula's eastern tip is near 41° E. At 51° E, 69° N, the point is in the Barents Sea (Pechora Sea) north of the Russian coast. The coast near Naryan-Mar is at about 68° N, 53° E, and the Kolguev island is at 69.2° N, 49° E. Kolguev spans roughly 48–50° E, so 51° E is just east of it. The island's eastern edge is about 49.5° E, so the point is likely water in the Barents Sea southeast of Kolguev.

Depth in that area is shallow, about 50–100 m. I estimate about -60 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

8899.text:
Latitude -9, longitude -21 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 9°S is at roughly 13°W, so -21 lies on the western flank. The Brazil Basin and Ceará Rise lie further west. The seafloor age there is roughly 40-50 Myr, with depths around 4500-5000 m.

Near 9°S, 21°W the ridge flank is probably about 4,500 m deep. The ridge crest at 13°W is about 2,500 m deep, and 8° of longitude is roughly 880 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 35 mm/yr, that is about 25 Myr of crust, giving a depth of about 2500 + 350·√25 ≈ 4250 m. I'll estimate about -4400 m, allowing for some local variation.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

2215.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -69.0 is in Baffin Island, Canada (Cumberland Peninsula is around 66-67N, -65 to -68). At 65N, -69W, the point is on the Cumberland Sound/Hall Peninsula area. Cumberland Sound spans roughly 64-66N, -62 to -68W. Pangnirtung is at 66.1N, -65.7W. The head of Cumberland Sound sits around 66N, -67W.

At 65N, -69W, that is west of Cumberland Sound, on the Hall Peninsula/Meta Incognita? Hall Peninsula lies at about 63-64N. The area between 64.5-65.5N and -68 to -70W is the interior of Baffin Island south of the Penny Ice Cap, in the Nettilling Lake lowlands area (Nettilling Lake is around 66.5N, -70.5W). So 65N, -69W is probably interior highland, with elevation of around 400 m. The Cumberland Sound west coast is at about -67.5W at 65N, so this is inland. Elevation is about 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":500}

3572.text:
Latitude 51.0°N, longitude 125.0°E lies in the Amur Oblast region of Russia, near the Zeya–Bureya plain and the Selemdzha River area. Longitude 125°E at latitude 51°N is roughly 100 km north-west of Svobodny. The Zeya River is around 127°E at this latitude, so this point is further west, in the Amur Oblast upland near the Selemdzha and Gilyuy areas, probably in the Tynda region foothills.

I'm estimating elevation at about 400 m for this area, with most of the surrounding terrain at 300–600 m. The point is almost certainly land rather than water.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":420}

6410.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 41.0°E is in Saudi Arabia, in the southwestern part of the country. The Red Sea coast is at about 39°E at this latitude, so this point is inland. It lies near the Al Baha/Asir region, east of the escarpment, probably around Bishah or Al Qunfudhah's hinterland. Al Qunfudhah is at 19.1°N, 41.08°E, which is actually on the coast according to my recollection of its coordinates, but I think I have that wrong. Al Qunfudhah is at 19.13°N, 41.08°E, and the coast there is at about 41.05°E, so the Red Sea coast bends eastward at that latitude. That puts the point very near the coast, probably on the coastal plain (Tihamah) or just offshore.

Since the coast is right there, I'll treat the point as land on the coastal plain, with an elevation of about 10 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":10}

7324.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 69.0°E is in the Arabian Sea, roughly 700 km west of India's southern tip and east of the Carlsberg Ridge region. Because it is open ocean, the surface type is Water.

The Arabian Sea basin floor is usually 3000–4500 m deep. The point sits at 9°N 69°E, south of the Indus Fan and west of the Laccadive/Chagos-Laccadive ridge, which lies around 72–73°E. The area between the ridge and the Carlsberg Ridge is the Arabian Basin, with typical depths of about 4000–4500 m. I estimate about 4300 m depth, so the seabed elevation is about -4300 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

8013.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 7.0°E is in the Gulf of Guinea. Land near that longitude: Nigeria's coast is at about 4.3°N, and Bioko lies at 3.5°N, 8.7°E. São Tomé is at 0.3°N, 6.6°E. At 1.0°N, 7.0°E the point is about 75 km north-northeast of São Tomé, in open ocean.

The São Tomé and Príncipe volcanic line runs from Annobón to Bioko. Príncipe is at 1.6°N, 7.4°E, and São Tomé is at 0.2°N, 6.6°E. The point lies between them, on the ridge of the Cameroon Line. The seabed there is probably about 2000 to 3000 m deep, since the platform around the islands is steep and the surrounding abyssal plain is about 3000 to 4000 m. The ridge between the islands is likely shallower, so I estimate about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

1936.text:
The point 69.0°N, 93.0°E lies in the Taymyr/Putorana region of central Siberia. Norilsk is at about 69.3°N, 88.2°E, so this point is roughly 170 km east of it. Lake Pyasino is near 69.7°N, 88.5°E, so it is not at this point. The Putorana plateau spans roughly 68–69.5°N, 90–96°E. Lake Khantayskoye is at about 68.5°N, 90.5°E, and Lake Ayan and Lake Lama are around 69.5°N, 91°E. Lake Keta is around 68.7°N, 90°E.

At 93°E, 69°N the area is probably the northern Putorana or the Anabar-Khatanga lowland edge. The Kotuy River is around 71°N, 102°E, and the Kheta River is at about 71°N, 100°E. The Logata and Bolshaya Romanikha rivers drain the area near 93°E, so the terrain there is plateau-edge upland, perhaps 500 m or so. I'm uncertain, but a figure around 400 m seems plausible for the surface there.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

602.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -55.0°W. Northern Greenland's coast near Nares Strait/Lincoln Sea lies around 83°N at about -30 to -60°W. Greenland's north coast is at roughly 83.6°N at Cape Morris Jesup (-34°W). At -55°W, the north coast of Greenland is around 82.0-82.5°N (Hall Land, Nares Strait). Washington Land and Hall Land lie around 80-81.5°N. Near -55°W, 82.5°N is Cape Lupton/Newman Bugt area, and Ellesmere Island's Nares Strait coast is at about -62°W. At 83°N, -55°W we are north of the coast, in the Lincoln Sea, so it is water.

The Lincoln Sea depth there: the continental shelf is around 83°N with depths of several hundred meters. The shelf north of Greenland is narrow-ish; at 83°N, -55°W, the depth is perhaps -400 m. The Lincoln Sea has a deep area of about 500 to 1000 m, so I'll estimate -450.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

15391.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 3°E is in Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land. The coast there is around 70°S, so 81°S lies deep in the interior of the East Antarctic ice sheet. That area is near the Recovery Glacier catchment and the Pensacola–Shackleton ranges, which are further west and south. At 3°E and 81°S the ice surface is probably around 2,500–3,000 m. I estimate about 2,900 m, based on rough recollection of the region rather than a lookup.

The surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid and the elevation is the ice-surface height.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

610.text:
Latitude 83.0 N, longitude -39.0 W lies in north Greenland. Peary Land sits around 82-83.6 N and -20 to -35 W. At -39 W and 83 N, this is near the northern coast of Greenland, close to Nares Land and Kaffeklubben. The coast near 83.6 N is at about -30 W (Kap Morris Jesup is at -33 W). At -39 W the coast is around 83.2 N (Cape Bridgman near -41 W, 83.1 N), so 83.0 N, -39 W is probably just inland on the ice sheet margin or in the mountains.

Land there is likely at a few hundred meters. Around Hunt Fjord and Independence Fjord, which are further east at -30 W, the terrain is lower. At -39 W, 83 N, the ice cap margin has elevations of roughly 500-800 m. I'll estimate 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

2375.text:
The point 63.0°N, 109.0°W is in the Northwest Territories, Canada, in the Canadian Shield barrenlands. It lies east of Great Slave Lake (which is around 61-62.5°N, 109-117°W) and southeast of Great Bear Lake. Lakes near this longitude include Aylmer Lake (about 64°N, 109°W) and Lac de Gras (64.5°N, 110.5°W). Artillery Lake is at about 63.1°N, 107.9°W, and Clinton-Colden Lake is at about 63.9°N, 107.5°W. Walmsley Lake is at about 63.4°N, 108.6°W. At 63.0°N, 109°W there are many small lakes, though the point itself is probably on land.

Elevation in this area is roughly 350-400 m. Aylmer Lake is at about 375 m, and Walmsley Lake is around 400 m. I'll estimate 390 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":390}

10649.text:
The point -29, -121 lies in the South Pacific, roughly 1,500 km east of Pitcairn and west of Easter Island (which is near -27, -109). It sits on the Southeast Pacific basin, near the East Pacific Rise's western flank, where seafloor depths are typically 3,500–4,000 m.

The East Pacific Rise itself is around -113 at this latitude, so -121 is about 800 km west of the crest. The seafloor there is young and shallow, perhaps 3,000–3,300 m deep. The Easter Microplate lies near -26, -112, and the rise crest is near -2,800 m. At 800 km off-axis, with an age of about 10–15 Myr, the depth is about 2,600 + 350·sqrt(age) m, which is roughly 3,700 m for 10 Myr. At a spreading half-rate of about 80 mm/yr, 800 km corresponds to about 10 Myr, so the depth is about 3,700 m. This area has some seamounts, but I'll go with about -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

1267.text:
The point 75°N, 165°W lies in the Chukchi Sea / Arctic Ocean north of Alaska and Siberia. Wrangel Island is at about 71°N, 179°W, and Barrow is at 71.3°N, 156.8°W, so this point is well offshore.

The Chukchi Sea shelf extends to roughly 72–73°N. Farther north at 75°N, 165°W, the Chukchi Borderland and Chukchi Plateau are nearby. The Chukchi Plateau lies around 75–78°N, 165–170°W, with depths of about 500–1000 m. The Chukchi Cap sits at around 77°N, 165°W with depths of about 400–500 m. At 75°N the seafloor drops from the shelf break, near 73°N, into the Chukchi Abyssal Plain/Northwind Ridge region. The Northwind Ridge is around 74–76°N, 155–160°W. At 165°W and 75°N the seafloor is probably on the slope or plateau at about 1000–2000 m depth. I estimate 1800 m, so the elevation is about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

10767.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 115 is in Western Australia. The coast near Geraldton is at about 114.6°E, latitude -28.8. At -29.0 the coastline is around 114.9°E (near Greenough/Dongara area is -29.25, 114.9). Dongara is at -29.25, 114.93, and Geraldton at -28.77, 114.6. At -29.0 the coast is roughly 114.7°E, so 115.0 is about 30 km inland, which is land.

Elevation there is likely around 200 m, since the area inland from the coast is on the Victoria Plateau and Gascoyne region. Near Mingenew (-29.2, 115.4) elevation is about 700 m? Mingenew is about 750 ft (~ 250 m). Around 115.0, -29.0 the elevation is probably around 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

13999.text:
Location: -65.0, 99.0E is in East Antarctica offshore of Wilkes Land, near the Shackleton Ice Shelf area (which is around 96-101E, at about -66 deg). At -65 the point is just north of the coast. The coast near 99E is at about -66.3, so -65 is open ocean, on the continental slope or rise.

The Davis Sea and Shackleton region has a continental shelf edge at roughly -65.5 to -66 near 100E. At -65 the depth is probably around 2500-3500 m. The Antarctic margin near the Gaussberg/Shackleton area has a narrow shelf, with the slope descending from about 500 m to 3000 m across a short distance. At -65 the depth is probably about 2800 m. I'll say -2900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

1084.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 171.0°W lies in the Chukchi Sea / Arctic Ocean north of Wrangel Island (which is near 71°N, 179°W). At 77°N, 171°W the point is north of the Chukchi Shelf, near the Chukchi Borderland and Arctic basin margin, in the area of the Chukchi Plateau or Mendeleev Ridge region.

The Chukchi Plateau is at roughly 77-80°N, 165-170°W, with depths of about 400-700 m. The point at 77°N, 171°W sits near the northern edge of the Chukchi shelf, where the slope descends into the Chukchi Abyssal Plain, which is at about 2000-2500 m. The shelf break near 172°W is around 73-74°N, so at 77°N the seabed is on the slope or plateau. The Chukchi Cap and Plateau lie around 75-78°N, 165-170°W, and the Northwind Ridge is around 74-77°N, 155-160°W. I'd estimate the depth at about 1500 m, which is a compromise given the uncertainty.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

7628.text:
Latitude 5.0 N, longitude -43.0 W lies in the equatorial Atlantic, roughly 1000 km off the northeast coast of Brazil, so the surface is Water.

For depth, this position sits west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, in the Ceará Abyssal Plain / Ceará Rise region. The Ceará Rise is centered near 4°N, 43°W, where depths are about 3000-4000 m. The Ceará Rise is a broad plateau with depths of roughly 3000 m at its crest (ODP Leg 154 sites sat at 2600-3600 m). Site 926 at 3°43'N, 42°54'W was at 3598 m. Site 925 at 4°12'N, 43°29'W was at 3041 m. At 5°N, 43°W the depth is probably around 3800 m, though it could be a bit shallower on the northern flank. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

786.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -47.0°W lies in northeast Greenland. Northern Greenland's coast near 81N is at about -60 to -20 longitude. At -47, 81N is inland, in the ice sheet north of the Nioghalvfjerdsfjorden area. Peary Land is at about -30 to -20 longitude, and the northwest coast near Nares Strait is at about -60 to -65 at 81N. At -47 and 81N, the location is likely on the ice sheet's northern margin, or possibly in the Hall Land/Wulff Land region. Further north, around 82N, the coast of Greenland (Lincoln Sea) is at -47, and at 81N the ice sheet is probably about 1000 m elevation. The Flade Isblink ice cap is near -15 to -20 longitude, so it is not here. Likely the northern Greenland ice sheet at around 81N, -47 has an ice surface of roughly 1200 m.

Another consideration is the Hagen Fjord area near -20, so that's not relevant. Also, the Victoria Fjord is at about -40 to -45, 81.5N. Victoria Fjord is at about 81.7N, -45, and its head is near 81.5N. So 81N, -47 is south of that and likely lies on ice sheet margin or in Warming Land and Nansen Land. The ice-surface elevation is probably around 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

6090.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude 121.0 is in Taiwan, near Yushan (Jade Mountain), which sits at about 23.47°N, 120.96°E. At 23.0°N, 121.0°E the point is in the Central Mountain Range's southern part, west of Taitung, around Haiduan/Lidao in Taitung County, close to the Southern Cross-Island Highway. Taiwan's east coast is at about 121.2°E at this latitude, so 121.0°E is inland.

The area is roughly in the Jhuosi/Haiduan region. The Southern Cross-Island Highway passes Lidao at about 23.1°N, 121.0°E, at around 1,000 m. Nearby Guanshan is at 23.05°N, 121.17°E. The Xinwulu River valley and the Lanyu mountain ridges are also in this area. The Central Range near 23.0°N has peaks of about 2,000–3,000 m. The Gaoxiong/Taitung border area includes Mt. Beidawu, which is at 22.63°N. Mt. Guanshan is at 23.25°N, 120.95°E and about 3,666 m. At 23.0°N, I estimate about 1,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1500}

4739.text:
The point 37°N, 61°W lies in the western North Atlantic, southeast of Nova Scotia and well east of Cape Hatteras. It sits over the Sohm Abyssal Plain, south of the continental rise and a little northeast of Bermuda (32°N, 64.7°W).

The Sohm Abyssal Plain is typically about 5,000 m deep. At this latitude and longitude the depth is probably around 5,000 m, though the New England Seamounts chain, which runs near 38–40°N at 60–65°W, may affect it. At 37°N, 61°W the point is south of that chain, so I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

12722.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 65 is in the southern Indian Ocean. It lies northwest of the Kerguelen Plateau, which starts around 68°E, and southeast of Crozet (about 51.5°S, 46°E). The Kerguelen Islands are at roughly 49°S, 69.5°E, so this point is west of Kerguelen, in open water.

The seafloor there is probably the Crozet-Kerguelen basin or the western flank of the Kerguelen Plateau. The plateau's western edge near 51°S is around 65-68°E. The Southern Kerguelen Plateau is at about 55°S, and the Central Kerguelen Plateau, at about 50-58°S, 75-80°E, is shallow at roughly 1000-2000 m. At 65°E the floor is probably the deep basin or the plateau's western slope, so I estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

6515.text:
Location: 17°N, 109°W is in the Pacific Ocean, southwest of Baja California and west of Mexico's mainland. It lies near the Revillagigedo Islands, which are around 18.8°N, 111°W, so it is roughly 250 km southeast of there. It is also east of the East Pacific Rise, which sits near 105°W at that latitude.

The seafloor there is about 3,500 to 3,800 m deep. Crust at 109°W, 17°N is about 10 to 15 Myr old, which suggests a depth of roughly 3,400 m. Allowing for sediment and local relief, I estimate about -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

2113.text:
Latitude 67.0°N, longitude 87.0°E is in Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia, on the Central Siberian Plateau near the Putorana region's southern edge. It lies north of the Arctic Circle, close to the Kureyka River area.

Kureyka is at about 67.4°N, 86.6°E. The Kureyka River flows near there, with elevations around 30-100 m. The Kureyka Reservoir (Turukhansk) at 66.5°N, 88°E is a small area. I estimate the terrain at about 100 m. This is a lowland just west of the plateau, so the figure is uncertain, but 100 m is a reasonable guess.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

2862.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 145.0°E. This falls in the Sea of Okhotsk, north coast. The Magadan region coast is around 59.5°N at 150°E, and Okhotsk town is at 59.36°N, 143.2°E. Near 145°E the coast lies around 59.2–59.4°N (Ayan is at 56.4°N, 138°E, so it is not relevant). Between Okhotsk (143.2°E) and Nagayev Bay (150°E), the coast runs along roughly 59.3°N at 144–145°E. The Tauyskaya Bay coast is near 59.5°N at 149°E. At 145°E there is the Koni Peninsula to the east (around 59°N, 151°E) and the Yama area at 59.1°N, 154°E, so the coast at 145°E is probably about 59.2–59.4°N. The Okhotsk coastline there is at about 59.35°N, so 59.0 would be in the sea, south of the coast by roughly 30–40 km. The Okhotsk shelf is shallow there, about 50 m deep.

I'll answer Water with a depth of about -60 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

14162.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 65 is in East Antarctica, near Mac. Robertson Land, in the Prydz Bay region. The coast there lies around -67.5 to -68 at longitude 65: the Mawson Station is at 62.87E, -67.6. At 65E the coast is near -67.8 or so, so -67.0 would be just inland on the ice sheet, at roughly 62-65E. Mawson Coast ice elevations a short distance inland are about 500-1000 m, and 60 km inland from the coast is around 1000 m.

The surface is therefore grounded ice, and I estimate its elevation at about 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

675.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 91°E is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Taymyr Peninsula and Severnaya Zemlya (which lies around 79-81°N, 95-100°E). At 83°N the location is in the Nansen Basin or near the Gakkel Ridge's southern flank. The Gakkel Ridge runs roughly from 85°N, 0°E toward the Laptev Sea, and near 90°E it sits around 82-83°N. The Nansen Basin abyssal plain is about 3,900 m deep, while the ridge axis is around 4,000 m or more. Near 83°N, 90°E the depth is probably about 3,500-4,000 m.

I estimate the seabed at roughly -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

941.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -97.0 falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Bathurst Island is centered near 75.5N, 99W, so it is too far south. Cornwallis Island is near 75N, 95W, also too far south. At 79N, 97W, the nearby land is the Queens Channel and Ellef Ringnes Island, which lies around 78.5N, 102W. Amund Ringnes Island is around 78.5N, 96W, spanning roughly 77.8–78.8N. Cornwall Island is near 77.6N, 94.5W. Axel Heiberg Island is further east at about 80N, 90W. Meighen Island is at 80N, 99W.

The point at 79N, 97W is therefore probably north of Amund Ringnes, in the channel between Amund Ringnes and Meighen. That channel is the Hazen/Massey Sound region. Massey Sound lies between Amund Ringnes and Axel Heiberg at about 78.8N, 95W. The northern tip of Amund Ringnes is near 78.8N, 96W. At 79N, 97W this is probably water, in the Norwegian Bay / Hazen area, where depths are likely a few hundred meters, though some of it could be shallow. I'll estimate the seabed at about -200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-200}

2941.text:
Latitude 57°S, longitude 57°W lies in the southwest Atlantic. It is south of the Falkland Islands (about 51–52°S) and north of the South Shetland Islands. It is also east of Tierra del Fuego and Isla de los Estados, which sit at roughly 54.5°S, 64°W.

At 57°S, 57°W the seafloor is probably in the Burdwood Bank area or south of it. Burdwood Bank is at about 54°S, 60–55°W. Further south, the Scotia Sea's northern margin has the North Scotia Ridge at around 54–55°S. At 57°S the seafloor is in the Yaghan Basin or the Ewing Bank region, with depths of about 3000–4000 m. The Yaghan Basin lies at about 57°S, 58°W and is roughly 3500 m deep, so I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

10845.text:
The point (-31, -89) is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, off the coast of Chile and west of the Chile Trench. It lies roughly 1,800 km west of the Chilean coast near 31°S, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor there is part of the Nazca Plate. The Chile Trench is at about 71.5°W, and the plate's seafloor west of it is mostly 3,500–4,000 m deep. The Easter Island region (109°W) is shallower, and the Chile Rise lies farther south. The East Pacific Rise is farther west, near 110°W, so the seafloor at 89°W is older and deeper, with an age of about 30–40 Ma. That would put it around 3,800–4,000 m. The Nazca Ridge is at about 20°S, and the Juan Fernández region is at about 33°S, 80°W. At 89°W and 31°S the ocean floor is probably about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11786.text:
The point (-41.0, -7.0) is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of southern Africa and southwest of Tristan da Cunha (which sits near 37°S, 12°W). It lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and north of the Agulhas Ridge region. In this area the Argentine and Cape basins have depths of about 4500 to 5000 m.

The ridge axis near 41°S is at roughly 14°W, so -7° is on the eastern flank. Depths there are probably about 4500 to 5000 m, which gives an estimate of about -4800 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

13253.text:
Latitude -57, longitude 47 lies in the Southern Ocean, southwest of the Indian Ocean sector. It is south of the Prince Edward Islands (around 46.9°S, 37.7°E) and southwest of Crozet (46°S, 51°E). The region around 57°S, 47°E is probably near the Southwest Indian Ridge or the Enderby Basin, and the Southwest Indian Ridge sits roughly at 50-55°S in this longitude range.

The ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (55°S, 1°W) northeastward to the Indian Ocean triple junction (about 25°S, 70°E). Near 47°E it is at roughly 45-50°S. At 57°S, 47°E we are therefore south of the ridge, in the Enderby Basin or the Crozet Basin's southern part. Depths there are about 4000-5000 m. The Conrad Rise lies near 54°S, 40-50°E and is shallower, around 2000-3000 m. At 57°S it is probably the southern flank, with the Enderby Basin at about 4500 m. I'll estimate -4400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

3429.text:
The point is at 51.0°N, 161.0°W, which is in the North Pacific south of the Alaska Peninsula and the Aleutian Islands. The Aleutian Trench lies to the north, and the Aleutians sit near 52–55°N. At 161°W the trench axis is roughly 54°N, so 51°N is south of the trench on the abyssal plain, or on the trench's outer slope.

Near 161°W the trench is deep, about 5,000 m or more, and runs along roughly 53–54°N. The Pacific plain south of it is around 5,000 m deep. At 51°N the depth is probably about 5,000 m, possibly a little less. I'll estimate -5,000 m, and since the true figure could be a bit shallower, I'll go with -4,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

12741.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 103 is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies north of the Southeast Indian Ridge crest at about 50°S, and the Kerguelen Plateau is farther west, near 70-80°E, so it does not apply here.

The Southeast Indian Ridge near 100°E runs at roughly 48-50°S. The Australian-Antarctic Basin lies south of it. Near 51°S, 103°E we are probably just south of the ridge axis, on its flank, where depths are about 3000-3500 m. Ridge crest depths there are around 2500-3000 m, and the Australian-Antarctic Discordance is further east, around 120°E. At 51°S the seafloor is probably about 3500 m deep, so I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9960.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude -59.0 falls in Paraguay/Brazil border region. Paraguay's northern Chaco lies around -21 lat, with the Paraguay River near -57.9 to -57.5. At -59.0 it is inside the Paraguayan Chaco (Alto Paraguay department), or just west of the river. The Chaco there is flat, roughly 100-150 m elevation. Near Fortín Teniente Rojas Silva, the elevation is about 120 m.

I estimate about 115 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":115}

10652.text:
The point (-29, -115) is in the South Pacific, west of Chile and southeast of Pitcairn and Easter Island. It lies on the East Pacific Rise flank, where the seafloor is around 3,000 to 3,800 m deep. The East Pacific Rise crest is near 110°W at this latitude, at roughly 2,800 m depth, so a point 5° west of the crest would be around 3,200 m. The Easter microplate region sits near 26°S, 110°W. At 29°S, 115°W the seafloor age is about 10 Myr, which fits a depth near 3,400 m.

This is an estimate from general seafloor structure, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

12674.text:
The point at 51°S, 31°W is in the South Atlantic, west of the South Sandwich Islands and east of South Georgia. It lies north of the Georgia Basin and east of the North Scotia Ridge. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far to the east, so the point is in open ocean.

South Georgia sits at about 54°S, 36°W. The seafloor around 51°S, 31°W is probably the Georgia Basin or the area near the Northeast Georgia Rise. The Northeast Georgia Rise is centered near 52°S, 41°W, which is farther west than this point. The Georgia Basin is roughly 4000 to 5000 m deep. I'll estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

5729.text:
Latitude 27.0 N, longitude 119.0 E is in Fujian province, China, inland from the coast. Fuzhou is at about 26.07 N, 119.3 E, and Ningde is at about 26.67 N, 119.5 E. The point is northwest of Fuzhou, near Luoyuan or Lianjiang, or possibly Gutian. Lianjiang is at 26.2 N, so this point is farther north.

Gutian is at 26.58 N, 118.75 E. Ningde's Jiaocheng is at 26.66 N, 119.55 E. A point at 27.0 N, 119.0 E is probably in Zhouning or Xiapu, in the mountainous area near Shouning (27.45 N, 119.5 E) and Pingnan (26.9 N, 118.98 E). It is therefore very close to Pingnan. Pingnan sits in the Min River valley at roughly 100 m elevation, and the surrounding hills reach 500–800 m. I'll estimate about 450 m, since the point could be on a ridge.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

3162.text:
Latitude 55.0, longitude 25.0 is in Lithuania, near Kaunas and Kėdainiai. Kaunas is at about 54.9°N, 23.9°E, so 25.0°E lies east of it, near Ukmergė (55.25°N, 24.75°E) and Jonava (55.07°N, 24.28°E). The point is probably in the Vilnius–Kaunas area, around Širvintos, with typical terrain of about 80–100 m.

The Neris river runs near 55.0°N, 24.9°E, at an elevation of about 70 m. The surrounding terrain is around 90 m, so I'll estimate 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

10596.text:
The point (-27.0, 133.0) is in northern South Australia, near the Stuart Highway and Coober Pedy. Coober Pedy is at about -29.0, 134.75, so this point lies north-west of it. The area is arid desert, probably in the Arckaringa Basin or near the Stuart Range, with elevations around 150-250 m.

I estimate about 200 m from general knowledge of the region, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

1574.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 89.0°E is in the Kara Sea region near the Taymyr Peninsula. The Taymyr coast lies around 75-77°N at 100°E. At 89°E, the coast near Dikson (73.5°N, 80.5°E) is further west. The Yenisei Gulf is around 72-73°N at 80°E. At 89°E, 73°N, the point is probably on the Taymyr lowland, east of the Yenisei and north of Norilsk (69°N, 88°E). The coast at this longitude is about 74°N, near the Pyasina Gulf and Khatanga Bay (Taymyr Bay, ~74°N, 86-88°E). The Byrranga Mountains begin further north at about 75°N. So 73°N, 89°E is likely land, on the lowland north of the Putorana foothills, with an elevation of about 50 m.

The Pyasina river runs at about 73°N, 87°E, and Lake Taymyr is at 74.5°N, 101°E. The area is likely tundra lowland at roughly 40 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":45}

14178.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 97E lies off East Antarctica, near the Shackleton Ice Shelf and Queen Mary Land, which sits at about 66°S, 100°E. The Shackleton Ice Shelf is around 66°S, 95-100°E, and the coast near 66.5°S at 97°E is roughly the Denman/Scott glacier area. At 67°S the point is probably on the continental shelf, or it could be on the ice sheet.

The coastline near 97°E is at about 66.5°S, with the Bruce Rise further north. The Shackleton Ice Shelf extends to roughly 66.3°S, so 67°S is likely inland on grounded ice, though it could be ice shelf. Longitude 97°E is on the western side of the Shackleton Ice Shelf, which spans 94-105°E. The ice shelf front is at about 65.5-66°S, and the grounding line is at about 66.5-67°S. At 67°S the ice would be grounded, with a surface elevation of roughly 200-500 m. I'll go with Solid at about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

11781.text:
The point is at 41°S, 17°W in the South Atlantic. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits near 13°W at this latitude, and east of the Rio Grande Rise, which is farther north and west. It is also north of Tristan da Cunha, which is at 37°S, 12°W, and the Walvis Ridge, which is around 30-35°S.

The area is likely the western flank of the ridge or the Argentine Basin's eastern edge, where depths of about 4000-5000 m are typical. The ridge crest here is about 2500-3000 m deep. At 17°W, roughly 4° west of the axis, the age is about 10 Myr and the depth is about 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

13184.text:
The point at 57°S, 91°W lies in the southeast Pacific, west of Drake Passage and south of the Chile Rise. It is far from any land, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor there is probably the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or the abyssal plain near the Chile Rise. The Chile Rise runs from about 46°S, 75°W southwest to the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge near 55°S, 125°W. At 91°W the Chile Rise lies north of the point, around 50°S. The point also lies north of the Antarctic Peninsula and south of the Chile Rise, in the Bellingshausen/Pacific basin, where depths are around 3,500–4,000 m. The Eltanin and Udintsev fracture zones are farther west, near 130°W to 145°W. I estimate about -3,900 m for the depth, from general knowledge of the basin rather than a specific lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14808.text:
Location: 75°S, 83°W is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen-Bellingshausen sector. It lies inland of the Eights Coast and Thurston Island, and south of Thurston Island (about 72°S, 100°W). Longitude 83°W at 75°S is probably near the Ellsworth Land coast, close to the Abbot or Venable ice shelf region. The Abbot Ice Shelf spans roughly 70-73°S, 85-100°W, so 75°S is further south, in the interior of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet toward the Hudson Mountains and Ellsworth Land. Pine Island Glacier's catchment is near 75°S, 100°W. The Ellsworth Land coast lies around 74°S near 80°W, and the Ronne Ice Shelf's western edge is near 75°S, 75°W. So 83°W could be grounded ice on the Ellsworth Land coast (Orville Coast), with an ice surface elevation of roughly 1000 m.

Could it instead be the Rutford or Institute ice stream area? Those are near 80°S, 80°W. At 75°S, 83°W, the ice is probably grounded with an elevation of about 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

33.text:
Latitude 89°N is in the central Arctic Ocean, about 100 km from the North Pole. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the area near the pole, running roughly from the Siberian side toward Ellesmere and Greenland. Its position at 89°N depends on longitude. The ridge crosses near 90°N at about 140°E–Greenland side, meaning along the 140°E/40°W meridian line. At longitude -113°, which is on the Canadian side (the 113°W–67°E line), the point is about 1° of latitude from the pole. The ridge axis lies near the 50°W–140°E line, so the point is far off the ridge axis toward the Amerasian Basin side.

The Amerasian side of the Lomonosov Ridge is the Makarov Basin, with depths around 4000 m. At the pole itself the depth is about 4200 m, and the Lomonosov Ridge there is about 1000–1500 m deep. The ridge is a narrow feature, around 60 km wide. The point at 89°N, 113°W is about 111 km from the pole. The ridge axis passes through the pole along the 140°E–40°W line, and the angular separation from 113°W to 40°W is 73°. So the perpendicular distance is about 111 km × sin(73°) ≈ 106 km from the ridge axis. That puts it in the Makarov Basin, with a depth of roughly 4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

11922.text:
The point at -43, -95 lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and south of the Easter Island region. It is near the Chile Rise and the East Pacific Rise's southern extension, though the Chile Rise is farther south, at roughly 36°S, 98°W.

The East Pacific Rise at about 43°S lies near 110°W, so this point is east of the ridge. The seafloor there is about 20-30 Myr old, and depths in that area are around 3,500-4,000 m. The Chile Rise runs southeast toward the Chile Triple Junction at 46°S, 75°W, and at 95°W the Chile Rise (Chile Transform) is near 41-45°S. The Chile Rise segments around there are shallow, about 2,500-3,000 m. I estimate about -3,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

14966.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -127 is in West Antarctica, in the Ross Sea sector. The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part lies near -80 to -77 latitude between roughly -160 and -150 longitude. At -127 longitude and -77 latitude, the location is near the Amundsen Sea/Marie Byrd Land coast, close to Getz Ice Shelf (which sits at about -75, -125). At -77, -127, I think this is inland Marie Byrd Land, near the Ruppert Coast and Hobbs Coast. The coast there is around -75.5 to -76. So -77 would be inland, on grounded ice.

Ice surface elevation there is probably about 500-1000 m. Marie Byrd Land interior near -77, -127 is the lower part of the ice sheet toward the Siple Coast, with surface around 700 m. I'll guess 600.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

1677.text:
Latitude 71.0, Longitude -65.0 is in the Baffin Island region. Baffin Island's northeastern coast lies around 70-72°N, 68-75°W (Clyde River is at 70.5°N, -68.5°W). At -65°W, 71°N, that is east of the Baffin coast, in Baffin Bay. The coast near Clyde River is at about -68°W, and the shelf extends offshore. Around -65°W at 71°N the point is roughly 100 km offshore, probably on the Baffin shelf, with depths around 500-700 m. The Baffin Bay central basin lies deeper, at 2000 m+, further east near -60°W. At -65°W and 71°N, the shelf edge is likely at roughly -67°W to -66°W, so this point is likely on the slope, with depth around 1000 m.

I'll estimate -900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

12082.text:
The point is at 45°S, 135°W, in the South Pacific Ocean. It lies southeast of New Zealand and well southwest of South America, in the southeast Pacific basin.

I'm estimating the depth from memory of the region's bathymetry rather than from a lookup. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is farther south, at roughly 55–65°S at this longitude. The seafloor at 45°S, 135°W is abyssal plain, with typical depths around 4000–4500 m, and the age of the crust there suggests roughly 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12184.text:
The point (-45, 69) lies in the southern Indian Ocean. It is southwest of the Kerguelen Plateau, which sits at roughly 49°S, 69°E, and southeast of Crozet (46°S, 51°E). Kerguelen's northern part extends to about 46°S, so -45 is just north of it. The Amsterdam and St Paul islands are at 38°S, 77°E, which is farther away.

The seafloor around 45°S, 69°E is probably the northern edge of the Kerguelen Plateau or the basin just north of it. The Crozet Basin lies to the northwest, at about 4000 m depth. The plateau's northern edge is near 46°S, and the Kerguelen main island spans roughly 48.5–50°S, so the plateau's northern part lies around 46–49°S. At 45°S, 69°E, I estimate the floor is a bit north of the plateau, at about 3500 m depth. The plateau's northern slope may be near 46°S, with depths of around 2000–3000 m. I'll go with -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

5603.text:
The point (27°N, 133°W) is in the open eastern North Pacific, roughly 1,500 km west of Baja California and north-east of Hawaii. It is deep ocean floor, so the surface type is Water.

Abyssal plain depths in this region are typically 4,000–4,500 m on the older seafloor. The area lies west of the Murray Fracture Zone and south of the Pacific-Farallon region, where the seafloor is about 40–60 million years old. That age implies a depth of roughly 4,500 m, so I estimate about -4,350 m. This is an estimate from regional depth patterns, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4350}

9813.text:
Latitude -19, longitude 7 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Angola/Namibia. The Namibian coast is at about 12-15°E at this latitude, so 7°E is roughly 500+ km offshore. It sits in the Angola Basin, near the Walvis Ridge to the south. The Walvis Ridge runs from about -20° at 12°E southwest toward the Tristan area, so at -19° and 7°E we are probably in the Angola Basin north of the ridge, where depths are around 4800-5000 m.

The Angola Basin abyssal plain is about 5000 m deep, and I'll estimate -4950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

11275.text:
The point is at 35°S, 51°E, in the southwest Indian Ocean. It lies southeast of Madagascar and south of the Mascarene area, in the Madagascar Basin or near the Southwest Indian Ridge.

The Madagascar Basin is roughly 4,500–5,500 m deep. The Southwest Indian Ridge sits further southwest, around 40°S, 50°E. The Madagascar Ridge runs along about 45°E, so 51°E is east of it. Near 35°S, 51°E the seafloor is probably about 5,000 m deep, which is an estimate rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

10545.text:
Latitude -27.0, longitude 31.0 is in northern KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa, close to the Eswatini border. It lies near Pongola and Louwsburg, roughly 150 km inland from the coast, so the surface is solid.

The point sits just south of the Pongola area. Louwsburg is at about -27.6, 31.4. The Lebombo mountains run along about 32.0 E. At 31.0 E, 27.0 S, the terrain is the Swaziland and Zululand lowveld-to-midveld transition, probably around 900 m. Piet Retief is at -27.0, 30.8 and about 1100 m. Just to the east of it, at 31.0, the terrain drops toward the Pongola river valley. Paulpietersburg is at -27.4, 30.8 and about 1200 m. Candover and Mkuze are lower. Eswatini's western highveld lies at about -26.5, 31.0. Since 31.0 E is only slightly east of Piet Retief, I estimate about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

10866.text:
The point (-31, -47) is in the South Atlantic, off southern Brazil. It lies east of Porto Alegre, which is near 51°W, and well offshore of the continental shelf. The area is the Brazil Basin, or possibly the Rio Grande Rise region to the northeast. The Rio Grande Rise is around 30°S, 30–40°W. At 47°W and 31°S the seafloor is probably on the continental slope or the São Paulo Plateau's southern edge.

The shelf break off Rio Grande do Sul is at about 50°W, and the Rio Grande Cone extends out to around 45°W. At 47°W the depth is probably about 3,000–4,000 m. I estimate 3,800 m, so the elevation is about -3800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

9606.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -47 is in Brazil, in Goiás/Minas Gerais near Brasília (which sits at -15.8, -47.9). The point is southeast of Brasília, around Unaí or Paracatu, on the plateau. Elevation there is about 800-900 m.

More precisely, -17.0, -47.0 is near Cabeceira Grande / Unaí in northwest Minas Gerais, on the border with Goiás. Terrain there is about 800 m, so I estimate 820 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":820}

8418.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 97.0 lies in the Indian Ocean off the west coast of Sumatra. The Mentawai Islands (Siberut, Sipora) are at about 98.5–100.5°E, so 97°E is west of them, in the forearc region. Between the Sumatran trench and the Mentawai islands, there is the outer-arc ridge with the Mentawai fault and the Siberut basin. The trench axis at around 3°S is near 98°E, so 97°E would be on the incoming Indian Ocean plate, or perhaps the trench itself. At the equator the trench is at about 98°E (depth about 5,000 m), and at 3°S it is around 99–100°E. So 97°E is on the oceanic plate about 300 km from the trench, where the abyssal plain depth is about 5,000 m. The Investigator Fracture Zone and the Wharton Basin are nearby, with depths of about 5,000 m.

I estimate a seabed depth of about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

570.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -119.0° lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Canada's Arctic Archipelago. Axel Heiberg Island reaches about 81°N and Ellesmere Island's northern tip is near 83.1°N at around -70° to -60°. Meteorological data puts the northwest end of Ellesmere around -90° at about 81°N. Borden Island and Meighen Island lie near 78–80°N. So 83°N, -119° is open ocean beyond the continental shelf edge.

The shelf edge north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago is at roughly 81–82°N here. At 83°N the position is probably on the continental slope or in the Amundsen Basin/Canada Basin margin, where the depth is perhaps 1500–3000 m. The Lomonosov Ridge is near -140° to 140°E at 88°N, so it is not relevant here. Judging from IBCAO, the area north of Ellef Ringnes at 83°N, -119° is probably on the slope into the Canada Basin, at around -2500 m. I'll estimate -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

4080.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude 61.0 is in Kazakhstan, southwest of the Aral Sea region. The Aral Sea's southern part sits around 44-45°N, 58-60°E, so 61°E is east of it. The Syr Darya runs near 45°N, 61-63°E, in the Kyzylorda region around 44.8°N, 65°E. At 61°E, 45°N, the terrain is the Aral Karakum desert, with elevation around 80 m.

The former eastern shore of the Aral Sea (the Large Aral) was at about 59-60°E, with a surface near 53 m in the past. The area at 61°E sits somewhat higher, in the Aral Karakum sands, at roughly 70-100 m. I'll estimate 75 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":75}

1756.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 93.0°E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Russia. Khatanga Gulf is around 73°N, 105°E. The Taymyr Peninsula's north coast lies near 76°N. At 71°N, 93°E the point is in the Taymyr lowlands, south of Byrranga, near the Pyasina/Dudypta area. Lake Taymyr is near 74.5°N, so this is not in the lake.

Around 71°N, 93°E there are the Putorana foothills to the south-west and the North Siberian Lowland to the north-east. Elevation is probably about 100 m. The Dudypta river is near 71–72°N, 92–95°E, and the terrain there is roughly 50–150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

182.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -175° lies in the central Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs from the Siberian shelf past the pole toward Greenland. At 87°N, the ridge is near longitudes 140°E to 50°W, roughly along the 135°E–45°W meridian line. At -175° (175°W), the point is on the Amerasian side of the pole. The Lomonosov Ridge crest sits at about 45°W and 135°E, so 175°W is about 40° of longitude from the ridge axis. At 87°N, 40° of longitude is about 1.0° of latitude-equivalent distance in arc, which is roughly 2×87 km×... in practice about 80 km.

Which side of the ridge it falls on: the Amerasian side, with the Makarov Basin lying next to the ridge, has depths of about 4000 m. The Makarov Basin is between the Lomonosov and Alpha-Mendeleev ridges. At 87°N, 175°W, this is likely the Makarov Basin, where the depth is about 4000 m. The Alpha Ridge is roughly at 85°N, 150°W. At 87°N, 175°W we are probably in the Makarov Basin at about 4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

14420.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -139 lies in the Ross Sea region, east of the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge. Marie Byrd Land's coast is around -77 to -75 at longitude -139, and the Ross Ice Shelf extends to about -78 at -160. At -139, the coast near 76-77°S is Edward VII/Saunders Coast area, so -71 would be open ocean in the Amundsen Sea/Ross Sea region, far north of the coast. The continental shelf edge there is around -73 to -74, so -71 is beyond the shelf, over the continental slope or rise. Depth is perhaps -3500 m.

The Marie Byrd Seamounts lie further north-west. At -71, -139, the depth is probably about -4000 m (abyssal plain). I'll estimate -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14799.text:
The point is at 75°S, 101°W, in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. Pine Island Bay lies near 74.5°S, 102°W, and the Pine Island Glacier ice shelf sits around 75°S, 100°W.

The Pine Island ice shelf front was near 75.1°S, 100–101°W in earlier years, and the shelf has since retreated. Either way the location is likely floating ice shelf or open water in Pine Island Bay, so the surface type is Water. The seabed there is deep, around -500 to -1000 m. Bathymetry from Bedmap2 and the trough in front of Pine Island Glacier suggests roughly -800 m near the grounding line area. A reasonable guess is -700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

5646.text:
The point 27°N, 47°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which lies near 40°W at that latitude. It falls in the western Atlantic abyssal basin, in the Sargasso Sea region northeast of Bermuda, where depths are typically about 5000 m. This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

The ridge crest sits around 2500-3500 m deep, and the flanks deepen with distance from it. At 47°W the seafloor is about 7° of longitude (roughly 700 km) from the ridge, which puts the age at about 40 Myr and the depth near 4800-5200 m. I'll take 5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

8224.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 69.0 is in the Indian Ocean, about 1° south of the equator and east of the Maldives (Maldives are at ~73°E). The location is near the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge region. The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge runs along roughly 73°E, so 69°E lies west of it, in the Central Indian Basin's western side or the Carlsberg Ridge region. The Carlsberg Ridge is at about 60-65°E near the equator, and the Maldives ridge is at ~73°E. Between them, around 69°E, is the Arabian Basin/Somali Basin side, with depths of about 4000-4500 m.

I estimate about 4400 m depth, so the elevation is about -4400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

4771.text:
Latitude 37.0, longitude 3.0 is in the Mediterranean Sea, off the Algerian coast. Algiers is at about 36.75°N, 3.05°E, so 37.0°N is roughly 28 km north of Algiers. The coast there runs at about 36.8°N, and the continental shelf is narrow, so the water deepens quickly offshore.

At 37.0°N, 3.0°E the seabed depth is probably around 1000 to 1500 m. The Algerian basin reaches about 2500 to 2800 m deep farther north, near 38°N. About 25 km offshore the slope is steep, and I estimate roughly 1,200 m of depth at this point. I'll go with -1,300 m. This is an estimate from the geography, not a looked-up value.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -1300}

2411.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude -37.0 is in the North Atlantic/Irminger Sea, southeast of Greenland. The southern tip of Greenland (Cape Farewell) is at about 60°N, 44°W, and the east coast near 63°N lies around -40.5°W (Kangerlussuaq is at 68°N, so this is further south). At 63°N the Greenland coast is at roughly -41 to -42°W. So -37°W is about 200 km offshore, over the continental shelf edge or slope.

The East Greenland shelf at 63°N is fairly wide, but the shelf break is around -38 to -39°W. At -37°W the point is likely on the slope, with a depth of around 1500-2000 m. The Irminger Basin lies further east, at about 2700-3000 m, near the Reykjanes Ridge at roughly -30°W. I estimate a seabed depth of about -1900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

10786.text:
Latitude -29.0, longitude 153.0 is off the northern New South Wales coast. The coast near Evans Head / Yamba is around 153.35 E at -29.0 (Yamba is at 153.36 E, -29.43; Evans Head is at 153.43 E, -29.1). At -29.0, the coast is near 153.4 E, so 153.0 E is about 40 km inland, on land.

Inland at this location, near Casino/Kyogle: Casino is at 153.05 E, -28.86. At -29.0, 153.0 E is south of Casino, in the Richmond River floodplain/hills. Elevation is probably around 50 m. Coaltown/Woodburn is at 153.34 E. Near Tabulam/Bonalbo there are hills, but this point is in the lowland near Casino, perhaps 30 m. I'll say 40.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":40}

3269.text:
Latitude 53.0, longitude -121.0 is in British Columbia, Canada, east of Quesnel and near the Cariboo Mountains foothills. Quesnel sits at about 53.0 N, 122.5 W, so this point is roughly 100 km east of it. Barkerville is at about 53.07 N, 121.5 W, and its elevation is about 1250 m. Going further east, the terrain rises into the Cariboo Mountains, but 121.0 W is still west of Wells Gray and the main peaks. The area around Bowron Lake is at about 53.2 N, 121.1 W, and the lake surface is about 1120 m. At 53.0 N, 121.0 W the point probably lies in the Cariboo Mountains foothills at around 1300 m.

The terrain is uncertain, so I estimate about 1400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1400}

10824.text:
This location (31°S, 131°W) is in the South Pacific Ocean, southeast of Pitcairn and east of the Austral Islands. It lies on the Pacific plate, and the seafloor there is probably around 3,500–4,000 m deep.

The East Pacific Rise is much farther east, near 110–115°W at that latitude. At 131°W the seafloor is roughly 15–25 million years old, which fits a depth of about 3,800–4,200 m. Mangareva and Pitcairn are near 25°S, 130°W, so this point is about 6° south of them. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is far to the south. I estimate the depth at about 3,900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3900}

6696.text:
The point at 15°N, 107°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, southwest of Mexico. It is south of the Clipperton Fracture Zone region and west of the Middle America Trench. It sits on the East Pacific Rise flank, roughly 600 km south of the Revillagigedo Islands. The East Pacific Rise axis is near 105-110°W at 15°N, so this point is close to the rise crest. Rise crest depths there are about 2600-2800 m, but 107°W may be a bit off-axis, since the rise at 15°N is near 105.5°W. That puts the point about 150 km west of the axis, where seafloor depths are about 3000 m.

I estimate a depth of about 3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

10876.text:
The point is at latitude -31, longitude -27, which is in the South Atlantic. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits near 14°W at this latitude, and east of the Brazil Basin. The location is in open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is probably the Brazil Basin or the western flank of the ridge. The Rio Grande Rise is at about 30-35°W and 30°S, but its crest is mostly west of -27. At -27 longitude the depth is probably about 4500-5000 m, so I'll estimate the seabed at around -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

10775.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 131 lies in South Australia, in the northwest of the state near the Stuart Highway and the Great Victoria Desert, close to the Northern Territory border area. It is about 50 km from Marla, in the Musgrave/Everard region. The surface is solid land, and I'm estimating the elevation at roughly 200 m from memory of the area rather than from a lookup.

Further north, near Mount Davies at -26, the elevation is about 500 m. Around -29, 131, which is near Wintinna and Mount Willoughby, the elevation is about 180 m. Coober Pedy at -29.0, 134.75 is about 220 m. At longitude 131 the terrain is likely 150-200 m, so I'm choosing 190 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":190}

13654.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 129 lies in the Southern Ocean south of Australia. It is well north of Antarctica, whose coast at that longitude is around -66 to -67. The location is in the Australian-Antarctic Basin, or possibly on the southern flank of the Southeast Indian Ridge.

The ridge near 129°E runs roughly along -50 to -51 latitude. Further south the abyssal plain of the Australian-Antarctic Basin lies at about 4000 to 5000 m depth. At -61 the seafloor is probably about 4,000 to 4,500 m deep, so I'll estimate -4300 m. I'm estimating this from general knowledge of the region, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

2890.text:
Latitude 57.0 N, longitude -159.0 W lies south of the Alaska Peninsula. The peninsula's coast near Chignik and Perryville sits at about 56.3 N, 158.4 W, and the Shumagin Islands are near 55 N, 160 W. At 57.0 N, 159 W, the Alaska Peninsula's Pacific coast runs roughly from (56.0, -160.5) to (57.0, -157.5)... Port Heiden is at 56.9 N, 158.6 W on the Bering Sea side, and Ugashik is at 57.5 N, 157.5 W. The peninsula is about 50 km wide here. At 57.0 N, the Bering side coast is near -158.7 W, so -159.0 would be in Bristol Bay, slightly offshore.

Checking Port Heiden: it is at 56.94 N, 158.62 W, on the Bering coast. Moving 0.4 degrees west, about 24 km, puts the point in Bristol Bay. The water there is shallow, about 30 to 50 m deep, so I estimate -40 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

1972.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 165.0°E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Sakha Republic near the Chukotka border. It lies roughly between the Kolyma and Indigirka rivers, close to the Kolyma lowland and the Anyuy mountains.

The Kolyma River is near 161–162°E at 69°N, so 165°E is east of it. The Kolyma lowland extends east to about 165°E at this latitude. The Anyuy highlands lie farther south, around 66–68°N. The East Siberian Sea coast is at about 69.5°N near 160°E, and farther east the coast is at about 69.5–70°N (Chaun Bay is at about 69.5°N, 168°E). So the point is likely in the tundra lowland, with elevation of roughly 50 m. Because it is on land, the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

11938.text:
Latitude -43, longitude -63 lies in the South Atlantic off Patagonia, east of the Valdés Peninsula (which is near -42.5, -64). At -63 longitude, 43°S, the point is about 100 km offshore from the coast near -64.5. The Patagonian continental shelf there is roughly 80-100 m deep, and the shelf edge is farther east, around -58 to -60 at this latitude.

So the point is on the shelf, with a depth of about 90 m. The Península Valdés coast is at about -63.6 longitude at its easternmost point (Punta Delgada at -63.6, -42.8), so the point is only about 40 km east of it. The shelf depth there is around 80 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-85}

2963.text:
The point 57.0°N, 13.0°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of Scotland and the Outer Hebrides. It is near the Rockall Plateau, but Rockall itself is at about 57.6°N, 13.7°W, and the Rockall Bank surrounds it.

The Rockall Trough lies east of the bank, with depths of about 2000 to 3000 m. The western edge of the trough is at roughly 13°W near 57°N. The Rockall Bank has shallow areas of about 200 m, and its eastern slope drops steeply into the trough. The bank's eastern edge sits around 13.5°W at 57°N, so 13°W is probably on the slope or at the trough's edge. Bathymetry around 57°N, 13°W is likely about 1000 to 1500 m deep. I'll estimate 1300 m deep, but this is a guess from memory of the regional bathymetry.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

2850.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 121.0°E falls in eastern Siberia, in the Sakha Republic (Yakutia) or near the Aldan and Lena basins. It is roughly north of the Stanovoy range, on the Aldan Highlands or the Lena–Aldan plateau area, probably near the Maya River or the Yudoma area.

Longitude 121°E at 59°N is east of the Lena bend. The Lena at 59°N is around 112–114°E near Lensk and Olekminsk (60.4°N, 120.4°E). Olekminsk sits at 120.4°E, 60.4°N, so this point is about 1.4° south of Olekminsk. The area is hilly terrain between the Olekma and Aldan rivers, with elevations of roughly 600–900 m. The Olekma River runs near 120–121°E at 58–59°N, so the point could be in the Olekma valley at about 400 m. I'll estimate 650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":650}

12954.text:
The point is at 53°S, 169°E, which is in the Pacific sector of the Southern Ocean, south-southeast of New Zealand. Campbell Island sits near 52.5°S, 169.1°E, so the point is very close to it, roughly 55 km south of the island's main body.

Campbell Island's latitude spans about 52.4°S to 52.6°S. At 53.0°S the point is about 40 km south of the island's southern tip, so it is open water over the Campbell Plateau. The plateau depth there is around 500–700 m, and the plateau edge drops off further south. I estimate about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

12469.text:
The point is at 49°S, 81°W, in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. It lies roughly 1,000 km west of the Taitao Peninsula (around 46°S, 75°W).

The Chile Rise and Chile Trench are further east and north. The trench at this latitude is near 75°W, so 81°W is on the Nazca/Antarctic plate side. The seafloor there is probably abyssal plain at about 3,500–4,000 m depth. The Chile Rise triple junction is near 46°S, 76°W, and the Chile Rise spreads southwest from it. The Antarctic plate seafloor west of the trench at 49°S is of moderate age, with depths of about 3,500–4,000 m. I'll estimate -3,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

5933.text:
The point is 25°N, 167°E, in the western Pacific, northwest of the Marshall Islands and near Wake Island (19.3°N, 166.6°E). That area is open ocean over the Pacific plate, where the seabed is typically 5000-6000 m deep, though a few seamounts rise above that. Several guyot chains lie in the region, such as the Marcus-Wake seamounts, which run roughly from 20°N to 28°N between 150°E and 170°E.

I have no specific bathymetry for this exact point, so I'm estimating from regional depths. I'll take a depth of about -5300 m as a reasonable value for the surrounding abyssal plain.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

13977.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 55 is in East Antarctica, off Mac. Robertson Land / Enderby Land coast. The Antarctic coast near 55°E is around -66.5 to -67.5 (Mawson Station is at 62.9°E, -67.6). At 55°E, the coast is about -66.5°S, so -65 is offshore in the Southern Ocean, on the continental shelf or slope.

The continental shelf off Enderby Land and Kemp Land is narrow, and the shelf break lies near -66°. At -65, 55°E, the seabed is probably continental slope, roughly 2000-3000 m deep. Around 55°E, the coastline lies near 67°S (the Cape Borley / Mac. Robertson Land area is around 53°E, -66.5). The shelf there is fairly narrow, and the continental rise would be at roughly -65. I estimate a depth of about -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

14457.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -65 is in Antarctica, on the east side of the Antarctic Peninsula base. Around -71, -65 the location is in the Larsen/Wilkins area? The peninsula at 71°S sits near 68°W. At -65 longitude, 71°S is south of the Bowman Coast... Actually the Larsen C ice shelf is at about 67-69°S, 62-66°W. At 71°S, 65°W, this is probably the Ronne Ice Shelf's northern part (Gipps Ice Rise area?) or the Orville Coast. The Orville Coast is at about 75°S, 62°W. Ronne-Filchner's western part reaches the peninsula at about 72°S, 62°W–70°W. Latitude 71°S, 65°W is likely on the plateau or in the Ronne Ice Shelf area near Rydberg Peninsula. The Antarctic Peninsula at 71°S lies roughly between 68° and 74°W (Palmer Land's coast on the Bellingshausen side). The east coast, along the Wilkins Sound/George VI area, is near 69-70°W at 71°S. So 65°W is east of the peninsula crest, probably on the Ronne ice shelf (the Ronne's northwestern edge near Orville Coast is around 61-65°W, 75°S). At 71°S, 65°W, this may be a grounded ice tongue on the Lassiter Coast (about 73°S, 61°W). Hmm.

The Lassiter Coast is 73-74°S. At 71°S, 65°W, it's probably on the Dyer Plateau, an ice plateau at about 70.5°S, 65°W, with elevation ~1,500-2,000 m. Yes, Dyer Plateau is at about 70°40'S, 64°W. So it's Solid, with ice surface at ~1,800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

7494.text:
Latitude 7.0, longitude 49.0 is in Somalia, in the Mudug/Nugal region near the Ethiopian border. Somalia's coast at 7°N lies near longitude 49.5 (Hobyo is at about 5.3°N, 48.5°E, and Garacad is at about 6.9°N, 49.1°E). Garacad is therefore very near the coast, so the point could be land just inland or the coast itself.

The coastline at 7°N runs roughly from Garacad (6.9°N, 49.1°E) northeast toward Eyl (8.0°N, 49.8°E). At latitude 7.0 the coast is at about 49.2°E, so 49.0°E is probably land, a few tens of kilometers inland. Elevation there would be low, around 100 m or less. Garacad sits at the coast, and the Mudug plains rise slowly inland. I estimate about 60 m at this point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

8172.text:
The point (-1.0, -35.0) is in the Atlantic Ocean, off the northeast coast of Brazil. Brazil's coast near 5°S is at about 35°W, and at 1°S the coast is at roughly 48°W, so this point lies well offshore, about 1,000 km or more from land.

The location is in the equatorial Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits near 30°W. It is probably on the Brazilian basin or Ceará Rise margin. The Ceará Rise is around 3°N, 43°W, so it is not at this point. Close to the equator at 35°W, the Atlantic abyssal plain is likely about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. Near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge flank at about 35°W, depths are around 4,000 to 5,000 m. The Romanche fracture zone is near 18°W to 25°W, and the St Paul fracture zone is near 29°W, so this point is west of both. I estimate about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

15731.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -37 is in Antarctica, in the area south of the Weddell Sea near the Foundation Ice Stream and the Support Force Glacier, close to the Pensacola Mountains and the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf region.

Longitude -37 at 85°S is where the Filchner Ice Shelf meets the Slessor and Recovery glaciers' region. The Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf grounding line lies roughly around 82-83°S at this longitude, so 85°S is probably grounded ice. The Foundation Ice Stream is near -70° longitude, and the Support Force Glacier is near -45° and 83°S. At -37°, 85°S, the position is in the Dufek Massif/Forrestal Range area, which is further to the west at about -50° and 83°S. So this point is probably the interior ice sheet near the Recovery Glacier catchment, with an ice surface elevation of about 1200 m. The Academy Glacier is near -60° and 85°S. Moving east, the Recovery Glacier at about -30° and 82°S has a surface near 1000 m, and further inland the Theron Mountains region is near -26°. At 85°S, -37° the surface is plausibly around 1500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1500}

681.text:
The point is at 83°N, 103°E, in the Arctic Ocean north of the Taymyr Peninsula. The Taymyr coast is near 77-78°N, and the Severnaya Zemlya islands lie around 79-81°N at 95-100°E. At 83°N, 103°E the point is north of Severnaya Zemlya, past the Kara Sea shelf edge. The shelf there is narrow, and the continental slope descends toward the Nansen Basin north of about 82°N. In that area, at 83°N and 103°E, the depth is probably around 3000 m.

From IBCAO, the Nansen Basin near 83°N, 100°E has depths of about 3500 m to 4000 m. The shelf break north of Severnaya Zemlya sits near 81-82°N, and the slope reaches about 3000 m by 83°N. I estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

3353.text:
The coordinates 53.0°N, 47.0°E fall in southwestern Russia, near the Volga region. Saratov Oblast spans roughly 45–50°E, and Penza Oblast lies near 45°E, so this point is probably in Penza, Ulyanovsk, or Samara Oblast. It sits at about 47°E, 53°N, which is east of Penza, near the Saratov/Ulyanovsk border region.

The Volga Upland there has elevations around 200 m. The Sura River valley lies near 46–47°E at about 53–54°N. Near 53°N, 47°E the point is probably in the Penza Oblast's eastern part, near Kuznetsk (53.1°N, 46.6°E), which sits at roughly 150–200 m. I estimate about 190 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":190}

4799.text:
Latitude 37.0, longitude 59.0 lies in northeastern Iran, in Razavi Khorasan province near the Turkmenistan border. It is close to Quchan (37.1, 58.5) and Chenaran (36.65, 59.1), and the Kopet Dag range runs through the area. Mashhad is at 36.3, 59.6 and sits at about 1000 m. Quchan is at about 1300 m.

The point at 37.0, 59.0 is southeast of Quchan, in the Quchan–Chenaran area. It probably lies in the Kashafrud/Atrak valley region at about 1200 m, and Chenaran is at about 1180 m. Bare-ground elevation near here is plausibly 1400 m, but I cannot pin it down further. The surface is solid land, so I'll estimate 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

4412.text:
Latitude 41.0°N, longitude 5.0°E lies in the western Mediterranean, between the Balearic Islands and Sardinia, roughly 150 km west of the northern tip of Sardinia. Sardinia's west coast is at about 8.2°E and Menorca at about 4°E, but at 39.9°N, so this point is north of Menorca, at 41°N.

That places it in the Algero-Provençal basin, on the abyssal plain. Depth there is about 2,700 m. The Balearic abyssal plain is around 2,800 m deep at 5°E, 41°N, so I estimate about -2,750 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2750}

3417.text:
Latitude 53.0 N, longitude 175.0 E is in the western North Pacific, south of the Aleutian Islands. Attu Island sits at about 52.9 N, 172.9 E, so 175 E lies east of Attu, between the Near Islands and the Rat Islands. Buldir is at 52.35 N, 175.9 E, which is further south. At 53.0 N, 175.0 E the position is north of Buldir, in the Bering Sea side of the arc. The Aleutian Trench lies south of the islands, and the Bering Sea basin lies to the north. The Aleutian Ridge platform near here is around 52-53 N. The Bering Sea side drops to about 3000 m in the Aleutian Basin, with the ridge slope in between.

Buldir at 52.35 N is on the ridge, so the ridge crest runs about 52.3 N here. At 53.0 N, about 70 km north, the Bering Sea slope would be at roughly 1500-2500 m depth. I estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

12837.text:
Latitude -53, longitude -65 is off the coast of Tierra del Fuego/Patagonia. Río Grande is at about -53.8, -67.7. Cape Virgenes is at -52.33, -68.35. The Atlantic coast of Tierra del Fuego at -65 longitude is east of the coast, so it is in the Argentine Sea (Patagonian shelf). Land at -53 latitude: the Atlantic coast near San Sebastián Bay is at about -66.5 at latitude -53. So -65 lies in water, roughly 1.5 degrees east of the coast. The shelf depth there is about 80-100 m.

The Patagonian shelf east of Tierra del Fuego at 65°W, 53°S is around 100 m deep. I'll estimate -95.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-95}

10912.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 45 lies in the southwestern Indian Ocean, south of Madagascar and southeast of South Africa. It is roughly east of Durban (31°E) by about 14° of longitude, so it falls in the open ocean.

The region is the Madagascar Basin / Mozambique Basin area. Longitude 45°E at 31°S is close to the Southwest Indian Ridge to the south, but the ridge is at about 40°S or farther south at this longitude. The Madagascar Basin lies east of about 45°E, while the Mozambique Basin is to the west. Between them near 31°S is the Madagascar Ridge, which runs around 44–46°E at 27–33°S. Its depth there is about 2000–3000 m. The Madagascar Ridge's shallow parts lie near 30°S, with depths of about 1000–2000 m at the Walters Shoal (33°S, 43.8°E). At 45°E and 31°S, the depth is probably around 3000 m, so I estimate -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

7719.text:
Latitude 5.0°N, longitude 139.0°E lies in the western Pacific Ocean. It is north of New Guinea (which is around 1-2°S at this longitude) and south of Palau, which is at about 134°E and 7°N. It is east of Halmahera and Palau and near the Caroline Islands region, to the west of Micronesia (Yap is at 138°E, 9.5°N).

The area is probably on the Caroline Plate or Ayu Trough. The Mussau Trench is further away. The Western Caroline basin has a depth of about 4000 m. The Eauripik Rise is near 142°E. Around 139°E, 5°N, the Ayu Trough lies near 133°E, so this point is probably in the Western Caroline Basin at about 4,000 to 4,500 m depth. I estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

8094.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 169.0°E is in the central Pacific, east of Nauru (which sits at about 0.5°S, 166.9°E) and west of the Gilbert Islands. Tarawa is at about 1.4°N, 173°E, and Banaba (Ocean Island) is at about 0.85°S, 169.5°E. The point at 1°N, 169°E is therefore open ocean, just north of Banaba.

The seabed in this region is the Nauru Basin or the area around it. Ocean depths near Nauru and Banaba are about 4000 m. The Nauru Basin is around 5000 to 5500 m deep, and the seamount slopes around Banaba are steep. At roughly 1°N, 169°E, about 200 km from Banaba, I estimate the depth at about 4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

3323.text:
Location 53.0°N, 13.0°W is in the Atlantic Ocean, west of Ireland. Ireland's west coast is around 10°W, so this point is about 200 km offshore. It lies near the Porcupine Bank and the Porcupine Seabight. The Porcupine Bank spans roughly 53–54°N and 12–15°W, and its shallow parts are about 200 m deep, while the surrounding area is deeper. The bank's southwestern edge descends into the Porcupine Abyssal Plain. At 53°N, 13°W the point is probably on the western flank of the bank, or perhaps just west of it.

The Porcupine Bank's northern part is shallow, around 150–500 m, at 53.5°N, 13.5°W. The southern part at 52°N, 13°W is deeper, roughly 1000 m or more. At 53°N, 13°W I estimate a depth of about 600 m, though the flank could reach 1000 m. I'll answer -700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

3774.text:
The point 49°N, 169°E is in the North Pacific, east of Kamchatka and south of the Commander Islands. It lies in the open ocean west of the Emperor Seamount chain, over the Northwest Pacific Basin, where abyssal depths are about 5,500 to 5,800 m. The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench lies further west, near 160°E at this latitude, so it does not affect this point. The Emperor Seamounts are around 170°E and further north, at 40°N and above. The Meiji Seamount is near 53°N, 164°E, which is also not close.

Based on typical depths for this part of the basin, I estimate about -5,600 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

1231.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 123.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea region, near the Lena Delta and the Taymyr coast. The Lena Delta sits around 73°N, 126°E, and the Taymyr Peninsula's east coast is near 76°N, 113°E. At 77°N, 123°E, the point is north of the delta in the open Laptev Sea. The New Siberian Islands are farther east, beyond 140°E. Laptev Sea shelf depths at 77°N are roughly 30-50 m near 123°E, and the shelf edge is at about 78-79°N.

I did not look up a bathymetry source, so this is an estimate. I'd put the seabed at about -45 m, which is plausible for that part of the shelf.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

1934.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 89.0°E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Russia. That puts it south of the Taymyr lakes and near the Putorana plateau's northern edge, in the Norilsk area. Norilsk is at about 69.35°N, 88.2°E. A point at 69.0°N, 89.0°E is about 40 km southeast of Norilsk, which is near Lake Melkoye and Lake Lama.

Lake Lama is at about 69.5°N, 90°E, and Lake Melkoye is near 69.3°N, 90.3°E. The Norilsk plateau area sits at around 69°N, 89°E. The Rybnaya and Ambarnaya rivers run through this area, and the Pyasina basin lies to the north. The terrain there is lowland and plateau edge at roughly 100–300 m. I'll treat it as land at about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

5980.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude -99.0 falls in Mexico, in the Sierra Madre Oriental near Ciudad Victoria in Tamaulipas. Ciudad Victoria is at about 23.74 N, -99.14 W, so this point lies roughly 80 km south of it. That puts it near the Tamaulipas/San Luis Potosí border, in the area around Palmillas and Jaumave, where the Jaumave valley sits at about 700-800 m.

Jaumave is at 23.4 N, -99.37 W, so 23.0 N, -99.0 W is further southeast. This is near Gómez Farías and Ocampo. Ocampo is at 22.85 N, -99.35 W. The point is probably in the mountains of the Sierra Madre, which gives an estimate of around 1,000 m. Nearby, El Cielo reserve at 23.05 N, -99.2 W has elevations from about 200 m to 2,000 m. Just east of there, at -99.0, the terrain descends toward the plains, so the elevation could be around 300 m. Santa Maria del Refugio is also close. Taking the eastern edge of the Sierra de Guatemala, I estimate about 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

13096.text:
Latitude -55, longitude 93 is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies north of the Southeast Indian Ridge axis, which runs roughly through (-50, 70) to (-48, 100) to (-55, 140). The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins south of the ridge.

At 55°S, 93°E the Southeast Indian Ridge is probably at about 49-50°S, so this point is on the southern flank. The seafloor there should be around 3500-4000 m deep. The Kerguelen Plateau is farther west, at about 80°E or less, so it does not matter here. I estimate -3900 m, though I'm not certain.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

13301.text:
The point at -57.0, 143.0 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Tasmania and southwest of Macquarie Island. It sits south of the Southeast Indian Ridge, in the area of the Australian-Antarctic Basin and the Macquarie Ridge complex.

The Macquarie Ridge is near 159°E, so it is far to the east. The Southeast Indian Ridge crosses about 50°S at 143°E and lies further south around 60°S at 150°E. At 57°S, 143°E the seafloor is probably abyssal or on the flank of the ridge. The ridge axis at 143°E is near 51°S, so this point is about 6° south of it. That puts the depth at roughly 3500–4500 m. I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4000}

5813.text:
The point 25°N, 73°W is in the western Atlantic, southeast of the Bahamas. It lies east of Long Island and Crooked Island in the Bahamas, which sit near 75°W, and north of the Turks and Caicos. This area is in the Hispaniola Basin and Nares Abyssal Plain region. The Nares Abyssal Plain is around 23-24°N, 62-65°W, with depths of about 5800-6000 m. At 25°N, 73°W, the position is on the Bahama Outer Ridge, or near the Blake-Bahama Basin edge. The Blake Bahama Outer Ridge is around 28°N, 71°W. The Bahama escarpment lies near 74°W.

At 73°W, 25°N, the seafloor is probably about 5,200 m deep, in the Hatteras/Blake-Bahama abyssal region. I'll estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

9879.text:
Latitude -19.0, longitude 139.0 is in northern Queensland, Australia, in the Gulf Country, south of the Gulf of Carpentaria. It lies near the Northern Territory border, around the Nicholson River and Lawn Hill area. The longitude of 139 is just east of the border at 138, so the point is inland west of Burketown, which is at about 139.5 E and -17.7 S. The terrain there is flat Mitchell grass downs at roughly 100 m elevation.

I'm estimating about 150 m from memory of the region rather than from a lookup. The area is the Barkly Tableland's eastern edge, around Camooweal at 138 E and -19.9 S, which is about 230 m. Going north and east toward the Gulf lowers the elevation, so 150 m is reasonable.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

15634.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 129 is in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. It lies roughly between Dome A and the Transantarctic Mountains, closer to the Wilkes Land interior. South of the Vostok area (Vostok is at -78.5, 106.8), the position is about 7 degrees of latitude south, or around 780 km. At -83, 129 the ice surface elevation is probably about 2900-3200 m. This sits near the upper part of the catchment of the Totten and Mulock glaciers, where the surface is roughly 3000 m.

I estimate the surface elevation at about 3000 m, and the surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

8272.text:
The point at latitude -1, longitude 165 lies in the western Pacific, east of Papua New Guinea and just south of the equator. It is east of the Solomon Islands and northeast of the Solomon Sea, and it is west of Nauru (which sits at 0.5°S, 166.9°E). The nearest land is Nauru, about 2° of longitude away, so this point is open ocean.

The area belongs to the Ontong Java Plateau and nearby basins, and the Nauru Basin lies to the northeast. At 1°S, 165°E the seafloor is probably in the Nauru Basin or its southwestern margin, with depths around 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Ontong Java Plateau is further west and southwest (roughly 160 to 162°E), with depths of about 2,000 m. The Nauru Basin is around 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. I'll estimate -4,300 m, which is an estimate rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

11167.text:
The point (-35, -165) is in the South Pacific, northeast of New Zealand and south of the Cook Islands. This area is open ocean on the Pacific plate. Seafloor depth in this region is typically around 5000 to 5500 m, and I'm estimating from general knowledge of the basin rather than a specific lookup.

The location is roughly south of the Austral Islands and west of the East Pacific Rise, in old seafloor of about 80-100 Ma. Depths there are around 5300 m, so I'll use -5300.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5300}

5920.text:
Location 25.0N, 141.0E is in the Pacific Ocean, in the Volcano Islands area. Iwo Jima is at 24.78N, 141.32E, and Kita-Iwo Jima is at 25.43N, 141.28E. The point at 25.0N, 141.0E lies about 30 km west of Iwo Jima, on the Ogasawara Plateau/Kyushu-Palau area side. This is west of the Izu-Ogasawara arc, in the Parece Vela Basin margin or the back-arc region. Depths there are probably around 1000-2000 m on the western flank of the Iwo Jima volcanic ridge, possibly deeper.

The Nishino-shima trough lies west of the arc. Around 141.0E the depth is likely about 1500 m, so I'll estimate -1500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

8741.text:
The point at latitude -7.0, longitude 23.0 is in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in the Kasai region. It lies near the Kasai River, south of Tshikapa, which is at about -6.4, 20.8. Longitude 23.0 at latitude -7 is in Kasai-Central or Lomami, near Luiza (about -7.2, 22.4). This is a plateau with elevations of roughly 700 m.

Elevation around Kananga (-5.9, 22.4) is about 650 m. The area near Luiza is about 800 m. Around -7, 23 the terrain is probably 750 m, since the Kasai basin slopes toward the Lulua and Lubilash rivers. I'll estimate 740 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":740}

13653.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 127 lies in the Southern Ocean south of Australia, in the area of the Australian-Antarctic Basin. The Southeast Indian Ridge runs roughly from 50°S at 130°E and reaches about 60°S near 150°E, so at -61, 127 we are probably south of the ridge and in the abyssal basin. The Australian-Antarctic Basin there is roughly 4000-4500 m deep. The Antarctic coast is at about 66.5°S, so this point is open ocean, well away from land.

I estimate a seabed depth of about -4300 m, from regional knowledge rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

697.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 135°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands, on the Laptev Sea side. This region is near the Lomonosov Ridge and the Amundsen Basin. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the Lincoln Sea past the North Pole toward the New Siberian Islands, and at 135°E it sits roughly near 81-87°N.

At 83°N, 135°E, I think the point is on the Eurasian side of the Lomonosov Ridge, in the Amundsen Basin or on the continental slope. The Laptev Sea slope drops from about 80°N, so at 83°N the seabed is likely deep abyssal plain, around 3,500 m deep. The Amundsen Basin is about 4,200 m deep near the pole, and near 83°N, 135°E it is probably about 3,800 m. The Lomonosov Ridge near 135°E is at about 87°N, so 83°N is well within the Amundsen Basin side, at roughly 3,000-4,000 m depth. I'll go with -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

8114.text:
The point (-1.0, -151.0) is in the central equatorial Pacific, south of the Line Islands (Kiritimati is at about 2°N, 157°W). It is far from any land, so the surface type is Water.

Seafloor depth in this region is typically around 4,800 to 5,200 m. The area lies southwest of the Line Islands ridge and north of the Marquesas, in the Penrhyn basin and central Pacific basin, where depths are about 5,000 to 5,300 m. I'll estimate -5,100 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

11709.text:
The point at 41°S, 161°W lies in the South Pacific, southeast of the Cook Islands and well north of the Antarctic region. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

For depth, this area is part of the Southwest Pacific Basin, with abyssal plains typically around 4,500–5,500 m deep. Ocean floor near 41°S, 161°W is roughly 5,000 m, and I am estimating that from regional knowledge rather than a lookup. The seabed is therefore about -5,000 m, and I chose -5,050 m as my single estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5050}

11851.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 123 is in the Southern Ocean south of Western Australia, roughly south of Esperance (which is near -33.9, 121.9). The Australian south coast is around -34, so -41 is about 7 degrees (roughly 780 km) offshore. This area lies on the Southern Ocean continental slope and abyssal plain, south of the Great Australian Bight and west of the Tasmania region.

The continental shelf off Western Australia ends near -35. Around -41 and 123 E, the Diamantina zone is further west, near -35 and 110 E. The Naturaliste Plateau is at 110 E, so it does not apply here. The seafloor south of Australia at -41 is likely abyssal, about 4,000 to 5,000 m deep. The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins around -45 to -50. At -41 and 123 E the depth is probably about 4,500 m, so I estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

8096.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 173.0°E lies in the Pacific Ocean, near Tarawa in Kiribati, which is at about 1.4°N, 173.0°E. Tarawa is a bit farther north, and 1.0°N falls south of it. Butaritari and Abaiang are north of Tarawa, and Maiana is at about 0.9°N, 173.0°E. Maiana's lagoon is on its western side, spanning roughly 172.9 to 173.05°E. This point could therefore be on Maiana, which would make it Solid at about 2 m, or in the lagoon or ocean.

Maiana is centered near 1.0°N, 173.03°E and is about 15 km long, running north-south from 0.85 to 1.05°N. Its land lies on the eastern side, at roughly 173.0 to 173.05°E. Longitude 173.0 is therefore at the western edge of the island, probably in the lagoon, where the bed would be around -20 m. Since the land is on the east side, the lagoon is the safer guess. I'll say Water, with an elevation of -15 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-15}

563.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 133°W lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Ellesmere Island is near 80-83°N but at about 60-90°W, and Axel Heiberg and the Queen Elizabeth Islands are around 100-120°W at 77-80°N. Borden Island and Meighen Island are near 80°N, so 83°N, 133°W is well north of these, in the Canada Basin/Arctic Ocean.

The nearby bathymetry is the Canada Basin margin, with the Alpha Ridge to the north. The Canada Basin abyssal plain at about 80°N, 135°W is around 3800 m deep. At 83°N, 133°W the seabed should still be Canada Basin floor or the lower slope toward the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge. The Alpha Ridge crest is around 85°N, 120°W at about 1500 m depth. At 83°N, 133°W the depth is probably around 3500 to 3800 m, so I estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

5842.text:
Latitude 25.0, longitude -15.0 is in Western Sahara, inland from the coast. The coast at 25°N is around -14.8°W (Dakhla is at 23.7°N, -15.9°W). At 25°N the coastline runs roughly at -14.7°W, near Bir Anzarane, which is inland, so the point is likely land.

Checking further: the Western Sahara coast at 24-25°N lies near -14.5°W to -15°W. Cape Bojador is at 26.1°N, -14.5°W, and the coast runs southwest from there, reaching Dakhla at -15.9°W at 23.7°N. At 25°N the coast is about -14.9°W, so the point could be right at the coast. Interpolating between Cape Bojador (26.12, -14.5) and Dakhla (23.7, -15.9), at 25.0 the longitude is -14.5 - (1.12/2.42)*1.4 ≈ -15.15°W. So the coast sits at about -15.15°W, and -15.0 is just inland. Taking this as land, the elevation is probably low, around 50 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

10190.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 41 lies in the Mozambique Channel, east of the Mozambique coast (coast at about 35°E at this latitude). Longitude 41°E is roughly 600 km offshore, southwest of Europa Island (22.3°S, 40.4°E). Europa Island is at 22.3°S, 40.35°E, so this point is about 0.7° south and 0.65° east of it, which is roughly 100 km away.

The area is the Mozambique Basin or the Mozambique Channel's southern part. The seafloor around Europa Island drops to about 2000–3000 m. The Mozambique Basin to the west of Europa is around 3000 m deep. South of Europa, near 23°S 41°E, depth is probably about 2500–3000 m. I'll estimate -2600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2600}

11698.text:
Latitude -39.0, longitude 177.0 is in the Pacific Ocean off the east coast of New Zealand's North Island, east of Hawke Bay. Mahia Peninsula is near 177.9E, -39.1S, so 177.0E is about 90 km west of it, which puts the point in Hawke Bay. Hawke Bay's coast near Wairoa is at roughly 177.4E, and the coast at -39.0 lies around 177.4-177.5E. So 177.0E would be inland, in the Te Urewera area.

Te Urewera lies around 177.0E, -38.8S. At -39.0, 177.0E, the point is in the Huiarau Range or the Ruakituri area, west of Wairoa. The terrain is rugged, with elevations of roughly 600-1000 m. Lake Waikaremoana is at 177.1E, -38.8S. The point at -39.0, 177.0 is south of that, in the Waikaretaheke area, with elevation around 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

2444.text:
Latitude 63.0 N, longitude 29.0 E is in eastern Finland, in the North Savo region near Kuopio. Kuopio is at about 62.9 N, 27.7 E, so this point lies east of it, near Tuusniemi or Juankoski. Tuusniemi is at about 62.8 N, 28.5 E. A point at 63.0 N, 29.0 E could be near Kaavi or Outokumpu; Kaavi is at 62.97 N, 28.5 E. That area has many lakes, so the point could be water, but land is more likely. Typical terrain elevation there is about 150 m.

I can't pin down whether the exact point is a lake, so I'll go with land at about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

4960.text:
The point 35.0°N, 21.0°E lies in the Ionian Sea, southwest of Crete and south of the Peloponnese. The Hellenic Trench runs through this area, with depths of roughly 3000-4000 m near 35°N 21°E. The deepest part, about 5267 m, is near 36.5°N 21.5°E (Calypso Deep), and the trench axis sits southwest of Peloponnese. At 35.0°N 21.0°E I estimate a depth of about 3000 m. This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

Crete's west end is near 23.5°E, so 21°E is about 2.5° west of it. The Hellenic Trench lies west and south of Crete, running from about 36°N 21°E toward 34.5°N 24°E. At 35°N 21°E the seafloor is probably on the trench's western flank or the abyssal plain, around 3300 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

11873.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 167 lies in the Tasman Sea, west of New Zealand's South Island. The South Island's west coast is around 171-172°E at this latitude, so 167°E is roughly 400 km offshore. This is near the Challenger Plateau and Lord Howe Rise to the north, and the Tasman Basin and the Macquarie Ridge to the south.

The area west of Fiordland and Southland lies at about 166°E, 46°S. At 41°S, 167°E, the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or lower slope, with depths of roughly 3,000-4,500 m. The Tasman Basin is about 4,000-4,800 m deep. Near 41°S, 167°E, the Lord Howe Rise and the Challenger Plateau sit further north, around 37°S and 159°E, and the Westland basin lies nearer the coast. The Stewart-Challenger or Bounty trough areas would be deeper, around 4,000 m. I estimate about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15571.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 3°E is in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land, south of the Recovery Glacier region and near the Pole of Inaccessibility direction. At 83°S and 3°E the ice sheet is grounded. Surface elevation in that area is about 2500-3000 m, so I estimate roughly 2700 m.

Checking against nearby features: the Shackleton Range lies at about 80-81°S and 25°W. The Theron Mountains are at 79°S. At 83°S and 3°E, I believe we are inland on the plateau. The South Pole is at 2835 m, and at 83°S along 0° the surface is probably around 2500-2800 m. I'll go with 2650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2650}

5805.text:
The point (25.0°N, 89.0°W) is in the central Gulf of Mexico, so the surface is water. That area is part of the Sigsbee Abyssal Plain, where the seabed is roughly 3,300 to 3,500 m deep.

The nearby Mississippi Fan lies to the north, and the abyssal plain at this latitude is about 3,400 m deep. I'm estimating this from memory of Gulf bathymetry, not from a lookup, so I'm using about -3,350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3350}

10704.text:
Latitude -29, longitude -11 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Namibia and South Africa. It is roughly 1,000 km from the African coast, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits near 0 to -13° longitude at this latitude. The Walvis Ridge is farther north at about -20 to -30°S, but it runs from Namibia northwestward toward Tristan da Cunha at about -37°S, -12°W.

The area near -29, -11 is part of the Angola/Cape Basin, with depths around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis at -29° is around -13.5°W, so -11 lies on the eastern flank of the ridge, probably 3,500 to 4,000 m deep. Ridge flanks about 250 km from the axis are usually around 3,800 m deep. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

647.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude 35.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Svalbard and Franz Josef Land. Nordaustlandet (Svalbard) reaches about 80.5°N, and Franz Josef Land's northern tip, Rudolf Island, is at about 81.8°N and 58°E. At 83°N, 35°E the point is therefore open ocean, probably on the Eurasian continental margin or the abyssal plain just beyond it.

The Svalbard shelf edge north of Nordaustlandet is at roughly 81.5°N. North of that the slope descends to the Nansen Basin, which is about 3,500 to 4,000 m deep. At 83°N, 35°E the basin floor should be near 3,900 m deep. Depths on the IBCAO grid for the Nansen Basin at 83°N, 30°E are around 3,800 to 4,000 m, so I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

296.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 53°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean. It is close to the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs roughly from Greenland/Ellesmere across the pole toward the New Siberian Islands. At 87°N the ridge sits near longitudes 140°E to 60°W, so 53°E would be on the Eurasian side of the ridge, in the Amundsen Basin.

The Amundsen Basin has depths of about 4200 to 4400 m. At 87°N, 53°E the seabed is probably around 4300 m deep, and the basin's abyssal plain is roughly 4200 m near the pole. I estimate -4250 m, from recalled basin depths rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4250}

1808.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude -163.0 W lies in the Chukchi Sea, off the northwest coast of Alaska. Point Lay is at about 69.75 N, 163.0 W, and Icy Cape is near 70.3 N, 161.9 W. Cape Lisburne is at 68.9 N, 166.2 W. At 69.0 N, 163.0 W, the coast near Point Hope runs from about 68.35 N, 166.8 W up toward Point Lay, so the coastline at longitude -163 is at roughly 69.7 N. That puts the point about 70 km offshore in the Chukchi Sea, where depths are about 40 m.

The nearest Alaska coast at -163 is around Point Lay, at roughly 69.75 N, so 69.0 N is offshore to the south of it. Depths in that area are around 40 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

7379.text:
The point is 9°N, 179°E, which falls in the western Pacific near the Marshall Islands. The Marshall Islands lie roughly between 160°E and 173°E, so 179°E is east of them and in open ocean. Ebon Atoll sits at about 4.6°N, 168.7°E, and Kiribati's Gilbert Islands are further south, so no land is nearby.

The seafloor in this area is the Pacific abyssal plain, probably with seamounts. I estimate a depth of about 5,000 m, perhaps nearer 5,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

15395.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 11 lies in Antarctica's Queen Maud Land interior, south of the Mühlig-Hofmann/Wohlthat mountains. Queen Maud Land at 81°S, 11°E is on the polar plateau, with ice surface elevation around 3000 m or so.

The Wohlthat Mountains are at about 71.5°S, so 81°S is far inland. Near 80°S, 10°E the ice surface is around 2800–3000 m. The Recovery Glacier region is at about 81°S, 20°W, and the Shackleton Range is at 80°S, 25°W. At 11°E, 81°S the ice is likely on the plateau flank, with the Pole at 90°S about 2800 m. Estimated surface elevation is about 2900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

5911.text:
Latitude 25.0°N, longitude 123.0°E is in the East China Sea, northeast of Taiwan and east of the continental shelf edge. It lies near the southern end of the Okinawa Trough, and just north of the Ryukyu arc near Miyako and Ishigaki, which sit at about 24-24.5°N and 124-125°E.

The East China Sea shelf is shallow, but the Okinawa Trough's southern part is deeper. At 25°N, 123°E the seafloor is probably on the trough's southern slope, at around 100-2000 m depth. The trough axis near 123-124°E at 25-26°N reaches roughly 1500-2200 m. Longitude 123°E is near the Senkaku/Diaoyu area, which is at 123.5°E and 25.75°N, with the shelf edge nearby. South of that, at 25°N, the trough is deep, around 1500 m. The shelf edge near 122°E, 25.5°N is at about 200 m. I'll estimate -1100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1100}

7742.text:
Location: 3°N, 175°W is in the central Pacific Ocean, in the Phoenix Islands/Line Islands region. Kiribati's Phoenix Islands lie around 3-4°S, 171-175°W, so this point is north of them, near the equator, east of the Gilberts (which are at about 173°E). Palmyra and Kingman are at about 162°W, and Howland Island is at 0.8°N, 176.6°W. So 3°N, 175°W is open ocean, roughly 250 km north of Howland.

Seafloor depth there is likely around 5000 to 5500 m, with the Central Pacific Basin depths around 5000 m. I'll estimate -5300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

8428.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 117.0 falls in Borneo (Kalimantan), in East Kalimantan or South Kalimantan. Borneo's southern extent at this longitude reaches about -3.5 to -4.0 (Kalimantan Selatan, near Kotabaru and the Meratus Mountains). At 117.0E, 3S, I think this is the area of the upper Barito or Tabalong region, which lies near the Meratus range's northern part and the border between South and East Kalimantan.

The Meratus range sits around 115.5-116 E, so 117E is east of it. The area is probably lowland to hilly terrain, with elevation around 100 m. This is likely close to Paser, with hills at about 50-150 m. I'll guess 80 m.

