10606.text:
Latitude -27.0, longitude 153.0 is off the coast of Queensland, Australia. Moreton Bay and Brisbane sit near -27.47, 153.03. At -27.0 latitude, the coast near Bribie Island is around 153.1-153.2 E. Bribie Island spans roughly -27.0 to -24.9... actually Bribie Island is at about -27.05 to -26.85, and its west side lies near 153.1. Longitude 153.0 at -27.0 is mainland, near Caboolture/Narangba (Caboolture is at -27.08, 152.95). So this point is on land, around Burpengary or Morayfield, at roughly 153.0 E.

Burpengary is at about -27.15, 152.96. At -27.0, 153.0 the point is close to Pumicestone Passage's western shore, near Beachmere (-27.12, 153.05). Sandstone Point is at -27.07, 153.12. So -27.0, 153.0 is inland, near Wamuran/Elimbah (-27.0, 152.95), with an elevation of about 20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":20}

5150.text:
The coordinates 33.0°N, 41.0°E fall in western Iraq, in Anbar province. The location is in the desert south of the Euphrates, near the Syrian and Jordanian border region. The Euphrates passes near Al-Qaim at about 34.4°N, 41.0°E, so this point is well south of it, on the Western Desert plateau.

Elevation in that part of the Western Desert is roughly 500 m. Wadi Hauran lies further south, around 32.5°N, and the plateau there is about 400–600 m. I'll estimate 560 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":560}

8290.text:
The point (-3.0, -159.0) is in the central equatorial Pacific, south of the Line Islands. The nearest land is Starbuck Island at about 5.6°S, 155.9°W, and Malden at 4.0°S, 154.9°W, both far from this point. Washington Island (Teraina) is at 4.7°N, 160.4°W, so it is also far away. That makes the surface Water.

The seafloor depth here is about 5000 to 5300 m. The area lies in the Penrhyn or Central Pacific basin, west of the Line Islands ridge. I estimate roughly 5200 m deep, so the elevation is about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

6504.text:
The point 17°N, 131°W is in the open eastern Pacific, southwest of Baja California and west of Mexico. The seafloor there lies on the Pacific plate, roughly 1,000 km or more west of the East Pacific Rise, and typical depths in this area are about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Clarion Fracture Zone is near 18°N, but it lies at about 3,800 to 4,500 m in this region, so I estimate a depth near 4,200 m.

This is an estimate from typical regional depths, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

10071.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude 163.0 is in the Coral Sea/Southwest Pacific, west of New Caledonia. Grande Terre spans roughly 164–167.5°E at these latitudes, so 163°E is about 100 km west of the northwest coast. This area lies in the New Caledonia Basin and on the Lord Howe Rise side. The Fairway–Lord Howe Rise sits further west, near 162°E, and the New Caledonia Trough lies between the Rise and the island, at depths of about 2500–3500 m.

At 21°S, the northwest lagoon edge sits near 164°E. At 163°E, west of the barrier reef, the seafloor probably slopes down to around 2000–2500 m. The Bellona Plateau and Chesterfield area are further north, around 19–21°S and 158–160°E. The Fairway Basin is around 162–163°E at 22°S, with depths of about 2500 m. I'll estimate -2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

14123.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -13 is off Antarctica's Dronning Maud Land coast, in the Weddell Sea/Lazarev Sea region. The Antarctic coast near 0° longitude lies at about -70, so -67 is roughly 300 km north of the coast. That puts the point in the open ocean, over the continental slope or abyssal plain.

Near 13°W and 67°S, the seafloor is probably deep, around 4,000–5,000 m. The Maud Rise is at about 3°E, 65°S, so it is not close. At 13°W the Weddell Basin and the Astrid Ridge region are possible, but Astrid Ridge is near 12°E. The depth here is likely about -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

10113.text:
The point is at 23°S, 113°W in the southeastern Pacific. It lies on or near the East Pacific Rise flank, west of Easter Island (which is at about 109°W, 27°S). The East Pacific Rise crest near 23°S is at about 113-114°W, so this point is probably right on the ridge crest or close to it. Crest depths there are roughly 2800-3000 m. At these southern latitudes the rise is fast-spreading, with a broad, shallow axis of about 2800 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -2900 m and classify it as Water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

8073.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 127.0°E lies in the Halmahera region of Indonesia. Halmahera spans roughly 0.5°S to 2.0°N and 127–128.7°E. At 1.0°N, 127.0°E, the western coast of Halmahera sits around 127.5°E near Jailolo (1.05°N, 127.47°E), so 127.0°E is west of the island. It would be in the Molucca Sea, between the Ternate/Tidore islands (127.4°E) and Sulawesi's north arm.

Ternate is at 0.79°N, 127.38°E. Going west at 1.0°N, 127.0°E, we are in the Molucca Sea, which is deep. Water depth there is roughly 1,500–2,500 m. The Molucca Sea has a central ridge, and the Talaud islands are farther east at 126.8°E, 4°N, so they are not nearby. The Sangihe arc is to the west near 125°E. At 127.0°E, 1.0°N the depth is probably about 2,000 m, so I'll estimate -2000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

12945.text:
Latitude -53, longitude 151 lies in the Southern Ocean, south-southeast of Tasmania and southeast of Macquarie Island, which is near 54.6°S, 158.9°E. The location is east of the Tasmanian region, in the Tasman Sea/Pacific basin boundary south of Australia. Seafloor there is probably abyssal at about 4500 m depth, and the area is west of the Macquarie Ridge and east of the South Tasman Rise.

The South Tasman Rise is near 48°S, 147°E, which is further north. At 53°S, 151°E the seafloor is the Southeast Indian Ridge/Tasman Basin region, with depths around 4000 to 4500 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1634.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -151.0 is on the North Slope of Alaska. Barrow is at 71.29, -156.8, and Harrison Bay lies around 70.5, -152. Smith Bay is near 71.0, -154.5. At -151.0 and 71.0, the position is east of Harrison Bay and near the Colville delta area, which sits at about -150.5, 70.4.

The coast near -151 is around 70.6–70.7 (Cape Halkett is at 70.8, -152.2). At -151 the coast is probably near 70.65, so 71.0 would be about 40 km offshore in the Beaufort Sea. Water depth there is probably about 20–40 m on the shelf. Offshore of Harrison Bay, the 20 m isobath lies roughly 30–40 km out. I'll estimate -30 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-30}

11145.text:
Latitude -33.0, longitude 151.0 is in New South Wales, Australia. Sydney is at about 151.2 E and 33.87 S, so this point is about 100 km north of Sydney. That puts it near the Hunter region, west of Lake Macquarie and Newcastle. Newcastle is at -32.93, 151.78, so 151.0 is about 75 km west of it. The point is inland, in the Hunter Valley and Wollombi area, probably around Broke or Wollombi.

Wollombi is at about -32.93, 151.14, with an elevation near 50 m. At 151.0 and -33.0, the terrain is hilly sandstone country in Yengo National Park or the Wollombi Brook area, probably 200 m or so. I'll estimate 220 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":220}

2408.text:
Latitude 63.0°N, longitude -43.0° is in southern Greenland's offshore region, southeast of the Greenland coast. Greenland's southern tip (Cape Farewell) is at about 60°N, 44°W. At 63°N the east coast lies around 41–42°W near Kangerlussuaq fjord (68°N is Kangerlussuaq, so not that). The coast at 63°N is roughly 41.5°W, and the area around Skjoldungen sits at about 63.2°N, 41.5°W. At 43°W, 63°N we are likely on the ice sheet margin, about 1.5° of longitude inland. One degree of longitude at 63°N is about 50 km, so this is about 75 km inland from the coast. The ice sheet there is high, roughly 2000 m. The southeast Greenland ice sheet at 63°N, 43°W is probably about 2000–2400 m in elevation.

I'll estimate 2200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

3755.text:
Latitude 49.0°N, longitude 131.0°E is in the Russian Far East, in Khabarovsk Krai. It lies in the Sikhote-Alin range's western side, near the Khor River and Bikin River area. The Bikin basin is around 47°N, 134°E, so this point is farther west, closer to the Amur lowlands. The Amur at Komsomolsk is at about 50.5°N, 137°E. Near 49°N, 131°E the terrain is probably around the Khingan-Bureya area, or the Amur lowlands near Obluchye (49°N, 131°E).

Obluchye is at about 49.0°N, 131.05°E, in the Jewish Autonomous Oblast, in the Little Khingan range. The elevation there is about 300 m, and the surrounding hills are around 400 m. So this is a solid surface, and I estimate about 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

2151.text:
Latitude 67.0°N, longitude 163.0°E is in northeastern Siberia, in the Sakha/Chukotka region near the Kolyma lowlands and the Omolon area. It lies north of the Arctic Circle, east of the Kolyma River and roughly at the Anyuy/Omolon watersheds. The Kolyma Lowland is around 68°N, 160°E. At 67°N, 163°E, the terrain is likely the Anyuy Plateau or the Kolyma foothills, at roughly 300–500 m.

I'm estimating from memory of the regional geography, not from a data lookup, so I chose a mid-range value of 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

9509.text:
The point is at -15.0, 119.0, which is in the Indian Ocean off northwestern Australia. The Kimberley coast is around 125°E at this latitude, and the Western Australian coast near Broome sits at about 122°E, 18°S. So 119°E, 15°S lies offshore on the Sahul Shelf or the Browse Basin area.

The Browse Basin is around 14°S, 123°E. At 119°E, 15°S, I expect the Rowley Shoals region to the south, near 17°S, 119°E. The Scott Reef is at 14°S, 121.7°E. The point at 15°S, 119°E is probably in deeper water, on the continental slope or the Argo Abyssal Plain edge. The Rowley Shoals' Imperieuse Reef is at 17.6°S, 118.9°E, and the shelf edge there is around 18°S. North of that, the Rowley Terrace slopes down to the Argo Abyssal Plain, which is around 5000 m deep. At 15°S, 119°E, depth is probably about 2000–4000 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

4173.text:
The point 43.0°N, 113.0°W is in southern Idaho, on the Snake River Plain. It lies near the Craters of the Moon area to the northeast of American Falls and southwest of Arco. Craters of the Moon is at about 43.2°N, 113.5°W, so this point is east of it.

The point is probably near the Snake River Plain south of the Big Southern Butte area. Big Southern Butte is at 43.39°N, 113.02°W. At 43.0°N, 113.0°W the terrain is about 20 miles southwest of the Idaho National Lab boundary. It is northwest of American Falls Reservoir, whose northern end is near 43.0°N, 112.8°W. The elevation there is about 1,400 m. Aberdeen sits at 1,340 m and Minidoka at 1,250 m. The point is likely on the plain west of the reservoir at around 1,380 m.

