173.text:
Latitude 89°N, longitude 167°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, very close to the North Pole. It is near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs through the pole area. The ridge crest lies roughly along 140°E–90°W, so at 89°N the ridge is on the 140°E side of the pole. At 167°E and 89°N, the point is probably on or near the ridge flank toward the Amerasian side, though I am not certain which side.

Bathymetry near the pole (IBCAO) shows the Lomonosov Ridge at about 1000–1500 m depth near 89°N. The Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side at about 4200 m. The Makarov Basin lies on the other side at about 4000 m. Longitude 167°E at 89°N is about 1° of latitude (111 km) from the pole, on the Makarov side, since the ridge near the pole follows the 140°E to 320°E line. The ridge axis near the pole runs roughly from Greenland (~ -40°) to the Siberian side (~140°E). The 167°E direction is 27° off the ridge axis, which at 111 km distance is about 52 km lateral offset. The ridge is about 50–70 km wide, so the point is probably on the flank on the Makarov side, with a depth around 1500–2500 m. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

1299.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -101.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Melville Island spans roughly 74.5–76.5°N and 105–117°W, so it lies further west. Bathurst Island is around 75–76.5°N, 97–101°W. Cornwallis Island is at 75.1°N, 95°W. Bathurst Island's southern part reaches about 75°N near 100°W, so the point is probably on Bathurst Island or in the water just south of it.

Bathurst Island's southern coast: Bracebridge Inlet is near 75.0°N, 98°W, and Cape Cockburn is at the southwest, near 74.7°N, 100°W. The Polar Bear Pass lowland is at about 75.5°N, 98°W. The west coast near 100–101°W runs from about 75°N northward, with Goodsir Inlet around 75.3°N, 99°W. Overall the point is likely on land at the west of Bathurst Island, or on the coast. Cape Cockburn at roughly 74.8°N, 99.5°W suggests the coast near 75°N is close to 100.5°W. Hence -101 is probably just offshore in Viscount Melville Sound or Penny Strait. I lean toward Solid at low elevation, since the coast there is close. Given that uncertainty, I'll choose Solid with an elevation of about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

11899.text:
The point is at 43°S, 141°W, in the South Pacific. It lies southeast of Tahiti and well north of the Antarctic, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor here is part of the Pacific basin. Around 43°S, 141°W the crust is fairly young, from the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The ridge crest is near 55-57°S at this longitude, so this point is about 12° north of it. The crust there is roughly 30-40 million years old, which gives a depth of about 4000 m. The nearby Pacific basin generally runs about 3800-4200 m deep, but this is an estimate from regional patterns, not a lookup of the actual value. I'll go with about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4050}

12223.text:
