The seabed there is on the continental slope or rise south of Tasmania. The shelf south of Tasmania extends to about -44°. At -45° and 147°E the depth is probably around 3000 to 4000 m. The South Tasman Rise lies at about -47°, with depths of about 1000 to 2000 m. At 147°E and -45° the water is probably around 3,000 m deep, so I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

13255.text:
Latitude -57, longitude 51 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of the Indian Ocean sector. It sits southwest of the Kerguelen Plateau and southeast of Africa, near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Antarctic-Indian basin. The Crozet Islands are at about 46°S, 51°E, so this point is about 11° south of them. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction through roughly 55°S, 0°E, and then northeast toward the Indian Ocean, passing around 50°S, 40°E and 47°S, 52°E. At 51°E, the ridge is therefore near 47°S, so -57 is south of it.

South of the ridge is the Enderby Basin, which has depths of about 4000-5000 m. At -57, 51, the area is likely the abyssal plain of the Australian-Antarctic or Enderby basin, with depth around 4500 m. The Conrad Rise is at about 53°S, 40-50°E, and its depths are about 2000-3000 m. This point is slightly south of the rise, so the depth is probably about 4000 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1000.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude 21.0 E is in Svalbard. Nordaustlandet spans roughly 79–80.5 N and 18–27 E. Lat 79.0, lon 21.0 lies near the southern edge. Hinlopen Strait is at about 19–20 E at 79.5 N, and Wahlenbergfjorden is around 79.9 N. At 79.0 N, 21 E, the point is probably in Storfjorden or Olga Strait, which is between Spitsbergen and Barentsøya/Edgeøya. Barentsøya is at about 78.3 N, 21 E, and Edgeøya is further south. Spitsbergen's east coast at 79 N is around 18–19 E, near Hinlopen's southern mouth. The Nordaustlandet southwest coast (Zorgdragerfjellet) is at about 79.5 N, 20 E.

At 79.0 N, 21 E, I think this is the open sea north of Storfjorden and east of Hinlopen's mouth, which is Water. The Hinlopen southern mouth is near 78.7 N, 19–20 E. Depths in this area are roughly 100–200 m, so I'll estimate -150.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

2197.text:
The point 65°N, 105°W lies in the Northwest Territories/Nunavut region of Canada, in the Canadian Shield barrens. Nearby features include Contwoyto Lake to the northwest and Dubawnt Lake farther east, and Great Bear Lake is farther west. Bathurst Inlet is at about 108°W, 67°N.

At 65°N, 105°W, the Thelon River lies to the east and Aylmer Lake sits near 64°N, 108.5°W. Lac de Gras is around 64.5°N, 110.5°W. At 105°W, 65°N the location is roughly north of Great Slave Lake, in the area of Beverly Lake and Garry Lake. Beverly Lake is at about 64.7°N, 100°W, so this point is west of it. Terrain there is probably around 400 m, and I'm treating it as land. Typical elevations in the area run from about 300 to 450 m, and I'll pick 400.

