Terrain there is likely 1000–1500 m, with a plateau at about 1000 m and a valley floor near 900 m. I'll pick 1250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1250}

13443.text:
The point at 59°S, 67°E is in the Southern Ocean, southwest of the Kerguelen Plateau and southeast of Crozet. The Kerguelen Plateau lies at roughly 50°S, 70°E, so 59°S is south of it. In this area the seafloor sits in the Australian-Antarctic Basin, or possibly the southern end of the plateau near the Princess Elizabeth Trough region. The Princess Elizabeth Trough lies near 62°S, 80°E.

At 59°S, 67°E the seafloor is probably deep, around 3,000 to 4,000 m. The Kerguelen Plateau's southern part (the Elan Bank and the area near 59°S, 80°E) is shallower. West of the plateau, near 60°S, 65°E, the depth is about 3,500 m, so I estimate about -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

10035.text:
Location: -21°, 91°E is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and Rodriguez, southwest of Australia. The Central Indian Basin lies north of about 20°S, and the area near 21°S 91°E lies close to the Ninetyeast Ridge (around 90°E). The ridge crest is at roughly 1,500-3,000 m depth at its northern parts. At 21°S, the ridge depth is about 2,000-3,000 m. The ridge lies near 90-91°E in this latitude range, so the depth is probably around 3,000 m.

The Ninetyeast Ridge near 21°S: DSDP Site 214 is at 11°S, Site 254 is at 30°S (1,253 m depth), and Site 253 is at 24.9°S, 87.4°E (1,962 m). At 21°S the ridge is at about 90°E, with depth perhaps 2,000 m. At 91°E we may be on the eastern flank, so a guess of around -2,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

2124.text:
The point is at 67°N, 109°E, in central Siberia, in the Anabar/Olenek region on the Central Siberian Plateau. It lies near the Markha River basin, south of the Anabar Shield. The Vilyuy plateau and Putorana are further west, so this is a rough regional estimate.

At about 67°N, 109°E, the terrain is in the upper Popigai/Kotuy/Olenek area. The Kotuy River is around 103°E, and the Anabar River lies around 113°E at 71°N. The Olenek at 67°N is near 112°E. This location sits on the Anabar plateau's southern part or the Central Siberian Plateau, where elevations are typically 300–500 m. I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

7032.text:
The point at 11°N, 155°W is in the central Pacific, south of the Hawaiian Islands. The Big Island of Hawaii is at about 19–20°N, so this point is roughly 800 km south of it, in open ocean. The seafloor there is abyssal plain, typically 5000 to 5500 m deep.

The Pacific plate in this region has an age of about 90 to 100 million years, and the seafloor depth is about 5300 m. I'm estimating from the general depth of this area, not from a specific chart value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

14206.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 153 is off the coast of East Antarctica (Adélie Land / Wilkes Land area). The Antarctic coast near 153°E is around -66.5 to -67, which is close to the Mertz Glacier region (around 145°E). At 153°E the coast is near 66°S, so -67 is probably inland on the ice sheet, or on the shelf near the coast.

Checking Wilkes Land: the coastline near 150°E is around 68°S, with the Mertz Glacier at 145°E, 67.5°S. Further east, the George V Coast ends near 150°E. Around 153°E, the Oates Coast begins at about 155°E and runs near 69°S. At -67, 153°E, the point is in the Southern Ocean, since the coast is farther south at about 68.5°S. The continental shelf there is deep, with a depth of roughly 2000-3000 m. The slope runs from about 500 m at the shelf edge to the abyssal plain, and the shelf edge is near 66°S. At 67°S it would be on the outer shelf or upper slope, so I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

7262.text:
The point at 9°N, 55°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, northeast of Suriname and French Guiana. The Suriname coast is near 6°N, so this point is about 3° (roughly 330 km) offshore. The continental shelf there extends about 100-150 km out, and the point is beyond it on the continental slope or the Demerara Rise.

The Demerara Rise is a plateau at about 5-6°N, 52-55°W with depths of 1000-3000 m. At 9°N the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain edge, at about 4500 m depth. Bathymetry north of the Demerara Rise drops to about 4000-5000 m. I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

12415.text:
The point (-47.0, 171.0) lies southeast of New Zealand's South Island, in the Pacific Ocean. The South Island's east coast near Banks Peninsula is at about 43.7°S, 172.7°E, and Dunedin is at about 45.9°S, 170.5°E. Because 47°S is south of Dunedin and Stewart Island's east coast, the point is east of Stewart Island (which spans roughly 167.5–168.2°E) and well off the coast, so it is open ocean.

This area is the Southland/Otago continental shelf and slope. The shelf east of Otago extends roughly 30–50 km offshore, and the Bounty Trough lies to the east. At 171°E, 47°S, the point is about 60 km east of the coast near 46.5°S, 169.8°E. The Otago shelf there is about 100–150 m deep at the shelf edge. Further south at 47°S, the Campbell Plateau begins, with depths of about 500–700 m. The Campbell Plateau's northern edge is around 47–48°S at 171°E, and the slope toward the Bounty Trough lies to the north. I estimate the depth at roughly 500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

7999.text:
The point (1.0°N, 21.0°W) lies in the equatorial Atlantic, just north of the equator and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge crosses the equator near 15-20°W, and the Romanche and Chain fracture zones sit around 14-18°W. At 21°W the seafloor is probably on the western flank of the ridge, with depths around 4000-5000 m.

Between the St. Paul fracture zone (about 29°W) and the Romanche fracture zone, the ridge axis near 1°N is at roughly 20-25°W. The Chain fracture zone is near 1°S and the Romanche near 0°, so a point at 1°N, 21°W could be close to the ridge axis or a transform valley. The Strakhov and Vema fracture zones are also nearby. I can't pin the exact local feature, so I'll estimate a depth of about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

13930.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -39 is in the Weddell Sea, off the coast of Antarctica. The Antarctic coast near 0°W to 60°W lies far to the south, around -70 to -78 latitude, though the Antarctic Peninsula's tip reaches about -63 at longitude -57. At -39 longitude the coast is at roughly -71 to -72 (Queen Maud Land and Coats Land to the east, Filchner area to the south), so this point is open ocean in the northern Weddell Sea.

The seabed there is likely abyssal, around 4000 to 5000 m deep. The Weddell Basin is about 4800 m deep. At -65, -39 the point sits north of the Weddell Abyssal Plain, near the South Scotia Ridge and the Weddell Sea gyre, so I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

14091.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -77 is in the Bellingshausen Sea, west of the Antarctic Peninsula. Adelaide Island sits at about -67.5, -68.5, so -77 is roughly 280 km further west, on the continental shelf edge or slope. The shelf there is about 500 m deep, with the shelf break around -70 to -75 west of Adelaide Island. At -77 longitude and -67 latitude, the sea floor is probably the continental rise or slope, so I estimate about -2000 m.

The Bellingshausen Sea shelf break lies near -71 to -72 longitude at latitude -68 to -69. Further north and west, the slope descends. At -77, -67, the depth is probably around 3000 m. I'll say -2800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

4617.text:
Latitude 39.0 N, longitude 55.0 E is in Turkmenistan, east of the Caspian Sea. The Caspian coast at Türkmenbaşy is near 53.0 E, and the Balkan region sits around 54-55 E. At 39.0 N, 55.0 E we are inland, in the Karakum Desert, south of the Great Balkan range and north of the Kopet Dag.

The Kopet Dag foothills near Kazandzhik lie at about 39.0 N, 55.5 E, and Kazandzhik's elevation is around 0 m or slightly below. Nearby Gyzylarbat (Serdar) is at 39.26 N, 56.28 E, with an elevation of about 100 m. Balkanabat is at 39.5 N, 54.37 E, with an elevation of about 80 m. The Uzboy area has low terrain, and at 39.0 N, 55.0 E the elevation is probably about 0 to 50 m. I'll estimate 30 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":30}

4745.text:
The point 37°N, 49°W lies in the North Atlantic, southeast of Newfoundland. It is west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and south of the Grand Banks. The New England Seamounts run along roughly 35-40°N, from 67°W to 50°W, and the Corner Rise seamounts sit near 35°N, 51°W. So the point is near the eastern end of that seamount chain, but probably on abyssal plain at about 5000 m depth.

The Azores-Biscay Rise and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 37°N are at about 30-40°W, so they are not a factor. Around 49°W the Atlantic basin is about 5000 m deep. The Corner Rise seamounts span roughly 34-36°N and 48-52°W, so the point at 37°N is on their northern fringe. The Nashville and Gilliss seamounts are near 35°N, 50°W. The seafloor there is probably about 4500-5000 m deep, so I'll estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

11542.text:
Latitude -39, longitude -135 lies in the South Pacific Ocean, well southeast of Tahiti and west of Chile. The location is on the Pacific plate, south of the Austral and Pitcairn island chains and north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge.

Seafloor depth here is probably about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The East Pacific Rise is far to the east, near 115°W at this latitude, so the crust at 135°W is older and deeper. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies farther south. I estimate a depth of about 4,300 m, which is roughly consistent with the ~4,000 m typical of this region, so I'll use about -4,250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4250}

14075.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -109 is in the Bellingshausen Sea, off the coast of Antarctica. The Antarctic coast near Thurston Island is at about -72 latitude, so -67 is well north of land. The continental shelf in this sector lies further south, so at -67, -109 we are likely beyond the shelf edge, on the continental rise or abyssal plain. The depth there is probably around 4000 to 4500 m.

I could not recall a specific bathymetry value for this point, so the figure is an estimate. I chose -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

1977.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude 175.0 (east) lies in the East Siberian Sea / Chukchi Sea region. Wrangel Island sits at about 71°N, 179°W, so it is not at this point. The Chukotka coast at 175°E is around 68.5-69°N. Cape Shelagsky is at about 170°E, and Chaunskaya Bay is at about 170°E. At 175°E the coast runs near 69.5°N, around the Pegtymel and Kolyuchinskaya Bay area (Kolyuchin Island is at 67.4°N, 174.6°W). So 175°E is east of Chaunskaya Bay and west of Cape Billings.

The Chukotka coast near 175°E, 69°N is probably inland, since the coast at that longitude is near 69.7°N (Ayon Island is at 69.9°N, 168.5°E; Pegtymel at 69.9°N, 177°E). So this point is on land, in the Chukotka lowlands or hills at about 69°N, 175°E. The Anyuy and Chukotka mountains lie in this area, so elevation is probably a few hundred meters. The Pegtymel river basin is lowland, and 175°E at 69°N is likely tundra hills at roughly 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

1305.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -89.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Lancaster Sound sits near 74.2°N, 84°W, and Prince Regent Inlet runs near 90°W. Somerset Island spans roughly 72–74.5°N, 93–96°W, and Devon Island lies around 75.5°N, 80–92°W.

At 75°N, 89°W we are near the south coast of Devon Island. Devon's south coast is around 74.6°N near 89°W, and Wellington Channel is further west at about 92°W. Maxwell Bay, on Devon's south coast, is near 74.5°N, 89°W. Devon Island's south coast at 90°W is roughly 74.7°N. So 75.0°N is about 30 km inland, on the Devon Island plateau, where the interior is higher and the terrain is rolling uplands with elevation roughly 300–600 m. Near the south coast of eastern Devon, the Cape Sparbo area is further north. I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

1752.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 85.0°E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Russia. The Taymyr peninsula's northern coast lies around 76-77°N. At 71°N and 85°E, the point sits in the Yenisei Gulf region, which runs roughly 72-73°N at 80°E. Lake Taymyr is at about 74.5°N, so it is not the relevant feature.

The Yenisei Gulf (Yenisei Bay) extends from about 70.5°N to 73°N, with longitudes of about 79-83°E. At 85°E and 71°N we are east of the gulf, on the Taymyr lowlands near the Pyasina and Dudypta area. This is the Putorana foothills region to the south and the North Siberian lowland to the north, so it is probably land. The elevation there is probably about 50-100 m. The Yenisei Gulf's east shore is near 83°E at 72°N. At 71°N, the Yenisei is around 83°E near Dudinka (69.4°N, 86°E). So 71°N, 85°E is probably land on the lowlands east of the river's lower reaches, with an elevation of around 40 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":45}

6729.text:
The point 15°N, 41°W lies in the central North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 15°N is at about 45°W, so the point is east of that axis. The Fifteen-Twenty fracture zone is near 15°N, and the ridge axis sits around 45°W there.

The point at 41°W is therefore on the African-plate flank, about 4 degrees (roughly 430 km) east of the axis. Seafloor age there is about 20 Myr, which suggests a depth of about 3,500 to 4,000 m. The ridge crest itself is about 3,000 m deep, and the flanks deepen to about 4,500 m at 40 Myr. For 20 Myr I estimate about 3,900 m. Fracture zone topography can alter this, so I settle on about 4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

935.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -109.0 falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Melville Island spans roughly 74.5–76.5°N, so it lies to the south. Prince Patrick Island is around 76.5°N, 119°W, also south. Borden Island is near 78.5°N, 111°W, and Brock Island is near 77.9°N, 114.5°W. Meighen Island is at 80°N, 99°W. Ellef Ringnes Island is around 78.5°N, 101.5°W, with its west coast near 103°W. Borden Island spans about 78.2–78.8°N and 110–112°W.

So 79°N, 109°W is probably in the Peary Channel or the sea north of Borden Island, in the Arctic Ocean/Sverdrup Basin. Between Borden Island and Ellef Ringnes there is open water, and Meighen Island is farther east at 99°W. The point is most likely water, with shelf depths of roughly 200–300 m. The sea in this area, the Wilkins Strait or the Massey Sound area, is probably around 300 m deep. I'll guess about -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

10925.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 71 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and well west of Australia. It lies roughly in the central Indian Ocean basin, west of the Ninetyeast Ridge and east of the Central Indian Ridge. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs further south, and the Mid-Indian Basin floor there is typically 4000-5000 m deep.

At 31°S, 71°E I believe the area is within the Central Indian Basin or its southern extension, where depths are around 4500 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

14501.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 23 is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The Sør Rondane Mountains lie around 72°S, 25°E, and the Wohlthat Mountains around 71.5°S, 13°E. At 71°S, 23°E the point is probably on the ice sheet just inland of the coast. The coast sits near 70°S at 24°E, with the Princess Ragnhild Coast and Lazarev Sea further west. The Sør Rondane range spans roughly 22–28°E at 71.5–72.5°S, so the point is just north of or at the edge of the mountains. The ice surface there is likely around 2,500 m, since the ice slopes up from the coast and the Sør Rondane ice is about 3,000 m at 72°S.

The coast near 23°E is at about 69.8°S, with the ice shelf edge near 70°S. At 71°S the point is about 100 km inland, which suggests a surface elevation of about 1,500 m, though the Sør Rondane blocks the flow so the ice there may be higher. I'll go with roughly 2,000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

12201.text:
Location: -45, 103E is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. This is south of the Southeast Indian Ridge? The ridge near 45S lies around 100-110E (the Australian-Antarctic Ridge region). At 45S, the SE Indian Ridge crosses about 45S at ~100E, with depths around 2500-3500 m. The Amsterdam-St Paul plateau lies at ~38S, 78E. At 103E, 45S the ridge axis is near 46S at 100E and ~48S at 110E, so this is near the ridge flank, with depth about 3000 m.

I estimate about -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

9363.text:
The point (-15, -173) lies in the South Pacific, northeast of Tonga and south of Tokelau, and west of Samoa (Samoa is around -14, -172). Savai'i is at about -13.6 latitude and -172.5 longitude, so the point is south of Samoa and west of the American Samoa islands. Upolu sits at about -13.9 and -171.7. At -15, -173 the point is about 100 km south of Savai'i, in open ocean.

The seabed there is probably the deep ocean floor, near the Samoa Passage region. The Tonga Trench is farther west, around -175 to -173. Depths near the Samoan platform south of the islands are around 4000 to 5000 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

9319.text:
The point at -13.0, 99.0 is in the eastern Indian Ocean, roughly 1,000 km southwest of Java and west of the Cocos (Keeling) Islands, which sit near 12°S, 96.8°E.

The seafloor there is part of the Indian Ocean abyssal plain, with depths of about 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Wharton Basin lies to the north. The Investigator Fracture Zone is near 98–99°E, though at 13°S the area is more likely abyssal plain. Cocos Island region depths are around 5,000 m, and the Java Trench to the northeast is deeper. I'm estimating from the regional pattern and have not checked a bathymetry dataset, so I'll take -5,300 m as a typical value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

4730.text:
The coordinates 37.0, -79.0 fall in southern Virginia, near the Blue Ridge Piedmont edge. This area is close to Bedford and Franklin counties, south of Roanoke. Smith Mountain Lake lies near 37.05, -79.55, which is farther west, so it does not affect this point.

The point is east of Roanoke (about -79.94) and near Rocky Mount (-79.89), so it is further east. Longitude -79.0 at latitude 37.0 is near Altavista (37.1, -79.28) and Gretna (36.96, -79.36). Moving east, Brookneal is at -78.95, 37.05. So the point lies close to Brookneal, in the Roanoke River/Staunton River area of the Piedmont. The Staunton River is just to the south, near Brookneal. Terrain there is roughly 150-200 m, and I estimate about 170 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

8350.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude -39.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean off northeastern Brazil. The Brazilian coast near Fortaleza sits at about -3.7 latitude and -38.5 longitude. Fortaleza is at -38.5, and the coast runs northwest from there. At longitude -39.0 the coast is around latitude -3.2 (Paracuru is at -3.4, -39.0). So -3.0, -39.0 is slightly offshore, about 40 km north of Paracuru.

The continental shelf is narrow here, roughly 50 km wide, with depths of about 40-70 m at 40 km offshore. The shelf break is at about 60-80 m depth. I estimate the seabed at about -40 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

8602.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 105.0 lies in the Indian Ocean, southwest of the southern tip of Sumatra (Lampung, around 105.2E, -5.5). Lampung's coast near Bandar Lampung is at about -5.4, 105.25. Sunda Strait is around -5.9, 105.8. At -5.0, 105.0, the point is in the sea west of Lampung, so it is water.

The area west of Sumatra's southern tip has a shelf that is shallow near the coast, but at 105.0E the point is about 50 km offshore from the coast near Krui (-5.2, 103.9)? Krui is at 103.9E, so the west coast sits at 104E and the point at 105E is on the other side. The south coast of Lampung (Teluk Semangka) is around 104.6E, -5.6. The Sunda Strait is at 105.8E to the east, and the point at -5.0, 105.0 is on the Lampung land mass. Lampung spans about 103.6–105.8E and -3.7 to -6.0. Lampung's east coast is at about 105.8E at -5.7 and at about 105.7E at -5.0. So -5.0, 105.0 is inland, in central Lampung near Kotabumi (-4.83, 104.9) and Gunung Sugih (-4.9, 105.2). That makes it land, with an elevation of roughly 50 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":45}

14992.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -75 is in West Antarctica, in the Ellsworth Land region. Longitude -75 at latitude -77 is south of Thurston Island and Eights Coast. The Pine Island Bay area is around -75 lat, -100 lon. Ellsworth Land and the Orville Coast sit near -75 to -80 lat and -75 to -80 lon. The Ronne Ice Shelf is at about -77 lat and -60 lon. Around -75 lon the Ellsworth Mountains are at about -80 lat and -85 lon.

At -77 lat and -75 lon, the position is probably inland on the Orville Coast or Ellsworth Land, with the ice sheet grounded and the surface elevation around 1000 m. The Fletcher Ice Piedmont/Rutford Ice Stream area lies around -78 lat and -83 lon, and the Ronne Ice Shelf front is near -75 lon at around -75.5 lat. So at -77 lat it could be Ronne Ice Shelf, which extends west to about -80 lon at -77 lat. The Ronne Ice Shelf covers lat -75 to -83 and lon -30 to -80, so -77, -75 is likely on the ice shelf. The grounding line near Orville Coast is around -77.5 lat and -75 lon, which makes it uncertain. The Ronne shelf's western part near Rutford and Institute ice streams lies at -79 lat. At -77 lat and -75 lon, I believe it is the shelf, with seabed around -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

8177.text:
The point (-1.0, -25.0) is in the equatorial Atlantic, west of Africa and east of Brazil. It lies near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is around 28-30°W at the equator, and the Romanche and Chain fracture zones are near 0°, so this is to the east of the ridge axis.

The ridge axis near the equator is roughly at 30°W at the St Paul fracture zone (about 1°N), where the St Peter and St Paul rocks sit at 29.3°W. At 25°W and 1°S the seafloor is on the eastern flank, about 5 My old, with typical depths of 3500-4500 m. The Romanche fracture zone runs from roughly 0° to -1° at about 18-24°W, and the Chain fracture zone sits near -1.5°S. At -1°S, 25°W, the point could fall in the Romanche trough area, but a typical flank depth of about -4000 m seems a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

12530.text:
The point is at 49°S, 41°E, in the southwest Indian Ocean. It lies southwest of the Crozet Islands, which are near 46°S, 51°E, and east of the Prince Edward Islands, which are near 46.9°S, 37.7°E. The area is south of the Southwest Indian Ridge, which runs roughly from 40°E, 47°S toward 50°E, 50°S. The Del Cano Rise is around 45°S, 45°E.

At 49°S, 41°E, I expect the seafloor to be in the Crozet Basin or near the ridge. The Southwest Indian Ridge axis lies near 52°S at 20°E and about 48°S at 40°E, so this point is near the ridge and likely on its flank. Depths there are about 3000 to 4000 m. The Africana fracture zone and the Discovery fracture zones are also in this region, so I'll estimate about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

8621.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 143.0 is in Papua New Guinea, in the East Sepik/Madang area. The Sepik River lowlands lie around 143°E at about -4°S, and the coast is near -3.5°S. At -5.0 the point is inland, within the mountainous region south of the Sepik plains, near the Schrader and Hunstein ranges. The Ramu River valley is to the southeast, and the Sepik headwaters are further south.

At 143.0E, -5.0, the terrain is likely the foothills north of the central highlands. The upper Sepik plain, around Ambunti at 142.8E, -4.2S, is at roughly 50 m. Further south, around -5.0, the terrain rises into mountains, perhaps 500–1000 m. The Jimi River area lies at about 144.5E, so this point is probably in the Hunstein range or the Sepik-Ramu divide, near Josephstaal and the Simbai area at 144.5E. I'll estimate about 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

3049.text:
Latitude 57.0°N, longitude 159.0°E is in Kamchatka, Russia. The Kamchatka peninsula at 57°N spans roughly 156°E to 162°E, so this point is on land.

Near 57°N, the Sredinny Range lies to the west, around 158°E, and the Central Kamchatka Depression, where the Kamchatka River runs, lies to the east at around 159–160°E. At 57°N the river valley sits near 160°E. At 159°E, the point is probably the western slope of the depression or the foothills of the Sredinny Range, so the elevation is likely a few hundred meters. The Sredinny Range at this latitude includes peaks such as Ichinsky (55.7°N), and the area near 57°N has lowlands around Tigil to the west. I estimate about 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

6440.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 101.0°E lies in northern Thailand/Laos border region. Near Nan province, Uttaradit, or Xayaburi in Laos. The Mekong is at about 101.0°E near 19.0°N in Laos (Xayaburi province, around Pak Lay at 18.2°N, 101.4°E). At 19°N, 101°E it is mountainous, in Thailand's Nan/Loei area or Laos's Xayaburi highlands. Elevation is probably around 500 m.

The Nan river at 19°N is about 100.8°E. The Thai-Laos border near Thung Chang is at 19.4°N, 101.0°E. So 19.0°N, 101.0°E is probably in the Laos hills of Xayaburi/Nan border with elevation around 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

9352.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 165E is in the Coral Sea / Solomon Sea region. Santa Cruz Islands lie around 10.7S, 166E, and Vanuatu's Torres Islands are around 13.3S, 166.6E. Banks Islands are at about 13.8S, 167.5E. So 13S, 165E is west of Vanuatu, in the North Fiji Basin edge or the Coral Sea basin. The area west of the New Hebrides Trench is the d'Entrecasteaux region... actually the trench is at about 166-167E near 13S. The point at 165E is in the Coral Sea basin, with depths of around 3000-4000 m. Rennell Island is at 11.6S, 160E, so it is not close. The Santa Cruz Trench is further north.

At 165E and 13S, the depth is probably about -3500 m. The area lies between the Solomon Islands and Vanuatu, in the Coral Sea basin/Santa Cruz basin, at roughly -3000 to -4000 m. I'll pick -3400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

14907.text:
