{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

10707.text:
Latitude -29, longitude -5 lies in the South Atlantic, west of Namibia and South Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. This area is in the Cape Basin or Angola Basin region. The Walvis Ridge sits further north at around 20-30°S, near 0° longitude. At -29, -5 the point is likely in the Cape Basin abyssal plain, with depths of about 4500-5000 m.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 29°S lies at about -13° longitude, so -5° is east of it, on the flank. Depth there is likely around 4800 m. The Cape Basin has depths of about 5000 m, and the Rio Grande Rise is far west, so I'll estimate -4900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1133.text:
Latitude 77.0 N, longitude -73.0 W puts this in northwest Greenland, near Wolstenholme Fjord and Thule (Pituffik) at about 76.5 N, -68.7 W. Moving west along 77 N, -73 W falls in the Baffin Bay / Smith Sound region. Smith Sound lies near 78 N, -74 W, and Kane Basin is further north. At 77 N, -73 W, the position is west of Greenland's coast, which sits near -70 W at that latitude (Inglefield Land is further north). Melville Bay is south of this. Northern Baffin Bay near 77 N, -73 W is open water, likely the North Water polynya area. Ellesmere Island's coast is around -78 W at 77 N, so this point is mid-water.

Depths in northern Baffin Bay are around 500-700 m, with the shelf off Greenland shallower. I'll estimate about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

15407.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 35 is in East Antarctica, in the area of the Recovery Glacier and Shackleton Range. The Shackleton Range lies near 80.5°S, 25°W, so it is not at this longitude. At 35°E and 81°S, the location is inland in Dronning Maud Land, near the Pensacola Mountains/Dufek region, further south of the Wohlthat Mountains. I expect the ice sheet there to be high, around 3000 m. The Queen Maud Land plateau at 81°S, 35°E is probably around 3200 m. Ice-surface elevations near the Dome Fuji region (77°S, 39°E) are about 3800 m, and at 81°S, 35°E they are probably around 3500 m.

I'll estimate 3400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3400}

3244.text:
The point is at 53.0°N, 171.0°W, in the North Pacific south of the Aleutian Islands. The Aleutians sit at roughly 52–53°N between about 172°E and 170°W, so I need to check for nearby islands. Atka is near 174°W, Amlia near 173°W, and Seguam near 172.5°W. Umnak and Unalaska lie further east, at about 168°W and 166°W. At 171°W the nearest land is the Islands of Four Mountains (Chuginadak, around 169.7°W, 52.8°N), which is about 80 km east. Yunaska is near 170.6°W, 52.6°N, so it is close, but 53.0°N, 171.0°W is probably north of it, in the Bering Sea side or the passage.

The Aleutian arc is at about 52.5°N around 171°W, with the trench to the south. Near 53°N, 171°W, the point is likely just north of the arc on the Bering Sea side. The shelf there drops off quickly to depths of about 1000–3000 m. The Bering Sea side north of the arc is typically around 2000 m deep, but at 0.5° north of the arc it might be only about 500–1500 m. I'll estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

5924.text:
Latitude 25°N, longitude 149°E is in the western Pacific, east of the Philippine Sea and south of Japan. That places it in the Philippine Sea Plate region, probably in the West Mariana Basin or near the Mariana Ridge.

The West Mariana Ridge runs near 137–143°E. The Mariana Trench arc is at about 145°E at 20°N. At 25°N, 149°E, the point is east of the Mariana Trench axis, which sits around 143–144°E at that latitude. That puts it on the Pacific plate, in the old seafloor east of the Izu-Bonin-Mariana trench, near the Magellan Seamounts and Marcus-Wake seamounts region. The Pacific plate depth there is typically about 5,500–6,000 m, though seamounts are possible. I'll estimate -5800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

15458.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 137 is in East Antarctica, on the Ross Sea side. Longitude 137°E at 81°S is inland, in the region near Wilkes Land and the Dome C / Vostok area. Dome C is at 75°S, 123°E, and Vostok is at 78°S, 106°E. At 81°S, 137°E, the surface is around 2,800-3,000 m on the plateau, with the Transantarctic Mountains farther east at about 160°E. This is likely grounded ice, with a surface elevation near 2,900 m.

The nearby Aurora Basin region lies around 71°S, 111°E, which is too far away to be relevant. A rough guess for the ice surface here is about 2,850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2850}

4395.text:
The point 41.0°N, 29.0°W is in the North Atlantic, just east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and north of the Azores. The ridge axis near 41°N is at roughly 29.5°W, and the Azores plateau lies to the south, so the point is close to the ridge crest.

Near 40°N the ridge is shallow, with depths of about 1500–3000 m. The Azores islands of Flores and Corvo sit at about 31°W, 39.5°N, which is west of the ridge, and the ridge axis near 38–39°N is at about 27–28°W. At 41°N the axis is at roughly 29.5°W, so the point lies on the ridge flank. Depths there are probably around 2500–3000 m, so I estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

1288.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude -123.0 W falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Banks Island spans roughly 71.5–74.4 N and 115–125 W, so 75 N lies just north of its tip. Prince Patrick Island sits around 76–77 N, 117–124 W. The point is therefore probably in the water between them: M'Clure Strait lies to the northeast, and the Prince of Wales Strait is farther east. Banks Island's northern tip, Cape Kellett, is at the southwest, and its northwest tip is near 74.3 N, 124.7 W, so 75 N is about 0.7° north of that, in the water.

This stretch is the northern end of M'Clure Strait, where it opens toward the Beaufort Sea and the Arctic Ocean. The water there is probably deep, since the continental shelf edge is nearby. Banks Island's northwest coast has a narrow shelf, and the shelf break is around 100–200 m. At 75 N, 123 W the depth is probably a few hundred meters. I'll estimate about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

11531.text:
The point -39, -157 is in the South Pacific, roughly east of New Zealand and southeast of the Cook and Austral Islands. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

For depth, the South Pacific basin in this region is typically 4,500 to 5,000 m. The area lies on the Pacific plate, with seafloor age around 60-80 Ma. I'd estimate about -4,900 m, but this is from general knowledge of the region rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

3500.text:
The point 51°N, 19°W is in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and west of Ireland. It lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, whose axis near 51°N is around 29–30°W (the Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone is at about 52°N, 30°W). At 19°W the position falls in the West European Basin / Iceland Basin area. The Iceland Basin there is typically about 3000–4000 m deep. The Hatton–Rockall area lies further north, and Rockall Plateau is near 57°N, 13°W. At 51°N, 19°W, depths are about 4000 m, since the abyssal plain of the West European Basin lies around 4500 m.

I estimate roughly -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14626.text:
Location: -73°, -87° is in West Antarctica, in the Ellsworth Land/Amundsen-Bellingshausen sector. At longitude 87°W and latitude 73°S, this is offshore of the Eights Coast and Thurston Island, which lies at about 72°S, 99°W. The Bellingshausen Sea is around 70-72°S, and Ellsworth Land coast runs near 73°S at 80-90°W, where the Abbot and Venable ice shelves sit further west. At 87°W, 73°S, this is probably near the Ronne Ice Shelf's western edge or the Ellsworth Land coast. The Rydberg Peninsula is near 76°S, 80°W, and the Hollick-Kenyon Plateau is near 77°S, 100°W.

The Bellingshausen Sea coast near 73°S, 87°W is probably the ice front of the Ferrero Bay / Stange Ice Shelf region. Stange Ice Shelf is at about 73.5°S, 74°W, so 87°W is west of it. Between Stange and Abbot lies Ellsworth Land coast with the Spaatz Island and Dyer Plateau. At 87°W, 73°S is probably the Bellingshausen Sea with Thurston Island to the west and the Eights Coast beyond, so this is probably open water or sea ice over a continental shelf of about -500 m. Seabed depth there could be around -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

549.text:
The point at 83°N, 161°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Alaska and the Chukchi Borderland. It sits near the Alpha Ridge and the Canada Basin margin. The Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. The Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge rises to roughly 1500-2500 m.

Longitude 161°W at 83°N is north of the East Siberian and Chukchi region, and the Mendeleev Ridge lies around 175°E to 180°. The Canada Basin spans roughly 150°W and the Alpha Ridge extends toward Ellesmere at about 120°W. So 161°W at 83°N is probably in the Canada Basin's northern part or on the flank of the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge. IBCAO depths in that area are around 2000-3000 m. I'll estimate about -2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

12465.text:
The point (-49, -89) lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of southern Chile. It is south of the Chile Rise and west of the Chile Trench, on the Nazca/Antarctic plate region, so it is water.

The seafloor here is probably abyssal plain or ridge flank. Depths in this part of the Pacific run about 3,500–4,000 m. The East Pacific Rise lies far to the west, and the Chile Rise to the north runs from about -46 at the coast to -38 at 90W, so I'm not sure of the exact position. At -49, -89 the Antarctic plate lies to the south and the seafloor is relatively young, so it may be shallower, around 3,500–4,000 m deep. I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7426.text:
The point at 7.0°N, 87.0°W is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 600 km southwest of Costa Rica. Cocos Island lies at about 5.5°N, 87°W, so this point is about 1.5° north of it, which is open water.

The seafloor here is part of the Cocos Ridge area and the surrounding Cocos Plate. The Cocos Ridge runs northeast from the Galápagos toward Costa Rica, and Cocos Island sits on it. At 7°N, 87°W the point is probably west of the ridge crest, on the flanks or on the plate floor, where depths are about 2500–3500 m. The ridge crest depth near Cocos Island is around 2000 m. North of the island the ridge continues east-northeast toward 8°N, 85°W. The point at 87°W is therefore likely on the ridge's northwestern flank, and I estimate a depth of about 2800 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

6405.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 31.0°E lies in northern Sudan, in the Nubian Desert west of the Nile. The Nile near Dongola is at about 19.2°N, 30.5°E, so this point is just east of it. The terrain there is desert plateau with an elevation of roughly 250 m. Lake Nubia reaches about 21°N and is not near this point.

This is an estimate from memory of the regional terrain, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":260}

15417.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 55E is in East Antarctica, inland. Near Dome Argus, which is at about -80.4, 77E. At 55E, 81S lies in Princess Elizabeth Land / Queen Maud Land, near the Recovery Glacier and Shackleton Range region (Shackleton Range is at about -80.5, -25). At 55E, 81S, the ice surface is high, around 2500-3000 m. The Lambert Glacier is at 70E, -73. The Dome Fuji is at 39E, -77.3 with 3800 m. Dome Argus is at 77E, -80.4 with 4093 m. Toward 55E, 81S, the elevation is probably around 3500 m.

Ice surface elevation of about 3500 m is reasonable, so I'll go with that.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3500}

7544.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 149.0°E is in the western Pacific, near Chuuk (Truk) in Micronesia. Chuuk Lagoon sits at about 7.4°N, 151.8°E, so this point is well west of it. The area around 7°N, 149°E is open ocean between Chuuk and Pohnpei or Satawan. It is probably in the Caroline Basin or near the Caroline Ridge.

The Caroline Islands lie roughly at 150-152°E in this latitude band. Points further west, such as Namonuito at 8.7°N, 150°E, and the Nomoi Islands at 5.5°N, 153°E, are separate. The Mortlocks are at about 5.5°N, 153°E, and Pulap is at about 7.6°N, 149.4°E. Pulap and Tamatam are atolls near 7.6°N, 149.4°E, so the point is about 60 km south of them. The ocean floor there is probably deep, though it could be on the Caroline Ridge area. I estimate a depth of about 4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

1912.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude 45.0 is in the Russian Arctic, in the Kanin Peninsula/Mezen area. The Kanin Peninsula spans roughly 43-47°E at 68°N, with the Barents Sea coast to the north. At 69°N, 45°E the point is in the Barents Sea (the White Sea mouth is further south), north of Kanin Nos, which sits at about 68.6°N, 43.3°E. The north coast of the Kanin Peninsula is around 68.5°N at 45°E, so 69°N is probably in the Barents Sea, about 50 km offshore. The sea there is shallow, roughly 100 m deep.

I could not pin the depth down more precisely, so I am estimating about -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

978.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude -23.0 W is in northeast Greenland. This is near the Nioghalvfjerdsfjorden (79 North Glacier) area, which sits around 79.5N, -22 W. The floating tongue spans roughly 79.3-79.7N and -18 to -22 W. At 79.0N, -23 W, we are probably south of the glacier fjord, on the land of Lambert Land or Hovgaard Ø... Hovgaard Ø is at about 80N. Westward of 79N, -23 W is likely the ice sheet interior or the Dronning Louise Land area. The coast at this latitude is near -18 to -20 W (Norske Øer is at -18). So -23 W is about 100 km inland, on the ice sheet, around 79N. The ice surface there is roughly 1000 m, though the Zachariae Isstrøm is farther south at 78.8N, -21 W. Inland of those, ice surface at -23 W would be about 900-1100 m.

I'll estimate 1000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

15070.text:
Location: -77.0 latitude, 81.0 E longitude, in East Antarctica. It lies inland of the Princess Elizabeth Land and Amery area, roughly near Dome A's flank toward Vostok. Dome A is at about 80.4S, 77.1E with an elevation of about 4093 m. At 77S, 81E the ice surface is probably around 3,000 m or a bit higher. Along the 80E meridian, the surface is near 2,800 m at 75S, rising to about 3,500 m at 78S. At 77S, 81E I estimate about 3,400 m.

The ice is grounded, so the surface type is Solid, and the elevation reported is the ice surface.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3400}

7736.text:
The point (5.0°N, 173.0°E) lies in the western Pacific, in the Marshall Islands region. The Marshall Islands span roughly 4.5–14.5°N and 160.8–172.2°E. Ebon Atoll is at about 4.6°N, 168.7°E, and Mili at about 6.1°N, 171.9°E. A point at 173°E, 5°N lies east of Mili and south of Majuro, so it is probably open ocean. The Gilbert Islands (Kiribati) start near 1.4°N, 173°E at Tarawa, and Maiana is at about 0.9°N. Neither is near 5°N, so the point is open ocean.

The seafloor here is likely abyssal plain or the flank of a seamount in the Pacific basin. The depth in this area is roughly 5000 m, and I am estimating that figure rather than recalling a specific value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

13207.text:
Latitude -57, longitude -45 is in the Scotia Sea, northeast of the Drake Passage. South Georgia lies around -54.3, -36.5, and the South Orkney Islands are near -60.5, -45. The point at -57, -45 is in the open Scotia Sea, south of the North Scotia Ridge, which runs roughly along -53 to -55. The Scotia Sea basin there is about 3000 to 3500 m deep.

The Scotia Sea floor in the central part is roughly 3000 to 3500 m deep. I'll estimate -3200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

3685.text:
The point 49.0°N, 9.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Ireland and west of the Celtic Sea shelf edge. The shelf break near 49°N is around 10-11°W, so at 9°W the point is probably on the outer shelf or the upper slope, in the Celtic Sea near the Goban Spur. Depths there are roughly 150-200 m on the shelf and 200-4000 m on the slope.

The Goban Spur is a plateau at about 49-50°N, 11-13°W, at depths of 1000-2000 m. The shelf edge in the area near 49°N runs roughly from 48.5°N, 9.5°W up to 50°N, 11°W. At 9°W, 49°N, the point is still on the shelf, in the Celtic Sea at about 49°N. The depths there are about 150-170 m, and the shelf deepens gradually toward the southwest, where the Western Approaches have depths around 100-150 m near 49°N, 8°W. I estimate about -160 m at this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-160}

9548.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -163 is in the South Pacific, west of the Cook Islands (Aitutaki is about -18.9, -159.8) and northeast of Samoa. Palmerston Atoll is at about -18.0, -163.2, which is close to this point, but at -17.0 we are about 1° north of it. The area is open ocean in the Penrhyn basin or the Samoan basin. Depths here are around 4,500–5,000 m. The seafloor near the Manihiki Plateau is shallower, but that lies further north-east, at roughly -11, -161.

I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

12308.text:
The point (-47, -43) is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of the mid-Atlantic ridge, in the Argentine Basin's eastern portion. It lies east of the Falklands and well east of South America. The North Scotia Ridge is further south, around 54°S. At 47°S, 43°W the seafloor is likely the Argentine Basin abyssal plain, with depths around 5000 m. The western Argentine Basin is about 5000-5500 m deep. Further east, near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge at about 15°W, depths rise. At 43°W the depth is probably about -5000 m.

I am estimating this from the regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

8284.text:
The point is at 3°S, 171°W, in the central Pacific. It lies near the Phoenix Islands (Kiribati), which sit around 3-5°S and 171-174°W. Nikumaroro is at 4.67°S, 174.5°W, so it is far away. Rawaki (Phoenix Island) is at 3.72°S, 170.7°W, which is about 0.7° south of this point, roughly 80 km away, so this point is open ocean. Birnie Island is at 3.58°S, 171.5°W, about 60 km away. The point at 3.0°S, 171.0°W is therefore in deep water between the islands.

The seafloor in this part of the Pacific, around the Phoenix Islands, is typically 4000-5000 m deep. I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7562.text:
The point (5°N, 175°W) lies in the central Pacific, east of the Gilbert Islands and north of the Phoenix Islands. It is close to Kiribati's Line Islands region, but those islands sit further east at about 160°W. The nearest land is probably Kanton in the Phoenix Islands, near 2.8°S, 171.7°W, which is far away. So this point is open ocean.

For depth, the central Pacific basin here is typically about 5000 to 5500 m deep. I'll estimate around -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

997.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude 15.0 E is in Svalbard. Spitsbergen's west coast near Krossfjorden and Kongsfjorden lies around 79 N, 11-12 E. At 15 E, 79 N, that is on the northern part of Spitsbergen, near Woodfjorden and Liefdefjorden. Woodfjorden's head is around 79.5 N, 14 E. At 79.0 N, 15 E we are inland, east of Woodfjorden, probably in the Haakon VII Land/Ny-Friesland region, perhaps on a glacier or mountains. Terrain there is about 500 m.

Points near 79 N, 15 E lie south of Woodfjorden. Mountains and glaciers around Lomfjorden at 79.5 N, 16.5 E are lower. At 79 N, 15 E the elevation is probably about 600 m, so I'll say Solid at 600.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

9007.text:
Latitude -11, longitude -165 is in the South Pacific, near Tokelau and the northern Cook Islands. Tokelau lies around -9 S, -172 W. Suwarrow atoll sits at about -13.2 S, -163.1 W. Point -11, -165 is between Suwarrow and Tokelau, and the nearby Penrhyn atoll is at -9 S, -158 W, so none of these land masses is at this point. The location is open ocean.

The seabed in this region is the Penrhyn Basin / Samoan Basin area, with depths of roughly 5000 to 5500 m. The Penrhyn Basin is about 5000 m deep, so I estimate -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

15767.text:
The point is at 85°S, 35°E, in East Antarctica. That is inland of Queen Maud Land, near the Pensacola Mountains and Recovery Glacier region, but at 35°E and 85°S it falls on the high plateau.

Ice surface elevation here is probably around 2,500–3,000 m. Dome Argus lies at about 80.4°S, 77°E with a surface near 4,000 m. At 85°S, 35°E, the surface sits between the Pole (2,835 m) and the Shackleton Range region (about 30°W), so roughly 3,000 m. I recall values near 2,900 m on the plateau south of Queen Maud Land, and I'll go with 2,950 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 2950}

7410.text:
The point (7°N, 119°W) is in the open eastern Pacific, southwest of Mexico, north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone and west of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor there is abyssal plain, roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m deep, with the age of the crust (about 20 to 30 Ma) setting the depth.

The East Pacific Rise sits near 110°W at this latitude, so 119°W is about 900 km west of the axis. At a half-spreading rate of about 70 mm/yr, that distance corresponds to roughly 13 Ma. Crust of that age lies at about 2500 + 350·√13 ≈ 3,760 m depth. The Clipperton Fracture Zone, near 10°N, is a nearby feature, but this point is south of it. I estimate a depth of about 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

10973.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 167 lies in the Tasman Sea, northwest of Norfolk Island (which is at about -29, 168). The point is east of the Lord Howe Rise and near the New Caledonia Trough and Norfolk Ridge. Norfolk Island sits on the Norfolk Ridge, which runs from New Caledonia to New Zealand. The Norfolk Ridge is about 167-168°E at these latitudes, so -31, 167 may be on or near the ridge. West of it is the New Caledonia Basin at about 3000 m depth, and the ridge crest is around 1000-2000 m deep.

Norfolk Island is at 167.95°E, so the ridge there is roughly at 168°E. At 167°E, one degree west, the point is probably on the flank toward the basin. The flank depth there is perhaps 2500 m. I'll estimate -2700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

1535.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 11.0°E lies in the Barents Sea/Norwegian Sea region, northeast of Norway. Bear Island is at about 74.4°N, 19°E, so this point is well west of it. The area is open sea between the Norwegian mainland and Svalbard, west of the Bear Island trough, near the Knipovich/Mohns ridge area.

Bathymetry here is uncertain. The Mohns Ridge runs from Jan Mayen to about 73°N, 8°E, where it bends into the Knipovich Ridge. At 73°N, 11°E, the point is probably on the western Barents Sea continental slope or the Bear Island fan. The slope there drops from about 500 m to 2500 m. The Knipovich Ridge sits around 73.5°N, 8°E at depths of about 2500 m. A point at 11°E is probably on the lower slope at roughly 2000 m depth, which gives an estimate of about -2300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

3171.text:
The point 55.0°N, 43.0°E is in European Russia, in Nizhny Novgorod Oblast near the border with Mordovia and Chuvashia. It is on the Volga Upland (right bank of the Sura river basin), roughly 100 km south of the Volga. Terrain there is typically 150–200 m above sea level.

The Sura River is further east, near 46°E. At 43°E, 55°N the point lies near Ardatov or Arzamas, where elevations are about 150–180 m. I estimate 160 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":160}

12535.text:
The point is at 49°S, 51°E, in the southern Indian Ocean. The Crozet Islands lie around 46°S, 51-52°E, so this point is about 3° south of them. The Crozet Plateau extends from roughly 45°S to 47°S, and south of it the seafloor drops into the Crozet Basin or the area north of the Kerguelen Plateau.

The Kerguelen Plateau lies at about 49°S but at 68-70°E, so it is not near this point. The seafloor at 49°S, 51°E is probably abyssal, at a depth of around 3,500 to 4,000 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge sits to the west near 40-50°E, and the Del Cano Rise is at about 45°S, 45-50°E. Southwest of the Crozet Islands, the Prince Edward Fracture Zone region is relevant, and 49°S, 51°E is probably in the Crozet Basin's southern edge or the Enderby Basin. I estimate a depth of about 3,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

12851.text:
Latitude -53, longitude -37 is in the South Atlantic, northwest of South Georgia, which lies around -54.3, -36.5. At -53, -37 the point is about 130 km north of South Georgia. The North Scotia Ridge lies to the west and the Georgia Basin to the north.

South Georgia's shelf extends out roughly 50-100 km from the island, and the shelf edge probably lies near -53.5. At -53, -37, the depth is likely around 3000 m, since the area north of the shelf drops into the Georgia Basin, which is 3000-4000 m deep. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

4317.text:
The point 43.0°N, 175.0°E lies in the South Pacific, east of New Zealand's South Island. The South Island's east coast is near 173°E at 43°S, and the Chatham Rise sits around 44°S. However, the latitude given is positive, so it is 43°N, not 43°S.

At 43°N, 175°E, the location is in the North Pacific, about 1,000 km east of Japan and south of the Emperor Seamounts. That region is the abyssal plain of the northwest Pacific, with depths of roughly 5,500 to 5,600 m. The Emperor Seamount chain lies near 170°E at 40–50°N, and the Hawaiian-Emperor bend is around 170°E, 33°N. At 175°E, 43°N, the point is east of the chain's seamounts near 170–171°E, so the seabed is probably abyssal plain. The Kuril-Kamchatka trench is farther west. I estimate a depth of about 5,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

10503.text:
Latitude -27, longitude -53 is in southern Brazil/northern Argentina, near the border of Santa Catarina and Rio Grande do Sul. It lies in the interior, west of the coast. In Argentina, Misiones is around -27, -55. At -53 longitude, this is in Brazil's western Santa Catarina or northern Rio Grande do Sul, near the Uruguay River, which runs at around -53 to -54 in this region. The terrain there is a plateau at roughly 400-600 m.

The Uruguay River is near -53.2 at lat -27.2 (Iraí, Rio Grande do Sul). Around -27, -53 is near Palmitos or Nonoai, so the elevation is probably about 500 m. Nonoai is at -27.36, -52.78 with an elevation of about 480 m. Chapecó (-27.1, -52.6) is at about 650 m. At -53.0 the point is probably near Frederico Westphalen or Planalto, with an elevation of about 450 m, so I'll estimate 480 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

13603.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 27 lies in the Southern Ocean, in the Weddell Sea/Atlantic sector, northeast of the South Sandwich Islands region and south of the Atlantic-Indian Ridge. The area is roughly 61°S, 27°E, which is south of the Indian-Atlantic Ridge and north of the Enderby Basin and Weddell-Enderby Basin. Typical depths here are around 3,500 to 4,500 m.

The Southwest Indian Ridge starts around 54°S, 0°E and runs northeast. At 27°E the ridge is near 50°S, so 61°S is south of it, in the Enderby Basin or the Weddell-Enderby Basin, where depths are about 4,000 to 5,000 m. I'll estimate about -4,500 m. This is an estimate from the general bathymetry of the region, not from a specific lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

6200.text:
Latitude 21.0°N, longitude -19.0° (19°W) falls in the Sahara region of Mauritania, about 400 km from the coast. The coast at 21°N is near -16.9°, so this point is inland. It lies in the Majabat al-Koubra area, or just west of it, near the Adrar/Tiris region, where elevations of roughly 200-300 m are typical. I'm estimating from that regional picture rather than a specific lookup.

Checking the geography: Nouadhibou is at 20.9°N, -17.0°. Going west from there reaches the coast at about -17.0°, so -19° is actually in the Atlantic Ocean. The coast at 21°N is around -17.0°, and -19° is about 200 km offshore. The continental shelf off Cap Blanc is wide, with depths around 100 m at the shelf edge near -17.8° to -18°. At -19° the water is probably about 1000-2000 m deep. Banc d'Arguin lies further south, and at 21°N the shelf break is around -17.7°. The slope descends from about 200 m to 2000 m over a short distance, so at -19° I estimate about -2000 m. The Cape Verde abyssal plain is around 4000 m deep further west. I'll go with about -2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

15718.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -63 is in Antarctica, in the area of the Foundation Ice Stream / Support Force Glacier, at the edge of the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf grounding zone. The Pensacola Mountains lie around -84 to -83.5 S, -55 to -65 W, and the Ronne Ice Shelf is at about -80 S. At -85 S, -63 W, we are south of the Forrestal Range and near the Foundation Ice Stream, which flows toward the Ronne Ice Shelf. This is grounded ice with a surface elevation of roughly 1000 m. The Support Force Glacier is at about -83 S, -55 W. At -85 S, -63 W, the ice surface is probably about 1200 m.

The Foundation Ice Stream's upper reaches near -85 S, -70 W are about 1000 to 1200 m, so I'll estimate 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

3975.text:
The point (45.0°N, 149.0°W) is in the North Pacific, south of the Gulf of Alaska, roughly 1,500 km west of Vancouver Island. That area is abyssal plain at about 4,500–5,000 m depth, west of the Surveyor Fan region and near the Patton–Murray seamounts. Those seamounts sit near 54°N, 150°W, so they are well north of this point. At 45°N, 149°W the seafloor is probably around 5,000 m deep, since the Pacific plate crust here is about 40–50 million years old, which fits a depth of roughly 5,000 m.

This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

9326.text:
The point at -13.0, 113.0 lies in the Indian Ocean, south of Java and west of Australia. It is near the Argo Abyssal Plain, whose depths run about 5,000–5,700 m. The Java Trench lies further north, around -10 to -11 latitude at 110–113 longitude, so this point is south of the trench on the abyssal plain.

I estimate the seabed at about 5,500 m below sea level. This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

8948.text:
Latitude -9.0, Longitude 77.0 lies in the Indian Ocean, west of Sumatra and southwest of the Maldives, in the central Indian Basin. The Ninetyeast Ridge is near 90E, so this point is not on it. Ocean depths in this area are typically around 4,500 to 5,000 m.

The point is east of the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge, which lies near 73E. At 77E, 9S, the Central Indian Basin floor is about 5,000 m deep. I estimate -5,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

8724.text:
Latitude -7, longitude -11 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and northwest of Ascension Island, which sits at about -7.95, -14.37.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis near 7°S lies at roughly 13-14°W. At -11 longitude we are east of the ridge, on the African plate flank. The ridge crest depth is about 3000 m, and the flanks there would be about 3500-4000 m deep. Near the Ascension fracture zone (around 7-8°S), depths could be around 4000 m. The Guinea/Angola basin side lies at about 4500-5000 m. At 11°W, about 3° from the axis, the crust is about 6 Myr old, which gives a depth of roughly 3000 + 350*sqrt(6) ≈ 3850 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8929.text:
Latitude -9.0, longitude 39.0 is in southeastern Tanzania. The Rufiji River basin lies around -7.8 latitude, and the Selous Game Reserve spans roughly -8 to -10 latitude and 36 to 39 longitude. The coast at this latitude is near 39.5 longitude, close to Kilwa, which sits at about -8.9, 39.5. That puts the point about 50 km inland, in the area north of the Matandu River.

The terrain there is a coastal plateau, probably 200 to 300 m above sea level. Inland of Kilwa, around Nanganga, elevations reach several hundred meters. I estimate about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

11120.text:
Latitude -33, longitude 101 is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia and west of Perth by roughly 1,500 km. The location is near the Wallaby Plateau/Naturaliste Plateau region, but those lie further east, around 110-115°E. At 101°E and 33°S, the seafloor is probably abyssal plain of the Perth Abyssal Plain or the Wharton Basin's southern part, at about 5,000 m depth.

The Perth Abyssal Plain is around 5,500 m deep, near 32°S and 108°E. At 101°E it could be somewhat shallower, near the Broken Ridge/Diamantina region, which lies farther south. The Wharton Basin, near 33°S 101°E, is about 5,000 m deep. I'll estimate -5,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

1496.text:
The point 73.0N, 67.0W is in northwest Greenland/Baffin Bay region. Longitude -67 at latitude 73 is in Baffin Bay, west of Greenland (Greenland's coast near Melville Bay is around -56 to -60 at 73N). Baffin Island's east coast lies near -72 to -78 at 73N, so -67 is in the middle of Baffin Bay. The central Baffin Bay basin is about 2000 m deep, but at 73N, 67W it sits on the western part of the basin, with depths around 1800-2200 m.

Baffin Bay's deepest part is around 2300 m near 73N, 67W. I'll estimate -2100.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -2100}

11259.text:
Latitude -35.0, longitude 19.0 is on the south coast of South Africa. Cape Agulhas is at about -34.83, 20.0, and the coast near Hermanus and Cape Infanta lies around -34.4, 19.3 to 20.9. At longitude 19.0 the coast sits near -34.4 (Hermanus is at 19.25 and Danger Point at 19.3, both near -34.6). Gansbaai is at -34.58, 19.35. So at -35.0 and 19.0 we are about 50 km south of the coast, which is ocean on the Agulhas Bank.

The Agulhas Bank shelf there is roughly 100 m deep near the coast, deepening to about 100–150 m at 50 km out. At -35.0, 19.0 the depth is probably about 100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

11569.text:
The point (-39, -81) is in the southeast Pacific, off the coast of Chile. The Chilean coast at 39°S is near 73°W, so this location is about 8° of longitude west, roughly 700 km offshore. It lies west of the Chile Trench, which sits near 74-75°W at this latitude, on the Nazca Plate. The seafloor there is around 4000 m deep.

The Chile Rise and the Juan Fernández area are farther north and west. At 39°S, 81°W the oceanic crust is fairly young, and the Chile Trench is at about -5000 m near 74°W. Depths of about 4000 m are typical of the outer slope and abyssal plain. I estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

827.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 35.0°E lies in the Svalbard/Franz Josef Land region. Nordaustlandet in Svalbard spans roughly 13–28°E, and Kvitøya is at about 80.1°N, 32.5°E. Franz Josef Land begins around 45°E. So 81°N, 35°E is probably open sea in the northern Barents Sea, northeast of Nordaustlandet and north of Kvitøya.

Water depth there is about 81°N, 35°E, on the Barents shelf edge. Depths near Kvitøya are ~200 m, and north of it the slope drops toward the Nansen Basin. At 81°N the seabed is probably around 300 m deep, perhaps up to 500 m, since the shelf break near 81.5°N is around 400 m. I'll estimate -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

7854.text:
Latitude 3.0, longitude 49.0 is in the Indian Ocean, east of Somalia. Somalia's coast near 3°N lies at about 47.5°E, so 49°E is roughly 150 km offshore. That puts the point in the Somali Basin, with depths of about 4,500 to 5,000 m. The continental shelf there is narrow, and the slope drops quickly to around 4,000 m or more.

This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup. I'll use -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

12613.text:
Latitude -51, longitude -153 is in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and west of Chile. It lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or nearby abyssal plain. The East Pacific Rise at that latitude sits further west, around -140 to -150 longitude at 51°S. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at 51°S is near longitude -138, so -153 is on the ridge flank, with depth of roughly 3,500-4,000 m.

Seafloor age increases away from the ridge, and the spreading rate is fast (~70 mm/yr), so 15° of longitude (~1,000 km at this latitude) is about 14 Myr old crust, giving a depth of roughly 2,500 + 350*sqrt(14) ≈ 3,800 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

3909.text:
Latitude 47.0, longitude 79.0 is in eastern Kazakhstan. Lake Balkhash spans roughly 73-79°E, with its eastern part around 78-79°E at latitudes of about 45-46.5°N. At 47°N, 79°E, the point is north of the lake's eastern end, which lies near 46.5°N. The Karatal River delta and the Alakol depression are further south-east. The area around 47°N, 79°E is steppe and sand, with an elevation of about 350 m.

Lake Balkhash's surface is at about 340 m, and the eastern shore is near 79°E at roughly 46.0-46.7°N. The terrain at 47°N is probably the Sary-Esik-Atyrau or Karatal plains, which would put it at about 400 m. I'll estimate 380 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":380}

1107.text:
Latitude 77.0, longitude -125.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Banks Island spans roughly 71-75°N, so it lies south of this point. Melville Island sits around 75-76.5°N and 105-120°W, which is also not at this location. Prince Patrick Island is around 76-77.5°N and 117-124°W, with its western coast near -124.5°W at about 76.5°N. At -125°W and 77°N the point is therefore just west of Prince Patrick Island, in the Arctic Ocean / Beaufort Sea margin.

The shelf off Prince Patrick is shallow near the island, and the continental slope lies further west. Treating the point as about 20-30 km offshore from the northwest coast of Prince Patrick, the water there is probably a few hundred meters deep. Mass Island and Brock Island are near 77-78°N and -114°W, so they are not relevant. Landerman Bay and Cape Alfred Ernest are on the island's northwest corner, near 76.8°N and -122.5°W. The point is about 2.5° of longitude west of that, roughly 50 km at this latitude, so it is probably in the Arctic Ocean shelf area at around -300 m. I'll estimate the seabed at about -350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

9831.text:
Latitude -19.0, longitude 43.0 lies in the Mozambique Channel. Madagascar's west coast is around 44.3°E at this latitude (Maintirano is at -18.0, 44.0; Morondava at -20.3, 44.3). Mozambique's coast is around 36-37°E. So the point is in open water, off the Madagascar coast.

The Madagascar west shelf is fairly wide here, about 100 km. At 43.0°E the point is roughly 130 km west of the coast, which would put it on the upper slope or beyond the shelf. Depths in the central channel reach about 2000-3000 m. The Davie Ridge runs near 41-42°E at this latitude. At 43°E, east of the ridge, the depth is probably around 1500 m, and I'll estimate -1300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

11666.text:
The point (-39.0, 113.0) is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies south of Cape Leeuwin, which is at about 34.4°S, 115°E, and west of Tasmania. The nearby seafloor includes the Naturaliste Plateau at about 33°S, 112°E, with depths of 2000-3000 m. At 39°S the seafloor is likely the abyssal plain or the lower southern margin of the plateau, so I estimate roughly 4500 m deep. I'm estimating this from the regional bathymetry, not looking it up.

The Diamantina zone sits farther southwest, at about 35°S, 105°E. The Great Australian Bight and the southern margin of Australia run at about 38°S near 130°E. South of Western Australia the Mentelle Basin is at about 4000 m and the Naturaliste Plateau sits above it. At 39°S, 113°E I think the depth is around 4,500 m, since the abyssal plain south of Australia lies at about 4,000-5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

8217.text:
