{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

2555.text:
Location 61.0N, 109.0W is in the Northwest Territories, near the eastern end of Great Slave Lake. The lake's eastern arm extends to about 109°W at roughly 62.5N, and at 61.0N, 109W the point is likely on the Canadian Shield, east of the lake and south of Artillery Lake. It lies near the Saskatchewan/NWT border, which is at 60N, so it is in the NWT, in the Taltson region. Terrain there is about 350-400 m with many small lakes.

Great Slave Lake's east arm runs from about 62.5N, 110W to 62.7N, 111W, so 61N is well south of it. Lake Athabasca is at 59N. At 61N, 109W the point is probably Shield terrain with lakes such as Nonacho Lake (61.7N, 109.5W) nearby. I will guess solid ground at about 380 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":380}

9651.text:
Latitude -17, longitude 43 is in the Mozambique Channel, off the northwest coast of Madagascar. The Madagascar coast near Mahajanga lies at about 46.3E, and the Mozambique coast is near 39-40E at this latitude. So 43E is in the channel's open water.

The Mozambique Channel is deep here, roughly 2000-3000 m. The Davie Ridge lies near 41-42E at about -17, and the Madagascar side has a continental slope. At 43E, -17, the water is probably about 2500 m deep, though the Mozambique Basin side is deeper. Madagascar's shelf edge is around 45E, and the slope descends to around 2000 m at 43.5E. I estimate about -2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

4514.text:
The point (39°N, 151°W) lies in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and well north of the islands. The nearby seafloor is abyssal plain, typically about 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. The Mendocino fracture zone is farther east, and the Hawaiian-Emperor seamounts are far to the west, so neither affects this point.

In this region the depth is roughly 5,300 m. The seafloor here is Cretaceous-age crust (about 100 Ma), which supports a depth of around 5,500 m, so I estimate 5,400 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5400}

11265.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 31 is in the Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa. The coast near Durban (30.0°S, 31.0°E) is far to the north, and at 35°S the point lies offshore, roughly 400 km from the coast. The Natal Valley and continental slope are in this area, and the Agulhas Plateau is farther southwest.

The coast at 31°E is near 29.9°S. At 35°S, 31°E, I believe this falls in the southern Natal Valley or near the Mozambique Ridge's southern end. The Natal Valley has depths of about 3000-4500 m. The Transkei Basin is around 33-35°S and 32-35°E, with depths of about 4500-5000 m. The Natal Valley floor is about 3500-4000 m, and 35°S, 31°E is probably at the Transkei Basin edge, so I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7772.text:
The point is 3°N, 115°W, in the eastern Pacific Ocean. It lies southwest of Mexico, south of the Clipperton Fracture Zone (about 10°N) and north of the equator, on the East Pacific Rise flank region. The East Pacific Rise itself is near 105-110°W at this latitude, so 115°W is on its western flank. Seafloor depth there is about 3,500-4,000 m. The crust is young, roughly 10-15 Myr old, so depth is around 3,800 m.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup of the actual bathymetry.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3750}

14133.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 7 lies off the coast of Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The Antarctic coast near 7°E is around 70°S, with the Fimbul Ice Shelf at about 70°S, 0°E. So -67 at 7°E is in the Southern Ocean, north of the coast, over the continental slope or the abyssal plain.

The Maud Rise is at about 65°S, 3°E, with depths of about 2000-3000 m. At 67°S, 7°E the seabed is probably around 3000-4000 m deep. The continental shelf edge lies around 69-70°S there. Between the Maud Rise and the coast the depth is around 4000-5000 m. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7164.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 109.0°E lies in the South China Sea, off the coast of southern Vietnam. The Vietnamese coast near Phan Rang is at about 108.9°E, 11.6°N, and Cam Ranh is at about 109.1°E, 11.9°N. At 11.0°N the coastline is around 108.1°E (Phan Thiet is at about 108.1°E, 10.9°N). So 109.0°E is roughly 90 km offshore.

The shelf there is fairly shallow, with depth increasing eastward. About 90 km offshore at 11°N the depth is probably around 100 m. The shelf break lies farther east, so I estimate about -90 m. This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-95}

11515.text:
The point (-37.0, 171.0) is in the Tasman Sea/Pacific, northwest of New Zealand's North Island. It lies west of the Northland coast, which is near 173.0°E, so this is open ocean, roughly 200 km west of Northland.

The sea floor in this area is part of the New Caledonia Basin and the Norfolk Ridge region. The Norfolk Ridge runs northwest from the Three Kings Ridge area. At 37°S, 171°E, I'd expect depths of about 3000 m. The Lord Howe Rise sits near 162°E and is shallower, while the New Caledonia Basin is around 3500 m deep. The Three Kings Ridge is at about 34°S, 172°E, so this point may sit in the basin just west of it, at roughly -3000 to -3500 m. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

620.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -19.0° is in northeast Greenland, in the Arctic Ocean just north of Greenland. Peary Land is at about 82-83°N, with Kap Morris Jesup at 83.6°N, 33°W. At -19° longitude, 83°N is north of Greenland's coast, in the area of the Wandel Sea / Lincoln Sea. The coast near 20°W is at about 82.0-82.5°N (Nord area, Hovgaard Island?). Greenland's northeast coast near Nordostrundingen is at 81.5°N, 12°W. At 19°W, the coast is around 82°N or a little north, so 83°N is probably ocean, on the shelf or the slope.

The Greenland shelf here is wide, around 100-200 km. The Wandel Sea shelf has depths of about 200-400 m, and the slope lies further north. At 83°N, 19°W, the point is about 100 km north of the coast, so the shelf depth is roughly -300 m. Some of the Belgica Bank shelf is shallower, at about 50-300 m. Belgica Bank sits at about 78-79°N, so it is not relevant here. I'll estimate -400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

3186.text:
Latitude 55.0°N, longitude 73.0°E lies in the West Siberian Plain, in Omsk Oblast, Russia. Omsk itself is at about 54.99°N, 73.37°E, so this point is roughly 20 km west of Omsk. The Irtysh River passes through Omsk, and at this longitude the point is likely on the floodplain or terrace. Terrain elevation in that area is about 80-100 m. The Irtysh at Omsk is about 77 m, and the surrounding plain is around 90 m.

I estimate the surface is solid ground at about 90 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

14609.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -121 lies in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. The Pine Island Bay is around -74.5, -102, and the Getz Ice Shelf is around -74, -125. At -73, -121 the point is probably offshore of the Getz Ice Shelf front, which sits near -74 to -75 at longitude -121. The coast there is near -74.5, and the Getz front lies around -73.5 to -74. So -73 is probably open ocean on the continental shelf, with possible sea ice that I'm ignoring.

The Amundsen Sea shelf is typically 500 to 700 m deep, and near the Getz it is around -500 to -600 m. I'll estimate -550 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -550}

9101.text:
Latitude -11.0, longitude 23.0 falls in the southern Democratic Republic of the Congo or northern Angola/Zambia border region. At 11°S, 23°E, the point is in the DRC's Haut-Katanga/Lualaba area, near the Angola border. The Kalahari sands plateau there sits at roughly 1100 m.

The Zambezi headwaters are near 11-12°S, 24°E, and the plateau there is about 1100-1400 m. At 23°E, 11°S, the terrain is near the Lualaba/Kasai watershed at about 1100 m. I estimate 1080 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1080}

1809.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude -161.0 W is on the North Slope of Alaska, inland from the coast. Point Lay is at about 69.75 N, -163 W, and Atqasuk is at about 70.5 N, -157.4 W. The Brooks Range foothills start further south, near 68.5 N. At 69.0 N, -161 W the area is in the Utukok River uplands, which are foothills, so the terrain is probably Solid with an elevation around 200 m.

The Utukok River headwaters are near 69 N, -161 W, in the foothills at roughly 150-300 m. I'll go with 220 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":220}

10855.text:
The point -31.0, -69.0 is in Argentina, in San Juan province. It sits west of the Sierra de Valle Fértil and near the Precordillera, close to the Calingasta–Iglesia area. Ullún-Zonda is near -31.5, -68.7.

At longitude -69.0 and latitude -31.0, the location is in the Precordillera, about 30 km south of Rodeo/Iglesia (Iglesia is at -30.4, -69.2). The Precordillera ranges there rise to 3000 m or more. The Tontal range lies near -31.5, -69.3. The Calingasta valley at -31.3, -69.4 is around 1400 m. The Rio Jachal valley is around -30.2, -68.7 at about 1200 m. At -31.0, -69.0 the point is likely in the Sierra Negra or Sierra del Tigre, with elevations around 2500 m. I'll estimate 2400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

13020.text:
The point is at 55°S, 59°W, which lies in the South Atlantic/Drake Passage region. The Falkland Islands are near 51-52°S, and Burdwood Bank is near 54-55°S, 56-62°W. Burdwood Bank is a submarine plateau south of the Falklands, with depths of about 50-200 m on top. Its edges sit around 54.5°S, and the bank is roughly 54-55°S between 55°W and 63°W. At 55°S, the point is likely on the southern slope of the bank, where depths fall off quickly toward the Namuncurá/Burdwood Bank edge. The southern flank drops to over 3000 m into the Yaghan Basin. Tierra del Fuego is at 54.8°S but west of 65°W, so it is not relevant here.

The bank's southern edge near 55°S is steep, so I estimate the depth there at around 1000 m. The bank's top is about 54°S, and at 55°S, 59°W the point is probably on the slope. I'll guess -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

8672.text:
The point (-7.0, -115.0) lies in the open eastern Pacific, southwest of the Galápagos and west of the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise crest is near 110°W at those latitudes, so -115 sits on its western flank. The seafloor there is roughly 3,500–4,000 m deep. At 7°S, 115°W the crust is about 5–8 Myr old, which fits a depth near 3,800 m.

This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a lookup of the actual bathymetry.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3750}

2521.text:
Latitude 61.0°N, longitude -177.0° (177°W) is in the Bering Sea. St. Lawrence Island sits near 63°N, 170°W, and Nunivak Island near 60°N, 166°W. The point is in the Bering Sea, west of the Alaskan coast and north of the Aleutians.

The eastern Bering Sea shelf extends to roughly 175°W at this latitude, where the shelf edge runs from about 56°N, 170°W up to about 60°N, 178°W and then toward Navarin Canyon near 61°N, 178°W. The point at 177°W is therefore close to the shelf edge, probably on the outer shelf at around 100-150 m depth. The shelf break near 60-61°N lies roughly at 177-179°W, with depths there of about 150-200 m. I'll estimate a seabed depth of 130 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-130}

10746.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 73 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and east of the Mascarene region. It lies near the Central Indian Basin's western side, or possibly the Southwest Indian Ridge region.

The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from about 50E at 40S toward the Rodrigues Triple Junction at about 70E, 25S. The Central Indian Ridge runs north from the triple junction, near 70E, so 73E at 29S is just east of the triple junction. The Southeast Indian Ridge runs southeast from the triple junction. At 29S the seafloor to the east of the ridge is probably the Indian Ocean basin at about 4000 to 4500 m depth. The triple junction sits at roughly 25.5S, 70E, and the SE Indian Ridge heads toward 30S, 77E, so at 73E the ridge axis would be near 27S. The point at 29S, 73E may therefore be close to the ridge flank, with a depth of about 3500 to 4000 m. I'll estimate -3800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

6653.text:
The point 17°N, 167°E lies in the western Pacific, northwest of the Marshall Islands, which sit at roughly 165–172°E and 5–15°N. Wake Island is at 19.3°N, 166.6°E, so this point is about 2.3° south of Wake. The surrounding area is open ocean on the Pacific plate, with abyssal depths of about 5000–5500 m. It may sit in the Marshall Islands seamount province, where guyots occur. No known island is at this location, so I take it to be water.

The Pacific floor in this region is typically around 5400 m deep. I estimate -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

589.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -81.0° lies in northern Ellesmere Island, Canada, or just offshore of it. The northern coast of Ellesmere is around 82.5-83.1°N at longitudes near -80 to -90. Cape Columbia lies at 83.1°N, -70°. At -81°, the coast is near 82.8°N (Ward Hunt Island is at -74°). The area near -81°, 83°N is probably just offshore in the Arctic Ocean, north of Ellesmere's coast near Cape Aldrich (82.9°N, -64°)... Actually, Cape Aldrich is at -64°, and the coast runs west-southwest from Cape Columbia (-70°, 83.1°N) to roughly 82.0°N at -90° or so.

At -81°, the coast is around 82.6°N. So 83.0°N is about 40 km offshore, over the Arctic Ocean continental shelf. The shelf there is about 100-300 m deep, and the shelf break lies further north. I'd estimate the seabed at around -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

576.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -107.0° lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Canada's Arctic Archipelago. Ellesmere Island's northwest coast is around 82-83°N at about -80 to -90°, and Meighen Island is at roughly 80°N, -99°. Borden and Ellef Ringnes islands are near 78°N. Axel Heiberg's northern tip is around 81°N, -97°. At 83°N, -107° the point is therefore in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Canadian polar margin.

The bathymetry there is probably the continental slope or the Canada Basin edge. Islands end near 80-81°N, and the shelf off Prince Patrick Island is narrow. At 83°N the water is likely deep, perhaps 2000-3500 m. That is near the Alpha Ridge's southern flank or the Canada Basin, and the Canada Basin is about 3800 m deep. Alpha Ridge is at about 85°N, with depths around 1500 m. For 83°N, -107°, I estimate roughly 3000 m depth, giving an elevation of -3000 m. This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

4515.text:
The point is at 39°N, 149°W, in the North Pacific, well off the California coast. That is roughly 1,500 km northwest of Hawaii and about 1,800 km west of San Francisco. It lies in the deep abyssal plain, in the area of the Mendocino Fracture Zone and the Murray Fracture Zone to the south.

The Murray Fracture Zone runs near 32–35°N, and the Pioneer Fracture Zone near 37°N. The seafloor age here is about 80–90 million years old, which puts typical depth around 5,200–5,500 m. I'll estimate about 5,300 m of depth, so the elevation is about -5,300 m. This is an estimate from regional seafloor depth, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

15982.text:
The point is at 87°S, 105°E, which is on the East Antarctic plateau. The South Pole sits at about 2835 m. Along the 105°E meridian, the surface rises toward Dome A (80.4°S, 77°E, about 4093 m) and Vostok (78.5°S, 106.8°E, about 3488 m). At 87°S on this longitude, the ice surface is probably around 3000 m or a little above.

The region between the pole and Vostok has a surface elevation of roughly 2900–3300 m. Based on that, I estimate 3150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3150}

6082.text:
The coordinates 23.0°N, 105.0°E fall in southeastern Yunnan or western Guangxi, China. The point is near the Yunnan–Guangxi border, close to Funing and Guangnan counties in Wenshan prefecture, which is karst highland. I did not look up a specific elevation, so the value below is an estimate from the typical terrain there.

Guangnan sits at about 24.0°N, 105.05°E. At 23.0°N, 105.0°E the point is in Funing county or just north of it, with elevations around 600–900 m. Funing town is at about 23.63°N, 105.63°E and sits at roughly 700 m. The area near 23.0°N, 105.0°E is probably Guangxi's Napo or Jingxi region, or Yunnan's Funing county, at around 800 m. I estimate 750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":750}

8340.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude -59.0 lies in the central Amazon of Brazil. It is south of the Amazon River and east of Manaus, near the Madeira River mouth. Manaus is at -3.1, -60.0, so -59.0 is about 110 km east. The Amazon main channel there runs near -3.3 to -3.5 at longitude -59.0 or so. Itacoatiara is at -3.14, -58.44. At longitude -59 the river lies slightly south, around -3.3, and the Madeira joins near -3.4, -58.8. So -3.0 at -59.0 is probably land, north of the river. This is terra firme plateau at roughly 50-100 m, with the area near Rio Urubu and Autazes around 30-50 m. Terrain at -3.0 north of the Amazon floodplain is probably about 60 m.

I'll answer Solid at about 55 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":55}

10836.text:
The point -31, -107 lies in the southeastern Pacific, far from land. It is west of Chile, and the Easter Island region is at about -27, -109. The location is near the Easter Island Microplate area, east of the East Pacific Rise, which sits at about -113 longitude at this latitude. The seafloor here is therefore young-ish, with depths of roughly 3000 m. The Sala y Gómez ridge is at about -26 to -27 latitude, so the point is south of it.

At 31°S the East Pacific Rise crest is near 111–112°W. At 107°W the point is about 400 km east of the crest, and the seafloor age is about 5 Myr. The depth would be about 2600 + 350*sqrt(5) ≈ 3400 m, and the nearby Pacific-Nazca ridge flank is a bit shallower. I estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

4368.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude -83.0 falls in northwest Ohio, near Tiffin and Fremont, in Seneca County. Sandusky County is slightly to the north and Fremont lies at about 41.35 N, -83.12 W. The point at 41.0 N, -83.0 W is near Bascom or Attica. The terrain there is flat till plain at roughly 230 m elevation.

Local elevations I recall: Tiffin is about 220 m, and Attica is about 250 m. I'll estimate 235 m for this point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":235}

1289.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -121.0 falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Banks Island spans roughly 71.5–74.5°N and 115–125°W, so 75°N sits just north of it. M'Clure Strait lies around 74.5–75.5°N and 115–122°W, and Melville Island is further north-west. Prince Patrick Island lies at about 76-77°N, 117-124°W. So the point is most likely in M'Clure Strait, which is water.

The depth of M'Clure Strait is about 300-500 m in its central part. Near Banks Island's northern tip (Cape Kellett is at the south; the northern tip is Cape Lambton at about 71.1°N, so that is not relevant). The northern part of Banks Island reaches about 74.4°N, near Cape Prince Alfred at 74.3°N, 124.5°W. At 75°N, -121°, the point is in the strait, with depths of roughly 400 m. I will estimate -350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

7887.text:
The coordinates 3.0°N, 115.0°E fall in northern Borneo, in Sarawak, Malaysia. This is inland from the coast near Miri and Bintulu. The Baram river region lies to the east, and the Usun Apau plateau is farther south. The Kelabit Highlands are around 3.5°N, 115.5°E, with Bario at about 1000 m.

At 3.0°N, 115.0°E the terrain is probably in the upper Baram or Tinjar area, which is hilly with elevations of a few hundred meters. The Tama Abu range lies along the Indonesian border at roughly 115.5–116°E. Because 115.0°E is west of that range, I estimate about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

8363.text:
The point (-3.0, -13.0) lies in the equatorial Atlantic, west of Africa and east of Brazil. It is south of the equator and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near 13-14°W at this latitude. The ridge axis is at about 13.5°W at the equator, and the Romanche Fracture Zone lies near 0°N. At 3°S there is another fracture zone, the Ascension FZ, located near 7°S.

The location is therefore close to the ridge crest. Ridge flanks near 3°S, 13°W are probably around 3000-4000 m deep. The ridge axis there is at about 13.5°W, with the rift valley deeper at around 3500-4000 m and the crest near 2500-3000 m. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

15865.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -129 is in West Antarctica, near the Ross Ice Shelf's southern end and the Transantarctic Mountains area. The South Pole is at -90; at 87°S, longitude 129°W lies in the direction of Marie Byrd Land / the Ross Ice Shelf region. Longitude 180 points toward the Ross Ice Shelf, and 0 toward the Weddell Sea. Longitude -129 is on the Pacific side, beyond the Ross Ice Shelf toward Marie Byrd Land, so it is likely the West Antarctic ice sheet interior.

Reasoning from known features: the Ross Ice Shelf's southern edge is at about 85°S for longitudes of 180 to -150, and the Horlick Mountains lie near 85-86°S at around 130°W. At 87°S, -129 should be the polar plateau edge, with Reedy Glacier nearby at about 86°S, 130°W. The ice surface is roughly 2500-3000 m there, so I estimate about 2700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2700}

10739.text:
The point (-29, 59) is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and well east of South Africa. It lies roughly on the Southwest Indian Ridge's northern flank, or in the basin region between the Madagascar Ridge and the Central Indian Ridge.

The Southwest Indian Ridge sits near 40°S at 59°E, so this point is north of it. The Mascarene Basin lies around 20°S, 55°E, and the Crozet Basin lies further south. At 29°S, 59°E the seafloor is probably the Madagascar Basin or Mascarene Plain, with depths of about 4500–5000 m. I'll estimate about -4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

11767.text:
The point -41, -45 is in the South Atlantic, southwest of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Argentine Basin. It lies roughly 1,500 km east of Uruguay and Argentina.

The Argentine Basin has depths of about 5,000-6,000 m. The Rio Grande Rise is farther north, near 30°S, so it is not a factor here. At 41°S, 45°W the seafloor is likely abyssal plain, with a depth of about 5,000 m. The Zapiola Rise is near 44°S, 47°W and is shallower, at about 4,500 m. The point at 41°S, 45°W may be on the flank of that feature, so I estimate about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

9413.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -73 is in southern Peru, in the Arequipa region. The coast near Chala and Atico sits around -15.8, -74.3, so this point is inland. The Pacific coast at latitude -15 runs near longitude -74.9, and -73 is about 200 km inland. That puts it in the Andes, near Cotahuasi and the Coropuna area (Coropuna is at -15.5, -72.65). The point is slightly north of Coropuna, near the Ocoña and Cotahuasi canyons, with a plateau at about 4000 m.

Longitude -73 at latitude -15 is probably near Chuquibamba or Andagua. Chuquibamba is at -15.8, -72.65. Cotahuasi is at -15.2, -72.9 at about 2700 m. The Cotahuasi canyon runs in this area, but the point is on the plateau at about 4000 m. Given the mix of canyon and plateau, I estimate about 3800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3800}

7439.text:
Latitude 7.0, longitude -61.0 falls in southeastern Venezuela, Bolívar state, near the Gran Sabana. That is east of the Caroní River and near the Guyana border region. Longitude -61 at latitude 7 is roughly in the Gran Sabana, around Santa Elena de Uairén (which is at 4.6N, -61.1), so this point is north of it, at about 7N.

Nearby, the Gran Sabana plateau is around 800-1000 m. At 7N, -61, the area is near the upper Cuyuní and the Kamarata/Ikabarú region. The Sierra de Lema is about 6.0N, -61.3. At 7N, -61 we are near El Dorado–Tumeremo, closer to the Cuyuní basin, where elevation is roughly 200 m. The Cuyuní River is around 6.7N, -61.5. Farther north, the Guasipati area is at 7.5N, -61.9. At 7.0N, -61.0, the terrain is near the Venezuela–Guyana border, in the Sierra Imataca / Cuyuní headwaters, so the elevation is moderate, around 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

4011.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude -77.0 is in eastern Ontario, Canada. It lies near the Ottawa River, west of Pembroke, close to the Renfrew County area. Pembroke is at about 45.82 N, -77.11 W, so this point is about 90 km south of Pembroke, near Calabogie and Burnstown. Calabogie is at roughly 45.3 N, -76.75 W, and the Madawaska River runs through that area.

Going further south, 45.0 N, -77.0 W falls near Lanark Highlands or the north of Frontenac County, around Dalhousie Lake, Lavant, and Clarendon. This is Canadian Shield terrain, with elevations around 250-300 m. Dalhousie Lake sits at about 45.0 N, -76.6 W, so it is further east and not at this point. I'll estimate a land elevation of about 290 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":290}

15005.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -49 is in the southern Weddell Sea region, which is the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf area. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly -60 to -80 W, and the Filchner ice shelf spans roughly -30 to -50 W. At -49 W, -77 S, the point is near the western edge of the Filchner Ice Shelf, close to Berkner Island, which lies at roughly -45 W and between -78 and -80 S. The Berkner Island northern tip is near -46 W, -79 S, so -77 S is north of the island, which makes this the ice shelf or the Weddell Sea.

Given the longitude of -49, the Filchner Trough runs near -40 to -45 W. At -49 W, -77 S we are likely on the Ronne Ice Shelf's eastern part, west of Berkner Island. The seabed there is about 400-600 m deep. I'll guess the type is Water, with a seabed depth of about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

10917.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 55 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and well south of Mauritius, southwest of Australia. The area lies roughly between the Madagascar Basin and the Crozet/Southwest Indian Ridge region, and the seafloor there is probably abyssal plain.

The Southwest Indian Ridge lies further south-west, near 30°S, 55°E, but it is not at this point. The Madagascar Basin lies around 25–35°S, 50–60°E, with depths of about 5000 m. Around 31°S, 55°E I estimate the seafloor at about -5100 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry and not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

7221.text:
This location (9°N, 137°W) is in the open Pacific Ocean, southwest of Mexico and east of Hawaii. It lies north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone, in the abyssal plain area of the northeastern Pacific. Typical depth there is about 4,800–5,200 m. The seafloor age is about 50 million years, which fits a depth of roughly 5,000 m.

I'm estimating from typical regional depths rather than a looked-up value, so I'll use about -4,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

5769.text:
The point 25.0°N, 161.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. It is roughly 200 km from Kure Atoll and Midway, which sit near 28°N, 178°W, and about 400 km from Nihoa, near 23°N, 161.9°W. Nihoa is at about 23.06°N, 161.92°W, so this point is about 2° north of it, in the open ocean beyond the Hawaiian Ridge.

The Hawaiian Ridge trends northwest from Nihoa toward Necker, which is near 23.6°N, 164.7°W. At 161°W the ridge is near 22°N, so the point at 25°N is north of it. That places it on the abyssal plain, which is deep, around 4700-5000 m. The Hawaiian arch and moat add some uncertainty, but the seafloor north of the islands is typically near 4800 m deep. I estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

4124.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude 149.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, southeast of Iturup in the Kuril Islands. Iturup lies at roughly 45°N, 147–148.5°E, and its eastern end is near 148.9°E at about 45.3°N, around Cape Van der Lind. At 45.0°N, 149.0°E I am probably just off the Pacific coast of Iturup, though I could be slightly inland.

Iturup's southeast coast runs from about 44.9°N, 147.5°E northeast to 45.5°N, 148.8°E. The northern tip near Kasatka Bay sits at about 45.5°N, 148.0°E. Cape Van der Lind is at about 45.2°N, 148.9°E. Cape Kasatka is near 44.9°N, 147.6°E, so the island's southeast coast trends northeast. At 149.0°E the island would be at about 45.3°N, and at 45.0°N, 149.0°E I am likely in the Pacific, southeast of the coast. The shelf there is narrow, so the depth is probably a few hundred meters, and I estimate about -1000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1000}

11866.text:
The point is at 41°S, 153°E, which lies in the Tasman Sea. That is east of Tasmania and southeast of New South Wales, well offshore. Eastern Australia's coast at 153°E sits near 28°S, so at 41°S this is open ocean.

The seafloor here is probably the Tasman Abyssal Plain, which is roughly 4,500–5,000 m deep. The Lord Howe Rise lies further east at about 160°E, and the Tasman Basin floor is about 4,500 m deep around 153°E at 41°S. I could not pin this down more precisely, so I am estimating about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

6562.text:
Latitude 17.0, longitude -15.0 is in Mauritania, in the interior south of Atar and near Aleg and Boghé. The coast at this latitude is around -16.2 (near Nouakchott, 18.1 N, -15.97), so -15.0 is inland. The area is Sahelian, in the Trarza/Brakna/Assaba region of sandy dunes, with elevations around 50 m. Aleg is at 17.05 N, -13.9 and sits at about 50 m. The area at -15 should be a bit lower, perhaps 30 m, since the Senegal River at Boghé (-14.3) is about 10 m. At 17 N, -15 there are fixed dunes (Trarza ergs), probably 30 m or so.

I estimate about 35 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":35}

7740.text:
The point is at 3°N, 179°W, in the central Pacific. The nearest land is Kiribati's Line Islands and Phoenix Islands. Kanton in the Phoenix group sits near 2.8°S, 171.7°W, and Baker Island is at 0.2°N, 176.5°W. Neither is close, so the point is open ocean.

The seabed there is likely abyssal plain or a slope near the Phoenix Islands, at roughly 5000 m depth. Baker Island is surrounded by deep water, and the area west of the Line Islands sits at about 5000–5500 m. I estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

10150.text:
The point (-23, -39) lies in the South Atlantic, off the coast of Brazil, east of Espírito Santo and Rio de Janeiro. The coast near Cabo Frio is at about -42°W, so -39°W is well offshore. This area is the Brazil Basin and the Vitória-Trindade Chain region. Trindade is at -20.5°, -29.3°, so it is not nearby.

The location is south of the Vitória-Trindade chain and sits on the São Paulo Plateau or its edge. The São Paulo Plateau lies around 23-28°S and 40-45°W, with depths of about 2000-3500 m. At -39°W and -23°S the point is likely on the continental rise or the abyssal plain, so I estimate a depth of about 3500 m. The seafloor here may be a little shallower because of the Vitória-Trindade seamounts, but those are mostly north of 21°S, so I'll keep 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

12193.text:
Latitude -45, longitude 87 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Kerguelen and southwest of Australia. It lies north of the Southeast Indian Ridge, which sits around 50°S at this longitude, and the Amsterdam–St Paul plateau is farther to the northwest. The Southeast Indian Ridge axis near 87°E is at roughly 47°S. A point at 45°S is therefore on its flank, where the seafloor is typically about 3000–3500 m deep. The Australian-Antarctic Basin lies to the south and east. The Broken Ridge is at about 30°S and is not relevant here.

I estimate the depth at about 3600 m, so the elevation is about -3600 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

14176.text:
Location: -67.0, 93.0 is in East Antarctica, offshore from the Davis/Mirny region. The Shackleton Ice Shelf lies around 95-100°E at about 66°S. At 93°E, 67°S, this is near the coast, close to the Davis Sea and the Western Shackleton area. The coastline at 93°E is around 66.5°S, where the Denman Glacier is near 99°E and the Scott Glacier is near 100°E. Along 93°E the coast sits near 66.3°S, and at 67°S there may be an ice sheet margin with elevation of several hundred meters. The continental shelf there is about 500 m deep.

Gaussberg is at 89°E, 66.8°S, and the coast near 93°E is ice-covered. At 67°S, 93°E the surface is probably grounded ice at around 1000 m elevation, but the coast near Cape Penck is at 66.5°S, 95°E. Given the Bedmap grounding line, I'd guess 67°S is about 50-100 km inland, with an ice surface elevation of about 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

14256.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -107 is in the Amundsen Sea sector of West Antarctica, off the Eights Coast and Thurston Island area. Thurston Island lies near 72°W, so this point is far west of it. Peter I Island is near 90°W at 69°S. At 107°W and 69°S the point is open ocean on the continental shelf margin, north of the Abbot Ice Shelf, which spans roughly 90–103°W at about 72°S. Because 69°S is north of that coast, I read it as ocean over the outer shelf or continental slope.

The Amundsen–Bellingshausen shelf break is around 70–71°S there, and the shelf is deep, 500–800 m. At 69°S the point is probably on the slope or rise, with a depth of roughly 2500–3500 m. I'll estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

12563.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 107 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies near the Southeast Indian Ridge, which here is at roughly 48-50°S and 110-120°E, and the Kerguelen Plateau is farther west at about 70-80°E. The seabed at this point is therefore probably the ridge flank or the ridge itself, with depths around 3000-4000 m.

The Australian-Antarctic Ridge region around 49°S, 107°E should have depths of roughly 3500 m. I'm estimating that rather than recalling a measured value, so I'll use -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

10109.text:
Location: -23, -121 is in the South Pacific, east of Easter Island (Easter Island is at -27, -109) and west of Chile. Near the East Pacific Rise's eastern flank, the seafloor depth is roughly 3,500-3,800 m. The East Pacific Rise is at about -113 to -115 longitude at this latitude. At -121, the crust is around 8-10 Myr old, so the depth is about 3,600 m. The Pacific-Antarctic ridge is not near this point. Pitcairn is at -25, -130, and the Easter seamount chain lies near -26, -112 to -105, so neither is close.

I estimate a depth of about 3,700 m, slightly adjusted for the region's shallow bathymetry.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

15716.text:
Location: -85°S, -67°W (longitude 67°W). This is in West Antarctica/Antarctic Peninsula's southern region, near the Support Force Glacier and the Foundation Ice Stream, close to the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf grounding line and the Thiel Mountains/Pensacola Mountains area. The Pensacola Mountains lie around 83-85°S, 50-65°W, and the Ronne Ice Shelf is farther north and west.

At 85°S, 67°W, I think this is in the Foundation Ice Stream or Rutford catchment, on grounded ice. Foundation Ice Stream is around 83-85°S, 60-70°W, and the ice surface there is about 500-1000 m. At 85°S the ice surface is probably around 1000 m. The Support Force Glacier is at about 83°S, 55°W. Further south, toward the Thiel Mountains (85°S, 91°W), the surface rises to about 1500 m. At 67°W the elevation is probably about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

15691.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -117 is in West Antarctica, on the interior ice sheet. It lies near the Ellsworth Land and Marie Byrd Land region, close to the divide area, and is grounded ice. The ice surface there is roughly 1,500-2,000 m.

Looking at nearby points: the West Antarctic Ice Sheet divide is at about 79.5S, 112W, with an ice surface of about 1,800 m. At 85S, 117W, the location is toward the Ross Ice Shelf side, upstream of the Reedy and Scott Glaciers. The Reedy Glacier is at roughly 85S, 135W. At 85S, 117W, I estimate an ice surface of about 1,900 m. That puts it near the Horlick and Thiel Mountains area, where the Thiel Mountains are at 85S, 91W. The surface is therefore likely 1,800-2,200 m, and I'll report 2,000.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

1798.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 177.0°E lies in the East Siberian/Chukchi Sea area. Wrangel Island is at about 71.2°N, 179.5°W, so 177°E is west of the island, in the Long Strait region or just north of it. The Long Strait is about 70°N, so 71°N at 177°E is in the East Siberian Sea or Chukchi Sea shelf, which is shallow, roughly 40-50 m deep.

The point is 71°N, 177°E. Wrangel Island spans roughly 178.5°E to 177.5°W, and its southwestern tip is near 70.8°N, 178.5°E. At 177°E, the point is about 1.5° west of that, which is about 53 km at this latitude, so it is in open water. The depth there is probably around 45 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

2621.text:
The coordinates 61.0°N, 23.0°E fall in southern Finland, in the Pirkanmaa region near Tampere. Tampere is at about 61.5°N, 23.8°E, so this point lies southwest of it, close to Lammi or Loimaa. Loimaa is at about 60.85°N, 23.05°E, and Forssa at about 60.8°N, 23.6°E. The point is likely near Urjala or Vammala. Vammala (Sastamala) is at about 61.33°N, 22.9°E, so the point is around Tyrväntö or Alastaro, in Finland Proper or Pirkanmaa. Terrain there is typically 80–110 m above sea level.

I could not pin down whether the point sits on a lake. The area around Punkalaidun (61.1°N, 23.1°E) and Loimaa is farmland at roughly 80–90 m. I will go with land at about 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

2082.text:
