Latitude 71.0N, longitude -11.0 (11°W) lies in the Greenland Sea, northeast of Jan Mayen. Jan Mayen is at about 71.0N, 8.5W, so this point is about 2.5° of longitude west of the island, roughly 95 km. The area sits between Jan Mayen and the East Greenland shelf, which is far to the west at about 20°W.

The Jan Mayen Ridge extends south from Jan Mayen, and the Mohn Ridge lies to the north. At 71N, 11W the seafloor is likely in the Greenland Basin or on the slope of the Jan Mayen platform. Depths west of Jan Mayen are about 2000-3000 m, in the Greenland Basin at about 3000 m. Near Jan Mayen the island shelf is shallow, but 95 km away the depth is probably around 2500 m, so I estimate -2600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2600}

3981.text:
The point 45°N, 137°W is in the northeast Pacific, roughly 1,000 km west of the Oregon coast. It lies west of the Juan de Fuca Ridge and the Blanco Fracture Zone, on the abyssal plain of the Pacific plate.

The Juan de Fuca Ridge sits at about 130°W, and the Gorda and Juan de Fuca plates lie east of it. At 137°W the seafloor is about 8–10 million years old. Depths in that area are typically around 3,000 to 3,500 m on the Cascadia Basin side, while the abyssal plain further west is deeper, near 3,800 m. The Surveyor Fan and Surveyor Channel lie around 50°N, 140°W, to the north of this point. At 45°N, 137°W the depth is probably around 3,500 m, so I estimate about -3,550 m. This is an estimate from regional depth patterns, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3550}

2795.text:
Latitude 59.0, Longitude 11.0 is in southeastern Norway/western Sweden. The Oslofjord is at about 10.6° E, and 11.0° E at 59.0° N is near the Swedish border, in Østfold (Norway) or Dalsland/Bohuslän. Østfold near Halden is at 59.12° N, 11.39° E. At 59.0° N, 11.0° E the position is just east of the Iddefjord/Hvaler area. The Ra/Hvaler coast is around 11.0° E at 59.0° N, so this could be near Skjeberg or Sarpsborg. Glomma is at 11.1° E at Sarpsborg (59.28° N). At 59.0° N, 11.0° E, that is inland, around Rakkestad or Skjeberg to the west. Land seems likely, with an elevation of about 100 m.

Further south, Hvaler is at 59.05° N, 10.95° E. The Singlefjord is between Hvaler and Sweden, at about 11.1° E. So 59.0, 11.0 could be right on the Hvaler islands or near the Oslofjord mouth. It is uncertain, but the coastline near Kirkøy is about 59.08° N. At 59.0° N the Hvaler archipelago's southern islands (Søndre Sandøy, Vesterøy) are around 59.0° N, 10.9° E. Close to 11.0° E there is Ytre Hvaler, mostly sea or islands. I'll guess Solid with a low elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":30}

7976.text:
