12346.text:
Latitude -47, longitude 33 lies in the Southwest Indian Ocean, south of Africa and near the Southwest Indian Ridge. The Prince Edward Islands are at about 37.7°E, 46.9°S, so they are well to the east of this point. At 33°E the point is west of the islands.

The Del Caño Rise is around 44°S, 40°E, and the Southwest Indian Ridge near 47°S, 33°E has depths of about 3000-4000 m. The Andrew Bain Fracture Zone is near 30-35°E at roughly 50°S. The ridge axis at about 33°E sits near 53°S, since the ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction at 55°S, 0°E up to the Indomed fracture zone at around 40°S, 50°E. So at 47°S, 33°E we are probably in the Crozet Basin or on the Africa-plate flank. The seafloor there is probably around 3000-4000 m deep, so I estimate -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

1818.text:
The point 69.0°N, 143.0°W is in northern Yukon/Alaska, near the Arctic coast. The Alaska–Yukon border is at 141°W, so 143°W lies in Alaska, within the Arctic National Wildlife Refuge. The coast there is at about 70°N, so 69°N is inland on the North Slope, in the foothills of the Brooks Range (the Romanzof/Franklin Mountains area).

The Sheenjek and Kongakut rivers lie further south and west. At 143°W, 69°N, the Hulahula River is near 144°W and the Okpilak near 144°W. The Jago River is around 143.5°W. At 69°N on these rivers, the elevation is probably about 600-1000 m. The Brooks Range crest is near 69.3°N, where peaks around Mt. Chamberlin (2749 m) sit at 144°W, 69.3°N. At 69.0°N, 143°W the terrain is therefore in the mountains south of the crest, and I'd estimate about 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

9943.text:
The point (-21.0, -93.0) lies in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and south of the Galápagos. It sits on the Nazca Plate, east of the East Pacific Rise and west of the Peru-Chile Trench.

The seafloor here is roughly 3,000-4,000 m deep. At 21°S the East Pacific Rise is near 114°W, so 93°W is about 21° east of the ridge. At fast spreading rates of about 15 cm/yr, that distance corresponds to a crustal age of about 15-20 Ma, which gives a depth of roughly 3,500-3,900 m. The Nazca Ridge starts near 15°S and runs southeast, so it is not at this location. The Easter Island seamount chain lies farther west. The Chile Rise is farther south. I estimate a depth of about 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

12770.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 161 is in the Southern Ocean southwest of New Zealand. It lies southeast of Macquarie Island (about -54.6, 158.9) and well south of Campbell Island (-52.5, 169). The area is on or near the Macquarie Ridge, which runs from New Zealand's South Island toward Macquarie Island. At 161E and 51S the ridge is probably nearby, but the ridge lies around 159-160E at this latitude, so the point is likely on the flank or in the basin to the east.

Seafloor depth east of the ridge here is roughly 3000-4500 m. The Emerald Basin lies to the southeast and the Tasman Sea basin to the west. The Macquarie Ridge crest near 51S is about 159.5E, and at 161E the floor is probably about 3500 m deep. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

10025.text:
Latitude -21, longitude 71 lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and southeast of the Mascarene Plateau. The Mascarene Plateau (Saya de Malha, Nazareth Bank) runs from about 60°E to 63°E, and the Mascarene Basin lies between Madagascar and the plateau at roughly 55°E.

At 71°E, 21°S, we are east of the Mascarene Plateau and near the Central Indian Ridge, which runs roughly through 66°E at 20°S. The Rodrigues Triple Junction is at about 70°E, 25.5°S. So 71°E, 21°S is probably on the flank of the Central Indian Basin or a ridge, with depths around 4000 m. The area east of the Central Indian Ridge near 21°S, 71°E likely has depths of about 3500–4500 m, so I estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

12335.text:
The point at 47°S, 11°E is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Bouvet Island (54°S, 3°E) and well south of Africa. It sits near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Bouvet triple junction area, but a bit north of it.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge runs roughly from Bouvet at about 54°S, 1°E northwest toward 40°S, 15°W. The Agulhas Ridge lies near 45°S, 5°E to 10°E and is part of the Agulhas–Falkland fracture zone, which runs from about 40°S, 15°E to 49°S, 5°W. The Agulhas Ridge is shallower, about 2500–3500 m. At 47°S, 11°E we are probably south of the Agulhas Ridge, in the Cape Basin or the Agulhas Basin, which is deeper at around 4000–5000 m. I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

11959.text:
The point at 43°S, 21°W lies in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 43°S sits at roughly 15-16°W, and the Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone region is further south. The Rio Grande Rise is farther north, so this point is likely on the western flank of the ridge, in the Argentine Basin's eastern margin.

Ridge flank depths at about 5-6° west of the axis are typically 3000-3500 m. The ridge crest here is shallow, around 2000-2500 m. At 21°W the crust is roughly 12 Myr old (spreading about 2 cm/yr per side gives roughly 500 km, which is too far), so I'll use a rougher estimate. At 43°S the spreading rate is about 3.5 cm/yr full rate, so a half rate of about 1.75 cm/yr over about 5° (about 400 km) gives about 23 Myr. Depth is then about 2500 + 350·√23 ≈ 4200 m. The nearby Mid-Atlantic Ridge is also somewhat elevated near 40°S because of the Tristan hotspot influence, so I'll settle on about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

4434.text:
Location 41.0N, 49.0E is in Azerbaijan, near the Caspian coast. The Caspian shoreline near Baku is at about 49.8E at 40.4N. At 41.0N, the coast runs from Sumgait (40.59N, 49.67E) up to Khizi and Siyazan (41.08N, 49.1E), and then to Khachmaz, so the coast near 41.0N sits at about 49.3E or further east. At 49.0E, 41.0N, that is inland, in the Shirvan/Gobustan foothills. Siyazan is at 41.08N, 49.11E, with an elevation of about 100 m. The point is just southwest of Siyazan, in the hills toward Shabran (41.2N, 48.99E), and the Khizi mountains are around there. Estimated elevation is about 400 m.

Looking at it more carefully, Shabran lies at 41.2N and 48.9E, at roughly 600 m. The area at 41.0N, 49.0E is the southeastern Greater Caucasus foothills, near Khizi (40.9N, 49.07E), where the elevation is about 600 m. The Khizi region has elevations of 500-1000 m, so I'll go with 500.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":500}

15024.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -11 is in Antarctica, in the Weddell Sea sector near the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf's eastern edge. Longitude -11 at 77°S lies near the Brunt Ice Shelf and Riiser-Larsen Ice Shelf, which are around -20 to -25 longitude and 72-75°S. At 77°S, -11°, I think this is inland in Dronning Maud Land, on grounded ice near the Shackleton Range (which is around -25 longitude, 80°S) or the Theron Mountains (around -26, 79°S). The Filchner Ice Shelf front is at around -40 to -30 longitude. So -11 at -77 is likely inland ice sheet, east of the Weddell Sea coast, with surface elevation around 1500-2500 m.

The coast at -11 lies near 71-72°S (Ekström/Jelbart), so 77°S is about 500 km inland. The surface there would be roughly 2500 m or a bit more, probably 2000-2600 m. I'll estimate 2400.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

15811.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 123 lies in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. It is roughly 500 km from the South Pole, in the direction of Dome A / Dome Fuji (Dome A is at about -80.4, 77.2; Dome C is at -75, 123). At -85 and 123E, the surface is on the plateau toward the Aurora subglacial basin and Vostok. Vostok is at -78.5, 106.8 with an elevation of about 3500 m. Toward the pole along the 123E meridian, the surface elevation is around 3000 m, perhaps 2900-3200 m. At -85, 123E, I estimate about 3000 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

14752.text:
Location: -73.0, 165.0 is in Antarctica, Victoria Land, near the Ross Sea coast. At 73°S, 165°E, this is near the Pennell Coast / Northern Victoria Land, in the area inland of Cape Adare... Actually Cape Adare is at 71.3°S, 170°E. Terra Nova Bay is at 74.7°S, 164°E. At 73°S, 165°E, it's inland of the Pennell Coast, around the Rennick Glacier region to the west and the Tucker Glacier. The coast at 165°E is around 72.5°S (Borchgrevink Coast near Lady Newnes Bay, with the coast around 73°S at 169°E). So this point is likely on land in the mountains (Admiralty Mountains/ Victoria Land), with ice surface elevation around 2000 m.

Admiralty Mountains lie at ~72°S, 169°E. At 73°S, 165°E it is between the Tucker Glacier (73°S, 167°E) and the Mariner Glacier (73.5°S, 166°E). Likely high plateau, maybe 2000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

3517.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude 15.0 is in southwestern Poland/eastern Germany, near the Czech border. It is close to Zgorzelec/Görlitz (51.15, 15.0), so 51.0 N is a bit south, near Zittau/Lubań. Lubań is at about 51.12 N, 15.28 E. Around 51.0 N, 15.0 E is the area near Zittau's northern side, Ostritz or Bernstadt, in the Neisse valley and foothills of Upper Lusatia.

The Neisse valley near Ostritz is about 200 m. Hillier terrain to the west reaches about 250-300 m. I'll estimate 240 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":240}

8826.text:
