The Penrhyn Basin has depths of about 5000-5500 m. At 9°S, 167°W the water is likely about 5200 m deep. The Tokelau seamount chain lies to the west, and the area around 167°W is probably abyssal plain at roughly 5300 m.


{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

554.text:
The point at 83°N, 151°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Beaufort Sea and the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. It is well away from land, so the surface type is Water.

The Canada Basin abyssal plain is roughly 3800 m deep. Further north is the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge, which rises to about 1500–2500 m. The Alpha Ridge runs from Ellesmere Island toward the Siberian side, around 85°N and 120°W–180°. At 83°N, 151°W the point is probably on the edge of the Canada Basin, near the Mendeleev/Alpha Ridge flank, where depths are around 2000–3000 m. I'll estimate about -2500 m, which is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

71.text:
Latitude 89°N is in the Arctic Ocean, near the North Pole. At 89°N, 37°W, the point lies on the Lomonosov Ridge side or in the Amundsen Basin. The Lomonosov Ridge runs near 140°E to 40°W through the pole, so its position at 89°N is around 50°W–140°E. At longitude -37°, it is probably in the Amundsen Basin, which is on the Eurasian side of the ridge. The Amundsen Basin is about 4000-4400 m deep.

The North Pole itself is at about 4261 m depth, and the ridge near the pole lies toward the Canadian side (roughly 90°W–180°). Greenland's side of the ridge is at about 60°W-ish. At 89°N, 37°W, which is 1° from the pole (111 km), the point is toward Greenland/Atlantic, which is on the Amundsen Basin side at about 4,300 m depth. The ridge crest is about 1000-1500 m deep and lies at roughly 88-89°N, on the 140°E–40°W meridian line. The point at 37°W lies close to that line, so it might be on the ridge, which makes this uncertain. Per IBCAO, the ridge at the pole is offset toward 90°W–ish... In fact the ridge crosses at about 88.5°N, 60°W–ish with a depth of around 1000 m. At 37°W, 89°N, it is likely on the flank, with a depth of about 2500 m. I'll guess about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

13121.text:
The point -55, 143 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Tasmania, which lies at about -43 to -44.

The area south of Tasmania and southeast of the South Tasman Rise is deep ocean. The Macquarie Ridge lies further east, at about 159E. At 55S 143E the region sits on the abyssal plain between the Australian-Antarctic Basin and the Tasman Sea. The South Tasman Rise is at about 47-50S and 145-150E. At 55S 143E, the depth is likely about 3000-4000 m. The Australian-Antarctic Basin there is around 4000 m deep, and the Southeast Indian Ridge sits at about 50S 140E, with depths of about 3000-3500 m. At 55S the seafloor is probably about 3500-4500 m deep. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15263.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 107 is in East Antarctica, on the high plateau near Vostok Station (-78.46, 106.84). Vostok sits at about 3,488 m ice surface elevation. At -79, 107 the point is about 30 km south of Vostok. The surface there is around 3,500 m, since the ice sheet slopes gently in this region. Lake Vostok extends roughly from -77.5 to -78.5 latitude, so -79 is probably just south of the lake's southern end and on grounded ice.

The ice surface there is probably about 3,480–3,500 m. I'll answer 3490.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3490}

13107.text:
The point (-55, 115) lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. It sits southwest of the Australian-Antarctic Basin, near the Southeast Indian Ridge. The ridge crosses around 50°S at 115°E, so at 55°S the seafloor is probably in the Australian-Antarctic Basin at roughly 3500–4500 m depth.

The Southeast Indian Ridge runs from about 48°S 90°E to 50°S 115°E and then southeast toward 60°S 150°E. At 115°E the ridge is near 49–50°S, and the Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies around 120°E, 49°S. At 55°S the seafloor is therefore south of the ridge, with an estimated depth of about 3800 m, and I'll answer -3800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

8375.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 11.0 is in the Republic of the Congo, near the Gabon border. The Atlantic coast lies around 11.8°E at this latitude, so the point is inland. It sits in the Mayombe/Niari region of southwest Congo, or possibly just inside Gabon (Nyanga province). The Gabon–Congo border near -3° runs roughly at 11.1°E. Terrain there is hilly, with elevations of about 300–500 m in the Chaillu massif foothills.

I'll estimate about 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

7952.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 115°W is in the eastern equatorial Pacific, roughly west of the Galápagos and south of the Clipperton Fracture Zone. The seafloor there is about 3,800 to 4,200 m deep on the abyssal plain.

The East Pacific Rise is near 110°W, and at 1°N, 115°W the crust is about 5 to 8 million years old. Seafloor of that age usually lies near 3,900 to 4,000 m depth. I'll estimate 3,950 m, so the elevation is about -3950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

14532.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 85 is in East Antarctica, south of the Davis Sea and west of the Shackleton Ice Shelf (about 95-100E). At 85E the coast sits near 66-67S, so 71S is about 500 km inland. That puts it on the ice sheet in Princess Elizabeth Land, where the surface is probably around 2000-2500 m. Gamburtsev-side ice there is high, and near 71S, 85E the surface is roughly 2400 m.

The question asks for the ice-surface elevation, since this is grounded ice, so I'll go with about 2300 m. This is an estimate from the general geography, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

1471.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -117.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Victoria Island spans roughly 69–73.5°N and 100–119°W, and its northern coast near Prince Albert Peninsula reaches about 73°N at around -116°W. Prince Albert Peninsula is at about 72.5–73.5°N and -115 to -119°W. Just to the north is Prince of Wales Strait (about -118°W) and M'Clure Strait.

Longitude -117 at 73°N is probably on the Prince Albert Peninsula, or possibly in Richard Collinson Inlet. Richard Collinson Inlet is on the northwest of Victoria Island, around 72.5°N and -114.5°W. The peninsula is a plateau of low relief, with elevation of about 200–300 m. Minto Inlet is at about -117.5°W and 71.5°N, and Prince Albert Sound lies further south. At 73°N, -117°W, this is likely the northern part of the peninsula, which is land. The northern coast of Victoria Island at Cape Wrottesley is about 73.1°N and -118.5°W. So the point is probably land, though close to the coast. I estimate an elevation of about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

9769.text:
The point (-19.0, -81.0) is in the Pacific Ocean, off the coast of southern Peru and northern Chile. The Peru-Chile Trench lies around 71-72°W at this latitude, so -81° is well to the west of it, on the Nazca Plate near the Nazca Ridge region.

The Nazca Ridge trends northeast from about 26°S, 85°W toward 15°S, 76°W. At 19°S the ridge sits near 79-80°W, so -81° is close to its western flank. Ridge crest depths there are about 2000-3500 m, while the surrounding abyssal plain is around 4000-4500 m. Because the point is near the ridge's western edge, I estimate a depth of about 3900 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

7314.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 49.0°E lies in Somalia, in the Nugal/Puntland region. The Somali coast near 9°N is at roughly 50.9°E at Garacad (about 7°N) and Eyl (about 8°N, 49.8°E). At 9°N the coast sits near 50.7°E (Bargaal is at 11°N, 51°E), so 49.0°E is inland. Garowe is at 8.4°N, 48.5°E with an elevation of about 300 m. At 9°N, 49°E the position is northeast of Garowe, in the Nugal valley or the Hobyo plateau, at around 400-500 m.

I estimate about 450 m, treating it as Solid terrain.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

14586.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -167 is in the Ross Sea, on the continental shelf, north of the Ross Ice Shelf front (which lies at about -78 to -77.5). At -73 and -167 (west of the Ross Sea center, near the Pennell Coast/Victoria Land is around 170E), this is in the open Ross Sea. The Ross Sea shelf is around 500 m deep, with the outer shelf at 400-700 m and the shelf break near -70 to -71. At -73, -167 the depth is likely about 500 m, though the Ross Sea has banks (Pennell Bank, Ross Bank) at around 200-500 m and troughs deeper. Longitude 167W at lat -73 is toward the eastern Ross Sea, near the Hallett/Glomar Challenger basin; depths there are about 500-600 m.

I'll estimate -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

1332.text:
Latitude 75°N, longitude 35°W is off the coast of northeast Greenland. The Greenland coast near 75°N sits around 18-22°W (Kong Oscar Fjord, Traill Island), and the ice sheet edge is further west still. At 35°W, 75°N, we are in the Greenland Sea or on the NE Greenland continental shelf. The shelf there extends to roughly 10-15°W, so 35°W is beyond the shelf, probably over the Greenland Basin or the Boreas Basin margin. Fram Strait lies at about 0°, and the deep Greenland Sea is further east. At 35°W and 75°N, the position is much farther west than I first thought.

Checking this: the Greenland east coast at 75°N is near 20°W, and the ice sheet interior lies west of that. Longitude 35°W at 75°N is therefore on the ice sheet itself. The ice sheet elevation at 75°N, 35°W is about 2,500-2,900 m. The summit region around 72.5°N, 38°W (GRIP) is 3,230 m, and NGRIP at 75.1°N, 42.3°W is 2,917 m. At 35°W, the elevation should be a bit lower, around 2,700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2700}

6673.text:
The point (15°N, 153°W) lies in the central Pacific, south of Hawaii. The nearby seafloor is abyssal plain at about 5,000 to 5,500 m depth. The Hawaiian Islands are far to the north, around 19–22°N, and the Line Islands are farther to the southwest and west. At this location the Pacific plate seafloor is about 90 Ma old, which fits a depth near 5,400 m.

I did not look up a bathymetry value, so I'm estimating the depth at about 5,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5350}

893.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 167°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands and east of the Severnaya Zemlya region. It is on the East Siberian/Laptev side of the Arctic basin, near the Lomonosov Ridge and the Siberian continental shelf edge.

The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands toward Ellesmere Island and sits at roughly 140°E at the Siberian end. At 81°N, 167°E the position is probably over the Amerasian Basin side, near the Mendeleev Ridge region. The Mendeleev Ridge runs around 175°E, 80°N, with depths of about 1000-2000 m. The shelf break near 77-78°N at 165°E is steep, so at 81°N the seabed could be in the Podvodnikov Basin, at roughly 2500-3000 m depth. The Podvodnikov Basin lies between the Lomonosov and Mendeleev ridges, and at 81°N, 167°E the depth is probably about 2400 m. I'll estimate -2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

2846.text:
Latitude 59.0 N, longitude 113.0 E is in Siberia, in the Sakha (Yakutia) Republic near the Lena River basin and the Vilyuy area. The point is likely north of the Aldan highlands, in the Lena-Vilyuy plateau region, which I estimate at roughly 300 m.

Close to the Vilyuy and Chara basins, it may be near the Olyokma River. Olyokma at 59N is around 120E, so 113E is further west, near Lensk (60.7N, 114.9E), where the elevation is about 150-200 m. The Lena at Lensk is at about 120 m, and the plateau around it reaches 250-350 m. At 59N, 113E, the area is toward the Nepa/Peleduy region, with Peleduy at 59.4N, 112.7E. Elevations there are about 250 m, so I'll go with that.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":260}

13099.text:
Latitude -55, longitude 99 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and southwest of the Southeast Indian Ridge region. The Southeast Indian Ridge runs roughly from (-50, 70) toward (-50, 110) and then further southeast toward (-60, 150), so at -55, 99 we are probably south of the ridge crest on the Antarctic plate side.

The ridge crest near longitude 100 sits at about -48 to -49 latitude. At -55 the seafloor is older and deeper, probably around 3500 to 4000 m. The Australian-Antarctic Basin there lies roughly 4000 m deep, and the flanks of the ridge are about 3500 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11890.text:
The point at -43.0, -159.0 is in the South Pacific, southeast of the Cook Islands and south of French Polynesia. It sits in open ocean in the Pacific basin, so the surface type is Water.

For the seabed depth, this area lies on the Pacific plate at roughly 43°S. The abyssal plain there is typically 4,000–5,000 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further south and east, and the Southwest Pacific basin is deeper than 5,000 m. At 159°W, 43°S, the seafloor is probably around 4,800 m deep, so I estimate -4800 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

9980.text:
Latitude -21, longitude -19 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa (Namibia is at about 15°E) and east of Brazil. The location lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near 13°W at this latitude, and the Rio Grande Rise is farther west, so the point is in the Angola/Brazil basin region. The Brazil Basin is deep, around 5000 m, and at 19°W the seafloor is on the western flank of the ridge. Ridge flank depths there are about 3500-4500 m.

The ridge axis at 21°S is near 13.5°W, and the crest depth is about 2500 m. At 5.5° of longitude west of the axis, the crust is about 10 Myr old, so the depth would be roughly 3500-4000 m. I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

13847.text:
The point at latitude -63, longitude 155 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and New Zealand and north of Antarctica's Wilkes Land coast. Antarctica's coast there lies near 66-67°S, so this is open ocean.

Seafloor depth in this region is probably about 3,000-4,000 m. The Australian-Antarctic basin lies to the west, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Balleny region lie to the east, around 160-165°E at 60-67°S. At 155°E, 63°S, the seafloor is probably on the abyssal plain at roughly 3,500-4,000 m deep. The Southeast Indian Ridge reaches about 140-150°E at 50-60°S, which is further north and west, so it does not matter here. I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15738.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -23 lies in Antarctica, in the area south of the Weddell Sea near the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf and the Support Force Glacier region. At 85°S, 23°W, the location is likely in the Foundation Ice Stream / Academy Glacier area, or the Pensacola Mountains.

The Pensacola Mountains sit around 83-85°S, 50-65°W. At 23°W and 85°S, the position is east of the Filchner Ice Shelf, roughly in the Recovery Glacier / Theron Mountains area. The Theron Mountains are near 79°S, 28°W, and the Shackleton Range is near 80-81°S, 25°W. At 85°S, 23°W we are on the East Antarctic ice sheet inland of the Shackleton Range, near the Recovery Glacier catchment. Surface elevation is probably about 1500-2500 m. Recovery Glacier's upper part is near 83-85°S, where the surface is about 1000-1500 m. I'll estimate about 1900 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 1900}

14847.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -5 is in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The Riiser-Larsen and Brunt ice shelves lie near 75°S at about -20° to -25°W, and the Fimbul Ice Shelf is near 70°S, 0°. At -75, -5 the location is inland on the plateau, south of the Ahlmann Ridge and the Jutulstraumen area. Ice-sheet surface elevation there is probably about 2500-3000 m.

Along the Greenwich meridian, 75°S is around 2900 m surface elevation. At -5° it is probably similar, perhaps 2800 m. This area lies south of the Maudheim escarpment, near the Heimefrontfjella (about 74.5°S, -11°W), where the ice surface is about 1000-2000 m. The Kottas Mountains are near 74°S, -9°W. The ice at -75, -5 is east of Heimefrontfjella, probably about 2400 m. I'll go with 2500.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

6707.text:
Latitude 15.0, longitude -85.0 is in the Caribbean Sea, northeast of Honduras and south of the Cayman Trough. It sits north of the Honduran coast, which is near 15.8°N at -85°, and the Bay Islands lie around -86 to -85.5. That puts it close to the coast, but the Honduran north coast at -85° (near Brus Laguna, at about 15.8°N, -84.5°) is still to the north, so the point is probably on land.

Honduras' Mosquitia region spans roughly 15 to 16°N at longitudes -85 to -83. At -85° the coast near Iriona is around 15.9°N. So 15.0°N, -85.0° is inland in Colón/Olancho department, in the hills south of the coast. The Sierra de Agalta is near -85.9, 15°N. At -85.0, 15.0 this is the Patuca lowlands and Olancho foothills, with an elevation of maybe 200 m. Rio Patuca runs near -85.2, 15.0. I'll estimate about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

11920.text:
The point is at 43°S, 99°W in the southeast Pacific, far west of Chile. It lies roughly west of the Chile Rise and the East Pacific Rise, which sits near 115°W at this latitude. The Chile Rise runs east-west near 38–40°S at 90°W, and the Chile Trench is at about 74°W. The seafloor here is therefore young to intermediate-age oceanic crust, probably 15–30 Myr old, and the depth is typically around 3500–4000 m. The rise crest at about 115°W would be near 2800 m, so a point 16° of longitude east would be a bit deeper, around 3700 m.

This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3750}

3301.text:
Latitude 53.0, longitude -57.0 is in the South Atlantic, near the Falkland Islands. The Falklands sit at about 51-52.5°S and 57-61°W, but this point is at 53.0 with no stated hemisphere. Read as 53°N, 57°W, it falls in Labrador, Canada, in the interior. The Labrador coast at 53°N is around 56°W (Cartwright is at 53.7°N, 57.0°W), and Lake Melville is further north at 53.7°N. Sandwich Bay is at 53.6°N, 56.5°W. So 53.0°N, 57.0°W is inland, southwest of Cartwright, in boreal forest and bog with many lakes. The elevation there is probably about 150 m, though I can't rule out a lake.

I'll answer Solid at about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

5417.text:
The point 29°N, 145°W is in the open North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and west of California. The seafloor there is abyssal plain at roughly 4,800 to 5,300 m depth, and the region lies on the Pacific plate, with the Murray Fracture Zone to the north.

The seafloor age here is roughly 80 million years, which fits a depth of about 5,000 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so this is an estimate from regional depth patterns rather than a measured figure. I'll use -5,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

5483.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude -13.0 lies in the Atlantic Ocean, just east of Lanzarote and Fuerteventura. Lanzarote spans roughly 28.85-29.25°N and 13.4-13.9°W, so -13.0 is about 40 km east of Lanzarote, which puts the point offshore.

The shelf between the Canary Islands and Africa is shallow in places, but the channel between Lanzarote and the African coast (Cape Juby is near 27.9°N, 12.9°W) is about 100 km wide. The Africa coast near 29°N is at about -10.2°W. So -13.0 is in the channel, where depths are around 1000 m at the deeper parts east of Lanzarote. Lanzarote's east side drops to about 1000 m at 20-30 km offshore. The Lanzarote-Fuerteventura shelf is shallow, but to the east, near -13.0, the seabed is probably around 1300 m deep. Further east toward Africa the depth drops to about 1500 m and then rises on the African slope. I estimate about -1300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

6009.text:
Location: 23°N, 41°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 23°N is near 45°W, so 41°W is roughly 4 degrees east of the axis, on the African plate side. That is about 400 km from the axis, on crust roughly 10 million years old, so the depth is around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Atlantis fracture zone and the Kane fracture zone at 24°N lie near 45°W. At 41°W the seafloor is probably abyssal hills about 4,800 m deep.

I estimate a depth of about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

3251.text:
The point 53.0°N, 157.0°W is in the North Pacific south of the Alaska Peninsula. The peninsula's southern coast near Sand Point and the Shumagin Islands sits around 55°N, 160°W, so 53°N is roughly 200 km south of that coast. That puts the point in the open ocean south of the Aleutian chain. The Aleutian Trench lies south of the Alaska Peninsula, with its axis near 53–54°N at 157°W.

The trench axis at 157°W is probably around 54.5°N, and the trench depth there is about 6,000 m. The trench runs southwest from Kodiak, passing roughly 55°N at 160°W and 52.5°N at 170°W. That puts the axis at about 54°N near 158°W, so 53°N would be just south of it, on the trench's outer wall or the abyssal plain. The depth there is probably around 5,000 m, so I estimate about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

8441.text:
Latitude -3.0, Longitude 143.0 is in the Bismarck Sea, north of Papua New Guinea's coast. The coast near Wewak is around -3.55, 143.65, and the mouth of the Sepik River is near -3.85, 144.5. At -3.0, 143.0 the point is roughly 60 km north of the coast, so it is offshore.

The seafloor there is on the shelf edge or slope. North of Wewak, the Bismarck Sea basin deepens quickly, and the Bismarck Sea depth is about 2000 m or more. The shelf off Wewak is narrow, and near 3°S there are the Schouten Islands (Wei Island area) at about -3.2, 143.5. At -3.0, 143.0 the water is probably around 1000-1800 m deep. I'll guess -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

6665.text:
The point at 15°N, 169°W is in the open North Pacific, southwest of Hawaii and north of Samoa, so the surface is water. I estimated the seabed depth from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup.

The Pacific abyssal plain in this area is typically 5000–5700 m deep. The point lies roughly between the Line Islands and the Hawaiian-Emperor chain, in the central Pacific basin. The seafloor there is probably about 5500 m deep, so I used -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

2709.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -161.0 is in southwestern Alaska. Togiak Bay is near -160.4, 59.0, and Bristol Bay's northern shore runs near 58.7 N at -160. The Nushagak Peninsula sits around -158.8. At -161.0, 59.0 N, the Togiak area is near -160.4, 59.05. The Kulukak Bay area is near -160.0, 58.8. Cape Newenham is at -162.2, 58.65. Togiak Bay opens to the south at about 58.9 N. Walrus Islands lie near -160.4, 58.6.

At -161.0, 59.0 N, the Togiak National Wildlife Refuge covers the area. The Togiak River is at -160.4. Going west, Goodnews Bay is at -161.6, 59.1 N. Between -161.6 and -160.4 there are the Ahklun Mountains foothills and the Togiak Bay coast, so -161.0, 59.0 is likely land inland from the coast. The coast near Goodnews Bay runs southwest, and Security Cove lies around -161.8, 59.0. At -161.0 the area is likely tundra with lakes at an elevation of around 100 m, as the Ahklun Mountains extend there. The Kanektok River is at -161.5, 59.8 and Goodnews River is at -161.5, 59.2. I estimate about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

9983.text:
Latitude -21, longitude -13 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa (Namibia is at about 15°E) and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 20°S sits at about 13°W, which is close to this point. The ridge axis at 21°S is around 13.5°W, so the point is probably on or near the ridge crest.

Ridge crest depths there are around 3000 to 3500 m. The Rio Grande Rise and Walvis Ridge are not nearby. At 21°S the ridge is a fairly broad, deep axis, and I would estimate roughly -3600 m for the area.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

5584.text:
The point (27.0°N, 171.0°W) is in the North Pacific, near the northwestern Hawaiian Islands. Kure Atoll sits at about 28.4°N, 178.3°W and Midway at 28.2°N, 177.4°W. Pearl and Hermes Atoll is at 27.9°N, 175.8°W. Gardner Pinnacles is at 25.0°N, 168.0°W, and Maro Reef is at 25.4°N, 170.6°W. French Frigate Shoals is at 23.7°N, 166.2°W. Laysan Island is at 25.8°N, 171.7°W, and Lisianski is at 26.1°N, 173.9°W. The point at 27°N, 171°W is therefore north of Laysan and Maro Reef, and no known island lies there.

The seafloor in this area is the Hawaiian-Emperor swell region, with typical depths of about 4500 to 5000 m. Because the point lies north of the ridge, I estimate the depth at about 4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

3378.text:
Latitude 53.0, longitude 97.0 is in southern Siberia, in Krasnoyarsk Krai or Khakassia. The West Sayan Mountains lie around 52-53°N, 92-96°E. At 97°E and 53°N, this is probably the Eastern Sayan, near the Tuva/Irkutsk border. That area is mountainous taiga, typically 1500-2000 m.

The Oka plateau and Eastern Sayan lie further east, around 100°E. At 97°E, 53°N, we are near the Kan and Biryusa headwaters, in the Eastern Sayan high ridges. The Tofalaria region is at about 54°N, 97°E. At 53°N, 97°E, the terrain is probably high mountains of about 1800 m, in the Eastern Sayan near the Tuva border. I'll estimate 1750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1750}

12146.text:
Latitude -45, longitude -7 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Africa and northeast of the Bouvet region. It lies north of the Antarctic Polar Front. The nearby feature is the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs near 0° to -15° longitude at this latitude. The ridge axis near 45°S sits around 14°W to 16°W, and the Agulhas Ridge lies farther east. At 7°W the seafloor is likely on the eastern flank of the ridge, probably around 3000-4000 m deep.

The South Atlantic ridge axis near 45°S is around 15°W. So 7°W is about 8° east, roughly 600 km from the axis. With seafloor age of about 20-30 Myr, the depth would be about 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15531.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -77 is in West Antarctica, inland from the Ellsworth Mountains and the Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ellsworth Mountains and Vinson Massif sit near 78.5°W, 78.5°S, so -83 is well south of them. The Ronne-Filchner ice shelf edge is around -82 to -83 near longitude -60 to -50, but at -77 longitude and -83 latitude the surface is most likely grounded ice on the Antarctic ice sheet, the Institute/Rutford catchment area or the Pensacola area. The ice surface there is probably about 1000 to 1500 m.

The Ellsworth Subglacial Highlands lie around -80 to -84 latitude and -80 to -90 longitude, with ice surface elevations of roughly 1500 to 2000 m. At -83, -77, I estimate about 1200 m, which is a rough guess.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

5894.text:
The point 25.0°N, 89.0°E lies in northern Bangladesh, near the Brahmaputra (Jamuna) river. Rangpur division is around 25.7°N, 89.2°E. Gaibandha is about 25.33°N, 89.53°E. Bogra is at about 24.85°N, 89.37°E. The Jamuna runs near 89.7°E at this latitude, so 89.0°E is west of it, in the area around Joypurhat, Naogaon, and Natore. The Bogra area is about 20 m high, and the Barind Tract at roughly 25.0°N, 89.0°E is about 25-30 m. Dinajpur is at 25.6°N, 88.6°E, and Joypurhat at 25.1°N, 89.0°E sits at about 30 m.

I estimate the elevation at about 28 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":28}

9229.text:
The point (-13.0, -81.0) is in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 500 km west of Peru's coast (Lima is near -12, -77). That places it over the Peru Basin and Nazca Plate, west of the Peru-Chile Trench, which lies near -78 to -79 at this latitude.

The trench axis is about 6000 m deep. West of the trench the seafloor rises to the abyssal plain, which is typically 4000-4500 m deep. At -81, about 300 km west of the trench, the depth is probably around 4200-4500 m. I estimate the seabed at about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

5407.text:
The point 29.0°N, 165.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of Hawaii. Kure Atoll is at 28.4°N, 178.3°W and Pearl and Hermes at 27.9°N, 175.8°W, so neither is near. Midway is at 28.2°N, 177.4°W, also far away. At 165°W the nearest islands are French Frigate Shoals (23.7°N, 166°W) and Gardner Pinnacles (25°N, 168°W), both well to the south. So the point is open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is Pacific abyssal plain north of the Hawaiian Ridge, at about 4,500 to 5,500 m depth. The Hawaiian-Emperor chain is near, but the point is about 5° of latitude north of the chain at that longitude. I estimate a depth of about 5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

11725.text:
The point (-41, -129) lies in the South Pacific, far from land. It sits roughly north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and west of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor there is probably abyssal plain at about 3,500–4,000 m depth.

The East Pacific Rise is near 110°W at 40°S, so -129 is about 19° west of it. Seafloor age there is perhaps 25–30 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3,500 + 350·√age. For 28 Myr that is roughly 2,600 + 350·5.3 ≈ 4,450 m, though the real depth is usually a little shallower. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region around 40°S, 130°W is near the Foundation/Easter area, and depths there are around 3,800–4,000 m. I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14293.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -33 is in the Weddell Sea, off the Antarctic coast. Queen Maud Land lies at about 0° and the Weddell Sea at about -30°. At -33° longitude and -69° latitude, we are north of the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf, whose front is near -75° to -78°. The Antarctic Peninsula is at about -60° to -65° longitude, so this point is in the open Weddell Sea. Sea ice is ignored, so this is open water.

The depth there is probably abyssal, around 4000 m. The Weddell Abyssal Plain is about 4800 m deep at roughly -65° to -70° latitude and -30° longitude, while the Weddell Sea continental shelf is further south. At -69° the water is probably around 4500 m deep. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

5248.text:
The point 31°N, 123°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 800 km west of Southern California. That puts it west of the California Borderland, on the abyssal plain.

Typical depths there are about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Pioneer Fracture Zone is around 37°N, and the Murray Fracture Zone is near 32-35°N. The seafloor at 31°N, 123°W is probably about 4,200 m deep, so I'll estimate -4200 m. This is an estimate from regional depths, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

573.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -113.0° lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Canada. Ellesmere Island's northwest coast and Axel Heiberg Island are farther south and east. Meighen Island sits around 80°N, 99°W, and Borden Island at about 78.5°N, 111°W. Ellef Ringnes is at about 78.5°N, 102°W. At 83°N, 113°W the position is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, beyond the Sverdrup Islands and past the continental shelf edge.

The shelf off the Queen Elizabeth Islands is narrow. Its edge lies at about 81–82°N there, and the Canada Basin and Alpha Ridge lie farther north. At 83°N, 113°W the seabed is probably the continental slope or the abyssal plain, with a depth of around 1500–3500 m. Because this position is west of the Lincoln Sea and the Arctic Ocean there is deep, I'll estimate about 2000 m of depth, which gives an elevation of -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

15510.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -119 is in West Antarctica, in Marie Byrd Land near the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern side. The Ross Ice Shelf's grounding line in this sector lies roughly near -80 to -82 at longitudes of -150 to -160. At -119 longitude, -83 is inland, in the Ford Ranges/Hollick-Kenyon region or the Pine Island/Thwaites catchment, so I judge it grounded ice.

At 83°S, 119°W the ice surface is probably around 1000-1500 m. This is the area between the Executive Committee Range and the Whitmore Mountains, closer to the Siple Coast ice streams. The Whitmore Mountains are near 82.5°S, 104°W, and the Ross Ice Shelf edge near 120°W sits around 78°S, so this is deep inland on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet's interior ridge. I estimate the surface at about 1200 m, a figure drawn from rough recall of ice-sheet elevations, not a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

4311.text:
Latitude 43.0 N, longitude 163.0 E is in the northwest Pacific, east of the Kuril Islands and Kamchatka. The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs along roughly 45N, 150-155E, with the trench axis near 43N at about 146-147E. At 163E the point is on the abyssal plain of the Pacific, which is about 5500-6000 m deep. The Emperor Seamount chain lies farther east, and the Hokkaido Rise/Detroit seamount region is farther north. The Shatsky Rise is at roughly 33-35N, so it isn't relevant here.

South of the Kuril trench, near 43N 163E, I'd estimate the depth at about 5600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

7968.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude -83.0° lies in the Pacific Ocean, west of Ecuador and the Galápagos Islands (which sit at about -90 to -91 longitude). The point is east of the Galápagos, in the Panama Basin, and the seafloor there is roughly 2,500 to 3,000 m deep.

The Carnegie Ridge runs from the Galápagos toward Ecuador at about 1°S to 2°S, so it is south of this point. At 1°N, -83°, the point is in the Panama Basin north of the ridge, where depths are around 2,500 to 2,900 m. I estimate -2,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

3444.text:
The point 51.0°N, 131.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, west of Haida Gwaii and Vancouver Island. Haida Gwaii sits at about 132–133°W at 53°N. At 51°N, the continental shelf edge is near 129.5°W, so 131°W is beyond the shelf. This area is close to the Revere-Dellwood / Tuzo Wilson seamounts and the Explorer plate region. The Explorer Ridge is around 49.5°N, 130.3°W, and the Dellwood Knolls are near 50.0°N, 130.3°W. The Winona Basin is near 51°N, 129.5°W.

At 51°N, 131°W the seafloor is probably around 2,500 to 3,000 m deep. The Pacific plate abyssal plain in that area is at roughly 2,600 m, and I'll go with -2,700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

13233.text:
The point is at 57°S, 7°E, in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies southwest of Bouvet Island, which sits near 54.4°S, 3.4°E, and north of the Southwest Indian Ridge and the American-Antarctic Ridge region.

The Bouvet triple junction is at about 54.7°S, 1.8°E. The American-Antarctic Ridge runs west from there, roughly along 55–60°S. At 57°S, 7°E the seafloor is probably the Atlantic-Indian basin, south of the Spiess Ridge area. I expect depths of about 3000–4000 m, so I'll estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

9552.text:
Location: -17.0, -155.0 is in the South Pacific, in the French Polynesia region. Tahiti is at about -17.65, -149.4, and Tetiaroa is at about -17.0, -149.5. Longitude -155 is about 550 km west of Tahiti. The nearest atolls are Tupai and Maupiti (-16.45, -152.25), and Scilly (Manuia) is at -16.5, -154.7. Scilly Atoll lies at roughly -16.55, -154.73, so this point is about 55 km south of it, in open ocean.

Seafloor depth here is likely about 4000 to 4500 m. The Society Islands sit on a seafloor of about 4000 m, and the area west of them is the Pacific plate at about 4200 to 4500 m. The abyssal depth there is around 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

15176.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -67 is in West Antarctica, in the area of the Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne-Filchner Ice Shelf spans roughly 30°W to 80°W, and its front sits near 75-78°S. At 79°S, 67°W, I think this is the southwestern part of the Ronne Ice Shelf, near the grounding line toward the Ellsworth Mountains and the Fowler Ice Rise area. The Ellsworth Mountains lie around 79°S, 85°W, so 67°W is well east of them. The Antarctic Peninsula base is around 70°W at 75°S. Rutford Ice Stream feeds in near 79°S, 80°W, and Institute Ice Stream near 81°S, 70°W. So 79°S, 67°W is likely on the Ronne Ice Shelf, which is floating.

The seabed beneath it is probably about -500 to -800 m. In the Ronne's southern part, the cavity is a few hundred meters deep, and Bedmap values there are around -400 to -600 m. I'll go with -500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

8956.text:
The point (-9.0, 93.0) is in the eastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Sumatra and west of Christmas Island. It is far from any land, so the surface type is Water.

The elevation I need is the seabed depth. The area lies in the Wharton Basin, near the Ninetyeast Ridge, which sits around 88–90°E. At 93°E the seafloor is likely abyssal plain at about 5,000–5,500 m depth. The Wharton Basin floor near 9°S is commonly around 5,200 m, so I estimate -5,200 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

12422.text:
Location: 49°S, 175°W, in the South Pacific southeast of New Zealand, east of the Chatham Rise and south of it. The Chatham Islands are at about 44°S, 176.5°W. At 49°S 175°W the point lies on the Pacific abyssal plain or the southern flank of the Chatham Rise. The Chatham Rise sits at 42-45°S, so 49°S is south of it, in the Bounty Trough region, which has depths around 4000-5000 m. The Bounty Trough runs east along about 45-47°S, and further south lies the Campbell Plateau (about 50°S, 170°E, depth ~500-1000 m), whose eastern edge is near 175°W. At 49°S 175°W we are east of the Antipodes and Bounty Islands (179°E), past the plateau's edge, so the depth is probably about 4500 m.

I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

7863.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 67.0°E is in the Indian Ocean, in the Arabian Sea/equatorial region, southwest of the Maldives and the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge. The Carlsberg Ridge lies to the northwest, and the Central Indian Basin to the east. At 67°E the Carlsberg Ridge runs roughly from 60°E, 10°N down to about 70°E, 0°N. At 3°N the ridge axis is probably near 65-66°E, so 67°E is on the ridge flank, and depths there are around 3500-4000 m.

I estimate a seabed depth of about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

13868.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -163 is in the Southern Ocean, north of the Ross Sea region. The Antarctic coast near the Ross Sea is at about -78, so this point is open ocean. Nearby features include the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge to the north and the Southern Ocean abyssal plain.

At -65 latitude and -163 longitude, the seafloor depth is probably around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge sits near 60°S, 150°W, and the Balleny Islands are near 66°S, 163°E, which is far from this point. The area is south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, in the Amundsen Abyssal Plain or the Southeast Pacific basin. The depth there is about 4,800 m, so I'll estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

10011.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude 43.0 is in Madagascar's southwest, near the Mozambique Channel coast. Madagascar's west coast at -21° is around 43.9°E (Morombe is at -21.75, 43.37; Andavadoaka is at -22.0, 43.25). At -21.0, the coast lies near 44.0°E (Maintirano is at -18.05, 44.0; Belo sur Tsiribihina is at -19.7, 44.5). So the coast at -21° is roughly 44.2°E, and 43.0°E lies offshore in the Mozambique Channel.

The shelf there is fairly wide, but at 43.0 the point is about 130 km offshore. Depth is probably about 1000-2000 m. The Davie Ridge is near 42-43°E at about -17 to -20°, and further south, around -21°, the Davie Ridge fades. The Madagascar slope descends to roughly 2000 m. I'll estimate -1500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

14386.text:
The point is at 69°S, 153°E, in East Antarctica. That falls in Wilkes Land, in the sector between Dumont d'Urville (140°E) and Mertz Glacier (about 145°E). Moving east of Mertz toward 153°E, the coast is near 66-67°S, so 69°S is inland, roughly 300 km from the coast.

The ice sheet surface there should be around 2000-2500 m. The Wilkes Land interior at 69°S, 153°E is probably about 2300 m. This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid. I'm estimating the elevation from regional knowledge rather than a lookup, so I'll go with 2200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

13077.text:
The point is at 55°S, 55°E, in the southern Indian Ocean. It lies southwest of the Kerguelen Plateau and southeast of the Crozet Plateau. Crozet is at about 46°S, 51°E, so 55°S is well to the south of it. The area is probably part of the Crozet Basin or the Enderby Basin, and the Southwest Indian Ridge sits further west. The Princess Elizabeth Trough lies further south.

At 55°S, 55°E I'd expect abyssal depths of about 4,000 to 4,500 m. The Del Cano Rise is at roughly 45°S, 46°E, so it does not affect this point. The Indian-Antarctic Ridge is further east. I'll estimate about -4,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

1890.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 1.0°E lies in the Norwegian Sea, northwest of Norway. Norway's coast at 69°N is around 15-18°E, so this point is far out at sea.

The area is west of the Lofoten Basin. The Lofoten Basin's deepest parts lie around 3,200 m, near 70°N, 3°E. At 69°N, 1°E the seabed is probably about 3,000-3,200 m deep. The Mohn Ridge is further north and the Vøring Plateau is to the south at 67°N, so this point is likely within the basin. I'll estimate about -3,150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3150}

11713.text:
The point at -41, -153 is in the South Pacific, west of Chile and southeast of Tahiti. It lies on the Pacific plate in the southeast Pacific basin, north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge.

The East Pacific Rise lies farther east, around -120 longitude at this latitude. At -153, the seafloor is older, probably 50-70 Myr, and typically around 4,000-4,500 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge sits farther south, near -55 to -60 latitude. Seafloor in this area is likely about 4,300 m deep, though the Easter-Tubuai region has some seamounts. I'll estimate -4,350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4350}

9365.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -169 is in the South Pacific, northeast of Samoa. Samoa lies around -14, -172 to -169.5, so Tutuila (American Samoa) sits at about -14.3, -170.7. Point -15, -169 is east of Tutuila and south of Ofu/Ta'u (Manu'a Islands at about -14.2, -169.5). It is roughly 90 km south of Ta'u, so open ocean.

Depth in this area is probably around 4,500-5,000 m. The Samoan Passage is further west. Southeast of Manu'a the seafloor is around 4,500 m deep, so I'll estimate -4,600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

15535.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -69 is in Antarctica, in the area south of the Antarctic Peninsula and Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne-Filchner ice shelf spans roughly 75-83°S and 30-80°W. At -83, -69 we are near the southwestern edge of the Ronne Ice Shelf, close to the Orville Coast and Ellsworth Land and the Fletcher Promontory. The Ronne Ice Shelf's southern limit is around 83°S at 60-65°W, and at 69°W the grounding line is perhaps around 82-83°S. The Foundation Ice Stream is near -83, -59. Institute Ice Stream is around -81.5, -75. Rutford Ice Stream is around -79, -80. Evans Ice Stream is near -75.5, -75.

At -83, -69 the point is probably close to the Bailey/Support Force Glacier region, near the Pensacola Mountains at -83.5, -57. The Ellsworth Mountains run from -78 to -80 at -85. At -83, -69 the point is inland of the Ronne Ice Shelf, likely grounded ice from the Antarctic ice sheet in the Ellsworth Land interior, with an elevation of about 1000 m. The Ronne ice shelf's western boundary at this longitude sits near -80 to -82 at around -75 to -65. I'd guess the point is grounded, with an ice surface of approximately 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

40.text:
This location is at 89°N, 99°W, which is in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole. It lies on the Lomonosov Ridge side, on the Canadian side of the Arctic basin. The Lomonosov Ridge runs near the pole from the Greenland/Ellesmere side toward Siberia, roughly along 140°E and 40°W. At 99°W and 89°N, we are on the Amerasian side of the ridge, toward the Canada Basin/Alpha Ridge. The depth there is probably the Amundsen Basin or the Lomonosov Ridge flank.

The Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side, at about 4200 m depth. The Lomonosov Ridge crest sits at about 87-88°N near 60°W, with depths of about 1000-1500 m. The longitude 99°W at 89°N is about 20 km from the pole. The pole itself is in the Amundsen Basin at about 4300 m depth, close to the ridge. The ridge passes near the pole at about 90°E–90°W, so the point sits on the Makarov Basin side, where depths are about 4000 m. I estimate roughly -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7005.text:
The point 13°N, 151°E is in the western Pacific, east of the Mariana Islands and south of the Marshall Islands. It lies in the Pacific plate region near the Caroline Islands and the Mariana Trench.

The Mariana Trench is around 142-147°E, so 151°E is east of it. Seafloor here is likely in the East Mariana Basin or the Pigafetta Basin area. The East Mariana Basin lies roughly 10-15°N, 153-160°E, with depths around 5500 m. The Pigafetta Basin is about 15°N, 150°E, with depths around 5900-6000 m. Near 13°N, 151°E, the depth is probably about 5800 m.


{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

2012.text:
Latitude 67.0, longitude -115.0 is in Nunavut/Northwest Territories, Canada, south of Coronation Gulf and east of Great Bear Lake. Great Bear Lake sits at about 66°N, 120°W, so this point is well to its east. The area is near the Coppermine River and the Kugluktuk region. Kugluktuk is at 67.8°N, 115.1°W, so this point is about 80 km south of it, in the tundra with scattered lakes. Elevation there is probably around 400 m.

The Coppermine River runs from Lac de Gras northwest to Kugluktuk, so at 67°N, 115°W it may be near the river's upper reaches. Lakes such as Contwoyto Lake lie at around 65.5°N, 110.5°W. Terrain near 67°N, 115°W is Canadian Shield with an elevation of roughly 450 m, and it could be Hepburn Island area terrain, though I'm not certain. I'll classify it as Solid at 420 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":420}

6993.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude 127.0 lies in the Philippine Sea, east of Samar and Luzon (Bicol region). Samar's east coast is around 125.5°E, so 127°E is about 150 km offshore. The Philippine Trench runs along roughly 126–127°E at 13°N, and its axis lies further south, around 10°N at 126.5°E. At 13°N the trench is less well defined, and the seafloor to the east is about 5000–6000 m deep.

The Philippine Trench starts near 14°N, where it is perhaps 5000 m deep, and deepens southward. At 13°N, 127°E the point is probably on the trench's eastern side or the abyssal plain, so I estimate about -5800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

12136.text:
The point at 45°S, 27°W is in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Argentine Basin. The ridge axis near 45°S sits at roughly 16°W, with the South Sandwich arc far to the southeast. At 27°W the seafloor is likely the western flank of the ridge or the edge of the Argentine Basin, with depths of roughly 4000–5000 m. The North Scotia Ridge and Georgia Rise lie farther south, near 50°S. The Rio Grande Rise is at about 30°S, so it is not relevant here.

The ridge-flank age at 27°W is about 25–30 Myr. Normal depth for that age is around 2600 + 350·√age ≈ 4400 m. I'll estimate about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

9626.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -7 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, northwest of Namibia and well to the east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. St Helena sits at about -15.9, -5.7, so this point is roughly 150 km southwest of it.

St Helena is on a seamount chain on the African plate, and the seafloor around it is about 3500-4500 m deep. The nearby seafloor is around 4000 m, and at -17, -7 it is probably 3900-4300 m. The abyssal plain there is about 4000 m deep, so I'll estimate -4000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

12825.text:
Latitude -53, longitude -89 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile (Chile coast at about -74). It lies roughly 1,500 km offshore, south of the Chile Rise. The Chile Rise (spreading ridge) runs at about -46 to -48 near the triple junction, but at -53 the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is farther south and west.

The seafloor here is the Nazca/Antarctic plate region, around the Chile Rise's southern extension, with depths of about 3,500-4,000 m. The Pacific–Antarctic Ridge lies near -57 at about -150 longitude. The Chile Rise segments sit at about -55, -80 to -90, and the Antarctic–Nazca ridge segments run toward the Chile Transform fault systems, such as the Challenger fracture zone and Valdivia fracture zone near -43. Around -53, -89 there may be the Antarctic–Nazca plate boundary, a ridge at depth of about 2,500-3,000 m. The Chile Rise runs from the triple junction at -46, -76 southwestward, with the Esmeralda transform near -50 and the Taitao and Darwin fracture zones further on. The ridge axis reaches about -55, -90 and then continues to the Pacific–Antarctic Ridge near -60, -115. So -53, -89 is near the ridge axis, with a depth of about 2,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

8706.text:
Latitude -7, longitude -47 is in northern Brazil, near the border of Maranhão, Tocantins, and Piauí. It lies in the Tocantins/Maranhão region close to Balsas, which sits at about -7.5, -46.0. The Tocantins river is further west at about -48.

This area is a plateau at roughly 250-400 m, and I'll estimate about 300 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 300}

1594.text:
Latitude 73.0N, longitude 129.0E is in the Laptev Sea region, near the Lena Delta. The Lena Delta lies around 72-73.5N, 123-130E. At 129E, 73N, this is probably the eastern edge of the delta, near Buor-Khaya Gulf or the Laptev Sea coast. The Buor-Khaya Gulf is around 71.5N, 129E, and the delta's eastern part reaches roughly 129.5E at 72.7N. The Sagastyr/Stolb island area is at 73.4N, 126.8E. The Bykovsky Peninsula is at 71.7N, 129E. At 73N, 129E, the point is likely in the sea just north of the delta's eastern flank, with shallow water of about 10 m depth. The Laptev shelf there is shallow, around 10-20 m.

The delta's eastern edge has the Dmitry Laptev strait further east. The coast of the delta near 129E is around 72.8N, so 73.0N is just offshore, with a depth of about 8 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-8}

13571.text:
The point is at 61°S, 37°W, in the Scotia Sea, just north of the South Orkney Islands and east of the South Scotia Ridge. The South Orkneys sit around 60.5°S, 45°W, so this location is well east of them and not on land.

The area is in the Scotia Sea, which is mostly deep, about 3000–4000 m. At 61°S, 37°W, the South Scotia Ridge runs from the South Orkneys east toward the South Sandwich region. The ridge continues to Bruce Bank at roughly 33–35°W and 60°S. Bruce Bank, Discovery Bank and similar features lie around 60°S, 35°W. This point at 61°S, 37°W is south of that, probably in the Weddell Sea side of the Scotia Arc, with a depth of about 3000 m. I estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

8499.text:
The point (-5.0, -101.0) is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, about 5°S and 101°W. That places it just west of the East Pacific Rise, which runs near 110°W at this latitude, so it is on the eastern flank of the rise.

Seafloor depth in this area is roughly 3,000–3,800 m. The Nazca plate lies east of the rise, and the Galápagos region to the north is shallower. The seafloor at 101°W, 5°S is about 2,000 km from South America. Its age is about 15–20 Myr, which suggests a depth of roughly 3,500 m. The Pacific-Nazca spreading center sits near 5°S, 106°W, so 101°W is about 5° east of it. At a half-rate of about 70 mm/yr, 550 km of spreading corresponds to about 8 Myr, which gives a depth of 2,500 + 350√8 ≈ 3,500 m. I estimate about -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

15732.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -35 is in Antarctica, south of the Weddell Sea, in the area of the Foundation Ice Stream and the Support Force Glacier, near the Pensacola Mountains. The Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf lies at about -80 to -82 latitude, and the Pensacola Mountains are around longitude -55 to -60. At -85, -35 the surface is likely grounded ice on the East Antarctic ice sheet's edge, in the Recovery Glacier / Foundation Ice Stream region. The Recovery Glacier lies at about -81 to -85 latitude and -20 to -30 longitude, so -35 is near the Argentina Range and the Dufek area.

Surface elevation near 85°S, 35°W is probably about 1500 to 2000 m. The Foundation Ice Stream's upper reaches are around 1000 to 1500 m. The Academy Glacier is at about -83, -50. At 85°S, 35°W the ice-surface elevation is about 1200 m, so I'll estimate 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

8386.text:
Latitude -3.0, Longitude 33.0 is in Tanzania, south of Lake Victoria's southern shore. Mwanza is at about -2.52, 32.9. The southern part of the lake near Mwanza Gulf reaches roughly -2.7 at its southernmost, so -3.0 at 33.0 should be land, around Shinyanga/Geita region, at elevation of about 1150 m.

Lake Victoria's surface is at 1135 m. Land at -3.0, 33.0 is near Nyang'wale/Kahama, with elevation around 1150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1150}

1703.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -13.0 is in the Greenland Sea, northeast of Jan Mayen. Jan Mayen sits at about 71.0N, -8.5W, so this point is roughly 150 km west of it. The area lies between Jan Mayen and the Greenland shelf edge, and the Greenland continental shelf edge is around -17 to -18 longitude at this latitude.

The Jan Mayen Fracture Zone and the Greenland Basin are nearby. The Greenland Basin at 71N, -13W is probably about 3000 m deep. The Mohns Ridge begins northeast of Jan Mayen. The Jan Mayen Plateau lies to the south, and the Greenland slope descends from the shelf at about -17W. At -13W, the depth is probably around 2500-3000 m, so I'll estimate -2800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

14906.text:
Location: -75, 113E is in East Antarctica, inland of the Budd Coast/Sabrina Coast, in Wilkes Land. The coast near 113E lies around -66.5, so -75 is about 950 km inland. This is near the Vostok Subglacial Highlands/Aurora basin area. Ice surface elevation around 2,800 m is likely.

Aurora Subglacial Basin is around 115E, -75 to -70. Surface elevation there is about 2,700-3,000 m. Vostok station at 106.8E, -78.5 has a surface elevation of 3,488 m. At -75, 113E the surface is about 3,000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

14340.text:
