12241.text:
The point is at 47°S, 177°W, in the South Pacific southeast of New Zealand. The Chatham Islands sit near 44°S, 176.5°W, so this point is about 3° south of them. That area is the Chatham Rise's southern flank, which drops toward the Bounty Trough.

The Chatham Rise crest lies around 42-44°S at depths of 300-500 m. At 47°S the seafloor is in the Bounty Trough or on the abyssal plain to its south, so depth is probably around 4,000-4,800 m. The Bounty Trough runs east along roughly 45-46°S and reaches about 4,500 m. Farther south the Campbell Plateau is at about 500-1,000 m, but it lies west of 175°W, around 170°E to 180°. At 177°W and 47°S the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain south of the Bounty Trough, near 4,500 m, or possibly the Bounty Platform. I'll estimate about -4,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

8118.text:
The point (-1°, -143°) is in the central equatorial Pacific, south of the Line Islands and northeast of the Marquesas. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

Depth in this region is typically about 4,000 to 5,000 m. The Marquesas Fracture Zone lies to the south, and the seafloor around 0°, -143° sits on the Pacific plate at roughly 4,400 to 4,600 m. I am estimating from general knowledge of this area rather than from a specific bathymetry lookup, so I chose -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

60.text:
Latitude 89°N is in the Arctic Ocean, near the North Pole. At 89°N, 59°W the water is deep. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near the pole, running roughly from Greenland/Ellesmere toward the Siberian shelf. Near 90°N the ridge is along about 140°E–40°W, with the pole region at roughly -4200 m on the Amundsen Basin side and ridge crest around -1000 to -1500 m.

The ridge crest passes close to the pole, roughly along the 60°W meridian on the Greenland side. At 89°N the ridge sits at about 80°W to 100°E longitudes, so 59°W could be on the ridge or just off it on the Amundsen side. Bathymetry: the Lomonosov Ridge at the pole has depths of about 1000–1700 m. The Amundsen Basin side at the pole is about 4200 m, while the Makarov Basin side is about 4000 m. Since the ridge is only about 50–60 km wide and 1° of latitude is 111 km, longitude 59°W at 89°N is about 2 km from the pole line in the perpendicular direction... Distance from the pole is 111 km. The ridge axis near 88–89°N is approximately along 50–60°W by some accounts, so this point is likely on or near the ridge, and I'll estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

11992.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 45 lies in the southwestern Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa and southwest of the Crozet Islands. It is south of the Southwest Indian Ridge's northern section. The ridge runs roughly from Bouvet toward the Prince Edward Islands (around 46°S, 37°E), then continues northeast to the Indian Ocean triple junction.

At 45°E the Southwest Indian Ridge sits at about 40–42°S, near the Du Toit and Andrew Bain fracture zones. At 43°S, 45°E, the point is probably on the Crozet Plateau's western side or in the basin south of the ridge. The Crozet Plateau is at about 46°S, 51°E, so this point is west of it. The Southwest Indian Ridge between 35°E and 50°E lies at roughly 39–50°S, with the Andrew Bain Fracture Zone near 30–35°E at 50°S. Around 45°E the ridge is near 41–42°S, so the point is likely near the ridge flank, with depths of about 3000–4000 m. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

1011.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude 43.0 E is in the Barents Sea / Kara Sea region, east of Franz Josef Land and north of Novaya Zemlya. Novaya Zemlya's northern tip is at about 77°N, 68°E. Franz Josef Land spans roughly 80-81°N, 45-65°E. Its westernmost islands, such as Zemlya Georga and Alexandra Land, lie near 47-50°E. Point 79N 43E is west of these, in open sea in the northern Barents Sea. Depths there are about 200-300 m. Kvitøya is at 80°N, 32°E, and the area around 79N 43E is likely the trough between Svalbard and Franz Josef Land, with depths of around 250 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

14330.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 41 is in East Antarctica, in Enderby Land near the coast. The Antarctic coast near 41°E sits around -66.5 to -67.5 latitude, so -69 is inland on the ice sheet, roughly 150-200 km from the coast. Ice surface elevation there is probably around 1500-2000 m.

Looking at Enderby Land more closely: at 41°E the coast is near Cape Borley (about 52°E) and Kemp Land (about 55°E), and the coast near 41°E is around -68°. That puts -69 about 100 km inland, where the ice surface should be around 1500 m. I'll estimate 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

12407.text:
Latitude -47, longitude 155 is in the Pacific Ocean southeast of Tasmania and south of Australia, east of the South Tasman Rise and southwest of Macquarie Island. Macquarie Island is at about 54.5°S, 159°E, so this point is well north of it.

The Tasman Sea basin and the Emerald Basin lie in this region. The Macquarie Ridge runs from about 159°E at 54°S up to roughly 160°E at 49°S, then toward New Zealand's Fiordland, so 155°E is west of the ridge. The seabed there is likely abyssal plain at around 4,500 to 4,800 m depth. The Tasman abyssal plain at 47°S, 155°E is probably about -4,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

10909.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 39 is in the Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa. The coast near Durban is at about 31°E, so this point is roughly 800 km offshore. The area lies in the Natal Valley / Mozambique Basin region, or possibly on the edge of the Mozambique Ridge.

The Mozambique Ridge sits at about 33-35°E, and the Mozambique Basin lies to its east. The Natal Valley is between the ridge and the African coast. At 39°E, 31°S the point is east of the Mozambique Ridge. This is likely the southern Mozambique Basin or the area of the Transkei Basin, where depths are roughly 5000 m. I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

15495.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -149 is in Antarctica, in the Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°E to 150°W, south to about 85°S. At -149° longitude (149°W) and 83°S, this is near the eastern edge of the Ross Ice Shelf, close to the Marie Byrd Land coast and the Shirase/Siple coast area. The eastern boundary of the shelf is around 155–150°W at the Edward VII Peninsula, but at 83°S the shelf extends further east, to roughly 140°W, near the Transantarctic Mountains and Reedy Glacier. At 83°S, 149°W the ice is likely floating shelf, so the type is Water and I need the seabed elevation.

Bedrock beneath the Ross Ice Shelf is typically about -500 to -600 m. Near the grounding line in the eastern part it is around -600 m, and in the Bedmap2 data the area near 83°S, 149°W is about -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

5789.text:
Latitude 25°N, longitude 121°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, far west of Baja California. Baja's west coast near 25°N is at about 112°W, so this point is roughly 1,000 km offshore. It is in the open Pacific, west of the Baja California Peninsula and southwest of the California Borderland.

The seafloor there is abyssal plain, around 3,900 to 4,000 m deep. The East Pacific seafloor at 121°W, 25°N is on crust about 25 to 30 million years old, which would put the depth near 4,000 m. Nearby features include the Guadalupe Island area at 118°W, 29°N, but this point is not close to it. I estimate about -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

8424.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 109.0 is off the west coast of Borneo (Kalimantan), south of Pontianak, which sits at about 0.0°N, 109.3°E. The southwest Kalimantan coast near Ketapang is around -1.8°S, 110°E. At -3.0°S, 109.0°E the coast lies roughly at 110.0°E, so the point is in the Karimata Strait, about 100 km offshore. The Karimata Strait is shallow, with depths around 30-40 m in this area.

The Java Sea and Karimata Strait floor in this region is about 30-40 m deep, so I estimate -35 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-35}

2293.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude 87.0°E lies in central Siberia, in Krasnoyarsk Krai. It is in the West Siberian Plain's eastern edge, near the Yenisei, which is at about 86°E at this latitude. The area is probably near the Turukhansk region or the Yeloguy/Kureika area. The Yenisei runs around 87.5°E at 65.5°N (Turukhansk is at 65.8°N, 87.9°E). Since 87.0°E is slightly west of the river, the point is probably on the lowland of the Yenisei's west bank, with an elevation around 50 m.

Taking the terrain there as roughly 60 m, I'll estimate the surface as solid land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

12094.text:
The point -45, -111 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile, so the surface type is Water and the answer is the seabed depth.

This location lies about 2,000 km west of the Chilean coast. It is near the southern East Pacific Rise flank, but the rise itself is farther west, around -110 longitude at about -55 latitude and farther north. At 45°S the Chile Rise and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge are not at this longitude. The seafloor here is probably abyssal plain of moderate age, roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about -55, -140 up to the Chile Rise triple junction near -46, -76. At -45, -111 the crust is in the Southeast Pacific Basin. That basin is typically 3,500 to 4,000 m deep. I estimate -3,900 m, which is a judgment from regional depth patterns rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8851.text:
The point (-9.0, -117.0) is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, southwest of the Galápagos and west of Peru. It lies on the Pacific plate, east of the East Pacific Rise (about 113°W at this latitude) and north of the Austral Fracture Zone region. Seafloor depth in this area is typically around 3,500–4,000 m.

At 9°S the East Pacific Rise crest sits near 112–113°W, and the rise flank at 117°W is about 4–5 degrees west of the crest. Young crust there is roughly 5–8 million years old, giving a depth of about 3,000–3,300 m. The rise at that latitude is fast spreading, with the crest at about 2,700 m, and the flank deepens gradually. At 117°W the depth is probably about 3,700 m. I'll estimate -3,700 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3700}

12774.text:
The point (-51.0, 169.0) is in the Southern Ocean, south-southeast of New Zealand's South Island. It lies about 300 km southeast of Campbell Island, which sits near -52.5, 169.1, and west of the Campbell Plateau's edge.

The Campbell Plateau is mostly 500–1000 m deep. Its northern part, around -51, 169, is probably about 500–700 m deep. Campbell Island is at the plateau's northeast end, and the plateau extends to roughly -50 and 166–170. The nearby Bounty Trough lies further north, around -46 to -45. Taking these together, the seabed here is probably around 600–700 m below sea level. I'm estimating from the regional bathymetry rather than a precise lookup, so I'll use -650 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -650}

2282.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude 65.0°E is in western Siberia, in the Khanty-Mansi/Yamalo-Nenets area. It lies near the Northern Sosva or Ob basin, east of the Urals. The Urals are at about 60°E, so 65°E is in the lowland plain. Elevation there is roughly 50 m.

Checking the geography: at 65N 65E the point sits in the Berezovo district, close to the Sosva and Severnaya Sosva rivers. Berezovo is at 63.9N 65.0E and has an elevation of about 30 m. Moving north to 65N, the terrain is the Northern Sosva upland, perhaps 50-100 m. I estimate about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

744.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -131.0° lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Canada's Ellef Ringnes and Meighen islands. Those islands reach about 80.5°N at most, and Borden Island is near 78.5°N, so this point is off the coast. Axel Heiberg's northwest tip is near 81°N but at about -97° to -90°, which is much farther east. Prince Patrick Island is near 77°N. So the point is ocean.

It sits north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, in the Beaufort Sea area near the Canada Basin margin. The Arctic continental shelf there is narrow, and the slope drops to the abyssal plain. At 81°N, 131°W the water is probably deep, about 3500 m, since the Canada Basin abyssal plain is around 3800 m. The Alpha Ridge is farther north, near 85°N. I estimate the seabed at roughly -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9412.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -75 is in the Pacific Ocean off Peru. The Peruvian coast near Nazca/Pisco runs around -15, -75.2 (Nazca is at -14.8, -74.9 but inland). The coast at about -15.3 is near Lomas, at longitude -74.8. So -15, -75 is probably just offshore, since the coast near San Juan de Marcona is at -15.35, -75.15 and Pisco is at -13.7, -76.2. The coastline between runs southeast, and at lat -15 the coast is around lon -75.3. Nazca is at -14.83, -74.94, which is inland, about 40 km from the coast. So -15, -75 is likely land, roughly 30 km inland of the coast near Marcona.

The coast at lat -15 is near Punta San Juan (-15.37, -75.17). At lat -15.0 the coast sits at around -75.5 (near Río Grande/Santa Cruz?). So the point is inland, in the coastal desert near Nazca on the Pampa. Elevation there is roughly 500 m, since Nazca is at 520 m and the area to the southwest, toward the coast, is lower. At -15, -75 the terrain is the Pampa San Jose, at maybe 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":550}

10839.text:
Latitude -31, longitude -101 is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. It lies roughly 2,000 km west of the Chilean coast, which is at about -71.5°. The Chile Trench is near -72°, so this point is on the open Nazca Plate.

Just south of the Easter Island region, the East Pacific Rise is at about -112° at this latitude, so this point is east of the rise. The seafloor age there is about 30-35 Myr, and depth is typically around 3,800-4,000 m. The Juan Fernandez and Easter chains lie further north, and the Nazca Ridge is at about -20° to -26°, so none of these affects this point. At -31 and -101 the area is likely abyssal plain at about -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

7858.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 57.0°E is in the western Indian Ocean, east of Somalia and roughly north of the Seychelles. The Seychelles lie around 4–5°S, 55°E, so this point is north of them. It is in the Somali Basin or near the Carlsberg Ridge region, where the seabed is about 4,500–5,000 m deep.

The Somali Basin floor is around 5,000 m deep, and the area near 3°N, 57°E is probably abyssal plain at roughly 4,800 m. I'm estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

1473.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -113.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Victoria Island's northern part reaches about 73°N, and Prince Albert Sound/Peel Sound lie nearby. Victoria Island's north coast sits around 73.0–73.4°N near longitude -113 to -115 (Cape Wollaston is farther south). The northwest of Victoria Island is at about 73°N, -118 to -115, near Prince of Wales Strait. At -113, the northern coast of Victoria Island is near Hadley Bay, which is at about 72.5°N, -108. Hadley Bay is at 72.5°N, -108 to -110, and Wynniatt Bay is around 72.8°N, -112. Wynniatt Bay lies between Collinson Peninsula and the Storkerson Peninsula. So 73°N, -113 is probably on land, on the Storkerson Peninsula or the north coast of Victoria Island, with a low elevation of around 100 m.

Victoria Island's northern coast near -113 is around 73.0–73.5°N, since Prince Albert Peninsula is at -115 and 72.5°N. The Viscount Melville Sound is to the north. I'll guess land at about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

14913.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 127 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. It lies inland of the coast, which sits near -66 to -67. This is near the Aurora Subglacial Basin region, and Vostok is at 106.8E, -78.5. The point should be on grounded ice, with a surface elevation of about 2,500 m.

Dome C is at 123E, -75, with a surface elevation of 3,233 m. At 127E, -75, the ice is only slightly off Dome C and close to the same height. Ice surface elevation there is probably about 3,100 m, so I'll estimate 3,150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3150}

557.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude -145.0°W lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Beaufort Sea. It sits near the Canada Basin and the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge region. The Alpha Ridge is roughly at 85°N, 150°W, so 83°N, 145°W is probably on the Canada Basin side or the southern flank of the Alpha Ridge.

The Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. The Chukchi Borderland lies near 78°N, 165°W. At 83°N, 145°W the depth is likely about 3600 m, on the margin of the Canada Basin and the Alpha Ridge. IBCAO shows roughly 3800 m in the basin at 82°N, 140°W, and the Alpha Ridge rises to about 1500 m to 2500 m further north. I'll estimate −3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

15884.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -91 is in Antarctica, on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet side of the Transantarctic Mountains, about 3 degrees from the South Pole. Longitude -91 (90°W) lies along the direction toward the Ellsworth Mountains and Thiel Mountains region. At 87°S and 90°W, the ice surface is on the polar plateau at roughly 2,500–2,800 m. The Thiel Mountains sit near 85°S, 90°W, and the Pole is at 2,835 m. Moving toward 90°W at 87°S, the surface is near the head of the Foundation or Academy ice streams, probably around 2,400 m.

I estimate about 2,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

882.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 145.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands, which are around 75-76°N. This is the East Siberian/Laptev Sea shelf edge. The shelf break is around 78-80°N at about 140°E, so at 81°N the position is likely over the continental slope or the Amundsen Basin and Lomonosov Ridge region.

The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near 140-150°E at 81-82°N. Near the Laptev Sea margin at 81°N, 145°E, depths are probably around 2000-3000 m. The Amundsen Basin lies on the Eurasian side of the ridge, and the ridge itself at about 88°N is 1000-1500 m deep. This location is on the Laptev margin slope, so I estimate a depth of about -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

13333.text:
The point at -59°, -153° is in the South Pacific Ocean, on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region or nearby. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from about (-65, -175) up through (-56, -140) toward the East Pacific Rise. At -59 latitude the ridge is near longitude -150 or so, so the point could sit on or close to the ridge crest, where depths are about 2500-3000 m. Away from the ridge the seafloor is about 3500-4000 m deep.

The ridge axis near -59 is around -151 to -152 longitude, so the point is probably near the axis. I estimate a depth of about 2900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

15327.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -125 lies in the Ross Sea sector of Antarctica, in the eastern Ross Ice Shelf area. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 155°W to 160°E, with its eastern edge near Edward VII Peninsula at about 155°W. At -125 longitude and -81 latitude, that is Marie Byrd Land. The coast there is near -75 to -77 latitude, so -81 at -125 would be inland, on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, possibly in the Ford Ranges or Executive Committee Range area. The Executive Committee Range is near 76.8°S, 126°W. At 81°S, 125°W, we are in the interior of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, between the Ross Ice Shelf catchments (the Kamb/Whillans ice streams are at about 83°S, 150°W). The surface elevation there is probably around 1000 m, and the bed is near sea level or a bit below.

Is it grounded? The Ross Ice Shelf's grounding line toward Marie Byrd Land runs along the Siple Coast and Shirase Coast, and the shelf's eastern side near 80°S at 150°W–160°W. At 125°W, 81°S, I think this is the ice divide area of Marie Byrd Land with surface elevation around 1200 m. I'll estimate the ice surface at 1000 m, grounded.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

8600.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 101.0 lies in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Sumatra. The coast near Bengkulu is at about 3.8°S, 102.3°E. Sumatra's southwest coast runs northwest to southeast, so at 5°S the coast is near 104°E, and at 101°E we are well offshore.

The Sumatra forearc basin sits west of the island, and the Mentawai islands (Pagai) lie around 100.3°E at 2.8°S. At 5°S, 101°E, we are about 400 km from the trench axis relative to the coast. The trench is near 98–99°E at 5°S, with depths of about 5,500 m. The point is east of the trench, on the forearc slope or in the Bengkulu basin area. The Mentawai islands end near 3°S, and south of that the forearc basin lies at about 1,000–2,000 m depth. At 5°S, 101°E the outer-arc high is absent, and the slope descends to the trench. The distance to the coast is about 300 km, so the depth is probably about 2,000 m. I estimate roughly -2,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

7931.text:
The point is 1.0°N, 157.0°W, in the central Pacific. That is near Kiritimati (Christmas Island), which sits around 1.87°N, 157.4°W, so the point is south of the island and in open ocean. Kiritimati's southern tip is about 1.7°N, so 1.0°N is clearly water.

For depth, the seafloor in this area is roughly 4,000–5,000 m below sea level. The Line Islands ridge runs nearby, but at 157°W the floor is probably in the deep basin. I estimate about -4,900 m, though I can't pin it down more precisely from memory.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

11452.text:
Latitude -37, longitude 45 lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, south of Madagascar and southeast of South Africa. It is roughly on the Southwest Indian Ridge's northern flank, and the Del Cano Rise is near 45°E at about 45°S, which is farther south. At -37, 45E the area is the Madagascar Basin or Crozet Basin margin, and the Southwest Indian Ridge sits at roughly 40°S near 45°E.

The ridge crosses about 37°S near 50°E, where the Marion and Prince Edward islands lie at about 46.9°S, 37.8°E. Around 45°E the ridge axis is near 40°S. At 37°S, 45°E we are probably on the plateau or abyssal plain north of the ridge, with a depth of around 4500 m. Some depths there are about 5000 m, since the Madagascar Basin is deeper, but near the ridge flank 4000 m is plausible. I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

12074.text:
The point is at 45°S, 151°W, in the South Pacific Ocean. That is southeast of New Zealand and south of French Polynesia, on the Pacific plate. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies farther south at roughly 55-65°S, so this location is likely abyssal plain or a gentle rise. The seafloor age there is moderate, and the depth is probably around 4,000-4,500 m.

At 45°S, 151°W, I'd estimate about -4,300 m, although this is a guess from the regional setting, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

204.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -131° lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands across the pole toward Ellesmere Island and Greenland. At the pole its longitude is roughly 140°E to 40°W, so the ridge axis near 87°N sits at roughly 50°W to 140°E, and -131° (131°W) is on the Amerasian side.

The Amerasian Basin lies on the side of the Lomonosov Ridge toward Canada and Alaska. At 87°N, 131°W the position is about 3 degrees from the pole, in the Canada Basin/Makarov Basin area. The Makarov Basin is the one adjacent to the ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge at the pole is on the 140°E to 40°W line, so 131°W is about 90° from the ridge axis. At a distance of 3° (about 330 km) from the pole, that puts the point in the Makarov Basin or toward the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge. The Makarov Basin depth is about 4000 m, and the Alpha Ridge is about 1500–2500 m deep. The Alpha Ridge reaches the area near 85°N, 130°W. At 87°N, 131°W, the point is probably in the Makarov Basin's edge or on the Alpha Ridge flank. I'll estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

10314.text:
Latitude -25, longitude -71 is in the Pacific Ocean off northern Chile. The coast near Taltal (about -25.4, -70.5) lies at roughly -70.5, so -71.0 is about 50 km offshore at latitude -25.

The Peru-Chile Trench is near 71.5-72°W at this latitude, and the continental shelf is narrow here. At 71.0°W the seafloor is probably on the continental slope, at about 2000-3000 m depth. The trench axis at around -25° is near 71.5°W and about 6000 m deep. The slope at 0.5° (about 50 km) offshore is likely about 2500 m deep, so I estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

1939.text:
Latitude 69.0°N, longitude 99.0°E lies in the Taymyr Peninsula / Putorana Plateau region of Siberia. The Putorana Plateau is further south and west (around 69°N, 94–95°E). At 99°E and 69°N the area is the northern edge of the Central Siberian Plateau, near the Khatanga lowlands. Lake Taymyr lies at about 74°N, so it is not nearby.

The Khatanga River basin sits at roughly 72°N, so 69°N is south of it. This puts the point in the Anabar–Khatanga uplands or the Syverma plateau, which is hilly with elevations of about 300–600 m. Ayan and Lake Essey are around 68°N, 102°E. Lake Keta and Lake Khantayskoye are near 68–69°N, 90°E, further west. The point is probably in the upland near the Kotuy River, whose valley at 70°N, 103°E is about 100 m. At 99°E, 69°N I estimate about 400 m on the plateau.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

13925.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -49 is in the Weddell Sea, off the east coast of the Antarctic Peninsula. The Larsen ice shelves sit near 65°S at about 60°W, so -49° is well east of them, in open Weddell Sea. The seabed there is likely abyssal or continental slope, at roughly -4000 m.

The Weddell Sea at 65°S, 49°W is northeast of the Weddell Gyre center. The South Scotia Ridge is further north, near 60°S. The Weddell Sea abyssal plain is around -4800 m, and at 65°S, 49°W the depth is probably about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

8911.text:
Latitude -9, longitude 3 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, in the Gulf of Guinea region far south-west of Africa. The Angolan coast lies near 13°E at this latitude, so the point is well offshore. The nearest island, St Helena, is at about 15.9°W, -16°S, which is far away.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is near 13°W at this latitude, so 3°E lies east of the ridge in the Angola Basin. Abyssal depths there are about 5000 m. Near 9°S, 3°E the depth is probably around -5000 m, perhaps slightly shallower on the Guinea Basin's western side. I'll estimate -4950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

9005.text:
The point -11.0, -169.0 lies in the South Pacific, north of Samoa and east of Tokelau. Tokelau's atolls sit near -9 S, -171 W (Atafu), -9.4 S, -171.2 W (Nukunonu) and -9.9 S, -171.8 W (Fakaofo). Swains Island is at -11.05 S, -171.08 W, which is about 2 degrees west of this point, so it is not at the location. The point is therefore open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is the Central Pacific basin, near the Samoan Passage region, at roughly 5000 to 5500 m depth. At -11 S, -169 W the depth is probably about 5200 m, though I am estimating this from regional knowledge rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

7165.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 111.0°E lies in the South China Sea. It is east of Vietnam and southwest of the Spratly Islands, near the Vietnamese coast of Cam Ranh, which is at about 109°E. The Paracel Islands are at about 16.5°N, 112°E, so they are not close. The point sits on the western side of the deep basin, where the Vietnamese continental slope descends. Depths at 11°N, 111°E are roughly 1,800 to 2,500 m, and I picked about 1,900 m from memory as a rough estimate rather than from a lookup.

The deep basin (about 4,000 m) lies near 14°N, 116°E. Near 11°N, 111°E the seabed is probably the lower slope or the Nam Con Son area, with depths of roughly 1,500 to 2,000 m. I estimate about -1,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

6977.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude 95.0 lies in the Andaman Sea, west of Myanmar's Tanintharyi coast and east of the Andaman Islands. The Andaman Islands are at about 92.5–93.5°E, and Narcondam is at 13.4°N, 94.3°E, so this point is east of those islands.

The area east of the Andaman Islands is the Andaman Sea basin, where the spreading ridge is at about 10°N, 94-96°E. At 13°N, 95°E, depths are probably around 2,500 to 3,000 m. This is the Andaman back-arc basin. Narcondam sits on the volcanic arc, and the basin to its east is about 2,000 to 3,000 m deep. At 95°E, I estimate about -2,800 m, but the Martaban shelf lies further east. I'll go with -2,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

4006.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude -87.0 is in Green Bay / Lake Michigan area. Menominee, Michigan is at about 45.1, -87.6. Green Bay's eastern shore, the Door Peninsula, is around -87.0 at about 45.0 to 45.3. At longitude -87.0, latitude 45.0, that is near Chambers Island (45.2, -87.4) and the Door Peninsula's west shore at about -87.3 near Egg Harbor (45.04, -87.28). So -87.0 is east of the Door Peninsula tip at that latitude, in Lake Michigan proper. The peninsula's width at 45.0 spans from about -87.3 to -87.0 (the east shore at Jacksonport is at about -87.18; Lake Michigan shoreline near Baileys Harbor at -87.12). So -87.0 is in the lake, about 10 km offshore.

Lake Michigan surface is 176 m. Depth offshore of Door County east side at 10 km is about 50 m, so the lakebed is around 125 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":125}

1925.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, Longitude 71.0 E places this point in northern Siberia, in the Yamal/Gydan region. The Gulf of Ob lies at about 72-73 E and 69-70 N, and the Taz Gulf is further south. At 71 E and 69 N, the point is on the western side of the Gulf of Ob, which extends from about 66 to 74 N. The Yamal Peninsula lies between the Baydaratskaya Bay at about 68 E and the Ob Gulf at about 73 E. At 69 N, the Yamal Peninsula spans roughly 66.5 E to 72.5 E. So 71 E is probably still on the peninsula, near its eastern side, which would make it land. Terrain there is low, around 20-50 m, and the eastern coast along the Ob Gulf is close by. Lakes are common but the point is most likely land.

I'll estimate an elevation of 30 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":30}

1564.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude 69.0 (east) is in Russia's Kara Sea region. At 69°E, 73°N the point lies north of the Yamal Peninsula, which ends around 73°N at roughly 70°E. The western coast of the Gydan/Yamal area is nearby, with the Kara Sea to the north and the Baydaratskaya Bay to the south.

Yamal's northern tip is near 73.0°N, 69–70°E. Its coast at the north runs about 73°N, and Cape Skuratova is around 72.9°N, 68.5°E. The Kara Sea western shore of Yamal is at about 66–68°E at 71–72°N, so at 73°N, 69°E we are probably just off the northern tip of Yamal, in shallow water. The Kara Sea there is shallow, around 20 m deep. Bely Island lies at 73.2°N, 70°E, so 69°E, 73°N is likely water just southwest of it, with a depth of about 15 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-15}

14938.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 177 lies in the western Ross Sea, which is covered by the Ross Ice Shelf in the east. The ice shelf front sits near 78°S, and 75°S at 177°E is open Ross Sea, close to the Victoria Land coast. The Ross Sea's western part there is near Cape Adare to Ross Island, with 177°E at 75°S roughly in the central-western Ross Sea. The coast at Terra Nova Bay is around 164°E, so 177°E is well offshore.

The seabed there is around 500 m deep. The Ross Sea banks are shallower, such as Pennell Bank near 72°S and Ross Bank, while the troughs are deeper (Drygalski Trough about 1000 m). The Joides Basin and Glomar Challenger Basin lie further east, at about 175-180°W and 77°S. At 75°S, 177°E the depth is probably 500-600 m, so I'll estimate -550 m. Sea ice is ignored.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

4781.text:
The point 37.0°N, 23.0°E lies in the Aegean/Mediterranean region, south of the Peloponnese. The Argolic Gulf is around 37.4°N, 22.8°E, and Kythira is at 36.25°N, 23.0°E. The Peloponnese's eastern coast near Monemvasia is about 36.65°N, 23.0°E, and Cape Malea is at 36.43°N, 23.2°E. At 37.0°N, 23.0°E, the point is east of the Laconian coast, so it sits in the Myrtoan Sea, south of Hydra and Spetses (37.3°N, 23.2°E).

The Peloponnese's eastern coast near Leonidio is at 37.15°N, 22.87°E, and Kyparissi is at 37.2°N, 22.9°E. At 37.0°N the coast sits near 22.95°E, close to Cape Malea's northern side. The Laconian Gulf lies at about 36.6°N, so this point is on the open sea or very near the coast. The Peloponnese's east coast near Tyros is at 37.25°N and 22.87°E, and Kosmas is at 37.08°N, 22.65°E. Near 37.0°N the coast probably lies around 22.9°E (Poulithra at 37.1°N, 22.9°E), so 23.0°E is just offshore in the sea. Depth there is probably a few hundred meters, and I estimate about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

15292.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 165 is in Antarctica, in the Ross Sea sector. The Ross Ice Shelf front is near -78 at about 165E, and the McMurdo Sound area lies around -77.8, 166.6. At -79, 165E, the point probably sits on the western Ross Ice Shelf, south of McMurdo and north of the Transantarctic Mountains' Hillary Coast. The Hillary Coast near 165E is at about -79 to -80, with the coastline running roughly from -78 at 164E to -79.5 at 160E. The Koettlitz Glacier region is around -78.5, 163E. Since the shelf edge there is around 163-165E, the point is likely on the ice shelf, which counts as Water.

For the seabed, the Ross Ice Shelf cavity in the western part under the Ross Ice Shelf near McMurdo is about 500-800 m deep. Bedmap2 gives the bed there at roughly -600 m, and the nearby Ross Island area is around -700 m. I'll estimate -650 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-650}

14972.text:
Location: -77.0, -115.0 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector, inland of the Getz/Dotson area. Longitude -115 at latitude -77 is near the Pine Island Glacier catchment's western side, or the Hudson Mountains region, which lies at about -74.5, -99. At -77, -115 it is the interior ice sheet of Marie Byrd Land, near the Kohler Range/Bakutis Coast; the Land is grounded ice with a surface elevation of about 1000-1500 m. Using this reasoning, the ice surface there is probably around 1200 m.

Ice shelves like Getz are further north, around -74 to -75 at longitudes -115 to -135, so -77 is inland. The Thwaites catchment spans about -78 at -110. Thwaites surface elevations at -77, -115 are around 1000 m or more. I'll estimate 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

12188.text:
The point is at 45°S, 77°E in the southern Indian Ocean. It lies southwest of Amsterdam Island (37.8°S, 77.5°E) and north of the Kerguelen Plateau, which is at roughly 50°S and 70°E. The Southeast Indian Ridge passes through this area, near 77°E at about 40–48°S.

The Amsterdam–St Paul plateau sits near 38°S. At 45°S, 77°E the seafloor is probably the flank of the ridge or the adjacent basin. Typical depths here are about 3,000–4,000 m. The ridge axis near 47°S is around 2,500–3,000 m deep, and the area between Amsterdam and Kerguelen is roughly 3,500 m deep. I'll estimate about -3,400 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3400}

7198.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude 177.0 E is in the central Pacific. The Marshall Islands lie roughly between 160 and 172 E, so this point is east of them. Nearby is the area north of Tuvalu and Kiribati and west of the Gilberts, in the open Pacific basin. The seabed there is likely about 5,000 m deep, probably near 5,300 m.

The point is probably over abyssal plain in the Pacific basin, with some seamounts in the area. I'll estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

7311.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 43.0°E lies in the Horn of Africa. It is in the Somaliland/Ethiopia border region, near Hargeisa (9.56°N, 44.06°E) and to the west of it. Borama is at about 9.94°N, 43.18°E. Further south, at 9.0°N 43.0°E, the area is the Ethiopian Somali region near Jijiga's western side, with the Gadabuursi highlands to the north. Terrain here is plateau, roughly 1,400–1,600 m. Hargeisa sits at about 1,300 m, and Jijiga at 9.35°N, 42.8°E is about 1,600 m. At 9.0°N, 43.0°E the terrain is probably lower, around 1,300 m, toward the Haud plateau, which is about 1,000–1,200 m.

I estimate 1,250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1250}

15833.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 167 is in Antarctica, in the Transantarctic Mountains region near the Ross Ice Shelf edge. The Beardmore Glacier is at around 83.5°S, 171°E, and the Shackleton Glacier is at around 85°S, 177°W. At 85°S, 167°E, the location is probably on the Reedy or Mill Glacier, or near the polar plateau edge. The Ross Ice Shelf grounding line there is at roughly 85°S, with the Leverett Glacier near 85.5°S, 150°W. Longitude 167°E at 85°S is on the western side, near the Beardmore and Nimrod areas, and in the Queen Alexandra Range (around 84-85°S, 165-170°E). The Lennox-King Glacier and Law Glacier are in that area too.

This is a mountain and glacier area, so the surface is Solid. The elevation is probably about 2000 m. The Queen Alexandra Range has peaks up to 4000 m, but glaciers flow at roughly 1000-2500 m. I'll estimate 2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

9065.text:
Latitude -11.0, longitude -49.0 falls in Brazil, in the state of Tocantins or southern Pará. The Araguaia River and Ilha do Bananal are near longitude -50. Around -49.0, -11.0 the location is in Tocantins, near Paraíso do Tocantins (-10.17, -48.88) and Miracema. Gurupi is at -11.73, -49.07, so this point is slightly north of Gurupi. The Tocantins River lies near -48.3 at that latitude, and Lago de Palmas (the Lajeado reservoir) is around -48.3 to -48.5, so the point is west of it. The terrain there is the Tocantins plateau at about 250 m elevation.

I'll estimate it as solid ground at about 270 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":270}

11551.text:
Latitude -39, longitude -117 lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. It sits roughly 2,000 km west of the Chilean coast, and the Chile Rise lies farther south, near 40°S at longitude -92 or so. The East Pacific Rise is near -110 at 39°S; it runs at about -113 to -115 at 35-40°S. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is farther south, so the East Pacific Rise does not extend to this latitude as such. The Chile Rise triple junction is at 46°S, -76°.

At 39°S, -117°, the site is probably on the flank of the East Pacific Rise, whose axis sits near -111° at 38°S. The rise crest there is at about 2,500 to 3,000 m depth, and about 6° of longitude (roughly 500 km) off-axis the depth would be about 3,200 to 3,500 m. The fast-spreading southern East Pacific Rise has broad flanks, and at 500 km off-axis the age is about 6 to 7 Myr at a half-rate of roughly 80 mm/yr. Depth is then about 2,600 + 350·√6.5 ≈ 3,500 m. I'll estimate -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

4820.text:
Latitude 37.0°N, longitude 101.0°E lies in Qinghai province, China, near the Qinghai Lake region and the Qilian Mountains. Qinghai Lake is centered near 36.9°N, 100.2°E and spans roughly 99.6–100.8°E, so 101.0°E is just east of the lake's shore. The terrain there is the Datong River area and the mountains near Menyuan and Gangca.

At 37.0°N, 101.0°E, I estimate the elevation at about 3,300 m, in the hills east of the lake. The Datong valley sits at roughly 3,000 m, and the Daban Mountains to the north are higher. The point lies south of Menyuan (37.4°N, 101.6°E) and north of Haiyan (36.9°N, 100.99°E). Haiyan town is at about 3,000 m, so the elevation here is probably around 3,100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3150}

12673.text:
The point is at 51°S, 33°W, in the South Atlantic. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Falkland Plateau, roughly between the Falklands and South Georgia. South Georgia is near 36.5°W, 54.3°S, so this point is northwest of it. The North Scotia Ridge runs along about 53–54°S, so 51°S is north of it. The area is probably the Argentine Basin's eastern part or the Georgia Basin, with typical depths around 5000 m.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 51°S sits at about 12°W, which is far to the east, so the ridge is not a factor. The Falkland Plateau extends east to about 45°W at 51°S. At 33°W the seafloor is probably the Georgia Basin or the southern Argentine Basin, at roughly 4500–5000 m depth. I'll estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

10785.text:
Latitude -29.0, longitude 151.0 is in northern New South Wales, near Bonshaw and Texas, Queensland, close to the border. It is roughly west of Tenterfield. Tenterfield sits at about -29.05, 152.02, so this point is about 100 km west of it, in the Macintyre River area near Yetman/Bonshaw. Elevation there is roughly 300 m.

Yetman is at about -28.9, 150.8 with an elevation of about 270 m. Texas, Queensland is at -28.8, 151.17 with an elevation of about 300 m. For this point I estimate about 280 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":280}

1055.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude 131.0 E lies in the Laptev Sea, north of the Lena Delta and the Taymyr Peninsula. The New Siberian Islands are further east, at about 140 E and beyond, and Severnaya Zemlya is to the west at about 100 E. So this point is open sea.

The Laptev Sea shelf is shallow in the south and deepens toward the north. At 79 N the shelf break lies at around 80 N, so the depth here is probably 100–300 m. Near 131 E the shelf edge is at roughly 78–79 N, and depths there are around 200–400 m. I'll estimate -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

8043.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 67.0°E is in the central Indian Ocean, southwest of India and east of Somalia. It lies near the Carlsberg Ridge region, which runs northwest-southeast between roughly 57°E and 66°E at about 3°N to 10°N. At 1°N, 67°E we are likely east of the ridge in the Arabian Basin/Central Indian Basin boundary area. The Maldives sit near 73°E, so this point is well west of them. The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge is near 73°E, which is also far to the east.

The Carlsberg Ridge near 1°N is at about 66°E, so a position at 67°E could be on the ridge flank or in its rift valley. The Central Indian Ridge starts around 3°S, 66°E. Depths near the ridge are about 3000–4000 m, and the nearby basin floor is about 4400 m. I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2464.text:
Latitude 63.0 N, longitude 69.0 E lies in western Siberia, in the Khanty-Mansi/Yamalo-Nenets area. The Ob River is around 66 E at this latitude, so 69 E is east of the Ob, probably near the Northern Sosva or Polui area. The terrain there is flat, so elevation is probably around 40 m.

More precisely, Berezovo sits at about 63.9 N, 65 E. At 63 N, 69 E the area is west Siberian lowland, roughly 50 m, close to Khanty-Mansiysk (61 N, 69 E) at about 40 m. The Northern Sosva rises in the Urals and flows east to the Ob. The point is likely swampy and about 50 m, so I'll answer 50.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":50}

6917.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude -25.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, west of the Cape Verde islands. Santiago sits near 15°N, 23.5°W, and the point is about 2 degrees south and 1.5 degrees west of it, which puts it in open ocean south-southwest of the archipelago.

The Cape Verde Rise has depths of around 3,000 to 4,000 m. Ocean floor in the surrounding basin is about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. At 13°N, 25°W, I estimate a depth of roughly 4,700 m, but this is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

13875.text:
The point is at 65°S, 149°W, which lies in the Southern Ocean off the Antarctic coast. Marie Byrd Land's coast is farther south, at roughly 75°S, so this is open ocean. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies to the north, around 62°S at 150°W. The seafloor at 65°S is probably abyssal, around 4000–4500 m deep, and I am estimating that from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than looking it up.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from 65°S, 175°W up toward 55°S, 140°W, so at 149°W it would sit near 58–60°S. The point is therefore south of the ridge, on the abyssal plain, and I would estimate the depth at about 4,500 m.

