1618.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 177.0°E lies in the East Siberian Sea / Chukchi Sea region. Wrangel Island is near 71°N, 179°W–180°, so 73°N, 177°E is north of Wrangel Island, on the shelf. Shelf depth there is roughly 50 m, and the seabed north of Wrangel Island is about 40–60 m deep.

This is an estimate from memory of regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -50}

2429.text:
The point 63.0°N, 1.0°W lies in the North Atlantic, between Shetland (about 60°N) and the Faroe Islands (about 62°N, 7°W). It is roughly 200 km north of Shetland, in the Faroe-Shetland Channel region.

The Faroe-Shetland Channel runs northeast-southwest. At 63°N, 1°W, the water is probably on the Norwegian Sea side, where depths are around 1000 to 1500 m. The channel axis at 61°N, 3°W is about 1000 m deep. Farther northeast, near 62.5°N, 0°, the depth is about 1500 m. At 63°N, 1°W the seabed is likely about 1200 to 1500 m deep, so I estimate about -1400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1400}

12615.text:
The point at 51°S, 149°W lies in the South Pacific, southeast of French Polynesia and well north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. The seafloor there is probably abyssal plain or abyssal hills at roughly 4,000 to 4,500 m depth.

The East Pacific Rise is much farther east and north, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge near 51°S sits around 115°W to 140°W. At 149°W the crust is older, so it should be deeper than the ridge crest. I estimate about -4,200 m, a rough figure from regional knowledge and not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

15276.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 133 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land inland of the coast. The Dome C region sits near -75, 123, and Vostok near -78.5, 106.8. Concordia is at 123E. The point at 133E, -79 should be on the plateau with an ice surface elevation of around 2,800 m. Along 133E, the coast is near -66, and the plateau rises to about 3,000 m by -75. At -79 and 133E I'd expect roughly 3,000 m, since Dome C at 3,233 m lies at -75, 123 and the surface slopes down toward the Aurora basin. I'll estimate 3,000 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

3376.text:
Latitude 53.0 N, longitude 93.0 E places the point in southern Siberia, in Krasnoyarsk Krai or Khakassia. Abakan sits near 53.7 N, 91.4 E. The Sayan Mountains lie to the south, and the Krasnoyarsk Reservoir is around 91 E. At 93 E, 53 N, the point is in the northern foothills of the Western Sayan, near the Kyzyl-Kuragino area and the Tuva border.

The Sayan Mountains' northern slopes there are covered in taiga. The Oya River and the Kantegir River are nearby. The Sayan Reservoir on the Yenisei is at about 52.8 N, 91.4 E, so it is not at this point. The Kyzyl-Kuragino region is mountainous, with elevations of roughly 700–1000 m. I'll estimate 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

7031.text:
The point (11°N, 157°W) lies in the central Pacific, south of the Hawaiian Islands, which sit around 19–22°N. That puts it in open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

For the seabed depth, the area is on the Pacific plate, south of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands. Abyssal depths in this region are typically about 5,000 to 5,500 m. I'm estimating from general knowledge of the region rather than a specific lookup, and I'm using 5,300 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

4649.text:
Latitude 39.0 N, longitude 119.0 E lies in the Bohai Sea region. Bohai Bay's western coast is near 117.7 E, and the Yellow River delta is near 119 E at about 37.7 N. At 39.0 N, the Bohai coast runs from Tangshan (about 118.5 E at 39.2 N) to Qinhuangdao (119.6 E at 39.9 N). Tangshan's Caofeidian is at about 118.4 E, 38.9 N. So 119.0 E, 39.0 N is slightly east of Caofeidian, in the Bohai Sea.

The Bohai Sea is shallow, averaging about 18 m. Near Caofeidian offshore at 119.0 E, 39.0 N, the depth is probably 15 to 25 m, so I'll estimate about 20 m of water, which gives a seabed elevation of about -20 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-20}

9650.text:
Latitude -17.0, longitude 41.0 falls in Mozambique. The coast near Angoche is around -16.2, 39.9, and Pebane is near -17.27, 38.15. At longitude 41 the point is well offshore, in the Mozambique Channel, east of the coast. The coast at -17 lies near longitude 39. At 41E the point is about 200 km offshore, on the Mozambique continental shelf/slope.

Offshore of Zambezi delta, the shelf is narrow in the north near Angoche, where the shelf is wide at about 100 km. At 41E, 17S, depth is likely a few hundred to about 1000 m. The Davie Ridge is near 40-42E at 17S, though it runs further north from about 11S to 17S, with the Mozambique Channel's deepest part around 2000-3000 m. At 41E the point is roughly 2 degrees east of the coast, which would put it on the slope or the basin, so I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

8117.text:
The point is at 1°S, 145°W in the central Pacific, south of the equator and east of the Line Islands. There is no land here, so it is Water, and I need the seabed depth.

This area sits in the Central Pacific Basin, between the Line Islands ridge to the west and the Clipperton fracture zone region to the east. Seafloor depths here are typically about 4,800 to 5,200 m. Seafloor age is about 60 to 70 million years. Depth for that age would be roughly 5,000 m. I am estimating from regional depth patterns, not from a specific bathymetry reading, so I take about -4,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

6012.text:
The point at 23°N, 35°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 23°N sits near 45°W, so this point is about 10° east of the axis... In fact the ridge axis at 23°N is around 45°W, and 35°W is to its east, on the African plate side.

The seafloor at this location should be abyssal plain or abyssal hills, with ages of about 40 to 50 million years, which typically gives depths of around 5000 to 5500 m. The Kane fracture zone, at roughly 24°N, has the ridge at about 45°W, so crust at 35°W is about 10° east, roughly 1000 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 1.2 cm/yr that gives about 80 Myr of age, and the depth would be about 5300 m. The area east of the ridge may include some rises, but I'll estimate about -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

15055.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 51 is in East Antarctica, in Queen Maud Land / Enderby Land interior. At 77°S, 51°E the point is in the interior, south of the Prince Olav Coast and the Shirase region, near the Amundsen Bay mountains. The coast there is around 67°S, so 77°S is about 1100 km inland. That puts it on the plateau near Dome Fuji (77.3°S, 39.7°E, 3800 m). At 51°E, 77°S the ice surface is probably around 3500 m.

The Dome Argus direction is toward 80.4°S, 77°E, and the elevation increases in that direction. Near 77°S, 51°E I'd estimate about 3400 m. The surface is grounded ice, so it is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3400}

3051.text:
The point 57.0°N, 163.0°E lies in the western Bering Sea, off the east coast of Kamchatka. The Kamchatka coast near 57°N is at roughly 156-157°E, so 163°E is well offshore. The Kamchatka Peninsula's eastern edge reaches about 163°E only near 60°N at Karaginsky Island (around 164°E, 58°N). At 57°N, 163°E, the point is southeast of Karaginsky Island, in the Komandorski Basin area or on the slope off Kamchatka. The Commander Islands sit at about 55°N, 166°E.

The Kamchatka shelf is narrow. At 57°N, the coast is about 156.5°E, so 163°E is about 6.5° of longitude away, roughly 390 km offshore. This is in the deep Aleutian or Komandorski Basin. The Kamchatka Strait lies to the south, and the Komandorski Basin depth is about 3800 m. The Shirshov Ridge is farther to the north at about 170°E. At 57°N, 163°E, the depth is probably around 3500-3900 m, so I'll estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

6747.text:
Latitude 15.0, longitude -5.0 is in Mali, in the Sahel region west of the Niger bend near Nampala/Niafunké. It is not far from the Mauritania border. The area is about 15°N, 5°W, with terrain at roughly 280 m.

The inland Niger delta lies to the east, around 4.5°W and 15.5°N at about 260 m. Lake Faguibine is at 16.7°N, 3.9°W. At 15°N, 5°W the surface is likely sandy land at about 270 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":275}

10897.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 15 is in the South Atlantic, off the west coast of South Africa. The coast near 31°S is at about 17.5°E, so 15°E lies roughly 250 km offshore. The continental shelf off Namaqualand is about 100-200 km wide, with the shelf break at around 300-400 m depth, and the continental slope lies beyond that.

At 31°S the coast runs near 17.7°E (Olifants River mouth is around 31.7°S, 18.2°E). So 15°E is about 300 km offshore. The shelf break there is at roughly 16°E, which puts 15°E on the upper-to-mid continental slope, probably around 2500 m deep. The Cape Basin floor is about 4500-5000 m, and the slope descends steeply from 16°E. I estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

13980.text:
