{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12999.text:
The point at 55°S, 101°W is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. It lies north of the Antarctic-Pacific Ridge and west of the Chile Rise. The Chile Rise runs from about 46°S, 76°W southwest toward roughly 60°S, 150°W, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is farther southwest.

The Chile Rise segments near 55°S, 115°W are shallow, around 2500–3500 m. At 101°W the seafloor is probably older and deeper. The Eltanin fracture zone region lies near 55°S, 125°W. Seafloor depth near 55°S, 101°W is probably about 3500–4000 m. I estimate 3900 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7263.text:
The point 9.0°N, 53.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, roughly 500 km off the coast of Guyana and Suriname. It sits on the Demerara Plain or the abyssal plain just beyond it.

The continental shelf off Guyana extends about 150 km out. Beyond that the Demerara Rise lies near 9°N, 54°W, but it is a feature at roughly 5°N, 54°W, so it is not at this latitude. At 9°N, 53°W the seafloor is likely the Demerara abyssal plain at about 4,500 to 5,000 m depth. I estimate -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

9601.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -57 lies in Brazil's Mato Grosso state, near the Bolivian border. It is probably in the Pantanal region, or just north of it. The Pantanal spans roughly 16–22°S and 55–58°W, so this point is at the northern edge of the Pantanal near the Paraguay River headwaters. The Paraguay River at Cáceres (16.07°S, 57.7°W) is at about 100 m elevation.

Checking the location: -17, -57 is southeast of Cáceres, about 1° south and 0.7° east. That is in Bolivia/Brazil, near the Bolivian border, which lies around 58°W at this latitude. Longitude -57 is therefore in Brazil, in the Pantanal north of Corumbá (-19.0, -57.65). The area is flat, with elevation of about 120 m. The point could be on the Pantanal floodplain near the Cuiabá or São Lourenço rivers, at roughly 100–150 m. I'll estimate 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

6331.text:
