This area is the Southland shelf and slope region south of the Otago coast. The Snares shelf sits near 48°S, 166.5°E. At 169°E, 47°S, the point is east of the Stewart Island shelf and near the Campbell Plateau's northern edge. The Campbell Plateau is about 500–1000 m deep, and its northern part near 47°S, 169°E is probably around 500 m. The Bounty Trough lies further north, at about 45°S. The southern flank of the Bounty Trough runs near 46.5°S at 170°E. Depths around 47°S, 169°E are probably 600–800 m, so I estimate -700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

12790.text:
Latitude -53, longitude -159 is in the South Pacific, southwest of Chile and south of Tahiti. It lies in the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region to the north, around the Udintsev fracture zone area. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs near 55-65°S in the 150-160°W range, and at 53°S, 159°W the seafloor is probably on the northern flank of the ridge or in the abyssal plain.

Further north, the Pacific basin is about 4000-5000 m deep. Near -53, -159, I estimate a depth of about 4,000 m, possibly less given the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's shallow crest at about 2,500-3,000 m. The ridge near 150°W sits at about 60-65°S, and at 160°W it is near 57°S. At 53°S the seafloor is therefore about 10 million years old or more, so a depth of around 4,000 m is reasonable. The Macquarie Ridge is far to the west, so it is not a factor.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

11263.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 27 lies in the Indian Ocean, south of South Africa and southeast of Port Elizabeth (Gqeberha, about 25.6°E, -34°). Longitude 27°E at -35° is roughly 100 km offshore from the coast near Algoa Bay. The continental shelf off Port Elizabeth extends about 60–100 km south of the coast, and the shelf edge there lies around -34.5° to -35.5°. The Agulhas Bank shelf edge is at roughly 200 m depth, and the slope descends beyond that.

At 27°E the coast sits near -33.7°. At -35°, about 145 km south, the point is probably on the upper slope or the outer shelf. The shelf edge east of Port Elizabeth is relatively close to the coast, because the shelf narrows toward the northeast. At 27°E I estimate the shelf break at roughly -34.7°, so -35° would be on the slope at about 1000 m depth. I'll estimate -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

5406.text:
Location: 29.0°N, 167.0°W, in the North Pacific northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. Nearby features: Kure Atoll is at 28.4°N, 178.3°W, Midway at 28.2°N, 177.4°W, Pearl and Hermes at 27.9°N, 175.8°W, Lisianski at 26.1°N, 173.9°W, and French Frigate Shoals at 23.7°N, 166.2°W. So 29°N, 167°W is about 5° north of French Frigate Shoals, in open ocean.

The Hawaiian Ridge lies to the southwest, and the seafloor around this area is roughly 5000 m deep. Nearby abyssal plain depths are about 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Hawaiian Arch and moat influence depths near the ridge, but at 29°N the seafloor is probably about 5,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

1124.text:
Latitude 77.0 N, longitude -91.0 W is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Devon Island spans roughly 74.5-76.6 N and 80-92 W, so 77 N lies north of it. Just north of Devon is Jones Sound, which runs from about 76 to 76.5 N. Ellesmere Island's southern coast sits near 76.3 N at around 90 W, and further north at 77 N and 91 W is the interior of southwestern Ellesmere, near Bay Fiord or Eureka Sound.

Bay Fiord is at about 78 N, 85 W, and Baumann Fiord is at about 77.5 N, 88 W. Stor Island sits in the Norwegian Bay area. Norwegian Bay lies around 77.5 N, 91 W, and the Svendsen Peninsula is near 78 N, 82 W. At 77 N, 91 W the position is probably on Ellesmere's southwestern part, close to the Hoved Island and Cornwall Island region. Southwestern Ellesmere around 77 N, 90 W is the Raanes Peninsula and the Bjorne Peninsula (about 77.3 N, 85 W). Further west, 77 N, 91 W is likely near the coast of Norwegian Bay, which has islands such as Graham Island at about 77.5 N, 90 W. I can't pin down whether this point is land or water. Land seems more likely, with moderate elevation of roughly 300 m, so I'll answer Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

4447.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude 75.0 is in the Tian Shan, on the Kyrgyzstan–China border region. Issyk-Kul lies at about 42.4N, so it is not a factor here. At 41.0N, 75.0E the point is in Kyrgyzstan's At-Bashy range or the Naryn area.

The At-Bashy valley is at about 41.17N, 75.8E, with an elevation of roughly 2,000 m. Near 75.0E, 41.0N the terrain is closer to the Kokshaal-Too range's northern edge. The Torugart pass is at 40.52N, 75.4E, at 3,750 m. The Naryn river is around 41.4N. At 41.0N, 75.0E the Kyrgyz-China border is about 40.9N near 75E, and the Kokshaal range lies south of there. The area is likely the Aksai or Naryn-tau region at about 3,500 m, with the Ak-Shyirak massif nearby at 41.8N, 78E. I'll estimate 3,600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3600}

12927.text:
