{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2650}

11005.text:
This location, 33°S 129°W, is in the South Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water. It lies roughly between the Easter Island region and the Pitcairn area, on the Pacific plate east of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor age there is moderate, and typical depths in this region are around 3,500 to 4,000 m. I'm estimating from general knowledge of the area rather than looking up a bathymetry value.

The East Pacific Rise sits near 113°W at this latitude, and 129°W is about 16° of longitude farther west. At the typical spreading rate, that corresponds to crust roughly 15–20 million years old, which would put the depth near 3,800 m. Seamounts and the Pitcairn–Gambier chain lie farther to the northwest, so I'll use the typical value of about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8516.text:
The point (-5.0, -67.0) is in western Brazil, in Amazonas state. It lies near the Juruá River basin, south of the Solimões River and northeast of the town of Eirunepé. The Juruá runs around -66.0 to -70.0 longitude at this latitude, so the point is probably in the interfluvial lowlands, away from the main channel. Terrain elevation in this region is typically 60 to 100 m.

The Purus River is near -65 to -64 longitude at that latitude, and the point sits roughly between the Juruá and the Purus. Terrain there is around 70 to 90 m, so I'll estimate 80 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

5485.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude -9.0 is in southern Morocco. It lies near the Anti-Atlas, close to Tata and Akka. Tata is at about 29.74 N, -7.97 W. Assa is at about 28.6 N, -9.4 W. Guelmim is at 28.99 N, -10.06 W, at roughly 300 m elevation. At -9.0 and 29.0 the site is east of Guelmim, in the western Anti-Atlas near Tighmert or Foum Zguid. Foum Zguid is at 30.09 N, -6.88 W, so it is not the nearby town.

The area near 29 N, -9 W is probably the Oued Draa region west of Akka, with the Jbel Bani ridge to the north. Elevation there is likely about 500 m. The Bani ridge sits at about 29.3 N, so 29.0 is probably just south of it, in the Draa plain at roughly 400–600 m. I'll use 520 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":520}

7133.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude 47.0 is in Somaliland/Somalia, in the Horn of Africa. The Gulf of Aden coast is at about 10.4°N near Berbera, so 11.0°N, 47°E lies in the Gulf of Aden, roughly 60 km north of the coast. Berbera sits at 10.44°N, 45.0°E. At 47°E the coast is near 11.0°N (Bender Ziada, Las Khorey area near 11.1°N, 48°E). Around 47°E the coast is about 10.95°N, near Heis (10.87°N, 47.8°E) and Maydh (10.98°N, 47.1°E). Maydh is at roughly 10.98°N, 47.1°E, so this point is essentially on the coast. It could be land at around 10 to 50 m, or shallow water.

Since the coastline passes through Maydh at about 47.1°E, a point at 47.0°E, 11.0°N is probably just offshore or on the coast. Coastal land there is a narrow plain, with the escarpment rising to the south. I'll guess Solid at a low elevation of about 5 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":5}

4925.text:
The point 35°N, 49°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and south of the Newfoundland Ridge area. It is near the western flank of the ridge, close to the New England Seamounts and the Corner Rise region. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis is around 40°W at this latitude, so 49°W is about 9° west of the axis. The seafloor age there is roughly 60–70 Ma, which suggests a depth of about 5000 m. The Bermuda Rise is at about 33°N, 65°W, which is further west, so it does not apply. The Sohm Abyssal Plain is around 5000 m deep, and the basin near 35°N, 49°W is probably about 5100 m.

I estimate a depth of about -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

1426.text:
Latitude 75.0N, longitude 153.0E lies in the Laptev Sea / East Siberian Sea region. The New Siberian Islands are at about 75N, 140E, and Kotelny Island spans roughly 137-143E. At 153E and 75N, the point is north of the Laptev Sea shelf's eastern part, east of the New Siberian Islands. The shelf there is about 50-100 m deep. The shelf break lies near 77-78N in that area, so at 75N the depth is probably around 50 m.

Bathymetry in the Laptev Sea at 75N, 130E is about 50 m. Near 153E, 75N, which is north of the De Long Islands (76N, 149E), the depth is around 60 m. The De Long Islands (Bennett, Jeannette, Henrietta) are at about 76-77N, 149-158E. So 75N, 153E is south of them, and the depth is about 40-60 m, so I'll use -50.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

6328.text:
Latitude 19°N, longitude 123°W is in the open Pacific Ocean, west of Mexico's Revillagigedo Islands and southwest of Baja California. The seafloor there is abyssal plain, with typical depths of about 4,000 to 4,500 m in this region. The Clarion Fracture Zone lies near 18–19°N, and the seafloor to its north is typically around 4,200 m deep.

I estimated this from typical regional depths rather than looking up a bathymetry value, so I'm picking about -4,300 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

6444.text:
Latitude 19.0, longitude 109.0 lies in the Gulf of Tonkin / South China Sea, west of Hainan Island. Hainan's west coast is around 108.6–109.0°E at about 19°N. Danzhou and Changjiang sit near 19.5°N, 109.5°E, and Dongfang is at 19.1°N, 108.6°E. The coast near 19°N is at about 108.65°E, so 109.0°E would be inland.

At 19.0°N, Hainan spans roughly 108.7°E to 110.5°E, so 109.0°E is on land. That puts it near Baisha or Dongfang county, around Limushan (Limu Mountain) at 19.2°N, 109.7°E. The terrain at 109.0°E, 19.0°N is in the hills of Dongfang/Changjiang, with elevations of roughly 200–400 m, and I'll estimate 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

10153.text:
Latitude -23, longitude -33 lies in the South Atlantic, east of Brazil. It is about 800 km east of the Brazilian coast (Cabo Frio is near -42), and the Vitória-Trindade chain sits near -20 latitude, but it spans longitudes -39 to -29. Trindade is at -20.5, -29.3, so this point is not on it.

The point is in the Brazil Basin, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The São Paulo Plateau lies at about -25 to -28 latitude, -38 to -42 longitude. The Rio Grande Rise is around -30, -35 and extends north to about -28 and further east to -20. At -23, -33 the seafloor is probably abyssal plain at about 4,500 to 5,000 m depth, though the northern edge of the Rio Grande Rise might raise it. The Brazil Basin there is roughly 4,000–5,000 m deep. I'll estimate -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14228.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -163 lies in the Southern Ocean, north of the Ross Sea region and off the Antarctic coast. The Ross Sea continental shelf is at about 180 to -160 longitude, with the coast near -78. At -69 the point is well north of the shelf, in the deep ocean off the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. The Pacific-Antarctic basin there is typically about 3000-4000 m deep. The Northern Basin and Adare area is around 170E, and the continental rise lies at about -70 near -165. The slope sits near -66 to -68 at that longitude, so -69 is probably abyssal plain, around -3500 m.

This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a lookup of the actual value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

754.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -111.0° (111°W) falls in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Meighen Island is near 80°N, 99°W, and Borden Island is near 78.5°N, 111°W. At 81°N, 111°W the point is north of Borden Island and Brock Island, and probably north of Ellef Ringnes Island, which reaches about 78.7°N. Ellef Ringnes spans roughly 77.7–79.0°N and 101–106°W. So the point lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the archipelago, where the Sverdrup Basin margin meets the continental shelf.

The shelf edge near 81°N, 111°W is probably at about 79–80°N, so this point sits just beyond the shelf break on the slope. Bathymetry there is likely around 500–1500 m deep. I'm estimating about -1000 m, since the area north of Borden Island and Prince Patrick Island has a narrow shelf, and the Canadian Arctic margin there drops into the Canada Basin.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

16048.text:
Latitude -89, longitude -123 is in West Antarctica, about 1° from the South Pole. The point lies along the 123°W meridian, on the Pole's "Marie Byrd Land side" (the 180° to 90°W side).

The South Pole's ice surface is about 2835 m. Moving toward 123°W at 89°S, the surface elevation is likely around 2700-2800 m. Along 90°W the surface slopes down toward the Ronne/Filchner side and the Thiel Mountains region. On the 120°W side the surface rises toward the Pole from the Ross side, and the ice is grounded there. The Pole's surface is about 2835 m. The Ross Ice Shelf is at about 85°S near 180° and the Amundsen sector lies near 120°W. At 89°S, 123°W, I estimate about 2750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2750}

7480.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 21.0°E falls in the Central African Republic, in the east-central part of the country near Bria or Ndélé. Bria is at about 6.5°N, 21.98°E, so this point is west of Bria. The plateau there sits at roughly 600 m.

The area is on the Central African Republic plateau, with elevations of about 500–700 m. I'll estimate 620 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":620}

8277.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 175.0 lies in the Pacific Ocean, in the Gilbert Islands region of Kiribati. Tarawa is at about 1.4N, 173E, and Abemama is at about 0.4N, 173.8E. Nonouti is at about -0.7N, 174.4E, Tabiteuea is at about -1.4N, 174.8E, and Beru is at about -1.3N, 176E. So the point at -1.0, 175.0 is probably just east of Tabiteuea North, which extends from about -1.2 to -1.5 and sits near longitude 174.8 to 175.0. This point is probably in the ocean.

The Gilbert Islands are atolls rising steeply from the seafloor at about 4000 m depth. Near an atoll the seabed drops off quickly, so a point a few tens of km away would probably be about -3000 to -4000 m. Tabiteuea's eastern side is near 174.95, and the point is about 20 km north of the island's northern tip. I'll estimate -3500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

13124.text:
The point is at 55°S, 149°E, in the Southern Ocean south of Tasmania and southeast of the Australian continent. It lies roughly south of the Tasmania–Macquarie Ridge area. Macquarie Island is near 54.5°S, 158.9°E, so this point is well west of it.

Seafloor depths at this location are probably about 3,000 to 4,000 m. The Australian-Antarctic basin lies further south, and the South Tasman Rise sits near 47°S, so 55°S is south of that rise. The area around 55°S, 149°E is probably abyssal plain at about 3,500 to 4,000 m depth. I'll estimate -3,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2180.text:
Latitude 65.0 N, longitude -139.0 W lies in the Yukon–Alaska border region, close to the Yukon–Alaska border at 141 W. The point is about 2 degrees east of the border, in the Yukon, near the Tintina Trench and Ogilvie Mountains area. Dawson City is at 64.06 N, 139.43 W. At 65.0 N, 139.0 W, the point is about 100 km north of Dawson, in the Tombstone area or Ogilvie River headwaters, probably in the Tintina or Ogilvie uplands. The Klondike Highway runs through that area, and the Ogilvie/Blackstone area lies further north. The terrain is likely hilly, with an elevation of about 1000 m.

The surface is land, so I estimate the elevation at roughly 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

10105.text:
This location is in the South Pacific at 23°S, 129°W. It lies east of the Gambier Islands (about 135°W) and west of Pitcairn, which is at 25°S, 130°W. The Pitcairn group is near 128.3°W for Henderson and Ducie, and 130.1°W for Pitcairn itself. This point is at 23°S, 129°W, which is about 220 km north of Pitcairn. That puts it in open ocean on the Pacific plate, near the Pitcairn hotspot seamount chain.

Typical seafloor depth in this region is about 3500–4000 m on the East Pacific Rise flank. At 129°W the crust is young, roughly 20–30 Ma, so depth is probably around 3800 m. The Easter Island–Pitcairn area has shallower swell, around 3000–3500 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

