{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3450}

14994.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -71 is in Antarctica, in the area of the Ronne Ice Shelf and the Ellsworth Land coast. The Ronne-Filchner ice shelf front lies around -75 to -78 latitude at longitudes -60 to -80. At -77, -71, I think this is within the Ronne Ice Shelf, near the Orville Coast and Ellsworth Mountains. The Ellsworth Mountains sit at about -78.5, -85. The Orville Coast is near -75.5, -64. The Ronne Ice Shelf's western part, near Rutford Ice Stream and Institute Ice Stream, is around -78, -80.

At -77, -71, the point is likely on the Ronne Ice Shelf, which is floating. The seabed beneath it there is perhaps around -500 m, since the Ronne cavity depth is about 400-900 m. Near Berkner Island, which lies at around -79, -45, the seabed is deeper to the west. I'll estimate -600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

1827.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -125.0 lies in the Northwest Territories of Canada. It is east of the Mackenzie Delta and near Franklin Bay and the Parry Peninsula. Franklin Bay is around 69.5N, 126W. Paulatuk is at 69.35N, 124.07W. Darnley Bay is at about 69.8N, 124W. So 69.0N, 125W is inland, southwest of Paulatuk, probably in the Melville Hills or the Horton River area.

The Horton River flows north to Franklin Bay around 69.6N, 126.9W. Terrain at 69N, 125W is likely tundra plateau at about 300-400 m. Some lakes may be nearby, but I'll treat this as land. The Smoking Hills area is lower. The Melville Hills reach roughly 500 m, and I'll estimate about 350 m here.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

10858.text:
The coordinates -31.0, -63.0 fall in Córdoba Province, Argentina, in the pampas. The point is northeast of Córdoba city and near the Mar Chiquita area. Mar Chiquita lake sits at roughly -30.9 latitude and -62.8 longitude, but that is wrong: Mar Chiquita is at about -30.7, -62.6, so it is not at this point.

Longitude -63 at latitude -31 is near the Río Segundo / Laguna area, around Villa del Rosario at -31.55, -63.5. The Sierras Chicas lie to the west near -64.2, so the point is in the plains east of the sierras. Elevation there is roughly 300 m. Mar Chiquita itself is at about 70 m. The Río Primero region near Santa Rosa de Río Primero (-31.15, -63.4) is about 300 m. Going east, the plain falls toward Mar Chiquita, so at -63.0 the elevation is probably about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

2495.text:
The point (63.0°N, 131.0°E) lies in the Sakha Republic (Yakutia), Russia. It is probably east of the Lena River and near the Aldan or Tompo area, in the Verkhoyansk foothills or the Aldan plateau.

The Lena at 63°N is around 129.5°E near Sangar (63.9°N, 127.5°E). The Aldan River at 131°E is around 62.5–63°N, near Khandyga (62.65°N, 135.6°E), which is farther east. So 131°E, 63°N sits in the central Yakutian lowland, east of the Lena, on the Lena–Aldan interfluve. That area is around 150–250 m in elevation. The Tatta and Amga rivers are near 131–134°E, so the point is likely land at about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

14490.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 1°E is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The Jutulsessen and Giæverryggen area lies around 71°S, 0-1°E, which is inland of the Fimbul Ice Shelf. The ice shelf edge is near 70.5°S at this longitude, so 71°S could be at the grounded ice margin or on the Jutulstraumen side.

Around 71°S, 1°E the ice sheet surface is probably about 1000-1500 m. The Fimbul Ice Shelf extends to roughly 70.8°S at 0°E, and the Maudheim area near 71°S, 8°W is on the shelf. Near 1°E, the coastal ice shelf front sits at about 70.2°S. The Ahlmannryggen ridge is around 72°S, 3°W. At 71°S, 1°E the surface is probably grounded ice at roughly 1000 m, with the Sverdrupfjella mountains to the south-east at about 72°S, 0°E. So this is likely grounded ice at about 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

6468.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 157.0°E (positive longitude is east) puts the point in the western Pacific, east of the Mariana Islands and northeast of the Caroline Islands. The Hawaiian Islands lie near 19°N, 155°W, which is a different longitude, so they are not relevant here.

The location is in the Pacific Plate interior, within the Marcus-Wake seamount region, east of the Marianas. Seamounts and guyots are scattered through the area, but the typical seafloor depth in the surrounding basin is around 5,500 to 5,900 m. The Pigafetta Basin lies near 18°N, 156°E and has depths of about 5,700 m. I could not confirm whether a seamount sits exactly at this point, so I am using the basin depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5700}

7839.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 19.0°E is in the Central African Republic, in the south near the Ubangi River and the border with the DRC. The Ubangi runs along roughly 18-19°E at around 4°N. At 3°N, 19°E the point is probably in the DRC, in the Sud-Ubangi/Nord-Ubangi area, which is lowland forest with elevations of about 350-400 m.

The Ubangi at Zongo (4.3°N, 18.6°E) is about 340 m. Going downstream to Libenge (3.65°N, 18.6°E) it is about 320 m. The point at 3°N, 19°E is inland, and the terrain there is around 380 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":370}

7288.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude -3.0° is in West Africa. That places it in northern Ivory Coast, near the Ghana/Burkina Faso border region. Longitude -3.0 is close to the Comoé River area, and the Black Volta lies further west of Ghana's border at about -2.9 to -3.2 at latitude 9, so water is possible but unlikely. The point is more likely in Bondoukou or Bouna, Ivory Coast, where elevation is typically about 250-300 m.

I'll estimate 270 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":270}

8593.text:
The coordinates -5.0, 87.0 fall in the central Indian Ocean, southwest of Sumatra and east of the Chagos region. This area lies in the Central Indian Basin, near the Ninety East Ridge, which runs along roughly 88–90°E. At 87°E the seafloor is probably just west of the ridge, in the basin at about 4,500 to 5,000 m depth.

Ninety East Ridge crests are around 1,500 to 2,500 m deep near the equator, and the ridge is about 200 km wide. At 5°S the ridge sits near 88–90°E, so 87°E is likely on its western flank or in the basin just beyond it. The basin floor is about 5,000 m deep. I estimate -4,900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4900}

2770.text:
The point 59°N, 39°W lies in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland's southern tip (Cape Farewell is at about 60°N, 44°W). It is over the Irminger Basin, east of the Greenland continental shelf and west of the Reykjanes Ridge.

The Reykjanes Ridge runs southwest from Iceland, reaching about 59°N, 31°W at 59°N. At 39°W the seafloor is in the Irminger Basin, where depths are typically 2,800–3,000 m. This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

12362.text:
The point is at 47°S, 65°E, in the southern Indian Ocean. It lies southwest of the Kerguelen Plateau, which is centered around 50°S, 70°E, and southeast of Crozet (about 46°S, 51°E). Prince Edward Islands are near 47°S, 38°E, so they are too far west to matter here.

The area between Crozet and Kerguelen is the Crozet Basin, with depths of roughly 4000 to 5000 m. The southwest Indian Ridge is further north, near 40°S at these longitudes. At 65°E the Kerguelen Plateau's western flank reaches down to about 47°S, but its edge is mostly east of 68°E. At 47°S, 65°E the seafloor is probably about 3500 to 4000 m deep, though there is a "Del Cano Rise" near 45°S, 60°E with depths around 2000 to 3000 m. The point is likely on the rise's eastern edge or in the basin between the rise and Kerguelen, so I estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

1381.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 63.0 E is in the Kara Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya. Novaya Zemlya's east coast sits near 60-68 E at these latitudes. At 75 N the island spans roughly 56-67 E, with the northern island's east coast near 68 E at about 76 N. At 75 N the east coast is probably around 66-67 E, so 63 E lies inland on the northern island.

Inland there, the terrain is mountainous and partly glaciated. The Severny Island ice cap lies farther north, around 76-77 N. At 75 N, 63 E, the elevation is probably a few hundred meters. Novaya Zemlya's mountains reach about 1000 m in the north, and 75 N is near Mityushikha Bay and Russkaya Gavan, so I'll estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

7155.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 91.0°E lies in the Andaman Sea, west of the Andaman Islands. The Andaman Islands span roughly 92–94°E, so this point is west of them, in the deep basin or on the slope. The Andaman Trench is just west of the islands, and at 91°E the seafloor is probably about 3,000–4,000 m deep. Bathymetry in this part of the Bay of Bengal east of the Ninety East Ridge (88–90°E) is probably around 3,500 m, and I am estimating rather than reading it from a chart.

The point is water, and I estimate the seabed at about -3,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

11025.text:
The point at -33.0, -89.0 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and about 2,300 km from the coast. It lies east of the East Pacific Rise and sits on the Nazca Plate seafloor, which is roughly 3,000-4,000 m deep here. The Juan Fernández Ridge is near 33°S but at longitudes of about -78 to -80, so it does not affect this point. The Chile Rise and Easter Island region are farther north and west.

Seafloor age at this location is about 30-40 million years, which typically corresponds to a depth of about 3,800 m. The abyssal plain west of the Chile Trench at this latitude is around 4,000 m deep. I'm estimating about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7827.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude -5.0° (west) falls in the Gulf of Guinea, south of the Ivory Coast and Ghana coast, which sits near 5°N. At 3°N, 5°W the point is open ocean, about 250 km offshore.

The continental shelf off Ivory Coast is narrow. By about 3°N the seabed is on the abyssal plain or the lower slope of the Ivory Coast–Ghana margin, probably 4,000 to 4,500 m deep. The Ivory Coast Basin reaches about 4,500 m near 3°N, so I estimate around -4,400 m. This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

5203.text:
Latitude 33.0°N, longitude 147.0°E lies in the northwest Pacific, east of Japan. Japan's Honshu coast is at about 140°E, so this point is well out to sea, south of the Kuroshio Extension. The region east of the Izu-Ogasawara arc is the Pacific abyssal plain, with depths of about 5,500–6,000 m. At 33°N, 147°E the point is east of the Izu-Bonin Trench, which is near 143°E at that latitude.

There are seamounts in the area, such as those of the Shatsky Rise and the Mariana-area chains, but the nearby Japanese Seamount chain lies farther east at around 150°E. I have no specific seamount at this position, so I'll estimate a typical abyssal depth of about 5,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

5375.text:
Latitude 31.0 N, longitude 131.0 E is south of Kyushu, Japan. Kagoshima's southern coast is around 31.2 N, and Tanegashima lies at about 30.6 N, 131.0 E. At 31.0 N, 131.0 E, the nearest land is probably the Satsuma peninsula's southern tip, near Cape Nagasakibana at 31.15 N and 130.55 E. The Osumi peninsula's southern tip, Cape Sata, is at 31.0 N, 130.66 E. Longitude 131.0 is east of that, in the sea. Tanegashima's northern tip is about 30.75 N, so the point lies between Osumi and Tanegashima in the Osumi Strait, or just south of the Osumi coast at Kimotsuki. The Osumi coast at 131.0 E sits around 31.3 N, so 31.0 N is in the sea, which is the strait or Hyuga-nada.

The depth there is about 100 m on the shelf, though it could be deeper. The Osumi Strait is roughly 100 to 200 m deep, so I'll estimate -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

5386.text:
Latitude 31.0°S (the coordinates are given as positive 31.0, so it is 31°N, 153°E). That puts the point in the western Pacific Ocean, east of Japan and south of Honshu. At 31°N, 153°E, the area is in the open Northwest Pacific basin, east of the Izu-Ogasawara Trench, which lies near 142-143°E. The seafloor there is abyssal plain, with depths of roughly 5500-6000 m. Seamounts are possible in this region, such as the Magellan seamounts farther east and southeast, but this location is likely abyssal.

I estimate a depth of about 5800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

5988.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude -83.0 lies in the Gulf of Mexico / Straits of Florida area, just north of Cuba's western coast. Cuba's northern coast near Havana is at about 23.1°N, -82.4°. At -83.0, the coast near Bahía Honda and Cabañas is around 22.9°N, -83.0°, so 23.0°N is slightly north of the coast and likely in water.

The shelf north of Pinar del Río is shallow, and the Cuban coast at -83.0 is about 22.95°N. At 23.0 it is roughly 5 km offshore, where depth is likely 10-20 m. The northern shelf there is fairly narrow, and the depth drops off quickly north of the coast, so I estimate about -15 m.

