Latitude 87°N, longitude -75° lies in the central Arctic Ocean, on the Canadian side of the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs roughly from Ellesmere Island/Greenland across the pole toward the Siberian shelf. At 87°N, the ridge lies near longitudes of about 140°E to -50°W, so -75° is a bit off the ridge on the Amerasian side. That puts it in the Canada Basin/Makarov Basin region, with depths around 3,500-4,000 m.

Near the pole at 90°N the Amundsen Basin is about 4,200 m deep. The Lomonosov Ridge crest is about 1,000-1,500 m deep. At 87°N, -75° (which is 105° from 30°E, the Amundsen side), the point is on the Greenland/Canadian side of the ridge. The ridge near 87°N is at roughly 150°E-ish, and from there it goes to Greenland at about -60°W. Meridian -75° at 87°N is near the ridge's Canadian end, which is about 83°N at -60°W. The ridge crosses the pole at around 140°E to -40°W, so -75° may be on the Makarov side (the Canadian side). Makarov Basin depth is about 4,000 m, and the point could lie near the ridge flank, so I estimate about -2,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

3226.text:
The point 55.0°N, 153.0°E lies in the Sea of Okhotsk, east of Sakhalin and south of the Okhotsk coast. The Kuril Basin is to the south, and the basin near 55°N, 153°E is probably part of the deep central Okhotsk region.

The northern Sea of Okhotsk is mostly shelf, about 100-200 m deep. Near 55°N, 153°E the area is east of Sakhalin (143°E) and about 10° of longitude from Kamchatka's west coast (156°E). The Derugin Basin lies at about 52°N, 144°E, and the Kuril Basin is at about 47°N, 148°E. The TINRO Basin is on the Kamchatka side, near 54-55°N, 150-152°E, with depths of about 800-900 m. At 153°E the seafloor is likely on the slope toward Kamchatka's shelf, so I estimate a depth of roughly 700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

6293.text:
The point 21.0°N, 167.0°E lies in the western Pacific, northeast of Vanuatu and New Caledonia. That is far from the Marshall Islands, which are around 10°N, 167°E, and Wake Island is at 19.3°N, 166.6°E.

Wake Island is close, but it sits about 1.7° of latitude south of this point, so the point is open ocean in the Pacific plate region. The nearby seafloor includes the Wake seamounts and the Marcus-Wake seamount chain. The area around 21°N, 167°E is probably abyssal plain or lower seamount slopes, with depths of roughly 5000 to 5700 m. Wake Island sits on a guyot rising from about 5500 m. I'll estimate about -5600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

9377.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -145 is in the South Pacific, in the northwestern Tuamotu region. The Tuamotus lie roughly between 135°W and 149°W and 14°S to 23°S. Atolls near this point include Takaroa and Takapoto, at about 14.5°S and 145°W, and Apataki and Arutua, at about 15.3°S and 146.7°W. Takapoto is near 14.65°S, 145.2°W, and Takaroa is near 14.45°S, 145.0°W. The point at 15.0°S, 145.0°W is about 30 km south of these atolls, so it is probably open ocean. Tikei is near 14.95°S, 144.57°W, which is also not at this point.

The seafloor around the Tuamotus is deep, roughly 3000 to 4000 m, and the plateau region is around 3500 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

15752.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 5°E is in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. It lies in Queen Maud Land and the southern part of the plateau, at the edge of the Pole region. Ice surface elevations here are roughly 2,500-3,000 m.

South of the Recovery/Shackleton range area, around 85°S 0°E, the surface is about 2,700 m. The Pensacola Mountains are at about 50-60°W. Near the pole (2,835 m), moving along the 0° meridian, the elevation is about 2,800 m at 88°S. Dome Argus is at 80°S, 77°E. At 85°S 5°E the ice is likely about 2,900 m, given the ice flows towards the Filchner and Ronne ice shelves. I'll estimate 2,850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2850}

10793.text:
The point (-29.0, 167.0) is in the Pacific Ocean, between Norfolk Island (about -29.03, 167.95) and New Caledonia. Norfolk Island lies roughly 0.95° of longitude east, so this point is about 90 km west of it.

The Norfolk Ridge runs from New Zealand up to New Caledonia, and Norfolk Island sits on it. West of the island, the ridge slopes down into the New Caledonia Basin, which is about 3000 m deep. At 167.0°E we are probably still on the western flank of the ridge, with a depth of roughly 1500-2500 m. The ridge crest near Norfolk is shallow, with Phillip Island and surrounding banks at about 100-1000 m. The Norfolk Ridge is about 100-150 km wide, and the basin to the west is around 3000 m at about 166°E. I'll estimate -2000 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

7747.text:
The point is at 3°N, 165°W in the central Pacific. It lies south of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands, west of Kiritimati and Palmyra (about 5.9°N, 162°W). The nearest land is Kingman Reef and Palmyra, and neither is close, so this is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is part of the Pacific plate abyssal plain, typically 5000 to 5500 m deep. Just to the north there are the Line Islands ridges, but at 3°N, 165°W the depth is probably around 5300 m. This is an estimate from the regional setting, not a specific bathymetry lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

6529.text:
The point 17.0°N, 81.0°W is in the western Caribbean Sea, between Jamaica and the Cayman Islands to the northeast and Honduras and Nicaragua to the southwest. It lies south of the Cayman Trough and north of the Nicaraguan Rise.

The Nicaraguan Rise has shallow banks, but the point is probably in the Colombian Basin or the Cayman Trough area, not on those banks. Jamaica is at about 18°N, 77°W, and the Cayman Islands are at about 19.3°N, 81.3°W. The Cayman Trough runs east-west at roughly 18°N. Honduras' Gracias a Dios coast is at about 15–16°N, 83°W. At 17°N, 81°W the point is within the Honduras–Jamaica Nicaraguan Rise, whose depths are around 1000–3000 m. Pedro Bank is at 17°N, 77.5°W, and Rosalind Bank is at about 16.4°N, 80.5°W. The point is about 60 km from Rosalind Bank, so it could be on a slope. I estimate a depth of about 1500 m, though this is a rough guess, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

12903.text:
Latitude -53.0, longitude 67.0 lies in the southern Indian Ocean. Heard Island is at about 53.1S, 73.5E, and Kerguelen is at 49S, 69-70E. The point at 53S, 67E is southwest of Kerguelen, on the open ocean floor, probably west of the Kerguelen Plateau.

The Kerguelen Plateau's western edge runs around 68-69E at 50S. At 53S, the plateau's southern part lies between roughly 72E and 80E. At 67E, we are likely off the plateau in the Enderby/Crozet basin area. The depth there is around 3000-4000 m. However, the Kerguelen plateau's western flank near 53S, 68E has depths of about 2000-3000 m, so I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15885.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -89 is in Antarctica, on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet near the Thiel Mountains and Pensacola Mountains region. Longitude -89 (89°W) at 87°S lies in the interior, south of the Ellsworth Mountains and toward the Thiel Mountains (about 85°S, 90°W). At 87°S the surface is on the polar plateau, with an ice surface elevation of roughly 2,000 to 2,500 m. The Thiel Mountains lie at about 85.5°S, 91°W, and the South Pole is at 2,835 m. At 87°S, 89°W, the ice flows toward the Ronne and Filchner ice shelves, and the surface is probably around 2,200 m.

I estimate the surface at about 2,300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

2778.text:
The point 59°N, 23°W is in the North Atlantic, southwest of Iceland and southeast of Greenland. It lies in the Irminger Sea, near the western flank of the Reykjanes Ridge. The ridge axis at 59°N sits near 31°W, so I need to check which side of it this point is on.

The Reykjanes Ridge crest runs from about 63°N, 24°W down to about 57°N, 33°W. At 59°N the crest is near 30°W. That puts 23°W east of the ridge, in the Iceland Basin, where depths are around 2800 m. The Iceland Basin lies between the Reykjanes Ridge and the Rockall/Hatton area. At 59°N, 23°W the depth is probably about 2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

847.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 75.0°E lies in the Kara Sea region north of the Taymyr Peninsula. Franz Josef Land is at roughly 80-81°N, 45-65°E, and the Severnaya Zemlya archipelago is at roughly 78-81°N, 95-105°E. At 75°E and 81°N, the point is in the open Arctic Ocean, north of the Kara Sea, so it is water.

Depth there: the northern Kara Sea shelf edge lies near 81-82°N at this longitude. The area near 81°N, 75°E is probably the shelf break or upper slope, with depths of around 200-500 m. The St. Anna Trough is further west, near 70-80°E at 80-82°N, and runs north with depths over 500 m. At 75°E it could be on the eastern flank of the trough. I'll estimate about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

4982.text:
The point 35.0°N, 65.0°E is in northwestern Afghanistan. It lies in the Ghor/Badghis area, probably near the Ghor and Faryab border, so I take it to be land.

The Hari Rud valley is further north, around 34.5°N, 64°E. At 65°E and 35°N the terrain is mountainous, in the Firozkoh region (Chaghcharan is at about 34.5°N, 65.25°E, with an elevation of about 2,200 m). Terrain near 35°N, 65°E is probably around 2,300 m, so I estimate 2,300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

2503.text:
Latitude 63.0 N, longitude 147.0 W is in interior Alaska, in the Alaska Range, east of Denali. Longitude 147 W at 63 N is near the Denali Highway, south of Delta Junction, around the Gulkana/Susitna area. The Alaska Range there has high peaks, but the Denali Highway at 63.0 N sits near 146-148 W, with Maclaren Summit at about 4,000 ft, and the Maclaren River near 147 W.

The area is north of the Denali Highway and probably in the foothills of the Alaska Range, where elevations run about 1,000-1,500 m. Tangle Lakes is at 63.03 N, 146.0 W, at about 880 m. Maclaren Summit is at 63.1 N, 146.5 W. The Susitna River at 147.0 W is near 63.0 N, with the upper Susitna Glacier at about 63.3 N, 147.0 W. At 63.0 N, 147 W it is south of the Alaska Range crest, near the Denali Highway between Maclaren and Susitna. Elevation is likely around 900 m, perhaps 1,000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":950}

5136.text:
Latitude 33.0 N, longitude 13.0 E is in northwest Libya, south of the Mediterranean coast near Tripoli (32.9 N, 13.18 E). The point is inland from the coast and lies west-southwest of Tripoli, in the Jefara plain, around Azizia/Aziziyah (32.53 N, 13.02 E). At 33.0 N the point is at the coast, close to Zawiya (32.75 N, 12.73 E) and Janzur (32.83 N, 13.0 E). The coast at longitude 13.0 is about 32.85 N, so 33.0 N is about 17 km offshore in the Mediterranean.

The shelf off Tripoli is fairly shallow, and the depth at 17 km offshore is probably about 50 to 60 m. I'll estimate -55 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-55}

12513.text:
The point (-49, 7) lies in the South Atlantic, south of Africa and near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Bouvet Triple Junction region. Bouvet Island sits at about 54.4°S, 3.4°E, so it is not at this point. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (about 55°S, 1°W) northeast to the Indian Ocean. At 49°S, 7°E we are probably near the American-Antarctic Ridge or the Southwest Indian Ridge, or on the abyssal plain, and I'm not certain which.

The Islas Orcadas and Meteor Rise (Meteor Seamount) region is near 46°S, 7°E. The Meteor Rise is a plateau at roughly 47–52°S, 2–7°E with depths of about 2000–3000 m. At 49°S, 7°E this is likely on the Meteor Rise or its flank, so I estimate about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

8972.text:
Latitude -9.0, longitude 125.0 lies in the Timor region. Timor island spans roughly 123.5E to 127E, and its north coast near Dili sits around -8.55 latitude. At -9.0 and 125.0, the point is on the north coast of West Timor / Timor-Leste. The coast near Oecusse is at about -9.2, 124.1. At 125.0E, the north coast is around -8.45 near Manatuto/Dili, and the south coast is around -9.3 to -9.5 near Betano.

So -9.0, 125.0 is on land, inland in Timor-Leste. It is near Aileu/Same, with Ainaro at -9.0, 125.5 and Aileu at -8.7, 125.6. Mountains in the area, such as Mt. Tatamailau, are at -8.9, 125.5 at 2963 m. At 125.0E, -9.0 is near Cassa/Hatu-Builico... actually Cassa is at 125.4. Around 125.0 is the Same–Maubisse area. Elevations there are about 500-1000 m. I'll estimate 700.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

6236.text:
Latitude 21.0°N, longitude 53.0°E is in the Rub' al Khali (Empty Quarter), in southeastern Saudi Arabia near the UAE and Oman borders. It is desert, so the surface is solid.

The elevation there is roughly 100-150 m. The Rub' al Khali slopes from about 600 m in the southwest down to sea level in the northeast near Umm al Samim. At 21°N, 53°E, the terrain is at about 150 m. The Shaybah oilfield sits near 22.5°N, 53.7°E at about 180 m. Farther south at 21°N the dunes rise somewhat, so I estimate about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

14762.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -175 is in the Ross Sea, which is covered here by the Ross Ice Shelf or open water. The Ross Ice Shelf's front lies near 78°S, so at 75°S and 175°W the point is probably open Ross Sea, to the north of the ice front.

The Ross Sea continental shelf is about 500 m deep in many places. Near 175°W and 75°S the area is part of the Ross Sea's central and eastern basins, such as the Joides Basin and Glomar Challenger Basin, where depths are roughly 500-800 m. The Joides Trough is around 176°W to 180°. A reasonable seabed estimate is about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

15114.text:
Latitude -77.0, longitude 169.0 is in the Ross Sea region, near McMurdo Sound and Ross Island. Ross Island is at about 77.5°S, 167°E, so 169°E at 77°S lies east of McMurdo Sound, over the western Ross Sea and the Ross Ice Shelf edge.

The area east of Ross Island, around 77°S 169°E, is likely the Ross Ice Shelf, which is floating. The seabed there is roughly 500-600 m deep. Bathymetry in the McMurdo Sound/Ross Ice Shelf area near 77°S 169°E is probably about -550 m, so I estimate the seabed at around -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

11021.text:
The point (-33.0, -97.0) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and roughly 3,000 km from the coast. It lies on the Nazca Plate, east of the East Pacific Rise and north of the Chile Rise.

The seafloor age there is moderate, perhaps 30-40 Myr. That suggests a depth of about 3,800-4,000 m. The area west of the Juan Fernández region has abyssal depths around 3,800-4,000 m, and the Chile Basin sits nearer the coast. The East Pacific Rise at 33°S is near 111°W, so at 97°W the crust is about 14° of longitude off-axis. At a half-spreading rate of about 7 cm/yr, that is roughly 1,500 km, or about 20 Myr. The depth would then be about 2500 + 350·√20 ≈ 4,070 m. I estimate a depth of about 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

10710.text:
Latitude -29, longitude 1.0 (east) places the point in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of the African coast. The Namibian/South African coast at this latitude is around 16-17°E, so the point is far offshore.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is near 0° to -14° longitude at these latitudes. Around 29°S the ridge axis is at about 13°W, so 1°E lies east of the ridge on the African side, in the Cape Basin or Angola/Cape Basin region. Depths there are about 4500-5000 m. Near 29°S, 1°E, the Walvis Ridge is further north (~30°S at its southern end, near 3°E?). The Walvis Ridge runs from Namibia around 20°S to the Tristan area at 37°S, 10°W, so at 29°S the ridge sits at roughly 5°E. The point at 1°E is therefore likely just west of it, in the Cape Basin, at around 4800 m depth. I'll estimate -4900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

8570.text:
Latitude -5.0 and longitude 41.0 fall in the Indian Ocean, off the coast of Kenya and Somalia. The Kenyan coast near Lamu is at about -2.3 latitude and 40.9 longitude. Further south, the coast at -4.0 latitude is near Malindi/Mombasa, at about 39.5-40 longitude. At latitude -5, the Tanzanian coast near Tanga is at about 39.1 longitude. So 41.0 longitude lies roughly 200 km offshore.

Seabed depth there is probably 2000-3500 m. The area is east of the Pemba channel, and the Pemba and Zanzibar islands sit at about 39.7 longitude. Past the shelf, the slope drops toward the Somali basin and the Kenya basin. At 41°E and -5°, the depth is probably about 3000 m. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

6096.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude 133.0 is in the western North Pacific, in the Philippine Sea. It lies east of Taiwan and south of Japan, in the region of the Philippine Sea Plate. Nearby features include the Daito Ridge, at about 25°N 132°E, and the Amami Plateau, at about 28°N. The Daito Ridge and Oki-Daito Ridge area lies near 24-25°N, 131-134°E.

The Oki-Daito Ridge is centered around 24.5°N, 131°E to 133°E. The Oki-Daito Ridge is a seamount-like ridge at about 2000 m depth. At 23°N 133°E we are probably south of the ridge, in the Daito basin, which is the West Philippine Basin's northern portion at roughly 4800 to 5000 m deep. The Oki-Daito Ridge extends from about 25°N, 131°E down to about 24°N, 134°E, so 23°N may be just at the southern edge. I'll estimate a depth of about 4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1337.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 25.0 W is in northeast Greenland. Land there is roughly 73-76N between 18W and 25W, in the Kong Oscar Fjord / Kejser Franz Joseph Fjord area. Traill Island lies at about 72.5N, 24W, and Geographical Society Island at about 72.7N, 24.5W. Ymer Island is at about 73N, 25W.

At 75N, 25W, Kong Oscar Fjord is further south (about 72-73N), so this point is probably in the Greenland coastal area north of there. Clavering Island is at about 74.3N, 20W. The northern coast of Kejser Franz Joseph Fjord is at about 73.5N. At 75N, 25W, the region is probably Lambert Land or Hold with Hope... Hold with Hope is at 73.5N, 21W. Wollaston Foreland is at 74.5N, 19W. At 75N, 25W, this is probably the inland ice sheet margin, or Dronning Louise Land further north, at 76.5N, 24W. The Greenland coast at 75N is around 18-20W, so 25W is inland. The ice sheet at that point is likely high, but the Stauning Alps and Bregne Ice cap region are further south. This is probably the interior, at about 1000-1500 m. A guess of 1300 m seems reasonable, perhaps a bit lower given the proximity to the coast.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

9329.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 119 is in the Indian Ocean, off northwestern Australia. The Kimberley coast is around 126°E at -14°, and the coast near Broome is about 122°E at -18°. At 119°E, -13° is therefore well offshore, in the Timor Sea/Browse Basin region.

This area lies on the Australian continental shelf or slope. The Scott Plateau is near 120°E, -14°. The Browse Basin shelf edge is around -14°S, 121°E. At -13°, 119°E there is likely the Argo Abyssal Plain edge or the slope of the Scott Plateau, so depth could be about 2000 to 5000 m. The Argo Abyssal Plain is at about 5700 m depth and lies around -15°, 118°E. The Scott Plateau is shallower, at around 1000 to 2000 m. At -13°, 119°E I estimate the seabed is on the slope toward the abyssal plain, roughly 3000 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

2554.text:
Latitude 61.0, longitude -111.0 is in the Northwest Territories, Canada. Great Slave Lake spans roughly 109°W to 117°W, with its eastern arm near 61.5-62.5°N. The East Arm sits around 62.5°N, 110°W. At 61.0°N, 111°W, the lake's southern shore is near 61.2°N at that longitude. The lake's southern shore near Hay River is at about 60.8°N, 115.8°W. Toward the east, the southern shore runs roughly 61.1°N at 113°W, and near 111°W the main lake's southeastern portion extends to about 61.5°N. So 61.0°N is probably just south of the lake, on land in the Canadian Shield or the Slave Province border area.

The area around Taltson River and Fort Resolution lies near 61.2°N, 113.7°W. At 111°W, 61°N, there are many small lakes, such as Nonacho Lake at about 61.7°N, 109.5°W. This point is likely land at an elevation of about 250 m. Great Slave Lake's surface is at 156 m, and the land nearby is around 200-300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":260}

10911.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 43 lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa, roughly 500 km off the coast of South Africa (the coast near Durban is at about 31°E). The area is south of Madagascar, and it is probably on the Mozambique Basin or the Natal Valley edge.

The Natal Valley lies at about 30–34°S and 33–37°E. The Mozambique Basin is further east, at about 35–40°E. At 43°E and 31°S, the Mozambique Ridge is to the west, at about 34°E, so the point is east of it. The Madagascar Basin is farther east still, and the Southwest Indian Ridge is to the south. The point is probably in the abyssal plain at roughly 5000 m depth, so I'll estimate about -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

15251.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 83 is in East Antarctica, on the interior ice sheet. Dome A is at about 80.4°S, 77.1°E, with a surface elevation near 4090 m. Vostok is at 78.5°S, 106.8°E, at about 3500 m. The point at 79°S, 83°E is east of Dome A and roughly 300 km from it. Surface elevations in this region run about 3,500 to 3,900 m, so I estimate about 3,600 m. The Gamburtsev Mountains lie under this area, but the ice is thick, so the ice surface is still high.

Since this is grounded ice, the surface type is Solid and the elevation is the ice-surface elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3600}

8752.text:
Latitude -7, longitude 45 is in the western Indian Ocean, southeast of the Somali coast and east of Africa. The nearest land is far to the west, since the African coast near Somalia/Kenya lies around 40-41°E. The Seychelles are at about 55°E, 4.6°S, and the Chagos at 72°E.

At 7°S, 45°E, the seabed is probably in the Somali Basin or Mascarene Basin region. The Davie Ridge lies near 40-42°E, and the Somali Basin extends to the east of it. The Amirante Ridge is at about 53°E, and the Seychelles plateau at 55°E. The area near 45°E, 7°S lies in the Somali Basin's southern part (the Comoros Basin extends to about 12°S), so the depth is about 4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

5943.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude -173.0 is in the North Pacific, west-northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. Kure Atoll sits near 28.4N, -178.3, and Pearl and Hermes near 27.9N, -175.8, so this point is south of the Northwest Hawaiian Ridge, in open ocean on the Pacific plate.

Seafloor depth there is likely about 5,000 to 5,500 m. The area lies south of the Hawaiian chain, where the deep Pacific floor is usually around 5,200 m. I'll estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

10318.text:
The point -25.0, -63.0 lies in northern Argentina, in the Gran Chaco region near the border of Chaco, Santiago del Estero, and Salta provinces. Ground there is flat, at about 200 m elevation.

Further checks: at -25 latitude the Chaco province boundary is around -26, so the point is likely in Formosa or Salta, in the dry Chaco. Longitude -63 puts it in eastern Salta, near the Santiago del Estero border. Elevations in that area run from roughly 200 to 250 m, and I estimate about 215 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":215}

1539.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 19.0°E lies in the Barents Sea, south of Bjørnøya (Bear Island, at about 74.4°N, 19°E) and north of the Norwegian coast. This area is the Bjørnøya Trough region, or its southern part, and the water there is deep.

The Bjørnøyrenna trough runs from the Barents Sea shelf toward the shelf edge southwest of Bjørnøya. At 73°N, 19°E, I'd estimate the depth at about 400 m. The Bear Island Trough is roughly 300–500 m deep, and just south of Bjørnøya the seabed is around 300–400 m deep. I'll answer with -380 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-380}

3786.text:
The point 47°N, 167°W lies in the North Pacific, south of the Aleutian Islands and well off the Alaskan coast. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is most likely abyssal plain. The Aleutian Trench is farther north, and the Emperor Seamount chain is farther west. Depths in this part of the North Pacific are typically 5000 to 5500 m. I am estimating from regional knowledge, not from a bathymetry lookup, and I chose about -5400 m as the single value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

6602.text:
Latitude 17.0°N, longitude 65.0°E lies in the Arabian Sea, east of Oman and west of India. The area is the open Arabian Sea basin, in the Arabian Basin. The Carlsberg Ridge is further south and the Laccadive/Chagos region is southeast. At 17°N, 65°E the seabed depth is probably about 3,500 to 4,000 m. The Arabian Basin abyssal plain is around 3,000 to 4,000 m deep. The Indus Fan extends into this area, so the depth is a bit shallower, near 3,500 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

12800.text:
The point -53.0, -139.0 is in the South Pacific, about 53°S, 139°W. That is well south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region? The East Pacific Rise at that latitude lies near 120-140°W; at 53°S the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is around 135-140°W. So the point could be close to the ridge crest, where depths are about 2200-2800 m, with the surrounding seafloor around 3000-3500 m.

The ridge near 53°S is at roughly 138°W, so the point is near the axis. I estimate a depth of about 2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

1110.text:
Latitude 77.0N, longitude -119.0 lies in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Banks Island spans roughly 71-74.5N, and Melville Island is around 75-76.5N, 105-119W. At 77N, 119W, this is probably in the Prince Patrick Island area (about 76-77.5N, 115-124W), or just north of it. Prince Patrick Island's northern tip is about 77.0N, 118-119W, but the island is narrow, and its northeast coast near Cape Manning is around 76.9N, 117.5W. Western Prince Patrick sits near 120-124W. At 119W, 77N it is probably close to the northern end of the island, or in the channel just north of it, which is Arctic Ocean-side water. Prince Patrick is low, with elevations of 0-100 m.

Prince Patrick's northern coast near Cape Provost is at about 76.8N, 119W, and the island's tip, Cape Manning, is around 76.9N, 118W. So 77.0N, 119W is likely just offshore, in the water north of the island. The continental shelf there would be shallow, perhaps 200-300 m deep at the shelf edge. I'll guess water at about -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

7859.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 59.0°E is in the Indian Ocean, in the Arabian Sea/equatorial region. It lies roughly east of Somalia, southwest of the Maldives and Chagos, and near the Carlsberg Ridge region. The Carlsberg Ridge runs northwest-southeast from about 10°N, 57°E toward 0°, 67°E, so at 3°N, 59°E the point is probably close to the ridge's western flank or the Owen Fracture Zone region, and the seabed there is likely about 3500 to 4000 m deep.

The ridge axis is near 3°N, 63.5°E, so 59°E sits west of it. The Somali Basin/Arabian Basin depth there is around 4500 m, and the Argo Fracture Zone and the Madagascar Ridge are not close. I'll estimate about -4200 m.

