The Lomonosov Ridge runs from Ellesmere/Greenland (about -60°W) over the pole toward the East Siberian side (about 140°E). Longitude -81° at 87°N is on the Canadian side of the pole, near the ridge's Greenland end, where the ridge is at about 88°N, -60°W. On the Amundsen side, at lon -40° to -60°, the basin floor is about 4200 m deep. On the Makarov side (the Canada side of the ridge), depths are about 4000 m. The point at 87°N, -81° is likely in the Makarov Basin, which is about 4000 m deep. The ridge crest near 87-88°N lies at about -60° to -50°, so -81° is probably on the Makarov side, where I estimate a depth of about 3900 m.

