The Prince Patrick Island west coast is around -124°W at 76°N. Moving west to -133 is about 9° of longitude, or roughly 190 km at this latitude. That is likely the continental shelf edge or the slope. The shelf off Prince Patrick is narrow, and the shelf break sits about 100-150 km offshore. Around 77°N, 133°W the depth is probably about 1,000-2,500 m. Banks Island's northwest tip, at about 74.4°N and 125°W, has a shelf break near 75°N and 128°W. At 77°N and 133°W the position is likely on the slope or the Canada Basin edge, where depth could be about 2,000 m. I'll estimate -1900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

3105.text:
Latitude 55.0, longitude -89.0 is in northern Ontario, in the Hudson Bay Lowlands. Hudson Bay's southwestern shore (Cape Henrietta Maria) is near 55.0N, 82.3W, and James Bay's west coast lies around 82W. At 89W, 55N, the location is inland, near the Severn River and Sutton Ridges area. Lake Winisk is about 55.2N, 85W, and Fort Severn is at 87.6W, 56.0N. So 89W, 55N is inland, in the lowlands west of Winisk.

Elevation there is about 150 m. Lakes such as Big Trout Lake (53.8N, 90W) are at roughly 220 m, and Windigo Lake and Sachigo Lake are around 250 m. Along the Severn River near 55N, 89W the terrain is about 170 m. The coast at 56N is at 0 m, so I estimate about 150 m for this inland point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

2205.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -89.0 falls in Nunavut, Canada. The Melville Peninsula lies at about 68°N and Southampton Island at about 64°N, 84°W. Repulse Bay is at 66.5°N, 86°W, so 65°N, 89°W is west of Repulse Bay and south of it. The Wager Bay area is around 65.5°N, 89°W. The northwest of Hudson Bay is at about 63°N, 90°W, with Chesterfield Inlet at 63.3°N, 90.7°W. So 65°N, 89°W is inland on the Keewatin mainland, northwest of Wager Bay and near Ross Bay.

Wager Bay runs from about 65.3°N, 87°W to 90.5°W, with its western end at around 65.8°N, 90.5°W. So 65.0°N, 89°W would be just south of Wager Bay, on land. The terrain there is likely 200–300 m above sea level. I'll guess 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

7210.text:
This location is at 9°N, 159°W, in the central Pacific Ocean south of Hawaii. Johnston Atoll is at 16.7°N, 169.5°W and Palmyra is at 5.9°N, 162°W, so neither is near this point. That makes it open ocean.

The seafloor depth in this region, north of the Line Islands and east of the Marshall Islands, is typically around 5000 to 5500 m. The area sits on Pacific plate abyssal plain, which is usually about 5200 m deep. I estimate -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

14585.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -169 is in the Ross Sea, in the eastern part near the Ross Ice Shelf front. The Ross Ice Shelf edge lies around 78°S at this longitude, so -73 is open Ross Sea, over the continental shelf. The Ross Sea shelf is typically 500-700 m deep, and near -169 longitude, -73 latitude it is the Pacific-Antarctic slope region. The continental shelf break there is around -70 to -71 latitude, so -73 is on the shelf at about 500 m depth. Northern Ross Sea banks such as Iselin Bank lie near 176°W to 180°. At 169°W and 73°S the depth is likely about 500 m (Glomar Challenger Basin is further east and deeper, around 1000 m).

I'll estimate -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

4140.text:
The point is at 43°N, 179°W, in the North Pacific east of the Kuril-Kamchatka trench and south of the Aleutians. It sits on the abyssal Pacific plate, roughly 1,000 km east of the Emperor Seamount chain's bend and north of the Hawaiian-Emperor chain.

The Emperor Seamounts lie near 170°E, so they are well to the west. At 43°N, 179°W the seafloor is probably abyssal plain, with depths of about 5,500 to 5,600 m. The surrounding region has depths near 5,400 m. I'll estimate -5,450 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

8852.text:
The point at 9°S, 115°W lies in the open eastern Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water. It sits west of the East Pacific Rise, which is near 110°W at this latitude, on the Pacific plate flank. Seafloor depth there is about 3,700–4,100 m.

I estimated this from the general bathymetry of the region rather than from a lookup. The crust east of the rise at about 115°W is roughly 10–15 million years old, which fits depths near 4,000 m. I'll use about -3,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

8300.text:
The point is at 3°S, 139°W in the central Pacific. That is south of the Clipperton fracture zone and east of the Line Islands, in the open ocean north of the Marquesas. Seafloor depth in this region is typically about 4,300 to 4,500 m, so I am estimating from the typical depth for the area rather than from a specific sounding.

The seafloor here is Pacific plate crust, probably 50 to 60 million years old. The Marquesas fracture zone and the Galapagos-area ridges lie farther east. Crust of this age usually sits at about 4,400 to 4,900 m depth, so I take roughly 4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4450}

1536.text:
The point 73°N, 13°E lies in the Norwegian Sea / Barents Sea region, northwest of Norway and southwest of Bear Island (which sits near 74.4°N, 19°E). Just south of Bjørnøya is the Bjørnøyrenna trough, and the continental slope runs near 73°N around 10–15°E.

The Norwegian continental margin at this latitude is complicated. The Vøring Plateau lies near 67 to 68°N, and the Lofoten Basin is near 70–72°N with depths of about 3200 m. At 73°N, 13°E, the Knipovich Ridge and the Mohn Ridge are further north, near 73.5°N and 8°E. The Bear Island fan (Bjørnøya trough mouth fan) spans roughly 72–74°N and 5–15°E. The shelf break there is near 15°E, so 13°E is probably on the slope at a depth of about 2000 m. The Bear Island fan slope drops from about 500 m near 15°E to 2500 m near 8°E. I estimate 13°E at roughly 1800 m depth.


{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1900}

10595.text:
Latitude -27, longitude 131 falls in northern South Australia, near the Northern Territory border and close to the Mann and Musgrave Ranges. This is the Central Australian desert region, near Mount Woodroffe, which lies at about -26.3, 131.7, and Marla at -27.3, 133.6. The ground there is likely sandy desert plain at around 500 m. Lake Amadeus is further north at -24.8, and the Everard Ranges are at about -27.1, 132.5.

At 131.0 E, -27.0, I'm in the Victory Downs / Mintabie area, southwest of Mount Woodroffe, so I estimate about 480 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

1436.text:
Latitude 75.0 N, longitude 173.0 E lies in the East Siberian Sea / Chukchi Sea region. Wrangel Island sits around 71°N, 179°E, so this point is well north of the Siberian coast. The De Long Islands are near 76°N, 149°E, so it is far from them too. The point is in the open Arctic Ocean north of the East Siberian Sea shelf, near the shelf edge.

The East Siberian Sea shelf is shallow, around 50 m deep, and the shelf break lies at about 77–78°N in this sector. At 75°N, 173°E the water is probably on the shelf, with a depth around 100 m. Wrangel Island's northern shelf has depths of 50–100 m, and the slope begins farther north. I'll estimate -80 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-80}

15094.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 129 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. It lies inland of the coast, which sits around -66 to -67 latitude at this longitude. This is the interior plateau near Dome C / Vostok, with Vostok at 106.8E and -78.5. Dome C is at 123E and -75.1, elevation 3233 m. At 129E and -77, the ice surface is probably around 3,000 to 3,200 m. The Aurora basin is nearby, at about 115E and -71 to -75, and it has a low bedrock but a high ice surface. Ice-surface elevation at 129E, -77 is probably about 3,150 m.

This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid and I report the ice-surface elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3150}

2210.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -79.0 puts this on Baffin Island's southwest side, near Foxe Basin and Hudson Strait. Baffin Island's southern coast near Hudson Strait sits around 62-64°N, and Foxe Basin lies at about 65-70°N and 75-85°W. At 65°N, 79°W the point is probably in the Foxe Basin region, on the west side of Baffin Island, close to the Foxe Peninsula (Nettilling Lake area at 66.5°N, 70°W). The Foxe Peninsula (Prince Charles Island region) is around 65°N, 75°W, and the Southampton Island coast is at about 64°N, 84°W. So 65°N, 79°W is likely in Foxe Basin, which is shallow, roughly 50-100 m deep.

The Foxe Basin is bounded in the south by Foxe Channel. Its depth around 65°N, 79°W is about 100 m, though the area northwest of the Foxe Peninsula may be shallower. Foxe Basin's eastern shore is at about 68°N, 77°W, where the Baffin coast lies, while the Foxe Peninsula's west coast is near 79°W at 65°N. That makes this point plausibly water just off the coast, or alternatively on the lowland of the southwest Baffin coast. I'll go with water at around -60 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

7709.text:
Latitude 5.0 N, longitude 119.0 E lies in the Celebes Sea, southeast of Sabah's Semporna area and northwest of the Sulawesi/Sulu region. Sabah's east coast is near 118-119 E at about 4.5-5 N, but at 119.0 E and 5.0 N the point is east of Tawau and Semporna (Semporna is at about 118.6 E, 4.5 N). The Sibutu Passage and Sulu Archipelago are further north and east, at about 119.5 E and 5.0 N. The nearest land is Sabah's Dent Peninsula, which extends to roughly 119.2 E at 5.0 N, but that is Darvel Bay. Lahad Datu is at 118.3 E, 5.0 N, and the Dent Peninsula's tip is at about 119.3 E, 4.9 N. So 119.0 E, 5.0 N is likely the northern side of Darvel Bay's mouth, near the peninsula's north coast at Tungku (118.9 E, 5.0 N). That puts the point in the Sulawesi Sea or Darvel Bay, probably at 5.0 N on the water side. Water depth is probably around 100 m or less in the shelf area, though the Celebes Sea deepens to the east and south.

I'll estimate it as water, at about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

4685.text:
The point 37°N, 169°W lies in the North Pacific, roughly north of the Hawaiian Ridge and south of the Aleutians. That puts it on the abyssal plain of the northeast Pacific, west of the Emperor Seamounts' eastern region. The Hawaiian-Emperor chain bends near 30°N, 170°E, which is far to the west, so it is not nearby. The abyssal plain depth in this area is typically about 5,500 m.

This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a lookup of the exact seabed value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

5910.text:
The coordinates 25.0°N, 121.0°E fall in northern Taiwan, near Taoyuan, Hsinchu and Miaoli. Taipei is at about 121.5°E, so 121.0°E is west of it. At 25.0°N the Taoyuan area lies around 121.0–121.3°E, and the coast is at about 121.0°E. The Taoyuan coast around Dayuan and Luzhu sits at roughly 25.07°N, 121.2°E, with the coastline near 121.05°E at 25.05°N. So 25.0°N, 121.0°E is probably right at the coast, near Guanyin, where the shoreline is at about 121.08°E at 25.0°N. That puts the point just offshore, in the water.

The water there is shallow, so the seabed is likely only a few meters below sea level. I'll estimate -5 m, with some uncertainty about whether the point is land or sea.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5}

5212.text:
Latitude 33°N, longitude 165°E lies in the northwest Pacific, east of Japan and south of the Emperor Seamounts. It is near the Shatsky Rise to the north and the Mid-Pacific Mountains to the southeast. The region is largely abyssal plain at about 5500-6000 m depth. I could not pin down any specific local seamount, so I'm estimating from the regional depth.

The Mid-Pacific Mountains run roughly from 20°N, 170°E to 30°N, 180°. The area near 33°N, 165°E is in the Northwest Pacific Basin, with typical depths of about 5600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

3539.text:
The point 51.0°N, 59.0°E is in northwestern Kazakhstan, in the Aktobe region, near the Russian border and the southern Urals foothills. The area is Orenburg and Aktobe steppe. I'm estimating the elevation from memory of the region rather than from a lookup. Aktobe city (50.3°N, 57.2°E) sits at about 220 m. Further east, near Kandyagash and Khobda, the ground is around 250–300 m. At 59°E, 51°N the terrain is probably around 350 m, close to the Mugodzhar hills, which lie to the south-southeast.

I'll estimate about 330 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":330}

7834.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 9.0°E lies in the Gulf of Guinea, off the coast of Equatorial Guinea and Gabon. The Rio Muni coast is at about 9.8°E near 1-2°N, and Bioko is at 3.5°N, 8.7°E. The point at 3.0°N, 9.0°E is therefore south of Bioko and west of the Rio Muni coast (Campo is at 2.3°N, 9.8°E). That makes it ocean.

The continental shelf off Rio Muni is narrow. At 9.0°E, 3°N, the distance to the coast at about 9.8°E is roughly 90 km. Depth there is probably around 1000 m or more. Bioko sits on a shelf connected to Cameroon, with shallow water to its north and east. South of Bioko at 3.0°N the water deepens. The Rio Muni shelf edge is near 9.3°E, so 9.0°E is likely on the upper slope, at about -1000 m. I'll estimate -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

14959.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -141 is in the Ross Sea sector of Antarctica, at the eastern edge of the Ross Ice Shelf near the Marie Byrd Land coast. The Ross Ice Shelf front is at about -78 at this longitude, and its eastern edge lies near 155–160°W. Longitude -141 is in the Ross Ice Shelf region, around Roosevelt Island (-79.5, -162) and the Sulzberger Bay area (-77, -148). At -77, -141 the terrain is probably the Marie Byrd Land coast, near Cape Colbeck (-77.2, -158) and Sulzberger Ice Shelf (-77, -150). Further east, around -141, lie the Ruppert Coast and Hobbs Coast. The Ruppert Coast is at about -75.5 to -76 and -145. At -77, -141 this is probably inland, on the grounded ice of Marie Byrd Land, perhaps near the Ford Ranges or the Executive Committee Range. The Getz Ice Shelf is at about -74 and -125. At -77, -141 the surface is likely a grounded ice sheet or the Sulzberger/Ross ice shelf, though that is uncertain.

Looking at the coastline, the Saunders Coast runs from about -77 at -146 toward -76 at -140. This puts the point near the coast, perhaps on the grounded ice of the Saunders Coast, such as the Ford Ice Piedmont, at an elevation of several hundred meters. The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge is at about -152 to -155 at the coast, so -141 is east of it. I'll go with grounded ice at about 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

1318.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -63.0 is in northern Baffin Bay, west of Greenland, near Melville Bay. Greenland's west coast at 75N is around -58 to -60 W (the Upernavik area is around 72.8N, 56W). Near Thule at 76.5N the coast is at about -68.8W. At 75N, the coast lies around -58 to -60W, so -63 is in the water of Melville Bay, in northern Baffin Bay.

Melville Bay depths on the shelf are typically 300–600 m. Farther west, the central Baffin Bay basin is about 2000 m deep, but at -63 and 75N the point is probably on the shelf or slope. Baffin Island is far to the west (around -80), and the Greenland coast with the ice sheet edge sits near -60. So the seabed here is probably around -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

7514.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 89.0°E is in the central Bay of Bengal, southeast of Sri Lanka's eastern side and west of the Nicobar region. It is open ocean.

The Bay of Bengal's abyssal plain is covered by the Bengal Fan sediments. At this latitude, around 7°N 89°E, the depth is roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m. The 85°E Ridge lies further west, near 85°E, and the Ninetyeast Ridge is at 90°E. At 89°E we are just west of the Ninetyeast Ridge, which rises at around 90°E. The seafloor here is probably about 3,300 to 3,700 m deep, so I estimate about -3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

16111.text:
This location is at 89°S, near the South Pole, on the Antarctic ice sheet. The South Pole's ice surface is about 2835 m above sea level. At 89°S, 3°E, the surface is on the polar plateau at roughly 2800 m. The ice there is grounded, so the type is Solid.

The value is an estimate from the known pole elevation. Going from the pole toward 0° longitude at 89°S, the elevation is about 2900 m, since the surface rises slightly toward Dome A on the Greenwich-meridian side. I'll use 2840 m, which is close to the pole figure with a small adjustment.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2840}

10165.text:
Latitude -23, longitude -9 is in the South Atlantic, off the coast of Namibia, which sits at roughly 14°E. The point is far from land, west of the African coast and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which lies near 13°W at this latitude. That puts it in the Angola Basin / Cape Basin region, probably on the eastern flank of the ridge or in the Walvis Ridge area. The Walvis Ridge is around 20-30°S and runs from the Namibian coast toward the Tristan area. At -23° the ridge is not yet prominent. The ridge's main trend runs from about -20°, 13°E to -35°, 0°. At 9°W, -23° the point is probably on the ridge's western part or in the Brazil/Angola basin.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis at 23°S is near 13.5°W, and the ridge flanks at 9°W are about 450 km east of the axis. Crust there is roughly 10-15 million years old, which gives a depth of around 3500-4000 m. I estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

11826.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 73 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa and southwest of Australia. It lies north of the Amsterdam and St Paul islands area (which are near 38°S, 77°E) and west of the Kerguelen Plateau. The Southwest Indian Ridge and Southeast Indian Ridge meet near the Rodrigues triple junction at about 25°S, 70°E, which is well to the north, so that does not apply here. At 41°S, 73°E the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain or the flanks of the Amsterdam-St Paul plateau region. The plateau is at about 77°E, so 73°E is to its west. The depth there is likely around 3500-4500 m.

I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9959.text:
Latitude -21, longitude -61 falls in the Paraguayan Chaco, near the Bolivia-Paraguay-Argentina border region. Paraguay's Chaco at about 21°S, 61°W lies in the Alto Paraguay department, which is flat and low. Elevations there are around 150-200 m. The Pilcomayo is further south, near 25°S. The Bolivian border is near 62°W at 21°S, so this point is in Paraguay's Chaco.

Elevation estimate is about 180 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":180}

8650.text:
The point is at 7°S, 159°W, in the central Pacific south of the equator. The nearest land is the Line Islands (Starbuck is at about 5.6°S, 155.9°W), which is too far away to matter, so this is open ocean.

Seafloor depth in this region, between the Line Islands and the Manihiki Plateau, is typically about 5,000 to 5,500 m. It lies in the Central Pacific Basin, probably near 5,200 m. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so this is an estimate from the regional setting.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

9912.text:
The point at 21°S, 155°W is in the South Pacific, southwest of Tahiti and east of the Cook Islands. Rarotonga lies near 21.2°S, 159.8°W, so this point is about 5° of longitude east of it, well clear of the island. The area is open ocean in the central Pacific. The Austral/Cook island chain is nearby, but no island sits exactly at this point.

The seafloor in this region is typically about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The Society Islands hotspot region and its swell raise the floor somewhat, so I estimate roughly 4,700 m of depth. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a lookup of the exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

8504.text:
The point is at 5°S, 91°W in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 400 km southwest of the Galápagos. It lies south of the Galápagos platform, near the Galápagos Spreading Center and the Carnegie Ridge region. The Carnegie Ridge runs east from about 91°W near 1°S to 2°S, so 5°S is south of it.

Seafloor in this area is about 3000 m deep. The Galápagos Spreading Center is at about 2°N, and the East Pacific Rise is farther west. The crust south of the Galápagos at 5°S, 91°W is roughly 5–8 Myr old, so the depth is about 2900–3100 m. The Nazca plate seafloor near 5°S, 90°W is about 3000 m deep, and I'll go with about 3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

8324.text:
The point (-3.0, -91.0) is in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 250 km southwest of the Galápagos. The Galápagos lie at about 0 to -1.5° latitude and -89 to -91.8° longitude. Isabela's southwestern tip is near -1.0° latitude and -91.4° longitude, so -3.0° latitude is about 200 km south of it and is open water.

The Galápagos platform is a shallow area, but the seafloor south of it drops to roughly 2500–3000 m. The Galápagos Spreading Center lies near 2°N, and the Carnegie Ridge runs east of 90°W at about -1° to -2° latitude. At -3°, -91° the seafloor is on the Nazca plate south of the platform. I estimate a depth of about 2900 m, on the assumption that the crust there is about 6 to 8 million years old.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

11854.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 129 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Great Australian Bight. The Australian coast sits at about -31.7 at this longitude, so this point is roughly 1000 km offshore. The seafloor there is the Australian-Antarctic Basin's northern edge, south of the continental slope and the Eucla shelf. The shelf edge is around -33.5 and the abyssal plain lies at about -4000 to -5000 m. At -41 this is probably the abyssal plain or the flank of the Southeast Indian Ridge, which lies further south at about -48 to -50 at this longitude. So the depth is probably around -4800 m.

I'm estimating from general bathymetry, so I'll go with about -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

5564.text:
Latitude 29.0°N, longitude 149.0°E lies in the western Pacific Ocean, southeast of Japan. It sits in the Philippine Sea / West Mariana Basin region, east of the Kyushu-Palau Ridge and south of the Izu-Bonin area. Nearby seafloor is probably abyssal, at roughly 5,000–6,000 m depth.

The Kyushu-Palau Ridge runs near 136°E, and the Izu-Ogasawara Trench lies near 143°E. At 149°E, 29°N, the point is east of the Bonin Islands and the trench, and west of the Marcus-Wake seamount area. This is the northwestern Pacific plate, which is generally about 5,500–6,000 m deep. The Mariana Trench's northern extension is south of this point, so I estimate about -5,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

1087.text:
Latitude 77, longitude -165 lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Chukchi Sea and northwest of Alaska. The Chukchi Shelf extends to about 73-74°N, and the Chukchi Borderland/Plateau lies around 75-80°N, 160-180°W. At 77°N, 165°W, the point sits near the Chukchi Abyssal Plain or the edge of the Chukchi Plateau. The Chukchi Plateau is at roughly 77-80°N, 165-170°W, with depths of about 500-1000 m. The Northwind Ridge is near 76-77°N, 155-160°W. Since 165°W at 77°N is probably on the western flank of the Northwind Ridge or in the Northwind Basin, I estimate a depth of about 1500 m.

Considering the bathymetry further, the Chukchi Borderland has the Northwind Ridge at about 74-77°N, 155-160°W with depths of 300-500 m on its crest. West of it is the Northwind Abyssal Plain at roughly 2000 m or deeper, and the Chukchi Plateau lies around 165-170°W. At 77°N, 165°W, the depth is probably 1000-2000 m, so I'll go with about -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

11884.text:
The point is at 43°S, 171°W, in the South Pacific southeast of New Zealand. It lies east of the Chatham Rise and south of the Chatham Islands, which are near 44°S, 176.5°W. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is far to the south, so the point is in open ocean.

The Chatham Rise runs along about 44°S, but only to roughly 176°W, and it extends west toward New Zealand. At 171°W the seafloor is probably the deep Southwest Pacific Basin, about 4,000 to 5,000 m deep. The Louisville seamount chain is farther north and west, so it is not a factor here. I estimate a depth of around -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

7560.text:
The point 5°N, 179°W lies in the central Pacific, east of the Gilbert Islands and Phoenix Islands. Nearby land is Kanton in the Phoenix Islands at about 2.8°S, 171.7°W, and Baker Island at 0.2°N, 176.5°W. Both are far from this point, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region, around the Line and Phoenix Islands and north of the Phoenix group, is typically 5000 to 5500 m deep. I'm estimating from general knowledge of the area, not from a lookup, and I'm choosing about -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

5664.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude -11.0 falls in the western Sahara, in the Tindouf and Zemmour region. The coordinates sit east of the Moroccan-controlled area, near the Mauritania border region at roughly 27N, 11W. That is in Algeria or Western Sahara, near the Hamada du Dra'a area. Elevation here is about 400 m. Tindouf is at 27.67N, 8.1W with an elevation of about 440 m. At 11W the terrain is probably the Hamada, around 450 m.

The point is land, so the surface type is Solid, and I estimate 430 m from the surrounding terrain.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":430}

6831.text:
The point 15°N, 163°E is in the western Pacific Ocean, east of the Marshall Islands' western edge and southwest of the Marshall chain. It lies in the Pacific basin south of the Marshall Islands, which sit around 5–15°N and 165–172°E. Nearby are the Caroline Basin and the East Mariana Basin. The East Mariana Basin lies around 10–15°N, 153–165°E, with depths of roughly 5,500 to 5,800 m. The Pigafetta Basin is further north, near 15–20°N and 150–160°E, with depths of about 5,800 m.

At 15°N, 163°E the seafloor is likely abyssal plain at about 5,600 m depth. This is an estimate from the surrounding basin depths, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

12359.text:
The point is at 47°S, 59°E, in the southern Indian Ocean. The Crozet Islands lie around 46°S, 51–52°E, which is too far west. Kerguelen is at about 49°S, 69°E, which is too far east. So the point is open ocean in the basin between them, southeast of Crozet.

The Crozet Plateau spans roughly 44–47°S and 46–54°E, so 59°E is east of it. The Southwest Indian Ridge lies further north, near 38–48°S at around 50–60°E. Its axis moves southward toward the Indomed and Du Toit fracture zones, and the ridge sits at about 47°S near 55–60°E. The Crozet Basin is about 4,000–5,000 m deep, and the ridge crest is around 3,000 m deep. The Southwest Indian Ridge is near 40°S at 70°E, and near 47°S at 57°E, close to the Prince Edward fracture zone. At 59°E the ridge would sit around 47–48°S, so this point is probably on or near the ridge flank. I estimate a depth of about 3,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

6280.text:
The point 21.0°N, 141.0°E lies in the western Pacific, south of the Ogasawara (Bonin) Islands and west of the Mariana Trench. Iwo Jima is at about 24.8°N, 141.3°E, so this point is about 380 km south of it. The area is the Mariana arc region, near the Kita-Iwo Jima and Minami-Iwo Jima volcanic area. Minami-Iwo Jima is at about 24.2°N. Farther south, around 21°N, the seafloor sits on the Izu-Bonin-Mariana arc, where there are West Mariana Ridge features and the Mariana Trough.

At 141°E and 21°N, the position is likely on the Pacific plate side, east of the arc, in the Mariana forearc. The Nishinoshima-to-Iwo Jima chain runs along roughly 141°E, and the Volcano Islands end near 24°N. At 21°N, 141°E there is probably a forearc basin or slope with depths of about 3000–4500 m. I'll estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

13842.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 145 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and well north of the Antarctic coast, which lies near -66 to -67 at that longitude. Antarctica is not nearby.

This area is south of the Tasmania/Macquarie Ridge region. The Southeast Indian Ridge and the Australian-Antarctic Basin lie in this part of the ocean. The Australian-Antarctic Discordance is around 120-128E, so at 145E the seafloor is probably 3500-4500 m deep. The Southeast Indian Ridge passes south of Tasmania, near -55 at 145E, and then trends southeast toward the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. At -63, 145E the seafloor is therefore on the Antarctic plate side, with depths around 3800-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9993.text:
Latitude -21, longitude 7 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Namibia. The Namibian coast at -21° is near 14°E, so 7°E is about 700 km offshore. This area lies on the Namibian continental margin and abyssal plain, near the Walvis Ridge, which is around -20 to -26 latitude at the Namibian coast and runs northwest.

The Walvis Ridge runs from about 13°E, -26° to about 3°E, -20°. At 7°E, -21° the ridge may be close, but the ridge crest sits near -26° at 8°E and extends toward Tristan, so the area at -21°, 7°E is probably north of the ridge in the Angola Basin. The Angola Basin floor is around -5000 m. The Walvis Ridge's northern flank near 7°E is at about -22° to -23°, and the ridge crest there is about -2000 to -3000 m. At -21° the seabed is likely around -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

14405.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -169 is in the Ross Sea region, north of Cape Adare and northeast of the Ross Sea continental shelf. Cape Adare is at about 170°E, which is -190°, so -169° is about 21° east of it in longitude terms (that is, east of the Ross Sea in the Pacific sector). The Ross Sea spans roughly 160°E to 158°W. At -169° (191°E) and 71°S, we are in the central-to-eastern Ross Sea, which is open water over the continental shelf. The Ross Ice Shelf front is further south at about 78°S, so this point is open sea.

Ross Sea shelf depths are typically 500 to 700 m, and the outer shelf break is around 70°S. At 71°S the seabed is probably about -600 m, though the continental slope lies near 70-71°S at this longitude. Around 170°W, the shelf edge sits at about 73°S, so 71°S is in the Pacific deep ocean or on the slope. Cape Adare's slope is at about 70°S, but further east the shelf edge is at roughly 73°S near the Hallett and Iselin banks. At 71°S, 169°W, the seabed is likely continental slope or rise, perhaps -3000 m. The Ross Sea shelf edge near 180° is about 73-74°S, so at 71°S we are north of the edge, on the slope. I estimate about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

39.text:
Latitude 89.0°N is in the Arctic Ocean, about 111 km from the North Pole. The Lomonosov Ridge runs close to the pole, and the seabed depth along this part of the ridge is roughly 1000–1500 m. The pole itself is in the Amundsen Basin, at about 4,200 m depth.

The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near 88–90°N, with its axis near 140°E on the Siberian side and 50°W toward Greenland. At 89°N, 101°W, the point lies on the Canadian/Amerasian side of the pole. Along the 90°W–90°E meridian line, the Amundsen Basin sits on the Eurasian side (the 0–180°E side) and the Makarov Basin on the Amerasian side. The ridge passes through the pole region toward Ellesmere and Greenland, roughly along 50–60°W. At 101°W the point is on the Makarov Basin side, 111 km from the pole, with depth around 3,000–4,000 m. Ridge crest depths are ~1,000 m and the Makarov Basin is ~4,000 m deep. Since 101°W is about 40–50° of longitude away from the ridge axis, the distance at 89° latitude is roughly 111 × sin(45°) ≈ 80 km, so this is probably on the ridge flank. I estimate about −2,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2500}

7130.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude 41.0 falls in northeastern Ethiopia, in the Afar region. This area is near the Danakil Depression, which lies at about 14°N, 40°E. At 11°N, 41°E the point is closer to the Awash River region and the Afar lowlands near Gewane and Lake Abbe (about 11.15°N, 41.8°E). Elevation in this part of Afar is roughly 400–600 m.

Gewane is at about 10.17°N, 40.65°E and sits near 600 m. Moving north, the town of Semera (11.8°N, 41.0°E) is at about 400 m. Since 11.0°N is between the two, I estimate about 500 m, though the Afar plain here could be lower, around 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

1104.text:
Latitude 77.0, longitude -131.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, near Prince Patrick Island and Melville Island. Prince Patrick Island spans roughly 74.5–77.0°N and 115–124°W. Melville Island lies at about 74–77.5°N and 105–119°W. Longitude -131 is therefore west of Prince Patrick Island, in the Arctic Ocean near the Beaufort Sea margin. Borden Island and Brock Island sit near -121 to -115, so they are not at this point.

Land is not expected here, so the surface should be water. Banks Island's northern tip is at about 74.5°N, -125°W, and the Prince Patrick west coast is near -124°W, so -131 at 77N is open ocean. This is near the Canadian Arctic continental margin, where the shelf edge lies around 76–77°N in this region. West of Prince Patrick, the shelf is narrow and the slope descends into the Canada Basin. At 77N, -131, the seabed depth is probably about 500–1500 m. I'll estimate about -800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

14400.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -179 is in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica, on the continental shelf north of the Ross Ice Shelf front. The Ross Ice Shelf edge sits around 78°S, so at 71°S we are in the open Ross Sea, close to the Pennell Coast and Cape Adare area. Cape Adare is near 170°E and Cape Colbeck is near 158°W, so 179°W at 71°S is well offshore.

The Ross Sea shelf is generally 500 m deep. At 71°S, 179°W, the continental slope is probably to the north, near 72°S or beyond. The Iselin Bank lies around 170-180°W and 72-76°S, with depths of about 500 m. At 71°S we are likely on the outer shelf or upper slope, so the seabed is about 1500 m deep. The shelf break in this area is near 73°S at 180°, so 71°S is probably the slope, with depths around 2000-3000 m. Further north, near 68°S, the Pacific-Antarctic abyssal plain is about 3500 m deep. The Iselin Bank extends north to about 70°S, so a depth of about 1000 m is plausible. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

13856.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 173 is in the Southern Ocean, south of New Zealand and north of the Ross Sea. It lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Balleny region, but the Balleny Islands are around -66.9, 163. At -63, 173E the area is the Pacific-Antarctic basin, probably with seabed depth of about -3000 to -4000 m.

The Southeast Indian Ridge and Pacific-Antarctic Ridge are far to the north and west, and the Emerald Basin is around -60, 170E. So the seabed here is likely abyssal at about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

6974.text:
Latitude 13.0°N, longitude 89.0°E is in the Bay of Bengal, east of India and west of the Andaman Islands (about 92.5°E). It is in the open ocean, well away from land.

The Bay of Bengal seafloor at this position is probably about 3,000 to 3,500 m deep. It lies on the Bengal Fan, east of the Ninetyeast Ridge (about 90°E at this latitude), though close to it. The ridge crest is at about 90°E, so 89°E is just west of the ridge. At 13°N the area west of the ridge is the abyssal plain, around 3,000 to 3,300 m deep, so I estimate -3,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3100}

11464.text:
Latitude -37, longitude 69 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Amsterdam Island, which sits at about 77.5°E, 37.8°S. At 69°E the point is west of the Amsterdam–St Paul plateau, near the Southeast Indian Ridge and the Central Indian Ridge triple junction area (around 70°E, 25°S is the junction). Around 37°S, 69°E the seafloor is probably part of the Amsterdam-Saint Paul plateau's western region or the abyssal plain.

The Southeast Indian Ridge runs from the triple junction (25.5°S, 70°E) southeast toward Amsterdam Island (about 38°S, 78°E). At 69°E, 37°S the area lies southwest of the ridge, and the ridge at that latitude is near 77°E. That puts the point in the Crozet-Kerguelen region, roughly 3500–4000 m deep. The Southwest Indian basin is nearby, and the Del Cano Rise is farther west at about 45°S, 45°E. I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15528.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -83 is in West Antarctica, in the Ellsworth Land / Thwaites–Pine Island interior region. Longitude -83 at latitude -83 lies south of the Ellsworth Mountains (which sit near -85 longitude, around -79 latitude). At -83, -83 we are in the interior of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, east of the Ronne Ice Shelf, which spans roughly -60 to -80 longitude and ends near -83 latitude at its southern edge. At -83 longitude, the Ronne Ice Shelf is probably not reached. The Fowler Ice Stream and Institute Ice Stream are nearby. The area is grounded ice with a surface elevation of roughly 1000–1500 m.

I estimate the ice surface at about 1200 m and classify it as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

8457.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 175.0 is in the Pacific, in the Kiribati region. The Gilbert Islands lie around 1°N to 3°S, 173°E. Abemama is at 0.4°N and Tabiteuea at about -1.3°S, 174.8°E. Onotoa is at about -1.9°S, 175.5°E. Tamana is at -2.5°S, 175.97°E. Arorae is at -2.6°S, 176.8°E. Beru is at -1.3°S, 176°E. Nikunau is at -1.35°S, 176.4°E. So -3.0, 175.0 is probably open ocean, south of Onotoa and west of Tamana.

The seabed depth there is probably around 4,500 m. The Gilbert Ridge seamounts rise from the seafloor at about 4,000–5,000 m, so I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

3657.text:
The point 49.0N, 65.0W lies in the Gulf of St. Lawrence. The Gaspé Peninsula's north coast is at about 49.1N, 65.3W (Cap-des-Rosiers is near 48.85N, 64.2W). Gaspé's north shore runs from Cap-Chat (49.1N, 66.7W) to Forillon (48.9N, 64.4W). At 65.0W the coast sits around 49.0N, near Grande-Vallée (49.22N, 65.13W) and Petite-Vallée (49.2N, 65.0W). Rivière-au-Renard is at 49.0N, 64.4W, and Mont-Louis is at 49.23N, 65.73W.

So the coast at 65.0W is about 49.2N, which puts 49.0N at about 20 km inland. That makes it Solid, in the Chic-Chocs foothills, with elevation of several hundred meters. Inland of Petite-Vallée, the terrain is a plateau at roughly 400–500 m. I'll estimate 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

12303.text:
Latitude -47, longitude -53 is in the South Atlantic, southeast of Argentina and east of the Falkland Islands' latitude band (the Falklands are at about -51.7, -59). The point lies east of Patagonia, beyond the continental shelf, which extends to about -55 at this latitude. The Argentine Basin is at roughly -5000 m. The shelf break is near -57 to -58 here, so -53 is off the shelf on the slope or rise.

There is also the Zapiola Rise further north, around -45, -45. At -47, -53 the depth is probably about 5000 m, though it could be a bit shallower. I'll estimate -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

13379.text:
Latitude -59, longitude -61 lies in the Drake Passage, northeast of the South Shetland Islands and southeast of Cape Horn. The Shetlands sit at about -62 latitude, so this point is open ocean.

The Drake Passage is deep, mostly 3000-4500 m. At 59°S, 61°W the seafloor is probably in the Yaghan Basin region, which is about 3500-4000 m deep. The Shackleton Fracture Zone lies further east, near 57°W, so it does not apply here. I estimate about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

9742.text:
Latitude -19, longitude -135 lies in the South Pacific, in the French Polynesia region, southeast of the Tuamotu Archipelago. The Tuamotus extend to about 138°W at around 18-23°S. Nearby atolls include Marutea Sud (about -21.5, -135.6), Nengo Nengo, and Actaeon. At -19, -135 there is probably open ocean, since Tuamotu atolls near that longitude are mostly further southwest.

The seafloor there is part of the Pacific plate, with typical depths of 3500-4300 m. The area north of the Gambier-Tuamotu chain lies on seafloor roughly 50-60 Ma old, which gives a depth near 4000 m. The Tuamotu plateau is shallower, around 3000-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14489.text:
Location: 71°S, 1°W, in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The Greenwich meridian region near 70°S has the Fimbul Ice Shelf at around 70.5°S, 0-3°W. The ice shelf front is near 70.8°S at 0° (Jelbart/Fimbul ice shelf edge near 70.7°S). At 71°S, 1°W, it could still be on the Fimbul Ice Shelf, since the grounding line is about 71.5–72°S there. Fimbul Ice Shelf spans about 3°W to 3°E in the east, and it extends to roughly 71°S at 1°W. Maud Rise is far to the north.

The ice shelf is floating, so the answer is the seabed depth beneath it. The cavity under Fimbul is typically 200–600 m deep below sea level, with a continental shelf bathymetry around -400 to -500 m. I'll guess -450.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -450}

11659.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 99 is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies roughly west of the Perth/Naturaliste area and south of the Wharton Basin, and the Southeast Indian Ridge is much farther south, near 45–50°S at that longitude.

The nearby seafloor is the abyssal plain of the Wharton Basin or the southern flank of the Naturaliste Plateau region. The Naturaliste Plateau sits near 34°S, 112°E, which is well east of this point. At 39°S, 99°E the depth is probably about 4,000 to 5,000 m. I'm estimating this from general knowledge of the region rather than from a lookup, and I'll use -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

3947.text:
The point 47°N, 155°E lies in the northwest Pacific, east of the Kuril Islands. The Kuril Islands chain near 47°N runs around 150°E, and the Kuril-Kamchatka Trench lies to the southeast of it. At 47°N the trench axis is around 153–154°E, so 155°E sits just east of the trench axis, on the outer trench wall or the abyssal plain.

The trench depth is around 9,000–10,000 m near 44–47°N. At 47°N the axis is at roughly 154°E. At 155°E, about 80 km east of the axis, the seafloor would be near 5,500–6,000 m on the outer slope. The abyssal plain nearby is about 5,400 m deep. I'll estimate -5700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5700}

14977.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -105 lies in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. Pine Island Bay is near -74.5, -102, and the Thwaites area is near -75.5, -107. At -77, -105 this is inland of the Thwaites region, in the Marie Byrd Land / Amundsen embayment, probably on grounded ice, possibly near the Thwaites/Pine Island divide. The Hudson Mountains are at -74.5, -99. The Executive Committee Range is at about -76.8, -126.

The ice surface there is probably about 1200 m, and the bed is probably near or below sea level. The question asks for the ice-surface elevation if it is grounded. Thwaites's upper catchment at -77, -105 has a surface of roughly 1000-1500 m. I'll go with 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

4854.text:
Latitude 37°N, longitude 169°E is in the northwest Pacific, east of Japan and north of the Marshall Islands. It lies south of the Emperor Seamount chain and east of the Shatsky Rise, so it is open ocean.

The surrounding seafloor is the Pacific plate, with typical abyssal depths of about 5500–6000 m. The Mid-Pacific Mountains lie further south, at roughly 20–30°N, and the Hawaiian-Emperor bend is near 170°E at 35°N or so. The Emperor seamounts, such as Koko, sit near 35°N, 171°E. That puts 37°N, 169°E fairly close to the seamount area, but the abyssal plain there is about 5500 m deep. I'll estimate -5600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

7783.text:
The point (3.0°N, 93.0°W) lies in the eastern Pacific Ocean, south of the Cocos Island area (Cocos Island is at about 5.5°N, 87°W). It is near the Cocos Ridge and the Cocos Plate, west of the ridge, close to the Cocos–Nazca spreading center (the Galápagos Rift is at about 2°N). The seafloor there is roughly 2,500 to 3,000 m deep.

The Galápagos spreading center near 93°W lies at about 2°N, with axial depths of roughly 2,500 m. Seafloor at 3°N is slightly north of the axis, and the Cocos Plate crust is young there, so depths of about 2,600 to 3,000 m are expected. I estimate -2,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

14332.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 45 is in East Antarctica, in Enderby Land / Queen Maud Land near the coast. The Antarctic coast near 45°E lies at roughly -68° to -69°. Looking at it more closely, the coast around 45°E sits near 68°S, with the Riiser-Larsen Sea and Cosmonaut Sea further east. At 69°S the point is probably just inland on the ice sheet, at an ice-surface elevation of around 1500 m.

This is a rough estimate. The Wilkins–Wild area and the Shirase Glacier are not close, and the Fimbul Ice Shelf is at about 0°. The Lazarev Sea is at about 0–15°E. Near 45°E the ice shelves are small, and the coast runs about 66–67°S at 50°E and about 68°S at 40°E. That puts 69°S inland by roughly 100–200 km, where the ice surface is likely around 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

5587.text:
The point 27.0°N, 165.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. Kure Atoll is at about 28.4°N, 178.3°W and Pearl and Hermes at 27.8°N, 175.8°W, so neither is close. Gardner Pinnacles (25.0°N, 168.0°W) and Maro Reef (25.4°N, 170.6°W) are also not at this location. The nearest named feature I can think of is the seafloor near the Hawaiian-Emperor chain, but at 165°W the point is east of French Frigate Shoals (23.7°N, 166.2°W) and north of it by more than 3 degrees, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is typically about 4,500 to 5,500 m deep. The Hawaiian arch and the swell raise it a bit, and 27°N, 165°W is on the northern flank of the Hawaiian swell, so I estimate about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

12590.text:
The point (-49.0, 161.0) is in the Southern Ocean, southwest of New Zealand and south of Macquarie Island (about -54.6, 158.9). It sits near the Macquarie Ridge region, east of Tasmania and south of the Campbell Plateau, so it is open water.

The ocean there is roughly 3,000 to 4,500 m deep. The Macquarie Ridge runs from about 160°E at -50° toward Macquarie Island, with the ridge crest near 159–160°E. At 161°E, -49° it is probably just east of the ridge, in the basin on the Pacific side. I estimate a depth of about 4,000 m, though I am not certain, since the Emerald Basin to the west is about 4,000+ m deep and the ridge flank slopes off steeply.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4826.text:
The coordinates 37.0°N, 113.0°E fall in Shanxi province, China. The location is near Qinyuan or Pingyao and the Taiyue Mountains, on the Loess Plateau. Pingyao sits at about 37.2°N, 112.2°E, and Qinxian at about 36.75°N, 112.7°E. At 113.0°E, 37.0°N, the point lies east of the Taiyue range, near Yushe and Heshun. Yushe is at about 37.07°N, 112.98°E, with an elevation of roughly 1,100 m. The surrounding terrain is hilly, around 1,200 m.

I estimate 1,200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

1391.text:
Latitude 75.0N, longitude 83.0E is in the Kara Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya and north of the Taymyr Peninsula. The Taymyr coast lies near 75N at about 100E, and the Kara Sea islands (Sverdrup Island at 74.5N, 79E; Arkticheskiy Institut Islands at 75.5N, 82E) are close by. Sverdrup Island sits at 79E, and the Arkticheskiy Institut Islands are around 75.3N, 81.5E. The Izvestiy TSIK Islands are near 75.9N, 82.5E. So the point is likely in open water near those islands.

The Kara Sea shelf is shallow, with depths around 30-50 m in this area. I'll estimate about -40 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

4376.text:
The point 41.0°N, 67.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, southeast of Cape Cod and on the southern flank of Georges Bank. Georges Bank's shallow areas lie around 41.5°N, 67.5°W, at depths of roughly 40-60 m. The bank's southern edge drops toward the continental shelf break near 41°N, 66-67°W. The shelf break south of Georges Bank is at about 41°N, and the Northeast Channel lies near 42°N, 65.5°W.

Georges Bank spans roughly 40.5-42°N and 66-69.5°W. At 41.0°N, 67.0°W, the point sits within the bank, likely at a depth of about 60-80 m. The shelf break lies near 40.3°N at 67°W, so this location is still on the shelf. I estimate a depth of about 70 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-70}

9544.text:
Latitude -17, longitude -171 is in the South Pacific, north of Niue and east of Samoa. Samoa lies at about -14, -172 and Niue at -19, -169.9, so this point is in open ocean between them.

The seafloor there is part of the Pacific plate. The area south of Samoa and near the Tonga Trench's northern end has depths of roughly 4500-5000 m. I'm estimating from general bathymetry rather than a specific chart, and I'll take about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

189.text:
The point at 87°N, 161°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, on the Amerasian side of the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from about 80°E toward the Greenland side, passing near the pole. At 87°N it lies roughly along 140°E to 50°W, so longitude 161°W is on the Amerasian side of the ridge.

On that side, the Makarov Basin lies near 87°N, 170°W to 180°W, with depths of about 4000 m. The Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge sits at roughly 80-85°N. At 87°N, 161°W the point is probably in the Makarov Basin or the Amundsen Basin. The Lomonosov Ridge, at about 87-88°N near 140°E to 50°W, would be on the other side of the pole, so the point is most likely in the Makarov Basin. IBCAO shows depths of about 4000 m there. I'll estimate -3950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

14573.text:
Latitude -71.0, longitude 167.0 is in Antarctica, in northern Victoria Land. The point lies near Cape Adare and the Pennell Coast, and about 71°S 167°E is inland from the Northern Foothills, near the Admiralty Mountains.

The Admiralty Mountains span roughly 71-72°S, 166-170°E. Cape Adare is at 71.3°S, 170.2°E. The coast near 167°E sits at about 70.5°S, since the Pennell Coast runs from Cape Adare southeast toward Cape Williams. At 167°E, the coast near Cape Hallett is at 72.3°S, 170°E. The coast west of Cape Adare near 70.5°S, 166°E is the Oates Coast region. So 71°S, 167°E is likely inland on the Tucker/Admiralty plateau, possibly on the ice sheet or in the mountains. This puts it in the Dunlop/Admiralty Mountains area, with an elevation of about 2000 m, though it could be a glacier like the Rennick Glacier nearer 160-164°E. I'll go with Solid at about 2100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2100}

775.text:
Latitude 81.0, longitude -69.0 falls in the Nares Strait region, between Ellesmere Island and Greenland. Kennedy Channel is near 80.5°N, 66°W, and Hall Basin is near 81.5°N, 62°W. Lady Franklin Bay is at about 81.5°N, 64°W, which is on the Ellesmere side.

At -69°, 81°N is on Ellesmere Island, west of Lady Franklin Bay. Ellesmere's Hazen Plateau and the northern ice caps lie there. Judging from this, it is likely the land south of Lake Hazen (81.8°N, -71°W). The area near 81°N, 69°W is probably the Hazen Plateau or United States Range, with elevation around 600 to 1000 m. The area could also be glacial ice. I estimate about 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

8874.text:
Latitude -9.0, longitude -71.0 falls in the Peruvian Amazon, in the Ucayali region near the Brazil border. The area is lowland rainforest, in the Purus and Alto Juruá region. Elevation there is roughly 250 m.

Lowland terrain near -9, -71 sits around 300 m, in the Sierra del Divisor and Alto Purús area. I'll go with about 270 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":270}

8090.text:
The point (1°N, 161°E) lies in the western Pacific, northeast of Nauru (0.5°S, 166.9°E) and north of Kosrae (5.3°N, 163°E). I don't know of any island at this location, so it is open ocean.

The area sits in the Nauru Basin or on its edge. Nauru Basin depths are typically 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Ontong Java Plateau lies to the south at about 2°S, 160°E, with depths of 2,000 to 3,000 m. At 1°N, 161°E I am at the northern edge of that plateau, where depths may be around 3,500 to 4,500 m. Taking the middle of the likely range, I estimate about -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

3150.text:
Latitude 55.0°N, longitude 1.0°E lies in the North Sea, east of Newcastle, about 100 km off the English coast and roughly on the western edge of the Dogger Bank region (Dogger Bank is farther north-east at about 54.5–55°N, 2–3°E). At 1°E, 55°N the water depth is probably around 60–80 m, since the central North Sea there is about 60–70 m deep.

I am estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than looking up a source, so I'll use about -65 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-65}

10934.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 89 E is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, west of Australia. It lies near the Broken Ridge and Ninetyeast Ridge area. The Ninetyeast Ridge is at about 88-90°E and runs north-south, with its crest typically around 1,500-3,000 m depth. At 31°S the ridge's depth is roughly 2,000-3,000 m. Around 30°S the ridge is at about 2,500 m depth. Broken Ridge starts near 30°S but at 100°E, so it does not apply here.

The Ninetyeast Ridge is fairly narrow, about 100-200 km wide, so the point could be on the ridge or off it in the abyssal plain at about 5,000 m. I'm guessing it is on the ridge, with a depth near 3,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

13922.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -55 is in the northwestern Weddell Sea, east of the Antarctic Peninsula. The Antarctic Peninsula tip lies around 63°S, 57°W, and the east coast at 65°S is near longitude -60 to -62. At -55 we are about 5-7 degrees east of the coast, which is roughly 250 km. Near there are the remains of the Larsen A/B ice shelf areas, which are close to 65°S and -60. At -55 it is open Weddell Sea continental shelf and slope. Seabed depth there is likely about 1000-2000 m, possibly deeper.

The Weddell Sea western shelf is about 500 m deep, and the slope goes down to roughly 2000-3000 m. Using the IBCSO bathymetry, near 65°S, 55°W the depth is probably around 1500 m, though it could be deeper, so I estimate -1500 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -1500}

3040.text:
Latitude 57.0°N, longitude 141.0°E lies in the Sea of Okhotsk region. The Russian coast near Okhotsk is at about 59.4°N, 143°E. At 57°N, 141°E the coast runs near the Shantar region, with Ayan at about 56.45°N, 138.15°E. Further east of there is the Uda Bay area. The coastline near 141°E sits at about 54°N, since the Shantar Islands are at 55°N, 137-138°E.

The coast at 141°E: Okhotsk is at 143.2°E, 59.35°N. Ayan is at 138.2°E, 56.45°N. Interpolating between these, the coast at 141°E would be at about 58°N. So 57°N, 141°E is inland, roughly 100 km from the coast, in the Dzhugdzhur Mountains. The coastal ridge there is about 1000 m high, and inland valleys are lower, so I estimate about 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

4448.text:
Latitude 41.0 N, longitude 77.0 E is in Xinjiang, China, near the Kyrgyzstan border in the Tian Shan, north of Aksu/Wensu. The area lies around the Muzart river valley and the southern slopes of the Tian Shan. The Kyrgyz border sits at about 41N, 78E, and 77E at 41N is further east of Kokshaal-Too, in the Tomur area. Aksu city is at 41.17N, 80.26E, so this point is well west of Aksu.

The location is likely the Uchturpan (Wushi) area, which is at 79.2E. At 77E and 41N, the terrain is the southern Tian Shan foothills near the Kyrgyz border (Torugart is at 40.5N, 75.4E). Kashgar is at 39.5N, 76E. Around 41N, 77E there are mountains, possibly with Kokshaal ranges at about 3500 m. The Keping area is at 79.0E, and Atushi is at 39.7N, 76.2E. The area just north is Kyrgyzstan's At-Bashy range at 41.2N, 75.8E, with elevations of 3500 m. At 77E, 41N, I'd estimate about 3000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3300}

15230.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 41 lies in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land / Enderby Land. The Queen Maud Land interior is at roughly 79°S, 41°E. That is inland of the coast, which sits near 70°S, so the point is on the high plateau. Nearby features include the Shackleton Range at about 80°S, 25°W, which is far away, and the Orvin and Wohlthat mountains at about 72°S, 10°E, also far away. At 41°E, 79°S the ice surface is probably around 3,000 m. The Dome Fuji area at 77°S, 39°E is about 3,800 m. Moving south to 79°S, the surface is likely about 3,600 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3600}

3860.text:
The point 47.0°N, 19.0°W is in the North Atlantic, roughly 1,500 km west of Brittany and southwest of Iceland. It lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, whose axis at this latitude is near 29°W, so it sits in the western part of the West European Basin. Seafloor depth here is typically 4,000–5,000 m. The Azores–Biscay Rise is further east, near 20–25°W at about 43–45°N, so the point may be on a slightly shallower flank, but I am estimating from memory rather than a lookup.

Around 47°N, 19°W, I estimate the depth at about 4,500 m, which gives an elevation of about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

2207.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -85.0 is in northern Canada. Foxe Basin lies around 66-70N, 75-85W. Southampton Island is around 64-66N, 80-87W. Repulse Bay is at 66.5N, 86W. Roes Welcome Sound is at 65N, 87W. Southampton Island's southern end sits near 63.5N, with Coral Harbour at 64.1N, 83.2W. The Melvill Peninsula and Boothia lie farther north.

At 65N, 85W, the point is north of Southampton Island and west of Foxe Basin. Southampton Island's northern part extends up to about 66N at around 84W, while the Bell Peninsula is on the northeast. Its west coast at 65N is near 87W, so 85W is probably inland on the island. Southampton Island's interior is low, with a plain around 60 m. The Porsild Mountains are at 64.5N, 84W, reaching about 300 m, so I'll estimate roughly 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

1161.text:
Latitude 77.0, longitude -17.0 is in northeast Greenland, east of the ice sheet's main body. Hovgaard Island sits near 77.5°N, -18.8°W, and Lambert Land lies near 79.5°N. At 77°N, -17°W the region is offshore of Dove Bugt, to the north of Store Koldewey (76.4°N, -18.3°W). Store Koldewey spans about -19.2 to -18.0, so -17 is east of it. Dove Bugt is around 77°N, -20 to -18, and Île de France is around 76.8°N, -19°W.

At -17°W the point is on the continental shelf in the Greenland Sea, east of the coast. The shelf there is deep in places, since the troughs run roughly 200-400 m deep, so I'll estimate about -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

907.text:
Latitude 79°N, longitude 165°W is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Chukchi Sea. It lies north of the Chukchi Borderland and Northwind Ridge, in the Canada Basin or on the edge of the Chukchi Plateau.

The Chukchi Plateau spans roughly 75–78°N, 165–170°W, with depths of about 400–1000 m. The Northwind Ridge runs near 76–80°N at about 155–160°W. At 79°N, 165°W, the position is probably in the Canada Basin's western part or on the Chukchi Abyssal Plain, near the northern edge of the Chukchi Borderland. Depths there are about 2000–3800 m. The Canada Basin abyssal plain is around 3800 m deep, and this location is likely on the slope north of the plateau, so I estimate about 2400 m of depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

1871.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -37.0 is in southeast Greenland, inland of the Kangerlussuaq area. The coast near Kangerlussuaq is at about 68.5N, -32W. At -37 longitude, 69N, this is on the Greenland ice sheet, east of the main divide, which lies near -40 at that latitude. The ice surface there should be around 2400-2700 m.

Greenland ice sheet elevation at 69N, 37W: the summit is at 72.5N, 38.5W with 3200 m. Moving south to 69N, the ridge elevation is around 2600-2800 m. At 37W it is probably near the ridge, since the divide in the south-east sits at about 36-40W. I estimate 2550 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2550}

14893.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 87 is in East Antarctica, inland of the Davis/Mirny coast. Wilkes Land lies around 100°E, and the Vostok region is at 78°E, -78°S. At 87°E and -75°S, the ice sheet surface is probably about 2500-3000 m. Moving inland from the coast at around -66°S, the surface rises to roughly 2000 m at -70°S and about 2800 m at -75°S. Near 87°E the area is south of the Shackleton Range region, and Dome A is at 77°E, -80°S with an elevation of 4093 m. At -75°S, 87°E I estimate about 2900 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so I classify it as Solid and report the ice-surface elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

15902.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -55 is in the interior of Antarctica, on the East Antarctic plateau near the Pensacola/Foundation ice stream region. The South Pole is at about 2835 m. At 87°S along 55°W (the Weddell Sea side of the Pole), the surface is close to the Foundation Ice Stream and Academy Glacier area, with the ice surface at roughly 2000-2500 m. The Thiel Mountains lie near 85°S, 91°W, so they are not relevant here. At 87°S, 55°W the surface is probably around 1,000-2,000 m on the ice sheet, so I estimate about 1,900 m.

Along the 55°W meridian the surface drops from the Pole (2835 m) toward the Foundation Ice Stream, which is near 83°S, 58°W at about 1,000 m. At 87°S it should be around 2,000 m or a little higher, perhaps 2,200 m. I'll go with 2,100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2100}

14321.text:
The point is at 69°S, 23°E in East Antarctica, in Queen Maud Land. The coast there lies near 69-70°S around 23°E, which is close to the Lützow-Holm Bay region at about 35°E, though the coast near 23°E is further south. Around 23°E the coastline sits at roughly 70°S, with the Riiser-Larsen Sea to the north. So 69°S is probably in the ocean. The Princess Astrid Coast spans about 5-20°E, and the Princess Ragnhild Coast about 20-30°E, with ice shelves at roughly 69.5-70°S. At 23°E the coast is likely near 69.5°S, so 69°S would probably be sea or floating ice rather than grounded ice.

The Riiser-Larsen Sea continental shelf is narrow, and the seabed there is likely about 1000-2000 m deep. The Sør Rondane mountains are at about 72°S, 25°E. At 69°S, 23°E, I believe this is open ocean near the continental slope, and I'll estimate the depth at about -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

13708.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -123 is in the South Pacific, in the Pacific-Antarctic region. It lies north of the Antarctic continental margin, in open ocean. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs near 65°S, 135°W to 55°S, 140°W, and further southwest. At -63, -123 the seafloor is likely on the ridge's eastern flank or in the Antarctic basin, probably around 3000-4000 m deep.

The ridge axis near 63°S is around 169°W, so -123 is far east of it. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge trends northeast toward the East Pacific Rise at about 55°S, 140°W. At -123, -63 we are in the Amundsen-Bellingshausen basin region, with depths of about 4500 m. The Udintsev fracture zone is near 56°S, 145°W, which is further away. I estimate a depth of about -4400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

14348.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 77 is in East Antarctica, near Prydz Bay and the Amery Ice Shelf region. Prydz Bay spans about 70-78°E, and its coast is near -68.5 to -69.5. The Larsemann Hills lie at about -69.4, 76.2, and the Vestfold Hills at -68.5, 78.

At -69.0, 77.0, the point is probably just offshore or at the coast. Vestfold Hills sits at about 78.2E, and the Sørsdal Glacier is around 79E. Between Larsemann Hills and Vestfold at 77E, -69, there is the Ingrid Christensen Coast. Davis Station is at -68.58, 77.97. Larsemann Hills is at -69.4, 76.2. So -69.0, 77.0 is probably in the area of the Dålk Glacier / Sørsdal, near the coast. The coast at 77E is around -69.2, which would put this point in the ocean in Prydz Bay, whose depth is ~500 m near the coast. The Dålk Glacier tongue is near 76.4E, -69.4. Point -69.0 is probably open water or sea ice, with a seabed of about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

10605.text:
Latitude -27.0, longitude 151.0 is in southern Queensland, Australia, near the Darling Downs west of Kingaroy. It lies roughly between Dalby and Chinchilla, a bit south and east of Chinchilla. Chinchilla is at about -26.74, 150.63, and Dalby at -27.18, 151.26. The elevation there is about 340 m.

Dalby is about 344 m. Bell is around 350 m. The point is south of Jimbour (-26.95, 151.2), where elevation is about 350 m. Nearby Jandowae (-26.78, 151.1) is about 340 m. At -27.0, 151.0 the terrain is likely about 330 m, so I'll use that.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":330}

269.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -1° lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the Greenland/Ellesmere side through near the pole to the Siberian shelf. Near 87°N, the ridge sits around 90°E–direction on the pole's far side. At the pole the ridge is at about 1000-1500 m depth. At 87°N, 1°W, the position is on the Greenland side of the pole (longitude 0 is toward Greenland/Fram Strait). The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole along roughly 140°E to 40°W, so the 0° meridian is not on the ridge. At 87°N, 0°, the point is in the Amundsen Basin, which has depths of about 4200 m.

The Amundsen Basin depth is around 4200–4400 m. I estimate -4300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

15076.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 93 lies in East Antarctica, inland of the Shackleton Ice Shelf and Queen Mary Land coast. The coast there is near -66 to -67, so -77 is about 1,100 km inland. That puts it on the high plateau, near Dome A's flank on the side toward Vostok. Dome A is around -80.4, 77.4. Vostok is at -78.5, 106.8, with a surface of about 3,488 m. At -77, 93 the ice surface is roughly 3,000 m. The ice there is probably about 3,000 to 3,300 m above sea level.

I'll estimate 3,100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3100}

9323.text:
The point (-13.0, 107.0) is in the Indian Ocean, south of Java and southwest of Christmas Island (which sits near -10.5, 105.7). It lies in the Christmas Island / Argo Abyssal Plain region south of the Java Trench. The Java Trench runs along about -10 to -11 near 108-110°E, and the abyssal plain south of it is typically 5,000-5,800 m deep.

At -13°, 107°E the point is roughly 250 km south of the trench axis. The abyssal plain there is probably around 5,500 m deep, so I estimate -5,450 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

14700.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 61 is in East Antarctica, in the Prince Charles Mountains region. The Lambert Glacier and Amery Ice Shelf are near 70-73°S and 67-70°E. At 61°E and 73°S, the location is inland of Mawson Coast, in the Mac. Robertson Land area. The Prince Charles Mountains sit at about 72-73°S, 60-67°E, so this point is probably on the ice sheet near the Mount Menzies or Fisher Glacier region. The ice-surface elevation there is probably around 2000 m.

Mount Menzies is at 73.5°S, 61.5°E, with an elevation of about 3355 m, and the surrounding ice plateau is about 2000 m. At 73°S, 61°E, the surface is probably around 1800-2200 m, so I'll estimate 2000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

7061.text:
The point (11°N, 97°W) lies in the eastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 700 km southwest of the Gulf of Tehuantepec. The East Pacific Rise is farther west, near 105°W at this latitude. The seafloor at 97°W is on the Cocos Plate, and the Cocos Plate crust there is about 10–15 Myr old, so the depth should be roughly 3,500–3,700 m. The Tehuantepec Ridge lies to the south-east, and the Middle America Trench is to the northeast. I'm estimating the depth from the crust's age rather than from a chart.

Estimated seabed depth is about 3,600 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

7206.text:
The point 9°N, 167°W is in the central Pacific, south of Hawaii and east of the Marshall Islands, so it is open ocean. The nearest land is Johnston Atoll at about 16.7°N, 169.5°W, and Palmyra at about 5.9°N, 162°W, so neither is close.

The seafloor here is probably abyssal plain at roughly 5000 to 5500 m depth. The Pacific plate at this location is Cretaceous-age crust, which usually sits around 5400 m deep. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so I chose -5300 m as an estimate within that range.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

12888.text:
Latitude -53, longitude 37 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa. Prince Edward Islands are near 46.9S, 37.7E, so this point is about 6 degrees south of them. It sits in the region of the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Crozet Basin, near the Del Cano Rise and the Conrad Rise.

The Southwest Indian Ridge runs roughly from 40S 40E down to about 55S 0E. At 37E the ridge sits near 50-52S, and the Conrad Rise is further east at about 50S 50E. The Enderby Basin lies to the south, and the Africa-Antarctic basin has depths of about 4000-5000 m. At 53S 37E the point is probably on the Antarctic plate just south of the ridge, with a depth of around 4000 m. I'll estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

10477.text:
The point (-27, -105) is in the southeast Pacific, west of Easter Island (which sits at about -27.1, -109.4). It is east of Easter Island, on the Easter Microplate and Salas y Gómez Ridge region. The East Pacific Rise lies near -113 at this latitude, so -105 is on the eastern flank, close to the Nazca plate. Seafloor depth there is likely around 3,000–3,500 m.

The Salas y Gómez Ridge runs east from Easter Island at about -26.5 to -27, reaching Salas y Gómez island at -26.5, -105.4. That puts this point near the island, at -27, -105, which is on or near the ridge. Seamounts along the ridge are shallow in places, but the ridge is narrow, so the point could be off it. I'll estimate a depth of about -2,500 m, between the ridge crest and the surrounding seafloor.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2600}

6411.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 43.0°E lies in southwestern Saudi Arabia, near the Asir mountains. It is north of Abha (18.2°N, 42.5°E) and near Al Namas and Tanomah (19.1°N, 42.1°E). The location is east of the escarpment, so I need to estimate where it falls on the plateau.

The escarpment crest at 19°N is at about 42.3°E, and the plateau slopes down eastward. At 43.0°E the elevation is roughly 1,500 m. Bisha is at 19.98°N, 42.6°E at about 1,000 m. Khamis Mushait is at 18.3°N, 42.7°E at about 2,000 m. Wadi Tathlith lies at around 19.5°N, 43.5°E at about 1,000 m. For a point at 19°N, 43°E, an estimate of about 1,300 m seems reasonable.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1350}

826.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 33.0°E lies in the Svalbard region, east of Nordaustlandet. Nordaustlandet spans roughly 14–28°E, and Kvitøya is at about 80.1°N, 32.5°E. At 81°N, 33°E, we are north of Kvitøya, in the northern Barents Sea / Nansen basin margin. Franz Josef Land lies to the east at about 45–65°E, so this point is open sea.

The bathymetry there is the northern Barents Sea continental slope or shelf edge. Around 81°N, 33°E the depth is probably about 300–500 m. The shelf north of Kvitøya is shallow, but the slope begins near 81.5°N. The Svalbard–Franz Josef Land trough, St. Anna Trough, is farther east. I'll estimate roughly -350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

355.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 171°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands across the pole toward Greenland. At 87°N the ridge is closer to 140–150°E on the Siberian side and about 50°W on the other side. At 171°E the position is probably on the Amerasian side of the ridge, over the Makarov Basin. The Makarov Basin floor is around 4000 m deep, and the Lomonosov Ridge crest is about 1000–1500 m deep.

Near the pole, the ridge crosses at roughly 90°E to 140°W, so the ridge axis near 87°N sits around 135–150°E. At 171°E the position is about 3° of latitude from the pole, so the longitude offset translates to a modest distance. The Makarov Basin lies on the Siberian-Canadian side of the ridge, between the Lomonosov and Alpha-Mendeleev ridges. The depth there is about 4000 m. The Alpha-Mendeleev ridge is near 180° at 85°N, with depths of 1500–2500 m. At 87°N and 171°E the position is probably in the Makarov Basin, with a depth of about 3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

10996.text:
The point is at 33°S, 147°W in the South Pacific, which puts it in open ocean. It lies west of the East Pacific Rise and east of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. The seafloor in that region is typically about 3,500 to 4,500 m deep, and this area is in the southeast Pacific basin on fairly old crust.

For comparison, the East Pacific Rise near 33°S sits at about 110°W. The Easter microplate region is around 26°S, 110°W. At 147°W and 33°S the crust is roughly 40 to 50 Myr old, which gives a depth of about 4,000 to 4,500 m. I'll estimate -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

2819.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 59.0°E falls in Russia, in the northern Ural region of Perm Krai or Sverdlovsk Oblast. The Urals lie roughly between 58°E and 60°E at this latitude. The Ural ridge near 59°N is at about 59.5°E, so 59°E sits on the western foothills. Typical elevation there is about 300 m.

Perm Krai's eastern part near Gornozavodsk is at 58.4°N, 58.3°E. Near 59°N, 59°E the point is in the Ivdel/Krasnovishersk area, close to Vizhai at about 59.2°N, 58.8°E. Terrain there is hilly, roughly 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":320}

1807.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -165.0 lies in the Chukchi Sea. Point Hope is at about 68.35N, 166.7W, and Cape Lisburne at about 68.87N, 166.2W. The coast near Wainwright is at about 70.6N, 160W. At longitude -165, the coast near Point Lay is at about 69.7N, 163W, so 69.0N, 165W is offshore in the Chukchi Sea.

The Chukchi shelf is shallow there, roughly 40 m deep, so I estimate the seabed at about -42 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-42}

7078.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude -63.0 is in the Caribbean Sea/Atlantic, just north of Margarita Island (about 11.0N, 64.0W) and east of it. Margarita sits near 64.0W, so 63.0W at 11.0N is to the east. Los Testigos islands are at about 11.4N, 63.1W, which is near but not at this point. Isla La Blanquilla is at 11.8N, 64.6W, so it is not relevant.

The point is on the Venezuelan continental shelf, north of the Araya/Paria peninsula (about 10.6N). The shelf there is shallow, roughly 50-100 m near Los Testigos. At 11.0N the Margarita-Tobago shelf edge is nearby. South of Grenada, the Tobago basin lies to the east. I estimate the depth at about 100 m, perhaps somewhat more.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

15009.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -41 lies in the southern Weddell Sea, in the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf region. The Filchner Ice Shelf spans roughly 30–45°W at around 78–80°S, while Ronne is further west. At -77, -41 the point is probably at the northern edge of the Filchner Trough, near the front of the Filchner Ice Shelf, which lies near 77–78°S. Another possibility is Berkner Island, but that is at about 45–50°W, so it does not apply here.

The Filchner Trough is deep, about 600–1200 m, and its seabed lies below the ice shelf. At -77, -41 the point is likely on the Filchner Ice Shelf front or just north of it, over the trough. The Filchner Trough runs along about 36–41°W, with depths of about 500 m on the shelf, so I estimate the seabed at about -700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

3637.text:
The point 49.0°N, 105.0°W is in southern Saskatchewan, on the Canada–US border near the Big Muddy Valley and Willow Bunch area. It is on land. The elevation there is around 800 m.

Nearby reference points: Willow Bunch Lake is at about 49.4°N, 105.6°W and sits near 790 m. Coronach is at about 49.1°N, 105.5°W and is roughly 800 m. Scobey, Montana, is farther west and is around 760 m. The point also falls near Wood Mountain Plateau to the east, which is higher at 900 m or more. At 105°W on the border, the elevation is likely about 850 m, since Wood Mountain Plateau starts around 105.5°W to 106°W and the ground is somewhat elevated there. I estimate 830 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":830}

13651.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 123 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and north of Antarctica. It lies south of the Southeast Indian Ridge, which runs near 50°S at this longitude, and in the Australian-Antarctic Basin, where depths are typically around 4000-5000 m.

Near 61°S, 123°E the seabed is probably the abyssal plain, which I estimate at about 4300 m depth. The continental rise begins further south, around 64°S.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

13004.text:
The point (-55, -91) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile and south of the Chile Rise area. The Chile Rise and the Chile Trench lie further east, near 75°W. At 91°W and 55°S, the seafloor sits on the Nazca/Antarctic plate region, and the Chile Rise (the Nazca–Antarctic spreading ridge) runs through roughly 55-60°S near 90°W. Depths there would be about 3000-3500 m on the ridge flanks. The Chile Rise intersects the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge near 56°S, 91°W (the Pacific-Antarctic, Nazca, and Antarctic triple junction is around 56°S, 90°W), so the seafloor is probably fairly shallow, around -3000 m to -3500 m.

I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

14388.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 157 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. It lies inland of the coast, which is near 66.5°S at this longitude. Wilkes Land at 157°E is approximately between Adélie Land and Oates Land, and the point sits on the ice sheet. Ice surface elevation in this region at 69°S is roughly 2000-2500 m. The Aurora Subglacial Basin is farther west, around 110-120°E. At 157°E, 69°S, the point is near the upper reaches of the Mertz and Ninnis glaciers' catchments, with a surface around 2200 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so I classify it as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

6102.text:
Latitude 23°N, longitude 145°E is in the western Pacific, east of the Mariana Islands and north of Guam. The area is in the Pacific plate's Mariana region, around the West Mariana Ridge or the Mariana Trench's outer area, and I did not look up exact bathymetry.

Pinning this down: the Mariana Trench axis sits around 143-147°E at latitudes 11-20°N. At 23°N the Mariana arc continues north with volcanic islands such as Farallon de Pajaros at 20.5°N, 144.9°E, Maug at 20°N, and Asuncion at 19.7°N. Further north is Iwo Jima at 24.8°N, 141.3°E. At 23°N, 145°E we are east of the arc, in the Pacific plate. There the seafloor is the old Cretaceous Pacific plate near the Magellan Seamounts region, with depths of about 5000-6000 m. The Mariana Trench at this latitude lies around 143.5°E, so 145°E would be a bit east of it, on the outer rise or abyssal plain. I estimate a depth of about -5700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5700}

464.text:
Latitude 85°N, longitude 29°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Franz Josef Land and Svalbard. That puts it in the Eurasian Basin, near the Nansen Basin and Gakkel Ridge region.

The Gakkel Ridge runs from about 85°N, 0°E toward the Laptev Sea, passing roughly 85°N at 60°E. At 29°E the ridge is probably near 85–86°N. The Nansen Basin floor is about 4000 m deep, and the Gakkel Ridge axis is about 3500–5000 m deep. My estimate for this point is about -3900 m. This comes from my recollection of the regional bathymetry, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2380.text:
The point 63.0°N, 99.0°W lies in Nunavut, Canada, west of Hudson Bay. Rankin Inlet is at about 62.8°N, 92.1°W, so this is roughly 350 km further west and inland. It sits in the Keewatin barrenlands near Kasba Lake and Ennadai Lake, and the Thelon River is farther west.

Dubawnt Lake is at about 63°N, 102°W, so it is not exactly at this point. The Kazan River runs near 63.5°N, 96°W. Baker Lake is at 64°N, 96°W. This longitude of 99°W is near Yathkyed Lake (62.7°N, 98°W), and it is also close to Angikuni Lake (62.2°N, 100°W). Yathkyed spans roughly 98°W, with the Kazan flowing through it. Parts of the area could be a lake, but I can't pin that down, so I'll treat it as land. Terrain there is about 100–200 m, and Yathkyed Lake is at roughly 135 m. I'll estimate 170 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

9373.text:
The point is at 15°S, 153°W, in the central South Pacific. The Society Islands lie to the west-northwest (Tahiti is near 17.6°S, 149.5°W) and the Tuamotus to the east. Takume and Raroia are near 16°S, 142°W, which is farther east. Scilly (Manuia) is near 16.5°S, 154.7°W and Bellingshausen is near 15.8°S, 154.5°W, so the nearest land is about 150 km away. This point is therefore open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is roughly 4,000 to 4,500 m deep. The region northeast of the Society Islands near 153°W is on Pacific plate crust about 70 to 80 Ma old, so the depth is probably around 4,500 m. I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

13406.text:
Location: 59°S, 7°W, in the South Atlantic sector of the Southern Ocean. It lies southwest of Bouvet Island (54.4°S, 3.4°E) and north of the Weddell Sea basin. The area is near the southern end of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which meets the American-Antarctic Ridge and the South Sandwich plate region, around 0°W to 10°W at 55-60°S.

The Atlantic-Indian basin lies near 60°S. The Weddell Sea abyssal plain is around -5000 m. At 59°S, 7°W, I'm probably in the Weddell basin, or the Atlantic-Indian Basin, at roughly -4500 to -5000 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge starts east of Bouvet, so the ridge itself is not here. The Maud Rise sits at 65°S, 3°E, which is farther south. The area is likely abyssal at about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

14557.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 135 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. The coast there lies near 66-67°S at this longitude, so 71°S is roughly 450 km inland. That puts it on the ice sheet, where the surface elevation is probably around 2,500 m.

Nearby reference points: Dome C is at 75°S, 123°E, with a surface near 3,200 m. Law Dome is at 66.7°S, 112°E. At 135°E, the ice surface about 400 km inland is probably near 2,300 m. The Aurora Subglacial Basin is around 70°S, 115-120°E, and Wilkes Basin is around 71°S, 145°E. The surface near 71°S, 135°E is roughly 2,600 m, which I'll use.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2600}

5211.text:
Location: 33°N, 163°E is in the northwest Pacific, east of Japan, south of the Emperor Seamounts region. It lies in the Northwest Pacific Basin, where the abyssal plain is about 5500-6000 m deep. The area near 33N 163E is east of the Shatsky Rise (which is around 32-35N, 157-160E). East of Shatsky Rise, the seafloor is about 5700 m deep. There are also scattered seamounts in the Mid-Pacific Mountains region, but those sit further south, around 20-25N.

I estimate roughly -5600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

5678.text:
Latitude 27.0 N, longitude 17.0 E is in southwestern Libya, in the Murzuq Basin region. The Murzuq sand sea lies around 26 N, 12–14 E, and the Tibesti is farther east. Near 27 N, 17 E the ground sits on the Libyan plateau south of the Hamada al-Hamra, possibly near the Wadi al-Hayat/Wadi ash Shati area. The Sabha region is at 27 N, 14 E, at about 430 m. Going east to 17 E, there is a plateau and dunes, probably the Ubari/Calanshio area. The Al Haruj al Aswad volcanic field is at 27.5 N, 17 E, but its center is around 27.5 N, 17.5 E, and its elevation is about 600–1000 m. Therefore 27.0 N, 17.0 E may be on the southern edge of Haruj at about 600 m.

I estimate about 560 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":560}

3510.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude 1.0 is in the English Channel / Strait of Dover region. Folkestone sits at about 51.08°N, 1.17°E, and Dungeness at about 50.91°N, 0.98°E. So 51.0°N, 1.0°E lies in the sea, a few kilometers east-northeast of Dungeness and southwest of Folkestone, in Hythe Bay or just offshore.

The coast near Hythe is at about 51.07°N, 1.08°E. Dungeness at 50.91°N extends to about 0.98°E. At 51.0°N, 1.0°E the point is roughly 10 km north of Dungeness. The coast of Romney Marsh runs from Dymchurch (51.02°N, 0.99°E) to Dungeness. Dymchurch is at 51.02°N, 1.0°E, and the coastline there trends north-northeast. At 51.0°N the coast is around 0.96°E at Littlestone/St Mary's Bay (about 51.0°N, 0.97°E). So 1.0°E is about 2–3 km offshore, and the water depth is about 3–5 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5}

13869.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -161 lies in the Southern Ocean, north of the Ross Sea region and west of Marie Byrd Land. This area sits on the abyssal plain/Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank, with typical depths around 4500-5000 m.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs near 65°S, 175°W at its southern end, and at -161 the seafloor is farther east, on the ridge's eastern flank. The Udintsev Fracture Zone is around 145°W-ish. At -65, -161, depth is probably around 4,000-4,800 m. I'll estimate -4600.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4600}

15829.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 159 lies in the Transantarctic Mountains region, near the Beardmore/Shackleton Glacier area. The Shackleton Glacier is around 85°S, 177°E, and the Beardmore is around 84°S, 170°E. At 159°E and 85°S, this is probably the polar plateau edge or an outlet glacier near the Mill/Leverett Glacier area. The Leverett Glacier is near 85.5°S, 147°W.

Longitude 159°E at 85°S falls near the Queen Alexandra Range, whose Mount Kirkpatrick is at 84.3°S, 166°E. The Law Glacier and Lennox-King Glacier are in the region. The Ross Ice Shelf edge near the Beardmore is at about 83°S. At 85°S, 159°E, the surface is probably the high plateau or glacier at roughly 2500–3000 m. Taking the Queen Alexandra Range and the plateau behind it, I estimate about 2800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

14048.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -163 is in the Southern Ocean, off the Antarctic coast. The Ross Sea region lies further south, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further north. At -67, -163 the position is in the Pacific sector, north of the Ross Sea and just east of the Balleny region.

The seafloor there is likely abyssal plain at about 4,000–4,500 m depth. The Antarctic continental margin near 165°W lies at about -75 to -78 latitude, so this point is far from the shelf. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crosses near -65 at about -150 to -170 longitude, and the Southeast Pacific basin near -67, -163 is likely about 3,500–4,000 m deep. I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

6321.text:
The point (19°N, 137°W) lies in the open Pacific Ocean, northeast of Hawaii and west of Baja California. The surface type is Water.

The seabed depth here is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup. Abyssal plain depths in this part of the northeast Pacific are typically 4,500 to 5,000 m. The area is south of the Mendocino and Molokai fracture zones and west of the East Pacific Rise. The seafloor age is about 60 to 70 million years, and the Pacific plate seafloor at that age usually lies at roughly 4,800 to 5,000 m. I'll use about -4,900 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4900}

16081.text:
The point is at 89°S, 57°W, close to the South Pole and on the East Antarctic ice sheet or the plateau edge. The Pole itself sits at about 2835 m. At 89°S along 57°W, the surface is on the side toward the Weddell Sea and the Pensacola Mountains, where ice elevation is around 2,700 to 2,800 m.

The ice sheet surface near 89°S is roughly 2,800 m. The Pole is at 2,835 m, and the surface slopes gently, so around 57°W I estimate about 2,750 m. The ice is grounded, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2760}

270.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 1°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, near the Lomonosov Ridge. The ridge crosses the pole roughly along the 140°E–50°W meridians, so 1°E at 87°N is on the Eurasian side of the pole, which is the Amundsen Basin side. The Amundsen Basin floor is about 4200 m deep, and the Lomonosov Ridge is at roughly 1000–1500 m depth.

The ridge sits at about 140°E–Pole–40°W. At 87°N, 1°E, the distance to the ridge axis is about 40° of longitude, and one degree of longitude at 87°N is about 5.8 km, so that is roughly 230 km away. The point is therefore in the Amundsen Basin. The Gakkel Ridge meets the basin near 87°N, 60–80°E, which is far from 1°E, so the depth should be about 4200 m. IBCAO values near 87°N, 0°E are around -4000 m, and I will go with -4150.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4150}

14828.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -43 is in the southern Weddell Sea region, near the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Filchner Ice Shelf and Ronne Ice Shelf sit roughly between -35 and -80 longitude at latitudes of -77 and below. At -75, -43 the area is probably the eastern Weddell Sea coast, which is open water or sea ice, and the continental shelf there is deep. Berkner Island lies near -46 longitude and -79 latitude, so -75 is north of it.

The Filchner Ice Shelf front is at about -78 latitude, so at -75 and -43 we are in the open Weddell Sea. The coast near -75, -30 is the Luitpold Coast and Coats Land (about -77, -30 to -35). At -43 the area lies on the continental shelf in the western Weddell Sea, with the Filchner Trough running near -37 to -40 longitude. Bedrock depth there is probably about 500-600 m, so I'll estimate it as water with an elevation of about -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

13029.text:
Latitude -55, longitude -41 lies in the South Atlantic, about 600 km southwest of South Georgia and northeast of the South Sandwich region. It is also east of the Falkland Plateau and north of the Scotia Sea.

South Georgia sits at about -54.3, -36.5. The North Scotia Ridge runs from the Burdwood Bank, near -54, -60, east to South Georgia. The point at -55, -41 is south of the ridge's eastern part, in the Scotia Sea's eastern basin, or possibly the Georgia Basin. The Georgia Basin lies at about -50 to -55 lat and -35 to -45 lon, with depths of roughly 3000-4000 m. Near -41, -55 the depth is probably around 3000 m, though the ridge's western extension (Shag Rocks, at -53.5, -42) is close. Shag Rocks is at about -53.5, -42.0, so -55 is 1.5° south of it, which puts the point south of the ridge on its slope. The ridge's southern flank drops to the Scotia Sea at about 3000-3500 m. I estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

11077.text:
The point (-33.0, 15.0) lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of South Africa. The coast near Cape Town is at about 18.4°E, and at 33°S the coastline is around 18°E. So 15°E is roughly 300 km offshore.

The continental shelf off the west coast is narrow there, about 100–200 km wide. Beyond it the slope descends to the Cape Basin, which is about 4,000–5,000 m deep. At 15°E and 33°S I estimate a depth of around 3,500 m. The Cape Rise and Cape Canyon are further southwest, and the shelf break is at about 17°E. At 15°E the seafloor is therefore on the continental slope's lower part or the upper basin, roughly 3,000–4,000 m deep. I'll go with -3,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

5229.text:
The point 31°N, 161°W is in the North Pacific, northwest of Hawaii and roughly 900 km north of Kauai. This is open ocean on the Pacific plate. Seafloor depth in this region is typically around 5,000 to 5,500 m.

I'm estimating from general knowledge of Pacific seafloor depths, not from a lookup of this exact point. The region north of Hawaii lies north of the Hawaiian arch and the moat, where abyssal plain depths of about 5,300 m are common. The seafloor age is about 90 to 100 million years, which also fits a depth of around 5,500 m. I'll use -5,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

4677.text:
Latitude 39.0°N, longitude 175.0°E lies in the northwest Pacific, east of Japan, roughly 1,800 km from the Japanese coast. It is near the Emperor Seamount chain, but that chain runs along about 170°E at 40°N and further north, so the point is more likely abyssal plain. Seamounts of the Hawaiian-Emperor chain at 39°N include Yuryaku (~35°N, 172°E) and Kimmei (~33°N), and Suiko is at 44.5°N, 170°E. Nearby 39°N, 175°E, the Shatsky Rise is much further south, so I treat this as abyssal plain.

The seafloor depth in the area is probably around 5,500 m. The older Pacific crust east of the Japan Trench lies at about 5,500–6,000 m, though the point is a bit north of the Mid-Pacific Mountains and slightly west of the Emperor chain bend. I'll estimate -5,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

7347.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 115.0°E lies in the South China Sea, east of the Spratly Islands area. The Spratlys span roughly 6–12°N and 111–117°E, with deep troughs between the reefs. At 9°N, 115°E, this is likely the Dangerous Ground region, though that area has mostly shallow reefs and some deep basins.

The Reed Bank is at about 11°N, 116.5°E. Dangerous Ground sits around 8–11°N, 112–116°E. The Palawan Trough lies to the southeast near 9°N, 117°E. Around 115°E, 9°N there is probably the Spratly platform with the Union Banks to the west at about 9.8°N, 114.5°E. Southern Spratlys' Swallow Reef is at 7.4°N, 113.8°E, and Louisa Reef is at 6.3°N, 113.3°E. Near 9°N, 115°E I estimate depths of about 1,500 to 2,000 m, since the area is a trough between the Dangerous Ground and the Palawan shelf. I'll go with around -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

7979.text:
Location: 1.0°N, 61.0°W is in northern Brazil, in Roraima state. It lies near the Rio Branco/Rio Mucajaí region, north of the equator and south of Caracaraí. Caracaraí sits at about 1.8°N, 61.1°W. At 1.0°N, 61°W the area is near Rio Branco, close to the Anauá/Jauaperi confluence, in lowland Amazon forest at roughly 50-80 m elevation.

The Jauaperi River is at about 1.0°S, so this point is further north. The Rio Branco runs near 61.3°W at 1°N, so the point may be land just east of the river. I estimate about 70 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":70}

13587.text:
The point is at 61°S, 5°W, in the Southern Ocean. It lies north of the Weddell Sea and south of the South Atlantic basin, close to the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Bouvet area. Bouvet Island is at 54.4°S, 3.4°E, so it is well away from this point. The South Sandwich Islands are far to the west.

The area is probably the southern end of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge region, near the American-Antarctic Ridge, which lies further to the northwest. The Atlantic-Indian basin floor here is typically 3000–5000 m deep. The Weddell-Enderby basin is around 4500 m deep at 61°S, 5°W, and I estimate -4300 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12939.text:
The point (-53.0, 139.0) is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and southwest of Tasmania. It lies north of the Southeast Indian Ridge, which sits around 50°S at this longitude, and south of the South Tasman Rise.

The Australian-Antarctic Basin lies to the south, with depths of about 4000-4500 m. The ridge crest near 140°E is at about 51°S. The ridge axis there is the Southeast Indian Ridge, and the area near 53°S is probably on its southern flank, where depths are about 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14638.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -63 is in the Weddell Sea sector, west of the Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf extends roughly to 74-79°S at longitudes of 40-80°W. At -73, -63 the point is near the Orville Coast or Ellsworth Land, where the Antarctic Peninsula meets the continent, and could be grounded ice on the Orville Coast.

The Orville Coast lies around 75.5°S, 63°W, and Smith Peninsula and the Lassiter Coast are around 74°S, 62°W. At 73°S, 63°W the Larsen Ice Shelf region and the Antarctic Peninsula's southern part lie around 64°W. The Dyer Plateau is at about 70°S, 65°W. The Latady Island area is at 70.8°S, 74°W. The Wetmore Glacier is near 71°S, 66°W. At 73°S, 63°W it is probably the Orville Coast or Gutenko Mountains area, near the Lassiter Coast (73-74°S, 62-63°W), which has ice-sheet plateau elevations of about 1000-1500 m. However, it might also be the Ronne Ice Shelf's edge, since the Ronne front extends to roughly 74°S at 62°W. The Lassiter Coast lies on the east side of the peninsula and borders the Ronne Ice Shelf, with the coast at about 74-75°S. At 73°S the point is inland on the plateau, so I'll choose grounded ice at about 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

15428.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 77 is in East Antarctica, inland of Prydz Bay. Dome A is at about -80.37, 77.35 with an elevation of about 4093 m. At -81, 77 the point is about 0.6 degrees of latitude south of Dome A, roughly 70 km away. The surface slopes gently there, so I estimate about 4000 m.

The surface there is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":4020}

14571.text:
Location: -71.0, 163.0 is in East Antarctica, Victoria Land. Around 71°S, 163°E is inland of the Pennell Coast, in the Transantarctic Mountains/Northern Victoria Land area. The coast near Pennell Coast lies at about 70.5°S, 166°E. At 163°E the coast is around 70.4°S (Rennick Glacier area is at about 70.5°S, 161–162°E). So 71°S, 163°E is inland, in the mountains, likely grounded ice at 1500–2000 m elevation.

Rennick Glacier flows north around 160-161°E. At 163°E, 71°S it is probably in the Admiralty Mountains/Concord range area, with an ice surface around 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

819.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 19.0°E lies in the Svalbard region. Nordaustlandet spans roughly 79.5–80.8°N, so 81.0°N is just north of it, in the Arctic Ocean north of Svalbard. Rijpfjorden is at about 80.2°N, and Kvitøya is at 80.1°N, 32.5°E. The northernmost islands, the Sjuøyane, are at about 80.7°N, 20°E. The point at 81.0°N, 19°E is therefore north of them, on the shelf or the shelf slope.

The Svalbard northern shelf break lies around 81.5°N at 19°E. The Yermak Plateau is to the northwest, around 81°N, 8°E. At 81°N, 19°E, the depth is probably a few hundred meters, around 250 m. The shelf north of Nordaustlandet is roughly 200–400 m deep, and the Sjuøyane area is shallow. I estimate -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

9540.text:
Latitude -17.0, longitude -179.0 is in Fiji's Lau region, east of Vanua Levu and Taveuni. Taveuni sits at about -16.85, -179.97, which is roughly 1° west of this point. At -179.0 (181°E), the nearest land is probably Rabi or Kioa to the west, though Rabi is around 179.97°E. Farther east lie the Lau islands, such as Vanua Balavu at about -17.2, -178.95.

Vanua Balavu spans roughly -17.15 to -17.35 latitude and -178.95 to -178.75 longitude. At -17.0, -179.0 we are therefore north of it, in the Lau Basin or the Lau Ridge area, and probably in open water. Nearby islands such as Cikobia (-16.7, -179.6) and Qelelevu (-16.77, -179.2) lie around the point but do not contain it. The Nanuku passage and Lau sea floor there are likely about 1500 to 2500 m deep. The area between Taveuni and the Lau Ridge includes the Lau Basin at roughly 2000 m, and I'll take about -1800 m as the estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

15241.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 63 is in East Antarctica, inland of Prydz Bay, near the Lambert Glacier basin's southern part. Lambert Glacier runs around 70-72°E at latitudes of -70 to -80. At -79, 63°E, this is south of the Mawson Escarpment, on the inland ice sheet toward the Gamburtsev Mountains' western edge (Gamburtsev is around 78°S, 76°E). The surface elevation there is likely around 2,500 m. Grounded ice sheet near Dome A is about 4,000 m at 80°S, 77°E. At 63°E, 79°S the surface is probably about 3,000 m. The geoid there is about -20 m, which is small.

I estimate about 2,900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

198.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -143° lies in the central Arctic Ocean. At 87°N, 143°W, it is on the Amerasian side of the Lomonosov Ridge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the Siberian shelf through the pole toward Greenland and Ellesmere Island. At the pole it lies along roughly 140°E to 40°W. At 87°N, the ridge is near 60°W to 150°E, so 143°W would be on the Amerasian side, on the Alpha-Mendeleev side, away from the ridge.

The Makarov Basin lies on the Siberian side of the ridge, and the Canada Basin lies on the Amerasian side. At 87°N, 143°W the point is likely in the Amerasian Basin, close to the ridge flank. Depths there are about 3,000–4,000 m. Lomonosov Ridge crest depths are about 1,000–1,500 m. The ridge at 87°N lies near 150°E–... and 30°W, so 143°W is about 37° of longitude from the 180° meridian side, which at this latitude is roughly 2 degrees of latitude of distance... Specifically, 37° of longitude at 87°N is about 37 × 1.94 km ≈ 72 km. The ridge is not far away, so the depth may be about 2,500–3,500 m, and I'll estimate -3,200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

12993.text:
The point is at 55°S, 113°W, in the southeast Pacific. It lies west of the Chile Rise and south of the Easter Island region, on the flank of the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise near 55°S sits at roughly 130°W to 140°W, since the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs from about 56°S, 143°W toward the Chile Rise triple junction near 36°S, 98°W. At 113°W and 55°S the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain of the Antarctic-Pacific basin, perhaps on the Antarctic plate side, at about 3,500 to 4,000 m depth.

The Chile Rise (Chile Ridge) runs from about 38°S, 92°W to 55°S, 125°W, so at 55°S it is near 125°W. That puts our point about 12° east of the ridge, on older crust. At roughly 3 cm/yr half-spreading, that is about 800 km, or around 25 Myr of crust age, which implies a depth near 3,300 m. The Louisville and Eltanin fracture zones lie further west, so I'll estimate about -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

2246.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -7.0 is in the North Atlantic, southeast of Iceland and northwest of the Faroe Islands. Iceland's east coast is at about -14 longitude, so this point is well offshore.

The point lies on the Iceland-Faroe Ridge, which is roughly 300-500 m deep. At 65°N, 7°W the ridge is probably a bit deeper, since it runs from Iceland's southeast coast toward the Faroes at about 62°N, 7°W. The ridge crest near 63.5°N is around 400-500 m deep. At 65°N, the point is in the Norwegian Sea basin, north of the ridge and southwest of the Jan Mayen area, where depths are likely 1500-2000 m. The southern end of the Norwegian Sea basin near 65°N, 7°W is about 1800 m deep, so I estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

3347.text:
Latitude 53.0 N, longitude 35.0 E is in western Russia, in the Kaluga/Bryansk area. Kaluga oblast is at about 54 N, 36 E. Near 53 N, 35 E, the point lies near Zhizdra or Lyudinovo in Kaluga oblast, or near Bryansk oblast's eastern edge. The terrain there is on the Central Russian Upland, with elevations of roughly 200 m.

I estimate about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":200}

14921.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 143 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land / Georges V Land region inland. Longitude 143°E at 75°S is inland of the Adélie Coast (coast around 66-68°S), so it is on the ice sheet, about 800 km from the coast. The elevation there is roughly 2,500 to 3,000 m. Near Dome C (75°S, 123°E) it is about 3,200 m. At 143°E, 75°S, I'd estimate about 2,800 m. The area is near the Wilkes Subglacial Basin, where the ice is thick but the surface is around 2,500 m.

I'll go with 2,600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2600}

8640.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude -179.0 is in the central Pacific, east of Tokelau and south of the equator. It lies near the Phoenix Islands and Tokelau region, west of the 180° meridian. Longitude -179 is 179°W, which is about 1° east of the dateline. Tokelau sits at about 9°S, 172°W, so it is far from this point. The nearest land is likely the Phoenix Islands (Kanton at 2.8°S, 171.7°W), so this point is open ocean.

The seafloor depth in this area, between the Samoan basin and the Phoenix Islands, is probably around 5,000–5,500 m. The Samoa passage area lies to the south. At 7°S, 179°W, the Central Pacific Basin's northern edge is likely about 5,300 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

9194.text:
Latitude -13, longitude -151 is in the South Pacific, northeast of Tahiti (which is near -17.6, -149.5). That places it in open ocean north of the Society Islands and just west of the Tuamotus. The Tuamotus lie around -14 to -18 and -140 to -148, so this is west of them.

The seafloor here is probably abyssal plain at about 4,000 to 4,500 m depth. The Tuamotu plateau region is shallower, but -151 is west of it. Bathymetry in this area, between the Marquesas and the Society Islands, is typically around -4,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

9891.text:
Latitude -19, longitude 163 lies in the Coral Sea/Southwest Pacific, east of the Chesterfield Islands and west of New Caledonia (which spans about 164–167E). The point is near the Chesterfield Plateau/Bellona Plateau region, or possibly in the Fairway Basin or Lord Howe Rise area.

The Lord Howe Rise extends northwest toward the Chesterfield Plateau at roughly 20S, 158E. The Bellona Plateau sits around 20S, 159E. The Capel Basin and the Santa Cruz-area basins lie further east. At 163E, 19S, the seafloor is probably in the New Caledonia Trough, or on its western flank, at around 163-164E. The New Caledonia Trough runs northwest of New Caledonia, with depths of about 2500-3500 m. Farther north, the Rennell Trough lies near 19S. I estimate a depth of about 3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

5658.text:
Location 27°N, 23°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Western Sahara coast and northwest of Cape Verde. It is in the open ocean, roughly 1000 km from the African coast. The Canary Islands lie around 15-18°W, so this point is farther west. The seafloor there is abyssal plain at about 5000-5500 m depth. The Canary Basin is roughly 5000-5400 m deep west of the Canaries, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is further west at about 40°W. At 23°W the depth is probably around 5000 m.

I estimate about -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

1236.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 133.0°E is in the Laptev Sea, north of the Lena Delta and the Siberian coast. The Lena Delta is at about 73°N, and the Laptev Sea shelf extends up to roughly 77-78°N at this longitude. The New Siberian Islands lie further east, at about 140°E and beyond. The Taymyr coast is to the west.

At 77°N, 133°E the water is on the outer Laptev shelf. The shelf break is around 78°N. Depths at 77°N are probably around 40-60 m, though the shelf deepens northward, so the value could be somewhat higher. I estimate about -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

14439.text:
Location: -71, -101 is in the Amundsen Sea / Bellingshausen area, west of Thurston Island. Thurston Island spans roughly 99°W to 90°W at about 72°S. At -71 lat, -101 lon, this is near the Abbot Ice Shelf region's western edge or open water. The Abbot Ice Shelf lies roughly from 89°W to 103°W, at around 72-73°S. At 71°S, 101°W, it is probably the continental shelf north of the Abbot Ice Shelf, which is open ocean or sea ice. The seabed here is probably around -500 to -600 m.

The Amundsen–Bellingshausen shelf is deep, about 500 m, so I estimate -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

9378.text:
The point is at -15, -143, in the Pacific within French Polynesia's Tuamotu archipelago region. The Tuamotus span roughly 14–23°S and 135–149°W. Atolls near -15, -143 include Hiti, Tepoto, Anaa (-17.4, -145.5), Faaite (-16.7, -145), Makemo (-16.6, -143.7), and Tuanake (-16.6, -144.2). At -15, -143, the nearest atolls are Takume and Raroia (-16, -142.5). Katiu is at -16.4, -144.4, and Taenga at -16.3, -144.6. Nothing sits exactly at -15, -143, so this point is probably open ocean north of the main Tuamotus.

The seafloor there is Pacific abyssal depth of roughly 4000 m. The Tuamotu plateau is about 3000–4000 m deep, and the area north of the chain is about 4000 m. I estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

1583.text:
Latitude 73.0N, longitude 107.0E lies in the Laptev Sea region. The Taymyr Peninsula's north coast is around 76N at about 100E, and the Khatanga Gulf is near 74N, 110E. At 73N, 107E, the Khatanga Gulf's western side is roughly at 73-74N, 108-112E. The Taymyr coast near Khatanga is at 72N, 102E. Anabar Bay is at 73N, 113E. The Khatanga Gulf extends from 71.9N, 102E northeast to the Laptev Sea around 74-75N, 112E. The gulf's axis near 107E is at about 73.5N, so the point is plausibly in the gulf or on land on the Nordvik peninsula (74N, 111E). Nordvik is at 74.0N, 111.5E, and the Khatanga Gulf's western shore runs along about 106E at 73.5N. The Taymyr coast near 73N 107E is probably the gulf's western shore, near Cape Vostochny, so this could be shallow water.

I'll guess water, since the gulf is wide here (about 100 km) with depths of about 10-20 m. I'm not certain, though. The Khatanga Gulf's southern shore at 107E is about 72.7N (Khatanga is at 71.97N, 102.5E, and the gulf widens northeast). The Nordvik peninsula lies to the east of the gulf. At 107E, the south shore is near 73.0N, and the north shore (the Taymyr coast) is near 74N. So 73.0N is right on the southern coast. I'll go with Water, depth about -8 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-8}

2063.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude -13.0 W lies in the Norwegian Sea/Iceland Sea, northeast of Iceland and southeast of Jan Mayen. Iceland's northeast coast is around 66°N, 15°W at Langanes (66.4°N, -14.5°W). At -13.0 longitude, 67.0 N is about 70 km northeast of Langanes, so this is open ocean on the Iceland shelf's edge or the Iceland Plateau.

The Iceland shelf extends northeast of Langanes, and the Iceland Plateau lies at about 1000-1800 m depth. The Kolbeinsey Ridge lies at about -18°W, so it is not nearby. At 67°N, 13°W the depth is probably around 1500 m. The Norwegian Sea basin is around 3000 m deep farther east, and the Iceland Plateau is about 1500-1900 m. The Jan Mayen Fracture Zone is farther north, near 71°N. I'll estimate about -1600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1600}

2864.text:
Latitude 59.0, Longitude 149.0 could be 149°E or 149°W. Longitude is given as 149.0, which is positive, so 149°E. At 59°N, 149°E, this is in the Sea of Okhotsk, north coast near Magadan. Magadan is at 59.56N, 150.8E. At 59N, 149E the point is in the Sea of Okhotsk, south of the coast; the coast near the Koni peninsula and Yamsky Islands is around 59.0N, 154E... Actually the northern coast near 149E lies around 59.3N (Okhotsk town at 59.36N, 143.2E; Ola at 59.57N, 151.3E). At 149E the coast is about 59.3N (Nagayev Bay is at 59.55N, 150.8E). So 59.0N, 149E is just offshore in the shallow northern shelf, with depth around 100 m.

The shelf there is gradual, with depths of about 100-150 m at 59N, 149E.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

3127.text:
The point (55°N, 45°W) is in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland's southern tip (Cape Farewell is near 60°N, 44°W) and in the Labrador Sea/Irminger Sea region. The Irminger Basin lies to the east of there. At 55°N, 45°W the point is probably southwest of the Reykjanes Ridge, which sits around 30–35°W at that latitude, and south of the Irminger Basin proper.

The Labrador Sea basin reaches about 3500 m depth near 55–60°N, 50°W. At 55°N, 45°W the seafloor is likely in the deep basin between the Labrador Sea and the Irminger Sea, at about 3,000–3,500 m. I estimate about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

5113.text:
The point 33°N, 33°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis and east of the Azores. The ridge axis near 33°N lies around 40°W to 42°W, and the Azores Plateau sits further north. At 33°W the seafloor is on the eastern flank of the ridge, in the Azores–Madeira region. The ridge axis near 38°N is at about 28°W, and near 33°N it is at about 41°W. That would put 33°W east of the axis by about 8° of longitude, which is roughly 750 km, so the seafloor age is about 30 million years. Depth there should be around 3500–4000 m, though the area could include seamounts.

I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3700}

6054.text:
Latitude 23.0, longitude 49.0 is in the Empty Quarter (Rub' al Khali) area of Saudi Arabia, near the UAE and Saudi border. It lies inland, south of Al-Hasa and west of the Qatar/UAE region. The Persian Gulf coast near Qatar is at roughly 51°E, so this point is well inland.

Elevation in this part of the eastern Rub' al Khali is probably about 100 to 150 m. The Uqayla area at 23°N, 49°E sits in the dunes of the Empty Quarter, and the terrain there rises toward the west. I'll estimate about 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

5597.text:
Location: 27°N, 145°W is in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and west of California. This is open ocean on the abyssal plain, with typical depths around 4,500-5,500 m. The seafloor age here is roughly 80-90 Ma, which corresponds to about 5,200 m depth, though the Pacific abyssal plain in this region (north of the Molokai fracture zone) is often near 5,000-5,300 m.

I estimate about -5,150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5150}

14744.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 149 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land / Victoria Land region. Longitude 149E at 73S is inland of the Oates Coast / George V Coast, roughly 200-300 km from the coast. The coast near 149E is around 69-70S, so 73S is about 350 km inland. Ice surface elevation there is probably around 2000-2500 m. The area is near the Wilkes Subglacial Basin, where the bed is low but the surface is still high, perhaps 2200 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so it counts as Solid, and the elevation reported is the ice surface. I estimate 2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

12751.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 123 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. It is south of the Southeast Indian Ridge's Australian-Antarctic Basin region, so I estimated the seabed depth from memory of that area.

The Southeast Indian Ridge crosses around 50°S at 120°E, and in this region the ridge axis runs roughly from 49°S at 120°E to 51°S at 140°E. The ridge there lies at about -3000 to -3500 m depth, and the Australian-Antarctic Discordance zone is deeper, at about -4000 m. At 51°S, 123°E the point is probably near the ridge axis or on its flank, so the seabed is probably about -3800 m. This is an estimate, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

12022.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 105 is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies south of the Southeast Indian Ridge crest? The ridge near 105°E sits around 40°S to 45°S, with the Australian-Antarctic Discordance region around 120°E. At 105°E the ridge axis is near 38-40°S, and the ridge crest depths there are about 2500-3000 m. At 43°S the seafloor is probably on the southern flank of the ridge, at about 3500-4000 m depth.

I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11987.text:
The point (-43.0, 35.0) lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa. It is south of Madagascar and east of the South African coast, and it sits near the Mozambique Basin and Mozambique Ridge region. The Mozambique Ridge runs around 33-35°E and reaches roughly 30-35°S, so 43°S is well south of it. At that latitude and longitude the area is probably the Agulhas Basin or the Southwest Indian Ridge region. The Southwest Indian Ridge near 35°E runs around 40-45°S, with the Du Toit and Andrew Bain fracture zones near 30-35°E at about 45°S. The Prince Edward Fracture Zone lies near 35°E, and the ridge crest there sits at about 2500-3500 m depth.

The point could sit on the ridge flank, so I estimate a depth of about 3000 m. This is a guess from the regional geography, not a lookup of the actual bathymetry.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

7180.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 141.0°E lies in the western Pacific, south of Guam and east of Yap. It is near the Caroline Islands region, north of Ulithi and Fais. Just to the east is the Mariana Trench's southern end, and the Challenger Deep sits at about 11.37°N, 142.59°E.

Longitude 141.0 is about 1.6° west of Challenger Deep. The area between Yap and the trench includes the Yap Trench to the west, around 138°E, and the Caroline Ridge. At 141°E, 11°N the seafloor is probably in the Philippine Sea/West Caroline basin, with depths of about 4500–5000 m. The Mariana Trench axis curves southwest toward Challenger Deep and then toward Yap, so at 11°N the trench axis is near 142.2°E. At 141°E we are west of the axis, probably on the outer, western side of the trench, which is the overriding plate side. This is the Mariana forearc, where depths are around 4000–5000 m. I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

11360.text:
The point (-37, -139) lies in the South Pacific, roughly southeast of Pitcairn and Gambier. It is on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise. The crust there is about 30-50 Ma, so the seafloor depth is likely about 4000-4500 m. Near 37°S, 139°W the seafloor is probably around 4300 m deep.

This is an estimate from regional seafloor depth, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

4234.text:
Location 43.0N, 9.0E is in the Ligurian Sea/Tyrrhenian area, west of Corsica's Cap Corse (which is at about 9.4E, 43.0N). Corsica's west coast at 43N is around 9.2E or so (Gulf of Saint-Florent is at 42.7N, 9.3E). At 43.0N, Cap Corse spans roughly 9.35-9.45E. So 9.0E lies in the sea, about 40 km west of Cap Corse. Depth in the Corsica Channel's western side, the Ligurian Sea basin between Corsica and Genoa, is about 1000-2000 m. West of Cap Corse, depth at 9.0E is probably around 1500 m, though the slope drops quickly from Corsica's west shelf to the Ligurian basin, which is about 2000 m deep at its center.

Estimate -1300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

15577.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 15°E lies in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land. It is inland of the coast, roughly on the polar plateau south of the Shackleton Range and Queen Maud Land mountains. The Shackleton Range is at about 80°S, 25°W, so it is not nearby. At 83°S, 15°E the ice sheet surface is probably around 2,500–3,000 m.

This sits between the Pole-facing side of the Recovery Glacier catchment and the interior plateau. The South Pole is at 2,835 m, and at 83°S, 15°E, toward Dronning Maud Land, the surface is likely about 2,700 m. Longitude 15°E at 83°S is about 7° of latitude from the Pole. The Recovery Lakes region is at about 81°S, 20°W, so it is farther west. Near 83°S, 15°E, I estimate roughly 2,800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2750}

11885.text:
The point (-43.0, -169.0) is in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region. It is well east of the Campbell Plateau, which lies around 170°E, and it is on the Pacific plate's abyssal plain. Depths there are typically about 4,500 to 5,500 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is much farther south, near 55-65°S at this longitude. At 43°S, 169°W, the seafloor age is moderate, so the depth is probably around 5,000 m. The Louisville seamount chain is further west, so I don't expect a seamount here.

I estimate the depth at about -5,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

15797.text:
The point at latitude -85, longitude 95 lies in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. It is roughly 500 km from the South Pole in the direction of 95°E, which is toward the Vostok and Dome A side, past the Pole.

Longitude 95°E at 85°S is in the interior of the ice sheet, and the ice-surface elevation there is probably around 3,000 m. Reference points: the South Pole is at about 2,835 m, Dome A (80.4°S, 77°E) is at about 4,090 m, and Vostok (78.5°S, 106.8°E) is at about 3,488 m. The area near 85°S, 95°E is beyond the Pole from Vostok, toward the Pole itself, and likely sits near 3,200 m. The Gamburtsev range is at about 80°S, 75°E and is farther away. I estimate the surface at roughly 3,100 m, which is grounded ice and so counts as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3150}

4640.text:
The point 39.0°N, 101.0°E lies in Gansu Province, China, in the Zhangye area. Zhangye city is at about 38.93°N, 100.45°E. The Hexi Corridor runs northwest to southeast here, and the Qilian Mountains lie to the south. At 101.0°E, 39.0°N, we are northeast of Zhangye, near Linze/Gaotai or Shandan. Linze is at about 39.13°N, 100.17°E. Shandan is at about 38.78°N, 101.08°E, at roughly 1,800 m.

The point is north of Shandan, toward the Heli Mountains (Longshou Mountains) at around 39°N. The Longshou Mountains run along about 39.0–39.5°N, with elevations of 2,000–3,000 m. The plains near Zhangye sit at about 1,480 m, and the corridor rises to the southeast. At 101°E, 39°N, the terrain is likely the northern edge of the corridor or the foothills of the Heli Mountains, at roughly 1,700 m. I'll estimate 1,750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1750}

7711.text:
Latitude 5.0°N, longitude 123.0°E lies in the Celebes (Sulawesi) Sea. It is south of the Sulu Archipelago and southeast of Zamboanga. Mindanao's southwestern coast is around 7°N and its southern coast near 6°N at 124°E, so this point is well offshore.

The Celebes Sea basin is deep, with depths of roughly 4,000 to 5,000 m in the central part. At 5°N, 123°E the point is in the central-western basin, where depths are about 4,800 m. This is an estimate from the basin's general depth, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

2491.text:
Latitude 63.0°N, longitude 123.0°E is in Yakutia, Russia, in the Lena basin region west of Yakutsk (which is at 62.03°N, 129.7°E). The point sits in the Vilyuy basin / central Yakutian lowland, near the Markha River or Lena tributaries. Terrain there is probably around 250 m.

The Vilyuy River lies near 63.5°N, 122°E, with elevations of roughly 100-200 m. The Lena at 63°N is around 126-127°E. At 123°E, the point is on the Vilyuy plateau, perhaps 200 m. I'll go with 220 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":220}

2682.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 145.0 (East or West?). Longitude 145.0 is positive, so 145 E. At 61 N, 145 E that is in the Russian Far East, in the Sakha/Magadan region near the Okhotsk-Kolyma area, in the Verkhoyansk/Suntar-Khayata mountain region. The point is roughly east of Ust-Nera (64.5 N, 143 E), and south of it. At 61 N, 145 E it lies in the Ulakhan-Chistay or Sette-Daban area, in the Yudoma-Maya highlands or the upper Aldan/Maya region. That area is mountainous, with elevation of about 1000 m.

Lying between the Sette-Daban range (about 61 N, 139 E) and the Kolyma highlands, the point is likely in the upper Allakh-Yun/Tompo area. Elevation is around 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":950}

12901.text:
The coordinates -53.0, 63.0 fall in the southern Indian Ocean. The nearest land is Heard Island at about -53.1, 73.5, which is roughly 10 degrees of longitude to the east, so this point is open water. The Kerguelen Plateau lies near 70°E and further east, and this point is west of it. To the west of the plateau is the Crozet Basin, or possibly the Southwest Indian Ridge region.

The Southwest Indian Ridge sits near 50-55°S at longitudes of about 40-60°E. Farther east, the Australian-Antarctic Ridge begins around 77°E. At 63°E, 53°S, the seafloor could be the Enderby Basin or the Crozet Basin, with depths of about 3500-4500 m. The Kerguelen Plateau's western edge lies near 66-68°E at this latitude, and the northern Kerguelen Plateau runs from 46-50°S. At 53°S the southern Kerguelen Plateau starts around 64°E or beyond, so this point is probably on the western flank, with depth around 3000-4000 m. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

14681.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 23 is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The Fimbul Ice Shelf lies around 0–3°W, so this point is well to the east. The coast at 23°E is near 70°S (Princess Astrid Coast, around 6–15°E, and Prince Harald Coast around 35°E). At 73°S, 23°E, the point sits inland, about 300 km from the coast, in the Sør Rondane / Queen Fabiola area. The Sør Rondane Mountains are around 72°S, 23-28°E, so this point is on or near them, on the southern side of the range, where the ice plateau is high at roughly 3000 m.

The mountains' peaks reach about 3000 m, and the ice sheet south of them is around 3200 m. I'll estimate 3100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3100}

1177.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 15.0°E is in the Svalbard region. Spitsbergen's west coast lies near 14-16°E at this latitude. Isfjorden is at about 78°N, and Bellsund is at about 77.5°N, 14°E. At 77.0°N the southern part of Spitsbergen runs from about 14°E to 18°E, and Hornsund is at 77.0°N, 15.5°E.

The coast at 77°N is around 14.3°E, so 15.0°E should be inland, on the land between Bellsund and Hornsund. That area is the Wedel Jarlsberg Land mountains and glaciers. Hornsund's fjord head is at about 77.0°N, 15.5-16°E, and the Hornsund fjord's northern shore sits at about 77.0°N. Its mouth is at about 15°E, 76.95°N. So 15.0°E, 77.0°N is probably on the north side of the Hornsund mouth, on land. The Hornsund station is at 77.0°N, 15.5°E. West of that is Isbjørnhamna, around 15.4°E. At 15.0°E there are mountains such as Fugleberget, which is at about 77.0°N, 15.8°E. The elevation at this point is likely a few hundred meters, so I'll estimate 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

10633.text:
Latitude -29, longitude -153 is in the South Pacific, east of the Austral Islands and southwest of Pitcairn. It lies in open ocean on the Southeast Pacific basin floor. The seafloor there is probably around 3,500 to 4,000 m deep, since the East Pacific Rise flank is roughly 3,000 to 3,500 m at 110°W and the older crust farther west is deeper.

The location is also near the Easter/Pitcairn seamount chains, but at -29, -153 the nearest features are Austral seamounts around -28, -145 (Macdonald is at -29, -140). So this is probably plain abyssal plain, at about 4,000 m depth. I estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

2546.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude -127.0 W is in the southwestern Northwest Territories, near the Yukon and NWT border region. It lies in the Mackenzie Mountains foothills, west of the Liard River and Nahanni area.

The Liard River at Nahanni Butte is at about 61.0 N, 123.4 W, so this point is further west, in the Selwyn or Mackenzie Mountains. The Frances Lake area is at 61.4 N, 129.5 W. Terrain at 127 W is mountainous, with elevations of about 1000–1800 m. The South Nahanni River lies near 61.5 N, 125–127 W, and the Flat River is near 61.5 N, 128 W. A point around 61 N, 127 W could be in the upper Hyland or Meister River region, with elevations of about 1200 m. I will guess 1250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1250}

504.text:
The point at 85°N, 109°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Siberian coast. It is in the Eurasian Basin, near the Gakkel Ridge and the Amundsen Basin side. The Gakkel Ridge runs through roughly 85°N at about 90–110°E, so the point is likely close to the ridge axis or on its flank. The ridge axis there is about 4000 m deep or more, and the adjacent Amundsen Basin floor is around 4200 m. The Lomonosov Ridge is at about 140°E at this latitude, so it is not a factor.

The Gakkel Ridge axis at 85°N, 109°E is roughly 4000–4500 m deep. I estimate a seabed depth of about 4100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4100}

285.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude 31°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, near the North Pole. It is close to the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from the Siberian shelf past the pole to Greenland, and it sits on the Eurasian side of the pole.

The Lomonosov Ridge crest is around 87–88°N near 140°E and 50°W. At 87°N, 31°E the position lies on the Amundsen Basin side, close to the Gakkel Ridge's end. The Amundsen Basin is about 4,200 m deep, and the Nansen Basin is about 3,900 to 4,000 m. The Lomonosov Ridge on the 90°N meridian runs from roughly 140°E to 40°W, so 31°E at 87°N is about 3 degrees of latitude from the pole, or about 330 km. The ridge there lies near longitudes 140°E and 40°W, which is on the Greenland-side branch. The point at 31°E is on the opposite side of the pole from the ridge and sits in the Amundsen Basin. I estimate the seabed there at about -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4290}

3419.text:
The point is 53.0°N, 179.0°E, which is in the western Bering Sea / North Pacific near the Aleutian Islands. Attu Island sits at about 52.9°N, 172.9°E, so it is well to the west. The Near Islands are around 173°E. At 179°E, 53°N, the closest land is the Rat Islands and Buldir, but those are at about 175.9°E. Further east, Amchitka is at 179.3°E and 51.5°N. At 53°N, 179°E, the point is about 1.5° north of Amchitka, which is the north side of the Aleutian arc in the Bering Sea.

The Aleutian Ridge runs along about 51.5°N at this longitude. The Bering Sea side drops off to the Aleutian Basin, which is about 3800 m deep. At 53°N, which is about 1.5° (roughly 165 km) north of the ridge, the depth is probably around 3000-3800 m. The Bowers Ridge is farther east, near 180°W to 175°W, curving north from 52°N, so it does not reach this point. The point is likely on the slope or the Aleutian Basin margin, so I estimate a depth of about 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

10936.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 93 is in the southeast Indian Ocean, west of Australia. The area is near the Broken Ridge and Wharton Basin. Broken Ridge lies around 31°S, 95°E, and its crest is about 1000-2000 m deep. At 93°E, 31°S the point is west of Broken Ridge, in the Wharton Basin or the Diamantina/Perth Basin area. The Wharton Basin is about 5000 m deep, while the Ninetyeast Ridge runs along 90°E. At 31°S the Ninetyeast Ridge is near 90°E and its southern end is at about 30°S, with depths around 2000-3000 m. At 93°E it is probably on the eastern flank or the basin, at roughly 4500-5000 m depth.

Broken Ridge spans about 30-33°S and 93-100°E, so 93°E is at its western edge. Its western end is near 93°E, and the Ninetyeast Ridge meets it near 31°S, 93°E. The depth there might be around 3000 m, so I'll guess -3500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

1003.text:
Latitude 79.0°N, longitude 27.0°E is in the Svalbard region, east of Nordaustlandet. Nordaustlandet spans roughly 18–28°E at latitudes 79–80.5°N. Its southern coast lies near 79.5°N at about 22–27°E, and Wahlenbergfjorden is at about 79.7°N, 18°E. At 27°E the land reaches around 79.6°N, near Duvefjorden and Rijpfjorden. South of Nordaustlandet is Hinlopen Strait, which is at about 79.5°N. Kong Karls Land is at 79°N, 26–29°E, so this point could be on Kong Karls Land.

Kong Karls Land islands are at roughly 78.9–79.0°N, 28–29°E. Svenskøya is at about 78.7°N, 28.5°E, Kongsøya at 78.9°N, 28.2°E, and Abeløya at 79.0°N, 25.0°E. So 27°E at 79°N is probably water between Abeløya and Kongsøya. Depths there are shallow, around 100–200 m. The Kong Karls Land shelf is about 100 m deep, so I'll guess −150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

2165.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude -169.0° (169°W) is in the northern Bering Sea, south of the Bering Strait and southwest of Nome. St. Lawrence Island sits near 63°N, 170°W, so this point is north of it. Chukotka's coast lies at about 66°N, 170°W, and the Alaskan coast near Cape Prince of Wales is at 65.6°N, 168°W. So 65°N, 169°W is open water in the Anadyr Strait / Chirikov Basin area, where depths are roughly 40–50 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -45 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

2176.text:
The point 65.0°N, 147.0°W is in interior Alaska, east of Fairbanks (about 64.84°N, 147.72°W). It lies near the Tanana Valley and the Chena River area, toward the Tanana Flats and the Richardson Highway near Salcha, which is around 64.47°N, 146.9°W. At 65.0°N, 147.0°W the terrain is in the hills north of the Tanana, probably near the Chatanika/Chena divide, in the Yukon-Tanana uplands. Elevation there is about 300-500 m.

Chena Hot Springs Road runs near 65.05°N, 146.05°W. The Steese Highway near Fox is at 65.0°N, 147.6°W at about 300 m. At 147.0°W the point sits in the hills between the Chena and Chatanika rivers, near Cleary Summit (65.05°N, 147.4°W, 600 m). I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

5721.text:
Location: 27.0N, 103.0E is in northeastern Yunnan/Guizhou border region, near Zhaotong/Huize area. Zhaotong is at 27.34N, 103.72E. At 103.0E, 27.0N, this is near Huize county (26.4N, 103.3E) and Qiaojia (26.9N, 102.9E) on the Jinsha River. Qiaojia county town is at ~850 m elevation, in the Jinsha valley. The Jinsha river around 102.9-103.0E... Baihetan dam is at 27.2N, 102.9E. The reservoir there has a surface of about 825 m. At 27.0N, 103.0E, it may be in the mountains east of the Jinsha, such as Dongchuan/Huize highlands, with elevation around 2000 m. The Xiaojiang river valley is nearby, but it lies further south. The area east of Qiaojia, around Yaoshan, is rugged, with elevations of 2000-2500 m.

I'll guess Solid, 2200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

12051.text:
This location, 43°S 163°E, is in the Tasman Sea southwest of New Zealand, west of Fiordland. The South Island's west coast sits near 167°E at this latitude, so 163°E is roughly 350 km offshore.

The seafloor there is part of the Tasman Basin margin near the Puysegur Trench and Macquarie Ridge region, which lies further south at about 160-166°E and 47-50°S. At 43°S 163°E the water is probably abyssal, around 4000 m deep. The Tasman Basin floor is about 4500 m deep in the center, and the Challenger Plateau and Lord Howe Rise are shallower and further north. West of Fiordland the Fiordland margin slopes down to roughly 3000-4000 m. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2636.text:
Latitude 61.0°N, longitude 53.0°E is in the Komi Republic or Perm Krai region of Russia. It is near the Vychegda River basin, in the Komi Republic's southern part, west of the Urals. The terrain there is flat taiga with elevations of around 100-150 m. The Vychegda flows near 61°N at 53-54°E, with river levels around 80-90 m, and the surrounding land is probably around 120 m.

I'm estimating the surface as land, at about 110 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":110}

5361.text:
Latitude 31.0 N, longitude 103.0 E is in Sichuan, China. It lies near Wenchuan/Lixian, west of Dujiangyan and Wenchuan (Wenchuan is at about 31.47 N, 103.58 E). The point is near Mount Siguniang to the northwest, and Wolong is at about 31.0 N, 103.2 E. Wolong sits around 1,900 m, so 103.0 E would be in the mountains just east of Wolong, probably near Gengda or Yingxiu. Yingxiu is at 31.06 N, 103.48 E, so that is farther east.

Between Wolong and Dujiangyan (103.6 E), 103.0 E is west of Wolong's Gengda, in the Qionglai Mountains toward Balang Shan. Balang Shan pass is at about 30.97 N, 102.88 E, at 4,500 m. The point at 103.0 E is east of that pass on the Wolong side, in the Pitiao River valley, at roughly 3,500 m. I'll estimate 3,600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3600}

13513.text:
The point at 61°S, 153°W is in the South Pacific Ocean, in the Pacific-Antarctic Basin. It lies south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which runs roughly along 55-65°S in this longitude range. At 150°W the ridge crest is around 57-58°S, so 61°S would be on its flank. Depths there are probably about 3,000-4,000 m, with the abyssal plain deeper still, near 4,000-5,000 m.

The ridge crest at 150°W is near 56-57°S and sits at roughly 2,500 m depth. Moving south to 61°S at 153°W is about 4-5° of latitude away, or about 500 km. Flank subsidence at the fast spreading rate here (about 90 mm/yr half-rate) would give roughly 3,500-4,000 m depth. I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14835.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -29 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, at the Filchner Ice Shelf front area. The Filchner-Ronne ice shelf spans roughly longitudes -30 to -60 at around -78 to -75 latitude. At -29, -75 the point is near the eastern edge of the Filchner Trough, around Berkner Island's eastern side or the Brunt Ice Shelf region. The Brunt Ice Shelf lies near -75.5, -26, so this point is probably close to it, and the area north of Filchner Ice Shelf is at about -76.

Longitude -29 at latitude -75 is probably in the Filchner Trough, whose seabed is around -500 to -1000 m. The Filchner Trough runs along about -35 to -30 longitude, with depths of about 600-1100 m. At -75, -29 it is probably near the eastern flank of the trough or the continental shelf. The Luitpold Coast is near -78, -30, and the Caird Coast runs at about -75 along longitude -20 to -25. At -29, -75 the point is probably over the continental shelf, covered by ice shelf or sea. I'll classify it as Water with a seabed depth of about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

13590.text:
The point (-61.0, 1.0) is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Southwest Indian Ridge and north of the Antarctic coast. It sits near the Atlantic-Indian sector, close to the Bouvet triple junction region. Bouvet Island is at about 54.4°S, 3.4°E, so this point is about 6.6° south of it. The American-Antarctic Ridge lies to the southwest and the Southwest Indian Ridge to the east-northeast, starting at around 55°S, 0°E.

At 61°S, 1°E the seafloor is probably the Enderby Basin or Weddell Basin margin, or possibly the flank of the Maud Rise region. Maud Rise is at about 65°S, 3°E, so it is farther south. The depth here is probably about 4500 to 5000 m, since the abyssal plain south of the ridge is typically around 4800 m. I'll estimate -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

1010.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude 41.0 E lies in the Barents Sea, northeast of Svalbard. Franz Josef Land is farther east, at roughly 45–65 E, and Kvitøya is at about 80.1 N, 32.5 E. The point is therefore probably open water north of the Svalbard-Franz Josef Land region, in the northern Barents Sea.

Water depths in the northern Barents Sea near 79 N, 41 E are about 200–350 m. This area is east of Kong Karls Land (about 79 N, 26 E) and not far from the Orvin Land / Victoria Island area. Victoria Island is at about 80 N, 36 E. Depths here are probably around 250 m, so I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

13698.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -143 lies in the Pacific sector of the Southern Ocean, north of Marie Byrd Land. It is far from any land, in the southeast Pacific, and sits near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from about 65°S, 175°W up toward 55°S, 140°W. At -63 latitude and -143 longitude, the area is likely the abyssal plain or the flanks of the ridge. The Udintsev fracture zone lies near 56°S, 145°W, so this point is south of it. The ridge axis near 63°S is around 170°W, so at 143°W the seafloor would be older and deeper, roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m. I estimate about -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

3872.text:
The coordinates 47.0°N, 5.0°E fall in eastern France, in the Burgundy region near the Saône valley. Locating it more precisely: Dijon is at 47.32°N, 5.04°E, and Beaune is at 47.02°N, 4.84°E. So 47.0°N, 5.0°E lies just east of Beaune, toward the Saône plain near Seurre and Chaussin. The Saône at Seurre is at about 47.0°N, 5.15°E, at an elevation of about 180 m. A point at 5.0°E would be on the plain, around 190 m.

The point is land, so the surface type is Solid, and I estimate the elevation at about 190 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":190}

2518.text:
Latitude 63.0 N, longitude 177.0 E is in northeastern Russia (Chukotka), inland of the Gulf of Anadyr. Anadyr city is at about 64.7 N, 177.5 E. The Gulf of Anadyr coast lies around 64 N, so 63.0 N is south of the gulf, in the Bering Sea. The Koryak coast near Cape Navarin sits at about 62.3 N, 179 E. Olyutorsky is farther south and west. Land at 63 N, 177 E would be the Anadyr Gulf's southern shore, which sits near 62.5 N, 177 E. The gulf's southwestern shore, around Meinypilgyno, is at about 62.5 N, 177 E.

Looking at the coastline, Cape Navarin is at about 62.3 N, 179.2 E. West of it the coast runs southwest to the Olyutorsky Gulf and Korf. Anadyr Bay's southern shore runs from Cape Navarin west to about 62.5 N, 177 E, then north toward Anadyr Bay's head near 64 N, 177 E. So 63.0 N, 177.0 E is probably at the coast of the bay, near the Meinypilgyno area. The bay's western coast lies around 62.9 N, 177.5 E. At 177.0 E, 63 N could be land, as the Gulf of Anadyr's southwest shore near Meynypylgyno (62.5 N, 177.0 E) is on the coast. The Khatyrka River mouth is near 62.0 N, 175 E. Taken together, 63.0 N, 177.0 E is probably inland just north of Meinypilgyno, at low elevation, so I'll call it solid at about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

5318.text:
Latitude 31.0, longitude 17.0 is in the Gulf of Sidra (Sirte), off the Libyan coast. The Libyan coast near Sirte is at about 31.2°N, 16.6°E, and the coast at 17°E lies around 30.9–31.0°N. The Gulf's southernmost point is near Ghadames/Brega at about 30.4°N, 19°E. Near Sirte at 16.6°E the coast sits at about 31.2°N. At 17°E the coast is probably around 31.0–31.1°N, which makes this point very close to the shoreline. It could be land at a low elevation of a few meters, or shallow water.

The coast runs from Sirte (31.2°N, 16.59°E) southeast to Ras Lanuf (30.5°N, 18.5°E). Linear interpolation puts the coast at 17°E at roughly 31.2 − 0.4 × (0.7/1.9) ≈ 31.05°N. So 31.0°N is slightly south of the coast, which means land. The coast near Abu Hadi and Sirte is low, so the elevation is probably around 10 m. That puts it a little inland, with the coast around Ghazala, where elevation is about 10 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":12}

13762.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -15 lies in the Weddell Sea region, northeast of the Antarctic Peninsula and west of the Maud Rise. It is open ocean, roughly in the South Atlantic sector of the Southern Ocean. The area is near the Weddell Basin and the southwestern edge of the Weddell Abyssal Plain, so the seabed should be deep. Depths at 63°S, 15°W are probably around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The South Scotia Ridge lies farther west, near 40-50°W. At 15°W, 63°S the seabed is likely abyssal plain at about -4,800 m.

This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

8106.text:
The point (-1.0, -167.0) is in the central Pacific, south of the equator and west of the Phoenix Islands (Kanton is at about -2.8, -171.7). I don't know of any land at this position, so it is open ocean.

The seabed in this region is probably about 5000 m deep. The area lies between the Phoenix Islands and the Line Islands, and the basin there is roughly 5000–5500 m deep. I'll estimate -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

1457.text:
Latitude 73.0 N, longitude -145.0 W is in the Beaufort Sea, north of Alaska's coast (about 70.3 N at that longitude). That puts it roughly 300 km offshore, beyond the continental shelf break (the shelf edge is near 71 N in this area). The Canada Basin continental slope and rise lie further north, so at 73 N the seabed is probably the abyssal plain or the lower slope.

Bathymetry in the Beaufort Sea at 73 N, 145 W is around 3,000 m deep. At 72 N, 145 W the depth is about 2,500 m. At 73 N it is likely about 3,600 m, since the Canada Basin floor is roughly 3,800 m deep. I estimate about 3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

3420.text:
The point is 51.0°N, 179.0°W, in the Bering Sea / North Pacific near the Aleutian Islands. The Rat Islands group lies around 51.8°N, 178–179°E, and Amchitka is at about 179.3°E, so this longitude is just east of the 180° line. The Andreanof Islands start near 179°W: Semisopochnoi is at 179.6°E, Amatignak at 179.1°W (51.25°N), and Tanaga at 178°W (51.9°N). Amatignak is at about 51.26°N, 179.1°W, so the point 51.0°N, 179.0°W lies about 28 km south of it, which puts it in the Pacific south of the island chain.

The Aleutian Trench lies south of the islands, and the shelf drops off quickly. About 30 km south of Amatignak the seabed is probably on the slope, with depths of roughly 2000–3000 m. The trench axis is around 50°N at this longitude, with depths near 7000 m. At 51.0°N I estimate about 3000 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

710.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 161°E is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands and the East Siberian Sea shelf edge. That area lies near the Lomonosov Ridge and the Amundsen Basin side. Lomonosov Ridge is around 140°E at 87°N, and at 83°N, 161°E the seabed is near the continental slope north of the Laptev/East Siberian shelf.

The shelf break there is at about 80°N, 160°E. By 83°N the seabed is on the Arctic Ocean slope descending into the Amundsen Basin or the Lomonosov Ridge flank. Depths there are probably about 2500-3500 m. The Makarov Basin lies on the other side of the ridge, with the ridge at roughly 85°N, 160°E. Since the ridge sits about 140-180°E at 81-88°N, 83°N 161°E could be in the Amundsen Basin side or the ridge's Eurasian flank. I'll guess about -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

1565.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 71.0°E lies in the Kara Sea, east of Novaya Zemlya and west of the Yamal and Gydan peninsulas. Yamal's northern tip (Cape Bely) is at about 73°N, 70°E, but Yamal's coast runs north to about 73.5°N near 70°E, and Bely Island sits at roughly 73.0°N, 70.0°E. At 71°E the coast would be near the Kara Sea side of the Ob Gulf, so the point is likely in the Kara Sea, west of the Ob and Gydan bays.

Yamal's western coast is at about 69°E at 70°N and about 66.5°E at 68°N, and the peninsula ends at about 73.5°N, 70°E. The Gydan/Ob Gulf coast lies around 72–73°E at 72°N. Longitude 71°E at 73°N is therefore probably just off the northern coast of Yamal, in the shallow Kara Sea. The depth there is likely about 20 m, but it could be land. Bely Island is at 73.0°N, 70.0°E, so 71°E is about 1° east, which is roughly 33 km, and that is open water to the east of the island. I'll choose water with a depth of about 20 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-20}

7495.text:
Latitude 7.0, longitude 51.0 places the point in the Indian Ocean, off the coast of Somalia. Somalia's easternmost point, Ras Hafun, is near 10.4°N, 51.4°E, and the coast at 7°N lies near 49.5°E (around Hobyo at 5.3°N, 48.5°E). Going north, the coast reaches about 50.8°E at 9°N. At 7°N the coast is about 49.8°E, so 51°E is roughly 120 km offshore.

The shelf there is narrow, and the seabed drops quickly to about 4000 m depth in the Somali Basin. About 1.2° offshore, the depth is probably around 3000–4000 m. I estimate -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

9362.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -175 is in the South Pacific. Wallis and Futuna sit near -13.3, -176.2, and Samoa near -172 at -13.9. Niuatoputapu (Tonga) is at about -15.95, -173.78. The point -15, -175 lies between Wallis and Tonga's Niuatoputapu, west of the Tonga Trench, so it is open ocean.

The seafloor there is probably around 2000-3000 m deep in the Tonga back-arc area, near the northern Lau Basin or Tonga Ridge. Bathymetry near -15, -175 is probably about -2500 m, perhaps a bit deeper. The Tonga Trench lies at about -173.5 at that latitude, so -175 is on the Tonga platform or slope west of the trench. The northern Tonga ridge (Niuatoputapu) is at -173.8. The area to its west, around -175, is the back-arc, which I estimate at about -2000 to -3000 m. I'll answer -2300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

8844.text:
The point is at 9°S, 131°W, in the central South Pacific. It lies east of the Marquesas Islands, which sit near 9°S, 139°W, and well west of the East Pacific Rise. That places it in open ocean over seafloor of moderate age.

The Marquesas Fracture Zone region has seafloor depths around 4,000 to 4,300 m. At 131°W the crust is roughly 50 million years old, and I recall depths in the area of about 4,200 m. I'll use a depth of about -4,250 m as an estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4250}

10802.text:
The point at 31°S, 175°W lies in the South Pacific, southwest of Tonga and north of New Zealand. The Kermadec Ridge and Trench lie to the west, around 178-179°W, and the trench runs from about 176°W at 30°S. The Kermadec Trench at 31°S is near 177°W, so 175°W is east of the trench, on the Pacific plate abyssal plain. The depth there is probably about 5000 to 5800 m. The Louisville seamount chain crosses near 168-170°W at 31°S, so it is not at this point. The trench axis near 31°S is at roughly 176.5°W, and the area just east of it is about 5500 m deep, so 175°W (about 1.5° east, around 140 km) would be around 5500 m deep, perhaps 5300 m.

I estimate the depth at about 5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

15680.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -139 lies in West Antarctica, near the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 85°S in some areas, and at longitude -139 (139°W) it lies near the Edward VII Peninsula and Marie Byrd Land side. The Ross Ice Shelf's southern portion, around 85°S 139°W, is near the Reedy Glacier, Mercer and Whillans ice streams, and the Transantarctic Mountains edge. The Transantarctic Mountains are at about 150-170°W at 85°S. At 139°W, 85°S, this is probably the ice shelf's eastern edge or the Marie Byrd Land interior near the Ross Ice Shelf grounding line.

The Ross Ice Shelf at 180° to 150°W extends to about 85°S, and the Siple Coast runs from about 150°W to 140°W. Kamb Ice Stream is at about 84.5°S, 145°W. Near 85°S 139°W, the ice is probably grounded, since this is the upper Kamb/Bindschadler area. The ice surface there is about 700-1000 m. I'd guess Solid, around 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

6087.text:
The coordinates 23.0°N, 115.0°E fall in Guangdong province, China, near Huizhou/Heyuan/Shanwei. Huizhou is at about 23.1°N, 114.4°E and Shanwei at about 22.8°N, 115.4°E. Lufeng is at about 22.95°N, 115.65°E, and Wuhua and Haifeng are in the vicinity. So the point is inland, in the hilly region between Huizhou and Jieyang (the Boluo/Haifeng/Wuhua area).

Hills in that area typically reach 100–400 m. Since the point is likely near Haifeng or Hailing, on the border of Huizhou and Shanwei, I estimate an elevation of around 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

12650.text:
The point -51.0, -79.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. The Chile Rise and Chile Trench lie to the east and south, and the trench is near 75-76°W at about 51°S. The Chile Triple Junction is near 46°S, so at 51°S the trench axis sits around 75°W. A longitude of 79°W is therefore about 300 km west of the trench.

This area is on the Nazca/Antarctic plate, where the seafloor depth is typically 3500-4000 m. The outer trench slope and abyssal plain near the trench there are about 3500-4000 m deep. The trench itself is shallower in this area because of sediment fill, roughly 3000-4000 m. I estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11425.text:
The point is at 37°S, 9°W, in the South Atlantic. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits around 14°W at this latitude, and east of Tristan da Cunha, which is at about 37°S, 12°W. Tristan sits on the ridge region, and the ridge axis near 37°S is around 13–14°W. So 9°W is east of the ridge, on the African-side flank.

The seafloor there is probably about 4000 to 4500 m deep. The ridge flanks at roughly 5 Myr age are about 3500 to 4000 m deep. The Walvis Ridge is farther north, near 30°S, so it is not a factor here. The Guyot/Tristan hotspot track runs toward the Walvis Ridge to the northeast, but at 37°S the seafloor east of the ridge is about 4000 m deep. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

16113.text:
This location is at 89°S, 7°E, close to the South Pole, on the Antarctic plateau. The ice surface there is about 2800 m above sea level. The South Pole itself sits at about 2835 m. At 89°S along 7°E, which is toward Dronning Maud Land, the surface rises slightly. I estimate roughly 2900 m.

The ice sheet is grounded here, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

13048.text:
The point is at 55°S, 3°W in the South Atlantic. It lies south of the Bouvet Island area (54.4°S, 3.4°E) and west of it, and north of the Weddell Sea. The location is near the southern end of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, close to the American-Antarctic Ridge and the Bouvet triple junction region.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge crest sits near 0° to 5°W at around 55°S. At 3°W the seabed could be on the ridge flank, with depths around 2500 to 3500 m. The Bouvet triple junction is near 54.5°S, 1.5°W, so this point is close to the ridge axis and likely fairly shallow. I'll estimate about -3000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -3000}

3778.text:
The point is 49°N, 177°E, which is in the North Pacific. It lies south of the Commander Islands and east of Kamchatka. It is also near the Emperor Seamount chain region and the Aleutian Basin, though the Emperor chain sits further east at about 170°E to 171°E at 49°N.

Around 177°E, 49°N, the northwest Pacific abyssal plain lies south of the Aleutian Trench. The Meiji seamount region is further north, at about 53°N. The seafloor in this area is about 5400 to 5500 m deep. Along 49°N, the Emperor Seamounts are near 170°E to 171°E, and the Kamchatka Basin's deep plain sits to the west at about 5000 m. I estimate a depth of about 5500 m, so the elevation is about -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

8430.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 121.0 falls in Sulawesi, Indonesia. That puts it in the southeastern part of the island's central region, near Lake Matano and Lake Towuti. Lake Towuti is at about -2.75, 121.5, and Lake Matano is at about -2.5, 121.3. Malili is near -2.6, 121.1.

At -3.0, 121.0 the point is south of Matano, in the mountains between Wotu and Towuti, probably in North Luwu or East Luwu, near the Kolaka border. I'd estimate the terrain there as mountainous at roughly 600 m. Lake Towuti's western shore lies near 121.4, so this point is not in the lake.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":650}

11224.text:
Latitude -35, longitude -51 is in the South Atlantic, off the coast of southern Brazil and Uruguay. The Uruguay coast near Chuy sits around -33.7, -53.5, and Rio Grande do Sul's coast near -32, -52. At latitude -35, the coast is around longitude -54 to -55 (Uruguay's Rocha/Maldonado coast). So -51 is about 300 km offshore, on the continental shelf/slope.

The shelf edge in this area lies near 1000 m depth or so, around -35, -51. The shelf there is wide, with the shelf break around 150 m depth near longitude -53 at this latitude. At -51, which is farther out, the area is on the continental slope, with depth around 1500-2000 m. I estimate about -1300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1300}

8231.text:
Latitude -1, longitude 83 lies in the central Indian Ocean, southwest of Sri Lanka and east of the Maldives. The region sits near the Central Indian Basin, where depths are typically 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Ninetyeast Ridge is at about 90°E, so 83°E is well west of it. The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge is at about 73°E, so it is also far away. That leaves the Central Indian Basin, where the seabed is around 4,500 m deep at this latitude and longitude.

I am estimating from memory of regional bathymetry, not from a lookup, so I'll use a depth of about 4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

2914.text:
The point 57.0°N, 111.0°W is in northeastern Alberta, northeast of Fort McMurray. That puts it in boreal plains near the Athabasca River region, east of the Athabasca oil sands. The Athabasca River is at roughly 111.4°W near 57°N, which is west of the point. The surrounding terrain is mostly flat, with elevations around 500-600 m. The Stony Mountain and Birch Mountains are further west, and the area near 57°N, 111°W lies in the Athabasca plain, where I estimate about 580 m. The Christina River region is south of there, so this is likely upland with muskeg.

I could not identify any lake at this exact location, so I classify it as solid ground.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":570}

3842.text:
Latitude 47.0, longitude -55.0 is in the Atlantic, southeast of Newfoundland. The Burin Peninsula's southern tip sits near 47.0N, 55.2W, so the point is just east of it, though its coast is around -55.0 to -55.4 at that latitude. Placenta Bay and Fortune Bay lie nearby, and Fortune is at about 47.07N, 55.85W. St. Pierre and Miquelon are near 46.8N, 56.2W.

The Burin Peninsula's eastern coast runs roughly from Marystown (47.17N, 55.15W) down to Lamaline (47.0N, 55.8W). That puts the peninsula's east side at about -55.1 at latitude 47.2, so 47.0N, 55.0W is likely in Placentia Bay's mouth, in the water east of the peninsula. Marystown is in Mortier Bay at 55.15W, and Burin is at 47.03N, 55.1W, so the point is just east of Burin, in the water. Depth there is probably about 100 m, perhaps a bit more, so I'll estimate -110 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

4106.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude 113.0 is in the Gobi/Mongolia region, or possibly the Montana/Idaho border. Longitude 113.0 is east, which is positive by default and points to Mongolia.

Mongolia at 45°N, 113°E sits in Dornogovi or Sukhbaatar province, in the Gobi steppe. Elevations there are about 900-1100 m. Near Sainshand (44.9°N, 110.1°E) the elevation is about 930 m. Near Choir and Mandalgovi the elevation is about 1400 m. At 113°E, 45°N, close to Bayan-Uul and Erdene, I estimate roughly 950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":960}

5.text:
This location is at 89°N in the Arctic Ocean, near the North Pole and on the Lomonosov Ridge side of the basin. The longitude of -169° puts it on the Chukchi/Alpha-Mendeleev side of the pole, but at 89°N the longitude matters little. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near the pole along roughly 140°E to 40°W, and the Amerasian side of the ridge sits at about 170°W to 10°E. At 89°N, -169° lies about 111 km from the pole, in the direction opposite 11°E.

The Lomonosov Ridge near the pole has depths of about 1000–1500 m on its crest, and the adjacent Amundsen Basin is about 4200 m deep. Which side of the ridge this point falls on is uncertain. Near the pole the ridge axis runs roughly from Greenland (about 20°W) to the East Siberian side (about 160°E). The meridian pair 160°E/20°W is close to the line through -169° (equivalently 11°E on the opposite side). Because -169° is near the 160°E/20°W axis, the point is likely over the ridge or just on the Makarov Basin side. The Makarov Basin side is about 4000 m deep, and the ridge crest is about 1200–1700 m deep. I'll estimate a depth of about 2000 m, given the ridge slope.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

5227.text:
The point 31°N, 165°W is in the North Pacific, northeast of the Hawaiian Ridge and Midway, in open ocean. The seafloor there is abyssal plain at roughly 5,500 m depth. The area north of Hawaii at 31°N, 165°W lies west of the Hawaiian Islands, whose chain runs northwest toward Kure at about 28.4°N, 178°W. At 165°W the ridge is near 23°N, so this point is well north of it, on the abyssal plain at about 5,300 to 5,600 m depth.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5500}

14111.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -37 lies in the Weddell Sea region off the Antarctic coast. Queen Maud Land is east of about 20°W, and the Antarctic Peninsula is at about 60°W. At -37° longitude the coast is near 77°S (Filchner-Ronne ice shelf front, Coats Land). At -67° latitude we are therefore in the open Weddell Sea, far from the coast.

The Weddell Sea there is deep, roughly 4,000 to 5,000 m. The Weddell Abyssal Plain lies around 65-70°S, 30-40°W, with depths of about 4,500 to 5,000 m. At -67, -37 I estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

6851.text:
This location (13°N, 157°W) is in the open North Pacific, south of the Hawaiian Islands and well away from any land, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain, typically about 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. The area lies in the Central Pacific basin south of Hawaii, where depths are around 5,300 m. I'm estimating from the regional depth range, not from a lookup of this exact point, so I'll use -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

14578.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 177 is in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica, off Cape Adare and north of the Ross Ice Shelf. Cape Adare sits near 170°E and -71.3°. At 177°E, -71° the point is on the continental shelf in the western Ross Sea, probably close to the Pennell Bank / Iselin Bank area. Iselin Bank is around 175-180°E and -70 to -73°, with depths of about 500 m. The shelf break lies near -69 to -70° there. At -71° and 177°E, the seabed is probably the Iselin Bank at a few hundred meters depth, or the continental slope.

The Ross Sea continental shelf edge near 180° is at about -72°, so -71° is likely on the Iselin Bank, with depths of 400-600 m. I'll estimate -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

4658.text:
The point 39.0°N, 137.0°E lies in the Sea of Japan, northwest of the Noto Peninsula (about 37.5°N, 137°E) and west of Sado Island (about 38°N, 138.3°E). Honshu's coast at that longitude is around 36.8°N at Toyama Bay, so 39°N is well offshore.

The area is in the Japan Basin or near the Yamato Basin. The Yamato Basin lies roughly at 38–40°N, 134–137°E, with depths around 2000–3000 m. Near 39°N, 137°E the eastern margin of the Yamato Rise and the Japan Basin edge are close. The Mogami Trough sits near 39°N, 139°E. The Sea of Japan's depth at 39°N, 137°E is probably about 2500 m, so I estimate -2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

4587.text:
Latitude 39.0 N, longitude -5.0 W is in central Spain, in the province of Ciudad Real or Badajoz. Longitude -5.0 at latitude 39.0 falls near the Sierra de Guadalupe or the Montes de Toledo area. Ciudad Real province's western part and Badajoz's eastern part (La Serena) lie around -5.0. At 39.0 N, -5.0 W, the point is near Cabeza del Buey (38.72, -5.22) and Almadén (38.78, -4.85). It is north of both, in the Sierra de Almadén / Montes area close to Cañamero or Puebla de Don Rodrigo (39.08, -4.62). Terrain here is about 600 m.

The Guadiana valley lies around 39.0 N, -5.0 W near the Orellana reservoir (38.98, -5.5), which is farther west. At -5.0, the area is in the Sierra de Siruela and Montes de Toledo southern flank, roughly 600 m. I'll estimate 620 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":620}

5660.text:
The point 27.0°N, 19.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, west of the Sahara coast and north of the Canary Islands (Hierro is at about 27.7°N, 18°W). It is roughly 100 km west-southwest of Hierro, so it sits in the deep abyssal plain or on the lower flank of the Canary volcanic rise.

Seabed depth near there is about 3,500–4,000 m. West of Hierro the depth reaches roughly 4,000 m at 19°W. I'll estimate 3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11498.text:
Latitude -37, longitude 137 lies in the Southern Ocean south of Australia. Kangaroo Island is near 137°E but at about -35.8°, so this point is south of it, off the coast. The continental shelf off Kangaroo Island extends to roughly -37°, and the shelf break lies about 60 to 100 km south of the island's south coast. The island's south coast is at about -36.05°, so -37° is about 105 km south of it. That puts the point near or just past the shelf break, on the upper continental slope, where depth is perhaps 1000 to 2000 m.

The Sorell and Lacepede area south of Kangaroo Island has a shelf edge at about 200 m depth around -36.6°. At -37° the point is probably on the slope, and I estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

10445.text:
Latitude -27, longitude -169 lies in the South Pacific, west of the Cook Islands and south of Niue (Niue is at about -19, -169.9). Nothing at -27 is land. The nearest features are the Austral seamounts and the Pacific abyssal plain. The seafloor there is probably about 5000 m deep, perhaps 4800 to 5500 m, since the region lies between the Tonga Trench to the west and the Austral chain to the east.

I estimate the seabed at -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

13022.text:
Latitude -55, longitude -55 is in the South Atlantic, southeast of Tierra del Fuego and roughly 600 km east-southeast of the Falkland Islands' southern region. The Falklands lie near 51-52°S, 58-60°W. The Burdwood Bank is near 54.5°S, 56-60°W, so -55, -55 is just east of it.

East of Burdwood Bank, the seafloor drops toward the North Scotia Ridge region and the Georgia Basin. The Burdwood Bank is at about 54°S and extends to roughly 55°W, with depths of 100-200 m on the bank. At 55°S, the point lies south of the bank, on its southern slope toward the Scotia Sea, where depths reach about 3000 m. The Falkland Trough lies south of Burdwood Bank, and its depth is around 3000 m. The location is probably on the slope or in the trough. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

11809.text:
The point (-41, 39) lies in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Africa and southwest of the Crozet Islands. It is south of the Del Caño Rise and near the Indian-Antarctic Ridge region. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs roughly from the Prince Edward Islands (around -46, 38) to the northeast, so the area is probably the Crozet Basin or Madagascar Basin.

At -41 latitude, 39 E the ridge may be a bit south. The Southwest Indian Ridge sits near -45 at 35 E and about -39 at 50 E, so at 39 E it is around -42. This point could therefore be on the ridge flank, with depths of about 3000-4000 m. I estimate about -3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

12747.text:
The point at 51°S, 115°E lies in the Southern Ocean, southwest of Australia. It sits south of the Naturaliste Plateau and in the area of the Southeast Indian Ridge's northern flank. The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins further south, around 55°S, and the ridge crest runs from roughly 50°S, 115°E toward the southeast.

The Southeast Indian Ridge crosses near 48°S, 100°E and continues to about 50°S, 115°E, and then 52°S, 125°E. At 115°E the ridge is probably around 49–50°S, so 51°S would be just south of the crest. Ridge flank depths there are about 3000–3500 m. The Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies further east, at 120–128°E. I estimate a depth of about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

3478.text:
Latitude 51.0, longitude -63.0 is in Quebec's Côte-Nord region, north of the Gulf of St. Lawrence. The coast near Natashquan is at about 50.2N, -61.8W. At 51N, -63W the point is inland, roughly 80 km north of the coast, near the Petit Mécatina river area. The lower Saint-Augustin river is around -58.8, so that river is not nearby.

The coast at longitude -63 is near 50.2N (Havre-Saint-Pierre is at 50.2N, -63.6W). At 51.0N the point is about 90 km inland, in the Canadian Shield with many lakes. Elevation there is around 400 m. The Romaine river basin sits near 50.7N, -63.2W with elevations of 300-500 m, and the Romaine reservoirs are at about 50.9N, -63.2W. Romaine-2 reservoir sits at about 243 m. Romaine-1 is at 82 m. Romaine-3 and Romaine-4 reservoirs are at 50.9N, -63.0W and 51.0N, -63.0W, so the point may fall in a reservoir. The Romaine-3 reservoir is near 50.7N, and Romaine-4 near 51.0N, -63.2W with a surface of 370 m. If the point is in a reservoir, the DEM may show the surface or a lakebed. I'll guess Solid at about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

12344.text:
Location: 47°S, 29°E is in the southwest Indian Ocean, near the Prince Edward Islands, which lie at about 46.9°S, 37.7°E. So 29°E is about 8° west of them, which puts it near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Del Caño Rise region.

The Del Caño Rise sits at about 45°S, 44°E, and the Conrad Rise is further east. At 29°E, 47°S, the Southwest Indian Ridge runs roughly from Bouvet at 3°E to the Prince Edward fracture zone near 35°E. The ridge axis near 47°S, 29°E could be at about 2500-3500 m depth. The Andrew Bain Fracture Zone is around 30°E, 50°S. So the point is likely on the ridge flank or in the Africa-side basin at about 3000-4000 m. I'll estimate -3200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

14390.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 161 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land / Oates Land. The Southern Ocean coast is near -69 to -70 at longitude 161. Oates Land coast is around -69.5 at 160E, near the Pennell Coast. Fairly close to the coast: the Rennick Glacier is at about 160E, -71, and the coast at 161E is near -70.5. Lat -69 at 161E is therefore likely inland ice sheet, in the Wilkes Land interior. Cape Adare is at 170E, so the Oates Land coast near 160E lies around -69.5 to -70. Longitude 161, lat -69: the coast there is about -68.5?

The Mertz Glacier is at 145E, -67.5. Oates Land coast near 158E runs around -69.5 (Pennell Coast starts at about 165E, -71). Around 160E the coast is about -69.3 (Cook Ice Shelf is at 152E). So -69 may be ocean, or may be right on the coast. The Southern Ocean continental shelf there would be about -500 m deep. I'm uncertain. The coast near Oates Land at 160E is roughly -69.8 (the Dillon/Tomilin glaciers). I'll go with Water, with a seabed elevation of about -1500 m given the continental slope.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

12665.text:
The point (-51, -49) is in the South Atlantic, east of the Patagonian margin and southeast of the Falkland Islands (Falklands are near -51.7, -59). At longitude -49 this is deep ocean in the Argentine Basin, north of the Falkland Plateau and the Malvinas/Falkland Escarpment. The Falkland Plateau lies roughly between -50 and -52 and extends east from the islands to about -45 to -50, with depths around 1000-2000 m. The Maurice Ewing Bank sits near -51, -46 at about 1200 m depth, so the plateau may reach this far east.

The Argentine Basin, north of the plateau, is about 5000 m deep. At -51, -49 the point is probably near the southern end of the basin, on the slope or the edge of the plateau. I estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

1602.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 145.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea, off the coast of the Siberian mainland near the Lena Delta and the Buor-Khaya area. The Lena Delta is at about 73°N, 126°E, and the New Siberian Islands are around 75°N, 140°E. At 73°N, 145°E the point is north of the Yana Bay coast, which is near 71°N, 136°E, and north of the Indigirka mouth, which is near 71.5°N, 150°E. The mainland coast at 145°E is at roughly 71.5°N, so 73°N is about 170 km offshore. That puts it in the East Siberian/Laptev shelf, where the sea is shallow.

The shelf here is probably about 30-50 m deep. The Laptev shelf at 73°N, 145°E is likely near 40 m. I'll estimate -35 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-35}

1445.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 169.0°W lies in the Chukchi Sea, north of Wrangel Island (which is at about 71°N, 179.5°W) and northwest of Point Barrow. The point is well offshore, in the Chukchi Sea or on the Chukchi shelf edge. Water depths there are about 40-60 m on the shelf. The Chukchi Plateau lies further north, at about 75°N and 170°W.

At 73°N, 169°W the seabed is probably around 50 m deep. The Herald Canyon is near 72-73°N and 175°W. The shelf in this area is approximately 40-50 m deep, so I estimate -50 m. This is an estimate from memory of regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

4611.text:
Latitude 39.0°N, longitude 43.0°E is in eastern Turkey, near Lake Van. Lake Van spans roughly 42.5–43.5°E and 38.3–39.0°N. Its northern shore lies at about 38.75°N near Ahlat and Adilcevaz, and the northernmost point is around 39.0°N near Adilcevaz at about 42.75°E. At 43.0°E the northern shore is near 38.65°N, so 39.0°N is probably on land.

The terrain north of Lake Van around 43°E, 39°N is near Süphan Dağı, which sits at about 42.83°E, 38.93°N. At 43.0°E, 39.0°N the ground is a plateau toward Malazgirt and Patnos, and Patnos is at 42.85°E, 39.23°N at about 1700 m. Just northeast of Süphan the terrain is likely 1900–2200 m. Erciş is at 43.36°E, 39.03°N with an elevation of about 1700 m, and the area between Adilcevaz and Erciş is probably 1700–2000 m. I estimate 1850 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1850}

16035.text:
This location is at 89°S, near the South Pole, which is on the East Antarctic ice sheet plateau. The South Pole station sits at about 2835 m ice surface elevation. At 89°S along longitude -149° (roughly 31°W of the 180° meridian, on the Ross Sea side), the surface elevation is about 2,800 m or a bit lower, since the ice descends toward the Ross Ice Shelf and the Transantarctic region. Longitude -149° is on the Marie Byrd Land side, so the ice surface is slightly lower, around 2,700 m.

I estimate about 2,750 m, which is a judgment from the pole elevation rather than a looked-up value. The surface here is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2750}

1921.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude 63.0 E is in northwestern Siberia, on the Yamal Peninsula's southern part or near the Baydaratskaya Bay area. The Baydaratskaya Bay lies at roughly 69-70°N, 66-67°E. The Yamal Peninsula spans about 66.5-73°N and 66-72°E, so 63°E is west of it. At 63°E and 69°N we are near the Kara Sea coast, west of Baydaratskaya Bay, around the Yugorsky Peninsula area (which lies at about 69.5°N, 62-66°E). The Yugorsky Peninsula is land with low hills. The coast at 69°N, 63°E is near the Kara Gate and Kara Sea coast, and Amderma sits at 69.75°N, 61.7°E. At 69.0°N, 63°E that would be inland, south of Amderma, in tundra with low hills, probably 50-100 m.

I estimate the elevation at around 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

15103.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 147 is in East Antarctica, in Victoria Land/Oates Land region inland from the Ross Sea coast. The Ross Sea coast near 77°S is around 163°E (McMurdo), so 147°E at 77°S is far inland on the plateau. This is near the Wilkes Land/Victoria Land interior, with ice surface elevation probably around 2000-2500 m.

Longitude 147°E at 77°S sits between Victoria Land (160-165°E) and the Wilkes Basin area. Surface elevation there is probably about 2300 m. The ice is grounded, so the answer is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

5213.text:
The point 33°N, 167°E lies in the western Pacific, east of Japan and north of Norfolk Island. It is in the Northwest Pacific basin, east of the Kyushu-Palau Ridge and the Izu-Ogasawara region. The area is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

Longitude 167°E at 33°N is far from Japan, which sits near 140°E. The Mariana Trench region is farther south, and the Marcus-Wake seamounts are around 20°N at 155–170°E. At 33°N, 167°E the seafloor is likely abyssal plain or low abyssal hills, with depths of about 5500–6000 m. The Shatsky Rise is near 32°N, 158°E, which is too far west. The Northwest Pacific basin near 33°N, 167°E is probably about 5600 m deep. I cannot confirm this against bathymetry data, so I am estimating from the regional setting.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

13945.text:
Location: -65°, -9° (9°W) is in the Weddell Sea sector off Dronning Maud Land. At 65°S, 9°W, this is offshore of the Fimbul Ice Shelf, which lies near 70°S, so 65°S is in open ocean, with the Maud Rise at about 65°S, 3°E. At 9°W the ocean depth is around 4500-5000 m, in the Weddell Basin or on the abyssal plain, and on the western side of the Maud Rise. Likely about -4800 m.

Seabed depth estimates in this area from GEBCO suggest roughly 4,500-5,000 m, so I'll go with -4900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

12089.text:
The point is at 45°S, 121°W, in the southeast Pacific Ocean. It lies west of Chile, roughly 3,000 km from the coast, on the Pacific plate. The East Pacific Rise is at about 115°W near 45°S, and the Chile Rise lies to the south, around 46°S. At 121°W the seafloor is on the Pacific plate flank of the rise, probably about 2,500 to 3,000 km from the rise crest. Crust there is young, so depth is roughly 3,000 to 3,500 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge sits near 55–65°S, so it is not a factor here.

Seafloor depth near 45°S, 121°W is therefore about 3,600 m, though this is an estimate from the tectonic setting, not a looked-up value. The Chile Rise-East Pacific Rise triple junction is near 36°S, 98°W, so this location is farther west and older. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at 45°S is near 112°W, which puts the point about 9° of longitude west of the ridge, roughly 700 km. At a spreading rate of about 70 mm/yr, that gives crust about 10 Myr old, with a depth of 2,500 + 350·√10 ≈ 3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

10610.text:
The coordinates -27.0, 161.0 fall in the Coral Sea / Tasman Sea region, east of Australia. The area is northwest of the Lord Howe Rise and southwest of the New Caledonia area. The Capel and Faust basins lie around 160°E, 27°S, and the Lord Howe Rise is near 162°E.

The Lord Howe Rise sits roughly between 159°E and 164°E at around 27°S. Its depth there is about 1000–2000 m. The Middleton and Elizabeth reefs lie near 29°S, 159°E, which is further south. At 27°S, 161°E the area is the Kenn Plateau/Capel Basin. The Capel Basin is about 2500–3000 m deep, and the Faust Basin is around 2500 m. The Lord Howe Rise itself is shallower, around 1000–1500 m, and I believe the point is on the western part of the rise or the edge of the Capel Basin. I'll estimate -2300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

3770.text:
Latitude 49.0°N, longitude 161.0°E lies in the northwest Pacific, southeast of the Kamchatka Peninsula and east of the Kuril Islands. The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs along roughly 50°N, 160°E, so I need to place this point relative to it.

The trench axis passes near 47°N, 155°E and then near 50°N, 160°E, and the point sits near that axis. Near 49°N the axis is at about 159.5°E. At 161°E the point is probably on the trench's outer wall or slightly east of the axis, in water about 6,000 to 7,000 m deep. The Kamchatka coast lies at about 156°E, so this is well offshore, and the Kuril Islands are farther southwest. I estimate a depth of about -6,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-6500}

1487.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude -85.0 is in the Canadian Arctic. Bylot Island lies at about 73.0°N, 79°W, and Baffin Island's northern tip is near 73°N, 80–88°W. Prince Regent Inlet is around 73°N, 90°W. Admiralty Inlet runs from about 72°N, 86°W northward, and the Brodeur Peninsula is near 73°N, 88°W.

Taking the geography more carefully: Brodeur Peninsula spans roughly 72.5–73.8°N and 87–90°W. The Borden Peninsula (northern Baffin) lies at about 73°N, 80–86°W. Admiralty Inlet's western shore is at about 86–87°W at 72–73°N. So -85 at 73°N could fall on Admiralty Inlet water or on the Borden Peninsula land. Admiralty Inlet's mouth near 73.5°N is at about 85°W, and the inlet runs from about 71.5°N, 87°W northeast to 73.5°N, 85°W. At 73°N the inlet is probably centered near -86 or -85.5, so the point may be in the inlet, or on its east side, which is land. Strait depth is a few hundred meters, and I'm unsure. I'll guess Water with a seabed around -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

13281.text:
Latitude -57, longitude 103 lies in the Southern Ocean south of Australia, on the southern flank of the Southeast Indian Ridge. The Australian-Antarctic spreading ridge is around 50°S at this longitude, so -57 is on the Antarctic plate side, roughly 600–700 km south of the ridge. Seafloor depths there should be about 3500–4500 m.

Depth at 57°S, 103°E is probably near 4000 m. Some of this region is around 4,300 m, as the Australian-Antarctic Basin deepens further south. The ridge crest near 100°E sits at about 49–50°S, and the Kerguelen Plateau lies far to the west. I'll estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

9511.text:
Latitude -15, longitude 123 is off the northwest coast of Australia. The Kimberley coast sits around 124-126°E at this latitude, and Broome is at 122.2°E, 17.9°S. At -15°S, 123°E the point is offshore, west of the Kimberley coast (Collier Bay lies near 124°E, -16°S). The Kimberley coast near 15°S runs roughly from 125°E at Cape Londonderry down to 123.5°E at -16.5°S.

So the point is in the Timor Sea / Browse Basin, on the continental shelf. The Browse Island area is at 123.5°E, -14.1°S. The shelf there is about 100-200 m deep, and Scott Reef is near 121.8°E, -14°S. At 123°E, -15°S the depth is probably around 150 m. The shelf edge of the Browse Basin is at roughly 200 m, so the seabed here is likely around -120 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-120}

15386.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -7 is in the Weddell Sea sector of Antarctica, on the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf front lies near 75-77°S, so -81 at longitude -7 is probably on the Filchner ice shelf or the grounded region near the Foundation Ice Stream or Recovery Glacier area. Filchner Ice Shelf covers roughly 77-80°S at longitudes -30 to -45. At -7 longitude, -81 is in the Coats Land / Theron Mountains / Shackleton Range region (Shackleton Range is around -80.5, -25). Longitude -7 at -81 is inland of Coats Land, which is grounded ice sheet with a surface elevation of around 1500-2000 m.

The Recovery Glacier is near -81, -28. Longitude -7 is further east, in the ice sheet interior toward Queen Maud Land, and the surface there is probably around 2000 m. The ice surface is perhaps 2200 m, since the Dronning Maud Land plateau near -81, 0 is about 2500 m. I'll estimate 2000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2000}

2042.text:
Latitude 67.0, longitude -55.0 is in west Greenland. Kangerlussuaq is at 67.0N, -50.7W, so -55.0 is about 4.3° of longitude west of it. At 67°N, 1° of longitude is about 43.5 km, so that is roughly 187 km west. The coast of Greenland at this latitude lies near -53.5W (Davis Strait), which puts -55 about 65 km offshore in the Davis Strait.

The sea floor there is the continental shelf, with banks of roughly 200-500 m depth. Off Sisimiut (66.9N, -53.7W), the shelf extends out to roughly -55 to -56W. The shelf is about 100 km wide here, with depths of 100-400 m, and the shelf edge lies near -56W. I'll estimate about -350 m for the seabed.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

7338.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 97.0°E lies in the Andaman Sea, west of the Thai/Myanmar peninsula and east of the Nicobar Islands. The Andaman Sea basin there is roughly 3,000 m deep at the central basin. At 97°E, 9°N, the seabed is probably the Andaman Sea's western-central basin floor. The depth near the Mergui Basin is about 1,000-2,000 m at this longitude, and the Thai shelf edge is near 98°E. At 97°E, 9°N the depth is probably about 2,500 m.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the basin rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

14634.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -71 is in Antarctica, in the Bellingshausen Sea / Ronne Ice Shelf region. Alexander Island lies around 70-72°S, 68-72°W, and the George VI Sound sits near 71-72°S. At 73°S, 71°W, this is likely the Ronne Ice Shelf's western part or the Orville Coast area, near the Rydberg Peninsula and Ellsworth Land. The Orville Coast is at about 75°S, 62°W, and the Eltanin Bay coast is near 73°S, 80°W.

At 73°S, 71°W, the point is probably in the Ellsworth Land interior, on the grounded ice sheet around the Sweeney Mountains or the Latady Island area. Latady Island is near 70.5°S, 75°W. The Sweeney Mountains are near 73.5°S, 75°W, and the Dana Mountains near 74°S, 71°W. The Ronne Ice Shelf's western edge is near 74°S, 75°W, along the Orville Coast. So 73°S, 71°W is likely the ice sheet at the base of the Antarctic Peninsula, with a surface elevation of around 1000 m. The Lassiter Coast runs about 73°S, 61°W. Further west, at 71°W, 73°S, the area lies in the Palmer Land/Ellsworth Land interior, inland of the Rydberg Peninsula, probably at 1500 m. I'll guess 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

1218.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 97.0°E is in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Russia. The Taymyr Peninsula's northern coast lies near 77.0–77.7°N. Cape Chelyuskin is at 77.7°N, 104°E. The Taymyr Gulf and Maria Pronchishcheva Bay are around 100–110°E. Near 97°E, the coast of the Kara Sea sits around 76–76.5°N, including Cape Sterlegov and Sverdrup areas. The Nordenskiöld Archipelago is at about 76°N, 97°E. Just north, at 77°N, 97°E, the point is probably in the Kara Sea (open water), north of the Taymyr coast, which is at about 76.2°N near this longitude. Depth in this part of the Kara Sea is shallow, perhaps 50 m.

Zero land is likely here, since Cape Chelyuskin is the northernmost point of the mainland and the coast near 97°E is at about 76°N. Therefore the point is in water, at an estimated depth of about 50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

14003.text:
The point is at 65°S, 107°E, in East Antarctica, off the Budd Coast and Sabrina Coast region. The coast near 107°E lies at about 66.5°S, close to the Vanderford Glacier and the Law Dome area. Law Dome is at about 112°E, and the Vincennes Bay coast is at about 108°E, 66.5°S. So 65°S is offshore, over the ocean on the continental slope or rise.

The shelf edge in this region is around 65.5–66°S, so the continental slope is near 65°S. Seabed depth there is likely about 2000–3000 m. Sea ice is ignored. I estimate a depth of about -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

789.text:
Latitude 81.0, longitude -41.0 is in northeast Greenland, in the Arctic Ocean north of Greenland. The Greenland coast near there is Peary Land at about 83N, so 81N, 41W is farther south. The northeast Greenland coast around 81N sits at roughly 18-20W, and the ice sheet extends west of that to about 60W at this latitude. Longitude 41W at 81N is therefore well inland, on the ice sheet, which is a Solid surface.

The ice sheet surface in northern Greenland at 81N, 41W is around 2,500-2,700 m. The summit of the ice sheet is near 72N, 38W at 3,200 m. NEEM, at 77.45N, 51W, is about 2,450 m. North of Summit near 81N, 41W, the elevation is probably about 2,400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

10094.text:
The point is at 23°S, 151°W, in the South Pacific south of Tahiti. The Austral Islands (Tubuai, Rurutu, Raivavae) lie around 23°S, 150°W, with Rurutu at about 22.5°S, 151.3°W. The point at -23, -151 is about 50 km south of Rurutu, so it is most likely open ocean.

Rurutu sits on a seamount that rises from about 4000 m depth. Several seamounts in the area could be shallower, but the surrounding seafloor is typically 3500–4500 m deep. The Austral chain lies on seafloor of roughly 4000 m. I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11549.text:
Latitude -39, longitude -121 lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean. It is west of Chile, which is at about -73 longitude, and in the open ocean. The location is roughly on the Southeast Pacific basin, north of the Chile Rise and east of the East Pacific Rise.

The East Pacific Rise at 39°S is around 111-113°W, so -121 is west of the ridge, on the Pacific plate flank. The crust there is young, about 10 to 15 Myr, and the depth would be around 3,500 to 3,800 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is further south, and at this latitude the East Pacific Rise is at roughly 112°W, so the point is about 9° of longitude west of it. At 39°S that is about 700 km, and with a spreading half-rate of about 70 mm/yr the crust is about 10 Myr old. Depth is 2,600 + 350·√10 ≈ 3,700 m. I'll estimate -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

6704.text:
Latitude 15.0 N, longitude -91.0 W falls in Guatemala. The Pacific coast of Guatemala lies near 14°N, so this point is inland, in the highlands. It is near Quiché and Baja Verapaz, close to Santa Cruz del Quiché (15.03, -91.15), whose elevation is about 2000 m. Slightly east, at -91.0, is the area near Uspantán and Cunén, and the Sacapulas valley sits at about 15.3, -91.1.

The point at 15.0, -91.0 is probably near the Chixoy river valley, between Joyabaj (14.99, -90.83) and Santa Cruz del Quiché. Joyabaj is at about 1,000–1,300 m. Zacualpa is at 14.95, -91.1 and about 1,300 m. So 15.0, -91.0 is roughly 1,400 m, and the Río Negro/Chixoy canyon could be lower. I'll guess 1,350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1350}

8723.text:
The point (-7.0, -13.0) is in the South Atlantic, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and west of Ascension Island, which sits near 7.95°S, 14.4°W. At 7°S, 13°W we are about 100 km northeast of Ascension.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis is around 14°W at this latitude, so 13°W is just east of the axis, on young seafloor. The ridge flanks there are roughly 3000–4000 m deep. Ascension's base lies on the ridge, and the surrounding seafloor is about 3000 m deep. The Bode Verde fracture zone is near 7.5°S, and the Ascension fracture zone is near 7–8°S, so the point may fall in fracture-zone terrain. I estimate a depth of about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

14182.text:
Location: -67, 105E is in East Antarctica, off the Wilkes Land coast near the Shackleton Ice Shelf region (which is around 95-100E, -66.5). At 105E, -67, the coast is near the Denman Glacier/Vanderford area (Vanderford is ~109E, -66.5). The coast near 105E is around -66.3 to -66.5, so -67 is probably inland, on grounded ice about 60-80 km from the coast. Ice surface elevation there is probably around 1000-1500 m.

Given the Law Dome at 112E, and the area at 105E being the Totten/Denman sector, the ice surface at -67 would be around 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

13770.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 1 E lies in the Southern Ocean, south of the Southwest Indian Ridge (which is around 50-55°S at this longitude) and north of Antarctica. This area sits in the Weddell Sea / Enderby Basin region. At 63°S, 1°E, the seabed is likely the abyssal plain of the Weddell-Enderby Basin, roughly 4,000-5,000 m deep. The Maud Rise is at about 65°S, 3°E, with its top near 2,000 m depth. At 63°S it is probably on the northern flank of Maud Rise or the surrounding basin, so a depth around -4,500 m is plausible.

GEBCO values near 63°S, 0°E are around -4,900 m or so. I'll estimate -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

15922.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -15 lies in Antarctica, on the East Antarctic plateau close to the South Pole. The pole itself is at about 2835 m. Moving along the -15° meridian (the Greenwich side, toward Dronning Maud Land), the plateau rises toward the Pole-Dome Argus direction. At -87° the elevation is probably around 2500-2900 m.

The Foundation Ice Stream and the Recovery Glacier area lie near longitude -30 to -40 and latitude -82 to -85. At -87° and -15°, the area is likely the plateau south of the Thiel Mountains, which lie at about -85°, -80°. Elevation there is around 2700 m. I estimate 2750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2750}

11801.text:
Latitude -41, longitude 23 lies in the South Atlantic/Indian Ocean south of South Africa. Cape Agulhas is at about 34.8°S, 20°E, so 41°S, 23°E is roughly 700 km south of the coast. This area is south of the Agulhas Plateau and the Agulhas Bank. The Agulhas Plateau sits near 40°S, 26°E, with depths of about 2500 m. The point at 41°S, 23°E is probably on the western flank of the plateau or in the Agulhas Basin.

The Agulhas Plateau spans roughly 38–41°S and 24–27°E. At 23°E the seabed is likely at the edge of the plateau, with depths around 3500–4500 m. The Agulhas Basin to the south is about 5000 m deep. I'll estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

10409.text:
The point (-25.0, 119.0) is in Western Australia, in the Gascoyne/Murchison region near the Little Sandy Desert. It is inland, so the surface is solid ground. Elevation in this area is roughly 500 m, and I am estimating that from general knowledge of the region rather than from a lookup.

Nearby reference points: Newman is at about -23.4, 119.7 and sits at about 540 m. Meekatharra is at -26.6, 118.5 and sits at about 520 m. The Collier Range lies near -24.5, 119. The Little Sandy Desert at around -25, 120 is about 500 m. I'll use 520 m for this point.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 520}

8467.text:
The point (-5, -165) is in the central Pacific, south of the equator and east of the Phoenix Islands. It lies west of the Line Islands, near Kiribati, and is open ocean. Nearby land is Kiribati's Tabuaeran (Fanning) at 3.9°N, 159°W, which is far away, and Jarvis Island at 0.4°S, 160°W, which is also far. So this point is water.

Seafloor depth in this region is about 5000 to 5500 m. The area lies in the central Pacific basin between the Tokelau and Line Islands, and depths there are typically around 5200 m. I'll estimate -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

15909.text:
Latitude -87, longitude -41 is in Antarctica, close to the South Pole and on the East Antarctic plateau. The longitude -41 (41°W) runs toward the Filchner-Ronne side, so the point sits along the Foundation Ice Stream or Recovery Glacier direction, on the plateau slope.

The South Pole is at about 2835 m. At 87°S along 41°W, which is about 300 km from the pole toward the Weddell Sea, the surface is probably around 2500-2900 m. The Pensacola Mountains and the Thiel Mountains lie near 85°S and 80-90°W, which is a different direction. Along 41°W the ice rises toward the Dome Argus side only at much higher latitude, and the area near 87°S, 41°W is near the head of the Foundation Ice Stream. I estimate the surface at about 2700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2700}

13916.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -67 is in the Antarctic Peninsula region, near the Wilhelm Archipelago / Graham Coast. Booth Island is at about -65.08, -64.0, and Anvers Island is at about -64.6, -63.5. Longitude -67 at latitude -65 is west of Adelaide Island, which lies at about -67.2 latitude and -68.5 longitude. That puts the point in the Pitt Islands / Biscoe area, in open water of the Penola Strait or Grandidier Channel, probably the continental shelf west of the Argentine Islands.

The Argentine Islands are at -65.25, -64.25, so longitude -67 is about 1.2 degrees further west, roughly 50 km. That is the shelf outside the islands, with depths of about 300-500 m. The Biscoe Islands are at about -65.4, -65.5, so -65, -67 lies somewhat west and north of them, on the shelf. The shelf there has typical depths of around 400-500 m, so I'll estimate -450.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

4276.text:
Latitude 43.0, longitude 93.0 lies in western Mongolia / Gobi region, though most likely in Mongolia's Gobi-Altai, or possibly just across the border in China's Xinjiang/Gansu. The Mongolia-China border near 93°E runs around 42.5°N. So 43.0°N is in Mongolia, a bit north of the border, in the Gobi desert area south of the Altai. Terrain there is about 1,300 m (Gobi basin near Shinejinst, around 1,300–1,500 m).

I estimate 1,450 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 1450}

4889.text:
The point 35.0°N, 121.0°W lies off the California coast. Point Sal is at about 34.9°N, 120.67°W, and Point Arguello is at about 34.58°N, 120.65°W. At 35.0°N the coastline is near Pismo Beach and Oceano, at roughly 120.63°W. So 121.0°W is about 30 km west of the coast, offshore of Point Sal and Purisima Point, in open ocean.

The continental shelf here is fairly narrow. West of Point Sal at 121.0°W, depths are likely about 300-500 m. Morro Bay is at 35.37°N, 120.87°W, and the shelf there extends out to about 120.9°W. The Santa Maria basin slope at 121.0°W, 35.0°N probably sits at about 500 m depth. I'll estimate -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

11681.text:
Latitude -39.0, longitude 143.0 is in Bass Strait's western entrance, west of King Island (King Island is at about -39.9, 144.0). Cape Otway lies at about -38.85, 143.5. So the point is about 50 km west of Cape Otway, off the Victorian coast, in the ocean.

Depth there: the shelf off western Victoria is about 80-100 m deep at this longitude, and the Otway shelf edge is further west or south. At -39, 143, the depth is probably around 80 m. The seabed on the western Bass Strait shelf runs about 70-90 m, so I'll say -80.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-80}

12802.text:
The point at 53°S, 135°W lies in the South Pacific, southwest of South America and well away from any land, so the surface is Water.

Its position is north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and east of the East Pacific Rise's southern part. The East Pacific Rise at 53°S is around 140–150°W, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge there trends toward the southeast. The Rise crest near 55°S is around 140°W, so 135°W at 53°S would be on the eastern flank. The depth on the flank is probably about 3,500 to 4,000 m. Since the ridge crest is at roughly 2,500 to 3,000 m, a flank depth of about 3,700 m seems reasonable.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3750}

14588.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -163 is in the Ross Sea, within the Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf front sits at about 78°S, so 73°S is open ocean on the continental shelf, not the ice shelf. At 163°W, the area is north of Roosevelt Island (which is around 79.5°S). The Ross Sea continental shelf there is roughly 500 m deep, and the shelf edge is near 71-72°S. At 73°S, 163°W, the seafloor is probably around -500 to -600 m. In the western Ross Sea the banks are shallower, but at 163°W (the eastern Ross Sea) the depth is around 500-600 m.

I estimate -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

14458.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -63 is in the Weddell Sea sector, on the east side of the Antarctic Peninsula. The Larsen C Ice Shelf lies around 67-69°S, so -71 is further south. At -63 longitude and -71 latitude, the area is probably the Ronne Ice Shelf's western part, or the Rutford/Evans area. The Orville Coast and Lassiter Coast lie around 74-75°S with longitudes of -62 to -65. At 71°S, 63°W, the Antarctic Peninsula's east coast is near 64°W at 70°S. This point is probably Palmer Land plateau or just off the east coast, on the Larsen ice shelf.

Palmer Land's east coast near 71°S runs around 62°W (Dolleman Island is at 70.6°S, 60.9°W). The Gutenko Mountains are near 71.5°S, 63°W. The Lassiter Coast begins around 73°S. At 71°S, 63°W the point is likely on the Larsen/Wilkins ice shelf area, where the Larsen D ice shelf is floating. Alternatively it may be the grounded plateau, with an elevation of about 1500 m. The Gutenko Mountains and the Scar Inlet area suggest an east-coast location with floating ice at 63°W. Dolleman Island at 60.9°W is about 70 km from the coast, so the coast is near 62-63°W at 71°S. This is ambiguous, so I'll choose Solid with an elevation of about 1200 m on the ice flowing off the plateau.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

2754.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -71.0 is in northern Quebec (Nunavik), near Ungava Bay's western side. Ungava Bay lies around 59-61N, 68-70W. At -71 longitude, 59N is inland, near the Hudson Bay/Ungava divide. The coast of Hudson Bay is at about -78, and Ungava Bay's west coast at about -72 at 58.5N (Tasiujaq is at 58.7N, -69.9). Koksoak River is at 58.5N, -68.5. So -71, 59N is about 100 km west of Tasiujaq, inland, near the Leaf River area. Leaf River is around 58.7N, -70.5, and the Leaf Basin is low. Elevation there is roughly 100-200 m. Lac à l'Eau Claire is at 56N, so that's not relevant. There are many small lakes in the area, but the point is most likely land.

Estimated elevation is about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

12835.text:
Latitude -53.0, longitude -69.0 is in southern Patagonia, Chile, on Tierra del Fuego's western side. The Strait of Magellan runs roughly from (-52.5, -70) to (-53.5, -71) and turns southeast toward Punta Arenas near (-53.16, -70.9). Further east, at about -52.5, -69.5, the strait reaches Primera Angostura near -52.6, -69.5 and then opens into the eastern section.

At -53.0, -69.0 the point is south of the strait, on Tierra del Fuego. The strait at -69.0 is around -52.6 latitude, and the north coast of Tierra del Fuego there is near -52.9 to -53.0. Bahía Inútil lies at about -53.0 to -53.5 and -69.5 to -70.5. The head of Bahía Inútil is near -69.2, -53.4. At -69.0, -53.0 the point is therefore probably land on the Tierra del Fuego steppe, which is low and rolling, with an elevation of roughly 80 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

15004.text:
Latitude -77, longitude -51 is in the southern Weddell Sea, in the area of the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly 60°W to 75°W, while the Filchner ice shelf lies around 30-50°W. At -51 longitude and -77 latitude, I believe this is the western part of the Filchner Ice Shelf, near the Berkner Island area. Berkner Island spans about 45°W to 60°W and from 78°S to 79.5°S, so -77 is north of it. That puts the point near the front of the Filchner-Ronne system, between Berkner Island's northern tip and the Weddell Sea. Berkner Island's northern tip is around 76.5°S, 47°W, so the point is probably on the ice shelf or sea to the west of it.

The Ronne-Filchner front sits at about 77.5°S, and at 51°W, -77 is likely floating ice shelf, which counts as Water. The seabed there is around 500 m deep on the continental shelf. The Filchner Trough is closer to 35-40°W, so here the seabed is probably about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

9900.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude -179.0 lies in the South Pacific, southeast of Fiji and west of Tonga. The Lau Basin and Fiji region is nearby, and Fiji's southern Lau islands such as Vatoa (-19.8, -178.2) and Ono-i-Lau (-20.65, -178.7) are close. The point is about 40 km southwest of Ono-i-Lau, roughly at -21.0, -179.0. Ono-i-Lau sits at -20.66, -178.73, so this is probably open water beyond the reef.

The seabed around there is likely deep. The Lau Basin floor lies at about 2000 m depth, and the Tonga Trench is farther east at -175. At 179°W the area is on the Lau Ridge or the basin flank. South of Ono-i-Lau the depths are probably around 1500 to 2500 m, so I'll estimate -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

8269.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 159.0 lies in the western Pacific, northeast of the Solomon Islands (Guadalcanal is near -9.5, 160). The point is near the Ontong Java Plateau, whose northern part spans roughly 0 to -5 latitude and 155 to 170 longitude. Plateau depths are typically 1700-2500 m on top, and around -1, 159 the seabed is probably about 2000-2500 m deep, though the edge of the plateau could be deeper.

The northwestern part of the plateau, around 159E, 1S, is less certain. The plateau is at about 2000 m in its central region near 2S, 160E. I will estimate -2200 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the plateau, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2200}

12852.text:
The point is at 53°S, 35°W, in the South Atlantic. South Georgia lies around 54°S, 36°W, so this point is just northwest of it, near the edge of the South Georgia shelf (the North Scotia Ridge area). The island's northwest coast is at roughly 54.0°S, 38°W, and the island extends from about 36°W to 38.5°W. A point at 35°W, 53°S is therefore east of and north of the island's northeast tip, which is near 54.2°S, 36.5°W. That puts it in open water.

The shelf around South Georgia extends a bit beyond the island, but this point is well to its northeast. In that area the ocean floor is probably about 3000 to 4000 m deep. North of South Georgia, the Georgia Basin is roughly 3500 to 4000 m deep. At 53°S, 35°W, the water may be somewhat shallower because of the ridge, but I'll estimate about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

3291.text:
The coordinates 53.0°N, 77.0°W fall in northern Quebec, east of James Bay. The James Bay coast is near 79°W at this latitude, so the point is inland. It is likely in the Eastmain River area or near the Opinaca Reservoir, which sits around 52.5°N, 76.5°W. The Opinaca Reservoir spans roughly 52.2–52.8°N, 76–77°W. At 53.0°N the point is just north of the reservoir, so it is probably land.

Terrain here is about 200 m above sea level, and the Eastmain reservoir area is around 200 m. I'll estimate 220 m for Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":215}

6670.text:
The point at 15°N, 159°W is in the central North Pacific, south of the Hawaiian Islands. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water. The seabed there is abyssal plain at roughly 5,000 to 5,500 m depth.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region's bathymetry, not a lookup. The Pacific plate crust in this area is Cretaceous in age, and seafloor depths of about 5,300 m are typical for it. I'll use -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

6731.text:
The point (15°N, 37°W) is in the central North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 15°N is at roughly 45°W, so this point is east of the axis, not west. The ridge crest is at about 45°W near 15°N, and 37°W lies on the eastern flank. The Fifteen-Twenty fracture zone is near 15°20'N and sits at the ridge axis around 45°W. The seafloor at 37°W is about 15–20 Myr old, and the depth there is roughly 4,500–5,000 m.

I estimate the seabed at about -4,800 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4800}

6950.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude 41.0 is in the Afar region of Ethiopia/Eritrea, near the Danakil Depression. The Red Sea coast sits at about 41.5°E at this latitude, so 41.0°E is inland. The Danakil Depression runs roughly from 40.0 to 41.0°E at around 13–14°N. Erta Ale is at 13.6°N, 40.67°E, and Dallol is at 14.2°N, 40.3°E.

At 13.0°N, 41.0°E the point is in the Afar lowlands, southeast of the Danakil Depression, near the Eritrea–Ethiopia border and Bidu. Elevation there is perhaps 400 m, but the area is lower toward Lake Afrera (13.25°N, 40.85°E, about -100 m). The point is a bit southeast of Afrera. Afar terrain at this spot is probably 200–400 m, so I'll estimate about 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

2098.text:
Latitude 67.0°N, longitude 57.0°E lies in northern Russia, in the Nenets Autonomous Okrug / Komi region. It is east of the Pechora Sea coast and near the Pechora lowlands, north of the Arctic Circle. The Pechora River lies near 53-54°E at about 67°N. At 57°E the site is in the Bolshezemelskaya tundra, with elevations around 100 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the area, not a looked-up value.

The Usa River basin sits around 65-66°N, so at 67°N, 57°E the terrain is probably low hills of the Chernyshev ridge or Bolshezemelskaya tundra. I'll estimate about 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

5899.text:
The coordinates 25.0°N, 99.0°E fall in western Yunnan, China, near Baoshan and the Nujiang (Salween) and Lancang (Mekong) river valleys. The Salween at this latitude is around 99.0°E near Lushui or Baoshan. Baoshan city sits at about 99.17°E, 25.12°N at roughly 1650 m. The Nu River gorge lies west of Baoshan at about 98.9°E, with a river elevation of about 700 m. The Lancang is around 100°E.

At 99.0°E, 25.0°N the point is probably on the mountains between the Salween and Baoshan basin, which is the Gaoligong–Baoshan area. The Salween at Baoshan latitude flows at about 98.8°E. East of it, the ridge rises to about 2000 m. The point is therefore on a slope, likely between 1500 and 2200 m. I estimate 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

5082.text:
The coordinates 33.0, -95.0 fall in northeast Texas, near Hopkins, Delta, and Franklin counties, close to Sulphur Springs and Mount Vernon. I'm estimating from memory rather than a lookup. Cooper Lake sits near 33.3, -95.7, and Lake Bob Sandlin near 33.05, -95.1.

Lake Bob Sandlin spans roughly -95.1 to -95.25 in longitude, and its dam is near 33.0, -95.766. Lake Fork is at 32.85, -95.55, which is also not at the point. At -95.0, 33.0, I think the point is on land, probably near Winnsboro or Pittsburg. Pittsburg is at -94.97, 32.99, so the point is just west of Pittsburg. Elevation there is about 120 m.

