{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8456.text:
Latitude -3.0, longitude 173.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, in the Kiribati region. The Gilbert Islands lie around 1°N to 3°S and 172–177°E. Abemama is at 0.4°N, Tarawa at 1.4°N, and Beru at 1.3°S, 176°E. Nikunau is at 1.3°S, 176.5°E, Onotoa at 1.8°S, 175.5°E, Tabiteuea at 1.4°S, 174.8°E, Tamana at 2.5°S, 175.97°E, and Arorae at 2.6°S, 176.8°E. Nothing is at 173°E, -3°, so this point is open ocean.

The seabed there is likely deep, around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Gilbert Islands chain sits on seamounts rising from a seafloor of roughly 5,000 m. The area west of the Gilberts toward Nauru has depths near 4,500 m, so I estimate -4,600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

12131.text:
The point is at 45°S, 37°W, in the South Atlantic. It lies northwest of South Georgia (about 54°S, 36°W), so it is open ocean.

The nearby seafloor is probably the Argentine Basin or the northern flank of the Falkland/Malvinas Plateau region. At 45°S, 37°W the Argentine Basin floor is roughly 5000 m deep. The Falkland Plateau lies further south, near 50°S. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far to the east. The North Scotia Ridge is near 53°S. The Argentine Basin floor at 45°S, 40°W is about 5000-5500 m deep. At 37°W the area may be slightly shallower, around 4900 m, so I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

5585.text:
The point 27.0°N, 169.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of Hawaii. Midway Atoll is at about 28.2°N, 177.4°W and Kure at 28.4°N, 178.3°W, so neither is nearby. Pearl and Hermes is near 27.8°N, 175.8°W, and Lisianski is near 26.1°N, 173.9°W. French Frigate Shoals is near 23.7°N, 166.2°W. Nothing emerges at 27°N, 169°W, so it is open ocean.

The Hawaiian-Emperor chain runs west-northwest from there. At 169°W the chain is near 24°N, around Gardner Pinnacles at 25.0°N, 168.0°W. The point is therefore about 2° north of the chain, on the abyssal plain or the flank of the swell. Depths there are around 4,500 to 5,000 m. I estimate about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

5766.text:
The point 25.0°N, 167.0°W lies in the North Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. The nearby features are Pearl and Hermes Atoll (about 27.9°N, 175.8°W), Gardner Pinnacles (25.0°N, 168.0°W) and Maro Reef (25.4°N, 170.6°W). Gardner Pinnacles is at about 25.0°N, 167.97°W, roughly 1° of longitude (about 100 km) west of this point.

East of Gardner, the seafloor of the Hawaiian Ridge region lies at roughly 4000 to 5000 m depth. The surrounding abyssal plain is about 4800 to 5000 m deep. Between Gardner and French Frigate Shoals (23.7°N, 166.2°W) the ridge is shallower, but at 25°N, 167°W the point is probably just off the ridge to the northeast. I estimate the depth there at about 4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

13459.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 99 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and southwest of the Pacific-Antarctic region. It lies near the Southeast Indian Ridge, which runs roughly through 50°S 110°E and further southeast. The Australian-Antarctic Basin lies to the south, with typical depths of about 4000 m.

At 59°S, 99°E the seafloor is likely on the southern flank of the ridge or in the basin. The Southeast Indian Ridge near 99°E is around 50°S, so by 59°S we are well south of the crest. The Australian-Antarctic Discordance sits around 120°E. Depths at 59°S 99°E are probably about 3800 m, and I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8466.text:
The point is at 5°S, 167°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of the Phoenix Islands and west of the Line Islands, near the equator, and I don't know of any island at this location, so I classify it as open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is probably the Central Pacific Basin, which typically sits around 5000 to 5500 m deep. The ocean floor near 5°S, 167°W is likely in the range of -5200 m, so I estimate that.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

12916.text:
The point at 53°S, 93°E is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. It lies north of the Southeast Indian Ridge and south of the Broken Ridge/Ninetyeast Ridge area. The Southeast Indian Ridge near 93°E sits at about 50°S, and the ridge axis depth there is around 3000 m. The Kerguelen Plateau lies to the west, around 70-80°E, so it does not affect this point. At 53°S, 93°E the seafloor is probably in the Australian-Antarctic Basin, on the ridge's southern flank. The ridge axis near 93°E runs at roughly 48-50°S, so 53°S is a few hundred km south of it. Depths there are likely about 3500-4000 m.

I estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

15604.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 69 is in East Antarctica, in the interior near the Lambert Glacier's upper catchment and the Gamburtsev Mountains. The Gamburtsev range is centered around 80°S, 76°E. At 83°S, 69°E the point is on the polar plateau near the pole-ward flank of the Gamburtsev region, where ice-surface elevation is about 2,800-3,000 m. Dome A is at 80.4°S, 77°E with a surface near 4,090 m, and at 83°S, 69°E the surface is probably around 3,300 m.

I estimate the surface at about 3,200 m and classify it as grounded ice, so "Solid".

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 3200}

9255.text:
The point (-13, -29) is in the South Atlantic, east of Brazil and west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It sits in the Brazil Basin region, which is deep abyssal plain, roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge lies near 14°W at this latitude, so -29 is well west of it.

South of the Ceará Rise and east of the Brazilian margin, the Brazil Basin floor is about 5,000 m deep. At 29°W, 13°S, the seafloor is probably on the western flank of the ridge, where depths run around 4,500 to 5,000 m. I estimate -4,800 m. This is an estimate from the regional setting, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

5434.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude -111.0 is in the Gulf of California (Sea of Cortez), off the Sonora coast. The coast near Guaymas sits at about 27.9N, 110.9W, and Bahía Kino is near 28.8N, 111.95W. At 29N, 111W the coastline is around -112.0 to -111.9 on the Sonora side, so the point is west of the coast near Kino and east of Isla Tiburón. Tiburón lies at roughly 28.9N, 112.4W, with the Canal de Infiernillo between it and the mainland at about -112.3. The mainland coast near Punta Chueca is at about -112.15. That puts -111.0 inland, about 100 km east of the coast, in Sonora near Hermosillo, which is at 29.07N, 110.95W.

So the point is land, near Hermosillo, where elevation is about 200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":220}

1745.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 71.0°E lies in the Kara Sea, off the west coast of the Yamal Peninsula. Yamal's coast sits around 70-73°N at about 66-70°E. At 71°N, the Yamal west coast is around 66-67°E, and the Ob Gulf region is near 73°E at 72°N. So 71°E is east of Yamal, in the Gydan area. The Ob Gulf lies at about 72-73°E at 70-72°N, and the Gulf of Ob's western shore is Yamal's east coast at about 72°E at 70°N. So 71°E at 71°N could be Yamal's northeastern part or the Ob Gulf's western shore. The Yamal peninsula extends up to 73°N at about 70°E, so 71°N, 71°E is likely land in the northern Yamal, where elevation is a few tens of meters. The Ob Gulf's western shore at 71°N is around 72-73°E, so this point is probably land on Yamal.

I estimate Yamal's elevation here at about 30 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":30}

10413.text:
The point (-25.0, 127.0) is in central Australia, in Western Australia near the Northern Territory and South Australia borders. That puts it in the Gibson Desert / Petermann Ranges region, on the Western Australian side near the Docker River area. The terrain there is roughly 500 m above sea level.

Docker River sits at about -24.87, 129.07 with an elevation of about 580 m. Moving west to 127.0 puts the point in the Gibson Desert's southern part, near Lake Mackay or the Warburton area. Warburton at 126.6 E, -26.1 S is about 470 m. The surrounding sand plain is about 450–500 m, so I estimate 480 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

4150.text:
The point 43.0°N, 159.0°W is in the North Pacific, south of the Alaska Peninsula and well away from land. It lies in the deep Pacific basin south of the Aleutian Trench, so the surface type is Water.

The Aleutian Trench runs roughly along 52-54°N here, so this point is south of it on the abyssal plain. Depths in that area are about 5,000-5,500 m. The Emperor Seamounts are farther west, near 170°E, so they are not a factor. I'll estimate a seabed depth of about -5,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

7103.text:
Latitude 11.0, longitude -13.0 falls in Guinea, West Africa. The Atlantic coast of Guinea is near -14.5 at this latitude, so this point is inland. It sits in the Fouta Djallon highlands, near Labé or Mali prefecture to the east, roughly in the Gaoual/Koundara/Labé area. Labé is at 11.3N, -12.3W with an elevation of about 1000 m. Gaoual is at 11.75N, -13.2W with an elevation of about 100 m.

At 11.0N, -13.0W the point is near Tougué or Koubia. Tougué is at 11.45N, -11.65W. This location is west of the Fouta plateau, where the terrain descends toward the coastal plain. I estimate about 300 m, and a figure between 200 and 400 m seems plausible.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

7731.text:
Latitude 5.0°N, longitude 163.0°E lies in the western Pacific, near Pohnpei (about 6.9°N, 158.2°E) and Kosrae (5.3°N, 163.0°E). Kosrae is at roughly 5.32°N, 162.98°E, so the point at 5.0°N is about 35 km south of Kosrae and clearly in open water.

The seafloor there is on the flanks of the Kosrae volcanic edifice or the surrounding abyssal plain. The ocean around Kosrae is deep, with the surrounding seafloor at roughly 4,500-5,000 m. About 35 km south, the slope would probably put the depth near 4,000 m. I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

7195.text:
Latitude 11.0°N, longitude 171.0°E lies in the Marshall Islands region. Ujelang atoll is at about 9.8°N, 161.9°E, Kwajalein at 8.7°N, 167.7°E, Wotje at 9.5°N, 170.0°E, Likiep at 9.9°N, 169.3°E, Ailuk at 10.3°N, 169.9°E, Mejit at 10.3°N, 170.9°E, and Utirik at 11.2°N, 169.8°E. Mejit is therefore the closest island, about 0.7° south of the point, which puts 11.0°N, 171.0°E in open ocean.

The surrounding seafloor is the Pacific plate, with abyssal depths of about 5000 to 5500 m. Mejit is a seamount summit, so the slopes drop off quickly. The point is roughly 80 km from Mejit, which would put the depth at about 4500 to 5000 m. I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

11875.text:
Latitude -41.0, longitude 171.0 is in the Tasman Sea, west of New Zealand's South Island. The west coast near Westport lies at about -41.75, 171.6, and Cape Foulwind is near 171.47. Karamea Bight lies around -41.3, 172.0. So at 171.0 we are roughly 55 km offshore from the coast at about 171.5.

The continental shelf off the west coast of the South Island is fairly wide here. Depths at about 50 km offshore are around 100-150 m. At latitude -41, longitude 171.0, the shelf edge sits near 170.5 or farther west, so the depth should be about 120 m. I'll estimate -130 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-130}

14764.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -171 lies in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica, near the Ross Ice Shelf. The Ross Ice Shelf front is at about -78 latitude at this longitude, and the shelf extends from roughly 77.5°S southward. At 75°S, 171°W, we are in the open Ross Sea, north of the ice shelf edge, off the Ross Sea continental shelf near the Hallett and Pennell banks area. The sea floor in the central Ross Sea is typically 500-700 m deep. Around 75°S, 171°W, the water is probably the Ross Sea's eastern part, with a depth of about 500 m.

The Ross Sea continental shelf has a depth of about 500 m in the central part, with the Joides Basin and the Glomar Challenger Basin in the east, reaching about 800 m. At 171°W, the location is near the Glomar Challenger Basin, which lies around 76-77°S and 175-180°W. At 75°S, 171°W, the depth is probably around 500-600 m. I'll estimate -550.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

10864.text:
Latitude -31, longitude -51 is in the Atlantic Ocean off southern Brazil. The coast near Mostardas and Tavares in Rio Grande do Sul sits at about -31, -50.9, so the point is close to the shore. The coastline there is around lon -50.9 at lat -31.0. Tavares is at about -31.28, -51.09, and Mostardas at -31.1, -50.9. The coast near lat -31 lies at roughly lon -50.85, so -51.0 is on the west side of that, which would be inland.

The Lagoa dos Patos lies west of the coast. At lat -31, the lagoon's eastern shore is near lon -51.1 and the ocean coast is near -50.8. The strip between them is narrow, only about 10–20 km wide. So lon -51.0 could be land on that strip or the lagoon itself. Near Mostardas (-31.1, -50.9), the town is about 10 km from the ocean and the lagoon is further west at around -51.2. That puts -51.0 on the land strip, with an elevation of about 5 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":5}

11067.text:
The point is at 33°S, 5°W, in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits near 13°W at this latitude. It is west of the Walvis Ridge, which is further north, and the Cape Basin lies to the east.

This area is probably the western flank of the ridge or the basin floor. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis near 33°S is at about 14°W. At 5°W the crust is roughly 15 to 20 million years old, so depths should be around 4,000 to 5,000 m. The Rio Grande Rise is farther west, so it does not affect this point. The nearby Tristan da Cunha hotspot track is at about 37°S, 12°W, also not close. I estimate a depth of about -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

10623.text:
The point is at 29°S, 173°W, in the South Pacific. That is southwest of Tonga and east of the Kermadec Islands, and Niue lies farther north. The area is on the Pacific plate, northeast of the Kermadec Trench. The Louisville Seamount chain runs through around 26°S, 175°W and heads southeast, so the point sits near that chain.

Away from seamounts, the abyssal plain there is about 5000 to 5500 m deep. The Kermadec Trench axis is near 177–178°W at this latitude. At 173°W, about 4–5° east of the trench, the seafloor should be roughly 5300 m deep. The Louisville ridge lies near 28°S, 176°W and then 30°S, 172°W, so the point might be near it. I will pick about -4800 m as a compromise.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

12659.text:
Latitude -51.0, longitude -61.0 falls in the Falkland Islands region. West Falkland spans roughly -60.9 to -61.5 longitude at latitudes -51.2 to -52.0. At -51.0 latitude, that is north of the northern tip of West Falkland, which is around -51.25 (Jason Islands are about -51.0 and -61.2). So the point is probably in the sea just north of the islands.

The Falklands shelf is shallow there, about 100 m deep. Around -51.0, -61.0 the water is probably 80-150 m deep. I'll estimate -110.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-110}

3273.text:
Location: 53.0N, 113.0W is in Alberta, Canada, southwest of Edmonton, near Pigeon Lake and Wetaskiwin. Wetaskiwin is at 52.97N, 113.37W, elevation about 780 m. Pigeon Lake is at about 53.0N, 114.0W. Ponoka is at 52.67N, 113.58W. Edmonton is at 53.5N, 113.5W, elevation about 670 m.

The point at 53.0, -113.0 is east of Wetaskiwin, near Camrose (53.02N, 112.83W), which sits at about 740 m. Nearby is Bittern Lake at 53.03N, 113.08W. Hay Lakes is at 53.19N, 113.03W. The terrain here is about 750 m, so I estimate 745 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":745}

7060.text:
The point at 11.0°N, 99.0°W is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 600 km south-southwest of Acapulco. It lies east of the East Pacific Rise, which is near 104°W at this latitude, and the Clipperton Fracture Zone is at about 10°N.

The seafloor in this area is typically 3,000 to 3,500 m deep. At this longitude the crust is roughly 8 to 10 Myr old, which supports a depth of about 3,000 m. Using a rough half-space cooling estimate, depth ≈ 2,600 + 350·√age, which gives about 3,700 m for 10 Myr. Nearer the rise, around 100°W, depths are about 3,000 m. The East Pacific Rise at 11°N is at about 104°W, so 99°W is about 5° east, or roughly 550 km. With a spreading rate of about 11 cm/yr full rate (5.5 cm/yr half rate), the crust is about 10 Myr old, so the depth is about 3,500 m. That is consistent with the cooling estimate, so I'll use about -3,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

5335.text:
The point 31.0°N, 51.0°E lies in southwestern Iran, in the Zagros Mountains. That area is in Kohgiluyeh and Boyer-Ahmad province, near Yasuj (30.67°N, 51.59°E), where the terrain is mountainous. Dena's peaks are around 30.9°N, 51.4°E. At 51.0°E, near Sisakht or Dehdasht, the elevation is probably about 1800 m.

This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value. The point is probably west of Sisakht, in the Zagros folds, and I'll take about 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

2849.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 119.0°E is in eastern Siberia, in the Sakha (Yakutia) / Zabaykalsky / Irkutsk border region. It sits near the Aldan Highlands and the Patom Highlands, north of the Olyokma River area. I estimate the elevation at roughly 800 m, but this is a guess from the general region rather than a looked-up value.

The Olyokma River basin lies near 120°E, 57–60°N, and the Vitim Plateau is at about 59°N, 115°E. The Stanovoy range is further south. In this area the terrain is plateau at about 700–900 m, so 800 m is a reasonable central figure.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":800}

7630.text:
The point is at 5.0°N, 39.0°W, in the equatorial Atlantic off the coast of Brazil. That places it in open ocean, so the type is Water.

Nearby features help narrow the depth. The Brazilian coast near 5°S lies at about 35°W, and at 5°N the coast of the Guianas/Amapá is at about 50°W. The point is therefore well out in the ocean. It sits north of the Fernando de Noronha ridge and the Ceará Rise. The Ceará Rise is centered around 4°N, 43°W, with depths of about 3000 m. The Ceará Abyssal Plain lies at about 4000-5000 m. At 39°W the point is east of the rise, in the abyssal plain, where depths are about 4500 m. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far to the east at about 30°W, so a depth near 4,400 m is a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

2935.text:
Latitude 57.0, longitude -69.0 is in northern Quebec/Labrador, so it's land. Nearby is Caniapiscau Reservoir at roughly 54.5N, -69.5W, and Lake Mistassini is farther south. At 57N, -69W the area is near the Ungava region southeast of Kuujjuaq (58.1N, -68.4W). Kuujjuaq lies in the Koksoak valley at about 60 m elevation. At 57N, -69W the terrain is probably plateau at roughly 450 m. The Rivière George runs at about 56.5N, -64.5W, and the Caniapiscau River flows near -69.5W at 57N. That river is at around 400 m, and the surrounding plateau is about 500 m.

I'll estimate 480 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

4.text:
This point is at 89°N, 171°W, in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole. That places it in the Amerasian Basin side of the Lomonosov Ridge region. The ridge runs roughly from the Siberian shelf through near the pole (about 90°N, 140°E–60°W) to Greenland.

Longitude 171°W at 89°N is on the Amerasian side, toward the Makarov Basin. Makarov Basin depths are about 4000 m. The Lomonosov Ridge crest near the pole lies at about 1000–1500 m depth. The ridge near the pole sits roughly along the 140°E–40°W line, so 171°W is about 50° of longitude from the ridge axis (which runs through 140°E to 40°W). At 89°N, 1° of latitude is 111 km, and the longitude offset of 49° from the 139°W side gives about 111 × cos(1°)... roughly 1.9 km per degree, so about 90 km away. The Makarov Basin flank lies on that side, so the depth is likely about 2000–3000 m. I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

3067.text:
The point 55.0°N, 165.0°W lies in the North Pacific, south of the Alaska Peninsula and the Aleutian Islands. Unimak Pass sits near 54.5°N, 164.5°W, and Unimak Island at 54.8°N, 164°W. At 165°W, the coast of the Alaska Peninsula near Sanak is at about 54.4°N, 162.7°W, so this is not that area. Unimak Island's western end is about 164.9°W at roughly 54.6°N. The point at 55.0°N, 165.0°W is therefore just north of the western part of Unimak, in the Bering Sea side near Cape Sarichef (54.6°N, 164.9°W). Land at 55.0°N would be north of the island's coast, so the point is probably in the Bering Sea.

The Bering Sea shelf north of Unimak is about 100 m deep. The shelf edge runs northwest of Unimak Pass, and at 55°N, 165°W the depth is probably around 100 m. The Aleutian arc's north side drops steeply, but this location is likely still on the shelf, so I estimate about -90 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-90}

9923.text:
The coordinates -21.0, -133.0 fall in the South Pacific, east of the Tuamotus and south of Pitcairn. Pitcairn is at -25.07, -130.1 and Mangareva is at -23.1, -135. The point is therefore open ocean in the southeastern Polynesia region.

The seafloor there is on the Pacific plate, roughly 4,000 to 4,500 m deep. The area lies north of the Easter Island and Pitcairn hotspot region and west of the East Pacific Rise, where the crust is about 30 to 40 Myr old. That age suggests a depth of about 4,000 m, and the broader area probably sits near 4,200 m. I'll estimate -4,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4100}

4176.text:
The point 43.0°N, 107.0°W is in central Wyoming. It lies in Natrona County, near the Powder River Basin and the Wind River Basin boundary, a bit north of Casper. Casper is at about 42.85°N, 106.3°W, and its elevation is about 1,550 m. Farther west, near 107°W, the terrain is around Waltman and Hells Half Acre. The area west of Casper along Hwy 20/26 at 107°W is near Powder River, with an elevation of about 1,570 m. I'd estimate about 1,600 m.

The location is on land, so the surface type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1620}

8603.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 107.0 is in the Java Sea, north of Java and west of Karawang. Java's north coast lies near -6.0 to -6.3, so -5.0 is about 100 km offshore. The Java Sea there is shallow, roughly 30–40 m deep.

The Java Sea floor near -5, 107 is probably about 35 m deep, so I estimate -37 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-37}

12504.text:
The point (-49, -11) lies in the South Atlantic, southwest of Africa and south of Bouvet Island (54.4°S, 3.4°E). It sits west of the Southwest Indian Ridge and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near 0 to -10°E at that latitude.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 49°S is offset by the Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone to the north and the Bouvet triple junction to the south. At 49°S the ridge axis is around 8–10°W, so -11° is close to the ridge flank. The ridge crest there lies at roughly 2500–3000 m depth, and the flank at 11°W would be around 3000 m. I estimate a depth of about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

11596.text:
The point (-39, -27) lies in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and east of the Rio Grande Rise. The ridge axis near 39°S is at roughly 13°W, so this point is about 14° west of it. The seafloor there is on the western flank, in the Argentine Basin's eastern margin. Ocean depths in that area are likely around 4500-5000 m. The Rio Grande Rise is at about 30°S, and the Vema Fracture and Hunter channel are not relevant here. The region near 39°S, 27°W is probably abyssal plain at roughly 4800 m depth, since crust of about 40-50 Myr age would sit at about 4500 m.

I estimate about -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

4965.text:
Latitude 35.0°N, longitude 31.0°E lies in the Eastern Mediterranean, south of Turkey and west of Cyprus (Cyprus spans about 32.3–34.6°E), so this point is open sea.

The area sits between the Cyprus arc and the Eratosthenes seamount region. The Eratosthenes Seamount is at about 34.0°N, 32.7°E, south of this point. The Anaximander Mountains lie around 35.5°N, 30.0°E. The Antalya basin and Finike basin are north of this point. Near 35°N, 31°E the seabed is probably the Florence Rise or the Antalya basin margin, at roughly 2000 m depth. The Herodotus basin, around 33°N, is about 3000 m deep, and the Cyprus trench area is about 2500 to 3000 m deep. I estimate about -2000 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

1022.text:
Latitude 79.0N, longitude 65.0E lies in the Kara Sea region. Novaya Zemlya's northern tip is around 76.5N, 68E. Franz Josef Land spans roughly 80-81N and 45-65E. At 79N, 65E the point is south of Franz Josef Land and northeast of Novaya Zemlya, which makes it open sea (the northern Kara Sea).

The seabed depth there is likely around 100-300 m. The St. Anna Trough is farther west at about 70E-ish and runs north-south, reaching more than 500 m. The Voronin Trough is at about 80N, 80E. At 65E and 79N, I estimate a depth of roughly 200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-220}

8112.text:
The point is at latitude -1.0, longitude -155.0, in the central Pacific just south of the equator. It lies southeast of the Line Islands (Kiribati), and Christmas Island is at about 2°N, 157°W. The nearest land is Jarvis Island, at about 0.37°S, 160°W, which is too far west to matter. So this point is open ocean.

For depth, the seafloor in this part of the central Pacific is typically 4,500 to 5,500 m deep. The area is east of the Line Islands ridge and north of the Penrhyn basin. I'm estimating from general regional bathymetry rather than a specific reading, so I'll take about -5,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

2275.text:
