The South Pole is at 2835 m. Along the 25°E meridian, the ice surface at -85° should be roughly 2500-3000 m. Estimates for the area near 85°S, 25°E, using Recovery Glacier and the Support Force Glacier region (which lie around 0° to -30°W, -82° to -85°), suggest about 2000-2500 m on the plateau. Because the longitude is east of the Filchner trough, the area would be higher up on the plateau, so I'll estimate around 2700 m.

