{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

7737.text:
The point is 5°N, 175°E, in the central Pacific. That places it west of the Marshall Islands, which lie roughly between 165°E and 172°E, and north of Kiribati's Gilbert Islands, which start around 1–3°N near 173°E. Nothing is land at this spot, so it is open ocean.

The nearby seafloor sits on the abyssal plain of the Pacific, probably around 5000 m deep. The Ratak chain's southern end (Mili and Knox atolls) is near 171.9°E, 6°N, and the Gilbert Islands (Butaritari and Makin) are near 173°E, 3°N. Past those, the seafloor to the east and northeast slopes down into the Pacific basin. I estimate the depth here at about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

9958.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude -63.0 is in the Chaco region of southern Bolivia/Paraguay, near the Bolivia–Paraguay–Argentina border area. Lat -21, lon -63 is in Bolivia's Tarija department, in the Chaco lowlands, east of the Andes foothills. Elevation there is likely around 400 m.

The Pilcomayo River sits near -21.5 to -22, so this point is in the Gran Chaco, east of Villamontes (-63.5, -21.26). Villamontes is at about 400 m, and at -63.0 the ground is probably around 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":330}

245.text:
Latitude 87°S or 87°N? It is given as 87.0, so north, which puts it in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole at longitude -49. This lies near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from Greenland/Ellesmere across the pole to Siberia. At 87°N the ridge crosses roughly along the 140°E–40°W meridian line. Longitude -49 is about on the Greenland side of the pole, so the point is close to the ridge's axis, though not exactly on it.

The Lomonosov Ridge crest is typically at 1000–1600 m depth. On the Greenland side, the Amundsen Basin is about 4200 m deep, and the ridge crest near 87–88°N is about 1000–1500 m deep. The ridge near 87°N, 50°W is probably the flank. I'll estimate around -1800 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -1800}

9367.text:
The point (-15, -165) lies in the South Pacific, northeast of Samoa and Tokelau and southeast of the Samoan islands. It is roughly 500 km east of Tutuila (-14.3, -170.7)... actually Tutuila is only about 5.7° west. The Cook Islands' Manihiki (-10.4, -161) and Rakahanga lie to the north-east, and Suwarrow is at -13.2, -163.1. So the point is in open ocean, west of Suwarrow and south of Tokelau.

The seabed here is the Penrhyn/Samoan basin, with depths of about 4500–5500 m. I estimate about -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

8211.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 43.0 is in the Indian Ocean, off the coast of Somalia. The Somali coast near latitude -1 lies at about longitude 41.5 (Kismayo is at -0.36, 42.54; Chula/Badmadow is around -1.5, 41.6). Longitude 43.0 is therefore roughly 130 km offshore, east of the coast.

The shelf there is narrow, and the Somali Basin deepens quickly. At 43.0E, -1.0, the depth is probably around 3000 m. The slope drops from the shelf edge near 41.8E to the abyssal plain, so I'd estimate about -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

2867.text:
Latitude 59.0 N, longitude 155.0 E. This is in the Sea of Okhotsk, near the northern coast. Magadan is at about 59.5 N, 150.8 E. The coast near 155 E lies around 59.3 N (Gizhiga and Penzhina Bay are further east at about 160 E). The Taygonos Peninsula is at roughly 61 N, 160 E. At 155 E the Okhotsk coast runs near 59.4 N, around the Yamsky Islands (59.2 N, 155.5 E) and Shelikhov Gulf's western edge. Yamsky Islands are at about 59.1 N, 155.3 E, so the point is very close to them and to the coast.

Is the point land or water? The coast near 155 E, between Magadan and Yamsk, includes the Koni Peninsula and the Pyagin Peninsula. Yamsk is at 59.55 N, 154.15 E. The coast there runs southeast, and the Yamsky Islands are at 59.35 N, 155.0 E. So 59.0 N, 155.0 E is probably just south of the islands, in water. Depth there is shallow, perhaps 100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-100}

172.text:
Latitude 89.0°N, longitude 165°E lies in the central Arctic Ocean, about 111 km from the North Pole. It is near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs from the Siberian shelf past the pole toward Greenland. The ridge is closest to 90°N along longitudes near 140°E on one side and about 50°W on the other. At 89°N the ridge crest sits at roughly 1000 to 1500 m depth, and 165°E is on the Amerasian side, where the Makarov Basin lies.

The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole roughly along the 140°E to 40°W line. A point at 165°E is 25° off that axis toward the Makarov Basin side. At 89°N, 25° of longitude is about 111 km × sin(25°) ≈ 47 km. So the point is near the ridge flank. The ridge is about 50 to 70 km wide, so the point is probably on the flank, with a depth of around 1500 to 2500 m. I'll estimate about -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

1880.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -19.0 is in the Greenland Sea/Denmark Strait region, northeast of Iceland and east of Greenland. Greenland's east coast near Scoresby Sund is at about -22 to -28 longitude and 70N, so -19 at 69N is offshore in the ocean.

This area lies north of Iceland and west of Jan Mayen, on the Greenland continental shelf edge or slope. The shelf extends to roughly -17 to -18 at 69N, but the East Greenland shelf is wide at this latitude, so the shelf edge could be near -15. The Jan Mayen Fracture Zone and Jan Mayen Basin lie further east, and the Kolbeinsey Ridge is to the south. At 69N, -19, the depth is probably around 300-1000 m. The Greenland shelf near Scoresby Sund is about 300 m deep in its troughs, and the coast is at -22, so -19 is likely mid-shelf at about 300 m deep. I'll estimate -400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

13860.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -179 is in the Southern Ocean, north of the Ross Sea, east of the Balleny Islands (which lie around 163-165°E, -66 to -67°S). This point is at the edge of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region, in the abyssal plain south of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. The ridge is near -65°S around 180°W... Actually the ridge runs from about -62°S at 160°W to -65°S at 180°, so the point may sit near the ridge crest. Ridge crest depths there are around 2500-3000 m, with the surroundings near 3000-3500 m. The Southern Ocean abyssal depths here run about 3000-4000 m.

I estimate a depth of about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

6366.text:
The point (19°N, 47°W) is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 19°N sits at roughly 45–46°W, so this location is just west of the axis, on young seafloor flanking the ridge.

Typical flank depths there are around 3000–4000 m. The ridge crest at 19°N is about 2500–3000 m deep, and the axial valley is deeper. At 47°W, about 1–2° west of the axis, the depth is probably about 3500–4000 m. I estimate 3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4336.text:
The point (41.0°N, 147.0°W) is in the northeast Pacific Ocean, south of Alaska. It lies in the Gulf of Alaska abyssal plain region, west of the Surveyor Fan area. Depths there are roughly 4,000 to 5,000 m, and I estimate around 4,300 m.

The Surveyor Seamount and the Patton-Murray seamounts lie farther north, near 54°N. At 41°N, 147°W the seafloor is abyssal plain, likely about 4,800 m deep. The Mendocino Fracture Zone runs near 40–41°N, and the seafloor there is about 4,500–5,000 m deep. I'll go with -4750 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4750}

14098.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -63 is on the Antarctic Peninsula's east side, in the Weddell Sea region near the Larsen ice shelf. The Antarctic Peninsula at 67°S runs around 65-68°W. Longitude -63 at 67°S is east of the peninsula, which places it over the former Larsen A/B area, or Larsen B embayment (Larsen B is around 65.5°S, 61.5°W). At 67°S, 63°W it is likely Larsen C ice shelf, around the Cabinet Inlet/Mobiloil Inlet region. Larsen C spans 66-69°S, with the front near 60-62°W, so this point is likely on the Larsen C ice shelf, which is floating. Water, with seabed elevation maybe around -500 m.

Larsen C bed depths are typically 300-700 m below sea level, and near the inlets they are deeper. I'll estimate -450.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

14548.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 117 is in East Antarctica, inland of the Wilkes Land coast. The coast near 117°E (Law Dome is at about 112°E, 66.7°S) lies near -66°S, so -71 is roughly 500 km inland. Ice surface elevation there is probably around 2,500–3,000 m. Dome C is at 75°S, 123°E with a surface of 3,233 m. At 71°S, 117°E, I estimate the ice surface at about 2,800 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so it counts as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

10220.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 101 is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia and west of Christmas Island. It lies roughly 1,500 km west of Exmouth, in the Wharton Basin area. Wharton Basin abyssal plain depths there are about 5,000-5,500 m, so I estimate the seabed near -5,300 m.

The Ninetyeast Ridge is at about 90°E, so 101°E is east of it, and the Wharton Basin floor there is about 5,500 m deep. I'll answer -5,400 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5400}

8505.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude -89.0 is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, west of the Galápagos (which are at about 0 to -1.5 latitude and -89 to -92 longitude). The point is about 5°S, so it sits south of the Galápagos platform.

The seafloor there lies on the Nazca plate, south of the Galápagos Spreading Center, at roughly 3,000 to 3,500 m depth. Typical depth in the region is about 3,000 m, and the Carnegie Ridge lies to the east, near -85 to -90 longitude at about 1 to 2°S. At 5°S, 89°W, the crust is about 3 to 5 Myr old, with depths of roughly 3,000 to 3,200 m. I estimate -3,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

6493.text:
The point (17°N, 153°W) is in the central Pacific, south of the Hawaiian Islands (Hawaii's Big Island is around 19-20°N, 155°W). That puts it in open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

Seafloor depth here is typically about 5,000 m. The area is on Pacific plate abyssal seafloor south of the Hawaiian Swell, in the 5,000-5,500 m range. The Hawaiian moat and arch affect depths nearer the islands, but at 17°N the seafloor is probably around 5,200 m deep. I'm estimating this from general knowledge rather than a lookup, so I'll use -5200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

7567.text:
The point is 5°N, 165°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands (Palmyra is at about 5.9°N, 162°W; Kingman Reef is at 6.4°N, 162.4°W). There is no land at this location, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor here is the central Pacific basin, near the Line Islands region west of the Line Islands ridge. Depths in this area are typically around 5000 to 5500 m. I'm estimating from the regional bathymetry rather than a precise lookup, so I'll use about -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

9951.text:
The point (-21.0, -77.0) lies in the Pacific Ocean, about 500 km west of northern Chile (Iquique is at roughly -70.1 longitude). The Peru-Chile Trench lies close to the coast, around -71 to -72 longitude at this latitude. Further west, at -77 longitude, the seafloor is on the Nazca Plate, west of the trench.

The Nazca Ridge is farther north, at about -15 to -20 latitude, and it trends southwest. At -21 latitude and -77 longitude, the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain or the southern part of the Nazca Ridge area, at roughly 4000 m depth. The ridge reaches about -22 latitude near -81 longitude, so -77 is east of it. Depth there is probably about 4500 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14887.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 75E is in East Antarctica, in the Prince Charles Mountains and Lambert Glacier region. The Lambert Glacier trends along roughly 70-72E at latitude -73 to -70, and the Amery Ice Shelf lies near 70E, -70 to -73. At -75, 75E the location is inland on the plateau. The ice surface there is around 2,500 m, rising toward Dome A to the south.

The area near 75E, -75 is a little east of the Lambert Glacier's upper reaches. The upper Lambert/Mellor Glacier area sits at about 2,000 m, and the surface at -75, 75E is probably around 2,400 m. That is an estimate from the regional pattern, not a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

13639.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 99 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and southwest of the Southeast Indian Ridge region. That area is south of the ridge crest, which runs roughly from 50°S at 90°E toward 50°S at 110°E and then further south. Near 99°E the ridge sits around 48–50°S, so 61°S is well south of it. This part of the Australian-Antarctic Basin has depths of about 4000–4500 m.

I estimate roughly -4300 m from the general bathymetry, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

5475.text:
The point is 29°N, 29°W, in the North Atlantic. It lies northeast of the Azores, which sit at about 38°N, 28°W, and well west of the Canary Islands. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge runs near 30–40°W at this latitude, so the point is probably just east of the ridge axis, or on its flank.

At 29°N the ridge axis is around 42–43°W, so 29°W is much farther east than I first assumed. The seafloor there is typically abyssal plain or hills at roughly 4,500–5,000 m depth. This area lies south of the Azores Plateau, and the seafloor at 29°N, 29°W is likely about 4,800 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

7802.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude -55.0° is in northern Suriname, in the interior, south of the coastal plain. The coast is at about 6°N, so this point is well inland. It lies in the Guiana Shield, in the Sipaliwini district, probably near the Bakhuis or Wilhelmina areas, which are further west. At 55°W, the terrain is likely hilly forest at roughly 250 m.

The Suriname interior has an elevation of around 200-300 m, so I estimate 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

3252.text:
The point 53.0°N, 155.0°W lies in the North Pacific, south of the Alaska Peninsula. The Alaska Peninsula coast near 55-56°N at 155°W is about 300 km north. The Aleutian Trench lies south of the peninsula, running roughly through 53°N at 160°W and 52°N at 175°W. At 155°W the trench axis is around 53.5°N, since the trench curves toward the Kodiak area at about 55°N, 152°W.

So the point is near the trench axis or just south of it. Trench depths there are around 6000-6800 m. The trench south of the peninsula at 155°W is probably about 6,000 m deep, with the axis at roughly 52.5-53°N. I estimate the seabed at about -5,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

5271.text:
The point 31.0°N, 77.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, roughly 400 km east of the Georgia/Florida coast. It sits on the Blake Plateau, east of the Florida-Georgia coast and near the Blake Plateau's outer part, where depths are about 800-1000 m. The Blake Escarpment is near 76-77°W at about 30-31°N. At 31°N, the plateau edge runs near 76.5°W, so 77°W is probably on the plateau at around 800 m depth, though the Blake Plateau's outer edge drops quickly to 2000+ m beyond the escarpment.

The Blake Plateau at 31°N, 77°W is probably about 850 m deep. Before settling on that, I recalled that the Blake Escarpment lies at about 28-30°N, 76-77°W, with the plateau at 700-1000 m. I'll go with -850 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-850}

13561.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -57 is near the South Shetland Islands. Elephant Island sits at about -61.1, -55.2, and King George Island is around -62, -58.5. At -61, -57 the point is probably in the Bransfield Strait or just north of it, in the Drake Passage area. Clarence Island is at about -61.2, -54.1, so it is not nearby. Between King George Island (-62) and Elephant Island (-61.1, -55) there is deep water, and the area north of the South Shetland chain is the Drake Passage shelf.

The South Shetland Islands lie roughly along the line from (-63, -62) to (-61, -55). At longitude -57 the island arc is at about -61.9 to -62.2, so -61.0 is north of it. The ocean there would be fairly deep, around 2000 to 3000 m. I'll estimate about -2500 m, though the shelf break near the islands could make it shallower.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

7205.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude -169.0° (169°W) is in the central North Pacific, southwest of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands and Samoa. There is no land there, so the surface is water. I'm estimating depth from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than from a lookup.

The area is probably abyssal plain in the Pacific basin, east of the Marshall Islands and north of Kiribati. The seafloor in this part of the central Pacific is typically 5,000 to 5,500 m deep. I'll take about 5,300 m, giving an elevation of -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

8244.text:
Latitude -1.0, Longitude 109.0 is in West Kalimantan, Borneo, Indonesia. The coast there is around 109.0°E at roughly 0°S to -1°S. Ketapang lies at about -1.8°S, 110°E, and Pontianak at 0.0°S, 109.3°E. At -1.0, 109.0 the point is likely land, south of the Kendawangan area and north of Ketapang. The coast runs southeast from Pontianak, and Padang Tikar is at about -0.7°S, 109.2°E. The Kubu Raya and Kayong Utara coast near -1.0 is at about 109.3°E. The coast at latitude -1 is perhaps near 109.4°E, so 109.0 would be offshore in the Karimata Strait. Offshore depth there is shallow, about 10-20 m.

Checking this: the Kayong Utara coast, with Teluk Batang at about -1.05°S, 109.97°E, suggests the coast at -1.0 is farther east, around 109.9°E. So 109.0 is offshore, roughly 100 km from the coast. The Karimata Strait there is shallow, about 20-30 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-25}

252.text:
Latitude 87°N, longitude -35° lies in the central Arctic Ocean, north of Greenland. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses near 87-88°N, running roughly from the Greenland side (around 60°W) toward the Siberian side (around 140°E). At 35°W and 87°N the position is on the Greenland side of the pole, and the ridge there lies roughly along the 50°W to 60°W meridians near 85°N and curves toward the pole.

The ridge crest near the pole is about 1000-1500 m deep. The Amundsen Basin sits on the Eurasian side (the 0° to 90°E side), with depths around 4200 m. The Makarov Basin is on the other side of the ridge, with depths around 4000 m. Longitude -35° at 87°N is on the Atlantic side of the ridge, which puts it in the Amundsen Basin. The Amundsen Basin's abyssal plain there is about 4200 m deep, though the position could be closer to the ridge flank, so I'll estimate about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

12948.text:
The point at -53.0, 157.0 is in the Southern Ocean southeast of Australia and southwest of Macquarie Island. Macquarie Island is near -54.6, 158.9, so this point is open ocean, northwest of the Macquarie Ridge.

The Macquarie Ridge runs from about 159°E at this latitude, and the area west of it is the Tasmanian Basin or the South Tasman Rise region. Nearby the seafloor sits around 4000 to 4500 m deep. At 53°S, 157°E I expect abyssal depths of about 4500 m, though the ridge lies close by and could make it shallower. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14235.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -149 lies in the Ross Sea sector of the Southern Ocean, off Marie Byrd Land. The Antarctic coast near longitude -149 is around -77 to -78 (Ross Ice Shelf edge near Edward VII Peninsula is at about -158). At -69 the point is well offshore, in open ocean north of the continental shelf, on the continental rise or abyssal plain.

The continental slope in this region is around -65 to -66 latitude, and at -69 we would be on the shelf only if the coast were nearby. The Marie Byrd Land coast is at about -75 to -77, and the shelf edge is at about -73 to -74. So -69 is north of the shelf edge, on the continental rise or abyssal plain, with a depth of about 3500 to 4500 m. The Pacific-Antarctic basin there is probably about -4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

12084.text:
The point (-45, -131) lies in the South Pacific, southwest of the southern tip of South America. It is far from any land, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is likely abyssal plain on the Pacific plate. The East Pacific Rise lies farther east, around 110°W at 45°S, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is farther south. At 131°W the crust is roughly 20-30 Myr old, which suggests a depth of about 3800-4000 m. My estimate is about 3900 m, though I am recalling regional bathymetry rather than looking up this exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4125.text:
Location 45.0N, 151.0E is in the Pacific Ocean, east of the Kuril Islands (Iturup is around 45N, 147-148E). The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench lies at roughly 46N, 152-153E, with depths of about 8000-9000 m. At 45N, the trench axis is near 151.5-152E, so 151E is probably on the inner trench slope, a bit west of the axis. The Kuril Basin (Sea of Okhotsk side) is further west of the islands, so it doesn't apply here.

Iturup's eastern coast is at about 148E. From there the shelf drops to the trench, which is around 150.5-151.5E at 45N. Depth at 151E is probably around 6500-7500 m. I'll estimate -7000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-7000}

2652.text:
The point 61.0°N, 85.0°E lies in western Siberia, in Tomsk Oblast. This is the Vasyugan swamp region, or close to it, and the terrain there is flat and low. I'm estimating from memory rather than a lookup. The Ob River is near 58-59°N at this longitude, and Tomsk Oblast's northern part sits around 60-61°N. Elevations there are about 80-100 m. The surface is probably Solid, since it is wetland or land rather than open water.

Estimated elevation is about 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

11982.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 25 lies in the Southern Ocean south of South Africa. South Africa's southern tip is at about -34.8 latitude, so this point is roughly 8 degrees farther south.

The Southwest Indian Ridge lies further southwest, and the Conrad Rise is to the southeast near 40-50°E. The Agulhas Plateau is around 26°E, 40°S, with depths of about 2500 m. At -43, 25°E the area lies south of the Agulhas Plateau, in the Agulhas Basin or Agulhas Passage. The Agulhas Basin is roughly 4500-5000 m deep. The Southwest Indian Ridge near 25°E sits at about -50 to -53 latitude, so -43 is likely abyssal basin at about -4800 m. This is an estimate from memory of the regional bathymetry, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

4700.text:
The point at 37°N, 139°W is in the northeast Pacific, roughly 900 km west of California (Monterey Bay is near 36.8°N, 122°W). It sits on abyssal plain, west of the Murray Fracture Zone region, so I need a seafloor depth typical of that area.

The Murray Fracture Zone runs near 32–35°N. At 37°N, 139°W the seafloor age is about 50–60 Ma, which gives a depth of around 4,500–5,000 m. Abyssal plain depths in this area are typically about 4,600 m, so I estimate roughly -4,600 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a lookup of the actual grid value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4650}

7378.text:
The point is 9°N, 177°E, which sits in the central Pacific near the Marshall Islands. The Marshall Islands lie roughly between 160°E and 172°E, so 177°E is east of Kwajalein and Majuro. Ebon Atoll is at about 4.6°N, 168.7°E, and Wake Island is at about 19°N, 166.6°E. No islands are nearby, so this is open ocean.

The seafloor there is Pacific abyssal plain or the area near the Ratak chain's eastern side. The depth is likely about 5,000 m. The Pacific plate in this area has an age of about 100 Ma and a typical depth of about 5,300 m, so I'll estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

9304.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 69 lies in the Indian Ocean, southwest of the Chagos Archipelago (which sits around 5-7°S, 72°E). The point is near the Central Indian Ridge and the Mascarene Plateau region.

The Chagos-Laccadive Ridge runs north-south near 73°E. At 69°E, 13°S, the area is in the Central Indian Basin's western part. The Carlsberg Ridge is further north, and the Central Indian Ridge runs from about 3°S to 25°S near 66-70°E. At 13°S the ridge axis is near 66°E. At 69°E the seafloor is probably on the ridge flank, with depths around 3500-4000 m. The Mauritius Basin and Argo Fracture Zone are also nearby, and I estimate roughly -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7365.text:
Location 9°N, 151°E is in the western Pacific, north of Chuuk (Truk) at about 7.4°N, 151.8°E and south of the Mortlock area. Pohnpei is at 158°E, so it is not near. The point is in open ocean in the Caroline Islands region, between the Caroline Basin to the south and the Pacific plate to the north.

The Caroline Islands are volcanic seamounts rising from seafloor roughly 4000 to 4500 m deep. Around 9°N, 151°E the seafloor is probably about 4500 m deep, possibly nearer 4000 m. The Mariana trench lies far to the north-west. I'll estimate -4400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

11791.text:
The point -41.0, 3.0 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Africa and north of Bouvet. It lies near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, but that ridge is farther west, at roughly 13°W at this latitude, while 3°E is east of it. The Atlantic-Indian Ridge starts near Bouvet Island at about 54°S, so it is not close either.

This area lies in the Cape Basin or the Agulhas Ridge region. The Agulhas Ridge runs from about 40°S 15°E to 46°S 0°E, so at 41°S it would be near 14°E, not 3°E. Further south the ridge reaches 0° at about 46°S. The point is therefore probably in the Cape Basin or the Agulhas Basin, with a depth of about 4500 to 5000 m. The Cape Basin abyssal plain is about 5000 m deep, and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge flank near 3°E would be around 4000 to 4500 m. I'll estimate about -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

8781.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude 103.0 lies in the Indian Ocean southwest of Sumatra and south of Java. Java's southwestern tip is at about 106°E, so this point is open ocean.

The Sunda Trench runs south of Java at roughly 10-11°S near 105°E. Near 103°E it lies further southwest, at about 10°S. At 7°S, 103°E we are on the outer-arc/forearc area. The trench axis near 103°E is around 6-7°S off Sumatra, but it curves, so the axis there is probably near 9-10°S. The Sumatra trench at 5°S sits about 104°E, and the trench at 103°E would be around 8°S. So 7°S, 103°E is likely close to the trench's northern side, at about the outer slope or the trench itself, with depths of 5000-6000 m. The Wharton Basin floor is about 5000 m deep. I'll estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

5241.text:
The point 31°N, 137°W is in the northeast Pacific, about 1,500 km west of Baja California. It lies in open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed here is abyssal plain, roughly 4,000 to 4,300 m deep. The Murray Fracture Zone runs near 31–33°N, but it would not change the depth much at this location. The seafloor age is about 30–40 Myr, which gives a typical depth of about 4,200 m. I estimate the seabed at about -4,150 m, based on typical depths for this region rather than a lookup of this exact point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4150}

1765.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 111.0°E is in northern Siberia, on the Taymyr/Anabar region's northern edge near the Khatanga Gulf and the Laptev Sea coast. The Khatanga Gulf lies around 73°N, 108°E, so 71°N, 111°E is well south of it. This part of the Taymyr lowlands near the Anabar/Popigay area is about 71°N, 111°E and is inland. Ground there is probably a plateau at roughly 100 m, since the Anabar shield lies further south-east and rises to a few hundred meters.

The Khatanga River is at about 72°N, 102°E. The Anabar River sits near 113°E and the Anabar Bay is at 73.5°N. The Popigay River is near 71.5°N, 111°E, flowing north through lowlands at about 50-100 m. I'll estimate 80 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

14677.text:
Latitude -73, longitude 15 E is in Dronning Maud Land, East Antarctica. The Wohlthat Mountains lie around 12-14°E at roughly -71.5 to -72. At -73, 15°E the point is south of the mountains, on the polar plateau, where the ice surface is around 3000 m.

Ice-surface elevation at that location: the plateau south of the Orvin/Wohlthat range is about 2900-3200 m, so I estimate 3000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

4421.text:
The point 41.0°N, 23.0°E is in northern Greece, in the Serres/Kilkis area of Macedonia. Kerkini Lake lies near 41.2°N, 23.1°E, and the Strymon river valley runs near 23.3°E. At 23.0°E and 41.0°N the ground is probably in the hills west of Serres, close to the Kilkis–Serres border and north of Lake Koronia (40.7°N). Its elevation is probably around 300 m. The Kerkini mountains (Belasica) are further north, and Mount Kerkini is at about 41.3°N.

The Kilkis area near 41.0°N, 22.9°E is hilly, and the Gallikos river valley is lower, so I estimate roughly 250 m. I'm not certain of this.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

5426.text:
The point 29°N, 127°W lies in the northeastern Pacific, roughly 1,500 km west of Baja California, well offshore. The seafloor there is abyssal plain or abyssal hill terrain on the Pacific plate. The crust is about 30–40 million years old, which suggests a depth of around 4,000 m.

Nearby reference points: the Murray Fracture Zone runs near 30°N, and the seafloor west of Baja at 127°W is typically 4,000–4,500 m deep. I estimate about -4,300 m. I'm not certain of that figure, since I'm estimating from regional depth patterns rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12099.text:
The point (-45, -101) lies in the southeast Pacific, west of Chile. It is far from any land, in open ocean.

The seafloor there sits on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern flank and the Chile Rise region. The Chile Rise is at about -46, -75 to -92, and the East Pacific Rise extends to the southwest. At -45, -101 the seafloor age is probably 20-30 Ma, and typical depths are around 3500-4000 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies near -55 at -120 and at -60 at -150. At -45, -101 the crust is likely from the Chile Ridge system, with a depth of about 3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2242.text:
The point 65.0°N, 15.0°W is in northeast Iceland, east of Vatnajökull and north of Egilsstaðir. Egilsstaðir sits near 65.27°N, 14.4°W. At 65.0°N, 15.0°W the terrain is the highland region around Jökuldalsheiði and Brúarjökull to the south, which puts it in the highlands between Kverkfjöll and Eyjabakkar.

Eyjabakkar lies at about 64.8°N, 15.5°W. Snæfell is at 64.8°N, 15.6°W. Along 65.0°N, 15.0°W, the area is likely the Fljótsdalsheiði/Hrafnkelsdalur region, with elevation around 600-700 m. Fljótsdalur is at about 65.05°N, 14.8°W, and the highlands west of it reach roughly 600-700 m. I'll estimate 650 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":650}

15355.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -69 is in West Antarctica, in the Ronne Ice Shelf region. The Ronne Ice Shelf spans roughly 75-83°S and 30-82°W. At -81, -69 it is likely on the Ronne Ice Shelf, near the Orville Coast and Rutford Ice Stream area. The Rutford Ice Stream grounding line is near 79°S, 83°W, and the Institute and Möller ice streams are around 81-82°S, 75°W. At 69°W, 81°S, that is probably on the floating Ronne ice shelf, west of Berkner Island (which lies near 46°W). The Ronne shelf runs from about 60°W to the Antarctic Peninsula's Orville Coast at around 65°W at 75-78°S.

So the point is likely floating ice shelf, and the answer needs the seabed elevation. Sea floor depth under the Ronne in that region is about -600 to -900 m; I'll say -700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

8194.text:
The point (-1.0, 9.0) lies in the Gulf of Guinea, off Gabon. Gabon's coast near Cap Lopez is at about 0.6°S, 8.7°E, and Port-Gentil is at about 0.7°S, 8.8°E. At latitude -1.0 the coast runs roughly from 9.3°E (Mayumba is further south), so longitude 9.0 should be offshore. The coastline near Cape Esterias is at about 0.6°N, 9.3°E, and near Gabon's coast at 1°S it is about 8.7°E, because Cap Lopez is the westernmost point. Below Cap Lopez the coast runs southeast, with Ozouri lagoon and Iguela around 1.9°S, 9.3°E. At 1°S the coast should be near 8.9°E, so 9.0°E could be close to land.

Looking more closely, Cap Lopez peninsula is at 0.62°S, 8.7°E. South of it lie Mandji Island and Sette Cama, at 2.5°S, 9.75°E. The coast at 1°S is about 9.0°E, since the coast runs from 8.7°E at 0.6°S to 9.3°E at 2°S. That is about 8.85°E at 1°S, so 9.0°E would be inland, in the Fernan Vaz lagoon area at about 1.0°S, 9.3°E. The Ogooué delta is at roughly 0.9°S, 9.0°E, which is land or swamp. I'll choose Solid with a low elevation, around 10 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":10}

8174.text:
The point (-1°, -31°) is in the equatorial Atlantic, about 600 km east of the Brazilian coast (Fernando de Noronha is near -3.85°, -32.4°). It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near -30° to -25° at this latitude.

The Brazil Basin and Ceará Rise sit in this region. The Ceará Rise is at about 2°N, -43°, so it is too far west. The Fernando de Noronha ridge runs east-west near -3.8°. At -1°, -31° the seabed is likely the Ceará Abyssal Plain or its eastern margin, at roughly 4,500 m depth. The St. Paul fracture zones are farther east, around -29°, and the ridge axis is near -30° to -25° at the equator. So the point is probably on the western flank of the ridge, where depths are about 4,000 m. I'll estimate -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

15695.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -109 is in West Antarctica, in the Marie Byrd Land / Ellsworth Land interior, roughly south of the Thwaites and Pine Island catchments, near the Whitmore Mountains region at about 85°S, 109°W. This is grounded ice on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet.

The Ross Ice Shelf is at longitudes of about 150°W to 180°W, so this point is not on it. The ice sheet surface near 85°S, 109°W is probably around 1500-2000 m. The Whitmore Mountains are near 82°S, 104°W. The Bentley Subglacial Trench is around 80°S, 110°W. At 85°S, 109°W the point is near the Horlick/Thiel Mountains side, with the ice surface around 2000 m. That is a rough estimate, so I'll go with about 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

8016.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 13.0°E is in northern Gabon, near the border with Equatorial Guinea and Cameroon, in the Woleu-Ntem/Ogooué-Ivindo region. It is inland and covered by forest, so the surface is solid.

Typical elevation in this plateau area is around 500 m, though I'm estimating from memory of the region rather than looking up a value. The Ntem river valley lies slightly to the north, at about 1.5°N, and Minvoul is at 2.15°N, 12.13°E at about 600 m. Near 1°N, 13°E the elevation is probably 450–550 m, so I'll answer 520.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":520}

7276.text:
The point is at 9°N, 27°W, in the tropical Atlantic Ocean. That is west of Cape Verde (which sits near 23°W, 15°N) and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs near 38–40°W at this latitude. The surrounding seafloor there is abyssal plain, with depths of roughly 4500–5000 m.

The Sierra Leone Rise lies near 5°N, 22°W, and the area around 9°N, 27°W is mostly deep basin. I estimate about -4700 m, which is an estimate from regional bathymetry and not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

4371.text:
The point 41.0°N, 77.0°W is in central Pennsylvania. It lies near the West Branch Susquehanna River region, in Lycoming or Union County, around 41.0N, 77.0W.

Williamsport is at 41.24N, 77.0W. At 41.0N, the point is south of there, near the Union/Snyder/Northumberland/Lycoming border. Montour County and Danville are at about 40.96N, 76.6W. At 77.0W and 41.0N the area is probably Union County, west of Lewisburg (76.88W) and near Mifflinburg (77.05W, 40.92N). The terrain there is ridge-and-valley, so it is likely in the Buffalo Valley or on Buffalo Mountain, with an elevation around 250-400 m. At 41.0N the point is north of Mifflinburg, probably near Buffalo Creek, where Buffalo Valley sits at roughly 200-250 m. White Deer Ridge lies to the north. I estimate 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

3711.text:
Latitude 49.0, longitude 43.0 is in Russia's Volgograd Oblast, near the Volga Upland and the Don river region, close to Mikhaylovka or Kamyshin. Longitude 43 E at latitude 49 N lies west of the Volga, which is at about 44.7 E at that latitude. The Medveditsa river area is nearby, and the Don is further west at about 43.5 E near Serafimovich (49.57 N, 42.7 E).

Terrain there is upland, roughly 150-200 m. The Volga Upland near Mikhaylovka is about 200 m, so I estimate 170 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

6326.text:
The point at 19°N, 127°W is in the open Pacific, well west of Baja California and northeast of Hawaii. The surface type is therefore Water.

That area sits on the abyssal plain of the northeast Pacific. Seafloor depths there are typically around 4,500 to 5,000 m, and the Clarion Fracture Zone lies further south. The Pacific plate crust at this location is roughly 40 to 60 million years old, which corresponds to a depth of about 4,300 to 4,800 m. I estimate -4,600 m, based on typical regional depths rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

10634.text:
The point is at 29°S, 151°W, in the South Pacific. That places it south of the Austral Islands (Tubuai is near 23.4°S, 149.5°W; Rapa is near 27.6°S, 144.3°W). The nearest land is therefore a long way off, so the surface is almost certainly open ocean.

The seafloor in this part of the South Pacific lies on the French Polynesia plateau and nearby basins. Typical depths are 3500–4500 m on older seafloor, but the Austral–Cook seamount chain may raise the floor locally. At this location I estimate a depth of about 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14826.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -47 is in the southern Weddell Sea, near the Filchner Ice Shelf front. The Filchner-Ronne ice shelf spans roughly 30°W to 80°W. At -47° longitude and -75° latitude, this is likely the Filchner Ice Shelf area, or just west of the Filchner Trough. The Filchner Ice Shelf front lies around 78°S at 40°W, and the Ronne Ice Shelf front is near 75°S at 60°W. At 75°S, 47°W, the Berkner Island region is around 79°S, 45°W, with its northern tip near 77.5°S. So 75°S, 47°W is north of Berkner Island, which puts it in open water (Weddell Sea continental shelf) at the Filchner Trough's western flank.

The seabed depth there is likely about 400-600 m on the shelf, and the Filchner Trough is deeper at roughly 1000 m. At 47°W the point is on the western side of the trough or on the shelf. I estimate -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

15108.text:
Latitude -77.0, longitude 157.0 is in East Antarctica, in Victoria Land. That places it inland of the Ross Sea coast, west of the Ross Ice Shelf and near the Transantarctic Mountains. The Ross Sea coast near McMurdo sits around 166°E, so 157°E is well inland on the polar plateau, in the area around the Mawson Glacier and the Skelton névé.

At about -77, 157, I'd expect a grounded ice surface on the plateau at roughly 2000 to 2500 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a looked-up value. The Skelton névé area near 78°S, 156°E is about 1800 m. The Mill Glacier and Darwin Glacier regions are further south. At -77, 157, the surface is probably in the 2000 m range. I'll answer 2100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2100}

4215.text:
Location 43°N, 29°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis. The ridge axis near 43°N is around 29-30°W. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge at 43°N lies approximately at 29.5°W, just south of the Azores–Gibraltar region and north of the Azores triple junction at 39°N. Ridge crest depths there are about 2500-3000 m, with flanks deeper. At 43°N the axis is near 29°W, so the point is likely on the ridge, with rift valley depths of around 3000 m or slightly more.

I estimate a depth of about -2900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2900}

13845.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 151 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia and well north of Antarctica's coast (which is at about -66 to -67 at this longitude). It is open ocean.

The Southern Ocean here is south of the Tasmania/Macquarie region. The Southeast Indian Ridge runs through the area at about 150°E, roughly at -62 to -63 latitude, and the Australian-Antarctic Basin lies to the west of that. Near 63°S, 151°E, the depth is probably about 3000 to 4000 m. The ridge between Macquarie and the Antarctic coast (the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge junction) lies further east, near 160°E at -62. The Southeast Indian Ridge reaches the Tasmanian region at about 147°E, -55 and then continues to the southeast, so at 151°E, -63 we could be near the Balleny area, but that is further east (163°E). I'll estimate the depth at around 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

13772.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 5 E lies in the Southern Ocean, south of the Southwest Indian Ridge and near the Bouvet Triple Junction region. Bouvet Island is at 54.4°S, 3.4°E, so this point is about 8.6° south of it. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs near 53-55°S at this longitude. At 63°S, 5°E the point is in the Weddell Sea/Enderby Basin area, on the abyssal plain of the Atlantic-Indian sector, where depths are about 4,500-5,000 m. The Antarctic continental margin begins around 70°S.

I estimate the seabed at about -4,800 m, based on regional knowledge rather than a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

870.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 121.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea region north of the Taymyr Peninsula and the New Siberian Islands. The Severnaya Zemlya archipelago is at about 79-81°N, 95-110°E, so 121°E is east of it. At 81°N, 121°E the point is open sea, probably over the continental slope.

The Laptev Sea shelf extends to about 78-79°N in places, and in the eastern part to roughly 80°N. The shelf break lies near 80-81°N, and north of it the slope descends into the Nansen Basin. At 81°N, 121°E the depth is probably around 2000-3000 m. In the IBCAO bathymetry, the 3000 m contour near 82°N, 120°E marks the slope. At 81°N I estimate the depth is about 1500-2500 m, so I'll say -2200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2200}

11545.text:
This location (39°S, 129°W) is in the South Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water.

It lies southeast of Tahiti, in the area north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and west of the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise at that latitude is near 115°W, so this point is on the Pacific plate flank. Seafloor depths there are typically 3500–4500 m. The crust is about 20–30 Myr old, which suggests a depth of roughly 4000 m. I am estimating from regional patterns rather than a specific bathymetry reading, so I'll use about 4000 m below sea level.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4000}

12884.text:
Latitude -53, longitude 29 is in the Southern Ocean, southeast of Africa. It lies near the Southwest Indian Ridge, with Marion and Prince Edward Islands at about -46.9, 37.7. The Del Caño Rise is around -45, 44, and the Southwest Indian Ridge runs between roughly -50 and -53 near 20-30°E. The Conrad Rise sits at about -53, 40.

At 29°E, 53°S, the point is probably near the ridge axis or on a flank. The Bouvet triple junction is at about -54.5, 1°E, and the ridge runs northeast from there. By 25°E the ridge is at about -52, and by 30°E near -52 to -53. The Du Toit and Andrew Bain fracture zones are near 30°E, at around -53 to -50 and 30°E. So the point is possibly on the ridge or in a fracture zone, where depths are roughly 3000-4500 m. I'll estimate about -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

12621.text:
The point is at 51°S, 137°W, in the South Pacific Ocean. It lies southeast of Tahiti and well west of Chile, on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern flank, or possibly near the Eltanin fracture zone region.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs near 55-65°S at this longitude, so 51°S is north of the ridge crest. The seafloor age there is moderate, and typical depths are about 3,500 to 4,000 m. I estimate about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7254.text:
Latitude 9.0, longitude -71.0 falls in western Venezuela, near the Colombia border. Lake Maracaibo lies around 10 to 11°N, 71.5°W, so this point is south of the lake. It is in the Zulia/Táchira/Mérida area, probably the Sur del Lago lowlands or the foothills of the Andes.

At 9.0°N, 71.0°W, the Mérida Andes run northeast to southwest. Mérida city is at 8.6°N, 71.15°W, and the Sur del Lago plain reaches about 9°N near El Vigía (8.6°N, 71.65°W). A point at 9.0°N, 71.0°W is northeast of Mérida, in the area around Estanques, Chiguará, or the Mucuchíes foothills. That area is probably the northern flank of the Andes, in the Mérida/Trujillo/Barinas region. Barinas is at 8.6°N, 70.2°W. The point sits on the Andean slopes toward the Sur del Lago side, near Guaraque, Mesa Bolívar, or Tovar (8.33°N, 71.75°W). I'd estimate about 1000 m elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

2069.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude -1.0 (1°W) is in the Norwegian Sea. It lies roughly 400 km west of the Norwegian coast at the Arctic Circle latitude and north of the Vøring Plateau. The Vøring Plateau is at about 67°N, 4°E, with depths of 1200-1500 m. To the west, the Norwegian Basin has a floor around 3000-3700 m. The Mohn Ridge is further north, near 71°N, so it is not a factor here.

At 1°W, 67°N, the position is in the Norwegian Basin, west of the Vøring Plateau's outer edge and the Jan Mayen Fracture Zone region. Depths there are about 3200-3500 m. I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

1575.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 91.0°E lies in the Taymyr Peninsula region of Russia, near the Khatanga Gulf and the Taymyr coast. The Taymyr Peninsula's northern coast is at about 77°N, and the Khatanga Gulf is around 73°N at 106°E. At 91°E and 73°N, that puts the point in the northern Taymyr lowlands, near the Pyasina River and Taymyr Lake, which sits at roughly 74.5°N, 101°E. The Byrranga mountains are further north at about 75°N.

The Gulf of Yenisei and Yenisei Bay reach about 73°N near 80°E. At 91°E, 73°N, the Taymyr lowland is mostly land, with elevations around 50 m. The Pyasina River is at roughly 73.5°N, 87°E. Near 91°E the terrain is probably tundra at about 40 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":40}

11647.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 75 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Amsterdam Island (about -37.8, 77.5) and southwest of the Broken Plateau. Amsterdam and St. Paul sit on the Amsterdam-St Paul plateau, and the Mid-Indian Ridge passes near 77°E at that latitude. At 75°E, 39°S, the position is just southwest of the plateau. The seafloor there is probably around 3,000 to 4,000 m deep, since the ridge flanks are shallower at roughly 3,000 m.

I'll estimate -3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

12297.text:
Latitude -47, longitude -65 is in Argentina, Patagonia, Santa Cruz province, though close to the coast. The Atlantic coast near Puerto Deseado is at about -47.75, -65.9. Bahía Bustamante is at -45. At -47, -65 we are about 100 km or more east of the coast near Puerto Deseado, so it lies offshore in the Atlantic, on the Patagonian shelf. The shelf depth there is about 100 m, so I estimate the seabed at about -95 m.

The coast near Cabo Blanco is at about -47.2, -65.8, so -65.0 is roughly 60 km offshore. Depth is probably around 80 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-85}

6040.text:
The coordinates 23°N, 21°E fall in southeastern Libya, in the Libyan Desert near the Kufra basin, northwest of the Tibesti and Kufra area. This region is Sahara desert plateau. Elevation there is likely around 500 m. This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a looked-up value.

The Kufra basin sits around 24°N, 23°E at roughly 400 m. Further west, near 21°E, the terrain is slightly higher, and the Tibesti Mountains lie to the southwest. I'll estimate about 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

10865.text:
Location: -31, -49 is in the South Atlantic off southern Brazil (Rio Grande do Sul coast is around -29.5 to -33 lat, with coast at about -50.5 to -51 lon at that latitude). So -49 lon is about 200 km offshore. The continental shelf there is wide, with the shelf break at about 180-200 km offshore, at depths of ~150-200 m. At -31, -49 the position is near the shelf break, and the upper slope depth is perhaps 400 to 1000 m.

The coast at -31 is around -50.9. Distance of 1.9° of longitude at cos(31)=0.857 is about 180 km. The shelf here is about 100-150 km wide off Rio Grande do Sul, with the shelf break at ~180 m. So at 180 km we're slightly past the break on the upper slope, with a depth of maybe -400 m. I'll go with -500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

16173.text:
This location is at 89°S, 127°E, which is on the Antarctic plateau about 100 km from the South Pole. The ice surface elevation there is roughly 2,800 m. The South Pole itself sits at about 2,835 m. Along the 127°E meridian, which runs toward the Wilkes Land side, the elevation increases slightly, so about 2,900 m seems reasonable at 89°S.

The surface is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 2900}

6050.text:
Latitude 23.0°N, longitude 41.0°E is in western Saudi Arabia, east of the Red Sea coast and the Hijaz mountains. It lies roughly northeast of Mecca, and Mecca is at 21.4°N, 39.8°E. Medina is at 24.5°N, 39.6°E. The point is therefore in the interior Arabian Shield region, east of the Hijaz, at about 41°E.

Elevations there are around 900-1100 m. Taif, at 21.3°N, 40.4°E, is about 1800 m. Near 23°N, 41°E the terrain is a plateau, probably around 1000 m. This is an estimate from regional elevations, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

4081.text:
The point 45.0°N, 63.0°E is in Kazakhstan, in the Kyzylorda region. It lies east of the Aral Sea, near the Syr Darya, and northwest of the Kyzylkum desert. The Syr Darya runs through Kyzylorda (44.85°N, 65.5°E), so 63°E is west of that city. This area is the Aral Karakum / Greater Barsuki region, with elevations around 70–100 m.

The southern Aral Sea's eastern shore is near 61°E at 45°N. The former Small Aral Sea is at 46.5°N, 61°E. The point at 45°N, 63°E is therefore about 200 km east of the Aral Sea, in the Syr Darya lowlands near Kazaly and Zhosaly. Kazaly is at 45.76°N, 62.1°E with an elevation of about 60 m. Moving southeast toward Zhosaly (45.5°N, 64.1°E, about 90 m), I estimate about 80 m at the point.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

7026.text:
The point is 11°N, 167°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of the Hawaiian Islands and roughly north of the Marshall Islands, which are at about 170°E and so are far to the west. Johnston Atoll is at 16.7°N, 169.5°W, which is too far north to be at this point. This location is open ocean in the north-central Pacific basin.

Seafloor depth there is likely around 5,000 to 5,500 m. The region lies on Cretaceous-age crust, so it is fairly deep, and the nearby Line Islands and Musicians seamounts may add some relief. I estimate about -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

5029.text:
The point (35°N, 159°E) lies in the northwest Pacific, east of Japan (Honshu is at about 140°E). That puts it roughly 1,700 km east of Japan, over the Northwest Pacific Basin.

Seafloor depth there is about 5,500–6,000 m. The Japan Trench is far to the west, and the Shatsky Rise is farther north at about 160°E, 33–36°N. The Shatsky Rise's southern part, the Tamu Massif, spans roughly 158–160°E and 32–35°N. Its summit is about 2,000 m deep, with some parts reaching about 3,500 m. A point at 35°N, 159°E could sit on the Ori Massif or Shirshov Massif, the northern parts of the rise, which are around 34–36°N and 158–163°E. The rise is about 1,500 km long, trending northeast from about 32°N, 158°E to 41°N, 164°E. Tamu Massif is the largest, centered near 33°N, 158.5°E. The Ori Massif is centered near 35.5°N, 161.5°E. So 35°N, 159°E is probably on the flank between them, at a depth of about 3,000 m. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

15162.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -95 is in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector, inland of the Pine Island/Thwaites area. It is near the Ellsworth Land and Thwaites Glacier catchment, around 79°S, 95°W. The Thwaites catchment lies roughly between 75–78°S and 100–110°W, so this point is further inland, toward the Byrd Subglacial Basin and the Ellsworth Mountains, which are near 80°S, 82°W.

At this location the ice sheet is grounded, with a surface elevation of roughly 1,200–1,800 m. The bed is probably near sea level or somewhat below, but the question asks for the ice-surface elevation, so I estimate about 1,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1500}

8763.text:
The point (-7.0, 67.0) is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Diego Garcia, which sits at about -7.3, 72.4. It is east of the Mascarene Plateau and near the Central Indian Ridge, which runs roughly along 66-68E at this latitude.

The ridge axis near 7S is at about 66-67E. The Carlsberg Ridge lies further north, near 3N 63E, and the Central Indian Ridge begins around 3S 68E. At 7S the ridge axis is near 66.5E, with depths of about 3000 m on the ridge flanks and axial valley floor depths of about 3500 m. I estimate the seabed here at about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

6668.text:
The point 15°N, 163°W lies in the central Pacific, south of the Hawaiian Islands and north of the Line Islands. It is open ocean over the Pacific plate, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain, typically 5200 to 5500 m deep. I did not look up a bathymetry value, so I am estimating from typical depths for that part of the Pacific. The area is north of the Line Islands and east of the Marshall Islands, and ocean floor of this age usually sits around 5400 m. I'll use -5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

4266.text:
Latitude 43.0°N, longitude 73.0°E is in southern Kazakhstan. It lies near the Kyrgyz border, north of the Talas Alatau and west of Lake Issyk-Kul and the Chu River. The area is probably the Betpak-Dala desert or the Moiynkum desert. The Chu River runs near 43°N at about 72–73°E, at roughly 400–500 m elevation.

Moiynkum sands at 73°E, 43°N sit at about 400 m. Taraz is at 71.4°E, 42.9°N and about 650 m. The Chu River near Shu town (43.6°N, 73.76°E) is at about 400 m. At 43.0°N, 73.0°E the terrain should be a bit south of the Chu and sloping up toward the Kyrgyz Range foothills. The Kyrgyz Range foothills are around 42.8°N, and Kordai is at 43.0°N, 74.7°E. The point is probably near Sary-Shagan or Moiynkum at about 500 m. I'll estimate 480 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

2677.text:
Latitude 61.0°N, longitude 135.0°E is in the Sakha Republic (Yakutia) / Khabarovsk border area, in the Verkhoyansk Range / Sette-Daban region, east of the Aldan River. The Aldan flows around 63°N at 135°E... actually the Aldan is further south, near 130-135°E at 58-62°N. At 135°E the Aldan River is around 61°N, so the point could be near the river, but the Aldan's valley there lies at roughly 150-200 m. The area around Ust-Maya (60.4°N, 134.5°E) is at about 100 m. At 61°N, 135°E, the point is near the Aldan below the Maya confluence, with elevation of roughly 150 m. The Aldan runs from Khandyga (62.6°N, 135.6°E) southwest to Ust-Maya, so 61°N, 135°E is probably west of the river, on the plateau at about 300 m, though it may be on the Aldan itself.

I'll guess Solid at 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

15613.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 87 is in East Antarctica, in the interior near Dome A. Dome A is at about -80.37, 77.35 with an elevation of roughly 4093 m. At -83, 87 we are further south and slightly east, toward the Gamburtsev Subglacial Mountains' southern flank and the area south of Dome A.

Ice surface elevation here is probably around 3,500 m. Along the route from Dome A toward the South Pole, the surface drops from about 4,000 m at 80°S to roughly 3,500 m at 83°S. I'll estimate 3,550 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3550}

9725.text:
Latitude -19.0, longitude -169.0 is near Niue, which sits at about 19.05°S, 169.87°W. Niue spans roughly -169.95 to -169.78 longitude, so -169.0 is about 0.8° east of the island, or roughly 85 km away. That puts the point in open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is the South Pacific basin between Niue and the Tonga/Samoa region. Tonga Trench is further west, near -173. The Niue platform is surrounded by deep water of around 4000-5000 m. Between Niue and the Cook Islands (Palmerston at -163.2), the depth is about 4500-5000 m. I'll estimate -4700 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

13638.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 97 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Southeast Indian Ridge. The ridge crosses around 50°S near 97°E, and the Australian-Antarctic Basin lies south of it. At 61°S the seafloor is probably abyssal plain, with depths of about 4000-4500 m. Near 60°S, 95°E the depth is about 4000 m. The Kerguelen Plateau is farther west, so it is not a factor here.

I estimate a seabed elevation of about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

2879.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 179.0°E is in the western Bering Sea, east of the Kamchatka/Koryak coast. The Koryak coast near Cape Olyutorsky is around 60°N, 170°E, so 179°E is well offshore. The Gulf of Anadyr and Navarin area lies near 62°N, 178°E. At 59°N, 179°E the point is on the Shirshov Ridge's eastern side or in the Aleutian Basin. The Aleutian Basin is about 3500-3800 m deep, and the Shirshov Ridge sits near 170-173°E. The Bowers Ridge is further south. The Navarin shelf edge runs from about 60°N, 177°E toward the southeast. At 59°N, 179°E the point is probably on the continental slope or in the basin, at roughly 3300 m depth.

I estimate the seabed at about 3500 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

2750.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -79.0 falls in eastern Hudson Bay, close to the Quebec coast. The Nunavik coast near Inukjuak is at about 58.45N, -78.1W, and Hudson Bay's eastern shore at 59N lies near -77.7W. At -79.0 the point is therefore roughly 80 km offshore, in the water. This area includes the Ottawa Islands and Belcher Islands to the south, but the Belcher Islands sit at about 56N, so they are not a factor here.

Bathymetry in eastern Hudson Bay at this distance from shore is typically 50 to 100 m deep. I estimate about -90 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-90}

1534.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 9.0°E lies in the Norwegian Sea/Greenland Sea area, north of Norway and southeast of Jan Mayen. Bear Island is at about 74.4°N, 19°E, and Jan Mayen at 71°N, 8°W, so this point is open ocean.

The nearby bathymetry is the Lofoten Basin and Mohns Ridge region. The Mohns Ridge runs northeast from Jan Mayen, passing roughly 73°N at about 8°E. The Lofoten Basin is about 3200 m deep at 71°N, 3°E. The Knipovich Ridge begins further north, near 73.5°N, 8°E. At 73°N, 9°E the point lies near the junction of the Mohns and Knipovich ridges, where depths are around 2500–3000 m. The Mohns Ridge axis at about 72.5°N, 1–3°E is about 2500 m deep. At 73°N, 9°E we are east of the ridge, on the Bear Island fan margin side, so I estimate a depth of about 2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

6154.text:
The point 21.0°N, 111.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, southwest of the southern tip of Baja California and west of the Revillagigedo Islands. Socorro Island is at about 18.8°N, 111.0°W, so this point is roughly 220 km north of it.

The seafloor here is abyssal plain or near the base of the Baja continental rise. Depths in this area are around 3,500 to 3,700 m. The East Pacific Rise lies farther east at the Gulf of California mouth, and the crust here is about 10 to 15 Ma, so a depth of about -3,600 m is reasonable.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

7204.text:
The point is at 9°N, 171°W in the central Pacific. The nearest land is Swains Island at about 11°S, and Howland and Baker are near 0°N, 176°W. Nothing at 9°N, 171°W is land, so this is open ocean.

The seabed there is probably deep Pacific basin, north of Samoa and east of the Marshall Islands. The Line Islands chain lies farther east. Typical depths in this area are about 5000 to 5500 m. I could not pin down a more exact figure, so I'm estimating -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

8287.text:
The point (-3.0, -165.0) lies in the central Pacific, south of the equator and west of the Phoenix Islands (Kiribati). Nearby land is Kanton at about -2.8, -171.7, and Howland and Baker are further north. The Line Islands are at about -157, so this point is in open ocean.

The seafloor in this region, between the Phoenix Islands and the Line Islands, is the Central Pacific Basin. Depths there are typically around 5000 to 5500 m. I could not recall a specific bathymetry value for this point, so I estimate about -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

10254.text:
The point is at 23°S, 169°E, in the southwest Pacific. New Caledonia lies near 166°E and Vanuatu is to the north. Maré and Lifou (Loyalty Islands) are around 168°E at 21-22°S, so they are not at this point. Matthew and Hunter islands lie near 22.3°S, 171.3°E, also not at this point.

The point falls in the area east of the Loyalty Ridge, near the southern New Hebrides Basin or the Loyalty Basin. The Loyalty Islands ridge is at about 167-168°E. East of it, the New Hebrides Trench lies to the north, and around 23°S, 169°E the seafloor is probably a basin at about 3000 m depth. Further east, the Norfolk Ridge/Three Kings area is at 23°S, 169-170°E; the Loyalty Basin is about 3500 m deep, and this region is probably a bit shallower than that. I estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

6170.text:
Latitude 21.0, longitude -79.0 is on Cuba's north coast, in the Camagüey/Ciego de Ávila area. The north coast near 21.5N, -78 has the Sabana-Camagüey archipelago. At 21.0N, -79.0W the point is probably inland, since the Cuban coast near -79 (Sancti Spiritus province) lies around 21.6N on the north side. The south coast near -79 is around 21.5N (Júcaro-Tunas de Zaza at about -79.5, 21.6N), so 21.0N would be in the Gulf of Ana María on the south, which is water.

The south coast near -79.0 is about 21.4N (Ana María gulf, Jardines de la Reina begins around -79.5). At 21.0N, -79.0 the point is in the Gulf of Ana María, which is shallow shelf, with depth of roughly 10 m. The Jardines de la Reina archipelago lies about 20.8N, -78.9, and the cays are at roughly 20.7N. So 21.0 is on the shelf lagoon, about 10 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-10}

3561.text:
The point 51.0°N, 103.0°E lies in Buryatia, Russia, in the Selenga/Dzhida area south of Lake Baikal. It is near the Khamar-Daban or Zakamensk region. Zakamensk is at about 50.37°N, 103.28°E, and the Dzhida river valley sits around 1000 m. At 51.0°N, 103.0°E, the terrain is likely in the Dzhida mountains or the Khamar-Daban uplands, or in the Tuva and Sayan foothills near the Oka plateau.

The Selenga flows near Ulan-Ude at 51.8°N, 107.6°E, which is far away. The Dzhida valley is at about 50.7°N, 104°E. The Zakamensk area at 103°E and 51°N is mountainous, with the Dzhidinsky range and Khamar-Daban's western end, so I estimate elevation at about 1700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1700}

2409.text:
The point is 63.0°N, 41.0°W, which lies in the Irminger Sea, southeast of Greenland. The southeast Greenland coast near 63°N sits around 41–43°W, so I checked whether the point falls on land. The coast near Cape Dan (65.5°N, 41°W) is farther north. At 63°N the coast is around 43°W, near Skjoldungen at about 63.2°N, 41.5°W. Skjoldungen island is at about 63.1°N, 41.6°W. So the point may be very near the coast, but it is probably offshore, at around 41°W.

The shelf off southeast Greenland is fairly wide at this latitude, though narrower than elsewhere. The Greenland coast near 63°N: Skjoldungen Fjord is at roughly 63.3°N, 41.5°W, and the outer coast islands extend to about 41.0°W. The point could therefore sit on the outer shelf or the shelf break, at a depth of roughly 300–1000 m. I'll estimate about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

560.text:
Latitude 83.0, longitude -139.0 is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Ellef Ringnes and Axel Heiberg Island and northwest of Ellesmere Island. Axel Heiberg's northern tip is at about 81°N, and Ellesmere's northern coast is at about 83°N but at longitude -60 to -90. So this point is in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, roughly in the Canada Basin margin.

The Canadian Arctic continental shelf is narrow, and at 83°N, -139° the water is likely deep. The Amundsen Basin and Canada Basin lie in this region, along with the Alpha Ridge, which is at about 85°N, -120°. At 83N, -139 the seabed is probably around 3,500 to 3,800 m deep, in the Canada Basin abyssal plain, which is about 3,800 m. I'll estimate -3,700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

10898.text:
Latitude -31.0, longitude 17.0 is in South Africa's Northern Cape, near Calvinia and Nieuwoudtville. Calvinia sits at about -31.47, 19.78, and Nieuwoudtville at about -31.37, 19.11. A longitude of 17.0 is well to the west of both, in Namaqualand, close to Garies (-30.56, 17.99) and Kamieskroon (-30.2, 17.9). Vanrhynsdorp is at -31.6, 18.7. Since 17.0 lies about 1.5° west of Vanrhynsdorp, the point is inland from the coast, which is near 17.9 at this latitude. The coast near Strandfontein is around 18.2, so at 17.0 the point should be over the Atlantic.

Checking this properly: at -31, the coast runs from Hondeklip Bay (-30.3, 17.3) south to Doring Bay (-31.8, 18.2). So at -31.0 the coast is around 17.6, and 17.0 is about 50 km offshore. The shelf there is roughly 100–150 m deep at 50 km out, and the shelf edge is further west. I estimate about -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

3474.text:
The coordinates 51.0°N, 71.0°W fall in central Quebec, on the Canadian Shield. This is north of the Saguenay region, near the Manicouagan area and the Laurentides Wildlife Reserve. The Manicouagan reservoir is at about 51.4°N, 68.7°W, which is farther east, so it does not apply here.

At 51°N, 71°W the location is north of Lac Saint-Jean (48.6°N) and near the Péribonka and Mistassini region. The terrain there is a plateau at roughly 500 m. I could not pin down whether the point lies on a lake, so I am assuming land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":520}

13009.text:
Latitude -55, longitude -81 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile (Chile's coast is near -74 at that latitude). The area lies west of the Chile Trench and south of the Chile Rise.

The Chile Trench near 55°S is mostly filled with sediment and is not deep there. Longitude -81 is roughly 500 km west of the Chile coast. The seafloor around the Diego Ramírez area and the Drake Passage entrance is about 3500-4500 m deep. At 55°S, 81°W the plate is the Antarctic plate, with the Chile Rise to the north near 46°S and the Antarctic-Phoenix ridge remnants to the south. The seafloor age is probably old, giving a depth of about -4000 m. I estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14565.text:
Location: -71, 151E is in East Antarctica, Wilkes Land / Victoria Land region. Longitude 151E at latitude -71 is near the Oates Land coast, inland. The coast at 151E is around -69.5 to -70 (the Mertz Glacier is at ~145E, -67). At 151E the coast is near -68.5 or so, so -71 is about 250 km inland. The ice surface there is probably about 2000 m.

Oates Land: the Rennick Glacier is at 160E. At 151E, -71 is the interior of the Wilkes Land margin, with ice elevation around 2000-2300 m. I'll guess 2150.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2150}

7640.text:
This location (5°N, 19°W) is in the equatorial Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which lies near 30°W at this latitude. It sits in the Sierra Leone Basin or Guinea Basin region, and the seabed there is likely around 4,500 to 5,000 m deep.

The ridge crest near 5°N is at about 30-32°W. The Sierra Leone Rise lies at about 5°N, 21°W, with depths of about 2,500-3,500 m. The rise spans roughly 3-8°N and 17-25°W, so 19°W is probably on it. Its summit depth is around 2,500 m, and I'll estimate about 3,800 m for this point, since the flanks are deeper.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

11724.text:
The point is at 41°S, 131°W, in the South Pacific Ocean. It lies southwest of the East Pacific Rise and well to the west of Chile. The seafloor there is abyssal plain, with an age of roughly 30-40 Ma.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies further south, at about 55-60°S near 140°W. The East Pacific Rise at 41°S is around 110°W, so this point is about 20° of longitude west of it. That is roughly 1,600 km at this latitude. Using a spreading half-rate of about 70 km/Myr gives an age of about 23 Ma. Seafloor depth is approximately 2600 + 350·√age, which is 2600 + 350·4.8 ≈ 4300 m. The Southeast Pacific Basin there is typically about 4000-4400 m deep. I'll estimate -4150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4150}

12277.text:
The point at 47°S, 105°W is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and far from any land. It lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern flank, roughly near the East Pacific Rise region. The East Pacific Rise at that latitude is around 112-115°W, so 105°W is east of the ridge crest. The seafloor there is probably around 3,500-4,000 m deep.

The Chile Rise and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge are at about 47°S, 115°W, with depths near 2,500-3,000 m. At 105°W the crust is older, so the depth should be about 3,800 m. I'll estimate -3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

11082.text:
Latitude -33.0, longitude 25.0 is in South Africa's Eastern Cape. Port Elizabeth (Gqeberha) sits at about -33.96, 25.6, and the coast near there is at roughly -34. At -33.0 and 25.0 the point is inland, north of the coast, near Uitenhage and the Darlington area. Uitenhage is at -33.77, 25.4. Further north at -33.0, 25.0 is close to the Klein Winterhoek and Zuurberg mountains. The Zuurberg is near -33.3, 25.7. The Sundays River valley lies near -33.2, 25.5.

Cradock is at -32.17, 25.6 and Jansenville at -32.95, 24.67, with an elevation of about 300 m. The point at 25.0 is east of Jansenville, in the Darlington/Klipfontein area, so the elevation is probably around 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":420}

8809.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude 159.0 lies in the Solomon Islands region. New Georgia is around -8.3, 157.5, and Santa Isabel is around -8, 159. Choiseul is around -7.0, 157. Santa Isabel's northwest tip is near -7.4, 158.4, so this point at -7.0, 159.0 is probably in the ocean north of Santa Isabel. The nearby Ontong Java Plateau lies farther north, at about -5, 159. The Indispensable Strait and the area between Santa Isabel and Malaita are to the southeast.

The point is northeast of Santa Isabel's northern coast, which runs from about -7.4, 158.5 to -8.0, 159.5. The water there is probably deep, with the New Georgia Sound (the Slot) to the southwest. Toward the Ontong Java Plateau the depth would be about -2000 m, but near Santa Isabel, north of the island, it is probably around -1500 m. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

11582.text:
Latitude -39, longitude -55 is in the South Atlantic, off the coast of Argentina and Uruguay. The Argentine coast near 39°S is at about 62°W, so -55 is well offshore. It lies east of the continental shelf break, which is around 100-300 km offshore at this latitude. The shelf break there is near 54°W, and the Brazil-Malvinas confluence region sits near 38-39°S.

At 39°S, 55°W, the point is likely on the continental slope or rise. The Argentine Basin has depths of 5000-6000 m. The slope runs from about 200 m to 4000 m. The shelf near 39°S extends to roughly 56°W, so at 55°W the point is probably on the slope at around 1500-3000 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

4051.text:
The point 45.0°N, 3.0°E lies in the Massif Central in France, near Saint-Flour in Cantal. Saint-Flour is at about 45.03°N, 3.09°E and sits at roughly 880 m on a plateau. The nearby Truyère valley and the Planèze plateau are at about 900 to 1000 m.

Just west of Saint-Flour, at 3.0°E, the terrain is the Planèze de Saint-Flour, at about 950 m. Nearby, Murat is at 2.87°E and 45.11°N, and the Allagnon valley runs near 3.0°E at 45.0°N. Brioude is at 3.4°E. The Allagnon passes Lempdes and Massiac (45.25°N). At 45.0°N, 3.0°E, the point is probably near Ferrières-Saint-Mary or Vieillespesse, at about 800 to 900 m. I'll estimate 870 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":870}

2373.text:
The point 63.0°N, 113.0°W is in the Northwest Territories, Canada, northwest of Yellowknife (62.45°N, 114.4°W) and east of the Great Slave Lake's northern arm. It lies in the Slave Craton region, which has many small lakes. Typical terrain there is around 300-350 m. Lakes in this area, such as Prosperous Lake and Hearne Lake, sit at about 200 m, but this point is further northeast, near Lac la Martre or Gordon Lake (62.5°N, 112.5°W). Gordon Lake is at about 62.4°N, 112.6°W, so 63.0°N is farther north.

The Camsell River and Hardisty Lake are near 63°N, 117°W, so those are not close. Near 113°W, 63°N, the land is probably barren upland with many small lakes at roughly 350 m. I'll predict solid ground at about 340 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":340}

12445.text:
The point at 49°S, 129°W lies in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and west of Chile. It is not far from the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which runs roughly along 55-65°S at this longitude, so it is somewhat north of the ridge crest. The East Pacific Rise at 129°W would be near 55°S or so.

The seafloor here is moderately young crust, probably 20-30 million years old, and about 2,000 km from the ridge. Depths in this region are typically 3,500-4,000 m. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crest sits around 2,300-2,700 m, and the surrounding seafloor deepens to about 4,000 m on the flanks. At 49°S, 129°W the depth is likely near 3,900 m, so I estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

7253.text:
Latitude 9.0, longitude -73.0 falls in northern Colombia, near the Cesar department. It is close to the Magdalena River region, or the Cesar valley to the east of it. The Magdalena at about -74.2 is farther west. The Serranía de Perijá lies around -73.0 to -72.8 at 9°N. The Cesar River valley sits near -73.5, so -73.0 is just east of the valley, probably in the foothills.

Near Becerril, at 9.7N, -73.3, the land is in the valley. At 9.0N, -73.0 is close to Chimichagua/Curumaní, around -73.5 to -73.2. The Cesar valley floor there is about 50 m, and the ciénaga of Zapatosa is at about 30 m. At -73.0 the terrain is probably the western foothills of Perijá, perhaps 100-200 m. Along 9.0N the Cesar valley is around -73.6 to -73.5 at Chimichagua. At -73.0 and 9.0N there is probably hilly terrain near Pailitas/Pelaya (-73.6), and to the east near La Gloria and Ocaña (-73.35, 8.25). The area is likely foothills at around 700 m, but I'll estimate 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

7556.text:
Latitude 7.0°N, longitude 173.0°E lies in the Marshall Islands region. Ailinglaplap is at about 7.3°N, 168.7°E, Jaluit at 5.9°N, 169.6°E, Namorik at 5.6°N, 168.1°E, Kili at 5.6°N, 169.1°E, Mili at 6.1°N, 171.9°E, Knox (Mili Atoll) at about 5.9°N, 171.9°E, Arno at 7.1°N, 171.6°E, Majuro at 7.1°N, 171.2°E, and Aur at 8.3°N, 171.1°E. Nothing is charted near 173.0°E, so this point is open ocean east of Arno.

The seafloor there is Pacific abyssal plain near the Ratak chain, probably 4,500–5,500 m deep. East of the Marshalls, around 173°E, depths are about 5,000 m. I'll estimate -5,100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

12623.text:
The point (-51, -133) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand and west of Chile. It is on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern side, where the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs near 55-65°S at that longitude. At 51°S, 133°W, the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or lower ridge flank, with a depth of about 4000 m.

The East Pacific Rise at around 133°W reaches roughly 56°S. Its axis near 51°S is farther west, around 115°W. So this point is on the flank of the ridge, perhaps 2 km of seafloor spreading away. The depth is roughly 3800 m, and I will go with about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2933.text:
The point 57.0°N, 73.0°W is in northern Quebec (Nunavik), in the Ungava peninsula region south of Ungava Bay and east of Hudson Bay. It lies near the Rivière aux Feuilles and Rivière George area. The Labrador Trough is further east. The area has many lakes, such as Lac Minto at 57.3°N, 74.5°W, which is close but not at this point.

At this longitude, the terrain is about 400 m above sea level. The Canadian Shield plateau there is about 300-500 m. I'll assume it's land, with an elevation of around 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

7407.text:
The point at 7°N, 125°W is in the open eastern Pacific, north of the equator and west of Central America. It lies in the abyssal plain, north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone and southwest of the Clarion-Clipperton zone, so the surface type is Water.

Seafloor depth in this area is typically about 4,000 to 4,500 m, based on seafloor age. The crust here is roughly 30 to 40 million years old, which gives about 4,300 to 4,400 m. I'm estimating from the regional pattern rather than a specific bathymetry reading, so I'll use -4,350 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4350}

753.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -113.0°W lies in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Borden Island is around 78.5°N, 111°W, and Meighen Island around 80°N, 99°W. Ellef Ringnes Island spans roughly 78°N to 79°N, and Axel Heiberg is at about 80°N, 90°W. At 81°N, 113°W the position is in the Arctic Ocean north of Prince Patrick and Borden Islands, so it is likely in the Arctic Ocean near the continental shelf edge.

Land at that latitude is limited: Brock Island is about 77.9°N and Mackenzie King Island about 77.5°N, both too far south. Lougheed Island is at about 77.5°N, 105°W. So this is open ocean, probably the shelf slope north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. The Arctic shelf edge near 80°N, 115°W is at roughly 300 to 500 m depth. At 81°N the seabed is probably in the 500–1500 m range, near the slope or in the Canada Basin margin. I'll estimate about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

568.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 123°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of Canada's Arctic Archipelago. Ellef Ringnes Island is at about 78.5°N and Axel Heiberg reaches about 82°N, so 83°N is beyond the land, near the northern edge of the Canadian Arctic shelf and the Arctic Ocean basin. Ellesmere's northern coast is at about 83°N but between 60°W and 90°W, so it is not nearby.

At 83°N, 123°W the seabed is probably in the Canada Basin margin. The continental slope north of the Sverdrup Islands lies at around 80-81°N, and the Canada Basin abyssal plain is about 3800 m deep. Farther north toward the Alpha Ridge, which lies around 85°N and 120°W, depths are about 1500-2500 m. At 83°N, 123°W the seabed is likely near the Alpha Ridge flank or the basin, at roughly 3000 m depth. In IBCAO, the Canada Basin is deep around 80-82°N at 130°W, near 3800 m. Near 83°N the Alpha Ridge begins to rise, so I estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

2440.text:
Latitude 63.0°N, longitude 21.0°E lies in the Gulf of Bothnia, specifically the Kvarken / Bothnian Sea area. Vaasa is at about 63.1°N, 21.6°E, so the point sits west of Vaasa, in the Kvarken archipelago region or just southwest of it.

The Kvarken archipelago is shallow. At 21.0°E, the point is about 30 km west of Vaasa's coast, in the open water of the Quark's southern part. Depths there are around 20-40 m, and the Bothnian Sea to the south is deeper. The Replot islands extend to about 21.2°E. At 63.0°N, 21.0°E the water is probably 20-30 m deep, so I'll estimate about -25 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-25}

10420.text:
Latitude -25, longitude 141 is in southwestern Queensland, Australia, in the Channel Country near the Diamantina/Georgina river area and the Simpson Desert's northeastern edge. It is south of Boulia and north of Birdsville, near the Queensland-Northern Territory-South Australia border region.

Terrain in this area is low, roughly 100-150 m. Birdsville sits at about 45 m at longitude 139.35. At 141E, -25, the point is east of Birdsville, in the Mulligan/Georgina area near Bedourie (-24.35, 139.47, elevation about 90 m). Further east at 141E the land rises to around 150 m, close to the Diamantina Lakes area (-23.8, 141.0, about 150 m). The point at -25 is likely sandy plain at about 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

15643.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 147 is in Antarctica. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about -85 at longitudes near 180, but at 147E the area is the Transantarctic Mountains region, near the Shackleton, Beardmore, and Byrd glacier area. The Byrd Glacier sits at about 160E and the Nimrod Glacier at about 166E. At 147E, -83 would be well inland on the East Antarctic plateau, since the Transantarctic Mountains front is at about 160-170E at that latitude. So this is likely grounded ice on the plateau, west of the mountains, with a surface elevation around 2500-3000 m.

Longitude 147E at -83 lies in the region south of Wilkes Land and Oates Land, inland of the Mertz area. Ice surface elevation there is around 2800 m, so I'll estimate 2700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2700}

2030.text:
Latitude 67.0 N, longitude -79.0 W is in northern Canada, on Baffin Island's southwest side. Baffin Island's western part near Foxe Basin lies around 68-70N, -75 to -80W. At 67N, -79W, the area is close to the Foxe Basin coast, south of the Melville Peninsula. Foxe Basin's southern coast sits around 66N at -79W (Southampton Island is to the south at 64N, -84W). The Baffin Island coast at Koukdjuak is around 66N, -73W.

Further west, Igloolik is at 69.4N, -81.8W. The Foxe Basin is shallow, around 50-100 m deep. At 67N, -79W, I think this is the Foxe Basin water, west of Baffin Island (Baffin's west coast at that latitude is near -74 to -75W), so the point is most likely in Foxe Basin. The Gulf of Boothia is further west at -87W and the Melville Peninsula is at about 68N, -84W. Foxe Basin depth there is likely about 50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-60}

12235.text:
Latitude -45.0, longitude 171.0 is off the east coast of New Zealand's South Island. Oamaru sits at about -45.1, 170.97, so this point is right at the coast near Oamaru, just north of the town. Oamaru's harbour is at roughly 170.98 E. The coastline near -45.0 lies at about 171.0 to 171.05 E (Cape Wanbrow is at -45.11, 170.98). Around Waitaki River mouth (-44.93, 171.15), the coast at -45.0 is near 171.1 E. At -45.0, 171.0 is therefore inland by about 8 km.

Inland terrain there is rolling farmland near Weston/Enfield, with an elevation of roughly 100 m. Weston is at about -45.07, 170.98 with an elevation of about 50 m. At -45.0, 171.0 the area is near Awamoko or Totara, around 100 m, so I'll estimate 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

13684.text:
Latitude -63, longitude -171 is in the Southern Ocean, southeast of New Zealand and north of the Ross Sea. It lies near the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region, though the ridge is farther east, around -65 lat and -175 lon at its nearest. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge crosses roughly 62°S, 170°W, so the point may sit close to the ridge crest.

Around 60°S the ridge is at roughly 2500-3000 m depth. Near 63°S, 171°W the area is probably ridge flank or the Udintsev Fracture Zone region, with depths around 3000-3500 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

2834.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 89.0°E is in Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia, in the West Siberian Plain / Yenisei region. It lies east of the Ket River basin and west of the Yenisei, which runs near 87°E at this latitude. At 89°E, this is the Central Siberian Plateau's western edge, near the Angara/Yenisei area. Elevation there is probably around 150 m.

The Yenisei at 59°N is near 90.5°E (around Strelka and Yartsevo). So 89°E is west of the river, in lowland terrain of roughly 100–150 m, possibly with swamp. I'll estimate 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

11977.text:
Latitude -43, longitude 15 E lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of South Africa and southeast of the Walvis Ridge region. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is near 0-10°E at this latitude, so 15°E is east of the ridge in the Cape Basin or Agulhas Basin area. Depths there are about 4500-5000 m.

At 43°S the Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis is roughly at 15°E? The ridge near 40°S is around 12°W, and near 45°S around 8°E. The Bouvet triple junction is at 54°S, 3°E. The ridge axis at 43°S is therefore near 0-5°E. At 15°E we are on the eastern flank, with depths of about 4000-4800 m. I'll choose -4600.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

4583.text:
The point 39.0°N, 13.0°W lies in the North Atlantic Ocean, west of Portugal. Portugal's coast is at about 9°W, so this is roughly 350 km offshore. The location is near the Iberian Abyssal Plain's western edge, close to the Madeira-Tore Rise region, which sits further south at around 37–38°N and 11–12°W. At 39°N and 13°W the seabed is probably the abyssal plain or the lower continental rise, at about 5000 m depth. The Iberian Abyssal Plain is about 5300 m deep near 15°W. The Estremadura Spur and the Tore seamount area lie near 39°N, 12–13°W, and the Tore seamount is around 38.8°N, 12.9°W. It rises from about 5000 m to a summit around 2000 m or shallower.

The Tore seamount is at roughly 39.5°N, 12.5°W, so this point could be on its flank. I'll estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

11963.text:
The point is at 43°S, 13°W in the South Atlantic. That puts it south of Tristan da Cunha (37°S, 12.5°W) and north of Gough Island (40°S, 10°W). It is west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which lies near 13-15°W at this latitude. It is probably near the ridge axis or its western flank, where the ridge crest sits at about 2000-3000 m depth. Around 43°S the ridge is at roughly 15°W, with the Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone further south near 49°S. Because the point is about 2° east of the axis, the depth is probably around 3000-3500 m.

The point is Water, and I estimate the seabed at about -3400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

15850.text:
This location is at 87°S, 159°W, in Antarctica on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet side of the Transantarctic Mountains, near the Ross Ice Shelf's southern end. The South Pole is at 90°S with an elevation of about 2835 m. Longitude 159°W at 87°S lies in the direction of the Ross Sea sector. In that sector the Transantarctic Mountains and the Reedy and Scott glaciers are around 85–86°S, and the polar plateau is at about 2,500–3,000 m. At 87°S and 159°W, the position is on the polar plateau, likely grounded ice, with an elevation of about 2,700 m.

Longitude 180° runs toward the Ross Ice Shelf. Longitude 159°W is toward the Ross Sea side, but a bit toward Marie Byrd Land. Going from the pole along 159°W, the Beardmore and Shackleton glaciers are around 170°E to 180°, and the Scott Glacier is near 153°W. The Scott Glacier at 86°S, 153°W starts from the plateau at about 2,500 m. So 87°S, 159°W is plateau ice at around 2,800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

1964.text:
Latitude 69.0, Longitude 149.0 — ambiguous about east or west. Positive longitude means 149°E, which is in northeastern Siberia (Sakha Republic), near the Yana-Indigirka lowlands. At 69°N, 149°E, this is north of the Verkhoyansk range, in the Yana-Indigirka lowland, near the Kolyma/Indigirka area. The elevation there is probably around 100-200 m.

The Chersky range lies further south, around 143°E and 66°N. At 69N, 149E, we are between the Indigirka (about 148E at 70N) and the Alazeya river (about 152E). The terrain there is lowland, the Yana-Indigirka lowland, with elevations around 50 m. I'll guess 60.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

10423.text:
Latitude -25.0, longitude 147.0 is in central Queensland, Australia, near the Carnarvon Range and Springsure area. It lies south of Springsure and north of Roma, around the Arcadia and Carnarvon region, on the Great Dividing Range. That terrain is highland sandstone, so the elevation is probably around 500 to 600 m.

A location near 25°S, 147°E is roughly north of Mitchell and west of Injune. Injune sits at about 25.85°S, 148.5°E, which puts this point further north-west. The Nogoa and Warrego headwaters are around Springsure (24.1°S, 148.1°E), and the Carnarvon Range at about 25°S, 148°E. At 147°E the point is west of the range, in the Warrego/Nogoa upland near Tambo (24.9°S, 146.25°E, elevation about 395 m). Moving east from Tambo toward 147°E, the ground rises, so about 450 m is a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

8473.text:
The point (-5.0, -153.0) lies in the central Pacific, south of the equator and west of the Line Islands, roughly 1,000 km southwest of Kiritimati. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there is abyssal plain at about 5,000 m depth. The Line Islands ridge is to the east, and this area sits in the Central Pacific Basin. I estimate the depth at around 5,100 m, which is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

14587.text:
The point is at 73°S, 165°W, which falls in the Ross Sea, in the eastern part of the Ross Ice Shelf. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 78°S at this longitude, and its edge near 77.5°S at 165°W. At 73°S the Ross Sea is open water (the continental shelf). The seabed there is the Ross Sea continental shelf, with depths around 500 m.

At 165°W and 73°S, the Ross Sea is close to the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the continental slope; the shelf edge is near 72–73°S around 170°W. Farther east, at 165°W, the shelf edge is around 73-74°S, so the depth could be around 500–1000 m. The Iselin Bank lies near 176°W. At 165°W the shelf edge is closer to 76°S?, since the Ross Sea at its eastern side along 160°W reaches Marie Byrd Land coast near 77°S. The Ross Sea therefore extends to open water north of 75°S, so 73°S is probably continental slope. I'll estimate -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

741.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -137.0° lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, west of Ellef Ringnes and Meighen Island. Meighen Island is near 80.0°N, -99°, and Prince Patrick Island is near 77°N, -119°, so this point is well to the west of both. It sits in the Canada Basin, off the Beaufort Sea margin and north of the Arctic Archipelago slope.

Mapping the nearby bathymetry: the continental shelf edge off Banks Island and Prince Patrick lies near 75-76°N. At 81°N, -137°, the point is in the deep Canada Basin, near the Alpha Ridge and Canada Abyssal Plain boundary. The abyssal plain is around 3800 m deep, and the Alpha Ridge to the north is about 1500-2500 m. At 81°N, -137° I believe the seafloor is about 3600 m deep, so I estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

804.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 11.0°W lies off northeast Greenland, in the Greenland Sea/Fram Strait region. The northeast Greenland coast at this latitude is around 18–20°W (Nordostrundingen is at about 11.5°W, 81.4°N). So 81.0°N, 11°W is just east of Nordostrundingen, on the continental shelf or slope. Nordostrundingen is at roughly 81.38°N, 11.8°W. At 81°N the coast sits near 13–14°W (Cape Bernstorff area), so 11°W is offshore.

The Northeast Greenland shelf here includes Belgica Bank to the west and the Norske Øer. At 81°N, 11°W, the shelf is probably about 200–400 m deep, and the Fram Strait slope lies further east. I estimate a depth of about -300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-300}

823.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 27.0°E is in the Svalbard archipelago, in the northern part of Nordaustlandet. Nordaustlandet spans roughly 79.5–80.8°N, and its northern coast is near 80.0–80.8°N, so 81.0°N falls just north of the island, in the Arctic Ocean. The nearby islands are Kvitøya at about 80.1°N, 32.5°E and Karl XII-øya at about 80.7°N, 25.5°E. Karl XII-øya is small and sits at 80.7°N, which is still south of 81.0.

The point is therefore north of Nordaustlandet, on the continental shelf edge or slope. The shelf north of Nordaustlandet is roughly 200–400 m deep, and the slope drops off toward the Nansen Basin near 81.5°N. At 81.0°N, 27°E the seabed is probably around 300 m deep, though it could be deeper. I'll estimate -400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

11567.text:
Latitude -39, longitude -85 is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of Chile's coast (the Chile Trench lies near -74 longitude). The Nazca plate seafloor here is about 3,500-4,000 m deep. The East Pacific Rise lies farther west, around -110 longitude at this latitude. The Chile Rise is farther south, near -46 latitude, and the Juan Fernández area is north of this point.

Seafloor age here is about 30-40 Myr, which gives a depth of about 4,000 m. The Chile Trench is about 5,000 m deep, but this point is well west of the trench and its outer rise. I'll estimate about -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14408.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -163 falls in the Ross Sea, in the eastern part near the Ross Ice Shelf edge. The Ross Ice Shelf front sits at about -78 latitude near 180 longitude, and at -163 longitude the shelf front is around -77.8. So -71, -163 lies in the open Ross Sea, north of the Ross Ice Shelf and off the Northern Victoria Land / Marie Byrd Land coast. Land at that longitude begins around -77 (Marie Byrd Land coast near Sulzberger Bay at -135), so this point is open ocean.

The depth there is on the outer continental shelf or slope. The Ross Sea shelf break lies near -73 to -74 latitude at the Pacific-Antarctic side, and -71 is beyond it. Around -71, -163 the seafloor is probably continental slope or rise, at about 3000 to 3500 m depth. The Iselin Bank, at about -71 to -75 and 175 to -175, is farther west. At -163 and -71 the depth is probably about 3300 m, which I'll use.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

12700.text:
Latitude -51, longitude 21 lies in the South Atlantic/Southern Ocean, south of South Africa. It is southwest of the Prince Edward Islands, which sit near 37°E, and is in the Atlantic-Indian basin. The Southwest Indian Ridge lies to the east, and the Agulhas Plateau is near 26°E, 40°S.

At 51°S, 21°E, the Atlantic-Indian Basin is about 4,000 m deep. The Islas Orcadas Rise is near 52°S, 5°E, and the Meteor Rise is near 52°S, 2°E. The Southwest Indian Ridge crosses about 54°S, 10°E and then trends northeast toward 50°S, 30°E. At 21°E the ridge is probably near 52-53°S, so 51°S would be on its northern flank, where depths are around 3,500-4,000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

5832.text:
Latitude 25°N, longitude 35°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at 25°N lies near 45°W, so this point sits on the western flank. The surface type is Water.

Seafloor depth at this longitude on the western flank is probably around 4,500 to 5,500 m. At 25°N the ridge crest is at about 45°W, and 35°W would be east of the ridge, not west. The ridge axis near 26°N is at roughly 44–45°W, and the African side extends east of it. So 35°W is on the eastern flank, about 10° of longitude from the axis, roughly 1,000 km. Crust there is about 25–30 Myr old, which gives a depth of about 4,800 m at that age. Seafloor topography in this area is also affected by the Canary Basin and the Madeira–Cape Verde region. I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1643.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -133.0 is in the Beaufort Sea region near Amundsen Gulf's western entrance. Banks Island's west coast lies around -125 to -125.5 longitude at 71°N, and the Tuktoyaktuk Peninsula/mainland is near 69.5°N. At -133 and 71°N, that is open Beaufort Sea on the continental shelf, northwest of the Mackenzie Delta and north of the mainland coast (69.5°N).

The shelf edge here is roughly at 71°N, 133°W; the Mackenzie shelf is about 60-100 m deep out to around 71°N. Canyon areas also exist (Mackenzie Trough, near 138°W). At -133 the shelf break is around 70.7°N, so 71°N is probably just past the break, with a depth of maybe 200-500 m. I'll estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

1526.text:
Latitude 73.0, Longitude -7.0 is in the Greenland Sea, east of Greenland. Jan Mayen is at about 71°N, 8°W, so this point is well north of it. The East Greenland coast near Scoresby Sund lies at about 70°N, 22°W, and the coast at 73°N is around 20-22°W, so -7° is offshore.

The point is in the Greenland Sea basin near the Greenland Fracture Zone / Boreas Basin. The Boreas Basin is about 3000 m deep, and the Greenland Basin is about 3500 m deep. At 73°N, 7°W the Mohns Ridge is further northeast, around 73°N, 8°E, and the Greenland continental shelf edge is about 15°W. This point is therefore in the deep basin, at roughly -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

13611.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 43 is in the Southern Ocean, south of the Africa–Antarctic region. It lies southeast of the Weddell Sea area, near the Enderby Basin and the Southwest Indian Ridge. The Southwest Indian Ridge sits around 50-55°S near 40°E, and the Antarctic continental margin is further south. At 61°S the seafloor is probably the abyssal Enderby Basin, or the northern flank of the Astrid Ridge or Conrad Rise. The Conrad Rise is near 53°S, 40-50°E. At 61°S, 43°E the depth is probably about 4,500-5,000 m.

I could not pin it down further, so I'll estimate about -4,800 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4800}

4350.text:
The coordinates 41.0°N, 119.0°W fall in northwestern Nevada, in the Black Rock Desert region. The Black Rock Desert playa is at about 40.9°N, 119.0°W with an elevation of about 1190 m. The playa center is near 40.95°N, 119.05°W, and the Black Rock playa floor sits at about 1190 m.

At 41.0°N, 119.0°W the point is probably still on the playa or at its northern edge. The playa extends north to about 41.1°N near Summit Lake, and its elevation is about 1200 m. I'll use 1195 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1195}

2684.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 149.0 — the sign is ambiguous. If it is 149.0 E, the point is in Russia's Magadan/Yakutia region, roughly near the Okhotsk coast at 61 N, 149 E, which is inland in the Kolyma/Verkhoyansk mountains. If it is 149 W, it is in Alaska, near the Chugach/Matanuska area.

Taking the stated value literally as 149.0 E, 61 N lies north of the Okhotsk coast (about 59.5 N). The location is in the Suntar-Khayata/Ulakhan-Chistay area, or the Kolyma upper basin, which I'd estimate at about 900 m elevation. It is probably mountainous terrain, so I'll say 1000 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

15781.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 63 lies in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. It is roughly 500 km from the South Pole toward the Princess Elizabeth Land / Dome Argus side. Dome Argus is at about -80.4, 77.4 with an elevation of about 4093 m. At -85, 63 the surface should be around 3000-3500 m. Ice-surface elevation near the Pole is 2835 m. Toward Dome A at longitude 63, 85°S sits in the Recovery Glacier and Support Force Glacier region, where the plateau is around 3300 m. Longitude 63°E is near the Shackleton Range/Pensacola Mountains side. The Pensacola Mountains sit at about 50-60°W, so that is not relevant here.

At 85°S, 63°E the elevation is probably about 3200 m, which is my estimate.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3250}

4627.text:
Latitude 39.0, longitude 75.0 is in western China, in the Xinjiang region near Kashgar. Kashgar sits at about 39.47N, 75.99E. The point 39N, 75E lies southwest of Kashgar, around Yengisar or Shache (Yarkant), at roughly 38.4N, 77.2E. West of those towns the terrain rises into the Pamir and Kunlun foothills, near the Kyrgyz and Tajik border.

The Kashgar plain is around 1300 m. Near 75E, 39N the terrain is mountainous, in the Tianshan and Pamir margin south of Ulugqat (Wuqia, 39.7N, 75.2E, about 1900 m). The area at 39.0N is south of Wuqia, in the Kashgar Kizilsu border mountains. Pamir ridges there reach about 3000 to 4000 m. The nearby Gez valley and Karakul are further south. I estimate about 2900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

1280.text:
Location: 75°N, 139°W is in the Beaufort Sea, northwest of Banks Island (which has its western coast near 125°W). At 139°W and 75°N, this is the Beaufort Sea, beyond the continental shelf of Banks Island. The shelf break is around 73-74°N here. At 75°N, 139°W the depth is probably about 2000-3000 m, on the Canada Basin slope.

Banks Island's northwest tip (Cape Prince Alfred) is at about 74.3°N, 124.5°W. So 139°W is about 14° of longitude west, roughly 200 km west. The shelf there is narrow, and the slope drops off. At 75°N, 139°W the depth is probably around 3500 m, though the Canada Basin abyssal plain is around 3800 m. Choosing about 3000 m is a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

12041.text:
Latitude -43.0, longitude 143.0 lies in the Southern Ocean southwest of Tasmania. Tasmania's west coast is at about 145.3°E, so this point is roughly 220 km offshore. The continental shelf off western Tasmania extends out to about 144.5°E at this latitude, and the shelf break is around 200 m depth. At 143°E the point is on the continental slope, where depths run from about 2000 to 3000 m. I estimate about 3000 m deep.

The West Tasmanian margin has a slope down to the abyssal plain at roughly 4000 m. The South Tasman Rise lies south of 44°S. At 43°S, 143°E the seabed is probably around 3000 m deep, so I'll go with -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

8722.text:
The point (-7.0, -15.0) is in the South Atlantic, about 7°S and 15°W. Ascension Island lies at roughly 7.95°S, 14.4°W, so this point is about 1° north and slightly west of it, in open ocean.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge runs near 14°W at this latitude. Ascension sits on the ridge axis, and the ridge crest is typically 2,000–3,000 m deep here. At 15°W, a little west of the axis, the seafloor is probably around 3,000–3,500 m deep. Just north of Ascension there are fracture zones, such as the Ascension Fracture Zone near 7–8°S, which could create deeper troughs. I could not pin down the local bathymetry more precisely, so I'm estimating about -3,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

4306.text:
The point 43.0°N, 153.0°E lies in the northwest Pacific, southeast of Hokkaido and east of the Kuril Islands. The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs northeast to southwest through this region, roughly 150-200 km off the Kuril chain.

The trench axis near 43°N is around 146-147°E, which would be about 500 km west of this point, so 153°E is well east of it. At 43°N, 153°E the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain of the Pacific plate, at roughly 5500-5600 m depth. The trench is deeper than 8000 m at about 44°N, 150°E, and 153°E is east of the trench's outer rise. I'll estimate the depth at about 5400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

10251.text:
The point -23, 163 lies in the Coral Sea/Tasman area east of New Caledonia. New Caledonia's main island spans roughly 164–167°E, so 163°E is west of it. Just west of the island is the New Caledonia Trough and Lord Howe Rise to the southwest. The Chesterfield Islands are at about 19–20°S and 158°E, and the Bellona Plateau is around 21°S and 159°E.

At 23°S and 163°E, the point is on the western side of the Norfolk Ridge, probably in the New Caledonia Basin or the western slope of the Norfolk Ridge. The Grand Passage and the Entrecasteaux area lie to the north. The New Caledonia Trough lies at about 163–165°E at these latitudes with depths of about 2500–3000 m. The Lord Howe Rise is at about 162°E and 23°S. The Fairway Basin lies at about 162°E and 22°S, at roughly 2500 m depth. I estimate about -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

9165.text:
Latitude -11.0, longitude 151.0 is in the Coral Sea, southeast of Papua New Guinea's tip. It lies east of the Louisiade Archipelago region, which sits around 152-154E and 11S. The point is near the Pocklington Rise or Pocklington Trough area.

The Pocklington Trough lies around 11S, 153E to 155E, with depths of about 3000-4000 m. At 151E, 11S, the area is likely the Papuan Plateau, which has depths of roughly 1000-2000 m. The Papuan Plateau runs from about 10S to 12S and 149E to 152E, with Eastern Plateau depths of around 1500-2500 m. I'll estimate -2000 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -2000}

6465.text:
Latitude 19.0°N, longitude 151.0°E lies in the western Pacific, east of the Mariana Islands, which are near 145°E. This area is in the Pacific Plate, near the Mariana Trench's eastern side and the Magellan Seamounts region. The Pigafetta Basin and the East Mariana Basin lie to the south, around 12–15°N, 153–160°E.

At 19°N, 151°E the seafloor is probably abyssal plain, with a depth of about 5,500 to 6,000 m. Seamounts in this area, such as those of the Magellan chain, could make it shallower, but I have no specific evidence of one at this point. The Pigafetta Basin floor is typically about 5,800 m deep, so I estimate about -5,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

2512.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude 165.0 E is in northeastern Russia, in the Kolyma/Magadan region near the Chersky Range. The point lies roughly between the Kolyma upper reaches and the Anadyr highlands, in mountainous terrain, probably the Korkodon/Omolon region.

The Omolon river basin is around 65N, 160E. At 63N, 165E, the area is near the Korkodon River, which is upland with elevations of about 500-800 m. I'll estimate 700 m.

{"type": "Solid", "elevation_m": 700}

6518.text:
Latitude 17.0, longitude -103.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, off the coast of Guerrero/Michoacán, Mexico. The coast near Zihuatanejo sits at about 17.6N, -101.5W, and near -103 the coast is around 18.0N (Michoacán coast, Lázaro Cárdenas at -102.2, 17.9N). So the point lies about 100 km offshore to the southwest.

The Middle America Trench runs roughly 60-100 km off this coast. At -103 and 17N the trench axis is around 16.2N, -102 to -103, with depths of about 5000 m. The point is probably on the continental slope or the trench's inner wall, so the depth may be around 3500-4500 m. The trench off Michoacán lies about 90 km from the coast, and the coast at -103 is at about 18.0N. The trench there is near 16.5N at -103, so 17.0N would be about 50 km north of the trench axis, on the lower slope. A depth of about 3800 m seems reasonable.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

2216.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -67.0 is in the Southern Hemisphere? No, +65 is north. At 65°N, 67°W, this is Baffin Island, Cumberland Peninsula area. Cumberland Sound sits at about 65°N, 66°W, and Pangnirtung is at 66.1°N, 65.7°W. At 65°N, 67°W, the point is southwest of Cumberland Sound's head, so probably the interior of the Cumberland Peninsula or the Hall Peninsula area. The peninsula's west side includes the Penny Ice Cap at about 67°N, so 65°N is farther south. Cumberland Sound runs from about 65.0°N to 66.5°N, with its west shore around 67°W near 65.5°N. At 65°N, the west coast of the sound is about 66.5°W, and the land west of there is the Hall/Meta Incognita side, so this is probably land.

I'd guess the point is inland, at moderate elevation, roughly 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

11090.text:
Location: -33, 41E is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of South Africa (Durban is at about 31S, 31E). At 33S, 41E the point is east of the Natal Valley and south of the Mozambique Basin. The area is likely the Mozambique Basin or Mozambique Ridge region. The Mozambique Ridge lies around 33-35E, so 41E is east of it, in the Mozambique Basin, where depths are about 5000 m. The Natal Valley is at roughly 31-35E. The Mozambique Basin extends to about 40E, with depths around 5000 m. Farther east, near 41E and 33S, it may be the abyssal plain at about 5000 m, though the southern basin sits near 5200 m.

I estimate about -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

1794.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 169.0°E (positive, so east) lies in the East Siberian/Chukchi Sea region. Wrangel Island sits at about 71.2°N, 179.4°W, and the Siberian coast near Cape Shelagsky is at about 70°N, 170°E. So 71°N, 169°E is north of the coast, in the Chukchi Sea west of Wrangel Island. Water depth there is about 40 m. The shelf in this area is shallow, around 30-50 m.

If the longitude were instead 169°W, the point would be in the Chukchi Sea at about 71°N, 169°W, with a depth of about 40-50 m. Either way it is water at roughly 45 m depth, so I'll use -45.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

7234.text:
The point (9°N, 111°W) is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, roughly southwest of Mexico and west of Clipperton Island (which lies near 10.3°N, 109.2°W). The area is part of the East Pacific Rise flank region. The East Pacific Rise crest is near 108-110°W at 9°N, so 111°W is on the western flank, where depths are around 3000-3200 m.

At 9°N the rise axis is about 104.3°W, so the rise is further east than I first assumed. The seafloor at 111°W is about 6.5° west of the axis. That is roughly 700 km, and at a half-spreading rate of about 70 mm/yr it corresponds to about 10 Myr of age. Depth is about 2600 + 350·√10, which is about 3700 m. Nearby abyssal hills and seamounts could change this, and the Clipperton fracture zone lies to the north at about 10°N. I estimate a depth of about 3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

669.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude 79.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Kara Sea and Severnaya Zemlya (which sits near 79°N, 100°E). Franz Josef Land is at about 80-81°N, 50-65°E, so it is well to the west and south of this point. At 83°N, 79°E the point is north of the Kara Sea shelf edge, at the continental slope or in the Nansen Basin.

The shelf break north of the Kara Sea is near 81-82°N, and the slope descends toward the Nansen Basin, whose abyssal plain is around 3,900 m deep. At 83°N, 79°E the point is likely on the lower slope or the edge of the abyssal plain. In IBCAO, the St. Anna Trough is near 80°E at about 80-82°N, and the 3,000 m depth lies around 83°N there. I estimate a depth of about -3,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

10480.text:
The point is at -27, -99 in the southeastern Pacific, west of Chile and east of Easter Island (about -27.1, -109.3). The Easter Island Salas y Gómez ridge runs east from Easter Island toward Chile, but at -99 the point is probably on the flank of the East Pacific Rise or in the basin to its east.

The East Pacific Rise crest sits near -113 at this latitude. Further east, at -99, the seafloor is about 20-30 Myr old. The depth there is probably about 3,600-3,900 m, since the Nazca plate seafloor near -27, -99 is mostly 3,500-4,000 m deep. The Salas y Gómez ridge reaches roughly -105 to -85, so a seamount could be nearby, but I can't confirm one at this point. I'll estimate a depth of about 3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

3904.text:
Latitude 47.0, longitude 69.0 is in central Kazakhstan, in the Karaganda/Ulytau region and west of Lake Balkhash. It lies in the steppe near the Turgay area, southwest of Zhezkazgan.

Zhezkazgan is at about 47.8N, 67.7E, and the Kengir reservoir is nearby. The point 47.0N, 69.0E is roughly 100 km southeast of it, and the terrain there is around 300-400 m. The Ulytau region sits at about 300-500 m, so I estimate 350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":350}

1605.text:
Latitude 73.0°N, longitude 151.0°E is in the Laptev Sea, north of the Siberian coast. The New Siberian Islands lie further east, at about 140–150°E and 73–76°N. Kotelny Island is around 137–146°E at 75°N, and the Lyakhovsky Islands are near 73.3°N, 141°E. Stolbovoy Island is near 74.1°N, 136°E. The Yana River delta and Buor-Khaya Bay lie near 71°N, 132°E.

At 151°E, 73°N, the nearest land is Bolshoy Lyakhovsky at about 73.3°N, 142.5°E, roughly 280 km west. The Anzhu Islands are at 75°N. The mainland coast near the Indigirka mouth is at about 71.5°N, 150°E, so the point is about 150 km north of the coast. That places it in the East Siberian Sea or Laptev Sea shelf, which is shallow, around 20–40 m deep. The shelf at 73°N, 151°E is probably around 30 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-30}

3279.text:
The coordinates 53.0°N, 101.0°W fall in Manitoba, near the Saskatchewan border. This is near The Pas and Cedar Lake. The Pas is at about 53.8°N, 101.25°W, and Cedar Lake spans roughly 53.0–53.5°N, 100.5–100.0°W. Moose Lake is at about 53.7°N, 100.3°W. At 53.0°N, 101.0°W, the area is probably south of the Saskatchewan River delta, near Summerberry Marsh or Saskeram Lake, and likely land. The Carrot River lies to the southwest, around 53.3°N, 101.3°W. Terrain there is flat at roughly 260 m.

Cedar Lake's western end is around 100.9°W at 53.2°N, so 53.0 is probably just south of it. Land elevation near the Pas is about 270 m, and the area south of it is about 260 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":262}

5051.text:
The point 33°N, 157°W lies in the North Pacific, northeast of Hawaii and roughly 1,000 km north of the island of Oahu. This is open ocean over the abyssal plain, where depths are typically around 5,000 to 5,500 m.

The seafloor age there is Cretaceous to Jurassic. Depth in this region is probably about 5,400 m. The Hawaiian arch and moat sit farther south, and 33°N is beyond the swell. The Murray Fracture Zone lies near 32-35°N, but that does not change the estimate much. I'll answer -5450 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

12129.text:
Latitude -45, longitude -41 lies in the South Atlantic, well off the coast of South America. It is east of Argentina and southwest of the mid-Atlantic area. The location is on the Argentine Basin's eastern side, near the Zapiola Rise or the western flank of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.

The Argentine Basin is about 5000-6000 m deep. At 45°S, 41°W, the depth is likely around 5000 m. The Zapiola Rise sits near 45°S, 45°W with a depth of about 4000 m, so at 41°W the seabed is probably around 5000 m deep. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far to the east, near 15°W, so it does not affect this point. I estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

9883.text:
Latitude -19.0, longitude 147.0 is off the Queensland coast. Townsville sits at about -19.26, 146.8, and Magnetic Island is near -19.15, 146.85. At -19.0, 147.0 the point lies northeast of Townsville, around Cleveland Bay's eastern side. Cape Cleveland is at about -19.18, 147.02, so the point is north of it, in the sea. Palm Island is at about -18.75, 146.58. The Great Barrier Reef lagoon is shallow here, roughly 20 m deep, so the seabed is about -25 m.

I estimate the point is Water, with a seabed elevation of about -25 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-25}

9653.text:
Latitude -17.0, longitude 47.0 is in northwestern Madagascar. The Mahajanga region lies near 15.7°S, 46.3°E, and Marovoay is near 16.1°S, 46.6°E. At 17°S, 47°E the point is inland, southeast of Mahajanga and south of Marovoay, in the Betsiboka area, probably on the lowland plains or low hills. Ambato-Boeny is at about 16.5°S, 46.4°E. At 17°S, 47°E the location is likely in the Mahavavy or Kamoro area, with elevation of maybe 100 m.

The coast is at about 46.0°E at that latitude, which puts the point roughly 100 km inland. Terrain there is rolling hills at about 100–200 m, so I estimate 130 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":130}

7984.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude -51.0°W is near the mouth of the Amazon, off the coast of Amapá, Brazil. Marajó Island lies around 0°N to -1°S, -50 to -50.5°W. The Amapá coast at 1°N is around -50.0 to -51.0°W. The Amazon's north channel runs near 1°N, -50°W, and the coast near -51.0 at 1°N is the Rio Amazonas estuary's northwestern edge, including Ilha Gurupá (-51.5, -1.0).

At 1°N, -51°W the area is inland Amapá, around Tartarugalzinho/Macapá. Macapá is at 0.03°N, -51.05°W. At 1°N, -51°W the location is north of Macapá by about 110 km, which is lowland savanna and wetlands on the coast side. The coast near Amapá at 1°N reaches about -50.0°W, so -51°W is inland. It is likely the Rio Araguari area (the Araguari mouth is around 1.2°N, -50.0°W), and at -51°W the river is upstream, near Ferreira Gomes (0.85°N, -51.18°W). So this is land with an elevation of roughly 20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":20}

8709.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude -41.0 is in northeastern Brazil, near the border of Piauí and Pernambuco, around Simplício Mendes or Paulistana. The area is inland caatinga on the Borborema/Piauí plateau, with elevations of roughly 400-500 m.

Paulistana (-8.14, -41.14) sits at about 300 m. Further north, around -7.0, -41.0, is near Marcolândia/Araripina in Pernambuco. Araripina's plateau is around 800 m, but that lies farther east at about -40.6. Near Fronteiras and Simplício Mendes in Piauí, the terrain is roughly 400 m. Simplício Mendes (-7.85, -41.9) is about 350 m. I'll estimate 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

1589.text:
Latitude 73.0N, longitude 119.0E lies in the Laptev Sea region, near the Lena Delta and the Buor-Khaya Gulf area. The Lena Delta sits around 72-73.5N, 123-127E. At 119E and 73N, it is north of the Khatanga Gulf and Olenek Bay. The Olenek Gulf is at about 73N, 120E. The Taimyr coast is to the west at about 104E. The Anabar Bay is at about 73.5N, 113E, and the Olenek mouth is near 73.0N, 120E. So 119E, 73N is probably in the Olenek Bay, which is shallow, or on the land of the Olenek delta's west side.

The Olenek Bay spans roughly 72.8-74N, 118-123E, and the Olenek delta is around 73.0N, 121-123E. The west side of the bay at 119E is probably water at about 73N, with a shallow depth of around -10 m.

