2334.text:
The point is at 65.0°N, 169.0°E. Longitude 169°E at 65°N is in far northeastern Russia, in Chukotka. The Anadyr Gulf lies near 64°N, 177°E, which is much farther east, so this point is inland.

The Anadyr River sits around 64°N, 170°E, and Anadyr city is at 64.7°N, 177.5°E. At 169°E the point is west of the Anadyr lowlands, near the Chukotka/Koryak border. The terrain there is the Anadyr Plateau or the upper Anadyr basin, with elevations around 300-600 m. The upper Anadyr River runs near 67°N, 168°E, and the area around 65°N, 169°E is probably mountainous, such as the Pekulney range or the Anadyr Plateau, so I estimate about 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

11304.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 109 is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia. The coast near Albany is at about 117.9°E, so this point is roughly 800 km west of the coast, off the continental shelf. The Naturaliste Plateau lies around 33-34°S, 112-115°E, and its depth is about 2000-3000 m. At 109°E, 35°S the seafloor is probably at the western edge of the plateau or the abyssal plain nearby, so I estimate a depth of about -4000 m.

More specifically, the Diamantina Zone and the Naturaliste Plateau lie south of 33°S. The plateau spans roughly 109-115°E and 31-36°S, with depths of about 2000-3000 m in the shallower parts. At 35°S, 109°E the point could be on the southwestern part of the plateau, where depths are roughly 3000-4000 m. I'll answer -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2842.text:
The point 59.0°N, 105.0°E lies in Siberia, in Irkutsk Oblast or Krasnoyarsk Krai. The Lena River upper reaches are around 57-60°N, 105-110°E, and Ust-Kut is at 56.8°N, 105.7°E. At 59°N, 105°E the area is probably on the Central Siberian Plateau, north of the Lena in the Ilimsk/Katanga region. The Nepa River region is at about 57.5°N, 108°E. The Podkamennaya Tunguska is further north. Elevations of the plateau there are about 400-500 m.

The Lena flows northeast from Ust-Kut and reaches Kirensk at 57.8°N, 108.1°E, so 59°N, 105°E lies west of that. The Ilim River at about 57.5°N, 104°E has elevations around 350 m. The Lower Tunguska headwaters sit near 58°N, 98°E, and the Kata River lies near 59°N, 105°E. The terrain is likely about 450 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

10629.text:
Latitude -29, longitude -161 is in the South Pacific, southeast of the Cook Islands and west of the Austral Islands. Rarotonga lies near -21, -159.8, and Maria Island (Hull) near -21.8, -154.7. At -29, -161 the area is open ocean in the central South Pacific basin, where abyssal plain depths are typically 4500-5500 m. The seafloor age there is likely about 70-90 Ma, which would put depth near 5000 m.

I estimate a depth of about -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

11607.text:
The point is at 39°S, 5°W in the South Atlantic. Tristan da Cunha sits near 37°S, 12.5°W, and Gough Island near 40.3°S, 9.9°W, so neither is close to this location. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge lies near 14°W at this latitude, which puts the point east of the ridge. That area is the western flank of the basin, with depths of roughly 4500 to 5000 m. The Walvis Ridge is farther north, around 30°S to 20°S. At 39°S, 5°W the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or the ridge flank at around 4,500 m.

The ridge axis near 40°S is about 13°W, and the seafloor at 5°W would be about 8 degrees east of it. That is about 700 km, and the crust there is roughly 15 to 20 million years old. A depth of about 4,400 m seems reasonable for that age and distance.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

5804.text:
The point 25.0°N, 91.0°W lies in the central Gulf of Mexico, in the Sigsbee Abyssal Plain region. Water depth there is roughly 3,400 m. The Sigsbee Escarpment sits farther north, near 26-27°N. At 25°N, 91°W the depth is about 3,500 m, which is consistent with abyssal plain depths of 3,500-3,700 m.

