3248.text:
The point is 53.0°N, 163.0°W. That is in the Bering Sea/North Pacific near the Aleutian Islands, south of the Alaska Peninsula. Unimak Island sits at about 54.8°N, 164°W, and the Shumagin Islands are near 55°N, 160°W. At 53°N the point is south of the Aleutians, which in this area lie around 54.5°N, so it is on the Pacific side.

The Aleutian Trench lies south of the arc, and it is deep at roughly 3000–7000 m. At 163°W the trench axis is around 52°N, and the depth there is about 5000 m. The shelf break lies near 54.5°N. At 53°N, about 1.5° south of the arc, the point is on the upper trench slope. Depths there are probably 2000–4000 m, so I estimate about 3500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

6768.text:
Latitude 15.0 N, longitude 37.0 E is in the region of Eritrea, Ethiopia and Sudan. Eritrea's western lowlands lie around 15 N, 37 E, and Sudan's border area near Kassala is at about 15.5 N, 36.4 E. So the point is probably in western Eritrea or just inside Ethiopia or Sudan.

The Eritrea-Sudan border runs near 36.5 E at this latitude. Ethiopia's Tigray region starts at roughly 14.5 N, and the Setit (Tekezé) River runs near 14.0 N, 37.5 E. At 15 N, 37 E we are probably in western Eritrea, in the Gash-Barka region. Further south, near Humera at 14.3 N, 36.6 E, the elevation is about 600 m. Gash-Barka near Tessenei (15.1 N, 36.7 E) is about 600 m. Moving east to 37.0 E, the terrain rises toward the highlands near Keren (15.8 N, 38.4 E), so I estimate about 700 m.

