414.text:
Latitude 85°N, longitude 71°W is in the Arctic Ocean, north of Ellesmere Island and Greenland. Ellesmere's northern tip is around 83.1°N, and Greenland's northern tip is around 83.6°N. At 85°N, 71°W, the point is roughly 200 km north of the Lincoln Sea shelf edge, so it is probably over the Arctic Ocean basin near the Lomonosov Ridge.

The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the New Siberian Islands across the pole toward Ellesmere/Greenland, passing near 85°N around 60–70°W. The ridge crest is typically 1000–1500 m deep. On the Greenland side, near 80°W to 20°W, the ridge approaches Greenland at about 83–85°N. At 85°N, 71°W, the point may be on the Canada Basin side, near the ridge, with depths around 1500–2500 m. The Lincoln Sea shelf is shallow, but 85°N is beyond it. I estimate a seabed depth of about -1800 m.

