{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3450}

14368.text:
Location: -69.0, 117.0 E is in East Antarctica, Wilkes Land, inland of the coast near the Law Dome / Vanderford area. The coast at 117°E lies around -66.5°S (near the Totten Glacier at 116°E, ~-67°S). At -69°S, about 250 km inland, the ice surface would be roughly 1500-2000 m. Total ice sheet elevation near Totten's upper catchment at -69 is roughly 2000 m.

Estimating around 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

8910.text:
Latitude -9, longitude 1 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, in the Gulf of Guinea region far to the west of Africa. It lies roughly east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which is near 13°W at this latitude, so this is the eastern basin, the Angola Basin side. At 1°E, 9°S the seabed is about 5,000 m deep, since the Angola Basin abyssal plain sits around 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Walvis Ridge is further south, near 20°S, so it does not affect this point.

I estimate a depth of about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

701.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 143°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the New Siberian Islands and the Laptev/East Siberian Sea shelf edge. The Lomonosov Ridge runs near 140°E at around 87-88°N, but at 83°N, 143°E the point is north of the continental shelf, on the continental slope or in the Amundsen Basin.

The shelf break in the Laptev Sea sits at roughly 78-79°N near 143°E. The slope then descends to the Nansen Basin at about 3000 m or more. At 83°N, 143°E the seabed is likely in the Amundsen/Nansen basin, with depths around 3900 m. IBCAO suggests that near 83°N, 140°E the depth is about 3,500-4,000 m. I'll estimate -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

1665.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -89.0 falls in Nunavut, Canada. The Boothia Peninsula extends to about 72°N between roughly -94 and -95°W. At -89°, 71°N, this is in the Gulf of Boothia, east of the peninsula. Parts of the Gulf of Boothia lie around -90 to -87°W at this latitude.

Prince Regent Inlet is farther east, near -90°W at 73°N. The Gulf of Boothia lies south of 72°N between Boothia Peninsula (west, about -94°W) and Melville Peninsula (south-west). Baffin Island's west coast lies near -85°W at 71°N, close to Admiralty Inlet at 72°N, -86°W. So at -89°, 71°N the point is in the Gulf of Boothia, which is water. Its depth there is about 200 m, so I estimate -250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-250}

10222.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 105 lies in the southeastern Indian Ocean, south of Java and Christmas Island (which is at about -10.5, 105.7), and roughly west of Australia's northwest coast. That puts it in the Wharton Basin / Argo Abyssal Plain region, where depths are about 5000-5500 m.

The Wharton Basin floor near 23°S, 105°E is likely about 5,000 m deep. The Australian abyssal plains lie to the east, and the Broken Ridge and Ninetyeast Ridge are farther west. At around 23°S, 105°E the seafloor is probably roughly 5,200 m deep, so I'll estimate -5200.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

4964.text:
The point 35.0°N, 29.0°E lies in the eastern Mediterranean, southeast of Rhodes and southwest of Kastellorizo. It is south of Karpathos and Rhodes, and north of Libya and Egypt.

Crete is at about 35°N but spans 23.5–26.3°E, so 29°E is well east of it. Rhodes is at about 36°N, 28°E, so this point is south of Rhodes at 35°N. The area between Crete and Rhodes, near the Strabo Trench, is deep. The Herodotus Abyssal Plain lies further south and is about 3000 m deep. The Rhodes Basin, about 4000 m deep, lies to the northeast at about 35.5°N, 28.5°E. At 35°N, 29°E, the seabed is probably around 2500–3000 m deep. I estimate -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

7913.text:
Latitude 3.0°N, longitude 167.0°E lies in the western Pacific, northwest of the Marshall Islands (Kwajalein is near 9°N, 167°E) and well north of Nauru (0.5°S, 166.9°E). It is open ocean in the Pacific basin south of the Marshall Islands and east of the Caroline Islands region. I did not look up bathymetry, so the depth is an estimate from the general character of the area.

The seafloor here is probably an abyssal plain or a gentle rise, with typical depths of about 4500 to 5000 m. The Nauru Basin lies to the southwest at roughly 4000 to 5000 m, and the area near 3°N, 167°E probably falls within the Nauru Basin edge or the Central Pacific Basin. I'll estimate about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

2268.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude 37.0 lies in Russia's Arkhangelsk Oblast, in the Onega Peninsula and Karelia region. The White Sea's Onega Bay is at roughly 64°N, 36°E, and Kandalaksha Bay is further west. The Onega Peninsula's coast lies near 64.5–65°N and 37–38°E, while the Onega river mouth is near 63.9°N, 38°E.

The Onega Peninsula's northern edge is the Zimny Coast, at about 65.0–65.5°N and 39–40°E. At 65°N, 37°E, the point is probably in the White Sea between Karelia's Kem and the Solovetsky Islands. Kem is at 64.95°N, 34.6°E and the Solovetsky Islands are at 65.0°N, 35.7°E. Moving east to 37°E, it is open water in the White Sea's Onega Bay mouth. The Onega Peninsula's western shore is at roughly 64.5°N, 37.5°E, and Cape Orlov is further north. At 65°N, 37°E, the point is likely water, with the White Sea depth there around 30–60 m. I'll estimate -40 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-40}

6618.text:
Latitude 17.0, longitude 97.0 lies in Myanmar, in the Bago region or the Kayin/Mon area. Moving east from the coast at about 96.5°E, this point is near Hpa-an to Taungoo, close to the Sittaung River valley. At 17°N, the Gulf of Martaban coast is around 16.5°N, 97.0°E. Mawlamyine is at 16.5°N, 97.6°E, and Bago is at 17.3°N, 96.5°E.

So 17.0°N, 97.0°E is east of the Sittaung river, in the Kyaikto area (Kyaikto at 17.3°N, 97.0°E). Kyaikto has the Golden Rock in the hills to the east, and the Sittaung plain lies to the west. At 17.0°N, 97.0°E the point is probably on the coastal plain or in low hills near Bilin (17.2°N, 97.2°E), roughly 20–50 m up. Thaton is at 16.9°N, 97.37°E. This point is south of Kyaikto and likely sits on the plain at about 20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":20}

14590.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -159 is in the Ross Sea sector of Antarctica, under the Ross Ice Shelf. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°W to 160°E, and its eastern part is near 158°W, so this point lies within it, probably in the eastern part of the shelf near Edward VII Peninsula and Marie Byrd Land. The Ross Ice Shelf front is at about -78, so -73 is not on the shelf. At -73, -159 we are inland in Marie Byrd Land, on the coast of the Hobbs/Saunders coast region, around the Ford Ranges at -77. At -73 and 159°W, we are in the Amundsen Sea sector, but the coast there is around -74 to -75 at 160°W, so this is probably the open ocean of the southern Pacific or the continental margin, though sea ice and shelf edges are not clear.

The Ross Sea shelf at 180° W extends to about -78. At 159°W the Marie Byrd Land coast is near -77.5, since the Ford Ranges are at -77 and the Ross Ice Shelf edge runs up to about -77.5 there. So -73 is north of the coast, in the ocean. The continental shelf edge there is about -76 to -73? In the eastern Ross Sea, the shelf break is around -73 to -74 at 160°W, since the Ross Sea continental shelf extends to about 73°S near the Iselin Bank at 170°W. At 159°W, -73 is probably the continental slope or rise, with depth of around -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

14645.text:
Location: -73, -49 is in the Weddell Sea, in the Filchner-Ronne Ice Shelf region. The Filchner Ice Shelf spans roughly longitudes -30 to -45 and the Ronne Ice Shelf -45 to -80, with latitudes of about -75 to -83. At -73 latitude and -49 longitude, this is north of the ice shelf front, which is near -75 to -77 at that longitude. It is probably open Weddell Sea, off the Antarctic Peninsula's eastern side (Larsen C is at about -67 and -62 longitude). The longitude -49 at latitude -73 is in the southwestern Weddell Sea, over the continental shelf or slope.

The Berkner Island region is at about -79, -45. The Ronne Ice Shelf front at -49 is around latitude -75.5. So -73 is open water north of it. The continental shelf in the western Weddell has depths of around 400-600 m, and the shelf break is near -74, -40. At -49 and -73, the depth is probably about 500 m, though the Weddell abyssal plain is deeper to the north. I'll guess about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

9646.text:
Latitude -17.0, longitude 33.0 is in Mozambique. It lies near the Zambezi River, about 100 km upstream of Caia, in Tete or Sofala province. Caia is at roughly -17.8, 35.3, and Tete is at -16.15, 33.58. The point at -17.0, 33.0 is southwest of Tete, in the Zambezi valley south of the river. The Cahora Bassa reservoir is further northwest, around -15.6, 32.7, so it does not affect this point.

The river near Tete sits at about 120 m elevation. The area southwest of Tete is lowland, with elevations around 200-300 m. I'll estimate 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

727.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -165.0° (165°W) lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Alaska and Chukotka, in the Canada Basin / Chukchi Borderland region. The Chukchi Plateau is around 75-80°N, and the Canada Basin abyssal plain lies further north. At 81°N, 165°W, the area is probably within the Canada Basin or near the Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge. The Alpha Ridge spans roughly 85°N, 120-150°W, and the Mendeleev Ridge is near 80-82°N, 180°W. At 165°W the position may be on the Mendeleev Ridge's flank or in the Canada Basin edge. The Canada Basin floor is about 3800 m deep, and the Mendeleev Ridge crest is around 1500-2500 m deep. I'll estimate roughly 2000 m for this location.

Considering the geography further, the Chukchi Borderland has the Northwind Ridge at about 75-77°N, 160°W. At 81°N, 165°W we are north of it, probably in the transition toward the Mendeleev Plain or Canada Basin, where depths are about 2000-3000 m. I'll go with about 2400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

5314.text:
The location 31.0°N, 9.0°E is in southern Tunisia/northern Sahara, near the Chott el Djerid area. Chott el Djerid spans roughly 33.7°N, 8.4°E, so 31.0°N is further south, in the Grand Erg Oriental region on the Tunisia–Algeria border. The border is near 9.0°E at around 31°N. Terrain there is sand dunes at roughly 100–150 m elevation.

I estimate about 120 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

712.text:
Latitude 83°N, longitude 165°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the New Siberian Islands. It is near the Lomonosov Ridge region, which runs from the Siberian shelf past the pole toward Greenland. The Lomonosov Ridge at about 88°N is about 1000-1500 m deep, and near 83°N, 140-160°E it is at about 1000-2000 m.

At 83°N, 165°E, the point is probably on the Amundsen Basin side or the Eurasian continental slope. The Siberian shelf edge lies near 78-79°N there, and the Lomonosov Ridge starts near 81-83°N at about 140°E. Longitude 165°E is toward the Makarov Basin side, which is east of the ridge. The Makarov Basin is about 3900 m deep, though near 83°N 165°E it may be the Mendeleev side or the ridge flank. The ridge heads toward 90°N along roughly 140°E to 150°E, so 165°E is probably on the Makarov Basin flank, with a depth around 2500-3500 m. I'll estimate -2700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2700}

11543.text:
The point (-39, -133) lies in the South Pacific, southeast of Tahiti and west of the Chile Rise. It is far from any land, so the surface type is water.

Seafloor depth here is a judgment from regional knowledge, not a lookup. The East Pacific Rise is near -113 longitude at this latitude, and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge lies further south. At -133 longitude and -39 latitude, the crust is about 20-30 million years old. That age gives a depth of roughly 4000-4500 m. The region between the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and the Mendoza Rise is about 4000 m deep. I estimate -4300 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -4300}

12780.text:
The point at 53°S, 179°W lies in the South Pacific, southeast of New Zealand. The Antipodes Islands are near 49.7°S, 178.8°E and Campbell Island is near 52.5°S, 169°E, so neither is close. The point is east of the Campbell Plateau and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is farther south and east, so I expect open ocean.

The Southeast Indian/Pacific basin in this area is likely about 4,500–5,000 m deep. The Antipodes Fracture Zone and Udintsev Fracture Zone lie near 55–57°S. The Campbell Plateau's eastern edge is near 175°W at 50°S. At 53°S, 179°W the seafloor is probably on the lower slope or the Pacific abyssal plain, around 4,000–5,000 m deep. I'll estimate about -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

8103.text:
The point (-1.0, -173.0) is in the central Pacific, in the Phoenix Islands region of Kiribati. Nikumaroro is at about -4.67, -174.5 and Orona at about -4.5, -172.2, so this point is well north of both and lies in open ocean.

The seafloor between the equator and the Phoenix Islands in this area is roughly 5000 to 5500 m deep. Water depth near the equator at 173°W is probably around 5100 m, so I'll estimate about -5100 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

9316.text:
The point at -13.0, 93.0 is in the eastern Indian Ocean, southwest of the Cocos (Keeling) Islands, which sit near -12.1, 96.9. It is therefore open water.

The seabed depth there is an estimate from memory of regional bathymetry, not a lookup. The area lies on the Indian Ocean abyssal plain, east of the Ninetyeast Ridge. The ridge is near 90°E, with crest depths of about 1,500–3,000 m. At 93°E the seafloor is probably in the Wharton Basin, with depths around 5,000–5,500 m. I'll use -5,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

11423.text:
The point is at 37°S, 13°W in the South Atlantic. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which sits near 14°W at that latitude. It is also near the Tristan da Cunha region, which is at about 37°S, 12.3°W, so it is close to that island group.

Tristan da Cunha itself is at 37.1°S, 12.3°W. The point at 13°W is about 0.7° west of the island, roughly 60 km. The seabed there is on the flanks of the Tristan platform or the ridge, probably 3000 to 4000 m deep. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge axis near 36°S lies at about 13.5°W, with depths of about 2500 to 3000 m, and Tristan lies east of the ridge on the African plate side. Since 13°W is near the ridge axis, the depth is probably about 3000 m. I estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

6358.text:
Latitude 19.0, longitude -63.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, northeast of the Anegada Passage and east of the Puerto Rico Trench's southern part. Anguilla and Sint Maarten sit at roughly 18.2°N, 63°W, so 19.0°N is about 90 km north of them. That puts the point north of Anguilla, on the edge of the Puerto Rico Trench's southern side.

The Anguilla bank shelf extends only a little north of the island. At 19°N, the depth is probably about 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Puerto Rico Trench axis lies at about 19.8°N near 66°W, and its depth there is roughly 8,000 m. At 63°W the trench is shallower, around 5,000 to 5,500 m, and the axis sits near 20°N. So at 19°N, 63°W, the seabed is probably on the southern trench slope, at about 4,800 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

3887.text:
Latitude 47.0 N, longitude 35.0 E is in southeastern Ukraine, in the Zaporizhzhia region. The location is roughly south of Dnipro and near Zaporizhzhia city, which sits at about 47.84 N, 35.14 E. At 47.0 N, 35.0 E the point is near Vasylivka or Kamianka-Dniprovska, at about 47.4 N, 34.4 E. It is probably steppe, in the Azov upland at the southern edge, roughly 100 m above sea level.

The Kakhovka reservoir lies further west at about 34.0 E, so it does not affect this point. Terrain near Melitopol (46.84 N, 35.37 E) is about 20–60 m. At 47.0 N, 35.0 E, which is near Tokmak and Mykhailivka, the elevation is probably about 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

10125.text:
The point (-23, -89) lies in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and south of Easter Island. Easter Island is at about 27°S, 109°W, so this point is well to its east.

The location is near the Nazca Ridge and Sala y Gómez Ridge region, but at 89°W it lies east of the East Pacific Rise (about 110°W at this latitude). It sits on the Nazca Plate, with seafloor depths of roughly 3,500–4,000 m. The Chile Rise and the Peru-Chile Trench are farther east, with the trench at about 71°W. The Nazca Ridge runs at 15–20°S near 80°W. At 23°S, 89°W the seafloor is probably about 3,800 m deep, and I estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

4865.text:
The point is at 35°N, 169°W in the North Pacific, well south of the Aleutians and northwest of Hawaii. That puts it in open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed here is most likely abyssal plain at roughly 5,500 m depth. The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain lies near 170°E, far to the west, so it does not affect this point. The area is probably on the Pacific plate north of the Hawaiian Ridge, where depths are typically 5,300 to 5,800 m. I am estimating this from regional knowledge, not from a bathymetry lookup, so I'll use a mid-range value of about -5,550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5550}

2848.text:
Latitude 59.0, Longitude 117.0 (east) is in Siberia, in the Irkutsk Oblast / Yakutia border region. This is the Lena River basin area near the Patom Highlands, and Lensk is around 60.7N, 114.9E, so the point lies to the south-southeast of Lensk. The Vitim River flows at about 59N, 112-117E. Vitim at Bodaibo (57.8N, 114.2E), Mama at 58.3N, 112.9E. At 59N, 117E the area is the Patom Highland's northeastern edge, or the Olyokma-Vitim watershed. Elevation is probably about 800 m.

The Vitim River near 59.3N, 116.5E is at roughly 350 m, but the surrounding highlands are higher, so I'd estimate 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":850}

4458.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude 97.0 is in the US state of Nebraska. Longitude 97°W is not stated explicitly, but I'm assuming the positive 97.0 is a sign convention issue. Taken literally, 97°E at 41°N lies in the Gobi Desert region of Gansu/Mongolia, near the Gansu–Inner Mongolia border.

Reading it as +97 E, the location is north of the Qilian Mountains and Dunhuang, in the Mazong Shan area or the Gobi. Dunhuang is at about 40.1°N, 94.7°E, and the Mazongshan area is around 41.8°N, 97°E. At 41°N, 97°E, the terrain is Gobi plain with an elevation of roughly 1,300 m. The Beishan region is typically 1,200–1,700 m, and Yumen is at about 40.3°N, 97.0°E with an elevation near 1,500 m, while the area to the north, such as Heihe valley, is lower. I'll estimate about 1,400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1400}

11969.text:
Latitude -43, longitude -1 lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of Africa. It is north of the Southwest Indian Ridge and Bouvet Island, and west of the Agulhas area.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is at roughly 0 to 15°W at these latitudes. The South Atlantic ridge near 43°S sits at about 15°W, and the Atlantic-Indian Ridge begins around 55°S, east of Bouvet. At 1°W and 43°S, we are east of the ridge on the African plate side, in the Agulhas Basin or Atlantic-Indian Basin. Depths there are about 4,500 to 5,000 m. There is also the Agulhas Ridge, but that is near 40–45°S and 5–15°E. Thus the point is in the abyssal basin, and I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

2664.text:
Latitude 61.0°N, longitude 109.0°E is in central Siberia, in the Sakha Republic (Yakutia) or the Irkutsk/Krasnoyarsk border area. It lies near the Vilyuy River basin or the Lena River upstream, in the Central Siberian Plateau. Ust-Kut is around 105.7°E, 56.8°N, and Lensk is at 114.9°E, 60.7°N. At 109°E, 61°N, the area is probably north of the Nepa/Markha region and close to the Vilyuy Plateau and Chona River. The terrain there is plateau at roughly 400-500 m.

I estimate about 450 m and classify it as land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":450}

10419.text:
Latitude -25, longitude 139 is in central Australia, in the Simpson Desert region of southwest Queensland / the Northern Territory–South Australia border area. The Simpson Desert's eastern part, near the Queensland border at 139°E, lies at roughly 100–150 m elevation. The Mulligan River and Georgina River area is around 100 m. At -25, 139, the location is in the Simpson Desert sand dunes, probably about 90–120 m.

I estimate about 105 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":105}

994.text:
The point 79.0°N, 9.0°E is in the Fram Strait / Greenland Sea, west of Svalbard. Svalbard's west coast at Prins Karls Forland and Kongsfjorden lies around 11-12°E at 79°N, so 9°E is offshore.

The seabed there is probably the continental slope or the Molloy/Knipovich area. Near 79°N, 9°E the bathymetry gets deep: the Molloy Deep is at about 79°N, 3°E and is 5,500 m deep. The Svalbard shelf edge is around 9-10°E at 79°N, and the slope descends westward from there. At 9°E the depth is likely about 1,500-2,500 m. The Hovgaard Ridge is further north, near 79°N, 3°E, so it does not apply here. I'll estimate a depth of about 2,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

16030.text:
At latitude -89, longitude -159, the point is near the South Pole, on the Antarctic ice sheet. The South Pole itself sits at about 2835 m. At 89°S the surface along the 159°W meridian is roughly 1° from the pole, on the side toward the Ross Ice Shelf and the Transantarctic Mountains.

Longitude 180 points toward the Ross Ice Shelf, and 159°W is close to that direction. The Ross Ice Shelf grounding zone sits around 85° to 86°S, so 89°S is well inside the ice sheet on the polar plateau. Surface elevation there is probably about 2800 m. Moving from the pole along 160°W, the Leverett Glacier area at about 86°S has a surface elevation near 2000 m, and the plateau at 89°S is around 2750 to 2800 m. I'll estimate 2790 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2790}

3423.text:
The point is 51.0°N, 173.0°W, in the North Pacific south of the Aleutian Islands. The Aleutian Islands lie at about 52°N, and the Andreanof Islands extend to around 175°W. At 173°W the islands are the Delarof Islands and Rat Islands, roughly 51.5°N at 178°E, so those are well to the west. Near 173°W the Aleutian chain is at about 52.0–52.5°N: Atka sits at 174°W, 52.2°N, and Amlia at 173°30'W, 52.1°N. Seguam is at 172.5°W, 52.3°N. The point at 51°N is therefore about 1° south of the islands, in the open Pacific south of the arc.

South of the Aleutians, the trench lies about 50 km off the island arc, and the depth there is around 5000–6000 m. At 51°N, 173°W the point is roughly 100 km south of Amlia. The trench axis sits near 51.3°N at 174°W, with depths of about 6000 m. The point might be on the trench's southern slope or in the trench itself. I estimate about -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

4013.text:
Latitude 45.0, longitude -73.0 is near the Quebec–Vermont border. Lake Champlain lies at about -73.3 to -73.4 at this latitude, so -73.0 is east of the lake, in Quebec near Venise-en-Québec or Saint-Armand. At 45.0°N the border runs along the 45th parallel. East of Lake Champlain, around -73.0, is Franklin County, Vermont, near Highgate and Swanton, where the terrain is farmland at about 50–100 m.

Missisquoi Bay's eastern shore is near -73.1, and Highgate Springs is at about -73.08. The Missisquoi River flows near Swanton at -73.12. At -73.0 the point is just inland, so it is land. Elevation there is roughly 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":70}

6956.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude 53.0 is in Yemen, in the Hadramawt/Shabwa area, at roughly 13°N 53°E. The Gulf of Aden coast lies near 14°N at that longitude, so this point is inland, north of the coast. The coastline there runs along roughly 14.0°N, near Bir Ali and Mayfa'a at about 48°E, and further east near Al Mukalla at 49°E and 14.5°N. Near 53°E the coast is around 15.8°N (Qishn is at 15.4°N, 51.7°E, and Nishtun at 15.8°N, 52.2°E). So 13°N, 53°E is well inland, in the Rub' al Khali's southern edge or the Hadramawt plateau.

Actually, the Gulf of Aden coast turns northeast. At 13°N, 53°E, that would be in the Arabian Sea, south of Socotra, which sits at about 12.5°N, 54°E. So the point lies west of Socotra, about 100 km to the west, in open water. Socotra spans roughly 53.3–54.5°E, and Abd al Kuri is at about 52.2°E, 12.2°N. The point at 13°N, 53°E is north of Abd al Kuri and west of Socotra, in the channel between Socotra and the mainland. The depth there is about 1,500 m or more. The Socotra–Guardafui channel is deeper to the south, while the Gulf of Aden basin north of Socotra is around 2,000 m deep. I'll estimate -1,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

15918.text:
The point is at 87°S, 23°W, which is on the Antarctic plateau, a few hundred km from the South Pole. Longitude -23 runs through the Filchner/Foundation Ice Stream and Pensacola Mountains direction. The South Pole itself is at about 2835 m. Along 23°W, near 87°S, the surface is in the Dome Argus/Thiel Mountains sector. The Thiel Mountains are near 85°S, 90°W, so they are not relevant here.

The area at 87°S, 23°W lies on the East Antarctic plateau side, upstream of the Foundation Ice Stream. Surface elevation there is probably around 2,700–2,900 m. The polar plateau near 86°S, 20°W is roughly 2,500–2,700 m. I'll estimate 2,750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2750}

2407.text:
