{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

2349.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude -161.0 is in western Alaska, in the Yukon-Kuskokwim Delta region. It lies east of Norton Sound and north of the Yukon River mouth. The Yukon River at about 62.6N, -164 runs near the coast, and at -161 it is further inland. Around 63N, -161 is near Kusilvak area (Marshall is at 61.9N, -162.1; Russian Mission at 61.8N, -161.3; Emmonak at 62.8N, -164.5; Mountain Village at 62.1N, -163.7). So 63.0, -161.0 is north of the Yukon, near the Kaltag/Andreafsky area. The Andreafsky Mountains are a bit north-west, and the Nulato Hills are to the north. This spot is probably lowland with ponds and hills of modest height, perhaps 100 m.

I'm judging this from memory of the area, not from a data lookup. The Yukon at about 62.9N, -160.5 is near Pilot Station, and the Yukon passes Holy Cross further east at 62.2N, -159.8. The point is likely in the low hills north of the river, at about 100 m elevation.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

8960.text:
The point (-9.0, 101.0) is in the Indian Ocean southwest of Sumatra. Sumatra's southwest coast near Padang is at about 1°S, 100°E. At 9°S, 101°E the point is roughly 800 km south of that and well offshore. It lies south of the Sunda trench axis or near it. The trench axis near 101°E is at about 10°S, with depths around 6,000 m. At 9°S the point is probably on the outer trench slope, or on the accretionary side just north of the axis.

The Java trench lies at about 10.5°S near 110°E. Near 101°E the trench axis is around 5–6°S, since the trench runs northwest–southeast off Sumatra. Near 0°N, 98°E the trench is at about 3°N. By 100°E it is at about 4°S, and by 105°E at about 7°S. At 101°E it would be around 5°S, so 9°S lies on the Indian plate about 400 km seaward of the trench. The abyssal plain there is about 5,500 m deep. The Investigator Fracture Zone and Wharton Basin depths are around 5,000–5,500 m, so I estimate -5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

8638.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 177.0 is in the central Pacific, east of the Gilbert Islands and south of the equator. Kiribati's Gilbert group runs from about 3°N to 3°S at longitudes 172-177°E, with Arorae near 2.6°S, 176.8°E. At 5°S, 177°E the point is open ocean to the south of those islands, and the nearest land is Tamana at about 2.5°S. Tuvalu begins further south at about 6°S, 176°E (Nanumea is at 5.7°S, 176.1°E). So this point is likely open water.

The seabed depth in this area is probably around 4,500 to 5,000 m, so I estimate about -4,800 m. The Ellice Basin lies to the south and east and is about 5,000 m deep. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

15520.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -99 places the point in West Antarctica, in the Ellsworth Land / Thwaites–Pine Island interior area. Longitude -99 at latitude -83 is south of the Amundsen Sea sector, inland of the Thwaites glacier catchment, and probably on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet divide region, which sits around 1,000-2,000 m ice-surface elevation.

The Ross Ice Shelf lies at about -180 to -150 longitude, so this point is not on it. At -83 latitude, -99 longitude is roughly 800 km from the coast. The ice-sheet surface there is probably about 1,500 m. The WAIS divide is at about -79.5, -112 with a surface elevation of about 1,800 m. Toward the Ellsworth Mountains (-80, -85), the surface is about 1,000-2,000 m. At -83, -99, the surface is likely about 1,200-1,500 m. I'll answer 1,400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1400}

14630.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -79 is in West Antarctica, in the Bellingshausen Sea sector. It lies west of the Antarctic Peninsula base, near the Ronne/Eights Coast area. Longitude -79 at latitude -73 is offshore of the Eights Coast (around 75°S, 80°W), in the Bellingshausen Sea, north of Thurston Island (72°S, 100°W) and well west of the Peninsula (~-70 to -75°S, -60 to -80°W). Alexander Island sits at about -72°S, -70°W, and the Bellingshausen Sea lies west of it at -73°S, -79°W. That would be open ocean or ice-shelf covered ocean, so this is likely water.

The Bellingshausen Sea continental shelf there is deep, roughly 500 m. I'll estimate about -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

11495.text:
Latitude -37, longitude 131 lies in the Southern Ocean south of South Australia. The Eyre Peninsula and Great Australian Bight coast are at about -32 to -34 latitude at this longitude. The coast near Ceduna is at about -32, so -37 is well offshore.

The Great Australian Bight shelf is narrow here. The continental shelf edge is around -34.5 to -35 at 131°E, and the slope descends to about 4500 m depth by -38 or -39. At -37, the depth is probably around -4000 m. The Bight's continental slope is gentle and the Eucla Terrace is a marginal plateau at about 2000-3000 m depth. In this region, at -37, 131°E, I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

1340.text:
Latitude 75.0, longitude -19.0 is in northeast Greenland, off the coast. Land around Hold with Hope is at about 73.5N, and Kong Oscar Fjord is at about 72N. At 75N, the coast near Shannon Island and Pendulum Islands lies near -18 to -19 longitude. Shannon Island is at about 75.2N, -18.5W. Germania Land is at about 77N, -20W. Ardencaple Fjord is at about 75.5N, -20W. Clavering Island is at 74.3N, -20W, and Wollaston Foreland is at 74.5N, -20W.

At 75.0N, -19.0W, the point is probably in the sound between Clavering Island and Shannon Island. Hochstetter Forland sits at 75.3N, -19.7W, so the point is near it. Shannon Island runs from roughly 74.8 to 75.3N and from -19 to -17.5W, so the point could be on the western tip of Shannon, but that is uncertain. Since Shannon's west coast is around -18.9W, the point is probably in Pendulum Sound or just west of it. Pendulum Sund is between Shannon and Pendulum Islands, and the strait between Clavering and Shannon is Sabine Island's area, around 74.4N, -18.8W. At 75N, -19W, I'll go with water and a seabed depth of about -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

12477.text:
Latitude -49, longitude -65 lies off the coast of Patagonia, Argentina. Puerto Deseado is at about -47.75, -65.9, and San Julián is at about -49.3, -67.7. At longitude -65 the point is east of the coast, on the Patagonian shelf in the Atlantic. The coast near -49 is at roughly -67.6, so the point is about 2.6° of longitude (about 190 km) offshore. The shelf there is typically 100 to 120 m deep.

Depths in that area, northeast of the Falklands and east of Santa Cruz, are around 100 m. Near 49°S, 65°W I estimate about -105 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-105}

12618.text:
The point at 51°S, 143°W is in the South Pacific, west of Chile and southeast of New Zealand. It lies in open ocean on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank or nearby basin.

The East Pacific Rise / Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs roughly from (-56, -145) to (-45, -112). At 51°S the ridge axis is around 114–120°W, so 143°W is well west of it, on the Pacific plate flank. Seafloor depth there is likely around 3,800–4,200 m. The ridge near 56°S, 145°W is the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge, which is shallow at about 2,500 m, but 51°S is farther north. The Udintsev fracture zone lies near 56°S, and the Eltanin fracture zone is near 54°S, 135°W. At 51°S, 143°W the seafloor is probably 4,000 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

6548.text:
The point (17°N, 43°W) lies in the central North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 17°N is at about 46°W. The point is therefore east of the axis, not west of it, since -43 is east of -46.

Abyssal plain depths on the African-side flank of the ridge at this distance are around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The ridge axis near 45–46°W at 15–17°N sits at roughly 3,000 m, with the crest near 2,500–3,500 m. About 3° of longitude off-axis is roughly 300 km. At a half-spreading rate of about 1.2 cm/yr, that is about 25 Myr of age. Depth is roughly 2,600 + 350·√25 ≈ 4,350 m. Rough topography on the flanks is possible. I estimate about -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

12363.text:
Latitude -47, longitude 67 is in the southern Indian Ocean. The Kerguelen Islands lie at about 49°S, 69.5°E, and the Kerguelen Plateau extends to the northwest of them. At 47°S, 67°E the point is northwest of Kerguelen, west of the northern part of the plateau. The plateau's western edge near 66-67°E is fairly deep. The Crozet Plateau is farther west, at about 51°E.

The northern Kerguelen Plateau spans roughly 46-50°S and 63-75°E, with typical depths of 1000-2000 m. The island's northwestern shelf extends to about 48.5°S, 67°E, so this point may lie on the plateau at a depth of about 1500 m. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

15633.text:
Location: -83°S, 127°E is in East Antarctica, on the plateau, south of Wilkes Land. The ice sheet there lies around the Dome C to South Pole region. Dome C is at 75°S, 123°E with an elevation of 3233 m. At 83°S, 127°E the surface is roughly 2800-3300 m. Near Vostok (78.5°S, 106.8°E) the surface is 3488 m. Toward the South Pole (2835 m), the surface at 83°S, 127°E is probably about 3200 m. The Concordia–Pole traverse suggests the surface is around 3300 m near 80°S, 120°E. Further south at 83°S, 127°E, I estimate about 3100 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so it is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3150}

3610.text:
The point 49°N, 159°W lies in the North Pacific, south of the Aleutian Islands and the Alaska Peninsula and well north of Hawaii. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The Aleutian Trench runs south of the Aleutians, but at 159°W the trench axis is near 53–54°N. At 49°N the point sits on the abyssal plain of the North Pacific south of the trench, where depths are typically about 5,000 to 5,500 m. This is an estimate from general bathymetry, not a looked-up value. I'll use about -5,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

16015.text:
Latitude -87, longitude 171 lies on the Antarctic plateau, close to the South Pole and on the Ross Sea side. The pole itself is at about 2835 m. At 87°S along the 171°E meridian, the route toward the Ross Ice Shelf passes near the Leverett Glacier and the Transantarctic Mountains. Leverett Glacier is at roughly 86°S, 175°W. The longitude of 171°E at 87°S is on the polar plateau, with an ice surface probably around 2900 to 3000 m.

I am estimating from memory of the area rather than from a data lookup. The surface there is grounded ice, so the type is Solid. The Transantarctic Mountains edge near 85°S has an elevation of roughly 2500 to 3000 m. I'll go with 2950 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

8110.text:
The point is at 1°S, 159°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of the equator, east of the Line Islands (Christmas Island is at about 2°N, 157°W), and north of the Cook Islands and the Manihiki Plateau, which sits near 10°S, 161°W. No islands are nearby, so the surface type is Water.

For depth, the central equatorial Pacific floor in this area is typically around 5000 to 5500 m. The Penrhyn Basin lies to the south and is about 5200 m deep, while the area near the equator at 159°W is likely around 5100 to 5300 m. I'll estimate -5150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5150}

688.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude 117°E lies in the Arctic Ocean north of the Laptev Sea and the Severnaya Zemlya archipelago, which is at about 79°N. The point is beyond the Laptev Sea shelf, near the Nansen Basin or Amundsen Basin and close to the Gakkel Ridge.

The Gakkel Ridge runs through roughly 85°N, 90°E and continues toward the Laptev Sea at about 78°N, 125°E. At 117°E the ridge axis is near 80–81°N. So 83°N is probably on the southern flank of the Amundsen Basin, or on the Lomonosov Ridge side, but the Lomonosov Ridge is at about 140°E in that area. The Amundsen Basin lies between the Gakkel and Lomonosov ridges and is about 4,000 m deep. At 83°N, 117°E the continental slope is further south, around 81°N. The seafloor here is probably about 3,500 m deep, so I estimate -3,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

810.text:
Location: 81°N, 1°E is in the Arctic Ocean north of Svalbard (Svalbard's northernmost point is about 80.8°N at Nordaustlandet, 20°E). At 1°E, 81°N, this is north of the Fram Strait, near the Yermak Plateau's western side and the Molloy/Knipovich area. The Yermak Plateau lies around 80-81°N, 5-10°E. The Lena Trough and Molloy Deep are further west and south. At 81°N, 1°E, the Lena Trough, with depths around 3000-4000 m, probably applies. I'd estimate the depth at about -3400 m.

Bathymetry (IBCAO) shows the Lena Trough at about 81°N, 0-3°E with a depth near 3500 m, with the Yermak Plateau to the east at around 800 m. I'll go with -3300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

13900.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -99 is in the Bellingshausen Sea, west of the Antarctic Peninsula. It is well offshore, roughly 600 km from the coast, so this should be open ocean. The Bellingshausen Sea abyssal plain there lies at roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m depth. The continental rise would be shallower, but the continental shelf edge sits near 70°S in this area, so at 65°S the point is probably on the abyssal plain or the lower rise.

I'm estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry, not from a lookup. The Bellingshausen abyssal plain around 65°S, 99°W is probably about 4,800 m deep, so I'll use -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

787.text:
Latitude 81.0N, longitude 45W is in northeast Greenland. Land there extends to about 83N at Peary Land, and 45W at 81N is inland on the ice sheet, north of Dronning Louise Land. The northern ice sheet near 81N, 45W has a surface elevation of roughly 2,000 m, perhaps a little less.

The NEGIS (Northeast Greenland Ice Stream) starts around 77N, 35-40W, and the ice divide is near 72N, 40W in the south. At 81N, 45W, the ice sheet surface sits at about 1,800-2,100 m. The northern ice sheet has a north-south ridge near 45W at about 80N with an elevation of around 2,000 m. I'll answer 1,900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

3043.text:
Latitude 57.0°N, longitude 147.0°E lies in the Sea of Okhotsk. The Siberian coast near Okhotsk is at about 59.4°N, 143°E, and the coast around 57°N is near 138°E (Ayan is at about 56.4°N, 138°E). The Shantar Islands are around 55°N, 137°E. So 147°E is well offshore, in the northern Okhotsk Sea.

The northern Sea of Okhotsk has a broad shelf. The Magadan shelf is at about 59°N, 150°E. At 57°N, 147°E, depth is probably about 100-200 m. The area south of 58°N between 145° and 150°E includes the Derugin Basin to the south (about 52°N, 145°E), so the site is likely on the northern shelf slope. I'll estimate around 150 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

8278.text:
Latitude -1.0, longitude 177.0 lies in the central Pacific, just south of the equator and slightly west of the Gilbert Islands (Kiribati). Nearby land: Tarawa is at about 1.4N, 173E, and Abemama is at 0.4N, 173.8E. Banaba is at 0.87S, 169.5E. Nikunau is at 1.3S, 176.5E, and Beru is at 1.3S, 176E. Onotoa is at 1.8S, 175.5E. Nikunau sits at roughly 176.45E, so a point at 177.0E is about 55 km east of it, which is open ocean.

The seafloor here is part of the Pacific basin near the Gilbert Ridge. The Gilbert Islands are seamounts rising from about 4,500 m depth, and the surrounding abyssal depth is roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m. I'll estimate -4,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

14614.text:
Location: 73°S, 111°W is in the Amundsen Sea / Bellingshausen region off West Antarctica. Marie Byrd Land's coast lies near 75°S at 111°W (Pine Island Bay is ~102°W, 75°S). At 73°S, 111°W it is open ocean on the continental shelf edge or slope, north of the Getz/Dotson ice shelves. The Amundsen Sea shelf extends to about 72-73°S near 110°W, with depths of about 500 m on the shelf and deeper toward the slope. The continental shelf break there is around 71-72°S, so at 73°S it should be on the shelf, with depth of roughly -500 m.

Bathymetry around Pine Island Bay's trough extends to roughly 108°W, with depths of 600-1000 m. At 111°W, 73°S, the depth is probably about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

3405.text:
Latitude 53.0 N, longitude 151.0 E is in the Sea of Okhotsk. Kamchatka lies at about 156–160 E and the Okhotsk coast at around 59 N, with Sakhalin at 142–143 E. At 53 N, 151 E the point is in the open central Okhotsk Sea, east of Sakhalin, and it is water.

The central basin here is the Deryugin Basin, which lies west of 148 E, and the Kuril Basin is to the south. At 53 N, 151 E the point is probably on the slope or shelf off western Kamchatka. The shelf off western Kamchatka extends to roughly 155 E. The Okhotsk Sea has a broad shelf in the north, and in the central region there are depths of around 800 m. Features such as the Lebed Plateau and the Academy of Sciences Rise lie near 152 E. I'll estimate about 900 m, though the TINRO basin trough at around 145-150 E is deeper.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-900}

1803.text:
Latitude 69.0 N, longitude 173.0 W is in the Chukchi Sea. Wrangel Island sits at about 71N, 179.5W to 178W, and the Chukotka coast at 69N is around 179W to 175W (Cape Schmidt is at 68.9N, 179.5W). At 173W, 69N, that is well east of the coast, so it is open water in the Chukchi Sea.

The Alaska coast is at about 165W, at 68N. The Chukchi Sea depth in this region is about 40-50 m. The Herald Canyon is further north-west, near 72N, 175W. At 69N, 173W the depth is roughly 45 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-45}

14605.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -129 lies in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector near the Getz Ice Shelf and Marie Byrd Land coast. The Getz Ice Shelf extends from about -125 to -150 longitude at roughly -74 to -75 latitude. At -73, -129 the point is probably off the coast, over the continental shelf, or possibly under the ice shelf's eastern end.

Dotson and Crosson ice shelves are near -112, and Pine Island Bay is near -100. Getz's eastern portion reaches about -120. At -129, -73 the point is most likely open ocean or sea ice on the continental shelf, with the Getz shelf front around -74 at this longitude. The shelf there has a seabed depth of about -500 to -600 m, so I'll answer Water with a seabed of about -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-550}

14384.text:
Latitude -69, longitude 149 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land. Dumont d'Urville is at 140°E and Mertz Glacier at about 145-146°E, around -67°S. At -69, 149E the point is inland from the coast, which sits near -66.5 at that longitude (Adélie/George V Land coast, around 148E the coast is near -68). Around 149E the coast is near -68.3 (Cook Ice Shelf is at 152E, -68.5). So -69 is probably just inland on grounded ice, with an ice surface elevation of roughly 1000 m.

Inland of the Cook ice shelf, the ice surface at 69S is about 700-1200 m. I'll estimate 1000.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1000}

12426.text:
Latitude -49, longitude -167 lies in the South Pacific Ocean, southwest of New Zealand and east of the Campbell Plateau. It is on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge's northern side, in the open ocean. The seafloor there is probably about 4,500 to 5,000 m deep.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge runs near 55°S, 140°W to 65°S, 175°W, so this point is well north of it. The Southeast Pacific Basin and the Southwest Pacific Basin have depths around 4,500 to 5,000 m, and the Udintsev fracture zone lies near 56°S. At 49°S, 167°W, the seafloor is likely about 4,800 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

12876.text:
Location: 53°S, 13°E is in the South Atlantic, southwest of Bouvet Island (54.4°S, 3.4°E) and near the Southwest Indian Ridge / American-Antarctic Ridge region. The Bouvet triple junction is at about 55°S, 1°W. At 53°S, 13°E, we're east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, in the Agulhas Basin/Enderby area. The Meteor Rise is farther north, around 52°S, 2°E. The Agulhas Ridge lies near 42°S, 15°E, which is far north. At 53°S, 13°E the area is likely abyssal plain, with depths around 3500–4500 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge extends from the Bouvet junction to the northeast, reaching about 53°S, 8°E, then 50°S, 20°E. So 13°E lies near the ridge axis, which would be about 52°S at 13°E. The depth there is probably around 3000–3500 m.

I'll estimate -3300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

13473.text:
Latitude -59, longitude 127 lies in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia, on the Australian-Antarctic Basin. The Southeast Indian Ridge is around 50°S at this longitude, and Antarctica's coast is at about 66°S. So -59 is in the basin south of the ridge, where depths are typically 4000-4500 m.

Near 127°E, the Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies around 120-128°E and 49°S, which is farther north. At 59°S the seafloor is probably about 4,200 m deep. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

10189.text:
Latitude -23, longitude 39 lies in the Mozambique Channel, east of Mozambique's coast (which is around 35°E at this latitude) and west of southern Madagascar (~44°E). It is open ocean, in the Mozambique Basin, so the seabed should be deep.

Near 23°S, 39°E the depth is roughly 2,500-3,000 m. The Mozambique Channel's deeper basin lies east of the continental margin, and the Mozambique coast at Inhambane sits at about 35.4°E. At 39°E, about 350 km offshore, the seabed is probably on the Mozambique Basin plain or the lower slope. The Mozambique Basin floor reaches about 3,000-5,000 m further south. I estimate about -3,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

8508.text:
The point (-5.0, -83.0) lies in the Pacific Ocean, roughly 600 km west of Peru's coast near Paita (about -5, -81). The Peru-Chile Trench runs close to the coast, so the position is west of the trench, on the Nazca Plate. The trench axis near 5°S is at about 81.5°W, where depths reach about 5,000-6,000 m. At 83°W the seafloor is probably on the outer trench slope or the abyssal plain, at around 4,000-4,500 m depth.

I estimate the depth at about 4,200 m, so the elevation is about -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

12872.text:
Location: 53°S, 5°E is in the South Atlantic, southwest of Bouvet Island (54.4°S, 3.4°E) and near the Bouvet Triple Junction. The area lies around the Southwest Indian Ridge / Mid-Atlantic Ridge near the American-Antarctic Ridge. It is about 1.5° latitude north of Bouvet and 1.6° east of it, so it is close to the Bouvet plateau area.

Depth there is likely around 2500–3500 m. The Spiess Ridge and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 53°S, 0–5°E have depths of about 2500–3000 m. The Bouvet Triple Junction is near 54.7°S, 1.5°E. At 53°S, 5°E, we are east of the junction on the Southwest Indian Ridge's western end, which has depths of ~3000 m. I'll go with -3000.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

6127.text:
The point 21.0°N, 165.0°W lies in the north Pacific, northwest of Kauai (about 22°N, 159.5°W) and well west of the main Hawaiian Islands. It is south of the Hawaiian Ridge's northwestern portion, which runs through Nihoa (23°N, 161.9°W) and Necker (23.6°N, 164.7°W). At 21°N the area is on the ocean floor south of the ridge. It is probably in the Hawaiian Deep or moat region, with typical depths of about 4,700 to 5,000 m. The seafloor in the open Pacific south of the ridge near 165°W is around 5,000 m deep.

I estimate a depth of about 4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

1199.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 59.0°E lies in the Novaya Zemlya region. The archipelago stretches from about 70°N to 77°N, and its northern tip, Cape Zhelaniya, is at about 76.95°N, 68.6°E. At 59°E and 77°N, the point is therefore west of the northern island, in the Barents Sea.

Novaya Zemlya's western coast at 76°N is around 62°E, and at 77°N the coast runs near 63–64°E, so 59°E is well offshore. The Barents Sea there is fairly shallow, probably about 150–250 m deep. The area east of Franz Josef Land and north of Novaya Zemlya is generally about 200 m deep. I'll estimate -200 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -200}

14086.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -87 is in the Bellingshausen Sea, off the coast of Antarctica. The Antarctic Peninsula and Alexander Island lie around 70°W and 68°S. At longitude -87 the coast is further south, around 72°S, so this point is open ocean over the continental rise or abyssal plain.

The Bellingshausen abyssal plain is roughly 4000-4500 m deep. At 67°S, 87°W the depth is probably around 4,500 m, since the continental slope begins further south. I'll estimate -4,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

12154.text:
The point at 45°S, 9°E lies in the South Atlantic Ocean, southwest of southern Africa and north of the Bouvet Island region. It is probably near the Agulhas Ridge or the Mid-Atlantic Ridge area, though I haven't checked this against a bathymetric chart.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 45°S sits at roughly 15°W to 5°W, so 9°E is east of the ridge. The Agulhas Basin and the Cape Basin lie in this area, with depths around 4500–5000 m. The Agulhas Ridge is at about 40–45°S, 10–15°E. The Agulhas-Falkland Fracture Zone runs from about 40°S, 15°E toward the southwest. At 45°S, 9°E the point is likely in the Cape Basin's southern part or near the southern flank of the Agulhas Ridge. I'll estimate the seabed at about -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

10271.text:
Location: -25, -157 is in the South Pacific, southeast of the Cook Islands (Rarotonga is at -21.2, -159.8) and northwest of the Austral Islands' Rapa area. Tubuai is at -23.4, -149.5. So this point is open ocean in the Austral Islands region, west of Tubuai and south of Rarotonga.

The seafloor depth in this area is probably around 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Austral-Cook chain runs northwest to southeast, and -25, -157 is roughly in the abyssal plain. The ocean there is about 4,400 to 4,900 m deep, and the seafloor age is around 70 to 80 Myr. I'll estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

3519.text:
The coordinates 51.0°N, 19.0°E fall in central Poland, in the Łódź Voivodeship near Radomsko and Bełchatów. The terrain there is the Polish Uplands, with elevations of about 200 to 250 m. Bełchatów is at about 51.37°N, 19.36°E, so this point lies southwest of it, near Kamieńsk and Radomsko. Radomsko sits at about 51.07°N, 19.44°E, at roughly 200 m. Moving west to 19.0°E, the point is near Kamieńsk or Przyrów, which is around 51.0°N 19.0°E.

This is an estimate from recalled place elevations, not a looked-up value, and I put it at about 230 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":230}

5025.text:
The point (35.0°N, 151.0°E) lies in the Pacific Ocean, east of Japan and southeast of Honshu. Japan's coast near Choshi is at about 140.8°E, so this location is well offshore.

The area is probably on the abyssal plain of the northwest Pacific, south of the Japan Trench and east of the Izu-Bonin Trench. Seabed depths there are typically about 5,500-6,000 m. The Shatsky Rise is farther northeast, at roughly 158°E and 33°N, so it does not apply here. The Mariana Trench and its outer rise lie farther south, and the Mariana trench system is at about 143°E. Around 151°E, 35°N the seafloor is probably about 5,800 m deep, so I estimate -5,800 m. This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

13625.text:
Latitude -61, longitude 71 is in the Southern Ocean, southwest of the Kerguelen Plateau and north of the Antarctic continental margin. The Kerguelen Plateau lies at roughly 50°S, and Heard Island at 53°S, 73.5°E. At 61°S, 71°E the seafloor is probably part of the southern Kerguelen Plateau or the deep basin to its south. The southern Kerguelen Plateau extends to about 59-60°S near 75-80°E. At 61°S, 71°E I think it is the Australian-Antarctic Basin's western edge or the Enderby Basin, which would put the depth at around 3500-4000 m.

I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2162.text:
Latitude 65.0 N, longitude -175.0 (175 W) falls in the northern Bering Sea, south of the Bering Strait. Chukotka's coast sits near 64-65 N at about 177-180 W, and Anadyr Gulf lies at roughly 64 N, 178 W. At 175 W, 65 N, we are east of the Gulf of Anadyr and west of St. Lawrence Island, which is at about 63.4 N, 170 W. So this is open water in the northern Bering Sea.

The shelf there is shallow, around 40-50 m deep. Near 65 N, 175 W the depth is probably about 50 m, so I'll estimate -50.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

9526.text:
Latitude -15, longitude 153 lies in the Coral Sea, east of Australia's Cape York and north of the Queensland Plateau. It is probably east of the Great Barrier Reef, in the Coral Sea basin, which is roughly 3,500 to 4,500 m deep. The Coral Sea basin floor near 15°S, 153°E is about 4,000 m deep. The Queensland Plateau sits around 14-16°S, 144-152°E, and the Osprey/Bougainville plateaus are near 147-150°E. At 153°E the area is in the deep basin, and the Louisiade/Coral Sea abyssal plain is about 4,000 m deep.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

6979.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude 99.0 is in Myanmar/Thailand border area. Around 99°E, 13°N: Prachuap Khiri Khan province, Thailand, near the Myanmar border. The Gulf of Thailand coast is at about 99.8°E at this latitude, so 99.0°E is inland, in the Tenasserim Hills along the border. The Andaman coast is at about 98.5°E at 13°N, so this point is land, in the mountains.

Elevation is probably several hundred meters. The border ridge near 99.0°E, 13.0°N is in the Thailand–Myanmar border area (Kanchanaburi/Suan Phueng). At 13.0°N the Thai side is Sai Yok/Thong Pha Phum. I'd estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

6044.text:
The point 23.0°N, 29.0°E is in the Eastern Sahara of southwestern Egypt, in the New Valley Governorate. It lies north of the Uweinat/Gilf Kebir area and in the general vicinity of the Abu Ballas region. Elevations in this region are around 200–600 m. Toshka and Kharga are farther east, and the Dakhla oasis (25.5°N, 29°E) sits at about 120 m, but the plateau to the south near 23°N is higher. Much of the Selima Sand Sheet and the plains around Bir Tarfawi (22.9°N, 28.7°E) are at roughly 250 m.

Bir Tarfawi is at about 22.95°N, 28.75°E with an elevation around 250 m, so the point at 29.0°E should be similar, perhaps 230–260 m. I'll estimate 245 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":245}

15572.text:
Latitude -83, longitude 5°E is in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land, south of the Shackleton Range and Recovery Glacier area. The Shackleton Range lies near 80-81°S, 25°W. At 83°S, 5°E, the interior ice sheet near the Pole of Inaccessibility direction is roughly 2,500-3,000 m in elevation.

The South Pole is at about 2,835 m. At 83°S, 5°E, the Antarctic plateau in this sector sits near 2,800 m. Examples: Dome Fuji is at 77°S, 39°E with an elevation of 3,800 m. Near the Theron Mountains, at 80°S, 28°W, the elevation is around 1,000 m. For 83°S, 5°E, the area sits on the flank of the plateau toward the Filchner area, with ice flowing toward the Recovery and Slessor glaciers. I estimate about 2,500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

4353.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude -113.0 is in northwestern Utah, in the Great Salt Lake Desert west of the Great Salt Lake. This is near the Utah-Nevada border region (the border is at -114), so the point is in Box Elder County. The area just south of Interstate 80 (which runs near 41.0 at Wendover, -114) is salt flats at about 1280 m. The Newfoundland Mountains lie around -113.2 and the Great Salt Lake Desert at about 1286 m. At -113.0, 41.0 is probably the Great Salt Lake Desert near the Lakeside Mountains or Hogup Mountains. The Hogup Mountains are around 41.4, -113.0, so 41.0 is likely the flats north of the Cedar Mountains, with an elevation of about 1290 m.

The Great Salt Lake's western shore lies at about -112.9 at 41.0, but the lake's southern part is south of 41.0 and Gunnison Island is at 41.3. Terrain at 41.0, -113.0 is therefore likely the salt flats just west of the lake, at about 1285 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1288}

10555.text:
Latitude -27, longitude 51 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and well east of southern Africa. The Madagascar Basin lies in this area, with typical depths of about 4500-5500 m. The Southwest Indian Ridge is farther south, and the Mascarene Plateau is to the northeast, so neither is nearby.

At about 27°S, 51°E the seabed is probably abyssal plain, near 5000 m deep. The Madagascar Basin's deepest parts are around 5000-5500 m. I'll estimate -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

2840.text:
The coordinates 59.0°N, 101.0°E fall in central Siberia, in Krasnoyarsk Krai on the Central Siberian Plateau. The area lies near the Podkamennaya Tunguska basin, east of the Yenisei, where the Stony Tunguska river runs at about 61.6°N, so 59°N is south of it. The Angara river is near 58°N at 98–102°E, so this point is just north of the Angara. The Angara flows through Boguchany at about 58.4°N, 97.4°E. At 101°E the river lies around 58°N, near Kodinsk at 99°E, so the point is on land.

The terrain there is plateau, roughly 300–500 m. I estimate about 400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

4148.text:
The point 43°N, 163°W is in the North Pacific, south of the Aleutian Islands and the Alaska Peninsula. The Aleutian Trench lies to the north and the abyssal plain to the south.

At 43°N the seafloor is part of the northwest Pacific basin, and the Aleutian Trench runs along roughly 50-54°N at this longitude. This point is therefore on the abyssal plain, with typical depths of about 5,000 to 5,500 m. The Patton-Murray seamounts lie farther east, in the Gulf of Alaska. Surrounding depths at this latitude near 163°W are about 5,300 m, so I estimate -5300 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5300}

9251.text:
The point is at -13, -37, which lies in the Atlantic Ocean off the coast of Brazil. The Brazilian coast near Salvador is at about -13, -38.5, so -37 is roughly 150 km offshore, east of the coast. The continental shelf there is narrow, and the shelf break is around 30-50 km offshore, so at 150 km the seabed is on the continental slope.

The Sergipe-Alagoas and Bahia margin is steep. Longitude -37 at latitude -13 should be about 2000-3000 m deep. The Bahia coast at -13 has a narrow shelf and the slope reaches 2000 m by about 100 km out. I estimate about -2500 m at this point, on the slope or near the base of the slope.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2600}

443.text:
Latitude 85°N, longitude 13°W lies in the Arctic Ocean north of Greenland. Greenland's northern tip (Kaffeklubben Island) is at about 83.6°N, so 85°N is roughly 160 km further north, over the ocean.

The seabed there is probably near the Lomonosov Ridge or the Morris Jesup Rise. The Morris Jesup Rise lies around 85°N, 10°W to 0°, with depths of about 1000 to 1500 m. The Lomonosov Ridge is nearer to 140°E and 40°W at the pole. At 85°N, 13°W, the area north of Greenland lies in the Lena Trough/Greenland shelf transition. The shelf extends to about 84°N, and beyond it the seabed drops into the Amundsen Basin edge at around 3000–4000 m. The Morris Jesup Rise extends from Greenland's shelf at about 85°N, 10°W to 80°W and has depths of about 1000–1500 m. I'll estimate around -2000 m for the location, which is a guess at the rise's flank.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

15761.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 23 is in East Antarctica, near the Pensacola Mountains and Recovery Glacier region. The Pensacola Mountains lie around longitude -55 to -65, so that is not a match. At longitude 23 and latitude -85, the point is on the polar plateau, south of Queen Maud Land and the Shackleton Range (which is around -80.5, -25). The ice surface there is probably around 2500-3000 m.

Dome A is at -80, 77 with a surface around 4000 m. At -85, 23, the surface is roughly 2700 m. This is a grounded ice area, so the type is Solid. I estimate about 2800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2800}

5764.text:
The point 25.0°N, 171.0°W is in the North Pacific, northwest of the Hawaiian Islands. Kure Atoll is at about 28.4°N, 178.3°W and Midway at 28.2°N, 177.4°W, so neither is close. The location lies near Pioneer Bank and Gardner Pinnacles, but those are farther west, around 25.0°N, 168°W. Between Necker and French Frigate Shoals the seafloor is about 3,000 to 4,500 m deep. South of the ridge, the abyssal plain lies near 5,000 m.

The Hawaiian Ridge trends northwest. Near 25°N it sits at about 167°W (Gardner Pinnacles is at 25.0°N, 167.98°W), and at 171°W the ridge would be at roughly 27°N (Pearl and Hermes is at 27.9°N, 175.8°W; Laysan is at 25.8°N, 171.7°W). Laysan Island at 25.77°N, 171.73°W is therefore close to this point, which sits about 80 km south-southeast of it. Laysan's flanks fall off quickly, and the seafloor south of the island is probably around 3,000 to 4,000 m deep. I estimate about -3,800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

10126.text:
The point at -23.0, -87.0 is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile and east of the East Pacific Rise. It lies roughly 1,500 km west of Easter Island's longitude (-109), no wait, Easter Island is at -109.4, so -87 is east of it. The point is therefore between Easter Island and the Chilean coast, on the Nazca Plate.

The Nazca Ridge and the Sala y Gómez Ridge lie further north and east, but near -23, -87 the seafloor is probably abyssal plain or the flank of the Easter Seamount chain. The Chile Basin is about 4,000 m deep, and the region west of the Peru-Chile Trench at 23°S, about 1,500 km offshore, is typically 3,800–4,200 m deep. The seafloor there is a broad area around 3,900 m deep. I estimate -3,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

10486.text:
The point at -27, -87 lies in the southeastern Pacific Ocean. It is west of Easter Island (about -27.1, -109.4), so it is far from any land, and east of the Easter Island/Salas y Gómez chain. The location sits near the Nazca Ridge region, and I think it is close to the Chile Rise or the Nazca plate's eastern side.

Seafloor depth on the Nazca plate at this longitude is probably around 3500-4000 m. The East Pacific Rise is at about -110 longitude. The Nazca plate has ages of about 30 Myr near -87 and -27, and the Chile Trench is at about -71. For that age, the expected depth is around 3800-4000 m. The area also lies near the Nazca Ridge at around -20 and -80, so it is off to the south. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14225.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -169 is in the Southern Ocean, north of the Antarctic coast. The Ross Sea region is further south, and Cape Adare sits near 170E. At -169 longitude (169W), the Pacific sector, the Antarctic coast lies around -74 to -78 latitude, so this point is open ocean.

The seafloor there is part of the deep ocean basin, north of the continental slope. The Pacific-Antarctic basin near 69S, 169W is roughly 4,500 m deep. The continental rise and Ross Sea margin are near 170W to 180, with the slope at about -66 to -70. The Ross Sea continental shelf edge is around -72 to -73 at 180, so at -69 the seafloor is likely abyssal, about -4,200 m, perhaps shallower with the Northern Basin and Iselin Bank nearby. I'll go with -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

6302.text:
Location: 19°N, 175°W is in the central North Pacific, roughly north of Tonga and east of the Hawaiian-Emperor chain. It is open ocean, with no islands nearby, and the seafloor there is abyssal plain at about 5000-5500 m depth.

The area lies southeast of Midway and well south of the Hawaiian chain, near the Line Islands/Central Pacific basin. Typical depths for this region are about 5300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

10136.text:
Latitude -23.0, longitude -67.0 is in northern Chile/Argentina border region, in the Puna de Atacama. It lies just south of the Salar de Atacama region, east of the salar. Salar de Atacama is at about -23.5, -68.25. At -67.0 we are in Argentina, near the Salar de Arizaro (-24.7, -67.75) and the Salar de Pocitos (-24.5, -67). At -23, -67, this is in the Puna of Jujuy/Salta, south of Salinas Grandes (-23.6, -65.9). Elevation there is likely around 3800-4000 m.

Longitude -67 at latitude -23 is west of Susques (-66.5, -23.4), where elevation is about 3600 m. Further west, towards the Chile border (-67.2 is the border near Paso de Jama at -23.0, -67.0?). Paso de Jama is at about -22.85, -67.02, with an elevation of 4200 m. So the elevation here is around 4300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":4250}

12071.text:
The point is at 45°S, 157°W, in the South Pacific Ocean southeast of New Zealand and southwest of Tahiti. No land is nearby, so the surface type is Water.

I estimated the depth from memory of the regional bathymetry. This area lies in the southern Pacific basin, north of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge and south of the Austral Islands. The seafloor age there is moderate, and typical depths are around 4,500 to 5,200 m. I'm taking about -4,900 m, which is an estimate and not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

5513.text:
Latitude 29.0, longitude 47.0 falls in Kuwait, in the desert southwest of Kuwait City. It is near the Saudi border and the Wadi al-Batin area, around 29.0N 47.0E. The Kuwait coast is at about 29.3N 48E, so this point is inland. Elevation there is likely about 100 m. Rough check: Kuwait's highest point is about 306 m in the west at around 29.1N, 46.6E. Near 47.0E the ground is around 150 m. Al-Abdali is at 30N, 47.7E. Kuwait's Ahmadi Ridge is about 130 m. Near Burgan, at 29.0N 47.9E, elevation is about 100 m. At 47.0E, 29.0N, elevation is probably around 190 m.

I'll estimate 180 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":180}

14761.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -177 is in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica, within the Ross Ice Shelf. The Ross Ice Shelf front is around 78°S at this longitude, so -75 is open Ross Sea, north of the ice shelf edge. At 177°W, 75°S the sea is the eastern Ross Sea, near the Hays Mountains and Marie Byrd Land coast, which lies farther east (around 160°W). So this point is open water with a seabed depth of roughly 500-600 m.

The eastern Ross Sea shelf is typically 500-600 m deep, and the Ross Ice Shelf front at 177°W lies near 78.5°S. Bathymetry at 75°S, 177°W is probably on the Ross Sea continental shelf, close to the Central Basin, with depths around 550 m. The Central Basin is at about 75-76°S, 180°, with depths up to around 800 m. I'll go with -560 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

15487.text:
Latitude -83, longitude -165 is in Antarctica, on the Ross Ice Shelf. Its eastern part lies around 160-150°W, and at -165° the point is within the Ross Ice Shelf, south of Roosevelt Island (which is near 161°W, 79.5°S). At 83°S the shelf is still floating, since the grounding line sits near 84-85°S, where the Transantarctic Mountains' glaciers enter. Longitude -165 at 83°S is toward the middle of the shelf, away from the mountains at about 170°E-180°.

Because the ice is floating, the answer is Water, and the value to report is the seabed depth. Seabed beneath the Ross Ice Shelf at this location is roughly -600 m, based on the Bedmap data, where the southern Ross embayment is about 500-700 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

7196.text:
Location: 11°N, 173°E is in the western Pacific, in the Marshall Islands region. The Marshall Islands span roughly 4.5–14.5°N and 160–173°E, so 173°E is near the eastern edge. Ebon Atoll is at about 4.6°N, 168.7°E. Mili is near 6°N, 171.9°E. Knox Atoll is near 5.9°N. At 11°N, 173°E, Bikini is at 11.6°N, 165.4°E and Utirik is at 11.2°N, 169.8°E. Taongi is at 14.6°N, 169°E. So 173°E at 11°N is east of the Marshall Islands and is open ocean in the Pacific.

The depth there is about 5000 m. The area lies between the Marshall Islands and the Gilbert Islands region, on Pacific plate seafloor of Cretaceous age, with typical depths of 5200 m or so.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5300}

14803.text:
Latitude -75, longitude -93 lies in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen Sea sector. The Thwaites and Pine Island glaciers are near -75 to -78 latitude and -100 to -107 longitude. At -93 longitude the area is offshore of the Abbot Ice Shelf, which spans roughly -95 to -100 longitude at about -73 latitude. Further east, around -90 longitude and -73 latitude, is the Eights Coast, and Thurston Island sits at about -72 latitude and -100 longitude.

At -75 latitude and -93 longitude, I think this is the Bellingshausen Sea sector, south of Thurston Island, where the coast is around -73. At this position the surface is probably grounded ice of the Ellsworth/Hudson Mountains area. The Hudson Mountains are at about -74.5 latitude and -99 longitude, so -93 would be east of them, possibly on the ice sheet in the Pine Island and Thwaites basin margins. Surface elevation there is likely around 1000 to 1500 m. Part of the area may be the Abbot ice shelf region, but I'll go with grounded ice at about 1100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1100}

3203.text:
Latitude 55.0, longitude 107.0 is in Siberia, Russia. Lake Baikal's western shore lies near 104°E at this latitude and its southern tip is near 104–105°E, so 107°E is east of the lake. The point falls in Buryatia, between Ulan-Ude (107.6°E, 51.8°N) and the Barguzin area. At 55°N, 107°E it is probably in the highlands near the Barguzin Range or the Ikat Range, where elevations run roughly 900–1500 m.

Baikal's eastern shore at 55°N is near 109°E, and the Barguzin Valley is around 109–110°E. So 107°E is well inland from the lake, in the Selenga highlands or the Khamar-Daban/Ulan-Burgasy area. The terrain there is hilly, about 800–1000 m. I'll estimate 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

6732.text:
The point 15°N, 35°W lies in the central North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 15°N sits at about 45°W, so this location is east of it, not west. The ridge crest at 15°N is around 45°W, and the Atlantic basin floor to the east, toward 35°W, is the western flank of the North American plate's ridge region.

Between 45°W and 35°W the seafloor is on the ridge's eastern flank, where depths run from about 3000 m near the crest to about 5000 m farther out. At 35°W, roughly 10° east of the axis, the crust is about 20 Myr old at a spreading rate of about 1.2 cm/yr per side. Depth for crust of that age is around 4500 to 5000 m. Near 15°N there is also the Fifteen-Twenty fracture zone, which would add variation. I estimate a depth of about -4800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

15329.text:
Location: -81°, -121° is in West Antarctica, in Marie Byrd Land near the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge. The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part extends to about -80 to -82 latitude at longitude -150 to -160. At -121° and -81°, this is inland, which is Marie Byrd Land, near the Hudson Mountains/Pine Island area? Pine Island Bay is at -75, -100. At -81, -121, this is in the interior of West Antarctica toward the Ellsworth Land/Marie Byrd Land boundary, roughly the Executive Committee Range area at -76, -126. Farther south at -81, -121 is likely the grounded ice of the West Antarctic Ice Sheet, near the Siple Coast's upstream part, with ice surface elevation around 1000-1500 m. I'll guess ice surface ~1200 m.

The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge is at the Edward VII Peninsula (-155). The Ross Embayment's grounded ice at -81, -121 is near the Ford Ranges/Thiel Mountains, and the surface is probably around 1000 m. The bed is likely below sea level, but the reported surface is for grounded ice.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1150}

7957.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude -105.0° is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of the Galápagos. This area lies near the East Pacific Rise's eastern flank, south of the Clipperton Fracture Zone and north of the equator.

The East Pacific Rise axis sits near 110°W at the equator, and the Galápagos spreading center lies at about 2°N near 100°W. At 105°W the seafloor is young, about 5-8 Myr old, so depths are around 3,000 m. Seafloor at 1°N, 105°W is probably near 2,900-3,200 m. I estimate about 3,000 m deep, but this is an estimate from regional knowledge and not a lookup of the actual value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

6107.text:
The point 23°N, 155°E is in the western Pacific, northeast of the Marianas and south of Marcus Island (Minami-Torishima, about 24.3°N, 154°E). This area is within the Western Pacific seamount province, near the Magellan and Wake seamounts, and it is likely in the Pigafetta Basin or among Mid-Pacific Mountains. Typical abyssal depths there are around 5,500–5,900 m, but seamounts are common and could make it shallower.

Without bathymetry data for this exact point, I'll go with the typical basin depth of about -5,700 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5700}

13331.text:
Location: -59, -157 is in the South Pacific Ocean, southwest of Cape Horn and far from land. This area lies on the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge flank or the abyssal plain. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge at around 60°S runs near 150°W to 170°W. At 59°S, 157°W the ridge crest may be about 56-65°S. The ridge axis near 150°W is at roughly 58-61°S, and near 160°W at about 63°S. So this point is probably on the ridge flank at a depth of about 3,500-4,000 m.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is fairly shallow, with its crest around 2,500-3,000 m. The Udintsev fracture zone is at about 55°S, 145°W. At 59°S, 157°W, the depth is probably about 3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

2055.text:
Latitude 67.0, longitude -29.0 is off the east coast of Greenland, in the Denmark Strait / Irminger Sea region. Kangerlussuaq is at about 68.5°N, -32°W, so 67°N, -29°W lies southeast of it. The Greenland coast near 67°N is around -33°W to -34°W (Skjoldungen is farther south), so -29° is about 4° of longitude east of the coast, roughly 150 km offshore. That puts it on the East Greenland shelf, or just at the shelf edge.

The shelf off Kangerlussuaq is wide, with troughs reaching 500 m depth. Near 67°N the shelf edge is around -30°W to -28°W, and the Denmark Strait sill lies to the northeast. The Kangerlussuaq Trough runs southeast across the shelf. At -29°W, 67°N the position is probably near the shelf break or upper slope, so I estimate a seabed depth of roughly -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

5605.text:
The point 27°N, 129°W lies in the open North Pacific, roughly 1,500 km west of Baja California and northeast of Hawaii. It is in deep ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor there is abyssal plain or abyssal hills on the Pacific plate. The crust is about 30 to 40 million years old, which suggests depths around 4,500 to 4,200 m. The Murray Fracture Zone lies near 30°N, and this location is south of it, where depths are typically about 4,300 to 4,600 m. I estimate about -4,400 m, which is an estimate from regional depth patterns, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

9398.text:
The point (-15, -103) lies in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of South America. It sits on or near the East Pacific Rise flank. The Rise crest is around 113°W at 15°S, so -103 is east of the crest, on the Nazca Plate. Seafloor depth there is likely about 3,000–3,800 m, with older crust at greater depth.

At 15°S, the East Pacific Rise crest is at about 114°W, roughly 11° of longitude to the west. Crust age at 103°W is about 11° × 111 km ≈ 1,200 km from the crest. With a fast spreading rate of about 14 cm/yr per side, that gives about 8–9 Myr. Depth is roughly 2,600 + 350·√8.5 ≈ 3,600 m. The nearby seafloor is probably about 3,700 m deep, so I estimate -3700.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

2121.text:
Latitude 67.0°N, longitude 103.0°E is in central Siberia, in the Putorana/Central Siberian Plateau region, north of the Arctic Circle. It lies south of the Anabar plateau and near the Kotuy River basin, on the Evenkia/Taymyr border. The Kotuy River runs around 71°N to the east, and the Moyero River is near 67°N, 103°E. Lake Essey and Lake Yessey are near 68°N, 102°E. The Moyero River flows at about 67.5°N, 103°E, east of the Putorana Plateau, on the Central Siberian Plateau. Terrain there is roughly 300-500 m.

I estimate the elevation at about 400 m, and I treat the point as land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":400}

2828.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 77.0°E lies in western Siberia, in Tomsk Oblast, in the Vasyugan swamp region. This is a flat, boggy area, and the elevation there is roughly 80-100 m. The nearby Ob River at about 59°N, 78°E is near 55 m. Since 77°E is slightly west of that, the terrain is probably around 85 m.

This is an estimate from regional knowledge, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":85}

12648.text:
The point is at 51°S, 83°W, in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. The Chile Rise and the Chile Trench lie further east and south. The Chile Trench is near 74-75°W at this latitude, so 83°W is well west of it, on the Nazca/Antarctic plate. The seafloor there is typically about 3500-4000 m deep.

The Chile Rise spreading center runs roughly from the Chile Triple Junction at 46°S, 75°W southwest to about 54°S, 115°W, and it has fracture zones. At 51°S the ridge crest would sit near 90°W or beyond, so 83°W is probably on the flank of the ridge, with depths of roughly 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2352.text:
The point 63.0°N, 155.0°W lies in interior Alaska, in the Kuskokwim Mountains region northwest of the Alaska Range. It is roughly 100 km west of Denali, in the area of the upper Kuskokwim and Big River. The terrain there is hilly to mountainous, and I'd estimate the elevation at about 400 m.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the area, not a lookup of the actual elevation data. Nearby features include Lake Minchumina at 63.9°N, 152.3°W, and McGrath at 62.95°N, 155.6°W, which sits at about 100 m. Since this point is only about 25 km east of McGrath, it is probably lowland or foothill terrain along the Kuskokwim, perhaps 150–300 m. I'll take 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

13811.text:
Latitude -63, longitude 83 is in the Southern Ocean south of the Indian Ocean sector, well north of the Antarctic coast, which sits near -66 to -67 at this longitude. The point lies south of the Southeast Indian Ridge, which is around -50 at this longitude, and north of the Kerguelen Plateau's southern part. The Princess Elizabeth Trough and the Australian-Antarctic Basin lie in this region, with depths around 4000 to 4500 m.

Near 63°S, 83°E, the seafloor is likely abyssal plain at about 4,000 m depth. The Kerguelen Plateau is farther west, around 80°E at -55 to -60, so this point is probably east of it and in the basin, at roughly -4,300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12389.text:
The point at -47, 119 is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. It lies on the Southeast Indian Ridge region, or perhaps just north of it. The Australian-Antarctic Basin lies south of the ridge, and the ridge near 119°E sits at about 50°S. At 47°S, 119°E the area is likely the flank of the ridge or the southern edge of the Diamantina/Naturaliste Plateau region. The Naturaliste Plateau is at about 34°S, so it is not relevant here.

The Southeast Indian Ridge crest near 120°E is at about 49–50°S, with depths of about 3000 m. At 47°S the seafloor would be on the northern flank at roughly 3500–4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

2221.text:
Latitude 65.0, longitude -57.0 is in Davis Strait, west of Greenland. Greenland's west coast at 65N is around -53 W (Nuuk is at 64.2N, -51.7W). Baffin Island lies at about -65 W at 65N (Cumberland Peninsula, around 65.5N, -65). So -57 W at 65N is in Davis Strait, which is open water.

Depth: the Davis Strait sill is at about 66N and is roughly 600 m deep. At 65N, -57 W, the depth is probably about 1500-2000 m, with Labrador Sea basin deepening to the south. The Greenland shelf extends to about -55 W at this latitude. At -57 W it is probably on the slope, so I estimate about -1000 m. Baffin slope depths at 65N are around 1000-2000 m, and the central Davis Strait is around 2000 m at 64N. I'll say -1500.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

5547.text:
The coordinates 29.0°N, 115.0°E fall in Jiangxi province, China. Poyang Lake lies roughly between 28.4–29.7°N and 115.8–116.7°E, so 115.0°E is west of the lake. The point is near Yongxiu or Xiushui county, around Wuning, in hilly terrain.

The Jiuling Mountains run through this area. Yongxiu is near 29.0°N, 115.8°E, and Xiushui is near 29.0°N, 114.5°E. At 115.0°E the point is probably around Jing'an or Anyi, near Wuning, where the Xiushui river valley and hills lie. I estimate the elevation at about 60 m, though it could be hilly and higher. Since this is a rough guess, I'll use 80 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":80}

968.text:
Location: 79°N, 43°W is in northeast Greenland, on the Greenland Ice Sheet. The northeast Greenland ice stream begins around 77°N, 35°W. At 79°N, 43°W the point is inland on the ice sheet, and ice surface elevation there is likely about 2500 m.

Summit is at 72.5°N, 38°W at 3200 m. At 79°N, 43°W the surface is probably around 2400–2600 m. I'll go with 2450.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2450}

11307.text:
Latitude -35.0, longitude 115.0 is in the Indian Ocean, southwest of Western Australia. Cape Leeuwin is at about 115.14°E, 34.37°S, so this point is about 0.6° south of that latitude, which puts it offshore and south of the cape.

The seabed there lies off the continental shelf, south of Cape Leeuwin and west of Albany. The shelf edge is around 34.5°S at 115°E. At 35°S the water is probably on the continental slope, with a depth of roughly 2000 m. The Naturaliste Plateau lies to the west, around 112–115°E and 34–35°S, and it is relatively shallow at about 2000–3000 m. At 115°E the seabed is likely about 1500–2500 m deep, so I'll estimate −2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

14418.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -143 is in the Ross Sea sector of West Antarctica, near the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge. The Ross Ice Shelf extends from about 158°W to the east edge near 150°W or so, and Edward VII Peninsula is around 155°W, 77°S. At -71°S, -143°W, that is offshore in the Amundsen Sea/Marie Byrd Land coast, which is roughly at 76°S for this longitude. So 71°S is open ocean on the continental shelf margin or slope.

The Marie Byrd Land coast at 143°W lies near 77°S, so 71°S is about 600 km north, which puts it in deep ocean beyond the shelf break. The depth there is likely around 3,500 to 4,500 m, on the abyssal plain or the lower slope. The Amundsen Sea abyssal plain is about 4,500 m deep, though the Marie Byrd Seamounts lie further north. I estimate about -4,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

6415.text:
Latitude 19.0, longitude 51.0 is in the Arabian Sea, off the coast of Oman/Yemen. The Oman coast near Salalah is around 17°N, 54°E, and the coast runs northeast from there. At 51°E, the Yemen/Oman border region (Al Mahrah) has its coast near 15.5°N. So 19°N, 51°E is inland, in the Empty Quarter (Rub' al Khali) region of Saudi Arabia/Yemen/Oman, with the border area around 19°N, 52°E.

That area is the southern Rub' al Khali, with elevations of about 500 m. Near 19°N, 51°E, I estimate roughly 600 m (the dunes there sit at about 500–700 m).

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}

8936.text:
Latitude -9, longitude 53 is in the western Indian Ocean, east of Africa. It lies southeast of the Seychelles (about -4.6, 55.5) and northeast of Madagascar, near the Mascarene Plateau region. The Mascarene Plateau runs from the Seychelles south toward Mauritius at around longitude 55-60, so -9, 53 is west of it. The Amirantes and Farquhar areas are near -10, 51. The Farquhar group is at about -10.1, 51.1, and Alphonse is at -7, 52.7. The Alphonse/Amirantes plateau is shallow, but -9, 53 is probably in the basin between the Amirantes Ridge and the Mascarene Plateau, called the Seychelles-Mascarene basin, with depths around 4000-4500 m.

The Amirantes ridge/trough lies to the southwest. At 53°E, -9°, the seabed is likely about 4,000 m deep. I'll estimate -4,300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

10438.text:
The point (-25, 177) lies in the South Pacific, east of New Zealand's north and south of Fiji. It is near the Kermadec Ridge region, but a bit west of the Kermadec Trench. The Kermadec Ridge runs roughly along 178-179°W at 25°S, and the Colville and Kermadec ridges lie further south. At 177°E, 25°S, the South Fiji Basin is to the west, with depths of about 4500-5000 m. The Lau-Colville Ridge is near 178-179°W, so 177°E sits west of it in the South Fiji Basin, where the seabed is around 4800 m deep. Minerva Reefs are at about 23.6°S, 178.9°W, so they are not relevant here.

I estimate the depth at about -4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

8795.text:
Latitude -7.0, longitude 131.0 falls in the Banda Sea / Arafura Sea region. The Tanimbar Islands lie around 131.0°E, 7.5°S, and Yamdena is at roughly 131.3°E, -7.5°S. The point at -7.0, 131.0 is probably northwest of Yamdena and north of the island's western coast, so it is likely water. The Aru Islands are further east, near 134°E. The sea between Tanimbar and Kai is the Banda Sea margin, and the Tanimbar Trough lies to the west of Tanimbar at about 131°E, where depths reach 3000 m or more.

To the north, the Kai Islands sit around 132.5°E, -5.5°S. At -7.0, 131.0, the point is probably on the Tanimbar slope or in the Banda Sea basin, with a depth of about 2000 m. I'll estimate the seabed at about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

