Latitude -57, longitude 31 lies in the Southern Ocean, southeast of Africa. It is south of the Atlantic-Indian sector, near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Enderby Basin. The Conrad Rise is further east, around 50°S and 40-50°E. At 57°S, 31°E, the area is probably part of the Weddell-Enderby Basin or the Atlantic-Indian Basin, where the abyssal plain is about 4000-5000 m deep. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs from the Bouvet triple junction (54°S, 0°) northeastward to the Prince Edward area at about 40°S, 35°E. The Bouvet junction is at 55°S, 1°W, so at 31°E the ridge is much further north, around 50°S.

The Southwest Indian Ridge at 31°E is around 52°S. So 57°S is south of the ridge, on the Antarctic plate flank, where depths are roughly 3500-4500 m. I'll estimate -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

2258.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude 17.0°E is in northern Sweden, in Västerbotten/Norrbotten near the Norwegian border. The Norwegian border at this latitude lies around 14°E, so 17°E is inland. The area is near Storuman or Tärnaby, which sits at about 65.7°N, 15.3°E. Storuman is at about 65.1°N, 17.1°E, and it sits on Lake Storuman, whose surface is at about 345 m.

The point could fall on the lake, since Storuman town lies on the lake's shore at roughly 17.1°E. At 65.0°N, 17.0°E, the point is probably just south of the town. Lake Storuman stretches from Storuman town to the northwest and southeast, and it is long and narrow. Land around it is about 350–400 m. I'll treat the point as land at about 380 m.

