The Surveyor Fan and abyssal plain lie in this region. The Pacific plate here is mostly abyssal plain at depths of about 3,000 to 3,500 m. The Juan de Fuca and Explorer ridges lie further east, around 130°W. Near 49°N, 141°W the seafloor is probably abyssal plain, with depths of about 3,300 to 3,700 m. I'm estimating from the regional pattern rather than from a specific bathymetry value, so I'll go with about 3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

1582.text:
Latitude 73.0, longitude 105.0 E is in the Laptev Sea region, near the Taymyr Peninsula's eastern coast. The Khatanga Gulf lies around 73-75N, 105-112E. The Taymyr coast near 104E is at about 76N, and Khatanga Gulf's mouth is near 73.5N, 110E. The Khatanga town is at 71.9N, 102.5E. At 73N, 105E, the land is on the west side of Khatanga Gulf, and the gulf's western shore runs about 73.5N at 107E. The gulf is roughly 100 km wide, from the Taymyr coast at about 74N to the Anabar side at 72N around 108E.

At 105E, the gulf's south shore is near Khatanga (71.9N, 102.5E) and the coast runs northeast, so at 105E the south shore is about 72.5N. The north shore at 105E is about 74N (Nordvik is at 74.0N, 111.5E). So 73N, 105E is likely in the Khatanga Gulf, which is water with a depth of perhaps 10-20 m. The gulf near Khatanga is shallow, so I'll estimate about -12 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-12}

14177.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 95E is off the coast of East Antarctica, near the Shackleton Ice Shelf region (which sits around 96-101E, at about -66.5). At 95E, -67, this is probably near the coast, west of the Shackleton Ice Shelf, in the Davis Sea / Queen Mary Land area. The coast near 95E is at roughly -66.5, and the Denman Glacier is at about 99E, -66.8.

At -67 and 95E the point is likely just inland of the coast, on grounded ice at about 1000-2000 m. The coastline in Queen Mary Land near the Gaussberg is at 89E, -66.8. Around 95E, the coast is near -66.4, with ice cliffs. The point at -67 is therefore about 30 km inland, where the ice surface is perhaps 500-1000 m. I'll go with Solid at about 900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

8935.text:
Latitude -9, longitude 51 is in the western Indian Ocean, east of Tanzania and northeast of Madagascar. It lies near the Seychelles Plateau to the north (Mahé is at about -4.6, 55.5) and the Amirante region to the southwest of that. The Mascarene Plateau runs from the Seychelles south toward Saya de Malha, but at 51°E it is well west of the plateau. The nearby Alphonse and Farquhar islands sit around 52–51°E and -7 to -10. Farquhar Atoll is at about -10.1, 51.1, and Providence Atoll is at about -9.2, 51.0. So this point is very close to Providence Atoll, which belongs to the Farquhar Group.

Providence Bank is a shallow area, but the point could fall on the atoll's reef or lagoon, or on the steep slope just outside it. Providence Atoll is small, about 2 km across, so the point is probably near it rather than on it. The surrounding seabed is likely deep, roughly 1000 to 3000 m, since the atoll sits on a steep seamount. Because the point is only about 0.2° from the atoll, I'll guess a mid-slope depth of about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

12125.text:
Location: -45, -49 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, east of Argentina and southwest of the Zapiola Rise area. The Argentine Basin there has depths of about 5000-5500 m. The Zapiola Rise sits near 45°S, 45°W with depths of about 4000-4500 m. At 49°W, the point is on the western flank of the rise, at about 5000 m depth.

The Argentine Basin floor is around 5000 m, and the Zapiola Rise crest is around 3500-4000 m. At 45°S, 49°W, I estimate about -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

15689.text:
Location: -85°, -121° is in West Antarctica, on the Ross Ice Shelf side or the grounded ice near the Transantarctic Mountains? Longitude -121 (121°W) at 85°S: this is in Marie Byrd Land interior, near the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern edge. The Ross Ice Shelf's grounding line in the Siple Coast area is around 150°W to 160°W at 82-85°S. At 121°W, 85°S, it is on the grounded West Antarctic Ice Sheet, near the divide toward the Pine Island/Thwaites region. The ice surface there is about 1,500-2,000 m. Ice surface elevation near the Whitmore Mountains (-105°, -82.5°) is about 1,500 m. At 85°S, 121°W, the surface is probably about 1,800 m. The ice divide in this region is at about 2,000 m or more.

I'll estimate 1,900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

3843.text:
The point 47.0°N, 53.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, southeast of Newfoundland. The Avalon Peninsula's easternmost point, Cape Race, is near 46.65°N, 53.07°W. The Avalon extends east to about 52.6°W at Cape St. Francis (47.8°N). At 47.0°N the coast sits near 52.7°W (around Cape Spear at 47.5°N, 52.6°W). Since 53.0°W at 47.0°N is west of that, I need to check whether it falls on land or in St. Mary's Bay or Placentia Bay.

Placentia Bay's eastern shore is the Avalon Peninsula's west side. The Avalon lies between roughly 52.7°W and 53.9°W at 47.0°N. The head of St. Mary's Bay is around 53.6°W, 47.0°N. The Cape Shore runs from Cape St. Mary's (46.83°N, 54.2°W) to Placentia (47.25°N, 53.97°W). So 53.0°W at 47.0°N is inland on the Avalon, in the area south of Holyrood and Conception Bay. Conception Bay's southern end is at about 47.4°N. Trepassey is at 46.74°N, 53.37°W. Around 47.0°N, 53.0°W is inland barrens near Salmonier or Holyrood Pond, with elevation about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

2155.text:
Latitude 67.0°N, longitude 171.0°E is in the East Siberian region of Chukotka. Longitude 171°E at 67°N is north of the Anadyr Gulf and inland, in the Chukotka Autonomous Okrug. Wrangel Island sits at about 71°N and 179°W, so it is not nearby. The Chaunskaya Bay is around 170°E, 69.5°N. At 67°N, 171°E the area is inland, near the upper Anadyr plateau and the Chukotka mountains, in the Anyuy/Omolon region. The Anadyr River is at about 66°N, 168°E.

The terrain here is probably upland, with elevations of about 400 to 600 m. I'll estimate 500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":500}

7388.text:
