5514.text:
Latitude 29.0°N, longitude 49.0°E is in the northern Persian Gulf. The Kuwait coast sits near 29.3°N, 48.0°E, and Bushehr is at about 28.9°N, 50.8°E. The Iranian coast near Bandar Mahshahr is around 30.5°N, 49.2°E. Khark Island lies near 29.25°N, 50.3°E.

At 29°N, 49°E the coast is well to the west and north, since Kuwait's Khiran coast is at about 28.7°N, 48.4°E. So this point is in the Gulf, probably 60 km or more from the nearest shore, off the Saudi–Kuwait waters. Water depths in this part of the Gulf are typically 20–40 m. Northwest of Khark the depth is about 20–30 m, so I estimate about -25 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-25}

1239.text:
Latitude 77.0 N, longitude 139.0 E is in the Laptev Sea region. The New Siberian Islands lie around 75-76 N, 137-150 E, and Kotelny Island is at about 75.5 N, 140 E. Bolshoy Lyakhovsky is at about 73 N, 142 E. The Laptev Sea shelf lies north of the Taymyr and Yakutia coast, and 77 N, 139 E is north of the Anisiy/Stolbovoy area. Land ends near 76 N at the northern tip of Kotelny (Cape Anisii, about 76.1 N), so 77 N is open sea.

Shelf depth there is about 40-50 m. Near 77 N, 139 E the Laptev Sea shelf is still shallow, perhaps 50 m, with the shelf break around 78-79 N. I'll estimate -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

15092.text:
Location: -77.0 latitude, 125.0 E longitude, in East Antarctica. It lies in Wilkes Land, inland of the coast and not far from the Vostok region, which is at 78.5S, 106.8E. Concordia (Dome C) is at 75.1S, 123.3E with an elevation of about 3233 m. At 77S, 125E, the ice surface is probably around 2900-3100 m. Moving south from Dome C toward the Aurora basin, the surface stays high at about 3000 m, so I estimate roughly 2950 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2950}

14530.text:
Latitude -71, longitude 81 is in East Antarctica, near Prydz Bay and the Amery Ice Shelf region. The Amery Ice Shelf sits around 70°S, 70°E, and Prydz Bay is near 68°S, 75°E. At 81°E and 71°S, the point is probably inland of the coast of Princess Elizabeth Land. The coast near 81°E (Ingrid Christensen Coast) is around 69°S, so 71°S is about 200 km inland. The ice surface there is likely 2000 m or more. Near the Vestfold Hills (78°E, 68.5°S), the ice sheet rises quickly, and at 71°S it may be around 2000 m.

I estimate the ice surface at about 2100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2100}

14542.text:
Location: 71°S, 105°E, in East Antarctica. It lies inland of the coast, which near 105°E (Vanderford/Totten area) sits around 66-67°S. At 71°S it is on the ice sheet, near Vostok Subglacial Highlands/Law Dome region... The Law Dome is at 112°E, 66.7°S. At 71°S, 105°E the ice surface elevation is probably about 2000-2500 m. Totten Glacier's catchment is around 70-71°S, 110-115°E. Aurora Subglacial Basin is around 71°S, 110-120°E. At 105°E, 71°S, the ice surface is about 2300 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so it is Solid. I estimate the ice surface at about 2400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

2564.text:
Latitude 61.0, longitude -91.0 lies in western Hudson Bay, which is open water. The coast near Arviat is at about 61.1N, -94.1W, so -91.0 is well offshore in the bay. At this point the Hudson Bay seabed is probably about 80 to 100 m deep, though the western bay is shallower, so I estimate roughly -90 m.

This is a judgment from the bay's general depth, not a looked-up value. The longitude is far enough east of the coast that I'm confident it's water.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-90}

13878.text:
Latitude -65, longitude -143 lies in the Southern Ocean, off Marie Byrd Land. The Antarctic coast at that longitude is around -77 to -78 (Ross Sea, Sulzberger Bay), so -65 is far north of it, in open ocean. The seafloor there is part of the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge region and the abyssal plain.

The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge is around -65, -175 to -150, so at -143 the seafloor is probably abyssal, around 3000-4000 m deep. The Antarctic continental margin is at about -74 in that sector, so -65 is on the deep ocean floor, which I estimate at about -4200 m. Nearby, the Udintsev fracture zone lies near -57 to -65 and 150W-ish, so some of the terrain could be shallower. I'll take -4000 m as my estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

4111.text:
Latitude 45.0 N, longitude 123.0 E is in Northeast China, in Heilongjiang/Jilin province. Longitude 123 E at latitude 45 N lies near the Songnen Plain, close to the Songhua/Nen River confluence area, and not far from Zhaoyuan or Zhaozhou. The terrain there is flat plain, with elevations of about 130-150 m.

The area is north of Songyuan (45.1 N, 124.8 E) and west of Zhaoyuan (45.5 N, 125.0 E). At 123 E, 45 N, the point is near the Nen River, which flows along roughly 123.5 E at this latitude, and near Tailai. That is in Heilongjiang's southwest, near the Jilin border, around Zhenlai (45.85 N, 123.2 E). So 45.0 N is south of Zhenlai, in western Jilin near Da'an (45.5 N, 124.3 E) and Taonan (45.3 N, 122.8 E). The elevation there is about 150 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":150}

1395.text:
Latitude 75°N, longitude 91°E is on the Taymyr Peninsula in Russia. The Taymyr Peninsula spans roughly 72–78°N, and Lake Taymyr sits near 74.5°N, 101°E, so this point is well to the west of the lake. The Byrranga Mountains run along about 75°N, but further east, around 95–105°E. At 91°E the point lies near the Taymyr river valleys and the Pyasina or Nizhnyaya Taymyra headwaters, or possibly near Minina Skerries. Minina Skerries is at about 77°N, 97°E, so it is not a match.

The Kara Sea coast near Cape Sterlegov is at about 75.4°N, 88°E. Lat 75, lon 91 is therefore likely inland on the northern Taymyr lowlands, close to the Byrranga foothills. The Byrranga western part, near the Faddey Gulf and Tareya, is lower. Elevation here is probably about 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

9154.text:
Latitude -11, longitude 129 is in the Timor Sea, north of Australia's Northern Territory, about 150 km north of Melville Island (which lies near -11.5, 131). At longitude 129 the point is west of Melville Island and north of Bonaparte Gulf, on the Sahul Shelf. Shelf depths there are about 100 m, and the Timor Trough lies farther northwest. At -11, 129 the depth is probably around 100-150 m.

The shelf edge is closer to the Timor Trough at about -10, 127. The area near -11, 129 is part of the outer shelf, with depths around 200 m. I'll estimate about -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

942.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -95.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago. Bathurst Island spans about 75.5-77.5°N, so it is too far south. Cornwallis Island is near 75°N, and Devon Island is near 75.5°N, -90°W, so neither fits. At 79°N, -95°W the candidates are Ellef Ringnes Island (about 78.5°N, -102°W), Cameron Island, Meighen Island, and Amund Ringnes Island (78.5°N, -96°W).

Amund Ringnes Island spans roughly 77.9-78.5°N and -97 to -93°W. Its northern tip is near 78.8°N, so 79.0°N is probably just north of the island. Axel Heiberg Island lies at about 80°N, -90°W, with its southern tip near 78.5°N and -92°W. At 79°N, -95°W the point is probably in Hendriksen Strait or Norwegian Bay, where Hendriksen Strait lies between Amund Ringnes and Axel Heiberg. Hendriksen Strait is at about -92 to -93°W, and Norwegian Bay is at about 77.5°N, -92°W. A point at 79°N, -95°W is probably in the channel west of Axel Heiberg and north of Amund Ringnes, an area that includes Massey Sound and Danish Strait. The Axel Heiberg west coast is around -92 to -94°W at 79°N, so this is most likely water. The water there is shallow, perhaps around 200 m deep, so I estimate -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

3715.text:
The coordinates 49.0°N, 51.0°E fall in western Kazakhstan, in the Aktobe/West Kazakhstan/Atyrau region, close to the Ural River basin. This is an estimate from memory of the region, not a lookup.

The Caspian Depression lies to the south, but at 49°N, 51°E the terrain is still part of the Pre-Caspian lowland. Elevation there is likely around 20 m, perhaps a little lower. The Caspian Sea surface is about -28 m, and 49°N is well north of the sea. The Ural River at Uralsk (51°N, 51°E) is at roughly 30 m. Farther south the land drops toward -20 m near Atyrau (47°N). So at 49°N, 51°E I estimate about 10 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":10}

362.text:
The point at 85°N, 175°W lies in the Arctic Ocean, on the Amerasian side of the Lomonosov Ridge. The Alpha Ridge and Mendeleev Ridge are nearby. The Alpha-Mendeleev Ridge complex runs from about 80°N, 180° toward Ellesmere Island. At 85°N, 175°W, I expect it to be near the Alpha Ridge or the Mendeleev Ridge, where depths are around 1500–2500 m.

The Mendeleev Ridge crest is at about 1500 m depth, and the Alpha Ridge crest is at about 1400–2000 m. The Canada Basin, by contrast, is about 3800 m deep. IBCAO shows the area near 85N, 175W as part of the Mendeleev/Alpha Ridge, at roughly 1500–2000 m depth. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

14031.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 163 is in the Southern Ocean, off the Oates Land/George V coast of Antarctica. The coast there is near 69-70°S, so -65 is about 500 km north of land. It is open ocean, north of the continental shelf, on the continental slope or abyssal plain.

The Southern Ocean south of Australia/NZ has the Southeast Indian Ridge, and further east the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge. At 65°S, 163°E, the Balleny Islands are at about 66.9°S, 163°E, so this point is just north of them. The Balleny Islands sit on the Balleny Ridge, and the seafloor around there is about 3000-4000 m deep. At -65, the point is a bit north of the islands, in the basin region, where depth is probably around 3,500 m. I'll estimate -3,400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

9547.text:
The point is at 17°S, 165°W, in the South Pacific. That places it west of the Cook Islands (Aitutaki is near 18.9°S, 159.8°W) and east of Samoa and Tonga. Samoa is near 14°S, 172°W, and Niue is near 19°S, 170°W. The Penrhyn atoll is at 9°S, so it is not nearby. The nearest land is probably the northern Cook Islands or Palmerston (18°S, 163°W), and the point at 165°W is west of Palmerston, so it lies in open ocean.

The seabed in this region, between the Cook Islands and Niue, is roughly 4500–5500 m deep. I'll estimate about -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

945.text:
Latitude 79.0 N, longitude -89.0 W is in the Canadian Arctic, in the Queen Elizabeth Islands. Devon Island spans roughly 74.5-77N, so it is further south. Ellesmere Island's south coast is around 76.5-77N at -89, and Cornwallis Island is at 75N. At 79N, -89W, that places us in the north-central part of Ellesmere Island, near Eureka at 80N, -85.9W. The Bay Fiord and Baumann Fiord areas are around 77-78N, -86 to -88W. Axel Heiberg Island lies around -90 and west, at 79N. The Eureka Sound is at about -86 to -90 at 79N. Axel Heiberg's east coast is around -88 to -89 at 79N, and Eureka Sound there is narrow. Stor Island is at 78.5N, -88W.

I can't pin this point down with confidence. Eureka Sound at 79N runs roughly from -86 (Ellesmere west coast) to -89 (Axel Heiberg east coast), so -89 is likely at Axel Heiberg's coast, probably land. Axel Heiberg's east coast has hills at a few hundred meters, and the Princess Margaret Range is inland. I'll guess land at about 300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":300}

1777.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 135.0°E (positive, so eastern hemisphere) lies in the Laptev Sea / New Siberian region, near the Lena Delta area. The Lena Delta is around 73°N, 126°E. At 71°N, 135°E, that is inland, near the Yana-Indigirka lowland. The Yana River delta is at about 71.5°N, 136°E, and the Buor-Khaya Gulf is at about 71.5°N, 129°E. The Yana Bay coast is at around 71.5°N, 136°E, and Yana Bay extends south to about 71°N. So 71°N, 135°E could be the Yana delta's western edge or the shallow bay.

The Yana delta spans roughly 71.0–71.8°N and 134–136°E. The point is probably on the delta land at low elevation, a few meters. I'll say Solid, 5 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":5}

15461.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 143 lies in the Ross Ice Shelf region. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°E to 150°W, and its western edge runs along the Victoria Land coast. At 143°E, 81°S, the location is in East Antarctica, in the interior of Oates Land or George V Land, which is grounded ice on the plateau.

Around 81°S, 143°E, the ice surface is probably about 2000–2500 m. This area is inland of the Oates Coast, near the Rennick Glacier region (which lies near 160°E). At 143°E and 81°S, the point is about 900 km from the coast, on the plateau near Dome Concordia's direction, so a surface elevation of roughly 2400 m seems reasonable. I'll go with 2500 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2500}

15434.text:
Latitude -81, longitude 89E is in East Antarctica, inland of the Princess Elizabeth Land and Wilkes Land coast. It lies near the area south of Dome A (80.4S, 77.1E) and toward the Vostok region (78.5S, 106.8E). The ice surface at 81S, 89E is probably around 3,300 to 3,500 m. Dome A is about 4,090 m, and Vostok is about 3,488 m. At 81S, 89E the surface is between them, probably about 3,500 m.

The surface type is grounded ice, so it counts as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3500}

5733.text:
Latitude 27.0, longitude 127.0 is in the Pacific Ocean, east of the Ryukyu Islands. Okinawa sits at about 26.5N, 127.9E, and Kume Island at about 26.35N, 126.8E. The point at 27N, 127E lies northwest of Okinawa and north of Kume, in the East China Sea. The Okinawa Trough is to the northwest and the shelf edge runs along the Ryukyu arc.

The Okinawa Trough axis near 27N lies at about 126-127E, with depths of about 1000-2000 m. The shelf break west of the Ryukyu islands is at about 127E at this latitude. Islands near this latitude include Iheya and Izena at 27.0N, 127.9E. At 127E the point is about 90 km west of them. The trough there is roughly 1000 m deep, and I estimate -1100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1100}

6714.text:
Latitude 15.0 N, longitude -71.0 W lies in the Caribbean Sea, south of Hispaniola. Hispaniola's southern coast is at about 18 N, and Venezuela/Colombia are at about 12 N. The point is in the Colombian Basin/Venezuelan Basin area, near the Beata Ridge, which runs from Cape Beata (17.6 N, -71.4 W) southwest toward Colombia.

The Beata Ridge is around -71 to -73 W at 15 N. Its crest depths are about 500-2000 m. The point at 15 N, -71 W is on the eastern side of the ridge, where the ridge runs roughly from 17.5 N, -71.5 W to 12 N, -73.5 W. At 15 N, the ridge axis is near -72.5 W. At -71 W it is on the eastern flank, in the Venezuelan Basin, at a depth of about 3500-4000 m. I'll go with -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

9956.text:
Latitude -21.0, longitude -67.0 is in southwestern Bolivia, in the Potosí department. It lies near the Salar de Uyuni's southern region, to the southwest of the salar and within the Altiplano area near the Nor Lípez province.

The Salar de Uyuni spans roughly -20.1 S, -67.5 W, with an elevation of about 3653 m. At -21.0, -67.0, the position is south of the salar, so it is probably in the Altiplano region with volcanic terrain. The Salar's southern edge is near -20.5 to -20.7. Going further south to -21.0, the terrain is the Nor Lípez plateau at about 3700-4000 m. Near -21.0, -67.0 there are the Cerro Uturuncu area (-22.27) and the Cañapa and Chiguana salars (-21.3, -67.8), but those are further away. I estimate about 3750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3730}

8610.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 121.0 is in Sulawesi, Indonesia. South Sulawesi's southeast arm sits at about 5°S, 121°E. The Gulf of Bone lies to the east at roughly 120.5–122°E, and the west coast of the Bone gulf is around 120.4°E at 5°S. The southeast arm's land at 5°S runs from about 120.4°E to 122°E, so 121°E falls on land, in the Luwu/Kolaka area. The Gulf of Bone's northern part is at about 3°S, and at 4–5°S the gulf is wide, between 120.4°E and 121.5°E.

The gulf's western coast at 5°S is near Bone, about 120.3°E. The eastern coast, the southeast arm's west coast near Kolaka, is at about 4°S, 121.6°E. At 5°S the east coast is near Tinanggea or Kendari at about 122.5°E, and the Kolaka coast near 4.0°S, 121.6°E runs southeast to roughly 4.8°S, 121.9°E. So at 5°S, 121°E the gulf of Bone is likely water, since the west coast is 120.4°E and the east coast is about 122°E. The Gulf of Bone is deep, around 1000 m or more in the middle, but at 5°S and 121°E, it narrows to the south. Bone gulf's southern part, near 5°S, has depths of perhaps 1000 m or more, and I'll estimate about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

1680.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -59.0 is in Baffin Bay, off West Greenland. Upernavik is at about 72.8N, 56W, and Uummannaq is at about 70.7N, 52W. The Greenland coast near 71N lies around 54-55W, so 59W is roughly 200 km offshore, in Baffin Bay.

The Baffin Bay central basin is deep, about 2000 m or more, at roughly 70-75N between 60W and 65W. At 71N, 59W the position is on the eastern side of the basin. The shelf off West Greenland extends out to about 57-58W at this latitude, with depths of 500 m or less. The slope then descends to the basin. Baffin Bay's central abyssal plain is about 2300 m deep. At 59W I estimate a depth of about 1500 m.


{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

14579.text:
Location: -71.0, 179.0 E is in the Ross Sea region, off the Pennell Coast of northern Victoria Land. Cape Adare sits near 170°E, 71.3°S. At 179°E, 71°S, this is the continental shelf/slope of the western Ross Sea, north of the Ross Ice Shelf edge (which is near 78°S). The area near 71°S, 179°E lies on the Iselin Bank, which is a shallow bank with depths of around 400-600 m. The Iselin Bank spans roughly 71-76°S along 175-180°W, though its northern part at 179°E may be the continental slope, where depths are around 1000-2000 m.

At 71°S, 179°E the position is just west of the antimeridian. The Iselin Bank is mainly east of 180° (around 177-170°W), and the Pennell Bank is around 71-72°S, 175-180°E, with depths around 500 m. So I estimate about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

805.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude 9°W lies in the Greenland Sea/Fram Strait region, north of Greenland's northeast coast. Greenland's northeast coast near 81°N is around 15-20°W (Nordostrundingen is at about 11.5°W, 81.5°N). So 9°W at 81°N is just east of Nordostrundingen, in the open ocean, on the Greenland continental shelf edge or the Fram Strait slope.

The Northeast Greenland shelf extends out to about 10°W at this latitude. Belgica Bank is at about 79-80°N. Near Nordostrundingen (11.5°W, 81.5°N), the shelf is fairly shallow, and the shelf break lies at around 10°W. At 9°W it is probably on the upper slope, with depth of around 500 to 1000 m. The Fram Strait's Molloy Deep is at about 79°N, 3°E. At 81°N, 9°W, the depth is probably about 300-1000 m. I'll guess -800.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-800}

4798.text:
Latitude 37.0, longitude 57.0 is in northeastern Iran, in Razavi Khorasan or North Khorasan province, near the Turkmenistan border. It sits north of Neyshabur and Sabzevar, around Quchan or Bojnord, which puts it in the Kopet Dag region and its foothills.

Bojnord is at about 37.47N, 57.33E and its elevation is about 1050 m. Shirvan is at about 37.4N, 57.9E. Quchan is at about 37.1N, 58.5E at roughly 1300 m. The point 37.0N, 57.0E is southwest of Bojnord, in the Aladagh mountains or the Miyaneh valley, and Esfarayen lies at 37.07N, 57.5E at about 1250 m. Terrain near 57.0E is probably around 1100-1500 m, so I'll estimate 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1300}

5649.text:
The point 27°N, 41°W is in the North Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 27°N sits at about 45°W, so this location is roughly 4° east of the axis. Wait, "west of" is wrong: -41 is east of -45. The point lies on the African plate flank of the ridge.

The ridge crest near 26°N is at about 45°W, and the flank depth increases with crustal age. At about 4° of longitude (roughly 400 km) from the axis, with a half-spreading rate of about 1.2 cm/yr, the crust is about 30 Myr old. That age corresponds to a depth of about 3500 to 4500 m. Rough topography and the nearby Atlantis Fracture Zone region could make it somewhat shallower or deeper, but the area is mostly abyssal hills at about 4,000 m. I estimate about -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

11103.text:
Location: -33, 67E is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and southwest of Amsterdam Island (which sits at about 37.8S, 77.5E). The area lies near the Southwest Indian Ridge and Central Indian Basin. The Mascarene Plateau is farther north, and the Crozet area is farther southwest.

At 33S, 67E, the seafloor is likely in the Central Indian Basin's western edge. The Southwest Indian Ridge is around 60E at 30S, and the Central Indian Ridge is around 66-70E at 25S, so the ridge system is some distance away. At 33S, 67E there is probably abyssal plain or an abyssal hill region, with depth around -4500 m. I'll go with -4400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

4167.text:
The point 43.0°N, 125.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean, about 150 km west of the Oregon coast near Cape Blanco and Coos Bay. The coast sits near 124.1°W, so this point is roughly 70 km offshore. At 125°W, about 0.9° of longitude (about 73 km) from the coast, the water is deeper than the shelf. The Oregon shelf is narrow, around 40 km wide, and the continental slope then drops to the abyssal plain.

The Cascadia basin and Astoria fan are at about 2,500–3,000 m depth. At 43°N, the Blanco fracture zone is further south, near 42.5°N and 126–128°W, and the Juan de Fuca ridge is to the north. Along 125°W at 43°N, the lower slope is probably at about 2,500 m depth. The Cascadia accretionary margin at this latitude runs from the shelf break at about 124.5°W down to the base of the slope near 125.2°W. At 125.0°W I estimate a depth of about 2,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

11759.text:
Latitude -41, longitude -61 is in the Atlantic off Argentina's Patagonian coast. The coast near Río Negro lies at about -41°S, -65°W (San Antonio Oeste), and the Valdés peninsula is around -63.5°W. At -61°W the point is in the Argentine Sea, well offshore on the continental shelf, so it is Water.

Shelf depths at 41°S, 61°W are roughly 80-100 m. The shelf edge lies farther east, around 55°W at this latitude. I estimate about -90 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-90}

7734.text:
The point is at 5°N, 169°E, in the central Pacific. The nearest atolls are in the Marshall Islands, with Ebon at about 4.6°N, 168.7°E, which is close but not at this point. Ebon Atoll sits around 4.6°N, 168.7°E and Namorik at 5.6°N, 168.1°E. Kili is at 5.64°N, 169.12°E. So 5.0°N, 169.0°E is about 40 km from Ebon and 70 km from Kili, which puts it in open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is the Pacific basin near the Marshall Islands and is typically 4,000–5,000 m deep. The islands are on seamounts that rise steeply, so a point 40 km or more away is probably on the deep flank or the basin floor. I estimate a depth of about 4,500 m, though I have not checked it against bathymetry data.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

4177.text:
The point 43.0°N, 105.0°W is in eastern Wyoming. It sits in Converse County, or possibly Niobrara County, east of Douglas and near the Powder River Basin's southern edge.

Douglas, Wyoming is at about 105.35°W, 42.76°N and has an elevation of roughly 1,490 m. Lusk is at 104.45°W, 42.76°N and about 1,520 m. The point at 43.0°N, 105.0°W is north of the line between them. It is near Lost Springs, which is at about 105.0°W, 42.76°N and roughly 1,600 m. That area is rolling plains, so an estimate of about 1,540 m seems reasonable.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1545}

14100.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -59 is in Antarctica, on the east side of the Antarctic Peninsula, at the Larsen ice shelf region. The Peninsula at 67°S lies near 66–68°W, so -59 is east of it, in the Weddell Sea. The Larsen C ice shelf extends to about -61 longitude at 67°S. Larsen C spans roughly 61–65°W at 67–69°S, so -59 is probably open Weddell Sea, or possibly a region of the Larsen D/Smith Peninsula... Larsen B was at 65.5°S, 61°W. At 67°S, -59 is about 200 km east of the coast of Cape Disappointment... Larsen C's front is around 60–61°W at 67–68°S, so this point is probably open water or ice-shelf front.

The seabed depth here is on the continental shelf of the Weddell Sea west side, probably around 400–600 m deep. I'll estimate -500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-500}

1247.text:
Latitude 77.0°N, longitude 155.0°E lies in the East Siberian Sea / Laptev Sea region. The New Siberian Islands are around 75-76°N, 137-150°E, and Bennett Island is near 76.5°N, 149°E. At 155°E and 77°N we are north of the New Siberian Islands, in the shelf area just beyond them. The shelf edge in this sector is around 77-78°N, and water depths there are probably 100-500 m.

Along 155°E the shelf break is near 77.5°N. At 77°N the depth is probably around 100-300 m. I'll estimate -150 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-150}

13232.text:
The point is at 57°S, 5°E, in the South Atlantic sector of the Southern Ocean. It sits south of the Bouvet Island area (54.4°S, 3.4°E) and near the American-Antarctic Ridge and the Bouvet Triple Junction region, with the Atlantic-Indian Basin to the south and east.

The seafloor here is probably around 3000 to 4000 m deep. The Bouvet fracture zone and the southwest Indian Ridge start near 54°S, 0–5°E. The American-Antarctic Ridge extends west from the triple junction at about 55°S, 1°W. At 57°S, 5°E the point is south of the triple junction and east of the ridge, so it is probably on the Antarctic plate flank in the Atlantic-Indian Basin. Depths there are about 3500 to 4000 m, so I estimate -3800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

14635.text:
