9730.text:
The point is at 19°S, 159°W, in the South Pacific near the Cook Islands. Rarotonga sits at about 21.2°S, 159.8°W and Aitutaki at about 18.85°S, 159.8°W. Aitutaki lies about 0.8° of longitude west of this point, roughly 85 km away, so the point is east of the island and almost certainly open ocean.

The seafloor in this area, east of Aitutaki and north of Rarotonga, is probably abyssal at about 4,000 to 5,000 m depth. The Aitutaki region is a seamount platform rising from roughly 4,500 m. I'll estimate -4,300 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

3858.text:
The point 47.0°N, 23.0°W lies in the North Atlantic, west of Europe and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis at about 47°N is near 28-29°W, so the point sits on the eastern flank of the ridge.

Seafloor depth on the flank there is typically about 3,000-4,000 m. Northeast of the Azores, near 47°N 23°W, the crust is roughly 40-50 million years old, which gives a depth of about 3,500-3,800 m. I'll use -3,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

3593.text:
The point 51.0°N, 167.0°E is in the North Pacific, east of Kamchatka and south of the Commander Islands. The Commander Islands sit around 55°N, 166°E, so this point is well south of them. It lies in the open ocean, in the northwest Pacific basin, close to the Aleutian-Kamchatka trench region.

The Kuril-Kamchatka trench runs along the coast and is far to the west. The Emperor Seamount chain is farther east, near 170°E at around 45°N, and the bend is at 50°N, 165°E. Meiji seamount is at about 53°N, 164°E. At 51°N, 167°E, the depth is probably the abyssal plain, around 5000 to 5500 m. The Aleutian Trench's western end near the Commander Islands is around 54°N, 168°E. The point at 51°N, 167°E is south of the Aleutian arc and likely on the Pacific plate abyssal plain, at roughly 5400 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

3921.text:
The point 47.0°N, 103.0°E lies in central Mongolia, in Övörkhangai or Arkhangai province. It is south of the Khangai range's northern part, near Arvaikheer and Bat-Ölzii. Terrain in this area is typically around 1700 to 2000 m.

Arvaikheer sits at about 46.26°N, 102.78°E at roughly 1800 m. At 47.0°N, 103.0°E the location is probably in Övörkhangai near the Khangai foothills, around 1700 m. I'll estimate 1750 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1750}

14183.text:
Location: -67, 107E is in East Antarctica, near Wilkes Land coast. The Shackleton ice shelf is at ~95-100E, and the Vincennes Bay area is around 108E at -66.5. At 107E, -67, we are likely on the coast near the Law Dome (112E, -66.7) and Vanderford Glacier (~109E, -66.5). Vanderford glacier at 108-110E. Around 107E, -67, this is likely grounded ice sheet just inland from the coast. The coast at 107E is about -66.3 (Sandefjord Bay region). So at -67 it's about 80 km inland, with ice surface elevation of around 1500 m.

Ice surface elevation approx 1300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1400}

7775.text:
Location: 3°N, 109°W is in the eastern Pacific Ocean, southwest of Clipperton Island (10.3°N, 109.2°W) and well west of the Galápagos. It lies near the East Pacific Rise region, whose crest sits around 105-110°W near the equator.

At 3°N the rise crest is at about 103-104°W, so 109°W is about 5° west of it (roughly 550 km). With a spreading rate of about 14 cm/yr, the crust there is about 4 Myr old. Crustal depth is about 2600 + 350·√age, which gives about 3300 m near the ridge, and about 3300-3500 m on the flank. However, the seafloor in this area is around 3000-3500 m deep, and the East Pacific Rise at 3°N lies at around 2800-3000 m. Taking the flank depth as about 3300 m, I estimate the seabed at about -3250 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3250}

15134.text:
Latitude -79, longitude -151 lies in the Ross Ice Shelf region, on the eastern side. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°W to 160°E, and at 151°W, 79°S it is within the shelf, near Roosevelt Island (which sits around 79.5°S, 162°W). So -151 is east of Roosevelt Island, still on the shelf. Marie Byrd Land's coast is around 78°S at about 150°W.

The shelf here is floating, so the answer is Water, with the seabed elevation below it. Bathymetry under the eastern Ross Ice Shelf is about 500-600 m deep. Bedmap values near the Ross Ice Shelf at 151°W, 79°S are roughly -550 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

13069.text:
The point is at 55°S, 39°E, in the Southern Ocean south of the southwest Indian Ocean. It lies southeast of the Prince Edward Islands (around 46.9°S, 37.7°E) and well north of Antarctica.

The area is near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Del Cano Rise. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs through roughly 52–55°S near 30–40°E, so the seabed depth is probably about 3,000–4,000 m. The Conrad Rise is near 53°S, 40–50°E, with depths around 2,000–3,000 m. This point may sit on the southern flank of the Conrad Rise area, or in the Enderby Basin side, but this is uncertain. I'll estimate about -3,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

2457.text:
Latitude 63.0°N, longitude 55.0°E falls in northern Russia, in the Komi Republic. It is near the Pechora/Vychegda upland, west of the Ural Mountains. The terrain there is a forested plain, roughly 150 m above sea level. The Timan ridge lies further west, and the Ural foothills are around 60°E.

Evaluating 63N 55E more closely: the Komi Republic's eastern part has the Pechora basin at about 100–200 m. Ust-Kulom is at 61.7N, 53.7E with an elevation of about 100 m. At 63N 55E, the location is likely in the upper Vychegda or Vym area, around 150 m, so I estimate 160 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":160}

15690.text:
Location: 85°S, 119°W is in West Antarctica, on the Ross Ice Shelf's edge or the grounded ice area near the Transantarctic Mountains/Marie Byrd Land. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 85°S at longitudes near 180 to 150°W. At 119°W, 85°S is probably in the Byrd Glacier / Ellsworth area, on the West Antarctic Ice Sheet near the Whitmore Mountains, with the grounded ice surface roughly 1500-2500 m.

The Whitmore Mountains are near 82.5°S, 104°W. The Horlick Mountains are at about 85°S, 120°W, and the Reedy Glacier is near 85°S, 135°W. The ice-sheet surface there is probably around 2000-2500 m. The Horlick Mountains lie at about 86°S, 125°W, with the Ohio Range at 84.5°S, 114°W. The nearby ice surface is around 2000 m, so I'll estimate 2200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2200}

7233.text:
The point 9°N, 113°W lies in the eastern Pacific Ocean, west of Central America and south of Mexico. It is near the East Pacific Rise, whose crest sits around 110°W at this latitude. At 113°W the seafloor is probably on the rise's western flank, where depths run about 3000 to 3500 m.

The crest of the East Pacific Rise at 9°N is at about 104°W, so 113°W is roughly 9° west of the crest. That is about 1000 km, and with a half-spreading rate of about 5.5 cm/yr it corresponds to about 18 Myr of crust age. Depth is approximately 2600 + 350·sqrt(18), which gives about 4100 m. The region also has abyssal hills and the seafloor near the Clipperton fracture zone (10°N) is shallower, so I'll estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

6991.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude 123.0 lies in the Philippines. Sibuyan Sea is around 12.5N, 122.5E. Masbate is at about 12.3N, 123.5E. Burias Island is at about 13.1N, 122.95E. Ticao Island is at about 13.65N, 123.65E.

Burias Island spans roughly 12.9–13.2N and 122.9–123.3E, with its southern part around 13.0N, 123.0E. The island is elongated northwest to southeast. Its west coast sits near 122.9E at 13.1N, and its east coast near 123.2E. At 13.0N, 123.0E, it is probably on the island, though possibly in the water on the west side of the southern part. The Burias Pass is to the east and the Sibuyan Sea to the west. The island's southern part runs from about 13.05N, 122.97E down to 12.95N, 123.05E, so the point is likely land. Hills there reach up to about 300 m, so I'll estimate about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

14119.text:
Latitude -67, longitude -21 is in the Weddell Sea, off Antarctica's Princess Martha Coast. The coast there lies around -70 to -71 at that longitude, so -67 is open ocean. That region is the Weddell Sea basin near Maud Rise (about -65, 3E) and the Weddell Abyssal Plain. At -21 longitude and -67 latitude, the seabed is probably abyssal, with depths of about 4500 m.

Because the Weddell Sea at 21W, 67S is deep, I estimate the seabed at about -4800 m. Nearby bathymetry along the Greenwich meridian at -67 is around -4500 m, and the Weddell Abyssal Plain at roughly 30W reaches about -5000 m. I'll use -4700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

11200.text:
The point (-35°, -99°) is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. It lies roughly 2,000 km west of the Chilean coast at that latitude, and just west of the East Pacific Rise's southern flank. The Chile Rise is at about 36°S, 98°W, but the East Pacific Rise itself is much farther west, near 110–115°W at this latitude. The seafloor at 99°W is therefore in the eastern flank region, where depth is typically about 3,500–4,000 m. The Juan Fernández area lies to the east at 79°W, and the Easter Microplate region is farther north, at 26°S and 110°W.

At 35°S, 99°W the seafloor is probably about 3,800 m deep, and I'll use -3,800 m as my estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

2950.text:
The point 57°N, 39°W lies in the North Atlantic, southeast of Greenland. It is west of the Reykjanes Ridge, which sits near 30°W at this latitude, so it falls in the Irminger Basin. Depths there are about 3000 m, so I estimate the seabed at around -3000 m.

The Irminger Basin floor is typically 2800–3200 m deep, and I'm settling on about 3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15740.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -19 is in Antarctica, in the region south of the Weddell Sea. It lies near the Foundation Ice Stream and Support Force Glacier area, or the Filchner-Ronne ice shelf's southern edge. Longitude -19 at 85°S is around the Recovery Glacier / Slessor region, with Theron Mountains and Shackleton Range further north (80°S). At 85°S, 19°W is inland on the East Antarctic ice sheet, near the Recovery Glacier catchment, with surface elevation about 1000-1500 m.

Recovery Glacier's upper part is around 82-85°S, 20-30°W. At 85°S, 19°W the surface is perhaps 1200 m. The Foundation Ice Stream is at about 84°S, 60°W, so that is further west. Near the pole, at 85°S, the Dufek massif is at 82°S, 52°W. The location is grounded ice, and the surface elevation is likely around 1100 m. I'll estimate 1200.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

7749.text:
The point is at 3°N, 161°W, in the central Pacific. It lies south of Hawaii and north of the Line Islands (Kiritimati is at 1.9°N, 157°W; Palmyra is at 5.9°N, 162°W). Of the nearby islands, Palmyra is the closest at roughly 3 degrees away, so this point is open ocean.

Seafloor depth in that area is roughly 5000 to 5500 m. The Line Islands ridge region and the Central Pacific Basin sit near there, and the equatorial Pacific basin floor at this longitude is about 5200 m deep. I'll estimate -5200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5200}

15765.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 31 is in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. It lies south of Dronning Maud Land, near the Pensacola Mountains and Recovery Glacier region. The Recovery Glacier is around 80-81°S at 30°W, so this is a different area. Longitude +31 at 85°S is in the interior plateau, roughly 1,000 km from the pole in the direction of 31°E.

Ice surface elevation in this area is probably about 3,000 to 3,500 m. Dome Argus is at 80.4°S, 77°E with an elevation of about 4,093 m. The point at 85°S, 31°E is on the slope toward the Recovery and Support Force glaciers, so I estimate about 3,300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3300}

11394.text:
The point is at 37°S, 71°W, in the Andes on the Chile–Argentina border. This is near the Antuco volcano and Laguna del Laja, which sits around 37.0°S, 71.3°W, and the Sierra Velluda. The Argentine side at 71.0°W is in Neuquén province, around the northern Neuquén area near Pino Hachado and Copahue. Terrain there is roughly 1500–2000 m. Since Laguna del Laja is at about 71.3–71.4°W, this point lies east of it and is not water.

At 37°S, the border runs near 71.0°W. Copahue is at 37.85°S, 71.17°W. Further north at 37°S, 71°W is near Laguna de la Laja's eastern side, with the border around 71.0–71.2°W, and the elevation is probably about 1800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

9990.text:
Latitude -21, longitude 1.0 (east) is in the South Atlantic Ocean, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and west of Namibia and Angola. The Walvis Ridge lies near 20-30°S, but it runs from the African coast near 20°S at about 13°E toward the southwest, so at 1°E and 21°S the position is probably on the ridge's southern side or the abyssal plain.

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge near 21°S sits around 13°W, so 1°E is east of it, in the Angola/Cape Basin region. The Walvis Ridge at 1°E is near 28-30°S, so at 21°S the seafloor is likely the Angola Basin's southern part or the Cape Basin, with depths around 5000 m. I estimate about -4900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

4326.text:
The point at 41°N, 167°W is in the North Pacific, south of the Aleutians and well east of the Emperor Seamounts. That area is abyssal plain, with depths of roughly 5,400 to 5,600 m.

I'm estimating from memory of the region rather than from a specific bathymetry lookup. The seafloor there is old Pacific crust, which typically sits around 5,500 m deep, so I'll use about -5,450 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

7242.text:
The point (9°N, 95°W) lies in the eastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 600 km south-southwest of the Gulf of Tehuantepec and southwest of Costa Rica and Panama. It sits north of the Cocos Ridge and the Galápagos area, and near the Middle America Trench's outer region. The Cocos Plate seafloor there is typically about 3,000 to 3,700 m deep.

Looking at it more closely, the East Pacific Rise is much farther west, near 105°W. The Cocos Ridge runs from about 5°N, 87°W toward Costa Rica, so it is well to the east. At 9°N, 95°W the crust is about 15–20 Myr old, which fits a depth of roughly 3,400 to 3,700 m. The Middle America Trench lies about 400 km to the northeast. I'll estimate -3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3550}

6559.text:
Latitude 17.0, longitude -21.0 is in the Atlantic Ocean, west of Cape Verde (Cape Verde islands span roughly -22.7 to -25.4 longitude). At -21.0 the point lies east of Sal and Boa Vista and west of the African coast, in the Cape Verde Basin region.

I did not look up a bathymetry source, so the depth is an estimate. The ocean floor in this area is roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m deep. The Cape Verde Rise sits closer to the islands, with depths around 3,000 to 3,500 m on the rise. At -21.0 the point is east of the islands, where the seabed deepens toward the basin. The abyssal plain east of the rise runs about 4,500 to 4,800 m, so I estimate about -4,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

1876.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -27.0 is in the Denmark Strait / East Greenland area. Scoresby Sund lies near 70.5N, -22W. At 69N, -27W, the coast of East Greenland near Kangerlussuaq (68.5N, -33W) is farther west. The Blosseville Coast runs from about 68N, -26.5W up to 70N, -22W. The coastline at 69N is around -26W to -27W, so this point is probably right at the coast or just offshore.

The Blosseville Coast near 69N: Kap Brewster is at 70.1N, -22W, and Nansen Fjord is at 68N, -32W. The coast at 69N is around -26W, which would put this point inland. East Greenland at that latitude has mountains and ice caps, with the coast near -25.5W, so the point is likely on land or on a glacier. The Watkins Range is further southwest. Inland of Blosseville Coast there are ice caps with elevations of about 1000-2000 m. I'll guess Solid at around 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

14025.text:
Latitude -65, longitude 151 lies in the Southern Ocean off East Antarctica, north of the Adélie/George V Land coast. The coast there is at about -67°S, so -65° is offshore, on the continental rise or abyssal plain. Seafloor depth here is probably around 3000–3500 m, so I estimate about -3200 m.

The continental slope near 151°E begins at about -66°, with the shelf break around -66.5° to -65.5°. At -65° the seafloor is likely on the rise at roughly -3000 m. I'll use that.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

15279.text:
Location: -79.0, 139.0 is in East Antarctica, in Wilkes Land, inland of the Adélie/George V coast. The coast there is around -66.5 to -67, so -79 is about 1,400 km inland. That puts it on the plateau, near Vostok Subglacial Highlands or Aurora Subglacial Basin. Ice surface elevation is likely around 2,800 m. Dome C is at -75.1, 123.3 with surface 3,233 m. At -79, 139, the surface is perhaps 2,900 m, so I'll answer with that.

The point is grounded ice, so the type is Solid, and the elevation is the ice surface.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2900}

10931.text:
The point (-31, 83) is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia and east of Amsterdam Island. It is near the Ninetyeast Ridge, which runs along roughly 90°E, so 83°E is west of the ridge. The seabed there is likely in the Central Indian Basin or the Southern Indian Basin, at about 4,500 to 5,000 m depth.

The Broken Plateau is farther south at about 30°S, 95°E, so it does not apply here. The Central Indian Basin floor is around 5,000 m at 20°S, and the area near 31°S, 83°E is probably about 4,800 m. I'll estimate -4,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4700}

12942.text:
The point is at 53°S, 145°E, in the Southern Ocean southwest of Tasmania and south of Australia. Tasmania's southern tip sits near 43.6°S, and Macquarie Island is at about 54.6°S, 158.9°E, which is far to the east. So this point is open ocean.

The seabed here lies south of the Tasmanian shelf, in the area of the Southeast Indian Ridge and the Tasman Sea's western margin. The South Tasman Rise is around 48°S, 147°E. At 53°S, 145°E, the seabed is probably the abyssal plain or the flank of the Macquarie Ridge system, where depths are about 3000–4500 m. The Australian-Antarctic basin begins further west and south. I estimate the depth at about 3,300 m, which gives an elevation of about -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

9137.text:
The point at latitude -11.0, longitude 95.0 is in the eastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Christmas Island (about -10.5, 105.7) and south-southwest of the Cocos (Keeling) Islands (about -12.1, 96.9). It is roughly 200 km west of the Cocos Islands, so it is open ocean.

The surrounding seafloor is the Wharton Basin abyssal plain, which typically lies at 5000–5500 m depth. The area west of the Cocos Islands, near the Ninetyeast Ridge, is deeper than the Cocos Plateau. The Ninetyeast Ridge is at 90°E, so 95°E lies east of it. The Cocos Islands sit on a small seamount rising from about 5000 m, and the basin floor near 95°E, -11° is probably around 5000 m. I estimate -5100 m, from typical basin depths rather than a specific lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

6764.text:
Latitude 15.0°N, longitude 29.0°E is in western Sudan, in North Kordofan or North Darfur near the Wadi Howar area. The terrain there is sandy, semi-arid plain at roughly 500 m elevation.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a looked-up value. Nearby reference points: El Obeid (13.2°N, 30.2°E) is at about 570 m, and the area around 15°N, 29°E is probably a bit lower, around 480 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

11181.text:
Latitude -35, longitude -137 lies in the South Pacific, far from any land. It is southeast of the Tuamotus and French Polynesia and well west of Chile. The seafloor there is on the Pacific plate, in the region west of the East Pacific Rise. The East Pacific Rise sits near 113°W at that latitude, so -137 is about 24° west of the ridge. Seafloor age is roughly 20-30 Myr, which gives a depth of about 3,800-4,200 m.

Typical depth for that area is around 4,000 m. I estimate the seafloor at about -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

12191.text:
Latitude -45, longitude 83 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Kerguelen and southwest of Amsterdam Island. It lies in the Indian Ocean basin, near the Southeast Indian Ridge. The ridge near 83°E is at roughly -40 to -50° latitude; the Southeast Indian Ridge passes around 77°E at -38 and runs southeast toward 88°E at -48. Near -45, the ridge is probably around 80-85°E, so the depth there would be about 3000 to 3500 m.

I'll estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

6899.text:
Latitude 13.0, longitude -61.0 is in the Caribbean. St. Vincent lies at roughly 13.25N, 61.2W, so 13.0N, 61.0W is southeast of the island. St. Vincent's southern tip is near 13.13N, 61.2W, and the Grenadines (Bequia at 13.0N, 61.23W) are to the southwest.

The point at 61.0W is about 25 km east of Bequia, on the Atlantic side of the island arc. Barbados is at 59.5W, so the point is well west of it. The water here is the Atlantic shelf edge or slope east of St. Vincent, and the depth is probably around 1000–2000 m. Just east of St. Vincent the seafloor drops steeply, and by 20 to 40 km out it is probably about 1500 m deep. The Tobago Basin lies to the east, and depths near 61W at 13N are probably 1000–2500 m. I'll estimate -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

12498.text:
The point (-49, -23) lies in the South Atlantic, west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and roughly south-southwest of the ridge crest near 0° E to -15° W at this latitude. At 49° S the ridge axis is around 8–10° W, so -23° is about 13° west of the axis, on the South American plate flank. Crust age there is about 30 Myr, and depth is typically around 3500–4500 m. The Agulhas-Falkland fracture zone lies further south, near 50° S. The area near 49° S, 23° W is probably abyssal plain at about 4000 m depth.

I estimate a depth of about -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

13277.text:
The point is at 57°S, 95°E, in the Southern Ocean south of Australia. It lies southeast of the Kerguelen Plateau and southwest of the Southeast Indian Ridge region. The Australian-Antarctic Basin sits further south, at roughly 60°S and beyond.

The Southeast Indian Ridge runs roughly from (50°S, 80°E) down to (55°S, 120°E), so at 95°E the ridge axis is near 50°S. At 57°S the seafloor is probably on the flank toward the Australian-Antarctic Basin, at a depth of about 3500 to 4000 m. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11637.text:
The point at -39°S, 55°E is in the southwest Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and southwest of Amsterdam Island. It lies south of the Madagascar Basin and north of the Southwest Indian Ridge crest, in the Crozet Basin area. The Crozet Basin is typically 4500-5000 m deep. At 39°S, 55°E the seafloor is probably around 5000 m, but the Southwest Indian Ridge, which runs from about 50°E 40°S toward the Rodrigues Triple Junction, may be nearby.

The ridge's segment near 40°S, 50°E is at the Indomed and Gallieni fracture zones, with the ridge axis around 45°S at 40°E and around 38°S at 70°E. At 55°E the ridge is at roughly 40°S, so this point could be on the ridge flank at about 3000-4000 m depth. I'll estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

803.text:
Latitude 81.0°N, longitude -13.0° is in the Greenland Sea/Fram Strait area, northeast of Greenland. Northeast Greenland's coast near 81°N lies at about -17 to -20° W (Kronprins Christian Land is around 80-81°N, 18-24°W). Station Nord is at 81.6°N, 16.7°W. At -13°, the position is east of the coast, over the Greenland shelf edge or the Fram Strait slope.

The Norske Øer ice shelf region is near 79°N, 20°W. Belgica Bank is at about 78-80°N, 15-17°W. At 81°N, 13°W the shelf is narrow, and the shelf break lies around 10-12°W. The Westward Molloy Deep and Fram Strait lie farther east. So this point is probably on the upper slope, with a depth of around -400 to -1000 m. I'll guess about -600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-600}

7355.text:
Latitude 9.0°N, longitude 131.0°E lies in the western Pacific Ocean, east of the Philippines and southeast of Palau's northern end. Palau is at about 7.5°N, 134.5°E, so this point is roughly 400 km west of Palau. It sits in the Philippine Sea or western Caroline basin area, north of the Palau Trench and Sonsorol.

The seafloor here is probably the Philippine Sea Plate's West Philippine Basin edge or the Palau-Kyushu Ridge. The Palau-Kyushu Ridge runs about 134°E, so 131°E is west of it, in the West Philippine Basin. Depths there are about 5,000 to 6,000 m. Near 9°N, 131°E, I estimate about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

15040.text:
Latitude -77, longitude 21 lies in East Antarctica, in Dronning Maud Land. This is inland from the Princess Martha Coast, south of the Orvin and Wohlthat mountains, which sit near 5–15°E and 72°S. At 77°S and 21°E, the area is on the polar plateau, with ice surface elevation of roughly 3000 m.

Antarctic ice sheet surface elevations near 77°S, 21°E are likely about 2,800–3,200 m, so I estimate 3,000 m. This is grounded ice, so the type is Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

6348.text:
The point 19.0°N, 83.0°W lies in the northwestern Caribbean Sea, south of Cuba and northwest of the Cayman Islands. Grand Cayman is near 19.3°N, 81.3°W, so this point is about 1.7° west of it. The area lies between the Cayman Trough to the south and the Cayman Ridge.

The Cayman Ridge runs east-west near 19.5°N, with depths of about 1000–2000 m on its crest. The Cayman Trough is south of the ridge near 18°N, with depths up to 6000 m or more. At 19.0°N, 83°W, the point is probably on the northern flank of the trough or on the ridge's southern slope. West of the Cayman Islands the ridge rises toward the Misteriosa Bank near 17.5°N, 83°W, and the trough lies between. I am not certain of the exact depth, so I estimate about 3000 m below sea level.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

5954.text:
The point 23°N, 151°W lies in the central Pacific, north of the Hawaiian Islands (which sit around 19-22°N, 155-160°W) and east of them. It is open ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seafloor depth there is a guess from memory of the region. The Pacific plate near Hawaii at this latitude is typically about 4,800-5,500 m deep, and the area is east of the Hawaiian arch. The ocean is about 5,000 m deep north of Hawaii. I'll estimate -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

12538.text:
Latitude -49, longitude 57 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of the Crozet Islands. The Crozets lie at about -46.4, 51.7, and Kerguelen is at about -49, 69.5, so this point is in open ocean between them.

The area is close to the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Del Caño Rise. The Del Caño Rise sits at around -46 to -45, 55 to 60 and is shallower, at roughly 2000 to 3000 m. At -49, 57 the depth is probably about 3000 to 3500 m. The Crozet Plateau extends east, and the Southwest Indian Ridge runs through roughly -50, 57, with depths of about 3000 m. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

9346.text:
The point is at 13°S, 153°E, which lies in the Coral Sea. It is east of Papua New Guinea's Louisiade Archipelago and north of the Coral Sea basin, in the Coral Sea Basin or near the Louisiade Plateau. The Louisiade Archipelago is around 11-12°S, 152-154°E. The Pocklington Trough lies near 11-12°S, 155°E.

At 13°S, 153°E, the seafloor is likely the deep Coral Sea basin at roughly 3,000-4,000 m. The Mellish Rise is further east, near 155°E, and 17°S. The Rennell Trough is around 12-13°S, 152-153°E, but I am not certain of its exact position. The Louisiade Plateau is to the south of the archipelago and extends to about 13°S. I'd guess a depth of about -3,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

3652.text:
Location: 49.0 N, 75.0 W is in central Quebec, north of the Saint-Maurice area and near the Gouin Reservoir region. The Gouin Reservoir sits around 48.5-48.8 N, 74-75 W, so the point could fall on water.

The reservoir's western part is near 48.7 N, 74.9 W, and Gouin's surface elevation is about 404 m. At 49.0 N, 75.0 W the point is probably just north of the reservoir, in the Laurentian plateau, where land elevation is around 400-420 m. The reservoir has many arms, including the Kinositew bay and the Marmette arm, so some risk of water remains. I'll guess land at about 410 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":410}

15260.text:
Latitude -79, longitude 101 is in East Antarctica, near Vostok Station (-78.46, 106.8) but to the west of it. At about 101°E, 79°S the ice surface should be around 3,000 to 3,300 m. Dome A is at 80.4°S, 77°E, and Vostok sits at about 3,488 m. Lines of constant elevation here run roughly parallel to the Vostok region, so 101°E, 79°S is likely near 3,300 m. Ice thickness is probably around 3,000 m or more, so the site is grounded ice and counts as Solid.

I estimate the surface at about 3,350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3350}

600.text:
Latitude 83.0N, longitude -59.0 is north of Ellesmere Island and Greenland. Ellesmere's northern coast is at about 82.5N near longitude -62, and Greenland's north coast (Nares Strait region) lies at about 82-83N near -50 to -60. Near -59, 83N is in the Lincoln Sea, north of the Greenland coast at around 82.3N. Cape Columbia on Ellesmere is at 83.1N, -70. At -59 the northern shore of Greenland at that longitude is about 82.1N, so 83N is open ocean in the Lincoln Sea.

The Lincoln Sea has a shelf depth of a few hundred meters. At 83N, -59, which is about 100 km off the coast, the depth is perhaps 400 m. The Lincoln Sea shelf there is about 200-500 m, so I estimate -400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-400}

10613.text:
The point at latitude -27.0, longitude 167.0 is in the southwest Pacific, northwest of Norfolk Island (which sits near -29.0, 168.0). This area lies on the Norfolk Ridge, or between the ridge and the New Caledonia basin. The Norfolk Ridge runs from New Caledonia to New Zealand at roughly 167–169°E, and at -27 it is probably a few hundred to a thousand meters deep. The New Caledonia Trough to the west is about 3000 m deep, and the ridge to the east is shallower, so I'm estimating the seabed depth rather than looking it up.

The point is water. I estimate the depth at about -1500 m, though it could be shallower if the point is on the ridge crest.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

14597.text:
Latitude -73, longitude -145 is in the Ross Sea region of Antarctica, near the Marie Byrd Land coast. The Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part extends to about longitude -150 to -145 at latitudes of roughly -78 and farther south. At -73, -145 we are north of the ice shelf front, in the Amundsen Sea/Ross Sea boundary area. The Marie Byrd Land coast is near -77 at that longitude, with Sulzberger Bay around -76.5, -148. So -73 is open ocean on the continental shelf edge or slope.

The seabed there is probably the continental slope or rise. The shelf break in the eastern Ross Sea is around -75 to -76, so -73 is likely on the slope or rise at roughly 3000 m depth. In BEDMAP and GEBCO, the depth near -73, -145 is about -3500 m, though this is an estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8946.text:
Latitude -9, longitude 73 is in the central Indian Ocean, east of the Chagos Archipelago. The Chagos Archipelago spans roughly 5-8°S and 71-73°E, with Peros Banhos at about 5.3°S, 71.8°E and Egmont at 6.6°S, 71.3°E. Diego Garcia is at 7.3°S, 72.4°E. The point at 9°S, 73°E lies south-east of these, probably on the Chagos-Laccadive Ridge flank. The Great Chagos Bank is at about 6°S, 72°E. The Chagos Trough lies to the east, and its depth there is about 4000 m. The ridge area to the south around 9°S is probably deep, with depths of 3000-4500 m. The ridge itself runs north-south at about 72-73°E, so 73°E may be just east of it, at roughly 3500 m depth.

I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

24.text:
Location: 89°N, 131°W is in the Arctic Ocean near the North Pole, on the Amerasian side. The Lomonosov Ridge runs from the Ellesmere/Greenland side across the pole toward Siberia. At 89°N, the ridge lies roughly along 140°E to 40°W, so the 131°W meridian is on the Canadian-side flank of the ridge, near the Amerasian Basin edge.

The Lomonosov Ridge crest at the pole is about -1000 to -1500 m. The Makarov Basin is at about -4000 m on the Siberian side, and the Amundsen Basin at about -4200 m on the Greenland side. The Amerasian side is the Makarov Basin, which sits toward 180°E and 130°W. At 89°N, the longitude 131°W is about 1 degree (about 111 km) from the pole. The ridge near the pole trends roughly from 80°W/100°E, with the Amundsen side at 0 to 90°E and the Makarov side at 180 to 270°E. A point at 131°W (229°E) lies on the Makarov side. The ridge crest lies at about 90°W–90°E, so 131°W is about 40° of longitude from the ridge axis, which is about 70 km at 1° from the pole. That puts it on the ridge flank or in the Makarov Basin edge, with a depth around -2000 to -3000 m. I'll estimate -2400.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

492.text:
Latitude 85°N, longitude 85°E lies in the Arctic Ocean. It is north of the Kara Sea and Severnaya Zemlya, on the Nansen Basin / Amundsen Basin side. The Gakkel Ridge runs roughly through 85°N near 85°E, with axial valley depths of about 4000-5000 m. The Amundsen Basin floor is about 4200 m deep. At 85°N 85°E the position is probably in the Nansen Basin or on the Gakkel Ridge. The Gakkel Ridge at 85°E is around 87°N, and at 60°E it is around 82°N, so at 85°E and 85°N we are likely in the Amundsen Basin southern part, with a depth of about -4100 m.

The surface type is water, and I estimate the seabed elevation at about -4100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4100}

2253.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude 7.0°E lies in the Norwegian Sea, off the coast of central Norway. The coast near Brønnøysund and Sklinna is at about 12°E, so this point is well offshore, roughly 300 km west of the coast.

The area sits on the Møre/Vøring margin. The Vøring Plateau lies around 66–67°N, 5–8°E, with depths of about 1200–1500 m. At 65°N, 7°E the point is likely on the Vøring Basin or the lower continental slope, where depths are around 1500 m. The Norwegian shelf edge at 65°N is near 8–9°E, and the Halten Bank is at about 64°N, 8–9°E. The Trænadjupet slide area is at about 67°N, 9°E. So 7°E at 65°N is just beyond the shelf edge, on the slope, and the depth is probably 1000–1500 m. I'll estimate 1400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1400}

4137.text:
The point 45.0°S, 175.0°E (the latitude is given as positive 45.0, which is north, but the longitude suggests the New Zealand region if the latitude were south). Taken literally, 45°N, 175°E is in the North Pacific, east of Japan and west of the dateline, well south of the Aleutians. That area sits in the open northwest Pacific. The Emperor Seamount chain lies near 170°E at roughly 40–45°N, so it is close, but 175°E is east of the chain. Ocean depth there is typically about 5,500 m on the abyssal plain.

I will take the point as 45°N, 175°E, with an estimated seabed depth of about -5,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

155.text:
Latitude 89.0°S or N? It is given as +89.0, so near the North Pole, which is in the Arctic Ocean. Longitude 131°E, at 89°N, lies along the Lomonosov Ridge area. The Lomonosov Ridge crosses the pole, running from the Siberian shelf (around 140°E) to Greenland (around 40-60°W). At 90°N the depth is about 4200 m on the Amundsen Basin side. The ridge's crest is about 1000-1500 m deep, and its position near the pole lies roughly along the 140°E to 40°W line.

At 89°N, 131°E, we are about 111 km from the pole in the direction of 131°E. The ridge axis near the pole passes slightly toward the Amerasia side. The Amundsen Basin lies on the 0-180° side (the Eurasian side), and the Makarov Basin on the other. Longitude 131°E is on the Eurasian side, close to the ridge axis at 140°E. Ridge crest depths at 88-89°N are ~1000-1700 m. The IBCAO map shows the pole at about -4200 m in the Amundsen Basin, and the ridge near 88-89°N, 140°E... I'll estimate about -2000 m, partway down the flank.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

15311.text:
Latitude -81, longitude -157 is in the Ross Ice Shelf region of Antarctica. The Ross Ice Shelf spans roughly 160°W to 160°E and extends north to about -78° at the front, reaching south to about -85°. At -157° longitude and -81° latitude it lies on the eastern part of the shelf, near the Marie Byrd Land and Edward VII Peninsula side, but still within the shelf.

This is floating ice shelf, so the type is Water and I need the seabed elevation beneath it. Bathymetry under the Ross Ice Shelf (from the Bedmap2 and RIGGS data) is typically 300 to 800 m deep, and in the eastern part is about 500 to 600 m deep. Near -81° and -157° I estimate the seabed at about -560 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-560}

16071.text:
The point is at 89°S, 77°W, in West Antarctica, about 100 km from the South Pole on the side toward the Ellsworth Mountains and the Pensacola Mountains. The region is grounded ice sheet.

From memory of ice-surface elevations: the South Pole is at about 2835 m. At 89°S along 77°W the surface should be a little lower, roughly 2,000–2,500 m. Along the 90°W meridian the surface falls toward the Ronne ice shelf, and the Thiel Mountains lie near 85°S, 80°W, with surface elevation around 2,000 m or more there. At 89°S the surface is probably about 2,700 m. Since 77°W is toward the Weddell Sea side, the ice there is likely a little lower than at the Pole, so I'll estimate 2,600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2600}

14421.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -137 lies in the Amundsen Sea sector of West Antarctica, off Marie Byrd Land's Ruppert/Hobbs Coast area. The Getz Ice Shelf is farther west, around -125 to -145 longitude, at about -73 to -75 latitude. At -71 latitude, longitude -137, the point is probably open ocean on the continental shelf edge or slope. The Marie Byrd Land coast is around -74 to -75 latitude here, so -71 is well north, in the deep Amundsen Sea or Pacific-Antarctic margin. The continental shelf edge there is near -73 or -72, so this point is likely on the continental slope or rise, with a depth of about -3500 m.

Seafloor depth near -71, -137 is probably abyssal, around -4000 m. Marie Byrd Seamounts lie further north, around -70 to -75 and -120 to -140, so a seamount is possible. I'll estimate -3900.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

14749.text:
Location: -73.0, 159.0 is in East Antarctica, in Victoria Land, inland of the Pennell Coast. At 73°S, 159°E it lies in the interior of northern Victoria Land, near the Rennick Glacier/Mesa Range area. Rennick Glacier is around 160-161°E and 71-72°S. At 73°S, 159°E the terrain is probably high plateau ice at roughly 2000-2500 m.

The Mesa Range is at about 73.5°S, 162°E. Around 159°E, 73°S is the Outback Nunataks / Tobin Mesa region, with ice surface around 2200 m. I'll estimate 2300 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2300}

12206.text:
The point at 45°S, 113°E lies in the Indian Ocean southwest of Australia, south of the Naturaliste Plateau and north of the Southeast Indian Ridge. Australia's southwest corner is near 34°S, 115°E, so this point is well offshore in open ocean.

The Naturaliste Plateau sits around 33–35°S, 112–115°E, so it is too far north to matter here. The Diamantina Zone and the Southeast Indian Ridge are further south, near 48–50°S at this longitude. The seafloor at 45°S, 113°E is probably abyssal plain or lower ridge flank, at roughly 4000 to 4500 m depth. The Australian-Antarctic Basin begins south of the ridge, and the ridge crest near 114°E is at about 47–48°S. The ridge flanks there are shallower, around 3500 to 4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

11107.text:
The point is at 33°S, 75°E in the southern Indian Ocean. It lies southeast of Amsterdam Island, which sits near 37.8°S, 77.5°E, and west of the Ninetyeast Ridge, which runs along roughly 90°E. The area is near the Mid-Indian Basin and the Southwest Indian Ridge branch. I did not look up a bathymetry source, so the depth is an estimate from regional knowledge.

The Amsterdam–St Paul plateau is around 38°S, and the Central Indian Basin has typical depths of about 4000–5000 m. At 33°S, 75°E the seafloor is probably in the Perth or Mid-Indian Basin at about 4500 m, though the Ninetyeast Ridge is too far east to affect it. I'll estimate -4600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

3703.text:
Latitude 49.0, longitude 27.0 is in western Ukraine, in Khmelnytskyi Oblast near Starokostiantyniv and Chornyi Ostriv. The area is on the Podolian Upland, roughly 300 m above sea level. The Southern Buh headwaters are nearby, and the point is probably just north of the town of Vovkovyntsi.

I'm estimating about 290 m from memory of the regional terrain rather than from a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":290}

13563.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -53 is in the Southern Ocean, north of the South Orkney Islands (which lie near -60.5, -45) and northeast of the South Shetlands (which lie near -62, -58 to -55). At -61, -53 the point is in the Scotia Sea / Bransfield region, west of the South Orkneys and north of Elephant Island, which sits at -61.1, -55.2. It is about 2 degrees east of Elephant Island.

The area east of Elephant Island is the southern Scotia Sea, with depths of about 3000 m. The Powell Basin is farther south and east. The Scotia Arc's southern boundary runs from the South Shetlands toward the South Orkneys, and the sea floor near -61, -53 is probably around 3000 to 3500 m deep. I'll estimate -3200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

2926.text:
The point 57.0°N, 87.0°W lies in northern Ontario, in the Hudson Bay Lowlands. It is near the Winisk and Attawapiskat rivers, about 150 km inland from the James Bay and Hudson Bay coast. The Hudson Bay coast at this longitude is around 55.2°N, so 57°N is inland. Elevation in that area is roughly 100 m. The Attawapiskat River region around 52.9°N, 82.4°W is much lower, and the Winisk River at 55°N is about 50 m.

At 57°N, 87°W the terrain is probably still lowlands, near the Ontario/Hudson Bay border region, with elevation perhaps 150 m. The Sutton Ridge is at about 55°N, 84.5°W. The Sutton Lake area at 55°N, 88.5°W sits at about 150 m. For 57°N, 87°W I estimate about 170 m, and the surface is solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":170}

8551.text:
Latitude -5.0, longitude 3.0 is in the Gulf of Guinea, in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies west of Gabon and Congo, about 1,000 km or more from the coast, and south of the equator.

The area is in the deep Atlantic basin, east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The Guinea Basin and Angola Basin sit around here, with typical depths of about 4,500 to 5,000 m. The Guinea Basin floor at 0°N, 0°E is roughly 4,500 m deep. At 5°S, 3°E, the seafloor is probably near 4,800 m, since the Angola Basin abyssal plain is around 5,000 m. The Walvis Ridge lies farther south at about 20°S, so it does not affect this point. I estimate a depth of about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

7003.text:
Location 13°N, 147°E is in the western Pacific, east of the Philippines and south-southwest of the Mariana Islands (Guam is at 13.4°N, 144.8°E). So 147°E is about 2° east of Guam, which puts it in the Philippine Sea/West Mariana region, probably near the Mariana Trench's western side or the Mariana Trough.

The Mariana Trench axis near 13°N lies at roughly 147.2°E. The Challenger Deep is at 11.37°N, 142.59°E, and the trench curves northeast, so at 13°N the trench axis is near 146.5°E. Guam sits at 144.8°E, and the trench is about 100 km southeast of it. At 13°N the trench axis is therefore around 147°E, which is a plausible match. Its depth there is about 8,000 to 9,000 m. At 13°N, 147°E the seafloor is probably near the trench axis or on the Pacific plate side, so I estimate about -8,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-8000}

7633.text:
Latitude 5°N, longitude 33°W is in the equatorial Atlantic Ocean. It lies west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, whose axis is near 30-31°W at this latitude, and east of the Ceará Rise. The Romanche and St. Paul fracture zones are close to the equator, so this point is probably on the western flank of the ridge or in the abyssal plain.

At 5°N the ridge axis is around 32-33°W. Just north of the St. Paul fracture zone, the axis sits near 5°N, 33°W or slightly east, at roughly 30-31°W. Ridge flank depths there are about 3500-4000 m. I'll estimate -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

1839.text:
Latitude 69.0, Longitude -101.0 is in Nunavut, Canada. It lies near the Queen Maud Gulf and Rae Strait region, to the south of Victoria Island and east of Coronation Gulf.

Reference points: Queen Maud Gulf is at roughly 68.5N, 98W. Kugaaruk is at 68.5N, 89.8W. Cambridge Bay is at 69.1N, 105W. King William Island spans roughly 68.5-69.5N and 95-99W. Longitude -101 at 69N therefore falls west of King William Island, in the Queen Maud Gulf area. The Queen Maud Gulf extends from about 67.5-69N and 96-102W, and there are many islands within it. The Simpson Peninsula and Adelaide Peninsula are farther east. The gulf's northern shore is the south coast of Victoria Island, near 69N at about 102-104W. The Victoria Island south coast near Cambridge Bay is at roughly 69N, and Wollaston Peninsula is farther west. At -101, 69N is likely to be water in the Queen Maud Gulf, at the northern part near Jenny Lind Island (68.7N, 101.5W). That area is shallow, so I estimate a seabed depth of about -50 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-50}

8635.text:
The point (-5.0, 171.0) lies in the central Pacific, south of the Gilbert Islands (Kiribati). Nearby islands are Nikunau at about -1.35, 176.5, and Onotoa at about -1.9, 175.5. The Marshall Islands are well to the north. Longitude 171 E at latitude -5 is west of the Gilbert chain, in open ocean between Tuvalu and the Gilberts.

Tuvalu's Nanumea sits at about -5.7, 176.1, which is farther east, so it does not affect this point. The point is therefore open ocean, probably on the abyssal plain or in the Ellice Basin region. Ocean depth there is likely around 4,500 to 5,000 m. I'll estimate -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

7444.text:
Location: 7.0°N, 51.0°W is in the Atlantic Ocean off the coast of French Guiana/Suriname. The coast of Guyana/Suriname is around 6°N at 55°W, and French Guiana's coast is near 5°N at 52°W. So 7°N, 51°W is about 250 km offshore, on the outer continental shelf or the upper slope. The shelf edge there lies roughly at 5-6°N near 50°W, with the shelf being wide. At 7°N 51°W, the Demerara Plateau lies to the northeast (around 9°N, 54°W). The seafloor depth there is probably about 2000-3000 m, since the continental slope off Suriname/French Guiana lies near 7°N at 51°W.

I'll estimate -2800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2800}

2724.text:
Latitude 59.0, longitude -131.0 is in northern British Columbia, near the Yukon border. It lies east of the Tatshenshini region, and Atlin is at about 59.57N, 133.7W, so this point is well east of Atlin. Teslin is at 60.17N, 132.7W. The point is in the Cassiar/Liard area, probably near the Rapid River or Dease River drainage. Dease Lake is at 58.4N, 130.0W. The point lies between the Stikine plateau and the Liard basin, roughly the Kechika/Turnagain region, with the Hyland River to the north.

The area is plateau and mountain terrain, with typical elevations of about 1000-1500 m. I'll estimate 1200 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1200}

7716.text:
Latitude 5.0°N, longitude 133.0°E lies in the western Pacific, east of the Philippines and south of Palau. Palau is at about 7.5°N, 134.5°E, so this point is southwest of it, in the Philippine Sea/Palau basin area.

The Palau Trench is near 134°E, but at 5°N, 133°E the seabed is probably the western part of the Palau-Kyushu region or the Philippine Sea basin floor. Depths there are likely about 4000 m. The Sonsorol and Helen Reef area is near 3°N, 131°E, and the Mariana/Palau trench region lies further east. I'm estimating from memory of the regional bathymetry rather than from a specific data source, so I'll go with about -4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

14449.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -81 is in the Bellingshausen Sea, off the coast of Antarctica's Eights Coast, west of Alexander Island. Alexander Island lies around 69-72°S and 68-75°W, so -81° is west of it. The area near 71°S, 81°W is probably open Bellingshausen Sea, or possibly the edge of the Venable or Abbot ice shelf region. The Abbot Ice Shelf spans about 72°S, 90-100°W, and the Ronne Entrance and George VI Sound are around 70°S, 68°W. So -81° at 71°S is probably open continental shelf, with a depth of roughly 500 m. The Bellingshausen Sea shelf there is fairly deep, perhaps 400-500 m, and the shelf break lies further north. Peter I Island is at 68.8°S, 90.6°W, so it is not nearby.

I'll answer Water with a seabed elevation of about -450 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-450}

14171.text:
Latitude -67, longitude 83 is in East Antarctica, off Princess Elizabeth Land. The Davis station is at about 68.6S, 78E, and the Zhongshan station is at 69.4S, 76E. At 67S, 83E the point lies north of the coast, which near Davis is around 68.5S. Near 83E the coast, close to the Shackleton Ice Shelf at 96E, lies further north, so the coast near 83E may be around 66-67S. Cape Penck lies at about 66.5S, 87E. The Zhongshan to Law Dome region is different. The coast at 83E is roughly at 67S, which could be the Sørsdal Glacier area, but that is near 78E. The Vestfold Hills at 78E are at 68.5S. Around 83E the coast is farther north, maybe 66S, with the ice sheet edge there. So 67S is probably grounded ice near the coast, with an elevation of about 1000 m. It might also be the continental shelf, but I'll go with ice.

Near the coast at 66.5S, the ice sheet rises quickly. At 67S, about 50-100 km inland, the elevation is perhaps 800 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":900}

6335.text:
The coordinates 19.0°N, 109.0°W fall in the Pacific Ocean, southwest of Baja California and west of Mexico's mainland. They lie near the Revillagigedo Islands, which are around 18.8°N, 110.9°W (Socorro), so this point is east of them. The point is also west of the Pacific coast of Mexico and south of the tip of Baja.

The seafloor here is part of the East Pacific Rise region, probably 3000-3500 m deep. The East Pacific Rise itself sits near 106-109°W at about 10-20°N, so the rise crest would be around 3000 m deep at that latitude. At 19°N the Rivera plate area is near 108°W, but the rise there is complex. The abyssal plain west of it is typically about 3500 m deep, so I estimate -3300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3300}

11317.text:
Latitude -35, longitude 135 lies in the Southern Ocean, off South Australia. Land near this longitude is Eyre Peninsula. Port Lincoln is at about 135.85°E, 34.7°S. Cape Catastrophe is at about 135.9°E, 34.9°S. At 135.0°E, 35°S the point is south-west of Port Lincoln, around 80 km west of the Cape, so it is in the sea.

Streaky Bay is at 134.2°E, 32.8°S, which is too far north to matter. The coast at 135°E is roughly 34.3°S (near Coffin Bay, which is at 134.8°E, 34.6°S). So -35 at 135°E is about 50 km offshore, on the continental shelf. Shelf depth there is about 80-100 m, and the shelf edge is further south, near 36°S. I estimate a depth of around -90 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-90}

3669.text:
The point 49°N, 41°W lies in the North Atlantic, roughly 1,000 km east of Newfoundland. It is west of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs near 30°W at this latitude, so the seabed here is in the Newfoundland Basin. The Charlie-Gibbs Fracture Zone is further east, near 52°N and 30°W.

Typical depths at this location are about 3,500 to 4,000 m. The ridge flank rises toward the east, and at 41°W the depth is probably around 3,800 m. I'm estimating from general knowledge of the region, not from a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3800}

3148.text:
Latitude 55.0, longitude -3.0 is in southern Scotland, in the Scottish Borders / Dumfries and Galloway area. It lies near the Scotland–England border, close to Newcastleton and Langholm. The Solway Firth is further west, at about -3.0 to -3.5 longitude near latitude 54.95, so that is not at this point.

Newcastleton sits at about 55.07 N, -2.81 W. Langholm is at 55.15 N, -3.0 W. The point 55.0 N, -3.0 W is a little north of Gretna (54.99 N, -3.06 W). Gretna is near the Solway coast, so the point is about 3 km east of Gretna, near Canonbie (55.07 N, -2.95 W) and Longtown (55.01 N, -2.97 W). Longtown is at around 20 m elevation, and the terrain there is low and flat. The point is slightly west of Longtown, near the River Esk, so I estimate about 15 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":18}

3263.text:
The point 53.0°N, 133.0°W lies in the Pacific Ocean west of Haida Gwaii (Queen Charlotte Islands). Haida Gwaii's west coast is near 132.5°W at about 53°N. The continental slope lies just west of the islands, and the shelf is narrow there.

At 133°W, about 30 km west of the coast, the water is probably on the slope. The Queen Charlotte Fault lies along the base of the slope, and depths there reach around 2000-2800 m. At 53°N, the shelf edge is roughly 20-30 km offshore, and 133°W is about 33 km west of 132.5°W (cos 53° ≈ 0.6, so 0.5° ≈ 33 km). That puts the point on the slope, at roughly 1800 m depth, and I estimate about -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

1771.text:
Latitude 71.0°N, longitude 123.0°E lies in the Laptev Sea region of northern Siberia, near the Lena Delta and the Buor-Khaya Gulf. The Lena Delta spans roughly 72-73.5°N, 123-130°E, and the Buor-Khaya Gulf sits near 71.5°N, 129°E. The Olenek Delta is near 73°N, 120°E. The Khara-Ulakh range and the Yana-Lena area lie around 71°N, 123°E.

The Lena's western edge is at about 125.5°E, near Tiksi at 71.6°N, 128.9°E. So 123°E at 71°N is west of Tiksi. The Bykovsky peninsula is at 71.8°N, 129°E. The Olenek Bay is at about 73.0°N, 120°E. The Anabar-Olenek lowlands are near 72°N, 123°E. At 71°N, 123°E, the point is probably land, on the Lena-Anabar lowland west of the Lena delta, close to the Chekanovsky ridge and Kharaulakh mountains (to the southeast). The Kharaulakh Mountains are around 71.5°N, 127-128°E. At 123°E the terrain is probably a low plain, perhaps 50-100 m. I estimate about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

948.text:
Latitude 79.0, longitude -83.0 is in the Canadian Arctic Archipelago, in the Nares Strait/Jones Sound region. Northern Devon Island's coast is around 76.5N, and Ellesmere Island's south coast near Jones Sound is about 76.5-77N. At 79N, -83W, this lies in Ellesmere Island's interior, west of Eureka (80N, -85.9W). Ellesmere Island is land at that point, and Baumann Fiord is further south at around 77.5N. Near 79N, -83W, we are east of Eureka Sound, around the Raanes Peninsula and Svendsen Peninsula region. Eureka Sound is at about -86W to -88W at 79N, so -83W is inland on Ellesmere, probably near the Prince of Wales Icefield, which sits at roughly 78.5N, -80W. The terrain there is plausibly high, around 600-1000 m.

I'll estimate about 700 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":700}

10920.text:
Latitude -31, longitude 61 is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and well west of Australia. It lies roughly between the Madagascar Basin and the Central Indian Basin. The Southwest Indian Ridge and the Central Indian Ridge are farther south and west, so this point is probably on the abyssal plain or a gentle rise, at a depth of about 4,500 m.

The Southeast Indian Ridge lies much farther south, and the Mid-Indian Basin around 30°S, 70°E is about 4,000–5,000 m deep. At 61°E, 31°S the depth is probably about 4,800 m. That is only an estimate, since I did not check a bathymetry source.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4800}

9866.text:
Latitude -19.0, longitude 113.0 is in the Indian Ocean, off the northwest coast of Western Australia. The coast near Port Hedland is around 118.5°E, and the North West Cape is at about 114°E, 22°S. At -19, 113 we are west of the continental shelf, probably on the continental slope or the Exmouth Plateau, which lies roughly between 19–22°S and 112–115°E.

The Exmouth Plateau is at depths of about 1000–3000 m. The northern part of the plateau, near 19°S and 113°E, is around 2000 m deep. Further north-west, the Argo Abyssal Plain is deeper than 5000 m. The plateau's northern margin slopes down at about 18°S. At 19°S, 113°E I estimate roughly 2500 m deep.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2400}

11323.text:
Latitude -35.0, longitude 147.0 is in New South Wales, Australia, near Wagga Wagga (-35.1, 147.37) and Junee. The point is slightly west of Wagga, on the Riverine plain near Collingullie or Galore. It is a few kilometers from the Murrumbidgee River, which runs near -34.8 at that longitude. Elevation there is around 190-200 m.

The area west of Wagga at 147.0 has elevations of roughly 190 m.


{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":195}

5654.text:
The point is at 27°N, 31°W, in the North Atlantic. That puts it west of the Canary Islands and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which lies near 40°W at this latitude. The Azores plateau is farther north, so this location sits in the abyssal plain on the eastern flank of the ridge.

Seafloor age here is roughly 60-80 Myr, which suggests a depth of about 4500-5000 m. The ridge axis near 27°N is around 45°W, with a depth of about 3000 m. At 31°W, about 14° of longitude east of the axis, the crust is roughly 40-50 Myr old, and depth would be about 4,000-4,500 m. A guess of around -4,300 m is reasonable.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

7222.text:
The point is at 9°N, 135°W, in the central-eastern Pacific Ocean, so the surface type is Water.

The seabed there lies between the Clipperton Fracture Zone (around 10°N) and the Clarion-Clipperton Zone, on abyssal plain. Seafloor depths in that region are typically about 4,800 to 5,200 m. The seafloor age at 135°W is roughly 50 million years, which fits a depth near 5,000 m. I estimate about -4,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4950}

9300.text:
Latitude -13, longitude 61 is in the southwestern Indian Ocean, east of Madagascar and northeast of the Mascarene Plateau. The Mascarene Plateau runs from the Seychelles down through Saya de Malha and Nazareth Bank to Mauritius and Réunion, around 55-60E. At 61E and 13S the point is east of Nazareth Bank (about 60.5E, 14.5S) and south of Saya de Malha (about 60-62E, 8-12S). It probably sits in the Mascarene Basin edge or the Central Indian Ridge area. The Mascarene Basin lies west of the plateau and the Central Indian Basin to the east.

The Saya de Malha Bank spans roughly 59-62E and 8-12S, so 13S is just south of it. Depths there are likely around 3500-4500 m. The Mascarene Plateau's southern part has depths of about 2000-4000 m. I'll estimate -4200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4200}

8095.text:
Latitude 1.0°N, longitude 171.0°E lies in the central Pacific, in the Marshall Islands/Kiribati region. The Marshall Islands are mostly north of 4°N and the Gilbert Islands (Tarawa, around 1.4°N, 173°E) lie to the east. Butaritari is near 3°N, 172.8°E, and Nauru is at 0.5°S, 166.9°E. Nothing in that vicinity is at 1°N, 171°E, so this point is open ocean.

The seafloor there is probably deep. The region west of the Gilbert Islands has abyssal depths of about 4,500 to 5,500 m, though seamounts are possible. I'll estimate about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

4362.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude -95.0 falls in southwest Iowa, near the Nebraska border. The Missouri River is at about -95.9 here, so this point is well east of it, in Mills or Pottawattamie County. Mills County is at 41.03 N, -95.6, and Pottawattamie County extends to about -95.0 on its east side. Nearby, Oakland, Iowa is at 41.3 N, -95.4 and Council Bluffs is at -95.85.

The terrain here is loess hills and rolling uplands, roughly 380 to 400 m. Glenwood sits at about 300 m, and the uplands around Malvern (41.0, -95.58) are about 330 m. Further east, Red Oak is at 41.0 N, -95.2 with an elevation of about 340 m. At -95.0 the area is near Stanton or Griswold, where the elevation is about 380 m. I'll estimate 370 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":370}

1263.text:
Latitude 75°N, longitude 173°W is in the Chukchi Sea / Arctic Ocean north of Wrangel Island (which sits near 71°N, 179°W). At 75°N, 173°W the point is north of the Chukchi Shelf, near the Chukchi Borderland and Chukchi Plateau area. The Chukchi Plateau lies around 75-78°N, 165-170°W with depths of roughly 500 m or less. The shelf break near 172°W sits at about 73-74°N. At 75°N, 173°W the point is likely on the slope heading into the Chukchi Abyssal Plain, or on the Arctic Mendeleev side. I estimate a depth of about 1800 m, but I'm not confident.

The Chukchi Abyssal Plain is about 2000-3000 m deep, while the slope is at 1000-2000 m. Since the shelf edge near 173°W is around 73°N, 75°N is about 200 km farther north, which puts it in deeper water, roughly 2000 m or more. I'll go with -2000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

2844.text:
Latitude 59.0°N, longitude 109.0°E is in central Siberia, in Irkutsk Oblast or Yakutia. It lies near the Lena River basin on the Central Siberian Plateau, with the Lena around 58-60°N and 109-114°E.

The Lena flows northeast from Ust-Kut (56.8°N, 105.6°E) toward Kirensk (57.8°N, 108.1°E), then Vitim. At 109°E the Lena is near 58.5-59°N, so the point could be close to the river, but most likely it is land. Elevation along the Lena near Kirensk is about 250 m, and the surrounding plateau is about 300-500 m. I estimate 380 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":380}

7577.text:
The point at 5°N, 145°W is in the central Pacific Ocean, south of Hawaii and north of the equator, in the area between the Clarion-Clipperton Zone and the Line Islands. That makes it open water.

I estimated the depth from typical values for the region rather than from a lookup. The seafloor there is abyssal plain at about 4,800 to 5,200 m depth. This sits east of the Line Islands and north of the equatorial region, where the seafloor is on Pacific plate crust roughly 60 to 80 million years old. Depths of about 5,000 m are typical for that age. I'll use -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

10524.text:
The point is at 27°S, 11°W, in the South Atlantic Ocean. It lies west of Africa and east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, which runs near 14°W at this latitude. It is also south of St Helena (16°S, 6°W) and north of Tristan da Cunha (37°S, 12°W).

The ridge axis near 27°S is at roughly 13–14°W, and its crest sits at about -2500 to -3000 m. At 11°W we are about 2–3° east of the axis, on the eastern flank. Ridge flank depths there are around -3500 to -4000 m. The Walvis Ridge is farther north-east, near 30°S at 3°E, so it does not affect this point. I estimate about -3900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

15796.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 93E lies in East Antarctica, on the polar plateau. Vostok Station is at -78.46, 106.8E, with an ice surface of about 3488 m. The South Pole is at 2835 m. Dome A is at -80.37, 77.35E, around 4093 m.

At -85, 93E the point sits between Dome A and the Pole, at the head of the plateau toward the Pole of Inaccessibility (-85.8, 65E, about 3700 m). Elevation near -85, 93E is probably around 3200–3500 m. From the BEDMAP/REMA surface, the area around -85, 90E sits at roughly 3400 m. I'll estimate 3350 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3350}

1641.text:
Latitude 71.0, longitude -137.0 lies in the Beaufort Sea, north of the Yukon/Northwest Territories coast. The Mackenzie Delta is near -135 longitude at about 69 N, and the coast at -137 is around 69.5 N. At 71 N, about 150 km offshore, this is the Mackenzie Trough and shelf edge area. The shelf break in the Canadian Beaufort is at around 70-71 N, at depths of about 100 m, and beyond it the slope drops steeply.

At -137 the shelf break is around 70.5 N, so 71 N is on the upper slope. I estimate a depth of about 300 m, though it could be anywhere from 200 to 500 m. I will go with -350.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-350}

5765.text:
The point is at 25.0°N, 169.0°W, in the North Pacific. It lies northwest of the main Hawaiian Islands, roughly between Pearl and Hermes Atoll (about 27.9°N, 175.8°W) and Kure. It is also west-northwest of French Frigate Shoals (23.7°N, 166.2°W) and Gardner Pinnacles (25.0°N, 168.0°W). Gardner Pinnacles is at roughly 25.0°N, 167.99°W, so this point is about 1° of longitude west of it, around 100 km.

That places it on or near the Hawaiian Ridge, in the area of the Northwestern Hawaiian Islands. Between Gardner Pinnacles and Maro Reef (25.4°N, 170.6°W) the ridge is relatively shallow, and Raita Bank sits near 25.5°N, 169.5°W with depths of about 20–100 m. This point is slightly south of Raita Bank, so it is probably on the ridge flank with depths of roughly 1000–2000 m. The surrounding abyssal seafloor is about 4500 m deep, so a flank depth of about 2000 m is a reasonable estimate.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2000}

5186.text:
The coordinates 33.0°N, 113.0°E fall in Henan Province, China, near Pingdingshan/Luohe/Zhumadian. That area is around Wuyue, Jia County, or Fangcheng, at the eastern edge of the Funiu Mountains where they meet the plain.

Pingdingshan sits at about 33.7°N, 113.3°E. At 33.0°N, 113.0°E the location is closer to Wuyang and Biyang, which is near the Nanyang basin's eastern edge and the Funiu foothills. Elevation there is likely around 100 m. Wuyang is at roughly 33.4°N, 113.6°E and about 100 m, and Biyang at 32.7°N, 113.4°E. Since 113.0°E, 33.0°N lies west of those, it is probably Fangcheng or Sheqi, a hilly area at roughly 120 m. This is an estimate from recalled locations, not a lookup.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":120}

1854.text:
Latitude 69.0, longitude -71.0 is on Baffin Island, in the interior west of Cumberland Sound and Clyde River. Clyde River is at about 70.5 N, -68.5 W, so this point lies southwest of it. The Barnes Ice Cap sits near 70 N, -73.5 W. At 69 N, -71 W, the point is probably on the Baffin plateau, northwest of Foxe Peninsula and north of Home Bay.

The Penny Highland and Cumberland Peninsula are farther southeast. Home Bay is near 68.7 N, -66 W. Further west there are lakes, such as the one near Generator Lake at 69.5 N, -72 W. The area is mostly rolling upland at roughly 500 m, though it could be anywhere from 300 to 700 m. I'll estimate 550 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":550}

9973.text:
Latitude -21, longitude -33 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, off the coast of Brazil. It lies east of Espírito Santo, near the Vitória-Trindade Seamount Chain, which runs along about -20.5° latitude. Trindade Island is at about -20.5, -29.3, so -33 is roughly 400 km west of it. The chain's seamounts include Vitória, Montague, Jaseur, Davis and Dogares. Jaseur is near -20.6, -34.5, and Davis Bank is near -20.7, -32.5, around -33 longitude. Davis Bank's summit is about 200 m deep, though I'm not sure of the exact figure.

At -21.0, -33.0, the point lies on the chain or just south of it. The base of the chain is about 4000 m deep on the abyssal plain, but the seamounts rise to around -500 to -1000 m on their flanks. Because the point is near Davis Bank, I'll guess about -1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1500}

4130.text:
Location: 45°N, 161°E is in the northwest Pacific, east of the Kuril Islands (Iturup/Urup). The Kuril Islands lie around 45°N at roughly 147-150°E, so 161°E is far to the east. The Kuril-Kamchatka Trench runs roughly 150-153°E at this latitude, with depths up to about 9,000 m. At 161°E we are on the abyssal plain of the Northwest Pacific Basin, which is about 5,400-5,600 m deep.

The Meiji Seamount and the Emperor chain lie farther north (around 50°N+), so this point is likely abyssal plain at about -5,500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

2624.text:
Latitude 61.0 N, longitude 29.0 E is in southeastern Finland, near Lake Saimaa. Lappeenranta is at about 61.05 N, 28.19 E, and Imatra is at about 61.17 N, 28.77 E. Savitaipale is at about 61.2 N, 27.7 E. The point at 29.0 E is east of Imatra, close to the Russian border. The border runs near 28.9–29.0 E at that latitude, so the point is probably in Russia, around Svetogorsk or Lake Sunskoye. Terrain there is about 50–80 m above sea level.

Lake Saimaa's surface is at about 76 m, and the border area near Svetogorsk is around 60–70 m. I'll treat the point as land at about 70 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":70}

12459.text:
The point (-49.0, -101.0) lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile, so the surface type is Water. The question is the seabed depth.

The East Pacific Rise lies farther west, around 115°W at this latitude. The Chile Rise is farther north, near 46°S at the Chile Triple Junction at 75°W. At 101°W and 49°S the crust is fairly young, so the seabed should be moderately deep. The Pacific-Antarctic Ridge sits further south and west, near 60°S at 150°W. The crust here is probably 20–35 Myr old, which gives a depth of about 3500–4000 m. I'll estimate about 3700 m, so the elevation is -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

2461.text:
Latitude 63.0, longitude 63.0 E lies in the southern Indian Ocean, well south of Africa and southwest of Australia. Nearby bathymetry: the Southwest Indian Ridge runs to the west and the Crozet Basin to the northwest. At 63°E, 63°N is wrong, though: the latitude is positive, so the point is 63°N, 63°E.

That puts it in Russia, in the northern Urals near the Khanty-Mansi/Komi border. At 63°N, 63°E the Ural Mountains lie roughly at 60-65°E, so this is on the eastern slope or in the foothills. The Ob basin (Sosva region) is lowland at about 100 m. The Northern Urals around 63°N sit at about 59-60°E, so 63°E is in the West Siberian Plain, near the Severnaya Sosva or Northern Sosva river. Elevation there is roughly 50-100 m. I'll estimate 70 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":70}

8416.text:
The point (-3.0, 93.0) lies in the Indian Ocean, west of Sumatra and south of the equator. It is roughly 400 km southwest of the Mentawai Islands and about 600 km west of Sumatra. The Mentawai forearc basin and trench are closer to Sumatra; the Sunda trench is near 98-100°E at this latitude, so 93°E is on the Indian Ocean floor west of the trench.

The Wharton Basin floor is about 5000 m deep here. Near 93°E the Ninetyeast Ridge sits near 88-90°E, so 93°E is east of it, in the Wharton Basin at roughly 5000-5400 m depth. I estimate -5100 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5100}

14309.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -1 is in Dronning Maud Land, Antarctica. The coast there is around -70.5 at this longitude, near the Fimbul Ice Shelf, which spans roughly 3°W to 3°E at about -70.5 to -71. So -69 lies north of the coast, which puts it in the ocean off the coast of Queen Maud Land.

The continental shelf there is narrow, and the shelf edge sits around -69.5 to -70. At -69 and 1°W, the point is probably on the continental slope or in deep water near the Astrid Ridge. Astrid Ridge is at about 12°E, so it is not relevant here. The Maud Rise is at about -65, 3°E, also not relevant. The depth here is likely around 3000 m, so I estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

10847.text:
The point (-31, -85) is in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of Chile. At 31°S, the Chilean coast is near 71.5°W, so this point is about 1,300 km offshore. It lies on the Nazca Plate, west of the Peru-Chile Trench. That region is mostly abyssal plain at about 3,500 to 4,000 m depth, though the Nazca Ridge and the Juan Fernández ridge are nearby. The Juan Fernández ridge lies at about 33°S and runs east to 80°W, so it does not reach this point.

Seafloor age at 85°W is about 30 Ma. Part of the East Pacific Rise flank has depths around 3,800 to 4,000 m. I'll estimate -3,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

666.text:
Latitude 83.0°N, longitude 73.0°E lies in the Arctic Ocean, north of the Kara Sea and Severnaya Zemlya (which is around 79-81°N, 95-105°E). Franz Josef Land's northernmost point is around 81.8°N, 55-65°E. At 83°N, 73°E, there is no land nearby, so this is the deep Arctic basin north of the Kara Sea shelf edge.

The shelf break is around 81-82°N here, and the continental slope descends into the Nansen Basin. At 83°N, 73°E the depth is probably around 3000-3500 m. In IBCAO, the Nansen Basin north of the Franz Josef Land and Kara region is roughly 3500 m deep at 83°N. The St. Anna Trough lies at about 80-82°N, 60-70°E, with depths of around 500 m. At 83°N, 73°E we are likely on the slope or the abyssal plain, so I estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

11104.text:
Latitude -33, longitude 69 lies in the southern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia and east of Madagascar. That is south of the Mascarene region and well north of the Kerguelen Plateau, so it is open ocean.

Nearby features are the Southwest Indian Ridge to the west and the Southeast Indian Ridge to the south. The Amsterdam and St. Paul plateau sits around 38°S, 77°E, which is farther east and south. Around 33°S, 69°E the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain of the Central Indian Basin or the Crozet Basin, or the Southern Indian Ocean basin. Depth there is typically about 4000–5000 m. I do not recall the exact bathymetry, so I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

12412.text:
The point is at -47.0, 165.0, which lies southwest of New Zealand's South Island (Fiordland is around 167°E at -45.5°). Stewart Island is near 167.8°E, so 165°E at 47°S is open ocean in the Tasman Sea/Southern Ocean, west of the Macquarie Ridge.

The Macquarie Ridge runs from about 166°E at -50° up toward Fiordland. Near -47°, the ridge sits around 165.5-166°E. The Puysegur Ridge and Trench lie near 166°E at -47° (the trench is around 165.5°E). So 165°E is west of the trench, on the Tasman Sea floor, which is probably around 3000-4000 m deep. Bathymetry west of Puysegur Trench at -47° gives roughly -3500 m. The area at 165°E is likely on the Solander Basin or the Puysegur Bank flank, so I estimate -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

4303.text:
Latitude 43.0 N, longitude 147.0 E lies in the Pacific Ocean, southeast of Hokkaido and south of the Kuril Islands. Kunashir and Iturup are at roughly 44°N, 145–147°E. At 43°N, 147°E, the point is south of Iturup (which spans about 45–46.5°N, 147–149°E), and it sits near the Kuril Basin and Kuril-Kamchatka Trench region.

The trench axis runs southeast of the islands. At 43°N, the trench is near 147–148°E, with the Pacific side deeper than 5000 m. The Kuril arc at 43.5°N runs from Shikotan (43.8N, 146.7E) to the southeast. Shikotan is at 43.8°N, 146.75°E, so 43.0°N, 147.0°E is about 90 km south of Shikotan, on the Pacific side. The shelf there is steep, with depths around 3000–5000 m. The trench axis at this latitude is about 146.5–147°E further out at roughly 8000 m deep to the east. I estimate a depth of about -4500 m, since the point lies on the slope of the arc.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4500}

4212.text:
The point 43°N, 35°W lies in the North Atlantic, just east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. The ridge axis near 43°N sits at roughly 29-30°W, and the Azores plateau lies to the south. At 35°W the location is on the western flank of the ridge, in the western North Atlantic basin. Seafloor depths there are around 3,000-4,000 m. Just west of the ridge crest, the flank at 35°W would be about 3,500 m deep.

This is an estimate from general knowledge of the region, not a lookup.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

11219.text:
This location is at -35, -61, in Argentina's Buenos Aires province, near Junín and Chacabuco. It is part of the Pampas, which are flat. Elevation there is about 80 m.

Junín is at roughly -34.58, -60.95 and sits about 80 m above sea level. Moving 0.4° south toward Bragado/9 de Julio, the terrain is around 75-80 m. The point at -35, -61 lies near Lincoln or Chacabuco, so I'd estimate about 85 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":85}

6722.text:
The point 15°N, 55°W lies in the western tropical Atlantic, east of the Lesser Antilles (about 61°W) and well offshore. The surface type is therefore water.

The seabed there is part of the Atlantic abyssal plain, roughly east of the Barbados Rise and north of the Demerara Plain. Typical depths in this region are about 5,000 to 5,300 m. I am estimating from regional depth ranges rather than a specific measurement, and I chose about -5,100 m as a central value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

3332.text:
The point 53.0°N, 5.0°E lies in the North Sea/Wadden area. The Dutch coast at that latitude is near Den Helder (52.95°N, 4.75°E). Texel is at roughly 53.0°N, 4.8°E. At 5.0°E, 53.0°N, the point is east of Texel, in the Wadden Sea or the Vlieland area. Vlieland is at about 53.25°N, 4.95°E. Terschelling is at about 53.4°N, 5.3°E.

At 53.0°N, 5.0°E, the point falls in the Marsdiep/Wadden Sea east of Texel, or perhaps in the IJsselmeer. The Afsluitdijk runs from about 53.0°N, 5.0°E (Den Oever at 52.93°N, 5.04°E) to Zurich at 53.07°N, 5.35°E. So 53.0°N, 5.0°E is just north of Den Oever, in the Wadden Sea side. The Wadden Sea there is shallow, with tidal flats and channels at about -2 m depth.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3}

12929.text:
The point (-53.0, 119.0) is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. It lies on the Southeast Indian Ridge region, southwest of the Australian-Antarctic Basin. The ridge near 119°E sits around 50°S to 51°S, so at 53°S the point is just south of the ridge axis, on the flank, where depths are probably around 3500 to 4000 m.

The Australian-Antarctic Discordance lies near 120°E at about 48°S to 50°S, with depths of about 4000 to 5000 m. At 53°S, the seafloor south of the ridge is probably around 4000 m deep, so I estimate -4000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

14253.text:
Latitude -69, longitude -113 is in the Amundsen-Bellingshausen sector of West Antarctica. The Bellingshausen Sea lies at roughly -70 latitude, and the coast near -113 longitude is the Abbot Ice Shelf region, which spans about -100 to -103 longitude and sits further east. At -113 and -69 the area is probably open ocean or sea ice over the continental shelf. The Amundsen Sea Embayment's western edge is near -115 to -130 at latitude -74, so -69 is well north of the coast.

The continental shelf there is deep, with depths around 500 to 1000 m, and further north the slope descends to the abyssal plain. At -69 and -113 the seabed is probably about -1500 m or somewhat deeper, since the shelf break is near -70 to -71 in the Bellingshausen Sea. I'll estimate -1800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

2891.text:
The point is 57.0°N, 157.0°W. That lies south of the Alaska Peninsula, which runs from roughly 55°N at 160°W to about 58°N at 157°W. The peninsula's Pacific coast near 157°W is around 56.5–57°N, close to Chignik and Port Heiden, and Port Heiden is at 56.9°N, 158.6°W on the Bering side. The Pacific coast near Chignik is at 56.3°N, 158.4°W. At 157°W the coast runs northeast, with Kujulik Bay and Aniakchak near 56.9°N, 158.2°W. Further east, Cape Kumlik lies near 57.6°N, 156.5°W, and Ugashik Lake and the Pacific coast near Cape Kumliun are around 57.1°N, 156.5°W. I'm not certain, but the Pacific side of the peninsula near 157°W seems to sit around 57.0°N, so the point could be just offshore in Shelikof Strait, or on land near the coast.

Kodiak Island is at 57.0°N but spans 152–154.5°W, so it doesn't apply here. The Shelikof Strait runs between Kodiak and the peninsula, so at 157°W the coast is probably around 57.5°N. That puts 57.0°N, 157°W in the Pacific, southeast of the coast. Shelf depths there are about 100–200 m, with the Shelikof Strait trough deeper to the east. I'll guess it is water at about -120 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-120}

9294.text:
Latitude -13.0, longitude 49.0 lies in the Indian Ocean, northeast of Madagascar. Madagascar's northern tip (Cap d'Ambre) is at about 12.0°S, 49.3°E, and the northeast coast near Vohemar is at about 13.4°S, 50.0°E. At 49.0°E, 13°S, the point is on the northwest side of Madagascar, west of Antsiranana. Antsiranana is at 12.3°S, 49.3°E, and the west coast runs south from there, with Ampasindava Bay and Nosy Be around 13.3°S, 48.2°E. At 13°S the coast on the west side is around 48.5°E, so 49.0°E is inland.

The land there is the Sambirano/Ambilobe region, where the Tsaratanana massif begins to the south. Ambilobe is at 13.2°S, 49.05°E, and its elevation is about 30 m, so this point is near Ambilobe in the Mahavavy valley. The surrounding terrain is lowland, so I estimate about 60 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":60}

15834.text:
Latitude -85, longitude 169 lies in Antarctica, in the Ross Sea sector near the Ross Ice Shelf edge and the Transantarctic Mountains. The Beardmore Glacier is around 83°S, 171°E. At 85°S, 169°E it is probably on the polar plateau side, or on glaciers flowing down from it such as the Shackleton, Liv, or Beardmore. The Shackleton Glacier is at about 84.5°S, 177°E. The Liv Glacier is around 84°S, 167°W. The Beardmore sits around 82-84°S, 170°E.

At 85°S, 169°E the Ross Ice Shelf's southern limit is about 85°S at roughly 170°E, near the Beardmore's mouth at the shelf. The Beardmore mouth is around 83.5°S, so 85°S is further up the glacier, which is grounded ice. The Beardmore's upper reaches near 85°S, 170°E are at about 2000 m elevation. The Mount Kirkpatrick area is at 84.3°S, 166°E. Near 85°S, 169°E, the glacier surface is perhaps 1500-2000 m. The Beardmore at the Cloudmaker is around 2000 m. I'll pick 1800.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1800}

4764.text:
The point 37.0°N, 11.0°W lies in the Atlantic Ocean, west of Cape St. Vincent (about 9°W) and southwest of Portugal. It is near the Gorringe Bank, which sits at roughly 36.5°N, 11.5°W. The bank rises to about -25 m at its summit, but its area is small. It is an elongated ridge that runs northeast-southwest, with the Ormonde seamount at 36.7°N, 11.2°W and Gettysburg at 36.5°N, 11.57°W.

At 37.0°N, 11.0°W we are probably on the northern flank of the bank. North of the bank is the Tagus Abyssal Plain, at about -5000 m, and the Horseshoe Abyssal Plain lies to the south at about -4800 m. The bank's northern flank drops steeply from about -3000 m to -5000 m. At 37.0°N the position is about 0.3° north of Ormonde, which is roughly 33 km away. That puts it on the flank, possibly around -3000 m. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

11657.text:
Latitude -39, longitude 95 is in the southeastern Indian Ocean, southwest of Australia and east of Amsterdam Island. The Southeast Indian Ridge lies further south, and the Ninetyeast Ridge runs along about 90E. At 95E and 39S the seafloor is probably the abyssal plain east of the Ninetyeast Ridge, which suggests a depth of roughly 4,500 to 5,000 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value. The Wharton Basin lies to the north, and the Australian-Antarctic Basin begins south of the ridge.

Near 39S the ocean floor around 95E is likely around 4,300 m deep, since the Southeast Indian Ridge crest sits near 40S at 78E but moves south toward 50S at 115E. At 95E the ridge is around 45S, so 39S is on the flank. Flank depth would be about 3,500 to 4,000 m. I'll use -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

2438.text:
The coordinates 63.0°N, 17.0°E fall in Sweden, in Västernorrland/Jämtland county. This is inland, west of the Ångermanälven area and near Sollefteå/Junsele. Sollefteå is at about 63.17°N, 17.27°E, and the terrain there is hilly forest. The Ångermanälven river is near 17.3°E.

The point at 63.0°N, 17.0°E is roughly 30 km south-southwest of Sollefteå, in the Ådalen highlands. Elevation there is likely about 250 m. I could not rule out a lake at this exact point, but I'll assume land.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":250}

848.text:
Latitude 81.0N, longitude 77.0E. That places it in the Kara Sea region north of Taymyr, near the Franz Josef Land and Severnaya Zemlya area. Severnaya Zemlya spans roughly 78-81.3N and 95-106E, so 77E is well west of it. Franz Josef Land spans 80-81.8N and 44-65E. At 77E, 81N lies in the northern Kara Sea, northeast of Franz Josef Land and west of Severnaya Zemlya. Ice islands are unlikely there, though Ushakov Island is at about 80.8N and 79E. Ushakov Island is at 80.78N, 79.5E, which is about 2.5° of longitude away. At 81N, 1° of longitude is about 17 km, so that is roughly 40 km. So the point is probably water.

Depth there: the northern Kara Sea shelf is around 200-400 m deep. Near the St. Anna Trough (at roughly 70-80E at 81N), the depth is more than 400 m. The trough axis is near 78-80E at 81N, so depths are probably around 300-500 m. I'll say -330.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-330}

4843.text:
The point 37°N, 147°E lies in the western Pacific, east of Japan (Honshu's coast is near 141°E). That puts it in open ocean, east of the Japan Trench, which is near 143-144°E at this latitude. The seafloor of the Pacific plate there is abyssal plain, around 5500-6000 m deep.

Checking the surroundings: the Japan Trench axis at 37°N is at about 143.5°E with a depth of roughly 7500 m. East of it, the outer rise and abyssal plain sit at about 5500-6000 m. Near 147°E, 37°N, the Japanese Seamount Chain and the Mid-Pacific Mountains are farther south and east, and the Joban seamounts lie near 35-36°N, 145-146°E. At 147°E the depth is probably about 5800 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5800}

6847.text:
The point 13°N, 165°W lies in the central Pacific, south of the Hawaiian Islands and north of Palmyra and Kingman Reef. Nearby islands are Johnston Atoll at 16.7°N, 169.5°W, and Palmyra at 5.9°N, 162°W, so neither is close. The point is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is Pacific abyssal plain, typically 5000 to 5500 m deep. Seafloor age is Cretaceous, so I'd expect roughly 5400 m of depth. The area sits north of the Line Islands and west of the Hawaiian Ridge, in the Central Pacific Basin, where depths are around 5300 to 5600 m. I'll estimate -5450 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5450}

2226.text:
Latitude 65.0°N, longitude -47.0° lies in southern Greenland, inland of the west coast. Nuuk is at 64.2°N, 51.7°W, so this point is about 250 km east of Nuuk. That is well inside the ice sheet, where the surface is roughly 2000 m or more. At 47°W and 65°N the ice sheet surface is about 2400 m.

The east coast near Kulusuk is at about 37°W. At 65°N the ice divide on the southern dome is around 44–45°W, with elevations of about 2500 m. At 47°W the surface should be a bit lower, near 2400 m, so I estimate 2400 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":2400}

12525.text:
The point is at 49°S, 31°E, in the southwest Indian Ocean. The Marion and Prince Edward Islands sit near 46.9°S, 37.7°E, so this point is well to their west and south. The Southwest Indian Ridge runs through the area, crossing roughly 30°E near 52°S, and the Del Cano Rise lies around 45°S, 45°E. At 49°S, 31°E I expect the seafloor to be on the flank of the ridge or the Crozet Basin margin.

The Atlantic-Indian Basin lies south of the Del Cano Rise at about 4000-5000 m depth. The ridge axis near 31°E is at roughly 53°S, and 49°S would be north of it. Depths there are likely around 3000-4000 m, so I estimate about -3600 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

10856.text:
Latitude -31, longitude -67 is in Argentina, near the border of La Rioja, Catamarca and San Juan provinces. It lies in the Llanos region, the plains west of the Sierras de Córdoba and east of the Sierra de Velasco and the Pampeanas ranges.

At about -31, -67 the area is probably the Salinas Grandes or the Bolsón de Pipanaco region. The Salinas Grandes depression sits around -30.5, -65 at roughly 200 m. The Sierra de Valle Fértil is near -30.5, -67.5. The Bermejo valley lies west of it at about 600 m. At -31, -67 the terrain is likely the plains east of the Sierra de Valle Fértil, in the Llanos de La Rioja and the Mascasín area, at roughly 450 m. This is an estimate rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":480}

10373.text:
Latitude -25.0, longitude 47.0 is in the Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar. Madagascar's southern tip, Cap Sainte Marie, is around -25.6, 45.1, so 47.0E is about 2 degrees east of it, in the ocean.

Bathymetry here is the Madagascar Basin / the southern Madagascar Ridge area. The Madagascar Ridge runs south from the southern tip of Madagascar at roughly 44-47E, with depths around 1000-2500 m on the ridge. At -25, 47E the point is probably east of the ridge's crest, on its eastern flank or in the basin. The Madagascar Basin to the east is around 4500-5000 m deep, and the ridge itself is around 2500 m. I estimate about -3500 m for this point.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3500}

8830.text:
Location: -9.0, -159.0 is in the central Pacific, east of the Tokelau Islands and near Suwarrow (13°S, 163°W) and Manihiki (10.4°S, 161°W). Penrhyn (Tongareva) sits at 9.0°S, 158.05°W, so this point is about 1° west of Penrhyn (roughly 100 km), which puts it in open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is the Penrhyn Basin, with typical depths of about 5000 to 5500 m. I'll estimate -5200 m.

{"type": "Water", "elevation_m": -5200}

15806.text:
The point is at 85°S, 113°E, which lies in East Antarctica on the polar plateau, south of Vostok (78.5°S, 106.8°E). Vostok station sits at about 3488 m. Moving toward the pole along 113°E, the ice surface stays high. Dome A is at 80.4°S, 77°E with about 4090 m, and the area near 85°S, 113°E is probably around 3000–3400 m.

From memory of the BEDMAP/REMA surface, the region near 85°S, 110°E is roughly 3300 m. Because the surface slopes up toward Dome A and down toward the pole, I'll estimate 3250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3250}

5357.text:
Latitude 31.0 N, longitude 95.0 E is in eastern Tibet, in the Chamdo/Nagqu area, near the Zogang and Bianba (Baxoi) region. Elevations there are around 4,000 to 5,000 m.

The location is probably near the Salween (Nu) River basin. The Salween at about 31N is around 95E near Bianba (Pembar), where the river valley sits at roughly 3,500 to 4,000 m and the surrounding mountains are higher. Lhorong is at about 31N, 95.8E, with an elevation near 3,300 m. At 95E the point is west of Lhorong, near Dengqen (Dêngqên), which is at about 31.4N, 95.5E and about 3,900 m. A typical elevation there is about 4,500 m, so I'll estimate 4,600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":4600}

7231.text:
The point (9°N, 117°W) is in the eastern Pacific, roughly 1,500 km southwest of Baja California and north of the Clipperton Fracture Zone. This area is on the Pacific plate, west of the East Pacific Rise, which sits near 105°W at this latitude. The seafloor here is about 10-15 Myr old and typically lies at 3,500-4,000 m depth. Seafloor at 117°W would be around 25 Myr old, so a depth of roughly 3,900-4,000 m is reasonable.

I'll estimate about -3,950 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3950}

4406.text:
Latitude 41.0, longitude -7.0 is in northern Portugal, in the Trás-os-Montes region. It lies near Vila Real, which is at about 41.3 N, -7.74 W, and Mirandela, which is at about 41.48 N, -7.18 W. The point is south of Mirandela, close to the Douro region around Vila Nova de Foz Côa (41.08 N, -7.2 W) and Carrazeda de Ansiães (41.23 N, -7.3 W).

Torre de Moncorvo is at 41.17 N, -7.05 W, so the point is about 20 km south of it. That puts it near Freixo de Espada à Cinta or the Douro valley, and perhaps around Mogadouro. The Douro river at Foz Côa is at about 100 m, and the Douro at -7.0 is around 41.0 N near Pocinho, where it sits at about 120 m. The surrounding plateau is about 500-700 m. The point is near Vila Nova de Foz Côa and the Sabor confluence, at around 41.0 N, -7.0 W, which is south of Torre de Moncorvo and east of Foz Côa. The Douro runs near 41.13 N here, so 41.0 is south of the river on the plateau, probably 500-600 m. I'll estimate 550 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":550}

14870.text:
Latitude -75, longitude 41 is in East Antarctica, in Queen Maud Land / Enderby Land. The coast there lies near 69-70°S, so -75 is well inland, roughly 600 km from the coast. The area is south of the Shirase Glacier region, and the Wilkes/Dronning Maud Land plateau reaches about 3000 m or more. At 75°S, 41°E the ice surface should be around 2800-3200 m, so I'll estimate about 3000 m.

The surface is grounded ice, so it counts as Solid.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":3000}

6467.text:
Latitude 19.0 N, longitude 155.0 W (assuming western longitude, since 155.0 E would be in the Pacific as well). Taking 155.0 W, this is just off the Big Island of Hawaii. The island's east coast near Hilo is at about 19.7 N, 155.0 W. At 19.0 N, 155.0 W, the point lies south-southeast of the island, which has its southern tip (Ka Lae) at 18.91 N, 155.68 W. The Puna coast around 155.0 W is at about 19.3 N. So 19.0 N, 155.0 W is in the ocean, about 35 km south of the Puna coast, on the submarine flank of Kilauea.

The depth there is on the Hilina slump and the submarine slope. The island's flank descends to about 5000 m at the base. About 35 km offshore, the depth is likely around 4000-5000 m. The Hawaiian moat lies about 100 km out at roughly 5000 m. I estimate about -4500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

9404.text:
Latitude -15, longitude -91 is in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, roughly 1,500 km west of Peru. It lies west of the Peru Basin and south of the Galápagos region, so this is open deep ocean.

The seafloor in this area is likely about 4,000 m deep. Near 15°S and 91°W the crust is young, from the East Pacific Rise flank, and the ocean floor there is around 3,800–4,200 m deep. The Nazca plate seafloor at this longitude is roughly 4,000 m deep, so I estimate -3,900 m. This is an estimate from regional bathymetry, not a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

12464.text:
Latitude -49, longitude -91 lies in the southeast Pacific Ocean, west of southern Chile. The Chile Rise and the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge are far to the west and south, and the Chile Trench is near -74. At -91 longitude and -49 latitude, the Chile Rise (the spreading ridge) runs roughly from the triple junction at about -46.5, -75.5 southwest to the Chile Transform fault (Chile Fracture Zone) near -75 to -90 around latitude -37 to -40. Further south, the Chile Rise segments run southwest toward about -60, -115 or so. So -49, -91 is likely seafloor of the Nazca/Antarctic plate, with an older seafloor depth around 3500-4000 m.

The ridge axis near -49 is probably near longitude -95 to -100, since the Chile Rise offsets at the Darwin, Taitao, and Guamblin fracture zones. That puts this point near the ridge flank, with a depth of about 3000-3500 m. I'll estimate -3400 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3400}

12766.text:
The point -51.0, 153.0 is in the Pacific Ocean southeast of Australia, east of Tasmania and south of the Tasman Sea. It lies near the southern Tasman Sea / Southwest Pacific Basin, where the seabed is around 4,500 to 5,000 m deep.

The Macquarie Ridge is at about 159°E, well to the east, and the Tasmanian abyssal plain at 51°S, 153°E should be roughly 4,500 m deep. This is an estimate from the regional bathymetry rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4600}

6769.text:
Latitude 15.0 N, longitude 39.0 E is in Eritrea, a little south of Asmara (15.3 N, 38.9 E) and near Dekemhare (15.07 N, 39.05 E). This is on the highland plateau, where elevations are about 2000 m. Dekemhare itself sits at roughly 1900 m. Just east of this point the escarpment drops steeply toward the Red Sea, so the exact point could be slightly lower or higher.

I estimate about 1900 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":1900}

10569.text:
The point (-27.0, 79.0) is in the southern Indian Ocean, southeast of Madagascar and west of Australia. It lies roughly at the latitude of Amsterdam Island's longitude band, but well east of it (Amsterdam Island is near 77.5°E, 37.8°S), so this point is about 11° of latitude north of it.

The Central Indian Basin and the Ninetyeast Ridge are the nearby features. The Ninetyeast Ridge sits near 90°E, so 79°E is well west of it. The Southeast Indian Ridge lies further south. The seabed here is most likely abyssal plain in the Central Indian Basin or the Western Australian Basin, with depths around 4,500 to 5,500 m. I estimate about -5,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

11092.text:
Latitude -33, longitude 45 is in the southwest Indian Ocean, south of Africa and southeast of the Madagascar Basin. This area lies between the Crozet Plateau to the south and the Southwest Indian Ridge to the southwest.

The Southwest Indian Ridge near 45°E runs at roughly 40°S or further south, with the Del Caño Rise around 45°E, 44°S. At -33 the seafloor is probably the Madagascar Basin or Mozambique Basin margin, with depths around 4500-5000 m. The Madagascar Ridge lies at about 45°E at 27-33°S, so this point could sit on it. The Madagascar Ridge runs from Madagascar south to about 33°S at roughly 45-46°E, with depths of about 1000-2500 m. Its southern part near 33°S (the Walters Shoal is at 33°S, 43.8°E, with a shallow summit of about 18 m) is a good anchor. At 45°E the ridge is probably around 2000-2500 m deep. I'll estimate -2300.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-2300}

3054.text:
Latitude 57.0°N, longitude 169.0°E is in the western Bering Sea, east of Kamchatka and south of the Commander Islands. I took the longitude as east because none was specified.

The Commander Islands lie near 55°N, 166°E, and the Aleutian Basin is to the north. The Kamchatka coast sits near 163°E at this latitude, so 169°E is well offshore. This area is the Komandorsky Basin or the Shirshov Ridge region. The Shirshov Ridge runs north from about 56°N, 170°E, and the Bowers Ridge is farther east, so the point could be on the edge of the Shirshov Ridge. The Komandorsky Basin to the west is about 3,800 m deep, and the Aleutian Basin is about 3,800 m deep. At 57°N, 169°E the point is likely in the Komandorsky Basin, so I estimate a depth of about 3,700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

15671.text:
Latitude -85, longitude -157 is in West Antarctica, near the Ross Ice Shelf's southern part or the Transantarctic Mountains' edge. The Ross Ice Shelf extends to about 85°S at longitudes near 180 to -150. At -157 longitude and -85 latitude, this is probably on the Ross Ice Shelf, near the Reedy Glacier/Horlick area. Reedy Glacier is around -85.5, -135. Longitude -157 at 85°S is likely the Ross Ice Shelf's eastern part, near the Kamb Ice Stream and Siple Coast. The grounding line of the Ross Ice Shelf near 85°S at -157... The shelf's southern edge reaches about 85°S around 180 to -160 (near the Scott/Amundsen glaciers at about -150 to -170 longitude and -85 latitude). Beardmore is at 170E. Near -157 and 85°S would be Leverett Glacier area (-146, -86) and Liv Glacier (-165, -84). So the point is likely in the ice shelf, near the Transantarctic Mountains front. Bed beneath the shelf there is about -600 m.

Ice shelf is floating, so the answer is seabed elevation. I estimate around -700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-700}

13560.text:
Latitude -61, longitude -59 lies in the Bransfield Strait / South Shetland Islands region. The South Shetlands run roughly from 61°S to 63.5°S, with Elephant Island at about 61.1°S, 55.2°W. So -61.0, -59.0 is just northwest of Elephant Island, west of it by about 3.8° of longitude. That puts it north of King George Island (62°S), in the Drake Passage / Scotia Sea area, so it is water.

The depth there is probably around 3000 m. Between the South Shetland Trench and the Drake Passage, the northern side of the South Shetlands has a trench reaching about 5000 m near 62.5°S, 60°W. At 61°S, 59°W, the point is north of the islands and the shelf, roughly 2500 to 3500 m deep. I'll estimate -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

139.text:
Latitude 89.0°N, longitude 99°E is in the central Arctic Ocean. It lies near the Lomonosov Ridge, which runs close to the pole, though the ridge sits closer to longitudes 140°E to 50°W, depending on where it crosses.

At 89°N, 99°E the point is on the Eurasian side of the ridge, which would put it in the Amundsen Basin. The Amundsen Basin floor is about 4,200 m deep. The Lomonosov Ridge crest is about 1,000 to 1,500 m deep. Near the pole the ridge runs roughly along the 140°E to 40°W line. At 89°N, 99°E the point is about 1 degree of latitude (about 111 km) from the pole. The ridge at 90°N is at roughly 140°E, so the point is offset by about 41° of longitude, which at 89°N is about 78 km. That puts it on the ridge flank or in the Amundsen Basin edge. The ridge is about 60 km wide, so the point is probably on the flank. I estimate a depth of about 3,500 m, though the true value could be a little deeper or shallower.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3900}

8142.text:
The point (-1.0, -95.0) is in the eastern equatorial Pacific, roughly 600 km south of the Galápagos and west of the Ecuador coast, so the surface is water. The seabed there is part of the Cocos/Nazca plate area south of the Galápagos platform, which sits near the Carnegie Ridge to the east.

The Carnegie Ridge runs from the Galápagos toward Ecuador, roughly along 0 to -2° latitude, and is shallower than the surrounding basin. Its depths are about 2000 m near the Galápagos and about 2500 to 3000 m toward the east. At 95°W the ridge is close to the Galápagos western edge, around 91°W, so this point is about 400 km west of that edge. That puts it in the Panama Basin and the area west of the ridge, where depths are typically 3000 to 3500 m. Seafloor of this age (about 5 to 8 Myr) near the East Pacific Rise flank would be near 3000 m deep, so I estimate about -3000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3000}

4377.text:
Location 41.0N, 65.0W is in the Atlantic Ocean, south of Nova Scotia, on the continental shelf/Georges Bank region. Georges Bank lies around 41N, 67-68W. At 65W, 41N, it is just southeast of the Northeast Channel (Northeast Channel is around 42N, 65.5W). At 41N, 65W the water is on the slope south of Georges Bank, with depth probably around 200-1000 m. The shelf edge is around 41.0-41.5N at 65W; the shelf break there is roughly 100 m depth near 41.5N. At 41N the slope is perhaps 1000-2000 m deep.

Georges Bank's southern edge near 66W is at about 41.2N, and the slope descends steeply to the south. At 65W the shelf edge is near 41.7N, so at 41.0N the depth is about 1500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-1800}

10159.text:
Latitude -23, longitude -21 is in the South Atlantic Ocean, west of Africa and east of Brazil. This is near the Mid-Atlantic Ridge region? The ridge is around 14°W at this latitude, so -21 lies in the Brazil Basin's eastern flank, west of the ridge. The Rio Grande Rise is farther west at about -30 to -35 longitude, at -30 latitude. At -23, -21, the seafloor is about 4,000 to 4,500 m deep.

The ridge axis near 23°S is around 13°W. At 21°W, which is 8° away (about 800 km), the crust is roughly 15 Myr old, so the depth is about 3,800 to 4,200 m. I'll estimate -4,000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4000}

9015.text:
The point at latitude -11, longitude -149 is in the South Pacific, northeast of Tahiti and south of the Marquesas. The nearest islands are the Tuamotus, which lie further south and west, around -15 to -17 latitude, so this point is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is Pacific abyssal plain, probably 4000 to 5000 m deep. The area between the Marquesas and the Tuamotus, near the Marquesas Fracture Zone, has seafloor of roughly 4000 to 4500 m. The crust is about 50 to 60 million years old, which would give a depth of around 4500 to 4700 m. I'll estimate -4400 m, which is an estimate rather than a looked-up value.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4400}

9191.text:
Latitude -13, longitude -157 is in the central South Pacific, west of the Cook Islands' northern atolls and south of the Line Islands, roughly southeast of Samoa. The nearest land is Manihiki (about -10.4, -161) and Suwarrow, so this point is open ocean.

The seafloor in this area is Pacific abyssal plain, typically about 5000 to 5500 m deep. The Manihiki Plateau lies to the northwest, around -11, -163, and is shallower. At -157, -13, the depth is probably about 5200 m, so I estimate the elevation at roughly -5100 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5100}

3393.text:
Latitude 53.0°N, longitude 127.0°E lies in the Amur Oblast of Russia, in the Zeya-Bureya region. The Zeya River and the Zeya Reservoir are further north, near 54°N, 127°E. At 53°N, 127°E, the area is near Magdagachi and Zeya, in the Amur region. Magdagachi sits at about 53.45°N, 125.8°E. The Selemdzha River is around 52.5–53°N.

Terrain here is lowland with hills. Elevation is probably about 300 m, though the Zeya reservoir spans roughly 53.5–54.5°N, so this point is south of it. Near 53°N, 127°E the Zeya River flows south at about 127.3°E, and Zeya town is at 53.75°N, so this point is a bit south of the reservoir and dam. Elevation is likely about 250 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":260}

3894.text:
The point 47.0°N, 49.0°E lies in the northern Caspian Sea region of Kazakhstan, in the Caspian Depression. The northern coast of the Caspian is around 46.5-47°N at 49°E. The Volga delta sits near 46°N at 48-49°E, and the Kazakhstan coast near Atyrau is at 47°N, 52°E.

At 49°E and 47°N, the point is probably land, northeast of the Volga delta, in the Astrakhan/Atyrau area. The coastline at 49°E is around 45.5-46°N, so 47°N is inland. The Caspian Depression is below sea level there, around -25 m. Astrakhan, at 46.3°N and 48°E, is at about -23 m. Moving north to 47°N at 49°E, the elevation is probably around -20 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":-22}

7440.text:
Latitude 7.0, longitude -59.0 is in Guyana, in the interior. It lies southwest of the Essequibo near the Mazaruni and Cuyuni region. Roughly, the Essequibo at 7°N runs around -58.6, and the area near -59.0, 7.0 is close to the Mazaruni/Potaro region.

The terrain there is probably forested lowland hills or the edge of the Guiana Shield, at an elevation of about 100 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":100}

1394.text:
Latitude 75°N, longitude 89°E falls in the Taymyr Peninsula region. The Kara Sea coast is near 75°N at about 90°E. Taymyr's northern coast runs from Cape Chelyuskin (77.7°N, 104°E) west. At 89°E, the coast lies around 75.5°N, near the Taymyr Bay / Yenisei Gulf area. Around 89°E, the Minina Skerries are at 77°N, 90°E. The Taymyr coast near 75°N, 89°E is Lake Taymyr? No, that is at 74.5°N, 101°E. The Gulf of Yenisei lies further west at 80°E.

On the west side of Taymyr, the coast near 75°N runs about 87°E (Cape Sibiryakov, Tsentralnaya station). Around 89°E there is Tsvetkov Bay, and Taymyr Bay between 75–76°N, with the coast near 75.5°N. So 75.0°N, 89°E is likely land, just south of the coast, in the Byrranga foothills or the lowlands. The elevation is probably about 30 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":30}

6721.text:
Latitude 15°N, longitude 57°W lies in the western tropical Atlantic, east of the Lesser Antilles (Barbados is at 59.5°W, 13°N). The point is therefore open ocean, on the abyssal plain of the Demerara Plain region. Water depth there is about 5000 m.

The Demerara Abyssal Plain lies around 10-14°N, 50-55°W at roughly 4800-5000 m. At 15°N, 57°W the seabed is probably near 5200 m, which is a bit deeper, partly because the Puerto Rico Trench lies farther north at about 19-20°N. Depths near 15°N, 57°W are likely around 5100 m, so I'll estimate -5000 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5000}

14413.text:
Latitude -71, longitude -153 lies in West Antarctica, in the Amundsen/Ross Sea sector. Marie Byrd Land's coast runs near -77 at this longitude, so -71 would be offshore in the Amundsen Sea, north of the Getz Ice Shelf region. The Getz Ice Shelf extends to about -74 at its seaward front, so -71 is open ocean on the continental shelf edge or slope.

The continental shelf break is around -73 to -74 in this sector, so at -71 we should be north of the shelf edge on the slope or rise. Hmm, the shelf in the Amundsen Sea lies at about -73 to -74 and the Marie Byrd Land coast at about -75 to -77. At -153 longitude, the Getz Ice Shelf front is around -74. So -71 is beyond the shelf edge, likely on the slope at about 3000 m depth, or perhaps the abyssal plain at 4000+ m. I estimate -3900 m, but the Marie Byrd Seamounts are further north, around -67 to -70 at longitudes -120 to -150, so there could be some rise in the seafloor. I'll go with about -3600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3600}

6967.text:
The coordinates 13.0°N, 75.0°E fall in Karnataka, India, near Chikkamagaluru and Hassan, on the Deccan plateau. This is inland, south of Shimoga and east of the Western Ghats. Tarikere sits at about 13.7°N, 75.8°E. Kadur is at about 13.55°N, 76.0°E. Sringeri is at about 13.42°N, 75.25°E. Mudigere is at about 13.13°N, 75.64°E. Dharmasthala is at about 12.95°N, 75.38°E. Charmadi Ghat is around 13.05°N, 75.45°E.

The point at 13.0°N, 75.0°E lies west of Dharmasthala, toward Mangalore (12.9°N, 74.85°E). It is probably near Bantwal or Moodabidri, about 25 km inland. Moodabidri is at 13.07°N, 74.99°E, with an elevation of about 100 m. Laterite terrain there sits at roughly 80 to 150 m, so I estimate about 90 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":90}

8637.text:
The point -5.0, 175.0 lies in the central Pacific. It is southwest of the Gilbert Islands (Tarawa is at about 1.4°N, 173°E) and near Banaba, which sits at about 0.9°S, 169.5°E. It is probably close to the area south of Nikunau and Onotoa, but those islands are at about 1.5°S and 1.8°S, 176°E, so the point is farther south than any of them. Arorae is at about 2.6°S, 176.8°E, and Tamana is at about 2.5°S, 175.97°E. Both are well north of -5.0, so this point is open ocean.

The seafloor in this region is deep. The Gilbert Ridge is a chain of seamounts, and the surrounding abyssal plain is about 4,500–5,500 m deep. The ocean floor near the Ellice Basin, to the south, is about 5,000 m deep. I estimate about -4,900 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4900}

5220.text:
The point is 31°N, 179°W, in the North Pacific. That is far from land, northeast of the Kermadec region and well north of New Zealand, in the northwest Pacific basin near the Hawaiian-Emperor chain region.

The Emperor seamounts lie around 170°E and westward, and the Hawaiian chain runs to Midway at 28°N, 177°W. At 31°N, 179°W the point is north of the Hawaiian ridge and likely on abyssal plain at about 5,500 m depth. Pacific plate seafloor of this age (Cretaceous) generally sits at 5,500–6,000 m, so I estimate about -5,600 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5600}

13290.text:
The point at 57°S, 121°E is in the Southern Ocean, south of Australia. It lies in the region of the Australian-Antarctic Basin, south of the Southeast Indian Ridge.

The Southeast Indian Ridge runs roughly from 50°S at 110°E to about 49°S at 135°E, so 57°S is well south of it. The Australian-Antarctic Basin there is about 4000 m deep, and abyssal plains in this area are around 4000–4500 m. The Antarctic margin is near 66°S, so this point is in the middle of the basin. I estimate a depth of about 4300 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-4300}

12150.text:
The point is at 45°S, 1°E, in the South Atlantic/Southern Ocean southwest of Africa. It lies near the Southwest Indian Ridge and the Atlantic-Indian Ridge. The ridge runs roughly from Bouvet (54°S, 3°E) northeast toward the Indian Ocean (around 40°S, 20°E), so 45°S, 1°E is probably somewhat south of the ridge's east-west section. The Agulhas basin is at about 5000 m depth, and the Atlantic-Indian Basin lies south of about 45°S.

The Southwest Indian Ridge begins at the Bouvet triple junction (54°S, 1°E) and runs east-southeast. The American-Antarctic Ridge lies to the southwest. At 45°S, 1°E the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is far west at about 15°W. The point is therefore in the Atlantic-Indian Basin, with a typical depth of about 4000–5000 m. Two features nearby are the Islas Orcadas Rise (around 50°S, 6°E) and the Meteor Rise (around 50°S, 3°E). The Meteor Rise is a plateau that extends from roughly 46°S to 53°S, with depths of 2000–3000 m, so 45°S may sit at its northern edge. I'll estimate about -3700 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3700}

13263.text:
The point at 57°S, 67°E lies in the southern Indian Ocean. It is southwest of the Kerguelen Plateau, which sits at roughly 50°S, 70°E. Heard Island is at 53°S, 73°E, so this point is well south and west of it.

The region between the Kerguelen Plateau and Antarctica includes the Princess Elizabeth Trough and the Southern Kerguelen Plateau. The plateau's southern part runs from about 55°S to 59°S at around 75–80°E. At 67°E, 57°S, the point is west of the plateau. This is likely the Enderby Basin or the Australian-Antarctic basin's western part, with depths of about 3,500–4,500 m. The Indian-Antarctic Ridge lies further west, around 55–60°S and 60–70°E, and the Southwest Indian Ridge sits near 50°S at 60°E. The Indian-Antarctic Ridge's depths there are about 3,000 m. I estimate a depth of about -3,200 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-3200}

6451.text:
The point 19.0°N, 123.0°E lies in the Philippine Sea, east of Luzon. Luzon's northeast coast is near 122.2°E at this latitude, so 123°E is about 80 km offshore. The Philippine Sea floor east of northern Luzon is deep. The Philippine Trench starts around 18-19°N, but it lies farther east, near 126-127°E. At 123°E, 19°N the seafloor is probably on the slope of the Benham Rise or the Luzon forearc area, or in the Philippine Basin.

East of Luzon at 19°N, the East Luzon Trough runs near 122.5-123°E with depths of about 5000-6000 m. I'll estimate around -5500 m.

{"type":"Water","elevation_m":-5400}

9246.text:
Latitude -13, longitude -47 falls in Brazil, in the state of Goiás or Bahia, near the Tocantins border. This is the Planalto region (Cerrado) around the Rio Paranã area. At -13, -47, the point is likely near Nova Roma or Cavalcante in Goiás, or close to Posse. The Paranã valley is around 400-500 m, and the plateau to the west runs about 700-900 m.

Longitude -47 sits east of Brasília (-47.9). Latitude -13 is north of Brasília (-15.8), close to Alto Paraíso (-14.1, -47.5). Going to -13, -47, the area is near Nova Roma/Divinópolis, around Rio Paranã and the Serra Geral, with elevation roughly 500-600 m. Terrain here is the Paranã valley floor at about 450 m, while Serra Geral to the east is about 800 m. I'll estimate 600 m.

{"type":"Solid","elevation_m":600}
